When we grab a cup of wine, we bring it to the nose, we start to sense the aromas that we describe as red fruits, mint, leather, the mountains of Cordoba, the mountains of wherever, this is the romantic and beautiful vocabulary used in the world of wine.
What happens if we get to gather with friends, open a bottle of wine, don't really feel like drinking it, leave it open and then return in a week to repeat the same occasion?
We bring the cup to the nose and the mountains of Cordoba and the red fruits are gone, instead a lemon taste has appeared, a bit of vinegar, something old, something oxidized, and automatically, the first thing that we do is to throw it away.
Because it wouldn't be suitable for human consumption.
2,500 years ago, a Greek man took that wine, and so that it wouldn't go bad, worked it through a botanical, an herb, which is wormwood.
This man, surely many of you know him, his name is Hippocrates.
If you google him, you'll find a face similar to what you'll find for Socrates on the screen.
Hippocrates is considered the father of Medicine.
And what he did was to study Botany as a way to cure different illnesses of the human body.
And he used wormwood as a natural preservative of wine and also as a digestive.
Imagine a time when there was no pharmacy, where you went to buy a blister, and took a pill after a giant feast.
It was simply more fun to take that same pill with a drink, a little cocktail, at the end of the day.
Hippocrates creates this product, but he doesn't know that he ends up creating it.
It's Galen that gives the name Hippocratic wine to this product.
He names it Hippocratic wine about 400 or 500 years afterward, because if you remember quickly, in history, the Romans conquered the Greeks, and they took part of their culture and their studies.
Galen studies Hippocrates, and gives it the name of Hippocratic wine.
And there's a fundamental event within the period of the Romans, which is that they adopt Christianity as the official religion.
So, there was something very important within Christianity, which was the Mass, the sacred Eucharist, for them.
And it's the part where they share the body and blood of Christ in the form of bread and wine.
The wine is made from grapes.
So, where there was a Roman there was a grape plant to make that wine, to be able to celebrate the Eucharist.
Thanks to the Romans the vineyards spread almost everywhere; even long after the Romans, following the wave of Catholicism, they arrived in other places, like Argentina for example, where the first grape plants arrived in the zone of Santiago del Estero with the first Jesuits.
But at the same time wine was spreading around the world, the information became more restricted, because the study remained controlled by those within the abbey.
Botany, or science, could not be studied outside of what the clergy said, and if they caught you studying what a botanical could do for you, or say, something in the human body, you practically became a heretic or a witch and you were on your way to the stake.
There's an interesting case in the world of drinks, which is a green colored drink called absinthe, if you drink it supposedly it makes you see little angels, excuse me, green fairies.
It's a complete lie!
Supposedly, what this product did was to produce a hallucination.
In reality that didn't happen, but the high alcohol content provoked that rumor.
Because absinthe was produced by someone who didn't belong to the clergy the church came out saying that it was a hallucinogenic and was diabolical.
And somehow, they were basically ruining the business because it was consumed outside the clergy.
But where there is a law, there is a loophole.
And that's where appears the figure, in 1786, in the North of Italy, in Turin, of a barman like me.
His name is Antonio Benedetto Carpano.
Carpano had a tavern where he served his wine, where there were vines, where there was wormwood, where there were botanicals at the foot of the mountains.
And he made his drink and sold it there.
Allegedly, this man was a big fan of Germanic culture and he decided to baptize his product under the name of Wermut, with "W", because in the Germanic language "Wermut" is "wormwood."
For a long time the study of Botany was prohibited, and in that moment, when it was prohibited to distil wormwood, Carpano found out a loophole of the law, and so he began to soak wormwood in wine and obtain similar results, not the hallucinations, but the characteristics that wormwood has.
Italians like the vast majority of us in this auditorium, we're children of those immigrants,
they arrived here, brought vermouth with them, and converted it into something that is culturally and ritualistically in our Argentina.
For me, something I love is the ritual that gathers us around the table.
These days, we're used to sitting at the table as if we were plugging ourselves in to charge like a phone.
We consume as if we need to load energy to carry on and we're leaving behind that wonderful ritual that gastronomy has, that the mid-day meal has, which is something that we do daily and that can give us that space, that time, that respite, in order to do that wonderful thing which is eat.
For 250 years, whoever had a recipe to make vermouth kept it under lock and key.
There are specific cases of big worldwide brands that guard their secret formula in banks, generally in Switzerland, and there are 4, 5, 7 people who have the key which is generally a fingerprint.
They have access to that secret formula which has been there for many years.
For the first time in this auditorium, there are 4,000 people.
I'm seeing the last ones at the end.
We're going to have access to some information that wasn't available before.
Hippocrates knew botanicals, but he didn't know what vermouth was about.
The Romans had wine but couldn't study botanicals.
Carpano knew the formula, but he kept it under lock and key. And you all, many of you should have a little brown paper bag. Do you have it? Yeah?
Perfect.
This is the moment to open it.
I'll tell you, we developed a recipe for vermouth, which isn't a secret, it's public, and you all have it.
It's the first time in the history of humanity that a recipe is being shared like this.
(Applause) (Cheering) And I'm going to tell you what it has
because there's quite a mix.
What do you sense, what do you smell?
Chamomile? There's no chamomile.
Pepper, coriander,
juniper.
More or less. More or less.
Clove, yeah that's right.
We made a formula, a recipe that contains, excuse me but it's a lot of words and I didn't want to forget: wormwood, anise, star anise and cumin.
Those three all have a similar profile and an anise flavor, and cumin is also known as caraway, clove, green pepper, and cinnamon as spices.
That green pepper isn't spicy, it's much more aromatic.
Oregano, rosemary, thyme, and sage as an herbaceous structure.
And that little bag that we've handed over to you, the goal or what we have in mind, is that when you arrive home, and when you find that wine that's going bad and that you're not going to drink, you can put the contents inside the bottle, or you can put it in a jar.
To that you can also add, or you should add, about 60 to 70 grams of something sweet, could be sugar, if you're feeling bold, brown sugar, and if you're feeling really bold, stevia.
Try it, brew it and add your own share, because what this recipe lacks is a bit of citrus.
We're in an area where many citrus fruits are produced, and we Argentinians fundamentally live on a base of citrus, there's no other fruits here.
Grab an orange, a lemon or a grapefruit, peel it, or if you're with somebody, mix it up and try each one.
Let it brew about 15 days, preferably in a fridge, and try it as you go.
Afterwards, filter it and it's ready to consume.
We're in a time when everything is pre-made.
We go to the supermarket and grab a bottle we're going to uncork or open 15 minutes later to mix it with a juice, that in general, is manufactured.
It's good to know that there are processes that last much longer than an Instagram story.
It's good to know there are things that take much longer to make.
Six years ago, I began to make craft vermouth.
But I didn't start by going to the supermarket and buying a bunch of things
to start with. Instead, I took the time to find somebody who knew about Botany, who knew about wine, to begin mixing things, to make the first test batches.
The person who we developed it with lives in Mercedes, in province of Buenos Aires.
And he mailed me flasks that would go to Buenos Aires, and then to Rosario, and I would drive to the station to get the flask, try it, taste the flavor, and then tell him:
"Hey, Nico, I didn't like this."
And back went the flask, more or less for the same route.
And we passed around two and a half years in arriving at a formula.
Today in Argentina, there are four craft producers, in Rio Negro, in Buenos Aires, in Mendoza and in Rosario.
If 1 % of all the people here are inspired to start making craft vermouth, that would be 40 people, which multiplied by 10 of the number of current craft vermouth producers that we have now.
It's something so beautiful and wonderful to gather around a table to prepare and serve a drink to somebody.
Vermouth has the capacity, because of its botanicals and herbs of whetting the appetite and also of quenching it as a digestive.
I propose to you all that when you arrive home, that you make it, and in 15 days that you call your friends, your family, and have the experience of making something analog, which sometimes, is much better.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
One night I had a dream where León was left without his mom. And his grandmother, Marita, and I would have to take care of León and raise him.
Sonsoles, León's mother and my goddaughter had a problem in her kidneys.
She already had a kidney transplant, a kidney that was transplanted in 2001. And with León's pregnancy this kidney began to deteriorate.
Her overall health began to worsen and little by little it became clear that she would need a new transplant.
Then I made myself this question: Is this my turn?
I am her godmother, I'm single,
I don't have children, I'm healthy.
Does this mean that I can, that it's me?
Does this mean I have to offer myself?
I think that this question was already a one-way journey, but I didn't say anything out loud.
At the next meeting we had with Federico Cicora, Sonsoles' nephrologist, -Sonsoles was not present, but Marita that was there like mute, just observing-
"What's necessary?"
"What's required to be able to donate a kidney?"
To which Federico replied, "Well, first you have to have the same blood type."
O positive, O positive.
And then you have to give a blood sample and the necessary "cross-match" test have to be performed to assess compatibility levels. To find out if it's possible or not.
This started and I felt like I was riding on a huge toboggan I got into without breaks.
It was like having already made myself the question or putting myself in that place meant already -- I'm in this journey, I'm already there.
When Sonsoles got to know was quite surprised, because she was actually quite scared with this of needing a new kidney, and she knew that nobody in her family could give it to her.
So she said, "Dear, you?!"
We went on a trip.
Sonsoles, "Dear, please take care of the organ!"
"Be careful with what you do!"
"You better come back and that the organ is intact!"
I said, "Well, I hope it will be intact."
We went on this trip. We came back.

When we came back, a phone call.
All the "cross-match" tests were done and all that stuff.
"Sandra, if the transplant has to be performed tomorrow, it can be done."
What a dance! A kind of...
As if a bucket of adrenaline dropped over me, like a shock, when you get a real fright.
And there was I, doing the dance.
Now the next part came.
First, to communicate it. Tell my mother, for instance.
"Sandra, why you?
Nobody in her family can?"
(Laughter) "No, mom, that's obvious.
If I'm offering myself is because there's nobody else.
What would you do, mom?"
"I think I'd do the same in your place."
Well, it's done, enough.
Afterwards, the doctor, my clinician -who was one of the last ones I went to for I presumed some sort of resistance could exist from that place- He told me, "Sandra, do you know that you are left with no 'back-up', right?"
What 'back-up'?
We don't have a 'back-up'.
I mean, life is life.
We have what we have.
We use it the best we can and that's it.
After that, the legal part.
If you are not consanguineous you can't give somebody a kidney without Justice getting in between to make sure this is an altruistic donation.
We went to Justice which behaved pretty well, with extraordinary celerity, measuring each step, because this was going to get public.
This was not: because it's Sandra, we give her a hand. Come in, come in, don't wait in line.
That was not the idea, quite the opposite.
It was, this will become public.
Everybody will witness this happening.
Justice was great.
The INCUCAI, the forensic doctors, the psychiatrists, psychologists, the social worker, prosecutors, the judge, they all gave the OK.
In August 13, 2012, with a half-hour difference, I entered an operating room, and Sonsoles, another one next to it.
It's pretty crazy, miraculous and extraordinary, that a doctor has the guts to get a healthy person in an OR, take a piece from them and put it into another person.
I bless the doctors of the world who do these things because I think it's an incredible thing.
The feeling... (Applause) The feeling is that of miracle.
Total miracle.
Because of this experience I lived, I dare say that, besides the media coverage and that I now wear the t-shirt and I'm the advocate of organ donation, I'm am a much happier woman.
My life is empowered, like with a bright highlighter.
I wake up in the morning and I say,
"Thank you!," literally.
I'm under the shower, feeling the hot water running down on me and I say, "Thank you! How nice!"
This is something I deeply feel.
It's not something I came here to say.
It's true.
True story.
When you give, you get more than what you give, and this is true, pure math. (Applause)
Don't ever doubt this for a second.
Because, apart from the blessing of being able to do it, of being compatible, we have... -there must be some doctors in the room- we have two antigens in common with Sonsoles.
This means that she has to take less medications in order not to reject the kidney, which by the way, is a very important contribution to the peacemaking of two potential or not-so-potential enemies.
Because the kidney is fan of Boca and it's inside a River fan.
This is not a minor issue, people!
(Applause) Truth is that in a thousand people who die four can donate their organs.
4 out of 1,000.
This is easy and concrete.
When our organs don't serve us anymore, when our body, which is so wonderful and goes with us our whole life, no longer serves us, there's no option.
The reasonable thing is to be a donor, is to make someone else live a better life or continue their live.
There are more than 7,000 people waiting for a transplant.
I know this is a complicated subject because it brings in the matter of death.
And it's not an attractive, extraordinary subject, but we not only have to become donors, we have to make those around us aware that we want to be donors, because they are the ones left when we are gone.
Then they are the ones who will tell the guy from INCUCAI: let's do it.
And if there's any doubt, that delay can make you not eligible to be a donor.
So for me, life is a before and after.
When you give, you get more than what you give and I'm a very happy woman.
But I want to put it in words of someone who wrote it in an incredibly unbeatable way.
(Sings) "To deserve life is not to unvoice or consent so many repeated injustices.
It's the same as giving truth and our own freedom a fair welcome.
That of lasting and letting time pass doesn't give us the right to brag, because living is not the same than honoring life."
(Applause) Thank you!
(Applause)
Let’s talk about video games.
For a lot of people, like me, video games are something great. For some other people, they burn your sight and are a huge waste of time.
I do wear glasses and suffer astigmatism, but I want to show you that we weren’t losing our time.
This is the story of how I became a gamer.
Everything started a long time ago, when I sat next to my dad and watched him play Doom 2.
Maybe it isn’t the best video game for a three-year-old to watch, but that hooked me up completely with computers and information technology.
I learned the keyboard keys, before learning the letters, and I learned to type my name before I could write my name.
Imagine what school was for me when everyone started with the A, and for me everything started with the Q!
I’m from a generation before the digital natives,
and for everyone in my generation it was still complicated to understand computers, but for me, and because of video games, it was something pretty simple.
All games were in English. So, for people like me, finding a motivation to learn the language was rather easy.
We wanted to play games, so we needed to understand the language.
It was like this that, with the use of a dictionary and a lot of time, almost a year, I could finish my first video game in English: The Legend of Zelda, Ocarina of Time.
I grew up and, as they just explained, I was the weirdo who was always bullied.
I hope not all of you had to go through that.
I had a different way of seeing myself than the way others saw me. I considered myself different else than what others considered of me.
But I was so shy, such an introvert. I sucked at sports.
(Laughter) And I still do.
And it was just too hard for me to meet new people.
It was then when I discovered the Internet and online RPGs.
It was then when he appeared for the first time: LordSP. That was my online identity
Online, I could be whoever I wanted to, but I chose to be that person I wanted to be in real life, and slowly realized that I could bring that online-self to my offline-self.
From this game, RuneScape, I want to mention something else I loved, besides communicating with other people, using English now to communicate with others.
I loved the in-game economy, buying and selling items and collecting lots and lots of gold, and without even realizing it, I was learning Economics, in a practical manner, things like supply and demand. Here for example is the curve of a price of an item over time, when to buy it and when to sell it, many years before learning about it at school.
Another RPG that really marked my life is a rather much famous one, which I’m sure somebody else here has played before: World of Warcraft. (Audience) [Unclear]
You see! In WoW, the economy wasn’t as complicated, the story was not as fun as in RuneScape, but it had one thing I love and miss: guilds and doing missions with your guild.
A guild is a group of players that formally gather together to accomplish a common goal.
The goal of my guild was to accomplish missions called raids, which are missions that requested 40 people to work their way through a dungeon with the objective of killing the last boss.
Back in the day, the game servers were not as geographically segmented as they are today, and it was fun to find my guild people from the US, Europe, some Asians and even some Australians. And when we did these missions, we were people from everywhere in the world and of different ages.
For you to see how a raid used to look like, it was really complicated to see, a lot of things on screen.
In these missions, we were 40 people, from different countries, of different ages, playing on different time zones, everyone with their own tasks, and on many occasions, I had the honor of coordinating these 40 people.
I loved it!
I learned a lot about team work.
Of course, not everything always went as planned, we fought for hours, one two hours through the dungeons, and then we ended up all dead.
But we didn’t give up.
At that time, we looked for missions that other guilds hadn’t accomplished before.
It was really hard sometimes because we didn’t have a guide that explained the procedure.
So we tried again, two more hours, and we died again.
This was because we wanted to accomplish something that hadn’t been done before and therefore we had to come up with new ideas every time.
At the end, we did make it through.
Sadly I don’t have any pictures of a finished raid.
When we were done, after four hours of playing, we turned everything off and went to bed.
A few years later, I had the chance to travel the world and see it with my eyes, and not through the computer’s screen.
It's kind of funny that exactly what made me so curious about the world, which were video games, was exactly what I had to quit for many years, but when I returned, I discovered something really funny.
I discovered social networks.
We were all into them already and I found out that we were all pretty similar, that we all spent hours and hours of our childhood trying to beat the Water Temple. In our teen years, we lost the train thousands of times. And now, in our young adulthood, we are still trying to figure out how to play Dark Souls.
(Laughter) But I also learned a few other things I want you to hear today.
The first is a paradigm change in how video games are created, which is called Early Access.
Reducing it to a simple image, this was the process before: you had an idea, you tried to find funding, you developed the game, what would be the pre-production. Then we found the testing stage, called alpha/beta stages, in which you would hire people to test the game. Then, it went to the marketing department, marketing distribution, and at the end, gamers would buy the game.
What does Early Access say? "Lets take the gamers from the end, and put them in the middle."
I have this idea, which I think is great, but I don't have money to develop it.
Maybe a lot of gamers think this idea is great: "I want to play that game", about whatever.
I put my money there, and now the gamer is not just a consumer, but somehow he's also an investor.
What’s in for the gamer? That the games that get published are good, and not always the same game, recycled every year.
Now when we have a lot of players testing the game, this process is done much, much faster, and when it's time for release, everyone knows about the game, and they buy it because all their friends already own it.
A good example of this is a game called ARK Survival Evolved, which was released last June, on Early Access.
Having a great idea is great, but it's better to have an idea, US$ 40 million financed by the gamers and two million players testing
and giving feedback, for free.
I played an Early Access game which taught me something which I want to share with you: it was DayZ.
DayZ is a multiplayer game, in which the player is a survivor in a zombie apocalypse.
He has to travel the world, looking for supplies, like drinks, food, clothes, weapons, and everything goes quite well, until you find the worst of all predators in the game:
other players. When you encounter another player, it is the moment which I call "the decision."
You don't know that person, you don't see his face, you don't know where he is from, he has no name.
Should I talk to him
and seek for a friendship, an interaction, so we can survive the apocalypse together? Should I trust him, that he will not shoot me as soon as I turn around? Or should I just kill him and take his stuff so I can survive longer on my own?
I'm a person that looks for interaction and team work. That's why I'm always friendly. A lot of times, we ended up gathering a great group of people with whom we could fight the zombies, and many other times, I had to face this image.
In that moment you feel like an idiot: "Why did I trust him?
Hours and hours of looking for weapons, I was fully built, and now I’m back in square one."
But then I realized that all that time I spent feeling sorry for this injustice was time I was not using looking for more supplies and pursuing what I really wanted.
I learned that even if you go through bad experiences, you have to try to stay true to yourself and to what you stand for. And so, I got to make a lot of friends. One of them is in the audience today.
The second thing I want to talk about is education in gaming,
because today we do see video games as something we can use for education, and I have to talk about this game.
Sorry guys, I know you don't play this game because it is for younger people: Minecraft.
Minecraft, for those who don't know it, is a game in which you use different cubes to create worlds.
You can create just one thing, like a house. With a bit more creativity, you can create a Gothic cathedral.
With help of other users, using the community, you can create existing buildings, like Budapest's Palace.
Go a bit into fantasy and you can recreate Minas Tirith.
Or go a bit more nerdy, and recreate King’s Landing.
(Laughter) Or you can go even nerdier and create a working computer, which can do calculations, in Minecraft.
This idea that gaming could be used for education reached such a level of acceptance, that, two years ago, a school in Sweden started giving compulsory classes as part of the school plan, Minecraft classes, to 13-year-olds.
Why is this important?
First of all, because it works, because we did learn things from video games, and now we will be able to say for example in a job interview: "I learned team work in World of Warcraft’s raids.” “I learned English from video games.”
I won't need a piece of paper from an institute saying I learned English.
It will be possible in the future. Lastly, I want to talk about the most enjoyable thing for me, which are eSports or Electronic Sports.
Although you might not believe it, there are video games which can be played competitively, and there are people organizing LAN events, events in which you see the players playing for an audience.
I see three different scenes in eSports: one standard scene, where you play when you want, called Matchmaking, in which you enter the game, the game engine searches for other four players to play with you, and you play with five other players which you don't know.
Then, we have something more organized, which are amateur eSports, which are teams that often play together, to improve their game, but without seeking an economic goal. And then, we have the professional scene.
The professional scene has grown so much that nowadays they fill stadiums. They fill football stadiums, like in South Korea last year, in the League of Legends Worlds Final.
They play for prizes of millions of dollars. DotA 2 International Championship had a prize pool of US$ 18 million.
That's more than Wimbledon, as you can see here in this picture.
Even the best European teams have, as couches, bald Spaniards with sexy beards.
(Laughter) And people still don't get it: how is it that there are people that sit to see other people play, instead of playing the game themselves, although we’ve been doing exactly that, for decades, every weekend.
(Laughter) This takes us to believe: "But this is just Europe, right? This doesn’t happen here."
But actually, it is happening.
If you follow TyC Sports, these are news posted in the past few days.
You can now find out about what's going on in the eSports scene first on TyC Sports than in any other place in Argentina.
This brings me to the final thought, which is how I want to end this talk.
The question worldwide is: are eSports sports or not?
I believe this shouldn't be the question.
For me, the question should be different: with so many kids who play these games voluntarily for hours and hours and hours, who aspire to become the best, some of them even want to become professional players and even want to go to Europe to play - which doesn’t sound out of my mind to me; people have always wanted this from football.
How can we instill values in them through eSports as we’ve been doing with conventional sports?
It's just that I believe that maybe you are not good at playing football, you are not good at playing basketball, you're not good at any sports, but you are good at this. And if we don’t show kids that what they are doing is not bad, that it's just different, that they can actually learn the same values of feeling part of a group, of the synergy of a group, that 1 + 1 is more than 2 - if we don’t respect this, we are denying these feelings to many many kids.
Now as a grown up gamer, I feel I'm responsible for guiding younger gamers in this path of understanding the eSports scene, so that they understand that it is something they can try to do, but not focus completely into it. You have to treat it with the same value, the same responsibility as when one used to go to a club to play rugby:
there is a lot of training, not going out on Friday night and then play on Saturday, and maybe, at some point, if you are really good, you can get to play in Europe.
And now the last thing I want to say is to parents who still don't understand this.
Maybe it’s because you need to learn to play again so you can guide your children along this path. Good game. (Applause)
I've got a four-year-old son who loves to jump on the couch.
I tell him he can get hurt and I ask him to please don't do it.
"But look, Dad," he says, while he keeps jumping.
And he's always an inch away from smashing his head on the table.
I shout "Goran!" -- his name is Goran -- "Goran, stop jumping right now!"
And when I scream like that he doesn't give me a damn, either.
(Laughter) Now let's imagine this other situation.
My son is jumping, up-down, up-down, and suddenly he slips and gets very close to smashing his brains out with the tip of the table.
So I, his brave and strong father, jump and save him.
I bring him down and after checking that the kid is OK, I say: "Goran, we have to talk."
I take him by the hand for a walk.
Imagine that we are not in my house.
We are in a huge meadow in Africa.
Imagine also a starry sky above us.
Imagine that suddenly an orchestra sounds.
(Music) "Goran, I'm very disappointed in you. You deliberately disobeyed me! You could've hurt yourself!"
"Dad, I was just trying to be brave like you."
"I'm only brave when I have to be.
Goran, you see, being brave doesn't mean putting yourself in danger."
"But dad, you're not scared of anything."
"Yes, I was today."
"Really?"
"I was today. I thought that something might happen to you."
And the moment I finish my sentence, my little son sees the footprint of my boot on the floor and compares it with that of his shoe.
And then realizes that I double him in height, that I weight five times more than him, and that I've been on this earth for 32 years more than him. And that this number, 32, is never going to shrink until the day I die.
(End of music) (Applause) It's not the same.
It's not the same because what I just did is a scene from "The Lion King."
Cartoons, with music, with characters, with stories, convey messages and they do it in a very powerful way.
We feel it in our bodies.
When I was a kid my favorite movie was "The Lion King."
I watched it all the time.
"The Lion King" has a phrase that I love in a song, that says "hakuna matata."
Hakuna Matata means "no problem" in Swahili.
And it's also the philosophy that little Simba adopts when his father dies.
The little lion is full of guilt, full of anguish, so he adopts a life philosophy that more or less goes like this: "no worries for the rest of your days."
However, when I grew up, had a son and watched "The Lion King" again, I discovered that "Hakuna Matata" is simply a stage in Simba's life.
Living in the forest, with your friends, eating bugs, not worrying about absolutely anything other than your desire and your freedom.
But what Simba really has to do is to assume his responsibilities, return to his troop, take the place of his father, defeat evil and restore the circle of life.
This is the absolute opposite to "hakuna matata."
"The Lion King" told me at age 10 how my 20's would be and what I would need, to engage in my 30's.
My sister liked "The Little Mermaid."
She knew all the songs.
Now, she says she doesn't like it much because "no women should give her voice for a man."
I think my sister never watched "The Little Mermaid" when she grew up because in the movie, princess Ariel wants to be with prince Eric, but lives repressed by the patriarchal power of King Triton.
It's true, she gives up her voice, but she acts in desperation!
And it's not until she gets her voice back that she can be with the guy she wants.
In "The Little Mermaid" a silent woman never gets what she wants.
In addition, Ariel is a character that during the whole movie never ever gives up on her desire.
And in that quest she makes her father change his mind.
"The Little Mermaid" is the story of repressed sexual female desire that has to come up, and the masculine power that has step aside and listen.
How much of my sister's discomfort with patriarchal mandates had to do with "The Little Mermaid"?
How many of the decisions I took in my life had to do with "The Lion King"?
Every time I talk about this on the radio or anywhere, every time I talk about cartoon philosophy, someone says: "They have no message, they are commercial."
Yes, they are commercial and, yes, they earn millions of dollars, but they can do it because they do have philosophical content.
A movie like "Frozen," for example, which cost USD 150 million -- without counting promotion costs -- it's bound to be meaningful.
In addition to the drawings, in addition to the songs, in addition to the huge marketing machinery, it's forced to resonate with its own historical time of existence.
And "Frozen" does it.
It gets into the feminist discussion about princesses.
And what does it do?
It makes them proactive and leads them far from romantic love.
All this without touching the shoes, the dresses and the crowns.
Cartoons create a worldview.
This was already said in 1971 by authors Dorfman and Mattelart in their classic philosophy book "How to Read Donald Duck," that's the name of the book.
That book in its final chapter has a phrase that I love, that goes: "As long as he strolls with his smiling countenance so innocently about the streets of our country, as long as Donald is power and our collective representative, the bourgeoisie and imperialism can sleep in peace."
For those authors the number one enemy of socialism was a duck with no pants.
(Laughter) And they were right, because the war between socialism and Donald Duck was won by the duck.
Fifty years have passed since this book.
Many of the values the left wings used to criticize Disney in the 70s were absorbed by Disney itself and by all the industry of kids' entertainment.
It was said, for example, that Disney was racist.
But in the 80s and 90s along with the white and Christian characters appeared Jews, Muslims, Gypsies and Chinese.
And then Mexicans appeared, African Americans and Polynesians.
Even today, recently, we have a movie like "Zootopia" which has, basically, a great anti-racist plot.
It was said that Disney was misogynist.
And, while princesses in the 90s were romantic, they were already empowered.
Ariel, like we saw, fights for her desire.
Belle, from "Beauty and the Beast," rejects the male Gaston who is murdered and tames the beast.
Jasmine, from "Aladdin," changes the Sultan's laws.
And Pocahontas, still being in love with John Smith, decides to stay in the land of her ancestors.
Even sexual diversity is included in 90's Disney.
"Mulan," for example, is the story of a girl who gets into war cross-dressed as male.
And she wins that war supported by a group of men cross-dressed as women.
The love plot is unquestionably bisexual and we could even say it's queer.
At the end of the movie, Mulan receives the blessings of her family, of her ancestors, and from the emperor of China himself.
In 2001, "Shrek" was released.
And with that movie a very interesting door opened up: the discriminated ones.
The three great sagas of Dreamworks -- "Shrek," "Kung Fu Panda" and "How to Train your Dragon" -- have ugly, fat heroes and people with disabilities.
Even the way good and evil are portrayed in kids' movies changed.
We hardly have really bad evil guys anymore.
There are even movies with no bad guys, like "Inside Out" or "Wall-E."
There are movies in which the bad guy reflects upon their place in the world and changes. Like in "Despicable Me" or "Megamind."
Historically frightening figures like ogres, monsters or vampires suddenly become characters with personal dilemmas.
This way of facing conflicts I call it "post-evil."
However, there is an argument from "How To Read Donald Duck" still standing 50 years later.
There is a conflict that remains silenced.
And that is the social class conflict.
Since 1990 to date, there is only one movie which deals directly with the issue of poverty.
That movie is "Aladdin."
Aladdin is a poor boy from Agrabah who lives with starving children.
At the end of the movie Aladdin's situation changes, Jasmine's situation, the woman, also changes.
But the poor remain poor.
Both in 1992 version and in 2019 remake, at the end of the movie there is an opulent palace full of riches and outside there are children starving.
Animated movies produced in the U.S. and consumed throughout the world, are strongly progressive in regards to gender, racism, discrimination, disability and fat phobia.
And they are conservative in social class conflicts.
I wouldn't be surprised if in the next 10 or 15 years this worldview would reign: a multi-racial feminist, sexually diverse and inclusive capitalism, where the difference between the rich and the poor only grows bigger.
(Applause) Because at Disney, at Dremworks, at Illumination, at Pixar, the values of our civilization are both reflected and also installed.
These movies are not innocent, but they are not the enemy either.
We can use them in our favor.
But for that to happen we have to understand them.
And to understand them we need a new critical approach.
To discuss what movies kids watch, talk about them between adults, and then with them.
And, above all, to know what voices and what values are being silenced.
But let's go back to my couch.
I have a four-year-old son and I want him to stop jumping.
So I call him: "Goran, come here please."
And I go down to his height.
"Goran, let me ask you a question:
who has the biggest paw, Simba or Mufasa?"
"Mufasa."
"Here, put your hand on mine."
"Who has the biggest hand, you or me?"
"You."
"Do you understand that if you jump on the couch you can smash your head?"
"Yes, Dad."
"So, are you going to listen to me?"
"Yes, Dad."
(Applause) Kids are sitting in this couch watching cartoons.
We think it's entertainment, that they are dumbfounded, but they are sitting in front of the most powerful ideological machinery of our time.
Let's not leave them alone.
(Applause)
One day, about 2 years ago, while talking to my mother she said to me, "Son, do you know what?
I've noticed that in the last 2 or 3 months I've been feeling a bit more tired than usual, in a way that has never bothered me before.
So I'm thinking about going to the doctors for a check up.
Which examinations do you think I should ask for?"
So then I listed loads of examinations, a variety of basic examinations and amongst them I suggested that she have an X-ray.
Not because she had some sort of specific respiratory symptoms to get a chest X-ray, simply as a way of checking.
So, a couple of days had passed when my mother arrived at my house holding the X-ray films and said, "Look, these are the X-ray films that I'm going to show my doctor tomorrow. What do you think?"
I picked up the films -- back then I was less experienced than I am now in regards to specific radiology diagnosis. However, even my untrained eye could see in the upper right part of the pulmonary area, which would be on this side at the top, I realised that my mother had a notch on her X-ray, roughly the size of a coin, more or less 2 or 3cm wide, in a round shape.
I didn't want to alarm her so I didn't give any medical comments, but I did suggest that she tell the doctor about it when she went to see him in case he hadn't seen it, in case he hadn't seen this lesion. And told her to ask for a computed tomography scan better known as a CT scan.
The weeks passed, my mother went through with the CT scan; I think it scared her. In the scan, my mother showed a tumour of roughly 2 or 3cm completely stuck to the lining of the lung, in the lateral sector of the lung, with very bad radiological qualities.
Qualities which made the doctors think it was a malignant tumour, that it was an advanced cancer.
So, in that very moment began this long, difficult, painful story of surgery, of diagnoses, of waiting.
My mother and I began to suffer the various processes that only people who have cancer, or who have had it, or who have families with the illness can understand.
Desperation, not knowing what to expect, being afraid.
During the following months my mother went through chemotherapy, radiotherapy, surgery. And the whole family was 100% focused on her.
I left university, my sister left hers too, and together with my father and step father we were 100% dedicated to making her happy every single day.
And one day, months after, my father calls me and says, “Pablo, do you know what?
For a couple of hours I've had abdominal pain, severe abdominal pain, my stomach is hurting a lot.” I said to him, okay, maybe you ate something bad. He says “No, this pain is really very serious, can you please come and get me?”
I immediately went to find him. My father was a very tough person, he had worked hard from a very young age, so for him to complain like this and ask for help had to mean it was something big.
I took him straight to the hospital and the doctors gave him an endoscopy.
Halfway through it, the doctor came out of the room and asked me: "Are you the patient's son?"
"Yes," I replied.
“Look, what I have to tell you is very serious.
Your fathers life is now at vital risk; we have just moved him to the intensive care unit."
I understood basically nothing of what was happening why was this sharp pain going to put my father into intensive care?
And he says: “Your father now needs respiratory support and drugs to maintain pressure within his normal range and keep him alive."
Of course, I asked him for an explanation, I couldn't understand what was happening.
Until he says: “This is what happened: during the endoscopy we saw a tumour, a neoplasm, which was forming a very, very big ulcer in your father's stomach, which has probably been there a long time and gradually punctured his stomach until it ruptured completely. Now he's unstable due to a large infection, a body-wide infection.”
If I could describe how it felt, when my mother was diagnosed and we were suffering through it, for me was like driving along and being hit by a car going 100mph. Head on.
When your life changes,
your priorities change.
But with this diagnosis, it was like a train had run over what was left of the car.
A freight train.
I didn't know what to do, or how to tell my family, generally, they tend to ask me about medical diagnoses of family members.
So, what I couldn't understand, from a more technical point of view, was why it had taken so long for them to come to this diagnosis.
Why did we have to wait until my father had a ruptured stomach to realise that he had cancer?
Why did we have to wait until my mother felt tired to realise that she had lung cancer?
This problem occurs extremely often.
We know that approximately one in three people suffer from cancer at some point in their life.
And one in four of these will die from it.
For me, and for many in the health field, the difficult task of receiving patients with advanced cancer falls on us daily.
Because medicine has done little to diagnose them early, so we receive them with haemorrhages, with considerable weight loss of 10 kilos in one or two months.
With lungs full of water, because of lung cancer.
So, this reality didn't fit with the medical logic that I was taught at school.
Why?
Because we as doctors, or people in the health field, know that chronic illnesses like cancer are generally a long and silent story.
The illness advances day by day and the person appears healthy because the concept healthy or ill still depends on the presence, or lack, of symptoms. The people here probably think they're healthy, because they're not coughing or losing weight.
But believe me, that more than one of them has a severe illness right now and doesn't know it.
It's for this reason that the sooner we can diagnose illnesses, the better the prognoses will be.
We know that there will be lower risk of complication. Surgeries will be lower risk.
And, also, and this is no less important, there will be lower costs.
For families as much as the health service.
For example, this curve shows the survival rate of gastric cancer.
If we detect the illness in its first stage it comes to around 70%
vs a meagre 4% in stage four, like my father.
Why is this happening?
Because now diagnoses are focused on illnesses that are already in the later stages, and on detecting symptoms and signs.
What's more, we have examinations that aren't universal, they're organ-dependent.
We have scanners, for example, for chest illnesses, for lung cancer.
We have mammograms, for breast cancer.
We have smear tests for cervical cancer.
And endoscopes or colonoscopies for gastric cancer as well as cancer of the colon.
And what happens if it's bladder cancer?
The patient dies.
That's what happens.
That's what's happening now.
What happens if they had a brain tumour?
And my head never hurt until very late on, until the tumour was very big?
So, basically, we aren't offering anything to these patients.
What are we offering them?
Chemotherapy, radiotherapy, surgery.

What for? So that they die?
To me, this made no sense.
And that's why five friends and I decided to take this problem, to work on it seriously and try to resolve it.
Try to resolve one of the biggest problems currently facing humanity.
Our objective was to create an examination that was an ideal screening.
That would be sufficiently economical that it could be used on the masses.
On people who are still healthy in order to detect illnesses in the early stages. That would be easy to operate, so that the equipment could be used even in the most remote corners of the world.
That would not be invasive, so that people
would not shy away from it. And the most important thing of all was that it would be able to diagnose multiple illnesses at the same time, because, as I mentioned, we want it to work when there are no symptoms or indicative signs.
What was our first difficulty?
We didn't know what we were looking for in the patients.
What was the common factor, the changes that could be detectable and what was common to the majority of illnesses, to the majority of organs?
And the answer was simple: their genetic material.
About a decade ago, a decade and a half, scientists discovered that in various pathologies, in various illnesses, the tissues release genetic material into the blood as a means of communication with other tissues.
They release small molecules of genetic material that become the words of this cellular communication.
The important part is that each tissue, healthy or sick, releases a pattern of these molecules called microRNA into the blood as well as into the urine and saliva, which can be detected with today's technology.
So we can see that the patterns are basically known examples of different types of cancer, of cancer of the colon, the pancreas, the liver, the lung, the breast.
So, the million dollar question was the following: Why were we not diagnosing and using these marvellous bio-indicators on people who were currently dying?
One of the important things, in addition to this genetic material being extremely prevalent in bodily fluids, had a quality that made it even more attractive.
It was released in neoplastic stages, or the very early stages of cancer.
For example in lung cancer, we know that that the mircoRNA pattern of lung cancer can be seen even two years before diagnosis.
And even five years before diagnosis for gastric cancer.
This really makes an massive difference for a patient, for a family.
The problem was the following: the machine that currently detects the microRNAs costs upwards from a million dollars, because its a laboratory machine, a highly complex machine, impossible for lower-skilled people in remote places to use.
Using it takes a long three hours and requires a specific PhD in microRNA as well as professional training.
This meant that the bio-indicator was very complex and impossible to use in reality.
So, motivated by this, and as part of a multidisciplinary team of technologists, biologists, molecular biotech specialists, engineers and doctors, we decided to get inventive.
We decided to take the risk and try to resolve one of humanity's biggest problems using a mixture of technologies currently used by the majority of people.
We created this: this piece of equipment is called Million and ours is called Miroculus.
With this equipment we can detect microRNA in practically any bodily fluid, with technology that is immensely cheaper than the previous version.
Our first prototype, with which we discovered the pattern of liver cancer, cost less than 500 USD.
The machine still being a million dollar machine.
And it works more or less like this: Every machine has a plate inside it.
That plate has lots of holes, which must be filled with patient's blood.
In each of those holes is a specific trap for each microRNA, which, if positive, makes the hole shine in the following way: In this picture you can see a real image from our laboratory, which is now in Germany, how it shines when it's positive where the microRNA was indeed in the blood samples.
So, with a machine that is actually very simple to use which is made up of a plastic framework made by 3D printers with an Open Source design code plus a mobile phone in the top part, we can generate a chemical reaction inside which makes patterns of holes shine, these are then captured by this phone in a revolutionary way, taking photos over and over again, accumulating all this information and using the internet to upload it to the Cloud.
On our servers the patterns are compared with patterns that are already known or existing and are then described in scientific writing.
In the case of there being a match with any sort of known diagnosis an image that is easily understandable for doctors is sent to the phone screen, where the exact diagnosis of the patient will appear.
And this is how the machine sees patterns.
For example, we can hypothesise that this was the first pattern of gastric cancer.
Or this was the pattern of liver cancer.
The important thing is this: that we know what we are looking for, and it's not based in symptoms or in signs.
We are looking for the illnesses that still remain hidden.
And it's with this machine that we are trying to break the paradigm that says that high-tech implies high cost.
We don't think so.
Starting with a principle as our team's vision, we took the initiative to generate diagnoses for the masses.
Because we believe that health is a right and that technology associated with high cost is only affordable for very few people.
Sadly, on the 3rd of January 2013, after fighting long and hard for about 2 months, my father died of cancer.
For my mother the story was a bit longer.
More painful. More exhausting.
A story which we saw
her worsen day by day, just as thousands of cancer patients do.
Regrettably, in the same year but 10 months later on the 21st of November my mother died of lung cancer. The important thing here that I want to stress is that for them, for my mother and father, who are no longer here and who, hopefully are now looking down on me, it was perhaps too late.
But it's through efforts like these, taken on seriously by people who are maybe a little crazy, who think they can resolve humanity's great problems, that perhaps for their families, and for future generations, and for the masses. it won't be too late.
Thank you.
(Applause)
In Silicon Valley, one in four people cannot cover their basic needs.
According to the World Bank, in the world's most important tech hub income inequality is comparable to Rwanda's.
In the Mecca of entrepreneurship more than 5,000 startups fight for financing, but only the strongest ones will survive.
Does this story sound familiar?
I bet it doesn't.
Two years ago, I left to San Francisco to study a Master in Design Thinking.
After six months I joined one of the world's best design and innovation firms, which allowed me to work for top international brands.
When I arrived to California, I understood that until then, I had been in a different league.
There's many talented people, things happen and happen fast, and eagerness to change the world is standard.
But these 2 years also demystified for me most clichĂŠs about living in San Francisco.
In every experience there are two sides of the coin and for me there is a critical side about Silicon Valley that at least in this country is not often explained.
If you are an entrepreneur, you must go there.
Everybody says that.
Well, maybe it's not true.
The London School of Economics affirms there is no evidence that starting up in Silicon Valley is better or more beneficial than starting up in Europe.
Indeed, Silicon Valley is a mature ecosystem, difficult to find in other countries, but it's not the only place in the world where things are happening for entrepreneurs.
A recent study from The Brookings Institution states that income inequality between the rich and the poor is growing faster in San Francisco than in any other city of the USA. Every day, 36,000 people sleep on the streets.
The rate of violent crimes in East Palo Alto area, right next to impressive mansions, almost triples the nation's average.
Black or white.
In Silicon Valley there's no place for grey.
The constant arrival of talent from all over the world, the high salaries in technological companies, and the little growth capacity of San Francisco's number of houses, make the cost of living in the city extremely high.
Almost every day, there are demonstrations in front of the private buses carrying Google or Apple workers to their offices on the outskirts.
The cost of the apartments close to that bus route is growing exponentially, so people with regular jobs and regular salaries, as myself, have to leave their homes because they can't afford the rent.
Evictions are frequent.
A few weeks ago, The New York Times published a wonderful article explaining how in Silicon Valley youth is overestimated and experience underestimated.
The article deals with the abyss created between the kind of talent demanded by startups and the kind of abilities offered by seniors.
For fresh talents arriving to town, working for a company developing technological infrastructure or new patents is not as cool as being a "ninja developer" and work for that app everybody is talking about.
But ironically, the most celebrated technological innovations in history have been manufactured products.
Does anyone think somebody will remember Candy Crush in a few years?
I don't think so.
San Francisco is a city for early adopters.
Every day you have tens of events allowing you to know the last apps or technological gadgets.
Everything happens fast,
which means you often lose perspective of reality.
During my first year there I never missed out on anything.
I constantly felt I had to be in the loop of everything.
We use to create business plans in barbecues with friends, talking on the latest Kickstarter campaign or on how many millions raised the startup of the moment.
Ths is not my iPad but it could have been, because I downloaded all the new apps.
This overstimulation, trust me, is exhausting.
Yes, in the end you know many things, but at a very superficial level.
If you are in San Francisco it's to be productive.
The idea that people is playing all day long foosball or pool is false.
Certainly, there is some schedule flexibility and you usually work by objectives, but there's hard competition and you have to proof your value every day; if you don't, you are instantly fired without a second thought.
In San Francisco, they hire fast but they fire even faster.
Investors do not give money to ideas, even if they will change the world for sure.
What matters is the execution, this is a basic principle.
Even if Silicon Valley is full of money, raising financing is very difficult.
You need a very clear competitive advantage, traction in your market of origin and in the USA's market, a brilliant pitch, an even more brilliant team and capital enough to support yourself in the city.
And even if you get it, then you have to proof that your product is viable over time.
Not easy!
90% of the startups will not survive.
Let's be realistic, to run into Marissa Mayer in a Palo Alto supermarket or into Mark Zuckerberg in a San Francisco restaurant doesn't mean you have access to them.
More high level people at reach in Silicon Valley than in this country doesn't mean they are available.
You meet interesting people in interesting events, that normally cost hundreds or thousands of dollars.
Creating and keeping a good contact network that will recommend you, support you and open doors for you, costs both time and a lot of money.
Investors, artists, politicians, families, Stanford teachers... 70,000 people of all kind get together every year in the middle of the Nevada desert.
During a week, Burning Man is the biggest city of the state.
There are huge sculptures, costumes, bonfires, naked people, yoga lessons, even a TEDx event!
People get married, with a partner or with themselves, this is rare but it happens.
(Laughter) Elon Musk said that Silicon Valley is like Burning Man, because people attend knowing that their values will match other participants' values, generating the necessary conditions to create innovation.
The biggest difference between Burning Man and Silicon Valley might be that in Black Rock City, during a week, there are no money and no labels.
And you know what?
Everybody is happy.
Isn't that ironic.
They say Silicon Valley is a state of mind, it is passion for work, curiosity to change the world, that constant sense of optimism making you believe everything is possible.
But Silicon Valley is also ruthless competition, social inequality and a lot of banal technology.
A bubble about which many of us wonder: when will it blow?
To conclude, next time someone talks to you about how wonderful and fantastic San Francisco or Silicon Valley are, be critical!
It's a unique place, but it's not the only one.
Thanks a lot.
(Applause)
I was born 36 years ago here in Malaga and I went to San Francisco 10 years ago. There I make films and advertising for a living.
I remember my granny asking when I moved there, "Fran, why are you going so far away, there in The United States?"
Who would have thought that the answer would come 10 years later and my going there was due to a story that changed my life.
That story came out of a tremendous cultural shock and that's what I'd like to share with all of you today.
The inspiring power that the cultural shock has for those who spend their lives telling stories.
But who experiences these cultural shocks?
Well, those who move around the world; today we call them immigrants or emigrants depending on which side you look on.
But actually, the human being has always been on the move.
It's been 70,000 years since our ancestors came out of East Africa to begin their exploration of the world.
These are our Homo sapiens ancestors literally leaving East Africa, starting their exploration of the world.
I think I'm going to continue without a PowerPoint.
(Laughter) And here we are today, entering into other planets.
We have been constantly expanding, like there's something in our DNA related with the Big Bang, that made it all begin.
The button is on the other side.
(Laughter) (Applause) Pretty cool.
They gave me the remote this way when I got out.
(Laughter) (Applause) Maybe this would be the reason this TED Talk becomes viral.
(Laughter) This is us going already to other places and as I told you, we've been in constant expansion, right?
As in our DNA there was something coming from the Big Bang.
Those cultural movements, migrations, they're history generators because they come along with discovery.
Discovers that surprise us, that shock us and break our patterns.
And because human beings are the only capable of transforming elements that we see in reality, in stories that transcend it.
Let me give you an example: Imagine these same ancestors of 70,000 years ago leaving the natural habitat from where they were born in Africa and discovering new things in their daily lives, like a new fruit, a new animal or a phenomenon they've never seen before, such as snowfall.
Think about it for one moment.
No other species would have made this new animal on a totem or in a protective emblem for the tribe; he wouldn't have spoken about this snowfall as a curse or as a divine blessing.
It happened something similar to me when I arrived to San Francisco.
I remember seeing two things I've never seen before and that called my attention so much.
Interestingly, one of them was also a meteorological phenomenon: fog.
I've never seen it that way.
I thought: "Wow, it seems like a sea of clouds."
I was fascinated to see how it was, covering all of San Francisco and making giant things like the Golden Gate Bridge, the famous red bridge, suddenly disappeared before your eyes.
The other thing I had no idea about and that shocked me a lot was the amount of people I saw begging in the street.
More than 6,000 people without a home in the main technological city, in the same place where Steve Jobs introduced the iPhone, in one of the richest cities in the world.
That number to this day, according to several NGOs, it stands at 12,000.
12,000 people without a home in San Francisco.
In my case, that was the cultural shock that changed my life.
Let me tell you how, let's go back ten years, until 2007, when I was just a lad.
I was studying at the San Francisco University and I remember I was ordered to make a short film for one of my lectures.
I was so shocked about the homeless theme that I somehow knew my short film was going to be related to that topic.
And so it was.
I ended up writing the short film that I titled "I wish," for which we needed not an actor, but a real homeless.
And what do you think we did?
We held a street casting.
We approached more than 20 people who lived on the streets of San Francisco to see if they were interested in being the main character of the short.
And well, for many reasons, no one seemed at all interested.
The thing is that the filming date was close. and we didn't have our main character.
I remember a conversation with the short film director, Martin Rosete, in which we discussed the possibility to change the script at the last minute and hire an actor.
But then something happened.
When we stopped looking for that person, it appeared.
In those days of uncertainty, I remember that I came out of one of my classes in the night, I was on my way home, to catch the subway.
And walking by the financial district from San Francisco, I remember I walked in front of one of these homeless guys.
But this time, there was something super different, because on this occasion it was the person who addressed me, unusual thing, and he did it in a very friendly way.
So, I stopped and we started to have a conversation and said: "My name's Moses," Moisés in Spanish.
So I told him: "I'm Fran, Francisco, like the town, San Francisco."
He laughed because he thought, "It's easy to remember your name."
And few minutes later I told him the idea about the short film: "If you're interested, why don't we meet in another place? Where can I find you?"
So he answers: "You can find me in my office."
And I ask: "You have an office?"
He answers: "Yes, this corner, where we just met, this is my office."
I've been living in the Financial District for the last 20 years.
I remember thinking at the time:
"Holy shit! This person has been on the street for 20 years."
The thing is the next day we met again.
And this is us in 2007.
Having a coffee near Moses' office
when he could read the scripts of the short film.
And he told me that yes, he did want to be the main character.
So we finally recorded the short film together.
None of us would have been able to ever imagine that this four minutes short film, made by students was going to end up generating a film about Moses' life that we've been producing and filming for the last 20 years.
Ten years, sorry, 20 is too much.
(Laughter) We Andalusians are very exaggerated.
(Laughter) And that besides, this story was including our beloved city of Malaga.
And you're probably wondering what does this character from SF have in common with Malaga.
Let me tell you.
The short film we made, "I wish" won the audience award at the Notodofilmfest in Madrid and the organization invited Moses to come and collect the prize.
So, imagine his face when we told him.
Besides jumping for joy, he told me that, curiously, Moses had had a fascination with Spain since he was a kid.
And that he had three desires: to bathe in the Mediterranean Sea, eat a real paella and see in person Picasso's Guernica.
Imagine this person coming out straight from the streets of San Francisco and landing in my kitchen at my home here in Malaga just to eat my mom's paella.
(Laughter) I can say he loved it because he had two extra plates.
(Laughter) And this is us in a picture we had after the big meal. And just before our nap we all lied down in the living room.
Another thing that put together this man from San Francisco with Malaga was Picasso's Guernica.
Moses went to Reina Sofia and I've never seen a person in my life getting excited like this in front of a piece of art.
Moses burst into tears in front of the Guernica and all the people there when he saw in the painting a reflection of his life.
A life in which a drug bomb and family pressures came down on him, making him lose his job, his home and finally his family.
When I got back to the U.S., I found out this artistic sensibility was inside a great musician: that Moses had been a guitar player for a long time in the 70's, that he played with people that ended up succeeding.
People that played with The Beatles, The Rolling Stones, you know?
The same way Moses met my family, I met his family.
All these relatives thought that Moses was dead after 30 years of not knowing anything from him, so imagine that time for family reunion 30 years later in Chicago.
Little by little, I discovered his life and documenting it with my camera, ending up recording 600 hours of material over 10 years.
Six hundred hours of material over 10 years.
During this time we all lived the financial crisis, which accentuated a lot more the problem of the homeless in the U.S.
I came to Malaga once a year, and I saw how bad things were here, unemployment, with incredible rates.
However, what called my attention is the fact that in Malaga or in Spain I couldn't see so many people living on the street despite the crisis, and every time I got back to the USA, I could see thousands of people living in a sleeping back.
So I thought: "But why here no and there yes?"
I knew I was looking for an answer, a quite complicated one, and I knew Moses story was only the tip of the iceberg.
Remember that the things that are against our thoughts may be our inspiration source.
In my case, from this constant collision it was born something that we titled: "Stories behind the fog."
A multimedia platform that is associated with the film and through which we're discovering, documenting and sharing the stories of 100 people who live in the streets of San Francisco.
Through these stories we can understand a little better the reasons of this very big problem San Francisco and the U.S. face. Besides, we've found such incredible stories that break the stereotypes that almost everyone's have on the homeless.
Among those 100 people, we found a CEO from Silicon Valley.
A CEO that lost it all and ended up living on the street.
A woman
that has been a domestic violence victim while she was pregnant.
She had to leave her house to eat, shower and sleep without anybody knowing at the Facebook offices, where she worked as a safety guard.
Another woman worked in Hollywood, in the great cinema industry
building sets, movies stages for great productions such as Matrix.
We have found stories of undocumented immigrants who have been trapped in the USA for the last 20 or 30 years.
And if they get out of the country they won't be able to come back.
We've found stories of lecturers that have a great PhD but they still live on the streets.
Stories of gays and transsexuals who have left from home because of the family pressure.
Stories of kids, teenagers, complete families even.
People with severe mental problems like schizophrenia and bipolarism who live on the street and come to the to street drugs to move on.
These stories, besides the personal level, help us to understand a little more the American system, and how this system is indeed related with the poverty of the country.
These 101 stories, one about Moses and these 100 we're collecting, are the last 10 years of my life.
And you may be wondering: "But Fran, what are you up to now?"
Finally, we are editing the film, in order to release it very soon.
We have 600 hours of material to edit and we're also compiling these stories in a book that we'll publish along with the launch of the movie.
Remember that the human being has always been on the move.
And that these migratory movements are generators of stories because they involve discoveries that shock us and they often inspire us.
Remember also that the human being, Homo sapiens, is the only species capable of observing reality and counting stories that transcend it.
That's why you've heard me speak today about the homeless through the fog.
Because their stories are hidden behind prejudices,
behind stereotypes,
and many times, behind our own ignorance.
In the same way as San Francisco is hiding behind the fog.
Take this with you, to San Francisco, if you ever go to this amazing city.
This is the lesson this gentleman Moses taught me.
And the most sincere answer to the question my granny made me 10 years ago.
But really, it's just my story, one story.
Imagine the number of people that are migrating around the world and that find in cultural shocks the spark they need to tell stories from a different point of view.
Telling these stories, these people get involved in more cultures beyond theirs and they inspire the rest of the world.
They inspire us to discover so many things!
Among them, that in fact all of us are more united than separate and that we are mirrors for each other.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
(Sings) "Dunce cap, dunce cap" they sang to me while I walked around the school playground.
If my first grade teacher called me "little turtle", how not to be the center of my schoolmates' jokes?
It was hard for me to memorize times tables, I didn't understand the texts that I read, let alone evoke them.
Obviously, for them, these were serious problems.
Diagnosed as a student with learning disabilities, there seemed to be no solution.
And the school did for me a sort of social promotion.
They told my parents that they would give me the diploma!
As I could not learn, repeating the course made no sense.
My destiny was to find a job: sewing, cooking... To my mom, the teachers' word was sacred, so I went to learn to sew, right on.
The only skirt I sewed in my life was more expensive for my mom than having it made by, you know, Elsa Serrano herself!
But my dad... My dad was always a rebel, and he thought he wouldn't lose anything by sending me to high school.
There the teachers found the name my serious pathology: I didn't know how to study.
Obviously!
And they also found the cure:
They taught me how to do it. Thus, my vocation was born: to be a teacher like them, to look and listen to my students, and devote myself in particular to those who were like me.
Because I wanted to teach to learn.
While I was obsessed with my training, life sent Catalina.
My daughter was born with severe cerebral palsy, deafblindness, generalized hypertonia, her muscles like contracted, quadriplegic, prostrated in a postural wheelchair, with no head control, with orthopedic splints in arms and legs, corset and elbow pads.
She was fed by a gastrostomy button and she breathed through a tracheotomy.
At times she was assisted with oxygen.
Despite this picture, I decided to live it differently, and I noticed that no one called her by name, --the one that we both dreamed with my husband, AgustĂn-- but for what she was treated: the girl of the hips, of the servo crib, the neurological.
Her social life was so limited!
It was only possible in the countless waiting rooms we visited.
I began to wonder: What do children play at this age?
Who with?
How does my Cata learn?
And I decided to look for a normal kindergarten.
Yes, normal.
Questioned by health professionals, who considered us deniers or crazy parents.
We were looking for a kindergarten instead of a rehabilitation center!
Why?, they asked.
Why not?, I replied.
Rebellion is in my genes.
A physiotherapist once told me: "Why do you want her to hold a rattle?"
Another said, "why do you spend so much money on clothes instead of on more therapy hours?"
I was convinced that in this way my daughter felt loved, that she understood me.
When I changed her, I sought to connect with her from another place.
And that people could experience the same, that they could see the girl, beyond her disabilities.
Finally a kindergarten chose us. Yes.

I always say that parents who have a child with a disability don't choose schools.
Schools choose us.
One day I go pick up Cata from kindergarten.
The headmaster receives me and says:
"Listen to this! Today Cata had a quarrel with two classmates, but later they made up again!"
"To think I was the crazy one," I said.
Now the mad one is the headmaster!
But no. She tells me that
a classmate runs past her and pushes her unintentionally.
She gets furious, hypertonic, her friend apologizes, but she was still angry.
Then another friend comes and says: "If you want Cata to get over it, touch her hands, like this.
She likes it a lot."
Wonderful! Not only they looked,
but they also touched her.
The body speaks to them, and kids always know how to understand it.
They learned from her.
Catalina was more than a body that needed rehabilitation.
She was a person who needed to live, and the school went even further.
It gave her a place, it named her, it turned her into more than just a patient.
From that moment that was my school model: an institution that guides, values, understands and cares for each and every one of its children.
There, children are recognized and hosted.
Catalina died at age nine.
Thanks to the school, she took with her a lot of experiences, more than I had at her age: trips, parties, going out to the movies, to the theater.
And since nothing is coincidental she changed my perspective on education.
So I started my specific training in Inclusive Education.
The great opportunity to implement all this experience, I had it when I became headmaster of a normal high school.
There I decided to open the doors to all, really.
I wanted that school, the school I experienced, which I was witness of.
That's the school I began to build.
Because it's at school where you can change the social representation of disability.
An inclusive school is a school that cares and looks after all and each of its students.
And by addressing their uniqueness, it designs diversified or specific strategies for intervention.
The goal was pointed to me by a student, Carolina, with myelomeningocele, a severe neurological disease, when she said: "Teacher, I don't want to stop learning."
So we should not underestimate the learning abilities of any of our children.
And we have and had students with cerebral palsy, myelomeningocele, spina bifida and hydrocephalus, autism, Asperger, psychosis, developmental delay, Down's syndrome, and more.
I could name many more.
We work with or without integrating teachers, on full or part time basis, with internal or external teams that advise us.
Because the presence of these kids in the classroom is the guarantee for students in general to learn to value and recognize human diversity, and to accept and deal with their own limitations.
How do we do it?
There are no recipes.
It is a public school, with limited resources.
I can only say we do it with passion, determination and training.
We believe in what we do, otherwise it would not be possible.
When we receive a student with disabilities, the first thing we do is interviewing their parents.
No one knows the child more than them.
After that we interview the child.
We learned to look at them, to listen.
We can't think them without knowing them, knowing what they feel, what they think, what they look for.
And then, with external teams.
Psychologists, psychomotricists, psychiatrists, occupational therapists --
Everyone who has the will and the capacity to team work, both from the private and public sectors.
Because we even team up with recovery schools, hospitals in the area, organizations and foundations that approach or welcome us to offer advice or free training.
Because it's true, we aren't prepared for them.
It's true!
Therefore we turn to specialists, who interview us all, to think classroom activities or specific adaptations as appropriate.
We also work integration in the group, in class, and with the entire community, for all to feel contained.
Because social inclusion is only possible in school inclusion.
As diversity is inherent to human nature, I am convinced that all schools can and must be inclusive.
Even more, let's drop the term "inclusive".
There is only one type of school, that adapts to all.
I remember being in line, waiting to enter the cinema, with my husband and my children, a kid begins to look at Cata, and waving his father's arm, he says: "Daddy, Daddy, look!"
pointing to my daughter, "A dead girl."
It was not an uncomfortable question, which I was used to hearing.
It was a statement.
The pain transformed my face, like now.
My eyes exploded.
But what was that kid's fault?
Then I wondered.
He could have said anything, but he said "dead".
Yes.
It is that they are dead for this society.
Nothing and no one is prepared for them.
Neither we, their parents.
We don't give room to them because by doing so we are faced with a reality that we deny, and they suffer for not being understood or accepted.
When I walk around my school I see how their presence becomes natural.
To our students, they are not dead.
Many volunteers are ready to help overcoming accessibility barriers.
These same volunteers on the street will not be indifferent to the needs of others.
Because they learned in school what is called empathy.
I contemplate the playground and I see Carolina arriving, by the arms of two classmates who help her go up or downstairs.
Fast, an assistant hands her the walker.
Franco passes by swiftly, wildly in his wheelchair.
And ValentĂn walks, in love with all, in love with life.
Ezequiel waves his arms testing approaches to this world which is adverse to his structure.
And Thomas... Thomas tells us fantastic stories every morning, he could even be a writer!
I could name many, so many.
They come together and coexist in an ideal and crazy recess.
Society itself, but better.
The ghosts of my childhood disappear.
There's no place for the dunce cap.
To recognize each one as an individual, to appreciate them for what they are, to stimulate their learning, encouraging us to push the boundaries of our own expectations.
Because they are our own!
An educational institution which doesn't give answers or room to these children is a dead educational institution.
At least for me, and the society I dream of and is possible.
Because for a society to change the change must start at school, one of the first places of socialization of man.
So let's start with what we have.
It's possible! We did it,
and we have the same or less than any school.
It can be done.
At least, that's what my daughter taught me.
And what my students and teachers show me every day.
(Applause)
Hello, good afternoon.
We have seen it with Estela, the world can change.
But I have news for you:
it will not change itself.
We need to act and work together.
And today, I am going to show you that this phrase that may seem meaningless, that the world can change but it can't change by itself, has a deep meaning.
How am I going to do this?
I will tell you a bit about me, my story. Why does anybody decide to work in the human rights field?
Even more, what are human rights?
How can one take them?
How do they affect me?
Then I am going to tell you a bit about why I think it's important to keep working on human rights issues
and finally I want to share with you why I think it is important to be here with all of you today.
I was born in a town called Tepoztlan in the State of Morelos,
which I am sure you all know.
It is quite touristic, magical and a pretty nice town.
But that is not the most important thing that happened in my town.
What marked my childhood was something that happened around 1995;
during those years they were going
to build a project, a golf club.
The people strongly opposed the project, and regardless of your being for or against these kind of mega projects, that is not the point.
What happened marked my life.
People got together, to such a degree
that the municipality became autonomous.
For three years, the municipality had its own municipal president its own police, its own tax-paying methods,
and all this, on a 10-year-old boy, leaves a mark and makes a big impression.
It was then when I realized that united people acting together, can make a change.
After that, like many people from other states, I had to come to Mexico City to try to have a better quality education,
and along the way, an association called Mexican Association Pro-Schools of the United World granted me a scholarship
to study in the USA.
This opened a world for me, it opened huge doors and another language,
but it also convinced me about something,
that I wanted to be a change agent.
I mean, my story is not as tragic as that of many other Mexicans, but I came from a lower middle class, and having received these benefits, I was convinced that I wanted to be a change agent so that other people, in my same situation, could have the same benefits.
Years went by and one day, a year ago, I got to work for Amnesty International.
Why it is important to work for Amnesty International?
First, because of the severity of the problems we have in Mexico.
For instance, immigrants crossing Mexico.
There is a specially vulnerable group: migrating women and children.
Just to give you some figures, 6 out of 10 women who cross Mexico are victims of sexual abuse.
Last year, only in Mexico, 9,800 children were retained in migration stations,
and this year, until May, they were 8,000.
We don't think the number is going to decrease.
On the other hand, we also have, - Estela has already mentioned it - a big number of missing people.
Over the last months numbers had come and go, but the last official number, issued by the Mexican Attorney-General office was of 22,000 missing people.
We don't know where they are,
and this is an excruciating wound for Mexico and for the families of the missing people.
Those are mothers of missing people looking for their children during a protest on May, 10.
This is Ángel Amlicar,
an immigrant from Honduras, who left Honduras in 2009, because his son, who bore the same name, Ángel, was diagnosed with cancer.
Having no other opportunities,
he set forth to the United States.
During his trip across Mexico, he was deceived by a “coyote,” who had him deprived of his freedom in a house in Baja California.
For Ángel's misfortune, that day, there was a police operation where he was tortured and forced to incriminate himself as a member of a criminal organization.
He was in detention for 77 days.
To this day, he has been in prison for 7 years in Tepic, Nayarit.
I am visiting him in Nayarit tomorrow because this is one the landmark cases we are working for.
That son, for whom Ángel left home, passed away while Ángel was and still is in prison.
This is one of the cases for which Amnesty International is fighting, and we are not going to stop until Ángel is released from prison.
This is Georgina's mother, a feminicide victim.
Georgina disappeared in 2011.
From that moment on, her mother started an incessant search
The authorities didn't tell her that a few months after the disappearance they had found her daughter's body, and just let her keep on searching over and over.
Years later, she was informed that her daughter was found naked, wrapped in plastic, with burns on her body.
But, don't panic, I also have good news.
Alberto Patistán, you can see him there, is a Tzotzil indigenous teacher.
He was incarcerated on 2002 accused of killing 7 policemen.
When he was put in prison, his son was four years old.
Since that day, his family, along with Amnesty International, worked tirelessly day after day, year after year, to get Alberto's freedom.
Finally, on October last year, Alberto was set free.
And this was a great victory for both Amnesty International and AI Mexico
Another case is that of Meriam Ibrahim
a woman from Sudan who was sentenced to death after being whipped 100 times, for the simple reason that she had married a Christian man. and for, allegedly, having given up the Muslim faith.
Amnesty International quickly moved in all the member countries,
but something very interesting happened:
about 1 million signatures were gathered, yet, 300, 000 of those signatures came from Mexico, from the Mexican section.
More than one third, or one third of those signatures.
And Meriam is free today.
She gave birth in prison, and she is now in Italy, living peacefully with her family.
This makes me think and reflect on the following:
yes, we have problems, very serious problems in Mexico,
however, it also makes us think. And this phrase we love in Amnesty International: "That many small people, in small places doing small things, can change the world."
Today I ask all of you, you, you, and you:
what are you going to do today to change the world?
Thank you so much.
(Applause)
I should confess that I wish I didn't have to be here.
And you'll probably wonder why.
Believe me, I think I am partly right.
Let's think for a minute about why all sexual rights activists are so important or crucial to Lesbian, Gay, Bisexual, Transsexual and Intersexed (LGTBI) people.
Let's think about why our constant effort, distress, and struggle are necessary or important.
If all that were not necessary, we would be living in a more just, diverse, emancipated world.
You probably are aware of the changes in Cuba after 1959. The question is: when did the sexual rights of LGBTI people become truly visible here?
They became visible in 2007, after the holding of the Cuban meetings against homophobia and the first sexual-reassignment surgical operations in the country.
This type of activism for the rights of LGBT people has produced changes and paved the way for the progress we enjoy today.
However, we need to further our activism.
This activism should be taken up by groups that are completely independent from the State - which is maybe questionable, but still legitimate - and by those that have requested support from the State to be better heard.
For example, Cristal is a transsexual who considers herself to be a woman, though she was born with male sexual organs.
Cristal was asked to dress as a man to have her ID photograph taken.
She refused to do so
because she feels and acts as a woman. But this was not all: she was distressed every time she was asked to submit her ID card.
This situation had such an impact on activist groups that fired up a controversy and so they held a dialogue with the relevant institutions.
What was the outcome? Fortunately, the procedures changed and situations like Cristal's shouldn't be seen again.
But that is not all.
Alejandro feels and acts as a man, and Daniela, in turn, as a young transsexual woman.
They were both working, but their bosses warned them that if they did not change, --and we all know they have the human right to be different-- if they keep being so peculiar, they would be fired.
Evidently, Daniela and Alejandro had been discriminated against because of their sexual orientations.
There was a lot of controversy around this issue.
The activism was so influential that there is now a law prohibiting discrimination against people like Alejandro,
there is a principle prohibiting discrimination against people because of their sexual orientations.
However, the law is not comprehensive enough. Daniela remains unprotected because transsexuals have not been included in the law.
As you see, I have to keep on trying.
I wish I didn't have to be here.
I wish there would be no need to organize meetings against homophobia.
I wish we, activists, didn't have to keep on fighting against these realities.
As long as there are people who, however, unwittingly continue discriminating against and infringing upon the rights of other people, we will have to keep on trying and moving forward.
(Applause)
I'm going to tell you a secret and I hope you will keep it.
To people responsible for creening: shh.
I have already seen films
that will have their premiere in 2015.
In fact, I'm one of the people who watch films in Spain before you all.
Even before the team
that has filmed it in America itself.
Why? I'm an audiovisual translator.
I take care of translating these films.
I ensure that you may watch them well subtitled or dubbed.
And the truth is, I love this job.
Not only because I can watch films before everyone else can, but because of the many advantages which make my work a pleasure.
For example, I don't have a boss.
I'm my own boss.
I don't have anyone to put pressure on me.
I'm free.
I can organize my own timetable, I don't have a fixed one.
I don't work 9 to 5, I can work a split shift.
I can work only on Monday and Tuesday.
I'm free to put off work, go to a party and work double shift the next day.
I can do what I want with my time.
The other advantage is that my office is where I am.
I am here.
What I need to work is just an internet connection, available almost everywhere nowadays, and a computer.
In fact, I will show you where I have actually worked.
This may be my office, and so it was.
With spectacular tropical views.
Beautiful.
Or this one, sitting in a hammock with the view on the park and on the pond, with the sea in the background.
Very pretty.
And also this one, with King's Landing in the background.
Game of Thrones fans will surely recognize this view.
I've been working there.
I've been freely working there.
Well, it can make us think: do translators work alone?
The truth is that we don't.
We are part of a chain, a cog, a link.
We have colleagues, but we're cooperating from a distance by internet.
My colleagues may be in Barcelona or anywhere else.
I will tell you a little about the process.
Well, the central cog is a coordinator.
A coordinator is the one who takes care of the communication within the team.
He sends me the film and the script, I translate it and give it back. He then sends it to the adaptator, whose role is to change my text a little, so it fits the lip movement of a character.
Because sometimes it's a bit too long or too short and it has to be adjusted to what the character is saying.
It has also to be adjusted, if the character's lips form an "o" and in my text there is an "a".
It should be changed, so it looks as real as possible.
After the adaptator, who may be the translator themselves, I am translator and adaptator, comes the dubbing director. They are in charge of choosing the voice actors and are present in the studio during the recording.
Then comes what I call the magic,
when dubbing is produced. The studio with the actors.
I will tell you something interesting.
I'm sure that you know that the same actor is dubbing Leonardo DiCaprio and Jim Carrey.
They have the same actor's voice.
But maybe you don't know that Leonardo DiCaprio is being dubbed by at least two different men.
There are at least two men, each one in a different film, and the voice always seems the same, but in reality it is not.
That's why I say that this is magic, the magic of the cinema.
A small part of this Hollywood magic is being created in Spain.
I brought you a video to help you understand it. It's a short Betty Boop cartoon.
I don't know if you know, but its copyrights have expired. So, you can download the Betty Boop cartoons and it's completely legal.
We're going to watch it.
First, we're going to see it in English, I'm going to tell you what is being said and then I am going to try to dub it.
Let's watch it in English.
"Padgy! I’ve done it...
I’ve bought the baby monkey!
From now on, he is your little pal."
Well, what happens in this clip is that Betty Boop was walking down the street, and she saw a man with a baby monkey, she loved it and wanted to buy it.
But her dog had already known this monkey, they met and had a fight.
That's why, when Betty says
"Padgy!", which is the name of the dog, "I’ve done it... I’ve bought the baby monkey!
From now on, he is your little pal."
Which means:

[she translates the text into Spanish]
Dog says "uf" and faints.
Now, let's see it with a dubbing.
Better look at the video, not at me.
I'm going to step away a bit so you can see the effect, ok?
No, I'm not going to step away.
"Padgy "I’ve done it...
I’ve bought the baby monkey!
From now on, he is your little pal.
Ok.
Thank you.
(Applause) Sure, it would be better with the soundtrack and all.
It was only for you to understand.
If you want to jump on board, if you want to form part of this chain and become audiovisual translators, the first thing you have to do after you finish school is to study Translation and Interpretation.
Today, we have this course at many Spanish universities.
Not yet in Asturias, the closest one would be Salamanca or Vigo, but there is a lot of them in Spain.
To specialize in audiovisual, after the bachelor's degree, the best option would be to master in audiovisual translation. We have very good courses in Spain, which can open many doors for you.
If you don't want to obtain a master's degree, because you don't feel like studying for another year or two, you can complete short courses, online or in-person ones.
They are cheaper and shorter than a master but they give you less options.
With master's degree in audiovisual translation, as in my case, you not only can translate films and TV-series, but also web pages and videogames, to which I also dedicate myself a lot to, and there is a lot of market.
And that's all, I hope to see you at some translators' conference.
I hope that I have inspired you a little. Thank you very much for coming.
(Applause)
An absolutely incomprehensible text laid before me.
I didn't understand a thing,
I didn't know where to start.
My first Greek II test, second year of Philosophy.
The clock was ticking and I could see my classmates standing up to ask the professor. He would talk and they happily sat back and wrote down.
It always seemed disloyal to me asking the teacher during a test.
I had never done it before.
I didn't even know the dynamics.
What should you do? You go ask,
they give you the answer, and that's it?
But I was desperate, so I stood up and said to him: "Professor, I have no clue on how to translate this.
I'm totally lost."
And to my surprise, he started telling me everything: "This is the verb, this is the subject, this is the predicate."
"Aha," I said to myself, "that's how others pass the test."
(Laughter) So I went back to my desk, I copied all according to his indications and I handed in my test.
After a few weeks he brought back the tests corrected.
Not even a little red mark.
Not one correction.
When he hands it to me, he says: "Carman, you got a D."
"How come? If it's perfect!"
"Yes, but I did it."
(Laughter) Rodolfo Buzón, that's his name.
At that time it didn't cross my mind that 20 years later we would end up translating a book together. A book never translated into Spanish before, by the great Aristarchus of Samos.
And not only translate it but also make a critical edition.
When you translate, you have a text in front of you, but you don't know how faithful it is to the original Aristarchus wrote, because Aristarchus's original was lost.
So what do we really have?
A bunch of manuscripts that were written a thousand years later, that don't even match because they are full of errors.
To make a critical edition means to grab all those manuscripts, determine which copied which, put together kind of a family tree, to finally propose the text that for you is the closest to the one that Aristarchus wrote.
And that is what we wanted to do.
We got all the manuscripts, about 30, and we started studying them.
Look, this is the oldest manuscript we have of Aristarchus.
What a beauty.
But, as you can see, it's not easy to navigate the text.
It doesn't have subtitles.
So I used the diagrams as my reference.
And then I realized that the diagrams on the manuscript were very different from those which appear in printed editions.
One had to be wrong.
Look, this is a diagram on a printed edition representing a lunar eclipse.
The largest circle is the Sun, in the middle there's the Earth and the little one is the Moon.
The Earth blocks the light to the Moon, because the Moon falls into the Earth's shadow cone.
This is the same diagram, but on the manuscript.
The manuscript is wrong; it's all wrong.
The Sun, the Earth and the Moon are all the same size.
The cone became a cylinder and the Earth, in the middle, looks like a rugby ball.
Weird.
I checked all the manuscripts and all the diagrams of Aristarchus and in all of them the same thing happens.
Could it be that Aristarchus didn't know how to draw?
But not only Aristarchus.
The same goes for the manuscripts of Euclid, Archimedes, Ptolemy and of all the Greeks.
They all have lousy diagrams.
I had discovered something unsuspected: The Greeks didn't know how to draw.
The Greeks, the fathers of geometry, didn't know how get a diagram right.
That was my first discovery.
My second discovery, which came very soon after, was that this had already been discovered before.
(Laughter) It was about 10 years already since works were published on how the Greeks did the diagrams wrong.
These works described the errors of the Greeks but yet no one had wondered why the Greeks did them that way.
And that was the most interesting question.
How could a Greek do a diagram wrong?
Then I thought: "The Greeks excelled in geometry."
The problems they solved with geometry, we solve them today with algebra.
That's why our abilities in geometry are sort of atrophied and we are much more dependent on diagrams.
We need to see the Moon drawn smaller to understand that it is smaller.
But not the Greeks.
And if they didn't have that limitation, they had much more freedom to deform diagrams as they pleased.
And then they could be altered to show other things.
I was trying to understand what Aristarchus wanted to say to me with each diagram?
I would study the text, see the diagram, and ask: "Aristarchus, why?" (Laughter)
"Why did you do it like that?"
And some answers came up.
In this corrected version of the diagram there's a terrible mess with the lines, they all overlap, and it's impossible to understand.
Perhaps Aristarchus deformed the diagram by zooming in that part so that the lines could be comprehensible.
That obviously deforms the diagram making the Moon larger and the cone a cylinder.
But a Greek doesn't care because they know how to read the diagram.
Others may be deformed because they include something like a caricature.
In a caricature you enlarge or exaggerate what you want to emphasize.
In this diagram you want to prove that the red line that goes through the center of the circle is larger than one diameter.
But it's not noticeable in this way.
If I draw the center line like in the manuscript's diagram it becomes more evident that it is larger than a diameter.
The one above is a caricature of the one below.
That obviously makes the line run off the center.
But, again, a Greek doesn't care, because he knows how to read the diagram.
This is a map of the subway projected over one of the city of Buenos Aires.
This, on the other hand, is a much more schematic subway map.
It has all that matters.
But to do it schematic they had to deform the map of the city.
This is how the Greeks' diagrams are like.
They are more schemes than exact representations.
So Aristarchus didn't do them wrong.
No. He did them on purpose,
to teach us something.
I was happy with this idea,
you can imagine.
It made me proud to think that I had deciphered a hidden message for centuries.
A message that Aristarchus had left for me in his diagrams.
But unfortunately the story doesn't end here.
I spent years defending this idea in all the congresses that I attended.
Until I attended one in Barcelona.
I was anxious because there was going to be a professor who had been a jury member of my doctoral thesis.
That guy is brilliant.
Not many people had come.
But I didn't care,
for me I was alone with him.
I remember I had him sitting on the right and that while I was talking, I was checking up on his facial expressions.
At first everything was fine.
But at the point where I explain a diagram of Aristarchus, the guy starts nodding his head.
(Laughter) At that moment, while I was still talking and looking at him, I crumbled.
I remember I said to myself: "This guy is right,
what I'm saying is bullshit."
(Laughter) I never asked him why he made that gesture.
But it was not necessary, because in fact his face forced me to confront something that I already knew but I didn't want to admit.
That there was something in my hypothesis that didn't work.
I had always assumed, surely, that the diagrams of the manuscripts were exactly like the ones Aristarchus would have himself done, or at least very similar.
But Aristarchus' manuscript was lost.
Luckily, before that, some disciple of his probably made some copies to distribute them among his students.
And some of the copies might have landed in some important library.
Maybe in Alexandria.
There some scholar probably copied it and took it with him to Rome.
And some ugly and gross Roman made cheap copies to sell in the market.
But some pious, plump, bald monk probably bought one and brought it to the monastery.
And the monks copied it there for generations to come.
Until one copy reached the hands of the king of France, who gave it to Pope Clement IV in 1266.
Since then it is preserved in Rome, except for the few years in which Napoleon stole it.
Perhaps, all that process of copies and copies and copies, was the key.
But how could I test something like that?
How could I recreate all this process in the lab?
In History you don't make experiments.
But as soon as I came back, I resorted to my students at the University of Quilmes and I told them that instead of having class, we were going to do an experiment.
That they were going to become old and medieval copyists.
That they were going to be plump monks and ugly Romans.
I picked up a student and gave him the diagram rightly done, and I asked them to copy it as best they could. And that they pass their copy
to their classmate.
The other would copy the copy without seeing the original and they would pass their own copy to the next classmate, and so about 20 times.
I'm going to show you one of the diagrams that I gave to my students.
It's from Euclid and it explains how the image of an object bounces in different mirrors.
The observer is in B.
The object is in A.
But the image of the object bounces off the mirrors and the observer ends up looking at it on the other side.
Euclid explains that the rebound of the image forms a perfect pentagon.
Now look at what happens with the copies of my students.
This is the first copy. The second.

The third. The fourth. The fifth.

The pentagon is no longer
a pentagon. It is a house.
(Laughter)
And the house starts to gain in height.
(Laughter) Now look at the diagram of the manuscript.
(Laughter) It's the same!
(Applause) The copy of the copy of the copy managed to turn the perfect pentagon into the house of the manuscript.
Is it then that the diagrams are like that because of the copyists?
I checked it a thousand times and the same thing always happens.
I show you another example.
Here Aristarchus wants to prove that the cone above is longer than the cone below.
But in the diagram of the manuscript the two cones look alike.
Guess what happens with the copies of the students.
First, second, third, fourth.

By the copy number five or six,
the two cones are the same.
And they stay that way, even if
they keep copying them until the end.
And this is my favorite.
(Laughter) Notice how quickly in the fourth or fifth copy, the Earth, the one in middle, becomes a rugby ball.
(Laughter) The cone becomes a cylinder and the Moon begins to enlarge until it becomes the same size of the Sun and the Earth.
The incredible thing is not that they are deformed, but that they always deform towards the same figure.
And once they reach that figure it stays the same.
And even if you keep copying it, it doesn't change anymore.
And that final drawing is the one to show up in the manuscripts.
So I was totally wrong.
It is not that Aristarchus did it that way because he wanted to zoom in on part
or exaggerate another.
No! He did them well.
The blame goes to the copyists.
The copyists were the ones deforming it, copy after copy, until reaching this state of equilibrium.
The same thing that happened to my students.
Truth is that this turn of events quite disappointed me.
Because while I thought that the diagrams were as Aristarchus had done them, I was happy working with the diagrams. Because when I worked with them I connected with Aristarchus.
And the copyists took that away from me.
(Laughter) But it would be unfair to get angry with them because if it wasn't for them we wouldn't have met Aristarchus.
Now I see each diagram as a work of art full of history that goes back to the Greeks but that is also a millenary and collective work of art.
Like those cathedrals that took generations to build.
Where each copyist was making his contribution with slight imperceptible modifications and probably unconsciously, but that led to this beauty.
I don't know where my research will take me in the next 10 years.
But I'm completely sure of something:
Whatever happens, never, ever, under any circumstance, I'm going to doubt a Greek again.
Much less, Aristarchus of Samos.
(Applause)
I want to show you something that I was fortunate enough to find in 2002.
At that time, I was living in Valladolid, and I was at the school of engineering with one of my partners and friends who is also here with us, Raúl Martín. And well, what I found was a video that, at a time before YouTube existed, but today is practically viral - it is the Tacoma Narrows Bridge, or should I say, it was in the year 1940.
This bridge was not designed to pick up energy from the wind, and, yet, it did.
It did it so well that it began to collapse.
It collapsed due to a phenomenon all of you may know, the phenomenon of resonance.
I'm sure that you have seen, at least once, an opera singer who is able to break a glass with her voice.
The first thing she does is tap the glass, listen to it, and place her tone at the same level.
With this, she is able to transmit energy from her voice to the glass.
Resonance is an excellent way to transmit force and energy from a fluid to a structure.
Well, what happened to the bridge was something similar, but it was not sound, it was the vortexes, the turbulence produced behind it by the passing of the wind.
And wind has a lot of energy. It is energy that has coexisted with us all of our lives, along with hydraulic energy, animal power and a few others.
There was a moment in the history of mankind, during the Industrial Revolution, when we thought perhaps we would be able to draw energy in a cheaper way from fossil fuels, from oil derivatives, etc.
In good faith, we forgot to account for the huge cost involved in reversing the damaging effects of its use.
If we do this, if we bear in mind a cost that surely will have to be paid, that our children, grandchildren, and we ourselves will have to pay, the cost of these energies, is between five to eight times higher than what we say it is.
Another thing that was done during the Industrial Revolution, at least in some countries, was that an enormous fabric of high voltage networks were distributed to transmit power from the points where it was produced to the points of consumption.
In Spain, for example, without relying on cables and only relying on metal structures, we've had to use more than 4,000 kg of steel to weave this infrastructure, and we as a country were fortunate to have been able to do so.
Sometimes, these numbers are difficult to quantify and experience. The best way is by comparing them to something we all know, for example, the Brandenburg Gate, which is not exactly small.
If we put all its steel in a cube, the cube would be 88 meters long.
Imagine what is involved to pull all that steel from the ground, melt it, mechanize it, treat it, bend it, install it ... It is an immense economic and energy cost.
Another aspect of this form of distributing energy is that, approximately, one of out of 10 electrons introduced does not reach its destination, regardless of whether this energy has been generated by renewable or non-renewable energy sources.
In terms of renewable energy, the king, without a doubt, is wind energy.
In Spain, we should be very proud of what we have done with it.
Even setting that trap I was telling you about before, wind energy is able to compete, at market price, with non-renewable energy.
Another source of energy exists that you all know as well, which are solar panels.
Solar panels do not produce such cheap energy yet, though they are starting to do very well, and the price is starting to go down a lot.
But, however, it has qualities that make it a very special energy.
Essentially, its cost of maintenance is very low, and furthermore, its environmental impact, once installed, is also very, very low.
Here is where the inspiration came from in 2002.
Would we be able to design a wind turbine that captures wind energy but includes the qualities that make solar panels an ideal energy source for what is known as distributed energy?
Distributed energy is generated very close to where it is consumed.
Let's give it a shot.
All of you know that as we move away from the ground, wind velocity increases.
What is not entirely intuitive to see is that the power contained in the wind grows faster, it [grows with the cube
of the wind speed]. This leads us to the idea that started with a horizontal structure. Our dream wind turbine has to be slim; it has to be vertical in order to move us as far as possible from the ground.
What shape can this wind turbine have?
We have many options, and all of these would be able to work with the same phenomenon that worked with the bridge.
However, all of you also know that the direction of the wind changes when it wants to.
This means that if what we want it to resemble a solar panel and have no mechanisms, gears, ball bearings, or axes, elements that can wear out due to friction and would increase its maintenance cost, this leaves us with one possible solution: a circular cross-section.
Our wind generator is going to be slim, tall, and have a circular cross-section.
How much energy can we pull from the air?
When we collect energy from the air, what we are doing is reducing its velocity because what we are collecting is its kinetic energy.
In other words, we might think that what we have to do is completely stop the air to absorb all of its energy, but of course in the same way we want to take in the air, we also want it to leave.
That is why we have to allow it to take some of its speed.
This causes us to reach maximum value.
That maximum value is the Betz limit. It says, at most, we can extract 59%, a little more, of the energy within the wind, with any wind turbine or device.
So what we are going to do with our wind turbine is try to increase to the maximum and bring us closer to this theoretical limit.
For that purpose, what we have used have been wind tunnels, because even though the answer has always had to come from mathematics, in the case of fluid dynamics, which is the technical area, the scientific area which studies fluids and therefore, the air, fluid dynamics has always had to be supported by test data.
We have also used computer simulations, which ultimately are something like a virtual wind tunnel that allows us to test and try things that are so hard to see in the real world.
Here you have it when we carried out one of these formulas for the first time.
At that time, we had a certain energy performance, and with this model, we increased that maximum performance by three.
We quickly realized that at the top we were not working well, and there a vortex formed just in one direction that broke, that disrupted the vortexes that we were using to oscillate.
What we did was introduce one more formula, and by doing this we increased its aerodynamic performance a little.
As you can see, do not tell me why, but every day it looks more like a bottle of good local wine.
Well, the next thing that we have to do is be able to operate for many hours a year.
Of course, one knows that wind speed constantly changes, so we have to try to resonate a structure that has a variable resonance frequency.
It is kind of like wanting to break a glass whose frequency, whose tone that it gives us when we tap it, changes over time.
Besides, we want to do this looking like a solar panel, also looking like the bridge.
For that purpose, what we had to introduce was a system that did not include axes, complex mechanisms.
Surely, you all have once played with a pair of magnets
and you have made them repel. When two magnets repel, they look very much like a spring with variable elasticity, but you would have also seen that its force increases very quickly when brought close together.
Introducing this type of system, what we get is that our wind generator, changing its amplitude a little, substantially changes its resonance frequency.
In the end, we managed to run many more hours per year, and by doing so reduce its amortization period, to recover the money that we invested in its installation.
This model is a field model four meters in height.
In it, we introduced an interesting geometry, which made it very efficient, aerodynamic, and also a system, what we call a tuned system, which is this magnetic system.
This other model, six and a half meters in height, more or less, well, it was essentially the same, but with more optimized features.
We are able to draw energy from the wind with a decent output.
We are able to run for many hours per year.
Now what we will have to do is convert our movement, our mechanical energy, into usable electrical energy, which in the end is what we are all about.
But of course, we are excluding the axes. And surely you have seen
the dynamos for bikes that we inserted, or we insert to be able to generate light.
Dynamos, alternators, almost everything, have an axis and a rotation, and it is something that, as mentioned before, we are trying to avoid.
We have an oscillating movement and it has some characteristics: it is slow, it is not a large movement, and it makes the job even harder for us.
We had to try many different alternators - we have made mistakes many times, and well, what we are now seeing is that in the end that principle of "divide and conquer" also works with this problem. And well, in recent weeks, we are getting outputs that are starting to be truly promising.
And that is what it looks like: A wind turbine that has different characteristics to those of the conventional ones.
And what characteristics are these?
Some of them are expected, for example: we have a low environmental impact - we do not damage the fauna around us.
Other characteristics have been unexpected. For example, one day, a great day, NASA got in contact with us and said: "Hey, we should test it, but the shape of your machine and the way it operates, is likely producing very low electromagnetic interferences."
And these interferences disturb the radars - the antennas, sorry - that this organization uses, right?
Also, we have learned over time that, for example, it is very rare to see conventional windmills along the U.S. coast; you know, all military matters are taken very seriously there, and this type of device there would force them to raise the radars.
This is something to see.
Well, it would be very good in the future if this were a strength of this technology.
Another feature that we count on is that we do not emit noise.
Yes, it is true that we oscillate, but we do it at a frequency that is much slower than what we are able to hear.
And the features we looked for from the start, the low manufacturing cost and the extremely low maintenance cost, in order to look like a solar panel.
But not all of the features are positive, for example, the first thing we knew that it wasn't going to be is that even if we do not have axes or mechanisms of that type, yes, what we are doing with a component is bending it constantly.
It is a component that is made with carbon fiber, which is a fantastic material, but it is also subjected to a phenomenon called "fatigue," which is very simple: simply by bending an element many times, fissures and cracks can appear, and it can end up breaking.
We analyzed this circumstance from the onset, both analytically, with school formulas, and computationally, and both methods tossed us decades of life.
There are other aspects we are engrossed in right now, related to the economy of scale, how many machines are we going to have to make in order to be in the market and be competitive.
And voilà, here we have it, right?
Two systems for generating energy that capture the forces of nature, wind and light, both share some features that make them ideal for distributed energy to be able to generate on a roof and consume elements one uses at home.
And finally, I simply want to highlight the most important thing which is nature.
It is the best engineering school that exists. From it, we learned to take advantage of sunlight with its leaves; and we human beings use solar panels.
It also taught us how the wind can give energy to a bridge, to a structure, and well, that it is simply very important to be able to take advantage of these forces that nature gives us without damaging it.
That you very much.
(Applause)
October 28th, 2017, a group of elderly were visiting Cabárceno Natural Park in Cantabria.
When they were around the hippopotamus enclosure, one of the animals surprised them with a big fart which caused some of the elders to fall down.
Three of them were taken to hospital.
It seems unbelievable, doesn't it?
Well, this headline, "The fart of a hippo sent three zoo visitors directly to hospital," was published in several mass media that we all consider serious, we think they have credibility, they seem trustworthy.
However, this headline is fake, It is a hoax.
My life changed some months ago.
I left my work at a TV station to create, along with a partner, an independent journalist project that fights against misinformation, that bets for data journalism, for trustworthiness, for transparency, and for memory.
It will help citizens distinguish what is true and what is not.
That is what I want to talk to you today.
Let's go.
(Sounds) When we hear this, we know we are going to hear a newsletter.
We identify these sounds as the consumption of radio information.
We see this: (Music) And we know that the TV news are about to start.
We associate these images and that music with the uptake of audiovisual information.
We touch this paper, we have it in our hand and we know that this, is an information consumption format, a newspaper.
Our senses associate these formats with news.
Even though they can lie to us, right?
I can feel it in the audience.
What we have internalised is that in those formats, we get information.
This is what we call "the anchor" in maldita.es
Stay with this, because you are going to hear it over again.
And then you will say:
What has changed? What is different?
(Donald Trump audio) "We are fighting the fake news.
I call the fake news
'then enemy of the people'
And they are. Fake news."
(Audio finishes) This is what has changed: to have Internet in our pockets at all times and to consume every time more and more news through WhatsApp, Twitter, Facebook, Instagram.
Which is alright, I agree.
But, here is where misinformation is most likely to mimic true information, to disguise like it.
And here is where the anchor we were talking about before gets spread.
How does this happen?
This is a real piece of news.
And this is misinformation, what many people call, I do not like this term though, hoax news, "fake news."
Because, when I say something is fake news I am not referring to the same as when Donald Trump says CNN is "fake news."
Because this is not news.
It has never seemed to be one.
It is just a disguise to misinform.
This is a very important thing to keep in mind.
However, what do they have in common?
It seems they all have the same format with their headline and a picture.
We do not have a reference that shows us one is true and the other one is fake.
And we have to add another problem to this: every time we tend to consume more news on its own, without any context.
Every time we spend less time on the webpage of a mass media, on its front page, to see what they decided to be important and we consume news through what comes on social media: through WhatsApp or Facebook.
Not knowing what kind of webpage we are getting access to read, or whether the resource is reliable or not.
For instance: "Urdangarín will have a payment in Switzerland for his expenses."
That headline went viral.
There are many people that, to this day, believe that, effectively, the king's brother in law, was condemned for corruption and the Royal House paid for his expenses.
Someone in this audience is probably realising now that this was a lie.
Why did this happen?
Because this piece of news went viral without context, without anchor.
We are going to show you why this is so important.
If we see this news on the cover of newspapers like El País o El Mundo, we could think by the context, by what surrounds it, that this news is true. it might be that the king's brother in law, condemned, was granted those privileges.
But, if we look at them here, in their true origin, where is this news coming from?
Precisely, from what is surrounding it, because of the context it has around it, there is something that makes us think that it might be unreliable.
Well this has another twist.
Sometimes, we do not even read the news.
Sometimes, we just read the headline and the picture.
And if we look at it like this?
We do not have any reference.
We do not know where it comes from.
We do not know its source.
We do not know its date.
We do not know if it is reliable or not.
I will give you another example: "Military service for people under 25 becomes compulsory again."
We get this through our phone.
It is a headline, a picture, there is no link, and no date, because it does not even have the year and we do not know where it comes from.
Where is our mind situating this?
In a reliable web or in a non reliable one?
What are our senses telling us?
We do not have context.
We do not have anchor.
And we add another key to this: misinformation becomes democratised.
Do you know where this headline comes from?
From here, from a web that was only made for you to transform your false rumour into news.
Power used to misinform, it was the one who was able to do it: economic elites, governments, the big mass media, the church.
Now, with tools like this, anyone can misinform.
And that is easier and more dangerous every time.
Let's check why is that these fakes news get made.
We have identified three reasons: the first one is just pure evil, it's just mere enjoyment.
Creating fake news just to see how far they go, how many people they are able to convince.
And there are situations where this becomes harmful.
Do you remember Frida Sofía, the 12 year old girl who was found buried under tons of debris of a Mexican high school?
A whole country looking for her.
Mass media all over the world were looking for her.
And it turned out that the girl did not exist.
It was fake news.
It was news created only for the purpose of generating chaos in an emergency situation.
We have lived this in Spain. In the fires of Galicia and Asturias in 2017, when the fire was almost reaching Vigo townhouses, and suddenly, a warning popped up on media:
"Tap water cannot be ingested.
It is not drinkable. It is contaminated."
This was quickly viralised on WhatsApp and it ended up being a false warning.
And notice that, it was not the only one.
This is a map of some misinformation that were viralised in a single fire night where neighbours, who were fighting against the fire, also fought against the fear that this misinformation were spreading through their phones.
This is very dangerous and I can tell you that if you happen to do something like this, we are watching you.
Let's go to the second reason: money; to make income.
Because there are people that earn money by making fake content.
During American elections, in Macedonia, a group of activists started making fake news.
They started making them against Trump and Hillary Clinton and, very quickly, they realised that news against Trump were not making any money, so they decided to go for Hillary Clinton.
And it worked.
They earned thousands of euros.
In Spain there are web pages that do something similar: they generate fake content for people to visit their website and in doing so they charge for publicity. And they say in the legal ad
that they are satire, they just do it for fun.
Of course, sometimes they don't do it to make fun out of it but to make money. And there might be
a skeptic in the audience who says:
"And who are you to decide
what is satire and what is not? Well we are none. What we do is we only tell you in the manuals about misinformation, we have to look at the legal ad from the web pages we read before we believe them.
We tell you with our technological tools that you are getting access to a satiric webpage, through our extension and we make sure citizens get informed as much as possible to avoid a strain of lies.
And there is a third reason, which I consider is the most dangerous: the hoaxes made from ideologies.
The ones that are made to distort our reality, to create a particular state of opinion that favours specific ideas.
We have lived this in Europe: with Brexit, with Cataluña, with the refugee crisis.
Who has not received a video in the phone, an audio, an image, a WhatsApp chain against immigration in the last months?
These resent messages between thousands of people, sometimes look like they are reliable, sometimes it is a headline and a picture, but it does not have a source, neither date nor anchor.
There are also headlines like this one: "A family welcomes a refugee who rapes their 12 year old daughter."
This headline is fake.
The man was not a refugee and it did not live with the family.
And, even though, to this day, this headline is still out there and people who have read it associate refugee with a rapist.
This is the ecosystem where we are and it is very dangerous.
I just want to say one thing: If some time you do not have something clear, do not share it.
It is the best advise I can give you today.
We are in an environment where fake news can go from being simple lies to become beliefs.
Misinformation soaks through and people stop believing in scientific evidence, for instance.
We see this in Italy with the anti-vaccine movement that is winning the battle with the vice-president as their leader.
We start believing that Earth is flat.
We start arguing that man has not reached the moon yet.
We start drinking raw milk.
But, how can we fight against this?
Well, legislation seems to be
a plausible solution. There are some people who maintain that a judge or even a state dependent institution is the one that should decide what is truth and what is not.
We believe that this is not the way, because the line between an anti-hoax legislation and censorship is excessively thin.
And we are not alone in this.
Experts from the European Commission, a group of experts that set up months ago where we are part of, also believe we are not ready yet to harshly legislate against misinformation.
We do not know the phenomenon enough, we need to keep studying it.
But we do have other tools to fight against it.
For instance, with data and fact journalism, with early warning technology against misinformation, with the community, with education and with you.
Because we need you to warn us about possible misinformation that is spreading in your networks.
Because we can't be everywhere, even less during family chats where more than once you have spotted misinformation, or in those closed Facebook groups where memes of a right wing politician are being posted, or a meme of a left wing politician, or from an actor or a historical figure, which are tremendously problematic, but do not have source or date.
When you give us that misinformation, our team works with a very strict methodology.
What things are becoming more viralised?
This is the first thing we are going to try to find out.
from that point on, it is journalism duty: pick up the phone, talk to the sources, verify the pictures and find out the origin of all the videos.
And when this job is done and we already know if it is a hoax or not the whole team has to audit the checker, and ask questions to know whether he or she has done a good job or not.
And then we vote.
There has to be a majority of votes supporting that denial and none against it for us to make sure and be ready to go out there and tell you that something is fake.
And there we need you once again, because it has to be you the ones who will come back to our WhatsApp and Facebook groups and will tell your contacts that in fact, that information is not true, it is a hoax.
Journalism, technology, community and education are the keys to be victorious.
Not everything is lost.
The journalists who want to do our jobs well, exist.
And we are the majority.
We know you do not trust us anymore, because we have failed sometime, and we ask for a lot of forgiveness.
But from this, from misinformation, they will not deceit neither with hoaxes, nor the executive, we just get out there together, citizens and journalists.
Because journalism is the best tool to fight against deceiving.
Thanks for listening.
(Applause)
I'm so very excited to be here. Moments ago, I was thinking of ways to communicate my excitement to you all, but I drew a blank.
I considered celebrating like a football player or talking like a radio host and asking something like, "Where's the TEDxPuraVida crowd?"
But the truth is, none of that feels natural to me.
However, I do want to share with you the reasons for my excitement: I love Costa Rica, and I love co-creating with people,
and we are about 2,000 people together for an entire day, thinking about how to create the education of the future.
What an opportunity!
I would love for this day to be more than nice talks that sound great but end up in nothing.
If there is anything from my talk that resonates with you in any way, I would love for you to contact me so we can talk during lunch or via email so that this talk transforms into actions.
Are you on board?
OK, let's start with a little research: Raise your hand if you have heard someone saying they learned nothing in school.
Many, right? Many of us.
For a moment, put yourselves in that person's shoes, think about a feeling, a word that this person has regarding that belief: frustration, disappointment, perhaps something even stronger, such as humiliation, shame, indifference. It's impactful, right?
It happened to me recently that I got in the taxi of a young man I know, and after chatting for a while, I said, "Do you like being a taxi driver?" "Well, more or less," he replied, "the truth is that I would like to work with animals, but since I didn't learn anything in school, I still have some high school courses to finish."
I was a bit skeptical, and in an attempt to inject some positivity, I said, "Bro, it's impossible you didn't learn anything in all that time."
He thought for a moment and told me, "You're right. I did learn a lot of stuff. I learned a lot of bad habits."
I went from being incredulous to being very thankful because what he said summarized something I've noticed happens to many young adults with whom I have interacted throughout Costa Rica and a few other corners of the world, and it is this: the expected learning didn't happen as expected, but there was learning nonetheless.
Everyone's attention was centered here, waiting for something to happen meanwhile ignoring all that was happening there.
But since all the attention was over here, no one noticed what happened over there, and hence that was left unguided and unvalidated.
So, what's going on?
That education has bought the idea that intelligence lies only within a person's head, and for that head to learn, it must be sat down and exposed to abstract concepts and knowledge.
We forget that for the majority of the time we have been on Earth, we have learned, evolved, and survived through direct and tangible interactions with our environment.
Let's do some more research.
Pay close attention to this video. How many times do you think you have to watch it to be able to pull the tablecloth without dropping the glasses?
I'm going to play it again
because I know it went by really fast.
Ready? What do you think?

Three? Ten? Maybe 20 times?
Let's take one more step
in our investigation.
If you thought I'd make a fool of myself attempting the challenge here on stage, you are absolutely wrong. What I just did is part of Kardas and O'Brien's study at the University of Chicago.
They made some participants watch the video, while others were allowed a tangible experience with the items from the challenge without performing the challenge itself.
The ones that watched the video believed that the more they watched, the better results they would obtain; however, more views did not deliver better results.
On the contrary, the ones that had a tangible experience with the items obtained much better results.
They knew better what it would take to succeed in the challenge.
This experiment, like many others, reinforces the potential of experiential learning, which is often more powerful and effective than abstract or merely visual learning.
I think that many of you agree with such power, but I'm not sure this translates to the day-to-day education of our youth.
Therefore, I think we should be asking ourselves the following questions: How do we make education more active?
How do we integrate the environment in which our youth develops into their education?
How do we guide, validate, and even evaluate the things that happen outside the classroom and the academic curriculum?
It seems to me that the question that summarizes all others is, How do we bring the neighborhood and the classroom closer together?
The concept of living ecosystems of learning changes our mindset of abstract, passive, and hierarchical education which starts with the ministry, drops to the teachers, and then is transferred to the students and expands our vision so we start considering all the stakeholders that might take part in the learning process - teachers, clearly, children's parents, the family as a whole, members of the community, the natural environment, and mainly, the students themselves -
and invite us to be aware of the wisdom of each of these stakeholders and in a creative way, find the emergent properties that arise from those interactions.
We call this "living ecosystems" because online learning technologies have monopolized the term "learning ecosystems," so now education is going from being almost exclusive to the classroom to being almost exclusive to computers.
But what about real and tangible environments?
What about our local context?
How easily are we skipping social and natural resources and going straight to virtual ones?
Costa Rica might not have the best technology in all its schools, but our natural richness and our human qualities are admired by the rest of the world.
When one visits the school of Alto Quetzal in the district of Chirripó, one notices they don't use the latest technologies of online learning, but the people there are rooted in the powerful and mysterious Cabécar culture and have the most majestic mountains in their backyard.
Our learning ecosystems are alive, they are organisms like us, they're our people, our forests, and our animals, and they are already teaching us, but we're not taking them into account.
So, how can we promote these living ecosystems of learning?
Let's take a look at some ways we can do it. Empathy. Empathy is the vehicle with which humans genuinely connect with what surrounds us.
To be empathic means to position ourselves in the world of the other and connect from there, and not from a place of judgment, critique, or selfishness.
It is essential to teach our youth to put themselves in other people's shoes, to see their needs, including those of nature, in order to ensure their success in the global society that we live in today.
We can practice empathy through simple but profound questions like these: What does my community need that I don't? What perspectives are others seeing that I don't see? What does nature need from me so it can provide me with what I need?
Teaching empathy is a complex science, but it also arises naturally from our curiosity toward the world of others.
Permeable walls. Educational institutions that function as living ecosystems of learning are schools with permeable walls.
There, both community and school see each other as parts of the same whole.
They grow together, take care of each other, share common spaces.
When the school needs something, everyone volunteers to help.
The teacher walks the streets of the neighborhood, and parents walk the hallways in the school.
This integration makes student learning more dynamic and lively and helps the community grow.
Liaisons. Within communities, there are people that are like cerebral synapses, connecting people to one another.
These people are essential for the type of learning that we are talking about.
Sometimes, it is a person who belongs to the educational institution, such as a teacher or a counselor. Other times, it is someone from the community, such as someone from a development association or a coach.
For a moment, I want you to think of someone who had a very important impact on your education:
that person who believed in you when you didn't even believe in yourself, who saw an ability in you that you hadn't recognized, that person who said something that changed your life forever.
These people usually have this kind of impact on us because they transcend the academic curriculum.
I want to mention four people, out of many that I would like to mention, with whom I have shared and witnessed the impact they have in their communities. Perhaps some of you know them: Yuba in Terraba, Mr. Ruben in Sepecue, Mrs. Eugenia in Chira Island, and Juradir in Coroma.
I'm sure many of you are also liaisons. To you and to all liaisons, we have to give you space, a voice, and resources for you to continue inspiring our youth.
Evaluation and validation. As long as what happens outside the academic curriculum and the classroom is not considered an essential part of learning, there'll continue to be many people who believe they learned nothing in their formative years, while what's really happening is no one validated their learning.
We already know it's difficult to evaluate everyday life, but I can vouch for the Ministry of Education's efforts to find ways to do so, because when we do, we are saying, "This is important," and then we all turn our attention to this type of learning.
But evaluation is not the only way we can validate living ecosystems of learning. We also do so when we mix things that are happening in everyday life with content from the academic curriculum.
For example, studying wars and battles that took place long ago can be difficult and feel sterile and distant for the students, but we can make the discussion more lively by relating this with the processes of conflict resolution they are going through in their lives with their peers, their family, and thus we use conflicts from yesteryear to enliven their learning in the present.
I want to share a couple of examples, out of the many I know that are occurring in Costa Rica, of educational institutions that are finding creative ways to activate these living ecosystems of learning. At Juanito Mora School in the Barranca District, their teachers and the interdisciplinary team have gone way above and beyond the call of duty to satisfy the many needs of the youth in that area.
And in doing so, they have attracted many other organizations so that through the school, they also do their job at satisfying these needs.
Out of this, they have created the Inter-Institutional Web for the Attention of Youth and Adolescence, which is now widely used by other schools in the area of Puntarenas.
The Curridabat Municipality has made a commitment to provide a more integral education to the youth of their community
and has started giving workshops in communication skills, mental health, physical health, and contact with nature to the students of the Technical High School Uladislao Gamez in Tirrases.
What's most interesting about this is that the adolescent participants have reported a significant increase in their sense of wellbeing after participating in the workshops.
This proves to us that this kind of learning is not only effective, but it also feels good. So,
what's the use of bringing the classroom
and the neighborhood closer together? This union helps us change our vision from an exclusive education which takes place only with some people, in some moments, with some topics to a diverse and accessible education where we are all teachers and we are all apprentices and any matter can be a learning topic.
Living ecosystems of learning strengthen the education people demand better results from, but are increasingly distant from.
And it reminds us that in order to change our education, we don't need an immense budget, orders from the ones up top, or a complete revision of the academic policies, but as we broaden our vision, use local resources, and pay attention to the learning potential that lies within reach, a different education is possible.
Because many things are difficult to change in our education and in our society, we can all leave this room to validate the wisdom of our youth, to guide the learning that is already happening, and to strengthen our education through living ecosystems of learning.
Thanks and let's talk soon.
(Applause)
What are those trails that we see in the sky?
You may think they have a scientific explanation.
You could think that they depend on atmospheric temperature, on the humidity in the air, or on airplane fuel.
But actually, there are people, thousands of people... that think that they are fumigations.
Those people think that according to a preplanned and global scheme those planes loaded with a bunch of chemical products are spraying microchips.
(Laughter) Nanotechnology that would find their way inside our bodies in order to somehow control us.
Plus, they think that with the excuse of enlarging the mobile phone network they are actually installing antennas on the roofs of the houses as part of a control system that's allowing microchips communicate among themselves using this sort of a global wi-fi network.
One of my passions is the cinema of ideas, the film-essay.
Topics on Chemtrails were absolutely fascinating.
Last year I made a documentary called "Chemtrail Revolution" about this topic.
While I was talking with the members of the anti Chemtrail collectives, I discovered something that got me even more interested: they tell their truth as if it was a story.
They truly give it the typical structure of a story: there is a villain, and obstacles to overcome, they are the few enlightened that know the truth...
This is something very common.
Fiction and stories are part of our daily lives.
I believe - here today, I dare to say - that everything is fiction and everything is a story and that even we, as people, are stories.
I remember that all my life my father told us a story from when we were children, three or four years old. He told us that we used to wash Gora, our grandfather's dog, in the bathtub, and that one day, Gora escaped.
She went to the living room and filled the room with foam.
My dad said there was so much foam that it was very complicated to spot the dog run around.
Years later, when I was washing my own dog, I realized that that was physically impossible.
(Laughter) I called my dad asking him about that story, and he told me laughing: "No, that was a story you liked to listen to." (Laughter)
That episode never happened, but my young mind had absorbed it like something that actually happened.
Even today, I'm able to remember it as if it were a real event.
The thing is that many times we reconstruct our memory using the stories of others.
Like when they tell us things we did when we were kids. When we are older, we are the ones who create that story and tell stories to ourselves, and it follows that when we'll pass away, there will be somebody 'telling' us, we will be another person's story.
Not only people can be stories but also borders and countries.
Last year, from Truth Behind 404, a group that I'm a part of, we invented a country, we invented a country in the M40.
(Laughter) The sovereign state of Hautôvia.
(Laughter) Hauôvia is located on an 80,000 m2 island, in a pretty unreachable place.
It is situated between highways.
To be specific, if you want to go there to visit, it is between the R2, the M40, and the far west of PAU de Valdebebas.
Hautôvia came into being as some sort of claim that has a lot to do with the kind of urbanism we would like to see implemented but also to point out how often we accept totally arbitrary truths such as borders and countries.
To make our story even truer, we used the code and the normally used elements in the stories that make a country.
Here we have license plates, passports (Laughter) stamps, money, everything.
We had everything.
Hautôvia has its own embassies, consulates and issues its own university degrees.
(Laughter) Hautôvia is a country in any aspect, except for a little detail: nobody, except a few thousand virtual citizen, recognizes its existence.
But what if they were millions?
Would we then start speaking about real borders?
In the end, borders and countries are something you can't talk about in terms of true or false; it depends upon a consensus or an imposition.
I believe the truth is largely a story everybody agrees on, it is a shared narrative.
A story is not there only to impose an order but also to explain better or to help understand reality, to make it more accessible.
Three years ago, I went to Beijing to shoot a documentary about a place that fascinated me.
A place that attracted me with the promise of a first rate aesthetic experience.
And indeed it was.
I am talking about Qianmen Avenue after its remodeling.
Qianmen has been, since the XIV century, the center of social and commercial life of the surrounding area: the hutong of Qianmen, the Qianmen quarter.
Which by the way, had been considered from an architectonic point of view a sort of miracle in the history of construction.
Qianmen was also a very important part of the identity and memory of Beijing and it was under all aspects a unique flower.
But sadly, the fake, the fiction replaced reality in Qianmen.
The neighbourhood was totally demolished and it was replaced with an exact replica, but this time in concrete with a globalized architectonic style a sort of theme park traditional China.
In the end, the remodeling of Qianmen meant to make it a set, a theme park for tourists.
Currently, there are 130 international brand stores in Qianmen, nobody lives there, and in fact, it is not a place to live in.
You could say it is not even a place, but a sort of gigantic consumption product.
To tell this story I invented a character.
I invented a botanist with the unlikely job of making a catalogue of artificial plants.
(Laughter) This idea helped explain the whole situation of Qianmen, because the fictitious job of my botanist, classifying plastic plants, was the best metaphor for what happened in Qianmen: The replacement of something real with a fiction made of concrete.
Getting back to the first story, the Chemtrails, one thing my interviewees told me, was that many of them made orgonite.
I think I have it... here it is.
The orgonite is a compound; a material that contains three elements: polyester resin, which gives it rigidity and shape, a quartz stone, and finally, steel shavings.
To this, something totally innocuous, several properties are attributed: especially protection against the effects of fumigations and antennas.
Orgonite was a defense weapon against control attempts.
They were the only ones who saw the truth about fumigations.
In their story, they were protagonists of that fight for freedom, in defense of freedom.
Speaking with them, you could notice their imagination struggled to find intentionality in things which in principle do not have any, such as the trails left by the planes in the sky. Or to find connections between unconnected things, such as telecom antennas and trails left by airplanes.
In the end, they dictated an order.
They were creating a story that gave meaning to their lives, to what was happening around them.
Lies have a terrible reputation, but we need them.
We need fiction, we need narratives.
Because stories have a beginning and an end.
They give us a place, a perspective, something that real life denies us.
Even in the weirdest and most delirious stories, our life gets meaning.
Life is a chaotic incomprehensible flow, but stories guide and give us purpose.
Thank you.
(Applause)
In Spain, there are 47 million people of which around 4 million have some type of disability diagnosed.
If we also count their families, that makes around 10 million people related directly or indirectly to functional diversity.
The question is: Why do the rest struggle so much when relating to disability?
Why do we sometimes not even see them?
Why do we keep a charitable look towards them?
And especially, why is it so hard to change that look?
Being eight months pregnant, the gynecologist told me: "Things are not looking fine". That was her way of telling us that our son had a series of malformations that were going to make his development harder if he ever got to be born.
And he got to; on February 4th, 2009, Mateo was born and clearly, things did not look fine.
At that moment, everything faded to black.
From that moment, a medical journey started full of words impossible to pronounce, uncertain prognosis and, the hardest of it all: no diagnostic.
No one was able to tell us what happened with Mateo.
I lived some really tough moments, I do not want to sweeten it.
Basically, because I get bothered by those phrases we say in a well-meaning way in these moments: "These kids are a blessing," "he has chosen you because you surely are an extraordinary mother".
And no, it is not a blessing nor something you would wish to happen to anybody but, like all adversity, it hides an opportunity, the opportunity of changing the look, of seeing things from a different perspective.
For a long time, I was focused on the things that my son would not be able to do, and paying little attention to those he did do, which were special and, furthermore, only he could do.
Lost in a world full of "no" and limits, because when something you thought that would never happen to you does happen there is also a loss of identity.
I stopped being Inés to become Mateo's mother, as we travelled from doctor to doctor, finding no answers.
And besides that loss of identity, I had to face going out to the street being a different me, go from being someone that nobody looked at when she walked down the street to have everybody turning their heads when I walked with my son.
Those stares that hurt so much, and I do not know if I would look at them like that if I had not taken this extraordinary journey, because we are still scared of what is different.
In Instagram's age, of seeming to be perfect, of seeming before being, what is different scares us.
In the age of "follow your dreams," "we came here to enjoy ourselves," "where there is a will, there is a way," seeing how fragile we really are reflected, scares us.
Fear, that fear of thinking: "Ugh, if that happened to me
I do not know if I would be able to overcome it!"
There goes some good news: you could, you have no choice because, in the end, you are yourself and your circumstances, and the only way to survive is accepting them, learning to live with what happens with you.
And what was happening to me?
Why was it all so hard?
Why, if there are almost four million disabled people in Spain, was it something so foreign to me?
And above all, what is a disability?
I started reading a lot about what disability is; what it means, what it implies, but all I read was about limitation, lack of, inability, handicap because we keep measuring functional diversity based on limitation. We label them medically and socially for what they are not able to do, and that did not make me feel comfortable.
Until I found a definition made by the World Health Organisation, that says that disability is a complex phenomenon that reflects the interaction between a person -- with some determined characteristics -- and the society in which she lives.
And this was what I was looking for.
Disability is not a health condition.
It reflects interaction, our cohabitation.
It is us, the society, who generate or erase barriers, we are the limiting factor.
What a responsibility, huh?
It is like that.
That was my first change of view, understanding that the limit was in me, not in Mateo; and I, who had been for a long time working in culture, started to ask myself: What are we, the creators and cultural managers, doing to cover functional diversity?
Does our culture reflect that diversity?
Clearly, no.
Not to talk about access to culture, which is collected in the Constitution.
Can a disabled person access culture on equal terms?
Neither can she.
So I started to look for solutions.
I found in the way one of those exceptional beings, Miguel Cuerdo, and together we kick-started the festival "A different look" in the National Dramatic Centre, with that idea, changing how we look at disability through performing arts, starting with getting disabled creators on stage that had been confined to amateur tours or art therapy until that moment. We gave them the opportunity to show themselves in a professional way onstage because what is not seen, does not exist, but what is seen, depends essentially on how we show it; and from there, from the power of the stage, we would change the look of the viewers, we would show them the ability that is inside disability and let them be enriched by diversity. And, slowly, our seating area started to fill with a different public,
a public that had felt before that what we did was not meant for them. One of those viewers, blind, told us that when he was a kid and his class was taken on a trip to the theatre, he was left in the classroom because, what for? And he was left alone in the classroom while the rest went to the theatre.
It was not a long time ago.
And that public that was excluded before started to find cultural spaces that do think about them and apply solutions to make their proposal accessible to everybody: sign language, magnetic induction loop, audio description, easy reading, etc. And the public that already came, the "normalised," started to mix with a new public and especially started to understand the effort that comes with a culture for everyone.
But that was not enough.
We also wanted the rest of creators to see that diversity onstage enriched the proposal if it was done in a proper way;
to lose the fear of working with different artists, and we put them to work together in spaces of stage research so they could try and test themselves, so they felt inside creative environments, and what we were expecting happened: fears, barriers, prejudices started to collapse.
And these creators started to catch the beauty that comes with difference.
And they started to include it in their own artistic proposals.
Laila Ripoll and Marga Labarga directed "Empty Shells," a cabaret about Nazism interpreted by actors with different disabilities.
It premiered in María Guerrero theatre, in Madrid, and was such a big success that they had to replay it the following year.
Andrés Lima told us his version of Romeo and Juliet.
Love does not last forever.
Interpreted by actors with intellectual disabilities that were given the opportunity to speak onstage about love and couple relationships.
A collective that is rarely allowed to love in an adult way, talking about families that do not let their children love each other.
It was a revolution.
There is a moment when Julia, one of the actresses says looking at the public: "love is justice".
Or Lucía Miranda, who showed us her Alices look for Wonders, where every character had some kind of diversity.
Because, why cannot Alice have a disability?
It is true that the book does not say she has one, but neither that she has not.
Let's open our minds, change the look.
And slowly, other creators started to get interested in this new way to talk about what is different.
And our stages have started to reflect diversity in a different way.
And that which started as a dream is slowly becoming a reality. There is a lot of work to do, a really complicated job.
But listening to Patti Benet, a blind actress, saying that when she went to register to an interpretation school, she was rejected and told she would never be a professional actress, and seeing her working now as an actress in María Guerrero stage in Madrid.
Or looking at Ángela Ibáñez, a deaf actress, releasing "Mother Courage" yesterday along with Blanca Portillo in the National Dramatic Centre.
Or seeing Jesús Vidal picking his Goya.
Just because of that, the effort is worth it.
A Goya, and I might have said it lightly
but what a big moment it was.
I believe that this year no actor wants to win the Goya because he would have to exceed Jesús Vidal's speech from last year.
Good luck with that!
Inclusion, diversity, visibility.
Three words that are already part of our imagination and our history.
Something inconceivable some years ago.
Because the "Champions" journey has also been an incredible one.
I suppose you have all seen it, right?
What a success!
I was lucky enough to have Javier Fesser calling me at the beginning of the project and end up being part of the casting group and then the interpretation coach of the ten disabled actors.
It was such a gift!
"Champions" is a model of inclusion because it does not show the limiting and negative part of disabilities, it shows the good they have to offer.
And in a society where we constantly fight to be the first, "Champions" comes and tells us how great it is to be the second.
Where are the second ones in this society?
And that society that measures success in economical terms such as profitability, productivity or social recognition, "Champions" comes and tells us that it can also be measured based on values such as happiness and the ability to enjoy life.
Living "Champions" alongside their main characters and their families was an incredible experience for me.
How did we start?
With a lot of fear, because comedy sets such a thin line.
And there could be people thinking that we were "laughing about" instead of "laughing with".
But everything we lived during filming, just that, was absolutely worth it.
Because Fesser had very clear that it was their story what he wanted to tell, that is why there is so much truth, that is why "Champions" works, because they did not have to look like somebody else to find their place; they have only needed someone that would show them as they are, with their abilities and limitations, as every one of us has.
And the message has been so powerful and well told that it has transformed an entire society.
I wish we never forget that!
"Champions" has helped us make a ten-year progress in inclusion.
And it has made, both for the collective and their families, the effort of all those years worth it.
By the way, Mateo appears in "Champions"; just for a second, you almost cannot see him; he passes through, dressed in green, with his grandmother, very excited, going side to side.
Fesser was really eager for him to appear, and for us, you can only imagine!
Because I cannot avoid remembering that day when everything faded to black and I thought I was sinking, and I could have never believed that adversity would hide such a big opportunity for me and for so many other people, but above all, for Mateo.
He is the driving force of all this.
I work so my son can grow up without being afraid of being different.
(Applause) Thank you.
Diversity has to be shown in our society in a normalized way.
We do not have to be constantly contextualising and explaining diversity.
Life is diverse, people are diverse, intelligence is diverse and our world is diverse, and that is what makes it rich.
Let's not complicate it by trying to adapt everyone to a standard.
Let's generate a more flexible context and environment where people with disabilities can be and do as they will without having to look like somebody else to receive the price of being included.
Because including means adapting the environment to a person.
We are the ones that have to be transformed and let the difference enrich us; and yes, inclusion has a cost and a big one.
It costs time, money, effort, resources, but there is no other option; because at the end of the day, who would you rather be? The one that puts the barrier or the one who puts the ramp?
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
How lucky you are to be here indoors.
Outside it's raining hard.
A terrible storm.
And although it's not true, no doubt some of you thought, "I forgot my umbrella, darn!"
(Laughter) This happens because the information that I gave you went in your ears, creating a sensory and emotional reaction.
Our brain builds this bridge across associative stories.
Newton said that every action causes a reaction.
I believe that all information causes a reaction.
For instance, if I say the word "love", What comes to your mind?
Perhaps happiness, joy or sadness.
If I say "rain"' perhaps you think about traffic, about a taxi or umbrella, for example.
And if I say the word "Mexico", what comes to your mind?
In 2013 I was offered the opportunity to come to Mexico.
When I spoke to my family and friends, they asked me, "Why?
Why are you going to Mexico, fool?"
(Laughter) I didn't know either.
The truth is that not only in Brazil, but in much of the outside world, the perception of your country is not so favorable.
We watch films or studies about perceptions.
According to them, Mexico is a corrupt and dangerous country, which deports migrants and has sunny beaches.
True or not, I decided to come and see it with my own eyes.
The point is that I believe Mexico exists in a matrix, where we wake up every day and are given for breakfast news like: "They stabbed him" you've read that, no?
"He cracked his skull", "Sex Amateur", "Both heads were shot".
"Crude!"
So we assume that as our reality.
We create a sort of bubble of insecurity, conformity and incredulity and we make ourselves hostages to the facts.
I asked a young girl to draw Mexico her own way, and I also asked an adult.
Here are the drawings.
We must preserve the way in which this child painted her own Mexico.
I am a journalist and a year ago I left psychological therapy, because I could no longer stand to be a voice for bad news.
I remember very well the time, when I went to report on insecurity in the streets of Rio de Janeiro, my city.
I interviewed several people who said that they were very calm, very safe.
I returned to the editors who told me, "No, Vinicius, look for a story about an assault, a robbery.
There must be one.
But hurry up, 8:00 deadline."
That changed my life.
I recognize from my position as a journalist that the impact that this type of information has on earnings, due to the demands of the market.
Nevertheless, I, you, him, and you too, there in the back, we are the market.
What do we want to consume?
Since I was a child, I wanted to be a journalist.
I saw Clark Kent being a hero and a journalist in his idle time.
I believed growing up that perhaps I could save the world that way.
I came to Mexico with a mission.
In journalism, we always have to show both sides of the story.
And Mexico also has its other side.
We cannot deny reality nor change the facts.
But we can move toward the future that we want.
"What Good News, Mexico!"
is an online periodical, focused on spreading the good news that happens in this country.
Journalists, journalism students, interested parties from all the states of the Mexican Republic set out voluinarily to be the eyes of the good of this country.
In Mexico there is good news every day.
What we do here today, for example, is excellent news, and what we must stress here are the little facts, the almost invisible day to day stories.
How about a headline which says: "Happy children have fun in a fountain in the Mexican summer"?

Wow! What do you think?
Or maybe: "Grandmother realizes her dream to see the beach at the age of 85?"
This changes the day, right?
It gives us a boost.
Stories like this deserve to be published.
But if we don't see them, they don't exist.
This is a piece of good news published in a big Mexico City newspaper. ["Rescue robot created"]
And this is the page it was published on.
[22] What importance does it have, right?
None.
This is a delicate subject.
Look, when we inform we also form,
the etymology is very clear: the prefix "in" comes from "from inside".
so to inform is to form from inside.
All of us here you, I, your cousin, your ex boyfriend, your neighbor have to be informers of the country that we want.
I'm not proposing that we live in a rose-colored world, nor that we hide problems under the mattress, but that we train our eyes to see beyond the front page.
In journalism, we have a theory of the establishment of subjects which postulates that the means of communication have a great influence over the public agenda, public behaviors and habits.
A question: should we allow our Mexico, mine and yours, to lose all its colors for one single color?
The color yellow?
Yellowism?
I say no, so if you allow me, I have two requests.
The first for members of the press present here and joining us online: Dear colleagues, I am also a journalist.
We are the fourth estate. We have a commitment to society, we can change this setting.
What would happen if we could look at one single piece of good news, every day on the front page
of the newspapers?
Just one. Take it slow.
My following request is for you, change agents.
Friends, I will achieve my objective today, if tomorrow morning we awake and see a Mexico which goes beyond the first page; a Mexico which has a soldier in each son; a Mexico of Jorge Ramos, you know him.
Of whom? Tell me. Of Barragan,

of Ana Gabriela Guevara, of Inarritu.
A Mexico of Octavio Paz, of Carlos Santana.
A Mexico of Maria Felix, of Del Toro, why not?
Of Sor Juana Ines de la Cruz, or the Mexico of Emiliano Zapata.
If I say "Mexico", what comes to your mind?
Thank you.
(Applause)
I was always interested in science.
And when I had to choose a profession, I chose meteorological sciences, today atmospheric sciences.
A not-so-popular profession;
we are just a few.
But I didn't want to be "the weather girl".
I wanted to do research
and investigate extreme phenomena.
Those phenomena that occurred quite occasionally, but had a great impact: heat waves, floods, droughts.
Sure, now with climate change those phenomena that were quite rare and infrequent, have become normal.
I'm a member, along with other scientists, of a United Nations organization which is dedicated to issuing reports about the state of knowledge on climate change.
It's called IPCC.
These reports are published periodically, are the basis of negotiation between governments, because governments guarantee them.
These reports contain what we know about climate change in the past, what we know about climate change in the future, what are the impacts, but most importantly, they contain the solutions.
We know that even though these reports have been published for over 30 years, sometimes climate change doesn't pull anyone's strings.
So we scientists become auto critical.
Are we communicating the problem wrong?
When we talk of climate change is correct, because there is a change in the weather.
But sometimes the clearest message is when we talk of global warming.
And when we talk of global warming: Do you know what was the increase in the average global temperature since the preindustrial age
until the present?
One degree Celsius, almost one degree.
It looks insignificant, but it produced so many changes.
But we also know that we want to know what will happen when it rises to one degree and a half, when it rises to two degrees.
That half-degree difference seems negligible, but you know what?
The impacts are exponential.
What do we call exponential?
Something that has an action of one, impacts two; of 2, 4; of 4, 16; of 16, 256.
That is the concept of exponential.
And the difference between temperature increasing one degree and a half or two degrees is quite relevant.
For example, if the temperature increased one degree and a half compared to the preindustrial era, the amount of extreme heat waves, those that occur once every five years, --if we here were the world population-- it would affect the first four rows.
If the temperature raised two degrees, the first ten rows of this auditorium would be affected.
Heat waves have a great impact on health, a great impact on mortality.
Think that in 2003 there was a heat wave that in France alone resulted in 15,000 deaths.
We also know that the difference between one degree and a half and two impacts on the loss of biodiversity of many species.
It can double or triple the loss of biodiversity.
We also know that almost 99% of corals will be affected.
The Great Barrier Reef of Australia.
Or think of the Caribbean corals.
We also know that half a degree more in temperature will produce that crop yields, for example, corn in tropical areas, will drop to a half.
Or fishing will also drop to a half.
With these reports some countries propose solutions.
Other countries deny them for their own interests.
And other countries directly ignore them.
I never thought my science would have so much to do with diplomatic sciences.
There is a recent survey among U.S. citizens which shows the following.
Luckily, most citizens said that climate change had affected them, regionally and even personally.
But another interesting result is that depending on their political party, if they are Republicans, even with high scientific knowledge, they "don't believe" that humanity is the cause of climate change.
But if they are Democrats, the higher the scientific knowledge, the more certain they are that climate change is produced by the action of man.
Another point of self-criticism when we communicate the problem is that we communicate the results with a certain level of uncertainty.
And sometimes from that uncertainty denialists take an advantage.
But we have many certainties.
We know that temperature will keep rising if we continue as we are. We know how much and how, we know that sea level will continue to rise.
And we can quantify the millions of people who live on the low coasts of India, for example, Southeast Asia, in the islands, will have to move due to the sea level rise.
We know that the level of the Río de la Plata will continue to rise.
And it will produce floods in neighborhoods that are on its coast, for example.
We know that ices will continue to melt.
We have many certainties.
But we also have certainty about the solutions.
Solutions are there and they don't need much greater technological development.
Solutions are available.
Let's focus on three.
The use of plastic.
To reduce plastic use, especially one-time use, has a double positive impact.
Not only it doesn't create pollution in the environment, but also we avoid using more oil to make it again.
The way we move.
We should be able to travel in public transport networks that use renewable energy.
We must keep this in mind to claim our authorities.
The third is the production of all the elements we use and buy.
That production should tend to be regulated to use renewable energy.

It seems difficult. It seems impossible.
But there are many positive examples.
Remember the ozone hole?
Ozone is a gas that covers the Earth and is vital for life. It's very important for our life. In the 80s it was discovered that a decline in the ozone layer had begun, that was called the ozone hole.
And it was also known what was the culprit.
They were certain gases that humanity manufactured for other uses, the chlorofluorocarbons.
In 1987, a protocol was signed in the city of Montreal -- the famous Montreal Protocol -- that determined that all countries of the world should stop making chlorofluorocarbons and replace it with other gases.
Slowly it stopped being manufactured and today ozone is recovering.
In health there are many examples.
When AIDS appeared, it was a deadly disease.
Today, luckily, it's chronic.
The recent black hole image, how was it made?
It was achieved because all countries united towards the same goal, sharing their technology and their knowledge, to get one image.
But we also have positive examples with our youth today.
Think of Greta, this Swedish student who a few months ago went on strike and sat in front of her Parliament with a banner that said "act against climate change."
Her perseverance in the fight, her message so clear, made that today across the globe more than a million and a half students are replicating these actions.
Because the information is there.
The information is there, but maybe you don't know how it will affect you in your activities or in the places where you live.
So, the information exists but is not yet democratically distributed.
Maybe it's not made transparent.
Some geologists call this era the Anthropocene, a new geological era, since the Industrial Revolution until the present.
A new geological era, why?
Because humanity interacted so much with nature that produced a change in the weather.
So if humanity produced the change in the weather, it should be able to repair it.
Every action counts.
Every day counts.
Each temperature degree counts.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good afternoon.
Good afternoon, can you hear me?
Yes? Okay.

A bit more than a year ago, I took this picture.
And quickly started becoming a very popular post.
Some Facebook and Instagram pages started sharing it, some people appropriated it for themselves and said they had taken it, and a few days after this photo I joined a friend in a pub. Then we started talking about photography and how much I like taking pictures and sharing them on social media.
Then, he asked me if I had ever thought about making a photo exhibition, which seemed like an incredible idea to me.
So, next day I went to House of Culture to see the situation, to do this exhibition.
When I arrived, they gave me a date, they give me one hour to exhibit.
I went home very happy and when I arrive, doubts, insecurities, fears started showing up, and maybe, what I was about to exhibit to the people, wasn't so good.
Or maybe, one of the things that bothered me the most was thinking at the moment that I didn't have the financial resources to carry out this exhibition.
So, finally, I washed away these fears, and the next day I went to look for sponsors.
In less than 15 days I collected the money for the photos and its framing.
In one month I had everything.
From this moment, a domino effect started developing, because it looked like destiny was helping me to introduce myself in several places.
One person, who works at the airport found out that I was going to exhibit and he asked me if I wanted to take part in an anniversary event that the company that manages the airport
was going to hold.
So I accepted. He told me: "You know what?
Bring your pictures tomorrow and assemble them as you see fit."
And next day, I arrive and I stumble upon an amazing surprise: Seven photos had been sold.
So, this makes me think that when you do things you are passionate about, success will come by itself.
And I don't mean success as the money for selling the photos, but about people willing to pay for having something that you did with passion.
And they want to have them at home, or at office.
And after this, there was a cultural event at my school, and one of the school teachers knew that I had an exhibition at the airport.
So he invited me to take part of this exhibition.
I accepted, and my photos were hanging at the reception's lobby, where all the boys arrived from all different schools, at a nationwide level, all of them watched the photos, it was amazing and very fulfilling.
As a result of this, I was invited to give a photography workshop.
How do you think I felt when they invite me to give a photography workshop, being my canvas a mobile phone?
These photos you see, which you have seen, and the next ones you will see, have been taken with a cell phone.
And while I tell you this, I like to think that imagination makes us infinite, in the sense of not limiting yourself to what you have but to do what you like doing, without the fear of what will people say or how photographers are going to judge you. And all this,
well, is amazing.
After this, I really like to think that life is too short.
And I think that this sentence we have already seen at many places, has also taught me not to stop doing what I do because, actually, this amount of things that I have done, have been really fulfilling for my life.
I also want to show you a picture that I think changed my life because it was one of the things that, when I saw it, I really wanted to do as it says: To make things happen.
And this is, in fact, really important to make things happen.
Because remember that nobody in this life is remembered for not doing something.
So, let me say goodbye with a question: What would you do if you weren't afraid?
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Disruption has always existed.
The only difference is that before, it only happened once in a blue moon, and now it happens day after day, all of the time.
Recently, in the last two or three years, they invented a nickname for disruption.
The Kodak moment.
Do you remember that Kodak
advertising campaign?
What happened to Kodak?
From 1996 to 2012, Kodak market value fell from in excess of 28 million dollars to less than 100 [million dollars].
And from 140,000 employees to 17,000.
And who did this to Kodak?

This man, Steve Sasson, a Kodak employee.
In 1975, he invented the digital camera.
That camera weighed eight pounds and produced photos with a resolution of 0.01 mega pixels.
They laughed at him in a Kodak management meeting.
They said he was crazy, that he'd lost it
"Our business is different."
And this accelerated disruption that we're experiencing, where is it coming from?
What is it based on?
I personally think it's coming from Moore's Law.
Gordon Moore said in 1965, that every two years, computing power per dollar doubles.
All those red points on the curve there are real technologies that have existed, and you can see that on a logarithmic scale; it's an exponential function that keeps growing.
But if Gordon Moore said that in 1965, why does this graph start at the beginning of the last century?
Well, this graph was done by Raymond Kurzweil, and what he did was extrapolate Moore's Law backwards, and discovered that humanity learned to process automated or mechanical information, and it has developed this capacity to process information exponentially.
And therefore, obviously, change is occurring ever faster, and there is no indication that suggests that this will ever stop.
And it's not just computing power, but technological convergence as well, and there are four different technologies: information technology, nanotechnology, biotechnology, and what's called cognotechnology, or what some call artificial intelligence or cognitive computing; it has different names.
These four technologies, this is technological convergence.
These four technologies help each other to develop ever faster, and they are really changing the world.
As I said, disruption is happening day after day.
We've seen some examples recently, in the last 10 years.
Bookshops, and I mean book shops because here bookshops sell all kinds of things.
Bookshops have gone through profound and severe disruption.
No one in their right mind would travel with a copy of "War and Peace", right?
That's disappearing.
And music shops have had it even worse because not only has the format of the product changed, the business model has changed as well as a direct result of the new product proposition.
Research:
I remembering spending almost a year going to the library for 10 hours a day to find out everything humanly possible about a specific subject.
Today, you can do this at your desk at home in, I don't know, two weeks, maybe a month.
A perfect candidate to suffer due to disruption in the next 10 years is the fossil fuel industry.
Just eight years ago, it was the world's largest industry;
by a lot.
Look what was happening.
The price of fossil fuels, for about 70 years, remained fairly constant with the odd jump whenever there was a crisis in the Middle East.
And here, on your right, you'll see the price of solar energy, and how it's falling, like a piano from a window.
The Sun gives the Earth all the energy it needs to keep the entire planet going for a full 24 hours.
There's no economic scarcity there.
And look what's happened to the price of solar energy.
In the last 37 years, the price has fallen by 100 times.
In the last two years, another 10 times.
And today, you're looking at solar power that costs between 4 and 5 cents per kW/h.
That's a lot less than what we pay here in Costa Rica,
where we are very proud of the fact that 90% of our energy is renewable.
The International Energy Agency has said that at 4 cents per kW/h generated by roof panels, it's unbeatable.
Now, solar energy does have a little problem: at night there's no sun.
There are some countries in the world, one of which is Costa Rica, that could perhaps avoid a great deal of disruption by using the batteries that we already have. We have huge batteries called "stoppers".
For me, it's not difficult to imagine generating solar power during the day and hydroelectric power at night.
And if we real get down to the basics of this industry, easily, in 10 years, we could be using energy at 2 cents per kW/h.
This is going to change everything.
Absolutely everything.
But it's going to destroy a sector of the economy.
Who's next?
Logistics.
Logistics, in the United States, consumes 16.5% of GDP.
I say "consume" because it doesn't add value, it just moves stuff from place to place.
And I don't know how many million people work in logistics, moving things from one place to another.
Turns out that 3D printers were actually invented 32 years ago three dimensional printers.
For 25 years, this technology was protected by patents.
Therefore, the printers were expensive, they were difficult to use they cost hundreds of thousands of dollars, and could only print I think it was 16 different materials.
Seven years ago, the patents expired, and the market exploded.
Today, we can buy printers for 200 dollars, like this one for example, to print plastic toys for the grandkids.
Or we can spend 800 or 1,200 dollars, and print useful things for the home, or crafts, or works of art that can be created simply with the precision of a laser printer.
Or we can spend several thousand dollars and print truly useful things like prosthetics for example.
And it seems there's no limit to what can be done with a 3D printer.
For example, I think it was in March of this year, in China, in 24 hours, 10 bedrooms were printed, and you can see, that on a logarithmic scale, it's an exponential function
that keeps growing.
The queue is long. And it's moving fast.
Another group that's definitely on the list is drivers.
And driving is a very, very common occupation.
In Costa Rica alone, there are 30-40,000 people registered in the Social Security system as drivers.
But these are the full-time drivers.
Because we know there is also a bout 1.5 million more of us 'mugs' for want of a better word, who spend 5 to 10 hours a week driving.
This doesn't add value.
Luckily, 11 years ago, the US Department of Defense offered 1 million dollars to whoever could produce the first autonomous vehicle
that could navigate an obstacle course in the desert.
I think it was in Nevada or New Mexico.
In 2004, no one completed the course.
In 2005, five teams managed to complete the course.
And since then, in the last 10 years, the technology behind autonomous cars has been advancing.
And these autonomous vehicles, in fact, just the Google cars alone, have traveled more than 2 million kilometers without any accidents.
And I don't know one single person, one driver, who could drive 200, 000 kilometers without a single bump.
And everyone is on to this.
This is the Nissan car, this is the Google car.
In all the cars we see driving around, not just in the United States but all over the world, typically what we see is a driver with a regular driving license sitting behind the wheel afraid of flooring it, because if they do, they literally put their foot in it.
They're going to crash.
Already, in Europe, today, you can buy a Mercedes or a Volvo, that guarantees that it will stay in lane, reverse park by itself, and in the cabin,
you simply press a button take out your laptop, and get to work.
And this is already available today.
This Mercedes car, which is one of the most advanced, has features that I love, like for example, the windows are screens.
You can decide that today
I'd like to drive in Tuscany.
So it shows the Tuscan countryside.
And this one, you have to, someone asked me once, but how do I give the car instructions?
It's certainly not with a keyboard.

How?! Like a screen?
No, you're not going to fill in the screen or even use your fingers.
You speak to the car.
Like that movie, Night Rider, exactly the same.
This Mercedes also has another feature that I think is really nice, that in a stressful cabin, it puts on calming music.
And when you are out on the open highway, it makes things a bit more exciting, it changes the music.
It's subtle intelligence, but very useful.
And this is the latest Google model, it doesn't even have handles or pedals, but it has rear view mirrors, as they are required by Californian law.
If you thought that this disruption would only affect drivers, think again.
What's going to happen to the insurance industry?
If cars don't crash, who's going to take out insurance?
It turns out that drivers are really dangerous.
More than 1 million people each year die in road accidents.
In Costa Rica, more than 1,000 people a year die in traffic accidents.
And if a thousand die, there must be 10,000 badly injured.
It's terrible!
So we have a technology that could save lives.
There's absolutely no doubt about this.
And it'll cause unemployment amongst drivers, it'll cause disruption in the insurance industry, it'll cause disruption to real estate. Did you see that coming?
Why?
Why don't we get rid of car parks in cities?
Why park the car?
The car takes me to work and I tell it go back, and run some errands or bring the kids to school or something. (Laughter)
If we got rid of all city car parks, what would happen to the price of real estate?
And the list of disruptions brought on by technology goes on.
Radio for example.
Radio is going to take a huge hit.
Why?
Radio is the only media that can be used to entertain drivers.
And if there are no drivers?
And this is happening very fast.
The prediction is that in 10 years, all major cities on the planet will function with autonomous vehicles.
So, nothing's going to happen
to white collar workers.
Think again.
65 years ago, Alan Turing published the famous article where he defined the Turing test which is a very simple test, that tells us if a machine is intelligent or not.
And in 1979, the American Association for Artificial Intelligence was founded.
And for years, when I was at university, there was a tremendous discussion around whether machines would be able to think or not.
So what, 17 years ago, 'Deep Blue' beat the world chess champion.
But then they told us, "No but to play chess, you don't need to think", right?
'Deep Blue' won out of brute force.
But it turns out that four years ago, a grandchild of Deep Blue's, called Watson, beat one of the best Jeopardy players of all time.
And this is very relevant.
Because for 20 years, the capacity or lack thereof, to process natural language, was always one of the arguments that was used against the possibility that a machine could think; and to play Jeopardy, you must process natural language.
Large quantities of natural language.
And now, Watson is working.
He's making a living.
Watson works in a hospital in New York State, where he recommends oncology treatments.
To recommend oncology treatments, Watson analyzes the patient's entire medical file.
And the entire medical files of all patients who have the same form of cancer.
And any related research articles ever published, up until the previous day.
No oncologist can do that.
And it presents the results in this way, three possible treatments with three possible successful outcomes.
And the oncologist double clicks on one and back-tracks, and it gives them all the information that led to this conclusion.
Sure, Watson couldn't read Don Quixote and summarize it for an exam on Friday.
But it can read Don Quixote, and come to a host of very useful conclusions.
Another field where we are using this type of deep learning technology is in the analysis of medical images. And we have a lot of different types of medical images:
X-rays, ultrasounds, MRIs, PET scans, functional MRIs etc.
The best doctors, the best experts, get it wrong 5% of the time when they study or examine a medical image.
This guy, Jeremy Howard, founded a company 3 years ago called "Enlitic" which does precisely this, analyze medical images.
How is an image analyzed?
One pixel at a time right?
And using deep learning,
this guy, Jeremy Howard, gets it wrong less than 5% of the time.
So here we have a technology that is as precise as the best of humans, but it can function 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, anywhere in the world.
Two years ago, Oxford University published this report, and we can question the methodology and the results and if it's this number or that one.
But I think that certainly it gives us a better idea of what is ahead of us.
This study concludes that 47% of white collar jobs in the United States are automizable today.
Two years ago, McKinsey published a report estimating the economic impact that technological disruption will make by 2025.
Obviously, the McKinsey study didn't give numbers for Costa Rica, but the global numbers are millions, or trillions, billions of billions, or millions of millions of dollars, numbers I can't even comprehend.
So I translated this to Costa Rica assuming that the proportion of Costa Rican GDP compared to global GDP will remain the same.
And we see that here it's all over.
We have sales and support functions, we have technologists, we have educators, we have health, we have legacies.
All of us.
The total estimated impact, - I think it's overestimated - for 2025, it's more or less 10% of GDP.
And no one is batting an eyelid at this.
This is important.
So, what are we going to do?
What can we do?
What will happen?
Machines are going to do the work of humans?
And humans? What are we going to do?
This is technology that generates wealth.
This is very good technology.
All of the examples that I've given you, are desirable technologies.
There are various alternatives.
Many alternatives.
There are always alternatives.
One alternative we use a lot in Costa Rica is to do nothing, and hope that the Sacred Virgin will help us.
(Laughter) Another alternative I don't recommend is that we become nudists like those who attacked the garment factories 200 years ago, at the start of the Industrial Revolution and prohibit exponential technology in Costa Rica altogether.
I think what we should perhaps do is generate artificial disruption, deliberately.
Disruption in the education sector, disruption in the government sector so that, in this way, we can change the way we educate, the way we govern in order to re-educate, re-train, and re-teach people who lose their jobs in a much faster way than we do now and get them back into the labor force.
If we get it wrong, we get up, go back and try again.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Today I will share with you two ideas that are difficult to believe.
I hope that these two ideas, these two seeds will be planted and will grow bit by bit until they become so big that there is no choice but to seek a forum like this to inform all the others and spread them out.
In my case, these two seeds were planted 10 years ago.
And it took living and working in more that 25 countries to be here with you today.
Today's topic is '3 Steps to Success'.
This idea isn't very hard to believe.
In fact, it is quite logical.
What is harder to believe is that these 3 steps are exactly the same wherever you may be:
in any country, in any culture, in any industry, in any job, for whatever idea that you may have.
Wherever you may be, it is the same.
Why do I say this?
I have worked with people, from small traders, leaders of the biggest companies in the world, to even heads of state.
In every country that I was in, I was told the same thing: "Rafa, it's a very good idea
and it's really great but... it cannot happen here.
Here in Thailand, here in Switzerland, here in the USA and Colombia, here in Kazakhstan.
"Here, you can't."
"There, yes, you can.
But here, you can't."
So I said, "Well, that's enough, right?
If not here, then where?
What are we left with?
There is one example that shows how illogical this idea is, shared by all people from any part of the world.
Let's think about it.
What is the best medicine in the world?
In Mexico, many would say: "Well, there is an ointment for the skin from a laboratory
in Switzerland. It's the best."
"It'll fix everything."
If you apply some here, your headache will go away."
In Switzerland, there's a thing called "Schwedenbitter", which means "Swedish elixir".
They use it for everything.
They use it when they have a headache.
In Sweden, there is an ointment that retains the miracle of the Mayans from Mexico.
What are we doing here?
Wherever it is, it is the same.
In any country, war and large epidemics aside, there will be the same obstacles, the same opportunities and those 3 steps to success will also be the same.
These 3 steps are vision, education and work.
The vision is what you are going to do,
and where you want to go,
seeing your goals clearly.
Education is about how you're going to do this.
Beyond primary, secondary and tertiary education, we also get professional development and we get experience.
Work is the "just do it".
There's no way around it.
If we want to develop an idea or a company, it will require a lot of work.
These 3 steps are just as necessary for the success of any project, wherever it may be.
But as a consultant in business development, I would make that first analysis of what is missing in a project. Most of the time, 9 out of 10, only one of these three steps is missing,
and that is the vision.
Unlike what most people think, it's not the education or the work that's lacking.
It's the vision.
The vision is that grand dream.
It is the clarity of your goals and objectives.
It is the strategy that helps you achieve those goals.
It is the focus that helps you walk in a straight line.
It is the ambition to grow.
It is aspiring to make an impact.
It is the attitude to endure the bad times and the composure not to lose it in the good times.
I mentioned that all these were equally necessary but there is one that is the most important.
And that is the vision.
A clear vision helps us realize what we're missing in the learning process and makes the education much more relevant.
A clear vision, with a relevant education, makes our work much more efficient.
Let's do a quick development analysis of a project called "Mexico".
I figured it'd be the most relevant issue here at TEDxTijuana.
Many people say, or the hypothesis states, that Mexicans don’t work hard enough.
Classic, right?
We, Mexicans, are lazy.
The image of a Mexican taking a nap next to a cactus.
But let’s look at the numbers. Mexico has ranked first among the 34 OECD countries for the last 6 years in the average annual hours per worker.
Clearly, it’s not a lack of hard work.
The second point: statistics show that, in general, the education in Mexico has to catch up and there's a lot of opportunity to improve.
Agreed, but let's put these numbers in context.
Let's compare them with Switzerland.
Switzerland has a population of 8 million.
I assure you, and everyone will agree, that we can find from all 120 million Mexicans, less than 7 percent of them, 8 million Mexicans that are equal or better educated than the entire population of Switzerland.
However, when we look at the largest global companies, Switzerland currently has 8 of them, at one point even 15.
Mexico only has 3.
When we we look for companies with the best worldwide reputation, Switzerland has 3, Mexico is not even on the list.
What is missing here?
Is it education? No.
We have equal or larger number of educated people.
For the project "Mexico", what this country lacks is more of the vision.
It's the vision to want to achieve what we propose.
A vision and ambition to become the best in the world.
Let's create a benchmark in this analysis, as we realize that what we lack is the vision, and let's see what a country with a lot of vision looks like.
We don't have to go too far.
1 km from here is the USA.
They have been working for generations and even created slogans that they market around the world.
The US has these famous slogans: “The land of opportunity," "The American dream".
It is a type of marketing for their people.
The children in the US grow up dreaming of great things.
This marketing is so successful that people from all over the world, well-educated and hardworking, when they have a dream, think of going to the US to achieve it.
So let me ask you something:
Where is the Mexican dream?
I mentioned that I would give 2 seeds that I hope would grow strong: Wherever you may be, it’s all the same.
So let's start here and now and build the Great Mexican Dream.
Thank you very much.
