Life is a dream.
In a text as old as the Bible, in the pages of the Genesis we find Eve, who comes from the dream of Adam.
Jacob, who dreams of Heaven and Earth and the communication between them.
And also with Joseph, who dreams so much and interprets so many dreams that his brothers say when they see him coming: "Here comes the dreamer."
There are countless stories of dreams in a lot of books.
That's why you may say that I'm a dreamer, but I'm not the only one.
Dreams seem to be something that defends us from routine and monotony, shuffling the images of ordinary life, thus interrupting the seriousness of adulthood.
But dreams themselves are a dream.
And since we were kids we were taught not to give them any credit.
It's funny how long we've been deaf, with wide open blind eyes, to the messages that come from that swampy land.
Dreams are important because we sense that they make up a fundamental part of us, and because we see reflected in them our innermost aspects.
For instance, I dream of snakes: sea snakes, all kinds of snakes.
In dreams we can make associations with more freedom and that can be translated into a creative activity.
To create is to transfer the attributes from one thing to another.
It is a process, like dreaming.
And any part or piece of creation doesn't fall from the sky; It has its origins on a previous thing.
When we create, like in dreaming, we work with what we already have in mind.
Poets and painters, they always quote works of other authors and recreate them in different ways.
Nowadays, there are compositions made exclusively of existing materials or existing texts, which are crafted and presented in a new way.
Nothing new.
It's what Borges did and what I'm doing now, because nothing I said until now was written by me.
However, this text didn't exist before.
It was created by me.
I made it from fragments of academic texts, songs, phrases of the popular saying, and information banks available for anyone on the Internet.
All stolen words, foreign words, which help me express something that I believe in.
Dreams can be a source of inspiration for their content, but also for their mechanism.
Because of their way of undoing and rearranging, of putting in the same script things that seem distant from each other.
Our childhood mates; the highway.
The barber; a movie we watched last night.
Dreams show us that we can't avoid to be creative and original.
Although we start from existing materials and texts, it's the combination that each one makes of those elements what gives them a unique, perhaps unrepeatable meaning, that defines us and reveals us better.
I like to think this, because being creative and being original many times can be a demanding and limiting requirement when doing artistic work.
In the same way I also like to imagine a perfect keyboard for me.
It would be a keyboard made only of three keys and the combination between them.
Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V, cut and paste.
A keyboard on which what I decide to cut and the place I choose to paste it is the manifestation of my most intimate feelings.
In the same way I also like to imagine an ideal image for a writer.
The DJ writer: Someone who is dedicated to mixing words as if they were musical tracks to achieve something moving, touching.
A while ago I went through a heartache, and like almost everyone in a moment like that I felt the need to write something about what had happened in that relationship.
The first thing I noticed was that on love and lack of love, especially the latter, there was already a lot written.
Second thing was that everything had been very special in that relationship.
And I couldn't find something to say and to write that was so special like what had happened in itself.
I was constantly bouncing from the chat history of the conversations I had with this person -- there were many messages because he lives in another country --
I was bouncing between this history and a blank page.
All the time, continuously re-reading the messages.
And that's how I realized that what I wanted to write, I had already written it.
And I had it there, on my cellphone.
So I downloaded the entire chat to a text file, I deleted a lot, cut, pasted, rearranged everything a bit, and I put together a text.
A small text that I called: "Smiling face, Cloud, Heart, Lightning."
This text can only be read from my cellphone and I'm going to read to you a fragment of each chapter.
