I'm Claudio Furnier.
I'm a cartoonist, not a speaker. However, just as every cartoonist, I have to speak up.
As every cartoonist, I have my signature-drawing for identifying my works. I discovered it in my early childhood, as I drew liittle penguin like this.
It was then when I realized that I had a talent.
It was a penguin like this one. You can notice a true talent, can't you?
Above all because it was my first professional job. I was paid for this drawing.
I got a good beating from my dad for drawing it on the wall.
So I said, "I don't think it's a good way, it doesn't make people laugh."
So it evolved.
When I was a teenager, I drew a rain of hens.
What a beautiful hen!
People liked it more than a little penguin. I created 500 hens like that, but no one understood it, so I said, "This isn't a good way either."
Evolution continued and per Darwin's theory, a bear survived, as there are a lot of them in Cordoba. I had an idea to create a liitle bear, which started to illustrate postcards. Because I said, "What is a bear to do, if not appear on a postcard?"
And then I discovered what I was going to do, my vocation and my job: the graphic humour.
That's how it all began. I started to publish my first drawings in the mythical magazine Revista Hortensia. I started to experiment a bit with humorous forms.
First was the humour from Córdoba.
If you were born in Córdoba, you surely came across this kind of humor.
I started to ask myself what distinguishes the humour from Córdoba and if something like that truly exists.
In the first place, there is language, words like: "che", "nero", "nosiguá", and just like in this joke, a typical "comechingón" laughing at conquistador, is laughing at the other, but in this affectionate manner.
It seemed like a way of laughing not only at someone
but also with them. There were also jokes that seemed to be characteristic for Cordoba, even though they lacked specific language. Even without a text, there was this way of laughing at somebody, but staying friendly, just like giving somebody a nickname.
This is an example of that.
That's how I realized that the humour of Cordoba was a lot more than Cordoba's regional language.
The possibility of publishing abroad, globalization and the Internet made me see the opportunities offered by the silent humour.
The European humour is more conceptual, maybe more philosophical. This is an example of that.
This drawing was one of the bestsellers in an agency that I collaborated with in England. Maybe because it united two important things. Sure, it was very international and humorous, but above all, it was a good critique. That was when I realized that humor can also serve as a message.
This synthesis of graphic humour and drawing is also frequently used in public campaigns, as it's an effective way of communication.
This is an example of a campaign against AIDS.
I discovered also physical humor, which always makes people laugh from the times of Chaplin until nowadays. A blow, a fall, as long as it's not serious.
When somebody falls, we laugh as long as they are OK.
Physical humor unites us.
When it came to the political humor, as I wasn't a good caricaturist, I tried to make the politician similar to my drawing and not my drawing to the politician.
That's how appeared this character, which represented the president as if she was in a fairytale. What's interesting about political humour is the identification with people, which lets the humourist tell things that a serious journalist can't say.
To represent in some way, what ordinary people think. One of the things, which we, artists, are afraid of, is the blank page, because there we see ourselves as in the mirror, and that's where we will meet ourselves.
Just as the reader finds in drawings, in art
what they want to find.
I recently found in comic strips something that reflects me. I started to use the watercolor technique, which became a kind of therapy for me.
It was very personal, El parque de vida was a form of self-expression, just for my own delight.
The reader may somehow identify with it or not, but it's not what was important, because the aim of this work was just to find myself.
Graphic humour offers so many possibilities because it's not only about eliciting laughter, but also evoking reflection, deeper thought and maybe even poetry, right?
Sometimes what it takes is to add only a few phrases and it already stops a bit being just humour. It becomes a way of contemplating life, self-discovery and meeting people to whom this comic strip gave this 'feeling'.
There is a story I'd like to tell about one filmmaker and his daughter, Sofia Coppola. When she was making her first film, she asked her father, 'What kind of film should I make?'
'What kind of advice would you give me?'
Her father had much experience, so she probably thought that he would help her in the technical or commercial part.
However, the advice was that she should film something that only she herself could film. Something that nobody could film better than her.
In one word, to be herself. Maybe this kind of drawings, are the drawings that only I could make, as they reflect all my history and my way of being.
To sum up, I believe that we all have a film to make, whatever as what we do. There's no need to be a cartoonist or filmmaker. Just in everything we do, in every task, every activity we can do something that only we can do in this way.
We have our film to make, to fulfill ourselves and to discover ourselves in our own originality.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
I want to ask you something: When was the last time you watched the stars?
I know most people forget they exist specially in this polluted city which is sometimes foggy, we never see them.
But since I was a kid, maybe not that young, maybe 12 or 13 years, I've been captivated by stars and would watch them whenever possible.
My dad gave me a small telescope by the time I was 14 and I spent hours watching planets and stars and always wondering: What is out there?
Is there someone watching me back with a telescope, wondering what is out here?
This allure went on for years and at some point I decided to be an astronomer.
In Mexico, in order to be an astronomer you need to be a physicist. So I studied physics.
I never got to be an astronomer, now I'm something in between, half an astrophysicist, half a physicist but this idea of stars and the possibility of reaching them remained with me.
When I started my major in physics I realized something many of you have heard of, even if you are not totally clear about it: that more than 100 years ago, in 1905, this guy named Albert Einstein, discovered that light speed is the fastest in the universe.
That might not say you a lot because light speed is huge.
Light moves up to 186,000 mi/s so high a speed that we can phone call someone in India or Japan with no problem at all. Even astronauts who went to the moon 40 years ago were interviewed and the President could talk to them with no evident delay even though the astronauts were in the moon, thing is that light speed is huge and the universe gigantic.
We cannot travel faster than light speed and that is a severe problem to reach for the stars.
Sun is 8 light-minutes away, that is, it takes 8 minutes for light to get here from the Sun, from the Sun to us.
If the Sun exploded right now, nobody would notice up until eight minutes later.
The closest star after the Sun is Alfa Centauri, it takes four years for light to travel and it's right besides the Sun.
We live in a galaxy that is a star spiral called Milky Way.
The center of our galaxy was 30,000 light years away, it takes 30,000 light years for light to travel from the center of the galaxy to us, and two million years in traveling from Andromeda galaxy that is one of our neighbor galaxies. The universe is huge, vast, if we ever want to reach the stars the light limit is very serious, a very serious problem, but why?
Why we cannot travel faster than light?
And it's not because Einstein said so, that is no how science works.
In science there is no authority principle; it's not because some famous guy says so.
There are reasons, theoretical reasons, observational reasons, experiments show it, it is a verified fact, but why?
Basically, the answer is that theory of relativity prevents it, so I will tell you a bit, a very quick course: Relativity in a couple of minutes.
Don't be afraid, I'll give a general overview, normally it takes six months to teach this to sixth and seventh graders of physics major. So right now I will explain it in two minutes and for non-physicists.
So, don't worry.
Relativity is an old concept though it wasn't called like that. The concept can be traced back from Galileo Galilei around 1620, in the 17th century. Galileo was the first one to realize something interesting, when it comes to movement, when I move, my movement is always relative to something else.
Movement is said to be relative, here on the stage, my movement is relative to the floor.
The measurement of your car speed is relative to the street, actually planes speed measurement is made relative to the air, not the floor. You might have noticed this while watching a movie. A plane speed measurement is relative to the air, and the Earth spins around the Sun, etc.
If you were somewhere in space in the middle of nowhere it would be useless to ask if you are moving or not because there is no reference to compare your position, so movement is relative, speeds are relative, because they are always measured in relation to something else. This was discovered by Galileo 400 years ago.
That was an interesting fact for all physic studies made after Galileo. Newton and all great advances of the 18th and 19th centuries agreed with Galileo. Speed is relative, that's fine, speeds aren't absolute.
But by the end of 19th century something strange happened, many physicists studying light discovered that light is quite weird, light speed is indeed absolute and though Galileo stated that speeds were relative, that's not the case of light, it's always the same number no matter who measures it, or how fast is going whoever is measuring, no matter how fast the lightbulb sending out the light is moving. The measurement always
yields the same number and this was a problem.
This might not seem interesting to you but physicists of the 19th century were going crazy.
Problem was: either Galileo was wrong and speeds were indeed absolute, which was apparently nonsense; or those who were measuring light speed were wrong and didn't know how to measure.
But nobody was wrong which was even worst. Decades went by, until in 1905 this guy appeared, a man called Albert Einstein, who devoted his life to bringing together Galileo's idea that speeds were relative and the apparently real fact shown by the experiments, that there was an absolute speed, light speed.
Long story short, because math is complicated, part of Einstein genius was realizing there was a solution.
There was a logical solution to the problem and that logical solution derived in what we now know as Einstein's theory of relativity.
It's a very interesting theory that changes what we understand by space and time, for instance, nobody tells us that space is relative distances, object lengths depend on their movement;
if an object moves fast, they shrink.
Time is also relative, any watch ticking relative to my movement, would delay this we have measured many times, even worst: simultaneity is relative; when I say two things happen exactly at once, someone moving in relation to me would disagree.
One of us sees something first and worst: we might be moving on opposed directions and watch things backwards: first this one, then that one. So the conclusion is that sometimes, the order in time of different things is not absolute, the order of time can change, one thing before, other thing after but this brings up another huge problem, if time order is not well defined, what about causality?
If something causes something else that something should had happened first, but if we disagree on what happened first, where does that leaves causality?
Another crisis. Part of what Einstein did was realizing that there was solution, a solution to protect causality was thinking that light speed is not absolute, but the maximum speed of universe. If nothing can travel faster than light we protect causality. If something could travel faster than light then we could travel to the past, we would be able to see the effect before the cause, which is not the proper way.
So light speed is the maximum speed and that is to protect causality.
So far as of the beginning of the 20th century, light speed was the fastest and if that was all left to say, my talk would be done and off we go. But, fortunately that's not the case.
In 1916, Einstein developed a second theory, also called relativity and that's why people get confused this one is called general relativity, and it's a theory about gravity.
Einstein tried to understand gravity, which we already understood a bit since Newton; but he noticed some problems, gravity was thought of by Newton as instantaneous, if someone moved the Sun, Earth would immediately react, this would go against the fact that nothing can travel faster than light, so Einstein began to develop a new gravity theory, it took him 10 years, an amazing achievement for someone as smart of Einstein, was based on a beautiful thing: the Equivalence Principle, also discovered by Galileo: all objects fall at the same speed if two things were dropped at once, they would fall at the same time, a bowling ball or a ping pong ball would fall just the same, the heavier one does not fall faster,
in case you were wondering. That means, from another point of view, that the trajectory of an object when there is gravity, does not depend on the object. All objects follow the same trajectory curved trajectories, you've seen that, I throw objects and they move in parables and ellipses but if those trajectories are curve and nondependent of the object then trajectory is a property of space, but they are curve, then space must be curve.
Einstein concluded that gravity is a space deformation.
An interesting and beautiful thing, and what is beautiful about it is that I can cheat, I can use space deformation to cheat on Einstein himself, so I use Einstein to cheat on Einstein. I can imagine ways to distort space to work around the statement "nothing can go faster than light" and, technically, I could reach a faraway star and then come back in time for dinner.
I will share with you two possibilities, that are allowed on Einstein theory, one is beautiful and its technical name is Einstein-Rosen bridge, sounds technical because Einstein and another scientist called Rosen had this idea in 1935.
But in literature and on sci-fi movies it is called a Wormhole.
If you like science fiction then you might have seen it recently, in the movie Interstellar. On the movie, they travel through a wormhole. A wormhole is like a tunnel, I enter here and I end up in Alpha Centauri, but traveling a shorter distance, like a shortcut in space.
These structures -- here is a beautiful diagram of a wormhole in two dimensions -- are allowed by the Einstein's theory, because this theory allows these tunnels, one thing is that the theory allows it and another is having an idea of how to do that. Nobody knows how to make a wormhole, but at least theory allows it.
Another idea, and I'll let you think about it, another idea is that it is not necessary to make holes in space, an idea that thought by some guy not so long ago, in 1994, this guy on the picture, and the idea is different; instead of making holes in space let's use another property of space. Space can bended, twisted and expanded, maybe you heard about how universe expands, galaxies move away from one another not because they are drifting away from a center where an explosion occurred, no.
The way we understand it in physics is that galaxies are still, and what is growing bigger is space;
space is expanding.
So I can use this idea in a small scale.
Imagine I'm standing here and somehow I can expand the space behind me. I would start to drift away from that wall, and if at the same time I shrink the space in front of me I would become closer to the wall in front of me.
If I combine the expansion and contraction, I could move from here to that wall without moving, because the space made all the moving.
This is called warp drive or drive through distortion and it's another way to travel faster than light, actually you could travel as fast as you wish.
These are two ideas on how we could travel faster than light.
But there is a price to pay.
Such is life, whenever you find something cool it's too expensive.
We have a similar case here: from those two ideas, both worm holes and warp propulsion require, whenever we do the math of something called negative energy, which might not be crystal clear for you, but remember what Einstein said: "mass and energy are equivalent", the same thing. Remember nuclear reactors and atomic bombs, mass and energy are the same.
So negative energy equals negative mass, and I'd never went to buy minus 9 pounds of tortilla.
There are no negative masses, without negative masses there are no negative energies and without negative energies none of this is possible,
No holes in space-time, no warp propulsion, and that's a problem.
Negative energy is not forbidden, physics laws don't forbid it, but we've never seen it. It's one of those things that simply do not exist.
So, with this initial question: Can we travel faster than light?
will we someday travel the stars and get back in time for dinner?
In 1905 Einstein told us it was not possible, that light speed was a limit, but sometimes, using Einstein's other theory, general relativity, we can cheat and bend the space, expand it, compress it, make holes on it, and travel faster than light, however, the price of it is finding energies or negative masses, though they might not exist.
So we are a bit stuck, but such is science and that's part of my message, science moves forward by making questions, and sometimes we find answers, sometimes we find answers we don't like, that aren't what we wanted, but universe is not how we want it.
It is what it is and we sometimes find out that we don't have enough information, to answer the question, and that is our current situation.
Currently, we do not have all the information. Luckily, in 20, 30 or 100 years someone will come and find the answer and tell us for once if we can reach the stars faster than light speed.
Before I go I will ask you to do something, tonight, if clouds allow it, please go out and take a look of the stars and think, what is out there?
Thank you so much.
(Applause)
I take the train every Monday.
Then I get off, walk a few meters and take a taxi.
I struggle a little to get into the car, tell the address and off we go.
Then, through the little mirror the driver smiles and says: - Nice day, huh?
- Yes, indeed.
- Are you hanging out?
I don't know you, but I'm not that lucky at 3 p.m. on Monday.
-No, working- I tell him as loud as I can.
- Oh, and what do you do?
- I'm an educational psychologist.
I work figuring out ways to empower others.
Poor guy, I left him speechless!
(Applause) Anyway, on Mondays at 3 p.m. I'm on my way to work.
I work in a school and with private patients with disabilities.
In the school where I work, the kids know I talk like this.
I speak weirdly and Salvador, aged 4, says it is because my tummy aches.
(Laughter) But no.
In fact, I speak like this because when I was born I went into respiratory arrest and into another one 12 hours later.
That lack of oxygen resulted in a brain injury affecting my entire motor function.
Cerebral palsy, said the doctors.
At that time, nobody knew what I would be able to do.
But one thing was sure: whatever I did, would be done differently.
During primary school, many teachers supported me.
They were able to see all I could achieve.
Of course, there were others who thought I would never finish primary or secondary school. University was out of the question.
Others preferred having me at the back of the classroom because it seems they didn't know what to do with me.
They were not prepared.
Nobody is prepared.
Disability doesn't send you a whatsapp to say: "Get ready, I'll be there in 5."
(Applause) Anyway, the best way of being prepared is valuing diversity as part of everyday life.
Valuing each student, each person, for their potential.
In the school I work, there is Miguel.
Miguel - aged 5 - finds it difficult to remain seated.
His teacher turns to me worriedly. I observe him and suggest he should sit on a chair, not on the floor so that his feet and back are supported, he feels secure and can pay more attention.
Just like me. When I'm seated I get less tired and can pay more attention.
And it worked.
Because focusing on what we can do always works.
Because based on what we can't do... (Applause) Nothing can be built, it falls apart.
After school, kids have football, dance lesson or hockey.
But children with disabilities have therapies.
Long hours, different faces, different perspectives.
And when I was a girl, that was reflected in my games.
Yes, my dolls had therapies.
(Laughter) When I was playing with a friend, she would say: "How strange, I'm playing to take them to the park and you always take them to a place called kinesiology?"
What you live as a child leaves a mark on you.
That's why it's so important that those of us who work with children know how to treat them; above all, they are children.
After graduating, I founded Desde Adentro.
A project through which I spread my approach on disability.
Because studying about a crooked leg is not the same as living daily with a crooked leg.
It seems the same formulas are repeated for cases that are already different.
For example, "Cerebral Palsy: strengthen the torso, stimulate the march, put splints, do surgeries",
a pinch of salt and pepper.
And the person? Did they look at them?
Do you know how they live with their difficulties?
Do you know their wishes and fears?
With kids, do they respect their playtime so necessary for their growth?
The answers will let us know what people need to be themselves.
And so the cabinet where we classify the diagnoses will be too small, because the person is always much more than a diagnosis.
(Applause) But it’s not easy for everyone to work in this way.
It seems disability is a mirror nobody wants to be reflected in.
When I was a girl, one day a nun approached.
She looked at me and said: "I hope you get well."
(Laughter) I looked at her and without hesitation I said, "I'm already well."
Sure, because in any case, to be cured meant learning to live with my condition.
And to learn it’s necessary to generate more flexible contexts, it’s not only about building a ramp - and not blocking it - but creating environments where people with disability can be themselves without having to look like anyone else to receive the inclusion award.
(Applause) What needs to be done is simple.
Make what is already difficult easier.
We are not poor little things,
nor little angels. We are not special, special effects are special.
(Applause) We don’t have a handicap, Golf players have a handicap.
(Applause) We are people with disabilities.
People.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
(Whistling of a whale) The Earth breathes through the oceans and the oceans are alive thanks to the sound of the life that inhabits them.
Light is unable to link visual information to more than a few meters below the surface, while the sound is carried hundreds of kilometers to bring this vital exchange to the equilibrium of the sea's ecosystems.
Contaminating this channel means condemning the sea to an irreversible imbalance.
(Sounds of the deep sea) However, less than a century ago, when we began to explore and exploit the sea for industrial purposes, humans introduced sources of acoustic contamination into the sea.
These sources are linked to all activities: oil and gas prospecting, the functioning of wind farms, maritime transport, military action, certain scientific experiments, but also the construction of ports and ocean platforms.
All these types of contamination are mixed together, occur in the same physical space, travel for a long time and over long distances, because the sea is capable of making sound travel five times faster than in air.
They mix and pollute every corner of this world.
Jacques Cousteau's world of silence exists, but only for humans.
It's enough to use a hydrophone, which is an adapted microphone, so that we can capture every aspect of sound like a dolphin, like the ear of a dolphin, to find ourselves in a world full of 20,000 sounds, in a world completely unknown to every one of us, in an extraterrestrial world in the literal sense of the word.
This world in which the codes are unknown to us, which we have invaded with artificial sources. that have been converted into a cacophony that threatens its existence.
To understand how these acoustic sources interact with the sea and to what degree we can begin to perceive the effects on these animals that live in the sea, here we have a simulator, a simulator so that we can understand what acoustic contamination is.
And we have a series of boats and each one of them has its own acoustic characteristics.
This is a fishing boat.
If we move away from this boat, we will be able to see the acoustic fingerprint that this boat leaves in the sea.
To get an idea of where we are, this is between Ibiza and Mallorca, facing the coast of Barcelona and what we see here is a fingerprint, namely, where the noise from this boat reaches and it's just a boat.
Of course, we can change it to a much noisier boat, we can take a merchant vessel, we can do the same exercise.
We see what fingerprint it leaves and see how this fingerprint practically reaches the coast of Barcelona, it's more substantial and it extends much farther.
And this is just a single boat.
We know that within a few years there will be more than 50,000 ships, that travel across every sea of the world.
But if we continue at this rate and insert a few animals into this environment and if we pay attention - if we can turn up the sound a little - if we pay attention to the noise they make, this is the song of a whale in the sea, but if it gets closer to this source, its call is completely masked.
We have a few dolphins that whistle - this is a sound without contamination - and they enter the proximity of this boat, its calls are masked, its channel of communication is completely blocked.
And this makes it so that every day, its whole life, this fauna is threatened.
It's not just the effects of masking that count, this noise can also kill.
If the animal finds itself close to the contaminator, if during these masking effects, it can't detect a boat getting closer, the animals die.
Some of them we see here, but there are also other species.
We know now that this contamination not only affects whales, but also invertebrates; these beings that can't even hear sound, but that have a few features that allow them to maintain their equilibrium inside the water column and when it's saturated it causes the animals to be exposed to the acoustic sources, they completely lose the possibility of staying alive in this habitat and after a few days, they die.
So, it's a global phenomenon.
What can we do?
Now in our hands, we have the ability to take action. Technology exists that allows operators to continue to use the sea in a way that limits the negative effects associated with their activities.
This technology allows us in this contamination that we have created, to be able to identify all of the sources that are found, every sound associated with all activities, and also those that the living beings produce and with this we can alert them about the presence of these animals so that they are not close to the activity that could affect them.
These are smart ears that analyze the data in real-time that characterizes each one of these sources and that transmit this to the boats and to the operators that travel on top of these living beings.
It works this way: It's software available on the internet, here you see the address.
Everyone can access it and it is a network of sensors in every ocean that allows with just one click real-time access to each one of these corners.
Once you access these sites - and here we will enter a cabled observatory - with all of the data that arrives in real-time to the coast, we are going to enter the seabed close to Barcelona and we know that we will hear live what is happening as we speak.
What we hear now, (Sound of the sea)
This observatory is located 20 meters below the surface and so, life is the life of the invertebrates that are there.
Sometimes boats pass; in fact, we are capable of identifying each one of these boats based on the sound that each one gives off.
We see some small colored squares underneath this drawing, this is a representation of the analysis that we are doing, it's an analysis based on neuronal networks that learn to recognize the parameters of each sound which is captured.
Here we see some boats that are traveling through this area, we know their identity, we know how fast they're going, we know what direction they are heading and thanks to the observatory, to this hydrophone, and to this smart ear, we capture the parameters that characterize each one of these boats.
And with this, we are able to construct an acoustic map of what happens in the sea in real-time.
This is the amount of noise that exists in this place that we can relate to the presence of animals and know at any given moment what dose of sound each one receives and this allows us to manage it to fairly limit this exposure.
And that extends into a network of smart sensors.
Now, 20 years ago, when there was talk of acoustic contamination in the sea, this fell on deaf ears.
Now the moment has arrived in which this technology allows us to combine our interests and the actors of the sea.
There is no time for conflict, NGOs, governments, industries, everyone has to start to listen in the same direction.
Technology has given us a seventh sense, as well as the gift of ubiquity.
Today, through this network of sensors we can connect our brain automically in real-time to any corner of the planet, underwater.
This implies a responsibility to provide adapted solutions to the necessities that we have just seen.
There's no time left, if we don't realize this, years will pass and the sea, it won't even exist.
This network is in many places, but there are other places where we still have time to stop this destruction there, like the Amazon, the Arctic.
We have little time.
But maybe this network, this technology is the only or the last opportunity that we have to return this vital, fragile equilibrium to the sea and allow the Earth to keep breathing.
(Piano music) Thank you very much.
(Applause)
I remember when I was taught compound interest in high school.
That if you put 100 pesos, the next month you had 110; the following month, 121; then 132; because it's going to accumulate: interests are on interest, as well.
I understood it, but what really drove me crazy was the fact that if you waited long enough you could reach levels otherwise unexpected.
For example, after 10 years, you reach almost 10 million pesos.
With only 100 pesos, doing nothing else.
What I wasn't taught in high school is that in life there are a lot of processes where nothing happens, nothing happens, nothing happens, and suddenly a lot of things happen.
They are processes with different cumulative mechanisms that produce unexpected results, unexpected effects.
Networks of friends are one example. Networks of contacts. If we go from one to two contacts, maybe there are a little more options, but if we move on to three, we have a lot more options than we thought.
We can do something with one, with the other, with another.
With these two, with these two.
With those two, with all three.
Alone.
The more contacts, the more friends we have, options multiply.
The same goes for learning.
We learn one thing.
Then we learn another one that maybe it has nothing to do with the other.
But we learn a third, and with the third we see a pattern that allows us to understand something new from the other two.
Then we do something new, we try something.
And it goes wrong.
But we learned something else, once again.
With every thing we learn, with each pattern, learning becomes easier.
In this way we are building a learning castle that instead of depleting our memory, allows us to learn more and more.
In all these processes happens the same like in compound interest.
One starts slowly, slowly, slowly, but if we have persistence, perseverance, and look at the long term, we can come up with results that we could not imagine.
I may or may not have money to invest in compound interest.
But I learned that I will always invest in my networks of friends and contacts and I will never ever stop learning.
(Applause)
Pepe Mogt: This and This is Nortec.
Ramón Amezcua: I love electronic music since I was a teen.
Most memories I have from Tijuana have to do with techno-pop, and progressive rock, the Tijuana radio beats of the time.
Then, I started my dental surgeon studies.
As taking care of my patients I would think of electronic music,
I even had a keyboard by my side.
Bostich was in project during those years.
I came to Mexico, Guadalajara and Monterrey,
and played in raves during weekends.
On weekdays, I would go back to take care of my patients.
Every sound of my city inspired me to create music.
Dentist sound, (Plays dentist sound).
PM: I started playing electronic music at an early age, with what was known at the time as industrial party music.
Afterwards, I mutated into the Fussible sound. The Fussible sound was very electronic, more of a dance floor sound related to acid house, break beat and similar beats.
In 1999 I was invited to a family party,
specifically a wedding. I'm not sure if fortunately or unfortunately, when I arrived, the table I was assigned was side by side with a northern band.
I despised northern music, (Laughter). I did not liked it at all.
I tried to switch the table, but I couldn't.
"This is your table," I was told.
The band kept playing out loud, and I really felt like going away.
After some drinks I finally settled on the party, I had to be there anyway, and my perception of it began to change so I wondered, "I don't like this, but this is what we have here." Or at least what we had on the party.
In Tijuana, a northern state, that's the most popular music.
And I said to myself, what if I remixed it with electronic music?
what if I extract it from its context, deconstruct it and turn it into something I like?
By a quirk of fate, that was the moment an idea came about. The idea of picking up the regional elements we had at home, instead of somehow denying them, because we had lived with electronic music.
First, I approached the northern group to ask how much they charged for recording a piece, so I could work upon it,
but it was too expensive. Definitely out of my budget, I realized I had to go to some local studios, where the red zone bands go and play. I found a studio where I obtained tapes of instruments played separately, badly played, but that was the essence, the street sound of Tijuana,
which is a very northern, a very Tijuana sound.
After I collected the material in 1999, Nortec emerged as a sound fusion proposal.
And as I previously said, both Bostich and I, Fussible, already had our own well developed projects, with concerts for hundreds and even thousands of people, at raves and parties in different cities.
No just Mexico, but abroad. Yet it was all electronic.
But then Nortec came about, and people just left.
They didn't get it, they thought
we would play "quebradita," or just something not cool.
Events for thousands became events for 40 people.
It stands out that most of those 40, were people who thought "something is going on here,"
Most of them came from artistic disciplines, graphic design, video.
That is how Nortec Collective was born. From there, a new path emerged, a way for us to say, this is Tijuana.
All these images, every musical creation, and the different disciplines of a group of people with no identity, is achieved through the 1999 collective.
RA: The first Nortec tracks were ready.
Pepe was creating Ventilador and I, Polaris.
We launched a callout for musicians from Tijuana.
We gave them the sounds, I even came to Mexico to distribute among my friends the CDR with the sounds, so that they started working on it.
Pepe and I were in charge of the tracks curatorship and focused in releasing a record and making a company.
The company name was Mil Records because we just made 1,000 copies of the record.
We gave that record totally for free and thankfully
one of those copies came into the hands of Chris Blackwell,
famous for making Bob Marley, U2, and other groups known worldwide. That is how the story of Bostich+Fussible or Nortec begun, in terms that we became known around the world.
PM: Out of those 40 people I mentioned, and after the release in Palm Pictures with Chris Blackwell and Island Records, things changed, there was a boost in our audience from hundreds to thousands of people because we were attending the main music festivals. Actually Coachella was the first show we attended as Nortec,
then we toured around Europe, played at the SONAR in Barcelona. Basically the most important electronic music festivals, though we were just a bunch of guys, with a Tijuana bar as a working place,
that bar was "El Dandy Del Sur".
There we would gather to drink some beer, and let ideas flow.
"Let's do this," and the designer said, "I will print some T-shirts you can take with you," "I have these visuals."
And we left the bar by 10 in the morning, (Laughter) every week because it was a tough work.
By then everything was set up
in a collective form of work. Had you been to Tijuana, you might have noticed
some very endemic behaviors, that might seem strange elsewhere, but are ordinary in Tijuana.
And, as my friend and writer Rafa Saavedra once said, Tijuana Makes me Happy. (Music)
RA, just a sample.
(Applause) PM, Tijuana is a young city, of about 125 years old, full of contradictions and migrations.
A city that is both downtown and the outskirts, that is north, south, east and west all at once.
Despite being a young city filled with myths, back in the thirties it had its first boom with the great Agua Caliente Casino.
Famous figures such as Al Capone, Rita Hayworth, the Marx brothers, Clark Gable, and other people would go to Tijuana during Prohibition, to do what they were not allowed in their own country.
Yet, this also gave Tijuana a musical background, and the city evolved from the collision of street culture, music, bets, and mafia.
RA: Tijuana, as a mutating city was embodied in our record "Tijuana Sound Machine." Each track reflects what we lived in those historic moments back in 2009, with all the chaos, clubbing and violence. In tracks like Akai 47, Retén or Tijuana Sound Machine.
PM: Fortunately, we reached the public scene by the time social networks emerged.
With YouTube, My Space,
which later disappeared, Then came Facebook, SoundCloud,
and we took advantage of the network to tell out musical story,
to share our work through videos, free music, different versions of the album tracks that where not available on record.
RA: then we launched the "Bulevar 2000" album, for us a very meaningful one because we proved that culture, specifically music, can change the perception of reality.
PM: When we released the "Tijuana Sessions" record, the press came to an event we made, called Nortec City. An event on a Tijuana abandoned building known as Jai Alai, a giant building of the forties or so,
Pretty old and full of dust, but we cleaned it, so that the artists could display their works. There were many exhibitions and videos. So, in came the press and rumor spread so effectively, that people from TIME Magazine, LA Times, NY Times, and Newsweek. Suddenly, TIME Magazine wrote 9 pages of Tijuana and its local artists. So having these media was a big surprise,
Not only did they wrote about Nortec but of each of the artists.
It was the artists determination, which made the institutions wonder what was going on,
because something was indeed going on. This picture on the screen is of a show, is when we composed the music and gave the opening concert at the Pan American Games Ceremony.
We had never played at such a big stadium.
We planned that concert long in advance,
upon arrival, all attendees were given a lamp, with a specific color depending on their seat location at the stadium.
So everyone had a lamp, they just had to turn it on and off
according to some instructions and hints of the selected tracks.
The track was an adaptation of Polaris, a special variation for that show.
That was our first synchronized show, it was people, light and music.
Every time the music played, people would follow.
At some point the 50,000 people in the stadium started to interact with our different tracks, and the fireworks displayed to the music.
The Pan Americans opening was remarkable, and many opportunities came with it, invitations here and there.
We locked ourselves in for our next record, "Motel Baja," here is the album cover.
We were at Valle of Guadalupe, just mixing the ideas we finally poured into the album Motel Baja.
But then we took stock of our story, "You know, we've done all these things already" What we did in our early years with industrial music, our first techno concerts in the nineties, the origins of Nortec and everything we did. We realized it was time to change
and step out of the box, so to speak.
We believe this last record is a collection that strings together our musical experience.
Everything we wanted to achieve with Nortec, is there. So let's leave this career path as a thesis you write in college,
The Bostich+Fussible Nortec is contained on that record.
RA, the Nortec sound in Motel Baja record, is what we always dreamed of doing.
Today we decided to leave Nortec behind, we are moving on, towards an uncertain, yet exciting future.
We don't know what might happen,
but we will soon meet again.
(Applause)
What if... when consulting your doctor about a normal abdominal pain, you are prescribed a fecal transplant because the problem is your intestinal microbiota?
Certainly fecal transplant sounds pretty scatological, but intestinal microbiota is all Greek to you.
Let's explain all this.
Let's start with where the tradition of fecal transplant began,
by the 4th century AD in China,
there is evidence in written documents of how they cured dying people's diarrhea
by feeding them a "yellow soup", just feces from a healthy person beaten up with water.
The patient would drink it and in the end the often deadly diarrhea was cured.
These are the first existing documents about fecal transplants.
But there are many other references throughout history, mainly associated with global disasters or great wars.
In World War I: imagine the trenches where soldiers died of dysentery, diarrhea, and there was no treatment whatsoever.
The only things at hand were animal feces, either camels' or horses', or feces of healthy people.
There's plenty of scientific evidence that during both world wars, people were treated with feces to recover their intestinal balance.
Actually, one of the most important discoveries in probiotics microbiology was in 1917 in Germany, when a whole battalion died of diarrhea except one soldier who kept his intestinal rhythm.
The rest were dying infected by numerous bacteria, undernourished and ill.
But from this soldier they isolated an Escherichia coli strain and named it Mutaflor.
Now we know its probiotic properties. It is marketed and even prescribed to low birthweight newborns.
There is plenty of fecal transplantation evidence in everyday life: Dogs smell other dogs feces on the street, eating only some particular feces.
We know this is correlated to the presence or absence of certain microorganisms.
When a dog lacks stable microbiota, it eats the feces with microorganisms that rebalance its microbiota.
As early as in 1953 an American researcher asserted in a scientific journal, documenting and showing evidence, that he cured 4 patients undergoing pseudomembranous colitis who were dying because of diarrhea, by administering them feces from a healthy person.
Now we know pseudomembranous colitis is caused by a bacteria called Clostridium difficile.
What happens is that when a person's intestinal bacteria disappears, Clostridium difficile begins to grow,
then it causes the secretion of toxins causing diarrhea and eventually leading to death.
Nowadays this infection by Clostridium difficile is one of the main diseases we are trying to cure in our hospitals.
Scientific evidence in recent years has shown that the best treatment for diarrhea by Clostridium difficile is fecal transplantation.
When I say microbiota I mean all microorganisms that live in our body with us.
Not only bacteria, the most popular, but also fungi, archaea, viruses and parasites.
This as a group is called microbiota.
Nevertheless many times the word microbiota refers specifically to the intestinal one,
because it's the one that is the largest, most complex and most studied.
Why?
Because we microbiologists have recently moved on from planting feces on plates where we could observe growth in 40 or 50 microorganisms, to massive sequencing with molecular tools.
By doing so we discovered the genetic fingerprint of 400 to 500 species living in our intestine.
We didn't know there were so many "people" living with us
(Laughter) and we discovered all this thanks to molecular tools.
Actually, now that we can quantify all these bacteria we know there are up to 10 times more of them than the cells of our body,
and taking them all together,
they amount to 2 kg of our weight.
Of course, every time we defecate we expel a lot of bacteria that gets mixed in our feces.
But the bacteria
duplicates itself in 20 minutes,
thus, the ecosystem recovers inmediately.
Suming up, intestinal microbiota is generally referred to as bacteria, but there are other microorganisms as well.
And why is this microbiota so important?
It is essential for us. What function do these "people"
inside us serve?
Why is it essential?
They help digest food, provide vitamins that we are unable to synthesize, and prevent other pathogens from infecting our body.
In recent years we verified that to be healthy, we need our microbiota to also be healthy.
Actually, many modern day diseases are associated with altered microbiota.
We're still figuring out if the disease is causing the change in the microbiota or simply the microbiota becomes used to intestinal pathology.
One of the diseases where we have more evidence that altered microbiota gets involved, is obesity.
Being obese not only requires a high-calorie diet.
There are also other factors and among them, metabolism of the microbiota.
In mouse models kept in sterility, free of bacteria, no matter how high-fat diet we feed them, they'll never get fat.
However, the same mouse when taken out of sterility, develops obesity.
Currently there are experiments to transplant microbiota from thin to newborn mice, reproducing the thin phenotype,
and in turn from obese to newborn mice, reproducing the obesity phenotype.
So we see there are a lot of implications of these microbiota.
There are implications even in daily little troubles we all have, such as intestinal rhythm disturbances between constipation and diarrhea, accumulation of gas, flatulence or any minor disorders, intestinal microbiota is involved.
Getting back to transplants, this is not a transplant itself, this is a transfer. Although for us the microbiota is a functional organ with metabolic capacity and taking part in our general health, what we actually do is just transfer feces from a healthy person to a sick person in order to restore their own microbiota.
In Clostridium difficile infection cases (the only ones with scientific evidence of the effectiveness of transplants) we select the donor among the sick person's family.
Why? Because in our immediate environment,
we share the same bacteria and the same clones, and what we are trying to do is to restore what we already had.
So we ask the patient to pick a donor,
relatives are studied to find the fittest, but there is always a trust mechanism.
Just imagine people's faces when they are told... (Laughter) to donate you their... poop.
Everyone has to trust each other.
However, my experience is that all the patients I had not just trust, they have blind faith.
Certainly they are very worn down, after months of up to 15 painful bowel movements a day.
Therefore, as soon as you say the words "fecal transplant", they reply: "When?".
They don't even ask.
At most, the donor asks: "Do I have to do anything special?"
"Do I have to eat anything special?"
We tell them that we are only interested in their bacteria and to fully trust that there is no risk at all with the transfer.
Our goal in the laboratory is to collect the freshest feces, (Laughter) we are very concerned about the freshness because of the anaerobic bacteria that die on contact with oxygen.
So, this is not easy to explain to donors, who are quite worried,
but we need a fresh sample. Just that.
(Laughter)
we beat the mix, liquefying it, and what we do is, after several filters, introduce it by colonoscopy into the patient with infection of Clostridium difficile,
depositing it in the blind gut.
Just that, the procedure
takes just half an hour, costs around 100 euros for colonoscope preparation, that's all.
So, two hours later patients relate not feeling pain anymore, and two days later they go back home.
Their only complaint at checkup is sometimes constipation.
So everything is fine with the transplant.
Simple, but there's a lot of buzz
around the world of feces transfer (or transplant) a lot of activity going on,
business minded, creating companies that certify universal donors of feces.
Specially in the United States, there are companies doing this.
You can find online where they sell frozen capsules,
or liquefied feces to introduce by enema.
Finally, they are creating public banks of feces,
one in Amsterdam and one in USA, where we can deposit our feces when we are healthy and then retrieve it at a time when our microbiota may be damaged.
For example, on chemotherapy or radiotherapy treatments, we could try to recover our microbiota.
Feces banks are going to be quite important in the future.
I also want to remark on the importance of this in cases of patients not suffering from Clostridium difficile infection,
inflammatory bowel disease and other types of disorders,
In such cases, donors don't have to be relatives because what we try is to completely change the microbiota.
Many patients with colitis or Crohn's disease want to be transplanted to "reset" their microbiota entirely.
These patients buy feces online and act in a local level as well, but we, the public health system, should attend such needs since they are so in demand now.
The experience worldwide so far is that transplantation is quite safe,
no major complications appeared,
just minor and short term issues.
There is the case of a young pregnant American woman with Clostridium difficile infection, she got transplanted and two years later she fattened a lot.
The fact is the donor was obese.
Such problems arise, or maybe solutions, because in the future we might consider to solve obesity with a transplant.
It is clear that the National Health Service has to regulate this very well, ethically,
because it's not an aesthetic but a health issue,
and in turn there's a very relevant legislative side we must not ignore.
In the long run we don't know yet if any alteration will happen that may be caused by the donor.
This legislative aspect is still unclear,
nobody takes responsability for this,
the National Transplant Organization says this is not a transplant,
health insurance companies wash their hands of this,
in the hospital we do it with consent from its administration,
but there is no regulation protecting us from any problems we may have.
So no question this must change some day.
I'll finish by telling you about the most recent research that makes us very expectant of its results.
A connection has been found between intestinal system and the central nervous system
that we call the "Brain-Intestinal Axis", explaining many things that happen to us every day: why we prefer some food; why in times of stress we suffer diarrhea; why when we're in love we feel "butterflies in the stomach".
This connection explains lots of things and we could influence it to improve our health.
Actually, we know that gut bacteria is able to eat tryptophan.
Tryptophan is an amino acid that we need to make serotonin.
If bacteria decides to eat more tryptophan than it should, we are left without it to produce serotonin.
Therefore, microbiota could also be linked to states of depression or anxiety.
And it could mean a big progress in treating those disorders in the future.
Thanks.
(Applause)
Two years ago my father was about to retire.
It was a long waited moment by the entire family because after 35 years of work at the same company we couldn't wait to finally have him at home.
However, months before his retirement he started to lose mobility,
could not walk properly and had to drag his legs, he couldn't even raise his arms.
It was very difficult for him.
He went to see his doctor, and the doctor sent him in for a bunch of tests. He was sent to rehabilitation and therapy, even a couple of surgeries,
but my father did not got better.
Today he is retired, and spends most of his time rehabilitating and trying to regain some mobility.
Retirement.
Such a beautiful word, it denotes joy, happiness, celebration.
It's reaching a goal, after a life of hard work.
That's what we are taught,
the circle of life:
We born, grow, reproduce, get old and die.
Though when I was in school, the only thing I could think of was reproduction, honestly. (Laughter)
Of course, you are young.
Young with plenty of time, you are healthy.
Then you grow up and start to work. You have money and health, but you don't have time.
And as a pensioner, oddly, you have time, the money you saved over so many years of work, but you are not healthy.
And I wonder, is this the only model?
Are we all going to reach this point when getting old?
Personally, I would rather have a retirement, with time, money and health.
I would like to have freedom, absolute freedom.
My father's real problem is physical disparity.
I don't like the word disability,
it sounds horribly discriminatory.
Disability.
Just as an example,
if I compare myself to Makau, the runner who set the world marathon record. He finished the Berlin marathon, in 2 hours 3 minutes.
On the other hand, I finished the 2013 Monterrey marathon, in 5 hours 40 minutes.
I am not sure why. I followed the Makau training program,
followed the same diet and trained the same hours.
Yet, it took me 3 more hours.
Obviously, between him and I, there is a clear physical disparity.
But sometimes the physical disparity is more noticeable, we have the case, for example, of Claire Lomas, a British woman who in 2007 fell off of her horse, and sadly, was paralyzed.
In 2012 she finished the London Marathon, in 17 days using an exoskeleton.
An exoskeleton -for those who don't know-, is an electromechanical suit, that gives the ability to have better mobility.
It's like what I'm wearing now.
This is an exoskeleton.
It allows improved mobility and physical abilities.
I wonder, will technology help our evolution?
Or help old adults with mobility problems?
These stories I am telling you
are surely familiar. My father is only one of 600 million old adults, living in the world.
Let me tell you that in 2050, God willing, we are going to be 2 billion.
And this is going to be thanks to exponential technologies.
The adoption of exponential technologies.
An exponential technology is one that follows, a growing behavior, where their performance improves, even doubles, in an 18 month period. Computer science is a good example.
I like to think of the Apollo navigation computer, that led man to the moon.
Let me tell you that after 45 years of progress, your cellphone computers, are 1300 times quicker, have 400 thousand times more memory, and even weigh 300 times less.
Exponential technology is fantastic.
What will progress bring
in the following 45 years?
I don't know, it's hard to say.
Human beings have a linear thinking.
If I tell you to take 30 steps, two meters each, then you know you will cover a 60 meter distance, the same as walking 2 basketball fields.
But what if I asked you to take 30 exponential steps, 2 meters each?
That would be a billion meters distance to cover, equivalent to circling the Earth 26 times.
It's something really hard to grasp.
Why? Because our human bodies,
have not undergone a hardware update, for the last 100 thousand years.
I think it is time to have one.
Will technology help us evolve?
I think it can helps us, indeed. Particularly helping old adults, to overcome their physical disparity.
They are the most affected population: By osteoporosis, chronic joint injuries like my father; or even strokes.
Currently, physical therapy is a way of improving your mobility,
however, it's painful, expensive and inaccessible.
For example, my father has to go 5 times a week to the medical center, for 2 hour sessions with a medical specialist.
This might seem absurd.
That is when we began to think, on what to do to improve his mobility.
My family and I are from Monterrey,
there, the steel industry is one of the most important.
My father actually worked in the steel industry for 35 years.
I was aware of his efforts, the same of all working people, with strong willpower.
I thought, how can his body be so fragile?
Will it not be possible to give older adults Ironman suits, so they can improve their mobility?
Well, Ironman is science fiction, right?
But exoskeletons and robots are real, they are existing technology, exponential technologies.
And holding power over this, was only an idea.
I had to turn to a group of collaborators, of exponential people, of exponential men and women, or as I like to call them: X-MEN, "Exponential Men".
We are friends who have worked a long time, in an Electronics and Robotics group in the Monterrey Tech.
We started to investigate, how exoskeletons work.
Our conclusion: They are expensive and not versatile, and their control is quite limited.
The reason of this:
An exoskeleton today costs between 60 to 150 thousand dollars.
And this is because it only comes as a complete suit.
You have to buy it complete.
Even if you only have a knee problem, it is the complete suit.
Also, today those exoskeletons, come in standard sizes.
Of course the links length can be adjusted, so that they fit a little better.
But, sadly, the problem is, that the rotary axes for the joints, do not match with different exoskeletons joint rotations,
and therefore cause more damage than benefit.
It is also absurd to think that today, the most advanced exoskeleton is controlled with a joystick or handle.
Or if you want to rehabilitate with an exoskeleton, you have to do it in a clinic, with even more specialized doctors.
I imagine that in the future exoskeletons are going to evolve.
Even become readjustable to adapt to the body, according to our needs.
They are also going to be bio-compatible,
most likely imbedded in the skin or even the bones.
They are also going to be interconnected, and make decisions beforehand, in order to protect our safety.
And probably our body will be connected to the internet.
But this is not the point, because for the first time, here at TEDxCuauhtémoc I will present, the advances with our exoskeletons. Video. We started by understanding each persons body. Then, using 3D scanning technology, we digitalized people,
to accurately locate all joints and their rotary axes are,
to design a tailor-made exoskeleton
that fits like a suit.
Click please. Thanks.
We also made them modular,
meaning they can be broken down into parts, and be adapted to the knee, elbow or shoulder, according to specific needs. Pieces can be assembled, to form a complete suit.
Also - click please, thanks - we are taking brain signals, and classifying them to understand movement intention,
to control the exoskeleton in an intuitive and natural way, as shown in the video.
We are also taking muscle signals, to understand the needed energy levels, and if there is pain, saturation, or if only support is needed.
Let me tell you that we just reached, a 96 % classification success, compared to the 86% of the systems on the market.
(Applause) We are also working with augmented reality.
Basically it is putting on a visor, where three-dimensional spaces are displayed, which the person follows with the exoskeleton.
All as part of rehabilitation routines.
We also have internet interconnection. Through an App, you doctor knows at all times, your current health state, and if you need an adjustment in your routine.
Therefore our exoskeletons are: Modular, Intelligent and interconnected
In fact I would like to use the stage, to first present you, the general operation of our exoskeletons, here in TEDxCuauhtémoc.
Ricardo and Eduardo are going to give us, a short demonstration.
They are basic routine movements like elbow movement.
Supported movement for biceps. Even walking assistance.
As you noticed -
(Applause) we still have along way to go. But we are going in the right direction, because every step that we take is exponential, to reach our main goal: The democratization of exoskeletons in the world.
We pictured a future where anybody, no matter social condition, illness or need, will have access to an exoskeleton.
This will help us to completely eliminate, physical disparity on the planet.
Everybody will have the same physical abilities, and "disability", that nasty word, will only exist in a history books.
We want to empower people, help them reach their dreams, and more broad ambitions, once any physical barrier is eliminated.
I would even want to see my father, your mother, your grandparents, or anyone who needs an exoskeleton, to continue doing, the activities they liked most when they were young.
Even trying new activities.
Soon, my dad will have grandchildren, and I want him to teach them everything he taught us.
This is how we imagine the world,
a world of absolute freedom. At least that is how I would use my exoskeleton.
For what purpose would you use yours? (Applause)
How many times you were told "no"?
That you couldn't study something you wanted.
That you couldn't devote yourself to art.
That you couldn't work.
A teacher or a boss -- it's full of those -- but also a stranger.
A friend, a boyfriend, a girlfriend, even your own parents.
Those "no" that hurt, that make you suffer.
And were you prepared to hear that "no", to accept it?
How do you face a "no"?
To answer to that "no", I thought of a challenge.
And for that need a volunteer of the 10,000 people here.
Please come.
Hi Ezequiel, how are you?
An applause for Ezequiel.
(Applause) You are going to stand here, Bautista is going to stand here and Augusto is going to stand exactly here next to this table.
