Good afternoon, I'm very happy and thankful to be here.
I'm going to share a song with you called "I say porridge," which is a very delicious recipe of an Argentine dessert.
I was thinking, I think that recipes hold people's memories and preserve and share them, just like they are in songs, I think that's why I like to sing.
The 'mazamorra' is a dessert made from corn, which is prepared in a similar fashion to the Mexican atole which is why I chose this song to sing here today.
Corn is a plant that from the start has been very connected to the birth and development of the ancient cultures on our continent. You might know that the gods made man out of corn, or that it was him, the son of god, they say,
the one who sank himself into the earth
and made corn sprout from his body. It's a sacred and very mysterious plant; something that sticks out to me is that it doesn't grow in the wild, rather it depends on the hands of men who thresh, sow, and harvest it.
So, it's a plant that connects men and women to the earth, and also connects them to the sky, because these people, watching their crops and observing the movement of the stars, created calendars and left us a legacy of immense knowledge that I hope to honor with this song today: "I say porridge." ♪ Porridge, you know, is the bread of the poor ♪ ♪ And the milk of the mothers with empty breasts ♪ ♪ I kiss the hands
of the Inca Viracocha ♪ ♪ Because he invented corn and taught farming ♪ ♪ It comes in a trough to keep the family together ♪ ♪ Greeted by the old, celebrated by the young ♪ ♪ Over there where the goats silently return ♪ ♪ And hunger is a cloud with wings of wheat ♪ ♪ Everything in it is beautiful: the ripe cob ♪ ♪ That is shelled on windy country nights ♪ ♪ The pestle and the maiden with braids over her shoulder ♪ ♪ To the grain she is adding blushes and sighs ♪ ♪ If you want it perfect, look for a clay pot ♪ ♪ And thicken it with slight, neat gestures ♪ ♪ From the rocking chair
cut from the branches of the fig tree ♪ ♪ That accompanies your nap with shades, little birds, and figs ♪ (Recites) You can add a touch of Jume to it, this plant found in desert valleys that lets its flames to feed it with its strength until it turns slightly amber.
When you eat it, you feel the people by your side along valleys and river bends.
When you eat it, you feel the Earth is your mother, more than the old sad lady who awaits your return from the field.
Its mother of your mother and her face is a stone carved by centuries.
♪ Many cities ignore its American taste ♪ ♪ And many had forgotten its Argentinian flavor ♪ ♪ But it will always remain what it was for the Inca ♪ ♪ A nursing mother for the Andean poor ♪ ♪ The night poets and singers will be shot ♪ ♪ For having corrupted and betrayed ♪ ♪ The music and the pollen, the birds and the fire ♪ ♪ These verses I recite might keep me safe ♪ ♪ These verses I recite might keep me safe ♪ ♪ These verses I recite might keep me safe ♪ (Applause)
I'm going to say something that will surely surprise many: Young people read.
Yes, we read.
I'm not talking about Twitter or Whatsapp.
No, young people read literature.
We love it, we're passionate about literature.
Besides, the worst thing is not that we read,
it's that we fear nothing.
Yes, we're rash.
We don't mind reading a tome of 800 pages, a book of poems of 50 pages, a 2-century-old book, a recent book, a romantic novel, realism, fiction, fantasy; we don't mind.
We read everything that's in front of us, and we love it, it's entertaining.
And I'm saying this can surprise many because of this widespread notion: "Today's young people... blah, blah, blah, blah".
(Laughs) I'm going to tell you a secret.
The phrase "Today's young people", existed long before today's young people.
We have always been criticised, and only a few times has our opinion been requested.
People talk and let those who are supposed to know us talk.
Eloquent people, scholars, they are easy to recognise: they have beards and wear glasses, and they talk about us, knowing so little, that I ask myself, "have they ever been young?"
They haven't, trust me.
Otherwise, I don't get it.
Because if they had been young, they would know that nobody knows more about feelings than a teenager Nobody gets more moved by a character's death, by the first kiss of the protagonists, by an unfair ending.
Nobody shares those things more fervently on Twitter: "Oh, God, how could this happen?"
That's the way it is, that's it.
I'll go even further and say that young people may not read more than adults do, but they certainly read more passionately.
And this is something that I, not considering myself an adult,
have been noticing over time. As you grow older, you become more passive.
So, enjoy this moment, when hormones are here, on the surface, when everything causes us to feel anger, rage, pain; but also sometimes calm, love, empathy.
Literature gives us all that. That's why we love reading.
I could talk about numbers. I could tell you that young adult literature represents 20% of the sales in the publishing industry.
Yes, I could say that. But it is not necessary.
Go to book fairs.
Go to the important ones. To the Madrid Book Fair, to Sant Jordi.
Which are the stands with more people in queue?
Which are the ones with more fans waiting for the author's signature?
Who wakes up at 6 a.m. to get Laura Gallego and Jordi Sierra i Fabra's signatures?
Young people. We read passionately and sometimes with fear.
There was a time in which I read with fear, of course, you were the library rat, some kind of "geek", which wasn't very good.
But we've taken the term "geek", and made it ours, and we celebrate it proudly By the way, May 25th, Geek Pride Day.
Yes, we love it!
We love when people call us that.
In short, young people love reading.
And that's curious, isn't it?
I was talking to some of you earlier, and I asked you what books you had read in high school, and you said stuff like "Don Quixote", "La Celestina".
Yes, fine, OK. My goodness.
No, really. My goodness.

I don't know if there are any teachers here, but I know there are students. and we know what we like, but also what we don't like
They want to teach us the force of the classics; instead, they teach us the classics by force. They make us hate literature.
We read those things and say "God, this is what reading's about?"
My God, I'll watch TV instead.
Yes, that is how it goes, and it's understandable.
A 15-year-old can't appreciate how unique the language is, how deep the characters from "La Celestina" are. No, sometimes philology studies are required to understand that work.
They follow the philosophy that there is a number of books you have to read in your lifetime.
I agree. You can't die without having read "Don Quixote" at least once.
But I don't agree with the methodology.
No, I don't agree.
I believe schools should encourage the passion for literature. creat readers, because if you are passionate about literature, you will eventually want to read "Don Quixote", that's obvious.
However, being so young, we were, and still are, forced
to read dreadful, never-ending books.
Yeah, yeah. Someone had to say it.
Góngora died four centuries ago, for Christ's sake.
(Laughs) What's happening to us?
Is there no newly-made literature?
Are there no classics in young adult literature?
Tell me, have you ever heard of "Huckleberry Finn", "Tom Sawyer"?
Mark Twain?
Have any of you read those?
Have you liked it?
Well, obviously No young person can read Mark Twain and not like it; and it's a classic.
I'm going to tell you a story, ok?
A 13-year-old girl falls in love with a 16-year-old boy, but their parents don't approve of that romance. It is a forbidden relationship, right?
And everything ends up very tragically.
Well, this is a typical plot in any current romantic novel. It's also the plot of Romeo and Juliet, by William Shakespeare, right?
They are so contemporary, they're feelings we see reflected in today's youth. Yet, can we feel as closely identified with Don Quixote?
He was round the bend, right?
Hello?
(Laughs) You have a lot of power.
I want you to go to school next Monday and tell your teachers what you want to read
and why. Stand by it!
(Applause) I want you to tell them... I want you to tell them: "Teacher,
shove Góngora in a drawer and don't get it out ever again".
(Laughs) But most of all, I don't want you to lose that passion for reading. Read what you like most.
That's what happened to me when I was 16; I read but because I made a living and read whatever I liked.
And I had trouble finding people who truly shared my passion for books, and I used a tool that everybody can use now, the Internet.
I created a literature channel in 2012. I was 16, really young.
And people told me: "This won't work, Sebastián. You're wasting your time. Today's youth is interested in videogames, fashion, television, tv series, movies; they don't read books anymore". Ha!
Today, my channel has 150.000 subscribers from all over the world. and counting. There are people from Spain, Mexico, Argentina, Chile, Venezuela. People from all those places, millions of views, letters that come from everywhere, from people that have shared my literary experiences, that have felt as angry as I have, that have liked my opinion about a certain book. The fact is that, whether some people like it or not, and no matter whom it surprises, young people read
Thank you very much.
(Applauses)
♪ I'm going to turn off the light, ♪ ♪ I'm going to turn on the stars. ♪
♪ I'm going to look for fireflies in the night ♪ ♪ the most beautiful ones. ♪
♪ I'm going to listen to the echo, ♪ ♪ the echo brought by the wind. ♪
♪ I'm going to sing without voice ♪ ♪ and to listen quietly to ♪ ♪ our silence. ♪
(Applause) I come from a big, loud and participative family.
We have our own Christmas choir, pretty off key by the way, where we all sing and nobody is left out.
In these family experiences, sharing is the most important thing, the encounter with the other through music.
Music generates this.
The opportunity of the encounter.
In many rural traditions, communities live everyday life accompanied by music.
People sing and dance in groups and have their own rituals along the different times of the day.
To meet, cook, travel and say goodbye.
This is very nice but what happens with us in the cities?
For us, collective music is a challenge.
Maybe, because we don't recognize our folklore or because we feel embarrassed when we sing in front of others.
Or maybe we are not used to that.
However, when we go to a football match something happens, right?
But for everyday life an experience like this is missing and we have to build it.
I do music and teach.
And along the way, I found out collective music is a great chance to make schools places for encounter, where everybody can unfold their voices and listen to others.
But, what do we need for this to happen?
Schools have to stop being the place where music is a fragmented activity in a classroom 40 minutes every week.
We need music to resonate in the entire school: in the sidewalk, halls, backyards and also accompany different everyday moments.
To make it a close, possible and easy language for all.
I started in schools as a teacher and musician.
And along this way, I discovered that music brings us closer, integrates the families and the school.
In order to be allies, and move forward together.
And it also teaches us that singing, dancing and playing make sense for kids but also for us, the grown ups.
I started working in schools, but over time I organized my own rotary playgroups in houses with small kids.
I went with a bag full of instruments, with the guitar, puppets, bowls, balls, foam and a towel for cleaning the foam.
Getting on the bus was a great feat.
When we sat in a circle, we shared the songs with the dads and moms and grandparents that before going to work stayed to play for a little.
I asked them to sing their favorite songs for us.
We listened to them and applauded.
Years later we didn't get in any living room, the reason why I opened Risas de la Tierra in 2005, a kid's school and cultural center.
The big challenge was taking all the knowledge from the houses to a formal institution.
We, adults and kids, create our own rituals to greet and welcome to ourselves.
Now I invite you all to join me with a beat with earth and wind
without hitting.
What would it be like? Good!!
And from Senegal, a welcome song comes and says, all together with an echo, ♪ Ye ye ye ye ye solmaye ♪ Audience: ♪ Ye ye ye ye ye solmaye ♪ ♪ Ye ye ye ye ye solmaye ♪ Audience: ♪ Ye ye ye ye ye solmaye ♪ ♪ Ye ye ye ye ye solmaye ♪ Audience: ♪ Ye ye ye ye ye solmaye ♪ Nice!
♪ Ye ye ye ye ye solmaye ♪ Audience: ♪ Ye ye ye ye ye solmaye ♪ One, two, three ... and it goes.
Good!
So nice having a singing audience!
(Applause) We take some minutes to say hello and it feels good, right?
And then, another big challenge in the school: Make a pause and capture the magic of silence.
Shh...
Listening makes way and the senses awaken.
When music starts being part of everyday life, when it comes and goes from school to the homes, and from the homes to school, the voice and knowledge of the community come to light.
Once, we went with the 4 year-old kids around the school.
We took a small recorder to capture all the sounds, from the bell to the bathrooms' faucet.
And also the flush of the toilet, which made the kids laugh.
Another thing we did was to record all the adults when we asked what they listened to and sang when they were kids.
The great surprise of the day was Willy.
Willy was the custodian from Paraguay.
And as a boy, he played the harp in a Paraguayan orchestra, but also, and most importantly, he taught us to make an authentic Sapucay.
Do you know what it is? (Sapucay)
Have you seen that deep cry?
Picture that moment for the kids.
Willy turned into something like Tarzan.
And from that day on, the welcome code was a Sapucay.
The thing is: how do we unfold this if we don't feel like musicians?
Or if we are insecure, or think we are out of tune.
There are many ways to reconnect with our inner music.
A possible way is going towards childhood, to go back to the musical sources.
What did we listen to as kids?
What did my granny from Spain sing to me, or my grandad from Santiago del Estero?
And if we don't remember, what do I like now?
What do I play when I get in the car?
Let me tell you what happened once in the school gatherings on Fridays, when we go out and share some of our music with the community and celebrate the end of the week.
I was carrying chairs, benches, microphone and instruments and in the middle of the central hall, there was a man I've never seen before.
He was an elderly, stern looking man who was staring at everything; he wore a scarf, a stole was hanging from his arm and he was still, standing in the middle of the flow.
Seeing him standing there I gently asked:
I beg your pardon, sir, who are you?
He replied: "I'm Vito's grandfather!"
With this tone of voice.
So, as he had a particular accent, I asked again:
"Welcome Sir, where are you from?"
He answered: I'm from Corrientes.
And as we do music and folklore, in an attempt to come closer and also to please him, I kept asking him, commenting: Great! Because we can sing chamamés.
May we sing something littoral?
He cut me off and said: "No, not at all."
Because I'm Greek.
These things that are so common in Argentina, that we come from so many places.
So, then, I smiled triumphantly.
Do you know why?
Because I love world music.
And we have a library of discs, with records from all over the world.
Then I smiled and thought: "Oh, so you're Greek, you'll see."
And ran to fetch Zorba the Greek. Do you know that dance? (Humming)
That one, right?
So, some minutes later, in the greeting circle, after singing with the families I stopped and solemnly said: I want everyone to know that today Vito's grandad is with us.
And do you know what he did?
He stood up and bowed!

He was really grandiloquent.
And people applauded.
Then I said: "We have a Greek surprise for this grandad from Corrientes".
We played Zorba and then, with this gesture, he cried with a lot of attitude: Uopa! as all Greeks do, he stood in the middle and started to dance. Because he was a dancer!
That's the reason for his grandiloquence
and attitude. And he taught us all at school how to dance Zorba the Greek.
He guided us into making three concentric circles and instead of being isolated, music joined us all instantly.
(Applause) Another possible way is to put music into words. To play with the voice and nonsense, (Voice sounds) as kids do.
Because sound is a child's game.
My granny tangled and untangled
little words. She made them fly and taught me this copla that his father had taught her, my great grandad Manuel.
And it goes like this:
Once upon a time there was a C... Audience: Cat. who after another one R... Audience: Ran. We turn up the volume of the audience.
Once upon a time there was a C... Audience: Cat. Now... who after another one R... Audience: Ran. and as he didn't Rea... Audience: ...ch. he sat and waited for a Wh... Audience: ...ile. And we do this, look: Tick-tock, tick, tock.
As old clocks did, remember?
Tick-tock.
And it goes: When, Zas!
A loud Zas!
With our feet to the ground to wake up this space.
When Zas! There was another C...
Audience: Cat. who after another one R... Audience: Ran. and as he didn't Rea... Audience: ...ch. he waited for a Whi... Audience: ...le. Tick-tock, tick-tock
♪ Once upon a time there was a C... Audience: Cat. ♪ ♪ who after another one R… Audience: Ran. ♪ ♪ and as he didn't Rea… Audience: ...ch ♪ ♪ he sat and waited for a Wh… Audience: ...ile ♪ Meow, meow, meow.
Great!
(Applause) Thanks.
All this is musical language.
It is much more than playing an instrument or sing.
And it is possible for everyone.
What if we musicalized school?
Also teachers have to dig in their musical sources. A honest path of acknowledging who we are.
And also finding our resources.
Because we need a repertoire reflecting our life.
Integrating those sad, intimate, or grief moments, that childhood also has.
Songs are alive and are not mechanical.
We need calm songs, in minor keys.
Such as that of the dove who "flew away and I started to cry". When I sang it, a 3-year-old girl paused and said: "My dad cried when my grandad died." And the boy next to her said: "I don't use the pacifier anymore." Those things that happen in life, right?
We also need songs to dance, to mosh, to have fun and create our own presentation rituals.
With a treasure chest from which flutes, little birds, emerge awakening the magic of looking, of playing and singing with no purpose.
Music is a wonderful personal and collective channel, leading us to wake our inner world up and share it with the others.
And teaches us that listening makes sense.
I invite you all to make this rhythm in our chest, awakening vibration of sound.
Shhh...
This is where kids say: "This is me, here I am".
