♪ From the pretty sun untangles ♪ ♪ my own life. ♪
Eeeee, Ha-Ha, Eee!
(Applause) Thanks a lot.
Good afternoon. My daily profession involves technology, the way it applies to people's day-to-day lives, and how people perceive this new technology.
And wearables are now on the market.
Everyone knows or has them. Many of you will have on your wrists devices that measure what you do and communicate with you. And they're already here.
They've gone through an adaptive phase that required people like me, designers, to tweak them a bit, but they now work.
The next step is the embeddable, which is when a device is inserted inside your body.
But before that, I'll talk about an intermediate step, which is this band known as 'emotiv.'
It's a head band that picks up thoughts and is capable of interpreting them to generate interactions with other electronic devices.
Why an intermediate step?
Because, for the user, the one wearing the band, that device disappears.
You, as a non-quantum observer (Laughter) can see this band and see something is there enabling that person to interact with his surrounding.
When you put it on yourself, it becomes an inherent part of you, and you no longer see it.
That has its dangers and possible challenges which is what I want to explore with you today.
It's something that worries me in daily life, but may seem a nonsense to you, but, when you'll see the next embeddable, you'll think it, too.
I'll also provide examples of how to solve these possible problems.
I repeat: what is an embeddable?
A device that you insert inside a person's body to get data, communicate things, or increase our interactive capacity with the environment.
You may say: "This has been around for years, like artificial hearts, for example."
Yes it was, but no, it's not the case.
The artificial heart, once introduced inside a person's body, stops communicating.
In fact, not only that, it needs external interaction, like a battery and a charger.
But it doesn't relay anything. It keeps you alive, which is no small feat but that's it.
(Laughter) And that's the difference.
The embeddable has to communicate, it has to say something.
So we face interaction problems. What problems are those?
There are three of them.
Well, there's many more, but these are the basic ones.
The energy source, our own senses, and invisibility, of which I spoke a little bit.
The energy source is very important.
In fact, many of us have mobile devices that ask us all day long to charge them.
Imagine something inside your body asking you to charge it all day long.
Artificial hearts, yes, because you have the incentive that, if you don't charge it, you die, but not the mobile phone.
So imagine what it would be like charging something every day you've inserted inside yourself to see how you run or whatever.
There are now ways to avoid that problem.
This is a probe that goes into the blood stream.
There are sensors on its head and a little stick in the end made of piezoelectric material that uses blood pressure to capture electricity, or convert it to electricity, so that the muscle cells scanned by the probe make the stick move like a sperm cell so that it moves in the blood stream, allowing the probe to gather data and to communicate with the exterior.
This is an example of how we can solve the lack of energy sources.
What happens?
Our ability to harvest energy is very limited and we can't make very complex components, meaning, we wouldn't be able to charge an iPhone like this.
This is a well-known project. It's a contact lens that Google is making.
You basically, put it on, and it measures the changes in your eye, changes in pressure to measure your blood glucose.
It's for diabetics; instead of having to check daily using blood drops, you wear this contact lens and it can tell you if you have low blood sugar, high blood sugar, etc. How does this contact lens reduce the energy source problem?
It has an antenna like an RFID, where the same energy that radiates from the mobile phone to provide the lens data, makes the sensor work. That's to say, it captures magnetic waves to self-generate energy.
It isn't an huge energy source either in order to solve the problem that a mobile phone can have embedded within us, but it's fine, it's a first step.
Second problem: ourselves, our own senses.
I'll give you a simple example, a pint of black beer.
I love it, I have a great time drinking it, but imagine that you don't like it. In fact, we drink it together and you say, "How bitter!" and I say, "Well, not at all, it tastes rather sweet to me."
It's the same object, an object we can both look at and argue about, but it has two different tastes once we internalize the sensation.
Well, if we internalize a device, like an embeddable, we create a cognitive dissonance problem.
What you feel and what I feel can be totally different.
An example that I love is from the world of body modification, tattooing, scarring, and such. There's a new trend for having magnets implanted inside finger tips. They insert them to have
superpowers, they say.
(Laughter) I don't know.
Well, you can take a clip off a table with a single finger if you want.
(Laughter) But they also say that it gives them a sensation, that it allows them to... What's the word?
...perceive the magnetic fields that surround us.
I have to believe it. Nobody is telling me that it's true or not because, until I implant a magnet inside my finger, I won't be able to agree with you, just like I can't agree with you on the taste of black beer.
We all have a cell phone in our pockets and many of us suffer of something well-known which is phantom cell phone vibrations.
"Oh, it vibrated, someone's calling me!"
No, it didn't. (Laughter)
No, they're not calling you.
Well, imagine if I implanted an embeddable inside you with a vibrator that could communicate anything to you.
Can you imagine having a phantom vibration at full body level? (Laughter)
Not only that but the opposite can also happen.
Our brain can omit that communication.
When you have a chronic injury, in the long run, your brain tends to make the noise of that injury diminish, disappear, and go away.
So imagine if I placed a device to relay something to you and it stopped working because your own brain got it out of the way.
What's its value? None.
And it's invisibility is a bit like
the head band I mentioned before.
Its invisibility is a big problem because, as soon as we stop looking at it, it's like when you take a photo album, put it in a box, in a closet, and forget the photo album until you move out one day and see it.
Well, the same happens in our body.
As soon as we stop looking at things, we forget they exist.
Tattoos seem to be a great example.
Not tattoos you can see, in the back, for example.
Unless someone reminds you that you have a tattoo, you tend to forget you have it, or you look in the mirror.
Because it's our mind that makes us do this. Plus, tattoos communicate things that are more enriching, just like an embeddable.
This one in particular is a Russian mafia tattoo that communicates the rejection of the Soviet system.
Meaning, we've embedded information in a tattoo in the same way that we can embed information in an embeddable.
This is a tattoo with a similar system to the Google contact lens.
First, it basically perceives vocal chord vibrations and communicates them to a device.
When you vocalize without producing any sound your vocal chords vibrate.
If you get this tattoo here it can decode these vibrations and you can speak, say, without really producing sound.
There have been for many years, especially in military courses, microphones placed here, that, without talking, can communicate with other soldiers, or speaking very low.
Well, this is the next level.
But, of course, this causes a series of problems of not only active but also passive invisibility.
Imagine that you're sleeping, they place it, cover it, you don't know it's there and someone can hear your thoughts.
Not thoughts really, but vibrations because thinking and talking are two different things.
But it causes a problem.
An example of this already happening is that many of us that like to play with consoles, now have them with devices that respond to our voices, to voice commands.
But, what happens?
Well, since we don't use them or they're always there, they become invisible, we take them for granted.
I'll give you an example. It's a funny video that has a lot to say about invisibility.
Excuse the language, it's an online game, multi-player, lots of shootings, and a player decides to take a name that is the same as the console's shut down command.
(Laughter) A player kills him and says, "darned so-and-so" and the console turns off.
So it does a double play, besides killing you, it also turns the console off, takes you out of the session, and makes you angrier still. (Laughter)
But there's a point of interaction in voice command that has become invisible, has disappeared. You forget about it because you're focused on something else.
Imagine I had that tattoo in this talk. They snuck in a microphone. I have the tattoo and, at a certain point, someone asks me a question, and I think, "What a stupid question," wearing the microphone and it's heard in the whole room.
It would be a problem, right?
The invisibility these devices have is another challenge we have to solve with respect to the interaction of these new systems.
"You're the interface,"
and something as simple as this phrase is a lot more complex.
We spend all our life learning how to interact with our environment
from when we are little: with language, with gestures...
If we insert one of these embeddables, we have to learn how to use it.
It's something we're doing all the time, so it won't be a new problem.
Well, it's one of my concerns, and maybe now, you are more interested.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
I was playing soccer with some friends, when my wife called to say her water had broken.
We were expecting our fifth child, but this one was different because he didn't weigh much.
My wife was only six months pregnant, but the baby was ready to come out.
So we rushed to the hospital, our heads spinning with thoughts of what could happen. And after two intense days, Juan Bautista was born. He weighed barely two pounds, just a tiny little thing.
At that moment, as you can imagine, we were bundles of joy and fear.
He was premature, in an incubator, surrounded by nurses and doctors. Four members of my family were outside asking for information and nobody could guarantee anything, because the baby was so small.
At that moment you just don't know what's going to happen.
What we did know was that we would not be alone and that the baby would want for nothing; we would take care of him in every detail.
And that's how it was. He's almost four now, and bangs up the walls kicking a ball around the house.
I imagine we all know what a newborn needs. But hardly anyone knows what someone who is at the other end of life needs -
what a dying person needs.
That's what I'm here to talk to you about today.
I'm privileged to have worked as a doctor for the past 15 years, accompanying terminally ill people through to the end of their lives.
It's called palliative care; the work of a team to take care of people the way any of us would want and would deserve to be taken care of at that particular time.
And when I talk about terminal illnesses, don't just picture elderly people, because, sadly, this affects people in every stage of life: workers, newly married people, teenagers, athletic people, gluten-free people, smokers and nonsmokers ...
In some way or another this touches us all, and I'm sure we all know someone who's fighting this kind of disease.
I can think of many people I am helping at the moment, or perhaps people that you know, people that may be in this room or watching this on a screen.
To honor each and every one of them, today I want and feel I must tell you that a lot of them are probably suffering unjustly.
Because in modern societies, only one person in ten receives the appropriate care in these situations.
That is really serious.
It means there are people in pain out there.
But not "I sprained my ankle" or "I've got a headache" pain - not a fleeting pain.
It's the kind of pain that doesn't let you sleep or think.
It means there are people out there who are really uncertain about the course of their treatment and of their disease. They notice they're losing weight, that they can't go to work anymore and nobody tells them what's going on.
And sometimes uncertainty can bring you down more than any symptom.
People obviously feel things - anxiety, distress, fear, doubts - and they need to be heard. But they're mostly ignored,
because listening takes time.
There are people bouncing around the system - and I hope this never happens to you - going from one emergency room to another, asking for help, feeling as if they were a nuisance, and then going back home, because nobody wants to admit them. It's because these are patients who cost the system money,
and so they're left out of it.
I believe this happens because, among other reasons, we have a hard time talking about death.
It's a topic that scares us.
We're paralyzed by the unknown, what it will be like to no longer exist.
We watch films about intensive care units, wires and tubes everywhere ...
We are frightened by physical decline, by the idea of fecal and urinal
incontinence devices and of depending on others. A woman told me the other day: "Matías, I already know what I have, but in a moment my body suddenly felt old, and I'm only 52 years old."
The terrible consequence of all this is that today, there are people dying anonymously, without saying goodbye, without settling their affairs and sometimes without knowing what's happening.
It's a difficult subject; not a good topic for socializing, nor is it a "sexy" one, as my friend, Juan, would say.
And that's an important part of the problem.
The first time I had dinner at my in-laws' house - I'll never forget this, it was the night we met. And right in the middle of dinner, my father-in-law says: "Well, you work in palliative care, I'm sure you don't get many presents for Doctor's Day."
Do you know who has the toughest time talking about death?
Doctors.
We are somewhat accomplices in keeping false hope alive.
We've been given the task of healing people, so we find it hard to recognize when we cannot do it and instead we prescribe all sorts of unnecessary treatments, water answers down - "We don't need to talk about this," but time keeps moving on. And this particular moment in time, for these people, is precious.
It is unique.
Just like when you got your degree, you got married or your child was born; moments in which you meet people, key events. Well, this is the moment in which your life is ending -
the last moment there is - and you should give it the place it deserves.
It's like the last summer afternoon at the beach.
The sea is freezing cold, a violent wind carries your beach umbrella away, but you say, "I'm going to swim anyway, who knows when I'll be back!"
Well, it's a bit like that. It's a time to enjoy yourself, to look ahead.
Everybody should have the chance to really live the end of their own life,
to be responsible for their own decisions, instead of having other people decide where or with whom they should be;
to spend time with their loved ones, without pain or symptoms;
to settle their affairs
and write their will;
to put their soul at peace; to write the ending to their own biography.
That might sound a bit bizarre, but it's a right we all have: to live in dignity until the very end, no matter your disease.
(Applause) The right to be taken care of just like a newborn.
And it's good to know this, because we need to demand it.
Palliative care has been around for a long time.
We have excellent teams doing this work in the major hospitals in big cities, covered by insurance plans, but that remain largely unknown.
Their numbers are growing.
And those least aware of these services and are therefore the ones who suffer most, are the people most in need.
That's why, back in 2009, a group of friends and I decided to build something different, something to reach more people and in a better way. We founded the Buen Samaritano Hospice, a facility aimed at assisting poor people living with terminal illnesses. Together with a team of volunteers, doctors, nurses and psychologists, we become like family to those who are on the outskirts of life and welcome them not so much as patients but as guests, who will find their name at the headboard of their bed: Leo, Liliana, Ángel, José.
They arrive out of nowhere, exhausted, distrustful, in pain, but I can assure you that here, that movie takes a turn.
Because we ask them who they are; we relieve them of those unexplained pains that they've been experiencing for months. We explain what's going on with them, and they realize there are people who will help, who'll take care of them, who are fully committed and will always be there for them.
And what happens then?
They start eating and taking naps once again, they rediscover that God is with them, now more than ever. They go back to praying and recover hope. They go from feeling like a diagnosis to feeling like a person again.
(Applause) And we realized that you don't need a huge budget, fancy technology or large facilities for this -
you need people who are eager to do it.
So, we got down to business and began offering our services in cities, in public hospitals. We provide not only care and medications, but we also offer classes, so that everyone knows what this is all about.
And what's happening is really good.
Thousands are being accompanied through this delicate stage of life.
Hundreds of volunteers are giving their time to take part in it.
Entire teams of doctors, nurses, psychologists and social workers are getting on board and moving things forward at their facilities.
And the best thing is that we all realized nobody has to suffer,
and that the famous phrase "There's nothing more we can do" is one of the biggest lies in medicine.
When you provide care, assistance and professional, humanized relief from pain, people's lives - even the lives of those about to die - change.
We see families reuniting at this point, talking naturally about what's going on, preparing for the end. We see children spending entire afternoons with their parents, sharing with them and enjoying them. We see mothers doing their last motherly duties for their children and providing final pieces of advice.
And when they thank you, that is incredible.
The other day, at the hospice, a woman told me: "Matías, I would've never imagined that I'd be able to call my father 'Dad' again."
As sometimes happens in life, they had grown apart 15 years earlier, and they were meeting again during that special week,
a week as special to them and to the grandchildren finally meeting their grandfather as it was to those of us who were part of that moment.
The truth is, we haven't seen miraculous comebacks, but we have witnessed profound personal and family healing during this singular stage of life. And that is very powerful.
Each passing year, I grow more convinced that this isn't just the job of doctors, hospitals or nurses.
If each and every one of us dares to talk about these issues, if we ask for the right kind of help when we need it, it will give many people the opportunity to live better, for many families to live better and for us to have a better society.
People always ask me: "You have five children, your home is surely like a kindergarten, and yet you have this unusual job. What is a good day at work for you? How do you feel at the end of the day?"
What I feel is a bit like what happens to me each time I approach a patient's bed in those last days of life.
For example, Viviana, a young woman of 44, whom I have known for several months and who is now asleep, her breathing is shallow, (Imitates shallow breathing) with some pauses, but comfortably, with her husband at her side, as always.
I greet him with my eyes.
We both know there is not much time left.
At this point, she's only getting saline with a bit of morphine, so she's not in pain or gasping for air.
There's a photo of her three little boys, the apples of her eye.
I get a little bit closer, take her hand, and at that precise moment I recall everything we shared, what we talked about, her smile, her look, her desires, her character.
What a great woman.
It moves me to think of her.
I check her pulse: barely perceptible. Some long minutes go by and she has no pulse.
She stopped breathing.
She was here a second ago, and now she's gone.
It's a mystifying second in which I feel that Heaven and Earth intersect, while she moves on, serenely and in peace.
(Applause)
Good evening, everyone.
I will begin my presentation with a question: [Am I weird?]
Do you think you are weird?
(Audience talking) Well, I see a difference of opinion.
Usually, we associate the word "weird" with something negative.
Before, Maria was referring to the importance of the way we use words.
We tend to associate the word "weird" with adjectives like "freaky", "lonely", "maladjusted" and sometimes something even worse.
But I think this happens because we don't know what being weird means.
Where do we go to learn the meaning of a word?
Audience: To the dictionary.
Susana Lago: Exactly.
To the Royal Spanish Academy.
According to RAE being weird means "behaving in an unusual way",
"being extraordinary or unusual",
"being exceptional or one of a kind",
"being excellent, outstanding or remarkable",
"having a special mind and personality prone to stand out from the rest".
Well, based on what RAE says, being weird doesn't seem so bad.
But I would add one more thing: being weird is having quirks.
What are quirks?
From my point of view, quirks are those skills that make us unique.
This afternoon, we have talked about looking for what makes us different, special.
After all, our quirks are some kind of superpower that sometimes we hide because we are afraid of being the kind of weirdoes we don't like, the so called freaks.
When you look at these definitions, what kind of people or characters come to mind?
Maybe people like these.
Einstein, Picasso, Mother Teresa... Well, this is a little unbalanced. Not enough girls, huh? (Audience talking)
OK. Maybe you think that... (Audience talking)
Hello? Calling all weirdoes!
Maybe you think that all weirdoes are extraordinary people, that do super cool things.
No. This afternoon, we've seen so many people that proved to be cool weirdoes who are not as famous as the ones we see here.
There is something they all have in common. They were all able to identify, accept and develop their talents, their quirks.
They have turned them into superpowers.
Have you ever thought about your quirks?
Do you know what makes you important?
(Audience talking) When I say quirks, I'm not talking about using your mouth to bite your toenails or making fart noises using your armpit. OK?
Those are unusual abilities... (Audience talking) Those are unusual abilities but they have no value in themselves.
When I say quirks... (Audience talking) I mean things that have some sort of value. Things that mean something
to the people you want to connect with.
Again, have you ever thought about what makes you important or different?
No. And most likely, you have not thought about it because no one ever asked you or you never wondered that yourself.
Many people never get to answer that question.
Because they never ask that question and because sometimes, it's easier to stay in our comfort zone, not stand out and go unnoticed.
Like uncle Ben said to Spiderman when he realized he had superpowers, "With great power, comes great responsibility."
It's similar to what we saw in the video about superheroes if you have gifts, you need to know how to use them.
And sometimes, it's not easy to assume that responsibility and we choose to stay right there.
But the challenge I present to you this evening is not that you become superheroes,
but that you learn to identify your quirks and to value them. That's what this is all about.
Valuing them means having a way to identify them.
There are no magic formulas. A spider won't bite you and you won't get superpowers overnight.
But I can give you five tips to identify your quirks and to value them. That's what this is all about. OK?
I'll give you five tips and the first one is self-discovery.
If I don't know who I am or what I like, it gets more difficult to develop any talent because I don't know myself and it's more likely to fail.
Hard work.
I provide this example because there are thousands of stones.
Without hard work, we cannot see that a stone can hide something so amazing. Right?
Discipline.
Javi Villa explained this very well on the TEDxYouth last year. He had to give up going out with friends because he chose to focus on sports.
That is his quirk, his talent. He was so sure about it that he gladly gave up going out at night like the rest of his friends.
Action.
If you have a talent, a quirk, you have to use it. Otherwise, it becomes useless.
You have to use your superpowers to change what you want around you.
Because action is the driving force for change.
Last but not least: Communication.
What is not said does not exist, guys.
It doesn't matter if you are the best of the best at home, because it stays there.
You need to share it
with the people you want to help.
Use those superpowers or quirks and show them what you can do. We are made of stories. We need to learn to share ours, value and communicate what makes us valuable and special.
So I'm asking you - don't conform to what others say about you.
Please, learn to find your own path, identify what is yours.
Right now you have all the tools you need at your fingertips in order to become the best version of yourself.
Being weird is not hard. We are all weird.
What is hard is to identify our quirks, discover them in time and be able to value them.
So do not waste your superpowers because that's what makes you big.
Thank you very much!
(Applause)
Hey, what's up?
I wasn't always interested in gender studies and feminism nor was I a director of cultural affairs, nor a movie producer.
Ever since I was very young, I loved to watch and share movies in the living room with my family.
And when I discovered Stanley Kubrick's "Space Odyssey," when I first saw Fellini's movies, or the stories of Agnes Varda that my parents would watch, that had a profound impact on me; watching cinema like that inspired me and I've always liked it, all my life; since I was young, and I'm talking eight years old.
One day I grew up, and at 16, with my first job, which was at a cartography company, when I began to receive my first paychecks, I invested all that dough in a really nice computer so I could do my homework, so I could download music, log in to Messenger, which was still around back then.
And something that really excited me was being able to get ahold of those movies which had made such an impression on me as a child, those series I watched on Channel 4, those albums that were popular.
And, well, I would spend hours downloading things; I used Napster and all that.
One day, on the way to work, I got in my head to share all these experiences with my coworkers, and I got in my head to carry out a little film club on the weekends in the company's conference room.
I went to my boss, all excited, and I said to him, I said, "Hey, I want to start a film club." He told me, "You're crazy. Get to work, stop wasting time."
My job in that company was to scan 40 cm x 40 cm aerial photographs and the time it took to scan them was between 10 and 15 minutes, so I had a lot of free time between photos to do whatever I wanted; so it was the perfect job for anyone who was 16 and wanted to study.
Soon enough, the company asked us to work overtime on the weekends, and to work night shifts, and, well, almost all the staff in the company loved to come in when I was coming in to work, especially when it was my turn to work nights,
or Sundays, exactly because our supervisors didn't work those hours, and I'd bring in my movies, although they'd said no to the film club, and we'd watch them there; every 10, 15 minutes we'd pause it, and we'd work and eat - they paid for our meals and we earned 3 times more - so it was an awesome job; it was like heaven for me.
One day, the company was no longer so productive and so well-off and we stopped coming in to work overtime.
However, the coworkers I'd hung out with all those times in the office were ticked off, they were saying, "Hey, what happened to the Stanley Kubrick series we were watching?" No more. We couldn't do it anymore.
And then I was coming in after the weekends, all excited, I'd say, "Hey, I just saw this movie," or, "I bought that," they'd get ticked and tell me, "Hey, loan it to me."
And I'd say to them, "Sure, I'll loan it to you." And then all of a sudden four friends already owed me 15 movies; so I decided, better burn them a copy; and, well, that was my way of not losing the movies, because they wouldn't return them to me, they were a bunch of freeloaders. And so,
this experience forced me -because I had so many movies - at 17 I had maybe 800 movies.
And I'm telling you, it was still expensive, that Blu Ray - well, there wasn't any Blu Ray yet.
But I had movies in .avi, bootlegs, movies I'd paid for... And so, this experience forced me to make a catalog, a very amateur catalog in Word with a little picture, and I'd write a synopsis; I had this catalog, and I'd tell my friends: "There it is. Whichever one you want, I'll loan it to you. Bring me your CDs, I'll burn it, and there you go."
Well.
This was a way that was, when I look back now, one of the first ways I had of becoming a professional, of becoming an expert, unintentionally, in film, out of sheer enjoyment, and for the sheer fun of it.
Film opened a new world to me, and it opened my mind. It caused me to ask questions, and it caused me to feel new emotions.
Before long, at 17, 18 years old, I had to enlist or go on to college; and I had to make that tough decision about what to major in, and truth is, I didn't feel comfortable as a geographer, or an engineer, or a mathematician, and, well, I decided to study philosophy. (Laughter)
So I'm studying philosophy, and there in my major I find a true social calling.
I had the good fortune of having very good male and female professors.
I think that, especially, I had very good female professors there.
I found a political and aesthetic calling, and throughout, all my journey in college was about nourishing my mind, nourishing it with film, with books, and, well, now I'm getting close to finishing my major, and I begin to worry about where I'm going to find a job, if my dad was right all along...
And so, without worrying too much, and without ripping all my hair out, I start doing my social service in the Museum of the City of Mexico in the historic center of the city.
And I have the good sense and the fortune of arriving there while it was under the guidance of two of my mentors, two very ballsy women, really, who were forging a path, launching projects that were incredible, with big budgets and incredible quality.
I show up on my first day, imagine, I show up like this with my bookbag, they welcome me, and they say: "Your first task is to write a budget."
And I said, "Look, wait, I studied philosophy, not accounting."
"No, no, no, in 15 days I'm going to Centro Pompidou, to handle a piece by Matisse and Picasso and I need the budget."
I said, "Fine, help me then," and they started helping me.
Realizing how much is involved in cultural affairs work was, truth be told, a shock.
For me, discovering that you need so much money, that you need insurance for the artworks, that if you're going to bring 50 pieces, Pepito has two, Juanito has another two, some company has another one... It's just, wow. Seriously.
Shortly after arriving, I realized that I had stumbled into an incredible place and that exactly everything I had learned in my philosophy major and in my previous work experience was very useful to me.
I realized that I had the tools to make a living.
I had articulated an idea of my own and brought people together to follow it.
I had achieved this even in adverse circumstances.
And so I pressed on, and, parallel to my forays into this world of cultural affairs, I was doing my thesis, my college thesis.
And this very thesis was about film and a French philosopher named Gilles Deleuze, whose work is all about dismantling power structures, criticizing the violence of society, and seeing in a different way what we, from society's perspective, consider abnormal or senseless.
And it was that coming and going between the museum, between the job with my mentors and my studies, that made it click for me, and that made me discover feminism and queer theory.
I realized that feminism isn't all about hating men, or insulting them, and it's not the reverse of machismo either.
I simply realized that feminism is a revolution, and I realized that maybe, the most important avant-garde in art is feminism itself, the history of feminism.
So you see, all at once, putting together everything I had been working on made it click; and I got the idea to do an international film festival with gender perspective.
In this festival I brought together philosophy, cartography, film, and feminism.
MIC Género, as a content platform, invites you to see the world from this perspective,
from the perspective of gender and feminism. And the way it works is by shedding light on the structures of power and domination that oppress us, the ones we submit to. But each year that we've done the festival, we've had new challenges and we've had new goals.
Each year, for example, we propose a curation and a type of programming, but this kind of curation requires us to fully understand feminist discourse and everything revolutionary about it. The revolutionary thing about it is, plain and simple, what I call feminism, and it's a bid for equality; and this equality will translate to liberty.
Therefore, MIC Género believes that human rights, gender equality, and the empowerment of all vulnerable groups is an issue that concerns all of us today.
I took art and culture as agents of change.
I discovered in this journey that liberty is a path and not a destination and that to walk in it is the way to breathe it in, the way to obtain it and be empowered.
MIC Género has required me to conquer this autonomy, and I call it autonomy because just when we are planning a project such as this, we touch on themes that are peripheral, themes that must strike against the norm and challenge us emotionally.
This film festival, as a grassroots initiative, invented its own way of being; and I admit, it's a way of being that's very fitting for its theme.
Maybe what we do is very queer, very abnormal sometimes, because every time we don't live up to others' expectations, we fail.
And gender perspective itself is a sort of dissonance; it deharmonizes, it dislocates, it goes contrary to the norm.
MIC Género has always wagered on failing at that.
We want to fail at the social mandate. We want to fail at everything that mass media expects.
Today, I encourage you all to fail.
Marx said in one quote, that humanity only poses problems it is capable of resolving.
Because problems are invented, not discovered.
Our project handles and visibilizes content, and this visibilizing of content is in itself an act of creation.
We must turn our most urgent problems, the tools for the change of tomorrow.
We must have the courage today to re-think the world because a creator always turns his obstacles into means.
Thank you very much.
Let's start with two pictures
taken in the tomb of Tutankhamen maybe one of the most visited monuments in the world.
The first picture was taken at the time of its discovery in 1922.
The second is a more recent one from 2012.
There's a 90 years-span between them.
And there is something else. There are more than 3,000 years between them as one of the pictures was taken in the original tomb whereas the other one was taken in Madrid, and represents the facsimile we created in order to preserve the original.
We work on facsimiles.
What is a facsimile?
It is an exact copy of an artwork aimed at preserving the original.
We are beyond negative connotations, like fakes or false reproductions.
Nothing to do with our work.
We usually create a facsimile whose purpose is to add value to the original work of art.
Most of the time, we start by digitally scanning the surface of the object.
Such as this Mappa Mundi at Hereford Cathedral, in England.
A medieval Mappa Mundi.
The aim is to contact-free high-resolution document the surface of an object,
and provide a 3D-model file which faithfully records the features of the object at a certain moment in time.
Our interest is to gather data about the object's both surface and texture.
When texture is properly documented, a lot of data is revealed about the object's biography.
What do we do when the current available technology is not enough?
We develop our own systems.
Lucida 3D scanner was specifically created to accurately record the texture of works of art like paintings or low reliefs which may initially seem flat, but that can provide valuable data on their texture and low reliefs.
Nevertheless, our goal is not to build a visual file or model, something people usually understand as new technologies.
We go a step further, as we focus on taking the qualities of those objects back into the material world.
In our job, the gathered raw digital data always returns to the material world.
Every work of art such as sculptures or paintings can be turned in facsimiles.
If we want to create a good facsimile the ratio color-low reliefs must be the right one.
The photographic color and 3D data are mapped back into different formats, preserving all of the qualities of the original.
We will see some examples of this.
What do we do when a work of art isn't to be found in its original context anymore?
For example, "The wedding at Cana",
one of the most important paintings by Veronese.
It was created for The San Giorgio Monastery, in Venice, but it is no longer there.
It was stolen by Napoleon, moved to Paris, and in permanent display since then at the Museum of Louvre, on the same hall as "The Mona Lisa".
It is presented to the public removed from its original context.
Our job was to create an accurate facsimile that was to replace its original in Venice.
But how do you go about scanning a more than 70 m2 work of art without removing it from its location?
It was impossible to use any supporting structure or any additional illumination.
We developed a non-contact, scanning system mounted on a telescopic mast, which scanned the whole painting illuminating only those essential areas for each shot.
Additionally, others conventional photography systems were used and hundreds of color samples were taken.
Once all this data was processed,
a digital impression on canvas was made, prepared and treated as the original one.
A plain multilayer color printer was used which printed successive layers with a perfect register, and the outcome mixed craft work and digital technology and that allowed us a richness and complexity very similar to the original.
There was a great expectation at the opening time to see how Venice will receive a copy.
Not only was the answer very positive but it was also very emotional.
People broke into tears
because, in some way, the work of art was back home or maybe because the space cycle was complete again, 200 years later.
It might seem a paradox: the original painting is still in Paris, but the copy in Venice could appear even more authentic.
What if the public has no access to the work of art? That is the case of the Bolonia's Hall, a private hall of the Apostolic Palace, in Vatican City.
It is not open to the public although it is one of the most interesting halls.
The entire Hall is decorated according to a sophisticated conceptual artistic program
that displays different views of the city of Bolonia.
Specifically, the south wall maps a plan of the city, one of the biggest urban reproductions created during the Renaissance.
We combined different systems to be able to document the Hall.
A panoramic photography of the hall, and we also scanned some parts of the wall in 3D.
Every project is a world in itself.
Once all the data was processed, the color had to be match-printed on the surface relief, but due to the irregularities on the wall it was impossible to do so as they surpassed the maximum height impression.
We then divided the problem into two magnitudes: a form magnitude to restate the original topography of the wall, and a texture magnitude reproducing the relief of the painting.
We specifically developed an elastic and flexible skin on which we could print the color and then adjusted it, and adhere it to the surface relief.
So, once it was mounted on light aluminium panels the outcome yielded the expected quality.
Finally, the facsimile was installed at the Bolonia's Museum of History. What is this project's main difference compared with the previous one?
In the previous one, the facsimile allowed a painting to be brought back to its original place.
In this case, the facsimile offered for the first time the possibility for many people to admire a work of art
in an interactive, natural and open way.
One of the most important reasons to work on facsimiles is to protect monuments or works of art from being damaged by overexposure.
The tomb of Tutankhamen, one of the most popular monuments in the Valley of the Kings has thousands of visitors every day.
It has been visited by millions since it was discovered.
Its popularity is most likely causing this site to deteriorate.
The humidity generated by the respiration and transpiration of thousands of visitors, together with some rather unfortunate past restorations contributes to the paint to literally peel off its walls.
In this case, the facsimile is the right approach to an immediate necessity, that of preserving and protecting a monument of such enormous value.
The four walls and the sarcophagus in the burial chamber were 3D scanned and then, the high resolution color and 3D data was digitized on a scale never made before.
This digitally obtained data is very important because it allows us to monitor the state of the tomb.
It is a digital record,
a permanent representation, of the state of the tomb at the time of the scanning.
In this particular case, in 2009.
For this project we developed a system which allowed the relief to be reproduced on glass fiber reinforced light resin panels.
A similar system was used for the color: an elastic skin that once the color was printed, could be manually adhered to the surface relief.
Once the adhesive has cured through a vacuum bag, the skin becomes harder.
Although the used material is completely different from that of the original, if observed under the same light and from the same angle it is practically identical.
From the beginning the idea was to display the facsimile back in Egypt.
Five years into the project, the right time to make it possible
was when at the beginning of 2014, an area was selected at the entrance to the Valley of the Kings in Luxor, for the the building of an underground space where the facsimile would be permanently displayed.
Today, tourists traveling to Luxor have the unique possibility of contemplating both tombs, the original and the facsimile and to compare them against each other.
That is, in our opinion, a decisive step towards sustainable tourism, because a facsimile allows an effective division between contemplation and preservation.
To conclude, beyond the technical challenge of creating a copy, our job seeks to draw attention on the importance and urgency to digitally document our artistic and cultural heritage in order to pass it down to the next generations as unchanged as possible.
For this reason, it is essential to understand that originality is a process rather than a strict state of things.
Many thanks.
(Applause)
Many of the stories of my life have had a lot to do with the question of whether to move or not to. And that is quite evident, don't you think?
I can identify moments in which in an instant I have been paralyzed, scared and at the same time very shaken on the inside.
Today I want to share what I learned in one of these moments that move on the outside but mostly on the inside, at least that is what happened to me.
Don't you think there are important life lessons? Sometimes there are these lessons that for some reason, are hard to forget and leave a mark on us.
I was lucky, like many Mexicans, to be in that surprise test the life gave us on September 19th, 2017, at 1:14 pm.
It felt horrible!
At one point, I felt I would fall of my wheelchair.
Being honest, the earthquake caught me eating tacos, and the plan was to pick up my son Pablo and go skiing.
Just so you understand my unconsciousness, I did so, I went to pick up Pablo, we even had to make a big turn around a bridge that fell down.
Now I realize that maybe I was so scared that the best way to deal with it was to act as if nothing was happening and keep going with the activities of that day.
Next day, we went to the Red Cross to help a little.
And a couple of days later, thanks to the blessing of having friends you can call useful crazy people, l was lucky to join my dear friend Carlo Clérico, a crazy tanathologist and engineer, to Jojutla.
The plan was to go with a group of teachers whose school fell down.
Many of them had also lost their homes and even lost a relative.
Arriving to Jojutla was quite an impression, it was like being on a war movie, everything was on the ground, fallen buildings, dust everywhere, tents in the park, and on the street, many, many people from all over the country, some foreigners, a lot of college students.
Tere, my wife, smart, sensible, practical and useful, as she is, started to make a plan with teachers to go back to school and dreamt of a new renovated school.
I was super confused, because picking up debris is not very easy for me.
So, I thought the best thing to do was to learn from Carlo and help people pick up their debris, their internal debris.
I started to work and participate in crisis support groups,
well, that's what they call them. I can say I had the privilege to learn with the best resilience teachers from Jojutla, Zacatepec, Tlaltizapan, Cuernavaca and Ciudad de Mexico.
I would like to share with you three lessons that this experience gave to me.
I share with you dearly the ABC of what I learned.
A for ABRIR = OPEN, open your eyes, first to see where to run to or, in my case, to ride fast - (Laughter) but also open our eyes to be amazed of what was happening.
A for ALTO = STOP. Make a useful stop in a world that goes to fast with an unclear destiny, in a society rushed by the clock but without a compass.
And suddenly the best compass: the heart and love as a common destiny.
A for ABRAZAR = to HUG.
A young woman told me she was in the 27th floor during the earthquake and when she was able to go down, she desperately needed to hug somebody; the first person she hugged was the head of security of the building, and then a kind of "hug epidemic" started. Many of these people had just looked to the head of security.
Earthquakes help us see people where, on a daily basis, we just see uniforms.
A for APRENDER = to LEARN.
Learn from that generation that taught us that you can rebuild a country with a sustainable development model, a model whose renewable energy are joy, happiness, company, community, work and shared effort.
I was surprised to hear many teachers talking, not only of the fear they lived during the earthquake, but a bigger fear: the fear of not being able to rise to the challenge of what their students showed them capable of.
A for ACOMPAÑAR = to SUPPORT. Support without judgement, with compassion.
An earthquake is a very democratic disaster: we all felt it regardless of our zip code, bank account or economic level. But the consequences aren't always as democratic, it depends on every person's story, that sometimes depends upon previous social earthquakes that make earthquakes not equitative.
A for ACEPTAR = to ACCEPT.
Accept emotions and feelings without judgement.
It was very confronting to learn how absurd and useless is moral judgement of feelings.
Labeling good and bad feelings is like telling someone: "look it is very good that you are hungry but it is very bad that you are thirsty."
Feelings are flags that invite us to act.
Fear invites us to run, to be safe.
Fear can paralyze us but fear can also make us raise our hands and ask for help.
What I saw is many hands willing to help, to join.
Fear, in that sense, builds community.
A for ALEGRÍA = JOY.
We talked with the youngsters and asked them to measure with by raising their arms how much of different feelings they felt.
I was surprised that when we said joy their hands were up to here (up) and when we asked them why, their answer was, "We are happy to be alive."
A for AGREDECER = to be GRATEFUL. To be grateful for what life gives us and we take for granted.
B for BUSCAR = to SEARCH or LOOK FOR. To look for what life is asking us, to try to be useful, to search for trapped persons, but not only persons trapped in buildings, also persons trapped by indifference, loneliness, fear and many more. B for BUCEAR = to DIVE.
Dive into our hearts to feel the same turbulences, the same waves that our brothers.
In the support groups when someone expressed a sincere feeling, we could see at the looks and heads nodding. You could see a wave, a wave that without any words says to you: "I understand you, because I feel the same."
Some earthquakes generate tsunamis that sink us with horrible power, other earthquakes generate caring waves, that hug you, support you and carry you to shore, to safety.
B for BORRAR = to ERASE. Erase adjectives to make shiny substantives.
A friend told me, before the earthquake when she visited this places she saw everybody as a kidnapper. After the earthquake, she had the chance to get to know them. What she saw were valuable human beings that were looking for the same that she was: to get ahead.
B for BARRER = to SWEEP. Sweep debris of the things that weren't firm.
A man told me, my building fell down, but not the building I worked in, my inner building, the one I have been constructing for 50 years.
Now, I need to rebuild it with stronger foundations.
C for CALLAR = to BE QUIET. Be quiet and raise our fist, asking for silence that longs life. Shot our mouths in order to give
the gift of silence, silence that repairs, that supports, silence that tells you what words cannot.
C for COMUNIDAD = COMMUNITY. Community that beats loneliness.
When life asks with a strong voice, the answer cannot be in the first person, it has to be in plural, the answer has to be us.
A friend from Oaxaca told me that a few days before they were asked at school to send some help for families affected by a prior earthquake.
While at the store, my friend asked herself: "Does a can of tuna really make a difference?"
And then she added: "Who would have guessed that ten days after that I would lose my house
and at the shelter when I looked for something for my kids to eat, I found a can of tuna with a message: 'Cheer up, from Taxco!'"
A can of tuna can make a difference. A can of tuna not only calms the hunger, it nurtures with hope to rebuild ourselves and move forward.
C for CONVERTIR = to TURN INTO. Turn our learning into service.
Zorba the Greek asked his boss: What do you do with what you eat?
After all this experiences and lessons learned, I am full with tasks and questions. In what will I turn my earthquakes into?
How can I become a seismic wave that proves everything without good foundations?
How can I help rebuild a world with the tightest standards of ethics, support, solidarity, love and work?
How can I have an inner seismograph that helps me be sensitive to the thousands of unseen earthquakes that a lot of people live every day around me?
How can I avoid going back to the day to day indifference and be able to keep living on an "earthquake mode"?
Being vulnerable, sensitive and useful.
The best strategy I have found for that is to keep being a student and surround myself with good teachers.
Let me share with you what one of those teachers taught me.
A friend told me that her biggest dream was to get a master's degree. But, when her house fell down, so did that project.
You can tell she was very disciplined because she told us how much she enjoyed working on her projects in a private exclusive place: her room.
And then she shared that after the earthquake, she wrote her last essays lying on the floor in some paper that came with the donations and she had to dry them on the sun because they got wet with the rain.
Fanny, my friend got the highest grade from her class. Today only months after the earthquake, she is in Milan studying for her Master's on a scholarship she won.
With this talk I thank and honor these teachers that taught me that nightmares can turn into dreams when there is understanding, love, community and shared dreams.
That nightmares turn into dreams when we wake up to live in earthquake mode. Being conscious, vulnerable, sensitive and useful.
That nightmares turn into dreams, when we wake up to be moved and touched with the seismic waves of others' hearts.
Thanks a lot.
(Applause)
Good afternoon.
I've always had a dream.
And that dream has been to build bridges between two seemingly distant worlds.
On one side, there is the rational and logical mind of the engineer.
And on the other, is the more emotional and creative side of the musician.
The theme of today's talk is to explore the limits of knowledge.
I will talk about my personal experience, about how I understand music, the creative process from which music is born.
I will also talk about all the stages that it goes through, from the very first idea until that idea becomes the song that you can hear.
I'll tell you an anecdote.
When TEDx asked me to give this talk, my engineer's rational and logical mind tried to find a solution.
It was filled with a cloud of thoughts that spun faster and faster.
The way for me to find a solution, given that this talk was about music and my experience, was to watch idly with my arms crossed how this cloud of thoughts were spinning.
And somehow, as I was focusing on that, my mind was slowly coming to a stop, each time slower.
There came a moment when there was silence.
Those thoughts stopped.
When those thoughts stopped a feeling arose, which was gradually becoming an idea.
This is exactly what I'll be talking about today.
I believe that music is born in silence.
When I'm in this situation, this silence, I become connected with the immaterial world of sensations and feelings.
I get to play the guitar without a score in front of me.
I start to improvize, let the music flow.
And occasionally some ideas emerge, certain melodies that catch my attention.
What I do then, is record them so that I can work on them.
Sometimes when I do this, I have this kind of sensation in my mind. I visualize experiences and feelings.
But there are other times when, in fact, there is nothing.
And I feel that music comes from a much deeper place.
Then there are times when I even feel that music is on the outside, in some sort of collective consciousness and that, somehow, I can connect to it as if it were a hard disk on the Internet and access that information and bring it out.
Once I have that first idea that is very simple and basic, I turn it into something greater, into a larger structure.
And this is what makes artisan work.
It is like doing a jigsaw puzzle and putting together a series of pieces.
I have to define a number of parameters.
For example, I need to define rhythms, harmonies, a musical style and an instrumentation.
It is as if this puzzle has infinite solutions.
In order to solve it, I ask for help from the rational and logical part. With musical knowledge and theory, I will provide solutions.
The emotional part is what chooses one from the other according to my musical taste and what I would like to achieve with this composition.
When I have this idea, I take it to the recording studio.
And, as a music producer, technology puts many tools at my disposal, which allows me to do the job.
From the simplest thing, such as a first idea on the guitar, I record it and play it along with another instrument and, that way, I start building a composition, instrument by instrument, element by element.
I can also mix real instruments with electronics.
I can even listen and see how the tune would turn out if, instead of playing it on a guitar, I used some other instrument, although at that time I wouldn't have it at hand in the studio.
This process basically consists in listening to the song again and again, and what I have to do is implement the music that I hear inside.
Then I have to translate it into something that everyone can hear.
And that is what I'm doing.
As I listen I'm adding one element, one instrument after the other, so that in the end it sounds as close to what I hear inside of me.
Once I have all these elements, as a sound engineer I need to make all of them be heard at once and heard perfectly.
I have to establish a series of hierarchy because there will be instruments that are more important than others, which play a role on the melody. There will be other instruments that have an accompanying role.
And with all that, I'll have a final result -- a mix -- that is turned into a file, which could be an mp3 or a disc that you can listen to anywhere.
For those who are not familiar with the world of music, I have prepared a short video.
I invite you to enter my recording studio where I'll do a demonstration on a theme to show how this theme develops from that first basic idea depending on the instruments that are added.
In this video, I will explain the process of composition and production of my theme "Life".
This is the initial idea from which the theme came about.
This first idea I recorded with a guitar and a celesta.
I added an electronic piano, another tune with an electric guitar, a violin, an electric bass, percussion instruments, the drums a piano, a keyboard and, finally, a vibraphone.
Thank you very much for your attention.
(Applause)
1,300 million years ago, there were two huge black holes that were dancing tango.
They were, while dancing, forming waves in space-time, deformations of space-time.
And they were getting closer and closer, turning faster and faster, almost at the speed of light, until, embracing each other, they formed a single black hole, 60 times the mass of our Sun, with a radius of 200 km.
And gravitational waves took the message of that embrace, at the speed of light, to the rest of the Universe.
This, sounding like science fiction, a Hollywood movie, was true.
And we know it was true because we measured those gravitational waves, last year in 2015.
The story of this discovery is also pretty long.
When this event happened 1,300 millions years ago in a faraway galaxy, on Earth, the first multicellular organisms were just appearing.
Life, humanity, societies grew, advanced, and a little over 100 years ago, in 1915, Einstein published his theory of general relativity.
It's a theory of gravity.
Relativity and gravity don't sound like the same thing, but they are.
His theory says that two masses attract each other, two black holes, or the Earth and the Sun, attract each other, not because, as we're taught at school, there's a gravitational force, but because all masses, according to this theory, deform space-time,
just as we do when we lie down on a mattress.
When we, a physical mass, lie down on a bed, we deform the mattress, making a depression.
And if someone else lies down on the bed, they will roll closer to us.
That is the force of gravity as Einstein imagined.
The Sun deforms space-time. The Earth does not feel an instant gravitational force, but it sees the space-time curvature, and the Earth spins around the Sun as we know it to do.
What is space-time?
We have to imagine space-time as a grid, not a two dimensional grid, but a three dimensional one, the three spatial dimensions that we can measure with rulers, with time, with clocks.
A grid of rulers and clocks.
Four dimensions,
and all of them are connected.
That is the space-time that gets deformed according to Einstein's theory.
The theory is pretty complicated, but what matters with theories are the predictions they make, and the checking of those predictions to prove them right or wrong experimentally, so as to know whether to believe the theory or not.
And Einstein's theory makes many predictions, all of them very crazy, quite incredible.
One, for example, the first to be verified a few years later in 1919, is that light does not travel in a straight line but deflects when it passes close to a mass, it deflects a little bit, by a certain amount.
And that was verified in 1919, and the theory was rather better believed.
But it also made other predictions.
For example, it being a theory of space and time, that clocks are not always synchronized.
If we start, me and you, with atomic clocks synchronized to the millionth of a second here, but then you go and hike up the Aconcagua, our clocks will no longer be synchronized.
Yours will be always ahead of mine.
And why is that?
It's because space-time is dynamic, and your clock is further from the Earth than mine.
Distances and gravity change time too.
Another prediction was this prediction of gravitational waves.
Since masses distort space-time, and masses are moving, those little ripples in space-time are also moving, and they are traveling at the speed of light.
And what they do, is to take distances and stretch and shrink them, stretch and shrink them, in proportion to distance.
But when Einstein, or any of the other scientists that followed him, calculated how much the distances distorted, it was very, very little.
He even wrote that this would probably never be measured.
And many people thought he was right, that this was one of those predictions that cannot be measured.
As I said, it is a long story.
In the 70s, people thought that it indeed could be measured.
There are some instruments that are widely used in physics and engineering to measure distances very precisely, which are called interferometers.
Interferometers because they use light, and the interference of light.
We take a ray of light, split it in two with a half-silvered mirror, reflect them back with mirrors, and when the two rays arrive back, they interfere. The waves interfere destructively, they destroy each other, and there is no resulting light, if this distance and this distance are the same.
But if this distance gets shorter and this one gets longer, or the other way round, then the interference out here is not totally destructive, and we can see a little light, or not.
So, by measuring with a photodetector how much resulting light there is, we can measure the difference in distance between this distance and that distance.
It looks simple enough to measure distances, and it is used a lot, but what's the amount we have to measure?
That is the big question.
The theory predicts that, resulting from these black holes, the distance between the Earth and the Sun changed by an atomic diameter.
And all smaller distances by less than that.
But those scientists in the 70s, starting with scientists at MIT, said this can be measured. If we construct interferometers 4 km long in a vacuum with hanging mirrors, we could get to measure differences between this 4 km and that 4 km of a millionth of a proton.
Many laughed; others didn't.
Many other people
started believing this.
The national science agency of the United States bet on this in the 90s.
From the 70s to the 90s, when these interferometers began to be constructed, two interferometers, two LIGOs, one in the state of Washington, and one in the state of Louisiana - quite close to where I live - 3,000 km apart.
They were completed in the 2000s.
First generation technology was operated and it went well. It didn't discover gravitational waves, but it was known that the technology had to be made more advanced.
In 2010, the second generation of this technology began to be installed.
It worked.
It works - more or less.
In 2015, we said that we should start taking a look at what is happening, in spite of needing to do more work on the detectors.
In 2015, we started collecting data with these two detectors.
And in September, on September 14th, 2015, these photodetectors told us that, 3,000 km away, we had signals indicating this gravitational wave.
We couldn't believe it.
And in December it happened again.
Listen.
(Sounds) That sound seemed impressive to us.
We marveled.
It left us speechless and dancing afterwards.
This is the sound of the Universe.
This is the music of the Universe.
We felt that from that moment -
Before that we were looking at the Universe with electromagnetic waves and telescopes and observatories, and now we were listening to it, with gravitational waves.
We had added another sense.
And from then on, we have not only been working to measure more gravitational waves, but we have also been talking about this.
And many people ask me: "And what are gravitational waves good for?"
And I say, "And what are gravitational waves good for?
What is astrophysics good for?
What is science good for?"
Ah, that we know. We all know
what science is good for.
All the technological advances that we use in communication, in transport, in medicine, all that is based on science, we all know.
But gravitational waves, astrophysics?
Actually the path
science takes, is very long.
It starts with basic theories relating to how the Universe works, and then, they are applied.
First they test to see if the theories are good or bad.
After that, ways in which they can be applied are discovered, in general, in other areas of physics or chemistry.
Engineers take hold of it all to build precision instruments.
And finally, sometimes, new useful technologies appear.
If Einstein had been asked in 1915: "What is your theory good for, sir?"
He would have said: "To better understand the Universe, to explain gravity.
What else does a theory need to be good for?"
Yet, today, many of you are going to use the theory of relativity when leaving here if you are going somewhere you don't know.
Because GPS needs the theory of relativity.
It needs many other things, but if it doesn't take into account that the clocks in the GPS satellites and ours, our clock, our little device, are desynchronized because they are at different distances from Earth, the GPS will lead us to the wrong place.
And I, that uses it all the time, would get lost.
So the theory of relativity, almost 100 years later, has practical applications.
And so it is with everything.
Science takes a lot of time in producing useful applications and technologies, and many people working with many different abilities.
If we think what technologies we wouldn't have today, what wouldn't have been invented if basic effects in physics, in chemistry, in mathematics, had not been studied a 100 years ago, there would be a lot of technologies that would not exist today.
The laser, GPS, medicines, medical applications.
A whole load of technologies.
The challenge I put to you is to imagine when you hear about a discovery like the one of gravitational waves or any other discovery in astronomy, in physics, in mathematics, that show up in the newspapers many times a year, when you hear about those discoveries, what technologies will there be in a 100 years that will use these theories?
That is the challenge for you.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I was 18 years old when a friend convinced me to start painting with the professor Marta Zucchetti.
I had been painting since I was a little kid, but only as a hobby. I remember that the first thing she made me paint was something called "still life". For those who don't know, it was a vase with a couple of fruits and other elements on a table.
It was a test to assess my ability.
After several practical exercises she told me I could begin my first painting.
She also clarified that she only let her students do their first artwork with pastel pencils but that she would let me use whichever technique I wanted.
At that time I was a fan of the "Redonditos de Ricota" and I thought it was a good idea for that to be my first painting.
I wasn't able to use oil nor to work with brushes but I still bought an 80 cm x 1m canvas and started.
It took me about six months to finish it.
A few days after I finished it, there was an exhibition in Rosario in which I took part with my classmates.
Before travelling, Marta mentioned to me that in every exhibition there is always one artwork that calls the viewers' attention.
I remember seeing how people gathered around my painting.
At that moment, a group of people called me to ask me some questions: How long have you been painting for?
How many paintings have you made?
When I told them that I had just started and that that was my first painting, I could see expressions of astonishment, followed by praise.
That experience encouraged me to go on painting.
The following year, I started the graphic design course of studies in Rosario.
While studying, I spent the weekends working as a designer for "El Alargue", a sports newspaper in the city.
Back then, I suggested to the owners that we should draw cartoons of the players.
They loved the idea and so every week the drawings were published on the back cover of the newspaper.
After several months, I received a call from Fernando Vergara, a soccer teacher who, at the time, worked at Los Molinos and he mentioned that he wrote stories about soccer as a hobby and asked me if I was interested in doing the illustrations.
He clarified that there was no pay, it was all for pleasure.
We've now been friends for 14 years.
We've published two books of stories about soccer, and this year we're about to publish a third one called "La Silvia".
Additionally, in 2018, we launched our website called "Por el chori y la coca."
Up to three years ago, I was used to drawing or painting on the desk at home.
Honestly, something very lonely which I was not enjoying that much anymore.
But one day, after seeing an artist painting a mural in the city, my curiosity was piqued and I had the desire to do the same.
That was how one sunny Sunday, I painted my first mural in my house's backyard.
For the second one, I had to wait until a holiday on a Wednesday. I picked the wall of a shed that faced a square around the corner from my house.
The location was chosen for two reasons: the first one, because I lived close by, and the second one, because it was a square that wasn't very busy, and if it turned out bad, it wouldn't be seen.
As I was painting, something interesting happened.
Kids who passed by riding their bikes got closer to watch.
Neighbours with whom I only exchanged words like "hi" and "bye" started to tell me stories and experiences.
Many shared the same passion as I did but life had led them towards other lines of work.
That was my first experience with people and I thought it was wonderful.
Not all of them knew about Nelson Mandela, but they did share his idea.
With this mural, I broke two records. The first one, finishing it in only three days. And the second one, it was vandalised one day after I had finished it.
The news went viral quickly and, thus, I realised that the people who congratulated me and shared my work on social media, now gave me words of encouragement and strength to go on painting.
This reminded me of a phrase by Facundo Cabral that says, "Good prevails, but it doesn't show because it's silent, a bomb is louder than a caress, but for every bomb that explodes, there are millions of caresses that build life."
If Mandela could endure 27 years in prison, then be released, become president and end racial inequality, How could I give up?
The truth is that I never took it personally.
I knew that painting in the streets could have these consequences.
I never imagined it could happen so quickly.
Some time ago, I had the idea of painting Mother Teresa of Calcutta.
I had the sketch ready but not the place, when something happened by chance.
It was a weekday, the weather was unbearably hot outside.
I was waiting for a friend to open his store.
Since I had some time, I took the chance to go and get ice cream.
So the teachers and children of a catholic school in Pujato came in.
My girlfriend, one of the teachers, introduced me to the headmistress and during our 15-minute talk, I showed her the sketch
and by the following week I was painting at the school.
From being in my backyard, to painting in another town. I was feeling new things.
I was restless, happy, nervous for not knowing who I was going to meet, how I would be welcomed and if I could really fulfill their expectations.
Once again, I met wonderful people.
Both the old and the young accompanied me at all times, and even invited me to the end of the school year lunch. But out of all the people that passed by that place, one was particularly surprising to me.
A man stopped his car and, brimming with excitement, thanked me for the artwork.
He told me the only thing left was for it to speak.
When he left, I was told he was one of the school's founders.
That was the exact moment when I understood why I paint.
I understood that, as an artist, it's my duty to connect with people, to try to excite, surprise, or inspire them.
Today, that place has lights, benches, flowers, a bust of the school's founder and the mural of Mother Teresa blessed by the bishop.
To be able to see how one simple action leads to other actions aimed at improving a place is incredible.
Today we're used to hearing words like crisis, corruption, violence.
The media daily show us the bad part of the world.
That is why I try, in every mural, to leave positive messages that bring us a bit closer as a society.
I usually paint people who inspire me and who maybe can inspire or change something in someone else.
I'm convinced that changing the world doesn't require extremely talented people, but small daily actions, like saying "good morning," "good afternoon," or helping the person next to us.
Plus, having a talent involves great responsibility.
And if that talent is not used for the good of society, it's worthless.
And remember that life, like murals, is ephemeral, but what we do with our hearts will live on forever.
Thank you so much.
(Applause)
I remember the wonderful day that my husband and I left the hospital with a healthy, beautiful baby,
we lived in Washington. I got to our apartment, looked at him and asked, What am I going to do?
They don't come with instructions.
In that moment, I never imagined that two years after that wonderful and scary day when I became a mother for the first time I would be told, he has autism.
What?
Autism.
Many questions came to mind,
among those...
Why my family? Why my son? Why me?

As selfish as it may sound, these are the questions that come to mind.
Raphael was diagnosed in California where we lived at the time.
1 in 68 children are born with autism, according to the last study conducted by the CDC in the United States in 2010 and published in 2014.
I want to tell you what it feels like for Raphael to be 1 of those 68.
Having a child with special needs feels the same but different.
The infinite love we truly experience when your child is born is there. But it is inevitable to feel cheated like something was taken from you. And you hold on to every word your loved ones tell you, like, don't worry, everything is going to be okay.
But deep down you know it is not that way.
You know that something is not right. When we are confronted with the reality, my son will never be normal. It is like a giant falls on us and we are faced with the tremendous challenge of having to read, listen and study about a diagnosis that was never part of our lives: Autism.
And suddenly all the questions, that parents ask when a child is born change.
Now they are: Will he speak?
Will he ever call me mom?
Will he be independent? And we
refuse to look into the future because it is filled with so much fear.
Birthdays are no longer a point of reference to what he may be doing.
Like those conversations that happen between mothers, like, "at two years of age your child is doing this and this and that," and yours is not.
However, there are achievements but these are different from those of others.
And to be able to really see these achievements, the first thing is the mourning process.
You bury the idea you had about your child. The dreams you had for him, to a new reality, an unknown world, which is autism.
And you suffer.
I remember that many times after Raphael was recently diagnosed, I drove six hours a day to therapy and many times I cried while I drove. At the time I was pregnant with my second child,
I was four months pregnant and two weeks after Raphael's diagnosis, I was told John Paul would be born with Down Syndrome.
John Paul was born normal.
And up to date, he is the best therapist for Raphael.
He helps him and is empowered
intuitively from an early age. From helping him change his shirt, or asking for cake at birthday parties when Raphael did not like to go. And being persistent until he could get a little bit of Raphael's attention.
If I knew then what I know now, I would have taken myself by the hand and told myself what to do in those moments of confusion and fear.
If I knew then what I know now, I would have told myself not to be afraid when I was told Raphael would probably never speak. Because doctors can also be wrong.
Raphael spoke when he was 4 and half, and this was the second time he called me mom.
(Video) Thelma: Say "Hi, mom."
Raphael: Hi, mom.
Thelma: Hi, Rapha.
(Video ends) (Applause) If I knew then what I know now, I would explain to myself what took me so long to comprehend. What is autism and what are the possible signs?
It is a condition that affects the central nervous system when processing information.
It has no cure and it can range from mild to severe.
It affects communication skills, social skills and they also have the tendency to engage in repetitive behaviors like this one.
(Video) (Video ends) They also appear to be in an isolated world, their own world.
I think this is one of the hardest things for parents. Because what parents do not want to connect with their child?
Raphael spent many hours running towards the wall. He touched the wall like he was turning on a light and would then look up. He would go back and forth doing the same thing over and over again.
One day I got up, ran towards the wall and did exactly the same thing. And for the first time he looked me in the eye and I said "there he is."
I entered his world.
If I knew then what I know now. I would explain sensory integration. Something vital for every properly functioning human being.
We have five senses.
For example touch, if I grab an object immediately my brain lets me know if the object is hard, soft, if it is warm or cold and I react according to what my brain tells me.
For children with autism these reactions are disorganized.
I will give you an example but before I do, what is more important than the five senses we already know, there are two more, proprioceptive and vestibular.
The proprioceptive is the sense of the relative position of neighboring parts. Our joints, our response to pressure.
If my legs are crossed I do not have to look at my legs to know if it is the right or the left because I feel it.
The vestibular system is the one that gives me a sense of where I am in relation to everything around me, spatial orientation. This lets me know if I am standing, sitting or laying down.
It lets me know if it is the room that is moving or if it is me.
It lets me know that if I walk more than five steps in that direction I would fall off the stage.
Like I mentioned for children with autism, these senses can be greatly disorganized, let me put it this way. Raphael split his forehead when he was 3 and half, and didn't cry. He did not feel that pain. But I would put a shirt on him and he would scream because he could not stand the tags.
What kind of disorganization of the processes of the brain must there be, that a three year old does not cry when he splits his head but cannot stand a tag of a shirt?
If I knew then what I know now, I would explain that sensory processing begins with primitive reflexes in utero.
What are they?
These are reflexes that during the first year of life are replaced by adult reflexes and these remain for the rest of our lives.
For example the Moro reflex disappears
around 4 months of age.
And it is replaced by the defense reflex.
Have you seen a baby, when he gets startled he raises his arms and slowly lowers them back down?
At 4 months of age it is replaced by the defense reflex, the one that tells me to use my arms instead of falling or when I fall I immediately use my arms to protect myself.
Now, all these reflexes are the ones that later help us function, have balance, feel and when they are not replaced and on time these are red flags that we can begin treating.
I have no examples to give you as far as Raphael's primitive reflexes, because I didn't know about them.
I would have loved to have known about this and perhaps had been able to detect red flags in my son before the age of two.
If I knew then what I know now. I would explain to myself certain characteristics that all children with autism share. One of them is that
they appear to be deaf. But is there something wrong with their hearing?
No.
I remember one day, in my desperation, I was calling out my son's name -- and of course he didn't respond -- I ended up shouting his name and he never turned to look at me,
hopefully no one heard me or they might have said "there is a crazy woman living in that house."
Another characteristic is that it is very difficult for them to make eye contact.
Why?
Because it is difficult for them to do two things at the same time. Like looking and listening at the same time.
Most of them cannot stand fluorescent lights, like those found in grocery stores.
Why?
Because for many, these lights flicker
and that bothers them. I would have loved to tell myself that if I put a pair of shades on my son before going to the grocery store, I would have avoided having to practically tie him to my hip, while everyone stared, because he was screaming as if he was getting killed
and of course there where always the judgmental stares going, "Why can't she control her child?"
The autistic mind has always one tendency. They tend to be good in some things and bad at others.
Why?
Because it is like there is not enough cables to connect everything well.
All human beings learn in different ways.
Some of us are more verbal, others auditory, others visual.
But the difference between normal people and those in the autism spectrum, is that we have the capacity to compensate in our weaker areas. While the child, the person with autism learns only through the way they perceive the world.
The visual thinkers who learn through pictures, we teach them through pictures and they will learn that way to perceive the world. But they learn.
Now, every case is different.
Another very important characteristic is that we learn from general to specific.
While the autistic mind learns from the detail to the general.
An example of this is:
Do you remember when you were little how you learned to differentiate dogs from cats?
After all there are dogs of all sizes, colors and breeds.
There is a Chihuahua and a German Shepherd.
There are dogs that look like cats.
I don't remember how I learned but I know I knew.
The autistic mind learns from details.
We teach them one detail that all dogs share. For example, the big or small one, the chihuahua or German Shepherd all have exactly the same nose. So we teach them that when they see that nose, it is a dog.
A few months ago I was giving a talk about this topic and a mother asked me, why her son with autism was no longer passing her the cup. After all, this was something he was able to do.
So I asked her,
"Are you using the same cup?"
She said "No, its a different one."
Well, he does not know that the new one is also a cup. Teach him a detail that all cups share, for example, all cups have a little "ear."
I did not know anything about this subject.
I would have loved to talk to myself 7 and half years ago and tell myself everything I know today.
Today I am a Certified Autism Specialist, but I continue learning every day because there are enormous challenges that I face with my son and thanks to all the people that also support me with all of this.
I call to empower yourselves with this topic, autism.
That you leave here with a desire to learn because we need help.
Because here in Nicaragua this topic is still not discussed much.
In the United Sates 1 in 68, there are no statistics here.
Why are there no statistics?
(Applause) Educators, I call you to take action and put programs in place and integrate these children so they receive what we all have a right to. A good education, belong to a community.
To the students that are here today, I call on you, that a few leave with a desire to study and help mothers and fathers like me, and our children because we need more people to help us.
Today Raphael is 9 and half years old, he is highly functional, academically he is advanced than most of his peers. He has great challenges in the emotional and social areas. But we take one day at a time.
The life of fathers and mothers of children with autism is different.
You realize that your life is different.
Your life is not the same as others. You are a constant therapist for your child.
The way you talk changes, everything changes.
But you see this little angel that depends completely on you. And you know at least a few things. He will never lie because he does not know how to lie. He doesn't understand sarcasm and after all,
for him, you are perfect.
There comes a day when you stop asking yourself, Why me?
And the answer is, Why not!
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good morning to you all.
To be honest, my morning didn't start so well.
If it hadn't been for the few steps between myself and my bathroom, of a warm shower, of my lime-scented soap, truthfully, I would've never noticed that a wonderful moment such as this was going to come.
By all means, that bathroom changed my mood.
And my mood changed even more when I had the pleasure of sitting on the toilet and, as it should be, newspaper on hand, wait a few minutes to fill soon after that relief, that weightlessness.
To be frank, in my case, shedding a few pounds.
(Laughter) That, which sounds so funny and we've come to take for granted, is not so for half of the world, where there are 2600 million people without access to basic sanitation services.
Without a toilet bowl, without a private area where to perform one of the, perhaps, most intimate of tasks that we face as human beings.
And this is not different in Peru.
Where one third, has no access to those basic sanitation services.
Where there are 12 million people for whom having a bathroom is an impossible dream.
And that happens in a country that has had a steady growth over the last 10 years.
One that has reduced, over the same period, poverty by half.
One with medium rental costs. One that almost reaches $7,000 annual GDP per capita
One that feels proud of it's history, of it's cuisine, of it's progress.
Some may ask: Why then, if it's such an important topic, is it not given the importance it deserves?
Sanitation doesn't only impact on our health.
Sanitation has to do with the survival of our boys and girls.
So, then, why are we doing nothing with regards to sanitation?
Sanitation has a fundamental impact, not only in our interaction with others, but as it relates to the topic of economy.
Sanitation impacts on tourism, on education, on productivity and in the country's competitivity
A gram of human fesces have 10 million viruses, 1 million baterias and a 100 parasite eggs.
Those virus, bacterias and parasites are the responsible of a group of diseases, like respiratory diseases, early death, skin diseases and a group of diarrhea related diseases like dysentery and cholera.
Even worse yet: this parasites won't ever allow that the physical and cognitive development of the children be the optimum.
Sanitation is extremely related to child malnutrition
and as you know, if a child doesn't achieve full development of their capacities and of their physical and cognitive potential in their first five years, they will never achieve them.
Sanitation kills, around the world, 1,8 million of people.
Peru tends to more then a million cases of diarrheic diseases per year.
5,000 children!
5,000 children die every day because of this diseases.
Those same children, in Peru, have between 10 to 12 diarrheas in a year.
It cost to the State around USD 12 to tend one of this diarrheas, lots of which end up in death.
Sanitation also has an impact on the environment.
Is calculated that every year, around the word, are poured into water sources about 200 million tons of human wastes.
And a lot more tons of non-sanitized waters.
So sanitation is intimately linked withe the vicious circle of poverty.
Sanitation depresses our souls, our productivity, our ability to thrive.
For example: if schools don't have sanitation, of course, the rate of frequency on diarrheic diseases in bigger.
But is even bigger and there's studies that prove it, When there's no separate environmet for boys and girls,
the rate of diarrheic infections increase, the absences from school increase, also the rate of drop-outs and desertion increase,
and that's even more alarming for the girls, that have to suffer through menstruation in this conditions.
While an adult isn't kill by a diarrhea, it does decreases its productive capability.
According to the UN, 10% of productive days are lost each year due to diarrheal diseases.
Yes, we want to sell the country as a brand related to the experiential tourism to the adventure tourism, How are we going to do it if our communities doesn't have the basic sanitation services to offer?
We want to be competitive in terms of agroexport.
Of course we are!
But we cannot be it, not when the Ica valley can't compete with the valley of Napa in California. Because a single contaminated creek is enough to not have access to the certification of the whole valley.
According to official data, Peru treats less than 20% of sewage waters.
Definitely, sanitation has a significant impact on economy, they are very clear and real.
According to studies by the World Bank, countries loses between 1% to 6% of their GDP because of their lack of access to basic sanitation services.
According to the UN, each dollar that's invested in sanitation has a economic return of 9,10 USD.
If the subject is so clear, why is sanitation the missing link in the development agendas, no only of the governments, but also of the social organizations and of the private sector?
For several reasons.
First of all: because it's very unpleasant and you are surely listening to me with a bit of resilience.
It's very unpleasant to talk about this subjects.
Who likes to talk about sewages, sewers, human wastes, of crap?
Second: because even if sanitation impacts on public matters, is, in essence, a private matter.
It was to do with modesty, with the most basic and essential thing as human beings.
From my point of view, it is more primitive than talking about sex.
Third: because people that live without access to sanitation, live in a context that overwhelms them.
They have taken the land by the storm.
With courage, bravery and persistance they have built they houses little by little, day by day, but without a vision of a medium or a long term plan.
It's built today, and wherever a room was put, it'll stay there.
And because I don't know where the bathrooms is going to be Where do I put it?
And then, some houses can have two, three rooms, a second floor, but lacking a bathroom.
Simply because there wasn't a design that included it.
And that might be the explanation of why of the 3 million houses without access to sanitation services in Peru, more than half are above the line of poverty.
They aren't poor.
What has been the experience of people that has use a latrine for years?
The latrine, in the mind of persons, and in all of ours too, is the most horrendous place that can exist.
A place that stinks, that has flies, and a place where you can fall...
I wouldn't go there for the world.
Or neither of you. Better to do outside than inside!
The latrine is a hotbed for contamination and farther from the house the best.
I will never forget a lady, of one of the studies we did, she told me a phrase that I will never forget.
She told me: "Miss, having a latrine is like having a sign in the chest that says: 'I'm poor'".
But that doesn't mean this people doesn't dream with
Of course they have dreams!
Miss Jovita wants a bathroom!
And she wants a bathroom because she has the reference, the experience.
Because she goes to a mall that has a bathroom, because her sons go to a school that has a bathroom, because in her husband's work there's a bathroom.
Because Peru has grown, gentlemen!
Because we have reached out and the wellbeing has reached a lot of people.
But people wonder in those conditions, in that referal, What do I have to do to have a bathroom?
I might want it, I might even have the resources, but I don't have the tools.
I don't have the information, I don't have anyone to tell me how to put everything together.
I don't have time to go buy all this pieces, I don't have a design.
In less word: nobody offers a complete solution.
The next question is: Why, if there is demand, there's no offer?
Why the private sector doesn't see this as a bussiness opportunity?
Because there's also barriers on the private sector's side.
Because from the point of view of the bussinessmen, that market for poor people is uninteresting market.
It's a small market, scattered, rural.
Besides, it is the market that the Government should answer to.
"I have my cheap, for poor people, linen."
"What else do you want me to do?"
But Carmen doesn't want that cheap linen.
Carmen wants a cute bathroom, a colorful bathroom with flower stickers.
A safe and modern bathroom, that make her and her family feel proud that she is part of the same society, that she is equal to all of us.
That's Miss Adelita.
A feisty woman that gave me, maybe the most interesting testimony that I've ever heard.
She told me: "Miss, I can die in peace now.
Do you know why?
Because, for the first time in 84 years, I showered in a washroom.
And because now I have something to give to my children and grandchildren."
Or like Mr. Ismaiel, an agricultor from La Mora, in Cajamarca, that after hearing me talk about the importance of having a bathroom, he came back after a few minutes with a wad of money, like a thousand of soles.
And he told me: "What does this buys?
What size of bathroom can I buy with this?
Because now, with this bathroom, my children and grandchildren that are in Lima will want to come visit me."
Or like Sara, a makeup saleswoman of the human settlement of Pachacútec in Ventanilla, who said to me: "You know what?
I want to sell bathrooms.
Because I do know how to convince my neighbors to buy less cellphones and TVs and to make them buy bathrooms."
In face of all of this, we began to think: How can we match up the expectations and necessities of the country with that very tangible demand that exists and besides, with the expectations of the private sector, that wants rentability and that have before them a real market, valued in 1,500 million USD?
And we said: "Well, we've tried everything -because there is 7 years of perseverance and trial and error behind this-
Well, lets try it again but now only from the private sector."
And we challenge the private sector to promote a process and iniciative, and to develope a market that didn't exist at the base of the pyramid.
Today, for the first time in Peru, 15 companies have signed a contract to carry out this proposal under the same label for waters,
They have worked for months to arrange a bathroom in a combo, so that the piece of one producer fits the piece of another, and like that, develop a commercialization system unique for the 15 companies that allows to deliver this bathrooms combos to the consumer's door.
They have negotiated with the finantial system, in order to offer this bathroom with a 48 month's installments and they have worked in what's most important in my point of view: a customer service that includes information, technical advice and education.
This work will soon see the light.
You are going to know the brand, if not by the end of the year, then by the following months of next year.
It has been a very difficult job.
It has been the most difficult job I've ever encounter because it has mean to gather a lot of different wills.
What will the outcome be?
Will it be super succesful?
Will it work and will we change the world?
I don't know.
But what I do know is that we would have set an antecedent that maybe other can replicate.
That to cooperate will always be better than competing, that innovation is possible in this country; and that there's a new rol for the private sector beyond philantropy and social responsability.
From my point of view, we are in a point of no return in the definition of new business models.
The world need new forms to tend to our problems.
I hope this new initiative and this brand will be the motor of change to impulse this revolution and that reflexion that we should do every day concerning to the huge inequities that we still have to confront.
But, more important yet, I wish this remind us everyday that what's common for all of us, it's not for other million of people in the world and in Peru.
Thank you.
(applause)
A while ago, I was in a bar.
There, was a woman at another table with her five-year-old son.
She was completely captured by her cell phone.
Meanwhile, the kid stared at her.
Maybe another kid would have messed up to try to get her attention.
But not this kid.
He waited for a while, he finally stood up, went behind his mother and began to stroke her hair.
He tried to summon her with caresses for several minutes.
And she never realized what was happening.
A question haunted me: How many times I might have been in the same situation without even realizing it?
What is happening to us?
Why do we live obsessed with our screens?
Today we unlock our cell phones 150 times a day.
This is once every 6 minutes during the time we are awake.
I decided to do some research on this and came to a conclusion that struck me.
What is happening to us with technology is not an accident.
Stanford University is located at the heart of Silicon Valley, the cradle of the world's biggest technology companies.
The Persuasive Technology Lab is based there, where brilliant researchers work -- in their own words -- to see how to use the websites and mobile apps we use to manipulate what we think and what we do.
Trying to persuade others is as old as the world.
But digital devices and the huge volume of personal information accumulated from our online activity are creating a new way to manipulate our thoughts and actions taking advantage of the vulnerabilities of the mind detected by behavioral economics, psychology and neuroscience.
Examples abound.
On the social scale, attempts to manipulate elections, the proliferation of fake news, the widening of all social gaps.
In the personal orbit, parents who don't see their children, and conversely, meetings where everyone is more concerned about what happens on their screen than with what happens around them.
A growing difficulty to focus.
This phenomenon began with the initial expectation that everything on the Internet had to be for free.
A group of companies had to find a way to earn money without charging users.
First thing they did was to place ads and start charging advertisers.
Then they started collecting this heap of personal data to ultra-segment the message they deliver to each of us.
Finally, to increase their profits, each company needed us to spend more and more time on their platform.
And that was how the attention hack was born.
When the product that companies sell is your attention, it's a dog-eat-dog race.
Facebook competes against other social media like Twitter, but also with very different products like YouTube, Netflix or Fortnite.
Every second you're not there hypnotized it's time they cannot sell to their advertisers.
That's why they use visual and sound notifications and all kinds of tricks to distract you from anything you are doing, even when you are using other platforms.
Not long ago, the former president of Facebook made public his regrets.
In a presentation he said that he and Mark Zuckerberg were absolutely aware of being developing a platform which would exploit vulnerabilities in our minds to maximize the addictive effect.
But he was not the only one to admit the use of these abusive mechanisms.
Recently, the founder of Netflix declared that their greatest enemy is sleep.
His goal is that we sleep less so we spend more time watching series.
Selling products that are not good for us is also not new.
But at least the executives of companies like big tobacco were on the defensive.
The founder of Netflix overtly tells us that his company is willing to induce habits which are totally detrimental to our health in order to make more money.
To achieve these goals they feed on our naivety.
When we are going to buy a product, say a pair of shoes, we have some distrust.
We wonder things like, "are they comfortable?"
"Are they good quality shoes?"
"Is this a reasonable price to pay?"
But when the product is free, we lower our guard.
If the product is free we should be much more wary.
Why a large multinational company would want to incur the huge costs of developing a social network, a video platform, an email system, for us to use for free?
Nothing is free in the business world.
If we are not paying with money, what else are we paying with?
An especially sensitive ground for manipulation is self-esteem.
The increasing use of photos and videos as the main language in the networks, gives an absurd, overblown importance to aesthetics and physique over all other dimensions of our being to the eyes of others and, therefore, to ourselves.
And networks take advantage of that.
Social media takes advantage of the fascination that causes in us to spy on the lives of others and make an impact on others with our own image to keep us endlessly mesmerized.
Moreover, since each of us shares very little spontaneous content about the highlights of our day, conveniently edited to look much better than it was, when we later ride a bus, cramped and bored surfing on a social network, it's inevitable to have the wrong feeling that we are the only ones who have an ordinary life more full of obligations and mishaps than laughter and sunsets.
It's inevitable that the comparison against these false ideals leaves us disappointed with our own lives.
The decision of who we follow and what we show is key to breaking the effects of this distorted mirror.
While it was always true that there were some popular people and some others more withdrawn, somehow this is implied.
Today it's measurable and visible to everyone.
The likes and the number of followers is the currency in which social acceptance is traded today.
And each action is subject to public quantification, for the amount of likes it gets.
As a result, we begin to live life to show it, not to enjoy it.
That is the narcissistic swamp in which social media put us and which, curiously, we don't want to leave.
The desire to find someone to love and be loved leaves us in a particularly vulnerable position.
Last month, the U.S. Department of Consumer Defense sued the world's largest dating company.
Charges are for taking advantage of the despair of those who are not being able to find a partner, allowing them to be contacted from false profiles, then inviting them to pay to get in touch with these non-existent people, who will never reply their messages back.
Is that really anything goes to retain us as users and take our money?
Another particularly attractive group which is an easy target is our teenagers.
Adolescence is the time when manipulations of our self-esteem find the most fertile ground.
But the problem starts long before that.
Babies have also been discovered as a new consumer target.
YouTube is full of videos specifically designed to hook them.
Although the recommendation of the World Health Organization says that babies under two shouldn't spend a second in front of screens, the reality is quite different.
The average time for two-year-olds today is 2.5 hours a day.
And a third of babies uses screens from before walking.
At this crucial stage of development of the nervous system and the psyche, the electronic pacifier is becoming a tempting escape for parents and it's replacing physical contact, the use of speech and upbringing games.
Many parents even take pride in their kids' ability to handle these devices.
They see what their children learn with these videos: the animals, the colors, but they don't see the lessons and experiences lost.
Several scientific studies are already beginning to show the negative consequences of this change in areas such as language acquisition, attention span and socio-emotional development.
The biggest risk for our children is not the early use of screens, but adults exceedingly slipping away.
(Applause) In the face of this, some people say: "After all, it's always the same.
In the 40's the villain was the radio, in the 60's, television, in the 80's, video games, and now is this."
For Socrates, the villain was writing.
There is always something the elders demonize simply because it's new.
But this time is different.
Because, while we keep calling this ultra-light supercomputer that each of us carries a "cell phone", this device is everything.
It's indeed a cell phone, but it's also a camera, a TV, a GPS, a game console, a camcorder, an Internet browser, a flashlight, an alarm clock, a calculator, a stereo... and many more things.
Not only it is everything.
It's with us everywhere and at all times offering the promise of an unlimited flow of content capable of filling the void at every moment of our life.
Yet, in this era of hyper communication, studies show that the number of people who feel lonely was never as high as it is today.
There is no worse loneliness than loneliness surrounded by people.
And all this is going to get worse.
So far only a handful of companies put these mechanisms into practice.
But new ventures are developing software to bring these manipulative methods to all digital products.
The use of manipulation and the hacking of our attention have just began.
What do we do?
Do we throw away our cell phones and delete our social media profiles?
No, it's not necessary to go that far.
The advantages of connected life are too great to give up.
But we are in an unequal battle between very sophisticated companies and users that act with naivety.
We signed a contract written by the other party without even being able to read or know what the hell we are signing.
To even things out, we need to understand how these mechanisms work in order to defend ourselves from manipulation.
If we asked ourselves how each of these companies makes money, it'd be easier to imagine what kind of behavior they want to induce in us and what kind of information they may be looking for.
For example, the next time you use a dating app, keep in mind that the business of these companies is that you search, not find.
Devices and networks keep us absorbed, distracted, impatient and focused on passive consumption.
But it doesn't have to be like that.
It's time to leave naivety behind and fight back.
We can reclaim the control of our lives to take advantage of the benefits of technology without being eaten up by it. To take advantage of the supercomputer we carry with us to create, not just to consume.
To use it to live shared experiences instead of being locked upon our own screen.
In short, the challenge is to put platforms and devices at the service of the life we want to live, not at the life that others need us to live.
(Applause)
Gerry Garbulsky: After a TED or TEDx talk, completely unbelievable and unexpected things happen.
And often times, they're marvelous things.
Jorge Drexler gave a very nice and interesting talk on identity, where he talked about the "décima," which is a poetic form, about milonga, and also about the identity of people.
A talk inspired by a song, "La milonga del moro judío."
And after Jorge's talk, something happened, which I think had never happened before on a TED stage.
But, really, wouldn't it be better if he told what happened himself?
(Applause) Let's give a round of applause to Jorge Drexler.
(Applause) Well, as that was something so amazing, and taking advantage of Jorge being in the middle of his world tour presenting his new album, we decided to steal some time from him and ask him to share with us how that was... What happened, Jorge?
Jorge Drexler: From that TED Talk a song came out, the one that opens the album, the song is called "Movimiento" and it's the one that opens an album that's called "Salvavidas de hielo" that I'm just now releasing.
The period of writing of an album, the period of composition, is a period of very strong emotional and intellectual opening.
And when one embarks in the preparation of a TED Talk, and many of the fellows that have just spoken or the ones that will speak in a bit know this well, how one gets involved emotionally and intellectually, what happens, so it was evident that it would mark the composition period.
Then, gathering ideas and gathering emotions and all, I realized that, in parallel to the talk, a song was emerging, this one that's called "Movimiento."
And somehow, a paradox occured, as the talk had a singing part, and in the opposite pole and complementing it, the song has a spoken part too so they emerged as complementary processes.
What I'm going to do first and foremost, so we don't talk in abstract terms, is to sing you a bit and talk a bit of the song and then I'll show you little by little, telling you the story of why I'm here presenting it, why it makes all the sense in the world here in TEDxRíodelaPlata, for this song that traced a parable to land once again today, and for me to present its video, I mean, which we haven't shown before the video of the song that we finished just a couple of hours before.
So I'm going to sing you a bit from "Movimiento".
In fact, I'm going to stand up.
(Applause) Do some a cappella.
We had just begun walking upright we began to migrate through the savannah.
Following the herd of bison beyond the horizon to new, distant lands.
The children in back. Expectant.
Eye peeled. All ears.
Sniffing out that puzzling new landscape, unknown.
We are a species in transit,
we don't have belongings, we have baggage.
We go with the pollen in the wind.
We're alive because we are in movement.
We're never still, we're nomadic. We are parents, children, grandchildren and great-grandchildren of immigrants.
What I dream of is more mine than what I touch.
I'm not from here, but neither are you.
I'm not from here, but neither are you.
Not completely from one place and a little from everywhere. We crossed deserts, glaciers, continents, the whole world from end to end.
Stubborn. Survivors.
The eye on the wind and the currents,
the hand firm on the oar.
We carry our wars with us, our lullabies, our path made of verses, of migrations, of famines.
And that's how it has always been, since before time, we were the drop of water traveling in the meteorite.
We crossed galaxies, the void, millennia.
We searched for oxygen. We found dreams. (Applause) Thank you.
GG: There's a phrase, Jorge, of the song you just gifted to us that sounds familiar.
JD: Yes, the TED Talk closes with "Not completely from one place and a little from everywhere."
But beyond that, it's the idea that structures the song.
After that, we went to record the song to Mexico.
The album was was largely recorded in Mexico.
One morning I was having breakfast in the house we were staying at, reading the press and I read one article that drew my attention that has to do with all of this.
I read an article that says "woman of the Tarahumara people wins an ultramarathon runing 100 kilmetres without sports equipment, with plastic sandals".
100 km, ladies and gentlemen, it's like going from here to Chascomús, except that this is is flat because these are the Pampas and that's the Sierra del Cobre, where they were running there's a total slope of 4,000 metres between downhills and uphills.
And this woman, who's Lorena Ramírez, and is 22 years old, and indigenous person of the Tarahumara people, marvelous --
I'm going to show you the pictures of the podium when Lorena, after winning the ultramarathon, is in the middle with her traditional clothing and her sandals.
(Applause) The story of Lorena moved me deeply because it's a -- I mean, it's in what's the tradition of the Tarahumara.
The Tarahumara are a people of the Sierra del Cobre, in the Northwest of Mexico, in Chihuahua, that have running as a tradition.
I suppose some of you have heard about them before, especially those here who run, because they're mythical runners, they're able to literally run hundreds of kilometers, they have cultural races, because they run as how one plays music, somtimes 200 kilometers, in their sandals, in their "huaraches", that they make themselves or with plastic ones in this case like the ones that Lorena has on.
Why do the Tarahumara run and why did this impress me so much in the moment I was recording?
They run as a way of survival.
From colonial times, they have been moving from the coast to the interior, to steeper areas, trying to keep their customs isolated and to safeguard them.
As it's an area that's very fractured by mountains, so to communicate between the many Tarahumara settlements, they would run.
And speed was very important for the agility of communications.
Therefore, to me it seemed like there were many connection to what I was telling you before, with the song that you just listened and to the content of the talk.
The first one is that to stay alive a culture had to be displaced, it had to move.
As in the case of the "décima," as I told you in the talk, it's born in 1591 in the Spanish Barroque and it's kept alive because it then crosses the ocean, it's displaced and it's preserved with love, alive from the "son Jarocho" to the "canto de Mejorana", in Panamá, to payadores here in Argentina and Uruguay.
In other words, the same way it had to move to survive, that's what happened in the song and what happened with the Tarahumara.
And there's another thing I really liked from the example from this marvelous picture, and it's something you see a lot in TED too, and we talked a lot with Gerry about it, that is cultural relativism.
In contemporary metropolises, we believe we've invented everything, but there's another song in the album that follows from that, that says "we all believe we've invented something, but the songs are still the same ones".
And we believe we've invented the act of running many kilometres, marathons.
Marathons have not only existed from Greece, they exist even from before, much earlier from the idea of running as we consider it, before the concept of sports.
And for them, for the Tarahumara and for Lorena, running is truly a cultural activity, something that the elderly, children, entire families do.
They throw a ball down the Tarahumara Sierra and they run for two days at times, until they find it and one brings it back and is declared as winner.
That's where it started then.
I had the idea of making the video of this song with Lorena and a different quest started.
First, we had to locate her.
I was lucky to connect with a Mexican film director, Lorenzo Haggerman, who also has a news agency as well and he told me: just these days we've been shooting with her in the sierra.
So a team went out to shoot the music video with Lorena, they spent three days in the Sierra del Cobre, in the Tarahumara.
Also, it's dangerouns because nowadays it's a drug trafficking area.
They were there three days shooting there.
I couldn't go because I was in Madrid in that moment shooting with Fran Torres, an Argentine filmmaker.
I was shooting my part of the music video but I wanted to say hi to Lorena because the truth is I was very moved by the fact we had found her and that we could take part in this so I sent her this video through WhatsApp that I made sure it got to her in the Sierra, where you see me...
Dear Lorena, delighted to speak with you.
I'm Jorge Drexler, the singer and writer of the song in which you're so marvelously taking part too.
The images Lorenzo has been sending me are of a beauty that has has really touched me.
I wanted to tell you this, since I cannot do it in person, at least by recording this video and thank you because it's of a degree of beauty to see you run with your green dress in that scenery, that is has deeply touched me and the most beautiful thing that could've happened to my song.
I send you a big kiss and hope we meet in person.

Bye. From Madrid.
(Aplausos) Well, thank you.
And Lorena sent me this message, and the survival strategy of the Rarámuri or the the Tarahumara is so effective, that she sents me in the Rarámuri language because they don't speak Spanish, they're truly -- they escaped, they very deep inside the sierra.
It's very hard to get to where she lives.
And she sends me this message that her brother then translates into Spanish, and I found it so nice that I want to share it with you.
(Rarámuri) Hi Jorge.
I don't know you, and I hope
to see you here someday
in Guachochi, here in Chihuahua, so we could run together.
-- You and me?
You and me.
