(Applause)
Good afternoon!
I would like to start by telling you the basis of today's talk. And this is that we all have something in common. All of us sitting here, all of the previous conference speakers, is that we all have screwed up.
We all have made mistakes.
Because there are as many mistakes as there are human beings on this planet.
In fact, if there is anything separates us, human beings, from deities, from gods, from God, it is that, on one hand we, all the mortals, at one point will fall into the grave when the time comes for us, and, on the other hand, we all make mistakes.
In fact, the walls of this auditorium, of this place, breathe mistakes.
They were designed to contain within people who had made deliberate mistakes, or perhaps accidental mistakes, but mistakes anyway.
Understanding this, I want to tell you that I have indeed mucked up in my lifetime.
I have mucked up at home, at work, sorry Minister for confessing this, but I have made mistakes in the corner, in the car, everywhere.
But there were three mistakes which perhaps marked a redirection or a refocus that I wanted for my life, and these three things are what I want to share today.
Mistake number 1: when someone is talking with a friend and the topic is so boring, that the friend turns around at that point and says, "This is great, you should write a book about it."
So I took the advice
and wrote a book.
The book was called The ABC of the Referendum: A non-legal analysis of Law 796 of 2003 by which a referendum is summoned and the people are called to consider a constitutional reform. A very appealing subject, by the way
What I was trying to do with this book was to bring an intention to life which I've always had, and that is: how to link together the public with the private.
How to make citizens work to build a State?
Because I couldn't imagine, and still can't, how there can exist a society in which the people don't care about the State, those who make the decisions about their lives, for God sake.
Obviously, the title of the book foresaw the overwhelming success that it had.
When the book was release
we sold 60 copies.
No, no. 60 in total, sorry.
I mean, 60 copies from which one was bought by a lady who keeps it fondly under a wobbly table giving the book great purpose.
The book had a purpose.
But, where is the mistake?
Why are people not interested in reading this book?
Of course, the main mistake was in writing a book about the referendum, which is totally a boring topic, but it was also in trying to impose ideas.
This was a law that passed through the Congress government at that time, and the ideas were other peoples', they were far from the ideas of those who eventually took the decision in their own hands.
They did not come from a joint decision.
So analysing that, I, at the beginning, perhaps in a feeling of rejection, did not believe that the fault had been my own.
I said that people weren't interested in participating in the public, and that's why they didn't buy the book.
I didn't realise that the first step in this virtuous staircase that we can all must climb if we want to turn mistakes into opportunities is precisely accepting and recognising that we have made a mistake.
And at that time I didn't want to do that.
Mistake number 2: Some years ago, for some time now, there has been an explosion of websites in which entrepreneurial, successful young people with a lot of money would simply invest their money in to have constant income and that allow them to retire.
Well, I was neither successful nor entrepreneurial, and I didn't have a lot of income either, but I decided to invest all our savings
in a website called yogobierno.org, where upon entering the site, users would be asked very simple questions, like whether they would rather invest in tertiary roads to connect towns, or in primary roads to interconnect the different neighborhoods, or whether they'd preferred to lower taxes to allow the free market to function or even whether to impose restrictions on the importation of products. Well, of course this matched my intention, my caprice, my convictions, that people have to get into building out the public sector. We have to link together
the public with the private.
The success of the website was almost as emphatic as the book's.
60 people visited, none of them came back.
Well, except one lady who asked me for another copy of the referendum because she had a crooked sofa -- So again, where was the mistake?
Why didn't anybody visit the website?
And if you ask my wife, to whom I am grateful for having sponsored me during these, and many other moments of madness, she'll say the mistake was to invest all our resources in a website.
But beyond that, it was that the ideas were imposed.
What we wanted was to present a disjunctive to people whose ideas never had been challenged.
And then not only did I recognise that I needed to accept the mistake, like I had done with the book, but that I also had to learn from them.
I had accepted the book mistake, but I hadn't learned absolutely anything.
And this was the second step that had to be taken.
It is necessary to learn from our mistakes.
Third and final mistake: I went to work for the national government.
Well, that doesn't sound too good.
(Laughs) Sorry again Minister.
In fact, the mistake was them trusting me, I was 27 years old, the task of creating a Bill for victims, about a recognition and reparation towards them and restitution of lands. Once we achieved to pass it through the Congress and approved it, we decided to socialized the law,
this law that we considered historic.
And of course I was extremely excited.
For the first time I was going to be able to interact with communities, understand what their needs were, extract ideas to create public policies, public policies as well as reparation,
And surprise! When we visited each auditorium, there wasn't a soul.
Not even my book buddy accompanied me in a single one of these forums.
And why?
Where, again, was the mistake?
We were spreading the message of a law that had already been passed.
We were institutionalizing other peoples' ideas.
We hadn't linked up with the people in the collective construction needed for this kind of politics, for this kind of norms.
And then I understood that the third and final step, after accepting the mistake, after learning from the it, was, of course, to act.
Act in accordance with all that learning that I had because of my previews mistakes.
Because I accepted my mistakes, because I learned from them, and, specially, because I wanted to act, was why a couple of months ago I resigned from the Vice Ministry of Justice.
And I resigned because I understood that if I wanted to start to talk about the private and the public, I couldn't do it once again from the public sector.
Now I find myself perfecting a tool that allows people to put forward their ideas to have a better city and also I'm insisting on creating some spaces for dialogue, from the local, from towns, to build policies in regards to a potential post-conflict.
And one find itself with mistakes that are absolutely common and that I´m pretty sure that you also found.
We all have a friend who has once said: "Why would I vote, why would I take part if all politicians are corrupt, anyway.
Politician equals thief."
Of course, now it´s all fault of politicians you did´t buy the book.
We also all have a friend who at one point has told us: "Look, I'm interested, but politics and me don't go together."
Of course, it means that the ideas that you put forward on your websites don´t apply because of other people.
And we all know people who take part, maybe to a minimum, but who say that they don't follow up because it is a lost cause.
Of course, the laws of their lives had already been passed and were unchangeable.
You see, Colombia is passing through an absolutely special situation,
a unique situation,
where for the first time we will be able to take the variable of armed conflict out of our lives.
It's not going to be the solution to all of our problems. We are going to continue having a whole range of problems, but at least we will be able to take the problem of armed conflict out of this range.
And if we want to really end this disconnection that has created so much violence, this distrust in institutions which has created so many problems across the whole nation, we have to learn from our mistakes, we have to recognise learning from them and act according with this learning.
It´s absolutely essential that we come up with a form of action that can be from the most simple to the most complex.
I´m not saying that all of you have to be applicants for some corporation.
What I´m saying is that there can be a mechanism of participation, of linking together the public, like
respecting the norms here in prison.
Another example would be in a city, were you can´t increase the music volume after a given hour.
Or it can be through a slightly more complex participation, where we demand others to respect norms.
Not only we are respecting the norms, but we are asking others to.
Imagine how good it would be to read in the media that there are not more bribes to civil servants.
Or even more complex: demand, for example, to jail directors to allow us participate in the design of programs where jail budget will be use.
Or in a city which allow us
to be involved in public spending.
If we want a bridge, or rooves for the TransMilenio station.
Finally, we have to recognise that this disconnect with the State has brought us many problems, learn from them and act.
And that is the invitation that I want to give you today.
Because if we act according with this learning process, if the whole country wants their voice to be heard, if citizens start to demand that there should be more constructions through collective intelligence, which is much more productive and valuable than individual intelligence; if we start being the true protagonists of our own story, this is the only way that we will be able to guarantee that we take violence and armed conflict out of this prison, out of this town, and out of this country forever.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Good afternoon.
Society is sexist, and so is medicine.
Firstly: What do women die of?
When someone asks what women die of, people, men and women, usually reply, "of gynecological conditions: breast cervix, ovarian cancer ..." That is not the case:
women die of heart attack.
From 2007 until now, the first cause of death in women was acute myocardial infarction.
Society still thinks that it's men

who die of heart conditions.
It is not the case. It has changed. Why?
Two reasons.
Firstly, women, nowadays, have the same chores they used to in the past: working at home, the kids, family, well-being, school, everything.
In addition, working, studying ...
They have a double whammy, much more than men.
That's what is called: "feminine stress."
Secondly: women smoke.
Tobacco companies were successful.
Women develop atherosclerosis more rapidly.
And both causes, feminine stress and smoking, determine why women have more heart attacks than men do.
A second point: cardiac pain.
The cardiac pain of an acute myocardial infarction, it's a typical intense chest pain, retrosternal, in the breastbone which is in the middle of the thorax, where "a tie" normally sits, as if it was a masculine pain, intense, oppressive, irradiating, it travels to other places, neck, left shoulder, right shoulder, left forearm, these two finger, stomach, back, accompanied by nausea, vomit and a general ill feeling, a feeling of arterial hypotension, perspiration.
That is a typical pain ... for men.
Women feel a different kind of pain.
"Doctor, my chest hurts a bit but it goes."
"Sometimes I sweat. I go up
the stairs and I have tachycardia."
"I feel nervous, my chest hurts."
So the doctors on night-watch give her a tranquilizer because, "it might be nerves."
"Yesterday it hurt, doctor."
"So what happened?"
"No, it hurt but I went to pick up the kids at school and it went."
Not long after, sudden death, acute myocardial infarction.
Why?
Because feminine pain is different
from male pain. Two.
Three: "Broken heart syndrome." "Broken Heart," "Idiopathic apical dyskinesia" "Stress cardiomyopathy" or "Takotsubo Syndrome."
What is it?
It's a patient who checks into hospital, goes to the intensive care unit, or the coronary unit, and shows a typical clinical picture of acute myocardial infarction, a typical electrocardiogram of a myocardial infarction, blood test results typical of an acute myocardial infarction, additional tests results like an acute myocardial infarction, and many times in addition, a clinical picture of heart failure, and it has a mortality rate of 5 to 7 percent.
If we study the coronary arteries, with a coronary angiography we can see they are healthy.
It was not a heart attack,
it was a sudden loss of heart function, with a sudden depletion.
95 percent of these patients are women.
When we ask further questions, we find out that a week earlier, or weeks or even months earlier, they suffered a strong emotional pain, like anticipating the cardiac pain.
"Broken heart disease."
The death of a relative, a son, a divorce, a heartbreak ...
95 percent of these patients are women.
I am going to show you a picture of me.
It's worth it as it was taken 59 years ago.
But that's just an excuse to show a picture of her, my mum, who gave me life, brought me up,
educated me; she died a month ago. One day she said: "Dani, never disrespect a woman."
Thanks.
