We all like to learn things.
Some of us like to learn a language, others learn to play musical instruments and others a new sport.
And there are things nobody likes to learn, but we would love to know. For example, I would love to know how to play the guitar, but I don't want to take any lessons.
Maybe learning Chinese, just because.
Frankly, who wouldn't like to learn anything they wanted with the least possible effort?
I need to curb your enthusiasm a bit, because I don't have a magic recipe.
However, what we know about the brain is changing at an incredible speed.
We still lack enough to be able to learn the keys to learning, but today I want to tell you how are we progressing in this regard.
I studied electronic engineering and what interested me most, was the area of ​​artificial intelligence and how to create smart machines that can learn things by themselves.
Then I became interested in how humans, and animals learn.
There I went to study neurosciences and I learned to measure neuronal activity and train laboratory animals: mice, monkeys and students.
(Laughter) Now, the task of a neuroscientist is not that simple.
In fact, the brain is the most complex and intelligent device that we know.
It's composed of 80 billion neurons, like one of these.
To make it worse, each neuron receives on average, up to 10,000 connections from other neurons.
This is as if each one of you could listen at the same time and be influenced by all the people present today at TEDxRíodelaPlata.
When we learn something, our brains change and they change physically.
Although you don't realize it, your brain is changing right now.
What does this mean?
It means that our brain, generates new connections as we learn.
There are neurons that start to connect with other neurons.
And some of these connections get stronger or become weaker.
How can we study what happens in the brain when we learn something new?
Well, we need a way to be able to measure neuronal activity very precisely.
But the most difficult is that we have to keep track of exactly the same neurons every day throughout the learning process.
This is what we couldn't do until some time ago.
A new technology allows us to do this, it's called two-photon microscopy and it has opened our scope so that we can study what happens in the brain when it learns.
In this video, I am showing you neurons responding.
These spikes are active neurons.
Those flashes of light, when they are more intense, indicate a more active neuron, responding better in every turn.
A lot of my work as a neuroscientist is about trying to relate the intensity of each neuron with the behavior of an animal.
These microscopes allow us to follow these same neurons I'm showing here for weeks.
The same, the same.
This opened the door to studying what happens in a brain for the first time.
And how can we do this?
How can we study how an animal learns?
Well, we have to get a lab animal and teach them something they didn't know while, at the same time, we measure their neurons to see how they change as they learn.
Well, I'm going to tell you how we did this in the laboratory.
We take a mouse and we teach them to detect the position of an object using their whiskers.
You might ask: why the whiskers?
Well, mice are night animals and, besides, they don't see very well, so they use whiskers to navigate their surroundings.
For them, whiskers are like our own fingers.
Here in the video I'm going to show you how a mouse solves this task.
That little black stick you see is the object that they have to detect.
And the mouse starts moving the whiskers like crazy trying to locate it.
The object may appear in different positions.
Now, what we need is the mouse to tell us where the object was.
How could we teach them that?
Well, we teach them to use their tongue to tell us if they had detected it or not.
The experiment was like this: the object could be in two positions, near the face or away from the face.
If it was close, they had to stick their tongue out and if it was far away, they had to keep their tongue inside.
And all this was solved in complete darkness.
The craziest of all is that we didn't give the mouse any particular instruction.
The only thing we gave him is a reward if it was done correctly.
And little by little, the mouse could learn.
Now, when we started the task, the mouse didn't understand a thing.
So they tried to do anything
to see if they nailed it. At first, they moved their whiskers like crazy to find out if the stick was there, it didn't care about anything else.
Then they got tired and stopped responding for the rest of the day, There was no way to motivate them.
And sometimes, when they were motivated, they'd regain their motivation and play hard and stick their tongues out all the time to see if they could win a reward.
(Laughter) Meanwhile we'd measure the neurons, and we'd see exactly the same: the neurons in total chaos, very hard to understand what they were doing.
But as we the animal was learning, the behavior would become increasingly consistent.
They waited for the object until it was positioned, and just then they moved their whiskers and looked for it very carefully.
Once they detected it, just then, they gave us their answer.
And the neurons also began to behave much more orderly.
The task was divided.
There were neurons that learned to respond only when they moved their whiskers.
Another group that responded when they made contact with the object. And another ones that spiked
when the mouse stuck out its tongue.
What did we learn from this experiment?
We learned that learning is a cooperative task at neuronal level.
It requires many neurons to do exactly the same.
Because one day they can disappear and stop responding.
That neurons do exactly the same, that many do exactly the same, makes learning robust and not so dependent on individual neurons.
If a neuron dies, there is another that can come to the rescue.
Also, what we discovered is that, although learning is very flexible and the neurons are changing as we learn, this flexibility has limits.
What does this mean?
It means that in the brain there is a built-in structure that, sometimes, can't change.
For example, the neuron that responded to the movement of the whiskers always did exactly the same thing: no matter the extra learning, it would never happened to be a neuron that responded to the contact with the object.
Each neuron respected its own task and their own union.
Now, we already know how it works one brain, approximately when the mouse knows something.
We are closer to considering the greater challenge: Could we take a new mouse and artificially modify its neuronal activity in a way that when we give them a task, they already know how to do it?
That is, can we sculpt neuronal activity while the mouse is chilled and relaxed, and that by the first time we give them a task they already knows how to perform?
Well, this is still pretty ambitious, but we are on the way to achieve it.
In the lab we set out to do something a little simpler: Can we grab a neuron, a single neuron of the entire brain and teach it to do something we want?
I'm going to tell you what we did.
We gave the mouse a signal, that time was running and what they had to do was to increase the activity of that neuron to a target and if they did they got a reward.
And how did we do this?
Well, what we did, and here you can see it in the green line, is to teach the mouse that this neuron had to increase that activity until it reaches the goal, that peak they see there, and there they got a reward.
To help the neuron, we gave they a sound.
A lower pitch sound if they were far from the target and a higher pitch sound if they were closer.
In the same way the mouse did with their whiskers in the beginning, the mouse didn't know what to do to change neuronal activity.
By insisting to make the link between the goal -- to increase neuronal activity -- and the reward, the neuron learned to respond faster and faster.
Even though we took a single neuron, this is the first step so that one day we can incorporate artificial knowledge in brains.
Also, what we learned from this experiment is that for the neuron to learn, the only thing we had to give them feedback information of their state, tell it how far it was from the goal and give it a reward when it succeeded.
Basically, to teach this neuron, we didn't do anything different to what we do when we learn something: we practice a lot, someone tell us if things are right or wrong and we receive, eventually, a reward if we do it well.
I hope I have convinced you, for now, that the brain is enormously flexible and that our neural circuits can change at any time.
Thanks to this, we can learn to use a lot of tools almost as if they were extensions of our body.
If you know how to play the guitar it's because in your brain there are circuits that know what movements the fingers do to make each note.
For your brain, after all, your hand is no different than a tool.
Now, if for your brain is not so different to learn to control your arm than controlling a tool, does it mean that we could add new limbs?
For example, could we add a module, a chip, that allows us to control a robotic arm?
Or even crazier, something that adds more learning capacity, more memory, that comes pre-loaded with languages -- Chinese in my case -- or help us do mental calculations much faster?
Although it looks like science fiction, these men and women cyborgs, are already among us.
They are beginning to do neuronal implants in paraplegic patients so that patients can control a robotic arm with their own brain and gain independence.
These implants measure the activity of a few neurons, decode that information and control the joints of robotic arms.
Just like before, in the beginning the brain doesn't know how to control it, but by effort and practice, trial and error, it can learn.
What you see here is a patient with one of these implants: that little box above their head.
With her own brain she is controlling that robotic arm.
Thanks to the huge brain flexibility, she has managed to change her neural circuits in a way to control the robotic arm efficiently.
With a lot of practice, movements become more natural and they begin to be part of her own body.
And, for the first time, in many years, the patient can help herself a drink.
(Applause) The final smile of the patient reminds us of how nice is to learn something new for the first time.
To achieve something considered impossible before.
We live something like this, perhaps, when we are babies and we get to say our first words and we manage to walk; as children when we learn to write and, perhaps, to play soccer; at twenty-something when we study a career, or a profession.
Sometimes as an adult we don't have as much patience or time to learn but if you have the curiosity and you want to learn something new, remember how nice it is and don't doubt that your brain is prepared for that.
Because, after all, if a neuron can learn, aren't 80 billion also able to?
Thank you!
(Applause)
90% of data today has been created in the last two years.
What does this mean?
The world is changing very fast at an exponential speed and it also means that most of this data has been born and grown, communicated and understood, in this digital environment.
The question is: is this digital environment the best for understanding this data?
Because today, when it comes to data we think: abstract mathematics, programming, statistics, however, data talks about people and human nature.
Now I want you to imagine this reality so close to you.
This moment; a son, a sister, a friend.
How this reality can change our life.
Humans are capable of turning these emotional realities into numbers.
In fact, since we're born we are numbers.
And this ability is used to understand more abstract and complex realities bigger, quantitative realities.
We are able to turn all of these realities into numbers, symbols, data that help us to understand better.
But, the queston is: do they help us to feel better?
This is the perception distance, the distance that exists between reality: a birth, and the reality we perceive: a number.
Two years ago we founded Domestic Data Streamers, a multidisciplinary team that works in the margins between disciplines
between science, technology, art and design explaining these realities to bring them closer. Here is an example of an installation we did a year and a half ago
at the Design Hub, the current design museum in Barcelona. 1200 people were asked two questions seemingly easy but hard to answer: how old are you? and
until what age would you like to live?
This would be the data base where we collected all of this data and converted it into an ordered data base with thousands of answers.
This would be the raw data, raw information. The next step is to turn this data into information, into something that can be read and understood.
Here you see an infographic where this is represented
there are two axis: a horizontal one that talks about the real age and a vertical one that expresses the ideal age to die.
As you can see children have their reality to live until they're 120 or 140 years old and when you grow up, one is aware of health issues and it balances a little bit until it reaches the curve of old age and then you start wishing for more years.
There is a small cluster of people in their 20's who want to die at 27 like Jimi Hendrix, Amy Winehouse, the 27 club that has created a real trend.
Without going into the data, what is most important in what is being investigated most, is how an era full of information can be turned into knowledge.
Theoreticians, scientists designers, communicators have been working for more than 300 years developping tools to explain these complex realities easier
by using typography, design interaction, digital media and we believe the next step aren't infographics, but infoexperiences.
The next video is an example of this data in an infoexperience.
(Music) [Lifeline] (Video) Narrator: In life, there are things which we are able to decide and others which we can't.
Death is one of those things that plays between these two possibilities
where one can decide when to die but not for how long they will live for.
800 balloons mark the point between one's real age and the age at which they would like to die in contrast with their gender.
The white balloons are the coordinates of those who don't know at what age they wan't to die.
Whereas the black balloons are the coordinates of those who do know at what age they want to die.
A volatile and ephemeral piece that questions our desire to live and an irrefutable end of a journey
or in other words a lifeline.
(Applause) Paul Garcia: With this experiment two things were proved. The first was that changing how we showed this data changed the way in which it was going to be read.
From reading individually on telephones, computers, newspapers, to a collective reading, in a physical space, we weren't only creating a space for representation but a space for conversation where people could compare their data with other people's data generating new conversations, new discussions.
The second important point was to break this monologue of information.
Almost all of data visualization is designed specifically to generate an impact on the public.
Here, what was interesting was that it was the public that generated an impact on the visualization.
It was people's answers that were changing the data, the information shown.
They were able to see themselves reflected in the data.
All of this was thanks to the context, because we see more each day that we lack the context of information and how we talk about it.
We see the same representations for weekend temperatures in bar graphs, as deaths in Iraq over the past 10 years in the same way.
How do we want to highlight, and give it the importance and value the data it has if we are showing it in the same way, with the same form of representation?
How do we want people to dedicate their time if we are showing it in the same way?
Time has to do with the next installation called Sand Falls, an installation that talks about time and that wanted to answer the question of how can art be quantified, the impact an artwork has.
Obviously it is subjective, as is its impact
but, what can be quantified is the interest generated the interest through time.
The experiment works in the following way: there was an exhibition: Big Bang Data in the Centre for Contemporary Culture of Barcelona
where sensors were placed all around the exhibition.
What each sensor was doing was calculating the time each visitor spent in front of each piece, of each of the five works that were being analysed. The second challenge was to convert something so abstract like time, something so subjective like art into something physical, and we used the most ancient metaphor that exists: sand.
We built five sand clocks that spoke in real time about the interest and impact that these five art works had on the visitors
once again what was interesting wasn't the visualization in itself, but the impact it had on people.
At the end of the exhibition, and people saw the visualization and they understood the data and that it was their own data what it generated was motivation to go back and see the works again to read them on a different layer of information.
How visualization that makes an impact is able to change human behaviour.
Today, when we talk about Data, Big Data and words that have come up in today's conferences
we talk about the resources and magnificent talent that is put into investigation in knowing what we can take from all of this.
However, there is another challenge which is to know how we can explain these findings, how we can explain what is really important.
From literature to art to film and television to even the most important contemporary events all of them have become history not because of what they have been but because of how they were told, because of the transcendence the channel being used has.
Five years ago I had the opportunity to go to Birkenau, in Auschwitz, and see the concentration camp where I had my first infoexperience something I will never be able to repeat. In one of the pavilions they keep the hair of the thousands of victims.
The nazis kept them to make milions of military uniforms and when the allies arrived they left it as proof of what had happened
The museum decided to keep it and when you are faced with this reality, with this gigantic room with these material numbers you forget about the history books all of the lessons, numbers everything you once knew about that reality.
This trascends much more.
The experience trascends much more.
And we don't have to be so dramatic, we can see this in nature.
As a child when I went to my town, I passed by the same reservoir called Pantá de Sau here in Cataluña.
It has something particular: you can read time through the reservoir. Normally when you think of reading the time you think of these visualizations something like a map colours, shapes that talk about rain, temperature.
But what made me look through the window every time I passed the reservoir, was that time had become physical it was a church in the middle of the reservoir and depending on the season depending on the rain you could see it emerge and submerge.
How a physical, real and natural infographic was much more capable of transmitting and explaining that reality to a 4-5 year old boy.
Again this trascended much more because of the channel being used.
In the last year we have been able to meet people that use these methods
in this case we met a Veteran of War on the beach in Santa Barbara and he told us that for 10 years he had been in charge of the Costs of War organization that basically explains war costs in the USA and what one could do with all of the money in education, health, culture, etc. And for nine years they have pushed to be in media, on television to generate a real impact. But it wasn't until last year when they did these installations on the beaches of South California very touristic places, where no one expected to see this data where suddenly they generated a gigantic impact all of a sudden they were on the news, on TV on loads of blogs on the web, in an incredible way
and they were explaining the same information from the past 9 years. The change of a channel was once again the most essential.
Today, when talking about the representation of data of visualization we have to be very aware that today we have the obligation of exploring these new channels, channels capable of explaining what exists behind a number, behind a piece of data, the most human and emotional part. Because it will be when we really understand what the pieces of data mean, that they will begin to matter to us.
Thank you very much. (Applause)
Are you happier now than what your parents were at your age?
In the 70s, a researcher revealed an utterly surprising fact.
Since the 40s until then, the purchasing power of North Americans had increased by no less than 60 percent.
And however, their average happiness had hardly changed at all.
In 2011, the researcher repeated the same study in other countries.
And the results... Were the same.
In the long run, higher income doesn't result in happier individuals.
Maybe some of you are thinking that this is simply another reason to confirm that money doesn't buy happiness.
But let's think about this.
Shouldn't we simply be just a little bit happier from the simple fact that greater purchasing power grants access to better leisure opportunities, more cultural and educational resources, better health benefits?
Ultimately, does progress make us happier or doesn't it?
One day I was visiting a country with much fewer resources then our own and I came across this kid who out of some old pieces of string and a couple of cushions had slapped together a swing and was enjoying it so much that I felt jealous.
With so little, that kid was happier than what I am many days of my daily life.
Much later, back in our country I encountered a much different scenario.
Imagine: a kid, sprawled on the floor in the main aisle of a department store -- quite well-known, mind you -- crying, screaming and stomping, you could see the whites of his eyes, on the verge of frothing at the mouth.
His head was spinning like the girl in The Exorcist, all because amidst so much abundance his parents had refused to buy him any more candy.
And I ask myself: In this country and in many others where we declare that progress exists, is it plausible for a teenage girl to give her mom the silent treatment for an entire afternoon just because the hotel they're at has no Wi-Fi?
I think it is.
So then, what good is progress if it fails to make us happier?
One of the most interesting characteristics of human beings is that we get used to what happens to us.
It's what happens, for instance, when we use cologne and after a while we no longer smell it.
And it's not because the effect has expired, but because our sense of smell gets used to it.
Some people don't realise this and continue spraying. (Laughter) The truth is that, growing accustomed to what happens to us is interesting in itself because we can, for example, go through stages in life where one must experience pain or simply go through unpleasant or hard experiences.
The problem with human beings is that we also grow accustomed to the good things that happen.
And this effect, already described in the last century, is known as the "treadmill effect."
And what it refers to, more or less, in relation to our happiness levels, though we may appear to be moving forward, in reality, we're not moving at all.
This is what happens, for example, when we're given a raise.
Well, in this country we have to think long and hard to remember when this used to happen.
But let's make that effort.
What happens to someone when they get a raise?
The first month they're ecstatic, the second month as well, but perhaps not as much.
Third, fourth, fifth. By the sixth month the effect has vanished entirely.
And this happens with everything, right?
Fascinating people we meet, amazing movies we see, when we buy a new car, purse, shoes...
Everything is fascinating to begin with, it's super-exciting.
But, after a while, we get used to it.
So it's possible that this is what happens with progress.
They are many changes, novelties, so many innovations, but in the end we get used to them and, although everything changes, things remain almost the same.
So if this is the case, then what can we do to increase our level of happiness?
What can we do in order for progress to really make us happier?
The first thing we need, obviously, is to define what happiness is.
What is happiness?
Fortunately, science has already provided us with a rather functional definition of this complex phenomenon.
Happiness is "the frequency of positive emotions in our lives."
"The frequency of positive emotions that exist in our lives."
What does this mean?
Quite simple.
Over the Christmas holidays I had a good time, and so I was happy, and the following summer I also had a great time and was happy.
What usually happens? We feel pretty good on holiday.
But the rest of the months, we led a somewhat dull life, with a somewhat dull boss, and somewhat dull friends.
We're actually unable to say if we were happy during a certain period of our lives because it's not emotional intensity what counts toward happiness but rather the frequency of positive
emotions in our life. That's happiness.
Alright then.
So then, what is it that causes us to have these positive emotions?
Well, science tells us there are three influencing factors,
the first of which, believe it or not, is genetic, and is responsible for no less than half of our total happiness.
Believe it or not, some people were born to be happier than others.
Think about those people you come across in the street.
Suddenly you cross paths with someone who's probably thinking about their grocery list, but they're wide-eyed, beaming, a radiant look.
And later, on the other hand, you cross paths with someone who's also thinking about their grocery list, but is also utterly bitter.
Some people are naturally happier than others.
The other two factors that affect happiness are intentional activity, that is to say, what we do in order to be happy, and our external circumstances.
Think about this for a moment.
Which of these two factors do you think is important?
Intentional activity, what we do in order to be happy, or our external circumstances?
Well, science says that intentional activity is no less than four times more important than external circumstances when it comes to happiness.
So there's actually a lot we can do to feel happy.
Fortunately, the talk doesn't end here.
So then, what can we do to be happy?
First of all, by simply observing the mind.
My hypothesis is that happiness depends on our awareness.
Awareness is the canvas upon which thoughts arise, the feelings and emotions that we have throughout the day.
Awareness is needed for many things.
Recently we carried out an experiment at the university that yielded utterly surprising results.
We divided a group of students in two and to each subgroup we asked that they copy down some quotes onto a sheet of paper, quite simply.
The difference that the first group was given what we call those "motivational" quotes.
Quotes such as: "I've failed over and over again in my life and that is why I succeed."
This quote is attributed to Michael Jordan.
The students in the second group had to copy down quotes like this: "A labour shortage has hindered the strawberry harvest in Huelva."
(Laughter) What were our observations?
That students of the first group, after having been subjected to a test of willpower, showed a substantial statistical increase in willpower simply because we altered the content of their awareness for a few minutes.
As far as I know, this is the first experiment that proves that motivational quotes actually work.
So now you know.
Awareness is important for many things.
Also for happiness.
They say that, many years ago, in a faraway country there was a man who couldn't sleep and was tossing and turning in bed.
His wife was beside him and said: "What's wrong? Why don't you sleep?"
And the man admitted that the next day he would have to pay off a debt of seven silver coins to his neighbour, but he didn't have them.
And that's what kept him awake.
His wife, resolute, hopped out of bed, put on a robe, went outside, stood under the neighbour's window and began to call for him loudly, and before long the neighbour emerged rubbing his tired eyes and said: "What is it, what is it, what is it? Who calls?
And the woman said: "Know that tomorrow you shan't get your seven silver coins because my husband doesn't have them."
That being said, she went home, removed her robe, went back to bed, and told her husband: "You can stop worrying because it's the neighbour's problem now."
(Laughter) Look... Each day thousands of thoughts arise in our awareness.
So one thing we can do in order to figure out our level of happiness is to calculate how many thoughts produce positive emotions over a length of time.
How many positive emotions do we have each day, each week, each month?
If you're calculating on a yearly basis, something's not right.
You need to realise that if happiness is the frequency of positive emotions and emotions depend on thoughts that arise in our awareness, that proves that happiness does depend on consciousness.
A completely overwhelming experiment was carried out on Facebook with no less than 700,000 users.
The following was observed: If they artificially increased the frequency of positive messages on user's walls, they in turn published more positive messages.
So just because user's awareness were artificially manipulated, they also became more positive.
Obviously, this isn't as easy as getting up in the morning, looking yourself in the mirror and saying: "I'm so handsome, what a nice day, I'm feeling so happy!"
It's not that easy.
But it's true that deliberately trying to introduce into our awareness reasonably optimistic and positive thoughts can increase our happiness levels.
At the very least we shouldn't reject those positive thoughts and emotions as we often do.
How often do we encounter the following scene?
A group of friends meets up at night for a drink, a girl from the group arrives later and someone remarks: "What a lovely dress! You look so pretty!"
The girl looks at her dress and says: "Oh, this dress? It's really old.
I got it on sale."
I don't see how both things relate.
(Laughter) If you're pretty, then you're pretty.
Where does this tendency to reject compliments and good things that happen in general come from?
Where does it come form?
Ask anyone about their defects and their virtues and they'll list all their flaws.
But virtues are always tricky.
Why is this so?
Where does this come from?
Simply being in good health and having someone who loves us should suffice in order to be immensely happy.
For me, one of the greatest truths about happiness is that appreciating the good things that happen to us is psychologically opposed to getting used to them.
Learning to appreciate the good things that happen is psychologically opposed to getting used to them.
What does this mean?
That the best antidote to the treadmill effect is to get used to appreciating the good things that happen to us.
Because are we or are we not happy when we celebrate our successes?
We're happier when we remember the good times, when we imagine better futures and, of course, we're happier when we learn to recognise and appreciate the other people that accompany us in life and whose affection we so often take for granted.
There's an unfortunately little-known, beautiful poem by Mary Jean Irion that begins with this verse I'd like to share with all of you.
"Normal day [sic], let me be aware of the treasure you are." "Normal day [sic], let me be aware
of the treasure you are."
To my knowledge, no one has expressed more beautifully or concisely the importance of bringing into our conscious thoughts our appreciation for those good people and, of course, the good things we have in life.
"Normal day [sic], let me be aware of the treasure you are." But the Facebook experiment
also taught us something else.
That happiness is contagious.
Users were influenced by positive messages that they read on their walls.
Mathematical models have been conducted on thousands of people to prove that throughout social connections happy individuals group together,
and that sad people group together also.
Take a look at this graph: the yellow people are happy, the blue people are sad, and the green people can't seem to make up their minds, apparently.
In this graph and others like it, we can observe beautiful clusters of happiness.
We can also take a peek at the helplessly bitter folk.
We can also see happy people and in their immediate social circle, many other happy people that are sort of blocked by sad bridges that stop them from reaching the other side.
(Laughter) And, evidently, we also see people who, whatever science may say, insist on feeling bitter despite being surrounded by happiness.
Take a look at the graph and ask yourself: "Where would I be?"
Where are you?
Are you people that live surrounded by happiness?
My most sincere congratulations.
Are you surrounded by
bitter people? Stop calling them.
And see how much better off you are.
(Laughter) I'd like to end with a final observation.
We often see people who have been successful in life and say: "Sure, if I'd been successful, I'd be happier".
We think a better job, a better boss, relationship will make us happier, don't we?
Well then, today science has demonstrated categorically that it's not success that leads to happiness, but happiness what leads to success.
Think about it for a moment.
Who would you rather hire? A happy person or a sad one?
Who would you fall in love with? Someone happy or bitter?
Well, I've seen all kinds of things, actually.
(Laughter) But I think we get the picture that those people who are happy show behaviours that bring them closer to their life objectives. At the start of the talk
I asked you: "Are you happier that your parents were at your age?"
Some of you might have thought so, others probably not.
Does progress serve to make use happier?
Mi answer to this question is "that depends, certainly".
Today we've learned that intentional activity, what we do in order to be happy is four times more important than external circumstances in regards to our happiness.
And progress is precisely those circumstances.
So reach your own conclusions.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Every day I have two, three and up to five meetings with people from different paths of life: judges, drug dealers, politicians, businessmen and the gossip in you neighborhood.
Yes, that gossip talks with me.
That's my job, because I look for information.
But of a very specific kind.
Information on white-collar criminals.
The experts in corruption, money laundering, drug trafficking, corporate fraud.
The so-called white-collar criminals who dress like this.
They don't dress as seen on TV.
Every day, for this reason, I have to meet with my editors because we have to cross information and try to tell facts from versions.
And this is quite more difficult than meets the eye.
Because, trust me: reality in this country exceeds fiction!
In this parallel dimension is possible that one of the biggest lobbyists in our judicial system for the last 25 years welcomes his guests, whether to talk about one government or the other, in his BBQ lodge, surrounded by two posters, a crystal box and a "persuader".
Poster number one: Al Pacino in Scarface.
Poster number two: Marlon Brando in The Godfather.
Crystal box with a golden cellphone inside --remember the old ones with a lid?-- When you ask him, he says: "That was the phone I would only use with the number one.
The day the number one died, I decided that this phone was never to be touched again."
And the "persuader" is a 120-centimeter-long aluminum bat that he himself notes that it is there just in case any of his guests crosses the line, they can be put back on track.
This gentleman is just one among the many who walk down Argentina's underworld.
I work in this underworld.
There, in fact, --let's make clear that this man is not a big fish-- in this underworld decisions are made on some of the most delicate and crucial matters of the institutional life of Argentina in the last 25 or 30 years.
And it's there where some of the most uncomfortable secrets of power are kept.
I'll tell you one: In Argentina, if you or anyone want to run for president,
you will need USD 100 million.
I say it again: yes, USD 100 million.
You think it's too much ...
If you want to run for Major in Escobar or La Plata, you need around USD 500 000 and USD 1 million.
In some cities, getting your spot for third elected candidate in the list of councilmen of a middle-size city costs USD 100 000.
Let's make it clear: everybody knows this.
It's not information hard to find.
Politicians know it, businessmen know it, the narcos know it, everybody knows.
Just as control agencies also knows this; the ones who should be controlling this.
Next year we will go through a presidential campaign. So, unless the economy or a last-minute reform in the law or the judicial investigations going on right now change this, anyone wanting to run for president or get to the Pink House will need USD 100 million.
That's why we use to define electoral funding as the original sin in politics:
You started out sinning.
You may have very good intentions and wonderful ideas, but you will end up with your feet in the mud.
What does this mean in practice?
It means that beyond the surface you may have candidates competing with each other, debating. And below the surface you may find one candidate funding another one's campaign because they eat up votes from their main opponent.
And this other, who is no fool, funds another one's campaign as well to eat up votes from their main opponent.
In the same way, another inconvenient truth of power is this: given that you need fortunes and if you comply with the law in effect you cannot raise that USD 100 million you need to ask for most of money -- which is calculated to be 90 percent -- to be dirty money.
What does this imply? That you need cash and bagmen.
On the other side, who are the ones putting the money?
First, businessmen.
Some of them we are now watching walking down the court of justice.
And as many of them cannot withdraw funds from their books, what they do is to offer payments in kind.
What does this mean?
You ask for USD 500 000,
I cannot give you that, I produce empanadas.
I offer you empanadas for all the campaign events, in all places where I have a store.
A car dealer or maker gives cars.
The one making or selling mobile phones gives the cellphones for the campaign.
This is a reality from the underworld.
You may ask: "what is this money spent on?" This, say, USD 100 million.
And on the other hand,
"Is this the only one?"
No, because you have businessmen who bid on candidates as if they were racehorses.
"I bid on the leading one, but also on the second one ...
and in case there is a surprise, I also put money in the third one.
And not only businessmen;
The police, for instance, prepares and
delivers bags with illegally raised money
to create a two-way pact:
now I know that you know.
It's mutual.
And the narcos, with each election, are getting farther and higher.
In terms of expenditures, there are two main categories we can pinpoint when we talk seriously with campaign teams with mics off.
Number one, printing paper ballots and the election controllers.
Number two, journalists and mass media.
In the world, it's calculated that you need 1.3 paper ballots per voter.
In Argentina? Five. Because they steal them.
(Laughter) With a twist: if they steal your paper ballots you have to train your election controllers.
You may think it's not much money, as they are volunteers.
True, but you have to train them.
You have to take them to the location.
They need a chief, who needs a phone.
You have to feed them, and bring them back.
Multiply this by 95,000 polling stations, which we had in 2015. There'll be more in 2019.
And on the other side, mass media and journalists.
This is one of the most expensive things.
Don't you find it strange how difficult can it be some times to talk with one candidate? Nobody can get through. And when finally that candidate accepts to give an interview, the hardest question is: Is it hot in Jujuy?
(Laughter) The most expensive interview in 2015 costed USD 42 000.
Reported by them themselves. The campaign teams.
Now, I focused on election funding and these are the customs and practices of the underworld of this area.
And this, at the same time, is part of a fabric that includes so much more.
Because it also includes, for instance, the policemen I talked about earlier with an additional tip.
Many Police Stations are bid for, they are auctioned.
The police chief who offers more money gets it.
So I bid for that Police Station to pay USD 100,000 per month.
If they accept the offer
I have to raise that money no matter how.
Where? How?
These are the police officers, and I let me be clear, in all areas there are honorable exceptions.
We cannot put everyone in the same basket.
I'm just describing a framework, a fabric.
In the same way there are journalists who don't inform,
but get paid for their silence.
Some prosecutors don't investigate, but shelve lawsuits.
Some judges let lawsuits exceed their statute of limitations.
Businessmen who don't compete but make cartels.
Union representatives who defend workers rights, while many others clearly understand that there's no better strike than the one which doesn't occur, but enables negotiations.
That's why if this is the framework we are dealing with. Me and many of my colleagues. If this is the framework we are working with, it's a mistake to just retain only those characters coming out of a B movie.
"Man throws bags of money over a nunnery wall". Or those driving Ferraris.
Because they are in fact, just the tip of an iceberg, that is way deeper, and includes what we call the "permanent power".
And I'll tell you a story.
One day, talking with a veteran, ex Secretary of Finances, he was telling me that in his first week at the Ministry he didn't even know how to use the intercom.
So to receive the bankers, he gets up to ask his assistant to bring in the waiter.
The waiter comes in, and says: "What will you order?"
Tea with lemon, he replies.
The waiter turned around and said to the bankers: "Same as always, right?"
Who is transitory; who is permanent.
Now, is this something
intrinsic to Argentina alone? No.
In fact, we have the examples
of Peru, of Italy, of Brazil in the last decades.
Which also shows us that they had problems of systemic corruption, or mega corruption, as it's usually named. And they also dealt with the aftermath.
For instance, in Italy
they had the Tangentopoli --investigated with the Mani pulite-- and they ended up with the Italian Prime Minister chasing minors in a Roman ranch.
Or we end up now in Brazil, after the Lava Jato with a far-right president elect, who advocates for dictatorship, is xenophobic and racist.
And this is one of the risks we face in Argentina.
To get out of one problem, only to get in a huge one.
This is a risk.
The other one is to fall in gatopardism.
What does this mean?
To pretend things change when they don't.
And this is a dicothomy between the real Argentina and the theoretical Argentina.
In the theoretical Argentina we have an Anti-Corruption Agency.
In real Argentina, this Agency has two offices.
One of which didn't have an Internet connection.
They used the wifi of the bar down the corner.
The day the bar was closed, there was no wifi, so there was no Internet.
All of you with your phones have more search power than that Anti-Corruption Agency office.
Example one.
Example two, the electoral justice court.
Their duty is to audit the real expenditures and income of all candidates. They have the so-called National Body of Auditors.
They are in charge of controlling every income, every expenditure, of all candidates, from all parties, in all elections, every two years.
Do you know how many they are?
--let me note, they are very good--
Seven.
(Laughter) They have to open an administrative file to ask for a photocopier.
(Laughter) Let me tell you something intimate.
Only once my dad came to see me give a talk.
When I finished he said: "Dear son, your stuff is brilliant, but it's so depressing."
(Laughter) "Try to pump it up a bit, because it makes you want to smash your fingers with a door."

(Laughter) Therefore, honoring the promise I made to my father, I tell you: Is it possible to correct this? Yes.

It's hard? Indeed.

I actually feed on what others have already learned, like the great investigator in the Italian investigation, Mani pulite, Antonio Di Pietro.
To give you an idea of his guts, he had to go live in another country.
Marco Canale was his fake name while he was chased after to kill him.
He and others like him say that the key moment, the turning point happens when citizens get involved and begin to exert pressure; they put pressure.
He says: it could only change when the Italians said "enough".
And that is possible.
If we do it, and we put pressure, we can achieve the electoral reform to establish the electronic ballot, to end up with paper ballot theft.
Or detach the Anti-Corruption Agency from the orbit of the Executive.
And many other examples, so many.

In fact, we have had it in Argentina,
infinite times.
The Marches of Silence in Catamarca to demand the resolution of the murder of the little girl, Morales.
Or the two-for-one; there was a sentence by the Supreme Court that had society rallying.
Or the resolution 125. You name it.

The debate over making abortion legal.
The congressmen didn't attend the debates.
And they suddenly realized that citizens were involved in something they weren't.
Whether pro or against, but they had to go and face it.
So, it is indeed possible, but it depends on us.
(Applause) And because it will depend on us and it's going to depend on putting a lot of pressure, is that the daily effort is key.
And until that doesn't happen, me and others like me, will keep moving down the underworld talking to the Tony Montana and the Vito Corleone while dodging the aluminum bats.
(Applause)
When I was a kid one of the games I liked the most was to dive under the water and see for how long I could hold my breath.
It was usually about 10 to 15 seconds.
Sometimes, 30 seconds.
And when I could reach 40 seconds I would feel like an amphibious superhero!
But, obviously, I would always come back to the surface to breathe.
These days I feel I do something similar with my cellphone.
I play not to look at it, just like when I held my breath.
Of course, it's not some seconds, neither some minutes.
It's for a few hours.
When I go on vacation, it can even be for a couple of days.
But eventually I go back and look at it.
And I go back to my cellphone, the social networks and the Web not only because they are tools, but for they have become the channel through which we breathe digital oxygen.
This digital oxygen is the air we need to inhabit a virtual context -- and by no means less real -- where a big part of our lives takes place: the digital environment.
The emergence of this digital environment is a fundamental historical change.
For thousands of years humans beings were nomads.
We survived thanks to hunting, fishing and gathering.
We lived conditioned mostly by natural phenomena, such as rains or snowfalls.
About 10,000 years ago we began to develop agriculture and became sedentary.
And with the emergence of urban civilization about 5,000 years ago our life began to depend not only on the natural environment but also on the urban environment, like for example the state of traffic or if there was a power outage.
I have been working for 20 years on studying how we inform ourselves.
Until very recently it was very common to listen in research interviews people telling me that one of the first things they did when waking up was to turn on the TV or the radio and swiftly check on the state of the weather and the traffic.
That is, having a screenshot of the natural and the urban environment before starting the day.
A few years ago, they started adding more and more often, grabbing the cellphone -- which is usually on the bedside table -- and see how many new messages there are WhatsApp and what's new in their Facebook Twitter, Snapchat and Instagram accounts. Since they went to sleep.
That is, more and more we start our day with a screenshot of the three environments where our life happens: the natural, the urban and the digital environment.
During the day we look to our cellphone an average of about 80 times before going to sleep.
If we add to that the amount of time we spend with our computers or with our tablets, it's really hard to find a moment when we are not connected.
It's not just a matter of time, but also of emotional intensity.
It's very common to hear that cellphones have become a part of our bodies.
An extra hand which, clearly, becomes difficult to get rid of.
For a research that we did with colleagues in Argentina a couple of years ago we interviewed more than one hundred people in different parts of the country.
One of them told us that on a trip, going from one province to another, upon arrival they realized they had forgotten their cellphone.
A relative said, "don't worry, I'll bring it to you in a couple of days."
Now, this person reported in the interview that even when she knew it was only two days of absence, she felt like dying without her cellphone.
She felt like dying because what she missed was more than the cellphone, it was digital oxygen.
This digital oxygen is different from that we breathe with our lungs.
A key difference is that digital oxygen allows us to breathe in multiple and diverse atmospheres at the same time.
Like when we take part in many interactions with different groups of people who live in different places and all this through different social networks.
It's like we're permanently seeing and being seen.
There is a double expectation.
On the one hand we feel that we have an almost permanent access -- as an open window, to the life of others; to what they do, what they say, even to what they feel.
On the other hand, of course, there is the inverse expectation.
And our contacts many times feel they have a right to have a window almost permanently open to our lives.
Like a voyeur running into an exhibitionist.
This much visibility attracts but also exhausts us at the same time.
Our sociability multiplies and we feel it's very difficult to disconnect.
And the younger you are, the harder this disconnection is.
One winter night this year, walking down Corrientes Avenue, in the heart of Buenos Aires, I saw something that struck me.
It was sadly an old and modern scene at the same time.
There was a boy and girl, both really young, living on the street, out in the open at the mercy of natural phenomena like thousands of years ago.
They were sitting in a couple of rickety chairs.
They had a big cardboard box turned upside down in front of them that served as a table, while many other boxes stacked on both sides delimited in some way their semi-private space on public space.
They were having dinner, together.
And while they were eating they looked at a cellphone.
They were young homeless people, but connected to the digital environment.
This scene sums up one of the important findings of our research:
Belonging to an age group, our age, is usually more important to understanding and explaining how we inhabit the digital environment than gender or socioeconomic level.
And this somehow marks the passage from the 20th to the 21st century.
Now, that this happens in a country where three out of ten people live below the poverty line is a remarkable sociological phenomenon.
The passage of time is something that never stops.
Every day we grow old.
What was once a novelty it will stop being it.
And the generational change is inexorable.
Then, if the passage of time, if age, is what organizes how we inhabit the digital environment that means we live in a society in movement where change is perhaps the only constant.
This creates uncertainty.
Sometimes we worry.
Other times it even gives us anxiety.
How will the future be?
Why if I'm used to something is it possibly going to change?
And this is why, at least in part, I think that many times people talk about the digital environment evoking apocalyptic scenarios.
For example, the storm of false news that will destroy the building of Democracy.
But a society in movement is a society in which it is also more possible to live changes, social transformations, that were much less likely before.
Voices that were historically marginalized and that didn't have a place either in traditional media have recently not only managed to be heard but also boost important collective reforms.
Three years ago a tweet was the kickstarter of the movement "Ni Una Menos".
Since then, the activists have made the digital environment their own to organize, to make their claims visible and empower important parts of society.
It's not because of networks that this social movement exists.
Nor it exists only in networks, obviously.
But without the digital environment it would be much harder to imagine its existence and its strength.
This doesn't mean that fake news don't go viral in the networks.
Indeed, many times they go viral.
But it means that these negative tendencies exist along with the possibilities of social reform that we see in such hopeful cases as "Ni Una Menos".
And it's in the fight between these negative and positive trends that much of the future of the digital environment is played.
And maybe that of society as a whole.
Today we read in the history books how the emergence of urban civilization unfolded. And its evolution through the centuries.
And many times, because of these readings we ask ourselves: what would have happened to our society if the cities were designed and built in different ways?
But we are the pioneers of the digital environment.
It is our descendants who they will read in books yet to be written how these fundamental years impacted the creation of the digital environment.
And possibly they'll make pretty similar questions to those we make ourselves when we read the history of cities.
What would you like that your granddaughters, your great-grandchildren, your great-great-granddaughters, would read about the beginnings of the digital environment?
And what place would you like to have? As subjects and main characters not just as spectators, in writing this story of the future?
Thank you.
(Applause)
Ladies and gentlemen, if it were true what doesn't kill you, strengthens you in Cuba we'd have 11 millions of Hercules.
Why am I saying this?
Because those Cubans who lived in Cuba in the 90's and could remain alive are outstanding human beings.
And I say outstanding, because they survived in really adverse conditions of economic block, disrespects and incomprehension.
For these reasons I ask for applause for all the Cubans who're here tonight.
(Applause) Despite this unfavorable situation the Cuban knew how to go on, and he has done it quite well.
The Cuban has not been behind and has become an expert in making up for shortages.
Today, there is a Cuban version of almost everything: bikes have become motorbikes, fans have been built with the use of engines of washers, we made cars which were over 60 years old work linking Russian motors and American bodies and Chinese spare parts.
How fast the problem among the three powers was solved!
But apart from our dabbling in culinary arts, law and economics, which are enough to write and encyclopedia the technological initiatives have been our major asset.
When we speak about technology, the Cuban must be taken into account.
Something called Amazon out there?
Well, we have Revolico!
Some things are found on Revolico but not on Amazon that range from ads for arranged marriages and private detective agencies to artificial tears at lower prices, with guarantee and delivery included.
For example, Thanks --
Is it possible to find something like this on Amazon?
A Cuban joystick to play racing video games.
I don't think so.
And you can remove it from your computer and put it again in your car and goes on being functional.
They have something called YouTube?
Well, we invented the weekly package!
1000 GB of audiovisual information available for all kind of people.
If a series in the USA shows on Sunday, in Cuba it shows very early on Monday with subtitles and commercials.
We also have the language in our favor, because in Spain this name should be changed.
Imagine a Spaniard gets home and ask him: "Sir, are you taking the package this week?"
Or a chat between two Spaniard mothers: "My son works in a cooling company, and yours?"
"Mine is selling the package."
The chat would be a bit uncomfortable.
The first steps are being taken for Internet with Nauta cards and Wi-Fi spots in different districts.
We ended up with the problem of social isolation linked to technology use.
Those who created the project thought: "Because in other countries people with Internet don't go out and fall apart from the social reality."
All right, very easy!
Let's connect in parks and streets, surrounded by people in the open air.
It is a divine message.
We should just see the crowds that gather around Wi-Fi spots to know the reach and the acceptance of this technology among Cubans.
It is a big irony that despite Cuba is in the 111st place in the ICT Development Index which measures the evolution of these technologies at a global scale, appears in the 14th position regarding knowledge and competences related to its use.
So, although Cuba is the second-to-last Pan-American country on the list with access to Internet, just over Nicaragua, Cuba has professionals of the technologies of communications and information that put the island in the 14th position regarding knowledge and abilities on the Web.
Just imagine in what position Cuba would be with a full access to the Internet.
That knowledge is due to the abilities acquired via the use of internal nets and other kinds of platforms such as SNET: The Street Network that is one of the greatest things in the last few years, capturing the interest of Cuban researchers and also of foreign professionals.
During my thesis I verified that thanks to SNET Cuba is on the list of countries where communities of wireless nets work, which are group of users, institutions and companies that decide to build a computer network to guarantee people access to contents.
The street net goes from Cotorro to Bauta, in the province of Artemisa and links all the places of the capital via Wi-Fi or LAN.
According to administrators, over 8000 computers are connected what turns it into the biggest one in Cuba.
What is important of SNET is that it can't get money for its services.
So, it is absolutely free of charge and it is run by its members taking into account their technical knowledge what turns it into an informal and self-managed net.
Inside the net there is a kind of hierarchy that rules the activity of it.
At the bottom of the pyramid are the rulers and subnodes that are those who assist up to 80 people in the node; on the next step are something known as the boss of zone that are those who assist the entire zone.
The zones can have up to 300 users.
On the next step are the main rulers that are those in charge of ruling big metropolitan areas.
These people are the same in charge of making the regulations follow inside the net that forbids to speaks of politics or religion, use sexual avatars, have disrespectful practices among users or towards rulers, use obscene language, and so on.
It's incredible how the rules are respected and accepted by users.
It's because the net has become a vital need for them.
Mothers don't threaten their children to tell fathers off.
If they behave badly they're threatened to tell the ruler to be disconnected.
Believe me. It's much more effective.
Despite SNET having social nets, FTP to share contents, music channels, DJs and many other services the main objective in the Street Net is video playing.
The game culture in Cuba is becoming stronger.
During the day over 6000 users can be seen connected at the same time enjoying video games such as World of Warcraft, Battlefield, Call of Duty, Dota or FIFA.
It was in SNET where the first great competition of electronic sports was born in Cuba: in La Habana Dota 2 Arena that took place in the Factory of Cuban Art.
The hostess of the competition was Dota, for its mechanics and for being the most used video game in Cuba.
Dota is played so much in Cuba that a language was created inside the communities that trespasses the limits of the virtual spaces.
Completely new words have been created and expressions with sense outside the game.
For example, how many of you have not heard of being in the cloth?
This odd expression was born in the districts of Dota to refer to those characters who did not have an armor so, they only had the clothes.
Today, the expression is used for all descriptions.
The one who is thin, is in the cloth.
But the one fat, is also in the cloth.
The one ugly, is in the cloth; the poor is in the cloth.
The one not knowing of a topic, is on the cloth.
What does the net lack?
A female presence.
In my thesis I demonstrate that only 5 percent of users of video games are women.
If you concentrate well, you feel testosterone going out via the speakers.
Once, I was asked what the difference was between an international gamer and a Cuban one.
The latter is the same as the Korean, Japanese, Ukrainian or American one, with the difference that the Cuban always says he has a date.
They don't waste time to blame you.
Ask him: How long have you used the video game? They say:
"Around 6 years, because I've had a date for that time."
Or if you ask: "How did you tie up with the video game?"
They say: "Don't know, I should ask my date who has better memory than me."
It's that the mass media have come nearer video games with so many prejudices that now people are afraid of recognizing they belong to an alternative culture, to a digital culture, to a gamer culture.
But video games in Cuba don't alienate.
Video games in Cuba work as activators of social relationships and the proof of this is all video gamers claim that the most frequent company when playing are friends or acquaintances.
The place to use them are houses of friends or relatives.
One of the reasons that encourage people to play is having contact with other people on the net and the first reason to increase the use of any platform is the existence of friends encouraging them to play.
For many people the use of video games only have sense if they play with others.
For these reasons, if I am asked how I imagine Cuba in the future I say I imagine an island without prejudices and open to the digital culture an island connected with the world free and independent; an island where everything that is Cuban where what was donemade in Cuba is still an idea and a concept worth sharing.
Purely and simply, an island that is not in the cloth.
The rest, ask it to my date.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
How many of us don't own a hard copy photo album?
How many of us were born after 1995?
Digital photography and the use of the Internet has changed the way we take photos and videos and share them with the world.
I can get out my phone and take a selfie and later share it with the world on the Internet.
Ten years ago, this wasn't possible.
I am passionate about photography for the impact it has on our reality and experiencing human diversity.
This is why a year ago I started a digital blog on photography named "Humans of Spain".
You may ask how a 16-year-old boy decided to start taking pictures at such a young age.
Well, my mother, who is over there, and my grandfather, also over there, are both photographers, so cameras have always been very accessible around the house.
My father is an executive coach, so he's constantly traveling around and connecting with people.
The initial idea of this project was to show the good side of Spain to encourage a positive change.
The process involves wandering the streets, looking for a human with good vibes, then taking a photo and doing a mini interview with him.
This photo is later published on a web page and in social networks.
Each photo is accompanied by a little caption telling this person's story in English and Spanish.
Today I have come to share four stories with you which I find to be honest, beautiful, and powerful.
Sunday, midday.

A fairly fresh morning. Madrid.
I'm walking up to the Plaza Mayor,
and suddenly, I start to hear something, and I'm curious to see who is playing.
So I go over, finding myself in the alley, and I see a guitarist with his guitar, his voice, and nothing else.
He doesn't have a hat to ask for money, nor was there anyone watching him.
So, I wait for him to finish his song and ask him: "But what are you doing here alone, playing?"
And he tells me he likes how different streets in Madrid echo.
He is giving something to the world without asking for anything in return.
He has a gift, and he's sharing it because he believes in life.
"Humans of Spain" has a very similar idea.
I offer a positive image, but don't ask for anything in return, I do it for merely the sake of art.
In the north of Spain, in a little town called Bermeo, - I don't know if anyone knows of it - it's 9 am, it's a quite sunny morning, and my mother and I are on our way to the church. And we see this gentleman walking slowly, but with a sense of nobility about him.
So we stop to talk to him, and he tells us that his name is Justo and that he is 91 years old.
And the most incredible thing of all is that 10 metres behind me, there is a church, and this is where he was baptized.
At that moment times are merged, I imagine Justo here, age 91, and then, some 91 years ago, Justo in the church, as a baby.
To me this story is truly beautiful.
OK, this is in Seville.
My mother and I have already spent all morning taking photos, and we're exhausted - because something I haven't told you is that this process is very tiring; we're talking to strangers, and a lot of the time they say no to us. I have spoken with more strangers than there are people in this auditorium -
so we take a little break to eat some stewed meat croquettes that were extremely good. (Laughter) And I turn to the side, and I see this guy with a golden light across the side of his face and beaming with life.
So I approach him to have a chat. He tells me that the day before he got his Master's in sexology and today he's celebrating with a little beer.
(Laughter) But this guy, as you can see in the photo, was an explosion of joy and happiness.
There are three or four people in my life like this, so refreshing for everybody around them.
One for example is a girl who works at the school canteen, who my friends here also know; her name is Yolanda, and when she's around you she helps you see life with a bit more optimism something you might need sometimes when you're at school.
This last story is really special.
My family and I were going to Germany.
We're making our way through terminal T4, again, looking for a human who holds that spark, those good vibes.
And we see a guy approaching from the corner; he's a little on the shorter side, but moves with lots of energy and purpose.
We stop to talk to him and ask: "What is it that you would like to do most at this very moment?"
He said to us in an Argentinian accent and quite a low-pitched voice: "I'm already doing it." (Laughter)
We asked, "And what are you doing?"
He replies: "I am a clown.
But not just any clown, a hospital clown."
This guy spends his time going to hospitals, bringing a bit of happiness to the suffering children and elderly.
I find it incredible that we're rushing through the airport, we need to arrive on time at some terminal, and we don't even realize that there are a load of people that could have something beautiful to say, if we would just listen.
Likewise, this project isn't changing much the image of Spain but nevertheless, it is teaching me who I want to be, and what it means to be human.
There are journeys that start in an instant, and I want to capture this instant, so that tomorrow if someone searches for it, he would find that story, that gaze.
Life is a surprise that deserves to be told.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I'm a lover.
I'm a lover of nature.
A lover of people, of my friends.
People who know me know I give hugs when I see them.
And of course, I love my children Alexis, Paloma, and my wife Geraldine.
I also have a fairly unhealthy obsession with gadgets such as my computer or my cell phone.
But I doubt I'm the only one like that nowadays.
A few years ago I became a lover of these two gadgets you see here.
One you know; the solar panel.
And the other, little less well-known, is the solar water heater.
The solar panel was invented in the 1960s because it was the only way at the time communications satellites could operate.
I dream, like my wife who works with me, and my friend Daniel, with whom I began this endeavor, that every roof in the world will have a solar panel. But first, we have to start with what we have here in Argentina
Throughout the world, solar panels have been a resounding success.
Everyone is listening.
Countries such as Germany, China, and Italy are installing astronomical amounts.
Here you can see the number installed in the last 7 or 8 years and how it is progressing.
This is one of the largest solar plants
in the US located in California.
It produces 550MW and, in comparison to all those installed in Argentina, they would fit in this little rectangle.
We don't need to compare ourselves with the Americans.
Just look at our region.
See how many are installed in Argentina despite the fantastic conditions, especially in the Northwest.
But countries like Uruguay have more than 10 times what we have.
Going back to the gadgets I love so much.
This, the lesser-known one.
This is a solar water heater and is used to heat water and not to generate electricity.
Basically, it prevents burning natural gas.
It's pretty simple how it works.
In the previous image we saw some tubes.
Each tube is actually two pipes: a clear one on the outside and a dark one on the inside.
Air is removed from between the two pipes just like a thermos, and the sun's rays that pass through the clear pipe hit the dark pipe.
It is the same thing when I am in the sun with a dark t-shirt: I am hotter than when I have a white t-shirt on.
It is as simple as that.
So the water in the dark pipe, even though it's -10° or -20° C outside, heats up due to solar radiation and gets up to 60° in the winter and between 80° and 90° C in the summer.
To give you an idea: you never bathe in water more than 43° C. Hotter than 43° C will burn you.
Another neat fact is hot water, being less dense than cold water, as it warms continues to rise going into the tank and the colder water inside goes downward.
So without any kind of pump during sunny times of the day, this system generates free hot water once I have it installed.
This is Martín, who came to see us around two years ago.
Anyone who has an organic garden and tends it with his kids is a good guy.
(Laughter) No doubt about it.
Martín told me, "I want to heat water in my house with solar energy.
Both for hot water as well as for heating." He showed me his home and said, "See, it's not a church."
(Laughter) So I told him, "Look, Martín, I'm going to explain a bit about this.
I'm going to be completely honest." 100,000 kcal is a unit of energy.
That's about what it takes to boil 190 liters of water.
Think about a bathtub.
If we had to boil all that water, it would be 100,000 kcal.
In Argentina, 100,000 kcal would cost, on a gas connection, less than 3.50 ARS, which is less than 11 m³.
If I'm part of the 34% of the population that doesn't have a gas connection, those same 100,000 kcal cost more than 110 ARS.
And if I use an electric water heater like are often used in the city - many new buildings have only electric without a gas connection - it would cost 26 ARS for the same amount of energy.
Accross the pond, we wonder, what the same amount costs in Uruguay.
It is logical to think that this must be the actual price of energy.
A solar water heater has a lifespan of around 20 years.
20 to 25 actually.
So I told him, "Look, Martín, when you use the sun with your solar water heater for those 20 years, you're going to get around 45 million kcal."
And 20 years, for those 45 million kcal, cost 1500 ARS on a gas-only connection.
Or from a gas cylinder almost 51,000 and 12,000 in electricity, with an electric water heater.
If Martín used the solar installation, even if he had the others, he would still have to buy the gas or electric water heater which cost 4000 to 5000 ARS at any home appliance store.
Adding the operation plus appliance costs, I come to these amounts.
It ends up that the solar power appliance, compared to using a gas connection, would pay for itself in no fewer than 17 years by subsidy as opposed to 1 year of liquefied gas and 4.5 years of an electric tank.
And in Uruguay, again, these numbers would be very different.
This is Martín's house now, he saves approximately 80% on gas bills for heating as well as hot water.
There is another case, Sebastián.
Sebastián moved to a rural area in a house where there was no electrical connection.
A very simple house.
He loves nature and I say to him, "How are you generating electricity?"
"I have a great generator. Well, kind of.

It's not great because it's noisy, smoky, I have to go to town to buy diesel fuel."
And he asks, "How about this solar stuff?"
"Solar panels for electricity have a lifespan of more than 30 years.
So, for those 30 years with your generator system, in fuel, maintenance - also, these generators break every 5 years - you will spend around 215,000 USD."
Otherwise, with my solar installation, Sebastián saves 144,000 USD in 30 years and would recover his investment in 4.5 years, which isn't half bad.
He says, "Well, the truth is that I'm convinced and I'm ready to go."
And this is the installation on his roof.
He's happy to not have to go into town.
Also, he had a lot of problems with road access from the countryside. When it rained he couldn't buy diesel fuel so he ended up pretty unhappy.
For those of us living in the city, looking at the bills, are stunned.
This is an actual bill from today: 23 ARS.
But the same bill shows how much that electricity would cost in cities such as Santiago, Chile and Montevideo, Uruguay, where we're talking about bills 7, 8, or 9 times more expensive.
Doing a little calculation, in order to talk about similar units, I get to, basically, the consumer pays 47 USD per Megawatt-hour (Mwh).
But for the country, to generate 1 Mwh, which is about what is used by a space heater plugged in for 14 days - that's a Mwh so you have an idea - costs the country just in fuel, because all new power coming in is made from thermal power stations by burning gas or burning oil.
There's hydroelectric and nuclear power but we're already using all of that.
The country's growth comes only from thermal power stations.
Operation and maintenance is 16 USD and 37 USD for transportation.
Since the country is so big, energy transport requires energy 14% is lost, while distribution alone costs 25 USD which gives me a total of 344 USD: the country's cost to generate 1 Mwh.
So, how much would solar panels cost?
A small installation to lower my bill in the city.
Panels alone would be 2,600 USD; 1,600 USD for inverters that transform the direct current to alternating current; and 1,500 USD in labor and installation.
Which gives me a total of 5,700 USD for this small installation.
Much more expensive than in other countries because there is no type of benefit for the parts to come into the country.
Doing more calculations puts my solar installation at 142 USD/Mwh.
It is clear that it's much more than the 47 USD I pay on my bill.
Basically, more than three times as much.
But it really is much lower than the country's cost to produce 1 Mwh.
So if I buy my solar system today, at the rates I pay from the bill, it would pay for itself after 98 years, or triple its lifespan.
Not very appealing, right?
But only in 12 years if we consider what the energy costs the country.
An issue to consider.
And I save 8,000 USD for 30 years, which isn't half bad.
However some people have a conscience and don't only look at the economic equation, but instead see the environmental equation and, ignoring numbers, install solar panels here in the city.
This is a hotel near the Congress.
But the important thing is how much this costs the country.
The fight against climate change.
It is critical to support it by adopting renewable energy and therefore many countries subsidize solar energy instead of fossil energy, making the investment pay for itself in 2 years or less.
In many places in the US,
companies install solar panels for free and guarantee you will pay a rate equal to or less than you paid each month prior simply to avoid the cost of these disasters.
So I can say very confidently that this is a good thing.
This is a brief summary of solar energy versus the generator group.
They will bring 26 years of free energy.
Versus energy from the network now, 18.
Solar water heater versus the gas network: 3 years free.
Very little, unfortunately.
Against liquefied gas, 19.
And against electricity, 16.
So I can confidently say that it's good to bet on the sun instead of the dollar.
Thank you very much. (Applause)
OK, raise your hand those of you who ever have received a message via Internet, yes, Internet, and this kind of message.
A message saying, "Looking back it's quite hard to believe that we are alive.
We travelled by car with no belts nor airbags.
We used to go on a 10-12 hours travel being 5 people in a 600 car and we hadn't the economy-class syndrome.
We didn't have medicine bottles with child-proof cover.
The swings were made of metal
and they had rusted corners. We spread body lices to each other in school, and our moms solved this with warm vinegar.
We shared soda bottles or whatever could be drunk and nobody was infected with anything.
We cracked our heads with stones while we played and all was fine, kids stuff, healed with Mercromina.
We broke our bones and teeth, there was no law to sue the guilty ones."
Who's received a message like this?
Who's ever received a message like this?
Well. And then you remember
they would always ask, "How did we do it?," always
in an ironic way like saying, "all this modern stuff is bullshit, right?
Look at us, we're here."
Well, there's a little problem and that's that not everybody could make it.
(Laughter) This was the death risk between 5 and 15 years old in Spain from 1975 to 2014.
Not everybody could make it.
Of course, it wasn't only those stupidities but more things. But all those stupidities, we'll now see it with and example, costed lives.
costed lives, costed members, costed diseases, costed everything.
We made it with better, as the doctor said before, we made it with better food and hygene, of course.
With progress in medicine and vaccines.
Having a less problematic society now than ever before.
I find this quite interesting. I grew up in the 80s of the heroine, and when I see now all that freaked out people, with the closed fences with a lot of security and all, I remember my neighborhood and say,
"If this people had lived in my neighborhood, they would have died because of a heart attack."
We're on an all-time low serious crime.
Generally, the increase of life and quality levels and also the overcoming of these charming stupidities, so nostalgic.
Cars are a great example.
As Spain was getting full of cars, the number of deads per million citizens hit great numbers, as we can see.
This is the road mortality rate from 1960 to 1989.
We had those 600 cars, those R4 or whatever, with no security bells, no airbags, driving in those obsolete roads, or as some would say "fixed," with safety regulations and road safety laws that were like jokes since each person did whatever they wanted to.
Yes, all that stuff, a lot of freedom and a lot of... "What?"
As this process passed and there started to be better cars, the road network improved, the road safety laws changed, they became stricter, among other things, it was what happened.
The last statistics I have are from this 2014 chart, but in 2015 we have exactly the same.
But, in fact, the good chart is this one.
The line from down here, the red one, is the one you've just seen, the one that represent the number of dead people per million.
The blue line is the number of dead people per million because of car accidents.
Those ratchets from the 60, yes, those were ratchets, even luxury cars, to be honest, those were coffins with wheels.
And if someone remembers the accidents from then, they know.
As those ratchets evolved into better cars, with more security, as the roads improved, as the traffic laws became more modern, more respectful with the traffic, as we started to use seat belts and airbags -- this was quite hard -- as people got used to this, well, this was the final result.
By the way, can you see that peak from 1989 I've told you about before?
It matches with the risk of death between 5 and 15 years old.
One of the reasons of juvenile mortality is the death in roads.
Youth usually, excepting a disgrace, don't die because any other things.
One of their death reasons is road mortality.
So having seven kids at the back of our 600 car, or our R4 or whatever car, to take them to the swimming pool has this kind of consequences.
All these are death lads, dead young boys and girls.
Look where are we now.
But to become a lad so you can die as a lad, you had to have the chance to become a lad first.
It means overcome this rate of child mortality that, as you can see, in 1975, it was on 19 and something percent; 19 and something percent per one thousand alive newborns.
This is how it approximately works in Brazil, Vietnam, or Spain in 1975. We have the same mortality rate that right now have countries like Brazil or Vietnam, and you know the actual conditions of those countries.
Yes, there are very fine areas, but let's just better go inside the house.
And if we go back a little more, and we go to the year 1950, the statistics are worse than the actual ones from Angola or Congo.
Worse.
We were in the same situation as the countries with the worst rate, the Central African Republic or Guinea-Bissau. I think there are only two or three countries with worse rate.
We could think this was because it was "that Spain," with an awful civil war that had only finished ten years before, with a centuries-old backwardness and an awful dictatorship that maintained us in the misery a lot more than we wanted to; and with an autarky.
Yes, all this could be true, but there you have Switzerland's data for the same period of time.
They were worse than Zimbabwe today.
Yes, the numbers are much better, look at the third part, but they have more or less the same order of magnitude.
They were worse than Zimbabwe today.
This is Canada.
Like Tanzania or India.
And this is Spain, just to compare.
We can see that the difference and the chart is almost the same.
We could also think that this improvement is something related to rich people and that the Third World is awful and everything is terrible.
Yes, it's true, there are awful places to live in; there are places from the Third World that are truly awful.
But this figure at the left is the child mortality and this figure at the right is the life expectancy between 1950 and 2015.
The red parts are the least developed regions.
Even in the least developed countries, the child mortality rate has dropped, how much? A third part of the total? And life expectancy has risen from 40 to almost 70 years old.
Past is something that some people miss, it doesn't look that nice now, right?
In fact, if we consider that between the Paleolithic age and 1900, the average life expectancy was between 20 and 35 approximately, maybe 40 for certain cases; let's just consider life expectancy 28, the fault being mostly those huge child mortality rates, a person that is born today --
Someone has told before that every newborn child is a new hope.
I'm adding something else, every newborn who is alive, is a new hope.
They died at a disturbing rate.
Children died at a disturbing rate.
There were a lot of diseases
that killed them. An otitis is nowadays a simple visit to the doctor's, any mom or dad here know it, An otitis is nowadays a simple visit to the doctor's, but back then it killed a child.
It killed a child.
Because there were no antibiotics, there was no treatment, there was none, it had to be invented.
I dare everybody that is watching me, I dare any person to tell me a particular past society, from 1950 and backwards, a particular society where a person, a normal person, a modest person, lived more and better than in the developed actual society. It doesn't need to be Norway, just a normal developed society from the actual time such as Spain or something similar.
If it gets into someone's mind they can send me a mail.
Now, where does this idealization of the past come from?
It comes, indeed, from the past.
From that shit past, when everybody dreamt of better places.
But they, their traditions, the worship of ancestors, the respect for them, it made them be from the past.
The Greek Golden Ages, Krita Yuga for the past Hindus, the worship of ancestors, starting from the Paleolithic age, even the traditions of our past generations, the Roman "mos maiorum," just because they were old had to necessarily be good, even if they exposed the newborn children or, for example, have the right to kill your wife or children, they had this right, the "pater familias" had the perfect right to kill his wife and children, and whoever lived in his house, better not to talk about the slaves.
This Nordic thing, I don't know how to pronounce it, and this stuff such as the earthly paradise or the Garden of Eden.
The Greek also left us the Arcadia.
The Arcadia, that has been painted so many times.
Arcadia is a Greek region that, back then, was the poorest one.
But there were a lot of trees and it was very bucolic.
Even nowadays that place is cool, but don't you think they're fine because emigration is constant.
It's in Peloponnese, you can visit it, the place is very nice, I'm telling you.
And it has some characteristics that are nearly always the same.
In first place, that lost dream of a rural and non-urban past, the cities, you may know, have always been related to the degeneration.
It's agricultural and anti-industrial. Industry, how scary, it contaminates!
It's from a bucolic pastoralism, a simpler life, at least for the minimum caste, as it is called now, the very small dominant class that used to write those things.
Mostly of the times what has arrived to us from the past history is what those who knew how to write wrote in these societies in which most of the people didn't know how to write.
A simpler life, of course, if I'm lying on my fields while the slaves are working, of course this is a very simple and bucolic life.
Sometimes, with anti-intellectual and unscientific characteristics and, of course, "natural."
This natural thing is great, I love natural, I mean, the fresh air, the birds, the trees, pretty boys and girls, cacti, botulinum toxic, smallpox virus, Ebola virus.
Even the lightning, the one that fills us with a natural electrifying energy, the lightning electrifies us.
Of course, with no chemistry.
I don't know what can it be without chemistry in this Universe.
At least, I'm made of chemistry, I don't know about you: carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, phosphorus, sulphur.
And we see this now, we fear everything related to chemistry.
But without the chemistry, we're fucked.
Without those things that are added to our food, we'll still die because of food poisoning as people died all over the history.
Going to a supermarket and having the guarantee that the food you're taking home, even just a little, you're being sure that the food is safe and you can eat it without getting sick. This is an absolutely historical innovation.
Heidi, we've all seen Heidi, drinking milk directly from the goat's udder. This is a perfect way to get carbuncles, anthrax attack, or just anthrax.
And without playing that we're gods.
I think this is very funny.
We've been playing gods from the very first day we started to be humans.
Since we started to use fire.
Since we decided that neighbor disturbed us and decided to attack that person with a stick, which is a tool.
Since we invented agriculture, and especially the slash and burn agriculture, which is the biggest environmental disaster from the humankind's history, and that we created in the Paleolithic age.
Without playing at being gods.
Playing at being gods is what got us out of the caves and what will take us to the stars.
It is what we do better.
There's no legendary past where to get back to.
All the progress and science problems, this I was told by Konstantin Novoselov, Nobel Prize in Physics, in an interview I made to him, all science's problems get solved doing more science, not less.
I add that all progress problems get solved with more progress, not less.
Otherwise, we are going back.
Societies don't get stuck, it's not true that societies get stuck.
A society that doesn't progress, starts going back immediately.
We're seeing it now in places like the ones dominated by the Islamic State and things like that.
There's no turning back, there's no place where to get back because that happy Arcadia is nothing else than a myth.
A very old myth, to be honest, from some old people whose world were, indeed, shitty.
We put this dream in our future.
There's still a lot to fight for, there's still a lot to be done. Of course the world is not fine, quite far from fine, we'll have to strive a little bit more, but always looking straight and never looking back, because back there, there's absolutely nothing, just misery, hunger, diseases, superstitions and death. And abuse, and randomness.
And if you were a woman, better not think about it. Thank you.
(Applause)
Do you remember the kids rhyme, "Stars fill the sky, thorns in the ground?"
Who hasn't spent hours lying in a field trying to avoid thorns or on the beach or a mountain, gazing at the starry sky?
Away from cities lights, the night sky is one of the most beautiful scenes of the natural world.
It is even an inspiring view: there are those who become poets, those who feel insignificant, and those who feel immense for being part of the cosmos and for feeling the cosmos is a part of them.
I could spend the whole afternoon talking about what we see in this picture I took from the other side of the lake, with the Milky Way arching above Bariloche.
But today I want to tell you about just a fragment of this kind of cosmic connection.
I think it is one the major discoveries of modern science; it should be at the core of our culture and known by everyone.
Naturally, it has to do with stars.
During the 20th century, a remarkable accomplishment was achieved: the development of the science of the functioning of stars. It is amazing how much astronomers know these days about stars, considering they are so, so far away and that their existences are so foreign to human life, in size, and time.
An astronomer can't go to a star and place a thermometer to measure its temperature, or take a sample to analyze it in the lab.
Everything we know about stars, we learn by watching from far away, with instruments and through the power of physics and math, which allow us to see, touch, listen, and even smell stars, even if we can't reach them.
A small fragment of the science of these stars is what I want to talk to you about today.
"What are we made of?",
we wondered earlier today. Of mud, inspired by the breath of God? Of corn, as in the Popol Vuh narratives? Of water, air, earth, and fire, like the Greeks said?
Every culture answered this question in its favorite way relying only on tradition.
And it is even funny for us today, but still, during the 19th century, some people believed that living beings, and humans in particular, were made of a matter different from that of inanimate objects.
Today we know that this is not so. We are made of chemical substances and chemical elements, the same matter that constitutes animals, minerals, and also stars.
If we want to know what it is: More than 99% of our atoms are carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, and nitrogen -- and a pinch of salt and pepper.
(Laughter) But this is not the question.
I don't want to tell you about the chemical composition of living beings, but where these atoms came from.
Where did they come from!?
I am sure even the question will be surprising for more than one of you. I hope the answer turns out to be amazing for everyone.
There it is, surprising question for one of you.
(Laughter) What's amazing, fascinating about the answer is that astronomy, the science of objects so foreign to human experience, found the answer.
It was a collective effort, around the middle of the 20th century, from which one particular work in 1957 stands out and which didn't get the media coverage dispensed today to each super-moon, or to each asteroid zooming past the Earth.
It looks far-fetched, but the authors are these four big boys we see here, having fun with a small toy train.
As it turns out, some time before that, astronomers had discovered that, in its beginnings, the Universe contained only hydrogen and helium, and practically nothing else.
Hydrogen, well, that's fine,
it's more than half our atoms. But helium is chemically inert;
we use it just for party balloons and making a squeaky voice.
Where did the rest come from?
It turns out the Universe itself produced it.
Let's see.
The Sun and all the stars make the dream of the alchemists come true: they transmute one element into another.
For millions of years, even billions of years, they have been turning hydrogen into helium in a nuclear reaction.
The energy of this reaction makes the Sun shine, as well as all the other stars we see in the sky.
But, we already had helium! The rest of the elements from the periodic table are still missing.
It all happens at the end of the life of a star. Because, what happens when a star runs out of hydrogen?
The star says, no problem, I've got plenty of helium. I can use helium! I tighten my belt and use helium to produce carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen.
These are more sporadic reactions, and they occur during much shorter periods of time, but stars still manage to produce fair amounts of carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen.
Most stars don't go any further. They can't keep tightening their belts when they exhaust each nuclear fuel.
When they get to the end of their lives, they swell enormously, they become giants, and finally, all this matter they forged vanishes into the cloud of gas and dust of the galaxy, from which new stars will be born.
Carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, and nitrogen: 99% of the matter we are made up of.
Hold on a minute, there's this 1% missing: that salt and pepper I mentioned.
This 1% is hard work and is assigned to the fewest, heaviest stars in Universe.
In a succession of belt tightening measures, each time faster and wilder, as one fuel runs out stars compress again and pass into another star.
In this way, elements go on filling the periodic table in a Universe that barely had hydrogen and helium.
Stars produce silica, neon, magnesium, sulphur, aluminum, and heavier elements each time, until stars get to iron.
When they get there, there is no belt tightening to save them.
Iron is around the middle of the periodic table, tough not even the heaviest star can draw energy from iron.
So, a pure, immense iron sphere is shaped at the center of the star until all nuclear fuel runs out.
Suddenly, it all stops. The nuclear furnace shuts down.
It's like when Wile E. Coyote is floating mid-air, with dangling feet in the void, with nothing to hold him; all he has left is falling.
The star, then, falls upon itself in seconds, with tremendous violence.
The outer layers bounce against the iron nucleus and the star, in the blink of an eye, tears itself apart in a colossal explosion, a supernova.
An explosion from which all the remaining elements of the periodic table are produced.
Just in the ultimate, violent instant of a super heavy star.
This is the mechanism. This is what the four big boys proved mathematically in 1957, when they weren't playing with their model trains. From a scientific point of view, it's fantastic, I tell you, it's extraordinary. It's great.
But for me, what's impressive is the fact that each one of our atoms, the oxygen we breathe, the carbon and phosphorous in our DNA, the nitrogen in our proteins, the iron in out tools, the aluminum in our pots, the fluoride in our teeth, the calcium in our bones, the gold in the rings, everything, each and every one of those atoms was forged in the super-hot furnaces of stars from generations previous to the Sun.
The iron in the hemoglobin in the blood of my left hand comes from a different star than the iron on my right hand.
In this constant recycling of matter in our galaxy, the stars drag their planets, asteroids, and satellites along with them. Even the Sun, when it reaches the end of its life, in a very far away future, will deliver its atoms and those of our planet, and those of ourselves, to the cloud of gas and dust of the galaxy.
We literally descend from stars of old times.
And, in time, we will be ancestors of future stars and planets.
Isn't that extraordinary?
Thanks.
(Applause)
Hi, I'm Aratxa, I'm 9 years old, and I like making robots.
The robots I make are called "printbots" because their parts are made on 3D printers.
And I'm here to make one.
As you can see, I have a few pieces already assembled to save time, but I just need to screw.
The first thing I'm going to do is to add the shovel.
I started building robots last year.
My father is a tech expert, and I didn't know what a computer program was
and to explain it in a way I could understand, he searched on the Internet until he found the "printbots".
And thanks to Alberto Valero, who's in the audience, we got the pieces for our first robot.

Ah! Hold on, now it doesn't fit!
(Laughter)
Finally! It didn’t want!
Well, just a second while I screw... Done!
The next thing I'll do is to add the sensor.
Apart from the printed pieces, robots have sensors that are like the robot's senses; with them it can see, listen, and so on.
For example, with the one I'm adding, the robot will be able to detect if it's following a black line or not, and thus, follow the circuit.
With ultrasonic sensors, same as bats, whales, or dolphins it can detect obstacles and avoid them.
There are also light sensors that help measure the level of light in their surroundings.
That being said, I'll add the tires.
Making robots is a lot of fun, and you learn a lot of stuff.
For example, my father knew nothing about electronics, and the first servos we had, he ruined them.
He's so clumsy!
Luckily, you can learn about everything on the Internet; and we improved, and bought four more servos, just in case.
The truth is when you say you make robots, no one believes you.
So we took robotics to my school.
We created workshops and made line-following robots.
When everyone finished, we raced.
We all loved it, but especially my classmates that did not know how easy and fun is to make robots.
One second while I screw this, and done!
There it is.
Now, I'm adding the sheet, the battery case, and the batteries.
The sheet is the robot's most important piece; it's like the brain; and in it you connect all the servo's cables and sensors.
The thing is that to make the robot do something you have to load a program, like a user's guide, which helps the robot know who it is, and what it needs to do.
I'm learning to program stuff but I still can't create a complete program for a robot.
But there are some tools that use blocks and make it easier for kids my age; and my father's age.
Now, I'm connecting the cables.
Using the cables, the sheet sends and receives information.
That's why is important to connect the wires correctly.
Now, I'm adding the straps; they're like the robot's shoes.
Hold on, they're a bit too tight.
And done.
Now, I'm adding a type of lids, they help the straps stay put.
And we're almost done.
Now, I'm connecting its power source, that is to say, the batteries.
Actually, we need to upload the program to the sheet, but because of lack of time, I downloaded it earlier.
I'm going to test it with a program to guide it with my cellphone.
Just a second while it loads.
(Applause) Well, that's it!
I hope you enjoyed it. And please remember that if I can make it, you can make it too.
(Applause)
All my professional life I worked for a multinational company that manufactures, among others, plastic chairs.
Over 100 million units of these chairs were produced worldwide
and were a worldwide success.
We asked why it was so successful?
We achieved to optimize the issues of design, price, and quality.
In reality, we "democratized" the products,
to make the them affordable to everyone.
With these ideas and experience, I decided that this is the right time in my life to bring this knowledge to the benefit of people struggling in the world.
I wanted also to achieve a lifelong dream,
to develop an affordable wheelchair for developing countries.
to bring some relief to that general need.
When we started to investigate existing wheelchairs in the world we realized what is already known: They are a medical device; they have a shady appearance, uncomfortable in some cases and expensive in some countries.
With this knowledge in hand, we realized we need to change paradigms
in order to change fixed ideas,
and as we did in my previous job in order to change the paradigms of design, cost and price.
Also we needed to change another idea that the "business world" where I came from and the "social impact world" generally, work in opposite directions
and we wanted to revert this as well.
With the support of my spouse and partner Ms. Chava Rothstein, we went on a research mission to the World Health Organization in Switzerland.
We asked them to learn about the needs
and the numbers that we heard were impressive
there are over 20 million people with a permanent need of wheelchairs,
specially in developing areas.
At that moment we did not have a clear plan but
we had the intuition that we can bring a better product reducing costs in a dramatic way.
bringing a better solution
After that meeting, at the World Health Organization in which fortunately, they did not kick us out of their offices ... they actually challenged us to show it is possible.
Then we meet the UNICEF team in India the United Nations Children's Fund and we understood from them that the need among children in the developing countries is also dramatic;
over seven million wheelchairs are needed permanently.
And we asked ourselves why?
Probably due to different priorities, to a lack of budgets to prejudices and different reasons that countries, people and all of us, we "relegate" the issue of disabilities.
We also learned from UNICEF about the relation between disabilities and education,
and the numbers are also frightening .
Ninety percent of children with disabilities in developing countries do not attend schools.
Nine out of 10 children do not take part in the educational process.
With these compelling numbers and the understanding of the UNESCO research on the critical influence of illiteracy in the economy of the different countries we decided that in order to generate a change; we need to investigate this issue more comprehensively.
This allowed us to focus our vision: Provide mobility, empower access to education and improve the future and independence for children with disabilities.
From here we defined the developing guidelines of the project.
From the children’s perspective, we believed that design would be the main change factor.
We needed to bring a more joyful wheelchair
that the child can feel better with, creating a different attitude towards disability among their close circle of friends and family.
With regard to the institutions' perspective, we wanted to change the relation of price and quality that limits their capacity to provide wheelchairs. to those in need.
From here we started a developing plan we create a multidisciplinary team to benchmark the exiting solutions and to understand what needs to be changed and improved.
It took about two years to investigate the alternatives,
and finally the chair was created.
Or as we call it, the champion!
The most emotional part was when we provided the first prototypes for test only,
the kids did't want to return them. They wanted to keep them
because of the change that the chair generated in the attitude of their close circle and in themselves towards their disability.
In reality we created a new approach toward disabilities a super hero in the world of disabilities.
We understood that we actually touched an important point as it allows to view disabilities from a different perspective.
To see disability beyond the irremediable which is obvious, but to see the hope that can create a more positive attitude of the child and their surrounding.
From here we actually refined the name of our project "Wheelchairs of Hope".
We were very happy that in December, 2016, we started the provision of wheelchairs worldwide.
We have already provided wheelchairs in Peru, Chile, Israel, Palestine, Ukraine, Vietnam and Swaziland.
And soon in South Africa, Panama, Ethiopia.
We are very ... excited ... with the possibility that this project will be implemented in Argentina for the benefit of those children in need.
We believe it is a proper solution that might bring a new approach in order to suit the needs of these children.
And to end my talk, for those of you that wish to get involved in social impact activities, in order to change the world and improve people's life, we summarized several points that we believe are important for all social impact entrepreneur in any area in which you plan to work:
Choose a need that can create an impact and honestly evaluate the impact it can bring.
Second, do not be afraid to change existing concepts, to change paradigms,
it is part of the social world we wish to change.
Choose an experienced multidisciplinary team. It helps a lot to stay focused.
Be ready to fail and continue.

It is inherent to any project.
You need to have that strength.
Lastly, believe deeply
in what you are doing.
Dream permanently that it is possible.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Can you imagine riding on a bus with passengers who don't know where they are going?
Can you imagine riding on that bus but also having no control or power to decide where you want to go?
Also, that bus was bought with your money, you filled up the tank,
you chose and hired the driver behind the wheel, but you are traveling blind.
Can you imagine?
It would be worrisome, right?
I would worry.
That bus is called Representative Democracy, and it presents three big problems that make the majority of us be passengers of that bus. And today I want to analyze those problems we saw on the screen living and studying them in a country that is representative, despite the redundancy, of this system, as Uruguay.
Uruguay is one of the best democracies in the world and the strongest democracy in Latin America along with Costa Rica.
If there are issues here, I assure you in the rest of Latin America they are more profound as in most of the rest of the world.
The first of these problems is the lack of representation.
We, periodically, in elections choose representatives, but which are wrongly named because they don't represent us, because they decide for us following other interests that are not the ones of the voters.
Let's look at some cases, in particular, of the three main political parties in Uruguay.
On occasions when, unfortunately, was probed.
When they voted to cancel the interpretive project of the Expiry Law.
This law pardoned the crimes committed by the military during the last dictatorship.
98 percent of the representatives of the Frente Amplio, the party that's been in power for more than 10 years, voted in favor of this annulment but only 52 percent of its voters agreed with it.
98 to 52.
When the Voluntary Pregnancy Interruption Law was voted on, or more commonly known as the "Abortion Law," all of the representatives from the Partido Nacional were against its approval, but only 45 percent of their voters agreed with them.
If we take a look and compare the votes from the Partido Colorado, another of Uruguay's historical party, to the voters' needs, the difference is even bigger.
So, who are they representing?
What are those people doing in there, in those four walls if they are not fulfilling their main purpose?
I invite you to find out what did your representative vote in the legislative area in your city, in your country, to see if they really vote for what you thought was best.
Unfortunately, most times, that is not the case.
Another big problem is that it's not a transparent system.
Surely many have the same feeling about this and we can see situations, but I want to mention one in particular.
When there are different issues, distrust, of mismanagement in government, in public entities, the creation of investigative commissions is requested to shed light on these facts.
In a systematic form, in the last period, the Frente Amplio, the ruling party, has denied the formation to these commissions.
I'm not attacking Frente Amplio because it happens with other parties.
I'm not attacking the representative in particular
I'm attacking, or talking about the system having this problem which has mechanisms to hide information of vital importance to the people.
Here we see various cases that happened in the last period, and many are about public finances, of public entities where all of us are the main investors.
If it was a business, it would be unheard of investors not being able to access their companies' finances.
And the third big problem is that it brings a legislative job of very poor quality.
Unfortunately, unfortunately, the quality, and the content of the laws, their impact, how many people it'll impact, and how profound the impact will be, it is not important.
It isn't crucial when deciding to pass or not pass the law.
What matters is the origin, who wrote it, what political party presented it.
On the last period, half of the projects submitted by the Frente Amplio were approved, but only 10 percent of the submitted projects by the Partido Nacional passed and an even smaller number of the submitted projects by the Colorado representatives.
Here we see it isn't important to legislate for the common good which should be the primary duty of the whole parliamentary system, representative, but what matters is saving their farms, their political party and the political career of each of the representatives.
We have a system that is notoriously malfunctioning, that is harming us.
Therefore, why do we all approve of it?
Because we all implicitly approve of it with our silence.
Let me tell you: Between March 1st of 2010 and December 31st, 2013 in Uruguay's Chamber of Deputies 1,243 bills were submitted.
1,243 some of them, not all, I know, but some of them influence vital parts of everyday life like education, security, economy.
So I asked all Uruguayans that are present here: Do you know at least 10 percent of the bills that were submitted?
Raise your hand, those who know 12 bills that were submitted in the last period.
Six? Five?
I see there one that knows five, well, not everything is lost, good.
But regrettably we don't know what is happening, that's why today I said that we are, we are passengers on a bus which we don't know where is going.
Today I want to invite you to dream with me but with our feet on the ground.
How could we give a solution to these problems using the technology and tools we have now.
How could we have a system where everyone can participate using, I repeat, the technology we have and that grants representation, transparency and high-quality work and I want us to analyze the main activities that our representatives develop today.
First, the development of the laws and for this, I want to share two particular experiences from which we can learn a lot.
First, the initiative for the cooperative development of Iceland's first constitution.
A couple of years ago this country wanted to develop its first constitution and randomly appointed 55 representatives, is a country with a small population, but they were random, didn't have political careers of different backgrounds.
Meanwhile, these representatives opened an enquiry through social media sites like Facebook and Twitter and they received proposals for the articles they wanted to add to the constitution.
A project for a constitution was reached unfortunately it was stopped in Parliment but anyway I consider this case a success because it showed the technology we have today like social networks could develop a document of high complexity like a constitution and a very interesting percentage of the population participated.
Another case that most of you probably know is the one of Wikipedia, the largest encyclopedia in the world that was developed and keeps developing collaboratively on the Internet.
Some of you may think that there are some errors, some mistrust of some material but in general it's an encyclopedia we all consult and that's built by everyone on the Internet.
But to create great laws, we have to consult, we have to train ourselves, and for that what better than to promote the participation of experts, of advisors from different areas.
Today our representatives consult these experts but if we did it through an online platform where we applied the best recent practices to develop laws a much greater quantity of experts could participate and take advantage of a greater collective knowledge.
The participation barrier would be lower, in comparison to the time and resources dedicated today to actively participate in the political arena.
You can share comments on the platform video calls or other digital resources that we could use.
And what can we do for the debate and the voting of laws?
Which we saw is not representative, is not transparent, and which we saw is not developing high-quality laws.
We could really make it representative, if we open it through the Internet.
I want to share a case that has worked and that you can look up on the Internet which is the platform Democracia en Red, from Partido de la Red, in Buenos Aires.
When a law is put to vote, an accessible summary is published so any person can read it, understand it, and know what's voted free of legal jargon and technicalities.
If that person is interested they can enter and see the original text, vote yes, abstain or vote no, and something that's equally or more vital, I think, is participation on the debate.
Bringing an argument, an opinion and that way achieving a quality debate.
I didn't come to propose a magical solution.
I'm not proposing a utopia, but simply from a position of collaboration and tolerance of neither saying that everything there is, is bad today but show to you that there are tools that can solve the problems that we are suffering from because we put up with it as well, and we can improve the results of the work that is developed today in the legislative branch.
We could enjoy of the system this way.
Not too long ago we were at a plaza in Montevideo, we were shooting a video about this, sharing this idea and well, I was done filming my part and I approached a group of four guys that were drinking mate, they asked "Dude and this, what's it?
What are you talking about?"
I tell them, there are some questions, and at the end, there were two people super in favor and went "Well, I want to participate, keep me informed."
There was a third that did not pay me any attention at all he kept talking through WhatsApp on his phone, he didn't lift his head for a minute.
There was another that was against it, against it, totally against it.
And he said to me, exactly: "You are crazy if you think I'm going to vote for laws, when we're already paying others to do it, you are crazy."
I looked at him and said: It makes sense!
I'm telling him of a platform he'd participate on and maybe he is not interested, but I replied, with a question: "When there's news you dislike, that you aren't into, on Facebook Don't you comment? Don't you publish
your opinion?
Or comment that you are not in agreement?
He says: "Yes, yes, you're right," and he told me a story, I forget now, the price of something had gone up and he had posted and commented about it.
I said: "Well, that comment is not useful."
Those unorganized comments on Facebook and on Twitter. We are all left full: "Oh, great, I'm participating."
It's a lie!
We have a delusion of participation on Facebook and Twitter with the exceptions of great group movements that are fantastic - today we saw one that is brilliant - but those unorganized comments, those comments about each topic are useless, it gives an illusion of participation.
But if we really had a platform on which the system brings participation and the hope to influence there would be that possibility to influence.
This would bring the possibility to participate on the work, not the obligation, the possibility to make something that we don't have today.
A big challenge this system has in favor and against it at the same time is the access to Internet.
I say in favor, because Internet is a technology that has developed at a great speed.
For example to get to the first 50 million people it only took four years.
If we look at television, it took 13 years and radio, 18 years.
But also, is true, there are a lot of people that have no access to Internet: two in five people in Latin America.
And there are inequalities among those who access.
That's why this, implementing and solving this problems that we all suffer from, it's a step by step way
The first objective is to decrease the digital gap so we can all be connected and can participate.
That's why, it's a path that we are going to build together a healthy democracy for everyone and by everyone.
That's why, online you will find all the information I used during today's talk, for those who want to know a bit more, who want to join different initiatives which are driving this change at a global level, you will also find them there.
Today we have a unique opportunity to stop being witnesses of historical events and become protagonists.
We are very connected, never had so many possibilities to make ourselves heard.
Don't you think it would be favorable for all of us to be able to fix the problems we saw using today's technology?
For those who said yes, I saw a few heads asserting, luckily, let me say, you gave the first step for this can be executed because together we'll implement it.
It will not be done by experts neither spontaneously by the representatives who have been benefiting from this system for years, but by all of us.
If it's not us, who?
And the second step is to believe that is possible, that it can be implemented.
I know many will say this as naive: "How are you going to improve democracy!"
"Are you crazy!
It's always been like this!"
And I've heard it been said.
But the naivety leaves when the small actions and initiatives show that it is possible.
And there are many initiatives that are already showing it.
We are the ones who support this system.
We are the reason to be a part of this system.
So, we are the ones to improve it.
Until today they have made us, they have made the majority of us passengers of this bus, but today we have the opportunity, a real opportunity to say if we want to continue being passengers or want to be the drivers of that bus and take it to a better destination.
The decision is in each one of you.
Thank you very much.
(Applause) [It always seems impossible until it's done, Nelson Mandela.]
(Applause)
Last year I was diagnosed with a birth deformity, a calcified diverticulum.
This condition caused me a series of health complications, very serious infections that made me seriously ill.
So, getting to the diagnosis wasn't easy, because it's not very common.
So, I had to stay in the hospital for a long time.
During all this time, many professionals were there to help me get better.
Their intervention was essential because they were all necessary for my recovery.
If any of these people are listening to me, I'd like to thank them, heartily.
At the end of this process, I was fortunate enough to find a specialist who knew exactly what I had.
And also, most importantly, how to recover from it.
To reach a definitive solution.
Something I realize many people don't have as a possibility.
They told me I had to have surgery.
Well, I'd have to have surgery.
I wasn't scared.
Conversely, I felt
very happy and very fortunate, because I could come out at the end of this problem.
And what I'm talking about now so briefly, took me a long time.
I lost the entire school year, last year.
I lost so much time, they say, but I'd like to think I gained it.
I gained experience, I gained the will to fight.
I gained a lot of values. I came to know myself.
There are moments in life, difficult situations in which, although we have many people around us, supporting us, we alone are the ones who have to face them.
They also say that when you go through a difficult situation in your life, may it be an illness or any other case, for instance, an accident, a difficult economic situation, a difficult family situation, the loss of your job.
Well, when you get over it, you get back to the rhythm of your previous life.
Today I come to tell you that this is not so.
Everything changes, when you return to your previous life, to these obligations that marked a constant movement, to return to class, to work, to the English academy on Thursdays, everything has changed.
And the world has changed, during this time, you have not been able to advance, you have had to stop.
This means that we have to adapt to a series of changes, because life is change and changes is necessary.
These changes make us feel bewildered and restless, and we think that maybe something bad is going to happen to us.
However, I think that changes are opportunities in disguise.
I would like to point out the importance of seeing situations in a positive way because I am aware that I am an 18-year-old girl, and I am a normal and ordinary girl, like many others, but I would like to contribute what I learned from my experience and it is to look at things in a certain way to see the good there is in them.
For this, I want to tell you a legend that I was told at the hospital.
This legend lit a light bulb on my head.
Legend has it that the famous King Solomon long ago wanted to test his most loyal minister and gave him an impossible task.
To find a ring with magical powers.
The requirement of this ring was that if a sad man looked upon it, he had to rejoice, and if a happy man looked upon it, he would be saddened.
"What a strange thing," the minister thought. "It's impossible."
Obviously the King had done it to prove his fidelity.
That was impossible.
He set him a deadline to find it and the minister left to look for this ring.
And when the deadline was running out, he was in one of Jerusalem's poorest neighborhoods, and he saw a jeweler, a jeweler out there, and asked him: "Hey, have you ever heard of this ring?"
And the jeweler said, "Yes." He took out a gold ring, engraved it, and gave it to the minister. and the Minister, so pleased, said: "Great, I'll take it to the King," then he left, took the ring to King Solomon, just in time, and the King was very surprised to see that he had succeeded.
He observed the ring and said: "This is a normal ring!" Then he read the jeweler's engraving.
The engraving said, "This too shall pass."
and realized that all his immense knowledge, his great material possessions, all his money, were perishable things, and that at some point it would be nothing but dust.
"This too shall pass" is a phrase that can be disconcerting when we are having a good time, but in bad times, we can focus on them because it is a reminder that negative situations are transient, temporary, however timeless they seem, because we get engulfed in that swirl of negativity that comes with them.
Good situations, on the other hand, are not eternal either.
We cannot think that we will enjoy a perfect situation forever, we have to be aware of this and value it, and when we have it, take advantage of it and enjoy the small moments, the small details, the smiles, the time with our family and friends, our families, our work, our vacations, enjoy everything.
I think that negative situations are always unexpected because, who expects something negative
to happen to them all the time? Nobody.
To better confront the negative situations we have to take into account these small details of which I speak.
I also think that the key is not to see the situations as in black and white, but with different shades of gray.
There are bad situations that aren't completely black.
In fact, situations aren't ever completely black, there's always a light, and there's always something positive we can learn.
In my case, what I learned was a vocation.
I stayed in hospitals for a long time living with other patients; with many professionals; nursing assistants who brought me juices at night when I was going to sleep, and told me stories; and many wonderful doctors. And I discovered I wanted to be a doctor.
Because I wanted to help other people in the future, too.
So I've come to tell you that, although I am aware that there are much worse cases than mine, I want you to look for the good, because negative situations always have that learning to contribute, that tastes like that sweet flavor that some coffees leave.
I come to say that ... what we have to do, instead of waiting for the situations of our life to bring us happiness, is bring happiness to all the situations of our life.
Every single one of them.
Because, in reality, it is our thoughts that build reality.
I also want to say that I don't know much about life, I'm here talking about life and I have no idea, sometimes I think all I know is that I don't like Mondays, and sometimes my toast gets burned.
I just want you to learn to see things in a positive way and I encourage you to remember that your ultimate goal in life is to be happy.
I've talked about studying medicine, which is my goal, I love it, I'm passionate, of course.
But there's something above this, which is to be happy.
I remember, when I was a little girl, the kindergarten teacher asked me what I wanted to be when I grew up.
I'd say,"I want to be happy."
And he told me that I hadn't understood the question.
Now I've grown up and I believe that my knowledge allows me to grasp the question well.
I'd still be answering the same thing.
I want to reflect on what the world would mean, what the world would look like if we were all happy.
Because it'd be much better, there wouldn't be so many injustices.
Everything would be much better.
For this, we only have to change the way we see the negative and also follow our heart, what it tells us.
Because the others around us tell us to follow many paths, but that is not usually the path our hearts dictate.
We have our own path to follow.
Our path has an end, but until then we have plenty of time to walk, to live, and to write our own history.
One hundred and sixty-three. This is the number of doors I walked through this morning from the time I left my house until I arrived here.
Normally, I don't go around counting doors.
It's not a calculation that particularly interests me.
But it was necessary for today's theme.
Doors are structures that usually go unnoticed in our daily life. But I propose the following suggestion.
Please place your hands in your pockets.
What do you have in them?
I came here with my house keys, my phone, the usual things.
Please check your pockets, not your neighbor's.
(Laughter) The phone, the keys.
If you think about it, your keys are obviously related to doors, but also your phone, your identity card which permits you to travel within the European Union, your credit cards
so you can go to banks to withdraw or deposit money.
Essentially, 50% of the items in your pockets are directly related to doors. And nevertheless, they go largely unnoticed.
Hardly anyone pays attention to doors. They don't even have a good definition.
In one dictionary, "A door is an opening in a wall which serves as an exit or entrance." And the very next definition of "door" is a "leaf" as in a door leaf.
As there is no real agreement on a definition, today I propose an alternative definition of "door."
A door is an opportunity for change.
It's not a bad definition.
Doors have a great vibe when they are open.
(Laughter) While a door slammed in your face is never interpreted positively, opening up doors, getting your foot in the door, open houses, open-door policy are expressions which transmit a certain warmth.
Open doors are welcoming.
Do you know why?
As an architect, I've asked myself this for a while, but I realize that the best explanation of the significance of open doors is given by an anthropologist, this old charmer, Arnold van Gennep.
He concluded that all societies are structured around a series of rooms with passageways and that the more civilized a society, the more the rites of passage from one room to the next are open and articulated.
To me, this is an interpretation which is particularly interesting because it has various meanings. The links between the rites of passage from our birth or marriage or bachelorette party or bachelor party or even a graduation are rites of passages in which a person enters into a new situation.
The number of these civilized societies, according to van Gennep, is constantly increasing.
Van Gennep's second conclusion actually has to do with the number of doors as an index or measurement of civilization.
Civilizations with the greatest number of doors can serve as the index. In my opinion, it would be interesting to classify a society based on this number with which we began.
Let's imagine, for example, that a society instead of being regarded as civilized by its energy consumption would instead be categorized by its number of doors
or by its literacy rate.
It's not a bad idea, right?
In fact, a number of doors in the West, as van Gennep observed, were becoming less visible at that time.
What surprises me is that van Gennep's discovery came at a time when Europe had gone through the Second World War, and specifically in a moment when Europe should've been opening doors.
And he encountered a situation, especially in Germany, where there should have been the most reconciliation in the world, in which a place was completely divided, overnight, by a wall.
We know this as the Berlin Wall.
In actuality, it was erected in two days in August 1961, first as a barbed wire fence, and immediately following, it was constructed as a concrete wall so well-known by everyone.
This was the first victim:
Günter Litfin. He tried to open a door.
He was a tailor who lived on the west side of the wall. He helped his brother move homes and ended up being trapped. He tried to cross over, but the soldiers along the border shot him.
In actuality, we were always told that the Berlin Wall fell in 1989 in November.
We all saw it. We all have the image stuck in our mind: the memory of the wall breaking apart and opening completely.
But in reality, for our discussion of doors, the first ones to break through and create openings in the Berlin Wall were the victims who were commemorated along with Günter Litfin. They created these openings, these doors.
Doors are opportunities for change, but undoubtedly, they require heroes.
At times, doors can even lead to a positive outcome.
This is the story of the other creator of a door in the Berlin Wall.
Meixner worked in West Berlin but was crossing over and had permission to go to the east side.
Each day, he crossed over for work.

He fell in love with Margarete Thurau.
However, Margarete was not allowed to cross over to the other side.
So he boldly decided to create a new door. He stopped by the border at Checkpoint Charlie, the exit to the west side, measured it at just over 35 inches. He crossed again and surveyed all the possible rental car options in West Berlin.
Not one measured less than 35 inches.
Nevertheless, he did the following. He disassembled the windshield, gaining just under three inches, lowered the tire pressure, gaining a little over half an inch, placed his beloved in the backseat of the car, his future mother-in-law ... (Laughter) in the trunk, (Laughter) placed 30 bricks around her in case the guards would shoot at them, and approached the border at Checkpoint Charlie. He was nervous, which was to be expected of anyone risking his life. The guards saw this and were about to detain him, but in the moment before checking his identification, Meixner stepped on the gas and crossed the border.
Can you imagine the sensation?
Can you imagine the feeling of crossing through this door, leaving the rest of the world behind you in shock?
It's a sensation we have all experienced.
I dedicate 80% of my time as a docent and architect fighting to experience this thrill.
It's the feeling when we've learned something new, the moment we get married, when we go through a significant event in our lives.
It is always the sensation of passing through a door.
Doors are always opportunities for change.
I have a confession to make.
Being here is a priviledge, but I realize that I have always regarded TED Talks as monumental.
Awe-inspiring.
What do you do here?
What do you discuss in a TED Talk?
Even Obama has been here;
the whole world has been here.
I recognize that only in this moment, I've become aware that all of these talks that took place before and the ones that you will hear later, in reality, are doors.
They are doorways that reveal new places to explore.
They are doors that open up new paths.
I encourage you, if you are so inclined - many will be half open - to approach one and to open it.
It's in your hands.
It even seems easy.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Have you ever asked yourself about the meaning of music?
The truth is that it seems a simple question if we think about a song.
But what really happens with instrumental music?
Does it really have a meaning or not?
To be honest, I have to admit that I don't have the answer.
The thing is that, since more than 2000 years ago, philosophers, composers and musicians have been asking the same question.
Greeks explained it like this: On the one hand, there was the virtuous Apollo, Zeus' most cherished son, God of Order.
Tall, handsome, attractive, the perfect son in law.
On the other hand, there was Dionysius, his stepbrother: an absolute disaster.
He liked joking, partying, bacchanals, and he was quite a boozer.
Actually, what Greeks wanted to tell us with this is that the correct music was what Apollo represented because Apollo was associated with the lyre.
The lyre is an instrument that is played while singing. so there were words and, if there were words, there was a meaning.
However, his shameless stepbrother, played a kind of wind instrument. You can't sing and play at the same time, so his music was instrumental.
There were no words, so there was no meaning.
It was pure pleasure.
And now, we reached "pleasure".
If we talk about The Church,
imagine this concept 2000 years ago...
For the emerging Church, this was very opportune, because if there was music to sing the word of God and the Scriptures, there was no place for that music which only look for pleasure, of course.
And that music was meant to be banned, in case it was possible.
That was what part of the Greeks and the emerging Church thought.
But, anyway, instrumental music was still being played for centuries.
Composers always were a bit doubtful about this because, even though that music was still played, it remained in the background compared to the music with words, the vocal music.
In order to solve this question, they used different resources.
Musical resources to express those ideas, to describe those landscapes, those feelings.
Let's give two simple examples of music that everybody knows, you don't have to be an expert.
If we listen to The Pastoral Symphony by Beethoven, suddenly it is like if you got into that green meadow, with those streams, you nearly can touch the trees, the animals.
Or in Vivaldi's Four Seasons, the most famous, of course, is Spring movement, isn't it?
It's energetic, full of power. However, Winter is cold and dark, with these short, strident, high-pitched notes of the violins,
which reflects it perfectly.
Sometimes, the main title is what gives us the pattern or the clue of what that music means.
This not only happens in classic music, but also in flamenco.
For example, we can allude the sceneries in Paco de Lucía's childhood in plays like "Plaza alta", "En la caleta" "La Barrosa", the famous beach.
Or plays about love, like "Casilda" or "Mi Niño Curro", dedicated to his son. I specially like "Oración", by Manolo Sanlúcar, another guitarrist who carries you to a mystical and spiritual world.
There is something that Romantics did in the 19th century, and I love it. In order to make the audience understand and connect with the message of the play, the artists explained it at the beginning of the concerts: it could be read in the concert programme or described by the director.
So that they were able to get into the meaning of the play.
Like a guide to the musical audition.
I would like to do something like that today, and get us into that magical world of the music.
To share experiences.
It's something personal, so you have to keep the secret, please.
Keep it under your hat.
The music I'm going to play now tells about a day in my life.
Actually, it is something that has happened to anybody.
It consists of four parts.
The first one is the moment when my heart was beating out.
I was going to meet a very special person. She texted me asking where I was because I hadn't arrived yet.
And we barely had five minutes to see each other.
The second part describes doubt, that moment when you wonder: "Is it something real or am I just imagining it?"
"Do I mean something to her or is she playing with me?"
There was a moment in which you wonder about anything in just a few seconds.
Finally, the moment when we meet had arrived.
It was like the time stopped.
Honestly, I barely remember what we talked about. Maybe it's not so important.
But I remember that she couldn't look me in the eyes and she couldn't stop saying that someone could see us there.
In the end, we said goodbye and I left I left walking slowly and thinking.
I had a strange mixture of emotions which defines the fourth part.
On the one hand, there was happiness but, without knowing the reason, happiness was mixing with sorrow, hopelessness, a huge emptiness.
As I foresaw that I could never see her again in the same way.
A few days later she told me: "It's the wrong time." And I have called this work like that. I would like to share it with you.
If you want to turn it into a much more personal experience you can close your eyes and live it from the inside.
Because the music might not have a specific message but it may be its greatest value, using that universal language what reaches directly into our hearts.
(Tunes the guitar) (Starts playing) (Finishes playing) (Applause)
When I chose to become a dermatologist, it was a big disappointment for my parents.
My mother from Valladolid, from Ureña, told me, "My son, with your CV you should have chosen a medical specialty to save lives; instead, you'll be looking at warts and lumps your whole life".
I'd like to show you three things: First, my mother was mistaken, and second, dermatology is a great help changing people's lives through the skin. And third, a doctor who only knows about medicine doesn't know anything in fact.
Why did I choose dermatology?
When I was a medical student at the hospital, we went to see a patient at the ICU. The patient was in a bad state, with injuries all over the body; the prognostic was bad. and he had also ulcers on his legs. The reason why a dermatologist was called.
The dermatologist explored the patient and said, "The patient is allergic to one of the creams that you are using."
Two days later, the team at the hospital stopped the cream and he got better.
I though that was incredible. A specialization, that within seconds, allows you to make a diagnosis, to start a treatment and to help a patient.
Let see, for example, Ines.
Ines came to our practice because she had a redness over their eyelids, and was very tired.
She was diagnosed with dermatomyositis, a sort of illness associated with some cancers. It turned to be true; Inés had breast cancer. She was treated and saved, because the cancer was in the early stages.
It was possible to help Ines, thanks to her skin.
Another example, Beatriz, came with these spots.
These white spots are known as vitiligo.
Beatriz was diagnosed with an illness of thyroid, sometimes associated with vitiligo.
Lucia: Lucia came to our practice because
she had these coffee milk spots and they were caused by a neurofibromatocis, an illness associated with brain tumours.
Lucía had a little cerebral tumor, which was treated and cured.
Dermatologists, we can diagnose many illnesses through skin.
But it is possible not only through the skin, but also through hair and nails.
Dermatology is the external face of the internal medicine.
In fact, dermatologists are the managers of the imperfections, some of them medical, and some others aesthetics.
Skin cancer is one of worst imperfections.
Did you know that here, four in ten people will be affected by skin cancer?
The statistics are harsh.
In the United States, every minute, a melanoma case is diagnosed.
And every hour a patient dies due to a melanoma because it is one of the most aggressive cancers.
Here is the case of Mateo who came to our practice because of one melanoma on his back.
The melanoma was extirpated, but the injury was very deep and the tumour has already spread to the brain and other organs.
Later, came his brother, who was very anxious about that,
and his own back which was full of freckles. One of them was a melanoma.
Which?
It is difficult to know at first glance;
this was the one.
Then, with a machine known as a digital dermascope, we can see the freckles inside. And know which will be bad or not. In other words, skin talks us.
What happens is that sometimes we need a higher technology to understand its language.
This was the melanoma, and in the case of melanomas around 100% of the cases are cured if diagnosed on time.
Let's look at Julian.
He came due to light acne. He had gone two years without leaving home.
He had no friends, no studies, and he was deeply affected.
Acne affects 90% teens, and for them, whose personality is not structured yet, this illness affect them greatly.
In this case, Julian was treated with a drug, and he was cured in 6 months.
His life changed thanks to his skin.
Or in the case of Jose Ignacio who had a hemangioma.
An hemangioma is a dilated blood vessel problem.
Let's see with a drug known as Propanolol, a drug which was known to be useful for hemangiomas 3 years ago.
Let's see before and after. It was possible to help him, and to change life of the whole family, through the improvements with the modern dermatology
Maria came to our practice because she looked like she was suffering from tonsillitis. She started developing some illness on the back, legs, arms and hair.
Maria had psoriasis,
which affects 5% Spanish population.
Maria started changing her way of dressing, going out less often, and avoiding the swimming pool or the beach. She even started to look for miraculous treatments on the internet. Finally she came to our practice and received a treatment known as "light therapy", which is just taking sun. Only with sun, the psoriasis disappeared.
Now, Maria has a normal life and the psoriasis is under control.
Before I carry on, I'd like to tell you something that happened this morning.
I was running because I had not been in Valladolid since I was a child, so, I went running to river shore, and I found a beautiful landscape. You realize how, like the changing skin of the banks of the river, you are also changing people's lives here.
People are practising paddle surf, canoeing, volley, practising sports. It's similar to Albania, capital of Tirana, which was a grey, and sad city three years ago; typical post-communism city, with lots of delinquency and the Mayor decided to paint all houses in brilliant colours.
A month later, delinquency had fallen by a 90%.
So, if we change the skin of things and that affects our behaviours, changing our skin will affect us greatly.
I am going to tell you the story of Adriana.
Adriana came to our practice because as she said, "Doctor, I do not recognise myself when I am watching myself in the mirror.
What can I do?"
The battle against aging is a lost battle since we're born.
But it is also true that modern dermatology has some techniques that can help our patients to become old with elegance and discretion.
We made a combination of treatments. She sent me an email some months ago, telling me, "My self-esteem is much better,
my relationships have improved, and I have just been promoted at work."
And the most important, "When watching myself in the mirror, how I look matches how I feel."
It means that aesthetic dermatology, as frivolous as it can sound, can help people.
It is true that if we're only looking for an ideal of beauty, the final results can be grotesque. And when looking around us, we can see very attractive people who have asymmetric eyebrows, big noses and prominent mouths; their faces are imperfect, however, they are very attractive.
It is known that a smile is the best first impression, rather than what clothing or surgery can do. Even if the smile is squint. As happens with the smile of my wife and my daughter Adriana.
Dermatologist has the duty to tell people when less is more, and that we have to live with our physical imperfections.
Picasso said, "We have to look for beauty within imperfection."
We should not be obsessed with removing the wrinkles of life but to put more life in our wrinkles.
Did you know that many of the techniques used in aesthetic dermatology are also used in medical dermatology?
An example is facial paralysis;
There are many patients with facial paralysis, and thanks to Botox, which relax muscles, they can improve their condition, and he quality of their lives. Patients have more symmetrical faces.
Or in the case of María José
who came to our practice because she was suffering from Hyperhidrosis.
Hyperdrosis is sweating too much, by armpits or hands. She destroyed the keyboard on her computer, her clothes, and she was very worried about her wedding day.
Finally, she was injected with Botox and she could enjoy her happy wedding.
What will the future of dermatology be?
The future is fascinating.
New and personalised drugs are being developed, according to patient's genetic code, or a tumor's genetic code.
It is possible to make a diagnosis via distance thanks to teledermatology. We are collaborating with some African organizations and they send us pictures to make a diagnosis, or start treatment.
Mother cells.
Did you know that the biggest store of mother cells is in our skin?
In the fat of the skin.
But now, I'd like to ponder, because we are talking about advances, the Internet, mother cells, high technology. And we cannot forget something very important: the patient.
Sometimes, doctors treat the illness very well, but not the patient.
When talking about health systems, either public, or private, concerted, or all of them, the best health system should be based on the patient.
The real personalized medicine is to be sitting down with the patient listening to them, touching them and being involved in their problems.
I remember a boss who always told me, "Every time a patient is coming with a very wide psoriasis or some sort of skin illness, you must sit down with the patient and touch them without gloves to show them that you're not rejecting them.
Because in medicine, as in many other fields there is much information but little wisdom.
It's time to come back to the roots of medicine. Which means, what the Mayo brothers said when they set up the Mayo Clinic, "Necessities of patients are the first".
As you can see, there is a fascinating relationship between skin and mind. Many skin patients suffer from depression or anxiety due to illness, but it also can happen vice versa.
There are many changes, for example, our skin gets red when we are nervous, our hair falls out often when we are anxious, or dermatitis is worsening. Or psoriasis.
Did you know that brain cells and skin cells came from the same embrionary cells?
This is something very important. because our skin is not just a cover on our body, but an organ. The biggest organ that we have.
It is an active organ in contact with all our being, and it is an organ which speaks to us, telling us what is happening inside physically and mentally.
One of the most difficult talks was a few months ago, when I had to talk at my four-year old daughter's school.
The talk was about being a doctor, and being a dermatologist.
I started by asking them a question, "What is most cool about being a doctor?"
A boy quickly got up and said, "You can see people naked"
(Laughter) And then a girl said, "You can help lots of people."
And it's true, the best thing about being a doctor is that you can help lots of people because although a cure is not always possible, at least we can mitigate pain
and as Nietzche said, "The doctor who cannot understand a soul wil never understand a body."
So, two messages for everybody to take home from today's talk: First, We can change people's lives throughh their skin and second, the doctor who only knows about medicine doesn't know anything.
I would like to finish with this case:
my mother.
Here she is with my six month old daughter. It was 4 years ago.
She was diagnosed with lung cancer,
and breast cancer, a year later. So she has received during this time chemotherapy, radiotherapy, etc.
You might not believe it, but the biggest concern for my mother was losing her hair; a secondary effect of the treatment.
It can sound frivolous but around 90% cancer patients are very worried about losing hair and eyebrows.
Modern dermatology can help these patients a lot; controlling the secondary effects from chemo and radio, over hair, skin and nails.
So, I would like to finish saying that finally my mother is not bothered anymore by the fact that her son became a dermatologist, because she has learned that there is nothing deeper, than our own skin.
Thanks.
(Applause)
One day in Córdoba, like in any other Latin American city, on a hot Monday morning, as I arrived at the office I found out that part of the staff was missing because of a public transport strike.
Just one more of many we have already had.
Thousands of people that couldn't go to work.
Thousands that couldn't go to college.
Thousands that wouldn't be able to fullfill their dreams.
And just in that moment, I was also wondering what to do with my future.
I was into several projects, some of them I liked, but I needed something more exciting, something that really thrilled me.
So I started looking into the advantages and disadvantages of different means of transport: buses, cars, bikes... and then I realized that the bike is the one with the brightest future.
Why do I say this?
Because it's one of the most efficient and sustainable means of transport.
Despite that, since it's been created, almost 200 years ago, it remains the same, exactly the same as the ones created 200 years ago.
It isn't this way with others, such us the car, the plane, etc, which indeed they had an innovative quantum leap.
I told myself,
"Good. I'll do it, this is going to be a market niche for innovation".
The thing was, how do I start?
I was broke, and being an economist I was an outsider to the technology world.
Anyway, I decided to get along with it, but other fears had been added up:
How am I going to make it through with all those big fishes in the market?
Like BMW, Audi, and like thouse huge companies that are also innovating in the urban bicycles field?
Another common question that innovators are stalked by is: How to do this from Argentina, where the context isn't perhaps the most suitable?
So I remembered that time when I was 10 or 11 years old,
and I was a tiny one-metre tall boy.
Not that I have changed too much.
I used to love cars. I still do.
I was living in Posadas, Misiones, when an auction took place in the city.
I'd read in the newspaper that there were cars at a price of zero.
I said to myself, "This is it!
This is my chance, I'll buy a car!"
I broke my piggy bank, took the coins, tied my bike to a tree, entered, gave my ID and sat down, sure that I was going to buy a car.
The auction begins, and the cars' parade start. Suddenly, the judge stops everything.
Everything is quiet. "Who's got the number 58?
A so-called Lucas Toledo".
Just imagine, I was very ashamed.
I didn't know wether to run or stay.
Slowly, I raised my hand, and everybody turned to look at me: "Sir, you are 10 or 11 years old, you are underage.
What are you doing in an auction?"
I stood up, all flushed, and walked across the place to give her my ID.
In that moment,
a man roaring in the back said:
"Well done, boy! Keep it up! Come on!"
So I left joyfully after all that encouragement.
Then I said to myself, I'm an economist, I dont't belong to the technology world, there are other giants operating in the same field, it isn't the best scenerio.
My mother used to tell me, "Honey, you're an economist, you should be working in a bank instead of doing this".
Anyway, I decided to get down to it.
I chose to follow my instinct, I had the feeling that something would come up.
The thing was: Where to begin?
With the main part, the work team.
I brought in two beautiful minds, Agustín Agustinoy and Eric Sevilla, who are the other co-founders of the project.
So we begun with the first designs of what was going to be our dream.
We had ideas, we had enthusiasm.
We had paper and color pencils to start to do the layouts.
Our goal was to make a prototype.
To make a product that we could show and see if it had a good reputation in the market and among the public.
The prototype it's just a sample of the final product
with few details in it.
That helps you to save a lot of time and also a thing we didn't have: money.
We didn't have a coin, so we started looking for any kind of financing: friends, girlfriends, the State, banks.
My mother expected me to give loans in a bank instead of asking for them.
Family also helped and supported us a lot.
While I was looking for those loans, I made the second big decition: to focus, and by this I mean: "I'm only doing this project".
In that moment I had other ones that I also liked, but I had to choose, Gi Bike wasn't going as I wanted.
As soon as I decided to do this and nothing else, when I finally focused, when I concentrated the energies, the project started to move forward and to get a whole new scope.
After two years of development, we came to the final prototype.
We had already obtained the resources, we had the inspiration; we were very drawn by what we were doing; we were focused; but we had never imagined all the technical and cost issues that we would have to face.
Imagine this: we were very close to the final prototype, the aluminium one, with which we were going to do the videos and the testing.
To make the aluminium parts, first we had to buy the moulds in which the aluminium parts were made.
When we asked for the quotation it was far beyond our budget.
They wouldn't lend us no more money, and my familiy was skeptical.
So I asked myself, "What to do?"
We had the great idea of buying a domestic 3D printer, needless to say, the cheapest one, that can print even a plastic pizza.
Perfect, thus we would print the parts and make the foundry moulds.
Problem solved. That would be it.
We had the inspiration, the focus,
the 3D printer, everything.
We never imagined it would take us two months to calibrate the printer.
And each time we printed a part, we had to calibrate it all over again.
That caused us huge delays.
I remembered that ten days before we travelled to E.E.U.U. because we'd already bought the tickets five or six months earlier, as a self-imposed deadline:
"Now we can't back down".
We hadn't still finished the videos and the photos.
The name Gi Bike was still being discussed.
We didn't sleep for three days, we almost died.
Until we made the journey.
We finally had the prototype ready to show.
How we would do it?
We thought a good idea would be, being our aim to show it but not having the resources to build it, to make a colective financing campaign.
It was a fashionable way of getting the resources we needed.
We sent dozens of mails to the press telling them about our project. meeting all the implied requirements.
Until we could finally publish our product on Kickstarter, a platform in which it was published for 30 days.
We had a great impact.
We received thousands of e-mails from people willing to buy it, and also showing their support.
We gave hundreds of interviews in more than 300 international media.
We were even offered to give a TEDx talk.
We raised 116,000 dollars.
You may say, "Isn't it wonderful?"
Well, no.
As we didn't reach the $400,000 that Kickstarter demanded, although 180 people had supported the project with their money, we hadn't achieved our goal.
Nevertheless, we achieved other goals that we had also been set.
Like showing our product to see what the impact would be.
We had set the demand, now we had thounsands of people who wanted our bike, hundreds of distributors in the world wanted to import it into their respective markets, which would help us to face the next stage, that is the round of investment, to raise the necessary funds in order to start the production and make it affordable.
If I think in my last years as an entrepreneur, I realized it's a long way, a way full of meanders.
There are my own fears: How to learn about technology?
What to do with all the big fish?
How to do it from Argentina?
There are also other kinds of fears: the fears of the people that surround us.
"Stop wasting your time!"
Or the challenge of being in a Latin American city where the conditions aren't the best ones to compete with the world.
Each one of those fears is a "no".
Some of these "nos" wouldn't let us even start, leaving us just dreaming how it would have been, and so we never begin.
Some "nos" show up in the way and make us quit, make us give up, not wanting to go on.
Yet more important are the ones that make us stop from dreaming, trapped by fears of our own and from outside...
It is then when we wonder, How we're going to do it?
Where do we start?
To tell the truth, the entrepreneur's life is very lonely.
The self-motivation, that inner engine we all have inside is what saves us from despair, makes us go on, makes us keep trying.
Like in the story of the auction that man cheered me up with his "yes", the entrepreneurs, sometimes we do that: we cling to a "yes" as castaways cling to their raft in the sea.
Those words of kindness, those pats on the shoulder, those "yes" that come from the visionaries, the entrepreneurs, but above all, the ones that really lift us up are the "yes" from the life's entrepreneurs, that in my case were my parents.
My parents have been my true source of inspiration.
They had a really tough life but they managed to move forward.
They taught me that the limits are inside us.
And I'm sure that if each one of us closes our eyes we'll find out that there's always someone encouraging us, lifting us up.
Or just people inspiring with their example.
Like Bernardo de Chartres used to say, "If I have seen further, it is by standing on the shoulders of giants."
We can see far beyond,
there in the forth. Not because of our eyes, neither for our height, but for the greatness of our giants.
I'm not talking of the giants from the movies or tales,
I'm talking about the everyday giants, about our mentors, the life's entrepreneurs that everyday show us that we can make it.
When I have those everyday fears or obstacles, I always say to myself, "I'm standing on the shoulders of giants. On the ones who supported and inspired us, the visionaries, or the fools who ventured". Thank you.
(Applause)
What if I told you in two years you run out of chance of having children?
Would you change your plans?
The good news is that this is not the case.
The bad news is when we let ourselves go, in the absence of a precise date when we lose the opportunity of being parents naturally, many times, it's already late.
Like the toads: you must have heard that if you put a toad in a pot with boiling water, the toad jumps instinctively.
However, if you put it in the pot with the water heating up slowly, the toad stays motionless, until it loses the ability to react. 30 year-old women have 20 percent chance of getting pregnant in each cycle, at 40 this percentage drops to 5 percent.
At present, women and men have more and more problems to get pregnant.
It seems in this part of the world, this phenomenon concentrates on sectors with greater resources.
The difficulty in having children is a silent epidemic we are not prepared for.
I’m 40 and it would have changed my life if 20 years ago someone had talked to me about this.
When I was 29 the idea of having a child came to my mind.
Actually, I wasn’t quite sure it was the right moment.
I got a little carried away by the desire and the impulse of my husband and we started trying for a baby.
It didn’t take me long to get pregnant.
After all, as I once heard, having a child is an act of bravery into the unknown, to make contact with our vulnerability. You have to be a little crazy, get carried away by something much more emotional than rational, especially in this society in which we are so focused on ourselves, in which we are accustomed to satisfy our pleasures through consumption, in which we are posting our happiness on social networks.
A son is an act of courage, an act of surrender, it means giving up our time, limiting our freedom.
In fact, there’s a movement called Child Free, free of children, that increasingly gets more followers.
And I think it's great everyone takes the decision that is best for their lives.
You can have a happy life without having children.
We, modern women, in the last 50 years grew up hearing about the contraceptive pill.
It gave us the chance to fully enjoy our sexuality, without being so worried about getting pregnant.
We were able to plan for the first time when we wanted to have children. It was a revolution.
But planning isn’t just not having kids when you don’t want to. It‘s being able to have them when you want to, as well.
It’s been an important object of study over the last years worldwide, largely because of the ageing of world population.
Biological times go by a lane and cultural times go by another.
While our mothers had us at their 20s, we started thinking about it at our 30s on average.
We want to have all our life planned, everything under control, that all the pieces fit together like in a puzzle.
Like Mafalda.
Do you remember that joke in which Mafalda is on the floor drawing in detail her life plan?
Then her mother distractedly
stands on the drawing. Mafalda raises her head and says: "Mom, you're standing on my postgraduate degree in China."
The ideal thing is to be able to decide the best for our lives.
Now, we all know, in life there’s much more unpredictability than predictability.
I’ve been there.
I thought in my life I could have everything planned, everything under control, like Mafalda.
Three years after my first son, Lucas, was born, after having experienced the wonders of motherhood, having discovered it together, we discovered it together, we decided to try for a second child.
I was 33 when diagnosed with early menopause.
Seven doctors told me it was impossible to get pregnant with my own ovules, that my options were adoption or ovodonation; that a woman donated me an ovule, that my ovarian reserve was old.
For two years my routine was having a blood test every day, dozens of transvaginal ultrasound scans to control my ovulation. One day, I arrived at a clinic for hysterosalpingography, I was given a contrast liquid and, as they tugged at my uterus, they asked me to move my body to one side and the other, to know if my fallopian tubes had obstructions or leakages.
Over time, with doctors, it's as if you learned how to be desensitized from the waist down.
You come dreaming of a son born out of love and pleasure and suddenly you are on a cold stretcher committed, body and soul and committing your body and soul is not innocuous.
After my last hormone stimulation, from all these shots, my belly looked like a strainer.
Although I come from an atheist family, I saw myself begging for another child at Virgen del Cerro Sanctuary in Salta.
Finally, after great grief and aware that there was another possible happy ending, that this was going to happen if we had the support required, psychological assistance, we took the ovodonation path.
But this tremendous course also brought other things into my life.
I am a journalist and writer. I work finding good stories from reality to narrate them with the tools of journalism and literature, called nonfiction literature.
I wrote a book about my story and the stories of nine other women that made me meet hundreds of men and women throughout the country who had been feeling and going through the same thing, they started telling me their stories about that unfulfilled desire.
Today, every day, stories keep coming.
And do you know who came too?
Joaquin, my second son. He came naturally and unexpectedly, to make me even happier with Lucas, and he has just turned four.
(Applause) Do you know how you were conceived?
Have you ever wondered?
Do you know if your parents had fertility issues? Or if you were born through a treatment?
This is still a taboo issue not talked about so much. However, one million women in Argentina, one in six couples struggle to get pregnant or to bring a pregnancy to term.
If all of you were of reproductive age, 2000 would have problems in the future to conceive and would not know it now.
Difficulties increase sharply as from age 35, because also from that age, the quality of our ovules falls abruptly.
I wish it was not like that.
And to the men who looked the other way, I'm sorry to tell you that it was just discovered that the quality of sperm also falls with age.
And one of the things I learned is that, if you have a partner, the problem involves both of you not just one.
And couples that are not close enough break up along this way.
Today science gives us a lot of opportunities and possibilities and this is great, but be careful.
First, it’s not 100 percent certain: only 1 out of 4 fertility treatments ends in birth. It’s also expensive and many couples try for many years and they still cannot make it.
It’s possible to freeze ovules, but you have to do it before you're 30 or as close as possible to that age.
Because afterwards and leaving business interests aside, it’s very likely that those ovules will not have the quality in the future when they thaw.
Obviously there's always the adoption path but if you want a baby it's something that takes years.
Although it’s different if one wishes to adopt an older child or a group of brothers.
Time is the worst enemy of fertility.
We all feel out of statistics, but the reality is that we all are within statistics.
I don't want it to be too late for you.
If you don't want children, fine; but not for not having decided on time.
We should choose based on decisions not from omnipotence.
We choose by option, action or omission.
We should know omission is also a decision and be aware of the consequences this decision brings along.
Not deciding is deciding.
Not choosing to have children in time can imply losing the possibility of choice.
Let it not be too late for you.
Thank you.
(Applause)
If I were to ask the audience, who does really think that the conservation of nature and economical growth can coexist, I'm sure very few of you would give a positive answer.
What I want to do today is, precisely, to transmit my personal and laboral experience, more so if it's in the place that made that possible: Cuba, in Jardines de La Reina.
If I were to ask now, who likes the beach, the response would be huge.
Almost all of us, if we don't go to the beach, we would think that our holidays
weren't nice. Well, that's what happened to me and my brother, as well as to our children, specially during our holidays when we visited different beaches.
We liked to explore the deepest places: we ventured into fishing and diving with our family.
Specially in a very beautiful place of the orient part of Cuba, where I had the fortune of been born, but I left very early because it was all pure coincidence.
And I've lived more than 40 years in the centre of the island, in Ciego de Ávila.
That interest for the sea and ocean followed me everywhere, and that's why, after I finished my pre-university course, I studied Biology in The University of Havana from 1990 to 1995.
Everyone remember those years as the hardest ones of the special period.
Thanks to my Chinese bicycle, the Flying Pigeon, I could go from 12 and Malecón to J and 25, I could go to work, and develop my diploma thesis in the Institute of Oceanology, which was 12 km away from where I slept.
When I got my degree, after spending 5 years in La Havana, I returned to my hometown.
From then onward, since 1995, I've been carrying on investigations and sponsoring conservation, specially to the north and south of my province, but also in other places of Cuba, and, fortunately, in world places.
The main investigations focused on studying fishes.
I became interested in turtles due to a reading I made from a publication that says, "Regarding Colon's second travel in 1494, in those 20 leagues, Jardines de la Reina caught sight of many green turtles.
The ocean was full of them, the biggest ones and so numerous that it seemed like the boats will run aground on them.
We don't know the current number of green turtles in Jardines de la Reina, but from my experience of almost 20 years working in this place, there are very few.
None of these photos are of green turtles.
These problems are not something recent.
In 1680, around 6,5 millions of green turtle spawned in the Cayman Islands.
A century later, hunting was forbidden because there weren't enough specimens.
Nowadays, only about ten turtles spawn in the Cayman Islands.
Turtle meat was the main food during the process of colonization in Jamaica.
Jardines de la Reina is an idyllic place with both terrestrial and marine spectacular ecosystems, from coast scrubs, swamps, sea grass and coral reefs, but it also gives the economy of Cuba countless of fishing resources.
A third of the fisheries in Cuba come from Jardines de la Reina and its surroundings.
Around 20% of the lobster captures, our main fishing resource, and almost 100% of the fisheries of wild life prawn.
Since 1996, Jardines de la Reina is being protected and it is a marine reserve of around 1000 km2.
14 years later, it was labelled as national park with almost 2000 km2.
This is a tool of preservation and a success of marine preservation in Cuba, and Cubans have to be proud of it.
To give you an idea, the see-areas protected in the world are smaller than 10 km2.
That is, we are talking about the biggest sea reserves and National Parks worldwide.
What effect did it have on the preservation of nature?
Specimen or species like gauss - groupers, which is an endangered species due to the indiscriminate use or the indiscriminate hunting that humans do, lives in these protected places in Jardines de la Reina, in numbers that exceed three times those from the non-protected places.
The numbers are even more important
when it come to sharks. There are eighteen times more sharks in the protected areas of Jardines de la Reina than in the non-protected ones.
Due to the size, sharks and groupers play an essential ecological role.
Through trophic cascades, they control the stability and health of ecosystems.
Moreover, sharks are fishing resources of great importance.
Around 50% of the fisheries that are developed in the central south part of Cuba capture sharks.
But the interesting thing came later.
In my master's degree, the most interesting subject I got was environmental economy.
The goal of environmental economy is to internalize costs.
What does it mean?
To take into account all the costs of producing or exploiting a resource.
To give you an example, a litre of petrol costs around one dollar.
When you add the environmental cost and the social cost of producing a litre of petrol, the initial value is quadruplicate, and that's what environmental economy is about: incorporating that value to take better decisions regarding the use of natural resources.
During that process I met my wife, and we carried on studies linked to the economical effects of this preservation.
Contemplative diving and leisure fishing of caught and release in Jardines de la Reina gives more than 3 million dollars to the country's economy, a lot more than fishing.
The contribution of sharks and rays are particularly important.
The contribution exceeds one million dollars.
But this preservation is not only important for tourism.
Preservation can also be relevant for fishing.
How?
The effects of protected areas are divided into two large groups: the first one trhough the export of eggs and larva from protected areas to fishing areas, but that process takes years and fishers need results in the short run in order to accept the protected areas.
Then the number and size of the fishes in the protected areas starts to grow.
As if you were filling a glass of water and you drop it.
Fishes go out and then it's possible that some fisher catches them.
This effect happened in Jardines de la Reina.
Thus, even though we have protected more than 2000 km2 of the seas, fisheries of that area are stable or have even grown — that's the case of lobsters.
I hope that, with this conversation, you have assimilated and understood why we say that preservation of nature can contribute to achieve a sustainable development.
If we make everyone a part of this process, we can achieve a prosperous and steady socialism for our children and the ones after them.
We hope that, someday, the problem to be solved will be how to avoid boats running aground on the oceans where green turtles live.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Thank you. I grew up in a very creative environment. I was exposed to architecture, art, literature, then, I studied industrial design as many of the speakers you have heard today, and I became a creativity enthusiast.
For 12 years, I've been leading one of the main consulting innovation firms and one of the questions I've been more interested in the past 15 years is how can we maximize our creativity?
I mean, what makes some people incredibly creative while others aren't.
What factors or elements can help people boost creativity to the next level?
I've seen how not creative people suddenly become incredibly creative, and what I came to share with you tonight is that I think I've found the answer to this question.
I'm going to share it now.
Although I would have loved to professionally investigate this area, the way a psychology scholar would have studied how creativity works. I actually took another way: Consultancy. This confronts me with real company cases and gives me a first-hand view of how people assimilates creativity, ideation and other subjects.
As a leader of my company, we have advised a lot of organizations. I've seen all sorts of people becoming creative. I've seen how working groups, like our designers team, or social scientists, understand creativity.
I've participated in over 1400 innovation projects. I've participated in thousands of brainstorming sessions, I've seen how some people find it difficult to be creative, and others have a constant burst of ideas. They easily come up with crazy and stupid ideas.
I've found that there are different elements, different variables that help people to be creative.
One of them is getting rid of fear. When you create a fear-free environment and reduce awful consequences, such as material or social consequences, or making a fool of yourself. People tend to be more creative without time constraints. People flow better and come up with more ideas.
When you take away the environmental risk, that is, not having the boss in front of them, suddenly people become a bit more creative. So, there are some external factors, like fear elimination, that help people boost their creativity.
Empowerment is another strategy to increase creativity. You can empower someone: State that creativity is part of their responsibilities. Tell people that they are the most creative person in the world, and you will turn them into creative people.
You can encourage people to feel brave enough to suggest ideas, without bad consequences, and boost their creativity self-esteem. That is one strategy.
Another strategy is to learn about creativity. That basically means becoming knowledgeable in psychology, design, understanding theory, learning techniques, methodologies and processes to foster creativity, like how to conduct a good brainstorming, and have that kind of stimuli, information to encourage creativity. That also works pretty well.
One of my favorite ones: Rules of space. Create a collaborative space, where people interact, where they can talk things over and exchange ideas on the hallway, paint the walls in colors, ping pong tables and darts. It is pretty fun, unless you are kicked out of the company for playing all the time. Yet, many companies do that, as if to say that they are breaking the rules and being creative. Like those narrow minded hierarchic companies, with a ping pong table, who say: "this is our innovation center." Unhappily. Another one I love is the incentive, "The carrot." Give people an economic incentive for example, to generate great ideas or social acknowledgement to the
person who gave the idea of the year. Great, right? Giving rewards, or just give employment to creative people. There are many ways to extrinsically boost creativity Like the four I just mentioned, the problem is that according to the difficulty of the problems we need to solve we need sometimes to apply different strategies to encourage creativity simple problems for example, where to get dinner tonight is a very easy problem, and we don't need to use any technique to think where we are going to have dinner, maybe the most standard or the least creative is having dinner at home as usual. If you use a little creativity it can occur to you
to make a picnic, at night in the park, hey, that's creative! That's cool. These are ideas out of the blue, for which you don't need too much help to come up with. On a product level, for example, if you have to design a shoe collection, now that we are here in León. The least creative thing to do is to copy what is done in Italy, copy and do the same here. And if you use a bit of creativity you can make a collection like the one Melissa does, a Brazilian company that basically creates jelly-shoes, from very recognized designers and sells them up to USD 70 each. It is quite profitable and creative too. At a product and service level, incentives or extrinsic motivators usually work at a service level.
For instance, if you are only focused on getting on time, but not providing any additional experience, or any creative services whatsoever, you become Aeroméxico. But if you want to give something more, you can become something like Virgin, that gives you an amazing and memorable flight experience. 
At a social issues level things turn even more complex. At the level of social issues, you can design a program such as Bolsa Familia, that is basically giving money to poor families to reduce poverty, or you can be a bit more creative and think of a program where rich people adopt a poor family. 
So, maybe now you are more aware of the creativity level needed to solve certain issues: creativity increases proportionally to the complexity of the problem. 
Nowadays, we need to solve more complex problems than we did in the past. therefore, the techniques and incentives, the creativity you need to turn a worthless idea into a wow idea is higher. 
Those strategies I previously shared with you, have a flaw. The flaw is that all those are extrinsic strategies That is, things you do on the outside so that someone becomes creative, but failing in teaching the person how to be creative from the inside. This is a problem, because using only extrinsic motivators that oftentimes can be wrongly used or overused, or if you stop the incentives, for example, you stop giving incentives for "idea of the year" people will question "where is my economic reward for ideas?" or they will only come up with ideas until the reward is granted, instead of inspiring them to be creative over time, besides, there is a limit to this kind of incentives. It's like using a waist trimmer, you're somehow cheating by using these external incentives. I used to feel frustrated by these external incentives failure. They were not helping people to be creative. Incentives are allies, yet, they don't change the person from the inside. So, I started to realize that actually these external incentives, or external motivations, have a limit. That means, they encourage creativity up to certain level. Beyond that level, external motivators are almost useless. All the ones I previously mentioned as helpful. This is a problem, like I said before, the kind of problems we need to solve nowadays, are more complex than just thinking of a product, or deciding where to have dinner. 
Then something happened, that helped me solve the question I just stated. Some months ago, it was a Friday night, 9pm. I had stayed at the office a bit longer, and was about to leave. I turned off the lights when suddenly, from the back, from a room in the back, someone said, "don't turn off the lights, we are here." And I thought: "Who is here 9pm on a Friday night?, people should be with their families, in the movies, As far as I know there are no deadlines for Monday that demand people to stay." So I went to see who was there. It was a group of consultants, five of them are here today. It was a group of consultants, working on a problem, they were generating ideas. Their project was basically about understanding how life of cancer patients is, and design ideas and solutions to improve patients quality of life. It was an innovation project, so I came over and I told them: "What's up guys?, It's nine o'clock, let's go. Don't you have things to do?, don't you have a family?, or something better to do? you don't deliver this on Monday." 
And they told me, "No, no, we are finding a solution. We are iterating and generating a cool idea that occurred to us this afternoon." "Tell me more about it," I said. 
The idea was indeed unbelievable! So I told them, you are in a flow, psychologist Mihály Csíkszentmihályi coined that term some years ago. They said: "Yes we are on a flow, we are working on the idea." But rather than that, I realized, that the team was surrounded by stories, images and photographs of the people they've interviewed the past week. To give you some context, what the team did the two previous weeks, was sitting down with cancer patients at their homes for two hours. They talked to them and talked with their doctors at their offices. They understood, how the public Mexican health system works. It is full of mistakes. They listened to the patient's stories: Totally depressed patients because they were going to die, and ecstatic patients because they were going to die -- yet they were happy. They heard terrible stories, such as the patient who remained misdiagnosed for many years thinking it was something simple. Then the tumor started to grow and grow, until he was correctly diagnosed at stage three or four, and had 3 months to live. He was frustrated with the health system, yet was happy because he was helping for future cancer patients. I realized that my team was surrounded by these people that is when I had this aha moment, and said, "Of course, what motivates my team for staying late on a Friday night, is not necessarily about work. They are designing solutions for those patients. Their motivation is working to make patients life better. That encourages them to generate better ideas. I then realized, that the key the most useful and fundamental element, the most efficient way to maximize creativity, is empathy. Actually, they were not working for themselves, they were not working for the company, not even working for the customer. At 9 pm, they were working to improve people's lives. The fact of feeling empathy and getting to know the patient, was lighting up ideas, extraordinary ideas. So, I realized that those incentives, those intrinsic incentives are much more powerful than any extrinsic incentive or motivation that you have to encourage creativity. By empathy I don't mean the definition available on the Royal Spanish Academy dictionary, A pretty flat one: "Mental and affective affinity of an individual with other people's mood." That is so flat. By empathy I mean not to step on someone else's shoes, but to walk for a month in those shoes, and feel the blisters that you get from walking in those shoes. 
Empathy means to deeply understand what makes that person happy or sad. Their needs. It is watching that person in that real context, and suddenly understand why things happen and why people feel that way and experience those feelings as your own. Wearing emotions on the sleeve means being able to acknowledge other people's feelings, and feel them yourself. Call it emotional connection, if you are cheesy you can call it love. Yet, that is the level of empathy you need to come up with the best ideas. If you can't reach that level of empathy, you won't understand the kind of ideas people need. What makes the difference between a creative person with good ideas, and an extraordinarily creative one, is the level of empathy with those who will benefit from the idea. When you reach that point, that connection and empathy, it will drive you to the edge of imagination, which is where you can create new ideas. Think of a professional athlete, who is always pushing the limits. The most creative and innovative person is the one that strives for the edge through empathy. Even better, that way of generating ideas, from the empathy of your heart, makes of you a creative person without any sort of shortcut. 
Incredibly, everybody has the potential for empathy, except maybe for politicians, they seem to lack that skill. When we, when babies are born, empathy is one of their first reactions, emotions. At the nursery crib, a baby starts crying and the other babies cry too. They don't cry out of fear of the first crying baby, That is false. they cry because they feel what the first baby is feeling. So, since we are babies, then children, we are empathic and closely feel what others feel. As we grow up, that capability fades away, we stop using it because companies dismiss its value. We dismiss empathy ourselves. I understand that some professions can't be too empathic, like doctors. I understand why they show little empathy, they would suffer every time a patient dies. In that case it's OK not to be empathic. but the rest of us, who work on the creative business, we need to be empathic and remain deeply aware of our users needs. That will lift us to the next level of creativity, Our almost forgotten empathy, is the reason why we need external motivation, like having the office walls in colors or buy a ping-pong table, or giving economic incentives and carrots so that people generate ideas. This is why people find so hard to create ideas, and why companies have a hard time encouraging their employees ideas. This is why governments are unable to solve our toughest problems. They have few and bad ideas. Yet, they are spending money, but doing no good, all because they lack empathy to notice people's needs. [Not investing in emotional capital means never being able to create the solutions our world needs] 
We can bridge that gap with empathy, and create ideas in accordance to what we need nowadays. 
If we don't invest emotional capital, that is, a bit of our own feelings in order to understand someone else, we are never going to generate solutions to our current complex problems. 
Sometimes I'm just too optimistic, but don't get me wrong, I travel a lot and get to see much of what happens in the world. The world right now, is living a fragile moment. 
There are problems, envies, violence, economic inequality and opportunity disparity. We are drowning in intolerance and incompetence. That kind of problems can be tackled by creative people. If our creativity will only depend on external factors, we will never get to solve those problems. If you design, thinking only on your own benefit from creativity, you won't get far. But if you keep others in mind, if you design with empathy and love towards other people, you will change the world.
Thank you so much.
Five years ago I met two gentlemen.
I am the one on the right.
(Laughter) We have different backgrounds, we never happened to meet elsewhere, we never ran into each other before.
We all have an academic career, an engineering degree, a business degree, and we work on four continents.
We are a physicist, a chemist, and an economist.
The three of us were at that time at an age where folks rather contemplate retirement than a bold launch into innovative ventures.
Nevertheless, 11 months later, we built something new together, something truly new: a device to tell the truth in public.
To tell the truth?
What exactly were we talking about?
Most of the statements we make everyday can't be classified as true or false.
Sentences such as: "Hi, how are you?"
"Let's have a coffee,"
fall under greetings, invitations, orders, opinions, suggestions, and they are neither true nor false.
On the other hand, those like "It's raining," "That's 365 pesos cash," "This house is mine,"
are statements that can be verified.
I am exclusively talking about the sentences referring to true facts.
Those that anybody with goodwill, common sense, and appealing to elementary logic might decide, faced with facts, if they are true or misleading.
I refer to something similar to what we use in our contracts, when we come to an agreement, when we negotiate our wages, upon purchasing a washing machine, for example.
"That will be 15,000 pesos in 12 equal installments from the date and to be delivered at your home tomorrow between 14.00 and 17.00 hours."
Nothing's vague, tricky, or ambiguous.
I doubt anyone can just walk away with the washing machine or get off Scot-free by arguing neoliberalism is harmful or objectivity does not exist.
So let's agree then the facts really tell in these cases and the truth about them really matters.
We take those sentences zealously, diligently, responsibly, because the data they contained has an impact on our lives.
Now, that's happening in the private sphere.
What about the public discourse?
That is to say the discourse poured in the public space by the politicians, the media, the press, businessmen, the trade unions, social referents.
Lately, the public discourse, - though it isn't something that hasn't happened in Argentina or in the world before - has started to move away from the facts.
The facts and the truth fade into the background, with narrative, gossip, and opinion taking the central stage, as well as the biased data, innuendos, even sheer manipulation.
The political discourse, - as well as the media's and every other social actor's - grew more and more dependent on the narrative.
And there is a reason for this.
Narrative is powerful if only because it bypasses a rational analysis of objective facts to hit home on emotions.
And driven by emotions we subscribe to proposals, political parties' and references' ideas.
The problem is when the narrative starts having a life of its own.
They are powerful because we think so of them.
Humans think in narratives and not about isolated incidents.
Take Coca-Cola for example and its use of the power of storytelling that sells 1.7 billion drinks a day, as well as other nonprofit, nongovernmental organizations that manage to eradicate childhood diarrhoea in areas where it is endemic, or bring at the forefront of social agendas causes such as the need for the environmental protection, gender equality, and human rights,
However, when the narrative spreads across the entire public discourse, the hard facts and the truth turn into unwelcome guests, into obstacles, or mere limits.
And they decide to ignore them, or that they must be left aside, banished from the public discourse or simply hidden from sight.
Thus, debate on the issue tends to slowly disappear because it is difficult to discuss when the facts or the truth aren't available.
This is how we came to witness a situation where the media, the politicians, the social referents enter into a state the kind where they completely reject to meet their commitments, or recognize the facts and the truth, and thus cheat, commit crimes, and enter in a kind of default state when it comes to their role and mission towards citizens.
And we, as citizens immersed in this narrative paradigm, also slacken in our efforts to claim reporting based on factual evidence, and to call for a narrative based on truth.
Thus, our involvement with the public discourse increasingly declines.
This means for many of us, and without even realizing it that we are undermining the essence of democracy itself.
Why do we do it?
Democracy is citizen-centered,
it's powered through citizen participation. It depends on us as social actors, as agents of democracy.
And to be an agent of democracy we rely on information instant, live information, ready for action.
Without timely and accurate information it is impossible to act as agents,
and we depend on others for that kind of information;
on the media, on those in whom we've placed our faith and trust.
And this is precisely what we have decided to do, reinsert that lost faith and trust into the democratic discourse.
To do that, I together with my partners who far from retiring are actively involved, devised a means that can ensure the quality of the public discourse, to ensure the quality of public discourse, and telling the truth publicly.
We founded Chequeado.com, a site dedicated to the restoration of the naked truth and hard facts back into the public discourse.
Chequeado verifies all statements made in the public discourse by all social actors.
It assembles quotes from politicians', media's and trade unionists' discourse, and analyzes the facts. It runs them against other
independent sources and contrasts them
with what other specialists and independent sources have stated.
It scans for all information and evidence available and establishes whether such information is correct, true or misleading.
For that, we have to also earn people's trust to be read and be believed.
Chequeado has its own style manual and checking methods and terminally prohibits any political biases, prejudice and partisanship, and advocates for the existence of mechanisms that ensure all this.
Chequeado has a large number of very diverse funding sources precisely to avoid relying on each of them,
and thus it depends a little bit on each of those who make a donation, it depends a little bit on everybody.
Our statutes prohibit any dependency and interference coming from its founders.
Chequeado has had a huge impact not only on the citizens but also on the political actors.
Ministers read us, legislators share data with us, the press is mentioning us, politicians need to retract due to us, and there are even some of them who choose to fight with us, but all, many of them, think twice before speaking.
Our community is causing the facts and the truth to be valued again.
I am part of the Chequeado community I read it, I follow it on Twitter and Facebook, I listen to it on the radio, I watch it on television, and the live feeds on key events, such as the presidential speeches, that are all gathered into the general pool of the data on our platform.
Chequeado has proven that a social partnership is possible.
and we dedicate our activity to that: to prove that the agency is necessary and possible, and ensures the quality of the public discourse.
Sometimes, the social inertia seems endless, as infinite as the power of the political world, as unbeatable as the power of corporations, as unshakable as the media power may also seem.
But the power resides in the citizen.
We delegate, but we do not abdicate.
Civic causes applied to just and specific causes have proven successful over and over.
Restoring the hard facts and the truth in the public discourse is key,
and a network of citizens determined to restore the truth as the essential element in the fabric of the public discourse can do it.
We will achieve it.
We are achieving it.
Thank you.
(Applause)
It's quite some time since I last danced.
(Background music starts) Do you think I'm dancing?
Do you believe that the dancers speak?
Dancers and choreographers tell stories, but with our body.
Let's assume for a moment that I have the twisted idea of talking about infinity.
