Let's assume for a moment that I want to talk about infinity.
For that I will take the time to create this recursive organization.
That is, it is repeated indefinitely.
Two dancers mark two points in space and a third one is located exactly in the middle.
And so on.
Indefinitely.
Ad infinitum.
Another idea I had, to tell with the body, was to represent time onstage.
So I created Rotonda, a circular space that speaks of time.
I organized it from very simple rules: counter-clockwise circulation, always turning left.
I added to these movements concentric orbits and travels along the diameters.
Thus, the parties began to resemble the whole and they gave meaning to an idea. An idea of time retracing its steps, an eternal return.
What if we focus now in one single orbit and diameter and then fall exactly towards the center?
Santiago, Facu, please stop.
(Background music stops) In the traditional way, inherited from classical dance, the choreographer creates starting from the music sheet.
In my case, I submit to a search for structures that are associated with ideas, ideas that make the dance emerge.
Therefore light, music, scenery, costumes, everything -- they create a network along with the choreography.
Fede, light please.
Music.
(Background music) How about if we now work with another idea?
Do you think that the dancer's body is a technology?
Aren't all our bodies?
I learned this exercise while I still was a dance student.
I was about 30-31 years old and I was a mathematics teacher.
Yes, I started dancing at the age in which others are already thinking of retiring.
I like it because it's like a game.
We call it Following the Leader.
The organization happens thanks to the many numbers making up the exercise.
Not because of the difficulty of the movements.
We are a small number, for this idea to be well understood.
So, I'm calling five volunteers.
Volunteers, please.
Come here.
It has to do with following those in front of you.
Come on, without fear.
You have to follow the movements.
Just that.
Try to keep the distance between each other, and follow the movements.
It happened when I was a physics student.
And by then I had a scholarship to research on acoustic emission in composite materials.
Then I realized that the physics lab was not the place to tell my ideas.
And then I decided to do something I always secretly wanted: take dance lessons.
You are doing it very well, but I think that we are still an insufficient number for this exercise, this idea-exercise to have power.
Very good, the new dancers are getting excited.
But I'm going to ask you to return to your places because I want to do this exercise with all of you.
It's simple, do not panic.
I repeat the rules.
It's about following whoever is next to you.
The other condition is to observe the distance among you.
We have this already solved because you are all located in armchairs.
So I'm going to ask you all to stand up.
All of us who can, stand up.
Those in the front row will follow the dancers.
Those in the second row have to follow those in the first row.
And so on, throughout the entire gallery.
Front row, the dancers.
Second row, the front row.
And so on.
Not everyone should follow the dancers, but those in the first row only.
That's it, come on.
Those at the back, I don't see you.
Come on, following the movements.
Very good.
That's it.
Simple, everyday movements.
They can work very well.
Greeting, for example.
Stretching, nodding.
If we repeat, we repeat with a difference.
We always try to innovate.
It's much better than I imagined.
It's an excellent game because we are all players. And we are all leaders.
It was a little over a year that I returned from a grant in France, for research and improvement in dance.
At that moment I felt that my days as a dancer were numbered.
But suddenly, I realized that a universe of possibilities arose in my hands.
And endless ideas to tell with the body spread out before me.
This body, all our bodies in motion, as we have just seen, are a means to develop and communicate ideas.
Ideas as diverse as time, infinity, self-organization of a large group of people.
Today, here, we all have put our bodies and ideas into action.
