You ask yourself what is happening.
And all of a sudden you hear a song, read a book, see a play or a movie, and you say: "Oh, that happened to me."
The typical example, when you fall in love or you break up, and then all the songs talk about you ... No.
It's not written for you. No!
But someone in some time throughout the history of life dedicated themselves to writing, and inserting a little code, so that later you can absorb it as yours and you don't feel so alone. And also, you save a ton on therapists.
Culture makes us live experiences that we couldn't live any other way.
And I'm not only talking about myself as an actor.
It would be impossible to be interpreting Don Juan all day, don't you think?
I'm talking about living an odyssey in space.
Of talking to a volleyball on an island after being shipwrecked; about feeling overwhelmed because you can't help the handmaid from The Handmaid's Tale escape. That's what I'm talking about.
Culture helps us take important steps in our lives.
I'm convinced of it.
In 2010, I released a self-produced record called "Viejos conocidos."
It was after the boom of "Los Serrano."
It was an album that I produced myself and it was a titanic effort.
And furthermore, it wasn't good, I know that none of you have bought it ...
Don't worry.
That experience left me exhausted and I stopped composing for a while.
I said ... Look, I can't anymore.
I'm ... feeling really down.
Well a few years ago Pedro Guerra invited me to a concert he was giving in Libertad 8, the mythic Madrid dive on Liberty street.
It was a beautiful concert, with only guitar.
Well, the Fran that went into that concert wasn't the same Fran that came out.
I was a different person.
It had completely changed me.
And that day I decided that I was going to return to composing.
I began to play the guitar again.
To compose ... And well, a few months ago I released a new record; this is advertising as well.
Do you realize the importance
that culture can have? Culture can give us that much desired feeling we seek: happiness.
A few days ago I read a study from Goldsmiths, University of London.
It says that going to a concert increases life expectancy.
What do you think of that?
(Applause)
Yes sir. It increases life expectancy.
The study says that 20 minutes in a concert increases
the sensation of well-being by 21 percent.
21 percent. That number rings a bell.
Maybe they charge us that in sales tax?
Damn it.
The study also says that if you're a re-offender,
if you go to concert, after concert, after concert, your life expectancy can increase up to nine years.
Who doesn't want to live nine more years?
Do I sense a bit of scepticism over there?
I have my second argument, OK?
The first was the soul.
If you don't like the soul argument, we have the education argument.
Culture is wealth for its importance in and support of education. Why?
Because it basically puts us
in other people's skin.
Culture helps us leave our own reality, stop self-reflecting, and reflect more on the realities of other people who have other problems.
And that helps us be more caring, more tolerant, and have fewer prejudices.
And to empathize.
Something that I honestly love.
From all the things I've learned, that's what I enjoy the most.
Experts say there are two ways to learn.
One is lifelong studying.

Wow, what an effort.
The other is listening to people who know
about a topic. You listen and you learn.
I'd like to believe that consuming culture, you learn about other realities.
There's a very recent example
that occurred a few days ago at the Goya awards.
The movie Champions by Javier Fesser.
What happened with that movie is wonderful.
The speech of Jesús Vidal, well I won't even go there.
But this movie has put the reality of the disabled in front of us ... or of the differently abled as they said,
with very good judgement.
And I think someone who has no contact with people in those conditions ... Well, I believe that that movie opens their eyes in a really nice way.
Effectively, putting ourselves in the skin of someone else makes us less egotistic and makes us strive to improve the lives of other people around us.
And that, in the end, gives us a wonderful feeling.
Is there still scepticism over there?
I have a third argument.
That's about this. The material.
Culture is wealth because of
the money it generates for a country.
The cultural economy consistently has more importance, and it's not me saying that.
That comes from UNESCO. They call it the "Orange Economy."
They say that no society can prosper without culture, and that without it, any development is unsustainable.
I'm not an expert in figures.
But I'm going to give you a few.
In the year 2017, live concerts raised EUR 300 million.
Theatrical arts raised more than EUR 200 million.
More than 100 million movie tickets were sold, and 13 million of the 82 million foreign tourists that came to visit us did so for cultural reasons.
I'm no expert in figures, but these sound quite good.
It's a good indication.
In 2013, I got together with a group of actors, and we created a theater play titled Feel Good. And we realized that culture was never on the table in political discussion.
And so we decided to make our contribution to the orange economy with a video that I'll play for you now.
Does the theater generate wealth?
We asked ourselves that very question.
So in 2013 we created a theater company and we decided to self-produce the play Feel, by Alister Beaton.
We started by creating positions for six actors, a director, an assistant director, and a translator.
Later came the theatrical production, which employed two more actors, a director and a film crew, a production company, lighting director, scenographer, musician, wardrobe designer and a theater distribution company, printing, stores of clothes and furniture, a publicist, photographer, web-designer.
We began the tour and the family grew.
Two technicians, set transportation, two communication agencies, a camera and video editor, and another photographer.
A single theater play created more than 30 direct positions.
And that's without counting the indirect positions.
Trains and airlines, rental car companies, tolls, gasoline, cafeterias, hotels, media, ticket sales platforms, giving work to all the in-house positions in the theaters that we visited.
Bars and restaurants, forgotten mobile phone chargers ...
In the two years of Feel Good, we had more than 100 performances and 60,000 spectators bought tickets, including 21 percent sales tax, to come to the theater. It's as if all of Avila, Cuenca, Segovia, or Huesca had come to see us. In the worst years of crisis in Spain, Feel Good generated and drove, between salaries, taxes, social security, and profits, more than EUR 400,000.
In the end, instead of an expense, let's see if culture turns out to be an investment.
Although after so many numbers, the best result, the greatest benefit, is without a doubt the surplus that the theater provides our souls.
Culture is something exceptional, it's not just leisure and entertainment.
Culture and creation are fundamental models in the formation of human beings.
There you have it.
(Applause) The best thing that can be done with culture is to share it.
"Let's restrain this wild rage, this fierce condition, this proud ambition, If we ever dream again:
and we will, for 'tis plain in this world,
That to live is but to dream: Man dreams what he is, and wakes only when upon him breaks death's mysterious morning beam.
The king is born, and in this delusive way lives and rules with sway; The cheers that ring, born of air, on air take wing. And in ashes, death dissolves
his pride and state.
Who would wish a crown to take, seeing that he must awake beyond death's gate?
The rich man dreams of gold, gilding cares it scarce conceals, the poor man dreams he feels want and misery and cold. Dreams who rank would hold, dreams who bears toil's rough hands dreams who for wrong demands, and in the end, throughout the Earth, we all dream, whate'er our birth, but no one understands. 'Tis a dream that I here am bound,
'twas a dream that once I found
a state of pleasantness.
What is life? 'Tis but a madness.
What is life? A thing that seems,
a mirage that falsely gleams, phantom joy, delusive rest, since is life a dream at best, and even dreams themselves are dreams."
(Applause)
I'm passionate about insects for two reasons.
For one, I'm mad about the infinite diversity of this kind of little beings.
30 million; not of insects but of species.
We say that humans invented the wheel.
But dung beetles would move balls of dung way before we would even think of standing up straight.
"Humans invented compost."
Ants would make compost long before any ecologist would in their balcony.
And just in case you used to think that we have invented air conditioning you are absolutely wrong.
Of course, termites did it before.
And there are bugs that serve as thermometers.
Here you are: the crickets.
The more they crick, the higher the ambient temperature.
It obviously varies with the species, but it's quite accurate.
And these are just some facts about four insects.
Figure this multiplied by 30 million!
Incredible!
(Laughter) Besides, insects are delicious.
(Laughter) And this is the second thing I wanted to talk to you about.
(Applause) When I was 11 years old, I was eating a hamburger at a fast food restaurant when a kid approaches me and says: "Do you know that hamburgers are made of insects?"

Hold on for a second! Are insects edible?
I later found out that the hamburgers thing was a myth but at the time it kept me wondering and I started searching.
And in effect, not only insects are edible but they have a lot of advantages to them.
Compared specially with agriculture and the livestock industry, they create more food in less space and using less natural resources.
A grass-fed cow needs, give or take one hectare to move.
Whereas insects don't need more place than what they already take.
Now, if you feed that cow 8 kg of food, it will generate 1 kg of meat; approximately an 8-to-1 ratio.
If I feed the 8 kg of food to insects they will generate 4 kg: a 8-to-4 ratio.
Besides, insects in general have more protein per 100 g than cows.
For instance, crickets have 22 percent more protein.
And if this was not enough, the livestock industry is responsible for the release of 37 percent methane due to human activities that contributes to the greenhouse effect.
And, guess what?
Insects create in between 10 and 100 time less methane than, say, pork.
Great, well, but if insects are so cool why don't we have them in the market shelves?
Precisely, for the faces I saw in most of you when I ate a cricket.
We don't have it in mind here, but in the world today, 2 billion people eat insects on daily basis.
As Latinamericans we used to as well, until not so long ago --
Our pre-Columbian diet was made of an important variety of insects: ant larvae, worms, crickets, etc. Until the Spaniards came in and put the same face you just put.
Probably, when you think about eating an insect, you will imagine yourselves biting a cockroach like this or cutting with your knife a tarantula this big, but you need not to worry.
A product that's becoming widespread is insect flour.
It has all the benefits I mentioned before plus, you don't even realize you are eating crickets in a slice of bread.
So, how can we get the most of what insects can give to us?
In 2017, we went to Chile with a group of friends.
We attended the first Latinamerican Summit for Young Leaders in Biotech.
There we introduced and idea I think it can work.
We suggested creating insects to produce food with added value in urban areas as a way to solve the problem of the lack of space and the cost of resources.
We did this thinking that if we youngsters don't tear down cultural barriers, nobody will do it.
In fact, we are already doing this regarding genders.
And also in Latinamerica the production of food is a neural part of our economy and we cannot stay behind amidst these changes.
And to do this it's not required a huge warehouse or a big investment.
For instance, I try to contribute in my own house breeding insects, studying how to cook them and transform them so they can be more edible.
And if they look at insects beyond our preconceptions?
What if we see them as thermometers, or as great inventors, and also as food?
Nature provides with a solution that is profitable, viable and sustainable. And it's even delicious!
Let's grab this chance and try to change the lens we look to insects with.
(Applause)
Hello.
My name is Elena.
I'm a teacher, I'm a puppeteer and I'm a volunteer.
I've been a volunteer since I was 13.
In this talk I'd like to share with you what has happened along the way.
When I was 13, I used to visit my grandmother a lot.
It was so special.
My grandmother lived in a retirement home.
And living in a retirement home meant that each room was a different world.
And I put on puppet shows,
you know where? In the home's backyard.
And that allowed me to meet other cultures, other people, other families.
When I turned 13, I started planning for high-school.
And I found out that my friends were planning which houses they were going to clean.
And that's when I felt a profound social debt.
They were no different from me, yet it was obvious that we were facing different opportunities.
That's why I decided to go, almost run, to the hospital.
When I was 13, it was 1976.
It was not an easy time for this country.
But still, I took my suitcase full of puppets, a bag full of makeup, and I showed up at the hospital saying that I wanted to visit the kids.
Of course, nobody understood.
I was 13.
A dream, a passion, and no theoretical framework.
Nothing to support what I said, what I wanted to do.
They asked me: "Are you here to laugh at the kids?"
"No, I'm here to laugh with the kids."
But evidently, they didn't let me in.
But from there on, the dream continued.
I took my puppets and I went to the plazas
But not the plazas where the kids were buying balloons, and other things with their parents.
No. We went to Once, to Constitucion,
to Retiro. [Train station plazas]
And that's where the journey began.
A journey that involved following the bread crumbs, like Hansel and Gretel, and following the kids.
The kids would come to the shows, in the middle of the plaza.
But it wasn't just the shows.
They needed to find a guide to resources.
We had to tell them which hospital would treat them,
where they could find a soup kitchen or a rest area.
And so, I started following the kids.
And the kids grew up. Some of them ended up in detention centers and called me so I went into the centers.
And the kids kept on growing, and many of them called for me from inside the jails, and I started volunteering in prisons.
Entering a prison is world unto itself, a pause in time.
Another reality, different coordinates.
People ask me what I find in the prisons.
People. What else is there to find?
People.
People who made mistakes.
People whom I ask what they will do when they get out. not because I justify anything that happened, but because I'm focused on what we're going to do.
Entering a prison as a woman is a shock.
Many of them just stare at me; and what I propose to them is to become volunteers.
Because nobody wants to do good, unless they've experienced it themselves.
So when I go in, I propose something as simple as looking into each other's eyes, and I don't put on puppet shows. Instead, I propose building a shared space together, where the puppet is an excuse.
Where everything that happens has to do with constructing a word that society has been trampling on: "us".
Not "you" and "me",
"Us".
99% of them want to volunteer and, obviously, they can't go out.
Many of these prisons are maximum security.
So what I offer them is building puppets that will end up in intensive care wards.
Two isolated worlds unite, for mutual benefit.
And, since we make so many puppets, some end up in aboriginal schools, in the poorest schools in the city, in schools where I travel throughout the country.
The project is called "On wings of cloth"
They ask me who funds it,
and I show them my wallet: my purse funds it.
Because there are things that are better said just as they are.
At the prison I tell them: "Gentlemen, nothing I do is free; what happens is that I'm not paid with money and there are things that make me a millionaire."
When I enter into a place of high vulnerability the first thing I do is lay out a tablecloth.
I do not come to lecture.
I come to share a space, to set the table, to look each other in the eye and say "so good to have you here"
And I never go in alone,
Because when you enter into a complex space, you go in accompanied.
In college I was taught so many things, in my teacher's training, but there's something that I learned on the streets and in prisons: That aside from everything you know and you're prepared to share, you have to learn to shut up, which is a fascinating experience.
To give the other person a chance!
Because if you don't, you'll talk, explain, guess.
And, how do I know what he's feeling?
I show them things.
And I tell them that I do not go in alone
In each difficult place, an intensive care unit, an isolation ward, I go in wrapped
in a blanket. I swear.
And on that blanket, I carry all the energy I need, because I take with me all those who have loved me.
I always enter with all of them.
With those who are present and those who are not, that accompany me on this dream.
At the prison they really like making puppets, knowing they'll reach their destination.
Each doll has a peace dove hanging from its arm.
The dove has on it the name of the inmate.
Below it, the name of the college student who helped me repair it or strengthen it.
Below, that of the child
who colored it, who may be from a children's home or a center.
Finally, the name of the kid who received it.
When the dove is full, the dove returns to the prison.
And I show the inmate that he was able to put together a lawful association, with a wonderful objective.
And that there's a kid in intensive care who is grateful for that.
Some of them say "Me?"

"Yes, you. Here are the names."
They ask me to speak to the younger generation.
To go to the juvenile detention centers and to tell them.
That they don't want them to get so far down this path that sometimes is like a funnel.
As we make the puppets, magical things happen,
Like when I'm showing them how and some of them stare at me like this.
So then I ask: "Did you get lost, sir?"
One of them, after the third time he got lost, you know what he said?
"Excuse me, teacher. If once in my life
somebody had looked at me like that I wouldn't have ended up in prison".
I don't know if that's so.
But I know that the experts in resilience claim that one loving look can reinstate you in life.
And that's how it's been for me.
I take with me puppets that they'll like.
Puppets that tell their story.
Like Water-Drop, who'd grow up

to be a jet. [slang: thief]
And they'll explain.
Right Drop? Tell them.
You bet! Happy to be here, a joy to come out here.
My name is Water-Drop. They said I'd grow up to be a jet. [thief]
But they were wrong!
Because somebody saw me with love.
And when I was seen with love, I felt I had rights, and I could lead a different life.
That's why we go to the prisons with Elena, and we talk to the boys, and we tell them to dream, to hang on to their ideals, to believe there is good in the world.
To highlight the invisible, because it's there.
Being there, we learn that sometimes it's not so hard to do what is needed, just have your eyes meet, create a space for human contact, say "you can do it".
And the puppets arrive at the I.C. ward.
And what they made, gets to a mother.
A mother who is waiting.
Waiting for what? Waiting for everything.

That's why we talk to them too.
We show them the puppets.
Come on Espera. [means wait]
Come on.
Don't you worry,
because they'll wait for you
You've waited for so many years,
why wouldn't we wait for you now?
So, relax; I'm here.
What can I say to you?
Seeing you in college, all sitting around.
My name is Espera.
I've waited forever for you to realize you're my brother.
When I got to the hospital, they treated my boy.
And a puppet arrived.
And they tell me it's from someone who's also in isolation.
I thought, how can someone remember me if they haven't met me?
Espera, but I told them about you.
Just knowing that somebody's named me that someone has spoken of my existence, it gave me a feeling.
So I asked Ms. Elena to share it with you today.
Perhaps there are better times to come.
Perhaps I may ask of you who are sitting there to not give up on your dreams to pledge allegiance to hope, to realize that the flag of utopia is possible.
And if it is torn, sew it up again.
But go forth into the world, with your studies or without them, to seek out others.
So, Elena, please, tell them what you told me when I was very frightened.
What I told you
when you were frightened?
Come, Espera. We're all frightened.
When you go into places
of high vulnerability, the people you find are always the same.
Look: Don't think they're so different from us.
Don't think you're going to see things you don't see outside.
We're all so much alike.
Being in vulnerable places means finding people who are very scared.
Very scared.
It seems so difficult for them.
They get so scared!
Some of the kids that I visit, are scared of thunder.
And people say:
Thunder? That's silly! 
Right?
Is it?
Was there shouting at your home?
Were there slamming doors when people were upset?
Did they beat you?
Then you don't know what thunder is.
Because thunder is so similar to blows.
But if there is a volunteer, somebody who puts their soul into it, who says: "Wait. 
Stop".
"It's only thunder. 
We're indoors."
"You're okay, you're not alone."
The thunder gets put away in the volunteer's heart.
It doesn't disappear, but it is softened.
Some of the kids I work with, and some young men are afraid of black clouds.
You may say it's silly.
Did your house ever flood?
Have you seen your belongings float away?
Have you seen the river take all your things?
No. Then you don't know
what a black cloud is.
But, when you step into another's shoes, you take the black cloud and say:
"Stop, it's a black cloud. Relax."
"I can't change it, but I'm here.
We'll figure something out."
And the black cloud gets put away in the heart of the volunteer.
Some of the adults I work with, are afraid of ghosts. Can you believe it?
Not the kind in stories. Those are great.

The real kind. The ones that judge.

That say "you can't do it".
"You won't amount to any good" you know?
"You don't stand a chance!"
Those ghosts take years of therapy to be silenced, if they ever can be.
So I ask them to look the ghost in the eye, Because ghosts are cowards, labels slide off.
If you stand up and say: "You don't know me.
You don't know what I'm capable of".
The ghost hides.
Because ghosts are so scared!
Finally, I work with people who are afraid of monsters.
And for good reason.
Because there are monsters with I.D.s.
They vote, they hold office, they live in homes, they terrify their people.
And those monsters, somebody who works with people looks them in the eye and says
"Enough! Not with the kids!"
Because something that a volunteer has, is a mouth to speak up and denounce.
But for that, you need someone, a person with a good pair of arms, not for hitting, but for hugging, for holding, so that in that magical space, the planets may come together
So that being human makes sense.
So that being alive and finding each other is worth it.
And perhaps, in that encounter, One and the others can be transformed into an "us", which is capable of highlighting all that which seemed invisible.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
A few years ago, I volunteered with an NGO in San José de Cusmapa a cute little town in the north of the country.
One afternoon I was walking, talking to children about 12 years old and they told me that they had never been to Managua.
I remember I was very surprised. As I continued talking with them, I realized that, they had never left their municipality.
So I decided to do a mini survey and I began to ask each person I encountered if they had been to Managua.
I realized that only 1 in 10 people had been.
And even fewer had traveled to the beach.
Surveys estimate that only 15 percent of Nicaraguans have left the country, and of this 15 percent only half have traveled beyond Costa Rica.
Then I thought: "If these people do not have many opportunities to leave their community, much less the country, what do they do for fun outside their home?
For inspiration?
To grasp new ideas?"
I inquired, and they said things like: "We take out the rocking chairs and talk with neighbors."
We go to the cockfights, the rodeo, we play the slot machines".
And I thought: Are these activities enriching?
The lack of entertainment that are rich and varied are lived by over 70 percent of Nicaraguans.
In Siuna for example, they have one public pool for the whole town.
The carnival rides arrive once a year for 15 days, and it costs 15 cordobas for each ride.
Even for those living in Managua, entertainment has its limit: Cost.
A concert is worth, on average, 600 cordobas.
The theatre costs around 120 to 250 cordobas.
Realizing this, I thought wow... people with lower incomes, many of them in remote areas which are the majority of Nicaraguans, have their world and imaginations limited to one place.
Many have not left their hometowns and they have little contact with the outside world.
They have few entertainment, and cultural activities and few opportunities for personal exchange.
I would like you to raise your hand, who here had already been to the National Theatre?
Well, all of you are a large minority.
Recently they published a Latin American survey on cultural practices, and it shows that 86 percent of Nicaraguan people have never attended the theater.
The highest percentage in Latin America.
In 2013, 82 percent of Nicaraguans did not attend the movies.
Again, the highest percentage in Latin America.
77 percent of Nicaraguans have never visited heritage sites.
Again, the highest percentage in Latin America, tied with Honduras.
There are several factors that correspond to these results, but an important factor is that people simply do not have access to it.
So all this information, what relevance does it have for me?
I am a marketer and psychologist, and one of the biggest challenges as communicators is find the ideal way to communicate effectively with our target audiences who are often far away.
For many companies in Nicaragua, the audience is the bottom of the pyramid: nearly 4 million people, many of them in rural areas, living with incomes lower than $ 4,000 per year.
These are the big markets.
So if I am in the business of selling cookies, or milk, or cell phone minutes, How do I reach these people?
With what do I reach these people?
What can I do so that they do not only read, watch, receive my message but understand, process, and accept my message?
Radio and television are excellent means but often they fall short, giving us 30 to 60 seconds to promote.
So if our marketing challenge is to reach the bottom of the pyramid, and at the bottom of the pyramid there are not many entertainment options. Why not solve this challenge with entertainment?
What do you like to do for fun, to get out of the house?
For me personally, I love going to the movies.
Then I researched and realized that in Nicaragua only 5 of the 153 municipalities in the country have a cinema.
The minimum charge for a movie is 75 cordobas on promotional days.
For most Nicaraguans, going to the movies is considered a luxury.
It is not accessible to the masses.
So why not transform the idea of going to the movies?
This is how my project was born, Cine Ambulante (Mobile Cinema).
What we do is use a mobile systems, inflatable screens that are easily moved.
We pay movies licenses and make public spaces as courts, sports venues and central parks into cinemas.
So we provide wholesome entertainment to hundreds of people completely free.
And how do we bring these events to fruition?
We have support from sponsors.
For each sponsor a movie night is of great benefit because it gives them the opportunity to better emphasize their message and further promote their products.
To date we have had an average of 800 people per event, and we have reached many areas of the country.
Here is a short video to show you little more.
"Where is your mother? Raise your hand
mom, if you did not take a bath today.
Sincere congratulations for such a wonderful event.
We hardly have in our town recreational events for the population and especially for children, so I feel wonderful.
We have never had such an event of this significance.
It is the first time that it brings together all of the children of Siuna, to make them feel happy, and in harmony."
(Applause) Thank You.
So what are we doing?
We are responding to a lack of entertainment with a marketing strategy.
We are joining a social objective to promote healthy entertainment at the bottom of the pyramid with a commercial purpose, to boost marketing effectiveness in our brands.
And, together, we are making a positive impact in an economically sustainable manner.
Everybody wins.
So why not do things like this more often?
And better yet, why not take it a step further?
This type of platform also offers a great opportunity to educate.
You can combine education with entertainment and do "edutainment".
Edutainment has been used for centuries.
From ancient Greek cultures who would sing the poem of Odyssey to share orally, to the modern world, where edutainment includes museums, educational games, documentaries, and more.
It has been said that edutainment is a communication strategy for social change and that through entertainment we can encompass social themes, such as health and nutrition.
An NGO or a company can also benefit greatly from edutainment.
For example, something we did once with a drink product was that before the film we put a video of a doctor talking about the importance of good nutrition and about the benefits of vitamins and minerals in the product.
So we are educating, entertaining and achieving a corporate objective.
While we were integrating corporate social responsibility to the same corporate brand strategy.
Again, everyone wins.
Next month we will be on tour with El Canto de Bosawas.
We will continue to raise awareness about the environmental problems of the reserve and we will share this Nicaraguan documentary with thousands of people who otherwise could not see it because they lack access to conventional cinema.
Like TED "opens people's minds", film, cultural industries and creativity does as well.
Opening minds at the bottom of the pyramid, for who have not left the community, can have a transformative impact for the country.
We can inspire new ideas, with new knowledge.
We can take them on a journey without leaving their own municipality.
Scholars of the best business schools have referred to the bottom of the pyramid as "the biggest marketing opportunity of the 21st century" books have been written as: "The Fortune at the Bottom of the Pyramid" encouraging companies to seek and find these remote audiences.
I want to emphasize this day that the bottom of the pyramid not only represents economic fortune but also fortune of human capital.
A fortune of minds, a fortune of potential actors of change.
It represents a population that with more inclusion could change the current landscape of Nicaragua, since they are the majority.
Transform film and attach a social objective with a commercial one, it's just a small example of the many opportunities we have at our disposal.
What else could we do if we think beyond that?
Why not transform "Going to the theater"?
Why not transform "Going to the museum"?
Why not urge more spaces of cultural exchange, and make art and culture accessible to larger audiences?
Why not "think big" and bring bigger ideas to the people?
I want to encourage all of you today to start thinking in unconventional ways to include these larger audiences often far removed.
What do you like?
Music? Reading?

Perhaps poetry?
Now think "How can I bring this to more people?
And how can I do it in a economically sustainable way?"
Let's unite more social and commercial objectives.
Let's do edutainment.
Let's use new media offerings as an enriching experience for the bottom of the pyramid.
This is how we will transform the country.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I have quite an issue with truth and conversations.
I'm a scientist but I ended up taking some weird paths.
Science is a way of asking questions to the world and of listening to the answers.
We will never know everything.
But that doesn't mean we know nothing.
There's a lot that we already know.
However, the evidence is sometimes set aside when making decisions or forming an idea of the world.
For example, we already know that climate change is real, but some still deny it.
We already know that vaccines work and are safe.
But some still doubt about it.
That was my first disappointment. Evidence is necessary but not sufficient.
This opened up a new path for me.
I thought the problem was education.
So I left the lab and I turned to teaching.
I love teaching.
The classroom is one of my favorite places.
But there I found the same problem.
I was teaching on vaccines and one student said she didn't get vaccinated because vaccines seemed dangerous.
My hunch was, "She says this because she doesn't know, if I explain, she will change her mind."
I explained to her, but it didn't work.
The evidence is not enough.
Education is not enough.
Second disappointment.
This that happened with my student was my first personal experience with post-truth.
It's what happens when, although the information is there, it's set aside and emotions or beliefs ensue.
With this, another new path opened up.
Maybe, it's a communication problem?
Given that science is a tool, I used it to study post-truth.
I started having conversations with people who don't trust vaccines, while meeting doctors and journalists in an attempt to improve communication on this subject.
And there I realized that I had never learned to talk with those who think differently.
For example, how do we dialogue when the problem is not the evidence but an ideological disagreement?
Experiments show that when people talk only with those who think alike their opinions become more extreme and homogeneous.
But in order to have a healthy democracy, don't we need that those who think differently engage in broad, honest and deep conversations?
This is far from happening.
Every discussion, every disagreement, every conversation looks like a battle between good and evil.
Our opinions, instead of being temporary, bridges to communicate with others, are immovable, a ditch that we dig and tells apart those on our side from the others.
Dialogue disappears, agreements are impossible, and the world shatters in an explosive combination of aggression and distrust.
Can we do something about this?
Not all opinions are born equal.
Some are weak, or short-lived.
Others are intense, or long-lasting.
And others become part of our identity.
When that happens, any doubt on what we think becomes a doubt on who we are.
And that is unbearable.
In addition, the need to protect our integrity makes us team up with those in our same situation.
This is tribalism.
That's why sometimes neither evidence nor education works.
We don't think something, we are that something.
(Applause) Let me ask you a question. Did it ever happen to you of going to a meet up with people you don't know and thinking something like, "Mm, I don't know what these people think, I better not talk about this or that"?
Did it ever happen to you?
Let's see, raise your hand those who lived something like that.
Look at you.
The harm of tribalism is not only that it creates a climate of permanent conflict but that it also creates silence.
Some of us withdraw from the debate, not because we don't have opinions or we don't care about what happens.
We are not halfhearted.
It's the atmosphere of aggression, it's things not moving forward, it's fear, exhaustion, and the social punishment of dissent. It's for one or more of these reasons that we leave conversations in silence.
A loud silence.
And so, the inability to dialogue causes the number of voices to go down.
Until there is only one left, sometimes.
Silence is confused with agreement.
And the illusion of consensus is created.
Because one opinion is heard, there seem to be only one.
And then any other opinion is not only different, but dissonant, alien, and must be eliminated.
In general, we think of censorship as a power which bans from above.
But there is another, more subtle way.
Censorship from below.
Through tools of social discipline, such as raising the tone of the fight, it makes us withdraw.
This is a threat to free expression.
And it makes me think that it's also a problem for democracy.
Both in our small environment and on a large scale.
It would seem then that we only have two options.
Whether we share our ideas despising those who don't think alike, or we shut up.
And by doing that, we give up control to those who speak up.
But this is a false dilemma.
There is another option, but we need to make it evident, because it is hidden in this sea of tribalism.
We can have defined positions, even very intense ones, without riding on the dynamics of intolerant speech.
It's one of the things I learned when talking to people who doubt vaccines.
To break tribalism, to find more voices, to break this "friends or foes" dynamics, I propose to distinguish between what we believe in and how we believe it.
And if we make this "how" non-tribal, we can raise our opinions without allowing what we think to become who we are.
The nuances reappear and conversations become possible.
And from that point we can build consensus which are the product of agreements, despite our differences.
However, when I talk about these ideas I usually get some criticism.
It seems that in order to avoid conflict I suggest that we let consensus happen anytime.
No, that is not what I mean.
If we don't express ourselves because we feel alienated or expelled we are not taking part in decision making.
But we all live with the consequences of those decisions.
So, since we do care, we need to talk.
But if we don't want to talk in this hostile atmosphere because it's exhausting and we see that it leads nowhere, let's try to move past tribal mode.
Beyond what we think.
We may have more in common with those who think differently yet want to talk, than with those who share with us same opinions but are intolerant.
(Applause) They also usually tell me that there isn't much we can do on the individual level to move past tribal mode.
But I think there are some very concrete things to do.
And I have three suggestions that could help.
First, look for pluralism.
Promote it actively.
So dissent becomes visible and this is important because only if we include dissent we can achieve a true consensus.
For this to happen we need to be able to talk without feeling that we are socially punished.
But it also requires listening to voices we don't like.
The moment to defend freedom of expression is now.
Taking care of it is easier than recovering it.
Learn to have better conversations.
To find better ways of disagreeing.
A conversation is not waiting for our turn to talk, trying to impose our ideas by force or insistence.
It's about listening to understand others.
Without listening there's no conversation.
Third, separate ideas from people.
Under tribalism, attacking an idea makes the person feel threatened because they feel attacked as a person.
But how are we going to improve ideas with that attitude?
We need to discuss them, so that the best survive.
People deserve respect.
Ideas have to earn it.
Humans are inventors.
At some point, somewhere, we invented the idea of sitting by the fire to talk.
And at some point, both conversations and fire look alike.
Both are always in between two dangers.
The danger of dying or growing out of control.
It took us time but we learned to handle fire.
We learned to keep it alive so it doesn't go out.
And to handle it so it doesn't destroy us.
Maybe, it's time to learn to do the same with conversations.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Are we alone?
That's the question that keeps me up at night.
Not very long ago, we thought that the human and the Earth were at the center of the universe.
Our beliefs were based on that, we didn't even ask ourselves if we were alone.
But over time, we started to understand that stars, planets moved in a regular form, there was something more complex outside Earth.
And we started asking ourselves, are we alone?
In the last decades we've learned a lot about the evolution of life: we know we come from a cloud of dust, that cloud of dust turned into molecules, those molecules made the soup of life; and from that soup of life comes life and now we're here.
If the conditions of life are so common in the universe, i.e. coming from a cloud of dust, the perception that we're alone becomes a bit strange.
We have information now that would tell us it's everywhere.
To answer this question, we have to do it in two steps.
The first step is to find out which places outside Earth could host life in them.
And the second step is, when we find those places, see if life exists in there.
So our search is more or less oriented.
I'm lucky to work at NASA and to be able to use very powerful tools.
I use the most powerful telescopes of space and the Earth and we send robots to Mars, for example.
Telescopes allow us to look into the Solar System and also outside the System, at great distances.
While robots allow us to explore as if we were human beings with a great precision.
So let's go to the first question: Which places outside Earth could host life?
That's the first base.
The thing is it's complicated, because our standard for life is based, obviously, on a single one that's the one in Earth.
But Earth has elements we have identified as related to life, such as water.
We know oceans are rich in life, where there's life, there's hydrology, there's life forms.
Therefore, water is a very important element for us.
But in reality you only need very little water.
Our planet's still a rock, with a very thin layer of water.
That very thin layer of water is so important in the evolution of life that makes it essential, but many other places can have a very thin layer of water and be inhabitable as well.
For example, let's think of Mars.
Mars, in fact, is very similar to Earth, it's big difference is in its water.
It has water that's in polar layers in the form of ice, but the problem of Mars is that that water is in solid state, while in Earth we're lucky to have it in liquid state.
Therefore, it's not inhabitable per se.
Looking at that water with great precision, with special instruments, I discovered Mars had an ocean that covered it 3500 million years ago, a great part of Mars had an ocean back then.
And over time that ocean started to disappear and become lost in space.
When that ocean was in Mars, life was just starting in our planet.
In the moment life was just starting in our planet imagine something so inhabitable was going on in Mars.
We think that while part of that water escaped into space, there's a part that went below the surface, so there might be inhabitable spots below the surface of Mars.
The focus of the exploration of Mars is based on studying below the surface of Mars, looking for those inhabitable spots.
But Mars isn't the only interesting thing in the Solar System.
Actually, we know two moons, Jupiter's Europa and Saturn's Enceladus, have oceans below their surface, in their ice cortex.
We don't know what they're made of, or if they really have liquid water, or what other type or liquid, or if it has the soup of life like the one we need to generate life, or what are the conditions of those places.
We know there's something liquid below there, but is it inhabitable?
We're lucky that in 2019, very soon, we're sending the biggest and most powerful telescope to space.
That telescope will measure for us the emissions of these oceans with great precision, it'll tell us what's the composition, its gases, if in that place the conditions for the soup of life are there or not.
We'll be able to know with great precision if those moons are inhabitable.
Well, we said that inside the Solar System there's three places that are interesting and might have something like life in them.
So we're already oriented, we don't have to go to places like Jupiter, Uranus, or others.
We have three well-defined places.
Now, the big question, how do we know if there's life if something is going on in the surface or in the oceans of Europa and Enceladus.
The trick is looking for chemical changes, the problem is chemical changes are produced by many other things.
For example, if a meteorite or a comet strikes the surface of Mars it's going to liberate a chemical change, gases that might be similar to what life might be.
Now, let's suppose that below the surface of Mars there's geological activity, this geological activity will also produce this gas we're looking for that might be related to life.
Now, in the most obviously interesting case, we see this cloud of maybe methane gas, for example, in Mars -- and in this case, not in the rest, in this case let's imagine there's a small bacteria that's producing it.
The thing is how we tell which case it is.
That's the big doubt, but there's ways.
The key is knowing if something is going on in Mars.
How can we know if there's methane gas in Mars?
We can do a trick, to look at light that has components, colors.
Now imagine the light of the sun travels throughout space, reaches Mars, is reflected in its surface, and the atmosphere absorbs it.
If there's gas, a molecule like methane for example, in the surface in the atmosphere of Mars, that molecule will leave a very precise trail in the colors of Mars, and if we have a very precise instrument, we could measure those colors to tell if there's methane in Mars or not.
And that's what we are doing.
I spent four years trying all the telescopes and looking for this molecule.
And in 2009, we found it.
We found there was methane, that in Earth is produced in 90 percent by life forms, we found it in Mars.
Imagine the moment of the discovery.
Really, the most intense thing is that we knew it existed from 2005, but we spent all those fours years actually validating, verifying, so that we wouldn't be wrong as it had big implications on the exploration of a planet like Mars.
The discovery was so radical that it changed how we now explore Mars.
All the space agencies, NASA, the European Space Agency, the Japanese and all the rest, are now focused on finding out how can we know what's going on and if we can measure these zones of activity that might be related to biological activity.
And when we see who can produce methane gas, we think of very simple things, the most likely.
Radiation, water, carbon dioxide, a small bacteria consumes this and produces a spacial methane flatulence.
That's the prototype of what we think of, when we think about life outside Earth. we're thinking of this.
And why are we thinking of this?
Because it's the most simple, and probably also the most likely.
That's the big search.
The thing about these two very important discoveries, is that first, we know that in the past Mars was inhabitable or much more inhabitable than what it is now, maybe it had big zone where something was happening, maybe life.
Maybe that methane we're looking at now is a residuary of the life that was produced, that was formed 3500 million years ago and is below the surface acting and emitting those gases.
But if the past of Mars is interesting what's most interesting is its future.
We're now working intensely to send human beings to Mars.
And I believe that in the next 10 or 20 years, all of us here will see a human walk on the surface of Mars.
(Applause) Everything I told you, all these research that's so hard to do with robots, with remote measuring, we'll be able to do them there, human beings will be able to do these missions and search and explore what's really going on in Mars.
Is there something outside the Solar System?
I mean, that search for life, too -- What happens outside the Solar System?
A lot happens.
In the last two decades, we've discovered more than 500 exoplanets or planets that are outside the Solar System in our galaxy.
I mean, imagine the possibilities.
They come in all sorts of colors and types, some form very interesting planetary systems.
Some of them are very similar to Earth.
If we've discovered that in the last two decades, imagine the possibilities.
And that's what keeps me up at night, knowing if we're alone.
And I'm convinced that we're very close to knowing it.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I suppose many of you have heard that we use only 10% of our brain.
And I'm sorry to say that is false.
Think if you use 10% or the whole brain when you are trying to seduce that gal or that guy, like you call them here, eh?
On a more scientific level, we now know by the use of neuroimaging techniques, that we make use of the brain's whole capacity.
We use the whole brain, not just a part.
And when we read, when we listen, when we remember, we use different parts of the brain. Many of them of shared use, but others are different.
Because this is like an orchestra, the violins come in, then the cellos, the violas, then the winds, and they end up working together.
Therefore, we don't use only its 10%.
Another explanation is related to brain damage.
You must have a friend or someone you know who has had a stroke or brain damage, and not one affecting 90% but a very limited part with consequences.
People stop talking, some have problems moving.
So, not just 10% of the brain is used.
These people after rehabilitation and intense training, what they achieve is relearning some of these functions.
Why? Because the brain,
even the adult brain, changes.
It changes with learning.
London taxi drivers have a big challenge, they have to learn a very complex map.
And when we compare the brain of these drivers with other people's, -people who are not taxi drivers- what we find is an increase in the volume of a part of the brain called hippocampus, here shown in red.
It has to do with the functions of remembering, memory and navigation.
We also did another experiment in which we compared people who had learned to read, and people who were illiterate.
And what we found is that in this part of the brain, which I point out in red, in that back part of the brain, there was also a change in volume.
It's like an athlete who works hard, for example, Nadal, and then he develops some iron muscles.
The same happens in the brain: when you work hard on it, it eventually swells.
Learning changes the brain, and what is education?
It's learning.
It changes the brain and our lives.
It's the weapon of social change.
Medicine made the transformation, thanks to science more than 100 years ago.
Now people don't die from the flu, from a flu epidemic.
They don't even die from cancer.
Many cancers can be cured if caught early.
That's what we want for education.
When an anesthesiologist puts anesthesia, they don't ask if it's progressive or conservative, but whether it works, based on the results from experiments.
That's what we want for education.
We need an education informed by empirical evidence.
Let's do an experiment here.
What we intend to do with this experiment: observe the computer screen, right? At the top there's a string of consonants, at the bottom there's a string of pads.
Now I am going to display another screen and the string of pads will appear above, and the string of letters, below.
Your task will be to tell me if they are the same or different, if there's a change between the part above and the one below.
You agree?
Since we don't have 2 buttons, what we will do is: if it's the same above and below, you remain silent, okay?
And if it's different, if something changes, you raise your hands.
We agree?
If they are equal you do nothing, if different, you raise your hands.

Okay? Let's begin with the experiment.
Are you asleep or is that a "yes"?
It's a "yes", alright.
Here we go, now, next. Remember:
if they change you raise your hands.
Ready? (Strings of letters and pads)
(Audience raises hands) Very good.

Next trial. Go! No, no, come, there, come.
Next trial. There!
(Audience raises hands)
They are different. Good.
There have been 2 conditions in this little experiment.
If you notice, what we did in the first was changing the 2 middle consonants.
In "B", we change the position of the same consonants.
When we do this experiment with illiterate or preliterate children, for them it is just as difficult, the first or the second.
When we do this experiment with you,
you take more time in the second.
The second is harder. Why?
Because the brain of illiterates and preliterate children see it as a whole, they don't break it into parts.
However, you break it into parts.
Because you have learned to read.
I still remember when I learned to read with my teachers Emilio and Caridad, in class they taught me to read, perhaps like many of you learned, with the "ma-ma" "Mama", the "h", the "o", the "m", the "e" is "home".
But later on, other methods appeared with a more progressive tone.
Global methods that seemed more inclusive, therefore more fashionable.
It's good that different approaches exist, but the point is that, eventually, it has become a simply ideological, intuitive dilemma.
This is better than that, then I do this or the other.
Well, what I have shown you here is that the brain goes to details.
And as it goes to details, why would we feed it with globality?
But well, you may be skeptics and you should be, skepticism in life is very important.
What we did, not satisfied with that, was an experiment with preschoolers.
We selected preliterate children and assigned them to 2 groups so that the 2 groups would be equal, considering some variables, IQ, etc. And then, we put half of them in an analytical method, that is, the one of "m" with "a" is "ma", and the other half learned to read with a global method.
After a year and a half, so that they could all read fine, we performed a series of tasks with them to see who executed better: if those from the analytical method or the global method.
Here I show you the results of 2 tests, there are more than 10, where we see how they behave, the analytical and the global method.
One of the tasks is phonological, they heard the word "lion". And we asked, "of these 2 words, now appearing, 'lens' and 'candle', which starts with the same sound as the word 'lion'?"
Obviously, for you this is very simple.
It took longer for the children learning with the global method, see the red bar.
Notice that the red bar is higher, it took longer than the green bar, who are the analytical method.
If you see the 2 circles in the middle, the red circle is above the green circle.
That means that in this task, children who have learned to read with the global method take longer and they are slower, than those who have learned to read with the analytical method.
You'd say, "sure, you're cheating, because phonology is practiced a lot in the analytical method and not much in the global method."
Well, we'll see another task, a more semantic task that would favor those on the global method.
The task was to select a word out of four.
That which didn't fit with the rest.
Here we see that there are 3 that relate to fruits and 1 that doesn't.
Yet again, what we have now, if you see the 2 circles, is that the errors are similar, but the red bar is higher.
This takes longer for the kids who learned with the global method, than for those who learned with the analytical method.
We summarize here all the tasks. A lot of tasks we performed: spelling, phonology, lexicon, semantics; the analytical method wins in all of them by a landslide.
Now I ask you: if you were ministers of education, because each of us carries a minister of education within, right?
Would you follow your adviser or this data?
Don't reply, because maybe tomorrow one of you becomes Minister of Education and we are not putting you on the spot.
But think that this would be a decision based on science and not on intuition, or the minister adviser's ideology.
This is an example, there are others.
I'll show you another example of reading, and I have focused today on reading because reading is the scaffold on which other cognitive abilities are built, it's the window to knowledge.
It's probably the most important thing that happened to us in life.
After love, yes.
Then, there is another part which is: can we make an early detection of children who will have reading problems?
For those who don't know, dyslexia is a problem that affects around 8-10% of children.
It's about having difficulties reading, when doing the translation between letters and sounds.
They are not stupid children, these children have a normal IQ.
But when they begin to see that they can't do as their peers, and their mates in turn see that they are not going well, kids start to avoid going to school, their self-esteem goes down.
They can end in social exclusion, because many of them are headed towards school failure.
Then, it's important to diagnose dyslexia, not only when it already manifests at 8 years, like it's diagnosed now, but to look at neuroscience to diagnose it before it manifests.
And thus we could design therapies, design appropriate interventions, to alleviate that problem.
This is what our lab is engaged in.
The speech signal I am emitting, the physical signal, is made of different waves.
At the same time, within your brains, now that you are listening, your neurons are communicating at different frequencies.
And your frequencies are aligning with the physical frequencies of speech.
Therefore we propose: if dyslexic children have phonological problems...
Phonological problems are: I say, if we take out the first sound, tell me what is left of this word:
"Parrot", what would be left?
(Audience responds) And "silver"? (Audience responds)
That which is so easy for you, is extremely complicated for a kid with dyslexia.
We reasoned: maybe the origin
of these phonological problems, is in the alignment of these frequencies that their neurons are establishing now with physical frequencies of speech.
And what we did was an experiment in which we compared dyslexic children with controls.
And what we found is that there were problems in 2 brain areas.
One, in the right hemisphere in the auditory cortex, and another, in the left hemisphere, where I mark it with the 2 circles, in the left hemisphere, in Broca's area.
Both are areas related with language.
Broca's area is related with phonological processes, like those we spoke about earlier.
Well, here we have something that not only can serve to make an early diagnosis, but that perhaps is the origin of these kids' problems.
Also the connection between these 2 areas is broken in dyslexic kids.
Therefore, if indeed the origin of phonological problems is speech, we can intervene not only in phonological problems, but in speech itself.
And it's super easy to do.
It's a task children can do before they start reading.
If that is so, and this is the challenge for our lab and others for the next years, we could diagnose children at risk of dyslexia before the problem starts.
Before their self-esteem goes down, before they start having reading problems.
This is the adventure we are at and it's another undeniable example of how neuroscience can help education.
How education can feed on the findings of neuroscience to evaluate educational policies; to see these kids who may have problems and may fail in school.
The future is full of challenges, opportunities and goals.
The first thing we have to do is to banish these neuromyths, like the one I mentioned at the beginning of the talk, because they harm the relationship between neuroscience and education.
And they only serve four crooks who try to sell us some program to train that 90%.
Another thing we don't have to fear is this symbiosis.
Neuroscience doesn't come to replace education.
Neuroscience will never create political or educational projects. It can help evaluating them; education creates them,
neuroscience has tools for assessing these educational policies, the introduction of new technologies, in short, many things that happen in the world of education.
And it can help with that early diagnosis of many learning disorders.
We started our private battle in that regard.
We have created a laboratory in a school.
It's within the school, physically.
Children come there, we work with them, we perform experiments.
In fact, the experiment I mentioned on the reading methods popped up at the talks we had with teachers and the principal.
They told us once, "can you tell us which method is better to teach reading?"
"Are we doing things right or wrong?"
And I said, "well, I don't know, but we can do an experiment and find out."
And there you have the results.
This September we'll go to another school. They called us to see when to introduce English reading and writing.
I think that's the way to go: an education not based on intuition, not based on good will or people's ideology, but based on empirical evidence, like medicine is.
So I would say to each of the ministers of education within you: let neuroscience enter the classroom.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
