♪ Just two peas in a pod ♪ ♪ like us ♪ ♪ Two who have the soul ♪ ♪ of a hundred ♪ ♪ Two who really love each other ♪ ♪ Two who really love each other ♪ ♪ Just two who love each other ♪ ♪ is enough ♪ ♪ Two who have the soul ♪ ♪ of a hundred ♪ ♪ Two who really love each other ♪ ♪ Two who really love each other ♪ ♪ Just two who love each other ♪ ♪ is enough ♪ (Applause) Thank you very much.
At home, when we have to store a substance that's potentially toxic --
I'm talking about an insecticide or a product designed for pest control -- we take precautions.
We either store it in a shed or in a separate room, or even on a top shelf, well marked and out of the reach of children.
However, what if I tell you that every single one of us without realising it, has been storing a potentially dangerous substance in our kitchens for years?
This substance is far more dangerous for pregnant women and for children.
Furthermore, we often take these substances, invisible to the naked eye, back and forth our workplace and they are in contact with our food and this substance, that some of you are probably still unaware of, is called bisphenol A.
Bisphenol A is a petrol-based chemical which is used to make hard plastics like polycarbonates.
Polycarbonates, and bisphenol in particular, are what give these plastics solidity and transparency.
And it's bisphenol A which is used to make most plastics, many of which we use to store or preserve food in our kitchens.
It is not the only place where we can find bisphenol A.
It is also found in the ink in supermarket receipts, in the inner layer of tins of food which are painted, or let's say, covered by a film to stop them from corroding; they are also in containers, dental fillings and baby bottles.
Bisphenol A weakly binds itself to the plastic which means that when exposed to heat and detergents when we clean these utensils, it comes off the plastic and passes onto the food.
When we heat up food with a microwave for example, whatever foodstuffs kept in the containers containing bisphenol, are contaminated and when we consume them these chemicals enter our bodies.
Once they are inside our bodies, they act as impostors
imitating the actions of hormones, imitating the actions of estrogens.
Estrogens are hormones that are normally produced in the ovaries, and less commonly, in the testicles and other glands, such as adrenal glands.
Estrogens are hormones that are connected to reproduction, and are crucially important to very sensitive biological mechanisms.
More than one of you will be wondering right now if a chemical substance capable of blending in with estrogens, imitating its actions and effects, can alter the process of reproduction,
and experiments confirm this fear.
With the research group of which I am part, we are working on designing experiments on animal test subjects with the aim of understanding and discovering what the effects are and the consequences for reproductive health.
What do we know so far about bisphenol A?
We know it's capable of -- Firstly, of all the chemicals considered environmental pollutants, it is one of the most studied globally.
What do we know? We know that
it doesn't behave like a normal toxin.
We all know that with toxins, or at least the idea, is that the higher the dose, the more profound the effect.
However, bisphenol A doesn't behave in this way.
It is a toxin that can function in extremely small doses, measured in parts per million,
just like hormones.
Supervisory bodies have established doses for bisphenol that they consider safe.
What is safe?
It's a daily dose of exposure that is unlikely to cause disorders.
However, in our experiments in which we expose newborn female lambs to bisphenol with the aim of studying them at a later stage, we find changes in their ovarian function.
This has happened not only exposing them to safe doses, but also to ones 100 times lower than the safe dose.
Another thing that we know about bisphenol is that it is part of a group of chemicals whose effect is not seen immediately, but rather long after the initial exposure.
And when I say 'long after', this can mean that exposure to bisphenol during the pregnancy or soon after birth shows its effects weeks, months, years and decades down the line, long after the moment of exposure.
What is happening?
Just as estrogens normally program reproductive organs so that in adult life they work correctly, the interference of bisphenol can cause what we call a reprogramming of those organs and therefore later on, in adult life, they won't function effectively, or will become infected or damaged.
There is increasingly stronger evidence regarding the effects
caused in animal test subjects. In studying the effects on the reproductive organs such as the uterus, we can see that the changes produced later on in adult life lead to errors in the implantation of the embryo.
In the case of the mammary glands and the prostate, there have been instances among laboratory animals of precancerous lesions caused by exposure to bisphenol.
In the testicles, there is an immediate decrease in function, a decrease in the sperm count, and in the semen quality.
As I've mentioned, we know that there is a change in the ovaries that could compromise the future fertility of the female in animals, and probably also in women.
But this is all about animals.
What do we know about its effect on humans?
In human beings, there have been cases of bisphenol being detected in urine, in blood, in the amniotic liquid that surrounds the foetus, and in milk.
This means that bisphenol is capable of not only penetrating the placental barrier and pass to the foetus, but also that if a woman has been exposed to bisphenol she can also pass it on both via breast milk and through the accumulation of bisphenol in tissue, which during something as necessary and common as breastfeeding, can be passed onto the baby.
All these facts are giving us a full overview of the effects that can be produced in humans, based on tests carried on animals.
Well then, what can we do?
Firstly, and most importantly, I think is to learn more about bisphenol A, learning by studying reliable information and sharing it.
This will lead to a situation like that of Canada, for example, who, having looked at studies also carried out in Argentina, made the decision to ban baby bottles made of plastic, becoming the first country in the world to do so.
The USA then followed suit, followed by the European Union, and finally Argentina in 2012, with the aim of minimising risks that could be produced by the passing of bisphenol from plastic baby bottles to the baby.
But there are other things that we can do.
First of all, not mixing heat with plastic and foods.
To the extent possible, not to heat food kept in plastic containers in microwaves.
Another thing would be to use an alternative: ceramic, glass, metal or simply to empty the plastic container into a plate that isn't made of plastic and then heat it.
Another recommendation is that people who work as cashiers in supermarkets use gloves to avoid exposure to the ink on receipts.
There are studies that show that those who work as cashiers have higher levels of bisphenol in their blood.
There are also studies carried out in fertility centres where it has been noted that women who are infertile have elevated levels of bisphenol
in the blood.
What else can we do?
As in life, there are options: we can bear this information in mind, we can ignore it, or we can wake up and take measures of precaution, such as those that I've mentioned, to minimise the risks of bisphenol exposure.
One such measure can be knowing what we are buying and what we are using and if we are going to use plastic containers, we should ensure that we use those of which we are certain that, dating back to their production, have always been free of bisphenol.
There are such containers in the market, and we should use these if we decide to use plastic.
Like everything in life, or like many things in life, we can either worry about it or do something about it.
The decision is for all of us to make.
(Applause)
Jose Adolph, a German writer, who died in Peru in 2008 let his imagination fly in 1971.
"That afternoon, when the tickers' bells chimed and the news spread like a miracle, men from all latitudes fused in one triumphant shout.
As it was predicted 100 before, man had finally conquered immortality, in 2168.
All newsletters remarked that great biology news.
A revolution.
I was also happy, naturally, at first.
We had waited a long time for that day!
A single 100 milliliter shot was all what was needed to never die.
A single shot every 50 years.
From that day, only an accident could finish a human life.
Goodbye to illness, aging and death for natural causes.
Until the second news came, complementing the first one.
The shot will only work for people younger than 20 years.
Only young people would be immortal.
Owners of the universe forever.
Free. Fertile. Gods.


But not for us.
Until yesterday.
When the first 15-year-old kid that received the shot decided to kill himself."
From that moment, a lot of questions came to my mind.
I was 11, 12, and I still ask myself the same questions.
Why can't humankind reach immortality?
Or at least, why can't we get to an older age in a better way, physically and emotionally?
Some people say that the man who will live 1,000 has already been born.
May be among you...
But how is he going to reach those 1,000 years?
In which way?
With which kind of life quality?
When I was little, my dad spoke to me about his job as a surgeon.
I remember many cases, but one of them in particular, about a 16-year-old girl, that came to the hospital with a disfigured face.
She had been in a fire and the sequels were permanent.
I asked myself, Is it impossible that our organism could recover completely without sequels?
He spoke to me about a group of cells that wandered around the body taking care of immunity and reparation.
I imagined them like a mysterious army wandering around to protect us.
"With that army's help", he'd say to me, and with more primitive cells, maybe there's a solution."
But I would repeat to myself: is that so hard, even for the skin, -that now I know that it takes 21 days to regenerate- to make a full repair?
Clearly, if the wound was very deep, there was something that didn't work;
the skin wouldn't regenerate.
Today, we know a bit more about those mechanisms.
Not so far ago, it was discovered that there are cells in our organism that aren't from any kind of tissue yet.
Potentially they can become any kind of tissue: skin, bones.
You must have heard about mother cells, those primitive cells my dad spoke about.
The body sends them signs with spatial information, chemical signs so that cells understand which kind of tissue should they turn into.
That information makes cells develop and differentiate to generate new tissues.
However, those signs could be compromised and even interrupted, when the wound is very deep, like in a severe burn or any other serious wound.
Without that spatial information in which cells understand how the have to grow, they get lost.
Without that information, cells do not know what to do, where to go and even which type of tissue they have to turn into.
When the tissue is healthy, cells themselves generate what we call an extracellular matrix.
It's a kind of platform where cells can get hold and grow without falling.
Like a creeper and its wall.
That extracellular matrix works as a substrate for cells, so they can hold, migrate and proliferate.
That kind of matrix has different shapes. It's not the same a plain matrix than a foamy matrix, and it shows the cells their way.
How can we recreate that matrix without losing chemical signs and showing cells that spatial information?
I remember my dad used to say: "it can't be that complicated.
Look at nature, it solves similar situations very simply."
"Look at those fictional characters, that have superpowers, they are inspired in nature.
For example, a spider that with its web, a super resistant material, a biopolymer, can carry the weight of its own body.
A spider creates its web, made of silk."
I was very little and I didn't understand completely what he was saying.
But he was right.
Marsupials, for example, can regenerate damaged skin within hours thanks to a protein that repairs the damaged DNA.
Humans can't do that, but imagine if we could repair a deep burn like in the case of that girl, instantly.
How can we develop a similar, effective answer to what nature is showing us?
The idea would be to orientate cells to recognize the path they have to follow and regenerate the right organ in the right way.
The chemical signs were quite clear.
By protecting the wound, we could keep it from dehydrating and generate that liquid vehicle in which signs can travel to cells and give them the information.
But for spatial issue it wasn't that clear.
There were studies done on different materials to generate platforms with different results.
Until I met some sort of a "Spiderman".
He's not Spiderman, right?
but he's quite similar.
His name is David Kaplan.
David Kaplan is a professor, an American scientist, who started to use silk as a matrix to build those platforms to indicate cells where to grow.
I decided to work with silk because it's a very interesting molecule.
It allows us to create very diverse structures.
We can create sponges, films, we can use it with liquid, create gel, solid materials.
Look at one of the structures that can be created using silk.
What you see there is an emulation from silk we made at the lab to rebuild the fluffy part of the bones.
We added calcium carbonate micro particles to it, so we can mineralize the structures inside it so cells can grow from those substrate.
But we didn't work only with bones.
We made capillaries; we made this at the lab.
See, that is a capillary made from silk that allows us to feed the bone with everything it needs and irrigate that tissue.
Those capillaries not only work for bones.
They can work for other tissues as well.
I work with bones,
but there are other teams that work even with heart tissue.
That seems crazy, right?
It seems taken from science fiction film.
Rebuilding damaged and even old tissues, and be able to rejuvenate them.
But we have to keep in mind that this is a natural process.
Take a look and remember what happens to our body when, for example, we remove three quarters of the liver.
The liver regenerates, and works again.
The skin, in 21 days, if the wound isn't very deep, regenerates.
Or the transformation into a supplementary organ, like the kidneys.
We remove one and the other goes through hypertrophy and covers the work of the other.
Even though the job of the scientist has to be rigorous and follow the scientific method, we shouldn't forget the inner child.
That curious and creative being that chases utopia.
That's the story of all scientific discoveries, right?
Man imagines and science makes it real.
So today, taking a break from the lab's table let me dream a little.
I dream that in my next TEDTalk in about 50 years, we can meet here, as young and happy as today.
(Applause)
Good afternoon.
Seems like a difficult task narrating 30 years worth of time in 15 minutes. But, I'll do it gladly.
Actually, it was in 1990, an afternoon like today's when a friend of mine, a famous philosopher, told me that humanity would not survive the 21st century if it wasn't capable of creating a new God.
The phrase lingered in my head in an obsessive way. So I decided to study it seriously.
I started by "What is the 21st Century?"
Why do we need a new God in the 21st but didn't need it in the 14th, or will need it in the 25th?
There are around 4200 active religions on the planet. Some of them worship many gods.
So there are many gods wondering about the planet.
But in order to simplify things, I'll refer to the most popular.
The most popular god on the planet says that Man lost Paradise because he ate from the Tree of Knowledge.
And it so happens that the 21st century is marked by knowledge being more important than raw materials.
The talk from a couple of minutes ago might have proven that to you.
The volume of information and knowledge is of such magnitude that is making this century different than the previous ones.
At one point I was tempted to create a manual for the construction of a god that would be more adjusted to the the 21st century.
The first thing that came to mind was that instead of making a sin out of eating fruit from the Tree of Knowledge, it would be a virtue.
It would be practical for this century and easier for the parents to tell their children to study as God wants them to. And while they are on it
they could have apples for snacks,
which in Latin is called "mala" - bad - because of that damn Paradise story.
A paradise where the woman was made out a man's useless little bone, just to keep him company. Not quite adjusted to the 21st century either.
Then I realized that other people had already thought about the invention of a new god as well as that this was the moment to do it.
One of them was Linus Pauling.
He is the only human to have been awarded with two Nobel prizes.
Some other scientists had them by teaming up, but Pauling had them on his own.
One was in Chemistry and the other one for Peace.
He belonged to a generation of American scientists, among other nationalities, who developed the Atomic Bomb.
They had a problem: they were atheists.
And in the US, in the 1950s,
atheists were thought to be communists.
They could lose their jobs, get expelled,
some had to flee the country.
So they decided to create a religion for atheists.
While researching, Pauling found that there wasn't already only one, but three: The universalists, the humanists, and the Unitarians.
So they decided to become Unitarians.
Unitarians basically believed that God is natural, not supernatural. That nature is God. And that those feelings we often get of transcendence, fear of the unknown, or the need to join with others, among other feelings, well that is "human nature."
I have been lucky enough to have spoken with Linus Pauling. And I asked him if this was just an alibi or if he was serious about it, if he went to church, he said "yes".
That he would go on Sunday mornings with his wife to a Unitarian Church, and that they would exchange information, scientific updates, they would sing, have picnics, the teenagers would date each other, and everyone felt good about spending the Sunday together.
This is to say, there have been other clues particularly since the onset of scientific thinking, where the feeling of the supernatural is incompatible with other feelings we've got and also require answers, which humans look for, and not always find.
If we look at our own history, we have around 25,000 centuries wandering around the planet.
From those 25,000 centuries, we have spent around 24,000 eating fruits and small animals, throwing rocks at each other, or using them as our only tools, and only near the end of that period were able to control fire, and to look for ways of creating and maintaining it.
The existence of fire has probably developed the possibility for language --
nothing like sitting around a bonfire and having a nice chat.
That's where language started to develop.
The primitive man probably knew more than we do about plants and foods that are good and bad for us, and, of course, as there was no GPS they surely had a better sense of orientation than we have.
By the end of those 24,000 centuries, when the last period begun, things started to move quickly.
It is only since the last 100 centuries that we started having cities, sedentary communities, and technology development.
Technology begins to exist, to sophisticate, and to go faster.
In this room there are people from the telegraph generation, like myself, from the generation of the valve radios -- when I was a child we had to wait for them to heat up --
of the black and white TV, of the transistors, of the microprocessors or of the touch screens.
And there might also be someone from the generation that still tries to figure out how to keep the fire burning.
This is to say that technology goes very fast.
However, it's not about the way of thinking.
For thousands and thousands of years it has been very difficult for us to explain where we come from, where we are headed to, what is the reason for things. Even understanding the relationship between cause and effect.
Primitive thinking -- which the human species is more experienced in -- is comprehensive. Everything has an explanation, and that explanation is linked solely to immediate experience.
One only hates or loves clouds when it rains, depending on whether one needs water or not.
We all posses primitive thinking be it at the stadium, or while driving. And that kind of thinking has been very powerful and comprehensive in every human community, and has been replaced by magical thinking.
Magical thinking is an improvement.
It is so because it establishes a relationship -- even though it's unreal -- in an effort to understand the relationship between cause and effect.
Magical thinking is getting up on the right foot, touching wood -- the complete group of superstitions that exist,
and continue to exist. It is only with scientific thinking, which doesn't go back that many centuries, that relationships between phenomena are sought outside themselves.
But why do we need to create a new god in order to survive the 21st century?
First of all, because this century doesn't leave much room for the supernatural.
We have enough knowledge, enough means to get to the information to allow us to try to provide an explanation, or to understand that where we run short of explanations one day explanations will be found.
After magical thinking, religious thinking arrived, which is in fact the subject I wanted to talk about.
Religious thinking adds a series of features to magical thinking regarding human needs, that had not been properly addressed up until then. The notion of being close to one another, connected with one another, communicating. Having a place where to look for comfort, company, hope. Being able to tell what is right from what is not.
Religion fulfils a very important role in the development of mankind because of feelings that are natural, not supernatural.
These are feelings of belonging to each other, of realizing ourselves as part of something bigger than ourselves, that we are ultimately brothers, sharing the planet with common destinies.
Sometimes I'm scared of what I trying to say.
I'd like you to see this as fictional theology, a type I invented for myself, which others seem to practice without admitting it.
The thing is that we've reached a moment in humanity, where before getting to this moment we are overwhelmed by information. We do thousands of things at the same time. We take out our phones, not for calling, but for taking selfies, to remind us of what we have just done five minutes ago.
We loose time,
not because we're doing something, but because we're distracted all the time. And there are few chances of using that time to think deeply about the grater issues that worry us.
If we think about today's technology, about which we have already talked and instead of 25,000 centuries backward, we think 25,000 centuries ahead? Where will we be then, if no catastrophe blocks our way and we survive the 21st century?
We will have probably explored everything that is explorable.
We will know everything that can be known, and we will communicate it instantly.
We are already doing it today, probably
with the future progress in biology we might be able to line up our brains in order to join the knowledge we have and think together and resolve gigantic problems at the same time.
That's to say we'll be omnipresent, omniscient and almighty -- which is how God is usually characterized.
That kind of God exists in the future.
But if we look at today, where we already have some of those characteristics, there is no doubt that we have the power to self destruct. Be it through nuclear arsenals, abusing natural resources, global warming, for which we are partly responsible.
In other words, we are somehow the gods of our own destruction.
Maybe that's what the philosopher meant, when he said, "Oh, we have to come up with a new god otherwise we won't make it through the 21st century."
So as it is quite difficult to narrate over 25 years of work on this subject I will share with you this last reflection I have arrived to, which is:
What connects us today, as we communicate with each other, is the Internet.
Our religion is our network.
Our temple is the planet and each one of you, each one of us is a god, who has the responsibility to respect life, believe in differences and in the power you have to learn from them, in brotherhood, freedom, and the possibility to motivate new generations to become better than we are.
My conclusion is that we are all a bit of a god and together we are the God of the 21st century.
Thanks a lot.
Amen.
(Applause)
The perception we have of a scientist has changed a lot in recent years.
Not long ago, a scientist was more or less this:
An old, half-mad, white-haired man who, out of malice or clumsiness, could end up causing a catastrophe.
Today, being a nerd and being into science is cooler.
We're on TV, not only in documentaries, but there are sitcoms about nerds, and people watch them.
We, as scientists, are so crucial, that people believe in us when they decide what to buy.
Science is used today as a selling point.
For example, in this ad, this lady persuades us that Omega-3s are good.
What leads us to believe this woman?
A lab coat?
Would she still convince us
if she had no lab coat on? This idea that scientists have all the answers is not only in advertising but also in school.
In science class, they answer our questions, and they persuade us that brilliant scientists obtained those answers in a eureka moment.
The perception I had of a scientist for a very long time was someone brilliant who could figure things out that others couldn't.
A giant capable of looking beyond, and who shared with the rest of us what the world's reality was like.
Then he would write it in a book, and then they would ask me
about it in an exam. Today I want to convince you that you don't have to be superior to be a scientist.
That we, scientists, aren't extraordinary masterminds.
In order to do it, I'd like to suggest going back to school and trying to remember what it was like to be there sitting in science class.
This has surely happened to you: coming up with a half-crazed idea, a question you were anxious to answer, and you realized that the lesson wasn't meant for that, but for the teacher to tell us what we should learn and not to answer what we were curious about.
As if the search for scientific answers occurred in a parallel universe that we are not a part of and science class was filled with answers to memorize.
I remember it happened to me once.
We were learning about the digestive system.
I had the idea that for me, it was very reasonable.
I thought that at some point inside us, there had to be a strainer because, yes, we consume solids and liquids, solids and liquids, and it turns out that they come out separately.
I sat down in that class, the teacher showed us all the organs, but none of them was a strainer.
I raised my hand and asked: How, with no strainer, could we separate solids and liquids?
I didn't need my classmates or teacher laughing to realize that the question didn't belong in that class.
A few years later, it happened again.
Someone in school convinced us that everything, absolutely everything was made of atoms.
Again, I had the feeling of being forced to learn something that didn't convince me.
Picture it, what this lady said is that us and this are made of the same stuff.
How could we both be made of atoms if I am alive and this isn't?
What's so magical about atoms in living things?
What these two questions have in common is that they don't belong in our science lessons, and since they don't belong in our science lessons and they don't belong in any other situation, most of us end up forgetting them.
I would have forgotten this question were it not for the fact that years later, at some science conferences open to the community, a group of scientists convinced me that it was still worth remaining curious about this topic.
At that moment, I learned that even real scientists were trying to answer what's so magical about atoms in living things. How are we different from this?
It's been 50 years since a group of science giants discovered that the difference between us and this is in the atoms' position, in the way they are organized.
Apparently, in every living thing, there's a group of atoms that's organized like a helix. The difference, then, was in the atoms' position, and for me, this answer was fascinating.
Especially, because after 50 years, scientists continue to come across questions in this field.
It seemed like science was built from this waterfall of questions.
It took me many years to understand that scientists are so focused on the questions.
During those years, I studied chemistry, and I started doing experiments in order to answer questions that no one had answered before, that we didn't know the answer to.
For the first time, I had this genuine feeling that I was trying something no one had tried before.
I was trying to answer a question I couldn't google.
As great as it sounds, these first experiments gave me no answer to the question, they just warned me about the importance of other questions that I should consider before making an experiment.
That's how I began to understand that we, scientists, don't dream of finding the answers but of finding questions we are able to answer. That's what we're interested in.
Once the answer shows up, we don't want to repeat it or memorize it but to challenge it by thinking what questions it generates.
Studying chemistry has allowed me to join the science community that designs techniques to see what we couldn't see until now and tries to ask itself what are we capable of imagining from this new perspective?
In recent years, in this community, we design techniques that allow us not only to tell where the atoms are and study their positions but also their motion.
So, these models or pictures formed by the atoms' position are just part of the information in living organisms.
It's like, all these years we've been watching pictures, and now we can watch the actual film.
Of course, it isn't that easy.
It's not that we invented a camera that goes inside the cell and tells us where each of the atoms are and how they move.
But one of the results we obtained with this technique is that the information about living organisms is not only used to determine the position but to tell us a lot about how the atoms move.
The movement of atoms is as important for their function as it is for their position.
So, the difference between us and this is not just about the position of atoms but about how atoms can get messed up and move.
But then, if I could mess up and move the atoms in this thing so they can get messed up and move just like mine, would this be alive as well?
Again, facing an answer like this one, it is much more interesting to think about which questions it produces rather than repeating it or memorizing it.
And the nicest thing about all these questions is that today, you wouldn't find them in a science exam.
Because in reality, we don't know the answers.
That makes me wonder, what questions should be asked in a science exam?
If in fact science is based more on questions rather than answers,
then what should we actually learn from science?
Maybe one thing we should know is that the essentials aren't what we see, or what we are told we should see, but what is beyond, where there are more questions than answers.
I like to think that if we all understand that science is not built by giants, or people standing on giants' shoulders, but by an entire society that shares their will to look at the world.
We can all, scientists and non-scientists, try to remember those questions that didn't belong in our science lessons,
those crazy ideas, those questions that gave us anxiety, and go search for their answers, knowing that even though we may not find them, we're going to fill ourselves with amazing questions.
No matter what you do, if you become psychologists, carpenters, acrobats, lawyers, do science or become professional tennis players, don't be fooled by advertising or a chemistry exam.
The important thing isn't that you come up with a good answer on your own but that we all add up our respective curiosity in order to have more and better questions to answer.
Curiosity isn't a scientist's privilege, it is and must be everyone's right.
Thank you very much. (Applause)
For many years, I've been Rafael Nadal's coach and I've met two important requirements to be a good coach.
The first one is that I'm his uncle, is always harder to fire a relative than somebody else, and the second is that as we are half Catalans I've been the cheapest coach in the circuit and that counts too.
For that ... (Applause) For that I've stayed in that position many years, not for other reasons.
Once, in Monte Carlo, we had to play the final against a not very good Swiss player, he played for a while.
So, we had to play against him and Rafael asked me, "How do you see the match today?"
And I told him, "How do I see it?
I see it a bit tough."
"Federer has a better drive than yours, his backhand is better volley is better too" ... And when I was about to tell him, "And the serve, there's no comparison,"
He told me, "Whew! Stop, Stop."
"You really support me before going to the pitch."
And I told him, "I could lie to you but in a while Federer isn't going to do so. It's better for you to know what you are going to deal with, from now on we are going to look for answers."
That has been the way I've understood, a kind of way that I've understood training.
Accepting reality is one complicated thing to do nowadays.
It seems today that we always have to give messages to our relatives, positive ones.
We have to say to them constantly that they are almost the best ones, that they are really good.
I think that this is not a good principle, I prefer the other one.
Knowing that I'm not good enough.
I think that when you know that you are not good enough and you know reality, that's the first step, the starting point, to reach your goals.
That's how I've always understood it.
I've always avoided an overvaluation of the boys I was training, mostly of Rafael.
He was, obviously my nephew and I could do it.
I remember I add after telling him that, I told him, OK, well, if you are capable of playing every point as if it were the last one, if you are capable of playing this match as if life depended on it, if you are more excited than him and you are ready to run faster than him, I think you'll have lots of chances of victory.
I've always believed in chances of victory although, the truth is, it hasn't gone out very well.
That girl I told her that she didn't know how to play, ended fourth of Spain in her category, which wasn't that bad.
This search of impartiality, of avoiding a hoax, as I was saying, it hasn't stopped me from having the maximum confidence in knowing that we'll reach our goals.
Always.
I've always had the maximum trust that Rafael would achieve his goals with work, obviously.
I remember, many years ago, at Carlos Moyá´s place, Carlos Moyá asked me -- Rafael was about 15 years old, he asked me, "Would you sign for Rafael to become an Albert Costa in the future?"
Albert Costa had just won Roland Garros, he was the seventh at the ATP.
And I told him, "No, I don't sign for that."
"I don't think that Rafael will be better, I want to believe that he'll be better."
Carlos Moyá was surprised by that statement
Because Rafael was just a young promise and aiming to also be a Roland Garros champion, wasn't that easy.
Immediately afterwards he asked me, "Would you sign for Rafael to become a Carlos Moyá in the future?"
I answered... "Of course!"
Carlos Moyá also won a Roland Garros, he was world number one, five years among the eight bests.
I told him, "Yes, I sign for him to be a Carlos Moyá."
We had dinner, he invited us.
We said goodbye, but just as the door closed, when Rafael and I left, I told him, "Well, there's no way I would sign it."
(Laughter) In his house, obviously, I couldn't let him down.
There's no way but to shape well the character.
I have been a coach who took care more of shaping Rafael's character, strengthening his character, than to form him well technically.
That's the reason behind those strange hits that he makes over the head, or serving as he does now, although I think he serves better, but for many years, I didn't manage to make him learn.
But the truth is, it never was my fault that he didn't learn how to serve.
It was always his fault ... (Laughter) because, as many years ago I told to the father of a pupil I trained, who, seeing how disastrous his son was, asked me, "Is this what you teach to my son?"
and I answered, "No, this is what he learns, what I teach is very different."
(Laughter) (Applause) I've never liked to assume the responsibility, that's why, since he was a child I made Rafael assume his responsibility.
I have the fame to be a strict coach, I have actually been strict, I have been a tough coach.
I don't believe in roughness as an aim, I believe in roughness as means.
I have been a demanding coach because I cared a lot a great fondness for my nephew.
I wouldn't have ever been tough to somebody who couldn't cope with it or who couldn't cope with that thoroughness.
But I would never be tough with somebody who I didn't feel a great fondness for, for somebody I didn't appreciate truly.
Since I wanted the greater good for my nephew, I was really strict.
I think this is a main condition.
And to be strict, well, this thoroughness I always tried that it turned into self-demanding.
One can't always be stretching constantly to his player, in this case, or to anybody who you train in any activity.
That's why I always tried that Rafael felt the responsibility himself.
I think that he assumed it, since: Many years ago I went with him to a tournament with another player.
I accompanied him and ... I remember that they scheduled the match at the same hour than the other player I was also accompanying.
I was seeing my nephew playing from afar, and he was being a complete disaster.
He was losing 5-0 against a not so great player.
In that moment, a friend of mine arrived, he was a former player, and he told me, "Hey! I think your nephew is playing with a broken racket."
The ones that have played tennis, with a broken frame is almost impossible to play, the balls always go out, except inside the court, they all go out.
I went to Rafael's court and I told him, "Listen, your racket is broken, I think your racket is broken."
The guy did like this, "Damn! Yes, it is broken, I'll change it."
Well, he changed it, the scene changed, but at the end he lost 6-0, 7-5.
So, when the match was over, I went to him and asked him, "Can you tell me that a boy" -- he was 15 years old, I think -- "Can you tell me that a boy after playing for so many years, doesn't know when a racket is broken?"
And his answer was very clear, he told me, "Look, I'm so used to always be blamed, that I didn't even think that the racket was the one that was making me lose."
(Laughter) (Applause) The speech has always been the same.
A few times ...
I think that a sense of self-criticism is necessary, it's very hard to progress, to improve, without a good sense of self-criticism.
I've always tried for him to have it.
I have always made sure that he never gave me excuses, neither about his defeats, nor about anything that happened.
It is too easy to justify, to justify oneself constantly.
In the academy I was asked to put up some phrases to motivate the guys.
I don't know if I motivated them, or if I discouraged them.
But I remember that one of the phrases that I put up was, "Never an excuse made us win a match."
And that's the truth.
Some years ago we were at the US Open and in that edition, the balls didn't give a spin that Rafael wanted.
We were playing and it was a disaster.
Everyday when we were going to train,
"These balls aren't right for me,
they don't give the spin."
"Interesting," I told him.
The next day was the same; excuses bother me a lot.
He won his first match, the second, the third; but tired of hearing him, I told him, "Listen, what you should do, is to lose, lose and, in Majorca, the ball will give a spin."
He listened to me: he lost.
(Laughter) He lost, but he didn't go to Majorca, he went to Beijing, and I went to Majorca and later we met again in Naples.
Curiously, in Beijing he won the next tournament.
He beat everybody, he defeated the world number three, the number five.
He made a great tournament, I saw in on TV.
I knew the balls he was playing with and were the same than the US Open ones. I said "f***!" when I met him again, I asked him, "By the way, what ball did you play with in Beijing?"
And he, a bit embarrassed answered: "No, with Wilsons."
So ... in Beijing they give a spin,
in New York they don't, but in Beijing they do.
Whatever, it's always the same.
Now he was in Australia and when he played his fourth match, and he didn't play too well I said to him, "Yuck!"
He called me after the match and I told him, "You haven't played too well."
And the first thing he told me was: "It's because it was too hot."
It seems that it was hot only in one half of the court, not in the other.
The other player did play really well.
(Laughter) (Applause) As I said, I tried to strengthen up Rafael's character, and I did trying to encourage his ability to endure because I think this is what is decisive in life.
The ability to endure.
Years ago I was in a school, and I heard a teacher that told us, "In life we have to learn to articulate the phrasal 'put up with'."
He articulated it in a different way.
Well, he told us, "I put up with, you put up with, he puts up with."
"We put up with," said the teacher.
Rafael learnt how to articulate it: "I put up with myself, I put up with you."
I was hard to put up with.
For many years I made him train with balls in bad shape, with tennis courts in bad shape.
I told him, "We are going to train for one hour and a half," and then I used to go on with the training indefinitely because I was interested in making him learn how to put up with everything.
I was interested in teaching him how to strengthen, mainly, his character.
I think that this is decisive, that is what has been decisive in life.
I believe that character builds up thanks to difficulty. And I think this is the great mistake nowadays.
Nowadays, the children having so many more things, having all the technology behind them, even having nutritionists, having biomechanical studies, audiovisual studies, analyzing the hits, statistics that tell you what to do, in the end, people find it harder to improve.
I give a very clear example.
When we arrived to the professional circuit, the first ones there were: Hewitt, 21 years old.
Roddick, 20 years old.
This Swiss was already there, 21, Federer.

Coria, 21. Nalbaldian, 21.
Ferrero, 23. Safin, 24.
I think Moyá and Agassi were there,
they were a bit older.
Nowadays, the top ones are: Federer, 36.
Rafael, about to turn 32.
Murray and Djokovic, 31.
Wawrinka, 33.
Del Potro, about to turn 30.

Čilić, 30. Berdych, 33.
What has happened?
Why does it take so much to the new ones to replace the old ones nowadays?
I think it is simply because they haven't understood what is essential.
The essential ...
Obviously, I'm not against technology.
The world progresses and technology is going to help us, but I think there are things that, mostly on the training stages, don't help.
Making the life of the young ones so easy I don't think it helps at all.
I believe that sometimes it's better to restrict a bit the advances and go back to the essential.
For us the essential was always: perseverance, it was the respect to the opponent, it was the effort, it was the sacrifice, it was the discipline.
Technologies weren't ever the essential.
I think that's what all the young ones should understand.
This is what I understand that was fundamental in Rafael: be sure that everything that happened was his responsibility, being ready to fight until the end.
Rafael had many problems, physical, of injuries ... I do not like complaints, when he used to tell me that he had many problems, I answered, "Well, this is what it is, that's what with have to fight with."
And I understand this is fundamental, not only for Rafael, but for anybody who aspire to reach high goals.
I've confirmed many years later that I used to think that Borg, McEnroe, Lendl, were exceptional people, also, I used to think that their coaches were also great managers.
I believed that they have been gifted with a special talent.
Well, I have verified that a boy from Manacor, a normal child, well, I don't know if he has reached McEnroe's or Lendl's level, but that with effort, with sacrifice, he has managed to reach many goals that he decided when he was young.
That's why I understand that if he has managed to make it, obviously many people, many of us, can make it.
I don't know if everybody could become world number one, right now, number two from Monday on, I don't know if we all can reach this but I'm sure that all, all or almost all, can improve and progress.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Imagine a 9-year-old kid. The only thing he could eat in all his life is amino acid soup since he is allergic to most of the food that we normally eat.
Imagine that this child is fed up of always eating this and he has decided to stop eating and he has to be artificially fed.
Well, this is a real story and it is increasingly a problem.
In Europe, more than 10 percent of the population is allergic to some kind of food.
Even among the kids, there are more and more cases of multiple intolerance. What can we do to help solve this problem?
Or an easier one, what can we do to return to a healthier food?
Or, thinking about the future, what can we do about a lack of food with an increasing population? Although it may seem shocking, 3D printing can help solve these problems.
First, let's think a little bit about the cause that led us to an increase like this of allergies and intollerances. I have identified two main factors that are very much related. One is the decrease of the size of the families.
We have changed from a state when families had 4 or 6 children to a state when the family unit is composed by 1 or 2 persons, mainly in the North of Europe.
Here in Spain is still slightly bigger, but we follow the trend.
On the other hand, there has been a dramatic decrease in the time we devote to cook at home. Think for a moment that in the '20s, five and a half hours a day were devoted to cook at home.
It has to do with the size of the family, of course.
Today, we devote less than half an hour.
Less than half an hour is in fact what it takes to get a pizza from the freezer and toss it into the oven.
And that, what effect does it have?
Well, that has taken us here, our nutrition is now based on mainly pre-cooked food and processed food.
These meals have a lot of not-so-healthy components.
They have a lot of chemicals, a lot of additives that are not good for us.
They also have a lot of preservatives.
But also, they use excessive amounts of salt, oil and sugar.
All of that impacts our health.
Is not only our health, if we think about all that you see in the picture. These portions are not thought for our own family size.
They are thought for an average.
That means that if you live alone, you go to the store and buy prepared food, in the end, there is a lot left over.
A very big waste of food.
Let's think a bit then, on how can 3D printing help us.
To give an overall view, 3D printing is a 30 year old technology.
It is not a new technology.
And it has been used mainly in the industrial environment.
Although you may not know it, you are all consuming products that have been 3D printed.
I'm sorry that for some this may be a problem, but factories use this technology.
Basically in 30 years what has happened is that now we can use this technology at home, so that we have a food mini-factory.
We can have a food mini-factory, the difference is that we can choose the ingredients.
We can have a healthier nutrition and more adjusted to our tastes.
Not to the average population taste.
To explain better what I'm talking about, I show you an example, it is a video of a 3D printer making ravioli.
Ravioli is a relatively simple dish, you all know that.
Making it by hand at home, it takes about two hours, including the kitchen cleaning time.
Making it with a machine, it's about 10 minutes.
You put the ingredients.
This can be done with the dishes that we usually eat.
The ravioli I showed you before and the pizza.
A lot of people think it's foolish to print a pizza.
It's easy to do it by hand.
But if you go to a supermarket you will see a lot of shelves full of pizza.
People don't spend that much time making it.
Neither pizza nor other food.
But we are not only talking about reproducing at home the food that we find in the supermarket. There is also an important playful aspect.
If you think about children, when they see something green in their plate they close the mouth.
What you see here is a dinosaur shaped spinach quiche.
I ensure you that once you put this on their plate, they will eat it all.
And it's the same ingredients.
We can also think of a vegetarian version of chicken nuggets.
The are nuggets, but they are made of chickpeas.
They are animal shaped.
They will also vanish fast.
But we have other uses, for special occasions.
This is a normal dish.
I suppose you will not identify it, in this shape, but it's fish and chips. On the outside, the more yellow part is the potato in the inside, the fish and the peas.
Here the funny thing is that the potato is part of the dish, is the dish.
So, you can eat it all.
In some occasions, also, we can prepare something special.
Here you can see a Christmas tree made of chocolate.
But it also a tool for chefs,
people that are already creative and surprise us with their dishes.
This tool allows them to make a new impact on customers when presenting them with the dish.
But let's think of other things that 3D printing can add.
We said that when we print what we want to eat, waste is reduced to zero.
There is no waste of food.
Tastes can be adjusted to each person.
In a house of four people not all of them should eat the same.
We can do something else with different ingredients, and even different shapes.
But besides that, it can help us control our diet.
Bear in mind that it is not the same a sportsman than an old person, or a kid or a pregnant woman.
Their nutrient needs are very different.
Besides that, for people that have intolerance or allergies we can create the same dishes with ingredients that fit those needs.
Or even in more severe cases.
People with dysphagia, produced by a cancer or Alzheimer or simply because they are old, have difficulty swallowing. With a 3D printer they can print forms and textures that are more adequate to those needs.
I'd like you to get this idea: 3D printing is a tool that can help us to cook again at home, with fresh food, in a healthy and funny way but without taking the time that we don't have.
It can also help us solve some of the future challenges we are facing.
I'd like you to imagine yourselves receiving the ingredients directly from the manufacturer or preparing them yourselves, you load the ingredients in the machine before leaving home. And when you are about to leave work you ask the device to have dinner ready when you get home.
That is possible.
I'd like to also give you the vision that this is a technology that in a short time will be as common in our kitchens, as today it is a microwave or an oven.
Thanks a lot.
(Applause)
Hello.
Please raise your hand if this year you went to a medical checkup, a doctor's appointment in 2017.
Good.
Opening an envelope with a possible diagnosis of cancer is a very hard thing to do.
The word cancer itself is very emotional, it paralyzes us.
I know that none of you right now want to be in a hospital.
Even fewer in the oncology department.
Even fewer upon imagining long hours of chemotherapy.
Despite medical advances, despite new treatments we keep associating cancer with the idea of death.
It is difficult to change this,
so I am asking everyone for help in that we are conscious of the importance of prevention and premature detection.
In the world right now, there are 32,600,000 people with cancer.
A 70 percent increase is expected in the next 20 years.
In Argentina, 120,000 cases are diagnosed annually.
But this is not what is most important.
The most important is knowing that cancer can be treated and be cured.
But for this, we need early detection.
We need access to medication.
Access to a timely and accurate diagnosis.
Without it, there is no treatment.
The foundation's role is simply to complement with our programs the excellent work done by all of the oncologists, nurses, and psychologists.
The foundation's goal, it's mission, is to humanize cancer treatments and to improve the quality of life for patients and family.
The family has to be incorporated.
The companion has to be incorporated.
Because often, the companion is more tired, more overwhelmed than the patient.
Our three pillars: art, reflexology, and the group dynamic.
We work in the rooms where chemotherapy occurs.
We transform the act of waiting passively to a creative and shared activity where we all are involved;
at the same time, we work in very small spaces, with a lot of respect, respecting the patient.
Because the patient is the protagonist of their own recuperation.
When they arrive in the room, in one arm is placed the medication and on the other
a work of art grows. Here it is.
The artwork is what permits them to express the feelings that cannot be said with words.
What cannot be said.
The pain, the agony, the fear, is made into art.
And they transform into true artists.
Because of this experience, they paint in their houses, in the foundation, they paint in workshops, and their artwork does not only decorate the hospital walls, it goes to museums, to competitions, to modern art galleries.
We can say that the art becomes a part of their lives.
At the same time, they receive reflexology sessions which help them to connect with their body and actually relax;
besides that, the group dynamic is what allows the patients to share their experiences, to feel like they are a part of the same team, of the same group.
The feeling of belonging is what actually gives us confidence and encouragement to return to the next chemotherapy treatment with more hope.
And hope is what is most necessary in trying moments like these.
And if you ask me
"Where did this idea come from?"
"From a team of professionals?"
"From a group of friends who decided to do an innovative project?"
I would have to confess that no, it was
born out a spiritual and physical need of my own I would say, to ease my own pain.
In the year 2000, my third son, Santiago, was diagnosed with a cerebral tumor, they admitted him to the Italian Hospital, where he spent four months.
They transferred him to a rehab center where we lived for nine months.
From there we went home, and there I learned what no mother wants to learn and beyond that, the beauty of recovery, is that I did reflexology with Santiago during the two years that he was in a coma.
In the morning, in the afternoon, and at night.
On May 31, 2002, Santiago died at home at 17 years old, surrounded by all his friends and family.
After a long process of mourning, in 2006 I designed and developed a program for high-risk pregnancies.
I brought it to the Clinical Hospital José de San Martín.
I had the luck of going to the wrong floor and instead of going to the maternity ward I went to the mental health ward;
there, the head of psychiatric internment did not intern me, but instead liked my manner of being so much that he invited me to work in psycho-oncology.
After 20 days, he brought me to the nurses and the chemotherapy room and there I entered and he asked them "do you want to do gynmnastics?"
"Yes, yes".
A week later: recorder, music, and gymnastics classes there.
A month later I invited Maria, my older sister, licensed in visual arts, to come create a painting of the patients because I knew this was important.
Following photography sessions, reflexology, makeup, we realized that the program was a success from the beginning.
The results, obviously, exceeded all of our expectations.
And now yes, it improves the atmosphere of the room from the first moment that the music is turned on, from the first moment that the painting begins. How could it not improve the atmosphere? How could it not cheer people up?
How would it not improve the drip of the medication?
If people are relaxed due to reflexology, the IV can be placed better, the links between the patient, nurse, doctor, and family are improved, the emotional tie is stronger.
And most importantly is that there is better adaptation and adhesion to the chemotherapy.
Something else valuable is that the people or patients, as they were called, each will have their own name instead of just a diagnosis and number.
They are able to have their identity again because they sign their works.
The oncologist comes to the room and instead of asking where the lab tech is or where the MRI is, they ask,
"What are you painting?"
That is very important;
now we are always implementing new things; now we are working
with virtual reality,
with videos in 360 degrees.
With videos in 360 degrees, we give the patients the possibility to stop looking at the IV drip and instead take them somewhere.
And they really are transported in an almost real manner to other places that are infinitely prettier than what they are watching: the drip or the serum obviously.
I think of Zulema Escubin, a patient with breast cancer since 2006, who wanted to travel to Córdoba, and her husband said "There is no budget, it is impossible, you have to do your chemotherapy treatments."
The anxiety was so high that Maria gave her quickly woodboards and she began to paint.
They decorated their house with the pretty mountains of Cordoba with the title "Where I want to be."
But where I want to be is not a geographic place, where I want to be, is a manner of brightening the hardest moments of the life of someone with cancer.
Now we are a very big family.
We are in 14 public hospitals in the headquarters of Belgrano since 2012 when we became a foundation;
and all the discharged patients can keep painting, keep doing reflexology in a group dynamic, and now, with 110 volunteers, I can tell you that we are going to keep growing.
And growing.
(Applause) Our dream is to reach the whole country.
But I want to share a reflection from Veronica, which you've been reading, that says, she is a poet and artist.
Veronica says: "Where I want to be is the place where the soul is preserved while the body is healing."
For me, where I want to be is a manner of living life.
It's like coming home after having done what we love, what we love more than ourselves.
We return to it with happiness in our body and peace in our soul.
Where I want to be is accompanying others from pain.
And here in this mutual surrender, is where real love appears, the authentic love, the only, the one that has the capacity to heal all the wounds and scars that are as deep as those of the human heart. And I say this
from my own certainty and experience: There is no love greater than that of mutual surrender, which is the love that is able to heal the pain of the death of a child.
Thank you.
(Applause)
The ancient Greek said that the Milky Way was born from a stream of Hera's milk.
Zeus had a son with a mortal, that was called Hercules.
Hera was a goddess, wife of Zeus, she was not Hercules' mother.
and Zeus loved that baby so much, that even when he was little he showed superhuman characteristics, but he was not a god.
he wanted to give closer characteristics to that of the gods, so he couldn't think of a better idea than to take him to the Olympus, so that he could drink some milk of Hera's breast and in this way turn into a god.
Since it was likely that Hera didn't agree with this proposal, Zeus waited for her to fall asleep, then he smuggled Hercules to the Olympus, then he attached him to her breast.
But Hercules, since he was little, had a lot of strength and when he got attached to the breast, Hera woke up, was surprised, detached the baby, that wasn't hers, from the breast a milk stream came out that crossed the sky and formed the Milky Way.
The human milk is part of what we are: we are mammals, and that helped our ancestors to survive and develop.
If we look at art as a history chronicle of mankind, we see demonstrations of this nourishing function of the breasts, which are the organs of the body that practice breastfeeding and we see that the breast, in its nutritional function, was the protagonist of the history of mankind throughout time, from the beginning of time.
We can see, for example, the Paleolithic Venus, with big breasts, that appear to be filled with milk, and we see images of different cultures and different movements, for example the ones of Maria Lactans, of the Virgin Mary breastfeeding baby Jesus.
Again, looking at art as a chronicle of history, at some point we notice a break and we see it clearly in this picture of the French School, where you can see Madame d'Estrées, who was a lover of Henry IV, the king of France, preparing to receive the king with breasts that no longer seem to be filled with milk.
And behind of Madame d'Estrées, a nurse breastfeeding the son of Madame d'Estrées.
This shows the first appearance of the breast no longer in a nutritional function, but in a erotic function and the breast then takes this changing role: it is no longer a source of life and food, but it is an object of desire.
Lactation and breastfeeding are something that I want to delegate or replace.
If possible, women hired a nurse like Madame d'Estrées to breastfeed their children and even lactation had a role in the survival of that baby.
Those who couldn't hire, or didn't want to hire, delegate breastfeeding, they appealed to some replacements that, if we look at them now in retrospect, they seem horrific to us.
There were babies, for example, that ate bread dipped in broth; other babies ate meat, new born babies, lightened meat with some water or broth so they could swallow it.
Other babies were given beer.
And others were given raw milk from animals, with the danger that entails, because raw milk was not safe food and less in those hygiene conditions
And those replacements were what they had, and to be honest, they did not have good results.
There were tragic situations: babies got sick, and some also died.
In 1861, a enterprising man, Swiss, named Henri Nestlé appears, who finds a way to preserve the overproduction of milk from Swiss cows, because they are very efficient, as they are Swiss and in the 19th century they produced more milk so that the Swiss could consume even cheese.
So, they had to preserve it in a certain way.
And this man came up with dehydrating milk and he invented milk powder.
And, Mr. Nestlé was an innovator and said: "Ah, if I add a little bit of carbohydrate and a little bit of fat, I call it Nestlé milk flour and I sell it to feed it to the kids."
And so it was: the milk flour from Nestlé was the first massive replacement of the human milk.
The apparition of this substitute, or the replacement of the human milk, already much safer than the previous ones that have been practiced throughout the history, allowed that -- plus the fact that women joined massively the labor market, after the industrial revolution, breastfeeding was not necessarily the normal way of feeding babies.
And throughout the 20th century, what became normal was to feed with formula, to the point that the bottle, that is used to feed with formula is the symbol of a baby
I am sure you must have seen the stickers, that shows the places to change a baby with a bottle, the ones we stick to cars and say: "Attention, baby on board," also with a bottle.
The bottle is a symbol for a baby.
The formula is an industrial product it is a derivative of the Nestlé milk flour.
The formula is based on milk from the cow
The cow is a mammal just like us, but that produces milk for their offspring that has four stomachs.
Then, the cow's milk is difficult to digest for a baby, so what the process to manufacturing the formula does is the thermally treatment in order to be digestible.
In this process it loses nutrients. nutrients that got lost are added and some others too, as we get to know a little more about human milk, to make it more similar to mom's milk, and finally, there is a process which is the industrial, dehydration, homogenization, pasteurization, aggregate of nutrients, canning and distribution.
It is an industrial process.
It is an industrial product.
As an industrial product, it consumes resources and generates waste in all of its processing steps.
We know that stockbreeding is one of the big contributors that generates gases of the greenhouse effect.
And the stockbreeding is the base of the formula of the industry
But not only that stage, also the other stages generate greenhouse gases, waste and consume resources.
To produce one kilo of canned milk, of formula, throughout the process, from the cow til the end four thousand liters of water is needed, so formula is an industrial product.
Maternal milk, human milk, instead, only needs a mom, or someone with a breast and a baby who is willing to grab it.
It does not generate greenhouse gases, it does not generate waste, virtually zero carbon footprint.
If we think of a sustainable human activity by its definition it is the human breastfeeding.
Now, with what I said, during the 20th century, the use of formula was normalized. and today, 21st century, with a worldwide lactation rate of only 39 percent.
Only 39 percent of babies receive human milk as the World Health Organization recommends: Human milk exclusively up to six months.
After that, the rate is even less.
And if we know that maternal milk is so complete, why are these lactation rates so low?
Well, one of the reasons is, that the formula is a big business.
The formula business moves more money than Coke Sorry, than the most famous soft drink of the world.
(Laughter) Look, it moves more money than the most famous soda drink in the world.
And you think that a person drinks a soft drink for a lifetime, while the formula takes only a very short interval of life.
Why, then?
What is going on?
Does milk have no value?
The topic that I cannot define as a market product,
What? Can't I make advertisement?
Because, obviously, if I have a product that generates such a business, I invest a lot in advertisement
And what happens to human milk?
Human milk does not have such possibility of having the same marketing access, for example.
There are some calculated facts about the value of human milk and, in fact, there is a milk market.
Milk is sold nowadays, there are countries where it is allowed, but not in ours. but in others it is.
For one liter of human milk, a person can pay USD 90 for one liter of human milk.
Now, I don't know if this represents the total value that milk has but, for example, the milk of a mother of a premature baby is different to the milk from a mother of a full-term baby.
And it has the nutrients that the premature baby needs to finish developing, and what he lacks, as he was born early.
If you are going to pick up your child at the kindergarten or day-care, or wherever he is, there is a baby that has a cold. and you are exposed to that virus you will start generating antibodies that you are going to pass on to your baby through milk.
What value does that have?
It is very difficult to give it a value.
And that is one of the problems that we have: milk is "free," human milk, but at the same time it is very valuable.
What happens then?
Why -again the question- are the lactation rates so low?
If we have the product that -as we know it- is made specifically for babies that has lots of properties that we don't even finish of knowing, why are the lactation rates so low?
The reality is that the idea has been planted that lactation, breastfeeding is incompatible with modern life, is very difficult to turn it compatible what a woman does outside her house while breastfeeding a baby.
And it is true.
Mothers find many barriers to be able to breastfeed
For example, the maternity leaves, that last less than the exclusive breastfeeding, that last six months and the leaves last three.
Also, for example, social work pays the formula for you, if you use it, but, if you breastfeed, they won't give you any incentive.
And in reality, you are improving the numbers because one baby that is breastfed, in average, breastfed babies get sick less.
Then, what do mothers need to breastfeed?
What does a mother who wants to breastfeed need?
She definitely does not need to be removed from a place when she is breastfeeding, like what happened in our country some time ago.
She neither needs a health system that says: "Your milk does not work," or "it is not sufficient" because it is not true.
The majority of the mothers produce sufficient and quality milk to breastfeed their children.
And when there are difficulties, the difficulties can be solved.
It neither has to absorb all the negative experience of other women who weren't able to breastfeed.
What happened to me was, when I was breastfeeding my older daughter, I had her in another country where I was studying, it was my husband and me alone, and my mother came to help us, and while I was in the postpartum, with huge difficulties to breastfeed my daughter, my mother cried, because she saw me breastfeeding and she got in a bad mood, as she couldn't breastfeed us three: my sisters and me.
Women who want to breastfeed they don't need advertisements that tell them what the baby milk three has and how good it is or which brand.
They don't need to receive information about lactation from the manufacturers of the product that replaces them.
Women who want to breastfeed need support.
They need support from organisations, like the organisation to which I belong.
Indeed, but they also need support from their family, they need support from the health system, they need support from their employees, they need support from the kindergarten where their children are taken to to be taken care of they need support from their work colleagues, from the communication media.
They need support from everyone.
There is an African saying that says that a village is needed to raise a child.
I tell them that a village is needed to breastfeed a child.
A mother cannot breastfeed in society, a family cannot breastfeed in society.
Breastfeed is not mandatory, but reflection must be made of whether we choose how to breastfeed our children.
We can do motocross, we must have reached the moon, but we are still mammals.
Thank you.
(Applause)
What would you do when, during Science Camp, you're put together with three friends, you are given a piece of thread and they ask you to calculate the width of a creek?
You discuss and try to see what to do with the thread, you cross the creek a thousand times, and try to see how many times will the thread fit in it. You will be tired and wet for sure.
Then, almost frozen, exhausted and soaking wet, I wondered if there was a way to calculate that width without getting more wet.
And then, I stood on the shore and started to watch.
And I understood that three distances were formed there. From my feet to my eyes, from my eyes to the other shore and from that shore to where I was standing.
And I remembered:
Soh Cah Toa!
(Laughter) A basic trigonometry concept that seemed unnecessary until that moment, was giving me the answer to a real problem.
And even though that situation was very simple the process I had to make at that time was very different to anything I had done before.
Because it was mine.
It was real.
And something inside of me woke up, something that was asleep just because I believed I couldn't.
But that time I could and I went crazy about knowing more about things and reality.
So I started searching for similar experiences to this camp.
I went to the Balseiro Institute, I convinced my friends to make a solar water distiller for a CONICET contest.
I got into scholarships, Olympics and camps to continue learning.
So, through the year, I became "science girl" at school.
But then this happened.
I got a 7 in almost all subjects.
I guess it was because I was growing, right?
I mean --
it's normal to lose those interests through time.
Even more when you are a kid that suddenly gets to a new world, full of obligations and things that take time from what you considered was essential.
Look, during all our high school years in order to pass all the subjects, all Argentinean kids are asked to get more than 6 or 7.
I remember that when I was little I had this concept of school as a place to learn exclusively and to be marveled with new stuff.
I dreamed to be an astronaut, to be a scientist, I remember I used to play with my friends to be what we wanted to be when we grew old.
My friend RocĂo would always start to draw, saying that someday she would be an animator.
Today, RocĂo is almost 19 and started the English Translator degree.
What happened with her dream during those 10 years of her life?
She must have changed her mind. She realized she didn't like that.
I asked her and her answer was something like: "What happens is that to be an animator you have to study math.
And I've always failed it.
However, I always got high grades at English.
It is easier for me."
And this happens because school is the only official reference kids have about our intellectual and academic performance.
And that's the result that a simple grade has inside the educational system: you end up not following your passion.
Because not only you get a 3 or a 4, you are a 3 or a 4.
And you will continue to be that 3 or 4.
Grades end up separating us from 1 to 10.
You, the 10 boy, are the best of all.
You, the 1, be ready for what will come.
That contradiction of being the science girl and a 7 at once, made me realize that actually, if you finish school and have the courage to go to university, you'll probably fall into what you were told before.
You make a balance of the best grades you had or the easiest subjects for you, and you look for a career that is similar to that.
So a lot of times what we learn at school is: "If I get high grades in this subject, I must like this, so this is what I should do.
And during the rest of high school, you just focus on passing subjects.
In the end, students learn to be students.
And to answer only what we are asked.
But, when do we actually learn?
It took me a lot of effort to understand that I was more than my grade.
That I am more than those sevens.
And when I did, my grades didn't change much.
What did change was that I started learning.
But how long can you be a rebel and not care about grades?
I mean, you can avoid them in December, March, or even the next year, but if your revolution fails you have to repeat the year.
We can't do this alone.
We need help.
The educational system needs a change.
And I am sure that the passion and interest that my friend has for drawing, is worth more than a 3 or a 4.
School must be the place that teach us to think.
To search for what we love.
To be how we want to be and not how the numbers tell us we are.
Today, my report will say I'm a 7.
My partners, teachers and my whole school will still have the contradiction of "science girl" full of sevens.
But for myself, I am more than a number.
Now, grades, are they really necessary?
I don't know.
But to show us, during all our learning process, that we are and can much more than those numbers and I'm convinced that it is absolutely essential.
Thank you.
(Applause)
We have fooled the aliens.
It was unintended, but we sent them a completely wrong message about who we are.
Think about this: How would you explain to these intelligent beings from other galaxies what the human race is?
Does it look like this?
This is the drawing we sent them in 1972 in the Pioneer 10 space probe.
It's estimated it will reach its destination in millions of years.
In the meantime, if an alien happens to find it, it can infer that in our planet there are two types of beings: those who do things (Laughter) and those who don't do anything.
My question is: Why did we decide to send this drawing and not this one, for example, or this one?
Or even this one.
If we intended to represent human life on Earth, none of these two drawings should have Caucasian traits, as there are more people with African or Asian traits in our planet.
However, we decided to send the one with the white man raising his hand and the woman next to him.
Today, these two figures are spinning around the universe and we don't know what will happen to them.
Something similar happens with the images sent by the media, images they cast off, adrift, without really considering if they represent us or if they have any affect in how we would be in a million years.
These images, the ones in the slides and the ones we see every day in the media, do not represent reality, they are simplifications, signs that we use to summarise information and to communicate in a fast manner.
To picture it better, our mind works like a computer.
What do you usually do when using a computer?
You don't just keep all your files in the desktop, right?
You create folders of similar files, and you name each folder and select a preview image for it.
This preview image of the folder is the sign that help us understand the content.
In these words, it seems to be logical, as a summary helps us to better manage our time and resources.
But it is also dangerous having only one preview image that represents a whole group of files, especially when we apply this to people.
Our biggest computer, our greatest window to the info about the world, is the media, and it is the media that decides the preview image of our folders.
These images are not innocent, they are not objective, and whoever has the power to select them, can also usually add their moral perspective towards what is being previewed.
For example, "Woman: Human being whose only duty is to serve as decoration."
These signs, which are often spread by media and have an implicit ideological bias, are stereotypes.
Let's have a look at the images that appear by default in the "Men" and "Women" folders of the media.
Let's take a closer look at the "Men" folder, where there are standing figures, all dressed up, with a powerful look, all low-angle shots.
And in the "Women" folder, things are slightly different.
We can see they are beings that feel a little bit warmer, as they have fewer clothes on. (Laughter)
They fall on their asses on whatever horizontal surface they see.
They fall on their backs, they collapse.
And if you look closely, you'd also see that they cannot keep their legs closed.
(Laughter) We don't know why for us, women, it's so hard to close our legs.
Maybe this is why we cannot stand up, maybe that's the reason.
This diverse way that media has to represent different genders appears also in the portrayal of different cultures, religions and sexual tendencies.
And every time they have to refer to someone specific in this group, they never open the folder to choose a picture in it, they just always use the preview image.
As a visual artist, I understand that my job consists of creating images.
I've always wanted to know how these images influence people; how images are capable of stripping our lives of meaning if we don't buy a specific product.
How they can move us to the verge of tears, or, on the contrary, how they can make us oblivious to someone else's suffering.
How they transform us to the point that we even change our own body to look just like them.
Or they make us even hate our body so much that we stop eating.
My job consists mainly of revealing these control mechanisms with which images function, of turning them upside down to see those things that go unnoticed.
In this piece of work, "Poses", I asked different women, of different heights and sizes, to strike those poses they see models do in magazines, but within the context of daily life.
(Laughter) In the streets, in the supermarket, at the queue in a museum ... (Laughter) The result was pretty much revealing, because people asked them if they were OK, (Laughter) some asked for help, and they even called an ambulance ... Something very serious must be happening to those women.
(Laughter) Some of them even called the police, as they were so worried, that they thought it might be dangerous.
(Laughter) Imagine if we had sent this image in the Pioneer 10?
(Applause) I am certain that if we'd sent these photos, none of the aliens would have wanted to come into contact with our planet.
(Laughter) Stereotypes are established in the collective worldview by repetition.
They come to our lives at a very early age, even before we can develop a proper judgment to question them.
All narrative is created with the same discourse: Girls are princesses, and they need to pay attention to their beauty.
Boys are beasts, they kick, they punch, and they solve everything with violence.
Successful people are fair-skinned.
Unsuccessful people are overweight, Latin, or African ... We have listened to this message so many times that we believe it's true.
We even ascribe this, and this is my favorite explanation, to biology itself.
Of course, if this is all about economizing language and mental energy, saying "it's in our nature" is much easier than saying "maybe we are so used to seeing and imitating this that we are incapable of considering other perspectives."
Yes, this certainly requires
too much effort. The media is riddled with stereotypes and it is basically impossible to escape from them.
The reason for this is solely commercial.
The media's objective is to reach the highest number possible of people to make a profit, and to achieve this, they have to speak to us in a language we all know, the folder language, because if they use a different code, we might not understand them and they will lose audience. Or they might train our brains a bit too much, and that's not good for them, as we may start thinking and rebelling.
They'd rather have us entertained and malleable.
What consequences can stereotypes bring about in our daily lives?
A lot, and they can affect us in many different levels.
The most direct consequence of stereotypes is that they suppress everything they don't represent, therefore, they stigmatize it, they transform it into an anomaly.
I don't know how many women here are mothers, but what do you think about these images in the net of women that have just given birth and have a flat abdomen?
Can you imagine if the only representation of women that just gave birth were these images?
What would happen to those women with stretch marks and a little belly?
They would be frowned upon.
"Look at that belly!"
The same happens with hairy legs or armpits, wrinkles and grey hair, all frowned upon because the media doesn't show them.
Everything that's shown is normalized, but what is not shown is rejected.
However, stereotypes can also have the opposite effect and they can normalize, via representation, aspects that are not necessarily positive for people.
This is something fashion photography masters, as they have been using violence for many years now to get people's attention and now they've created this very dangerous trend.
They think they are creating these super impressive images, but they are actually normalizing violence against women through scenes that are meant to be glamorous but which tell horrifying stories.
Let's listen to the opinion of some people that are still not very much exposed to the language of fashion and let's see what they think about the last campaigns.
Yolanda: Tell us what you see in this image.
(Music) Children: She seems to be ... scared.
She seems to have an illness, because her arm is here, but her shoulder is here.
(Laughter) Yolanda: How do you think she is feeling?
Children: She feels ... lonely.
Scared.
And hungry.
Here they are throwing a girl into a trash bin. And the lady is laughing, I don't know why.
Here, they look like ... heroes.
They are studying to go to university.
Three girls have had a fight and ... one of them just passed out.
These are two dead girls on the road, because they were run over by a truck or something like that.
If I were there, and I was passing by, I would help them and take them to the doctor.
She's like this ... with her eyes closed as if she was dead or passed out.
She could be starving ... and she might die.
I would tell her to cheer up and not to be sad.
(Laughter) (Applause) Well, I think there's no need to say that violence is not normalized at birth, as children clearly reject it.
It is so difficult to avoid stereotypes that we start internalizing them and we even get to assess ourselves according to that simplified image that does not represent us at all, and we are capable of doing anything to stop being who we are and to resemble that image, as we do not want to be left out from the folder.
This happens because there is a whole industry that feeds on our fear of not belonging to a group to sell us all types of products.
There's this example that is paradoxically funny, and it's all these creams there are to change your skin color.
In some part of our planet, they are certain that successful people have a fair skin and there's a whole range of products to bleach the skin that can even pose a risk to your health.
In some other part of the planet, they are certain that successful people have a tanned skin, and this means they have money, resources, and free time to go to the beach, and there is a whole range of products to keep our skin tanned throughout the year.
When we are scared and have complexes, we can be manipulated, and we lose control over ourselves.
What can we do -as there's always a solution - to free ourselves from all these stereotypes, this oversimplification and this disinformation?
Let's start by reflecting on the idea of freedom.
I have attended some advertising symposiums and it's very normal to hear advertising agents say: "I have freedom of expression, so I can create whatever discourse I want."
And they are right, that's true.
But what happens with the freedom of the people that see their images?
It is to these people that take pride in their individual freedom as communicators that I always say something that's actually a question: Does the exercise of your freedom give freedom to other people, or, on the contrary, does it limit their freedom?
Freedom means having the opportunity to choose.
Those who create stereotypes have this opportunity, but not those who get them.
I suggest we change.
Not change, but broaden this concept of freedom of expression towards a responsibility of expression.
This is the only way to give real freedom to everyone.
It is not about censorship or imposition of a specific discourse, that's what stereotypes do.
It's about putting into practice a conscious and committed expression.
When creating my next image, I can stop and think: Is this image reinforcing a limiting model, a normative model? Or, on the contrary, is it bringing diversity to the people that see it?
This is what I tried doing in my last project called "Little Black Dress."
I've tried to represent the diversity of female bodies through stereotype.
The stereotype would be that short, tight, M size, black dress.
With that same dress, I have shot photographs of women with different size, age, and skin color.
For some of them, it ran small; for others, it ran big, for some of them it fitted,
for others, it didn't. But all of them posed straight, showing pride in their bodies, and certainly none of them were laying on the floor.
The result was a big installation with the purpose of making its audience feel included in this diversity.
Not excluded from any folder.
The project is still open, and I urge all women who want to participate to write to me and wear this dress to become part of it.
Images are the main means of communication these days, and they are a powerful tool of social transformation that we can all make use of.
It may be that millions of years ago a well-built, aggressive male had more chances of reproducing and this might have been even good for the species.
But today, our situation as a species and as a society has changed.
Why don't we create other models that satisfy our current needs?
We must demand expertise from those that generate images, and consciousness on their work.
That's a nice word, right?
"Consciousness: the state of feeling, thinking and acting with knowledge."
All those companies that only want to sell a product or expand their audience, will be isolated and obsolete, out of our folder with projects we want to support.
Communication must be a means to getting closer, understanding each other, and it shouldn't isolate us or build walls between us.
Let's create images that would make us feel happy with our own bodies when we stand in front of a mirror, whatever shape we have.
Let's create images that make us want to talk to people that are different from us, and get to know them.
Let's create images that stimulate our imagination with new perspectives.
Let's create images that make us free, not as individual beings, but as a whole race.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Do you see that image?
That's the picture of violence in soccer.
You don't see a hooligan there, do you?
You're looking at victims of soccer violence.
You will say: are you crazy?
You don't see anyone bleeding,
or rolling down the grades.
They're another kind of victims.
Do you know why I talk about this and not about tactical systems or the offside rule?
For a lot of people of my generation, for most of them I guess, soccer was a very important emotional bond with our parents.
Do you see that us, now, if we have children, we hug them all the time.
We are all the time, physically, close to them.
This wasn't like this before.
And at least in my case soccer was what gave us that kind of tie with our fathers.
My father is a Ferro fan.
In consequence, I support Ferro.
And since I remember, my father took me to watch the game.
Let's make this clear: when I say "going to watch the game"
I mean it. Ferro played in Rosario, we went to Rosario.
Ferro played in Santa Fe, we went to Santa Fe.
In Córdoba, we went to Córdoba.
Ferro played in Misiones, we had to cross the country, we went to Misiones.
It was my greatest pleasure to make those trips with my dad.
I don't remember a bigger hug than when the Burro Rocchia --a great 6 what we had in the 80s-- scored the goal to give Ferro the first national championship.
I don't remember better lunches than the ones we had by the end of the road while the smoke of the barbecue came out and we were eating asado.
Not talking, but looking at each other we said everything, at that moment.
Then friends of my father joined us.
And then their sons.
And then my friends.
And we all went in a big group, we cared a lot about the result.
I won't deny that.
We cared about winning.
But it was the trip what really mattered.
Today, we don't have that.
None of us had the chance to do that with their kids.
First, because we are pretty conscious, and to some stadiums, we decided: I won't take my kid.
And then the state forbid it.
seven years ago, they said:
"If you go as fan of the away team, I can't take care of you.
You can enter the stadium, but you may not get out alive,
so I don't let you go".
The fight against soccer violence is given by all of us who what are looking for is to rebuild that emotional connection that completed our identity.
I want to show you another picture now.
Maybe many of you remember it.
It's Riquelme.
This photo was taken some years ago.
With a boy, who is 15 or 16 years old,

who is spitting at him from behind.
Compare this photo with the first one.
How different!
What a different time!
Many people say: "soccer reflects our society".
Could be.
What outrages me, and what we should think about is how many people in a stadium, supports that photo.
I assure you it's a huge amount.
When anyone in a stadium believes that the other is an enemy just because they wear a different shirt, we are completely screwed.
Because that's the ideological support in which a big, millionaire business is based held by the hooligans and that generates tens, but tens of deaths each year. Well.
How can we deal with this situation
which is given?
What does media say?
It presents this as an external phenomenon.
I think it's not.
That this is inherent to us all as a nation.
Let's classify the violence in a soccer stadium.
I think there are three.
First, the violence related to the sport.
It's a contact sport.
There is friction, nothing too serious.
The worst that could happen is a broken bone, never a death.
Then, there's another violence, which I call spontaneous violence.
You like the way the 8 plays, he doesn't like it, you argue, you fight.
Another fan puts you apart and we keep on rooting for the same team.
That doesn't kill anybody.
But then there's a third violence, made by this pack, the hooligans, and most people say: "They are always the same misfits".
And I think they are not,
they are the most adapted to a system that is completely corrupt, in Argentinian soccer, that pays them to have its own army for anything they could need.
I'm always asked: Are the hooligans a new group?
No, hooligans have always been there, but the way they work has changed.
Until the mid-90s, at least in people's imagination, hooligan violence was clearly defined.
It was one team against the other.
A kind of misunderstood manliness.
Let's see who is the worst on the block.
I steal your shirt, your flag, I paint the name of my team on the wall, and you don't.
Then, it started to transform in the mid-90'sl
And if you see the statistics, in the last 20 years, 90% of people killed in Argentinian soccer, are not related to that type of fighting. They are fights inside the same team.
What happened?
They don't fight for a team anymore, they now fight for money. Big big money.
So big that the hooligans are not just in the stadium.
Thanks to the money they receive, they become political delegates.
Because with money you can buy people's wills.
And then they make little armies and start to serve other kinds of power.
First, the police, but also the politicians,
and the unions. All the last deaths by union violence or political violence, the one who pulled the trigger was a hooligan.
Mariano Ferreyra is the most obvious example, a worker's party activist, killed by a gang of the rail union.
Killed by Harry Favale.
I can spend the whole day giving examples so you can understand this new reality of soccer violence that becomes violence in all other social spaces.
Because what we had before was clear: a business between police and hooligans.
You have your business, resell tickets, get the car watchers, and sell illegal merchandising and we go 50/50.
When hooligans start to work for politicians and union leaders, things get worse.
Let me show you some images, so you can see how this works and the level of complicity.
Here's a photo.
There was an operation in Boca's stadium.
The police knew:
"Boca's hooligans go in two cars with lots of evidence that could relate them to the club managers and their political supporters."
They take everyone out of the car and this is what they supposedly had inside.
This photo is the only picture that gets to the lawsuit.
Two membership cards, two guns.
You say: "That's a lot, one has muffler".
Now look at this other photo.
This is what was confiscated that day.
See the membership cards?
There are more than 500.
But don't look just at this.
Look at those spreadsheets with the names of all hooligans, how much they took, how much of that was for the police, how much for the club's management, and how much for their political and legal supporters.
But this didn't reach the court case.
Only the other photo.
And here's another picture.
You may not know them.
Once the police confiscated all that, they call two people.
Boca's security chief and a man well-related to politics.
And they say:
"Look what we found. What shall we do?".
Well, for many reasons, they decided not to take it to court.
Do you know how this ended?
Do you remember the guns I showed in the first photo?
The guns went to the 24th Commissioner.
There, they broke the gun's hammer.
Those guns couldn't be used, because they couldn't be triggered.
If they cant' be triggered, guns are not guns.
So there's no gun.
Everyone went to court.
Do you know how many went to jail?
The answer is the one
you're thinking: none, none.
When we have this complicity: think that the hooligans work as security guards for politicians, for union leaders, the problem is much bigger.
Now, we have identified the problem and its actors.
Why don't we have a solution?
And this is where I invite you to think which is our position in front of this?
This happens in Argentina, they don't have this in Europe.
Everyone says: "Let's do the same England did with their hooligans".
But there's another thing here that must have to do with the destruction of the collective dreams in the 90's, and people held on to what they could.
And many held on to the hooligan pack.
So you go to see a match and people root more for the fans than for the team.
So our soccer fan became much more violent, centered on the results, and much less tolerant.
I ask all of you: What did you do?
What did we do to let this happen?
And what didn't we do to stop it?
And there's where we find the solution.
Don't look for it in the government.
Don't look for it in the state.
No one will deal seriously with something that makes millions of pesos to share among all its actors.
You may say: "How much money is it?"
I'll give you a very short example.
The Rolling Stones will come to Argentina.
Five shows in River's stadium.
3,000 tickets of each show are for hooligans, what the organizers have to pay in order to make the show there.
The official price for each ticket is 1000 pesos.
How much could they cost in resale?
Let's guess something low: 2,000 pesos?
Will you do the math with me?
15,000 by 2,000. How much is that in a week?
Yes, it's a fortune.
Which is the solution then?
We all have the solution.
I always say this:
Stop going to the stadium.
We don't go to the stadium anymore.
(Applause) Have you ever heard that five covers of a certain newspaper could bring a government down?
Well, in this decade we showed this wasn't true.
It doesn't matter.
I'm sure that five images could make a government, this one, the next one, any government of any party, take the issue more seriously.
What do you think would happen if Boca and River played, we have 600 hooligans on each side and the rest of the stadium is empty?
What would happen if we made the same in San Lorenzo vs Huracan, in Independiente vs Racing?
What would happen if we made this in all our soccer for one, two, three, four weeks?
Whatever it takes.
No one goes to the stadium.
Totally empty stadiums, with 600 hooligans on one side cursing at other 600 on the other?
And if besides that, we decide not to watch the matches on TV, not to listen the matches on the radio?
Don't you think it would be
a very powerful message to send to a government?
(Applause) I appreciate your applause, but there's a problem.
When I say this to the supporters, do you know what they say?
Ask us anything except to stop going to the stadium.
We won't do that.
So I always give the same example.
Let's see.
Sometimes the price of tomato goes up.
And we all agree.
Don't buy tomatoes.
It's a perishable product.
It will come down.
Two weeks later, it's half its price.
It seems that Argentinians can live without tomatoes, but not without going to the stadium.
And as long as Argentinians keep thinking this way, this picture will be utopic.
The power to have soccer in peace and to rebuild ourselves in our identity, with our emotions, won't be possible.
I insist, in order to have this image again, we all have to do something.
And I assure you that if we decided to do this, it will be important.
Because soccer, I can assure this, is more than eleven guys running after a ball.
Thank you!
(Applause)
(Speaks in Mexican Sign Language) (Applause) If you didn't understand anything I just said, please look at the person by your side.
Don't be alarmed, he or she is most likely as confused as you are, you might tell by their face expression.
I probably showed that same expression the first time I stumbled upon a group of deaf people on the subway,
while they were communicating the way I just did, in sign language.
I thought, "it looks so nice", right?
And I felt bad for not being able to understand them, and also because they might not understand us either.
I am an actor, director, producer, and improviser, so you might think, "Oh, he was acting."
No, I was really using Mexican Sign Language, LSM, a sign language for the deaf.
And I am here today to tell you how improvisation can be used as an inclusion method for disabled people.
I don't have any disabled relatives.
I started learning five years ago out of curiosity, for what I told you about the subway, and because I watched this TV news anchor speaking with her hands, and I wanted to do that too.
Not Lolita Ayala, she can only say, "See you tomorrow, on Tuesday," but Perla Moctezuma, the one in the little box below.
I loved that both deaf and hearing people could know what was going on in Mexico and the world.
I started learning and just like anyone who is learning something, and gets into a new community, I bumped into good and bad things.
For example: experiences like weird glances, they mistrusted me, because I might take advantage of them;
to the point where they would trust me too much and say, "You look chubbier, are you overfeeding?"
(Laughter)
And typical things like misusing a sign and asking for sex instead of ketchup. (Laughter)
It happened to me.
This is ketchup and this is sex.
(Laughter) So after messing up with the same sign several times,
(Laughter) with that one and many more of course. (Applause) As I went deeper into the deaf community I understood not only their language is different, but also their culture is different.
I became familiar with their needs and their situation.
Just to give you some context, Law for Disabled People stipulates that "people with disabilities are those who have a physical, mental or sensitive deficiency which limits them in one or more everyday activities."
In Mexico, there are about 7 million disabled people, which accounts for 5.9% of Mexican population. Of which, almost 700, 000 have a hearing disability.
I would like to clear up: they are not deaf and mute, they are deaf.
They don't produce sound because they lack a sound reference, but that doesn't mean they are unable to speak.
In fact, many deaf people can speak.
This is one of the most neglected disabilities because it's not as obvious as a wheelchair.
Also one of the most neglected because in Mexico deaf people cannot aspire to education beyond middle school.
For them, homeschool is
the only way to finish high school And if they want to go to college, they have to pay a private one and hire interpreters.
In terms of employment, government and companies would rather give them money than creating jobs for them.
And regarding arts, my focus area, there is no real cultural offer nor is there access to entertainment, or an offer where they can develop themselves.
A few years ago a friend invited me to the theater,
There I was, and in comes a group of deaf people whom I had known for some time.
They sit, I was still very happy. The show starts.
Ten minutes later, they stand up and leave.
Concerned, I went after them.
"Hey, what happened?
Why did everyone leave?"
One of them said, “I am sorry but we need an interpreter. We were told there would be one but there was none.
We don't understand anything, so we are leaving now."
At that very moment I realized that as a stage creator, I had the responsibility of not letting this happen again.
I was responsible of creating inclusive art.
The idea brought me to Germany a year ago, - sponsored by an organization - to create an inclusive art project.
There I could for the first time put myself in the deaf people shoes.
I went to a performance show... in German. (Laughter)
I don't speak German, just a bit of English.
The show started, audience began to talk and participate, everybody was laughing and I was like...
For the first time ever I understood their feelings,
and realized how important these inclusive spaces are.
So I created this project of merging improv and theater to create spaces where both deaf and hearing people could access entertainment, have fun, and enjoy art; but also interact and create art for themselves and others.
For those who don't know what improv is: Theatrical improvisation is the art of spontaneously creating stories; original stories are created out from audience ideas. Stories that have never been seen and will never be seen again.
You might wonder why improv?
Because it has three essential foundations, three pillars useful on stage, but also as a teaching method and life philosophy.
The first one is Acceptance.
Can you please pass me the ball?
I accept every proposal I am given, and I will make it thrive.
The next one is Generosity.
I share everything about myself; my ideas, no matter how crazy or silly, because someone might accept and develop them.
The third one is Attention. (Laughter)
If I don't pay attention to proposals from life or other people, how could I make them thrive?
And these 3 pillars gave me one of the best lessons in my life,
which I'm going to share with you.
Have you ever played Simon says?
Everything Simon says is done, and what it's not said, doesn't get done, right?
In theater and improv instead of Simon, we have Maya.
We do whatever Maya says.
For example, Maya says "right hand up."
Nice, very good.
Just to add a bit more fun to this game, if we make a mistake, we will cry like a mariachi. (Cries like a mariachi)
Don't worry, it will be fun, I promise.
Very well, let's do this. Ready?
Maya says,"Right hand up",
Maya says,"Left hand up."
Maya says,"Hands on your head."
Maya says,"Cross your arms."
Hands up.
How nice!
1,2,3! (Everyone cries like a mariachi).
(Applause) That goes for you, please.
What happens if we make a mistake?
Nothing!
Absolutely nothing happens!
We have been taught that mistakes are wrong, that we have to be perfect, that we have to reach perfect.
That having a disability is an error, a limitation that disables us to do things.
But in improv errors don't exist.
I agree, they don't exist but path changes do exist.
When we accept these proposals, new ideas emerge and we can continue creating new things.
As I was happily in Germany, there was this proposal and when I came back to Mexico, someone helped me develop it.
That is how Manik, Art for Everyone A.C. was created. Along with my dear team Laura, Vero and Santiago - three people who trusted the project from the beginning - we took over the task of creating workshops for disabled people, we trained them and adapted theater plays for them.
How do we make these workshops?
They are both for people with or without disabilities, that is, we make them bilingual with sign language interpreters. Yet, using sign language and Spanish might be a setback,
so we use body expression and mimics to communicate on stage or even paint together.
Yes, it has been a very tough job, because inclusion must come from both sides: from people with disabilities and people without disabilities.
It happened to me, I was having a random conversation with a deaf girl, and she asked me if I had a girlfriend. I did had a new girlfriend,
and said "yes", all excited. "Ah, she is deaf?", she asked. and I, "No, she can hear."
(Laughter)
I thought it was a nice comment, my girlfriend didn't. But it's understandable.
Deaf people have been discriminated for so long, they have been segregated and discriminated. It's understandable.
Now, it's up to us to create these workshops to teach them how to paint or dance. But we also teach them how to make their own workshops, so that they can have their own companies and guilds.
One of my teachers in Germany told me, - after I asked him - are you not bored of teaching the same for so long?
And he said, "Well, the time comes when you have to train someone else to take your place in the project. In the end, your efforts are not just for you, are for them."
To conclude: both worlds, deaf and hearing are part of the same society, hence the importance of inclusion.
If we stop seeing disability as a limitation, and accept it the way it is. If we accept disabled people just as a different reality, a different way of living or doing stuff, we can take that idea and continue building this story:
acceptance.
If you, from your different activities, create projects for deaf or disabled people, that small idea will grow and benefit more people.
Generosity.
After today you might not know what to do for deaf people or any kind of disabled person.
But I am sure that from today, these people will no longer remain invisible for you. Whether you see them on your job, on the street, or the subway.
Attention.
Thank you so much for giving me yours.
(Applause) Fernando Rivera: Thank you Antonio.
Adelina Filigrana: Toño, thank you so much.
I very much admire your work because I have a brother with disability.
I wanted to ask you how can we join your efforts?
How can we help?
It's not like we go out and say, "How do I start doing something for the rest of the people?
F. R: Or those of us who make cabaret, theatrical shows, how can we integrate interpreters to reach a wider audience? Antonio Zacruz:
What we are looking forward is to make everything more inclusive.
There are interpreters, associations, independent interpreters. It's a matter of getting together and join efforts.
For instance, we started interpreting plays of my theatrical company.
At the beginning people were like, "It's a bit distracting to see a guy speaking with his hands."
But it's all part of integration, not just having an interpreter, but to make him or her part of, right?
There are plays where they are blended among the actors that is called "shadow"
or even train the actors so that they can use sign language.
Train deaf people to help them make their own plays, their own presentations.
F. R: That's amazing.
A. Z: About how you can help? If someone has an idea, like cabaret, we have dance, plastic arts, audiovisual arts, theater.
Those are our starting points,
but if anyone wants to join efforts, if they have an idea, we can help them too.
A. F: Joining, that is what I meant.
There's no need to start
something out of nowhere, you can join people who are already doing something.
F. R: Also remember that in Mexico we find it easy to speak sign language.
All you have to do is drive during a rush hour, to see how everyone does it naturally,
so we just need to dig a little deeper.
A.Z: Exactly, those small ideas. I read in a improv book, that we don't share our ideas because we think they are not smart enough to be expressed, so we keep them to ourselves.
But if we share these ideas, we can create things like this,
like what we are seeing on this event. A.F: Congratulations Toño.
A.Z: Thank you.
(Applause)
Every time I turn on the TV, every time I read the tabloids, I come across many openly gay presenters, an openly lesbian presenter, I come across famous singers, actors, actresses from Hollywood, even Spain, hand in hand with their same-sex partners.
Recently a girl who was crowned Miss Spain appeared in a photo with her girlfriend, which spread all around social media.
I love this kind of news.
However, if you walk through the door of an office of any company, it seems that the gays and lesbians of this world have disappeared off the earth.
It seems that we don't exist.
I don't hear any boss talking about his recent holiday with his boyfriend.
I don't see any manager with a photo of her wife on her desk.
I don't hear any colleague talking about the great weekend they had in Chueca.
Of course, at first I thought maybe I was in the wrong profession.
As Belén said, I am a lesbian woman and I am a business consultant, I work in the business world.
I thought that, perhaps, gays and lesbians only exist in the show business or in the media.
What I did then, was set up a professional network for lesbian women and I was surprised to find that hundreds of lawyers, businesswomen, consultants, like me, have been joining and continue to do so, so I am very pleased because I realized that I hadn't chosen the wrong profession.
I realized that the LGBT population makes up 10% of the population and that we also exist in the business world.
The issue is that we aren't very visible.
According to a study by Ben Cappelli, a researcher at the ESADE Business School, more than 50% of gay and lesbian professionals
try to hide their sexual orientation at work.
And since the business world revolves around doing business, I asked myself, is it good business for a company to come out of the closet?
Well, if we take a look at the relevant research and findings it seems that the answer to this question is yes.
In the US, the country of reference for the corporate world, there is an index, the Equality Corporate Index. It measures to what extent companies promote the management of sexual diversity within their workforces.
Throughout 10 years, this index has gone from containing less than 15 businesses to more than 300 businesses with a grade of 100%. 304 businesses to be precise who proactively enagage with the gay community through providing training to their staff on sexual diversity or by doing something which I believe is very important: show their support, public commitment, to the gay community.
Another example that this must be good business for companies is the success seen by organizations like Out &amp; Equal.
Out &amp; Equal is an organization founded 20 years ago by Selisse Berry in San Francisco.
Out &amp; Equal organizes annual meetings which are attended by more than 2,500 executives of US businesses and their international affiliates during which they share their best practices in managing diversity.
IBM and Disney are two of the main sponsors of this organization but hundreds of organizations support Out &amp; Equal and are committed to the cause.
And why are businesses so interested in their gay employees coming out of the closet?
Is it because it affects their balance sheets?
Or is it an issue of corporate ethics, of social responsibility?
Well, it seems everything plays a part.
It's been shown that businesses that promote diversity generate more creative and productive workforces and this is reflected in their balance sheets.
According to the IT giant SAD's diversity department, it seems that the US businesses that are committed to diversity are up to 30% more profitable.
In addition, businesses that manage the sexual diversity of their staff well are able to attract and retain gay talent, which makes up 10% of the work force.
And, of course, we consider carefully whether or not the companies that we work for will have any problems with it and whether this will have a bad impact on our professional career.
We look for businesses where this doesn't happen, And the organizations that are committed to us ensure that we are more productive workers that we are more authentic, more authentic leaders within our organizations.
If not, they should speak to the former CEO, the ex-Chief Executive of British Petroleum; a man who had to quit his job when a media scandal broke out.
He was under a lot of media scrutiny because he was outed as gay.
Today, this man dedicates his life to giving talks and presentations, to explain how things would have been different if he could have been open about his orientation at work.
And I understand him, truly, because it's true that we've all gone through stages when we behave a bit like robots, when it feels like we have no life outside of work, or when, perhaps, life seems rather puzzling.
I have some female friends who, so not to seem like robots but also to not reveal their sexual orientation at work change the name of their partner. So, instead of talking about her girlfriend Maria, it's her boyfriend Mario. But imagine how stressful it must be to keep this up on a daily basis.
It is estimated that gay or lesbian employees who hide their condition at work are up to 20% less productive. And even more so today, when businesses demand a lot from us.
Sometimes, they demand that we be involved almost on a personal level.
Because it is very common to have work dinners where you can invite your partner. It's very common to have business trips where you can invite your spouse.
It's also very common to have photos on your tables, your desks in your offices, of your family.
And these small details test the homosexual professional: their level of acceptance and openness surrounding the issue.
Because being homosexual doesn't just mean that you sleep next to someone of the same sex at night. In reality, being homosexual means that the most important person in your life, the person you want to do everything with, to travel with, go to parties with, go to the cinema with, the person you want to start a family with in the future, is someone of the same gender as you.
This is what being homosexual means.
But let's return to the issue of whether it's good business for a firm to come out of the closet.
We can look at it in terms of a company's income, how sales are affected.
Businesses that engage with the gay community in the medium and long-term see a positive impact on their sales.
Why?
Firstly, because the gay community makes up 10% of the population. We are particularly sensitive and loyal towards the companies that respect us and that sometimes even nod to us.
But in our society today, in which we are witnessing an unstoppable normalization, this effect is much more significant because it's magnified when we take allies into account.
An ally is a heterosexual person who is close with a gay person.
In my case: my parents, my brother, the rest of my family, my colleagues, my heterosexual friends.
So if we add together the gay community and its allies, we can see that in Spain, this figure amounts to 88% of the population.
This figure was released last year in a ranking of 39 countries. Spain came out on top as the least homophobic country, as 88% of the population said yes, answered yes, to the question, Should society accept the homosexual population?
88% of Spaniards said yes.
So, of course, when a company is publicly homophobic or causes offence, you may find that this 88% of Spaniards or another percent in other countries, but which is high nonetheless, opposes it.
This was the case, for example, with the Italian pasta brand Barilla. Last year, its president made some unfortunate statements about traditional families and the gay community, and saw its sales boycotted.
And I understand that managing these issues within businesses can be complicated.
In fact, what large companies do is set up diversity committees, which are involved with senior management, to handle, amongst other things, these types of situation.
Now, consider that things become even more complicated with large multinational firms.
Because it could be that a firm has headquarters in the US where it's promoting LGBT diversity among its staff, setting an example.
But this same company has local offices across the world, in other countries.
What happens when you have local offices in countries where this is considered a crime?
Today, there are almost 80 countries where homosexuality is considered a criminal offence, where, if I walked holding my girlfriend's hand, or kissed her on the street, I could be taken to prison, I could be given a life sentence, or even killed, because capital punishment exists in many countries.
Ultimately, in these countries, discussing diversity policies within companies makes little sense, because human rights are not being respected.
But I'm going to focus on a less extreme case, the case of countries like Spain, where, thank goodness, we're legal we even have rights, and can get married, adopt children. What happens in these countries?
Well, what happens here is that companies, large international firms that promote diversity in their US headquarters don't impose their policies in their other offices, although these policies exist in these countries.
They establish a general framework for behaviour and a few principles, but they give every local office, every subsidiary, the power to articulate them into concrete measures, because they aren't issues that affect the main business of the firm, and because, ultimately, they are sensitive issues that depend on the political and social situation of every country.
This is where we, the gay professionals, the lesbian professionals, have to actively participate, actively participate and move from within our organizations, and from outside through lobbies, so that these policies are implemented, so that diversity is managed within our own companies.
We are the ones with the power to provoke change and to make things happen.
And the higher our role, the more responsibility we have, and the better organized we are, the more easily things will happen because it has to happen.
It has to happen that when I walk into an office I see photos of the male boss' boyfriend or husband.
It has to happen that when I go to the Christmas party I see a couple of women or a couple of men dancing together, like how I saw the US ambassador to Spain dancing with his husband in front of more than 1,000 people at the 4th of July celebration in Madrid.
It has to happen that we are not embarrassed to talk about our orientation in the office, that we can talk about our partner using their real names.
It has to happen.
And now, back to the question I posed at the start: Is it good business for a firm to come out of the closet?
The answer is yes: yes for the firms, for all the reasons we've discussed.
Yes for us, the gay and lesbian employees, because we will be happier, more productive and more authentic.
Yes for our colleagues, because we will help them become more open-minded ensuring that they, too, will be more creative and productive.
Yes for those that meet with our colleagues, because our colleagues will talk about us and society will realize that we exist, that we are here and that we are normal, ordinary people, that we don't have two heads or eat anyone, that we are happy, we have our jobs and our lives and that all we want is to live like everyone else, no more, no less, including in the office, where we spend 5 days a week and several hours a day.
Because we can only be happy if we can be ourselves in all walks of life.
Thank you
(Applause)
You may ask why do we sleep, or when do we sleep, or how much do we sleep, or how do we sleep.
The good thing is that science has the answer to all these questions and it is surprisingly simple.
The answer of science to why, how, when, how much do we sleep, is very easy: no idea.
(Laughter) Wait.
Scientists don't know something as obvious as what happens to us every night or what should happen every night?
No, we don't know.
And that's wonderful.
In a way it turns all of us into scientists.
We can all experiment with our sleep, or that of the person next to us, our friends, our family. That's also science.
In fact, it's not a full "no idea"; some things we do know.
For instance: why do we sleep?
It seems quite obvious: we sleep because we are sleepy or tired.
Is this actually so obvious?
Let's see, think with me: when you are on vacation, you are not tired, you do nothing. Do you sleep less?
No.
And when you work out hard or you are doing a very demanding physical work, do they sleep more? No.

So this is not just about being tired, even when there's some of that in it.
There must be more to why we sleep.
We do know something: sleeping is important.
Many years ago, experiments were done where animals would be sleep deprived, they wouldn't let them sleep.
You know what happened to them?
They died.
And they died in two weeks, as if they wouldn't have eaten.
Sleeping is vital, just as important as eating.
And what else happens while we sleep?
A lot of things: We grow, the body is repaired, we recharge our batteries.
A lot of things that wouldn't happen if we didn't sleep.
Memories are consolidated.
The worst you can do if you have a test or something important, is not to sleep to study more, because you will surely fail.
So we know something about the why, but not much.
How about the "when"?
When do we sleep?
We evolved as day creatures. We have to be active during the day and sleep at night, locking ourselves up in the cave, away from dangers, away from what is happening outside.
And why is that?
Because we have this little piece of our brain that keeps the time and tells the body what time it is.
Our biological clock.
In a way, we are clocks with legs.
Everything we do happens in time, not only in space.
Sleep also occurs over time.
It happens or should happen at night.
But less of this is happening, as we take hours away from our nights in favor of our waking life.
Since the invention of electric light, that great sleep thief, we increasingly go to bed later.
Our body is not prepared for that.
In fact, it's true that some people have more or have less need of sleep, or need to go to sleep earlier or later.
There are morning people, we call "larks".
Some of you are having a very good time now. Others are "owls", evening people, who are yet quite sleepy now: "What is this? Where am I?"
(Laughter) In fact, there are extreme morning people, the extreme larks, and extreme evening people, the extreme owls.
It can happen every so often, that an extreme lark meets an extreme owl and they get married.
(Laughter) In such case, children are a miracle. (Laughter)
But we have to listen to that clock which tells us when to do things: when to be awake, when to sleep.
Sometimes it clashes with reality, with society, with our work schedule, with school.
For example, teenagers are typical owls and they do everything much later.
You think it's cultural, they go to bed late, they chat with friends until late night, they go out late.
A lot of it is cultural, but that culture is based on a biological need, their clock tells them "do everything later".
What's the problem?
The next day, at what time does school begin?
At seven o'clock in the morning, which is absurd: the kids are asleep, literally asleep during the first hours.
We have to work on this: we should listen to our clock and so should society.
And the "how much"?
How much do we sleep?
How much do we have to sleep?
If I take any of you, or to anyone to the sleep lab and I tell them: "Sleep all you feel like" the average for an adult person is eight hours, twenty minutes. Eight and a half hours.
Let's see, do you sleep eight and a half hours?
No.
We sleep quite less than that.
It is estimated that we are sleeping an hour less than fifty years ago, and about two hours less than a hundred years ago.
That's a lot, it really is a lot.
We are finally stealing about two hours of sleep.
What's more, we could make a projection, right?
If we sleep two hours less than a hundred years ago and one hour less than fifty years ago, if we draw a line and follow it, more or less by 2758 We won't sleep anymore.
(Laughter) That cannot happen because sleep is a necessity.
But the need is also of hours of sleep.
And that is for adults.
Teenagers should sleep nine hours or more. Kids, between nine and ten hours, and they are not sleeping either.
For many reasons, the main one is social.
Society is shrinking the hours we sleep and that brings us problems.
Screens also harm us because the light coming out of them -- and nobody sleeps alone lately, we all sleep with a screen in bed, just in case -- it's a light that tells to the clock that is daylight, therefore meaning: "wake up, all good, keep going."
And surely, the next day we have to wake up.
We sleep less, we sleep bad, we sleep very little.
"And what is the problem?",
you may ask.
"I'm doing great, in any case, I catch up during the weekend.
What is the problem with not sleeping well?"
Lots of problems.
One of the problems is that we are going to be sleepy.
Clearly, we will be very sleepy during the day.
You might think: "Not that serious, I can take a nap."
We will be cranky.
Not sleeping changes our mood and that's the least serious issue.
Being sleepy or cranky is not as serious as getting sick.
Not sleeping means illness.
At first, we will get sick due to more infections.
The immune system needs to sleep to protect us from what's out there.
But we are also going to fall ill with metabolic disorders.
We are going to get diabetes.
When this is chronic, when sleep deprivation turns chronic, diseases show up.
Therefore, not sleeping is not only an individual problem, it becomes a public health problem.
And we all have to be aware of this.
Sure you heard about something called "jet lag".
Jet lag is what happens when you fly.
You go across time zones, you get to your destination.
For the world it's a time, for you it's another.
And you're a zombie for a few days until you adjust to that new time.
If you travel for pleasure is not serious, but if you travel for work, it can get tricky.
The things is that you can experience jet lag without leaving home.
Then the social schedule differs from the schedule you have here, you get what is called "social jet lag".
And we all have social jet lag: we all wake up with an alarm.
Therefore, we are not waking up at the time our biological clock says.
What's more, it could happen a much more serious social jet lag.
Imagine flying from here to Africa and stay for a week.
From Africa you fly to Asia and stay for a week.
And from here -- Oops this one is in the middle of the Pacific --
you fly to Easter Island and stay for a week and come back.
Pretty crazy a thing to do that, right?
That's what workers do when they are on rotating shifts.
One week they work by day, another in the afternoon, another at night.
As if they were constantly flying around the world.
And obviously they get ill with those things we said before: metabolic disorders, cardiovascular disorders. In addition, WHO, the World Health Organization listed work on rotating shifts as a risk factor for cancer. So to sleep poorly or at the wrong time, is no joke and we have to pay attention.
We research into this subject in the lab.
We research into when we sleep, how much we sleep because we need data, we need evidence to later pour it back into society.
We also study what happen with kids who have to get up so early for school.
And our secret hope is to delay a little bit school timetables.
You can imagine every time we say this in school, we are heroes.
We also conduct research to see what happens with our workers on rotating shifts to improve their quality of life.
And why do we do all this?
First, because what we do in the lab we hope it gets to the people one day.
Also, because it is what we dream of.
We dream of a clock that works, adapted to the world, that doesn't make us sick.
And, like everybody else, we want to fulfill our dreams.
Who doesn't want to fulfill their dreams?
But to fulfill your dreams, you have to start with something quite obvious: you have to dream.
And to dream, what must be done?
Sleep.
Thank you.
(Applause)
We're going to do an activity that's also a scientific experiment just as we've been doing in past editions of this space we call TEDxperiments, where the idea is we make the most of the fact we're 10,000 of us here, so we can all do science with pencil and paper.
Now the moment's come for you all to open your envelopes.
There you'll find a paper sheet that, for now, I'll ask you not to open, stay in the cover where it says TEDxperiments and your participant number.
One important clarification: your participation in this experiment is completely voluntary, you can quit the experiment at any moment without further explanation.
If you'd like to take part in it, mark a cross where it says "yes" and write down your age and gender.
So we're going to start with the experiment consisting of three parts.
Let's start with the page where it says Part 1.
There you'll find five phrases and what you'll have to do is read them carefully and answer them by marking the corresponding checkbox depending on your selection.
You also have a third option saying "Don’t Know/No Answer".
This part is individual, do it by yourselves, no cheating.
Go ahead.
Very well then, with this we're done with Part 1 of the experiment and we can go on to the next page where it says Part 2.
In this part you won't play by yourselves, you'll play with someone else, but not just anybody else: by assigning you a participant number, we already set up all the couples in the stadium.
Look for the person with the same number as yours. and remember the participant number in your cover.
Find each other but for now don't do anything more than meeting, we'll see the instructions further ahead.
(Whispering) And now I'll ask you for a favor to the pairs that have already found each other, raise your papers.
Whoa! The experiment's
already worth it for this, huh!
Well, thank you, please put down the papers.
Now we'll see the instructions of what you'll do.
You'll play a game called "The debating game," that has two simple instructions.
In each case, hear what I say in the instructions and wait for me to tell you "go" to start following it.
The first instruction is that each of you will write in your own paper the five answers the other one gave in the first part of the experiment.
Go ahead.
(Whispering) We'll go on to instruction number two, you have five minutes, starting when I say "go" to debate freely about each of the five topics in Part 1.
You can use time as you wish, but here's a clarification.
You don't necessarily have to convince each other, you can exchange opinions, arguments, chat, but it's not necessary, to convince each other.
From now on, players of the audience, you have 5 minutes to debate.
Go.
(Whispering) Very well players in the audience, time is up, stop, debates are over.
That's it, time's over.
Now we'll go on to the nicest part of this experiment, where each player will say goodbye to their partner with a special gesture,
Look up that in the paper, below everything, it says how each pair of you has to say goodbye.
Players, say goodbye through that gesture.
(Whispering) This scene is really moving.
But we have to go on to the final part of the experiment so I have to ask you one last bit of effort.
In the last page you'll see a series of questions where each of you, individually, is going to tell us the impression your assigned partner caused on you.
I insist, this part is individual, do it by yourselves, and actually your answers will be confidential and anonymous, so as soon as you're done answering, put your papers in the envelope again.
I'll give you a minute to answer those questions.
Go.
Very well then, time's up, put your papers again in the envelope as with this, players, the experiment's over.
(Applause) First of all, I'd like to thank you and also to congratulate you.
I have to applaud you, because you've just taken part in the biggest in-person social psychology experiment in history.
(Applause) Very well then, before finishing, I'd like to tell you some of the hypotheses, we had for this experiment.
We based our study on one made in the 60s in the US, which shows that every time we meet someone, we'll like that person better if we agree on many topics with them.
The more topics I agree with someone, the better I'll like them.
What we wanted to see was if that effect was stronger when we talked about public policy or when we talked about personal taste.
That's why we designed two types of phrases we put in two different forms.
Raise your hands those of you who were assigned talking about high school student strikes or the airing of soccer matches.
Very well, put them down.
Now raise your hands those who had to talk about more personal topics, like whether you like blood sausage, or if you use a bidet when you use the bathroom.
(Laughter) Very well, thanks, put them down.
Our hypothesis is that agreeing or not on public policy topics has a bigger impact on our appreciation of other people than if we talk about personal tastes.
But facts will be the ones to tell us if this is so.
Lastly, what about the goodbye gesture?
You saw we asked more or less half of you, from here to there, to shake your hands, and to the other half, from here to there, we asked you to give each other a big hug.
We thought, our hypothesis was, that those people who gave each other a hug, at least for a moment, and despite their differences, felt close to each other.
And if you did, there's the chance you might've liked each other a bit better than those who simply shook each other's hands.
To all of these matters, we don't have the answer today, but thanks to you and your participation, we may have them soon, in fact, the goal of this section, the goal of TEDxperiments, is not only that we learn of a new idea, but that we all build scientific knowledge together.
Thank you.
(Applause)
When I chose to study medicine, I did it convinced that by doing it I could help to improve the health of people. With time, I've realized that improving health is an everybody's thing and not just something of us, doctors.
The first one who opened my eyes was my preventive medicine professor with a very simple graph: "Evolution of the Mortality Rate by Tuberculosis."
The mortality rate by tuberculosis decreases continuously and slowly from over 4000, to less than 10 deaths per million inhabitants in a country like England.
As the rest of the class, I knew that by the end of the 19th century how the disease is transmitted was discovered.
By 1950, the first antibiotic, and soon after, the first vaccine.
What we couldn't imagine, what surprised us all, was seeing how this mortality rate had already started to decrease long before the bacteria was discovered.
And that the discovery of the antibiotic and the vaccine barely modified the decrease rate.
But so, what caused such a decrease in the mortality by tuberculosis?
There were mainly two things: the sanitation of cities and homes, and the improvement of nutrition.
There I started to realize what I've come to explain to you.
If we want to improve our health, if we want to progress in health issues, we have to look beyond health services.
We have to change our focus and we have to, first, invest in what is really affecting our health, and second, take action on a level that includes the entire population, not just a few.
Let's consider the first idea: What's affecting our health?
What defines it, what is known as "health determinants," we can split it in four groups.
The first of them is life conditions.
The neighborhood we live in, our working conditions, the education our children get, nutrition, exercise, whether we smoke or not...
I'm going to give you two examples based on real data.
A city, Barcelona, and its difference between life expectancy between its poorest and its richest neighborhood.
Ten years. A difference of
life expectancy of ten years in a single Spanish city.
Another example: Andalusia;
people without education or just with basic education have a health twice as bad as those with higher education.
It is estimated that life conditions determine almost half of our health.
The second group of health determinants is our environment.
It is estimated that it determines around 20 percent; without a doubt, the most important one, is atmospheric pollution, that each year, globally, it kills 3.7 million people.
In a city like Seville, if its air pollution levels were reduced to those of London, 300 early deaths would be prevented.
The third block of health determinants is our biology, which is estimated to determine around a fourth part of our health.
Our complexion, our genetics, as you know, there are certain diseases that have a genetic base.
And ultimately, health services, which are estimated to determine 11 percent of our health.
The quality of the health service, the proximity of health services the kind of health service.
In Spain we are very lucky because we have a high quality health service, and most importantly, of universal and free access which allows everyone to access it irregardlessly of our pocket.
Irregardlessly of our economic condition.
As you can see, our health depends on health services, of course, but is also depends on our life conditions and our environment, that together determine over 60 percent of our health.
Let's now see, of that invested by a country on health, what part of it is invested on each one of these health determining groups.
How much do you think they invest in our life conditions and in our environment?
3 percent.
And on health services?
90 percent.
Currently, we are investing a lot of money on health services, which is good, it's necessary, but the problem is we're investing very little on that which determines over 60 percent of our health.
Yet, we have some good examples of health investment beyond health services.
By the end of the 60's, some studies were published that concluded that lead had serious effects on human health, particularly, on the neurological development of children.
Scientists were able to convince governments, and they started a series of actions to reduce lead levels in gasoline, among others.
The reduction of lead in gasoline led to the reduction of lead in the atmosphere.
Thanks to this intervention, the IQ of children increased, no more and no less, than four points.
Countries that did it improved the health of their children a lot.
Moreover: it's been estimated that the economic benefit of these policies is over, pay attention, $300 billion a year only considering productivity.
But we still have a lot to do.
An area in which we can do a lot for our health is atmospheric pollution.
As I was saying, in a city like Seville, 300 deaths per year could be prevented if its pollution levels were reduced to those as the ones of a city like London.
And I mention London because among all big European cities, it is the one with the pollution levels closest to those recommended by the World Health Organization.
In Bilbao 300 deaths could be prevented, 700 in Madrid, 800 in Barcelona, keep adding, and that without mentioning the millions spent on cardiovascular and respiratory diseases that could be saved.
Or the thousands of pediatric urgencies due to asthma attacks that could also be prevented.
As you can see, our health is determined by other things than just by health services.
Currently, we are investing very little on that which affects our health the most.
Let's go with the second idea: What's affecting our health, and how can we improve it?
We have seen what affects it;
first of all, what we would have to do is to invest more on what's affecting it the most.
What we do, what we eat, the air we breathe, where we live, and so on.
But it's very important to keep in our minds the entire population and not just a few, and I'm going to explain you why, with a paradox.
It is known as the "Preventive Medicine Paradox."
In this graph you can see how any Western population is distributed as a function of its cholesterol levels.
As you can see, most of the population has intermediate cholesterol levels.
On the other hand, those with high or low levels are few.
This bell-shaped distribution is known as a "normal curve."
Today, most risk factors fit this "normal curve" distribution.
As everyone knows, high cholesterol levels are harmful for our health because they are related to a higher risk of cardiovascular disease.
The red line you're seeing represents how cholesterol levels are related with the risk of a heart attack.
With a higher cholesterol level goes a higher risk of a heart attack.
But now let me invite you to see how deaths due to heart attacks are distributed in this very same population.
Where do most of the cases occur?
Among those with intermediate cholesterol levels.
Why? Because even though their risk is
relatively lower, there are more of them.
This is the Preventive Medicine Paradox.
A big number of people, exposed to a small risk produces way much more cases than a small number of people exposed to a higher risk.
As most of the population has intermediate cholesterol levels, even though their risk is relatively lower, there will be many more sick people and many more deaths in this population group.
And, as I was saying, this "normal curve" distribution also fits with many other risk factors, as blood sugar levels or blood pressure.
Today, however, where are we focusing prevention?
On those with a higher risk.
Those with high cholesterol levels will be told to change their diet and they will get medicine.
Those with high blood sugar levels will get their sugar consumption restricted, and they will receive medicine.
Those with high blood pressure will be prohibited to consume salt, and they will receive medicine.
But with this strategy we're only capable of reducing, of preventing, a small number of heart attacks, of diabetes cases, of ictus.
And what if I tell you that it's possible to shift this entire curve to the left?
That through population strategies it'd be possible to decrease cholesterol levels, sugar levels, and blood pressure, of everyone, not of just a few.
And this way, besides preventing more sick people and more deaths, we would also be able of treating less people with medicine.
The next logical question is: "OK, well, what are we going to do to shift the entire risk factors curve?
In this example I mentioned, we could persuade people of consuming food with a high amount of saturated fats, or sugar, or salt.
We could also promote exercise and healthy nutrition.
I'm going to give two examples, one of each.
As you know, sugary drinks are one of the main causes of obesity and diabetes around the world.
Some countries implemented something known as "sugar tax," which is a special tax over food and drinks with excessive levels of sugar.
The United States and England are debating about it.
In Spain we're not talking about it that much, but it would not be bad to think about some strategy of this kind because obesity, specially child obesity, is a serious health problem in our country.
And as an example of promoting exercise we have shared bicycle services, which already is on many cities in Spain, for example the "Málaga bici."
In Barcelona, shared bicycle service, which is called "Bicing," increased the use of bicycles by 30 percent as a usual mean of transport.
But many of you will tell me that everything I'm explaining is utopian and not realistic or practical.
I am going to give you two examples of "where there's a will there's a way."
Do you remember the lead example at the beginning?
The pressure exerted by opposing lobbies was impressive.
Oil companies had to develop new lead-free gasoline and car manufacturers had to modify their engines.
But even so, it was achieved.
Another example: just ten years ago it was out of mind to think that the 2011 anti-tobacco law ever to see daylight.
The opposition from the hospitality industry and, of course, from the tobacco industry, was fierce.
Even so, it was possible to convince governments and the policy was implemented.
And today, we have evidence of the smoking reduction and, therefore, of improved health benefits.
And as where there's a will, there's a way, I propose you a change of perspective.
I propose you to see beyond health services and to think about improving our life conditions, the environment, and the society we live in.
That way, we will be able to take actions from the root of the causes.
I propose you to think beyond those few who have an elevated risk, and to consider the entire population.
Globally.
That way we'll be able to prevent better and more.
Together, through democratic participation we have to put pressure, to demand governments for more policies and interventions that include the entire population.
Move, find the way, join campaigns and platforms that are already making pressure.
Because they work.
On a local level, on your neighborhood, and at state level, nationwide, or at international level.
Policies that improve the quality of the air we breathe, the neighborhood we live in, our working conditions.
Policies that help us to eat and drink healthier, and that get rid of that already known to be harmful.
And that every policy takes our health into account.
Only if we invest on the true health determinants, only if we take action including the entire population, only then we'll be truly able to improve our health.
(Applause)
In the learning process, questions are not appreciated.
Teachers don't teach us to question ourselves.
They tell us how things are and how we should respond.
In my life, there has always been room for questions,
both those I have asked myself as well as those others have asked me.
Actually, when I was invited to talk about informal education one question was stuck in my mind and I wanted to share it with you: Why is it that when they capture the A-Team, they keep them in a garage?
I am sure that at one point you have all wondered that.
For the young people: the A-Team was an 80's TV series where a very diverse group of ex-soldiers helped a community in danger.
In every episode, they were captured and held in a barn, a warehouse, or a garage, and in every episode, they used the tools and materials they found in the garage in order to escape.
I often asked myself: Why did they put them in a garage again?
Don't they see that it's full of amazing things?
But the bad guys never realized it.
For them, it was a place to leave useless objects, abandoned things, a place to leave garbage.
Recently, I read that garbage is the product of the limits of imagination.
And that's what separates bad guys from the A-Team: where bad guys see an abstract pile of objects, the A-Team sees a paradise full of possibilities.
I guess that's why, years later, the studio where I work with my partners looks just like that, in fact, notice that some of them could have been in the series.
(Laughter) The fact is that at the end of every episode, the A-Team leader placed a cigar between his teeth and elegantly said: "I love it when a plan comes together."
And I always wondered: What plans?!
They have captured you, you fell into a trap, they locked you in a garage and you've miraculously escaped.
But he was kind of right.
Isaac Asimov, another man filled with questions, said: "To succeed, planning alone is insufficient.
One must improvise as well."
If Isaac Asimov says it, it must be right.
The fact is, that is exactly what the A-Team did: improvise.
They were able to coordinate their skills, abilities and values and combine them to tackle diverse situations.
They were definitely able to put their talents into practice.
I have been a wild educator for almost half my life;
educator because I don't know of any other way to see life than through shared learning;
wild because I only know how to think with my hands.
In other words, I ponder when I set myself to work.
When I face a limit or challenge, the first thing I think is how can I remove it.
Just as the barbarians did with Hadrian's Wall.
I learned all of this at home.
My parents are teachers and they filled our dinner conversations with talk of students, faculty, education models...
I, as a good teachers' child, became a model student.
I finished what was then the UOC and is now high school, with Honors.
Later, I entered the university and spent 14 years finishing my degree.
(Laughter) In architecture.
I can admit to my parents today, here in your presence, that I did not join a music group, nor was I champion of the card game, mus.
I was blessed to be connected with other classmates who, like me, decided to put aside their established plans, improvise, and look for other options.
During these 14 years, together with my classmates, I have carried out projects on four continents and I have been invited to give speeches and workshops in universities around the world.
How can it be that a university contracts a guy who only has a Bachelor's degree to teach a class?
Is it a failure in the system?
Or is it a way for the system to correct certain deficiencies?
Let's return to the A-Team.
You can be an exceptional pilot, a marvelous mechanic, or even a sharp-shooter like Lucky Luke, but if you can't work in a group, detect problems, and come up with creative solutions you'll never get out of the garage.
The capacity to improvise definitely comes from the realm of abilities.
How do we obtain these abilities?
Well, in the first place, education occurs throughout the entirety of life.
It doesn't start in grade school nor end with a Master's.
To say it another way, how do you learn English faster?
By enrolling in a language school or by getting an Irish girlfriend?
(Laughter) How do you learn mechanics?
In a professional training module or by tuning a van with your friends?
In essence, this is a trick question.
Rather, both types of learning are complementary and necessary.
Informal education is not a "low cost" version of formal education.
Informal education is the vital engine that allows us to articulate desire and curiosity, which is what feeds our hunger to know.
We are genetically designed to keep learning throughout our life.
Unless, of course, a flawed education system crosses our path.
For example, a system forcing us to memorize the answers instead of helping us to wonder.
Can we regain the excitement after passing through these situations?
We are accustomed to systems that combine a group of rules and laws that decide what is valid and what isn't.
And, the people that are not able to adapt to those systems end up being rejected.
Can we create a system that adapts to the characteristics of individuals and that allows them to develop to their full potential?
How?
During these 14 years as an educator, I have realized that it is easier than it seems.
First, we have to create situations of exception.
Small gestures that take us out of the routine and that allow us to transform a bland space into a magical space where the unexpected can occur.
For example, fill a school for architecture with trash and promote a competition for creative construction for a day.
Or, fill the Plaza del 2 de Mayo in Madrid with plastic beach balls.
We could even fill the courtyard of the Conde Duque close to this location, with secondhand clothing.
We were surprised when people began dressing up in the clothes, then began tying the clothes together to make giant ropes so they could play jump rope.
For hours, the courtyard of Conde Duque became a playground.
It doesn't have to be that fancy.
Sometimes, it is enough to hang a swing on a road in São Paulo in order to feel weightlessness.
But wait! Let's not confuse
wild spaces with domesticated ones.
Let's not confuse the zoo with the jungle, the offices of Google with a workshop, or television with the Internet.
We look for magical spaces, ones that excite us, surprise us, stimulate us, but also where we can act, create, and be empowered.
Spaces where we can work on universal values in a natural way.
For example, we can talk about gender while we build clouds with secondhand bags and cartons for the city of Cairo.
We can also talk about racial integration while we build an ocean out of plastic that acknowledges the ecological problems on the coast of South Africa.
We don't have to go that far.
In southern Madrid, in San Cristóbal de los Ángeles, a group of neighbors, seniors, children, elderly, youth, adults, get together to recognize their abilities while they recover an abandoned area and transform it into a cultural and educational center.
Can we translate, transform these wild spaces and make them organized spaces?
In a study done in 30 schools in Catalonia, it was found that a playground was one of the few, if not the only, place designed exclusively for the recreation and enjoyment of children that is also found within an educational center.
What's more, the children spent some 30 minutes per day in that playground, which is some 525 hours more per year than what is spent in courses or subjects like Citizenship Education or Foreign Language.
In other words, recess time has become a very important space for a student's education in regulated educational centers.
This same study also asked: How is it that, in the last 90 years, these playgrounds have just barely changed?
Can we transform domestic spaces into magical spaces?
Can we do it together?
Students, teachers, fathers, mothers, administration... How would we do it?
What do we need?
I would follow a bit in the footsteps of the A-Team.
All around us, we have almost everything we need.
For example, in a refugee camp in Palestine, in Suf, Jordan, we got together for three days to reuse canvas and tires to build a recreation zone and a shaded area.
In Addis Ababa, in Ethiopia, we had a week to convert that space into a play area with an outdoor classroom.
In Niger, in Niamey, a group of volunteers from these cultural centers got together to learn how to design and build the first public playground in the country, and, without any previous experience, transformed the area where they used to burn waste into a play area.
The simple morphological transformation of courtyards is not enough.
The educational model has to be present.
We cannot miss the golden opportunity to develop and implement abilities that are usually stuck in the corners.
For example, multiple intelligences, shared responsibility, collaborative teams, and we have been doing this during the last few years in schools in Madrid.
Transform courtyards with these values.
We have done it from Las Tablas to Caño Roto.
If you notice, all these examples that I am giving have one thing in common: they are abstract objects.
Because, at the end of the day, a hoop makes you play basketball, and a goal makes you play soccer.
But, a 25,000 liter barrel... makes you think.
(Laughter) A lot!
And it makes you imagine.
And it makes you wonder.
If you asked me now where would I lock up the A-Team to keep them captive for life, I would not say a garage.
I would rather say laboratories, offices, classrooms, even a class at Harvard.
The thing is, the dinner conversations left something in my mind that I have never been able to forget.
And that is: as educators, we have the obligation to dream.
And to create spaces where everyone has the opportunity to be their best selves.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Hello! Excuse me.

This has to coincide
with the picture. Good.
Should I put it on?
Yes... There it is!
Ten years after I graduated as a mechanical engineer, I started taking an interest in another kind of knowledg I call cross-functional knowledge or lateral knowledge.
I believe that the easiest way to describe this kind of knowledge is actually by what they are not rather than what they are.
And specially cross-functional knowledge are things which are not related with our formal education, right?
It's the direct opposite of specialization or expertise in the knowledge one possesses.
So, it is said cross-functional knowledge is the source of creative thinking or as it is called nowadays, the necessary development for creative intelligence.
As I said, at the same time I began studying these two fields, I took an interest in Human Resources management in companies, and in baking.
At least a year after that I graduated as baking master with, I would say, respectable results.
So from that moment on, when I introduce myself in professional meetings as "Néstor Freddo, engineer."
They say, "Oh, that's great!
What's your branch?"

I say, "Baking, I am a baking engineer."
And clearly... this situation is somewhat funny, am I right?
Because I am actually so convinced of my title that I repeatedly visited my university so they would validate it, but it seems the Ministry of Education are still confused by my request, right?
So, why does this happen?
Because engineers carry a stigma, a "label" that says our heads are square.
Sort of like this.
So I am so worried about this stigma that, what do I do?
Everyday I try to make myself rounder.
So I describe myself as an engineer with a square head, yes, but that has rounded edges.
And the way to file the corners is precisely through cross-functional knowledge.
The acquisition of cross-functional knowledge is not so much a journey but an expedition. An expedition into unknown territories.
It's an uncomfortable journey. Why?
Because we are leaving our academic comfort zone.
Don't forget we become ignorant once again.
And essentially, what is the point?
That we are questioning a tradition, especially in educational matters, that say, "You have to become more of a specialist or enhance the knowledge you already have."
So what I want to share with you now is specifically the surprises I had while I was studying these two new fields of cross-functional knowledge. I started finding coincidences.
I said, "It's amazing how all this works."
So let me tell you,
when you study mechanical engineering one of the raw materials, one of the main ingredients, a fundamental tool for the engineer, specially the mechanical, is iron.
Iron has a very special characteristic, specially pure iron, that depends on the type of crystallization system it has, which can be the "Alpha" or the "Gamma" system. It has different properties, for instance being magnetic or not,
that is to say if it is attracted by a magnet or not.
This is explained in a very hostile graphic something like this, which between us and I hope nobody finds out. I still haven't understood completely.
So I said, "well, when I went to the baking school they told me, "We also have a basic ingredient, raw material, an irreplaceable product."
"Amazing!" I said.
So they showed me this.
This is cocoa seeds, which when processed correctly are turn into chocolate, and one thing we do with chocolate is this: an Easter egg.
What surprised me was that to turn chocolate into an Easter egg, we use a process called chocolate tempering.
This consists in transforming all the chocolate into some sort of crystals, sounds familiar?
In this case it is the Beta crystals.
And chocolate's Beta crystals have three particular properties: firstly, they make chocolate crunchy; secondly, they make it shiny, and thirdly, and most importantly, it melts at 35ºC. This way it is not only solid at room temperature, but you can also pick it up with your fingers and it won't melt, right?
Also you can consume it and your organism, at 37ºC can digest it. So I thought that, no doubt, there is a relation between these two fields of knowledge.
But there were two pieces of evidence that are purely scientific. I had to use all scientific procedures to demonstrate it and come to certify this relation.
When was that?
When the engineering university taught me to design this and in the baking school I came across this.
I was extremely surprised.
And the last and most important piece of evidence was when at university I learnt how to design this,
and suddenly, in the baking school they taught me this. I said, "OK, that's it."
The conclusion is indisputable.
So, well... I decided to give a step forward in these knowledge fields, in this knowledge crossing. I said, "Is there a relation between bread baking, a bakery specialty, and Human Resources management in companies?"
Specially in leadership and teamwork.
Let me tell you.
When I knead bread dough, I need three fundamental ingredients: flour, yeast, and water.
Flour primarily comes from grinding a cereal called wheat so it can be transformed into flour. And flour contains something very particular, a protein called gluten, which inside the dough forms a sort of multidimensional web. If, for example, you slice the bread, do you see those small, defined lines?
OK, imagine those lines are the tridimensional web formed by gluten.
This is very important because it's what gives way to the dough fermentation process.
The second primary ingredient or fundamental ingredient is yeast.
I believe yeast is a product half magic and half mysterious.
It is a living organism that comes from the fungi family and in conditions of an adequate temperature and with the right additives, produces carbon dioxide, which is absorbed by the gluten web and allows the dough to rise and swell, right?
And the third ingredient, the most simple one but not less important, is water.
Simply water, but why?
To integrate all the other ingredients, right?
The right amount at the right temperature form a dough that is both soft and firm.
With regards processes, there are three: Kneading, which is the integration of the raw materials I just mentioned; the rising, which is the fermentation of the dough, and the baking, the transformation of the dough into bread. So one of the many occasions in which I was making bread I said, "There has to be a relation here as well!"
So I began investigating.
Our baking master must be able to combine the ingredients to form the gluten web, right?
So I have to ask: isn't the leader's roll precisely to combine the team members?
And that gluten web they have to create, does it not mirror a web of interpersonal ties, links between the members of a company?
So I said, "First similarity,"
while I kept on kneading I found another effects of gluten.
Gluten has a very particular quality, the more you work on it, the more elastic and resistant the dough becomes. What does this mean?
That when one wants to shape the piece, it goes back to its original state.
So, what does the baking master do?
He simply leaves the dough to rest between 20 and 30 minutes, and when the dough has rested long enough it can be shaped in whatever way necessary.
I have to ask, "Have you ever heard
about that famous subject of 'overcoming change' in companies?
When teams adopt new tasks or they have to take on new roles."
So I asked myself, "Should we do the same as the baking master and let the team rest in order to assimilate these new roles and obligations to then adopt the new ones?"
Let's move on to yeast.
I believe that the effects yeast has on the dough are the most similar motivations in teamwork. What does this mean?
Motivation is all those experiences, expectations, dreams, illusions, projects that somehow need to be in a team.
And how do we achieve this?
It can be achieved as long as the leader creates that web of interpersonal connections, you can't have one without the other.
What does this mean?
That even if I have motivation, without the "web" that motivation leaves the dough, leaves the team. And vice versa, even if I have a web, without motivation the dough doesn't rise, the team doesn't grow and develop.
Finally, the task of the baking master, once the dough achieved its adequate state, is to bake it so that these effects can't be altered with time.
And in our particular case, for the leader, this will be the moment in which we have this bread, in which we have a team that is clearly cohesive, strong, and highly effective.
I want to tell you about the three ways in which you can apply cross-functional knowledge.
The first one is the one we have talked about, it is the analogies.
Analogies establish similarities between things that in the beginning seem very different.
Analogies are very strong,
because they link simple, every-day processes, like baking bread, with a complex scenario, such as Human Resources tasks inside a company.
They are a lot stronger when they link fields of knowledge that are very different, and a lot more memorable when the examples are original and the scenarios simple and basic.
Another thing in which we can use cross-functional knowledge is for problem solving. How?
If the analogy is correctly explained we can think of a solution in the simple scenario and then apply it on the complex one.
Haven't we just done it when we said: don't we have to let the dough rest? To then apply it in the complex scenario, don't we have to let the team assimilate the new conditions?
And, lastly, what I call, a "contagious effect."
The contagious effect of analogies thanks to our cross-functional knowledge, is precisely to turn the enthusiasm for what you do into passion for those things we are obliged to do. So, as a conclusion, I invite you to embark on this journey, this expedition into unknown territories, to not be afraid of this, because I guarantee you it is almost always productive and always fun.
And if you are afraid, don't forget you were told by the "baking engineer."
Thank you very much! (Applause)
When I was 12 years old, Elena, my literature professor, asked the students in my class to prepare a story, to tell a story about something that we really liked.
Some chose movies, others chose TV shows, some rock band, things like that.
I, in that moment, already wanted to be a magician.
I really liked magicians and stories about circuses.
I prepared a story about magic and circus arts.
At the same time, I was going to an educational psychologist that helped me pay a little more attention in school because I was a bit disperse.
(Laughter) There was also another alternative to that which was a pill that at a time was given to kids that didn’t pay attention, and it made people pay attention to those things society wanted them to pay attention to.
Luckily, they didn’t choose that and they sent me to Liliana’s.
That’s where I went. To learn how
to pay a bit more of attention at school, which wasn’t going completely well.
If I had taken the pill back then, now I’d be the sort of person that drinks very dark coffee, with no sugar, and is angry at the entire world because they don’t act the way he wants them to and Messi doesn’t play hard enough, and all those things.
(Laughter) Luckily that didn’t happen.
I was at Liliana’s.
Liliana helped me concentrate a bit more at school and I told her I had to write this sort of story about magic and that I wanted to finish the lesson with a magic trick, the most decent trick I had, at the moment.
I didn’t have the guts.
In reality, it scared me a bit.
And her, with the sweetness that characterized her with her love and tranquility, told me: -Don’t be a wimp, do the trick, if nothing happens, it’s just a trick, you do the trick, it’s a nice finishing touch.
I brought myself to do it, told the story, showed my empty hands showed that a handkerchief appeared from my hand, put it back, showed my empty hands again, showed again that the handkerchief appeared and my classmates and teacher clapped.
(Applause) I did very well on that class day, and got a very good mark, and Elena was amazed by what I had done and said to me: -Look, in five months there’s a literary competition and in the closing night the school is hosting an event.
We’d like you to do a magic show.
Do you have a show prepared?
-Obviously, I said.
It was obvious, I was 12 years old, I didn’t have a prepared show, but I did have five months to actually prepare that show.
And in that show, I mixed music, magic, and comedy, which were the things I liked the most.
I realized that it wasn’t that I had a hard time paying attention to things, but that I didn’t want to pay attention to geography, math, physics, basically.
That what I liked was to pay attention to other things.
I was very scared.
The day of the show came.
I was feeling very confident, because when I was younger, at eight I played the drums in a jazz band and did scat singing
(Scat singing) Very strange for eight year olds, but that’s what we did in Bahia Blanca, where I lived, there was a jazz band and I played the drums, sang scat, and was the presenter.
But the day of the show, just before coming out, those five months passed, the day of the show came.
In a very dark place of myself I wished a catastrophe happened, that the theatre exploded and killed everyone inside, (Laughter) even my mum and dad, who were there watching me, just not to do that show.
The time came, they said: -Ladies and gentlemen, Radagast.
I came out to perform and it came out great.
It came out okay, I’m not saying it was... It was a nice show, really.
A 12-year-old showed up, did some magic, comedy, and some music that I included because I really liked music.
People asked for my business card after the show.
Business cards, I say this because you’re all very young, were pieces of cardboard with your name on them.
The equivalent to today’s contacts in your phone or the handles in social networks.
Damn millennials.
(Laughter) I had business cards, I had a lot of confidence, I was 13 years old but to be honest, at 13 years old one is very confident.
I got business cards printed out.
They said Radagast, below it said comedic magic and my house phone which was 49018.
I handed out the cards and soon enough my house phone started ringing.
They started to hire me to perform at events, kindergardens, then schools, parties, churches, weddings.
I started to work a lot.
I was very young but I worked a lot.
All the money I won I invested it back in some course I could take, buy tricks remotely.
There was no internet to study through the internet.
In Bahia Blanca, there were no magicians and I started buying things, I started doing shows and such.
I started working lots, but I started to get bored because it was always the same thing: I made the jokes that worked, the tricks I knew, I didn’t have much more tools than those, I couldn’t study magic.
So, my very wise parents told me: -Why don’t you take some theatre class or some other thing that will
nurture you and such?
I started. This was some time ago.
I started the theatre class and a friend showed up, a classmate, not a friend, a classmate with juggling cubs.
Something clicked. I said:
-I want to learn how to juggle, I want to learn how to juggle, I want to learn how to juggle, I want to learn how to juggle... I want to learn how to juggle.
The kid realized that I was a bit too insistent.
And put me in touch with some guys from Rosario that were in Bahia Blanca teaching some juggling classes and busking for them.
They had just come to Bahia.
I invited them home. I told them:
-You don’t have a place to live?
You can stay home, my folks won’t have a problem.
I took them home.
One was a Rastafari and another one had half of his face tattooed, I swear you by my mother.
They were in the living room teaching me how to juggle, my mum comes in and says: - Who are these guys?
I go to the kitchen and I tell her: -Mum, they’re the juggling teachers that are going to be teaching me how to juggle and are staying at home for some days.
-Perfect.
So, they stayed for some days.
They taught me how to juggle with clubs, with a diabolo, with balls, I learned how to spit fire and it blowed my mind, I couldn’t believe it.
I would go to stoplight, and I would do some juggling and spit fire with my friends.
We’d put up the show in the street
and collect donations. I started to mix that with some magic tricks I knew.
The show started to grow.
It was great, I was mixing things on the way, it was really awesome and I kept making money to re-invest in what I liked doing.
I started to get bored again.
It started to become automatic again and just in that moment, my folks with much love and generosity, told me: -Are you going to stay here?
Why don’t you go somewhere else and see how it goes?
To Buenos Aires.
Someone could interpret this like they were kicking me out.
Maybe, but no, they were inviting me to dare do something else.
I moved up here, to Buenos Aires.
I told you guys I get distracted a lot, right?
Sorry. I’m imagining there’s
a ninja turtle and a Teletubbie next to me dancing.
That’s why I’m get a bit distracted.
(Applause) I moved to Buenos Aires, started taking classes in different things, clown, pantomime, more juggling, acrobatics and more.
I was waiting for the phone to ring, that phone nobody had the number of because I’d just gotten to Buenos Aires, to do events in Buenos Aires.
There was no phone, so I’d go to the cyber café in the corner of my place and send emails to every producer or event organizer I could think of so I could keep doing my shows and live on my own in Buenos Aires.
In reality, it wasn’t me sending the emails, but my representative, who was actually me, (Laughter) but I’d sign off with another name.
I thought it was so fun that it was some other guy.
(Laughter) To me it was much easier to sell someone else’s act than mine: -You’ve no idea of this show I do, you’ve got to hire me!
And the guy would say: -No, you don’t know what this guy does!
No, he’s amazing!
Nobody has seen him yet in Buenos Aires!
I’d just gotten here, it was obvious.
Nobody called me for a long while.
Emails would bounce back, or they’d say: -The truth is the market is already collapsed, no, no.
Until I caught one off guard and he hired me.
From that show came others and I started to work again in Buenos Aires, looking for new things, new quirks and that sort.
My colleagues weren’t fans of the act, because in reality, I didn’t really prepare my shows the way magicians usually do as I had never received proper training.
I thought it up from the side of comedy.
So, my colleagues didn’t love the idea of a guy doing magic but that screamed and wore clown shoes, that sometimes sung, that threw juggling clubs or spit fire.
Therefore, I wasn’t well accepted by my colleagues, but as I had my representative that told me: -Everything’s fine. I kept on going.
I just kept doing my thing.
At 22, my girlfriend at that time tells me she’s pregnant with Bianca.
My daughter Bianca is born.
I kept working as a magician, travelled abroad a lot.
There’s something I really like that’s serendipity.
Serendipity is the word that defines when a fortunate event happens to you without you expecting it while you’re looking for something else, as long as your spirit is prepared for new things to happen.
When’s someone’s searching and searching and searching serendipities happen that can make you change course.
If I have a clear course to go from here to there and I only look up there, up there, and only up there, a lot of things are going to happen around me and I won’t realize it.
In one of those serendipities I had, a foreign producer got in touch with me, I started working in Colombia, in Peru, in Venezuela, lots of things.
I was a father, I should’ve stayed in that place of comfort, where I had events and everything.
A really good opportunity came up, to be the clown… You guys know that clown that looks like It, that eats a lot of burgers?
He has some restaurants, he’s doing really well.
I went through five auditions.
The last call I told them: -I’m not a fan of this, I’d rather keep doing my own thing- I didn’t want to be a 9 to 5 clown, to be honest.
It helped me realize what I really wanted to do, which was to keep playing.
I told them no and I kept doing my thing, believing it was going to be alright.
And really things went along fine, but I was getting bored again.
It happened to me that I wanted to play with something, I needed new tools, I started looking for new things, I wasn’t feeling very comfortable.
I did a season in Villa Gesell, that I shared out of chance with a guy that was like a guru to me that showed up in my life, that made me... (Sound effect) And I know a lot more of sound effects to, the vacuum cleaner... (Vacuum cleaner) (Pigeon cooing) (Feline roaring) And I know a lot more (Applause)
And it kind of calmed my soul because I was really unsatisfied with what I was doing because I had heard those colleagues I really admired tell me: -But you’re a clown, not a magician not a comedian, not a musician, so what in hell are you?
Blah, blah, blah.
And he told me: -You’re Radagast, focus on being Radagast and focus on being the best Radagast you can be.
What do you want to do tonight?
I was doing shows in that place, in Villa Gesell, that was a restaurant that had a theatre inside.
I say: -Look, I’d like to do this.
I show them what I did in stoplights in Bahia Blanca when I was a kid.
I saw: -But this isn’t good, I don’t know…
-No, no, you have to do that, and look, let’s add it this and let’s do it with a background music you like.
It was doing something completely different to what I had been doing.
That night something happened that was similar to when I did the trick for Elena, or when I did that first show, or when my folks told me: -Are you gonna stay here?
I heard that again and went out to do the scene the routine I had prepared.
To this day I still do that routine, with some changes in the way.
But that night, when I came out to the stage, my soul exploded.
The applause I received must’ve been the most powerful I’d even heard before, not because it had been stronger than others, or before the applause had been very powerful, but I felt it very powerful because I felt I was back playing, doing what I really liked doing, which is to play on the stage without any sort of rules.
I felt very happy.
I was truly satisfied.
That’s where everything changed again, I started to play again, to sing again, I started putting on the kind of shoes I wanted to, I stopped listening to the masters who said: -No, no, no, no, no, no.
And I kept doing my thing trying each day to be the best Radagast.
A while ago, once again by serendipity, three years ago I met a Fernanda, my girlfriend, and she immediately realized I was unsatisfied, because time went by, and I got bored again of what I was doing and said: -Why don’t you start studying something that you’ve never done before?
Why don’t you learn standup comedy?
-Standup comedy?
No, that thing with a guy with a mic that says: "you saw it...?"
No, no, no way.
No, I don’t like it, I don’t like it.
-But it’s because you don’t know it because you’re ignorant.
Go study standup comedy.
-No, no.
-Go study standup comedy.
I signed you up for a standup comedy class.
I went to study standup comedy.
-Yes, my love.
And I went to study standup comedy.
It blew my mind again.
Obviously, I was trying out a tool I didn’t know well yet, it was like a new Batman toy, to take out, to play with.
I started meeting lots of people, I started getting in touch with others, I took another theatre class, some dancing lessons I started to sing again, I started to dare to do again, and in the way I met Federico Cyrulnik, an Instagrammer I really admire, and he told me: -You have to play with Instagram, start playing with Instagram.
-You think so?
-Play with Instagram.
When I started playing with Instagram, my head was blown again.
I didn’t have a schedule or rules, I could do what I wanted to, I could play just how I wanted to.
I started to bring back a lot of stuff from when I was a kid in the carpentry workshop of my grandfather, for example (Horn), something I did in my grandpa’s yard, or I’d start to play an invisible flute, what I did with my brother when he played the double bass and I didn’t know what to do so I’d do-- (Flute) and played the flute along.
I started to play again with my daughter, in reality, to bring back all those games I did all the time with my daughter, with my folks or with my friends, I started to play again and I started to feel really happy again.
That Instagram account also brought a bunch of things.
It allowed me to begin a tour all throughout the country and also abroad, which I had always wanted and dreamt about.
Everything happened.
I’m fortunate enough to be often asked by journalists, when I’m doing interviews, if I feel I’m in my best moment, and I always answer that yes, because I have always been in my best moment, because I always understood as the best moment all that was going on for me, from putting up posters with my friends with glue, so that people would come see us in the theatre, or when we’d go to a corner to juggle and spit fire, or today acting in a theatre, or today giving a TED talk here to a bunch of people that I don’t know why they’re making so much silence and listening me with so much attention, I thank you very much.
(Laughter) (Applause) In the field I work in, there’s always a damn word going around, “success,” and to me, success isn’t where you reach, what I told you about before, to say -I want to be there.
To me success is all the way you go through and all that happens on it.
If you do devote yourself to what you love completely, and this doesn’t mean that you’re always going to succeed, things will go badly thousands of times, and this has to happen so you can learn and keep on going forward.
In fact, in a festival a while ago I was yelled at by 150 Colombians because I made a very unfortunate joke and I was declared an undesirable person in the world of magic.
(Laughter) I carry that title, ladies and gentlemen. (Applause) When I was younger, 500 people stood up and asked for their money back.
I had made a conceptual act of some Martians that kidnapped me and took me to a very dark planet and I had to do magic tricks and comedy for them to set me free.
It wasn’t a good show. (Laughter).
All the media on Bahia Blanca had me on them and read: "Radagast screwed it up."
(Laughter) But what was going on with me?
If I didn’t do what wanted in life and devoted myself to doing my thing, the next day, after failing or having a problem, I’d think again: what a bummer, this thing I’m doing!
My job is a piece of crap.
Instead, the day after failing with the Martians or in Colombia, the next day I woke up and kept loving what I did.
Another question they ask to me sometimes is: -And now that you’ve made it?
I haven’t made it.
I haven’t made it at all.
Being in more theatres or being better known isn’t making it.
To me, making it is grabbing two chips, go gambling and winning at the casino.
That’s making it rain from above.
And to me, the only things made from above are holes and poop, those also come from above. (Laughter)
I’ve been going since I was 12 and I love doing what I do to play and to play for a living.
I love to play, but sometimes I forget, but luckily, I’ve a lot of people by my side, my ninja turtles, who are my daughter, my folks, my girlfriend, my friends, or my work team, who, when I forget, tell me: -Play, Rada, play.
So please accept my apologies if I leave playing.
(Applause) When I was much younger, when I saw my parents eating with their friends, I, instead of toothpicks stuck on charcuterie, imagined they were swords for my Rambos.
Or when I was a bit scared at night I’d meet up with Batman to chat and he’d pass along some tips.
Or when I saw the snowballs, that you shake them and the snow inside moves, I imagined I got inside.
I like, with my acts, to bring back that kid who is still looking at the world of adults.
Thank you very much.
(Scat singing) (Applause)
