(Music) (Humming) (Applause) Thank you.
[Explicit content]
Hello, hello, do you hear me?
I am very nervous, really. Moments ago some people were like:
"Are you nervous?"; "Yes! I am nervous!"
But why? You have 800,000 plus followers and you make radio broadcasts.
Yes, but in the end, 'Sopitas' is just human. We are people and that's the story I want to share today.
Many times we just see a face or hear a voice, but we don't know the story behind all that we perceive.
My personal story, that of 'Sopitas', me, began in 1980 when my mom... No, it starts before; the first moments I remember of me as a kid is when I'm taken to school by car. I remember listening to the car's radio, I listened to Martín Hernández, and thought: "That guy lives inside that little box!" I was 5, I imagined that people lived there, that those voices on the radio were of people who were talking to me, telling me stuff and making me feel something.
How cool!
At 8, I remember watching
the 1988 Olympic Games in Seul, I watched the commentators Toño Valdés and Pépe Segarra, - there was no cable TV back then,
just open TV, and by watching these two guys I felt like:
"Cool! I want to be there one day."
They are at the greatest sports event, they are getting paid for working. I decided I wanted to be there one day.
But life goes on, and you reach that moment in life when everyone starts to ask you:
"Son, what will you be when you grow up?"
For 15 years of your life you dream of becoming a doctor, an accountant, a firefighter, a policeman... Well, RobotiX kids are way ahead on that decision. (Laughter) And I felt like: "What will I do? What am I going to do?"
I truly didn't know. Then something happened. Something that unfortunately we will all face sooner or later: I was 15, and my dad suddenly died.
I felt lost, somehow abandoned, and thinking: "Darn! What am I going to do?"
Now I only have my mother and my sister and now I am supposed to be the breadwinner.
And it is like, "How am I going to I do that?"
And once again, I found the radio, which I had never left, it had been a constant in my life, but at that moment it turned into more of a key player. I started to realize that there were many things I liked: music, ideas, responsibility. So when in high school I was given an assignment to go to the place you'd like to work at,
I said: "Ah! I'll go to Radioactivo."
So I went to Radioactivo... Yes, ooh! Long ago. (Laughter)
I went there and what struck me the most was that everything you listen to on the radio, now in headphones, that sounds powerful, gigantic,
like, "Wow, this mega promo,"
was made in a 3 by 3 meter room and what amazed me the most is that it was made by people. People like you and I, who liked music, who felt the music the same way I did. We all found a very special common thread.
I said, "This is where I want to be."
I came back the next day, and the day after, and then again, until someone asked me - I don't know if out of pity, desperation, or a way to get rid of me - "Would you like to work with us?"
"Yes, yes, please, me, me!"
So I got into Radioactivo in 1997. I was 17, and began as a promotion assistant, then I took charge of all the promotional strategies.
It is now very common to see on the streets these vans giving out stuff.
We already did that back in 1997, the first Mexican radio station to do so.
I was in charge of designing these strategies.
Afterwards, in the year 2000, someone was irresponsible enough to give me a morning radio show, along with friends from the radio station, Cha, Julio, Rulo, and we started a show called "El Mañanero," until 2004.
From 1997 to 2004, my life was limited to Radioactivo. I was there since 7 a.m. when the show started, and totally busy until 8 or 9 p.m.; that's how it was.
Somehow I think, I was able to distract myself and start building something by myself, with my own energy, interest, and tastes, above all.
In 2004, the station was sold, and the new owner said: "I don't like music, I want this to be a news station."
Overnight, Radioactivo disappears.
My second tough loss in life. Life, after all, begins to be defined by the collection of small losses we experience, and the learnings we gain from each of them.
Suddenly, all you've done for 7 years, from 7 am to 8 pm, disappears. It's gone, you are empty-handed, in a limbo.
It was then when, subconsciously surely, sopitas.com was born. At 24, I said to myself: "I can't afford losing again something I have dedicated so much time, care and effort.
I want something I can defend, at least, try to respond back before it's taken away.
I started to hang out with friends, and they told me I should create a website, What for? I didn't see the point.
Actually, I was scared by technology, maybe someone will upload dubious pics... By 2006, I was writing soccer columns for a newspaper called Record, and the newspaper told me: "We want you to go cover the World Cup in Germany."
Oh, man, my dream! I am going to the World Cup, for free, three months, and I am even getting paid!
But then I thought of me alone in Germany for three months, how will I keep in touch with my friends? to share the experience and the feeling of being in a World Cup, I mean, not many people are that lucky.
That is when I decided to create sopitas.com. It started it as a joke, a very personal thing as every blog is, yes, we did uploaded pictures of the drunk friend, the friend who was cheating on his girlfriend...
We started making fun of each other. Yet I also shared the experience of being at a World Cup, what was heard, seen and felt.
That kind of things, opposed to traditional media, and its very well planned coverages.
So if something was going on with the national team, why the TV guy wasn't saying a thing? Then I said, if he doesn't, I'll write about it.
In fact the website started as a personal and independent space.
What we realized as time went by, was that there were many others like me, like when I had started listening and working in Radioactivo, who shared many interests with me.
I uploaded music, records, concert pictures, and movies.
Until it was 2012, when this student phenomenon called "Yo soy 132" happened. Probably you all remember.
So this happens, and traditional media starts saying: "those weren't students, but guys payed to be there."
But how so?
So we took over the task and decided that if it's OK to talk about football, and it's OK to talk about movies and TV, but when something very serious is going on, both with our country and our youth
- sorry for using that word -
but when something was going on there, traditional media was trying to bury. They were trying to deny it as if it was not happening.
So as an independent media, during such social events, we have the responsibility to tell what is going on, and what people really care about.
I don't know when the media decided news will be all about their agenda, and what they had to say, when, from my point of view, media is supposed to talk about what people care about, media should give people a voice.
At that moment, on our site, - I say 'our' because, though I am Sopitas, over time, we have created a family, a team to nourish one another - in 2012, we searched for those students to film them and report: "These are real students, with ID, and what they really want is to show their dissent." (Applause) Afterwards, more things would come, that up to this day continue to amaze me. Suddenly, one day you get a phone call,
"Hey, the Foo Fighters want you to.."
What? The Foo Fighters know about me?
Or some guy that happened to be in Cairo who said: "Thanks to you I watched the match, I was stranded in the Egyptian desert, but I watched the Champions League through your link." I was amazed!
Then one day I had to interview a movie actress.
"Are you the Sopitas from sopitas.com?"
Yes, that one.
"Ah! The other day I was
with such and such, then Ricky Martin called, and I told him, you have to watch this video."
At that moment I thought, "How on earth Ricky Martin ends up checking out my website? (Applause) We are extreme opposites.
But well, those are the nice ones.
Recently, as you may know, with the ongoing discussions on the federal reforms... Again, traditional media are praising the reforms, saying everything is wonderful and convenient because your electric bills will be reduced, And you might ponder over:
"OK, I'll pay less,"
but there are many hidden things no one bothered in explaining. I, a 30-something guy who didn't go to school, what is preponderance? what does it do?
We decided we had to translate all these concepts so that people could decide whether it was OK or not to pay less.
Maybe it's better for us to keep paying what we pay and not give up on other things.
We published that content, and it became a global trending topic.
About 300 million viewers worldwide.
It was insane, to the extent that one day, while you are goofing around, picking your nose, you get a call from someone saying: "Hello, we are from the President's office," and you say, "Shit!"
" Everything you are saying is wrong and we'd like to request..." - Funnily enough, the Presidency never requests for things - "We want to request a rebuttal time."
OK, it's OK that they at least those who are making the laws,
actually explain us what they are about. We filmed a video where all Secretaries said what they had to say, - I wasn't convinced at all, and I believe many others didn't believe it either -
but it is still a good example of how ideas become relevant, even those you and your friends started. And when those ideas transcend, you start to realize: first, how bad traditional media are, because they have lost their way, and say, "We don't care about politics agenda, we care about what is happening to us as a society,
and we have to give people a voice."
So, today, I am here, like the rest of the speakers, if you think about it, throughout the day we have been listening about the lives of great people, associations efforts, everything born from an idea.
Sopitas started as an idea, a joke, and then you begin connecting with friends, you begin to create a community, you begin to realize that not only you give a voice, but you have one of your own and that you have to use it.
Thank you.
(Applause) Adelina Filigrana: Sorry Sopitas, you interview everyone,
now it's my turn, how cool!
You invented your own job?
Sopitas: Yes, it was an accident, and I think a very lucky one, but it was born from listening to ourselves which we often forget, and we let others pollute us with their opinions.
We care too much about what people say.
I explain it with an example, let's see, all of you have notebooks, do you have your notebooks?
Make a drawing of the person next to you.
Nobody is doing it, why?
Because you are saying, "Man, I am the worst at drawing, what will they think."
And that's the point, when we are kids we are not embarrassed because we have imagination, and we don't care what others say about it.
When we grow up, we get polluted by what people think. So instead of expressing ideas or carrying them out, we say: "No, it's best not, I wanted to do this but I'll do something else, because that is what my parents or friends expect me to do."
At the end of the day, who we are and what we do is a personal decision, one that mistakenly, we put in other people's hands.
(Applause)
Immigrants have no shame.
They steal our jobs.
Overweight people are lazy and undisciplined.
Homosexuals are perverts, and they are
a bad influence for our children.
How uncomfortable, no?
I'm discriminating out loud.
And it's uncomfortable.
But this uncomfortable feeling is a seed.
A seed that demonstrates a social agreement among us that recognizes that discrimination causes harm.
Poor people are poor because they don't want to work.
Jews are dirty. And they steal.
How much harm is caused
by discrimination?
As a family, we found out. By surprise, and decades after the fact, but we found out.
My grandparents were Hungarian, and they emigrated to the USA before the Second World War.
Meanwhile, their siblings arrived to Córdoba, Argentina, from Hungary before and after the Second World War.
When questions arose about our family history, the only person left who could answer these questions was my grandfather's brother who lived here in Córdoba.
I came here in 1994 to speak with him.
Today, we are going to call him János.
From János we learned that we were Jewish.
My grandfather hid it in the USA to protect us.
János, here in Córdoba, did the same.
His children and grandchildren grew up unaware of their heritage.
Why?
Because János learned in the most definitive way how much harm discrimination can cause.
In World War Two, János' son, an infant, was murdered because he was Jewish. In his wife's arms. János' sister was also murdered,
with her two children.
Boys aged three and seven.
Along with his brother-in-law, his father- in-law, his other niece, and neighbors.
Every one of them transformed into smoke in concentration camps.
We had no idea.
As a species, how is it possible that we are capable of such atrocities?
How is it possible that they continue to happen even today?
Is there anything that I, or we, can do to revert this?
These are questions I visit every day.
And with these questions, I always recall a memory my great-uncle shared with me -- one of the many gifts he bestowed me: In the spring of 1944, the last year of the war, they began to march the Jews from the ghetto where they were enclosed to the train station.
They did not know where they were going.
Despite the rumors, they could not imagine what destiny awaited them.
When they crossed the familiar city center, they saw a ghost town.
No one dared to come outside.
If you were a Jewish sympathizer, your life was at risk.
Either you discriminated, or you died.
My family members were among the last group marching out of the ghetto that spring.
Despite the silent streets, someone walked with them.
Despite the risk to his life, he walked with them.
An older man, a Christian, a neighbor who had been a friend of our family.
He could not raise his voice.
He could not save the lives of his friends who were different.
But he could be a witness.
This was his seed; and he planted it where he was, in the moment in history that was his to live.
It is the same seed that makes us uncomfortable even today when we hear or see discrimination.
How much harm is caused by discrimination?
In the camps, side by side with the Jews, the homosexuals, the Gypsies, the disabled, communists, any kind of sympathizer—all were murdered.
All of them transformed into smoke.
Today, 73 years later, the seed our neighbor planted lives on.
And it shows us how much we have evolved.
But it also demonstrates that we continue to harm others, as individuals, and as a society.
The Muslims are terrorists.
People from the USA are brutes, and racist.
Is that so? What do we achieve
by perpetuating the jokes?
By not looking at ourselves?
More questions I visit each day.
If my great-uncle could not go to the grave with our family's history, neither could I.
So I wrote a book to honor all that he returned to us as a family, to honor our magical time together. And also to illustrate these ceaseless questions of mine.
I was convinced that I created the book for personal reasons.
Some teachers in Córdoba taught me otherwise.
As soon as the book came to life, they wrote me to share that they were using the book in their classrooms.
The book has a very unusual format-- it's all color, a vibrant mix of testimonies, photos, maps, art.
And it's full of mystery.
Right away these teachers saw something in the book that I-- despite having been immersed in this project for more than 20 years-- never saw: unique educational material that is potent and versatile for teaching a wide variety of subjects in the classroom:
discrimination, identity, history, immigration, visual arts, ethics.
And they could explore these topics simultaneously in different classes.
I readily confess that I do not have the courage to be a school teacher.
But I do have the courage to listen to them.
Because for me, there is no force more potent for generating positive change in society than an inspired teacher.
And I began to wonder.
If just a couple teachers with a single copy of the book were so motivated by what happened in their classrooms that they wrote me to share the news, without knowing who I was, what would happen if a lot of teachers had several copies of the book?
There, I found the “why” of absolutely everything that happened to us as a family and to my great-uncle and me along the way.
I found our seed.
It is this.
How about a suitcase?
That is a traveling library, that travels
by bus. Free of charge for its users.
With this excitement, I reached out to people I knew, and I reached out to total strangers, to help me create the traveling library.
Through what are now various suitcases, we equip teachers with several copies of the book, a study guide, and a registry where each school shares its experience with the traveling library.
The seed grows and grows.
Each year via crowdfunding campaigns, individual sponsors from more than five countries and from all of Argentina extend their hands so that the traveling libraries —the suitcases— continue to travel to schools, museums and libraries throughout all of Argentina.
They travel non-stop, and they travel without cost.
Each place has the traveling library for one month.
The first year, we had just one suitcase. The second year, two suitcases, then three.
Now there are four.
Since they began traveling in July, 2013, they have been in the hands and minds of over 6,000 people.
They have visited over 150 schools.
The suitcases come and go, they arrive and they take off.
I'm the crazy lady at the bus station in Carlos Paz.
I pick them up and send them off again.
Suitcases, sponsors, students, teachers, books, buses.
Time and again in the registries that accompany the traveling libraries, and in the messages from the teachers, one can read and perceive that in the encounter, there is transformation.
There is empathy.
I was 20 when I met my great-uncle, and I was innocent and unaware enough so as to admit to him that I didn't have much hope that the human condition was going to improve.
How he yelled at me. He was not very tall.

And his light blue eyes were scary.

“What? How can you not be hopeful?
I have seen the end of the world,” he yelled.
“And it was not the end of the world.
I breathed every moment of the end, and it was not the end; so then how
can you, at your age, not be hopeful? You silly little girl!”
Truly, a very silly little girl.
Everyone János held dear, everyone
close to him was murdered. Everyone.
Despite that, he died in peace here in Córdoba, at 93.
Not without pain, but in peace.
So then how can we not follow his example?
How can we not believe that it is possible to illuminate the moment in history in which we live?
With encounters, with our gaze, with dialog, with ideas, with art, with music, in any way possible, wherever possible.
Planting seeds.
(Applause)
I bet you wondered where the accent was!
The journalist Hector de Mauleón, in a column he has on a local journal recalled the story of Madame Coussin, a French woman who in the mid-19th century wanted to open her business in Mexico City.
This woman shipped her belongings, opened various stores, and when the time came to brand her logo,
the sign read: "Madame Coussin, Paris slut."

Well, Hector de Mauleón also writes that half way through the 19th century, a geographer, Mr. Antonio García Cubas, City Council member at the time,
decided the time had come for a debate on public image and orthography.
He took matters into his own hands, and he himself started correcting language misuses in public banners.
But there was an anonymous citizen later known as José Meza, who said: "I want to participate, too many sluts from Paris awaiting correction, so, for the sake of it and free of charge I volunteer as the city corrector."
In his column, Mr. Mauleón clearly shows us that the project Lost Accents is not new at all.
Lost Accents was born in a publicity agency in Mexico City. After some chatters among peers
and out of the discomfort created by those clients who demanded perfection in our messages, didn't respect language themselves.
Which was obvious in the rest of their communication.
We chose an adhesive with the shape of an accent.
Why?
It was more subtle than placing a C over an S or placing a Z over a C.
It is raising awareness
of our own language, and that it is a matter of identity.
The accent was printed with one of six phrases corresponding to the main rules of accentuation.
Below, another phrase was written: if you write correctly, you look good.
But after a century,
we still find mistakes. Schools that write their values with grammar errors.
Public banners that seemed to be written by an early Spanish student, because all three words are missing their accent. I even had the bad luck of receiving this SMS from my mobile service supplier with three misspellings. Some worse than the rest.
What was our target?
The responsibility that, in my opinion, this big brands were not taking,
and the institutions undoing the job that other institutions had done in favor of language, values and identity.
That is why Lost Accents was born.
We were mostly thinking on those kids in their formative years and wondering how do they manage to learn in our cities and countries, with streets so full of traps.
This was one of the first actions,
we have a wide variety of examples.
This is how an accent is applied
when we can’t reach high places.
We found invitations to read better
that failed to teach any reading skills.
You name it. A political party created by teachers that promised a misspelled "education".
For me, a professional editor and writer, this makes my blood boil.
But I wanted to propose something fun. I now share with you my political party mistake collection.
But don’t call me biased,
I have from all of them,
I can show you mistakes from all parties.
We found accentless "orthography" manuals
that make you sound as a foreigner.
And someone told me: could it be a sort of wake-up call, to encourage the kid to open the book and learn?
I doubt it because the publishing house,
of dubious reputation by the way, is "pelicano", with no accent.
In Spanish, that accent less word means "gray-haired".
So I don’t think it was a proposal,
it wasn't at all.

Luckily, the project was disseminated
and a lot of teachers got involved. They even used the protect as a practice on their language classes.
In several countries 14 blogs were made by volunteers who joined the cause of writing better on their streets.
This is a special place for Fernando Catelotti, an Argentinian who went with his students to the entrance of the city, to set clear that they had nothing to do with human bowels, but with a city by the name of Colón, on the province of Entre Ríos, Argentina.
Fernando was very helpful.
Of course there were problems.
Not because people got mad,
we would always ask for permission,
and told people it was a project aimed towards kids who were becoming aware of their language. Yet, law came forward
with the "Graffiti Unit", part of the Mexico City Ministry of Public Security, and detained Brenda Joana Mendoza, who was helping us to place accents along with her teacher. We called accent marathon
to the activity of placing accents, because a Peruvian a girl came up with that name, so we decided to replicate it here.
As a result, we infringed
article 24 of Civic Culture Regulation, for placing an accent on a street name.
Luckily, our project was already known and passers-by told the police: "These guys are doing something other people did not do."
We were given a ticket and sent
to a Public Prosecution Office.
But things got better:
An important TV channel went to ask, "Why were you detained?"
"For pasting accents," we said.
The obvious paradox made it into prime time news, on a national broadcast. That was really good for the project.
But the best was yet to come.
Someone told us to go back to the Public Prosecution Office and with good back-up information we could be given a safe-conduct,
to keep placing accents without trouble.
Guess who told us that:
the entrance guard.
He called us and said: "I understand you, I love reading too."
(Laughter) (Applause) Now you understand why this project is ideally for everyone,
not just big brands.
I believe that everyone is entitled to write correctly. At least to know the basics,
then decide how to put across ideas,
that is why we widened the project.
In the end it's all about the message.
How many times have we heard:
"Relax, people get the point,"
whenever accents, letters, or punctuation are missing. We get the point, but what do we understand?
This Ministry wanted to convey a message:
Education, not intimidation,
is a misspelled "obligation".
Two secretariats challenging each another.
The way we write says more than we think.
Says if we are diligent, meticulous. If we are capable for a leading position.
Says if we respect our readers, if we understand, if we are sensitive.
I believe this is important.
Brands spend good money on image. I don't know why they forget these details.
A misspelled road safety campaign that urges you to drink.
A government, that represents a population sector, but promises a misspelled 4 axis "strategy".
I wonder if the strategy derailed with that wrong accent.
Not everybody has to write correctly, but you can pass down or receive advice.
It is about image and identity.
Here a Mexican bank with an Argentinian style of writing.
It says "professional" with a C.
A guy from Jalisco sent the picture.
Well.. There are mistakes I just can't understand.
Like accents in wrong places.
There are places where you can mess it up.
Any store or stall. No matter the country. And places where you should not mess it up.
This was the worst place in Spanish speaking world, to fail so miserably.
Cervantes would have died again, (Laughter)
had he read that error.
five years have passed since the Project started, and I truly believe it is no longer a matter of accents, nor of orthography.
It is a matter of identity.
It is important to have a stance, and consistently stand by it.
This one amused me.
A real state agency, that has nothing to do with Gabo.
But Gabo, García Márquez, stood up for the elimination of accents. Yet, he made a relentless defense of letter Ñ, when Europe wanted to eliminate it from the computer keyboards of Spanish speaking markets.
Three accents are missing here. You might want to identify which.
It is identity.
I share this banner too. This Brand pays me nothing, but I believe it's a good example of a good designer knowing how to put a necessary accent,
without blaming it on typography. The accent is there.
There are ways to express.
In the end, how are we not going to feel related to our streets, if we are able to relate to one another through a t-shirt?
We all build our streets and language everyday and everywhere.
So if these pictures have not yet made you sick, please remember the feeling when a foreigner writes our country name:
“mejico”, probably with no accent,
or even with a big and nasty J.
Let’s not allow this on our streets.
Thank you.
(Applause)
(Silence) This feeling that you have in this moment, it's how it feels to live as a deaf person 24 hours of the day, every single day of their lives.
And what I mean by deaf is someone who was born with this condition.
We all learned to speak because we could hear. We started with awo, da-da, du-du that later started to become words like water, papa, daddy. Those words allowed us ask for food, protection and affection.
What happens when a deaf child is born in a family where everyone can hear?
Deaf communities have created a language that compensates for the lack of hearing, and this is sign language. It's a natural language, one of gestures of expression and spatial configuration, and visual perception. Thanks to this, deaf people can establish a way of relating to their environment.
Sign languages are ancient languages, and so was the first deaf person who needed this language to communicate.
But around this language, there are a lot of myths and prejudices that have been present in our society since the beginning, and now I am here to end that.
To start, one of the classics is: "sign language is universal."
No, it isn't; sign language is unique in each deaf community.
We have sign language in Argentina, Uruguay, Italy ...
There are even variances between territories in our country, as is with Spanish. If I say gurí, chango, crío, pibe, all of you would think that I am referring to a child.
Same happens in sign language.
Another myth is to believe that sign language is iconic and concrete.
If this was true, you would have understood what I said at the beginning of this talk.
I asked you to raise your hand if you were wearing blue socks today.
There are signs that we all understand, for example: to eat, to drink
or sleep. But the majority of signs are arbitrary. For example: blue, 28 or pain.
In sign language, you can transmit any concept, even abstract ideas.
For many years it was believed that sign language wasn't grammatical, but thanks to profound linguistic studies in our country, María Ignacia Mazzone discovered what we know today, that sign language has grammar, with a structure and a vocabulary that is all its own, that differs from spoken language.
For example, in Spanish I would say: "Today I am going to your house." If I say the same thing in sign language, I would say, "House yours I go."
But I believe the most widespread and prejudiced myth is that if a young boy learns sign language, he is not going to develop his spoken language.
This is not true.
As told you all earlier, sign language is a natural language with the same characteristics and properties that linguists have described in all human languages.
We can say that it has the same status as all languages.
And how can learning sign language benefit a young boy?
Fundamentally, this allows him to communicate with his parents, with his family, with the world around him.
He has the power to express his wishes, his interests, his emotions.
He has the power to receive corrections, but also encouragement, stimulation.
He has the power to ask for help.
He can anticipate situations.
Today, I want to tell you the story of Maria.
Maria is a three-year-old girl who was born deaf. She uses hearing aids because her family doesn't know sign language.
On any given afternoon, Maria is bathed, prepared and dressed, and she goes out with her family.
But Maria doesn't know where is she going.
Is she going to grandma's house?
Or maybe a birthday party?
One of those days she goes to the doctor.
Sometimes Maria perceives: "Let's go to the doctor."
But until they arrive, María doesn't know if she's going for a check-up, if they're going to take out a syringe, if she's going to get poked with a needle or if it's really a check-up for her little brother.
These are some examples of situations that deaf children live through each day.
Without an efficient and complete language, it is very difficult to anticipate situations like any other child would: "Look, Maria, today we need to take blood.
If you stay calm, close your eyes and breathe slowly, it won't hurt.
And afterwards we can go get ice cream."
Sign language gives meaning to the lives of these kids that isn't only a succession of images or events that are happening.
Another essential benefit is that it allows them to develop cognitively, without restrictions, and within the corresponding chronologically set times.
It gives the power to develop their capacity to reason, to have abstract thought and creativity.
It doesn't matter what learning environment each of you has had.
If you all don't share the same language as your professor, you aren't going to understand anything that they are trying to teach.
And this is what happens to María with her teacher.
Sometimes she doesn't understand the assignments and chooses to copy what her schoolmates are doing.
Sometimes she doesn't do anything, and even worse, when she gets bored, she bothers people, takes things and fights.
These situations have caused teachers and professionals to say things like: "Maria does terrific at school. She does well; she does the same as her schoolmates" without noticing that she is only copying what her schoolmates do.
Other times we hear: "Maria, apart from her hearing loss, she must have another problem, because I talk to her, I explain to her, I talk to her slower, and she don't understand me."
And the problem is that the language we speak is unintelligible to her.
Sign language allows deaf people to understand the world, to interpret reality, ask questions, assert, deny, argue ...
It also allows them to transform their education in school lessons, and not in therapy.
A deaf child needs specific treatments, of course. But the child also needs to express and communicate freely, without impediments. Playing, expressing himself, like any other child would.
In my teaching experience, in School 515, Jose Antonio Terry, a child from this city - that is a bi/multilingual school, which means that they learn and teach sign language, Spanish and also a foreign language - the children that learn sign language as their main language also learn Spanish with more ease in their two modalities.
Spoken language is going to depend on the level of hearing loss and on the technological aid that they can obtain, that could be either hearing aids or cochlear implants.
The Spanish language is a right of every deaf child to be correctly literate, as it is an element of communication and also an access road to knowledge.
Every language is learned in a community,
together with others. This is why it is fundamental that families and their environments know this language. They should also know other deaf children and adults who use sign language who they can interact and identify with.
The truth is that for deaf people, the power for change is in your hands.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
I am going to start asking you a question: Why do we eat meat?
We eat it all the time in many different ways.
In fact, it is for many the maximum culinary experience.
Human beings, as the omnivorous species we are, get our nutrients from many sources, and one of them is meat.
In a healthy and balanced diet, it is recommended that 35 percent of ingested calories belong to proteins.
Proteins are made of basic components called amino acids which help us to regulate many different aspects of our body: they help build and regenerate tissues and synthesize hormones that will help us in our metabolism, such as insulin.
Now, let's talk about numbers.
According to the UN, there are 7.3 billion of us people in the world and, on average, each one of us eats 76 g of protein per day.
If we do the maths, it turns out we have to produce 202 billion tons of meat every year, and we are talking about today; if we go up to 2050, we'll be talking about 9.7 billion people; and in 2100, there'll be 11.2 billion people.
Human beings, intelligent beings, made a model to supply the whole planet: the intensive livestock farming model, which has been feeding us for a long time.
But we are still growing and asking for meat to eat, and this system has reached its limit; it is under overexploitation.
We are seeing that day to day in different areas.
We are exposed to sanitary risks, exposed to pandemics: bird flu, swine fever, mad cows -- We have health risks: 80 % of the antibiotics produced in the world are not for human consumption, but cattle's.
Those antibiotics will remain afterwards in the meat that we ingest without any type of control, and, besides, we develop resistance to them.
So, one of the biggest inventions and discoveries of medicine is running out of efficiency.
Furthermore, there is a hormonal dose that we are ingesting, poorly controlled, and we also have access to meat with a high amount of fats which affects our cardiovascular diseases.
We cannot forget about ethical issues.
Nowadays, we are worried about the well-being of animals; in this overexploitation, there is a lot of animal sacrifice and suffering that is already in question.
It is also one of the main causes of gas emissions related to greenhouse effect; it has 14.5 percent of the global share, higher than the transport fee, with planes and boats included.
And it is also the main cause for global deforestation because, on average, every animal, to produce 1 kg of meat, has to consume 6 kg of animal feed, which means that we have to produce a lot of animal feed.
Most of the fields we see while driving on the highway are for animal feed, not human consumption.
If we add to that the contamination of aquifers and soils produced around intensive livestock farms, we have a huge global problem.
Many food innovators are trying to find solutions and alternatives.
For example, how they are changing vegan burgers.
We have seen them for a long time in supermarkets, oriented towards a vegetarian and vegan public because carnivores were not really keen to try them.
But nowadays we have new options to appeal to a carnivore public and try to reduce animal meat consumption.
The problem is that animal protein and vegetal protein are not the same.
What is measured is the net useful amount of protein that we can absorb and use, and the human digestive system is prepared to metabolize animal proteins, but it struggles with vegetal proteins, so we cannot sustain all of the additional demand with vegetal proteins.
In 1931, Winston Churchill, as the good visionary he was, predicted that human beings would escape the absurdity of creating a whole chicken if we would only eat the breast or wing.
And 88 years later, that has already come.
It comes hand in hand with tissue engineering.
For those who are not familiar with this term, it is a branch of regenerative medicine.
It tries to apply functional reconstructions to repair body tissues or organs.
Some examples of this that you surely have seen are skin, cartilage, trachea grafts --
That has existed for a long time, but only today we have the necessary tools to be able to think about creating muscle tissue, which is meat, in high amounts and develop them as food products.
How does the technique work?
Imagine an animal, let's visualize an animal in perfect conditions; the better it is, the better the sample we will obtain.
A cow, a chicken, a pig -- We extract a meat sample, for example, from its leg.
We know that the sample has muscle tissue, veins, fat, nerves -- There are a lot of known techniques in medicine that allow you to isolate the cells you want from those tissues.
As we are interested in protein, because we want to provide a good nutritive amount of it, we will isolate muscle cells.
This is done in a short period of time so that the tissues are kept alive.
You can put those tissues in a controlled environment and favourable for them, which means we will put them in an aqueous medium that has proteins, amino acids, mineral salts, air to live; and they keep growing and doing their function.
And muscle cells function is to create muscle tissue full of proteins.
This way, if they are put afterwards into a fermenter or bioreactor that simulates the animal's body, you can get muscle mass free of fats, if we desire so, without having to kill an animal.
To give you an idea, from pig samples obtained during a year we can have the same productive capacity as from having 400 pigs slaughtered.
This meat respects the animal's well-being and, as you have seen in the numbers, reduces a lot the resource consumption and, furthermore, it does not have any kind of exposition to pandemics; the animals we use to get the samples are perfectly healthy, and during this process they are exposed to neither viruses nor bacteria.
Well, how is the global picture?
Here we have some quadrants that explain how it is.
Downwards we have traditional livestock, which is on its sustainability limits and cannot keep increasing its production.
We have the alternatives that arise to help satisfy this demand, the alternatives of vegetal origin that emulate that meat to try to reduce the consumption, and the sustainable alternatives related to the production of animal protein through what is called cultured meat technique.
How real is this?
In 2017, there were five businesses; today, there are 25 of us.
This is unstoppable, this is real.
I have marked in red those that belong to the EU.
Not only are new businesses that produce cultured meat being born; the meat industry is helping these businesses because they are aware of the lack of resources that we have to keep feeding the world's population.
Not only in the meat industry, but in all industries associated with the chain of production and distribution of this kind of products.
And most importantly, or as equally important: governments are supporting these initiatives and regulatory authorities are ready to regulate them.
In the EU, the authority that validates the food we eat is EFSA.
In 2015, article 2283, if anybody is curious and could see it, they describe novel foods use.
A novel food is any food that has not been used frequently since 1997.
In 2017, inside the category of novel food, nourishment from cultured cells was accepted.
EU is already available to regulate this kind of food.
These are the first nourishments that have been made.
You can see different models, there is chicken, pork and cow meat.
And we can imagine how the factory of cultured meat will be.
If this is what we are used to seeing, which is intensive livestock, this is what we will see.
This is not unusual, it already exists in the food industry; all yogurt and dairy products we eat come from fermenters and bioreactors like these.
Wine is made in these fermenters, and brewer's yeast lives there.
At this point in the film, you could be wondering: What does the final consumer think, which is all of us?
We obviously also wondered that ourselves, so we did a consumer acceptance study, nationwide.
Some polls were carried out in Spain's most populated cities, and what we obtained was really gratifying because we are more prepared than we think.
Between yes and no, yes credits are always the winners, and people are willing to consume this kind of meat.
What all of them are certain of, both yes and no, especially 80 percent of the population, is that in five years this meat will be on the supermarket shelves.
And with all this information, you can think whatever you want.
Meat consumers can even lie to yourselves, keep thinking we will keep eating meat as usual indefinitely.
Vegetarians and vegans may even have a more flexible diet, or maybe not, as it depends on your reasons to choose that diet.
What is clear is that the question I asked you at the beginning, about why we eat meat is on the table, and sustainability problems, will not disappear, and neither will our needs as a species.
This is the less contaminated meat that has been made in the world.
You can call it however you want, it has different names: cultured meat, "in vitro" meat, clean meat in the US, ethical meat --
But what is clear is that it will reach the supermarket shelves, it will be available to you, a friend or relative will put it on the table in front of you, and then, you will have the ability and possibility to choose.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
(Video starts) (Video ends) (Applause) Thank you.
My name is Pachi Tamer and I'm Elena's dad.
This video is a good example of how we can transform a stressful moment into something fun and creative.
When we shot this video Elena was less than a year old and a few months later her parents were already divorced.
A divorce is not easy for anyone.
Neither the kids nor the parents.
And to go through that difficult situation I tried to use the same philosophy that I used to do that video.
(Video starts) Changing the reality into something fun and creative.
Changing the reality with music.
I realized that I didn't need much to do it.
I didn't need money to go to the mall or get together with friends so the kids can play.
I had Elena, and Elena had me.
We were together and that was enough.
We didn't need anything else.
(Video ends) And that's how we start shooting videos.
A simple day of drawing and painting could get into something like this.
(Video starts) (Video ends) By then her favorite color was purple.
Now I think she changed.
But we did a purple party.
(Video starts) (Video ends) Then she was all about pirates and treasures.
So I came up with the idea of hidding a treasure.
We had a little forest in the complex where we were living.
So I hid a treasure, a bag with toys and candies, and I picked her up at daycare, and I throw a map.
I did a little map with real references of the forest. And I burned the borders with a lighter, and I left it on the floor.
When she got out she found it.
And she asked me: "What is this?
It's a map to a treasure!"
So we went home and we dressed as pirates and we went to look for the treasure.
(Video starts) (Video ends) Now please watch it without music, I think it's fantastic.
(Video starts) (Video ends) By then her birthday was coming, I was working in an agency in Dallas and at the Christmas party I won two tickets to anywere in the States.
And she was obsessed with princesses, Cinderella was her favorite.
So I booked two flights to Orlando,
we rented a Cinderella's room at the hotel.
We had everything ready -- and the day before, something unexpected came up and we couldn't go.
I had two problems. Firstly, how I tell her.
And secondly, how we celebrate her birthday.
So I prepared a Cinderella surprise party at the forest.
I went to pick her up and when she got home she found a letter from Cinderella saying: "I'm Sorry you can't go to Disney, but I have a surprise party at the forest for you!"
(Video starts) (Video ends) That's the party.
Then my father died in Argentina and I couldn't take her with me
so she couldn't say good bye.

I was telling her my dad was in a star.
She was always waiving to a star.
So I decided, as a farewell, that we sent a small letter to the sky with helium balloons.
What I did after that, the next week was take her to the forest
and there I had another box with balloons so she believed that was a present from my dad, coming from the sky.
And this is the video.
(Video starts) (Video ends) Have you seen Breaking Bad?

Well, if you haven't here it is.
(Video starts) (Video ends) Finally, for Thanksgiving I had five days off for the holidays. I didn't have much money, like $1500 and I wanted to take her to Disney.
So I took my car a Wednesday and we travel four days to spend just one day in Orlando. Sleeping in cheaps hotels on the road and we got to Disney.
(Video starts) (Video ends) And then I want to teach Elena this: Money is not that important.
And she's really into golden stuff.
So we made a video called "House of Gold" that shows you -- now you gonna see it but shows you how to turn nothing, into gold.
(Video starts) (Video ends) We spent our lives working to leave our kids material stuff and we miss the opportunity of enjoying them.
Life goes too fast.
Today they are 6 years old and tomorrow 15.
And they leave.
I think this is the best legacy for her.
I always think about her watching this videos in thirty or forty years, after I'm gone.
And I think the best gift I can leave are these images, this talk, this message: I love you Elena.
Thank you.
Let's play a game.
Here I have a common remedy, something we may find anywhere.
I'll read the information leaflet to see if you can guess what it is.
"It contains: ascaridol, 1,8-cineol, and excipients.
Adverse effects: skin irritation, liver damage, elevated hemorrhage risk.
Overdose: vomiting and spasms.
Contraindicated in case of hyper sensibility, liver disease, pregnancy or breastfeeding.
Not enough is known regarding security in children and adolescents. Attention: do not use for more than two weeks.
Interaction with anticoagulants."
So, were you able to guess?
In fact, what I have here, is no less than a little Boldo plant.
You weren't expecting that, were you?
Why yes, if plants came with information leaflets that's what they'd say.
How come a plant with contraindications, adverse effects?
I'll tell you a story.
More than 5,000 years ago when the Egyptians had headaches they couldn't cross to the next pyramid and buy some Aspirin.
They took what it was available.
They knew that chewing willow bark, alleviated fever and pain.
Nonetheless, this treatment had two inconveniences: stomach irritation and in the long run, it caused not very enjoyable hemorrhoids.
Hoping to separate it from the compounds that caused adverse effects, in the beginning of the 19th century the compound responsible for the analgesic activity of the willow bark was isolated.
Unfortunately, it turned out that this same compound was responsible for the side effects.
However, lacking a better alternative, it was highly used during that century for treating patients with rheumatism.
One of those patients was the father of an employee from Bayer.
And it occurred to this employee that if he could chemically alter the compound, he would be able to keep its analgesic activity, while minimizing the adverse effects.
He did it and that's how acetylsalicylic acid was born, which nowadays we all know as aspirin.
This story shows us that, of course, many plants have medicinal properties due to the existence of different compounds in their leaves, their stalk, their roots.
But that does not mean that using them in their original case, is always the better option.
For millions of people, traditional medicine represents their main, and often only access to sanitary attention.
For others, is economically accessible and those are valid reasons.
But when we can access a better choice, why do we insist on making some tea, chewing a leaf or suck a tree trunk?
As chemical researcher, I could not avoid the question and when I wrote my book, "Grandma's Remedies," I found myself once and again with similar answers.
"Because natural is healthier."
"Because if it is millenary, I can surely trust it."
"And because the pharmaceutical industry is a monster."
Let's analyze each of these cases.
Let's start with "the natural."
"Natural" only refers to the origin of the product.
It tells us that it comes from nature.
But that does not mean that it is better or worse.
We tend to associate "natural" with "innocuous."
But, although more than 50% of the drugs we use nowadays had their origin in natural products and their byproducts, the most powerful poison are also found in nature.
Often we think that if it is a little tea, a little plant or a little herb, - in diminutive for it to sound more inoffensive - how bad could it be?
Ask Socrates and his hemlock's tea.
That should suffice, or at least to Socrates, to end the association between natural and innocuous.
Furthermore in a plant we may have beneficiary compounds, but also compounds with no activity, or even so, with harmful activity.
And they are all mixed up.
One argument strongly used in favor of natural products, is the absence of secondary effects.
But we have already seen this not to be true for the willow bark and furthermore many children suffered liver damage for consuming Echinacea and severe allergic reactions to compounds containing pollen.
The thing is that, as in many countries these natural products are sold as supplementary food instead of medication, they are not required to report their secondary effects.
Then it isn't strange to assume they don't have them.
But things don't end here.
Because not only natural products have secondary effects, but as we think they don't have them, we are at risk of an overdose.
For example, consuming more than 15 grs per day of Ginseng may cause hypertension, insomnia, restlessness -- But who knows how many leaves is 15 gr of Ginseng?
How many infusions per day?
In plants is very difficult to know the exact quantity of the active compound, and so, the dosage.
It depends on how many leaves were put in the infusion, the time left to infusion, the water temperature, the amount of active compound in the original plant.
So, something being natural doesn't account for its benignity.
Natural is not necessary innocuous: it has secondary effects, contraindications, medical interactions, and the risk of overdosing.
Another concept very much incorporated, is our idea of "millenary."
If I say "millenary," what comes to mind? what do you imagine?
Great and wise civilizations, the Chinese, the Hindus --
But before the arrival of medicines, of antibiotics, they'd die like flies.
Human life expectancy up to the beginning of the 19th century was lower than 35 years.
Nevertheless we keep associating our concept of "millenary" to something reliable, safe, and proven through time.
For hundreds of years, rhinoceros horn in powder and tiger bone powder is used in Chinese traditional medicine for, among other things, increase virility.
It has no results.
(Laughter) Actually, it has one property: to endanger those animals.
The thing is that "millenary" is not necessarily "effective."
In the case of plants and herbs, it is indeed possible that time had selected and eliminated those with most immediate toxic effects.
"Robert died after eating that, better not to eat it anymore."
But it is very difficult to prove significant improvements or adverse effects in the long run.
And our Boldo plant is a clear example.
For centuries it has been used to treat digestive disorders.
Nonetheless we now know that its use for more than two weeks is potentially toxic due to the presence of ascaridole, a compound present in its leaves.
So, something being millenary does not account for its efficacy, its security or its reliability.
As millenary as it may be, without systematic studies we can't asseverate that a compound has beneficial or detrimental effects in the long run.
Thus, we come to the last argument I wanted to discuss with you: the great mistrust towards the pharmaceutical industry.
Is the pharmaceutical industry a monster?
We hear about dishonest practices, pressure to medical doctors, falsification of results, conflict of interests.
But let's not be naïve, because when we hear about natural products it seems as though they were promoted by Tibetan monks or long-bearded Hindus, and truth be told, natural products are also a business.
A business that in most countries is not regulated and less so controlled.
For a drug to be released into the market, it has to successfully pass in vitro assays, animal tests and clinical trials.
Such process may endure from ten to twenty years.
And it is estimated that only 1 between 10,000 tested compounds reaches the market.
And once in the market, research continues, because a registry is open to account for all secondary effects reported by people. Being a extreme measure, the product to be retired from the market
This regulation and control system is far from ideal.
There are interests at stake and lobbyists advocating for them.
But at least they exist.
Such is not the case for natural products.
Many are homemade; many can be bought in streets.
Only in recent years, member states of the World Health Organization are implementing policies oriented to the promotion of a secure and effective use of traditional medicine.
But there is still much to do.
We lack more studies, more evidence of therapeutic efficacy, and further control.
So, what shall we do with the Boldo tea?
Shall we take it? Shall we not?
When chewing a plant or sucking a trunk the illusion is of not being incorporating chemical compounds to our bodies.
But, truly, we are all made of chemical compounds.
And talking strictly in a medicinal level, there is no much difference between the compounds I can incorporate when chewing a plant than of those which I incorporate when buying a pill in a pharmacy.
The thing is that in the plant I do not know the exact dosage, the compound is not pure, and I am not even aware of all the possible adverse effects.
That is why I think the most important is that we may ask ourselves questions.
To ask ourselves: "why am I choosing to take a Boldo tea?"
"Because it will soothe a discomfort."
Maybe.
"Because I am in the middle of nowhere and I don't have access to a pharmacy."
Okay.
"Because I have it in my cupboard."
Perhaps.
But "because it has no secondary effects,"
it has them.
"Because for being natural is safer,"
not necessarily.
"Because in this way I am not self-medicating,"
definitively I am.
So, at the time of taking some tea, let's think about it.
Let's ask ourselves the questions, and when choosing, let's not do it for the wrong reasons.
Thank you.
(Applause)
In 1914, two members of the Italian royalty performed a tango in front of Pope St. Pius X.
The reason was to seek the approval or win the disapproval of that dance.
This rare ceremony held at the Vatican which finally wouldn't condemn the tango was the result of the great success the dance had enjoyed and continued to do so, among its strong upper-class supporters from major European capitals, especially in Paris.
However, there were some voices who deemed it immoral,
hence this need for examination by the Pope.
In those aristocratic salons, the dance fascinated the audience mainly when the couple tangoed in a close embrace, pressing against each other's chests.
Tango music was still at its beginnings and the choreography design was fairly simple.
And meanwhile tango was evolving, a historical event took place: the private act of a man and a woman embracing gained public acceptance.
In other words, that ancient gesture of love broke out of its seclusion in the private sphere.
Today, a century later, the tango is danced in over 300 cities worldwide.
The current success of the dance is because it grew and passed to a more mature stage.
Today, the essence of tango aims to bring one body and four legs with a passionate body language into a union with complex ornaments.
And all that accompanied by a half-nostalgic, half-sensual music.
It is no longer about a mere embrace, but an artistic one.
Maybe the new digital era will allow us to reorganize and compile our repository of tango culture.
This repository is a chest full of treasures
and there is no metaphor to say that its jewels are innumerable.
No one knows how many works have been recorded. 20,000?
50,000?
Or how many bands were there. 200, 300?
It is true that not everything in it is gold, there is also cheap jewelry, but most of that treasure includes very valuable works from the three genres of tango: music, song, and dance.
I started to listen to tango when I was five years old.
That's the earliest memory, but most likely before that.
No lullaby, no crib.
My mother would lie next to me and listen to tangos until I fell asleep.
When I started getting out to get social, as they say, there were two strong cultural mandates I needed to consider in order to get accepted in the group: to be a good football player and to be a good tango dancer.
I started to dance when I was 17 years old, but my milonga experience was short-lived.
Buenos Aires itself was no longer a party, and I was determined to follow a university degree.
20 years ago I returned to my home city after living other 20 years in Spain.
As soon as I got back, the tango found me again.
It never let me go, and I was engaged me again with the embrace of its dance, a close relative of the emotional and the erotic embrace.
All things considered, they are three different love embraces because, when you dance a tango, that instant becomes timeless.
In other words, when you dance the tango, you can live a three-minute love story.
(Applause) (Tango music) (End of tango music) (Applause)
This is Campo de Cebada, a public square, basically a 2,500 m2 concrete area in the heart of La Latina and currently managed by a group of neighbors after a period of somewhat sloppy management by City Hall who got rid of the only sports center in the Central District of Madrid. Campo de Cebada means
defining the city in a new way by bringing the politicians who make decisions about the city closer to us, those who have to live with their decisions.
This type of urban planning allows for complex regulations to enable things to happen that are not usually done in a city.
It is a fully equipped square that opens its infrastructure.
Innovative details like water taps or plugs allow for citizen participation to become active engagement so that people decide at all times what they want to do.
Probably the most important thing is trying to get as many people as possible involved in making decisions on managing this space daily, allowing all kinds of people to do all kinds of activities at the same time and place.
For us, that is innovation.
Having a TEDx at Campo de Cebada involved learning about the many things that make a place like this special and unique.
We managed to have the first TEDx event in the world that took place at night, outdoors, on a public square, created by and for the residents, and completely free.
Besides the invaluable collaboration of the people from TEDxMadrid, we managed to get the local residents to design what went on that night.
Jota, in the back of this picture, who usually plays basketball at Campo de Cebada, was the one directing the sound; Dani, who you see here with his friends, designed the whole audio visual part.
Jacobo, the 'professional neighbour', helped everyone that was collaborating,
just like Zuloark, a collective that we work with at the square, alongside many of the neighbors and 60 students of the Javeriana University of Bogotá who over the previous week designed and built all the benches and stands that about 1000 people enjoyed that night.
But it wasn't only neighbors, many others pitched in and put on the T-shirt to help organize the key points of the event.
Other venues in Madrid helped providing chairs for this big crowd.
Just before the event started everybody helped out, from the Bangladeshi who usually sell beer, to the kids who play basketball; they were all helping, sweeping, setting up, making that night so special.
As the night fell, hearing the chords of maestro Isasa, on a square dressed up as never before, in front of almost a thousand people, we heard people speak about the citizen innovation project as a catalyst for social transformation in the city.
Each and every one of the themes of the talks referred to an aspect that defines Campo de Cebada.
Like they spoke about open infrastructures that allow for active participation.
People from Cebada came to talk about the People's Summer University, the series of Open Air Songs, or the play Don Juan Tenorio, that they perform once a year.
A square where decisions are made in open assemblies had 'El Pera' come to talk about managing a place like Ciudad de los Muchachos in Leganés.
Authorship for this space belongs to Campo de Cebada, not to some architect or urban planner whose name is on the project. So Gerardo talked about Cinema Without Authors, where the concept of authorship is also dealt with differently.
This handmade urbanism, of a neighbor designing the furniture to be made, led to Alberto Peralta talking about the network of urban gardens in Madrid.
And managing conflict differently, embracing it instead of suppressing it, led to people coming to talk about StreetFood Mad, or the living room through the office of political innovation.
For me, the key thing that night
goes back to what I said at the beginning: so many people watching the talks - at La Cebada they usually behave differently to what you see here. They do their hair or kiss while they're there, discuss the price of a beer with a Bangladeshi, or eat one of Floren's potato omelettes, one of the neighbors from the allotments who makes the best one; many people weren't even there to listen to the talks.
I remember that just before starting, Antonella came over and said proudly that many people had never been to La Cebada before, and that they were here because of TEDxMadrid.
I smiled and said that the opposite was also true: many of the people here would never go to a TEDxMadrid but were here now because they usually are at the square.
(Applause) What happened on the 14th of July 2014 at Campo de Cebada, that TEDxMadrid Salon event, contrary to what you might think, is completely repeatable.
In fact, there are thousands of spaces in Madrid that are managed with this idea of social innovation, just like Campo de Cebada.
Next summer, some of you might be wherever the TEDxMadrid Salon event takes place, maybe as a speaker, a collaborator, or in the audience, maybe you're doing your hair, or kissing, or maybe even eating the omelette made by their own Floren.
Lastly, one of the most exciting things was that after each talk there was no big applause that evening.
The day before, a very engaged neighbor called us, worried about the noise that a thousand people would make in a place surrounded by houses.
The organisation decided that all clapping would be silent.
[gesture of silent clapping] You can't imagine the disappointment when after a talk, nobody claps and you don't hear a thing.
(Laughing) For that reason, I ask you all to finish what we started that evening, and that you are the stars that close what happened at TEDxMadrid Salon at Campo de Cebada.
Don't applaud me, but applaud the people that participated, making that night one of the most special ones ever.
(Applause)
(Alarm ringing) It's 6 am; you wake up, take a shower, get dressed, check that the schoolbag is ready, check school clothes, make breakfast, school lunch, you go over everything you have to do for the day;
you have to buy groceries, pay bills, take your mother-in-law to the doctor, make an appointment with the pediatrician, do the laundry, iron a little, go to the dry-cleaner's, to pick up clothes you took a few days ago,
clean the bathroom and the kitchen, and call the plumber because there's a leak in the bathroom.
Oh! And, think about your child's birthday and buy the helium, because balloons with helium are all the rage, so you have to rent the helium tank. You wake up the kids, give them breakfast, get them dressed, take them to school, come back home and start doing what you have to do, you get a phone call.
Your daughter is crying inconsolably. Her tummy hurts, so you have to go pick her up. You return home, attempt to organize the day, however possible, You bring her home; you go pick up your other son at school, return home, oversee homework, give them an afternoon snack, check the communication notebook from school to see if you have to buy a Bangladesh map for the next day.
(Laughter) You take them to swimming, tennis, soccer, English classes, music, ballet; you come back home, give the kids a bath, get them into their pajamas, cook dinner, your partner arrives, eat dinner together, take the kids to bed, read a story, and they fall asleep. (Sighing)
(Laughter) It may not be one of Alvar Puente's day, but it is something.
(Laughter) It's ten o'clock at night, you haven't paid the bills, and are due today.
Thank God somebody invented e-banking.
So you sign in, pay the bills, and it turns out the bank offers you a new service that is OK, and anything that makes your life easier is a good thing.
And you say to yourself, "I am going to sign on for this service,"
and you fill out the information the bank asks for, you get to the section "Activity" and the system gives you five options:
"Employee", "Self-employed," "Business owner," "Student", "Does not work,"; you are not an employee, you are not self-employed, you are not a businessman nor a student.
The only option left is, "Does not work."
And that is how reproductive, care, and domestic work is defined today.
It is not work.
If you aren't paid, it isn't work,
and nobody pays you to do that.
Now, I as ask you:
What do you think? Is it really work?
Well, if we go over the to-do list I mentioned a while ago,
it is indeed work, a lot of work;
and it demands a lot of responsibility.
However, when a woman -- because in general it's women, although there are more and more men doing this kind of work -- when a woman has to say what she does, as a mother, the choice in general is she "does not work."
This was not always the case, and let me pause for a second to give a heartfelt thanks to the Germans, who have been making polls since the beginning of times and kept the data.
And thanks to that, we know now that in 1881, a first census took place in Germany in which the work done within the family was not considered an economic activity.
Before 1881, the economic activity rate for men and women in Germany was identical and equal to 98 percent.
In this 1881 census, the economic activity rate for men remains at 98 percent, while the figure for women falls magically to 42 percent.
Less than half. Why?
Because in that census,
what a woman did at home wasn't seen as an economic activity.
Well, OK, it's laundering,
cooking, ironing.
But it was also making preserves for the winter, making clothes, taking care of the vegetable garden; however, that was not considered an economic activity any more.
Since then, since the Industrial Revolution, when the axis of production shifted away from the family, work is what you do outside your home and get paid for.
Everything else, the things we do so that our family can live their lives, for their well-being, that doesn't exist; no one keeps record of it; it's invisible.
Nevertheless, it's essential.
And what happens if I have to replace it?
If I can't take the children to school, I have to hire a transportation service; if I can't clean my house, I have to pay someone to do it for me; if I can't breastfeed, I have to formula-feed the baby, and if I can't take care of my children, I need to pay a nanny or take them to a daycare center.
Then the reproductive, care, and domestic work has a price if I need to replace it, but it has no value if I do it myself.
In his play "Lysistrata," Aristophanes proposes that the women of Athens go on a sexual strike to end the Peloponnese War.
The Peloponnese War had been going on for a long time now and women were tired, so Lysistrata summons her friends, asks them to bring their friends and she tells them, "Well, ladies, look, let's tell the guys that while the war is on... Nothing happens."
(Laughter) What would happen if a modern Lysistrata summoned to a strike not for sex but for care work?
How can the economic system stand without the invisible work of thousands of millions of women that cook, clean, do laundry, iron?
But they also tell stories, sing songs, dry tears.
I try to picture a world like that, and the truth is that it looks very similar to those Sci-Fi dystopias that show future worlds, dark worlds.
That is how I imagine the world without the work of all those women.
Work, indeed, because reproductive work is work.
This comes on top of productive work if a woman is also employed outside the home, and there're a lot of women who, vocationally or due to need, work outside their homes.
In Argentina, almost 50 percent of women are employed outside their homes.
Women earn less than men.
The average gender pay gap in Argentina is 27 percent on average.
It varies with level of education
- larger for lower levels of education - and also geographically.
In Patagonia, the pay gap jumps from 27 to 41 percent.
Why? Because of the oil industry.
The oil industry
is a male-driven activity, and it pays very well, which increases the pay gap.
There are women who earn less for the same job.
And that can only be called unfair.
But there are other women who earn less because they work less hours, and you may tell me, "Well, that's OK, it's fair. If they work less hours it's OK that they earn less."
Now, why do women work less hours?
Well, women work less hours because in general they have two jobs: one paid for and another one, for which, if they are lucky, they get a thank-you and an appliance as a gift for Mother's Day.
(Laughter) And if we try to find a solution?
I insist, our day may not resemble Alvar Puente's days, but it is something. We look for a way to cope with it and buy self-help books.
And all the solutions we get are based on personal decisions:
Be better organized, make lists, ask for what you need, hey, learn to negotiate, negotiate with your partner, negotiate with your employer.
Why do we let something as important as care work and parenting be done based solely on personal sacrifices and relinquishing personal interests?
Do we fully understand how important the presence of mothers and fathers are during early childhood for the healthy development of a human being?
Are we aware of the sacrifices that men and women are making - mainly women, but men as well - to combine productive and reproductive work?
And who says there's such thing as "men stuff" and "women stuff"?
Because the truth is, as I've said, women have two jobs.
When we have small children this is very obvious, because a mother who works in and outside the home and has a small kid, puts her body on the line both at work and at home, for instance, by breastfeeding or by taking care of her child, but there're many other things that remain within women's sphere of action, within families, because it seems women have a natural skill for those activities;
such as the emotional support of adult children, the caring for older adults as well,
organizing birthday parties, or getting the helium tank for the balloons, supervising the vaccination calendar, checking the communication notebook; these are all things that seem to fall within the mom's field of action.
Who says there're "men stuff"
and "women stuff"?
What's the reason for this sexual division of work that is not sexy at all?
If I think of someone to clean my house - and I've been thinking about these issues for quite some time - I automatically think of a woman.
And if I think about a painter, carpenter, plumber, almost automatically I think of a man, and if I go looking for a woman, I'll have a hard time finding one. Why?
Well, maybe it all starts here, in the toy store, because children learn through play;
the toy store is organized by colors.
It seems that boys like primary colors, and girls only like pink or purple (Laughter) and then, as I said, as kids learn through play, and boys learn to be a carpenter, they learn how to be a superhero - that is a good one - they learn how to be a race car driver, but they don't learn how to be a dad.
Boys don't play the daddy.
Girls, instead, we learn how to be cooks, how to be princesses, how to be moms.
Things are changing, and I'm sure that in this audience there're many who are breaking out with these stereotypes.
Today, there're women who work and are very successful, and fathers that work part-time to care for the kids; single mothers raising kids by themselves and building support networks to be able to take care of everything they have to do, so the situation is changing
mostly through personal attitude, but we need this change to go beyond the personal. We need to highlight the value that care work has for society.
This can only be done through government policies.
Some things have changed, and, for example, in Argentina, we have an insufficient maternity leave at national level.
It's the minimum granted by the International Labor Organization. Three months, that's it. And we are, oh, so smart because, on the other hand, we're telling mothers "exclusive breastfeeding for six months," but you have to go back to work within three months.
But in some places, some provinces, there's a six-month maternity leave, though this is not generalized.
In Argentina, there's been a significant amendment to our Civil Code, which puts money contributions within the marriage contract on a par with care work; this is almost revolutionary
and it happened in 2015. We also have retirement for housewives, which is a recognition, but it's coming a little late.
We can take a look at what is being done in other countries, for example, in Peru and Costa Rica, where they are starting to calculate the money value, the economic value of care work, and looking for a way to include it in the GDP.
A few months ago, Uruguay started a debate on public care policies, trying to give families support from the state for reproductive work, ranging from care in early childhood all the way to care of elderly parents, but including also care of disabled people, which also falls primarily on women.
And leading the way for a long time now is Scandinavia, which has an integral approach to the valorization of care work that starts with a six-month maternity leave but continues with a shared parental leave, by which each parent takes half - in Argentina the paternity leave lasts two days -
It's for fathers to do the paperwork, just in case they do something else. Two days, that's it. In Scandinavia both parents have leaves that last months and are shared.
They also have gender quotas, not only in elective political positions but also in private companies BOD.
And something very interesting: an incentive for men to study and follow careers traditionally considered as feminine, such as early education teaching and nursing.
An integral approach that shows the value care work has for society and one that makes it evident through an actual policy.
That's what we need.
I invite you again to imagine a world where modern-day Lysistratas call women out on a care work strike. What would happen then?
Thank you.
(Applause)
For starters, two things: if you fight to reach your dreams, maybe you won't get them.
(Laughter) And second, If you think there's something impossible, maybe it is.
Let's go home then!
(Laughter) No, I'll explain.
I'm not what I dreamed when I was 15.
I wanted to be an actor, but not any actor, I wanted to be a world-famous actor.
I wanted the Oscar.
I was so convinced they were going to give it to me.
I even wrote the thank you speech.
(Laughter) Seriously.
So I study dramatic arts in Seville, at the Andalusian Theater Center, the CAT.
I was convinced that when I finished my degree someone was going to come to me and make me a star.
I just had to wait for that someone. It was just a matter of waiting, as simple as that, because of my natural talent.
(Laughter) Clearly, that didn't happen!
Because I'm not famous worldwide, not even nationwide, maybe regionally or provincially.
Most of you here or those watching this video don't know my name, and those who know it, don't know how to spell it.
(Laughter) That's how it is!
So, I'm not a famous actor.
I'm an actor who works, and a lot,
given the choice, right?
And it's true that... It's true, many famous actors don't work.
(Laughter) It's true they called me to get involved in series or movies, which have been very successful and that's great.
But it's also true that, for years, they haven't called me at all. However, I have always kept working because I've always got what I call "my self-management."
"Self-management" is an ambiguous term but relating to work I think you understand.
I discovered the "self-management" while studying in Seville, in the CAT.
There, we started improvising shows every Wednesday night in a stage of the college cafeteria.
Whoever wanted to participate, got on stage and did whatever they wanted with no rules or premises. The only rule was that you must manage yourself, you had to self-manage, hence the term "self-management."
The truth was we had a great time and we made great shits.
(Laughter) But that was all about: getting on there, giving your all, no matter what.
We learned a lot and, eventually, I realized these workshops changed my life and the life of the others, because after all, those workshops made us look inside and see what we had, what we could get, what we could offer, to rack our brains and create something, right?
And we all discovered something, I found out that I could write things to make people laugh, and that encouraged me to keep going.
The only good thing about this crisis, we have gone through or what we're going through, is that it generated such a need that it made many people look inside to see what they got and wonderful things have come out. Not only in my field, in any other, many people have become entrepreneurs and they've got to focus on self-management.
I'm a fan of self-management and a big fan of entrepreneurs, because they chose the most difficult path: getting by on their own.
Let us not deceive ourselves: we'd all like that someone would knock our door and tell us: "Here you have a job for life, a lot of money for little work."
But that usually doesn't happen, right?
You can send your resume everywhere and wait to be called, or you cannot wait for their call and engage in self-management.
Self-management begins by asking oneself: what would I like to do?
Or what am I good at?
Or what makes me happy?
It can be something very simple like learning how to play the guitar because you've always wanted to.
Or something as big as setting up a cooking oil industry.
You can become a multimillionaire, which happens sometimes.
Or you can create your job and get your own salary, that will allow you to live with dignity or have an extra income or just fulfill a dream of your childhood you looked forward.
The beginnings, of course, are not easy because there's always someone who tells you it's not possible, that it cannot be done, usually someone in your surroundings.
You, 40 years old, learning to play the guitar?
(Laughter) Because people don't know what to do with their life, but with other's life, that's pretty clear to them, right?
(Laughter) (Applause) And then, here my golden rule comes: never let yourself down by the first "no" because they're always going to tell you "no", that you cannot; it's inherent to the human being. Saying "no" in the first place, especially, means that person makes a minimum effort, you know,
just to get rid of someone, we say no and we just cannot leave it like that.
And with this, I return to the words I started with: maybe there's something impossible, there're things cannot really be done, but the road forward since they say "no" until you really discover that it couldn't be done is very long and I guarantee you this journey is worthwhile.
It's worth exploring because on the way you can find out amazing things.
If you fight for your dreams, maybe you don't get exactly what you want, but I'm sure and garantee you that you'll get many other things
that can be even better than your dreams.
They can be better, basically because it's what it is.
(Laughter) And because you discovered it, because you built it.
And that's the real value in all this and the amazing thing. Because it's amazing when you really discover that after that "no", and another "no", and another there's a huge "yes" and, indeed, you can do what seemed so difficult to others. We sometimes believe "Well, if they said I cannot, I cannot."
But you have to see for yourself.
It's amazing when you realize you hit the correct keys you had to, that you insisted over and over again that you didn't let yourself down by the first "no" or the second one that you were the worst pain in the neck. You have to be a real pain!
I would love this on my gravestone: "In the end, he got it being a pain in the ass!
(Laughter) For me ... (Applause) I also like this other epitaph: you cannot be everywhere, but -- (Laughter) It has to do with this too. Maybe I'll keep both.
The best I did when I finished my degree, was, though I was sure someone was going to arrive, it was not to wait for it.
So, I put into practice what I learned in the self-management workshops and I kept writing my monologues and my songs which I performed in bars, squares, little squares, streets and in small theaters; it began to grow and, finally, that was enough to live on.
And it's been good, until now.
Let's not fool ourselves, I don't want to give a false image, I'm happy with the life I have.
But it doesn't look much like what I dreamed when I was 15.
Sometimes it's hard to face the reality is different as you've always dreamed.
And of course, I get angry.
I get angry with my self-management.
It's a pain in the ass!
I wanted everything on a silver platter and I had to get to work. What the fuck!
(Laughter) Really.
I get angry, I get frustrated because I think I deserve much more than what I got.
I get angry when I see they didn't call me in seven years and I had to do all on my own.
And I get angry because I didn't meet my expectations you know, my dreams didn't come true my imagination failed me, because I'm not what I dreamed when I was 15, neither when I was 35. (Laughter) Every year I renew dreams, (Laughter) to see if they're being accomplished. I make them up until one day I hit.
I get angry because my dreams, people, the industry let me down, but who didn't let me down is "me".
And my self-management has always been there.
Little things I built little by little and became big, I got them there. It's always been there, when they stopped calling me. I've always drawn on my own resources. I cannot stand still because I don't want, because I've always been searching and I'll always keep searching and after all, if that someone doesn't arrive, it doesn't seem like -- (Laughter) If that someone doesn't arrive, I will be satisfied at least with what I did, that I was constant, I'm still a pain in the neck, I keep searching, and I'm still looking for something new.
Now I have a cooking channel on YouTube.
What the fuck is that?
(Laughter) (Applause) If you saw how happy I was when I made that Galician Pie. (Laughter) When it came out from the oven I almost cried. Incredible! (Laughter) Well, it's true! I keep doing things.
I accept the reality like it is, although I keep dreaming and I keep thinking and I keep trying
to figure out what comes next.
Look, I cannot guarantee you that if you fight for your dreams you'll get them, that nothing is impossible --
But it's clear to me that if you stay at home waiting, you'll get nothing. We all have something we'd like to do, something we're really good at and if we cannot do it, maybe we find out we didn't like it.
But we discover we like something else or we meet someone to work with. Things are discovered along the way because the important thing is the way not the end.
And that's incredible!
We cannot be slaves to our dreams but we cannot stop chasing them, and while searching you find many things. Maybe, this isn't what I was after, maybe it's not what I dreamed when I was 15.
Maybe it's not the Oscar, It's very good for sure because you got it.
And you built it.
I've been waiting all my life for that someone and in the end, it's going to be that "that someone" is me.
(Applause) Thank you very much.
A few days after the 1985 earthquake in Mexico City, while I was among the boxes of basic goods and foods from all over the world, and while we were preparing these bags with provisions that would later be given out, I chose to emigrate.
I didn't require a lot: A place to hide from bad luck out of my daughters' reach.
I thought of migration as a solution not a problem.
6 years went by before I could leave this much loved but terrible city the Federal District, Mexico City.
It wasn't terrible just for the tremors, well by then, it was already the worst place for assault, for car theft, express kidnapping made it's debut and I needed to escape.
Leaving meant leaving this all behind, but also leaving everything.
When we arrived in Cancun, my husband's family, a beautiful family, happy, supportive, offered us a house in the neighbourhood Vicente Lombardo Toledano and we settled in there.
I don't remember why but, now that I think back, I feel like it rained all the time.
My Mexico City daughters and I felt like we were in the tropics and we went out to play in the puddles and gather up the turkeys until we realised that the rain water was mixing with sewage water.
I sold shawls in the hotel district.
When I saw the sewing machine, this beautiful thing my elderly mother had given to me with her furniture, it came out in the move, as I thought it would be great as decoration in my new house in Cancun.
I went from civil engineer to seamstress.
A little after that, they needed a maths teacher and well, there I was, a little bit closer to what I knew how to do and not just to what I had learned but what my father had taught me, because he had always been a maths teacher.
Shortly after that they needed a construction teacher and, of course there I came even closer to my profession.
So I quit drawing out shawls in material to sit down once again to drawing out on the drafting table.
The friendships that we were building during this period told us invariably, "No, Cancun no longer has the sea of opportunities, that came before the hurricane." Those were opportunities!
I thought to myself, "No, of course not, the opportunities are here, here we will build opportunities."
I realised that the word emigrant applies to anything that arrived after me and that the characteristics of this condition to which I belong, are resistance, persistence.
I didn't come to hurt anyone.
Already being in the academic field knowing a bit of maths, statistics and being in the middle, I was offered to form an association --
the Social and Domestic Violence Association -- from the ground up.
This didn't exist in the country, and a little later on, coordinate the local Urban Association.
This allowed me to sort of integrate two parts of my life, one was theoretical work, the academic work, research, numbers, big databases, and the other that I didn't want to give up, was my ability, my longing, to meet people.
Not just to see their faces, but to offer my own.
And being in this place, already allowed me to integrate what is my true calling.
That has allowed me to get to know people there's lot of figures too, but also meet people.
For example, Eliza.
Eliza comes from Las Margaritas, Chiapas, she has two sons.
Her dream, what she calls happiness is for them to go to primary school, all clean, well groomed, well fed, doing homework, following the rules; this for her is happiness.
She won't let them be like her.
Eliza told me how when she was younger she received an average of three men a night and now that she is older, the average has risen to six.
She is scared because she thinks she has HIV.
When I ask her if she has sat and thought about the possible transmission to all these men and them in turn to all their other partners, she turned to me quickly and said that she was also worried about her sons and due to her possible absence, she has to worry for a whole army.
She didn't come to hurt anyone.
And in this way, she is like Geovanna, who tells me off when I say "Hey, Yoana,"... she says, "Geovanna."
Geovanna decided to get pregnant when she was 14 years old.
It was the only way she could leave the house of her biological father who abused her and what hurt her most, was her mother's silence.
As she had raised her three younger brothers it didn't seem like a big deal to her make her own and raise it.
She knew that she needed a man, as she needed someone to provide for her and give her a house.
She didn't finish school, she assumed nobody would give her a job.
She has two sons and a daughter, each of them is a failed attempt at keeping a man.
This is why she came to Tabasco, because she has faith in what she will find there, that they will register them, they will say her name, as she says it, they will give her a house, they will give her something to eat and so, she will be very happy here in Cancun.
She has no idea that that could hurt anyone.
Being in the Association, seeing these stories analyzing the figures.
Finding myself, not with statistics, of point zero, zero, zero but with people, has allowed me, in any case, to make like a map, like a representation of the city.
What is there in the city?
What opportunities does the city offer to these women?
What decisions can they take?
Because I have real trouble thinking that they made these decisions faced with a wide range of possibilities.
But what possibilities does the city really offer?
The people that can't be of use to a city's services are unreported.
The sons of Geovanna are unregistered, they have no medical card, they can't be vaccinated they can't go to school, they have no identity, they are unreported.
These people that have no drinking water at home, these entire neighbourhoods that have to turn off the light, so that on nights with this heat, they can have a tiny fan, are unreported in the public services.
To enter the city, to become part of the city, to get to know how the city moves, what there is, what there is not, how to get, how to make use of such services, and know where each thing is, a support network is needed.
I had my family, but it's not like that for everyone.
A support network tells you where everything in the city is. It also gives you signs of hope.
A support network gives you a sense of security so that you don't fall down, but if you do fall, the hit will not be fatal.
Who does this here in Cancun?
Well, there is an organisation called Huellas de Pan, which gives food to girls and boys whose parents can't afford to.
And not just children, if an adult today, like many, like many times, wakes up with nothing to eat they can go to Huellas de Pan, where they will find a delicious meal.
The organisation Hands for Support and Life is defined as -- they say that each step they take, they find an abandoned youngster and then they lead them to comfort spaces, like helping them to complete high school, become part of a team, walk away from addictions.
These organisations set signs, teach, like the Cancun Choir, Plant for the Planet, and many other organisations and programs, such as Communitary Bond from the Caribbean University that not only cares for today's urgent need to have food to eat, but also the urgent need to play an instrument, to learn how to read to express ideas, how to write to express ideas, to express themselves with their bodies.
Yes, what we do at the Observatory is to decode figures, analyze large databases, make representations of the city.

Who does what? Who needs what?
Where is which service?
How do phenomena move around the city?
But when I see those representations I feel part of them, I feel I'm an actor in that representation but to do it, to truly act out I have discovered I must re-found myself, I need to re-found my relationships.
How do you achieve this?
I take the subsidiarity principle or the first Christians.
The principle of subsidiarity states that everything one could do on their own, with their own strength must not be transferred to another sphere.
The principle of subsidiarity, help me to remember them, evoke them, was created by the Christians from the catacombs, chased, poor, unattached families, impoverished, they had their leader cruelly killed and what they did was take action within that small space they could, within the small space remaining and they took action to solve the problems they were facing, what needed to be done in that moment.
They had to do it because letting others do it would have been suicidal.
And it was working together with what they had, that is, with willingness, with intelligence, with their heart, that they re-founded themselves.
This is migrating to me.
It is leaving behind all you know, carrying out a re-founding process using your compassion, the ability to feel with others.
"I am an immigrant, and wish to continue being one.
Let's not leave that immigrant category, because the strength, the insistence the ability to persevere, the physical endurance, the emotional strength, the mental strength are the tools that make way to hope for others,
for us
(Applause)
Let's see.
Raise your hand if you fear robots will achieve self-awareness and attack humanity.
When robots evolve enough, they will take over our jobs?
Since this is my job, it worries me there is no robot capable of ironing shirt collars.
(Laughter) Look for years we have lived together with robots.
Did you know, for example, the first book where robots appear?
Half a point to those who say Asimov.
But this book in reality is the Iliad. where Hephaestus employs golden robots for heavy-duty jobs.
Even more incredible, a few years later in classical Greek period the first designs of real robots, for example, the dove of Archytas, able to move wings using water vapor energy.
Later on, multiple designs for an automatic theater of Heron of Alexandria.
This type of robots continues to grow the following centuries in ways much more sophisticated.
For example, in the 12th Century, Al Jazari designs a large number of fountains, watches, musical machines, where many parts capable of accomplishing complex jobs utilizing hydraulic energy or spring energy.
This allowed us, in the 14th century, that "Works on Anatomy" of Leonardo da Vinci give more complexity to the number of actions performed by these figures as the articulations are implemented by using pulleys, springs, gears, and with that Leonardo proposes his own robot: the Knight.
Later on, in the 16th Century, more complex robots appear like the Monk.
This robot ordered by King Felipe II to thank God for the salvation of his infant son, Charles, after a serious fall from the stairs
The robot was capable of kissing a cross and strike his chest with his hand continuously without tiring, give thanks to God eternally for the miracle.
From an engineering perspective, the most interesting thing about the robot is that, for the first time, all the necessary mechanism for its functioning were included inside its structure.
Later on more robots appeared, for example the duck of Vaucanson, which included a simulation of the digestive process of the bird and furthermore it could be considered the great-grandfather of the dinosaurs. of Jurassic Park, as the first known examples of animatronics.
Seen this, one wonders why the scientific community got excited when Honda in the late 20th century, presents a robot capable of climbing stairs,
Well, let's be fair, these are not robots, but automats, much more sophisticated versions of wind-up toys which we all know and they repeat the same movements time and time again.
A real robot is capable of sense its environment. analyze the information and make decisions on its own about the actions that it must carry out.
And, unfortunately, in this era the necessary technology did not exist for this.
And technology is one of the required pillars for the progress of robotics.
Fortunately, in the 20th century, with the appearance of the vacuum tubes, electronics propels, which allows it, later on, in 46 design the first computer that was as large as this room.
But the arrival of the transistor, shortly after allows to reduce the size of all these to make it suitable to introduce it in the body of a robot.
And with this, I want to say that since the mid 20th century, the technology necessary to make this type of robots of which we speak about, was already available.
Even though, I do not see a single robot among us?
How is it possible that more than 60 years later the robots have not gotten closer to our lives?
And the answer is simple: for technology to reach closer to us there must be a convergence of two factors: that technology and also a need.
To understand: in Switzerland they are capable of creating a Hadron Collider does not mean that we want one on top of the TV.
This is why that maybe we are not eager where robotics permeates for the first time in the 20th century.
And this environment is precisely the industry.
In particular, the automobile industry.
Let's think that all happens around the 60s. and the date is not a coincidence.
The first world has just ended a World War.
It was attempting to boost the recovery promoting, like always, consumerism.
Unfortunately, to boost consumerism skill labor is lacking and let's not forget that the same WWII has just ended, it has decimated a huge part of the male population on working age.
It is why it is thought to substitute skill labor force with robots, called the "unimates," who are given the most heavy and hazardous work of the industry.
The second area where robotics permeates in the 20th century also follows political causes.
In this case, the space war between USA and Russia.
While it is true that the USA
was the first country to place a man on the moon. Russia was the first to place a robot the Lunojodvez, a few years later.
But maybe, we are more familiarized with the successive vehicle-robots, the rover, that NASA has sent to Mars in the last years and that transformed this planet, as far as we know, as the only planet to this moment, populated solely by robots.
Many people ask how does it benefit us, humans, to have robots in space.
Let's not forget that the same technology implemented in them is what is used here, on Earth, for work such as: deactivation of explosives, rescue of people in catastrophic zones or various jobs in dangerous zones.
Let's not think, not even for a moment, that currently Fukushima is being cleaned up like Chernobyl was cleaned in its time.
Nevertheless, thinking about this type of applications, the robots continue without reaching our homes and this happens because there is a third factor to consider to get robotics in our homes, and it is the cost.
When cost and need are not compensated. with difficulty we are going to acquire a new technology.
Think, for example, in the smartphones that currently sell like candy, but, to reach this point, it was necessary to first lower the price of the data bandwidth in mobile phones.
This is why, in general, robotics has reached our homes Currently, it is centering towards three axes.
The first is educational robotics.
Children like robots.
What they do not like so much is mathematics, physics, and all sciences courses.
And that is why many toy companies, some known as Mecano or Lego, have created educational robotics lines.
The purpose of this lines is to motivate in the early years vocations towards so-called professions of STEM type: science, technology, engineering and mathematics, which are careers that traditionally, have had good professional opportunities, but are not capable to attracting as many students as other careers.
In these cases, many times the robot kits are acquired by institutions like colleges, institutes, hobby clubs and even companies which provide special courses, workshops and seminars about subjects of robotics, therefore the cost is affordable because it is diluted
The second point is "assistive robotics," and in an obvious case where necessity supersedes the high cost.
What we wouldn't pay for the ability to walk again if we had lost our ability to use our legs?
The purpose of "assistive robotics" is to grant autonomy to people with disabilities to improve their quality of life; in case they are under the care of others, to lighten the burden of the caretakers, and, on the extreme, to avoid at all costs their institutionalization. with the consequences, or personal cost, and also, why not say it?, the health system
The third point where the robots come to our lives is the so-called "consumer robotics".
In this case, we are talking of very simple robots, which carry out simple tasks, but do cover necessities that share a big part of the population.
Therefore, the mass production, in general, allows to break down the costs.
I assume that many of us have seen these robot vacuum cleaners.
I would like to highlight that in none of the cases I mentioned robotics is taking our jobs.
The introduction of robots in education allow that the teachers can simply focus in transmitting knowledge instead of spending all his time in inventing strategies to get students attention
Using assistive robotics does not remove, in any way, the role of the doctor, the caretaker, the physical therapist.
Simply it allows them to concentrate in the most important of the case, that is the human factor.
Lastly, consumer robotics limits our freedom of some daily routine tasks to let us concentrate our energies in things that are more interesting to us.
Thus, the day in which technology allow us to create an affordable cost robots capable of covering the necessities that we have this precise moment or that could come up in the future, that day we will stop having problems with shirt collars.
(Laughter) (Applause)
(Tango music) (Tango music ends) This is the hooded grebe.
Hooded.
Grebe.
Hooded for the coloring of its head that is black like a hood.
It's a bird that lives in Southern Patagonia.
Specifically, in the province of Santa Cruz.
It's exclusive of the Argentines.
It does not exist anywhere else in the world.
It is in serious risk of extinction.
So, if we do not do what is necessary, in the next 10 years it may disappear from the face of the Earth.
It pretty peculiar.
Makes its nests in the west, in the high mesas.
In the high mesas of the province of Santa Cruz.
In the winter, after breeding its young, it migrates to the coast and inhabits the estuaries of the province of Santa Cruz.
It's important to say, and I always point this out, that is really a marvel that we Argentines have.
And that, sometimes, we ignore or don't know of its existence, because it's so close, right?
Atahualpa Yupanqui used to say: "How can he play the guitar well ... if he lives next door!"
And sometimes that happens a bit to us Argentines.
Most of you, surely, loves animals, or at least respects them.
In my particular case, I made contact with the animal world at about four years old.
Dad gave me this book: "Marvels and mysteries of the animal world" from the Reader's Digest.
And in this book I saw spectacular photos of animals but that were basically animals from other continents.
Especially from Africa.
There, I started to make contact with the word extinction.
I think most of you are familiar with the idea of extinction.
But, above all else, I was impacted by the idea of the irreversible.
That species like [the panda bear] or [the gorilla] could disappear from
the face of the Earth forever. Aged 18, I started a trip that changed my life, as a naturalist, as a lover of nature, precisely to the high mesas of Santa Cruz.
I was about six months travelling around those parts.
Walking between lakes, looking for grebes, sleeping in the most incredible places you can imagine.
Including abandoned outpost like [the one in the image]
Why?
To understand what was happening with the hooded grebe.
How many specimens there were.
World science became interested in this species when it was discovered in 1974.
And we are talking of a bird that weighs half a kilo.
Meaning, pretty big, within the world of birds.
Years passed, we grew older, with a group of technicians, biologists, park warden friends, and we started to visit hundreds and hundreds of lakes.
Why?
Because from messages of coworkers, friends in the province of Santa Cruz from other organizations, that work with Argentine birds, we started to see a drop in grebes.
In practice, it was difficult to see in many, many places.
And we must make clear that the grebe is an important tourism resource.
Thousands of persons come from around the world to watch it every year.
So, it's important for regional development, specially in the province of Santa Cruz.
So, in these lakes, we camped for days. and walked 25 to 30 kilometers between lakes, now guided by GPS.
When I was younger there was no GPS.
We spent a lot of time in these places that, I assure you, are the most remote of Argentina.
We walked through wonderful places like this one which today is a National Park.
When we said walked, or when we say walked, we must think that we are talking of a place with winds that, sometimes, are above 100 km per hour.
It's a very "coarse" environment, but it was the only way to gather information.
Technical and scientific information that allowed us to understand what the problems of the hooded grebe were.
This way me managed to determine based on the camping of scientists, technicians and assistants from all over the world, we started to find problems.
Introduction of exotic species like the rainbow trout where it should not be.
The American mink that attacks its nests.
And we went there in winter too and visited the high mesas in winter.
Here I spent the coldest moment of my life, I assure you.
With 20 degrees below zero.
At this point of the talk you must think we are crazy.
And we might be a bit crazy.
But, really, we are passionate about what we do.
And, of course, this started a joint project with our friends from "Ambiente Sur" to promote this subject, to show the community of Santa Cruz this problem.
Birds are an indicator of the state of the environment.
Birds tell us what is happening.
So, the problem became, over time, what I would call, and important environmental cause for Santa Cruz.
So all this scientific information, that we were gathering, would help us, now and in the future, to take actions.
Actions of management.
Let's see some now.
See, for example, in this image a group of reporters, from the most important media of Argentina, watching a colony of grebes, one of the few left, year on year.
And so we started a project what we call, rebirth in captivity.
Breeding grebes, is very complex, to bring them back to nature.
Up to now we have managed a few things I would like to point out.
We mentioned raising awareness with the population of a province and, today, I would say all of Argentina about the issues of a bird which is a genetic heritage of the Argentines.
It's something that is ours and that we are solely responsible of caring for.
We managed, also, with the biologists and the technicians, to stabilize the populations.
To stop the population drop.
We developed a biological station.
A place were dozens of researchers spend their days in the middle of nowhere but, at least, they have a place to shelter, to eat and to bathe.
And, also, we managed to create a National Park.
This National Park is a beacon for tourism development for all the communities in the region.
This National Park we also protect the "Cave of the Hands" a World Heritage site.
See how we went, from this little duck, that's not a duck, really, to the creation of a National Park and all it gives to us Argentines.
Now ... Why all the effort?
You must still think we are crazy.
Why so many human resources?
Why so much money?
Well, we are going through what scientists call, the "Sixth Great Extinction."
That is, there are always extinctions, in the history of life on the planet but this one, in particular, is tied to human activity above all things.
And if we compare with the rest of the process of life on Earth, we are 200 or 1000 times above the extinction rates that always existed.
The natural extinction rates.
So, we are responsible for this.
In the last 30 years, three grebes have disappeared from the face of the Earth.
And we do not want that by 2050 the hooded grebe be one more.
So, to finish up, I will use a couple of analogies.
One of them I share from my great friend Claudio Bertonatti: "If you open the hood of your car and see a loose part, you do not simply pull it out and throw it away, without knowing its use."
Well, the same thing happens with species in nature.
Our planet has 10,000 species of birds.
I think about 5000 species of mammals.
Plants ... many more.
The fact that these species go extinct and, that we do not really know what is their function, is something terribly dangerous.
And an ecologist from the US, Paul Ehrlich, once made an analogy, a metaphor, that's very interesting: "We live and travel in a great spaceship Earth."
Once, a family was about to get on this spaceship and they saw a person standing on a wing, removing rivets.
Alarmed, they said:
"You are crazy!"
"We all travel on this spaceship."
And, this man answers: "Don't worry, I've been removing rivets for years and, still, nothing happened."
"Also, we sell them at a good price."
Well, each one of those rivets are equivalent to the species that inhabit our planet, our rivers, our forests.
The planet is weakening at a fast pace.
15 years ago, when we environmentalists talked about climate change they laughed in our faces.
Today, we are seeing the consequences of this process.
The process of extinction is the "mother" of all crises.
But it something that does not affect us immediately because it is a very slow process.
But it is still something very worrisome.
And you may say: "Well. What can we do?"
We must take our kids to school, work every day, study.
"Another worry?"
"Really, it's too much!"
Well, I tell you it's not so impossible to help and follow this process.
There are many organizations like "Aves Argentinas" in Argentina that are concerned with the conservation of nature and that all these marvels, that we described, do not disappear.
The fact of approaching one of them is a way of helping and be present in these issues.
The one you prefer.
The one closest to your home.
With which you share idiosyncrasies. But these organizations do an enormous job, with a lot of economic effort, and human resources.
And it's good that we are many.
It's what we call "the power of many."
This is the "BirdLife International" network, for which we work worldwide.
There are organizations, worldwide, that are consulted by the national governments when a big project is going to be started.
Or when there is an important environmental issue.
Why?
Because they have thousands, hundreds of thousands, millions of members.
So I invite you to take part.
To visit them and learn about the issue.
It's the way that, among all of us, we can keep our nature from disappearing.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I would like to talk about the history of America.
It is a very simple, easy matter.
America's history can be divided into four parts: The first part is Spain. It lasted 300 years.
It started with Columbus and ended October 21, 1805 at noon.
It is difficult to think that history can change in a day.
But sometimes, during the process of change, there comes a day when the last straw is drawn and change occurs.
Spain turned America into an utter monopoly.
Non-Spaniards were not allowed to come here.
Not even all Spaniards could.
For example, Catalonians could not come here until 1800.
We spent 300 years without the possibility of becoming Indians.
What was Spain doing in America?
Utter monopoly.
Every piece of merchandise that could come in, came from Spain.
And what came out of America?
Metals.
How much? How much
came out of America?
81,000 tons of silver in 300 years.
With that money, Spain became the most powerful country in the world.
It built an empire where the sun never set.
Silver went from Spain to Genoa.
Or rather, as Quevedo states in his story: "It was born in America, it went through Spain and was buried in Genoa."
At the time, Spain was the country with the most powerful army.
Silver from America paid for it.
At that time, Spain represented the Church.
It produced the Council of Trent.
Charles V, who was Flemish and did not even speak Spanish, was crowned in Bologna.
He defeated the Turks at Lepanto,
fought against Protestants,
fought against Calvinists.
And Spain was protected behind the Pyrenees.
And during those 300 years, Spain said: I'm not interested in a political revolution; it's of no use.
I'm not interested in a religious revolution either.
I'm not interested in an industrial revolution either.
And America was left on the sidelines.
America lived outside that reality for 300 years.
And when did that change?
Who was Spain fighting against?
The fight was over the seas.
And with whom?
First with the Dutch, then with the British.
The Invincible Army.
Until one day, October 21, 1805 at noon, a one-armed, one-eyed man named Nelson, at the Battle of Trafalgar, disbanded the Spanish and the French fleets.
He did something nobody thought anyone could do.
Ships were sailing ships, as we see in the movies.
They stopped next to each other,
fired from the sides and boarded the ship.
No. Not this one.
He did something completely different: He sailed the ships down the middle.
And since there were no canons in the bow or stern, he fired the canons on both sides to turn over the masts.
And when the sail ships were left floating like bathtubs, they were hunted one by one.
They defeated 25.
They destroyed the fleet.
England took over the sea,
as it already had a very strong industrial growth, it already had Adam Smith and Locke, it already had a democratic parliament, it already had a coal industry - it produced three times more coal than the rest of the world - it already produced steam energy, made engines move with steam,
and made looms work with steam.
So, what did it bring us?
An open world or a closed one?
The previous one was closed, a monopoly.
This was an open world.
Absolutely open.
Over 1,800 miles of railroads were built.
Drinking water was hooked up in Montevideo in 1868.
Gas was brought in.
Good cattle was brought in.
Good sheep were brought in.
Refrigerators were built.
Financial organizations were created.
And we opened ourselves to the world.
And we reached any part of the world
with whatever we produced. And we grew - Argentinians, Uruguayans, Brazilians, Chileans, all of us - throughout 100 years of economic stability and open markets.
It was indeed a world that grew up in the always-open world of the Atlantic.
And that is why they made it to Maldonado in 1806,
because they had been victorious in Trafalgar in 1805.
Otherwise, they would not have made it to Maldonado.
And that is why they made it to Colonia del Sacramento in 1680, founded between them and the Portuguese.
The Portuguese provided the workers, the English, the ships. They unloaded the merchandise and that is when the only real Mercosur started to operate.
They rowed the merchandise at night from Colonia to Buenos Aires.
How did they pay for it?
With the black silver that came from Potosi, where silver was found.
And that was an open world.
Completely open.
Until when?
Until the Treaty of Versailles after World War I.
And from that day on, the world closed up again, with World War II.
And until when was it closed?
It was closed until the century ended.
And when the century ended, first, the Berlin Wall fell,
Gorbachev said it was the end of communism in Russia,
Deng Xiaoping ended the Mao Zedong era and opened China,
and a man called Bill Gates allowed us to manipulate time and space.
Did you hear Lacalle talk about electronic things?
Yes, I have a tablet with a panic button, too. And in the morning, when I wake up, I send a message to a friend who lives in New Delhi, and he replies immediately.
So, we manipulate time and space like human beings never did before.
Consequently, the world is totally global.
It is open.
And today's youth put on their backpacks and don't even need a passport.
They travel around the world.
The world is open again.
And it is in this open world that Uruguay has to be.
It cannot be shut in with three or four more, no matter how linked or related they are,
because it is neither good for them nor for Uruguay.
Those decidedly different four eras: The first was closed, the second was open, the third was closed, the fourth was open. They have, however, something in common, all four of them.
When I went to a meeting where there were only lawyers and accountants, who tried to figure out how not to pay taxes, they came from everywhere in the world, I asked them: "So, what do you have in common?"
Nobody knew.
What they have in common is that America's destiny is always determined from the outside.
Because we are, since then, since Columbus first arrived here until now, sellers of raw materials.
We do not sell knowledge;
we sell copper, iron, meat, soy. We sell some dairy products, cotton - Peruvian cotton is the best in the world. We are sellers of raw materials.
How do you change that?
With time, of course.
Nations are not happier because they are bigger or richer.
They are happier when they have more philosophers, scientists, and teachers.
The problem with Uruguay, the number one problem, is that 50 percent of youngsters are not in a position to understand how the world in which they live works.
50 percent.
We need more philosophers, more scientists, and more and better teachers.
We need to join the world!
Because that is how we grew up.
When all the veterans like me, and I am almost 100 years old, go out on Nostalgia Night, what do we remember? The good old days of our grandparents.
And we have to do that again.
With determination, with freedom, and a little bit of knowledge.
Have a good day.
(Applause)
We make three mistakes: the first is underestimating the quantity of information that we produce every day; the second is depreciating the value of that information; and the third is thinking that our principal problem is a distant and super powerful agency that is called NSA.
And it is true that NSA has the major access, better resources, the best tools, but they don't need any of that to spy on us, because we have everything there;
we live in glass houses.
This is Malte Spitz, a member of the German Green Party. In 2009, Malte asked his telephone company to send him all the data they had on him.
And the Deutsche Telekom, which was his company, told him no.
Two lawsuits later, they sent him a CD with an Excel table of 30,832 lines; like "The War and the Peace", but three times larger.
This endless document contains information from September of 2009 to February of 2010; it has exactly six months.
This isn't accidental, it has six months because in the summer of 2008 the European Union presented the Data Retention Directive, where they demanded each telephone company with more than 10,000 clients keep the data of all their clients for a minimum of six months and a maximum of two years.
They gave Malte data of 6 months, as if saying, "This is everything we have because the law forces us."
They probably owe him a year and half.
Malte, who couldn't process this amount of information, sent it to a weekly magazine which, in turn, contacted a data visualization agency to do something with it.
They took the data from Deutsche Telekom and Malte's public information, like, for example, information from his Twitter account or his blog.
And with this they created the map that you are looking at now, that is more than a map, it is more like a creepy automatic diary of Malte's life.
We can see when he catches a train, which he's doing right now, when he stops, where he stays, when he goes, how he goes, how much time it takes him, when he is eating, when he is sleeping, like right now;
when he takes a flight, who calls him, how long they speak, who sends him messages, what are the messages. We see everything. This is on the Internet. You can see it.
The reason why this happens
is because Malte
has a mobile in his pocket that every five minutes makes a "ping" to the closest antenna and tells it, "Do you have something for me?
Do I have mail?
Do I have any WhatsApp messages?
Has something happened in the world?"
Raise your hand if you have a mobile in your pocket that does the same.
Every five minutes our mobiles are saying, "I'm here, I'm here, now I'm here, hey, I'm here..." That's what happens.
And this doesn't have precedents.
We didn't have this before.
The direct equivalent of that is what we have now on the screen.
This is the profile of a person watched by the Stasi for years.
Looks like a napkin, doesn't it?
With 46 posts including his aunt, the milkman, the priest of his church --
These are five minutes in the life of Malte Spitz, and this information is automatic.
And the worst thing is that this is only the data on Malte, but Malte is surrounded by people like us with mobiles like his that are producing the same information; and that company sees everything.
This is a photo made by my colleague Juanlu Sánchez, a photo from manifestation 15-M.
But let's look at it from a telecommunications' viewpoint.
This square is full with mobiles.
Using those mobiles we can know exactly who is at the square, almost as if we put a cordon around;
with names and surnames.
And not only we know when they have come, where they come from and with whom, whom they leave with, whom they call to; we know everything about them. 
We even know if they are on the 4th floor or down at the square.
This is how that photo is seen by an algorithm of traffic analysis.
And here we begin to see some cool stuff.
We see that not all the people at the square are the same. There are people more important than others,
and if we have control over these telecommunications, this network, we can do things, like turning off the nodes on this square, in other words, isolate those who gather others around them from the rest.
Also we can do what the Ukrainian government did about a year and half ago: send out a message saying, "Dear user, you've been registered as a participant in a massive illegal manifestation."
We don't behave the same when we know that we are watched.
Since Jeremy Bentham we know that the best way to watch a population is for them not to know when they are watched and when not.
In Malte's case, during those 6 months, they were watching him for 78 percent of the time.
And we're only talking about our mobiles.
We are not talking about our computers, nor the cameras that are on the streets, in the stores and shop-windows, in the airports and trains, and wherever we are, being watched. We are not talking
about the radars on the roads that register us if we overspeed.
We are not talking about what we have in a wallet.
How many personal identification chips do we have in a wallet?
We have a lot: an ID card, a driving license or a transport ticket, credit or debit cards, reward cards.
A supermarket reward card.
20 years ago, the biggest personal data base in the world was not owned by NSA, neither by the Stasi, poor thing. It was owned by Wal-Mart, the American supermarket chain.
Why? Because when they
give you a reward card, what you're doing is telling a company who you are, where you live, how much you earn, what you buy, what you eat, how many kids you have, when you go on vacations, when you get sick. And all this we give them hoping that in six months or in a year, if we spend a lot of money, they'll give us a Tupperware.
(Laughter) And it's not different from what we do on the Internet, because Deutsche Telekom is a legit European company that has to obey the Data Protection Law, the same way as Telef贸nica.
Telef贸nica, here, has to obey the data protection laws, but it's not the same with the companies that make mobiles, operative systems, offer us "free" mails, give those apps that we download and ask us for access to a bunch of strange things, and we say, "They might need it for something."
They need it to sell it.
Why would Angry Birds want to have access to your GPS?
To make money!
Our observers don't care if we are nobody, if we are unimportant, because they're algorithms, not people. And our profiles are automatic; they exist even if nobody looks at it.
And the day somebody looks at it and changes your fate, your profile, your history, becomes your record.
You can end up being stuck at the airport in one of the 75 countries where being homosexual is illegal.
Or you could end up in a country where taking a picture of a pharmacy of mass production from the other side of the street is terrorism; this happens in the United States.
Or you could end up in Syria, where people are shot on the streets; activists, especially journalists.
You could end up in Mexico, D.F.,
where the Zetas use their access to the information of phone companies to see who contacts the police and cut their heads.
There are thousands of ways to be in the wrong place at the wrong time, and sometimes you don't even have to move.
In Holland they had a census. It was a census that included religions with high devotion rates in the world.
They wanted to know how many Protestants, Catholics, Jews they had to know how much money they had to put in each community, in each church or synagogue.
What happened? When the Nazis came,
they had their homework done.
Only 10 percent of the Dutch Jews survived in the Second World War.
If that database hadn't exist, the figures would've been very different.
What I mean is that our problem isn't the NSA, neither our corrupt governments, neither ambitious companies that want to sell our data, neither bad people, and it has nothing to do with their intentions, nor with their bad intentions.
The problem is that the very existence of that information makes us vulnerable in the ways that we can't anticipate right now.
We have to put curtains in our houses; we can't expect that somebody will do it for us.
We have to put them now.
We have to start using cryptography in our mobiles, in our communications, in our computers.
Start thinking twice every time someone offers us a reward card, and say, "Mmm..." Not only for us, because besides everything, this state of surveillance is one of the worst sicknesses that a democracy has.
So, I invite you, upon your arrival back home, start using Tor, and if someone wants to see what you are doing, he should ask for a search warrant.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
The political system in Venezuela and throughout the world is terribly outdated.
The democracy that we have nowadays does not respond to the needs of a modern man that is accustomed to answering that question that Facebook poses everyday: What are you thinking about?
As we feel completely helpless, as we feel we're not being represented by our politicians, as we feel we are not being heard, something similar happens as to when you are at the airport, and you go to one of those vending machines where you have to insert a bill, so you take out your bill, you insert it into the vending machine, you select your item, it returns the bill.
You stretch the bill a little, you try to insert it again, and it gives it back again.
You take out your wallet again, you get another bill, and you insert it. This time it takes it, you choose your drink, but it doesn’t give you the drink or the bill.
So, what do you feel like doing?
You feel like shaking the machine, shake it to see if it gives the drink or at least the bill.
Something like that happened on February 12 last year.
Thousands of students of this university and other learning institutions went on a great march, that filled the entire Venezuela square and the streets with lots of energy and an urge to express ourselves to
the prosecutor’s office to talk and ask for the release of some students who were unjustly imprisoned.
And not only for the unjustly imprisoned students, but also to make a complaint for a series of irregularities that are happening in our country.
You know them. You know what they are.
Well, a group of friends and I were there, at the march. We walked to the public prosecutor’s office, and when we arrived, the “leaders,” who had organized the march magically disappeared.
The person we were looking for, who was the Attorney General of the republic, didn’t respond to our claim and didn’t come out to listen to us. This translated into a great frustration, which translated into a feeling of being unheard again, which then translated into chaos,
which later translated into brutal police repression. --excuse me-- brutal police repression, which translated into dozens of dead and wounded students.
My friends and I, who like to write, went home that night and started to write opinion articles relating to what was happening and how we saw the political climate in the country.
My friend, Victor Becerra, a genius, wrote an article called “About the 23rd of January.”
The article, “About the 23rd of January,” is an article written from the perspective of a young man who lived during the 23rd of January, and he explains why the public sectors were not joining the protests, which was the big question, that everyone was asking.
Why weren’t the public sectors joining the student’s protest?
In a short time, the article had more than 300,000 views on the internet. It went viral.
The servers of Panfleto Negro, the magazine where we published this article, completely collapsed.
Parallel to that, I was also writing an article about how I saw the country’s political climate and compared it to one of my favorite movies: The Matrix.
What is the Matrix?
The Matrix is control.
A system that controls everything and has millions of minds connected and dependent on it, and it makes them believed that everything is okay, when in reality, there is a world of insecurity, scarcity, and inflation.
Any resemblance to reality is not a coincidence.
The article about the Matrix immediately goes viral. Hundreds and thousands of people read it on the internet, and the most interesting thing about this article is not that it was written, but the question that it generated afterwards in the big chat created in this blog.
One of the most interesting questions that arose in the this article was from an internet user, now that I remember, his name is Josh,
Josh asks: “If according to your analogy, we are in the Matrix, who do you think is our Neo?”
Josh, I am doing this TED talk today to give an answer to you and to the thousand other people who read the article and are probably asking the same thing. Who is Neo?
That question, I hope this evening or this afternoon, gets an answer.
For that purpose, we are going to analyze something that happened and that is happening with respect to our media and the way the internet completely transformed the way we communicate, the way we interact. The internet is changing everything.
Remember that before, the media was designed to be from the few to the many.
What does that mean?
Those who had money to buy a means of communication, a radio, a TV station, a newspaper, could spread a message, and the many, who were the recipients, inactive in this communication, absorbed this information, and there was no response.
Now, the means of communication, the internet, completely revolutionized this, and it makes communication from the many to the many. A young man on the 23rd of January can write an article that can reach More than 300,000 people.
Or another young man, wherever he lives, writing on his computer can reach the director of Zeta Magazine, who called me personally to ask me: “Please, let me publish your article on our magazine’s next edition.”
Speaking of Zeta magazine, here’s an interesting fact: Zeta magazine was traditionally an opposition magazine, but it is the most read magazine by people who support officialism.
Why?
At the end of the article, we include an email address where we could be contacted to give us feedback about the article and Zeta magazine. I only got, well, not me, my mum got many greetings and regards.
On top of that, the article’s last paragraph had a call for action. a call for action in case you agreed with us, if you think there is a different way to do things, if you think the solution is not through violence, if you think there’s a Venezuelan who can innovate, who can propose wonderful ideas, come.
Join us.
The email collapsed with hundreds of people writing: “I want to participate.” “How can I help?”
We didn’t know what to do with this group, with the guys, Victor, Mari, Franz. We were worried. Then we started to make a… We began to think… We are going to tell all these people that we are going to meet next Saturday at the Cubierta square at the Central University.
We got there that Saturday, and almost 100 people were there Saying: “I want to do something.”
I remember that day as if it were yesterday. There were 100 people there, and they tell me: “Well Javier, congratulations on your article, excellent. I also think the same. I want to do something different.
Let’s sit down.”
We introduced ourselves. There were housewives, professionals, sociologists, anthropologists, psychologists, programmers, everything, and many students.
Then they tell me: “Ok, what’s the plan?
How will we solve this problem?”
Guys, I don’t have a plan.
I have a dream.
And the dream is that this can be different.
Lets sit down, and together lets build proposals to know what we can do different together.
I tell you this: I don’t know how this ends, but I know how it starts, and it starts with you. It starts with me. it starts, because we are all conscious about our role as citizens, and that small share of influence that each of us has can have an incredible domino effect that can greatly transform society.
So there we were, at that meeting, and I began to know what political activism meant.
It meant getting up early on Saturday and coming to the Central University, instead of sleeping a bit more, and meeting up with these people.
The first impression was that of those 100 people, who were there on the first day, on the following Saturday, only half of them came, and the next Saturday, there were only 12, because the cost of political participation was too high. Not everyone can sacrifice a Saturday morning, not taking your son to the park, not going grocery shopping, the queue to make your purchases, in order to come and do activism and to come and think how we are going to come up with real proposals.
Then we stared to think what to do. What do we do?
We only have 12 people, all from the Central University, and we started to… and we said the following: “Ok, we want to come up with proposals for the country, but it so happens that at our university, we can’t even agree on putting up a fence.
How, then, are we going to agree on making proposals for the country in general,
if here, at our own home, were you are, you can’t do it?
That was happening.
We began to think and to investigate. Through TED conferences we found very interesting experiences like something that is going on in Argentina.
It is called the Net Party. The Net Party is a simple idea.
They created a phone app where all citizens can find out what is being debated in the legislature.
On that app, you can log in, and you can vote: yes, no, affirmative, or even refrain.
And more importantly, it translates the information from a legalistic language to a common language, a language that we can all understand
because political information is usually encrypted.
Only a few people, lawyers, can understand, and those lawyers are the only ones who can truly participate in the conversation.
What happens with the rest?
What happens with us?
We can’t.
The party’s idea… I met up with Santiago the founder of the Net Party, here in Caracas.
We were thinking about how to start this app project here in Venezuela,
and we decided to start With the Central University.
It turns out that we also elect Student representatives.
We choose student representatives, who go to the school council, and then bring the information to the students.
I got this from a Twitter account of one of the student representatives.
After the information about the decision that's made at the school council goes public, he takes a picture of the printed copy, he posts it on his Twitter account and you have to zoom in to be able to read it, and by the way, it is written… considering that that resolution number 257 in his apartment…
Please.
Who reads that?
Then, I ask myself: Could it be that students are apathetic?
Or could it be that the system deliberately (since we still use the old systems of the 18th century) fail to motivate us to participate?
To me, that is exclusion.
So we decided to take the idea of the Net Party, bring it to Venezuela, to the Central University, start developing this open code app, and set up people to work on it from all the fields of the University, to develop this, and make our “UCV Online” project.
UCV online is basically about bringing the ideas of thousands and thousands of students, who are eager to participate, into a single place.
I attended many student assemblies, and the experience is really interesting.
A student assembly is arranged for 10 in the morning.
Remember that you have to study for your exams. Remember that you have to work. Remember that you have to wait in line at the supermarket, and at 9 a.m. the student assembly gathers up to debate any topic: the scholarship, the library, any of the topics that interests us.
A few arrive at 9 a.m. They start complaining, because the others haven’t arrived yet.
“Lets wait a little more, until 9:30”
At 9:40 we start.
At 9:42 everyone starts to speak, to disagree, and to express their opinions,
and then, after half an hour of this, time is up. Everyone has to leave, and they start to plan the next conference, the next assembly
This method is extremely ineffective.
This method discourages participation.
What we want to do is use this open code tool to begin reaching better solutions with more consensus, to use the tools the internet provides us to update the system.
I always hear… There is like a cliché:
Towns have the government they deserve. I don’t
believe that.
I think that perhaps we don’t have enough options to choose from.
That is why we have what we have.
My dream, my intention today is not only to create participation at the Central University,
but also that there is a “UCV Online,” a “Metropolitana Online,” and why not, a “Bolivariana Online.”
That we all start to talk, to converse, to listen to each other,
and not just have a decision, but a conversation.
Guys, I don't mean to tell you that the system, the political problems in Venezuela, will be resolved with a software.
It's not that.
Many more things are required,
but if the internet and technology allow us and give us the tools, Why not use them?
The message I want to convey, here this morning, for each and every one of you, is that we've had enough of waiting for a savior,
enough of waiting for Neo to come with his magic powers and strength to save all Venezuelans.
Josh and all the people who read the article, I want to tell you this morning: You are Neo.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Designer drugs, artificial intelligence and development, in particular and in general, will change the map of diseases over the next few years as we know it now.
Many of the diseases that you worry about in the next decade will start to subside or disappear.
However, progress will come with changes in the environment we live in.
Sophisticated antibiotics sterilize our intestines against many bacteria.
Last generation soaps clean our houses, leaving them dust-free.
Gel alcohol takes away bacteria from the kids hands when they come back from the park.
Parks filled with tiles to which we used to arrive through dirt roads and that are now paved.
Dirt is as important for us as oxygen.
We chew dirt, we eat dirt, we breathe dirt and we touch dirt from the same day we begin to breathe oxygen.
Mankind will suffocate in the next decades for the lack of dirt.
Because there are molecules in dirt which are important to develop our immune system, to sophisticate our intestines, to grow our lungs, to prevent allergies, to improve asthma.
Mankind in a bubble will start seeing diseases come up that today are rare or even nonexistent, for having divorced from dirt.
So medicine, once it starts and finishes the celebrations for having cleared from our lives the diseases we so much fear, will have to start looking for dirt.
But intelligently, with science, when we, all of us living today and in the future, become richer and live better lives, to look for dirt, safe dirt.
Dirt for the rich.
(Laughter) (Applause)
Are you ... are you seeing me?
Audience: Yes!
OK.
Are you looking at me?
Audience: Yes!
Hopefully, there were doubts in between your answers.
Because it is not the same to see than to look.
I propose you a really short experience.

Not mandatory, for those who feel like it.
Turn to the person closest to you and
hold their gaze for a couple of seconds.
Nothing happens.
(Laughter) Well.
I won't ask you if something happened between the two of you ... (Laughter) but I am dying of curiosity.
I am almost certain that something happened, because, I insist, to see is not the same than to look.
Seeing is a matter of the eye, it's a matter of "I."
I see here there are people, there are men, there are women, there are families, there are lights.
When I look, I look with all of my being.
That's why, for example, when a tourist group stands in front of a landscape will only have a few people shed a tear.
The Little Prince, seems you were right!
What is essential is invisible to the eye.
What's the problem with looking?
Number one, fundamental: I don't know anything about my look.
If someone asks you: "Why are you looking at me like that?"
"How should I look at you?"
"I don't know."
If I go and look for it in a mirror, I'll find my eyes looking back at me.
I lost the look on the way to the mirror.
Becuse the look is always built upon the encounter with the other.
That's why so many love stories start with a look, isn't it?
"Guys, I met someone and you won't believe how he looked at me."
(Laughter) "He's going to talk to me."
And how many love stories come to an end before the other says there's no love anymore.
But in his look you'll find nothing indeed.
It is because the look has unconscious elements, unmanageable.
I cannot build it.
Try it out, if tonight you have a promising saturday, to rehearse a sexy look.
(Laughter) Try coming home and say: "I want to have an Ingalls mother or father look," and turns out I'm living like the Addams or the Simpsons (Laughter)
Try it out, teachers, to manufacture a Pichimahuida look.
(Laughter) It won't come out if I lost my teaching vocation ... or if I never had it.
We live our lives looking and being looked at, we live calling upon looks.
"Look at me when I'm talking to you!,"
says an angry parent to his child.
The teacher at the brink of a nervous breakdown: "Guys, if you don't look at me how can you pay attention?"
A teacher that tried to surprise her students, the day I came, she had a helmet with tinkering lights on.
(Laughter) And that's how she taught Math!
(Laughter) I asked: "Does she always
bring that helmet?"
"No, she also has wigs of many colors."
(Laughter) And I said: "What a vocation!"
(Laughter) Some rich husband maybe, that buys her so many wigs.
They are truly expensive.
But then I thought: "She does it to be looked at, or seen."
Maybe looked at.
From there to be paid attention to, there is another step further, there has to be an interesting proposition.
I was a teacher for 33 years and things happened to me with looking, more than twice.
And in that sense I am very Freudian.
I learned through Freud that when something happens more than twice you have to analyze it.
What happened to me?
At the end of the year reunion, my students were graduating as teachers.
A perfect exam, perfect ten.
And when I signed their grades I told them: "Why didn't you take part in class if you knew this?
Why hold back this knowledge?
Why not share it with the rest?"
"No, teacher. I once
asked a question in March
and you gave me a look that made me never want to ask another question."
Wow.
Direct wound to the teaching vocation.
So then my classes often started like this:
"Guys, hello. My name is Liliana.
I have big eyes.
(Laughter) I have hypothyroidism.
I looks like my eyes have great decision.
And every once in a while they stop someone from making questions and you, future teachers, cannot allow it.
Next to the institute there is a little bar called the "Psico Bar," how about we take some coffee and talk about our look?"
Because you, future teachers, will spend your lives look at patients, parents, families, schools, other teachers, directives, and cannot afford to stop someone with your look.
Even worse, prevent questions from being made.
Because when you stop asking, you stop learning.
And a teacher that stops learning, is kind of weird, right?
Our whole discipline revolves around it.
We live looking, and being looked at.
Technology started coming into our lives.
And then something happened.
Just as I said you cannot build a look, what I want to say is a look builds subjectivity.
It impacts on the subject.
It is not the same that a newborn child meets a mom loving look that transmits love just by looking
than saying "I love you" several times. A baby does not understand because there is no linguistic universe to understand that "I love you" is love, but the look goes right through.
What happens when this babies meet their mothers and they cannot stop checking their phones while breastfeeding?
What happens when this kids are having lunch with Sapo Pepe, or Peppa Pig, with screens and not loving looks from their parents that are happy to see them eat their food?
Or parents that cannot leave their phones to hear how they are doing in life in school recess, in class, or with friends.
What will happen to these families that have hacked the dialog and communication between them because they cannot have lunch with no screens around?
What will happen to schools that do not break lines?
Dear teachers, let's break lines!
If not, they go from screens to a classmate's back
Let's sit in tables that encourage collaborative work so they research, they look, they listen, they know each other so they learn from diversity, that diversity that's at stake in every work table in a classroom.
Our kids were born and technology was right there waiting for them.
I don't think they were born digital natives.
There's no proof that says they were born digital natives.
I do know they were born in a society that awaited them with screens since very little.
And kids have always wanted to hold what they see their parents hold.
Technology is a wonderful tool.
It's here to stay.
We'll have more and more, but it should always be used in favor of the human being
It's now always like that.
And, you know what?
I do not know of any technological tool that gives meaning to life.
If not, we wouldn't have so many teenage suicides.
Life's meaning is a result of an internal turism, a search within oneself.
"Why did I come to this world?"
"What do I want to be?"
"What do I want to do?"
And it doesn't come from technology.
It's a journey with many stations, and in each station there is always "another" or a few others.
And may there appear the coordinates of love.
Hopefully!
Because if not, the road leads to solitude.
Psychiatrists tell us the biggest cause of depression is solitude, at any given age, it is solitude.
Man was not born to be alone.
God said it, right?
He may have ruined Adan's life, but he said:
"It is not good for men to be alone," And then there were Eves. The life's meaning must be found
within oneself. Within oneself, in that journey I'm proposing to you, where technological tools do not help. Help comes from another,
from the encounter with another, human encounter.
Truth is, I'm saddened to see kids alone.
I'm saddened by little patients whining about loneliness in a classroom or recess, of the telephone not ringing on weekends to get together in a moment of friendship.
And that is our debt to childhood, and I want to share it with you.
I want to leave you with that debt and at the same time with a strategy that I learned from a man with an impressive look, Eduardo Galeano, Uruguayan writer.
What a look and what a voice!
In that fabulous story about the sea in which a child wanted to know the sea and asks his father to take him there.
His father takes him.
And when the sea is there, with its immensity, the kid, trembling, holds his father's hand and says: "Dad, dad, help me look!"
It is the adults' duty to help childs look because the world is very complex, very difficult.
And it doesn't happen naturally like before when there were no screens.
Today kids press a button and the world comes to them if we are not there to filter and choose for them.
Aside from paying that debt to childhood, I also profoundly wish for us to re-encounter ourselves, to repair that breach that separates us that's anything else than the results of intolerance to one another, the one who thinks, feels and dreams different than us.
We do not deserve humanity to be taken from us.
We do not deserve the "-isms" of post-modernism: individualism, egoism, narcissism, capitalism, consumerism, now-ism.
All of that lessens our humanity.
And you will ask me, how do we repair that breach.
With what look?
Looks have to be loving, peaceful, inclusive.
"But, Liliana, you've just told us you cannot build a look."
No, you cannot.
But If we want to have loving looks, if we want our looks to caress one another and not do any harm, we have to work on ourselves, on our own world, by looking at us, listening to our desires, looking for what's missing in order to be happy.
To look for that happiness, to look for that frustrated dream, that pending matter, to work the anger and surround myself with positive people and, most of all, become hopeful again.
This is a debt we owe teenagers.
If they see us without hope, it will cost them very much to build a life project.
I'd like to say that "Nano" Serrat used to say: "Today can be a great day," to let go of emotion, to communicate again, to re-encounter with the look.
Let's not allow screens to hack the times for love.
Truly, today can be a great day to look at each other again.
It's your homework.
(Applause) (Cheers)
Good afternoon, everyone.
Today I'd like to talk about a topic that is rarely spoken about:
Who does machismo really threaten?
All of us, men and women, know that is threatens the lives of women, it's a matter of browsing the newspapers every day or of watching the news to realize the severity of the problem.
But do we really know how machismo threatens the lives of men?
Nowadays, it's a risk to be born a man.
Eighty percent of the homicides committed in the world are perpetrated and suffered by men.
It's a daunting statistic.
Being born a man is truly a risk factor.
In Central America in particular, this is a serious situation.
Nine out of 10 people murdered are men.
One out of every 50 men over the age of 20 will die before he turns 31.
In other words, in Central America, mortality rates for young men are high, and the main reason is cultural conditioning that pushes men to die to prove their manliness.
Last year in Honduras, 91 percent of homicide victims were men.
In Argentina, the same thing: 86 percent of those who died a violent death were men,
as were 80 percent of the suicide victims.
In Mexico, of the 205,000 people deprived of liberty, 95 percent are men.
I mean, imagine the risks of being a man.
Men are victims of machismo, but many don't know it. And if they do, they remain silent
for fear of being rejected.
They don't talk about this.
This matter is not discussed.
Men endure machismo when they have to prove it by flexing their muscles to see who's stronger.
And this way they risk being hurt, mutilated, killed, incarcerated, or physically or psychologically wounded.
What's behind this overwhelming risk that men face, just by virtue of being men?
It's the belief that a man must show his manhood by using violence -
that is to say, manliness is proven by violence.
There's strong cultural conditioning that influences the way in which we educate children with these beliefs, with this mentality.
From early childhood through adulthood,
boys are supposed to prove in games, in fights, who's more of a man.
Sometimes parents say: "Don't let that kid beat you up or I'll beat you up myself!
Come on, let him have it!"
Then you have the gangs of young teens who confront each other every day in Central America. At least 20 of them die every day in El Salvador, in Guatemala. And to a large extent, they die because of their gender.
But that isn't talked about. Instead we talk about poverty, security issues, crime.
But no one mentions the fact that they die flexing their muscles to see who's more of a man.
And that's an important part of what I'd like to talk about this evening:
dying as men.
When you're dead, who's left thinking you're more of a man?
A patriarchal culture instills doubts in men about their identity.
It dictates that men, as proof of their manliness, completely distance themselves from so-called feminine behaviors.
Like what?
Like expressing emotions or feelings, showing empathy for the suffering of others, opting for dialogue rather than confrontation,
giving up the need for revenge and many others.
In other words, anything that's considered feminine or a sign of weakness.
The worst that can happen to a man is to be considered girlie.
Meaning, that he could be like us -
as if women were the worst!
(Laughter) They don't realize this is an insult that offends not just women, but also the human dimension of every man.
Because everything defined as feminine isn't just feminine - it's human.
But men are deprived of this from an early age, because they're emotionally castrated from childhood on,
and distanced from their emotional being.
When emotions are thus repressed, when a man can't express his emotions for fear of what others would say - unless he's drunk - (Laughter) Right? Then that man slowly turns - (Applause)
That man slowly turns into a time bomb.
A teenager, for instance, that has had to keep quiet and can't express his pain at having been abandoned, mistreated, hurt, damaged, abused, in his teen years, in his adult life, will someday explode, because he has repressed too many negative emotions, because he's never in his life been allowed to express them.
I remember a young kid, one of the thousands we work with, who once told me in a workshop: "Please don't ask me to tell my mom I love her.
"I can knife or rob someone, no problem, but I'm afraid to tell my mom that I love her."
Why so much fear around expressing feelings?
And his palms were sweating - he was very nervous.
Because from an early age, men have been taught that machismo is a successful personality trait.
All of these macho behaviors and roles that most of us are familiar with are presented as indicators of success, of how to be macho man.
"I want to be like him, he gets tons of women, he has this and that ..."
As if that were the ultimate.
But actually, they are the victims of this recipe for masculinity that sickens a man and distances him from his human dimension.
This is what he's taught at home, with his father as a role model, or by other adult men, older brothers, or in school.
The amount of male violence on TV is overwhelming.
I remember that as a kid, we'd see just two cowboys shooting at one another in movies. Now it's Terminator, killing this guy and that, and the more killing there is, the more manly it is, and the more successful that manliness is. We are overwhelmed by violence -
in the movies, in video games - which is being sold to us as successful manliness.
And all this is happening at a very interesting historical moment.
Men have been stripped of their role associated with manliness, which is the role of the breadwinner.
How many men in the world have lost their jobs?
And with that, they haven't just lost their income; they're in crisis over their own masculinity.
This is a man who feels frustrated, who feels he cannot perform the male roles that he is expected to perform.
At the same time, this unemployment has generated a deep crisis in the roles themselves,
a crisis in traditional machismo.
Many men feel frustrated, uncomfortable, because at this same historical moment, women have started earning money. They've entered the workforce and beome part of public life. So there has been a change in power relations between men and women.
Women are earning income, which has shifted the balance of power within their families. Men used to be the breadwinners while women earned less, didn't work, or stayed home.
Now in many homes, it's the women who earn a higher income, and that has shifted the power, but the mindsets haven't changed.
Women started to take an active role in public life, but men don't participate in family life.
Women perform roles that they have never performed before, and we are surprised to see more and more women in these roles every day.
But have men really begun
to participate in their families
and in domestic life?
No, they haven't.
Men aren't spending time with their children, hugging them, kissing them, involving themselves in their education and upbringing.
This isn't the case yet.
Trying to conform to macho roles doesn't actually lead to success in life.
It leads to unhappiness.
So machismo is not just a recipe for dying young, it's a recipe for being unhappy.
Boys are taught from an early age that even fatherhood is taboo.
"You play with dolls?
You're going to turn gay."
"That's for little girls." And worse things than that.
A man feels pressured from an early age to prove his manhood, through his actions or through violence.
Sexual promiscuity is another requirement for manliness that is not necessarily a road to happiness.
Men are viewed in terms of economic value. A man without work, without a job?
"Oh, no - I don't want to go out with you. You have no money? I'm not interested."
So men are seen as economic objects and women are seen as sexual objects.
That's an exchange of money for sex.
There's also dependency on women.
A lot of men are overly dependent, and the truth is that we as mothers create and maintain the dependency oursleves.
"No, son, don't wash that, don't cook. Your sister will take care of everything. I'll take care of everything."
And that keeps him from developing life skills. And it encourages alcohol abuse, because a lot of the addictions to drugs and alcohol have to do with that emotional repression.
A man can cry under the influence of alcohol and nobody is going to call him a little girl.
They'll say, "You poor thing! Let me make you something to eat."
(Laughter) (Applause) We have to be able to laugh at it, but there is a lot of pain involved.
Men's liberation from machismo needs to happen now.
Just as for many years women's liberation was a critical issue, the time has come for men's liberation.
It truly is a necessity.
A man free of machismo has more self-esteem.
A man liberated from machismo is more capable of giving and receiving love.
This is such an important step.
We have seen changes in thousands of men, by focusing on a different approach to manliness,
one that's free from these roles.
At the Center for the Prevention of Violence, we've seen how rapidly crime rates decrease.
For example, we did a study in one neighborhood,
where we applied this model and methodology for 6 months, and the crime rate dropped by 53 percent.
In the control neighborhood, during the same 6-month period, the crime rate increased by 16 percent.
This means it can work, and that's why we believe that this approach to preventing violence deserves attention.
That's why we've trained people in Guatemala, El Salvador, Honduras, Nicaragua, Chile, Argentina, Uruguay, Mexico, Colombia - we've trained many people in several countries,
but in Central America, there are 12,000 proponents of this methodology.
I would like to ask you right now
what do you think would happen - and let's dream for a moment - let's imagine we wake up tomorrow in Nicaragua, and in 80 percent of the households there's a father present, not only physically, but full of affection, involved in his children's upbringing capable of communicating with his children from the heart and as interested in what happens in his children's lives as their mother is.
What would happen in those families?
What effect would it have on the country?
When I ask this question - and I've posed it in many venues - there is overwhelming consensus:
There would be a profound and decisive social transformation.
Less violence within families, less violence on the streets, less crime in general, fewer people in prison, less addiction to various substances, to alcohol, drugs, tobacco, to all that is destroying us.
But above all, there would be an even more transcendental change: we would be happier as a society.
There would be fewer drop-outs, public health would improve and it would even result in a more effective fight against poverty.
Because this relates to poverty, too,
these beliefs and mindsets that impede human development,
because that really affects family relationships.
How can we achieve this?
We have to start now,
in our own homes, with our children, with the children next door, in our schools, in our most immediate environments.
Let's educate children to freely express their emotions.
Let's show them how. Let's not be afraid to do this,
let's stop being afraid of what others may think.
"Oh, what will people say if my boy is playing with dolls?"
No - what you'll tell him is:
"Sweetie, you're practicing to be a dad.
Just like your sister is learning how to be a mom, you're learning how to be a dad."
(Applause) Let's stop being afraid, because there's a lot of fear about what others may think. It probably won't be easy, but it's worth a try.
When we do it and start to see - I remember a friend telling me: "I never bought him any dolls, and what would my son do?
He would grab the little truck that I bought him and sing it a lullaby.
So men do indeed have these tender feelings, but we don't allow them;
we force men not to develop them.
Governments and societies in general, as well as the media, have an immense responsibility.
It's not enough that each of us commits to this.
Institutions need to promote this first and foremost in education policy, in the media, in cultural policy, and we need to start changing these gender roles that are so damaging to men and women.
I would now like to tell you something on a more personal note.
My dad died when I was seven years old.
He was an immigrant who left his country and his mother, and he never had the chance to see his mother again.
He would suffer in silence and I saw him as distant.
He was a good man, but very distant.
And to be honest, I would have liked to know what was in his heart that was so difficult for him to let out.
So I dedicate this talk to my father and to all men like him, who suffer in silence from machismo, this cultural mandate, and who, just like him, die prematurely, and to a great extent, because of it.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Summer sunset on the beach.
A couple is sitting on the warm sand watching the Moon rising over the sea.
In a romantic burst, one tells the other: "I give you the Moon."
I don't know about you, but if I pass by, I stand up and say in a firm tone: "That is not possible.
The 1967 treaty on the principles that govern the activity of countries regarding the use and exploration of outer space, including the Moon and other celestial bodies, bans the expropriation of the Moon.
And it arises from civil law that nobody can give away what does not belong to them."
(Laughter) That's all, see you!
(Applause)
I am a space lawyer. That exists.
My work is to think legal frameworks to prevent and resolve conflicts in space.
But, are there laws in space?
Why should I worry about what happens up there?
Our lives depend on space much more than we think.
Without satellites we could not watch TV, send an email, make a bank transaction, find a street on the GPS and worst of all, we could not find a match in Tinder.
(Laughter) Unlike what we usually think, today space is a congested environment, a disputed and highly conflictive area.
And where there are conflicts, we know, there are lawyers.
(Laughter) But what is happening right now in space?
Let's suppose that at this moment all of us are inside a large spaceship and we want to make a tour through the solar system.
We take off, everything is going well, until the first problem comes up: Where does space begin?
What is the upward border of countries?
There is no international consensus on it.
And it's difficult to achieve, because when you establish a border you put a limit on your jurisdiction.
For example, if the U.S. limit is 300 kilometers above sea level, at kilometer 301, China or Russia could place spy satellites.
We continue on the ship.
We are travelling at an average speed of 27,000 km/h.
Suddenly, an out-of-service Russian satellite collides us on the side. It dents a solar panel and breaks our antenna.
Who is responsible?
How do you prove liability in space?
Is there any all-risk space insurance?
(Laughter) This is nothing extraordinary.
In fact, it is becoming more frequent because nobody takes care of space junk.
Today, 94 percent of objects in space are garbage.
It is as if 94 percent of the cars we see on the street were abandoned junk.
Even worse, because that junk moves at 27,000 km/h.
But we continue our cosmic journey.
After a few months we make a stop on an asteroid to stretch our legs and get water to drink mate.
There, we discover that it contains heavy metals such as gold and platinum, so we decide to take a souvenir.
It is established that the Moon and other celestial bodies are a world heritage site and don't belong to any country or company.
But what happens, for example, with minerals or water extracted from asteroids?
Is asteroid mining allowed?
It looks like science fiction, but it's not.
In fact, the U.S. and Luxembourg have already passed laws regulating the exploitation of natural resources in space.
Even Argentina has a similar bill in Congress.
We run out of fuel, so we come home.
But soon after we receive an invoice for parking on the asteroid.
Sounds weird, but this happened
in 2001, when a NASA probe called NEAR landed on the asteroid Eros.
A year later, NASA received an invoice for parking of the probe in the asteroid at a rate of USD 20 per day.
The invoice and the payment claim came from a guy, Gregory Nemitz, who claimed to be the owner of the asteroid based on rights granted by the U.S. Constitution. Obviously, NASA replied negatively.
But Nemitz insisted and decided to bring the case to court.
It took three years and the intervention of two courts to definitely dismiss the claim.
There is a topic I didn't talk about yet and I am especially concerned with:
The militarization of space.
Since the 60s there is a military presence in the different orbits of the Earth.
But now we are facing a new threat: That space itself becomes a new battlefield.
In recent years, the great powers intensified their military activity in space, where they tested different weapons such as missiles that destroy satellites, or satellites that collide with each other.
What is called "kinetic kill" or "death by movement".
Due to this, the U.S. decided to create by the end of 2019 a new armed force in space called Space Force.
What we had only seen in the movies today is becoming a reality.
We are at the dawn of a new space age.
And we aim to become a multiplanetary species.
Space activities have created countless benefits.
But also huge challenges.
Space debris, asteroid mining, privatization of space, tourists in orbit, permanent bases on the Moon, colonization of Mars, and the militarization of space.
Those are some of the issues that space lawyers must resolve.
But new questions arise.
How should we govern space in the future?
Is space a place where we should compete or cooperate?
Space is an infinite source of dreams.
And there lies the collective future of humanity.
That's why, for me, the great challenge of space law is to guarantee a rational and responsible approach in the use of space for the benefit of all humanity, so as not to commit in space the same mistakes we made on Earth.
But most of all, to keep those dreams alive.
(Applause) Also, if you see me on the beach, rest assured that I will not sue you.
If necessary, I also give the Moon.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I've already said this to my companions in Block 10 at Soto Del Real. I hope you all understand what I mean, just as they did at the time.
A while ago, I was having a hard time, and I became depressed.
It was difficult personally, and thus everything else was too.
It put me out on the street.
I thought I would never end up
that way. Me? Not me, impossible.
But yes, I had become
yet another homeless. I begged in the streets, with others; some begged for drugs, others for alcohol.
Luckily, I didn't.
But then again, why not?
I could have begged for drugs, for alcohol, and end up really bad.
But instead, I turned to crime.
I was offered a chance, and I took it to get off the street.
That's how I ended up in prison.
And when I got there, I tried to make myself strong saying: "I don't judge anybody," "Not me, I'm not so proud."
But yes, I did judge others, I am prideful, I yell, I lose my temper.
When we're in there, we have a lot of time for self-assessment.
It's hard work: reflection, introspection, and all that along with a very heavy guilt for what you have done.
It's pretty tough.
Do me a favor: look at those around you, to your right or left. [Look them] in the eye and ask yourselves, please, "How do I relate to this person?"
Well, keep in mind that we are so much more related to one another than we think.
The things we do everyday, some 30 or 40%, are involuntary, and have nothing to do with who we are, are the same in any social environment; it doesn't matter we're in prison or not.
Therefore, we shouldn't judge others for the mistakes they make because those are the same mistakes that they judge us for.
What I'm trying to get at is that I believe that everybody, absolutely everybody in here, in one way or another, wants to achieve a certain emotion stability, a balanced life with their families ultimately, to be happy, be it in prison, wherever we may be.
I'm going to keep doing it.
And I believe that for the rest of your lives, every day, - I'd like you to say it with me, please - Gerson Garcia Fluxa: Me too!
(Audience) Me too.

Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Do you remember what did you do on 11th July, 2010?
Don't you?
I do! I not only know what I did
but I also know what all of you were doing. You were watching on TV how the Spain National Football Team was proclaimed World Champion for the first time in history.
You remember now, right? Of course!
Well, I know there is somebody
over there who is thinking: "I didn't watch it because I don't like football."
You may not like football but you've heard about it, for sure.
So, little by little, we remember the details.
The World Cup took place in South Africa, we played the final against Netherlands
and we won 1-0.
Who scored the goal? Iniesta.
Minute? 116, when it seemed that
the match was about to finish and we were going to start penalty kicks. So everybody was thinking: "If Iker Casillas doesn't catch the balls, who knows what will become of us."
Football World Cups are held every four years.
The first was on 1930, and there's been 20 since then. Spain has won only one by the skin of its teeth.
However, in the donation and transplantation world championship, which is held every year, Spain is world champion for 26 times consecutively.
(Applause) So congratulations because all of us here are world champions for 26 times in a row. And not precisely by winning 1-0 on the extra time: but defeating all the matches heavily,
5-0, 10-0. And why am I saying this?
Because the organ donation index from deceased donors in Spain is the highest in the world.
In order to gain a precise idea about it, European Union's average is around 21 donors per million people, which is more or less the same average in the United Kingdom.
Australia and Canada have around 20, USA has around 30 donors per million people.
In 2016, Spain had 43 donors per million people. But in 2017 these figures increased even more, 46.9 donors per million people.
However, the organs are donated in order to be transplanted, and Spain is also world leader in transplantation, because we are the only country where there are more than 100 transplanted people per million, much more above the average in the EU, which has around 64.
And also, above the average of USA, which reaches 100.
There is more than 100.000 people in Spain who has been transplanted an organ, and more than 500.000 people who has been implanted tissues, cells, corneas, bone marrow, cells of umbilical cord...
More than 600.000 people had already benefited, directly from the Spanish Donation and Transplantation System.
Spanish are lucky to be the citizens with more opportunities to access a transplant in the world, in case we need it.
But do you think this is just a matter of luck?
Obviously not.
Luck can help you winning a match, but not all the championships.
The extraordinary figures we have in donation and transplantations are the results of a very good job that we've been doing for years.
The formula of the Spanish success doesn't have any secret ingredient, but the combination of three fundamental elements.
On the one hand, the public healthcare, with general accessibility, defrayed by our taxes, where all those people who need it, has the same opportunity to access a transplant, and where anybody must receive any compensation for being a donor, but also, anybody must pay to be given a transplant.
On the other hand, we have an organizational system called the Spanish Transplantation System which has proved to offer the best results not only in Spain, but also in all those countries which has taken its steps, completely or partially and since, they've realised that donation and transplantation figures have increased significantly.
In what measures is the Spanish transplantation model based on?
It is based on the role of the transplant coordinators.
And who are the transplant coordinators?
We are nurses and doctors, usually from the intensive care unit, where donations take place, and, in addition to our usual work in the ICU, we have received a specific formation about all that things related to donation and transplantation.
We are on call 24 hours every day of the year in order to locate potential donors, to arrange meetings with their families so we can offer them the possibility of donation in order to explain to them the details of the process, to make the organs of the person that has just died of brain dead keep working and evaluate them thoroughly to assure the quality and security of the organs that we transplant. But also, to make sure that we don't reject any organ unjustifiably.
We contact with the National Transplant Organization to be informed about where the best organ receptors are, and to prepare a very rigorous logistic against the clock, not only in the city where the donor hospital is, but also, in the cities of the recipient hospital.
Sometimes, we have to bring surgical staff across Spain, and we have to coordinate judges, forensic surgeons, ambulances, taxis, against the clock.
It's a very hard work but it brings the great reward of helping the dream of getting a transplant come true.
In 2017, more than 5.200 transplants have been done in Spain, an average of 14 transplants per day. So, it's almost certain that in this precise moment, a transplant is being done in some city of Spain.
This is possible thanks to the generosity of more than 2.100 donors and their families, who after grieving the death of a loved one had shown an extremely generous gesture by allowing the organs to be transplanted to other people that didn't have any other options in their treatment than this, than receive that gift, which is the transplant.
More than 87% of the families in Spain agree to the donation in a very emotionally intense moment, just a few minutes after being told the worst news.
So, in these situations, an average of 6 families in Spain make possible every day that unknown people could have a second chance.
I told the first two relevant links of this chain, but the most important of all of them, is the first link, the link of the donors.
They are the link that start all this solidarity chain, because it would be useless that the team had a very good organisation, very good technicians, however, this team wouldn't succeed without good players. In Spain we have good players, our donors. But not only Spanish donors.
In Spain, the figures of foreign donors are increasing every year by a donation acceptance percentage higher than those shown in their home countries which proves that solidarity doesn't distinguish nationalities but cares about information and organization.
When I was a child I wanted to be a footballer, and I told it to everyone who asked me: "What do you want to be when you grow up?"
"Me? Footballer."
I told to my parents, uncles, cousins... to everybody.
But suddenly, I realised that I needed some qualities to become a footballer: I needed a good physical condition, to be skilled on the ball, to be able to hit the ball with the head, a lot of training.
I was not skilled on that at all, so, I got tired of football soon and I started to study, first medicine, then I specialized in intensive care medicine and then I became transplant coordinator in a hospital in Malaga, where I am currently working.
I don't know if medicine gained something with this decision, what is very clear is that football didn't lose anything.
(Laughter) In contrast with what is required in order to become a footballer, it's unnecessary to have something special in order to become a organ donor, just wanting to be and tell is enough.
But whom shall I say it to?
To your family, those who are going to talk on your behalf when you couldn't do it anymore.
In this situation, it's possible that someone like me get close to them and ask them: do you know if he or she wanted to be a donor?
If they knew your will, they won't have any doubts in answering, but if they don't know, they will have to try to answer that question thinking about what your answer would be. And maybe they don't know what to say and begin to have doubts. That's why you shouldn't leave the responsibility to your family. If you have clear your decision
about donation, tell them
so that you can resolve doubts. Anytime is a good time for that, you can take this time today and tell it to the person who has come with you to this event.
Well, as long as that person is important for you, of course. if it's someone that you just have met this morning in the admission queue, It could be a bit precipitated, isn't it?
(Laughter) You can do it tomorrow while you're taking a coffee or having lunch with the family: "Well, the other day I heard a talk about donation and transplantation, and I was really thinking about that, so, I want you to know, that if there is any chance someday I would like to be an organ and tissue donor, for this reason."
Or rather: "look, no, I don't want to be a donor for these other reasons."
That's it, it's said.
And if you are watching this talk on video, you can take the chance, pause it, and tell your decision to your family. But then you have to come back and watch the talk until the end.
Well, better said, you should watch it, pause it when you finish and then you go and tell your family the decision you made.
Besides, footballers professional's sporting life is very short. If they're lucky and they don't have any serious lessions, they can play until they're 35 years old, some of them until 40 years old. However, there is not any age limit to be a organ donor nobody is too old to be generous.
More than a half of the donors in Spain has more than 60, almost one third of them has more than 70, and the 10% of the organ donors are older than 80 years.
Every year, livers are transplanted from donors who are older than 90 years which save straightaway the lives of the people who receive them, and also kidneys from people older than 80 years, hearts and lungs from donors older than 70 years.
So you can guess if we talk about lessions. A 70 years old person has had a lot of lessions throughout his life, a lot of health issues.
Hypertension, diabetes, arrhythmia, heart attack, but it doesn't matter, none ot these issues could stop a person who wants to be a donor in becoming one.
Only some kind of aggresive cancer and certain infections could disqualify the donation, but a very few ones.
In football, there is the discussion about if the players from past generations are worse or better than today's players. In the end, nobody agrees with it and they say that they weren't neither worse nor better, they were just different.
So, the same happens with today's donors.
They're quite different from before, not that long ago.
20 or 30 years ago, donors in Spain were youger people, and almost half of them passed away due to a traumatic brain injury caused by traffic accident.
However, nowadays more than 60% of the donors have deceased as a consequence of brain hemorrhage.
And who suffer brain hemorrhages?
Elderly people with extensive record.
When footballers retire, it's very usual to celebrate a tribute match.
Our players, our team, the donors, also deserve the greatest tribute.
Society is indebted to them, because giving something that is yours to someone who loves or know, involves generosity. But giving something that is yours to someone who don't know, and never would be able to meet, implies a higher level of generosity. And probably it'd be the greatest gesture of kindness that a person can make for another.
(Applause) I whish the day when donors are not neccesary anymore would come, that would mean that many ways of treatment and prevention had been discovered, which doesn't imply receiving an organ. It would also mean that the manufacture of artificial organs had been developed; or even much better, that donation and transplantation figures had increased so much in all countries, that made the waiting lists dissapear in a way that every person who need an organ transplant, could have access to it immediatly and equally.
Then, and only then, wouldn't be neccesary beating the transplantation records over and over again, increasing the number of donors or being world champions again.
Until that day comes, if ever comes, we will continue to need donors.
That's why, my friends, let's play the match of our lives as good as possible, and when the referee blows the final whistle, the best thing we can do is taking our shirt off, and give to another person, so that he can play his match.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Luis Paz: As you see, 'repentismo' (improvised sung oral poetry) is one of the most authentic expressions of Cuban country music.
We all have wished to explore improvisation, a difficult stanza such as the 'décima,' due to its hermeticism, rhyme, and metrics.
The 'repentistas' should versify using language at its rawest, a limited vocabulary, and develop new ideas, new images around or within hermetic stanzas.
They have only one minute to come up with a message, assimilate suggestions from the audience, and meet the challenge from another improviser.
Tomasita Quiala: And to give you a better idea, we're going to interact with the audience,
let's get it rolling, as we say in jargon.
You... (Applause) You give us a phrase and we, the repentistas will build a décima, and you'll see that we're very much in tune with the topics of this event and in tune with all of you. Shall we, maestro?
LP: The evening won't be enough.
OK, give us one.
Audience: "Kimba pa' que suene" (Laughter) LP: "Kimba pa' que suene." ("Let's get it rolling") TQ: Let's get it rolling!
LP: Let's go over the metrics.
First rule, it must be octosyllabic.
Let's get it rolling.
(Guitar music) ♪ Being a Cuban ♪ ♪ I feel proud, you see ♪ ♪ Being a Cuban ♪ ♪ I feel proud, you see ♪ ♪ Because for the first time, the event is held in Havana ♪ (Applause) ♪ Affection bring us together ♪ ♪ We welcome the visitor ♪ ♪ Cubans have a gift granted by God ♪ ♪ Give them rumba, guaguancó, and let's get it rolling ♪ (Applause) (Guitar music) TQ: But now I'd like to up the ante.
LP: Even better?
TQ: No, no. Let's ask not only
for one or two, but for four,
going from the beginning to the end and then backwards, together.
and not individually,
let's do it together!
Four 'pies forzados' now!
LP: And how do we do it?
TQ: You'll see!
LP: Is that part of Technology, Entertainment or Design?
TQ: That is part of art, technology, in and out and of whatever you want.
Yes, but let's make it special: I improvise one verse, you follow up with it and I flip what you sing and you flip what I sing. LP: Does this have anything to do with technology, Tomasita?
TQ: Let's get started!
LP: We need four 'pies forzados'!
Audience: Ecology, freedom.
LP: "Bravo for ecology,"
What were you saying over there?
Audience, LP: "Once again in Havana."
"It walks like a penguin," "Once again in Havana," "Great for the Ecology," and there is one missing.
Over there, over there!
"Un ortoedro truncado." (A truncated rectangular prism).
TQ: That's a good one!
LP: Oh, Tomasita!
(Guitar music) ♪ A truncated rectangular prism, blocked his road ♪ ♪ A truncated rectangular prism, blocked his road ♪ ♪ He walks like a pinguin, although it's not frozen ♪ (Applause) ♪ Now, with every improvised verse ♪ ♪ An idea is shared ♪ ♪ TED brings us together ♪ ♪ Bravo for ecology ♪ ♪ Let's come back another day, once again in Havana ♪ ♪ Once again in Havana, let's come back another day ♪ ♪ Bravo for ecology, As you see it bring us together ♪ ♪ Although it's not frozen, he walks like a pinguin ♪ ♪ His road has been blocked ♪ ♪ By a truncated rectangular prism ♪ (Applause)
I'm going to tell you that retouching and manipulating images is not something new.
It's been with us for more than 150 years.
It's also important to point out, that these two terms are totally different.
Retouch means to correct an image because of technical issues in taking it, to enhance it; while manipulation refers to intervening on an image to the extent that its message can change.
This isn't meant for you to look thinner or to make everybody look like a Barbie.
It had a different purpose at first, something greater, politics.
The photographers that worked for Castro, Lincoln, Mao, Stalin, followed instructions and deleted entirely unwanted persons the so called "political enemies."
Incredible.
From 1910 on, it rather started to include absent people or relatives, that lived far away or even in another country.
Here you have relative floating ghosts.
This isn't a simple practice.
It takes many hours in the dark room, dyeing.
One more time, the absent family.
It takes dyeing, painting.
Many hours in the dark room.
A lot of patience.
All of this, these techniques, brought the art forth and they began to be used in montages, like a means of communication to convey ideas.
In 1917, Elsa Wright creates a series of images with fairies, that were pieces of paperboard or trading cards placed on top of an image already amplified and that was taken again.
I never would have thought doing that, much less in 1917.
Little by little, these practices became so common as today, when we see that something doesn't look real, we immediately think if there was any digital tampering.
Like in the case of photographer Melvin Sokolsky.
A very famous fashion photographer in the 70s, he made a series of images with this model inside a bubble of Plexiglas over the city of Paris.
Everyone's first reaction was: "This is totally photoshopped."
This was in 1963 and it ran until the late 80s, early 90s.
Impossible.
Then it came the best moment, in my view, in the world of photography.
The first digital montage was done in 1988
I was born in 1988.
John Knoll took a photo of his wife in the beaches of Bora Bora and he duplicated her and that was the first digital montage.
This demo shows us a world of possibilities.
Everything we can do with this program.
We've got Photoshop for 25 years.
It turned 25 the past week.
And all this time impressive works of art were made by many artists.
Also, monumental frauds were made by some media, like the cover of the Times magazine
of O.J. Simpson in 1994. They changed the image and the problem was that Newsweek also ran the same cover, so, obviously, the entire world knew that something was wrong there.
Another case was that of the conflict between Lebanon and Israel, where a photographer that worked for Reuters admitted, after much time, that he duplicated the smoke columns of the image in order to dramatize it, to make an impression.
But here we reached a very important point and I believe this is the purpose of my talk.
It's not important who it was -- the photographer, editor, retouch manager.
The important thing here is the intent.
What was the purpose of this action.
Why they're manipulating them and why they're doing it.
We were never as much exposed to photography as we are today.
And in this post-photography era, in which absolutely everything is questioned the old saying: "the camera doesn't lie," is being replaced by: "the image can lie."
Because of the form in which photography has evolved and the way it's used nowadays, many countries have recognized the necessity to create laws to regulate these practices in the media.
An example is the country that did it to prevent people from developing nutritious disorders as a result of seeing things like this one.
And it's Israel, where a legislation was proposed in order to avoid the use of digital editing tools.
Another, in 2012, when the magazine "Seventeen" promised never to use digital editing tools again.
Technological changes are happening everywhere and this gives us two options: either we adapt, or we'll be left behind.
In the case of photography these changes are highly accelerated and force us photographers to relearn virtually everything we're doing these days.
I think that it's important to use these tools.
The people that work with images have to experiment, to use, what is given to us and put it through our own creative process.
I don't think it's bad.
It's not cheating.
Photoshop isn't a bad word.
It's simply a digital dark room that allows us to perfect our images, or why not,
to create images from scratch
by taking elements of different images and creating a new world.
Let's remember that the means aren't important, as long as the result is what we've been expecting, and also, that nobody gets hurt.
Next time we see and image, let's avoid asking ourselves if they used Photoshop or not.
Instead, let's analyze, search, ask, which was the creator's intent, what's behind this image.
In this way, we'll enjoy even more the art of photography.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I came to Havana for the first time in the year 93.
And I have been visiting Havana regularly for the last 20 years because I always have been attracted and like to see how it's doing.
Let's picture a daily image for you.
That fresh out-of-the-oven bread from the bakery is going to arrive to our balcony on the third floor.
The bakery's dependent calls the woman by her name, and she drops off the basket, he places the bread in - here comes the money - and she pulls up the basket.
We're on a street that could be El Vedado's neighbor street, that has two-floor houses, three, four, five-floor buildings.
And there's always space for a balcony, there's always space for a tree in the front, for a forecourt, a boulevard, for parking the car when we get home, when someone is picking us up.
All these things are incredible, simple, daily things we've got.
And for us, in the rest of Latin America they're selling us this image of this environment, of this bread basket, and it turns out we are not so sure of what they're selling to us.
That's what they show us on TV to tell us to buy an apartment, a little box on a 40-floor tower, and they're telling us, "Your life is going to be just as the Cuban's."
Well, no. Turns out we've watched the transformation of our cities in Latin America that are not in Cuba.
It has not been as good as they have told us.
From the 70's and 80's we got very bad architecture.
There are very few good examples.
And it turns out that we have a dome here.
It's incredible how it has stayed - for the reasons we know - Cuba and Havana, under a dome of glass that has protected it from the land speculation.
What is land speculation?
Maybe some of you don't know.
It is simply taking a building lot and multiplying it n times to be able to sell it countless times because everybody wants to be here.
That has not happened yet.
That's a miracle.
That this city is so well preserved in its housing environment, town planning - that every single space was planned, every house, every building, every porch, every balcony was planned by someone.
That's architectural design.
It was so planned that the Avenida del Prado was an international competition.
There were various sketches and finally, the one that we have built won.
So the architectural and urban design, from my experience consists of 50 percent concept - What are we going to do? -
10 percent creativity and occurrences, to finally be composed by a 40 percent of coordination and hard work, so things can be built.
So it's not a dream in the architect's life that people believe rides a sports car, creating, creating, creating.
No, that needs a lot of coordinated work.
It's important to appreciate your daily life.
Appreciate that your kids can be on the streets and you can say, "Hey, come here, it's time to go up."
That doesn't happen in other countries.
We've seen our cities being destroyed.
You have architecture from every age.
And they're preserved almost intact.
Let's be realistic, there hasn't been any money to damage it.
But now that they have come all the way here, and we know that the glass domes are not forever, or they can change.
And inside that dome there has been a social dynamic in this country that has allowed changing land use; that has allowed different families from different places to enjoy this architecture that I insist is so important to preserve.
Since domes are not forever and there are many siren calls to come to tell us that we want to compact the city, that we want to build higher, gentlemen, don't believe everything.
Don't believe everything they say to you.
Just by appreciating what you've got, the scale of a person in the city is how one relates to a wall like this.
No way.
I'd rather have many doors, many balconies, many windows.
That's how I relate to the city.
That's the scale, how do buildings relate to one another, with the space it has up front, the boulevard, with the parks, with the beach.
So we want to make a wake up call to value and protect, to preserve this beautiful environment in which it has been hard to live due to some shortcomings.
But since all that effort has been done, we should not let it be destroyed.
Thank you.
(Applause)
We all need maps.
We all want to know where we are.
We want to know where we're going and how much longer til we get there.
My relationship with maps began when I was tiny.
I started school at a very young age - I'm the little guy in the photo - by following my brother, who started at four, because I didn't want to stay at home.
One day, the teacher asked us where we lived, and my brother, with the innocence of a child - which he still possess - said, "Teacher, I live next door to Oscar, a man who has a dog."
And of course, that afternoon when we got home, we had to repeat, until it stuck in our heads and couldn't be erased, "I live at 169 Honda Street, between San Clemente and San Rafael."
Later, when we were bigger and learned how to write, I subscribed to one of those 80s magazines - Dynamite, 3-2-1 Contact, Popular Mechanics - that would send you contests and you'd have to respond, solve brainteasers or complete a circuit, which always had errors, or write a simple program in Basic.
But as I was about to write the address on the envelope, even though the house was the same, we lived on the same block and the between streets hadn't changed, I found out the written address was different now. Now it was, "169, February 24th Street, between Matías Varona and Raúl Lamar."
Imagine being seven years old and trying to understand that when you say the address, the guys are saints, but when you write it, they're comrades.
So I asked my mom about it, and my mom, who is a saint, gave me a very mom-like answer, "You write it that way because that's how it is on the map, sweetheart."
I grew up thinking that maps were made by old guys who didn't even live here, because they didn't even know the street names, and who tried to impose upon us a picture of the world that even they didn't know.
But this isn't just a problem here.
This also happened at the end of the 1920s in London. When they attempted to create a map of the metro, they were hell-bent on showing exactly where each station was, and the exact routes of all the lines.
They say that trying to read a metro map back then was like trying to read a plate of spaghetti.
But then in 1931, a new guy showed up. Very smart guy.
His name was Harry Beck.
And Harry Beck had a new idea.
He showed up and said: "I have a new metro map."
Harry was an electrician.
He worked for the metro but as an electrician. In his free time, he had converted the available information into a topological diagram, using the tools he used for designing circuits.
He based it on some very basic principles.
He said, "If you're getting on the metro,
you go through a tunnel. And if you go through a tunnel, you're not interested in knowing exactly where you are.
All you want to know is which station is next, and which one comes after that.
So I'm going to make all the lines straight so they look nicer.
And I'm going to represent the stations not by their actual position,
but by the distance between stations
and by what order they're in. So on the map, you'll see the stations and the order they come in. And the longer the line between two stations, the longer it takes to travel between them."
Lastly, he decided to use different colors to represent the different lines.
That seems very basic to us now, but at the time, the idea had never occurred to anyone.
He included only one surface element on the whole map: the River Thames, because for Londoners, it's very important to know which side of the river your metro stop is on.
And like all new ideas, or the majority of them,
the new map idea
wasn't an instant success. They said, "This is very nice.
Yes, very good work.
So just go along home now,
and we'll give you a call."
Two years later, they meet with him again and say, "OK, we're going to take a chance. We're going to print your map."
And that map was so successful, that to this day, 82 years later, they're still printing Beck's metro map.
Here it is.
This map, made by an electrician in his spare time, using his non-cartographic skills and know-how, has been the inspiration for the majority of transportation maps of the world's largest cities - with a few regrettable exceptions.
Even though it was very hard work, and though he afterwards spent many years updating the map, he was only paid five guineas.
So, feeling a little inspired by Beck's story, and to be sure no one ever makes maps on my behalf again, I decided, OK, now I'll make my own maps.
Let's make a map of Cuba. Let's not let anyone else make a map for us anymore,
we'll do it ourselves.
And on our map we'll indicate the bus routes, the bus stops, where you can buy tickets.
We can also show all the urban furniture -
the parks, the benches, the trees -
Because for people like us, who live where it's sunny, it's really important to know where the shaded benches are.
We can also indicate street direction, street names, both the names we know and the ones only the postman knows, what they were called before.
We can show places of business, what time they open and close, what kind of food they serve and what time happy hour is.
And they're working on a project called "Take a Spill," that's plotting out all the potholes.
But since everything is changing - and changing very quickly - where there used to be a multi-family dwelling and a big baseball fan, now there's a bar. And we can annotate that, too.
So we have a map where we can also show where things have changed.
It's our own made-to-order map.
And now, the detractors: "OK. You say we can make this map, that we can participate, but we don't have access to technology.
We don't have the Internet, either. We don't have ..."
And it becomes all about what we don't have. But I'm here to talk about the things we do have, that will help us make a map.
The first thing we have is local knowledge.
Nobody knows more about this place than we do -
we're the ones who are here!
And what we don't know, we'll ask.
I see a lady, so I ask her, "Where's the Combinadito?"
That's probably not what it's called,
but she says to me, "Head that way, turn on the next block, turn there, down the block," and then I always find it, "Oh! There it is."
Earlier I mentioned a community of volunteers, remember?
Who knows more than we do about volunteer work?
Who could possibly give us a spiel about volunteer contributions?
I'm too young to have ever received one of these vouchers, but...
Plus, we're famous for finding solutions for things that are all jammed up, and for things that don't work.
When we first started plotting out the maps, we realized there were certain zones where the satellite image coverage wasn't very good, and we had to draw by hand.
Meaning, we had to cover an area using global positioning units - which you couldn't use at that time - cover an area, set down the plot points, and then draw them.
Since I couldn't use global positioning units, or GPS, Perry Mason, my friend and adventure partner, agreed to carry with him what I was prohibited from carrying.
The people on the south side of Havana probably remember seeing us pass by their houses on the weekends. We were something that, beyond just a friendship, had turned into a symbiosis between a cyclist and a dog.
So now the people who live on the south side of Havana can find their houses on this map, and know that the map is there, thanks to my friend Perry Mason, wherever he may be.
The biggest lesson I've learned by making maps is about sharing.
It's about sharing.
And we have a long tradition of sharing.
Sharing has made projects like Wikipedia and Open Street Map the successes that they are.
Sharing has made them popular, because these projects feed off of the support of the masses, and all of us are the masses.
But we're a heterogeneous crowd.
We don't need to think alike. We don't have to have the same ideas.
We only need to have common goals.
We only need to have the ability to come to an agreement and take collective action. Solutions from individuals will always surface first - they always come about fastest.
Solutions from one person appear quickly, but a collective solution, a solution from everyone together - that will carry us further.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
This mask represents Medusa a mythological creature able to petrify with her gaze whomever stood in front of her.
Can you imagine bumping into a monster like this one in a dark alley at midnight?
That's what happened to me on my first day of secondary school.
I faced not just one Medusa, but 40.
Throughout all of primary school I had gone to Catholic schools.
We were all boys there.
And it turns out that on the first day of secondary school there were girls.
We were all mixed in.
I felt like I was in a zoo of some sort.
but it was something quite bizarre because I was a visitor seeing the animals but I was also being observed by the rest of the animals.
Our teacher had the clever idea of having each of us go up to the board, in alphabetical order to introduce ourselves to everybody else.
My last name is Alvarez.
Can you guess who was the first to go up to the board?
That was hell.
There I was in the spotlight.
All the boys, and what's worse, all the girls, were looking at me.
The teacher said, "OK then, tell them a story."
But what was I going to say to those 40 Medusas with their eyes set on me?
I was petrified.
The teacher rescued me by asking me some trivial questions:
"What's your name? Where do live?
Which sport do you like?"
And slowly like with a corkscrew, he started pulling out some answers, after what seemed to me like a thousand years of questioning he put an end to my suffering and I was able to flee back to my seat, red in the face with embarrassment.
Who hasn't felt something similar too when speaking in public at some point?
Who hasn't seen oneself petrified by Medusa in front of an audience?
In this talk, I will share with you the key that helped me free myself of Medusa when speaking in public.
I will explain that there are two types of fears: one toxic and the other healthy,
and how to go from one to the other.
Put this change I am suggesting into practice and you'll be able to fearlessly speak in public in front of any audience.
You'll go from stage fright to stage joy on just one step.
For many years, every time I was asked to make a presentation, do you know how I spent the days leading up to that?
Mulling over my fears:
"Will I make a fool of myself?
Will my mind go blank?
Will I forget an important detail?
Will they show me up with a difficult question?
Will I do it better than so and so?
Will they hire me?
Listen closely.
What are all those thoughts focused on?
Me, me, me.
I was just thinking about myself.
The more I mulled over the presentation, the more nervous I felt.
Sometimes, I wouldn't be able to get a wink of sleep the night before.
Which strategy do you think I resorted to to get through this difficult experience?
Well, I would work on PowerPoint files and fill them with text, visuals, graphs, so that Medusa would look at the screen instead of me.
The Greeks have a word, 'Phobos, ' to refer to toxic fear the irrational fear that paralyzes you or makes you run away frightened, like this Medusa.
Faced with having a presentation, 'PowerPoint paralyzes you.
Instead of devoting your energy to researching the audience, gathering useful material, decidedly, preparing a presentation that adds value, you spend hours mulling over your fears.
But of course, if you just think about yourself, what about the audience?
Your presentations will be pointless suffering for everybody.
They won't be added value to anybody.
Fortunately, that all changed for me
during my doctoral placement.
Cambridge. Summer of 1998.
There it was customary for visiting students to make a presentation about their center's results.
When I was told I had

to present, I panicked:

Why? A presentation? Me? In English?
What am I supposed to talk about?
I was so nervous trying to impress the English with our results, that they would see that us Spaniards are in the same league as them trying to prove that I was a smart and promising researcher, what do you think I thought of doing?
I worked on a PowerPoint presentation and filled it with slides, equations, graphs.
Luckily, before I presented in front of the whole department, I rehearsed for the director.
I was only two minutes into it reading slide after slide filled with text, when the director interrupted me:
"Let's see, Gonzalo.
These slides, who are they for?
For you or for the audience?"
Now, think about your own presentations.
When you work on a slide, who is it for?
Is it for you or for the audience?
This question made me understand that I had spent all my time thinking about myself.
Instead of devoting time to reflect upon what the audience might need, how they could understand me better, once again, all my thoughts were focused on me:
"Me, me, me. Impress. Dazzle."


What was the audience to me?
A terrifying Medusa.
From that day on, I understood that the way to look at presentations, the metaphor you use is what determines the type of presentation.
From that day on, I stopped seeing the audience as a terrifying Medusa or an enemy that I needed to beat.
I changed my metaphor. You'll see how.

One time, after giving a chat, I was approached by an attendee who said to me: "Gonzalo, I wrote a book about marketing, and I dedicated a chapter to how to speak in public.
Would you mind reading it and sending me your comments?"
I said: "Sure, send it to me and I'll be delighted to read it."
I received the chapter.
The author had called it "Facing an audience."
And this is how it began, with this comparison: "From the ancient gladiators that filled their circuses with blood, to the more sophisticated conference presenters of our time, the experience of facing an audience is the same."
Let's experiment.
Close your eyes, please.
Let yourself be taken by the images that come to mind when I say 'gladiator.'
Open your eyes.
Raise your hand if you've seen blood.
Who here has seen blood?
Raise your hand. A few.
Raise your hand if you've seen
weapons, swords, axes, tridents.
Practically all.
Raise your hand if you've seen dead bodies.
Any dead bodies in the room? A few.

Have you noticed the underlying metaphor?
A presentation is a battle.
It's a mental framework of a battle, of opposition, of confrontation.
What is the audience?
A rival against whom you must fight tooth and nail.
If you stem from that metaphor, that a presentation is a battle, what will your experience presenting be like?
You'll try to win, demonstrate your superiority, not show the slightest vulnerability, trampling on mutilated cadavers of the Other.
They will be tense presentations because you'll have that image or the fear that all
of this will happen to you.
Kill or die. There's no other option.
Fortunately, my metaphor changed.
Let's do a new experiment.
Close your eyes, please,
And now let yourself be taken by the images that fill your mind when I pronounce the word 'trip.'
Open your eyes.
Raise your hand if you the thought of a beach came to mind.
Who has seen a beach? A few.

Who saw friends, relatives?
Also a few.

And smiles? Smiley faces? Who? Also many.


If we stem from this metaphor, that a presentation is a trip, these will be the thoughts that accompany you.
Change your metaphor and the way you see the world will change.
To me, a presentation is an intellectual and emotional trip.
You are the guides that accompanies the audience from the starting point up to its destination.
All audiences attend a presentation with a question, a need, a problem, a set of objectives.
Your mission as presenter is to help them answer that question, to satisfy that need, to solve that problem, to fulfill those objectives.
The starting point of this trip is a situation of curiosity, of doubt, of passiveness, and your goal is to take that audience to its destination of knowledge, certainty, action.
When you take your audience on a trip, on an adventure of change, of their ideas, their feelings, their actions, when you understand you're not at the center of the presentation, and put your audience at the center by helping them solve their problem, then, toxic fear disappears.
Does that mean that you will never again have fear?
No. You will continue having fear,
but it is a completely different fear
because not all fears are the same.
Do you think fear is always something bad, something negative?
Of course not.
Fear exists for a reason of survival.
Fear is a wonderful alarm system that alerts us of danger so that we may face it better.
You see a snake on the floor and you jump and back off.
You see a lion amidst brushwood and you climb up a tree.
You come up to the edge of a cliff and you stop yourself in your tracks.
Without fear, you wouldn't be able to survive, because without fear you would not react to a snake, a lion, or a cliff.
You would quickly die.
The Greeks had another word, 'deos, ' to refer to that healthy fear, to that reflexive fear, rational.
Deos is the fear that pushes us to analyze danger and prepare us for action.
It is the fear that many extreme sports athletes feel.
This helmet illustrates to me what Deos is.
I am a hiking aficionado.
I know that when go up a rock I may fall.
In fact, I know that I will fall.
Instead of staying home, I train in a rock climbing facility, I prepare for the tracks, I equip myself conveniently.
Likewise, as a presenter, I prepare, I get informed, I practice, in order to give the best of myself in each presentation.
A lot of people ask me: "When you're up there hanging on the wall,
do you not feel fear? Of course.
If I didn't feel fear in the slightest, I would not prepare, I would not study the track, I would not equip myself and the story would end badly,
As bad as the next story could have ended.
In 2006 I went up Mont Blanc with my wife and a friend of mine.
We chose the route that crosses a narrow path of about 30 meters, know as the Bowling Alley, because you're a pin that needs to steer clear of falling rocks.
This is what the Bowling Alley looks like.
These are the rocks that fall down
the alley. This is on a bad day.
There are lots of rocks there, big ones, some the size of a washing machine.
Not all days are as bad as this one.
But rocks fall every day.
in fact, every year people die in this path.
That day we arrived to the Bowling Alley.
I was the first to cross.
A few rocks fell far from me.
Next, my wife started crossing.
Halfway down the road a couple of rocks fell whistling next to her.
She felt fear and paralyzed.
She sat on a rock and wouldn't move.
From opposite ends my friend and I yelled at her to move;
"Run!" But she, paralyzed in fear, wouldn't do a thing.
At that moment and without thinking twice, just thinking I needed to get her out of there, I ran all the way to where she was, I pulled her arm and pulled her out of the rock, and just like in the movies, right after, a rock the size of a tennis ball hit the other rock where she had been a second before that.
Dragging her, running as fast as we could, we managed to shield ourselves form the rocks.
When you forget about yourself and just think about the other, there is no more fear.
When you accompany your audience in a trip, if you forget about worrying about how you'll do it and just care about how you can help your audience reach their objectives, then that fear disappears.
You no longer see your audience as something threatening,
you no longer see a Medusa in it, nor do you see a rival that you must fight in battle.
All you see is people like you, with doubts, fears, dreams.
People that, with your presentation, you can offer a gift to with the ability to change their lives for the better.
Now, instead of feeling fear, you will feel satisfaction, joy, from being able to add value.
The questions you ask yourself before the presentation will be completely different:
What does the audience need?
How can I add value from my knowledge and experience?
How can make this topic seem entertaining?
How can manage for them to understand this concept?
You will keep feeling fear, that's for sure,
but it will now be Deos, the fear that keeps you alert before the audience's needs.
When you understand that you have the chance to be generous, of instructing, leading, your fear fades away.
You leave your fears behind, and tell the audience the truth that they need to hear.
This fear disappears just like when you need to save a person from dying squashed by a rock in the Bowling Alley of Mont Blanc.

Deos moves up. Phobos backs down.
You too can leave behind the fear of public speaking.
You can free yourself of the tyranny of Medusa.
Draw the spotlight away from yourself and focus it on the audience and then you'll speak in public without fear.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Florencia Polimeni: In many of our conversations before we got here I kept thinking about the big differences that exist between the indigenous peoples worldview and the worldview of the Western world, or the white world as you say sometimes.
I would like that you share with us some of those differences.
Especially, the ones related to identity.
The way indigenous people see identity.
Félix Díaz: Well, first of all I want to thank you for the opportunity you give me to talk in such an immense space.
The truth is that it is quite a historical moment for us indigenous peoples.
My name is Félix Díaz, I descend from the Qom people, an indigenous people from South American Chaco.
Our ancestors have lived in that region.
And we are here today because we were able to overcome testing times during the first invasion on this continent.
Note the name of this building, Colón [Columbus], who came to conquer this continent, and afterwards so many times they tried to finish with the indigenous peoples.
And today I am here, alive and present, which gives us a great opportunity to tell society who we are, what we want, and what we look for.
The indigenous world for us, for thousands of years, have survived due to the relationship with Mother Earth itself, and with nature, sheltering in difficult times.
Those days there were no stores, no paved roads, no whites.
It was indigenous.
And what provided us with food?
Nature itself.
And we could also take care of the environment.
We were able to preserve those areas where you could hunt, fish, gather.
There was no selfishness, envy nor competition.
We were a hospitable world, full of love, and respect towards human beings.
And also towards non-human beings.
Because of that way of life we ​​could subsist in difficult times.
Back then there was no drinking water.
There were no pharmacies.
There was nothing.
The indigenous world was free until colonization came in.
They put barbed wires, established borders, and many of the people who lived together, some were left in the region of Paraguay and others in Bolivia. FP: Tell us how this affected the way to name them.
FD: The truth is that since this continent was invaded they have imposed names on us.
Even for the people.
They called us Toba Indians at that time -- which is a Guarani word -- with disdain.
They gave me a name I didn't choose.
My original name is Felle, which is the name my parents gave me.
And when they took me to the civil registry to register me, the civil registry officer did what he wanted. At that time, I was about 16 or 17 years old, and they put me against a wall, they made fun of me.
Then they asked my name, who are my parents, what is my last name, things I didn't understand.
And there they put me Félix Díaz.
Since then, that became my name before society, Félix Díaz.
FP: And what about the subject of time, which we find so special here in TED?
How do you see the difference with the concept of time of indigenous peoples?
FD: For us time is not in our domain, because indigenous people when the sun rises, or the sun comes in, they do what they can in those days.
If something is not finished, it's left for the other day.
And there are no schedules, no holidays, and when the elderly gather together in assembly they never think of breakfast, lunch, tea time or dinner.
A topic is talked through till the end of the day.
Nobody stands up. Nobody says
"I have other things to do."
It's all focused on a place where they try to solve an important issue.
FP: So they talk until there's nothing more to be said.
FD: Until resolving the issue at hand.
That's why it's important for us that you know us.
Because many times they want to impose a foreign doctrine to ours and we cannot adapt to it because we have another way of life.
The indigenous leadership has been manipulated too.
The word "cacique" [chief] is not the title a leader should have.
Because it sounds like "almost" in Spanish which is also a kind of mockery from the criollos of the area, as if they were mocking us.
Indigenous leaderships have their own name depending on the different peoples.
In our case I am a "qarashé" of the Qom Potae Napocna Navogoh community, which means "leader with his people."
It's like a stake held up by strings.
And that string is not stuck in the ground but held by the floor and holds the strings that represent the Councils of Elders.
For us the old man is our guide, like our wise man, and our teacher because he is the one who knows things the most.
A person who can tell you how to do things.
And always articulated as well with the elderly women. They have a pretty interesting wisdom.
That's why it's important for us that you come to know us through this space.
FP: What about property, the concept of property which is so important in a society like ours?
How do you live property and limits?
FD: It's a very serious problem for us that of private property because in the indigenous world there is no property.
When you make a house, you leave it and a family comes in.
And nobody can claim or say: "this is my house, it's mine."
Or a piece of land, if someone comes.
That's why an indigenous person living in common territory is always circulating,
leaving the farm, a house. They share.
Nobody says: "this is mine."
FP: What do you mean by circulating?
FD: You go like changing places, in the mountains, in the countryside, beside the lagoon, there is no fixed settlement.
The mountain belongs to everyone.
Nobody says, "the lagoon is our property."
The field, the agriculture, indigenous people don't feel owners but share it all with others.
For that reason the state has a social debt with the indigenous people. Because the national state and the provincial states deny the law of common property to indigenous peoples.
Lawmakers have to work hard, listening to the proposals of indigenous peoples so that we are not expelled from the different places, because we don't hold ownership on the land.
FP: Is this the fight that keeps you awake at night?
FD: That is the key challenge in the indigenous struggle.
Today we are undergoing a very difficult situation.
Justice is used to condemn us.
I have a civil lawsuit for usurpation of an ancestral territory where my parents are buried.
And Justice is trying to condemn me for something that is not of the state.
It belongs to the indigenous peoples.
Because we don't come from a distant country.
We are native to those lands.
We are not foreigners.
Why are they kicking us out?
Because of the wealth in natural resources in there.
So the states keep our territories to keep the natural resources which are inside them: oil, mining, soybeans. Everything related with agricultural or livestock farming makes the indigenous people end up pushed to a corner.
And this turns into many deaths for not having the mountain, the lagoon, for not having the ample territory.
And we cannot develop our cultural activities for lack of territory.
FP: What's your dream?
FD: My dream is to be able to tell society who we are.
Because I think you don't know much on the subject of indigenous peoples.
And when I was young I dreamed with being a teacher, a nurse.
Being a teacher to educate others in the way of life as indigenous people.
And a nurse to be able to heal those people who need to relieve their physical pain.
So without realizing it, I am making that dream come true, this dream I have now, and it makes me feel calm to be useful for the indigenous world because I can tell society what we are, what we think.
And the life of indigenous peoples is not in the city.
It's in the territories.
That's like our supermarket.
There we find our medicine, our spirituality, our food, the raw materials for crafts like this chawar the Wichí people make.
Made of a fiber called chawar.
It's a beautiful work that makes us proud to wear, because it is indigenous people's work.
It's what gives us the change to show the world the potential we have.
But many times that potential has been ignored for a matter of political and partisan interests.
And we are fighting for the indigenous people to be free, because we don't want to be sponsored by no government, by no political party, nor for any religion.
We want to be who we are, the natural pre-existing beings before the state.
FP: If you had to close this conversation and choose something to say to the people here before you leave what would it be?
FD: What I would suggest to you is to reconnect with yourselves.
Don't wait for others to tell you what do you have to do.
We have our ability to develop what we are.
We don't own anyone.
We are not a product of science nor technology.
We are natural beings with different abilities, from different cultures, and we have to be proud of what we are, to belong to a culture who has been able to pass the message from generation to generation.
If we don't find ourselves again it is difficult to solve problems.
Because the problem is not in the other, but in ourselves.
If we change, we can help others change.
But I cannot change the other by forcing them, lying to them or buying them.
I have to change myself to be able to transmit to the other the desire for coexistence in peace, in harmony with others.
FP: Thank you, Félix.
FD: Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Before we start, let me get rid of this rascal because I've had it here all day, and I've earned a bit of freedom.
Speaking of rascals, I was one when I was little.
Because I was naughty, but also because, if I got hold of a toy, I would take it apart completely. But I would always put it back together.
This tendency to take things apart today can be included within the "maker" movement. This new Do It Yourself, "maker" concept.
And looking up the definition of DIY on Wikipedia, I was stunned when I saw that it refers to a counter-culture that originates in the punk movement.
As it turns out, in the 80s, well, I was a little "punky".
(Laughter) And since I didn't have a cent to go to London, I made my own wristbands and belts.
I had a little workshop with a friend.
And this characteristic of DIY-er, handyman, customizer, what nowadays you could call a regular person who knows how to do anything, has always come to me from the need to repair, in situ, the orthopedic devices that I've used since I was four years old.
Because, I don't know if you've realized, I'm handicapped.
Well, handicapped ... or mutant?
Ever since I saw the movie Acción Mutante, wow, I went ...
ever since the punk movement and that movie (Applause) with the movie Accion Mutante by Alex de la Iglesia I gathered tremendous momentum.
It's a funny movie, a comedy, but it's really in tune with the punk-crippled thing I've got going on.
(Laughter) You just have to have this type of humor, because if you don't, it's not going to be easy living in this world, in this jungle, that eats you alive, because this world is designed for normative bodies.
For example, how many of you wear glasses?
Who wears a hearing aid can't hear well?
Well, if they don't hear me, they're not going to do it.
(Laughter) Who uses crutches?
Walking sticks?
I saw someone over there with one.
Or, I'm sure some of you have children and use pushchairs.
Every one of us, sooner or later, will find ourselves in this situation because of illness, an accident, or simply, because we've seen many winters.
There is not one person who will escape this.
I wasn't born in a wheelchair.
My mother didn't give birth to me and the wheelchair together.
It's something temporary, we are impermanent and changing.
We need to be aware of the fact that we are subject to change.
Don't kid yourself; no one is going
to have a perfect body their whole life.
If you're "weird", like I am, you find yourself subject to the orthopedic catalogue the state catalogue of technical assistance.
If, for example, you need a wheelchair, you're subject to the ones that are in the state catalogue.
If you want any refund at all.
But, what if you don't like
what they offer you? Because usually... Do you know what the Universal Design is?
The Universal Design is the design of an object that will fit any body or a design for everyone.
Well, I believe that design is more of a utopia worthy of Wonderland.
Because I honestly have never seen it anywhere.
The truth is, we need to make life to our own measurements.
This is how my technical skills developed, when I went from using crutches to having to use a chair, because I have poliomyelitis.
Well, the chair needs maintenance, repairs, and improvements.
But I also need to thank the chair for making it possible for me to discover integrated dance.
Because my performer streak also comes from being from the 80s.
But, until just a few years ago, I hadn't thought of the possibility of expressing myself with movement through contemporary dance.
Dancing with the chair allows me to forget about my busted legs, but it does have one disadvantage. Since I dance with the chair, I need to travel a long way to get to the performance locations.
So, I had to devise a way to motorize the wheelchair, in a way that wouldn't make it a heavy monstrosity that would stop me from dancing, and that could be connected and disconnected.
This is when I had to apply the Do It Yourself concept.
Because the technical assistance didn't... So I invented the predecessor of this gadget, which I call Handiwheel.
It all started in 2011, with the Indignants Movement, in Barcelona's Plaza Cataluña,
where I was part of the Functional Diversity Committee.
There I found a group of people who were very tuned into my beliefs. We needed to act: lodge a complaint against the government for failing to fulfill the rights of those with functional diversity through aggressive actions.
We focused on the public interest, which is to build a more accessible world, for everyone and with everyone.
In 2012, one year later, Concerning The Chair was founded, in order to keep working on what was started in Plaza Cataluña one year prior, and had one concrete focus: building one's own assistive technology through the Functional Diversity philosophy and through open design.
Concerning The Chair is a diverse group made up of activists for independent living. We work together to design and create tools that transform wheelchair users, their surroundings, and the connection between the two.
We also work on awareness projects as well as projects protesting inaccessibility.
We have built a portable ramp, a ramp that can be carried in a bag on the back of the chair, and which goes beyond being a mere accessory. It is a means of colonizing spaces.
Think of it as a gangplank used by pirates to board other ships.
It's a metaphorical attack method to baldly indicate those places which "have forgotten" accessibility. And with these types of actions we make a scene, and the property owner is thus profiled.
But seriously, it's not about making a scene like the escrache demonstrations. It's about creating a mutual understanding that changes how people look at accessibility of stores and locals. This way, the problem stops being ours, of not being able to enter, and begins to involve the property owners and those who are within the building.
Thus unfolds a new situation, one of empathy, of criticism, conversations start, tables are moved, chairs are pushed aside, and the problem of accessibility begins to be everyone's.
Sometimes, the owner expresses his or her intention of fixing the problem - lowering the price of the drinks is out of the question - but someday, they'll put in a ramp, they say.
An object as ordinary and simple as a table can create many problems, especially when not even a table from an orthopedics catalogue fulfills the requirements of our colleague Marga Alonso.
She designed La Mesi; we all contributed to its construction.
It's a special table tailored to her needs.
It's a place to rest her hands when she's out of the house.
And it is a tool to make a very difficult dream come true which is to be able to read wherever she wants and to be able to complete her university degree.
So we could say, "I'll see your table and I will raise you a university degree."
(Applause) Nuria Gómez, because of tetraplegia, has trouble using conventional cutlery and finds that the tools for holding cutlery that are sold in the catalogue are not comfortable and are manufactured using materials that need to be washed often because they become dirty during use.
And she had the ingenious idea of soldering a curved piece of metal onto a typical piece of cutlery, so that she can hold the fork with just that. Plus, you can wash that piece of cutlery with the others.
Almost all technical assistance tools on the market are made with a hospital aesthetic. They fulfill their rehabilitative function but forget the day-to-day side of that object.
For example, an electric wheelchair facilitates movement, but its design doesn't take into account something as simple as being able to carry the things you need throughout the day.
Antonio Centeno, a great representative of the independent living movement, just like Marga and Nuria, wanted to take advantage of the space below the armrest that holds his joystick. So he fitted it out with a little suitcase to carry his day-to-day essentials like keys, his cell phone, money... As if it were a type of glove compartment.
These indivitually-built creations aren't mere assistive devices, but the means in which to empower those with functional diversity.
All of these devices are "Open-Source Hardware"; they are free designs and are low-cost to make.
For us, they are a kit that allow us to have relationships in our day-to-day lives whether or not our friends use a wheelchair or not.
Because we have friends of all types.
It is a kit for friendship, one that facilitates interactions.
One way we strengthened this web of friendship was when, in Spring 2014, when we were still a small group, we saw that we needed to expand and from this idea emerged the "Gadgets Net".
The Gadgets Net is a system that connects people and groups that think in the same way we do.
We use social media and blogs to share our knowledge.
We needed to find each other, create alliances, share ideas, and knowledge.
We organized an event in Can Batlló in Barcelona and we called this event Spring of Gadgets.
There we presented many ideas about do-it-yourself and maintenance.
For example, the prosthetic hands from Maker Convent, made with a 3D printer.
We had presentations on adapted sexual objects. It's a series of piezoelectric devices that emit sensations when touched, and we call it Pornorthopedics.
(Laughter) These were brought by a group called Post-op, which is part of the feminist and trans-feminist movement. We have built alliances with them.
The 15-M Functional Diversity Group from Madrid, from Sol presented a manifesto on how to organize accessible assemblies.
And Silvia and César presented chairs and standing frames for children in rural Bolivia.
Another event we held was the Gadgetathon. Those who had come to the Gadget Net contributed to this event by helping develop four main ideas during one conference.
The process of choosing these four ideas to develop was carried out with a virtual platform called CoCreable.
We thought up a hood for a wheelchair, a retractable hood that protects from sun and rain.
We thought of how to adapt a space which ended up becoming the Gadget Net.
We thought up gadgets and adapted clothes for people who use wheelchairs, and prototypes for emptying systems for incontinence bags so you can empty it if you find yourself on the street because you've been out all day.
I'd also like to highlight that at Concerning The Chair we know the importance of spreading these ideas through thorough documentation, of creating tutorials with diagrams, with the most detailed diagrams, photos, and maps possible. So that whoever wants to
can replicate them, improve them, and disseminate them, so that others can in turn take advantage of them.
We disseminate these tutorials under Creative Commons license, and we've begun to experiment with how to make and spread our tutorials through our blog entornoalasilla.wordpress.com.
But for us, it's not only important to know how to make these objects but also to know the lives that are behind these objects.
Because of this, we are developing an interactive documentary that we'll put online and that will allow the visitor to surf it in a non-lineal manner as if it were a website.
The content and the videos are of events that we've done previously and will contain interviews with people who are active in the world of functional diversity.
We are hoping to fund this documentary through a crowdfunding campaign.
So that it's clear: we need things we cannot find in the market, things that allow us to not always need to ask for help from third parties.
We live in a world of functional diversity. Every body is how it is and we need to rethink the design of our cities and our environments.
Because the problem is our environment not our bodies.
Functional diversity in itself is not a tragedy.
Our bodies are our fighting instruments, our instruments of visibility.
We want to escape from the medical assistance model and focus on an objective of ability and not disability or handicap.
Nothing about us without us.
The revolution will be accessible, or it will not happen.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Ania was left in Poland during the war. She was 12.
She begged for alms, served as a maid.
She learned to pray.
But she lived scared because she mispronounced the "r" sound, and she was convinced that this was how they would find out she was Jewish and kill her.
With her nearly transparent blue eyes and her tiny, delicate voice, she would tell me, when I was a child, that she spent all the war years without using any word containing the letter "r".
I found that impossible.
My mom would say "it is possible,
as is so much more.
Let's hope that life never challenges you."
Hanka was 7 years old.
Hidden with her mother in a closet, they held their breath, as they listened to the shouting in German.
"Why do we have to hide, mom?"
"Because if they find us they'll kill us."
"And, why do they want to kill me if I've been good?"
Stories like these accompanied me during my childhood, with questions that harass me and don't let me sleep.
I am a child of the war.
I was born in Poland while the bombs fell in Japan over Hiroshima and Nagasaki.
My parents survived by hiding in a tiny attic for several years.
We arrived to Argentina in 1947.
My fairy tales were stories like these.
Some wonderful, heroic.
Others sad, terrifying, which I heard the survivors tell, sitting around the table while drinking tea with pastries and cake.
I didn't live the war, but I've always had the feeling that the most important things of my life had happened before I was born.
The war itself.
The miracle of my parents' survival.
The loss of Zenus, their first son, my older brother who I've never known.
They had to give him away to a Christian family to ensure his survival.
Theirs seemed impossible.
When the war was over they went back for him.
"He's dead", they were told.
They asked for his body.
They didn't remember where they had buried him.
It seemed obvious, Zenus was still alive and was being kept by them.
They searched and searched. But they never found him.
His absence was a tangible presence in my house.
This is the only photo that remained.
It's strange to live with the feeling that maybe somewhere, there is someone with my blood, alike, doesn't know who he is.
Imagine having to leave your children behind to save their life. What could a two-year-old have done?
What were my little brother, Ania, and Hanka accused of?
"Why do they want to kill me if I've been good?"
These questions led me to think about Evil.
Not the interpersonal evil, the everyday one, done in the middle of an argument or a heated moment.
No, no. Uppercase Evil.
Impersonal, systematic, political Evil.
The Evil perpetrated by someone in the name of a system against others belonging to a group that has to be destroyed.
The one done by following orders, that creates wars, massive killings, and genocides, but without guilt.
How do we answer the question posed by uppercase Evil?
I believe the answer relies on education.
An education where ethics is central.
Neither religions nor cultural norms have been able to prevent uppercase Evil.
Yet the future of humanity depends on this.
It should be part of every curriculum.
But if it were to be, how to introduce it at school?
Also, how can we highlight its importance so it does not become just another class?
9am: English, 10am: Gym, 11am: Genocide?
The protagonists
in the classroom are the key.
The one who was there, catching us with their voice and presence. They open our ears and help us know the human perspective of any historial event.
But what will it be like
when there are no witnesses left? What is going to happen to stories like Ania's or Hanka's, buried in the page of some history book, unreachable?
How can we keep the motivating force of the live testimony in the classroom?
In Fahrenheit 451, Bradbury describes a world in which books are forbidden.
Each rebel decides to learn a book by heart, in order to keep it alive.
This is the solution.
Like those rebels, let us rebel against tides of forgetfulness to ensure that each story continues to be heard.
And this is how the Apprentice Project was born.
A very simple idea: bringing two people together.
One who has something to tell, and another who wants to hear it, and commits to continue telling it.
Like the master shoemaker who teaches his art to an apprentice.
So the protagonists pass on their experiences and story to a witness, who receives and embodies them as their own.
I passed my enthusiasm to the people at Generations of the Shoah, an organization that deals with the Holocaust, and we began to work.
At first, we had no idea how to do it, but the project engaged people.
We persisted, and through trial and error we learned.
And we are doing it!
It's a simple conversation between two people.
Eye to eye.
In the flesh.
You can't ask questions to a written or recorded testimony.
But to someone in front of you, you can.
And they tell stories like the ones I told earlier.
Human, universal, stories that anyone can understand.
It doesn't matter where or under which circumstances.
They allow us to walk in someone else's shoes.
It's something vibrant, like in the theater, people are there and what's happening touches us all.
This can't be registered by any camera, it's pure energy.
The project is spread by word of mouth.
The candidates are young adults from 20 to 35 years old.
They first make a training, before the long awaited moment comes: the pairing event.
That day each apprentice meets their teacher.
The matches are made and each pair chooses its own path. They meet wherever and whenever they want, for as long as they need.
The only requirement is that the apprentice must keep a journal, logging the memories of the journey.
Their memory for the future.
The pairs meet several times, before the final moment arrives, at the closure event: a rite of passage, in front of their friends and family.
Throughout their meetings, each pair has developed a powerful relationship that is formalized that day in front of all those in attendance by signing an ethical commitment:
that each story will continue to be told.
To this point, 90 pairs have completed the project.
Ninety are the apprentices who have made their teacher's story a part of their own lives.
Since we began, five years ago, we've learned many things.
That it's easier to talk with a stranger than with your own family.
When Dora died, all her grandchildren surrounded Sol at the wake and asked: "Tell us what grandma told you, because she never told us anything."
We've also learned that on top of keeping oral storytelling alive, new kinship networks were created: "foster" grandchildren and grandparents, invitations to parties, celebrations, the apprentice's family meeting the teacher's family.
Gabriel happily says: "I have a new granddaughter."
Ariana invited Eugenia to be a witness at her wedding.
Brian danced Lea's story turned into a choreography.
We've learned that these conversations between teacher and apprentice are a bridge between past and future.
We've learned that we, old people, are the owners of an indispensable archive.
We are how we are because of what happened before.
We will be who we will be if we learn from the rowers, to gain the power to move forward by looking backwards.
Imagine the power produced when sharing in the classroom the live testimony of witnesses of a culture in danger of extinction,
of the Falklands War, of the military dictatorship, or of human trafficking.
The apprentices are these voices of History.
Pame asked Judith if she had ever felt ashamed during the war.
Surprised by the question, Judith said: "You know what? Yes, I have,
and I had forgotten.
It was the day we arrived at Auschwitz.
Hundreds of women cramped in that horrible place. And we were ordered to undress.
I was 14 years old, I had never stripped in front of anyone before. I was very ashamed, but the fear was stronger.
I imitated the others and kept removing my clothing piece by piece.
Until I was down to my underwear. I had reached my limit.
But in front of me there was this German soldier. A young man, he wasn't more than 20. Blond, light blue eyes, handsome as could be. When he saw me in my underwear, he raised his gun and fiercely shouted: "Everything, all of it!"
Shivering, I took off my undershirt, and when I pulled down my panties I was horrified to see there was blood, and that he saw it as well.
I wanted to die.
That was the worst that happened to me in Auschwitz.
I know you won't believe me. But it was worse than lice, worse than hunger, worse than thirst.
My intimacy was there lying on the ground in front of everyone.
I was 14 years old and I was no longer the owner of myself."
These words speak about dehumanization with more eloquence that any essay could.
Judith died while I was preparing this talk, but her story is still alive in me.
I tell it every time I can.  I will never ever forget it.
And now that I've told it, you won't be able to forget it either.
This is the essence of the Apprentice Project:
When you listen to a witness,  you become a witness.
(Applause)
We have worked very hard to achieve living longer and yet today we consider aging as a problem.
I also used to consider population aging as a problem, that's why some years ago I packed, left everything behind and enroled a top university in the USA, Johns Hopkins University, to acquire tools that'd help me to fight the aging challenge.
What I was not expecting was to realize that my initial vision, my perspective on aging, was distorted.
I had a series of beliefs, stereotypes and prejudices that prevented me from seeing clearly.
We can think of aging either as positive or as negative but we all share one thing: That idea is conformed very early in our lives.
Studies in the '70s conducted interviews on 4 to 11 year-old children, a gerontologist, Seefeldt, and others asked children about their perception of the elderly.
Results are horrifying.
Two out of three children believed that older people were helpess, unable to take care for themselves, and generally passive.
And that negative perception they had about aging would also become an expectation of their own aging.
60 percent of those children already predicted that when they'd be old, they were going to feel awful.
And how can any 4, 5 or 6 year old know how'd they feel when elderly?
Probably, what goes on there is that we are absorbing that stereotype and that belief from our environment.
Actually, in the Western world, Europe and North America, there's generally a very negative perception of old age,
it's what we call "ageism" or discrimination by age.
And what happens when those children grow up?
By then the stereotype is reinforced, the belief is replayed by the mass media, advertising, movies --
If you give a closer look, how is aging usually portrayed?
Generally not positive.
At most it's warm, but never as an active aging.
Actually, in the 90s another very interesting experiment was conducted by Burke on healthcare professionals.
He wanted to analyze why some health professionals would talk to older people as if they were children, we call this the "elder talk", probably most of you have done it or at least have seen it: "Hi Carmencita, how are you today?
Here is your little pill for the heart".
Right? And Carmen is 75 years old, a professor emeritus at university and she is in perfect cognitive state.
(Laughter) Burke proved that healthcare professionals would speak like this to the elderly regardless of their cognitive state, based only on their own beliefs about old age.
I invite you to never talk like this to an older person.
(Applause) In 2008 an article in The New York Times gathered all the scientific evidence of why talking like that to older people is harmful to their health.
In a very simple way we can realize we are reinforcing their idea that they are useless and incapable.
No, the elderly are not like children.
In another very interesting study also starting in the 70s a gerontologist, Ashley, went to a town in Ohio and managed to convince 2/3 of the population over 50 of participating in it.
That's an achievement for any researcher because nobody wants to take part in scientific studies, less if they are periodic.
These people were periodically asked about their mental health status, their physical health status, their working environment, family, etc. And they were also asked some questions on their perception of old age: Do you think you become less happy as you get older?
Do you think that as you get older you are less useful?
Well, this study was very interesting, and had the most powerful results 15 years later.
A social psychologist, Becca Levy, from Yale University, has revolutionized the world -- at least for me, my world -- by proving that the negative stereotype of old age becomes a self-fulfilling prophecy.
Meaning that it affects our health, if we think we are going to age badly, we most probably will.
Becca Levy with her Ohio study resolved to test an hypothesis: do people with a positive vision of old age live longer?
She went to Ohio and retrieved all the death certificates from people participating on the research who had already died and compared the life expectancy of people with a positive vision with those who had a negative vision of old age.
And she found that those with a positive vision lived longer.
How much longer do you think they lived?
One year? Two years?
It's 7.6 years!
That is 7.6 years more of life expectancy
in those with a positive vision of old age.
Monitoring all other factors.
Do you know how much it costs as society to increase life expectancy ... in 3 months?
"With surgery ... got another year of life expectancy ..."
Changing our attitude.
A change in our beliefs about aging.
And how can a belief become a change in your cells, in your life expectancy?
It's very revolutionary.
Well, they've managed to understand how the negative stereotype is associated to things like memory, to our performance in cognitive tests.
In 2013, they managed to associate the negative vision of old age with the size of our hippocampus.
So, our ideas change the anatomy of our brain.
And to apply it a little in our day to day there are many hypotheses of how this happens, what is the biological mechanism, how does this happen?
But there's one thing that we have all said or listened, that is: "Me, at my age..." "What am I doing wearing sneakers at my age?"
"What am I doing in the gym at my age?"
"What am I doing, being 40, with those who are 20?"
Suddenly, we stop doing things that are good for our health because there's some kind of expiration date from which we throw in the towel.
It seems like the day we turn 65, "game over", we are done, we can no longer introduce any positive activity for our health because it's not worth it, there's no turning back, aging is negative by definition, it is inexorable.
Actually that number, 65 years, I tried to find out where it came from, and the most probable cause is called Otto Von Bismarck, who was the German Chancellor in the late 1800s.
In 1893 he introduced the world's first retirement policy.
The right to retirement is very recent, the workers were protesting a lot and he wanted to calm things down a bit, so he stablished the retirement age in 70 years.
When I researched a little about which was the life expectancy in Germany in 1893 -- (Laughter) How long do you think people lived in 1893?
Life expectancy at birth was 39 years old.
(Laughter) Otto Von Bismarck was a genius -- (Laughter) Those who were lucky and managed to reach 30, would live on average another 30 years, that is, 60.
Otto Von Bismarck lived up to 78, more or less, good for him, but not for the rest. (Laughter)
Can you see there's no ground for this?
There is no medical basis for 65.
The other countries acquired that number, 70, 60 -- actually, in the United States it's said that 65 was adopted because someone said "60", another said "70", and a third said "let's not argue: 65".
And now in the collective mind it has become some kind of halfway point in which we go deep into not having a solution nor remedy, and we do have a remedy.
There are changes in our lifestyle that'd benefit us no matter when,
for example, if after this talk you resolve to quit smoking, tomorrow, in 24 hours, your cardiovascular risk would have decreased, regardless of your age: 20, 40, 60, 80...
In two days, you'd recover your senses of smell and taste.
Reducing our overweight, that constant struggle, at any time of our life, even if we are 80 years old, reducing overweight is positive, especially abdominal fat, the belly, which is a very important predictor of cardiovascular events, I'm sorry to break it to you.
(Laughter) And let's point out not only the most physical things but also the cognitive and social, how about signing up to do something new, enroll a course, meet new people.
The social environment, the people we meet is also essential in our aging, in our life in general.
When I started researching, my goal was to find preventive medicine interventions that could improve the quality of life and life expectancy of older people, not only consider prevention in children, but also in older people.
And I, as a doctor, thought about the classic issues: overweight, tobacco, excessive alcohol, sedentary lifestyle --
However, as I was researching, I had a US database which is representative of the whole country, its results are very solid, and people over 65 were asked three questions about their neighborhood: Do you think that your neighbors know each other well?
Do you think that those neighbors are willing to help each other?
Can you trust your neighbors?
It's a measure of social cohesion that sociologists used for other areas, not for preventive medicine.
But it caught my attention and I included it in my models.
And it turns out that people who had a high social cohesion, feeling that in their neighborhood people knew, helped and trusted each other, they have a 40 percent lower risk of suffering a cardiovascular event.
Besides the classic risk factors, there's also the social risk factors, and our neighborhood, our environment, is very important for our health.
Your neighbors affect your health.
(Laughter) And you, are you good neighbors?
Do you greet?
There's a lot of scientific evidence being published and accumulating on how neighborhoods affect health, not just the social environment, but also the physical, sidewalks that are walkable.
Actually here in Spain there is a leading group at the University of Alcalá de Henares, I recommend checking their results because they are fascinating.
I believe that in the future the real state ads will say: two bathrooms, one bedroom, and social cohesion.
(Applause) And how should your neighbors be, how a neighbor affects you, -- besides you liking them more or less -- how does a neighbor affect your cardiovascular risk, it's a leap.
There are many mechanisms of action and also different hypotheses, but I believe that a very simple one is walking, specially if we are considering people over 65, to whom walking is perhaps the easiest physical exercise and the most recommended.
Do you think people not feeling safe or sheltered in their neighborhood will practice physical exercise in it, or go for a walk?
How important it is to go for a walk accompanied.
I was fascinated by a paper published in 2016, also in Madrid, at Universidad Autónoma, at the Department of Preventive Medicine, leading in cardiovascular research,
they are doing a research relating hypertension or blood pressure with social support.
A very intelligent researcher had the idea of introducing this question: Do you walk alone or accompanied?
People who walked accompanied had less nocturnal blood pressure.
It was hugely beneficial for their health because it is not just walking and moving the muscles, legs or heart.
It's also the company, talking.
Aging is not a problem, aging is a privilege and a fact.
In little more than ten years we will be one of the oldest countries in the world.
Of every three persons, one will be over 60 years old.
Where do we want to grow old?
Where do we want our parents to age, or our grandparents?
In what society?
We have to get rid of that negative vision of old age, first because it affects our health, and second because it affects the health of others.
I invite you to question these stereotypes that are sometimes unconscious, about what a person can or cannot do based on their appearance.
And I also invite you to build a society in which we all fit, a person of 20, a person of 80, a person pushing a cart and a person carrying a cane or in a wheelchair.
A society in which we can age with quality, in which we can age with health, in which we can grow old walking together.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Many years ago the Star Trek series began each episode with the phrase: "Space: the final frontier."
The main starship, the Enterprise, had the mission of exploring strange new worlds to reach unknown places nobody had never arrived before.
While the original series it's a bit old, I still love it, because in each episode they actually find new worlds.
And they challenge what they knew until that very moment.
I always found incredible the chance to make those discoveries.
The question: "Are there other worlds outside the solar system?", is a question we always had.
It's of the oldest questions of humanity.
The Star Trek exploration was science fiction by then.
But today it's ceasing to be thay way.
When I became interested in astronomy we only knew planets in our solar system.
But today we live in unique times.
Because for the first time in history we know that other planets exist around stars that are not the Sun.
The first planet outside our solar system was found a little over 20 years ago.
And it was the beginning of a true revolution in astronomy.
Today we can spot on our galaxy map more than 3000 planets around other stars.
Planets that are outside our solar system or "exoplanets", as we call them.
And the count continues.
We know that the star that is closer to our solar system is only four light years away and it has a planet around it.
And the farthest planet we know of is 27,000 light years away.
That means that if we sent a WhatsApp message to an imaginary friend who lives on that planet we would have to wait 54,000 years to find out they left in on seen.
(Laughter) And, yes, it's true.
We don't have an Enterprise ship to take us there yet.
But we do have the technology that lets us discover and explore them.
I am an astronomer and I belong to this new generation of space explorers.
I'm in the scientific team of two space missions.
I'm on NASA's Juno mission, which right now is around Jupiter changing our knowledge of that planet.
And I'm also on the scientific team of the Ariel mission, from the European Space Agency, which is a space telescope that we expect in 10 years to be watching the atmospheres of exoplanets with.
My work is like trying to put together a jigsaw puzzle having just a couple of pieces.
I want to know what these planets are like, how they were formed, how they evolved. And for that I study their atmospheres and their insides.
I take the few observations that we have available and with those bits of information I think up how these planets would be from a physical and chemical standpoint.
Then, I design computer models to create simulations to show me how these planets that we are finding might be.
And the truth is that it took us a lot to find the first exoplanets.
In the 1990s we just found them.
That's because finding exoplanets is super difficult.
Stars have a light so much brighter than that of planets.
They dazzle us and don't let us see what they have around them.
To give you an idea, imagine a lighthouse 6 miles away.
What we want to find is a firefly right next to that lighthouse.
Finding exoplanets is that difficult.
But we are finding them.
We are studying them.
And it's not science fiction, but reality.
And as it happened in Star Trek every time they found a new world, each new exoplanet pushes the frontiers of our knowledge.
Today, we know that there are really crazy planets.
Planets that could have come out of a science fiction work.
There are so many out there, that even the strangest ones you may imagine possibly exist.
We were able to observe newborn planets, baby planets, or very young planets like these you are looking at now.
When planets are young, they still have heat left from their creation and that's why they shine more brightly and it's easier to detect them directly.
On the other end, as well, we find very old planets.
Planets almost as old as the universe.
This one you are seeing here is called Methuselah and we believe it to have 12.5 billion years.
That's almost three times the age of our planet.
Of course, that planet doesn't shine as much as the young planets, so we don't have a real image like the one I showed you before, but I am showing you an artistic representation of what that exoplanet would look like.
We also find exoplanets around binary or multiple stars.
That is, a planet that has two or more suns.
We find planets around dead stars.
Stars very different from our sun.
Places we didn't think possible to have planets.
And the next ones now are my favorites, because I was studying their atmospheres.
They are rocky planets like ours, but they are very different from our planet in fact, because they are super close to its central star.
So close that they have temperatures of more than 2000 ºC on the surface.
So at that temperature their surface is an ocean of lava that evaporates and forms an atmosphere of vaporized rocks.
Imagine: just like it rains water here, on those planets it rains rocks.
So extreme are the planets we are finding.
The exoplanets challenge us all the time.
The first ones we found already put us to the test.
Think of our solar system where we have the Sun, which is a pretty normal middle-aged star.
The planets that are closest to the Sun are the rocky planets.
Those in which we can stand on the surface.
And those who are much further away are the giant planets.
It's easy and logical: the rocky planets are closer to the star, and the giants, farther.
For centuries we thought that this was the normal structure for a planetary system and we built models to explain and sustain this.
Until the first exoplanets appeared.
Especially, the first one that we found around a star similar to the Sun.
That planet is called Dimidium and it's pretty similar to Jupiter -- nothing weird there -- but it is seven times closer to its central star than Mercury to the Sun.
The truth is that we were not prepared for that discovery.
Actually, the people who made the discovery had to check everything several times before announcing it.
And that was not all.
We also found a new class of planets that we didn't know that existed.
Planets that we call super-Earths, or mini-Neptunes, because they are so much bigger than our planet, but not as big to be a giant planet.
It is an intermediate size that we don't have in our solar system, and we didn't know it existed.
And these planets, apart from being a new class, a new species that we didn't know of, they make up for the vast majority of exoplanets that we are finding.
The truth is that I learned a lot of things in these years of studying exoplanets.
I learned how to face the unknown.
What do you do when the information you receive makes you question your own beliefs?
Dimidium and the super-Earths did that.
They forced us to change the paradigms, to change the way of thinking we had until that moment.
I also learned that sometimes, to learn more about ourselves we have to look very far away.
Studying exoplanets helps us understand a little more about the formation our own solar system.
It helps us understand what creates the conditions for the origin of life, and put our planet Earth in context within the universe.
And I also learned why this kind of research is important to us all.
The huge technology we developed these past years to study these exoplanets pushed us to the limit of what we thought we were capable of doing.
And that is the great value of the studies in basic sciences.
They help us create new technology that in the long-term is applied in unthinkable ways, helping to improve our quality of life.
Einstein was not thinking about the GPS when he developed the Theory of Relativity a hundred years ago.
And yet that theory is fundamental to have an accurate measurement of positions.
That is, for the map that we all have in our cell phones and that we use every day.
And this is a very small example.
The life of all of us is affected by astronomy, much more than we realize.
From the Big Bang, the formation of our planet, the perfect distance between Earth and the Sun so there is liquid water on the surface, and so that all of us can be here.
To even more everyday things, such as the seasons, the day and night cycle, the way to measure time.
Think of the meteorite that made the dinosaurs go extinct.
And even more, that all of us are made of star dust.
The carbon in our DNA was generated inside a star.
And astronomy affects us in other ways as well.
It inspired generations of artists and it is present in the mythology of all the ancient civilizations.
And it's us, the astronomers, who are after the question that may alter the course of our history.
The Enterprise, that ship I told you about at the beginning, had another mission besides exploring foreign new worlds: It was also seeking out new life and new civilizations.
And now that we know that there are so many exoplanets out there what we want to know is: Are we alone in the universe?
To find signs of life in any of these exoplanets would be incredible.
And perhaps a little scary, too.
But it would also be wonderful, and definitely terrifying and lonely, to think that we are the only ones in the vastness of the universe.
The next time you look at the stars, think about that question.
Finding the answer is our next step.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Seven years ago I was in Santiago del Estero presenting a book and I was approached by a boy I didn't know.
His name was Nestor and he was born in a town of 700 inhabitants.
Today, there is still no electricity or water in his town.
His dream was to become an agronomist, but since there was not even one secondary school in town, when he finished elementary school, he went to a shelter school 60 km away to get closer to his dream.
Now that he was finishing it, he realized that his dream was still far away, for he had no chance of going to the cities where universities were located.
He looked at me that day and said, "What can you do to help me become an engineer?"
I went back to Buenos Aires, I didn't share this with anyone.
Within a few days, a company called us to say that they would donate a house and that they will build it any place in the country we want.
That house and Nestor's question gave rise to a new project: residence halls for "Fundación Sí".
What are they?
They are large houses in the cities where universities are located so that young people from remote rural areas of Argentina have the chance of living there completely for free and study.
When we started we thought that by solving the distance and resources issues the problem was solved.
We were completely wrong.
The academic level they leave secondary school with is so low, we found out that the kids didn't understand what they read.
We had to add tuition, tutors, educational psychologists -- and then it began to improve.
But as we got deeper, we saw that they came with very hard stories affecting their academic performance.
High rates of abuse, violence, neglect.
We had to bring in psychologists, support volunteers and then it started moving forward.
Today, we have 13 residence halls and in 2020 there will be 464 young people living in them.
Sabri was born in a very small town in northern Santa Fe.
At 12, every Monday she walked 40 km to get to the school where she lived all week in order to attend secondary school.
She was the first in town to finish high school.
When she was finishing, she'd hear the rest of his classmates saying "I'm going to be an engineer", "I'm going to be a vet", "I'm going to be a teacher".
And she would say, "I want".
Because the distance between being able and wanting was enormous.
Today, she studies Industrial Engineering.
That is called will power.
We called Selena for an interview to participate during a selection process in La Rioja -- she lives in a town 200 km away -- That early morning the only bus that goes through town didn't show up.
We don't know why.
Far from giving up, knowing that it was the only opportunity she had, she started knocking on every neighbors' door who she knew to have a car, begging if anyone understood her and could take her to the city.
One neighbor understood and took her.
Today, she studies medicine.
That is called conviction.
(Applause) When Mili heard about the project -- Mili is from San Salvador Maza, in northern Salta, on the border with Bolivia -- we had no more room in Salta residence.
We had only one place left and it was in Neuquen.
2300 km from her home.
We offered it to her.
Obviously, we thought she was going to say no.
But her answer surprised us.
"To study, I will go any place in the country."
That is called courage.
(Applause) Two years ago, Micaela joined the residence hall. She wanted to study Economic Sciences.
I've never seen someone study that much in my life.
We gave her all kinds of support you can imagine.
She could never take the application test.
Three weeks ago, she wrote us asking to sign up again, this time to study something else.
Education.
There's a small fragment of her letter that would like to share with you today: "I decided to study Education Sciences because I had the need to understand, to find answers to my "why", but mostly because my niece is one year old today and that was the big push.
She's going to grow up in the same house as me, in the same place as me, she will go to my same school, and maybe also my sister -- her mother -- will have to chart with a stick and a shovel -- like my parents did every time the holidays ended -- the 3 mile path that had been deserted for months for us to return to school.
Mom always noted her desire for a better future for us, but she trusted it to our school.
I would like the education in my town to improve because as well as my nephews, more children were born.
And as I know the truth because I lived it, I don't want to keep sowing false hopes.
Those who break the rules and want to go on studying know that the road will be hard, it's almost to start almost from scratch.
And of course you can do it and with much effort some succeed.
But that is the problem, that many succeed, but not all."
Mica clearly explains why we do what we do.
The whole project is entirely carried out by volunteers, who are very different from each other, but share the same essence.
Why we do it?
Because we are completely convinced of the power of transformation of human beings.
And if we have this power, we have this responsibility.
Because studying cannot be a privilege for some, it has to be a right for everyone.
(Applause) For kids to find out about this project, we cannot notify them either by phone or the Internet.
We travel to the farthest schools, we have more than 1456 schools visited.
When we arrive, do you know what we find?
Kids don't even know which careers exist.
"What does a music teacher do?" they ask.
The first thing we have to do
is tell them what the options are.
No one can choose what they like if they don't know what exists.
And I said choose.
Choosing, something that for us is normal, for them is completely unthinkable.
The place where we are born cannot be an impediment for those who want to continue studying.
Nestor, the kid we spoke about at the beginning, who came to ask us what we could do for him, two months ago took his last exam to become an engineer.
Today he is in Spain doing his thesis.
Not to stay there, but to return here and put everything he learned to the service of the development of his town.
Because he knows where he comes from and knows the responsibility he has today.
Because each of these kids is not just transforming their family.
They also transform their peers' in their communities.
School dropout levels is very high because kids feel that whether they finish school or not, their fate is written.
When they see that a kid who lived just like they do, in the same mud house, and he could make it, they see that they can make it, too.
And then, each of these students, are a clear sign of hope, a clear example that they can do it.
Every time I talk with them it touches me deep because I see that not even the most difficult stories made them give up, because they stand with their desires intact, and when I see the effort and will they put in spite of the few tools they were given, they reaffirm the huge faith I have in the new generations.
Let nothing and no one, kids, come tell you that you cannot.
The house where Nestor lives today is managed by Flor.
Flor also studied in that residence and graduated living there.
The day Flor joined in to live in that house, she told us: "I have to choose today: I buy a bag of rice or buy a book, I cannot buy both".
Today, she works of what she loves.
Today, she rents and she pays her rent with her paycheck.
That is called breaking the circle of poverty forever.
That is called dignity.
And Flor today, manages the residence voluntarily because she wants others to have the same possibility she had.
In order to hack poverty, you need will power, yes, you need conviction and you need courage.
But without opportunities it's impossible.
Talking about the next 10 years or talking about the future makes no sense if we don't rethink the education system and the opportunities that all of us who make up this society, provide.
If we have the power,
we have the responsibility.
Everyone. It is up to us.
(Applause)
Hello everybody. I'm going first, so let's get started.
We're going to start by talking about words.
And more than about words, we're going to talk about language.
You've all heard the phrase "In the beginning was the word" That phrase, although religious, does in some way define all of us. Because we don't know - anthropologists, sociologists, linguists that have done studies - nobody really knows which was first, homo fablis or homo sapiens: if we could think because we could talk, or, if we could talk because we could think.
When the human brain began to develop, and we stood up on two feet, and started using our hands with these thumbs, at that moment we also began to construct a code which reflects our lives, which makes us see the world in distinct ways, because you realize those that have a mother tongue in my case it's Spanish - for most of you it probably is too; we see the world around us according to the language we speak.
That is, language is a kind of filter which allows us to see reality or can also prevent us from seeing other realities. Because we think in Spanish, we see the categories of the world in Spanish.
I'll give you an example, in Mexican Spanish we use the word snow to say 'it snowed', or 'there was snow'.
Of course, we don't live somewhere that gets snow, generally it's "he went skiing on the snow", that's it.
In the case of the Inuit which sometimes we mistakenly call eskimos, which means "those that eat raw meat" which is slightly disrespectful.
Anyway, the Inuit of the Arctic have more than 20 words for what we know as snow.
There's snow that fell recently, snow that's about to fall.
There is snow that's good for cooking, that is, to melt and use to make soup, there's snow for building igloos there's lots of kinds of snow.
This is all in the Inuit language which views the world differently.
Another example might be Polynesian languages, like the Tuvalu language.
For example, what we know as green or blue are two colours on a spectrum from green to blue, and in Tuvalu there are more than 20 words to define the different tones between green and blue: emerald green, turquoise blue, sky blue, indigo, etc., because the Southern Sea has so many colours it requires that many words.
These categories, our way of talking, define us and makes us think in a certain way.
There's a language spoken in Australia, an almost extinct language called Dharawal, in which all nouns are divided into groups, just as Spanish has masculine and feminine nouns, it's something similar. There are 4 groups: balam, bala, bali and balla. These 4 groups define all objects, all the nouns in that environment.
Balam is for masculine things and anything related to male animals.
Balli is for all edible things Bali is all those things that aren't in the other groups, and finally Balam is women, fire and dangerous things.
(Laughter) Women, fire and dangerous things,
all go together. In this world, an arrow, a bonfire a dart, a mortar, a poisonous spider go in the same group along with wives.
So you realize that language informs how we see the world around us.
It's difficult to imagine anything that we don't have in our language, for example, how do you explain to a foreigner, in the case of Mexicans, what an 'itacate' is?
An 'itacate' is not a doggie-bag, it's not food to-go.
It's complex, it's when there's too much food at a family meal and your auntie says to you "Here's your 'itacate' of the stuffed chillis, you never had any of those." It's very cultural, so when we have to learn another language we need to learn both the language, and that language's culture.
Mexicans, for example, we have a lot of things about our way of life, which also define us, they define Mexican idiosyncracy.
Let's look at the most obvious example.
One day there was this guy, from another country, Columbian, and there was a Mexican, they studied together in an American university and it was very cold.
They were roomies, they arrive and see there's nothing in the fridge, "Hey, there's nothing to eat, we'll have to go out", said the Coloumbian and the Mexican says "yeah, in a minute"
So the Columbian puts on a jacket, a coat, gloves and the Mexican is still wearing shorts.
"Aren't we going to get something to eat?"
"I said in a minute, not in a second" (Laughter) There's a big difference between 'a minute' and 'a second'
In Mexico, 'a minute' can last years.
It's a passage of time with absolutely no definition.
Or maybe we'll say, for example: "Honey, have your little egg with its little beans and its little corn bread" The corn bread belongs to the beans, the beans to the egg and the egg to the child.
In a study I did, I realized that mothers use 'su', the possesive pronoun, which in other Spanish-speaking countries is only used to refer to a possession, or a possessive relationship, as an endearment or term of affection.
Example: "the baby, poor thing he's hurt in his leg, they'll have to cure his little leg" Madam, why do you say his little leg?
We know they'll cure the leg, the leg belongs to the child.
"No, he's only little, how can I say the leg, it's his little leg because he's little" And that's how things are because Mexican Spanish also has this form of affection which we use everyday.
For example, the mother says, "Look after yourself for me" "Look after yourself for me" what's going on with that?
You look after yourself, but look after yourself for me because what affects you affects me.
Or you could say, look after her for me to a boyfriend, because you're affected, because you're involved.
So you see with Mexicans, our language defines us, it defines our way of thinking and being, so for each one of us, as individuals, we say words and we tell stories and we believe them.
Our words define us.
Everything we tell ourselves as individuals, as Lacan said, "the subconscious through language" We say things and everything we say in some way defines us.
Words have power over us.
When we say, for example: "I'm so fat" That "fat", in that moment, means that I have the idea that I'm like that
When I say, "I always screw it up", well you always screw it up.
Keep on repeating it, and you'll keep screwing it up.
The typical girl who says, "No, when I feel like doing exercise, I go to bed until the urge subsides" (Laughter) Well, obviously that's exactly what she does.
That is to say, we're all constantly defining ourselves.
Each time we make a mistake "oh what an idiot, what an idiot" Well yes, what an idiot and it'll stay that way.
Because we're like that, we're made of words.
We are homo fablis, and we have these preconceived ideas.
"Mexicans are lazy" "People from Mexico City are jerks" and we go on believing these ideas and internalizing them in our subconscious every day.
That's why Freud described a lapsus.
Which is just the gap between language and thought. It's like when children get their words mixed up and say something quite different
to what they mean,
but which sounds phonetically the same. There's an unconscious meaning that comes from saying things that we've repeated until we're tired of them until we believe them and that is very important.
My Grandmother said that where you put yourself you stay, and it's true, if you put yourself in a role you put yourself in a situation and there you stay.
If you want to move, you have to change the words that you use, to change places.
You have to change your discourse, because it's how you talk that defines you.
If you don't change that, and you keep saying, "Oh, poor me", "I'm so fat", "I'm stupid" or "the poor thing" "poor me, I'm such a middle child" or "oh my parents" ...
Or you live with a nickname: 'el pulque' because you're all pale, like the drink.
Poor bastard! Going through life
with "what a pulque". So he goes through life, along with his nickname.
If we want to change the way we define ourselves, we have to change the way we talk.
I'll tell you something, when I was 18 years old I was at the end of prep school and I got electrocuted.
I was in a jacuzzi, in my father's house and I got electrocuted.
I spent 6 months in the hospital, one month in a coma. I spent 6 months in the hospital unconscious, with septicemia.
38% of my body suffered burns, third degree burns.
It was very serious.
I was leaving prep school, couldn't go to university. I spent a year with no study. Because after leaving the hospital I had operations.
When I got out of there, what they said about me was "You know the girl who got electrocuted in a jacuzzi?"
"The sick girl", "the poor girl", "the one that got burnt". Friends of my mum came to see me, and I went to pray to the 'Senor del veneno' with that narrative of "the poor girl" "the one that got burnt, the unlucky thing" and, "why should it happen to her, what bad luck" and the whole world saw me with a pitying face. I was just 18 about to turn 19.
Suddenly, I realized that if I stayed there, I would always be that "poor girl" "the poor girl who couldn't cope" Now, you'll get to know me, and see my twitter or you'll read 'Algarabia', the magazine I edited, or the publishers I ran, you'll know me for many other things. Because I'm a linguist, I have a doctorate in linguistics. Because I like words, I like ideas, because I love cinema, because I jog, because I do yoga, because I smoke cigars,
a rare thing for women. I smoke cigars.
Because I like to drink - I really like alcohol.
For lots of reasons.
Because I invented the Chingonary, and believe in the verb, chingar, and I believe in the power of chingon words and that Mexicans are chingon. You don't have to use the Spanish Royal Academy's dictionary instead, everyone must use their own dictionary. Most Spanish-speakers in the world are Mexican. Out of 440 million Spanish speakers, 120 million of us are Mexican.
And so I believe in this. I believe in the chingonary I believe a lot of things and I have other characteristics.
I sing like a chingon, badly but I like singing and lots of other things I'd like to do.
My words were defining me.
When I see myself I don't see that poor sick woman that had an accident and was burnt.
No, I see someone that did other things
That means for good and for bad. I see who I am now through a discourse I created.
A discourse we are all creating, an we all have the power to create our own discourse. Sometimes you have to lose fear of language and words
People ask me, but there's a way to say things, that's what the dictionary says.
But see, no: we all have the power to have our own language.
That's our right, our weapon our tool to move forward.
Words have power. The words we say to ourselves everyday have power.
And those powers empower you or leave you powerless standing where you were.
If you say to yourself, repeat to yourself the right words, you'll get to where you need to be.
What you have in your head will take you to the finish line that you want to reach. Because we are beings of words, beings of discourse, we are that homo fablis, which was born one day and which has made a whole culture, and development through words.
So just as where you put yourself you stay, place yourself with the right words at the right moment, with the right speech, and the right verb.
(Applause)
When I was a kid, my school life drifted from the curiosity of the outside world to the nonsense of having to memorize so much that I didn't need.
I unknowingly suffered from what's known as bulimic learning, that is, a huge ingestion of data that's vomited in a written test and leaves no caloric benefit whatsoever.
(Applause) It's a still dominating practice in many schools.
I was bored, my mind was always dreaming.
They even told me I wasn't good at studying.
Until one day something happened that would change me profoundly, something I didn't understand until much later.
It was the last years of General Franco's dictatorship.
In Spain people were already breathing the scent of the fight for liberty.
Instead, in Chile, the military had bloodily finished the era and life of Salvador Allende.
I was then studying in a school of Jesuits in Barcelona and had a teacher, Father Ignacio Vila, that one day did something completely different to the rest.
He came into class and instead of saying the usual "open the book in page 25," he challenged us with a question: "Is it possible to peacefully achieve an alternative way to capitalism?
Do any of you want to explore what's going on in Chile these days?"
We all looked at each other in awe.
And something clicked in my head suddenly connecting me with the school and the world.
I signed up excitedly with two other friends.
For a couple of weeks we left behind textbooks, frozen information, and we read newspapers, magazines from those days, and even talked to Chileans that lived in Barcelona and were on one side or the other.
We explored the information, exchanged opinions, while we studied sociology, history and practiced language without realizing it.
It was the life that vibrated inside of us.
And, what a joy, when it was the time to present our work!
There was a huge expectation from the class.
And it wasn't even going into the exam!
The exposition was controversial, fun.
Not even us three agreed.
There were tense moments.
I even remember my passionate ending: "It's impossible to peacefully achieve an alternative way to capitalism."
(Applause) It made a big fuss.
Father Vila reacted against it and so did my two classmates.
But in that school we had learned to debate and to respect our ideas.
Something my country needs urgently.
(Applause) And I had never seen my class vibrate so passionately.
So, is it possible for the school to understand that knowledge isn't there to be saved into the fridge of the mind?
Is it possible to apply it to answers in questions that relate to our lives?
Look, I believe yes, it is.
And let me tell you the story of a teenager.
María is 14 years old and studies in a high school in Barcelona.
One Saturday night, watching TV, she hears something that impacts her deeply.
The police has seized in the port of Barcelona a big shipment of clothing intended to be brought in illegally.
What most impresses María is that the clothes have been made by 8-year-old girls and boys in another country.
María felt goosebumps.
Could it be possible?
The next morning, María wants to share her concern with her classmates and teachers.
As every morning, students sit in their seats ready to share the start of the school day.
And María expresses her worry: Why does child-exploitative labor exist?
Why do we buy clothes made by 8-year-old girls and boys?
The question echoes in her classmates' hearts and their antennae agitate energetically.
That scene is usual in María's school.
Her class is composed by 60 students and three teachers of Language, Sciences, and Humanities.
The teachers feel inquired and suggest to work in a project that answers María's question.
The issue fits perfectly with projects the teachers have prepared related to the official syllabus.
So they propose to study feudalism, the Industrial Revolution and some of the effects of globalization.
But furthermore, they propose to explore information, to order it, exchange points of view, and even write their findings in groups of four to check their truthfulness.
They also propose to design an artistic creation that exemplifies those conclusions.
And finally, they propose to share it with the big group.
In reality, the teachers are activating competences related to language, history, critical analysis, artistic creation.
They're helping them to create collective knowledge.
They're helping them to succeed in tomorrow's world.
When the day's over, teachers ask students to meet again in their seats.
Because now it's time to acquire consciousness of how we learn.
Becoming aware of how we learn is one of the best investments you can do in the future of a person.
It's the moment to focus on the processes, step aside, and look inside of ourselves.
And it's also the moment of saying thank you.
One of the most usual practices of Jesuits' spirituality.
María comes back home and she's a passionate torrent she wants to explain to her parents what she has lived and learned in school.
Her parents look at each other astonished at a teenager wanting to share what she learned at school.
This scene I'm explaining to you exists.
It's called Horizon 2020.
I was privileged to be part of the directing team that conceived it, designed it, and launched it 9 years ago in the Jesuits schools of Catalonia.
All of them situated in different social contexts: 8 schools, 13,000 students, 1,300 educators working as a network.
Horizon 2020 is a systemic change project starting from a series of simple actions, that don't require big budgets and can be applied in many types of different schools, that need illusion, calling, and different learning times.
Let me explain to you the three pillars of this project: The first one is to connect the school with the life of the students.
Learn what we want to be.
In María's case, her concern comes from her life experiences, and emotions.
And the teachers propose to work in a project connected to that concern.
To learn is to live through experiences that leave a mark on us.
Father Vila pointed this out to us and we're putting it at the center of the purpose of the school.
The second foundation is to start schoolwork with questions that students must research and answer.
It's the opposite of teachers that start giving out all the answers for students to memorize.
As in María's case, the current reality gives us many possibilities of questions for the students.
In our schools, students work during many weeks on projects that teachers have previously prepared.
Teachers from different disciplines.
But in order to do that we had to introduce radical changes in the school, breaking down myths such as the division by subject, or that students learn better in smaller groups.
We've put students in large groups that allow us to have three teachers in the classroom.
Which also allows us to socialize learning, mix up disciplines, reach a bigger diversity of students, and also contribute to make the project economically viable.
The third pillar is to change the professor's wiring.
From our dream, we had to learn to work in a way we'd never been taught how to--
Unlearn in order to learn in another way.
Learn the tools that students must use in class, such as cooperative work or work by projects.
We learned a process of more protagonism and empowerment in order to go beyond reciting information.
We've also given ourselves tools for integral evaluation, that allows us to accompany students so they're aware of what they know and what they're able to do.
We've changed the traditional purpose of teachers.
And this isn't only happening in our schools, it's happening in many other places
in the world.
Do you imagine what it means, after so many centuries of doing the same thing, that a change of this magnitude is happening in so many schools around the world?
In Catalonia, for example, in the last two years, more than 500 schools, public and private, are working together to further deepen this process of transformation.
In reality, it's a sort of educational spring that broke from the dream of so many teachers and the demand of so many families.
In the beginning, our teachers were scared that this model could have a negative effect on standardized tests.
But five years after the beginning of the project, not only academic results are still high, but we've managed to reach a higher diversity of students.
And what's more important, three international university research groups found significant advances in the classroom climate, in the creativity of students, and in the effects of cooperative work.
Even if I didn't understand it until much later, Father Vila showed us the way in that school in the 70's.
We've drawn from this inspiration and tried to go much further.
Look, I think we're waking up from a long lethargy and reaching a clear conviction: to educate is to connect the school with the life project of each one of our students.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Do you like ice cream?
Audience: Yes!
Raise your hand if you like ice cream.

Everybody likes ice cream.
I used to love it too, but for a long time, at least 10 years, I didn't eat any more.
All of the sudden,
I said I didn't like it, or even worse --
My taste for ice cream was gone, for real.
Anyway, I'm not going to talk about ice cream nor about my drama, which is a lot, I'm here to share with you how your noggin works, the one we have here, above our shoulders.
How we get to do what we do, how we become who we are.
The risk we take of getting stuck in our own story, like this.
And if I'm lucky enough, I will need to be, I may share with you some clues to get out.
However, let's begin at the beginning.
Ever since we are little, everybody writes a story: our story.
We do so to find out who we are, what we do, what we like, what we don't -- Look, I have two children.
Anita, who is three and a half, and Benja who is two.
Sometimes, my wife and I try to imagine and guess what they will be like when they grow up.
Anita, as an adolescent, isn't going to listen to us at all.
She will be super challenging.
On the other hand Benja -- Benja might be more mellow, lazy.
I'm sure he'll complain about homework and things like that, but he'll be easier to get along.
We don't imagine this because we have a crystal ball, in fact, we don't have one, we do so because we are already reading their stories and these are the pages that would follow.
I'm sure that those who have children, grandchildren, nephews, or younger siblings have played this game at least once, right?
Guessing what's next in their stories.
But anyway, this is not kid stuff. All of you have your own story.
That story joins all your experiences in time: the way you feel, the way you think,
the way you act.
It's like that. We all have a story.
Now, who writes this story?
Who puts it together?
Have you ever sat down
with a pen to write it?
No, your mind does.
And it does that on its own, without asking permission, without asking much. Every day it spends time sorting out images, experiences, setting ideas, setting methods.
Your mind is like an editor.
Do you know what book editors do?
They check that there are no errors, they make sure that the story follows a chronological order, and above all that the story is consistent, coherent, and there are no contradictions in it.
The editor isn't a visible figure, it doesn't appear as the author of the book, but its work is very important.
Your mind is the editor.
Now, there are stories that leave a mark on us. and it's in those stories that the editor focus on.
What is said, what is explained from that situation is turned into truths.
Afterward, those truths are stored in our memory, and those truths is what will define us later on, and will set our course.
Sometimes it's about rough times, as we all have gone through.
Some others, just a word can be a turning point, it can be even something silly, I'm going to tell you one.
One night, my lifelong friend, Rodri, told me at a bar -- (Voice-over) "Luqui, man, go and get that girl, If you don't ask, you won't get anyway.
One out of ten will say yes."
(Laughter) I guarantee I can still hear that, it was exactly like that.
I was very shy, I was such a pussy.
That's how I felt, that's how I acted.
That is how it was written in my story but for some reason, I was encouraged by his words.
In a second, a truth from my story, a truth from my editor, shattered.
From then on, my story changed completely, I tried to get them like crazy.
That same night. (Laugher)
I must tell I didn't get anything anyway, well, I must admit that's another story.
So, it's through moments and situations that we build ourselves.
One day we tell ourselves things a certain way, the editor turns that into a truth, and from then on it has only one task: keeping it consistent.
From that moment on, we will spend a lot of time empowering what we say and to do so, the editor relies on this whole machine.
It only deals with what's interesting to it, it erases, filters, removes things that don't fit and empowers the things that do fit in the story in progress.
Would you believe me if I said the editor even makes things up so the story has a happy ending?
Indeed, it does.
That's why the editor doesn't read reality, it makes it up by adapting what actually happens to the story being written.
Now, let's go back to my story about the ice cream. I'm done anyway.
Every Sunday we all got together at home
to barbeque. My parents, there are five siblings, a boyfriend, a girlfriend, sometimes cousins, sometimes aunts and uncles, there were a lot of people.
After all the ritual of the starter the barbeque and clearing the table it was time for dessert, ice cream.
One of those Sundays, when I was given I said no: "I don't like ice cream!"
Truth is that I did eat ice cream and I loved it, but that Sunday I said no.
That was when my editor wrote a truth and, afterward, stand up for it.
More than 10 years went by until one day my neurons communicated across better synapsis
and I asked myself: Don't I like ice cream?
(Laughter) That afternoon I walked toward the freezer, I pulled out the tub, I looked around, I didn't want to be seen, since I didn't eat ice cream!
(Laughter) I stuck the soup spoon into the caramel spread, it was still half frozen and I ate it.
I loved ice cream! (Laughter)
Of course, I loved ice cream!
Everybody loves ice cream!
I went in on that tub like the worst rainy and depressing Sunday until it was empty, and that was when I realized, at that moment I realized.
The truth that I didn't like ice cream melted itself. (Laughter)
That Sunday, everything was well at home everyone was chatting, enjoying, having a good, sharing, and that was me.
(Laughter) I was a teenager, I was at odds with life, I was at odds with the world.
The ice cream wasn't important, I said no because I wasn't in tune with pleasure.
I said no because I wasn't in that fellowship of happiness, that's what was important in my story: to be at odds.
The ice cream thing was just by chance, but it stuck to my editor, and afterward, stand up for it over time.
This is the thing: We all build our own identity based on truths that we bought one day, and afterward we no longer question,
even if the context changes. I'm talking about relevant things, not silly like the ice cream.
It's like that.
It can be a little sad sometimes.
I'll tell you something else, because all of this happens while we are unaware, the mental editor does all of this work without us realizing, and in part that's OK, this has a biological function and evolutionary importance.
Just imagine the time it would take each day upon awakening be told who you are, what you do, and at the end of the day gather all those experiences, images and organize them as if they were a sticker book.
Impossible.
You couldn't do anything else but that.
That's why evolution, assigned this task to the editor, so it could do this painstaking work without bothering us, without asking, so that we can have our time and our attention for other things, for example, be here, enjoying TED while your mind writes in silence.
I know it sounds quite comfortable to have things done by someone else, but trust me, there is a huge risk in this game.
Everything we say we are is nothing but a bunch of so-called truths we wrote up one day.
The good and the bad, OK?
Afterward, it's the editor who empowers these ideas, values, feelings, attitudes, that often define us, condition us.
And because of that damn obsession of being coherent eventually the editor empower old ideas about us instead of finding the way to change and grow, and we don't even notice, we are on autopilot
Everything you say you cannot change and you should give up, are not truths.
I want you to start thinking they are lies from your editor.
I even want you to doubt about your memory accuracy because it's also involved there.
Many of your obstacles could have been real when you set it up but later,
it becomes the editor's obsession.
I want you to know that the editor is trained not to let go those truths, it's a Doberman. It mustn't do it, it can't do it, you have to do it yourself.
My story about ice cream can be silly or can be fun but I couldn't explain how many limitations, and how much suffering you can get if you get stuck in your own story.
I experience this every day of my life.
It's my job to try to unmask the editor.
Anyways, it's always surprising.
Now, what's your ice cream story?
I don't want anyone to leave today without realizing where the ice cream is in your story. Because you only live once.
It's not worth leaving it on consignment. It's not worth being a mere interpreter of what our mind says.
Anyway, it's up to you. (Laughter) Opening your eyes, looking inside and questioning every single truth of your story, and rewrite it
so that your editor isn't the one who writes your destiny, but yourself.
Cheers!
(Applause)
I am a dreamer and for the past 20 years I've had a recurrent dream.
The dream of building a hospital that works as a bridge between medicine and ecology, by using the tools of sustainable architecture.
My occupation is oncologic surgery.
That means that every day my fundamental job is to operate people with cancer.
Throughout 25 years, I operated on more than 2000 cases like this one:
This is a CAT scan of a patient with lung cancer and, unfortunately, is a really frequent part of my daily job.
But it shouldn't be that way.
Ideally, I should be able to let go of the scalpel thanks to an effective prevention.
In retrospective, when I look at the spring creek of my life I see that my daily contact with cancer, its study, has change the way I see medicine and the way I see the world.
Before, I just thought about just cutting... My mind now is more preventive.
This led me to become a vegetarian in '94 and to study the ecological, economic, political, and environmental subjects to be able to solve the problems, or part of the problems that affect our planet.
In this course, throughout life, I found inspiring the life and work of revolutionaries like Albert Schweitzer, winner of the Nobel Peace Prize whose slogan was "reverence for life", and in that reverence, logically, he was also a vegetarian.
And Potter's work, who created the modern concept of "bioethics, the ethic of life" which he saw as a bridge between humanity and planet Earth, towards the future.
I like to compare what we do to our bodies with what we do to Mother Earth's body.
You know about the basic food groups for a healthy life.
And you also know the concept of "junk food", "fast food."
Unfortunately, in the pictures you can see the four basic food groups for many Cubans.
And here are the consequences: cancer is the leading cause of death for Cuban people.
And when we walk on the country's streets, we witness the growing obesity epidemic that is associated with diabetes, hypertension, ischemic heart disease and many other things that probably many people sitting in the audience are also suffering from.
This is not a normal situation.
I make the comparison between junk food and what we feed Mother Earth with.
Look at those chimneys that are polluting our air, our earth, our water, our food, and is exactly that, and we're also doing it in Cuba, what causes that when we look at the horizon in the morning, we see a gray layer of smoke that covers the city and we're breathing it every day.
What you're seeing right now is not a factory's chimney, is not a thermal power station's chimney, nor a refinery's chimney;
is a hospital's chimney, and now, let me tell you the concept that a traditional hospital, worldwide not just in Cuba, heals and kills at the same time.
A hospital is a huge contributor to the planet's pollution.
Is a huge user of fossil fuels' powered
energy, is a huge user of products that after being used, are thrown away, not recycled, contributing to Earth's pollution.
That results in cancer and many other things.
Here's the picture of a whole lung, the left lung of a patient I had to operate due to lung cancer.
A lot of times we blame the smoker for his own cancer: "You got cancer and is your fault, who told you to smoke?"
But each day, we, the people that eat healthy, work out, are thin, anyway, have a healthy life style,
are more, but because of a sick planet, we're also getting cancer, including lung cancer.
Even worse, the childhood cancer's epidemic is increasing both in Cuba and worldwide.
These babies, still in their mothers' wombs, where they're supposed to be protected and warm, right?
isolated from trouble, are already receiving, "breathing" through the blood provided by the umbilical cord, the poisons the mother has received and is receiving during pregnancy.
They are not to blame; we are, the society.
There are still people that, for spurious, political, or economical reasons say that no, nothing is happening, we're not destroying Earth, and that global warming is false.
But, as you can see, there is a lot of evidence.
Nobody can argue this anymore as it's scientifically proven, and is time to act.
And here is what I want to propose to you: to build an ecological hospital, a sustainable hospital, or what I like to call, a green hospital.
It is a society and nature friendly hospital, that solves all of the previously explained issues.
It would be a hospital that we could see as a sustainable company, a socially responsible company, that, on top of healing people, it would respect all of the principles, also called the three pillars of sustainability, and those are the social, the economical, and of course, the ecological one.
As an example of what is wrong with buildings in general, and hospitals in particular, let's see some of the data of my own center.
The IPK, who is a world leader in tropical medicine and infectious diseases, consumes 20 ton of oil monthly.
Imagine to spill 20 tons of oil anywhere in the country.
And it consumes 10 megawatts of electricity daily.
It was built without eaves to block rain and sun light.
The huge surfaces of the roof are painted in a dark color, absorb heat from the sun and they transmit it to the rooms below them.
If I could cover these surfaces with solar panels, well, we could cut the thick electrical cable that feeds the center.
Plus, these are some of the materials I use daily in my operating room.
There are materials of high economical value: they're more expensive every day, and on top of that, they leave a huge ecological print.
A lot of them come from oil.
Imagine what will happen in 30 or 50 years when the oil world reserves run out and they don't have anything to make latex gloves, syringes, catheters, or serum bags with.
How are we going to practice medicine then?
As a doctor and a surgeon, it scares me to think of that future when I will have the know-how to do things
but not have the tools to do them. At the end of the day, these high economical value products aren't recycled: they are taken to a dumpster, or worse, they are taken to a furnace where we spend even more oil to burn them producing toxic smoke that is released to the atmosphere.
Meaning that it is a very problematic cycle.
I present to you my dream: to build a green hospital.
I want to cut the tumor that's destroying humanity with the scalpel of sustainable architecture.
This dream hasn't been built yet, but in my mind, it has been. Is a castle on the clouds, is the work of young architects with whom we're developing these ideas.
And I see it as a patient and and a planet friendly hospital.
The initial concept we have is that of a surgical clinic, because that's my job, conceptualized around four operating rooms, and everything else is according to these surgical units.
It could be built within the city, but in my dream I see it on the outskirts of it, by the sea or by a hill.
Its roofs and facades will be covered of solar panels, solar heaters, wind generators, in a way that would have 100 percent energy autonomy, while, at the same time, the internal energy consumption would be low due to the many technologies that we now have available.
This way, we achieve the philosophical, bio-ethical basic idea I've been talking to you about.
Plus, it would be a hospital that in its physical architecture, and its workers' spiritual architecture, it wouldn't scare the patients and their families.
It would be a joyful place to work in, and if joy is possible when you go in as a patient, we would work our hardest to achieve that goal.
The time has come to achieve this,
Cuba is an ideal place to develop this type of project.
The green hospital is a win-win situation, is a social, economic, ecologic, political win. It would be an additional way of establishing: is a revolution and an evolution that establish even further Cuba's well deserved fame in the field of medicine.
This way, we could hope that by building green hospitals and, also solving all the issues that the planet has, in 100 years from now these images were yesterday's nightmares, yesterday's bad memories.
And I hope that in 100 years from now my job could cease to exist.
The evidence is enough: is time to stop talking.
Join me to build the green hospital.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
There were people running, screaming, fire, spears.
The city was being invaded.
But it seemed like he didn'’t care.
He was still sitting, drawing mathematical shapes in the sand.
Until a Roman soldier threatened him with his sword and wanted to capture him.
The old man, calmly, raised his eyes, looked at him, and told him that firstly he would finish his demonstration.
Then he looked down and kept drawing in the sand.
When Romans invaded Sicily, Syracuse seemed an easy target.
However, it took them more than two years to conquer it.
It was the first battle in which technology made a difference. Catapults and big cranes were used to spin ships up in the air, and many other innovations.
Plutarch says that Romans were so scared that they saw a rope or a pulley and started running.
All those inventions were the work of only one man.
Who was that giant who, with his inventions, made the Roman army tremble?
No less than Archimedes, the most important mathematician of Antiquity.
Finally, the Romans conquered the city.
The soldier was obviously upset with Archimedes' attitude, and killed him with his sword.
A terrible mistake for which he paid a very high price, because his General, Marcus Claudius Marcellus, deeply admired Archimedes.
In fact, Marcus Claudius, while he could have taken all the treasures of the city, he chose one that fitted in any bag.
A device made by Archimedes.
Cicero says it was a kind of portable planetary, that showed the position of the Sun, the Moon, the stars; and when he made it work, it predicted eclipses.
How could this portable planetary be?
Cicero says he never thought that a human being could be such a genius to do something like this.
The Roman general chose it before any other treasure.
How could it have been?
Can you imagine what was it like to have it in your hands?
Having the chance to study it, investigate it, touch it?
However, as many ancient marvels, it was lost forever.
Or at least it seemed so, because in 1900 a sunk ship was found at the bottom of the Mediterranean Sea, close to Antikythera’s island full of treasures from the Greeks'’ time: statues, jewelry, coins and something quite odd.
This: a bunch of spare parts of what looked like an ancient clock full of gears.
This in itself was quite revolutionary because devices like this appear in the late Middle Ages, 1500 years later.
Could they be the remains of Archimedes’' device?
To answer this question we would have to first rebuild the original device from the parts and make it work again.
A Quixotic challenge that is taking more than a century.
In the 50'’s, an American named Solla Price worked on it; And then two British men: Michael Wright in the 90's, and Tony Freeth and his team in 2000.
Freeth’'s team made extremely high resolution CT scans that let us see the interior of the fragments.
See for yourselves (Tomography video) we start from the outside and little by little we enter the fragment.
This is really important because in the Museum we are not allowed to break the device to see what is inside.
Look at those teeth, look how they are conserved after 2000 years underwater.
Now we are advertising the first submersible tablets.
This is submersible! 2000 years underwater!
Look at it: it’'s crazy.
According to what we could rebuild so far, the mechanism was protected by a wooden box and had a cover at the front and a cover at the back.
At the front, it had two concentric scales: one was divided by the twelve zodiac signs and the other one by the 365 days of the year.
Over these scales, a needle spins and shows at the same time the position of the Sun in the sky and the day of the year.
But over those scales there is also another needle that shows the position of the Moon in the sky.
The mistake of that needle, according to current values, Is 0.0002 days per turn.
But that’'s not all.
The Moon doesn'’t move at a constant speed. Sometimes it goes faster and sometimes slower.
These guys could keep the Moon needle from going at a constant speed, so that it speeds up when the Moon goes faster and slows down when it goes slower.
And as if it wasn'’t enough, the Moon’'s needle had a little ball painted half black and half white, that spun showing the phases of the Moon.
(Video) If you ask me, only Archimedes could have done something like this.
At the back we have two big spiral clocks.
The first one is an eclipse predictor.
Do you remember that Cicero said Archimedes'’ clock predicted eclipses?
Eclipses repeat themselves every 223 months.
For each one there is a cell, most of them are empty. But when there'’s an eclipse, the cell informs if it is solar or lunar, if it would be observable and even when it would happen.
The one above has two little clocks inside.
One of them makes a complete turn every four years and showed the user when the Olympics would take place.
(Laughter) The big spiral is a lunisolar calendar. that divides 235 months in 19 years.
It'’s an extremely accurate calendar.
We have to add a day every four years to our calendar on the leap years, so it doesn'’t disarrange.
To this calendar, we have to remove a day every 76 years.
Of course, we can say: Who is going to remember to remove a year every 7 decades?”
To do that, the mechanism had another little clock that took one turn every 76 years to remind the user that he had to skip a day.
The calendar has the names of the months and that was very important because every Greek city had its own calendar with its own names.
And we could determine that the calendar of the mechanism matches with the Corinth calendar or that of its colonies.
Do you know which city was a colony of Corinth for a long time?
Syracuse, Archimedes'’ city.
Is this then Archimedes’ device?
Truth is Archimedes’' and Antikythera’s' device cast the same type of spell on those who get in touch with them.
We know that the Roman General was haunted when he saw it and he chose it over any treasure.
Something similar to what happens to Gollum in “The Lord of the Rings” with Frodo'’s ring.
Cicero was also amazed when he saw it work and became Archimedes'’ fan.
Archimedes'’ mechanism had that kind of charm.
But a very similar one has Antikythera’s'.
Solla Price, once he saw the fragments, worked with them until he died.
Michael Wright was almost fired for investigating this device.
Tony Freeth mortgaged his economic future for the investigation.
I myself have to confess that I fell under its spell.
And I’'ve been working on it for 5 years nonstop.
I remember the first time I saw the fragments at the museum in Athens’ I was hypnotized, I couldn’'t stop looking at them.
I must have spent several hours watching the fragments from every possible angle.
Until the museum'’s security got nervous thinking I wanted to steal it.
And actually, I wanted to!
But it'’s not just its spell what makes us suspect it’'s the same device.
There are several signs pointing at Archimedes: it could be Syracuse'’s calendar, Cicero’s description matches a lot with what we could rebuild, the eclipses’ predictor also leads to Archimedes.
However, there’'s still a very important proof missing.
Without that, we have nothing.
It'’s the mechanism's construction date.
To give it a date, we tried to study the shape of letters, of the script, but the margin of error is too big, more than a hundred years.
Carbon-14 only works on organic material and the gears are made of bronze.
But there’'s another chance.
In the eclipse predictor we have the hours and the days in which eclipses will happen.
If we found the series of eclipses that better fit these data, we would know when it was built.
This proposal is valid in theory but never worked in practice because there is a lot of data of the eclipse predictor that we don'’t come to understand well.
Something very annoying is that while it predicts every lunar eclipse, it only predicts some of the solar eclipses.
For some reason, the person who made the device decided to skip some solar eclipses.
Those forgotten eclipses don'’t seem to follow any logic, they seem randomly skipped.
They are those red eclipses there.
One evening after working with the CT scans, I simply thought about relocating the eclipses from the spiral below to the spiral above to see how they would fit.
I didn'’t have any reason to do it, I just did it.
And look what happened. It seemed magical.
Suddenly all the eclipses organized in forming rays, and what’'s most important, those skipped eclipses, formed two very defined rays.
I have found the logic of this eclipse skipping.
I communicated this to my research partner, James Evans and he immediately understood what it was about.
That gave us a lot of information about the eclipse predictor.
Maybe now we could find the date of construction.
We started working immediately.
It was years and years of burning our heads.
Of following dead-end strategies.
Of getting excited with a result to later realize that we had made some silly mistake with calculations.
Until one Sunday of February, last year, I was at my colleague’s house in the US and we realized that there was no way to move forward.
That even using all the data we had, we had too many possible dates.
I remember that day, we said goodbye with the bitter feeling that the project that was taking more than three years had failed.
I couldn'’t believe it.
I kept myself together in front of him but when I went down the stairs -- I was sleeping in his basement, which was full of books on the history of Astronomy, it'’s spectacular -- when I went downstairs and I sat on the bed, I couldn't believe it.
I couldn'’t mourn.
So many years working and we didn’'t find anything.
I started to revise, to make the calculations again, I reviewed everything.
Until suddenly I thought about considering data about the lunar anomaly we hadn'’t consider.
And that data was extremely powerful.
I did the calculation and it eliminated all but one candidate.
That night we found the result.
And we were about to quit!
And we were very close to the solution.
Today, after one year and a half of reviewing carefully every calculation and checking all data, I can officially announce that the first eclipse of the device happened on June 25th, 205 B.C,
at 15:35, Athens local time.
(Applause) That means that the calendar could have started in September or August of 216 B.C., when Archimedes was still -- our dearest Archimedes -- alive and kicking.
Yes, ladies and gentlemen, this mechanism might have been made by Archimedes himself.
I imagine you will arrive home today and you will start looking up information on the Internet, if you aren'’t doing it already.
You can do it, there is a lot, but if you suddenly realize that you'’ve spent the last 10 hours in front of the computer, that you forgot to have dinner or lunch, or that you skipped work, be careful.
Be careful.
Maybe it'’s too late and you have fallen, like many of us, under its spell.
(Applause)
What is the best job in the world?
Well, this is the question that we all or at least most of us ask ourselves at some point in our lives.
All of us have a purpose, a mission, a contribution that makes the society.
Something that makes us happy.
Having an occupation, activity or professional career, normally allows us to express who we are.
What we love and that's why we do it.
I'm sure that we are born knowing what we came here to do.
But throughout our lives emerge some factors that gradually block our anxieties, our tastes, our desires, our dreams.
That's why those experts advise that we should recall what we used to do in our childhood, to be able to decipher what is our mission in this life.
How does one realize that his work is the most enjoyable one?
How to discover my mission in this life?
Well, I am not experienced in the matter, but today I'm here to share how I figured it out.
My name is Erika Fierro, I'm a teacher, my ancestry is Tijuana.
I come from a privileged family.
I'm the second of four wonderful siblings, and the life provided me with my model parents.
Hardworking, generous and loving others with principles.
At an early age, or at 13 to be exact I met the love of my life.
My first and last boyfriend, now my husband and the father of my two children, my best friend.
When it comes to decide what career to pursue, I sank into an internal conflict
as one part of me is always passionate for cooking, and the other part for the children.
So,I was in hesitation of whether pursue a career as a chef or as a teacher.
But as is common for those indecisive couples, when it's time to make the decision, my boyfriend was fundamental in determining my fate
As at that time in this city, a chef was not an option for career as now.
And it meant leaving the city.
That was something I wasn't ready to do because I didn't want to be away from him,
nor did I wanna leave my parents.
I've always been a family-oriented homebody, I call myself "a blue lady"
And that is when I decided to become a teacher.
I worked for education several years in a school to which I'm really grateful.
And after a time,arose an opportunity to open my own business
Owing to the unconditional support from my parents.
A project in collaboration with my sister,my lifelong companion
And in the course of searching for doing something I truly enjoyed, I discovered a new educational approach.
So new and different, that once we opened the school, no one believed in us, or the project, or in what we could provide for their children.
Only three families put faith in us.
Thank you.
And we worked hard for two years, but things were not that looking good.
Not only it was a bad deal, but also I had to invest money in it so that the work team could be saved and offered a qualified education.
But our economic resources had been run out.
Then we were almost about to give up and close the school.
At this point, I felt it necessary to do some self-reflection.
And I realized that something inside was falling through.
Although the product we offered was great, that school, lack of a key condiment: the gratitude.
For this I began to feel and be gratitude for everything I had, for how lucky I had always been, for those people believing in the project.
At that moment I started to find those positive changes in my life.
And as if by magic, everything was going smoothly.
People, families and students set out coming in.
And the school began to grow.
Even though everything was going marvelously, I felt the need to do something more.
It was imperfect.
So I started looking for answers, and guides,through various sources.
I took refuge in meditation, I started to read a bit more than I was used to. To find answers, I got into painting classes, which gave me the time to paint.
I love art.
I felt the need to connect with the nature and I started practicing gardening.
I also felt the need to enjoy and value my family, so I started inviting them to the meetings, cooking for them,
I love cooking.
And then I look for the moments that I could savor.
But above all, I became more aware of our having an impact on the planet.
There were so many feelings coming to me
Those helped me to live, and experience new things.
And it greatly helped me develop new skills.
Today I can say I've managed to integrate everything, or nearly everything that I am enthusiastic about in my job.
I feel a great responsibility to promote a sustainable lifestyle for children and raise awareness of the impact of our actions.
I love the idea of boosting all the artistic skills of the children.
The plastic arts, urban dance... Excuse me, the slides moved.
The piano.
I am convinced that it could maximize children's artistic abiilities in this case,significantly grows the ability of cognizance, the intellectual faculty, abilities to argue to think, to analyze, and to create.
That results chidren in the school getting better learning
I understood that living with spirit is essential to live gratefully.
And I feel a commitment to guide our children the way.
We do this throughout the daily practice of meditation, focusing on full consciousness or mindfulness.
Finding my life mission was a process.
A process that definitely required an exercise of self-development.
An exercise that helped me discover the best of me and that helped me find the perfect place where I could fit in according to my capacity.
Transforming into the best job in the world for me.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
This man sounds familiar to me. It's not that I don't know who he is: this man is my father, Primitivo Langarica.
If I tell you he sounds familiar, it's because despite the thousands of lights and bulbs his lamp shop in Victoria sold, for me, more than a man of light, he was a man of sound. Because my father spent half his life chasing a song.
(Music) (Music ends) Every weekend, for more than 30 years, my father went out searching for 'Palomas' ['Doves'].
Other fathers went out hunting or looking for mushrooms; mine went looking for 'Palomas'.
He found them spinning around in CD shops, in the Rastro market in Madrid, in Bayona.
And one by one, he managed to gather together more than 1,000 versions of this song.
There you have it, years and years dedicated to a collection that started as an argument in a bar, about which song has the most cover versions in history.
An argument that today we would resolve in a minute with a smartphone, consulting Mr Google.
In fact, the internet had a lot to do with my father's fortunes as a collector, because in the eighties, with a damaged modem, one of those that sounded like an old telephone, he was stunned to discover that many more 'palomistas' like him existed in the world, who collected versions of the song and studied its history.
And he was not so much stunned as stupefied when he discovered that only a musicologist from Germany had more versions of 'Paloma' than he did.
Boom! From that moment on, my father dedicated himself to showing that 'Paloma' is ours, that it's from here, from Álava, from Lanciego, from Victoria, that it belongs to us.
He grumbled every time he found the song in an anthology of Argentine tangos, or in a collection of Russian songs.
When a presenter referred to it as the Cuban song par excellence, he grumbled.
Here today, in memory of him and his legacy, I'd like to share with you the story of the song that is the most cosmopolitan and widely travelled you can imagine.
Let's begin.
The first palomista in history, Don Sebastián de Iradier y Salaverri, a Basque musician, composed this melody in 1860. More than a great symphony, it was a melody.
Today we'd call it an international hit, but it, in the middle of the 19th century, after a few months, was to be heard all over the south of Europe, not least because Señor Iradier y Salaverri was music teacher to Empress Eugenia de Montijo, the Empress of France.
It's success was overwhelming, and from the start, the first cover versions appeared, with thousands and thousands to follow.
To give you an idea, in 1865, five years after its composition, there were already 'Palomas' listed in Italian and French.
And thus the dove came to nest in the south of Europe, in Greece, in Italy, in France and in Spain,
the 'La Paloma' that we all know, that lyrical and romantic 'Paloma'.
To give you an idea of this pan-southern European dimension of 'La Paloma', we're going to hear a short excerpt from a version recorded in Greek, at La Scala, the opera house, in Milan, by the singing teacher of María Sofía Kalogeropoúlos - the latter you might know better as María Callas - a performance that would forever unite our 'Paloma' with her native Greece.
(Song: 'La Paloma', a Greek version) (Song ends) Vinyl grooves going round for the sound there. But 'La Paloma' doesn't content herself with success in southern Europe and decides to migrate.
To follow her on her first journey, we need a story, a story about princes and princesses.
(Video) She: ... not beyond my faith in your capacity to be great. You'll be the enlightened ruler of a new era in monarchy. ('La Paloma' heard in background) Listen. Listen, Max. He: Beautiful. She: 'La Paloma', the song of the dove. It's the loveliest melody I know of. He: I wonder who's singing. She: Who knows? Wife of some gardener ... sweetheart of some sort. He: Mexico. (Video ends) 'To Mexico', he says to her.
You will recognise, at least those of you with grey hair,
a very young Bette Davis playing our fairy-tale princess, Charlotte of Belgium, strolling with her lover, Maximilian of Habsburg, the archduke of Austria.
They meet in Vienna, at a ball, with the song of the moment, and decide that it will be their song for ever. And we heard him say to her at the end, 'We're going to Mexico', because they have been proclaimed emperor and empress of the fledgling Mexican Empire.
And that's how our 'Paloma' crosses the pond for the first time.
Taken to Mexico by the emperor and empress, she, of course, triumphs again, among the upper classes, at the palace balls.
But sometimes history turns stories into nightmares. And five years after having arrived in Veracruz, Maximilian will ask to hear the song for one last time before being assassinated by Juarista revolutionary troops.
This is the first, but not the last, war that will liberate our song.
While the imperial troops make of 'La Paloma' a musical standard, and play it before entering battle, among the working classes of Mexico, satirical compositions start to appear that ridicule the emperor using his own melody, and, of course, extol the figure of the revolutionary heroes.
And so in Mexico, another 'Paloma' appears, different from the one we know here, which, for them, is a purely Mexican 'Paloma'.
It's a rebellious and revolutionary 'Paloma', which from then right up to today, they consider to be a patriotic song that talks about class warfare.
Dozens of versions like the one we're about to hear. (Song: Revolutionary Mexican version of 'La Paloma') Fly dove, to the borders Take a star from their flag For we don't want imperialisms We are sick to the back teeth with their cynicism For the air above us is sovereign We are guerrillas and Mexicans (Song ends) 'Pure' Mexican-Americans make this their song, a song which can be heard in Chiapas praising their Subcomandante Marcos.
Or right now, it's connected to the problems that exist on the border with the people who aren't being allowed to enter the giant to the north.
But if there's any country that has made 'La Paloma' their own, it's without doubt Germany.
In Germany, 'La Paloma' has taken root, or maybe 'root' is not the right word to define what the German people feel for 'La Paloma'. Because for them, it will always be that song that appeared in their northern ports, on a boat and with the sound of an accordion.
It's in Germany where our 'Paloma' has had the best time and put down the most roots, and has had most chicks, 'Palomitas'.
Nowadays, we can find hundreds of versions of 'La Paloma', from dodecaphonic operas based on the song, each section speaking about a part of the dove, from when it's born until it dies, to Heavy Metal versions, by groups with unpronounceable names, which you'll allow me not to share here, but take it from me that they exist.
To give you an idea of this love affair between the German people and our 'Paloma', in May 2004, the local government in Hamburg requested its inclusion in the Guinness Book of Records under 'biggest choir in history' after 88,600 people gathered together to sing in unison a single song: 'Die Taube', 'La Paloma'.
(Song: German version of 'La Paloma') To the sailor, ooh-ee On the rolling sea Dark thoughts waver and flee As fast as storm and wind Speaker: Now you see, a 'Paloma' that, for us, is on Mount Gorbea, at the highest point. (Song ends) look where she's reached.L
And if 'La Paloma' accompanied the German people all their lives, she accompanied another race to their deaths.
It has been documented by hundreds of survivors that 'La Paloma' could be heard as endless swathes of Jews were on their way to the gas chambers of Auschwitz and Treblinka.
But why did the German Nazis use their sailor's song in killing people, as a death march?
It doesn't make sense. But we can see why if we look at a map of the time, and we remember the love story.
The Nazi extermination camps were not to be found in Germany; they were all in Poland. And Poland formed part of the Austro-Hungarian Empire, that of Maximilian,
and in Austria, Hungary and Poland, 'La Paloma' was and is a funeral march,
a march that those thousands of soldiers brought from Mexico, wounded and defeated, and with which they buried their dead, and also bid farewell to their assassinated emperor.
But 'La Paloma' was not only present during the war in the extermination camps;
she was also present on the battlefields.
Paradoxically, 'La Paloma' was to be heard on both fronts in World War II, in French, Italian, English and German.
But sometimes, even on opposite fronts at the same time.
Wonder who sang her loudest during battle pauses.
Just things about 'La Paloma'.
Anyway, I would like to travel with you now, to a country that made her its own to the rhythm of the guitar.
And it's not ours, despite the marvellous arrangement for classical guitar by Francisco Tarrega that introduced her to chamber music, or the most wonderful arrangement by the great Paco de Lucía that brought her to flamenco.
In the country I'm talking about, our 'La Paloma' sounds like this.
(Music) Aloha, Hawaii, flower garlands, and 'La Paloma'.
How's that possible?
Let's see.
Let's travel to Hawaii ... to meet ... (Music ends) an emperor they had there with a name out of Pokémon, Kamehameha III. (Laughter)
It's true, Google it.
Kamehameha III had the brilliant idea of importing tens of thousands of heads of cattle. But they ended up just grazing away on the slopes of Hawaii, since no Hawaiian knew how to manage them; they only knew how to fish.
To put this right, Kamekameha - fish on a stick, Google it - writes to the Mexican government and asks for a few hundred experienced cowboys to 'take the bull by the horns'.
And what did those cowboys bring on their one-way trip to Hawaii?
They brought their saddles,
they brought their guitars, and they brought their 'Paloma', a 'Paloma' that the Hawaiians immediately adapted to their local rhythmic cadences: they laid the guitar flat and made her the most famous melody of their slide guitars, those lap guitars
sometimes played with a metal slide rather than pressing fingers to the fret.
And that's how, as a song, absolutely Hawaiian, a melody with no lyrics, that's anonymous, that inspires the people of Hawaii, another king who visited those islands 80 years later would get to know her - the king of rock and roll.
(Song: 'No More' by Elvis Presley) (Song ends) Everything but the swimwear is marvellous in that one.
(Laughter) Well, that's how Elvis found her. He went to Hawaii to film one of his first iconic films, 'Blue Hawaii', the first of three he filmed on the islands, and there he finds this melody - since it is anonymous and has no lyrics, the title and lyrics need to be invented - and he uses this melody to create one of his most personal compositions, his very famous 'No More', which he recorded on four separate occasions throughout his musical career.
Other English-speaking artists, of the likes of Chubby Checker, Chuck Berry, Charlie Parker or Artie Shaw, have managed to put 'La Paloma' in English-language hit charts at various times as Jazz, Swing, or Rhythm and Blues.
Now you see that there are 'Palomas' and 'Palomas', that there are versions of versions, that there are very famous 'Palomas' and 'Palomas' no one knows,
that you can hear it in an opera house, that it's to be found on a movie soundtrack,
in TV ads, or if you're walking down the street and there's a musician playing an accordion.
There are 'Palomas' written in dozens of languages, telling different stories under different titles, and which, depending on where on the map we're to be found in the world, will have meanings that are even contradictory: revolutionary, romantic, retro, funereal.
If I had more time, if they weren't already making signs at me, I would tell you why in Cuba, it's a Cuban song, why they play it in Russia with an instrument called the domra - the melody being the exam piece for that instrument, like a balalaika but more cultured, and has one more string -
or why, on the island of Zanzibar, they've made the song their own and use it at weddings.
My father spent all of his life defending the idea that 'La Paloma' was from here, that she was ours, that she belonged to us, defending her origin, and defending her authorship.
Well, I share the conviction that 'La Paloma' originates from us.
But she isn't ours, because no matter if we are from Victoria or the Basque region, or Spain, or Álava,
and we make the song our own every time we sing it
at family meals and when we dine to the rhythm of the habanera,
if other countries, other people, other cultures, want to sing it, well, we should be proud, we should be glad. And we should remember that a dove is, by definition, a creature that travels, and since time immemorial, we have used them to send messages from one place to another.
And the message that I would tie to the leg of this dove would be a simple phrase written on a musical score that would say the same thing its author did more than 150 years ago: 'If a dove arrives at your window, treat her with the loving affection that is me.'
Thank you.
(Applause)
Who of us doesn't wake up in the morning thinking about what to wear based on a weather forecast?
Who doesn't dream of having accurate information about what will happen within hours or days?
Or even further, what will happen the next summer when planning a good vacation.
Undoubtedly, weather events pierce our lives through in multiple ways.
Since childhood I was passionate about the clouds and especially those which lead to lightning, heavy rain, hail.
During summers, in my grandparents' house in San Nicolas, we often suffered the brunt of the weather.
I remember one particular day in which the conversation on meteorology was scaling in the family unexpectedly.
New Year's Day: Where do we lunch?
Do we take the table out to the backyard, do we eat outside or inside?
You can't imagine the discussions between my mother and my aunt!
They both looked at the forecast, but they also consult other sources of information.
There my mother was the more resourceful: she'd go to the garden, watch the clouds and apply her own forecasting tools.
"If the sound of the train is heard, it won't rain tomorrow".
It never ceased to amaze me how the topic on taking the table out or not could give so much.
But eventually I realized that the problem of predicting the rain was much more complex than what I originally I imagined at that time.
So I investigated it.
With my team from UBA and CONICET I focused my studies in eastern Argentina, looking at the map, from the center to the right.
Not only because there it is San Nicolas, my hometown, but also because there occur the most intense storms.
It's where most of our population lives,
and very sensitive activities to rain take place, such as agriculture or hydroelectric power generation.
But to forecast the rain better, we first needed to understand more the physical processes that explain it.
I'll share with you then, the results of two studies we performed with that purpose.
One of them aimed to understand where the water that rains in eastern Argentina comes from.
Although it may seem incredible, 15 years ago it was not clearly understood where the water of our rains came from.
That is, in a storm in which 100 millimeters fell, our studies showed that only 20 millimeters came from water previously evaporated there.
Necessarily, moist air was being transported from elsewhere.
We had a good idea on how the air flows in South America: air masses, laden with moisture, come from the Atlantic through the north coast, they get wet further in the Amazon rainforest and then they go South because of the presence of the Andes.
In Argentina -- as shown in this animation of the cloudiness on any day -- the airflow moves away from the mountains and slows down, leading to the formation of beautiful storms like those we are seeing there.
But it was a mystery to us what happened with the airflow along the Andes, specifically in the area that goes from Brazil, Peru, Bolivia and western Paraguay.
We needed to know it in order to predict well how much water vapor is transported from the North to Argentina.
But we couldn't pinpoint it simply because in that area the necessary atmospheric observations didn't exist.
We assumed that the airflow should accelerate in this zone.
As shown in this animation we did with a mathematical model, a jet stream of moist air should form, or as they say in Brazil, "a flying river".
We estimated that this jet stream, had to be at a height about 4,900 ft above Bolivia.
But to confirm these theoretical speculations, there was no option but to perform an experimental measurement campaign.
The problem was that the area to measure was so great that exceeded a single country.
Then, we performed a project with researchers from all the countries involved and also from the US,
to seek out and measure the jet stream.
This initiative ended up being the biggest atmospheric observation experiment ever made in South America so far.
As shown by this info graphic published by a newspaper at the time, the star of the experiment was a hurricane hunter aircraft.
Yes, one of the few hurricane hunter aircrafts in the world came to Bolivia to cross horizontally and vertically the jet stream, and it also flew over Argentina hunting storms.
All community committed to this experiment.
We installed multiple sites to launch weather balloons operated by researchers and students, like this group in Peru.
And we also installed hundreds of rain gauges which the farmers, the Wichi communities and the police measured the rain with.
All proud to participate in an initiative like this.
The experiment was a success because it allowed us to confirm for the first time the existence of the jet stream.
In addition, we were able to confirm that 80 percent of the rainfall in eastern Argentina comes from moist air that is transported from the Amazonas and Atlantic.
Now, there was another problem which we knew very little about: the second study that I mentioned.
Why are there summers in which the jet stream is very intense and persistent and results in excessive rains in Argentina?
Or why are there hot, dry summers where no jet stream is detected, with little or no rains?
I think having being raised in an area so affected by both flood and drought, led me to study this.
I have memories from an early age of flooded fields that you could see the water get lost in the horizon; or conversely, those dry and hot summers when my mom used to say: "How much good water would do to the fields!"
I must say she said this with great social awareness, because my family doesn't have one square meter of cultivated land.
But to predict changes in the rain within months in advance, we had to overcome the chaotic nature of the atmosphere.
The well-known and famous theory of chaos, that about the butterfly effect, was raised by a meteorologist, Edward Lorenz, who showed that it isn't possible to make a forecast beyond a few days, due to atmospheric chaos.
But Lorenz also showed that the atmosphere is capable of responding to external influences.
For example, changes in tropical oceans can alter the atmosphere for months or even years.
So this opened the possibility to extend our forecasts.
But the influence of the ocean is not the same everywhere.
So, would it be possible that the variations in the rain in Argentina every year were due, at least in part, to oceanic influence?
Our research confirmed that this was the case.
But what was surprising was that the biggest influence is not the nearest ocean, the South Atlantic, but it is generated by the Indian and Pacific oceans, located thousands of miles away.
In these oceans there is a very large area, where the water temperature is above 82 degrees Fahrenheit.
It is this red area of the image.
So small variations of temperature in this vast pool of hot water act as a burner for the atmosphere. They not only alter the weather of this particular region, but they also alter the high and low pressure centers moving in the Southern Hemisphere, which in turn modify the jet stream and the rain in Argentina.
We are currently working to apply this tremendous knowledge to improve the mathematical models we use to make forecasts.
But it is still a huge challenge to represent the complexity of atmosphere and the ocean, as well as other factors influencing the weather: vegetation, icecaps, urban areas.
All that, put on a computer.
It took us 15 years since we began to think about the jet stream and the aircraft experiment until we made improvements in the forecast with this information.
In the US it took 20 years
to extend the nowcasting of severe storms and tornadoes from five to 16 minutes.
16 minutes is the maximum advance in the world with which can be predicted a tornado or a hailstorm, for example.
In turn, the research in Argentina is three years away of becoming the second country in America to bring this forecast to 16 minutes.
Yes, I know that 16 minutes seems little, or nothing.
But if we think in the so serious and negative impacts of these events, you can imagine what would be to have 20 minutes in advance to take action to protect people, in case of a tornado or a flash flood, for example.
This enormous scientific problem is then hidden, behind the daily forecast.
It is unrealistic to expect the forecast to be ever perfect, but we can expect it to improve.
Thanks to science and technology, and especially thanks to a critical mass of researchers that persists in addressing this problem.
In the near future not only we will have better tools to forecast rainfall the next day, but we will also be able to anticipate what will happen in the coming weeks and months.
Now, every time we check the forecast in the morning, let's realize that after hundreds years of research we managed to get only some lead time to the powerful forces of nature.
Let's use it the best we can, then. Like my mom did successfully many times, in order to take the table out for New Year's lunch.
Thank You.
(Applause)
