Anyway, a fail is OK, because for those searching on YouTube "fail videos" at 4:00 a.m. like we all do, (Laughter) well, like some of us do -- this video will show up there on the right side, and there are more chances that people view this talk.
The algorithm will work in our favor on YouTube.
Do we all know what the algorithm is?
We know it exists, but what is an algorithm, specifically?
I did a little research and here I put it into words we can all understand.
An algorithm is a preset of instructions or rules -- well-defined, ordered and finite -- that allows for an activity to be carried out in successive steps which don't generate doubts to whoever performs it.
Applied to networks, the algorithm has the order to record the contents we consume to offer something similar later.
The algorithm knows what we like and it offers us things that we like.
Quite a stalker!
The algorithm knows all that we want and it offers us the things we want.
So, applied to networks this is the way it works.
And we see it clearly on music platforms.
We know, for example, that because you listened to R.E.M.,
we recommend you Radiohead.
Then, because you listened to Radiohead we recommend you Adele.
And because you listened to Adele we recommend you ... James Blunt.
And because you listen to James Blunt we recommend you ... antidepressants.
(Laughter) You don't get off James Blunt without medication, mind you.
(Laughter) If you are at a James Blunt phase in life, call us, we know what to do. We've been there.
Put it in plain words, the algorithm is like a completely sadistic waiter.
One day you go to the restaurant and the waiter algorithm assists you. You say: "I would like to eat cow kidneys in thin slices, golden brown."
And it already records: Client likes cow meat, offal, golden brown, and brings them to you.
Next day you ask for pork flank steak, can it be golden brown, too?
And it records that.
Client eats other non-cow things, golden brown as well. And you go a third day, and it says: "I have something to recommend you."
Already the algorithm learned something about us.
"I have boneless chicken golden brown, you will love it."
You say: "Super, OK, I love it, great."
And you go another day, the algorithm is on fire and says: "Don't ask me anything, I know what to offer you.
Here it is: monkey prostate, golden brown." And you say, there is something in your recommendation that you don't finish to understand.
You see, the algorithm sometimes fails, sometimes it messes up.
Also, another sadistic aspect of it is that it doesn't stop.
I bought an air conditioner one month ago.
Today I still get deals on air conditioners.
On the mail, on Mercado Libre, on Facebook, on Instagram, on YouPorn, everywhere ... (Laughter) Air conditioner deals. (Applause) Then, how do we escape the algorithm?
How do we make people find out that the algorithm offers us only things that we like?
And there is a lot of contents to discover, and that's what TEDxRíodelaPlata is about.
To discover a lot of things that we didn't know about.
Here we are 3000 people, plus those watching on streaming, and those who will search for this talk, it's a lot of people.
But nobody will watch this talk about the algorithm unless they search for a talk about the problem of the algorithm.
