This is an easy question and I want you all to answer it with me, it's easy, don't think too much, did dinosaurs go extinct or not?
Audience: Yes! No!
Yes. Not all of them, maybe the

paleontologists over there got it right.
There's a group of dinosaurs called theropod dinosaurs, these dinosaurs that walk on two legs, that have three fingers, that eat meat, the bad guys in movies like the -- And a much bigger one?
Audience: Tyrannosaurus rex.
Tyrannosaurus rex.

I don't know. I don't know.
One group of these dinosaurs obviously smaller evolved to be all of the birds we know as of today, absolutely all of them.
However, there's a big difference between a theropod dinosaur and a bird, which is the fleshy tail.
This fleshy tail reduced its size throughout evolution, so the center of mass stayed in this point, if I hang a body, the body stays in balance, it's the center of mass.
This center of mass became moving forward, from the hip to the knee, what for?
To control flight better, but also, this change of mass center made these animals change their way of walking.
Wow! How, you're wondering.
Your faces have a "how" look,
especially you guys over there.
We have an ostrich as a bird, as a chicken, and if we see it walking, the first segment which is called the femur of the leg -- (Whistle) (Laughter) The first segment, the femur, virtually doesn't move during locomotion, because the center of mass is more towards the knee, because it allows them to control flight better.
You're following, right?
Audience: Yes.
But how do we think dinosaurs moved if they had their center of mass behind because of the presence of this fleshy tail?
According to the 3D reconstructions, does the femur move or not?
Audience: Yes.
The femur moves.
So with some colleagues we started to think at the School of Sciences of Universidad de Chile, if it's that simple then we can take a chicken, put an artificial tail on it, we change it's mass center from front to back, will it walk like we think dinosaurs walk, or walked?
So then chickensaurs appeared.
(Laughter) Basically, here are the results published in an important journal and besides the graphic where you can see the walking of a normal group and of a group of chicken with tails, let's look at the first group for example, let's see the first segment (Whistles)
Come on! This first segment, doesn't move much, even if its speed-dependent, it doesn't move much.
What happens with a chicken with a tail?
It moves much more.
If we look at a full walking path of a chicken, I introduce to you a chicken walking, in case you had never seen a chicken walk before, and I introduce to you a "chickensaur".
(Laughter) It moves like a dinosaur.
We don't know how dinosaurs moved.
At least as in reconstructions, it moves as in reconstructions.
That made us win a prize, called an Ig Nobel, for research that first makes us laugh and then think.
Here's me receiving an Ig Nobel (Laughter) (Applause) with a plunger on my butt and walking like a dinosaur.
But after seeing so many videos of chicken walking, I went to the park, I seemed like an old man looking at doves walk, and asked myself another question I'm currently trying to solve at the School of Engineering of Universidad de Chile.
I, a biologist, went to the Engineering school to do my post doctorate because I wanted to solve other things that have to do with the locomotion of birds and their great-great-great-great sons... of their grandparents, theropod dinosaurs.
(Laughter) What are those questions?
What's the important question I want to solve?
What's up with the bobbing?
You've all seen the bobbing, this is the bobbing.
I introduce to you again a chicken walking, and a crane walking, they do this bobbing.
If this bobbing is not very clear,
I can represent it myself.
(Laughter) (Applause) Why do some birds bob?
According to what scientists say, it has to do with an optokinetic reason, that hens for example don't like the world moving around so they push their head quickly forward and then they keep it stationary
with reference to the world. And that's why if one takes a hen and, like, moves it, then the hen sort of -- (Laughter) I want to put a GoPro on a hen's head, put then hen above my head and ride downhill on a bike.
It's a perfect steady cam.
But I think that on top of optokinetic reason, I think his bobbing also gives locomotive efficiencies to a bird's walking, in other words, it makes it more efficient, reduces the cost of transport.
It can be one, it can be the other, or it can be both.
What's important is that if I get to effectively understand why birds do their bobbing, because they reduce their transportation cost then, why don't we imagine T-rexes doing the bobbing, if maybe they did?
How are today's reconstructions of T-rexes?
Do they bob or do they not?
Public: No.
Then, I'm innovating!
And when we see Steven Spielberg's movies, Jurassic Park 1, 2, 3, 4, The lost world, it doesn't bob so, maybe we should remasterize all the movies unless I get a paycheck now and I can just drop my investigation.
(Laughter) Spielberg, Spielberg, Spielberg, I'm talking to you.
How am I doing this?
I'm in the School of Engineering of Universidad de Chile doing comparative anatomy of different animals, of different theropods, how they relate to maximum speeds, which ones of them bob, which ones don't, then with an analysis of hens walking in different -- in running mills with different inclinations to start experimentally asking the hen, quite evidently, if it's doing the bobbing for visual reasons or if its doing it for energetic reasons or both.
Then by doing a 3D model of Tyrannosaurus rex -- I love saying it, Tyrannosaurus rex; What's your work about?
I work with Tyrannosaurus rex -- (Laughter) I'll make this 3D model walk and I'll measure its metabolism with bobbing and without it and I'll see the difference.
Then after this, and I'm working on this, I want to make a robot theropod which I called... "Theropobot".
It's too good, thanks, it deserves an applause.
Theropobot deserves an applause.
(Applause) Now, doing this Theropobot in Engineering, I realized engineering not only gives us biologists tools to test our biological hypothesis of basic sciences, but also biology gives engineering inspiration for new technologies.
This inspiration of new technologies inspired by nature are called biomimicry technologies, a new science that's starting out, and one of the classic, typical results of biomimicry is velcro -- My favorite onomatopoeia, that velcro is inspired in those seeds that get suck on clothes, they're called epizoochory; a spread of a seed; lot's of hooks, little hair, lot's of cohesion.
And then there's the cat's eyes on the roads, which reflect light inspired by... Audience: Cats.
Amazing.
And another thing we're developing specifically, with a group of students is, for example, an aircraft with three wings, this is between us, really, three wings inspired by the way big migrating birds migrate and thanks to the Bernoulli effect, these V formations are generated, have you seen them before?
Well, we're making a plane to replicate it.
These vessels with bubbles that reduce dragging inspired by the bubbles penguins make when they swim, we all watched Happy Feet.
(Laughter) And even a motorbike with an inertial appendix, a motorbike with a tail, inspired by the cheetah, so it has much better maneuverability.
Now, you might've found this interesting but what I find thrilling is how a kid, that still had hair on his head, who liked doing circuits, making robots, taking toys apart, wanted to be an engineer, but then saw Jurassic Park, his mind was blown, and said I want to study biology, I want to understand how animals move, he made an experiment with a chicken with a plunger, he understood how dinosaurs walked and now he's in Engineering making inventions again.
He came full circle, and here I am and I'm grateful to be able to be telling you all you can come full circle and I'll give you two pieces of advice in order to achieve it, and basically in this case it's a plea begging.
First, be skeptical, don't believe anyone, you have to think by yourselves, as much as anyone stands up here to tell you something, okay?
And second, please, be innovative.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
(Speaking in Wolof) Clearly, you couldn't understand a thing and you'd like to know what did I say.
What I've just done is what we do every day: translate.
Translate our Senegalese African culture into Spanish, European, Western culture.
It would be easier for me to speak in one of my native languages. But today I'm going to talk to you in Spanish, one of the languages I've adopted together with French and English.
What I said in Wolof is my introduction.
Hello, my name is Tida Coly, I'm from Senegal and I feel a citizen of the world.
I want to share with you what brought me here and a bit of my culture.
Usually, when people ask: "How long have you lived here?"
I've lived in Spain for 16 years.
The first thing they say, without even letting me explain what brought me here, which was their question, the first thing they say is: "You're so lucky of being here!
Life in Africa is so harsh!"
Usually, I remain thinking for a while and then answer: "But, what Africa are you talking about?"
What Africa are they talking about?
But, I always give an answer.
"Africa isn't a country, it's a continent, it has more than 50 countries, and Senegal, where I come from, is just one of them, with its own beauty. It is true that there is a, say, negative side but it's not what defines the continent."
Hence my job, what brought me here.
As you can see I'm not a great speaker, despite being the older of ten siblings, considering what that entails in my Senegalese culture, the decision making, setting the example for your siblings.
Sometimes it's a nightmare, but well, I do it with pride.
My point is, with this constant questions
I realized that something had to be done.
We had to be presented in a different way.
It was necessary that people knew where I come from without staring at me with compassion, because I didn't want to cause pity.
So, I decided to organize an event. It started one afternoon in my workplace. I used to work at a French bank in Madrid.
I decided to present an event, a detailed project to the Local Council of El Escorial, to ask for permission to use the public plaza and organize that event.
The answer was a pleasant surprise. Not only did they accept and gave me the consent but they were at my complete disposal, offering all the help they could, to make it come true.
That's how I organized the first event, which for me had a huge intercultural meaning.
I was able to gather, for that event, live African music, a Cameroonian storyteller ... literary talks with poets from different countries, a photographic exhibit of pictures of Africa by a Spanish photographer, and a fashion show.
I really enjoyed that last part, because ... we presented ... how can I say this? African fabrics on a small scale. Fabrics from Africa but with typical Spanish designs and cuts, so that people could see that African fabrics are not just for dark skins, which is something I hear, used to hear often, because times are changing.
"Hey, these fabrics suit you, black skinned people, but not us."
Luckily it is changing.
It was a success, but I ended up exhausted from running back and forth.
I realized I needed more training. So, I signed up to an organization and event managing workshop, which came in handy for subsequent editions.
By the end of the event, I realized that thanks to it something had changed.
People started to count on me, on us.
Those frequent questions ceased.
We started organizing events to have a follow up on the project.
I created a blog of what we do, with the help of my husband. And we started with the social media ... which we still have.
But in 2015 everything changed.
One afternoon at the office, I suddenly realized the time had come, not that I had ... induced it, no, it's just the circumstances of life, or even fate itself, as my mom would say. I quit the bank after eight years. I decided it was time to open a store.
Not a regular store, but a space, I like calling it a space where I could continue to share what I like the most, my culture, and also collaborate and change the idea people have of our continent.
I tend to say Africa doesn't rime with disaster, as they usually sell it.
For me, it rhymes with joy, well it doesn't but it should.
Bit by bit we are making it possible. With the help of a lot of collaborators that are counting on us, such as foundations, associations and private companies. In Africa we have people supporting us as well. Not only family, but also friends and people "related" to the area we are working on, which is culture and crafts, but always related to the continent.
What was I going to say?
My mind went blank.
It has gone blank. To me, this project is more than just a way of making a living.
It's related to freedom and to reaffirming who I am and what I can share with those around me and if possible going further;
if they let me.
When you enter this space, "Colores del Mundo," you realize the importance of fabrics in what we're doing.
That is why I visit Senegal occasionally.
In my last trip, two months ago, I witnessed something that really caught my attention and I returned empowered: it was how the life of the African Senegalese woman, in this case, is evolving.
I saw that women are no longer waiting to be told what to do or where to go.
On the contrary, they make important decisions together with men; and men have accepted it, because they are working together.
This is of great importance to me, because some years ago the situation was different.
So I came back thinking: "Wow, that is possible.
It's great. Now we can negotiate
as equals."
And I liked it.
Going back to fabrics, which are important in my job as I've mentioned. I want to convey through African textiles both manufactured and handmade, the important cultural connotation behind them, if you pay enough attention you can get to know a whole culture just through the fabrics.
When it comes to African textiles, people usually think -- Those are just samples, there are some stunning prints. People usually think about "Wax" fabrics, which are the ones everybody knows, and the most common printings of the continent. Those you can find anywhere you go, markets, homes, and they truly carry a cultural identity.
The names may change as well.
"Wax" is sometimes called "Kanga" here or "Capulana" in Mozambique, but it's just the same.
And from "wax" there's also "sëru njaago."
"Sëru njaago" is a fabric that -- I mention it because I like it, I used it in my wedding. It's a fabric that has a very traditional and symbolic meaning in our society.
This fabric is originally from Guinea-Bissau, although nowadays it can be found in Senegal, Ivory Coast or Burkina, but it's a Bissau-Guinean fabric, OK?
It's name is "sëru njaago" and it's woven by the Manjak people.
I encourage you to look them up to learn a little more about them.
They're very interesting.
Moving on we have Mali with its bogolans.
Bogolan, although the picture doesn't do it justice, is an impressive and marvelous fabric.
Bogolan means clay fabric, because the dying process is done with clay, leaves and roots.
The Bambara of Mali call it their second skin, but I won't enter in specificities, so that you come to see me and I will tell you all in a "petit-comité".
It's a fabric that carries the cultural connotation of and represents a whole culture, a whole ethnic: the Bambara.
That one's also bogolan.
Kente isn't widely known, because it's from Ghana. It's mostly used by the Ghanese royalty
It's not so well-known but in this country's traditional events, south of Ghana, they're now more common, but they are related to Ghana's royalty. OK?
Why am I mentioning this? I'm talking
about fabrics to tell you that we, in our daily work, use these and more fabrics that I lacked time to show you.
We use them in our creations, clothes, decorative objects, but keeping the cultural meaning within them, trying to do a sort of cultural mix, mixing up African and Spanish cultures, to show people the story behind each garment or article they touch or we exhibit.
That's it.
My mind went blank again.
In Senegal, we have a saying in every ethnicity, there are five ethnic groups and the translation is the same in every one. In English it would be something like: "Bit by bit, one can achieve every goal they pursue or anything they seek" and its literary translation would be: "Stealthily, you can hunt a monkey in the jungle."
In Wolof it's: (Speaking in Wolof) I make use of it at work.
Little by little, I try to achieve my goals, this idea of interculturality, what I want people to know about me. So they don't look at me like: "You're safe here," no.
Jere jef. Thank you.

(Applause)
I want to tell you that I'm a political artist.
I belong to a generation that thought art was going to change the world.
So, since I thought of myself as an artist I've been trying to make my practice one of resistance and transformation.
But, it's like my destiny was to be an artist.
For example, my mom was a painter.
She died when I was really small, so painting was somehow, the only option I had.
It was like the way I found to be close to her.
I started painting in the late 80s, and painting wasn't a comfortable language.
I didn't really find it easy, but it was what I had at hand.
And I'm a feminist.
And what I like most is discovering, understanding -- I discovered it through my practice, right?
For example, in the 90s I painted on black fabrics, women's underwear with household objects.
These fabrics were hanging on a wooden cross-shaped frame and for me they were like big banners for a street demonstration of women.
Then, I painted all the female birth control methods.
Like in all my work was always present all this reflection on domestic violence.
But in the 90s, the truth was that art was discussing other things and my work wasn't circulating, so I got really frustrated.
I was angry because I'd send my work to painting contests and they systematically rejected me.
So one day I decided, "I'm leaving art, I'm going to open a bar by the beach."
I started dismantling my workshop, angry.
And I was ordering, putting some things momentarily on the wall, that I would hold with painter's tape.
One day a friend comes to see me and says, "Hey, that's a great work!"
It left me astonished and when he left, I kept thinking and went to the kitchen, opened the cutlery drawer and pulled out skewers, spatulas, forks, spoons, knives, and repeated the gesture of taping them to the wall with the painter's tape.
That gesture became a work that was part of a large installation that I called "Skeptical Matter".
Those instruments were kind of an archive for me of everyday objects that would turn into surgical instruments for an at-home and clandestine abortion.
All that piece reflected on violence in the illegality of abortion.
And I was still uncomfortable anyway.
I felt that all my work was still installed in a kind of bombastic, pretentious art.
To me, I now understand it as "patriarchal deconstruction".
In that eagerness to be that ideal artist, I was out of myself.
And one day it happened, that suddenly I started realizing that I was getting old.
Suddenly, at 45, I became a woman in her menopause.
Then, I confronted a totally different violence.
Gone was, I don't know, that smell of apparent useful fertility and they stopped looking at me.
I disappeared from every possible horizon.
So I didn't know what to do with that, really.
And I had to reinvent myself.
And I decided to go through it, obviously, in my practice.
I had to think about making a piece, an artwork, that would give visibility to these new emotions.
So, I decided to look for women, women over 60 at the time, who wanted to talk with me and who would help me a bit to tell this that happens to us.
And my work took another turn, totally different.
So, since that time I have been working on the construction of an artistic work.
It consists of actions, performances, videos and that I call "School of Aging".
Where I try to give visibility to the violence of aging.
What I do is work with these retired women and who are currently doing, for example, all those activities they wanted to do when they were young. Those desired activities that they couldn't do because they had to work, take care of their children, husbands.
But above all, they couldn't do them for all the prejudices and prohibitions especially at the time, regarding the female gender.
I ask them to become masters
of these current, desired activities. That they truly become masters of their frustrated desires.
They are very powerful now.
And what I do in a first instance -- this is divided into two parts -- In the first instance I meet with them, in intimate spaces of great affection and we shape these actions with all their emotions and with the intensity of their memories.
For example, a few years ago I was invited to a triennial in Brazil and I worked with a woman who was 87 at the time, named Mariko Sakata, of Japanese origin.
She had had a very hard life, filled with a lot of work, so when she retired she didn't know what to do with her spare time.
What did she do?
To resort to her deferred wishes, which obviously were dancing and singing.
She put together a Japanese dance company with her friends from a social club they all went to.
All between 70 and more than 90 years.
She put them all together and Mariko would teach them the choreography she invented and she also designed all the costumes, she sewed and painted by hand for each of her friends.
So when I met her, I asked her to please teach me how to dance.
Mariko also fulfilled her dream of being a singer and she became a regional karaoke champion.
I asked her to please teach me how to sing karaoke.
In a second instance, these actions or what we do in the intimate space, goes out already designed into the public space.
And we give these performative workshops to a general audience as part of an exhibition, usually in an artistic institution.
For me, School of Aging forces us to rise to our frustrated desires.
Like there's no way around that.
I try to give value to whim and failure, as academic values, as academic knowledge.
Also trying a little to argue with the art system that is usually quite authoritarian and prejudiced regarding knowledge.
I feel that this work also suggests to end that violence that has been in our lives as women like forever.
And also reviewing a little bit my whole artistic history I realize that I am still that artist who is still trying to do a practice of resistance and transformation.
Now I want to share with you, and that you also be a part of my work.
I fulfilled one of my dreams thanks to Mariko Sakata, I always wanted to be part of a rock band, I guess everyone does.
But I fulfilled the dream with my friends, all of them visual artists, we formed a karaoke band.
We call ourselves "Desperate for Rhythm".
They're here today, they will take the stage and come with me.
And I want everyone to stand up.

All stand up. Up, everybody.
And we're going to sing and dance to a fragment of a song we really like, called "I'll Resist".
Here are all my friends.
They're the best for being here today and we're all going to sing a karaoke, the song called "I'll Resist", that I guess you all know it.
(Music begins) Everybody sing, huh?
♪ When you lose all the games ♪ ♪ When you sleep with loneliness ♪ ♪ When the exits close to me ♪ ♪ And the night won't leave me alone ♪ ♪ When you're afraid of silence ♪ ♪ When it's hard to stay on your feet ♪ ♪ When your memories rebel ♪ ♪ And you're put against the wall ♪ ♪ I'll resist, upright facing everything ♪ ♪ I'll turn to iron to harden the skin ♪ ♪ And even though the winds of life blow strong ♪ ♪ I'm like the reed that bends, ♪ ♪ But it's always still on. ♪ ♪ I will resist, to continue living ♪ ♪ I'll put up with the blows and I'll never give up ♪ ♪ And even if my dreams break into pieces ♪ ♪ I'll resist, I'll resist! ♪ (End of Music) (Laughter) (Applause)
We probably all know that the atom is composed of vacuum.
(Laughter) Well, it seems everything is made of nothing.
What we might call, if you allow me, the minimum fraction of matter is, believe it or not, composed mostly of nothing.
To put it simply, this is basically like: the atom's nucleus is composed of two sub-particles, a lot of subparticles, named neutrons and protons; and all around them orbit non-stop and at a very high speed the so-called electrones, creating a sort of cloud around this tiny nucleus.
For a second, think that between that cloud and this tiny nucleus there's absolutely nothing.
This is it, the atom and I hope you don't believe me.
(Applause) I hope this isn't enough for you and I hope that tonight, at home, when all this has finished, you search: "Is the atom empty,"
(Laughter) "Is everything made out of nothing?" How?!
Search, ask, and, why not, get in touch with Mr. Einstein, who may tell you something like summing this whole problem up in just one phrase, "All of them are fields in space."
And, what's the space without those fields?
I would say it's nothing.
Anyway I live asking myself questions.
What shape does a song have?
Or, what shape does a moment have?
What shape does this moment have?
Did you ever think if the colour blue that you see and read as blue is the same I or the person next to you also see?
Do you know we build everything we see?
Two people, if it was possible they were standing in the same place, at the same time and staring at the same thing they wouldn't see the same thing.
Each being is a different perceptive universe.
This is due to none of them going the same way as the others through their entire life.
For example, these days I'm doing the things I really like: I sing, I write, I read, I songwrite.
And when I think about it, I realize that this, from the beginning, is sending me a beautiful message:
Everything is built from the vacuum.
If you want to write about something you'll need a blank page, if you want to speak, you will need words: eloquence and singing are built from silence.
The same happens when you imagine: to think about a story I need an empty place in my mind.
This leads me to ask myself questions all the time; I live asking myself questions like any other teenager.
(Laughter) (Applause) Why do we suffer?
Why do we suffer?
Or, is the world really as bad as I've seen on TV and in movies?
One day I found the answer to these questions.
Do you realize that everything we see is our past?
I mean, it is everything we already know because it's in front of us.
I'm not surprised of being on stage.
And you? No? OK.
The truth is the word
"experience" gives us
an introduction to all of this.
Experience is this moment, but also is everything I learned, all the experience I gained.
If I now showed you an item that you've never seen before two things may happen: it could turn into your future, with a question: "Where am I going, what's this?" or maybe you wouldn't be interested in it, and if you were not interested in it, it would quickly disappear in your past.
Now, if all I see, if everything is my past, what's the point of classifying it?
Does it makes sense to say if it's beautiful ugly, sad, or happy?
I mean, summing it all up on a simple opinion about my experience, my past and my present, but not about the things that haven't happened yet.
If you really want to know the world, you'll have to dedicate entirely to it, you won't classify it, because, if you do that, you'll get stuck on a single idea.
When I was first told that the atom vas empty, yeah, I got crazy.
(Laughter) I got crazy.
How is it possible that everything is made out of nothing?
Now that I have understood that everything is made out of nothing, I sleep calmly (Laughter) because I understood that our world is going to be whatever we do with it.
Now, I'm leaving.
I will build on nothing an everything that gives meaning to my life.
After all, are we not made out of nothing?
(Cheers)
(Applause) Thank you very much.
(Thunder, rain) I wouldn't want to ruin the rain for you, or the feelings you relate to it, but the truth is that for the people that work with other people that are in an situation of extreme social vulnerability, the beginning of a storm no longer means a warm cup of coffee, or an afternoon of binge-watching a show, or snuggling under the covers. Now it causes us a lot of distress, worry and even despair. We wonder:
Where's Damian now?
Did Vivi find shelter?, or, Miguel's papers will get soaked with this rain and we'll have to begin the paperwork again.
I'm not sure if it happens to you as much or maybe it does, but the truth is that's really difficult to see someone sleeping in the street and feel nothing. Because, luckily, as a concept we understand that the street is no place to live and that, that person shouldn't be there.
I believe that, from the bottom of my heart and luckily so does my husband Nahuel. Which is why we created "Multipolar", and NGO that aims at helping people living under extreme social vulnerability, amongst them, people living in the street, so that they can thrive by means of a proper job.
Something that isn't easy, that doesn't happen on its own and that requires plenty of willpower.
To ground this topic I'm going to tell you about Aldo.
Aldo lives in the street, but that's not where we met him, instead, we met him along with other NGO's that provide other means of primary care: a hot shower, a meal a counselor, a doctor...
We specifically met Aldo at "Duchas del Sagrado", an amazing project that complements our own.
So when we set up the Employment desk, near the cafeteria, he first, watched it from afar. Kind of glances at it, but can't bring himself to come, he seeks for our eyes, slowly approaching us.
If I see that he doesn't come, I ask: "Are you waiting for me?"
So maybe he dares to come.
As soon as he sits down, I greet him and get down to work with the quirks of conversing with someone who spent the night in the street, who may be poorly clean or not at all, but that, nonetheless, wants to work.
And at first, we did what politeness and tradition tell us to do, such as: "Hello, Aldo. How are you?"
But after a while we realized how out of touch that was,
because the answers would crush us.
"How can I be, if I slept in the street?"
or, "I'm freezing, ma'am".
Or, the worst for me, "How do you think I am, Malena?
Last night it rained and my mattress got soaked".
So then, we realized that it wasn't a good idea to start off like that, and we changed the question and started by saying: "So you'd like to work, then?"
The answer is usually a yes, with more or less enthusiasm. Then we chat a little to understand where they are in life. What skills do they have, and other less pleasant questions such as if they have a criminal record, if they're currently doing or overcoming drug abuse and where they're staying.
So that's what we start with, gathering some basic information to also understand what's their concept of work. Because often times, like in Aldo's case, we say "So, Aldo, tell me about your work experience" And hear: "No, ma'am. I never worked" "What do you mean you never worked?
You're 25 years old. "No, ma'am, I never worked. I was simply a baker's helper, I loaded and unloaded trucks in a meat-processing plant and I handled my aunt's register machine she has a little grill diner in front of her shack."
"Well, Aldo, all of that counts as work".
So, why is it that Aldo thinks that none of that is work?
Well, perhaps he was never paid or never paid with money, in exchange he was given food or shelter to spend the night, or protection.
So after explaining to Aldo, that all of that is work, we asked him to bring in writing, in a paper we gave him, all the work he now knows he did, to put a resumee together.
So, why don't we put the resumee together that same day?
It's something that's really hard for everyone involved but that we need to do, to understand if they can sustain it.
To understand that if next time they'll show up because they really want to work, and that if a job opportunity comes up they'll seize it.
We learned to do this along the way, like with many other things, those of us who work with people who are in situations of social vulnerability.
Because for us, who sleep under a roof in a dry mattress and eat four meals a day, our expectations are not the same as those who we work with.
So, the first thing we needed to learn was to calm down.
Because if we didn't, we could be unintentionally disrepectful.
Because saying to a young man, who slept on a porche and who was kicked out and chased away with shouts to move his matress that it's all up to him and that if he tries really, really, really hard he'll succeed, out of nowhere, is to be disrespectful.
(Applause) Or maybe saying to a mom living in a park or under a bridge with her children, that if she attends a magical training course she'll become an enterpreneur and she'll be able to provide for her entire family.
If we don't provide the conditions for her to attend that training, we're just lying to her face.
So, first we learned to calm down and wait for the next meeting. If they come again, we can put a resumee together.
And that's not easy either. Because, for example, someone who's been in the street for so long cannot remember as easily as we can our work experience cronologically, and graduation dates.
Or for example, if they have a criminal record, they don't know what to put down for the time they were "put away".
Sometimes they'd like tell the truth but know that if they put: "1995-2004: Olmos Penitentiary", they will not exactly have the same opportunities to be considered for a job.
Here, we had to learn how to listen, listen to words, and listen to silences, to respect the silences. To find the right time when to ask the question, when a person is with their head down and breaks down in tears, whether it's ok to hold their hand, but mostly, we had to understand that in order to explain to them that wherever they're coming from, if right now they're facing us and most of all, facing themselves, it's the only thing that matters to move past this.
So, we create a resumee and once it's finished it stays in our database, and then one of the most wonderful things in our organization happens, that is, a lot of people who wanted to give us a hand but couldn't go to the field with us are now volunteers, and they're sponsoring people living in the streets that are looking for a job.
What do they do?
They take that resumee, edit it put it nice and neat and create an e-mail account on their behalf, they upload their profile online to all the employment websites there are and they submit application, after application, after application, inscesantly to do precisely what the person living in the street cannot do.
And in doing so, they find themseleves jumping through the same hoops we do.
Because, what do you put down for "address", for example?
Or how do you fill out basic information if there is no phone, no address, no e-mail?...
And it's a shame but it also drives the social transformation we seek, because even though this person joins us virtually the issue of people living in the street stops being invisible. But mostly, they stop believing that they're homeless because they want to be, and realize that if helping them out is hard, getting out of the street on their own is much, much harder.
And so, after creating a resumee, we remember what they say to us: "I can manage to get food", "I know the hours in which there are night rounds in the meal centers"; "I know where to go if I need to use the bathroom"; "I can get some clothes if I need dry clothes."
But there are two things they still struggle with.
The first one is the rain, that drenches their matress and that takes too long to dry. And the second is our judgement and prejudice that keep us from seeing them as a part of our society.
Therefore, if Aldo gets a job but has no one to celebrate with, it won't matter.
If Aldo needs a clean shirt but has no one to lend him one, it's all the same.
But most of all, if he manages to get out of the streets thanks to his work but nobody invites him to their house as a peer, or to celebrate his birthday, the only thing that Aldo got was just a job.
This is why we always say that this just isn't about me or my organization.
This is a colective work that needs all the stakeholders working together.
You need the state guaranteeing rights, you need centrals safeguarding such rights.
You need sensitive businessmen and willing to offer opportunities, and organizations working collaboratively.
You need society, willing to not look the other way when we they see someone in the street.
But going back to Aldo's story, if we're very very lucky, that resumee that a volunteer sent out gets to a company that wants to interview him and my phone rings, because he doesn't have one, and there the true challenge starts which is to actually find him. Easier said than done.
Aldo usually lives in one of two places.
In a goverment-run shelter where he can spend the night, or in the "street-street" as we sometime say in our jargon.
If he's in the shelter, we call crossing our fingers, hoping that there was a bed for him, because that's not always the case, and praying that whoever gets the message can find him and tell him.
If he's in the street we go out to find him always crossing our fingers to find him. We usually have super accurate references, such as "he's at Plaza San Martín, next to the Torre de los Ingleses, fourth bench, left to right."
And there we go.
If we find him, we let him know that he has an interview, thrilled, but not overwhelming him. If he decides he can face the interview at that time, we begin with everything we need, to get him there.
We need a place where he can shower, clean clothes, a bus card, money for the bus ride, a printed map with instructions to get there.
And put together the tiny pieces of his broken heart and mind because he lives in the street, and cheer him so he can face an interview.
And if need be, and he asks, even go with him to the corner of where the interview will happen and wish him good luck, so he can muster the courage to go inside.
And when he comes out, if he gets the job we celebrate like crazy, as if it were us who got the job, or someone from our family. But then we have to start doing everything we need to do so that he can keep that job.
Maybe see about finding him a place to rent so that he doesn't go to work after spending the night in the street.
See about ensuring that his basic hygene needs are met so he can go to work.
See about where he can leave his bag, people living in the street often carry one with all their belongings in it.
Basically ensuring everything so that the job he was able to find is not lost because of a lack of opportunities.
So, why am I telling you all this?
Why?
Why am I entitled to tell you this?
I tell you about this because, as I said, the social integration of a person is not up to just them finding a job. And it isn't something that happens on its own.
How do I know this if I don't live in the street myself?
I do not speak for those who are homeless because I'm quite priviliged for having a roof above my head, but I come to you as the leader of an organization.
An organization that lives by the philosophy of the "ayni".
"Ayni" is an Aymaran word meaning "reciprocity", and it means that you get what you give, maybe not from the same person, or the same hand, but it goes back to the world, the universe, as a common good.
I believe that have to be able to evolve solidarity into reciprocity.
(Applause) From the bottom of my heart, I believe we have to work with each other and not for each other.
And I say this from the one place I share with you all, which is our mere humanity.
Yourselves, from your own humanity, can make the decision to do something.
At most, challenge it.
Ask difficult questions.
Get together with others, talk about it.
Create a strategy, join one that appeals to you, but do something as a community.
Also, if someone living in the street calls out to you, don't ignore them look at them, smile at them, share some cookies.
If you can, help them with what
they ask you. And start knocking down prejudices and stereotypes, and trade them for a little love.
And at least, if you think it isn't up to you, if you're fighting for some other cause, if you can barely make it by yourself I'll humbly ask you that if you can't give, don't take. Because every scornful glance, every prejudice, every random phrase, every "they don't want to work", it's another downpour that falls on that matress and that will take a lot of time to dry up.
(Applause) And as I said before, I wouldn't want to ruin the rain for you or the feelings you relate to it, so that's why I'll tell you that everything I told you about Aldo is true and that we did it, he found a job, and now works at a restaurant.
The other day he sent me a picture in his uniform, and he couldn't fit any more teeth in his huge smile.
(Applause) And he isn't just happy about that, he's happy because he mustered the courage to see his family again, and saw his daughters again, after six months, so that family is also happy.
And Gastón, the HR Manager of the company is also happy. He gave him the opportunity and realized it was hard but possible.
And Migue is happy, the volunteer who saw the job posting and applied for him and got him the interview.
Everyone is happy when someone stops living in the street.
And everyone can do something.
For this cause, or any other that you think it's worth fighting for.
So, I mean this, choose something, commit, participate.
It's hard, it hurts, it takes time,
but it's so worth it.
When you see the smile of someone that thanks to the support of a community was able to overcome adversity, I promise it's all worth it.
It's all worth it.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good afternoon.
The giveaway message from this talk is that veterinarians and particularly the specialists even though we are in a continuous learning dialogue with the death as veterinarian forensic pathologists we can also turn bad news into good news.
And here comes our story.
On September 24th, 2002, in the Canary Islands and specifically in the islands of Fuerteventura and Lanzarote, a group of beaked whales mass stranded. They belong to the Ziphiidae family, in temporal and spatial association with military exercises, named Neo-Tapon 2002.
This was not the first time.
It did happen before in the Canary, Bahamas, Madeira, Greece...
Showing some common elements and some particularities.
Common elements were: Military exercises where high-intensity mid-frequency anti-submarine sonar was used.
And on the other hand, the most commonly involved species were beaked whales, belonging to the Ziphiidae family.
In the previous cases of Greece, Bahamas, Canary Islands, some studies were carried out but only basic information was reported, but no professional or scientific research was conducted based on pathology.
Other relevant elements were, in the case of 2002, Fuerteventura as you all very well know, is no longer a deserted island since many years ago where intellectuals were sent to "reflect".
Nowadays, it is an island that receives more than a million tourists, this way at the time of military exercises there was what you can see in this slide.
At the same time, there were not only tourists but journalists who reported in real time about the event. Replacing the news from how military exercises were executed with the appearance of several dead whales during the exercises.
This changed all the context, generating high social impact and social concern.
So, the society put pressure with a big political impact.
At that time, the Canary Islands Government contacted the Veterinary School and that was the moment when we started taking part in this story.
The question was: Are you able to carry out an investigation in order to determine or identify who killed the whales?
Obviously, that question about "who" is not a question for a technician or scientist.
We are able to answer "what", "how" and "why".
But "who", that is a question for other professionals sometimes in the area of jurisprudence or even politics.
And then, is when our study of "what", "how" and "why" began.
We started to carry out analyses.
First on the whale's carcasses.
The heads were transported to the Veterinary School.
Obsessed with the idea that ear could answer all questions in this case as a cause-effect relationship but that was not the case.
During necropsies we should pay attention to all organs because all of them are important although some of them are of vital importance.
The data started to indicate that the death had been caused by an internal multi-organic hemorrhage.
A hemorrhagic shock.
This way we could understand how animals weighing more than two thousand kilos in good body condition, meaning, they should had been healthy, could have died in a short period of time.
We knew that the military exercises had started 3 am and at that time they started using the sonar.
At 7 am there were already dead or alive stranded animals
Therefore, the animals have had suffered something very severe in a short period of time to cause them the death.
In complementary analyses we observed together with hemorrhages, the presence of gas bubbles in the circulatory system.
As well as in other tissues such as the nervous system.
Thus, we started building a possible explanation.
These bubbles could cause lesions in the circulatory system, in some specific areas, principally, where the diameter of the vessel is smaller than the bubble so this can make pressure and break the vessel and therefore a haemorrhage occurs.
So we had an explanation on "how".
But then we had to find out what kind of pathology were we... facing.
And... we considered comparative pathology.
We reviewed pathologies of land animals, as we know them better, and also human pathology
We actually looked for cases where bubbles were present, a "gas embolism" involved as a likely cause the death.
And we reached the gas embolic pathology associated to decompression sickness of scuba diving.
In those cases where the nitrogen-saturated tissues can generate bubbles in the circulatory system, as well as in other tissues when a severe or sudden change in the dive profile happens breaking properly decompression tables.
And also when we reviewed laboratory animal pathology, in research experiments with animals and a long time ago,
rabbits that were experimentally compressed and decompressed in hyperbaric chambers showed same results.
That is, a systemic gas embolism, also associated to haemorrhages.
So, we had on one hand,
a likely pathology, and lesions.
And on the other, then, we were able to build an hypothesis which was published in the journal "Nature".
A journal that, as all of you may know, has a huge scientific, as well as social impact.
The lesions were named "gas bubbles", bubbles responsible of gas embolism.
The explanation of it was that these animals could experience a panic situation and suddenly modify their dive profile, their behaviour, and get into a situation from where they could not be able to be recovered, suffering a severe gas embolic pathology similar to decompression sickness in human divers.
This caused a huge impact, scientific and social.
A scientific impact because it obviously faced the dogma that the marine mammals have been evolving during thousands of years to prevent or be immune to decompression sickness.
And a social impact because it showed a cause-effect relationship of the noise, which is getting more frequent in the aquatic environment, may bring these excellent divers to die, when they have been evolving during millions of years to avoid this kind of diving disease.
Summarizing, a cause-effect relationship,
"The noise kills".
With this big concern, a meeting took place in Baltimore, in 2004, involving a good number of experts.
Some of them were in favour, some of then were against "bubbles".
We were discussing during three days.
In these three days even some people that realised that Canary Islands are not in the Pacific, but in the Atlantic Ocean.
During these days someone also realised that the islands do not belong to Africa, but Europe and that they do have universities.
And, therefore, that in these islands some research is being carried out.
Conclusion:
Gas bubbles, the bubbles were worth investigating.
A research program was set up along with a series of questions that, obviously, were not easy to answer, and much less for a small research group, we took the ones we were able to answer using pathology but evidently we addressed them with the mind that it was also a big challenge for us to answer the questions on the basis that the hypothesis if it was not already rejected, was plausible.
The first question was: Why do mainly beaked whales and no other deep cetaceans species strand and die?
Common sense.
In the same areas where beaked whales stranded linked to sonar specifically in the Canary, there are sperm whales and also pilot whales.
Deep diving cetaceans.
And, fortunately, at that time, two excellent researchers, Peter Tyack and Mark Johnson had developed a technology that in my opinion revolutionized cetacean research may be compared with monoclonal antibodies, being a great technological development, which allows us to investigate what is happening to these animals under the surface.
Therefore, I think it is a key tool for finding out what is happening when these animals are diving.
In those first investigations, they showed that beaked whales seemed to perform a different dive profile.
A dive profile that took more time even to ascend, more to ascend than to descend, and even on the surface, these beaked whales performed descensions... some ascents and descents similar to what it could look like as a decompression table before going back to the deep.
These pieces of information were clearly evidences that supported the hypothesis that we established on the basis of the pathology.
If these animals would exceed their physiological limit they could suffer a diving pathology, and therefore, a decompression sickness.
Equally, some of those questions started to be answered thanks to very expensive research projects.
Also... Dr. Tyack and other important research groups in the world have used sounds similar to sonar at sea, using electronic devices, DTAGs, attached to the animals so that they have noticed that when the sound is played, they changed their behaviour and dive profile.
Thus, these are more facts that supports the idea that is very possible, that these animals can suffer, in certain circumstances, a gas embolic pathology.
Another question is the reproducibility of our data.
We published a first series of pathological and epidemiological data although it was necessary to prove if this happens in other new cases.
Unfortunately, the number of mass strandings of beaked whales associated with sonar, not so often in Canary, we'll see why later, but in other places in the world, particularly in Mediterranean Sea are common.
Our group has been attending these strandings and also performing pathological studies and we've found the same characteristics in all of them.
The characteristics shown in this slide showing "gas embolism" even more evident, since then as we know or we knew what to look for and of course, affecting vital organs such as brain or heart as you can see in these slides.
Another question -- crucial -- about the decompression sickness, that was done in Baltimore, was why we did not analyze the chemical composition of the gas.
The chemical composition of these bubbles that characterize the decompression sickness
must be mainly nitrogen. Well, in this case we chose the right person and during a 4-year thesis, doctor Yara Bernaldo de Quirós, along with a chemist, Óscar González, and also a mathematician and ourselves as co-directors, developed a methodology that allows to sample, store and transport the gas and, most of all, to analyse it.
By using this technology in the protocol that was set up and attending those strandings, we have noticed that these gas bubbles are mainly nitrogen, and therefore, it is another evidence which supports the hypothesis that these animals have possibly suffered decompression sickness.
And also, always being a very cautious research group.
Another statement was... You are always blaming human activity.
Human activity, in this case, represented by the anti-submarine military sonars are the killers.
If that pathology exists in a diving animal, could be also occurred by a non-anthropogenic cause?
Not originated by human activity.
Clearly, during the last ten years we have also been focused on answering this question.
And... specifically, Grampus griseus dolphins, which eat, among others, squids... sometimes very big ones.
We've seen in three of many that we've analysed, that the interaction between the hunter and the prey, sometimes... they may want to hunt a prey which is too big and even after eating it they end in a fight where both the hunter and the prey die.
During that hunting changes in their dive profile overlapped with asphyxia finally occurs.
When we carry out the autopsy or necropsy, as you prefer, of these animals look... they show again a gas embolic pathology similar to decompression sickness.
Therefore, we were already demonstrating that affirming that we the humans were actually causing this pathology wasn't an obsession, and also that there are causes of interspecific interaction that can provoke it.
This question is interesting as well as... well, you may think that only cetaceans and specifically beaked whales are suffering this pathology.
They are animals, mammals...
Sea animals which breathe air, there are others that also descend to great depths, which have a dive profile, etc., etc.
Well, in collaboration with veterinarians from Oceanographic Park in Valencia who once discovered that the entangled turtles that were rescued by fishermen, some of them alive, some dead,
in the living ones they observed neurological signs,
and in the dead ones, after performing necropsies they found a systemic gas embolic pathology, consistent with... consistent with decompression sickness very similar to the one that we described in cetaceans.
But the most interesting idea, since that, and that was also a key point, in decompression sickness, its final diagnosis cannot be done pathologically as the pathology described by us in cetaceans, to prove it definitely, it should be done
clinically.
If you see an animal with these neurological signs and you put it inside a hyperbaric chamber and perform a compression- decompression cycle, the animal come out without signs, without symptoms. Well,
the living turtles were studied using RMN and TAC showing gas inside.
They were placed inside a hyperbaric chamber and after compression- decompression cycle the animals were... flapping.
They did again an imaging diagnosis and found that the gas was gone.
So, if the pathological diagnosis in cetaceans was important, diagnosis in turtles was even more important.
But why?
Because the decompression sickness was demonstrated to exist in sea animals.
Science doesn't use to line up planets but in this case, curiously, the pathological diagnosis, although not definitive, really helped. can you imagine putting a 2000 kg beaked whale inside a hyperbaric chamber?
Indeed, I could also question myself that, because until it's demonstrated... We remain the same...
But, evidently, it's clear that the publication of our results in Nature helped to make the European Union to take a non-binding resolution and countries like Spain, along with Canary Islands Government and Regional Environment Department, to set an anti-sonar moratorium since 2004.
From 2004 to this date, thanks to the Canarian stranding network and the individualised studies of each animal we have been able to demonstrate that no more massive strandings of beaked whales have happened in twelve years.
Therefore, that's another obvious evidence that a cause-effect relationship exists.
This results have also been published in Nature
Evidently, I haven't walked this way alone.
I've done it with many more people who, besides, will make possible to keep on answering questions because, in the end, what we once did, while doing our routine work, was to close a door and open two hundred more.
That's science. And so it advances.
And in our specific case, the pathologists, I hope that the idea that speaking with the death in this case, it's a good example of we can give not only bad news but also good news
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Good afternoon.
Your mother's cunt.
(Applause) Let's analyze that phrase.
Public: Bald! Bald! Bald!


Let's analyze that phrase.
Not "good afternoon," nor "bald" but "you mother's cunt."
What does that insult mean?
What do we say to someone?
We point them to the place of origin, right?
Where they came from or should've been born.
When I was a kid there were many guys that when told "your mother's cunt" said: "Don't dare mess with my mom."
Ridiculous, right? Oedipus complex.
That common place of seeing your mom
as someone immaculate, without sex.
I always thought it was ridiculous this "don't dare mess with my mom."
Even when I realized that my mother had sex.
An insult can get so absurd that the cunt can belong to your mother, but also to your grandmother, to your aunt, to your sister.
Another thing that called my attention was guys saying: "Your sister's cunt," "I don't have a sister, I have fun with yours."
(Laughter) There I realized that the sister, although she's not the mother, there's also something obscure about messing with the sister.
I don't know, I don't have one.
And truth is, I didn't come here to have fun with anybody.
Anyway, the most revealing insult of my childhood was not something said, but something I read.
There was this old car in Pompeya, my childhood neighborhood, on Pirovano Street. I think it was abandoned, very dirty, and it had written with a finger over the dust of the windshield: "Wash me, dirty."
And on the hood, also with a finger: "Fuck the reader."
(Laughter) To see those two writings in a car was revealing for me.
Imagine a kid, 6 or 7 years old, I was just learning to read and saw that a car had all that to tell me.
So I realized that there was two things there, different.
On one side, the "Wash me, dirty."
The "Wash me, dirty" was, clearly, a message from the car.
It was the car that was talking to the owner.
It was demanding something.
The car putting the owner in evidence.
"Fuck the reader" was something different.
Because nobody said it, it wasn't the car speaking there.
It gave the impression that it was a message from nobody.
Which implies that it can be a message from God.
(Laughter) It was at that time when an atheist like me, raised by a Marxist mother and father, understood what a miracle is.
(Applause) No one writes "Fuck the reader," I thought.
Because if someone writes it, reads it.
That is, you can't write without reading.
There were then two options: whether it was God or someone cracking up at me, at whoever reads that, at humanity as a whole.
And since I didn't become a believer, I went for the second option, for the mockery, for the irony.
And irony is one of the keys of an insult.
With insult comes wit, mischief, creativity.
An insult is a sophisticated thinking exercise at the service of something crappy.
(Laughter) The excitement of the one who writes "Fuck the reader" lies in thinking what will happen to whoever reads it. That is, excitement, imagination.
A wanker!
(Laughter) Therefore, an idealist.
When I read that phrase on that dirty car, I not only had a revelation but also a command.
I felt it like a command.
On one hand, I had to write a book titled "Fuck the reader" and on the other, I had to create a dictionary of insults to plunge into that shit.
That's how I wrote "Fuck the Reader:
Argentine Dictionary of Insults, Name Calling and Expletives."
(Applause) It is strange what happens with insults.
Because if we are what we eat, we are also what we discard, the waste we make.
And if language is food, the insult is excrement.
And there comes a problem because if the insult is excrement it means that we like coprophagy.
There are some confusions in regard to insults.
Think of a text that includes the words "dick," "pussy," "fuck," "ass" and "shit."
(Laughter) Some may say even technically, if a movie contains that, it's "adult language."

No! No! It's not adult language,
it's children language.
My fascination when I read "Fuck the reader" on that dirty car was a childhood fascination.
To insult is to go back to childhood, is to re-activate those mechanisms of ingenuity and also of emotion.
It's to rediscover the world with the intensity we do when we are kids.
Another thing that's said: the insult is irrational.

No! It's rationality.
The insult is part of our rational side.
A childish rationality if you will, like in "fuck the reader", yes, but rationality in the end.
Surely, it is the last station, the frontier of rationality.
Because an insult is violence.
A representation of violence.
It can be violent, but it is a representation.
Beyond the insult, there's the punch.
And that is objective violence.
That's why we need to insult.
(Applause) But of course, how do we do it when we know that many insults are racist, macho, homophobic, sexists and classists?
And many times the insults serve as a pretext or theoretical sustenance or social acceptance for these aberrant crimes, like femicide, easy trigger, repression, enforced disappearance.
(Applause) How to fight this rampage?
A few years ago, in his wonderful presentation in the Spanish Language Congress, the master writer Roberto Fontanarrosa asked for pardon for the bad words.
So, back to that idea, I come here today to propose that we think about a program for the mitigation of damages of insults.
Mitigation of damages, that is, to give technical information for responsible use.
Like with drugs.
But for that we must be aware that, if we are going to replace an insulting word, it has to be with another as blunt as the one we replace.
Not this silly and cheap political correctness.
A serious and courageous program for the mitigation of damages of insults should take into account that we are not going to destroy machismo until we don't find a word to define cunnilingus -- oral sex to women -- as we have "blow job" for fellatio.
(Applause) Currently, there are reasons to think that a paradigm shift in insulting is possible.
Mostly, when we see that some insulted groups take charge of the insult, use it and thus neutralize it.
Think of the terms "fag" -- with the Peronists fags in the lead -- the term "bitch" -- with the feminist bitches in the lead -- or "black slums" and the slum pride of the slum music.
(Applause) Something that had already begun with soccer teams.
Think about it: shitholes, chickens, lepers, rogues, crows, "quemeros," "tatengues," niggers, etcetera.
They are all terms born as insults, that fans of those soccer clubs took charge of.
But there are also reasons to think otherwise, that a change is not possible in the insulting paradigm.
Because the insult became so massive that it can become innocuous.
Social networks eliminated the fear and calculation that insulting in person implied.
You had to be careful when you insulted someone in their face.
Social networks created the virtual insult.
Then you can say anything from anonymity.
And virtual insult is to insult what virtual sex is to sex.
It is not the same to tell someone "your mother's cunt," from a false Twitter profile, than saying "your mother's cunt" in their face, like I am saying it to you at this time.
(Applause) Insults are the sewer of the language.
And the sewers, even if they can't be seen, are necessary, they are essential.
The insult is a fundamental expression of language.
That is why it's absolutely necessary to plunge into the sewers if you want to understand a language, to begin with, but also the depth of the human soul.
They say what defines best a society is how it treats its outcasts.
So rather than looking at the streets, the buildings, bridges, hospitals, etc. we must go and see how people live in prisons and madhouses.
From this idea, we can think that in order to understand how we talk, more than reading poems, novels, listening to songs and watching movies, we have to see how we insult.
We are what we insult.
Let's admit that we are capable of creating and believing in God, but that we are also capable to shit on God.
Let's admit that the only thing we care for in this life is to keep being children.
Let's take charge.
Your mother's cunt!
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Is there a future?
Do we have a future?
Do we want a future?
It seems an obvious question.
It's a biological determinant.
And even so, for the way we react, for the way we do things, I'm not so sure that's the case.
If you ask the apocalyptic environmentalists, the future is quite dark: climate change, the invasion of exotic species, pollution, overuse.
We could say that, from that perspective, the future will not last very long.
If you ask those who prefer development at all costs, the future is not only marvellous, but also very promising.
From that perspective, technology, permanent economic growth, fairness are going to produce a welfare state.
And neither of them is lying.
Each of them has some reason to imagine it is possible.
For science, neither optimism nor pessimism is an option.
Science has to start from data generation towards information, towards knowledge, and more recently, towards political interface to demonstrate all that knowledge when making decisions.
It also depends on who you ask.
If you ask the planet - and we are permanently arguing about this - the time the planet has left is at least double the time that it's already been spinning around the sun.
The planet needs solar energy and has had it permanently for the last 4,500 or 5,000 million years.
And the sun has a reserve of nuclear fusion, of hydrogen and helium, enough for another 5,000 years.
The planet doesn't seem to have big problems.
It's not a question that bothers it.
And additionally, around 1970, in that decade, James Lovelock, an American author, suggested the Gaia theory.
In the Gaia theory, the proposal is that the planet is a kind of conscious being who can regulate her cycles: her carbon cycle, her water cycle, her nitrogen cycle, her phosphate cycle
so that she doesn't have big problems.
And in fact, we are the lesser evil, a kind of fever that doesn't generate much concern.
Now, if the question is linked to the species: "How much future does the species have?"
the worry isn't a big one either.
This is a relatively new species.
Mammals have an average of between one and two million years of duration before turning into another species.
And we are only around 400,000 to 750,000 years old.
So, in the worst case scenario, the species could even have double the time that it's already been on the planet.
However, if what we're asking about is if civilisation, if humanity, if what we recognise as society has a future, then we are on the threshold of some quite big uncertainties.
And that is where it's advisable to put a finger on the issue.
We are exhausting the planet's resources.
The WWF raises, for example, that 89% of wild populations of the flora and fauna of Central and South America has disappeared.
The two degrees of climate change which the Paris Agreement requested a few years ago has been responded to timidly by governments.
From these perspectives, we really do have difficulties imagining that resilience.
However, the situation in Colombia is not the worst one.
Unlike what I mentioned before, Columbia is a country that has the proportion of natural and developed areas inverse to the rest of the planet.
If the planet is one-third natural areas and two-thirds developed areas, the opposite is true in Colombia: we have two-thirds natural and one-third developed areas.
Scarcely one percent of the land that exists on the planet, of the continental territory of the planet, is home to 10% of the world's population.
That means that we have 10 times more than the average, and 100 times more than the one who has least.
Eleven countries have 70% of the planet's fresh water.
Colombia is one of them.
Colombia is the first country for numbers of birds and orchids.
It's the second for amphibians, butterflies and freshwater fish.
It's the third for plants and reptiles.
It's the fourth for mammals.
And it isn't the biggest country.
It's a country of only one million square kilometres.
It's medium-sized.
And a large part of the other countries which have a better ranking are considerably bigger countries.
However, not everything is calm.
Our country has immense problems with sustainability.
Resilience seems to be a great support for society, and yet there are many things that need to be done.
To mention a few: the Convention on Biological Diversity, which is the governing body for countries for issues of environmental conservation, proposed a goal four or five years ago that we should have 17% of our country's land conserved and 10% of our marine territory.
Colombia achieved this excellently with the marine territory.
And it will be achieved with the land very soon.
The countries of the region have also achieved some conservation goals above and beyond those suggested by the Convention on Biological Diversity.
And yet, we keep on losing biodiversity.
In this context, what are we doing wrong?
Is it the mechanism we're using to conserve biodiversity?
Or is it because of the paradigm with which we're managing biodiversity?
In the 1970s, when I was working with protected areas for an environment agency, the context was 'the tragedy of the commons', proposed by the biologist Hardin in 1976.
In 2008, Elinor Ostrom won the Nobel Prize for Economy, speaking about the environment and proposing a different paradigm, which is community governance of natural resources.
We have to believe in that paradigm shift.
We have to turn it into an element of management.
In fact, it should be raised that the very concept of preservation can even be anti-natural.
Preservation means leaving something static.
And the only static thing in biodiversity is its dynamism.
So preservation can leave many doubts about what a good model for the conservation of biodiversity is.
At least we have to imagine that we can't keep doing more of the same.
We have to innovate.
We have to be consistent with those paradigm shifts and invest more and more in better tools for biodiversity management.
The economic balance of the country is based on elements of extraction, of resources extracted from the land: coal, oil, gold.
And in none of these fields is Colombia in the top 10.
In oil, we're number 21; in coal, we're 11.
In gold, we don't even manage to be inside the top 20.
At the same time, the investment in matters of biotechnology, biomimicry, 'bio' elements, is quite limited.
So, we would have to undertake an enormous process around investigation, science and knowledge, around patents and the possibility there could be elements of the economy where the planet is the one moving it.
Nothing new there.
One of the approximations which is best being developed is that of the socio-ecological transition towards sustainability.
This concept aims to enter into Anthropocene environmental management
and not keep thinking about Pleistocene environmental management, from long ago, to go back and recover natural territories.
Everything changes.
But it changes alongside the possibility of still remaining functional.
Stockholm, in 1972, at the first Earth Summit, brought up sustainable development as the big slogan.
The Rio de Janeiro Summit in 1992 suggested 'act local and think global'.
These are still valid.
We need to keep thinking about that locality, about acting.
And a large part of you and environmentalism is very strong in Colombia about the 'act local' part.
However, 'think global' is necessary.
But even at the margin of what we expect of governments, of whatever we do as a state, the responsibility for conservation of biodiversity and environmental management is down to all of us and each one of you.
Any of the daily decisions that you take related to plastic, the market with bags, sustainable movement depend exclusively on you.
We can't keep demanding that governments do what we haven't started doing.
And then, of course, put pressure on the government so they implement changes.
I'll mention a short matter, quickly.
I get around by bicycle, at least during this century.
That's what I do.
But not even because of environmental awareness or social commitment.
Just that the generation of endorphins which are produced by cycling around Bogota generate levels of addiction which I'm already subject to.
I invite you to reflect and make conscious decisions.
Thank you.
(Applause)
We were going home on the Mitre train with a group of volunteers.
We had been going to that soup kitchen in San Fernando for the past two years to provide educational support and do recreational activities.
I was leaning on the window, waving at to those that had come to say goodbye.
As soon as the train started moving, chaos broke out.
A sharp stone went through the glass and cut my face.
Right here.
Above my left eye.
Those that had thrown it knew us; they had tried to hurt us.
I got home with my t-shirt covered in blood, and among my parents' cries I made a promise: "Tomorrow I quit the project."
But when the time came, I couldn't.
I felt that if I let the fear in, I would never be the person I was; that if fear and anger took over, they would subdue me forever.
During those years in the soup kitchen many mothers and fathers had told me they couldn't even provide their children with the basic needs.
I had met those children and I knew their future was totally conditioned
by that fate which places a person under a certain roof, in a certain context, and marks them for the rest of their lives.
At that moment, I decided I would create a mechanism to challenge that fate.
After a few years, my dream became reality.
I created a social program from scratch, in a foundation.
After working with young people in different situations I knew what I wanted.
I wanted to create something out of school
for real.
I knew the education system was always one step behind children's interests with its times and bureaucracies;
that school couldn't cover it all
and more spheres were needed to complement it.
I knew what I wanted to do but I didn't know where to start.
So I started researching, walking around the Abasto area, talking to organizations that were working there and looking inside colonial-style houses.
What I saw were a lot of lonely children.
Hours and hours all by themselves.
As if it were a puzzle, there I found the first clue.
We need a place that belongs to them where we can keep them company.
We assembled an interdisciplinary team with professionals from the technology, recreation and social working fields.
They were all highly experienced in their own discipline, but we all agreed about the desire to produce something new, something mystical, something magical, something brimming with love.
After months of planning we set up the physical space in a two-storey house on the corner of Zelaya street in Abasto, filled with natural light.
That's how Puerta 18 was born, a free after-school social program that focuses on artistic, scientific and technological creativity for kids aged 3 to 18.
It didn't make sense to wait for them to come, so we went out looking for them.
We would bring computers, cameras, robots and show up at schools of the area during recess to tell them an idea.
We invited them to come.
One of the first to come was Agustín, who was throwing bang snaps on the street when I approached him.
He told me he was 14 years old and was fond of music and video games.
We invited him to come and see the place and we told him our proposal: to discover vocations and talents through technology.
He stared at us with eyes wide open but didn't say a word
because he lacked even the most basic skills to use a computer.
Soon we realized that teaching was going to be only one part of the process.
We had to be able to teach and contain at the same time.
It wouldn't be the standard social program in which those two aspects are separated;
in which those that deal with social issues are on one side and those that share the contents are on the other.
We wanted to do everything at once.
Delegating tasks while providing support became our own unique way of doing.
As time went on, the number of children began to rise and we resumed our original idea with much more strength: to find vocations without restrictions; let talent and effort be the only key.
How can you know whether your thing is animation, robotics or special effects?
There are still many disciplines that are of difficult access to.
You need someone to teach you and a technological infrastructure in order to learn.
We began dismantling each discipline, content by content, incorporating games, dynamics, making easy what was difficult and ensuring that everyone could learn and try.
Agustín kept coming.
But one day he sat down with a grave face, arms crossed, and said: "You know what? Stop asking me
what I want to do.
Why don't you tell me what to do and be done with it?"
It came as a shock.
In our innocence, we thought that once the space had been created, lots of children would come along with ideas and projects and that we would only show them the necessary tools to communicate them.
But none of that happened.
It didn't happen to Agustín and it didn't happen to a lot of children that came after him. "Tell me what I have to do."
From that moment on, our work focused on one thing only: to spark their interest. To spark their interest.
How could we stimulate the desire to know?
How could we encourage them to have something to tell?
We worked out a plan.
We were going to make them try everything. Every day a different thing: graphic design, music, special effects, animation, robotics, programming, from the simplest activities to the most complex.
Developing other people's ideas until they had their own.
That was when The Padawans were born.
We called them like that in honor of the Jedi apprentice from the movie Star Wars.
The Padawans are the older kids; those that are more knowledgeable and are willing to share their knowledge. They bring ideas and lead teams.
Being a Padawan is an honor, something you earn and everyone else respects.
They were a great discovery when searching for tools to spark the interest of other children.
As time went on, that tiny little program became a real community for experimental learning in which more than 2,500 children have engaged in.
(Applause) Technology has both forced us to and helped us break with the asymmetry of the teacher-learner interaction,
because someone who knows a lot about programming knows nothing about filming movies.
We dismantled the lecturer-listener model and transformed it into a circle in which we all teach and learn;
in which all the time you can hear someone say "let's try together!"
because the truth is nobody has an answer for most of the things we experiment with.
The first kids to participate in the program were now turning 18, and they were still taking part in it.
It was an opportunity, but also a decision, to start working with the 18 to 24 age range. It immersed us fully into the labor market
and challenged us to enlarge our team and broaden our scope of work.
Just to give you some data: in Argentina in 2014 the technological market had more than 10,000 job vacancies.
It is estimated that this year that figure will double.
There it is, once again, the possibility of bringing them closer to valuable, life-changing experiences.
The challenge is not just to get them a job, but to allow them to work doing what they choose to do, and in skilled positions.
Agustín went in no time from playing with bang snaps to needing money to help at home.
We thought it a good option to apply for a scholarship but we were met with some surprises.
On the one hand, most scholarships are for academic excellence. That means only academically brilliant students can be eligible.
If he is an average student who puts a lot of effort that's it, the scholarship doesn't recognize that.
How can effort be measured on an application form?
How can passion be assessed?
On the other hand, scholarships are relentless.
If you chose architecture, you'll end up being an architect.
There's no room for change because a scholarship is a door to a course of studies, not to a person.
Once we understood that, we began creating genuine scholarships based on knowing them and investing in their path.
A path that may have its ups and downs just like real life, because I refuse to think of choosing a vocation, dreaming, and above all making mistakes as luxury goods.
Eventually, Agustín got his scholarship, but soon afterwards he wanted a job.
He was doing his second year of engineering and his account of how the interviews had developed was conclusive.
He said: "when I tell them where I live
their faces change. It all goes to rack and ruin."
Once again the dreaded story.
Once again that "where are you from?" marking how far you can go.
We spent weeks thinking strategies to dodge that moment in interviews.
We asked each other how we had gotten our first jobs
and we all replied the same: "through a friend," "through an acquaintance," "through a relative."
Then someone said: "that's what we should be:
their social capital. We should be the guarantor of that relationship."
What I'm sharing with you today is a work model still in development based on knowing the options, choosing, delving into and professionalizing; and it is powerful enough to break limits.
The one thing that matters is who you want to be from this moment on.
Soon after starting the program we were visited by a high government official.
He liked the experience a lot and made us a proposal: to open 10 spaces like that in the city.
We refused, arguing we weren't ready. Today, we still think the same way.
We want to make 1,000 and we want you to help us.
Each day I hear the bell ringing and I see hundreds of children coming in and focusing on their work, proud of their projects. I see them going to production companies, studios and businesses to show what they can do.
I see their families' realities changing for the better.
I see myself fulfilling my own dream.
I like to think that afternoon on the train, I chose my fate;
that the stone that injured me was also my foundation.
Every now and then, I think about it and hope that whoever threw it has, at some point, been to Puerta 18.
(Applause)
I want to talk to you about our place in the Universe.
It wasn't always the same.
Or at least, we didn't always know it was the same.
At first - you remember,
all the stuff we studied -
we saw ourselves as the kings of creation.
We were the center of the Universe.
Everything had been created for us to admire.
Naturally, it didn't last long.
As usual, some ill-bred guy turned up -

a Polish guy named Copernicus.
He told us we were not the center of the Universe, but that the Sun was at the center.
We didn't like that at all.
But we were actually more or less close to that Sun,
so we had no choice but to accept the idea of relying on another celestial body, that we were orbiting around something else and that we did not occupy the most important place in the cosmos.
And we lived with that for a long time,
until another rude type showed up - there's no shortage of these guys -
almost 100 years ago.
His name was Edwin Hubble.
Yes, same as the telescope, but
there was a person with the name first.
He said that, actually, we lived in a teeny corner of the Universe,
and that the Universe wasn't everything;
there were another 100 billion island universes - galaxies - scattered around.
Our place in the Universe was extremely small.
And humanity was on the verge of commiting suicide over it, when we realized that perhaps this was something to be proud of.
The Universe, we believed up until then, was something that we had always had,
and that we had always existed.
But that wasn't the case, either.
Someone once compressed the age of the cosmos, 13.74 billion years, into one Earth year, and said, "If the Universe began the first second on the first of January, then the human race would enter the story
in the last four seconds of December 31."

That's amazing. We missed the whole movie.
Even so, it's as if we just watched a movie in the dining room, and then went into the kitchen to ask our mom, "Do you know where they placed the cameras?
And how did they do the casting?
What about for the two previous movies that gave birth to this one?
And for the next two to come?"
And we tell absolutely the whole movie,
because that's what we do with the great tool we call science.
Science looks at our relationship with the environment.
Our relationship to the environment is very important.
Everything we do and how we do it relates back to the link we have with everything that surrounds us.
In our relationship with the environment, we grow as curious people.
We can become good scientists, if that's what we want.
But we can also become an excellent dancer, a great painter, a person who enjoys knowledge, or at least - better yet - a person who wonders about everything that surrounds us.
We can be someone who truly forms part of the landscape.
Because the landscape is everything -
a mountain that we see from afar, the sound of a river flowing nearby, a bird we hear singing, an animal we can touch.
And the sky; the constellations
are also part of the landscape.
Throughout history, we've made our fears and longings part of the sky:
the things we feared, that we wanted to be far away,
and the things we loved so much, that we wanted to observe them every single night.
Imagine this:
In ancient times, those things were bears, mythological animals, ships, hunters.
What would they be today?
We'd probably put a mushroom cloud made by an atomic bomb,
to remind us of what not to do again.
Or we would put technology.
We'd have constellations of a cell phone, a TV or some pro athlete.
And Argentines, at least, would for sure put the Pope.
It's a weird constellation, but probably a lot of people would like to see it every night.
This sky that we consider such a vital part of our landscape -
that sky, for a blind person, doesn't exist.
Some years ago, I was working as a writer for a radio program, and the announcer had the craziest idea: to climb a mountain located in Neuquén province, Argentina - it's a volcano, actually, a bit more than 3000 meters high - with a group of teenagers with no climbing experience.
That idea alone is pretty weird.
Moreover, all of them were blind.
The idea sounded fascinating to me.
I had no intention of climbing even the tiniest stone, but I made myself the "brewmaster," and took part in all the planning meetings.
Everything was going great, until they starting asking me things like how many moons does Saturn have, who was Galileo ...
And of course there was one typical, cheeky kid who asked me, "What does the sky look like?
I've never seen it. I have no idea.
You people talk about the Moon, you write songs and poems about it.
I've never seen it.
You talk about stars ... it sounds like a romantic idea, but I don't have a clue how to observe them."
And since I'm Argentine, in order to explain that, I stole an idea.
Yes, I stole it from - well, OK, as long as I'm owning up to it - I stole it from a Greek man who is no longer in a position to protest.
His name was Hipparchus, and he lived a long time ago. He built an observatory on the island of Rhodes, in the Aegean Sea.
He made a bunch of discoveries, he predicted eclipses, he even averted wars, thanks to his knowledge of astronomy.
But he did something interesting.
He was perhaps the first to realize that all stars have different brightnesses.
You all probably know that.
You'll have noticed that we can see certain stars very easily every night, or at least on starry nights, but that others are hard to see with the naked eye.
He called those "magnitudes."
He was wrong, actually.
Hipparchus thought that the stars we could see easily were bigger, so he called that "magnitude," from "magna-," which means "big." And he thought the stars
we couldn't see easily were small.
Today we know that's not the case, and that it's actually a combination between how far the object is, and its actual brightness.
It's like if we had different lightbulbs, each of varying wattage, some near us, some far away.
They'd each seem brighter or less bright, depending on that combination.
And I had to explain that to blind people,
people who wanted to feel inspired by this thing that inspries me.
So I turned light into something they could understand: sound.
I turned those magnitudes into sounds.
I assigned 10 decibels to the stars we can barely see.
That's the limit of human hearing.
It's like the sound leaves make in autumn as they blow along the ground.
I gave 20 decibels to fifth-magnitude stars, which are a bit brighter.
Then 30 decibels, 40 decibels.
The brightest stars ended up with 70 decibels, which is the volume at which I'm talking to you now. So now I had a conversion between light and sound,
and the stars - any star in the night sky - were audible.
And now these kids could interact with that landscape, perhaps for the first time.
But stars also have colors.
Did you realize that?
The majority of stars are white.
But there are some stars that are yellow, others are red or blue.
As you know, color is wavelength, and so is sound.
So, really, the scheme was already in place.
Perhaps there's someone in this room who likes auto racing.
You might recall that when you watch an auto race, they put a microphone along the turn, and you hear something like (Imitates car sound).
There are no sound effects, so I had to do that myself.
This is a low-budget TED talk.
(Laughter) We hear that.
At first we hear the (Imitates car sound) of the sound pressing against my ear.
And then we hear the (Imitates car sound) of the sound that stretches up to my ear.
The same thing happens with light.
Blue stars are blue because their light is squeezing against my eyes.
And red stars are red because their light is stretching,
just like the red in sunrises and sunsets, because the sunlight has to go through more atmosphere before reaching my eyes.
Again, the scheme was in place.
Blue stars would have a high-pitched tone.
Red stars would have a low-pitched tone.
White stars would have a neutral, intermediate tone.
So the sky now had an appearance.
And I ran the workshops for some time, because this happened a long time ago, the code was created in 1999, so about 15 years ago.
And after a while, what did I do?
Well, I Googled myself.
I wanted to know if there were other crazy people out there, showing the sky to the blind.
And I found a bunch of people doing the same,
including some who based their work on my idea.
Others took different approaches.
We're basically all in touch nowadays.
We form part of an international group that enables people to touch a Moon that has craters, mountains, seas.
We touch simulated soil from Mars, made by NASA.
We run our fingers over Mercury's craters.
We use touch to see what little spaceships are like.
We interact with that environment, with that universe, in a different way.
Because, really, nowadays all of us are interacting with the Universe in a different way.
Today, astronomy is the science of the invisible.
Most likely, you all imagine an astronomer as a man or a woman hugging their telescope.
That's not exactly the case.
Nowadays we have infrared telescopes -
we can actually feel the warmth of the objects.
We have ultraviolet, x-ray telescopes -
we take x-rays of the galaxies.
We have telescopes that capture subatomic particles.
Really, what they're doing is making several water basins, like the ones in the south of Mendoza province, here in Argentina, that let us study a sky that no one - not only a blind person, but no one - can see.
This project took me by surprise.
When I started giving talks when I was very young, I never thought that there were people who could perceive this Universe that I love so much, in a different way.
Imagine a UN meeting.
They don't discuss the inalienable right everyone has to enjoy the sky and to become a part of the stars, the Moon, the Milky Way - a part of their landscape.
There should be people doing that.
Somebody should be working to return the poetry, the mystery, the magic, to the sky.
Fortunately, there are people who do that.
I know, they're not the people debating things at the United Nations.
They're the people who attended my astronomy for the blind workshops all these years, and who show me a completely different universe from the one I see in my books.
All of them, each and every one, throughout all this time, have made me feel like I'm in heaven. (Applause)
The art of movies has existed for more than 100 years.
And the way we make movies hasn't changed much from a dimensional perspective.
Placing a camera and press the rec button, this has not changed at all.
Cinema is still a frontal experience, and it's still perceived as such.
The experience of watching movies is the same since their creation.
You stand in front of a screen and look at the image displayed.
In fact, there's nothing wrong with that.
I love going to the movies, watching movies on TV; the experience of watching stories in motion can be so powerful to even move us emotionally.
The question we ponder on is how stories in motion can go beyond the flat screen.
If we look at the media of the future there is an opportunity to create content that adapts to new platforms.
When we shoot a film, we traditionally imagine a scene inside a frame, creating depth with foreground, middleground and background, as the camera moves.
Obviously, with new devices like virtual reality or augmented reality goggles or new smart platforms that allow us to navigate in 3D, there is an opportunity to create much more immersive content.
In a way that we could move inside it and try to live the experience from a new perspective.
I'm not talking about video games or computer-created characters, even if they look super realistic.
I'm talking about real actors.
Flesh-and-blood performance.
Artistic performance.
To do such a thing we would have to start redefining or thinking about new technologies and methodologies to enable these new experiences.
We already know about 360-degree filming, putting a camera in the center that allows us to create a 360-degree immersive image. This already takes us one step further in an interaction.
But it's still a frontal experience.
To start investigating how we could get into the scene we would have to capture the light from all angles, putting cameras and sensors to capture all the light from all perspectives.
These solutions allow us to recreate the subject in three dimensions.
It would be like a 3D photograph.
This allows us to not just record the image on one single plane but capture it as a volume.
This is what we call volumetric video.
It is the process of capturing light and storing it inside a voxel.
But what is a voxel?
A voxel is like a pixel in three dimensions.
Instead of being a flat square with light information, it's like a cube in space, with position X, Y and Z, saving all the light information from all perspectives.
This methodology allows us to generate a volume of light visible from all sides.
And with this, we can start thinking and opening up the opportunities for new content.
Obviously, to capture and create film and scenes and stories with this kind of solution we would have to create a space large enough to accommodate entire scenes and montages, and powerful enough to install hundreds of cameras and process that huge amount of information.
It sounds like a crazy idea, but it's exactly what we did.
For the last three years we've been building the studio of the future.
We installed hundreds of cameras.
And we built a gigantic dome that allows us to create exactly what we wanted.
We called it Intel Studios.
It is the world's largest volumetric camera, created to develop, research, and define new methodologies for interactive content.
But what does interactive content mean?
What does it mean to get into the scene?
To test these new hypotheses and ideas we decided, for the first time we used the studio, to choose a classic scene.
We picked a scene from the Far West.
We brought scenarios, filled the studio with dirt, actors, four horses, to recreate an entire scene but this time captured from all angles.
The actors, to work on these new media, faced a challenge.
They had to act flawlessly to be seen from all angles.
There is no longer a frontal position that you just refer to.
But now we have to think of all perspectives.
Very similar to theater.
Let's see now what came out with the first result.
What you see here opened our eyes to the opportunities we can have with these new technologies and media.
The result was a complete full scan of the entire scene.
And it allowed us to move inside in a free way that we couldn't before.
With this new solution we can see not only the image or light emitted on a screen but also get into that light and navigate inside its volume, perceiving it from new angles.
Now let's look at one of the shots we rendered using this technology.
What you will see is done entirely with a virtual camera that moves in space.
(Volumetric video) (End of volumetric video) (Applause) Thank you.
As you can see, with this solution we manage to have total control of space and movement, allowing new angles and freedom for content creation.
But what else can we do with this freedom?
Can we put a virtual camera in the eyes of the actors, from their points of view?
What if instead of the actor we chose the horse?
Let's look at the same scene -- there wasn't a new take -- but this time, from the horse.
(Volumetric video) (End of volumetric video) (Applause) No horse was harmed and no camera was placed on his head.
Actually, this frees up the possibilities of navigating within the scene.
This is perfect for filmmakers or content creators as it gives them the tools to create these new visualizations.
But what about the audience?
We can look into how the audience can get immersed.
That's why we partnered up with Paramount Pictures.
We researched how the experience and interactions within stories in motion would be like in some Hollywood movies.
Together with director Randal Kleiser we got together and recreated the 1978 film Grease.
Bringing 20 dancers to the stage, we recreated the song "You're the One That I Want" in a brand new take.
What you see here really allowed the directors and us to break down the barriers of 2D thinking.
And start thinking in a whole new way and let new tools come up for content and for creating new stories.
But why see it in a flat way?
Let's invite them to the stage.
Not really, we'll bring them in augmented reality.
I'm going to use this tablet, I'm mapping the scenario, recording the information in 3D.
(Applause) Let's see what they say.
(AR video) (Clapping) (End of AR video) (Applause) Thanks to the augmented dancers.
As you can see, the content can be experienced as a traditional movie or as an immersive experience.
It all depends on how and what we're seeing it with.
We're not trying to change the traditional ways of creating content, but to enhance them.
We are at a unique moment in the history of cinema, opening up and understanding new ways to create immersive content.
The journey has just begun.
I invite you all to join us.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Hello.
When I'm asked what I do in my free time, I always say I paint walls in my city.
They always ask me, "How come?
What do you mean you go out and paint?

Why? Do you get paid?
Are you involved with a church?
Are you involved with the city?"
I answer, "No; I do it with Poetic Action Santo Domingo."
Over 16 years ago, a person by the name of Armando Alanis Pulido decided that his city was full of negativity.
And just like him, three years ago, with others from TEDxSantoDomingo, the idea came out at a meeting that we needed to eliminate that negativity from our city.
At that time, all we'd see was political flyers, or ads for products we didn't want to buy.
So, we decided we were tired of it.
And on December 30th, 2012, at 7 that night, it was a bit dark, we went out, and painted our first mural.
After painting our first mural we understood what Poetic Action truly is.
It's not about going out and painting a mural so people can say, "Look how pretty!"
and share, "I saw this on social media."
No. It's about going to
a neighborhood corner store where we could paint a phrase that maybe wasn't read by 1,000 people, maybe not even five, but it's about that one person that stopped and felt an impact.
And said, "Wow, I like that statement."
Smiled and said, "I like what you do."
This inspired me to find another wall, and that same day we painted another.
It's about the four year old that said he wanted to grab a brush and paint with us.
About a passerby that was on their way home from work saw the mural and said, "I have to paint with you." This person stopped, took a brush and painted with us that afternoon.
That's what it's about.
Going further than one believes.
It's about using words and poetry to transform our city.
Not stopping to think: "No, I can't do anything about it."
"I don't have money or time."
One of our murals, that's now gone, states, "There is always time."
So, more than anything, I want to leave you with this: as long as you want it, it can be done. Because while I had no money and nothing to do with my year off I, along with a group of people, decided to start this.
And it can always, always, always be done, as long as one wants it, as long as one has passion, dedication, it can always be done.
We've painted 55 murals.
20 out of the 55 have been erased.
But we've kept on painting.
And we will reach 1,000 murals.
They may erase 500.
They may erase 999.
So long as one remains, and one person sees it, we'll be happy.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
I opened a blind man's head.
I didn't make him think or reflect; I cracked his head open, literally.
He was walking grabbed by my shoulder, I didn't correctly estimate the space between the two and I made him hit against a gate.
(Laughter) Five stitches on his forehead.
I felt the worst teacher in the world,
I really didn't know how to apologize.
Luckily, El Pulga is one of those people who take things quite well.
And today he goes on saying that I was the coach who left the most important mark in his career.
(Laughter) The truth is that when I started working at the institute for the blind, I was surprised by a lot of things.
A lot of things they did I didn't imagine they could: they swim, do athletics, play cards, drink mate, pour it themselves and don't get burned in the process.
But when I saw them playing soccer I found it amazing.
They had a field with two rusted goals and broken nets and the blind who attended the institute would play their games there. Just like I do in a vacant lot near home, but there they were playing without being able to see.
The ball had a sound so they could locate it; there was a guide behind the rival team's goal to know where to kick ball, and they used an eye mask.
There were kids who could still see and to play under the same conditions they wore that eye mask.
When I got confident, I asked for a mask myself, I put it on and tried to play; I had played soccer all my life.
I found it more amazing still, in two seconds didn't know where I was standing.
I had studied physical education because I loved high performance.
I had started working there by chance.
My other job was with the Argentinian National Rowing Team, and I felt that was my thing.
Here, everything took me double.
I'll never forget the first day I did the warm-up with the team. I put them in front of me, - I had a lot of experience from when I worked with the national rowing team - and I said, "Now, everyone down," and I did this. (He bends) When I looked up, there were 2 seated, 3 lying around, others squatted.
(Laughter) How do I do here what I was doing there?
It took me a lot.
I started looking for tools, to learn from them, from the teachers who work with them.
I learned that I couldn't explain a play on a chalkboard like a coach does, but I could use a plastic tray with caps so they could interpret me through touch.
I learned that they could also run on a running track if I ran with them holding a rope.
Then we started looking for volunteers to help us run with them.
And I was enjoying it and I was finding the purpose and meaning to the activity.
It was difficult, it was uncomfortable, but I decided to overcome this discomfort.
And there came a time when it became the most fascinating job of all.
I think that's when I wondered, why couldn't we be, with the blind, a high-performance team as well?
Of course, the other part was missing: to know what they wanted, the real protagonists of this story.
Three hours of training on that field we played soccer on were not going to be enough.
We would have to train differently.
We started to train harder and the results were great, they asked for more.
I understood that they also wondered why they couldn't be high-performance.
When we felt ready, we knocked at CENARD's doors, our National Center of High-Performance Sports we have here in this country.
It was hard to have them open doors, but it was considerably more difficult to get the other athletes training there to consider us their equals.
In fact, they would lend us the field only when no other teams used it.
And we were "the blind", not everyone knew what exactly we were doing there.
The 2006 World Championship was a turning point in the team's history.
It was held in Buenos Aires for the first time. It was our chance to prove to our people what we had been doing all this time.
We made it to the final, we were growing as a team.
On the other hand, Brazil was leading, so far the best team in the tournament.
They were winning every game by a landslide.
Almost nobody believed that we could win that game.
Almost nobody, but us.
During training, in the locker room, during each warm-up, there smelled of champions.
I swear that smell exists.
I smelled it several times with the team, but I particularly remember the day before we played that final.
The Argentine Football Association had opened their doors to us and we were preparing in AFA, where Verón, Higuain, and Messi train.
For us, it was feeling like a national team for the first time.
The day before at 7:30 pm, we were in the lounge during the technical talk and a young man knocks on the door, interrupting our conversation, suggesting us to go to church; he came to invite us to go to church.
I try to dissuade him, replying it was not the best of times, that we better leave it for another day.
And he insists asking me to please let him take the kids to church because that day a pastor came who performed miracles.
I asked with some fear what miracle he was talking about, and he easily replied, "Coach, let me take the team to the church, when we return I'm sure that half of them will see."
(Laughter) Some laughed, but imagine you are blind and someone says that.
I didn't know what to say, I kept silent, an awkward silence.
I didn't want to make him feel bad because he truly believed this could happen.
And one player saved me when he stood up and confidently said, "Juan, Gonza already told you it's not the best time to go to church.
Besides, let me make this clear: we get to go to that church, and I'm part of those half of us who see when we return here, I beat the shit out of you, for tomorrow I won't be able to play the game."
(Laughter) (Applause) Juan left laughing in resignation, we continue with the technical talk, and that night, when I went to sleep, I began to dream the next day's game, to imagine what could happen, how we would play it.
And then I felt the smell of champions I mentioned a while ago.
Because at that moment I felt that if the other players had the same desire Diego had to play that game, we could only be champions.
The next day was going to be wonderful.
We got up at 9 am, the game was at 7 pm, and we already wanted to go and play.
We left AFA, and the bus was full of flags given to us, we were talking about the game and we could hear the honks, and people cheering who were saying, "Go Bats, today is the last day, the last effort."
The boys asked me, "Do they know us?
Do they know we are playing?"
There were people who went to CENARD following the bus.
We arrived and found a pleasant scene.
Along the lane from the locker room to the game field I was walking with Silvio, who grabbed by my shoulder, let me guide him; fortunately, no gates on the way. When we reached the field, he was asking everything and everybody, he didn't want to miss a detail. Then he said, "Tell me what you see,
tell me who is playing those drums."
I tried to explain what was happening, with as much detail as possible.
I would say, "The stands are packed, a lot of people could not get in, there are blue and white balloons all over the field, they are opening a giant Argentina flag that covers the entire grandstand." Suddenly, he cuts me off me and says, "Do you see a flag that reads San Pedro?" - the city where he lives -
and I start looking into the stands and I find a little, white flag written in black spray paint that read, "Silvio, your family and all San Pedro is here."
I tell him that and he replies, "That's my old lady,
tell me where she is, I want to I wave at her."
I orientate him, I show him, with his arm, where the flag is, and he waves his two arms at that direction.
About 20, 30 people stand up to give him an ovation, and when that happens, I see how his face changes, how moved he is.
It was moving for me too; 2 seconds later, I had a lump in my throat.
It was strange, because I felt the excitement of what was happening, and the anger and the anguish that he could not see all this.
A few days later when I told him what had happened to me, he was reassuring, because he said, "Gonza, don't feel bad, I could see them, differently, but I swear to you I saw them all."
The game started; we could not fail, it was the final.
People had to stat still, like here, because in soccer, for the blind, the public has to be quiet so that the players hear the ball.
And they are only allowed to cheer when the game is over.
About 8 minutes to go, they cheered what they hadn't in the first 32.
When Silvio nailed the ball at angle, they cheered that goal from the heart, in an incredible way.
If today you go to CENARD, you will see a huge poster on the door, with a Bats photo.
They are a model national team, everyone knows who they are in CENARD, and after having won 2 World Championships and 2 Paralympic medals, no one doubts they are high performance.
(Applause) I was lucky to train this team for ten years, first as trainer and later as their coach.
The feeling I have is that I received much more than I gave.
Last year I was suggested to coach another national team, Power Soccer.
It's a national team of young men who play soccer in wheelchairs.
Motorized wheelchairs that they drive with a joystick, as they have no strength in their arms to drive a conventional chair.
They added a bumper to the chair, a safeguard that protects their feet while allowing them to kick the ball.
It's the first time they stop being spectators and turned protagonists.
It's the first time that their parents, friends, and siblings go to see them play.
For me, it's a new challenge.
Again the discomfort, insecurity, and fear like when I started with the blind.
But I face it all from a more experienced position.
That's why from day one, I treat them as athletes on the field, and I try to put myself in their place outside the field.
With no prejudice, because treating them naturally is how they feel best.
Both teams play soccer; it was something unthinkable for them.
They had to adapt the rules to do so.
And both teams broke the same rule, precisely that which said they could not play soccer.
When you see them play, you see competition not disability.
The problem starts when the game is over they leave the field, and they come out to play our game
in a society that sets rules that almost don't take them on account, that don't care for them.
I learned from sports that disability greatly depends on the rules of the game.
So I think that if we change some of the rules of our game, we can make their lives a little easier for them.
We all know that there are people with disabilities, we see them daily.
But by having no direct contact with them, we are not aware of the problems they face every day.
How hard it is for them to get on a bus, find a job, take the subway, or cross the street.
It's true that there is an increasing social responsibility regarding the inclusion of people with disabilities.
But I think it's not enough yet.
I think change should come from within us.
First, by leaving behind the indifference towards them, and then by respecting the rules that do take them on account.
They are few, but they exist.
I cracked a blind man's head open: El Pulga.
I can assure you these 2 teams also opened mine, because they taught me how you have to get out there and play every game in the beautiful championship that life is.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good afternoon and thanks to the organization for the invitation.
What brings me here is a matter I have been keen on for a long time, it is health.
In particular, global health has had great advances thanks to the technology incursion in the past decades.
But it seems that unfortunately these benefits have not been shared fairly in all areas of health. In the case of restorative dentistry which is what I want to talk about today, technology may has become a barrier due to certain features such as the high costs or the difficulty of accessibility.
What has this sad reality entailed?
Well, in much of the world, even in the cities which are usually considered to be perhaps high technology rectors, all the manufacturing procedures of prostheses are handmade.
It involves a large amount of material, it is made totally dependent on very specialized skills and in some specific cases you need more than three or four appointments for the elaboration of a single prosthetic device.
Or even worse, you need more than three people to be able to manufacture a prosthesis.
That's something that might seem inconceivable and many of us do not know when we are out of this circle.
But it turns out that it has not a very good impact for investment in time nor costs.
Starting from this problem, a team of Venezuelans and - I feel very proud to say - and me, we co-founded a company which has a mission: to create a technology capable to design and manufacture dental prostheses with anatomical hyperealism, with highly functional features and esthetics in the dental surgery in ten minutes, and without human manual intervention.
It may sound a little bit complex but it turns out that the concept was relatively simple.
Basically we rely on multiple technologies that had something to give us according to our requirements, as for example mechatronics, or photogrammetry, among others.
And, in parallel, we were also developing one of the largest digital data library today in the world of dental anatomical structures and of prosthetic fields, to join them so that we can establish our four main pillars, which are artificial intelligence, 3D printing, nanotechnology, and robotics.
Our technology has an intraoral scanner capable of capturing high-definition images which are sent to our restorative software, guided by an artificial intelligence and able to design and manufacture, relying on 3D printing, the dental prostheses according to the specific requirements of a particular patient.
And a very important thing too, supported by nanotechnology: we were able to manufacture the materials according to the current dental requirements.
All these entrepreneurial processes, of creation and generation of new ideas may start with some uncertainty.
It also starts with a briefcase full of ideas.
But with great effort, we can go on achieving whatever we want.
It's not easy to say this, but I assure you that if you are able to diagnose some kind of problem and you may have an idea to fix it as long as you seek to support yourself in some kind of resource, you may be able to break the paradigms of science that always set out certain barriers and maybe you could be able to contribute something to humankind.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Many times the greatest wish of boys and girls who have some intellectual disability is to be independent. To be autonomous, do not depend on anyone throughout their life.
In fact, very recently, a young woman who has Down syndrome told me that her dream was to travel alone in the subway, from Congreso station to Plaza de Mayo, from one terminal to another, on her own.
Technology, little by little, is allowing these dreams to come true.
How? Starting with education,
because it let us respect their times, respect their needs.
Today, by the hand of virtual reality and artificial intelligence we are being able to simulate situations with these children that they will live later on.
From a visit to the doctor or the dentist, to even more complex things, like, for example, undergoing a surgery.
So when it actually happens, they are prepared to react.
This is very important.
Being able to predict facts is very important, not only for them but also for their environment.
What's more, there are already some initial prototypes of clothes, particularly jackets, that are able to predict through sensors -- You know, those tantrums kids with autism sometimes throw?
Well, the jackets are able to predict them to help them before they happen.
Social networks. Today, through the networks, kids are coming out of isolation.
They can share their experiences.
Moreover, they are proving fundamental for the inclusion of people with disabilities in the workforce. Something that, by the way, is increasing worldwide.
Finally, with the advent of the so-called smart homes, today we are beginning to think that they, as adults, will be able to live alone.
The Internet of Things, this that we hear about enables the design of devices that prevent accidents, that facilitate communication, making it even easier for the management of the several aspects of a house.
Attitude to integrate all people in a society.
That is inclusion.
The good news is that through technology, we are going to make it.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
96.
96 is the number of shoes a Mexican man will purchase in a lifetime.
Unfortunately, most of those shoes won't be made in Mexico.
Though the first pair is.
It is made with much love by his mom, by his grandma, or the aunts who knitted them for their children.
Then we buy our children those high-quality Spanish shoes or those baby sneakers that are the latest trend.
Once our children start secondary school, we'll buy them that pair of shoes "that all their friends have."
And from that moment, it will remain engraved in their minds that a brand is worth more than a product.
Later on we'll buy them a pair of leather shoes, Italian perhaps, for our graduation ceremony and doubtless a couple of job interviews.
And so it goes on, until we buy a comfy pair when we are old.
According to the INEGI figures for 2013, in round terms, there are 60 million men in Mexico.
If those 60 million men bought 10 of the 96 pairs locally made, it would mean that we would have, after dividing by 60 years, 10 million pairs a year bought on the home market.
It would be a great help to the Mexican economy,
not to mention that Mexican shoemakers would give it a vote of thanks.
As a shoemaker, I concern myself with shoes constantly,
and would like you to take a look at your closet.
You'll realize what you have there: Chinese shoes, Italian ones, maybe some Spanish, but few Mexican.
Even right now, if you look at the shoes you have on your feet, few, unfortunately, will be Mexican.
Let's change that.
Let's try to change that.
As a shoemaker I deal with shoes, and with those items closely related to them: socks.
This piece of cotton and elastic, that for some is a necessary evil, has taught me many important things.
I've seen how my grandpa used socks to make a fan belt for his car
- I don't know if you know this, in grandpa's day fans were belt-driven,
but that's no longer the case.
Also, a friend of mine stuffed ice into a sock, and then put it on his head to alleviate a hangover.
We could use them as weapons, to protect us from the cold, or even use them as a basketball ball.
I'm José Luis Rocha, Mexican, from León, Guanajuato, but I now live here in this area by the side of a lady, whom I took off guard for a few minutes when she said "yes" to marrying me.
23 years later, I am thankful to this region, and I am very proud to be Mexican. And as a Mexican, I like sayings a lot.
And their contradictions because once they tell me: "The early bird catches the worm," or "The sun is not hurried by early risers," sleep on a little longer.
One of my favorites: "In lack of beauty, resort to accessories and charm."
But seriously, one that comes to me often now is: "Adapt or die."
As a shoemaker, I am an entrepreneur by necessity, I've had many businesses that I've started up because I've needed to eat, right?
I need to be able to keep my girl.
She wants to eat and buy things.
I've had import-export companies, I've run transport services, I've run water distribution companies, I've been a marketing specialist;
Just like the sock, right?
And this is a good idea for everyone: we can all do more than one thing, we all have that capacity within ourselves.
The idea is clear: my new shoe company is destined to conquer the biggest market in the world, which is the USA.
And I want to do it by making shoes designed by a Mexican, made in Mexico, manufactured with Mexican raw materials and, in this case, the hands of artisans from Guanajuato.
I invite you to buy Mexican products.
They are worth it, they are well-made, and the quality is there.
Remember, "Made in Mexico" means is well made.
Thank you.
(Applause)
In Mexico, we spend seven hours a week watching football, three more playing it, and only 17% of Mexicans have ever attended an art, science or cultural exhibition.
Dramatic, don't you think?
Drastic.
Very well, the reason for this - according to investigations conducted at San Miguel Totolapan - is that football is easily accesible, and easy to have. We just need a ball, players and a simple goal post, made of sticks and stones.
In my town we play street football, with dirt and dust, yet it's good football. Someday we'll create a third division team.
I am from San Miguel Totolapan, in the state of Guerrero, Tierra Caliente region and very hot indeed, sometimes 100ºF in the shade.
In my community we are 3,500 inhabitants. We are encompassed within the 10 most marginalized municipalities of the state. Being one of the most messed up 10 in Guerrero means being one of the most poverty stricken states nationally.
All in all, life is beautiful in my town, hard, but beautiful. All this begun with a story of "whys", of questions and answers.
When I was a 5 year old at the kindergarten, my teachers were unable to answer some questions. like why flowers had stems, and in the stems there were leafs aligned in symmetry.
I later found out that it is a Fibonacci succession.
Little by little I realized that there are two types of people:
Those who ask questions and those who don't.
Fortunately I'm in the first group, or at least I believe I am.
At 11, already in elementary school, I was about to be the flag bearer, part of the flag escort, a huge honor.
Unfortunately, during that time, something happened in my family, my father left.
But in return, he left behind two big treasures: My family and books, a library.
There I found answers to my own questions.
Since then, my favorite book is Physics by Paul Tippens.
After that, I even had to work, as a bricklayer, plumber, farmer, grocer, in a vet, at a stationary shop and a corner store.
One day, with my uncle Poncho the plumber, a bit of a drunk by the way, I started asking why he used a hose with water, to level from one point to the other. He could not answer. He said he didn't care why, he would just do it because it was useful.
Delving into it, I later read in that book, which had a beautiful blue cover, that there was something called air pressure.
Yes, each of us is carrying a load of air mass, as if life loads were not already enough.
Gradually, developing myself in that field, I achieved small things.
In junior high I started competing in all subjects: Spanish, History, Sports. I loved to learn. Every afternoon I would scatter my books over my bed, or the bed mat of the living room, and devour them eagerly. In one of the contests I made it out of my town, and went to Arcelia city. I earned first place among 8 junior high schools.
Then I went to the regional competitions, I came in forth place.
Not bad, right?
Bad if there were only four participants,
which was the case. I competed again at a state level in my second year, and in my last year I won a beautiful title: Champion of champions, there I competed with private, technical, federal and state high schools. A junior high schools gathering. I was first out of nine, and won that title.
After a teacher's advice I entered "Prepa 6", high School. made my entrance exam and was accepted. The "posh" 6, High school 6 of UNAM. It wasn't easy. It took me two hours getting to school, spend eight pesos in every bus. I was even discriminated, some people learned that my grandpa spoke Náhuatl, and I was called a rustic hillbilly, "Tizoc" [a Mexican indigenous character], but instead of feeling small, I rose proudly.
My mom, with dialysis and kidney failure, made me strong, made me build myself upon hard work and dedication. During my second year in high school I learned about a project, called Youngsters for Investigation, where you could develop a project during the school cycle, and if the project abstract was among the best, you could make a summer stay with a researcher at UNAM.
You know what I did?
A reaserch on Luffa Cylindrical: a loofah, the ones we use to take a bath. The point was to develop a cream to relieve skin burns,
to find the substrate.
Guess what. We made it.
The aim is to start a family business in my community. A green business, and let people know that they can themselves sow useful plants and vegetables, and gain benefits for them and others. That is how I came to the Physics Institute, where I chose Dr Efrain Chávez Lomelí as my adviser. I chose him because he was a Chávez, just as the only UNAM dean at Tierra Caliente: Dr Ignacio Chavez Sánchez, so, if he was a Chavez,
he was surely good,
and he has been good indeed.
It's been two years already. After the 4 week summer stay, he invited me to continue attending. In one of those visits, I made a proposal for something new.
We had worked with big things already, like particle accelerators; those million dollar machines.
Those with which you can make transmutations, that is, transform a chemical element into another.
I wanted to build one. Small enough to bring it to my town, and show my people how to spread science. To show my peers, the youngsters, that things like these can change our lives, and that we will not always be in the dust, that there can be a better future.
That is how we started, and 8 months and 23 days later, we built the cheapest particle accelerator in the world.
To create a particle accelerator per se, we first needed a source of particles, a particle acceleration system. A particle optical-electronic focus and yes, a particle detector.
That is how we made it. In highschool I would bring a box with PVC, aluminum, wires and cables. They called me the freak of the hallway, because I would go everywhere with my box. So after several months, we finally built the particle accelerator, an it is like football. You can go anywhere and find someone playing football, because it is all-terrain, it is all over the place. The aim was to make it easy to carry, something portable: if Pedrito won't come to the particle accelerators, the particle accelerators must come to Pedrito.
An interdisciplinary team emerged: engineers, physicists and chemists.
And as our particle source a sort of thread was used to create the Edison effect.
Current must pass through this filament, and that is how free electrons are created around: so we had electrons and particles, and when passing current through an aluminum system we created a field towards which electrons are attracted.
Maybe you recall it from your high school days: opposing charges approach, similar charges repel. If we translate it into soap operas, then bad girls get good guys, right?
So all this remains vacuum-encapsulated. Well, sort of: 4.8 Torr, 5 mm of Mercury, a partial vacuum. That is how we created the accelerator, with effort but also with a very special ingredient: Mexican wit. Reusing or simply picking what others call waste. that is Mexican style: whenever we could with whatever we had. And Mexicans are quite good for that.
This aroused in me the urge to help my people, and do more. Then came Science without Borders, a Non-profit foundation with two objectives: spreading science and culture, and most important, supporting young project developers.
Maybe not just accelerators, yet, all based on one idea: Every youngster who keeps studying, is a double gain for the country.
Is one less soldier for drug lords and violence - I can tell, I'm from Guerrero - and one more for Mexico's development. I have other projects.
Like X rays with adhesive tape.
MIT did it already
and we made it in Mexico on April, it needs more development, but we are getting there.
In Taxco, in Guerrero we are developing fuels, with plants that can grow in cold or warm places.
All Mexican style.
At the end of the day we achieved what we wanted, making things transportable, real all-terrain science.
Carrying all stuff in suitcases
or in backpacks, right?
So here it is: The worlds most affordable particle accelerator
(Applause) Thank you. (Applause)
Everybody knows that to save a file we need to click on this icon, this one.
But fewer people continue to know why.
An average teenager has never seen this object in real life, and for those of us who have worked with floppy disks at some point of our lives, we stopped using the word about... 15 or 20 years ago?
Usually, forgetting or no longer using some words is a sign of low literacy, but there is one case in which burying words means progress: Technology.
Nowadays, it would be absurd to loose my wedding pictures due to a fogged film or to loose my music because the CD got scratched.
Technology is constantly burying words to give space to new concepts and give us better tools.
But there is a word that technology wants to forever bury.
Remember the helplessness of a broken cassette?
Or the frustration of a ruined floppy disk?
What feeling remained after that?
Resignation.
There was nothing left to do.
Resignation is contrary to development, and technology couldn't have made so much development if developers and users were resigned.
Each time we stop saying CD, cassette or disquette, we are also leaving behind the resignation that came when these objects failed.
Why we not do the same?
Why not bury words of the systems that no longer work?
Economic, political, social or school system?
Because we are resigned.
Today, I want to share how to use technology to collectively fight our resignation.
During Mexican presidential elections in the year 2000, I went with my grandma to vote.
She always spoke of how much she despised corruption in our country, she complained a lot of Mexican political system
and that she would like to see another party in power.
Yet, I saw she was about to vote for the same party she always complained about.
I stopped her and questioned her, she didn't say much instead she gave me the pencil and told me to vote as I wished.
I know these decisions should not rest on 11 year olds.
But I voted for another party and it worked.
The next day I woke up with the news that that party had won the elections and I realized that my grandma had learned to be resigned to Mexican political system.
Mexico had been resigned for decades, and up to that moment even I was taking over that behavior.
I'm not here to criticize our grandparents generation.
They grew up in a much more pragmatic world than ours where fighting systems was a huge endeavor usually a violent one, and communication among millions was practically impossible.
But what's our excuse?
Today we can communicate with millions of people through the Internet, yet we remain resigned.
I don't know how many of you remember or want to remember this.
(Sound of an Ethernet connection) Ugh!
Anyway, during the 90s when both CDs and floppies were still in our vocabulary, this sound would tell us we had an Internet connection, an Ethernet cable connection.
That meant that every call of that aunt that was constantly calling home, resulted in a lost connection.
I mean, truth is that our activities were not that important, we would chat for hours with people we just saw, it took us days to download music while collectively discovering what we could do with the Internet.
Figuring that out took us a whole generation.
We were amazed with Napster legal scandals, with those mail spells that would come true if sent to at least 10 people, I can't believe my mom keeps sending me those.
Internet opened the door to an immediacy we had never experienced before and which we didn't let go.
Messages travel overseas in seconds.
Search engines with massive databases and free long-distance calls were the beginning of that "something" that promised to shake up the world,
and when we use it for that purpose, it had.
In 2011 Wael Ghonim started the Arab Spring in Facebook.
In 2015 Gustavo Huchin, a Mayan beekeeper from Campeche stopped Monsanto from sowing transgenic soy in his community, through Change.org.
Also in 2015 a group of designers from California crowdsourced 20 million dollars through Kickstarter, to produce their own smart watch and competing with Apple.
They launched it a moth before the official AppleWatch release.
When we organize and act collectively through the Internet, we are unstoppable.
We are bigger than any business or government.
But we need to decide if we will use that technology for that or for chasing Pokemons.
Personally, I decided that my career impact should be a social impact, I want to create social impact so I started working for the private sector, I was in big businesses, yet I realized that the lack of budget was always an issue.
Then, I moved to public sector and you can imagine how things were over there.
Lack of budget was even worst and I noticed that the projects my team and I proposed, were always limited by the lack of resources.
What is worst, I became accustomed to that, just as we get used to the ever increasing prices of gas, though we were promised otherwise, o being charged high interest rates.
I got used, like my grandma had gotten used to the Mexican political system.
The day I realized I was resigned, I quit.
The next day I panicked because I had no plan, no job.
But my goal remained the same: Create social impact.
I decided I would use my work to collect funds in Mexico, budget for Mexican development.
But then I wondered, what is development?
Where do I get that?
What kind of investment a developing country does?
and the answer was simple: Research.
Research in science, academia, journalism, humanities, technology.
Research is the tool with which societies spot their problems and figure out ways to solve them.
It's the human species method to gather information about our surroundings.
For that reason businesses and governments have divisions, Research and development department.
One stems from the other.
I also found that USA annually invests
400 billion dollars in research, while Mexico invests 6 billion dollars.
This is one of the main causes of our brain drain, mainly into the USA,
where there are more than 12 thousand Mexicans with Ph.D. that work, live and develop there, and not here.
12,000 Mexicans with Ph.D. that we prepared in our universities, paid with our taxes, sourced from our work.
This number accounts for more than 6 years of Ph.D. graduates, from all our universities put together.
Why do we allow this?
For two reasons; first is we are not aware, or we don't want to be;
second is that we believe that systems are so big we cannot bring them down.
But careful, both reasons are filled with resignation.
Tim Minchin, an Australian artist, says that to evolve as a society we must individually take our beliefs, and hit them with a bat all the time.
Because most of the arguments that remain alive in our society aren't alive because they are true, but because of our inability to validate new arguments.
So let's make an exercise.
Let's validate the following argument.
Today, with the current technology, we don't need to fight against a system.
We don't need to clash with anyone.
The Internet allows us to propose our own alternatives and put them to work without needing permission.
Once your alternative is supported by a collectivity, traditional system will look at you because you're taking their market, like it happened with Airbnb and Marriott.
Or because you found a better way of doing something, like Uber, or simply because you deliver better results, like Amazon and Google.
In terms of research, traditional system is our government.
It collects our taxes and decides how much budget goes to each of its dependencies.
Unfortunately, year after year, decade after decade, Mexican government has decided to give insufficient budget to research.
Maybe we are now thinking that this has no direct impact in our lives.
But remember this is resignation speaking out.
Lack of research has direct impact in our economy, our job creation and incomes, our global competitiveness and more importantly in our health and quality of life.
In 1980, Mexico and South Korea economies were usually compared, for being similar as moderately populated countries allies and highly dependent on the USA.
Back then, Mexican economy was
four times bigger than South Korean one and our GDP per capita was 124 percent higher than theirs.
But in the 80s Korea decided to invest in educating its population towards research and development.
Today, South Korea GDP doubles ours.
Its economy is one percentage point higher in global economy than it was in the 80s.
While Mexican economy is almost a percentage point below.
South Korean research budget increases an annual average of 13 percent and in Mexico, only in the period between 2015 and 2016, the government reduced 31 percent the Conacyt budget.
Today, South Korea is a tech industry leader like chips, screens, smartphones, which we all use at least once a day.
And Mexico?
Where we are going to end if our government does nothing, it showed that they'll do nothing.
Why not becoming active and do it ourselves?
Why not building a platform through which we can tell what subjects we want to support?
What investigations can benefit us?
Investing in Mexican talent with Mexican resources.
That is why my team and I created Investigga, a platform (Applause) Yeah, that's it!
Investigga is a crowd-funding platform to finance research.
That way a scientist, a scholar, a tech developer, a journalist, can tell us about the projects they have in mind and want to research, and the potential direct positive impact in our lives.
If a chemist wants to clean the city water, that we know gets polluted in pipelines, wouldn't we support that?
If a physicist wants to install oxygen machines in our cities so that we can breathe better quality air and fight climate change from our cities wouldn't we all support it?
If a journalist knows that there is a corruption case he wants to research, but nobody wants to fund it due to a conflict of interest, wouldn't the population support it to know the truth?
In the past, overthrowing a system was necessary to establish a new one and that was called a revolution, but now we can create evolution from technology, proposing our own systems and uploading them on the Internet.
When we are collectively supported, we can make those useless systems disappear because we turn them obsolete.
Ask Blockbuster how it was with Netflix.
Investigga is a proposal from my team and I to fight our brain drain, and become accountable of our own quality of life, and through our pro-activeness get the government to listen; not from complaints.
But what are your proposals?
We need thousands of such tools working to create a change in society.
Good news is that we aren't thousands but millions.
It's OK to sign petitions and thumb up things on the Internet, what's not OK is to let that be the sole thing we do.
Let's not allow resignation make us feel that we already helped by sharing a post.
These superficial ways of participating in goodwill causes must not fulfill us or they will become a huge social disease.
The only thing worse than a society that doesn't contribute, is a society that mistakenly believes it's contributing.
So what if we all work collectively and coordinate all together through technology and start building up from every corner tools that can assemble our collectiveness into tangible actions to solve problems wherever we want to create development.
What if we start demanding better practices from those in charge of our resources?
Or take over the administration if we are more capable.
Investigga and all proposals from a coordinated society must focus on that, so that once and for all, together here and now coordinated and collectively let's burry our resignation.
Thank you so much.
(Applause)
Emotion is a complex set of chemical and neuronal responses characterized by a stimulation or disturbance that predisposes to an organized response.
The immediate result to this response is a temporary change in the state of the body itself.
The final result is placing the organism in circumstances suitable for its survival and well-being.
These responses are automatic.
(Stomping and clapping) Sorry for not knowing the script by heart. It's not that I haven't prepared the talk, but I have a bit of dyslexia, a bit of attention deficit, and, in fact, it is very difficult for me, well, it's the cool thing to say, especially if you're an artist, right?
So I find it really difficult to memorize words, or reading, concentrating.
The only thing I memorize are movements and emotions.
But I would like to read this text from Pascal Quignard for you, that says: "Music doesn't dwell in the most beautiful instruments.
Neither it dwells in the worst of them.
The most suitable instruments for music are, without a doubt, those that move but are perishable, like the bodies that involve mankind."
(Stomping) (Heavy breathing) Purity: Quality of pure.
That one free and exempt of any mixture of another thing, not including any condition, exception or restriction of any period or that is exempt of moral imperfections.
(Heavy breathing) Well, now that I see myself... (Heavy breathing) it could be considered that I'm an impure flamenco dancer, furthermore, full of moral imperfections, (Laughter) (Heavy breathing) That's right.
Could anyone in the room tell me if they are morally perfect, or if they are pure?
Raise your hand, please.
(Silence) Just what I was afraid of.
(Tapping) Take it, take, ah.
The problem for me is that women, for example, are supposed to shake their hips from side to side, you know?
Wiggling, seducing with movement, but I don't get it right, to be honest... (Laughter) I prefer to move it forwards and backwards, more like men do, or should do. (Laughter)
Well, sometimes I just mix it, too.
(Laughter) (Stomping) (Laughter) Flamenco dancer!
"Boquerona!" A native of Málaga.
Boxer, creator, body, woman, impure, passionate, frisky, bland, annoying when necessary, shameless, imperfect, incorrect, unstoppable! (Clapping) Less gypsy than an olive tree!
(Stomping) I do not evolve.
I am.
(Applause)
A few weeks ago, I was dining with some colleagues in a fancy hotel restaurant in Buenos Aires.
While looking at the menu, I saw a well-known personality coming in who, some time ago, had consulted me about a treatment.
This person walked very close to our table and then sat at her table, but not only she ignored me, she also asked to be sat somewhere else.
Had I been a doctor, a cardiologist, or even better, a neurologist, this person would have come to say hello immediately.
My being a psychiatrist made it unacceptable for her to do so.
Because if you go to a psychiatrist, you are crazy.
Never mind that psychiatrists treat other conditions such as depression and anxiety issues.
Psychiatry is no glamorous.
People have no problem talking about heart problems, ulcers, even about the prostrate, but no one will mention they go to the psychiatrist.
At most, you will know someone goes to the psychoanalyst, or that someone has panic attacks.
Panic attacks are very cool.
When I was seven, I knew I would be a doctor.
By ten, that I was going to dedicate to something related to the brain.
I went into a neuropsychiatric hospital for the first time when I was 20, when I went to the old neuroanatomy lab at the Borda Hospital run by my professor, Diego Luis Outes.
A wise man who taught me that the brain has life, and that we have to study it to understand a healthy human mind
as well as one that is not. Nowadays, neurosciences are the rage, but back then, to become a psychiatrist, to study the brain went frankly counter to the norm. Right now, I am fortunate to work at the Institute of Neuroscience at the Favaloro Foundation and at the Cognitive Neurology Institute - INECO.
They are our neuroscience headquarters.
But I am not coming here to talk about me.
I would like to talk to you about stigma,
about the rejection and misunderstanding that even today, people with mental disorders suffer.
To admit that you see a psychiatrist is like saying you were in jail, it is not something you tell just anyone.
It conveys a social stain, a stigma.
A stigma is how slaves were branded in order to know their condition.
If my being a psychiatrist caused that behavior in this well-known personality, what would be the rejection endured by those people who suffer mental disorders?
Statistics tell us one in four people here present has some type of mental disorder: a psychosis, bipolar disorder, depression, OCD, or some anxiety disorder.
This is applicable to the workplace, clubs, Thursday's soccer team; to each group.
However, it would be very difficult for us to find out.
Because people with mental disorders know that if their condition is known, they are immediately subject to rejection and isolation.
The stigma is also transferred to the patients' families.
Relatives avoid speaking about the subject or use euphemisms.
We know that relatives of people with severe mental disorders get sick and depressed as a result of the suffering and rejection produced by an environment that doesn't understand them.
The causes of the stigma are complex, but they have to do with fear and ignorance.
It is as if insanity were dangerous and contagious.
Serious studies demonstrate that people with psychosis are no more dangerous than the rest of the population.
Andreas Lubitz was not crazy nor did he commit the mass murder of Germanwings flight because of depression.
He was a psychopath who knew exactly what he was doing.
Insanity is not contagious either. Although we don't know all the theory, we know that a psychosis is due to a complex interaction between genetic and environmental factors which affect the normal development of the nervous system from gestation, and that certain environmental factors favor the development of a psychosis but are not the cause.
We know that for people predisposed to psychosis, situations like living in a big city, being an immigrant or a child of one, and consuming toxics like marihuana increase the chances this predisposition activates.
These socioenvironmental factors need to be seen not as the cause of the psychosis but as the ideal environment for the psychosis to occur.
When you think of immigrants, don't think of your brother-in-law in L.A. who has a barbecue business or fixes computers.
Better yet, think of the Somalis, Jamaicans, and Asians who live in the poor neighborhoods of London and Amsterdam where this phenomenon was studied.
This situation produces something called the minority group position:
situations that help circumstances of domination, abuse, and social failure.
All of this favor a predisposed person to manifest his psychosis.
The way it would work is stress acting over a vulnerable brain due to a neurodevelopment disorder.
Let's think about bulling.
People who are going to develop severe mental problems usually have a behavioral pattern different from others.
They are timid and withdrawn, have other interests, and are not good at sports, which encourages abuse.
One of the most frequent forms of psychosis is schizophrenia.
As time goes by, the diagnosis of schizophrenia, has become very stigmatized because it is believed to be incurable and with a profound decline in performing everyday tasks. However, this is not always the case.
This stigma is so important, that in some Asian countries, like Japan, they have changed its name.
We ask ourselves if we have to follow this path, if this is going to provide some benefit to people with this disease.
To complete the picture, people with schizophrenia and also those with bipolar disorder have lower life expectancy, and get sick often, especially of cardio pathologies.
Even more, the health system takes very little care of them, and this happens all over the world.
During the 20th and 21st centuries, society tried to solve the problem of psychosis by locking patients up in institutions.
These people spent so much time locked up that in some countries, they were given postcards like the ones you see so they would write to their relatives as if they were on vacation.
Due to lack of specific treatments the only thing to do was to wait until the crisis that caused the intervention ceased.
There is an additional problem: some of these psychosis severely affect people's capacity to fend for themselves, and this explains why they remained hospitalized for so long. It was not due to the psychiatrists' malevolence.
Confinement does not cure psychosis but generates prejudice against psychiatrists.
We are not someone who wants to help patients; we are the ones who want to lock them up.
This prejudice is shown in movies a lot.
In movies, the psychiatrist is never logical and empathetic.
He is either mean, authoritarian, and cruel, or is someone naive and somewhat silly.
Think of the psychiatrist in the trilogy "Millennium." He was a sadistic abuser.
However, in the past few years, we have seen this change dramatically.
We are better at diagnosing, and to be a psychiatrist today means to articulate complex medical, neuroscientific, neuropharmacological, psychologic, and psychotherapeutic knowledge.
However, diagnoses are still done by talking to patients; we don't have an electrocardiogram.
The most expensive experiment in the history of humanity, the Hadron Collider, or God's machine, was done only to prove a physics hypothesis.
Similarly, neuroscience carries out complex experiments everyday that give us little bits of information that allow us to advance our knowledge of how our brain functions and deteriorates.
By the end of the 50s, we had anti-psychotics.
For the first time, people with psychosis have their symptoms under control.
This way, they can return home, but not everything was easy.
As we can see, the great psychiatric revolution came thanks to scientific research not to psychiatric prejudice.
Medicines like anti-psychotics, antidepressants, or lithium carbonate, which were discovered soon after penicillin, greatly changed the lives of hundreds of people who suffered mental disorders and improved their lives.
The anti-psychiatry movement hurts the lives of people with serious mental disorders because it misinforms.
It started in the the late 60s, it claims mental disorders don't exist, that they are a way to deal with an ailing society.
Also, there are some who claim that mental disorders were created by the pharmaceutical industry in order to sell more drugs.
Ridiculous.
People no longer spend years locked up waiting to get better.
Treatments allow that patients recuperate while close to their families.
However, in spite of all the advances, people with mental disorders continue to have difficulties to get a job and to be taken seriously. And their status continues to be misunderstood.
People with mental disorders sometimes behave strangely,
but not all the time.
We also behave strangely sometimes.
Think about yourselves at a soccer game. Having a normally dignified nature, you become enraged because an unfair foul was called on your favorite player; you would not accept to be called a crazy person or a loony because you fell under the spell of soccer.
For the most part, this person acts normally. People with mental disorders do too.
However, we have no qualms in calling them with all kinds of pejorative terms: 'nutcase,' 'deranged, ' 'few peas short of a casserole, ' 'doesn't have all his dogs on one leash.' Take your pick.
Every time you talk about someone with mental disorder this way, you have one in four chances to unknowingly be causing pain to someone who is listening to you.
This is why I will ask you to acquire a few habits to become warriors against stigma.
First, do not label. Don't say that he is psychotic, bipolar, depressed, or has OCD.
People are not their diseases. That's why we tell them that they suffer them. I prefer to say that they fight them.
Second, do not be afraid of them. Do not cut them off.
Third, do not judge nor blame people with a metal disorder for having it.
No one tells someone with cancer, "You need to pump yourself up, throw away the chemo, and go for a walk in the sun, and you will you get better."
You would not do that.
Do not do it to people with mental disorders.
It is not as easy as you imagine; it is not easy at all, I can assure you.
To suffering you are adding incomprehension.
Fourth, do not offer advice.
Try to listen to them.
Try saying, for example,

"I know you are not well. Do you want
to talk? OK. Don't want to? OK, too."
Remember that a helping hand is worth more than a million words.
I leave you with this quote by Bill Clinton - I consider it very telling - "Mental illness is nothing to be ashamed of, but stigma and bias shame us all."
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Have you ever considered the possibility of opening up a restaurant?
(Audience) Yes!
EN: Very well. But surely none of you would have thought of doing it on the third floor of a building without an elevator in one of the poorest neighborhoods of the city.
I would not have thought of it, had it not been for the fact I was born in that building,
and that something extraordinary happened in that place.
I belong to a generation marked by a very deep economic crisis all Cubans knew as 'The Special Period.'
My professional life started back then.
Those were times of extreme shortages and daily difficulties. We all had experiences that, when we bring them back to mind, seem stories of survival after a supernatural event.
I had to ride my bicycle for 15 kms
to get to my workplace. I was working as an engineer at a research center of the electricity company, making 200 Cuban pesos a month. At the time, the exchange rate stood at 200 Cuban pesos to 1.30 dollar.
I realized I had to devise an alternative way to make a living.
And I talked to a young colleague who had a garage at home, and I told him: "Let's set up an electronics workshop,
we can surely do something."
The only problem was we had to get rid of some of tenants.
Everybody knows that, at that time, everybody was looking for alternatives to make ends meet.
Little by little, our business started to prosper.
We came up with the idea of manufacturing current converters
because power cuts lasted over 12 hours a day and our converters would make it possible
for people to turn on a TV set, several lights, a fan.
And believe me, I have many friends who still keep them and see them as a blessing.
Little by little, our business prospered.
We were very happy. However, when we least expected it, a government decision expressly banned all professionals from working in state and non-state sectors simultaneously.
That was how, all of a sudden, we found that we had to do something else.
Shortly afterwards, a friend from the Cuban Film Institute made me an offer. He was trying to find a location for a Cuban film
which was going to be directed by Cuban filmmakers Tomás Gutiérrez Alea and Juan Carlos Tabío and based on a short story by local writer Senel Paz.
According to him, the house where I was born met all the characteristics of the place they were looking for.
Since I had no other alternative, no other option, but to help my friend, I asked them to come.
When Titón and Tabío saw my place, they immediately decided my house would be turned into the location for 'La Guarida' in the film "Strawberry and chocolate."
I had no idea what was going on with the film, I was unaware of its success;
I only knew it had been awarded a prize at the Havana Festival.
But one day, I was at home with my wife and a group of tourists showed up
hoping to see the place they had seen in the film.
I couldn't believe what was going on, but without giving it a second thought, I asked my wife: "Why don't we make use of the house to earn a living?
People would certainly come with high expectations, hoping to see the place they had seen in the film, and we could make a living.
I was amazed to hear that she not only thought it was a good idea but also supported it right away.
We immediately got down to work.
We figured it out as follows: It wouldn't just be a restaurant;
we're going to turn it into a cultural project.
We're going to build a space where people can come and find the best of Cuban art,
and where they can enjoy good local Cuban music.
Let's try to change the image of the unfortunate, Cuban catering industry,
an old image of Cuban cuisine.
Let's ask our artist friends to help us out and show their work here.
Thus, step by step, it all began.
Private enterprise was starting but full of obstacles;
there were many limitations.
We couldn't hire the staff we needed, they had to be close relatives.
We couldn't serve what we wanted,
we couldn't offer seafood or beef.
We could only accommodate 12 customers.
I vividly remember one time when we had a group of 20 visitors from Chicago and at the same time a group of inspectors from the Ministry of Labor showed up.
The doormen warned us, and we asked the visitors to wait in the room available next door
don't move and remain silent.
(Laughter) The problem came when the inspection took longer than expected,
and we were told that one of the tourists was not feeling well, he was a diabetic.
Thank goodness, we are in a country that is strong in baseball. I had no choice but to throw a Coca-Cola from one balcony to the other.
(Laughter) Fortunately, nothing happened.
When the inspectors left, the visitors came out, applauding in excitement.
I remember one of them told us that this had reminded him of the Prohibition in the United States. (Laughter) That experience and many other things that happened gave us food for thought. In the summer of 2009, we came to the conclusion that we could not go on working within such a narrow legal framework.
We took a tough decision to close down La Guarida.
I decided to leave Cuba with my wife and children for the United States. We were about to open a new business
at a place that suited our needs, and it was then when we received a call from one of the managers who had been working with us for several years.
He told us that, all of a sudden, the entire legal framework for businesses like mine in the private sector had changed.
We were not only going to be able to hire all the staff we would require, but we would also be allowed to offer what any restaurant is expected to offer
and, above all, the 12-seat limit had been removed
and we would maximize the space we had available.
At that moment, we had to face a difficult dilemma: giving up all the advantages and opportunities of what living in a country like the United States entailed - where our children were already feeling at ease,
going to great schools -
or, on the other hand, coming back to live in Havana.
We realized that many things had remained unfinished,
especially La Guarida, which was, by no means, a personal project.
Many people had identified with it,
and felt they really belonged to it.
Most of our staff members were unemployed.
Finally, we decided to come back,
not only to reopen La Guarida, but also to finally embark on projects we had put away for years.
That was how the project "State of mind" came up:
refurbishing the building, along with all our neighbors, where "Strawberry and chocolate" had been filmed.
In our view, it is a building with a huge cultural heritage, of social and historical value.
The idea of the project was to restore the communal parts of the building with the support of our neighbors
and we also had the intention to set an example for other businesspeople like us to understand that we live in a marvelous city,
and that it's important to intervene not only in our private but also public spaces as much as we can.
We understand that it is worthwhile
making an effort, one way or another, to save the beautiful city where we live.
Man 1: We live in one of the most wonderful cities of the world.
And you still have time to see some things before it collapse and be swallowed up by crap.
Man 2: Don't overexaggerate, man! There are so many things...I don't know--
Man 1: They are letting it crumble, that is unquestionable!
Man2: We are a small country, with everything against us.
Man 1: Yes, but it seems they don't care.
They don't give a damn!
Man 2: Some people care. You and I do.
Let us, once and for all, stamp out
the stale, deeply rooted notion that since nobody owns anything, nobody is accountable for anything.
You and I do care.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)

Hi! What a mess, right?
I'd like to start off thinking and analyzing those little things that can change our life. Sometimes they can go unseen, those things could go from songs, books or even a casual encounter with someone. In my case what really changed my life was a quote, and if it wasn't for it I wouldn't be standing here before all of you. When I had chosen to study design and visual communications, - my family is a little traditionalist
they're all doctors, or lawyers - So in all family gatherings, my cousins would mock me, "How is scissors and crayons class coming along?"
So in a way, these comments would discourage me from believing that I could actually find a way to these dreams that I had. One day, I found this quote, "Whoever devotes his life to what he loves, will be forever condemned to success."
Strong statement, right?
But beyond a quote, this synthesis of thoughts made me believe that all that I wanted to do could actually be possible.
Later on I found out that going deeper into that phrase focusing on dedication, on commitment, passion, in giving yourself to a dream. I later on realized that a quote wasn't the only thing that would keep me on the road, but to carry on. It's about dedication, I mean the phrase doesn't say, "He who does what he likes whenever he wants, will succeed." (Laughter)
I realized it was about determination, commitment and passion. It was about creating something everyday, being disciplined on a daily basis. Somehow I feel that us, artists or designers, we stigmatize ourselves, believing that it's society the one stigmatizing us.
I like to think that, "Every artist is first an amateur." Sometimes we dream too far. We have goals, "I want to be famous, I want to have a lot of money and a big house somewhere." But we don't seem to realize that fame and fortune aren't objectives but consequences.
I believe that it's very important to have our life goals very clear.
Also I gladly noticed that many guest speakers have also talked about having someone to look up to, some place to get to.
There's this actor, Bill Murray who says that if you are in a room and you're the smartest person there, it's time to switch room.
In a way I've always loved admiring great thinkers and one day I had a revelation.
I was in Pablo Neruda's house in Valparaiso in Chile, I found myself admiring this boat-like mansion, filled with memories from around the world and windows facing towards the ocean.
So I thought, "Wow! What an amazing thing
that such a great poet could build this."
As I went by, still fascinated, through the house, I finally reached the last floor and came across a text not quite about any creative procedure, but the process Pablo Neruda followed everyday
Everyday he would wake up and read for 2 hours then write for 3, then he would invite his friends for dinner, but made them wear costumes and pretend to be someone else.
Later on in the afternoon he would get back to writing. And he would do that everyday.
He had a determination that enabled him, to feed his motivation and inspiration every single day.
Little by little this made me realize, for instance, being an artist myself; imagine I wake up one day and my mom said, "You're getting up at 3 in the afternoon!"
"No mom, I'm an artist!" (Laughter)
These things keep creating stigmas against us.
Fortunately, my job allowed me to put together an add agency in Curitiba in Brazil, so every time I wanted to travel or I felt a little tired of work I'd visit some place over there I love
this story because the more I traveled, the more my mindset evolved and my work became better I was so motivated that ideas flowed and remained readily available for any project I was working on.
One day me and my friends went to the Florianopolis Island and we literally got lost in the jungle. We made our way through it fighting against anacondas. (Laughter) When we reached the end of the island, I found myself with the amazing view of a cabin in front of the sea.
So this thought "traveling is evolving", makes you sensitive, makes you see the world in a different way. For example...
I could've seen that cabin and thought, This cabin. (Laughter)
I could've thought, "What kind of hippies live there?"
"What are they doing so isolated from society?"
But this deal that I'm telling you, traveling is evolving, made me see beyond what my eyes could see. It made me think a little in the lives of those people In their families, the jobs they had to quit, and how it was all a consequence of a dream.
I'd like to think that those who lived there had the dream of waking up every day and have the ocean in front of them.
That same view made think of my life. It made me wonder if what I was doing today, was actually getting me closer to where I wanted to be tomorrow. Whether a place, a person or anything.
So this thing about traveling is amazing because it turns you into someone who appreciates the small details, who misses their family.
Not long ago I watched a movie, "Hector in the search for happiness," It's a psychiatrist who's highly unhappy with his life. He's married and all,
but one day he decides to start a journey around the world. I love this analogy that after going all around the globe, after reaching so far he realizes that the answer to his happiness was always there with him in his reality. So he goes back.
I hope you don't watch this movie because I just told you the end.
(Laughter) Traveling makes us sensitive, makes us miss our families, and then we come back as better human beings.
In other of my many trips, this time to Rio.
I was also tired of work and it was the Carnival, I said, I'll go to the Carnival and see how it goes
and many friends told me not to don't go, it will be so expensive and you won't find a hotel."
But I didn't care, I went and when I got there, I was like, "Dang! This really is chaos."
There were people everywhere, all drinking beer and I had no place to stay all hotels were full.
So I became good friends with the guy who sold caipirinhas, and asked him if he knew of a good, cute and cheap hotel. He said yes, then wrote down an address. "Ask a cab to take you there", he said.
So I took a taxi and turns out it took me to a favela, which actually had the best view of Rio.
I love to think how flexible this trip made me, it pushed me to improvise and bring out the best I had in me and in a way make the best of this trip.
Being in the Favela I realized that there were some other foreigners who got the good, cute and cheap deal as well.
(Laughter) So I met this German girl. We started talking and she asked me what I do. "I'm a photographer,"
"Let's see your pictures."
I show her my work and suddenly, she sees one and says, "That photo isn't yours."
I was like, "How could it not be mine?"
She says, "That photo isn't yours, you stole it or copied it from someone."
And I was like, "Why, what makes you think that?"
And she says, "I've seen this photo somewhere else."
So I showed her the full collection of the trip I made to the Sierra Queretana.
She was stunned with nothing else to say.
Then she said, "When I lived in Germany, I had the dream to travel around the world. My first stop was in Portugal.
Right after a really bad experience I was sitting on the computer, about to buy my ticket back to Germany, and in Stumble Upon I saw your photograph and that photo and that quote were strong enough to make me gather all my courage. So instead of buying a ticket back to Germany I bought it to Brazil."
So beyond bragging about this story, I love to think about our actions, its impact and how far the words we say can go.
You can say something good or bad and you don't know whose life you'll change.
A quote changed my life and maybe the thoughts I shared online, made people change their lives radically.
Going back to "To travel is to evolve," and how it makes us incredible human beings, more perceptive, more patient. It makes us care about strangers, it makes us want to start relationships. well, not relationships…
Conversations with strangers. (Laughter)
Well, relationships too.
Truth is, I don't know what happens when we travel, that we become better human beings.
I believe that here, everyone, or almost everyone has had the chance to travel and I'm sure many can feel identified with that feeling, late at night at your hotel or hostel, and the very next day you have to wake up really early, yet full of energy, you don't complain. You come and go and go up and down and everything is amazing.
We can all feel identified with this person we became. The question is has anyone remained this new person once they're back in their reality?
Because when we come back from a trip, from holidays, we go on Facebook and post, "Depressed, Back into reality." (Laughter)
And I think that's a huge problem, do we regress when we come back? [Going back is going backwards?]
This is the main aspect I'd love to share. If we have the ability to turn on the good life mindset, and endless energy attitude when we're in other countries; why can't we do that in our routines as well?
That's something that I've found myself practicing often now that I'm back in Mexico for a little while.
Nowadays, I have the fortune of living in the city of Queretaro. I live "a song and a half away" from Los Arcos Queretanos.
And I say "a song and a half away" because someone just reminded me how important is to measure life with something much more significant and personal than time.
So every day early morning I go running by Los Arcos, always at 5,30 I'm there running.
I'm not sure if it's for running or because I want to see the sunrise. I know, sounds too hippie and romantic, (Laughter) but truth is that every day I get to see this amazing event that we sometimes forget, right?
This down of a new day of which we can learn so much.
So one day I found myself admiring the sunrise, like a total hippie. (Laughter) I realized that there were tourists all around me, watching the sun rise as well, and they're drooling all over, they're recording or even taking selfies with the sun.
I noticed, and thought, "Wow, it's a blessing I'm not the only one who can really appreciate this."
And I look back and I also notice lots of passers-by, and it's like the early sunlight is caressing their faces but they don't turn around to see what's going on.
Not one stops and says "Wow, that's gorgeous." How fortunate to get to see this daily.
I think this is something that society teaches us nowadays we're always on a hurry, always on the go, and we're always getting late to meetings, events, everywhere.
Society keeps telling us that the best road is the shortest. I disagree, the best road is the most beautiful, or the one that make you happy.
I also believe a big deal of this is because we live too much in the future, so we stop appreciating the present. "I'm sure that when I get my studio or such things my work will be better." or "when I get such instrument, my music will be better."
Sometimes we stop living in the present believing the future will be better.
A couple months ago, I had a talk with a colleague from work. We started off talking about work issues.
but when we were done talking about work projects, he asked me, "Hey, what's up with your life?,
How do you travel so much?, What's next for you?."
I told him that I was focused to head over to Japan. I told him that not being able to picture how my life will be in Japan actually excites me a lot.
So an ordinary meeting related to work, turned into something motivational.
He suddenly said,"Hey that's awesome! When you're in Tokyo let's partner up and open a Mescal shop or something." (Laughter)
And I was like, "Sure!"
Then the talk turned a little sentimental,
he said, "You know what,
right now my job isn't great,
is just something provisional."
The talk went on until we went on our separate ways. Later I wrote him an email, "Hey, here's the information. Hopefully we can have these kind of conversations more often,
to keep motivating each other."
And he didn't reply.
He didn't even send
the job stuff or anything.
Little time went by, I sent another email, "Hey, I really need this, sorry to bug so much, but I really need that information."
And more time went by and still no answer.
After receiving no reply and with very little time left for the project, I called him at his office, and they told me he had passed away a few days before.
And I wish they had told me "You know what? He quit
his job to travel the world."
But such is life, so fragile and brief. We sometimes forget.
And the most ironic thing is that he died on an accident on his way to the job. We think so much in the future, we don't do our best to create a better present.
To wrap this up, I'd like to leave you with this traveler mindset. Let's make the most of every moment. [Make every moment worthy...] But not tomorrow, not on holidays. Right now. Life is too short not make the most of every moment.
Thank you so much.
(Applause)
(Zapotec) Good morning everyone, thanks for being here today, for sharing your morning at this beautiful space.
Good morning everyone, thanks for being here today, for sharing your morning at this beautiful space.
I am a daughter of an indigenous woman, and an indigenous man, to whom life and circumstances denied the opportunity to attend school.
My parents cannot read or write.
My mother had 10 children.
My elder sister was given for marriage at age 12, by age 13 she was a mom already.
At 31 my sister Claudia had 9 children.
But I cannot hold my parents accountable, because at that time they thought they were doing the right thing.
I come from a community called Quegolani one of the 570 municipalities of my state.
I am indigenous, my mother tongue is Zapotec.
I had to take over this language I am now using in order to learn my rights and obligations.
That is why I had to escape from that environment, with deep pain and sorrow, that day, more than 25 years ago, my heart ripped in two: one part remained there in the mountain 7 hours away from my state, home to my background, my essence, my roots, my tongue, my outfit; the other part is still with me,
To understand why my eyes could not grasp or why I did not like what I was seeing.
Why my mother was the first to wake up and the last to go to sleep?
Why I had to wake up with her?
I had to learn to make tortilla, bring firewood with the donkey, because that was what women were supposed to do.
I did not want that, by the way, when I left my homeland, there was no light, no highway; I used to sleep on the ground.
Had I known that sleeping in a bed was cool, I would have chosen that.
But to understand that, I first had to take over what I thought of as the tool to transform much of what hurt us: Education.
I had to wrest for education because life did not grant it to me.
I had to work in the morning and in the afternoon I studied high school.
I am a public accountant.
Time went by and I returned to my land. My mom stilled woke up at 3 am, to make tortilla, lime corrodes because from a previous day you have to prepare the nixtamal with limewater, then place three tortillas on the hotplate because if we fail to do this, it is said we are not women.
But I don't blame this environment.
It is the circumstance that must be hold responsible, the poverty and marginalization.
Not poverty and marginalization from [stomach], but from [head].
When a mind is not nurtured, is afraid of questioning, afraid to demand but most important, is afraid to decide what it wants.
When a mind is educated, you discover freedom; you discover your rights and obligations, but never denying your roots, I am proud of being indigenous, of speaking my tongue and go to my homeland to celebrate.
But that does not make me less, neither it means that my community or that 16 millions of Mexicans that live in this country, indigenous, cannot access health or education.
But I understood something, nobody was going change that if I didn't give the first step.
This means breaking cultural paradigms that the society imposes upon us.
Suddenly saying indigenous makes people think of a walking museum and we are not walking museums, we have opinions and we can decide for ourselves.
When I finished my major, I had to fight for that too by working, selling whatever I could on the streets, yet, I don't regret because that is how I found freedom.
I was a community instructor, then I wanted to become a doctor, but I had no means so I became an accountant that is how I know that 1 + 1 makes 2: rights and obligations. Every time I went back to my community I couldn't understand
why women were always empty handed.
The answer was "you are a woman, first get a husband and you might get something."
But at school I was taught that one article of my country Constitution said that we are all equal, but that was not a reality there.
Customary law blocked that right.
I will say it again: I do not hold these men and women responsible, most of them don't even know what Human Rights are, not even what an obligation is.
When I understood that, I started going to the community playground to play, I became the crazy woman.
Attending mass, I was crazy.
But I took that responsibility because nobody would do it for me.
I made it for me,
then for my family, for my community, for my state, to become visible for this country.
I am capable of deciding what I want.
Afterwards, I wanted to become president of my village.
But customary law hindered my right.
But that was not the worst.
Government institutions response was the worst.
That is was the community customary law, the autonomy of the community was a common right, and there was no law that could protect me.
21st century, 2007.
It wasn't about the answers I was given in my town, but the answers I was given by the government, that was what hurt me the most.
Not because I wanted to be a President, but because that was the entrance door to visibility of our own community, we voted already for political parties, because we had credentials.
But the aim of this was becoming visible because our families only knew customary law.
I didn't stand still, I denounced and thanks to that, I made my way into politics.
As questioned as it is, I took that responsibility. Nowadays, politicians are all judged the same, but no.
I believe that politics needs to help citizens to start changing what they don't like.
It's a less painful path to change what hurts us.
That is why I started my way in politics, to change the answer I was given in the past, Where was this answer?
I had to change my state Constitution, so that women should never hear again "No", it's not for me, in the end
I am already an accountant.
It is for all those invisible women.
All those women who are not even asked if they want to have 10 kids.
Having 10, 5 or 4 kids is not bad, as long as it is our own decision.
This was the way I found so that women in my town can exercise their rights.
Today in my village, women can vote.
Today in my village, three women are part of the town council.
Out of the 570 municipalities of my state, 417 are still under customary law.
Out of the 68 existing ethnic groups of the country, 16 are in my state.
Nobody should tell us that due to our culture we cannot access development or we cannot give our opinion regarding how we want to change our stories and situations.
Politics should be a tool to help us say: "This is Mexico, this is our color, our flavor."
I gained this with education.
Through education I've found freedom.
Today freedom is for me the most sacred.
Thanks to that I became a federal deputy and said: "Not just for my state but also for those 16 million Mexican women that live in this country, so that nobody denies their rights."
In 2014, I succeeded in achieving an amendment of the Constitution, so that nobody should hear again the answers I was once given.
Not only that, also in 2014, UN adopted that amendment so that any place with an indigenous population, heads of state are obliged to change practices that threaten the development of our indigenous communities.
Everybody speaks of democracy, I think of democracy as a dove, with a female and a male wing, without each of them we cannot reach democracy, especially if we are indigenous.
Today, I'm grateful for the presence of the most important person, my son, the reason why I won't back up, I will continue striving, because an amendment is not enough, it now needs to be enforced. It cannot remain just as an amendment it must become a reality that in the 417 municipalities, at least 5 or 20 women can be heads of a municipality.
Then again, voting is not the main aspect, what is important is becoming visible in Mexico. (Zapotec) I now understand freedom as the fog that flows by the hillsides of my village.
Thank you so much.
I now understand freedom as the fog that flows by the hillside of my village.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I recently had a date.
I will spare you the details, for good.
It was awful.
It started more or less like this, with him asking me: - And, what do you for a living?
- Well, I'm a researcher at CONICET, I'm a scientist and I am currently working on the study and development of liposomal formulations that encapsulate mucolytics for the treatment of lung diseases, both chronic and acute.
(Laughter) (Applause) - And what about you?
He never called back, can you believe it?
But why was he so scared? What?

Aren't there female scientists?
There are a lot, but it seems to be that for every famous female scientist that we all know, there are many more famous male scientists.
There are female scientists, but it seems to be that men go further.
And why does this happen?
Well, in the mid-1800s there were scientists that after weighing the brains of women and men, came to the conclusion that women, we are less intelligent for having a lighter brain.
So let's see, you Mr. Objectivity, the brain of the woman is lighter than a man's brain, but the whole woman is lighter than the man, right?
(Applause) Not me, maybe, but it doesn't matter.
The summer season is complicated, it can happen to anyone.
(Laughter) Intelligence doesn't have to do with the brain's weight.
Women are not less smarter than men.
But they in fact are more sensitive.
And it seems that this is a big problem.
Recently, a scientist called Tim Hunt, was awarded a Nobel Prize.
He said the following: "It's not good that men and women work together in a laboratory because three things happen when there are women.
First, you fall in love with them."
I won't question that, I'm quite adorable.
(Laughter) "Second, they fall in love with you.
Third, if you criticize them, they cry."
I'm not going to stop at the third point because it already makes me violent.
But let's go to the second point: "they fall in love with you."
This is Tim Hunt.
(Laughter) (Applause) Do they really fall in love with you, Tim Hunt?
Come on, Brad Pitt, give me a break!
Many women complained about Tim's sayings, and he had to quit his position at the University of London.
The only thing he regrets is of having said all this in front of the media, not of what he said.
Sometimes, I start thinking what was it that led me to pursue a career in science.
I remember a couple of things.
I remember it was Children's Day and I would be about six years old, and all my friends were given dolls, those that look like real babies -- because, when were are girls they give us dolls or mirrors, never magnifying glasses or a meccano --
When I was six years old, on that Children's Day, my mom gave me a chemistry game.
I didn't expect a chemistry game.
I couldn't believe the amount of things it could do.
I was happy with that chemistry game.
My mom later realized that it would've been better if she had given me a doll.
The house would've been cleaner and tidier.
Chemistry in the hands of a six-year-old is quite messy.
But maybe that led me to pursue a career in science, I don't know.
I love stories of women of science who achieved a lot of things because they were able to overcome a lot of prejudice.
Elvira Rawson, for example.
Elvira Rawson was one of the first Argentine doctors.
In 1890, during the Revolution of the Park in Buenos Aires, she was treating the injured for three days.
They say that she even changed the horseshoe to one of the horses in the ambulance she was working.
And this earned her a recognition by Leandro N. Alem, who said that women like Elvira were an inspiration for their husband and children.
An inspiration!
(Laughter) She changed the horseshoe to a horse and that's just an inspiration?
What else did she have to do?
Alignment and balance?
(Laughter) (Applause) I love stories.
There are a lot of women in science with great stories.
Anyway, note that Leandro N. Alem was one of the most open minded guys of that time.
What he said about Elvira was a real compliment.
There were worse people.
I love them.
There are many women in science
so interesting, take Rosalind Franklin.
Rosalind Franklin was the one who helped discovering how the DNA's structure was.
And this discovery was worth a Nobel, not awarded to Rosalind, obviously, but to three scientists called James Watson, Francis Crick and Maurice Wilkins.
This happened: Rosalind was the one who took the photo of the DNA molecule.
And this is not an easy thing to do.
It's not that you stand in front of the DNA molecule and you say firmly: - Freeze and say cheese!
No, it's a very difficult image to obtain and very difficult to analyze.
And it's done with X-rays. Rosalind obtained this image and it seems that Wilkins stole it and took it to his friends Watson and Crick.
They published the results and, for when they were given the Nobel Prize, they didn't even remember mentioning Rosalind.
She had already died from ovarian cancer, probably caused by working with X-rays to take pictures of DNA molecules.
Today, the DNA structure is known as Watson and Crick's double helix.
I call it double helix of Rosalind, okay?
(Applause) I always hear about that women are neater, more orderly and get more in the details than men.
That if such a minister of economy manages the country's funds like my aunt Adela, we come out of the crisis.
I don't know if we are tidier, orderly or look more into details than men.
What I do know is that many labs, use us as secretaries.
Men are the ones who have the great ideas.
It would seem that we are good at accompanying, but that we are not good leaders.
A bit strange.
When I got my degree, my mom was happy to have a professional daughter, very happy.
And she gave me this, see how nice.
This little black suit. Beautiful, right?
I used it for the graduation day,
because all my thesis I worked with mice,
and mice pee and poop, which doesn't bode
pretty well with that suit. Anyway I would love to use it again, because, besides, it would mean that it fits!
(Laughter) It would make me very happy.
In the Researcher Scientist career at CONICET there are a lot of women, but only in the initial levels of the career.
In the higher levels the majority are men.
We are good at accompanying.
And because we accompany, many women were overshadowed.
She is Mileva Marić.
Mileva Marić was the most outstanding mathematician of her time, She was incredible.
She wasn't quite in the knowledge for using accessories, right?
That ribbon there, Mileva!
She didn't have girlfriends to tell her: "Less is more, Mileva."
(Laughter) She was Einstein's wife.
This that behind a great man there is always a great woman, in the case of Mileva, it was very real.
She was always behind Einstein.
Not because she wanted to, obviously.
They were married until 1914, then they broke up.
In 1922, Einstein was awarded a Nobel Prize for describing the photoelectric effect.
And they say that everything was with the help of Mileva with whom he had scientific discussions when they lived together in Zurich.
Mileva doesn't appear in any of Einstein's works.
But if I tell you: "female scientist", Marie Curie comes to mind and it's good that it's like that, she even won two Nobel Prizes, Two Nobel Prizes!
She was the first person to achieve that.
Not the first woman. The first person.
And besides all this, Marie Curie
had two daughters, she was a mother.
These are the daughters of Marie Curie.
One is Ève and the other is Irène.
Ève never stepped on a research lab in her whole life.
Irène was a scientist like her mother.
Which is which?
It's obvious, isn't it?
(Laughter) At that time, science was still more of a men's field.
Then Irène had to be one of them, and it seems it went pretty well for her.
Einstein wrote in a letter that Irène looked like a "grenadier".
And watch out, Irène won a Nobel Prize, too.
To give you an idea, since they began to give them until now, they were around 900 Nobel Prizes given.
Of those 900, only 50 were given to women.
Of those 50, the vast majority are Nobel Peace Prize Laureate and only six are Chemistry and Physics.
And of those six, half are Curie's, two of the mother and one of the daughter.
How difficult must have been to be Ève Curie!
They didn't give you a Nobel, nothing.
- Mom, I got an A in chemistry.
- Your sister got a Nobel, sorry.
(Laughter) But anyone would think that science is so demanding, that Irène didn't have time to fix herself up.
But I think it's the other way around.
To be credible, Irène had to pay little attention to her looks.
To be credible, Irène had to look like a man.
Let's be clear: we can be leaders, without a doubt, we can be leaders.
We can be Rosalind and Mileva, being duly recognized.
And we can be Irène, earning us a Nobel Prize
with lipstick. If you ask any child today to draw you a person who works in science, they surely draw you someone old, with glasses and male.
Never a little chubby blonde who goes on bad dates.
(Laughter) But I'm not old -- no, I'm not old! -- I don't wear glasses and I'm not a man, but I am indeed a scientist.
And this is not against scientists, at least not against every one, but in favor of us, women in science, for we have a lot of things to say.
Because we can be missing out ideas.
Imagine what would've happened if Einstein, instead of listening to Mileva, ignored her and asked her a capuccino.
Let's be careful, because we can be missing out on great discoveries and revolutionary ideas, just because they come from a lighter brain.
Thank you!
(Applause)
It's important to have a job, right?
Yet, it's so difficult.
I've come to tell you why I'm going to lose mine. And why probably, many of you will also lose your jobs, unless we take advantage of right now.
We live in the age where we over prepare, we live in the age in which, we rival with people who have more degrees than ourselves more masters than ourselves, more languages.
You or your children go to job interviews and there is that awkward moment when you cross paths with the other candidate.
That person who is you rival for that job.
But imagine in the future, much closer than you think, if the person who you cross paths with isn't a person, but a robot.
Imagine arriving at the office one morning and your boss says: "Meet your new work colleague" And it's a robot, or a computer.
Many of you will say: well, robots have been here for years and we are still here working, they are
in factories and it's not a problem.
And it's true. But only to an extent.
These are KUKA robots.
They are robotic arms that work in manufacturing plants for electrical components, for cars, and they are especially good at their job.
A company called Chinese Precision Technology, which produces semiconductors, mid last year made a decision that today still surprises many people, but a decision that is becoming more popular.
It changed 90% of the company's workforce in one of its plants, in exchange for these robots.
And what happened?
The company's productivity tripled, almost increasing output from 8,000 to 21,000 units, and more important, the product failure rate fell from 25% to 5%.
So if any of you work in a factory assembling electrical components, or assembling cars, I recommend that you look for another job.
But I'm sure many of you don't do that for a living, and the robots that you don't have to fear aren't KUKA, they are these.
It looks like Matrix, right?
What you're seeing in the screen is called neural coding. To say it straight, it is a set of equations with the purpose of analysing data and making decisions.
What does this look like in real life?
After all, it is software.
Well, in real life it looks like that.
It's Google's self-driving car.
It doesn't have arms, legs, it doesn't look human like, not like C3PO or R2D2, but it's a robot, I'm not kidding.
It's a robot that's made for moving around for dodging pedestrians, for reading traffic signs and obeying them.
Very likely, in a few years, this robot will pick you up when you finish having dinner.
Google isn't the only company manufacturing self-driving cars.
Tesla, which is one of the main manufacturers of electric cars, has already announced that all of the cars they produce from now on are going to be compatible with automated technology.
Uber, the company which is turning the taxi industry upside down, is trying automated taxis in Pittsburg.
They're free and carry real passengers. Just now
they have someone at the wheel, but this person doesn't drive. They are simply taking notes.
Uber has a subsidiary called Otto, which specialises in self-driving trucks.
The first self-driving trucks have already traveled more than 100 kilometres to deliver beer.
They aren't the only ones.
Amazon are trying drones to deliver packages en rural areas.
Domino's have delivered the first pizza using drones.
If any of you work on the roads transporting goods, if any of you have a taxi, you're running out of time.
Look, the whole time I've been saying that both Ubers' cars as well as Tesla's self-driving cars are already equipped to go solo
but yet they still aren't. Why?
There's only one issue - the law.
These aren't university projects or something that will take ten or twenty years to reach the market.
They are already on the road.
All they need is someone in the government to say: okay, you're licensed to operate.
Anyway, lets assume that we're lucky and that we don't work in any of these sectors.
Is anyone here a lawyer?
I introduce you to Ross. Ross is a lawyer.
Ross is a project developed by IBM
based on the super computer, Watson
it's a server that's been set up in a buffet in the United States and is an expert in case law.
It understands human language, and when a lawyer asks: Ross bring out cases on car accidents involving aninmals.
Ross takes out these cases.
But not only this, he also updates them.
Every time there is a new precedent case, Ross adds it to the database.

Doctors? Da Vinci Robotics.
Da Vinci Robotics is a company that's been operating for years, and has carried out more than 200,000 surgical procedures. Currently,
there's always a doctor behind the robot.
They are operated robots.
The doctor operates, the robot makes a small incision, it does what it needs to and closes up.
It's minimally invasive and works marvellously.
Currently, they need a doctor, but it's a matter of time before it is no longer necessary.
The same company that developed Ross, works on diagnostic systems by artificial intelligence. In the end, a medical diagnosis is nothing more than a collection of data. Your leg hurts, you have a spot here, you have a headache, you have this.
Who works in customer service?
Who works in Zara, a hotel?
This is hotel in Japan. The Japanese
are especially inclined towards robots,
they like them a lot. Obviously, this is only the one from the reception and the hat is to add humour, but the entire hotel is automated.
Absolutely everything in the hotel.
The room service, the check in, check out, suitcases, everything.
You can look it up online, you can stay in the hotel and you won't see a single person apart from the other guests.
Well, I'm a journalist, and recently Facebook decided to fire all the journalists who work on the social network's newsfeed, and replaced them with algorithms.
Algorithms which select news stories and write them.
And without spelling mistakes.
So I've arrived at a point that's a bit dark, What do futurists say?
What do they say those who truly know a lot about robots? Artificial Intelligence?
Where are we heading? Well,
basically they say that robots will destroy jobs
but also create them. It makes sense.
This is the argument they used against the Luwians. The Luwians were those gentlemen that during the industrial revolution got very angry because there were machines taking their jobs away.
What they were basically told was: Don't worry.
You're a blacksmith, you make horseshoes. And all the time more people are travelling by car but you can reinvent yourself as a mechanic, you can reinvent yourself to work in a car factory.
This was true in the 19th century, but not anymore.
When self-driving taxis begin operating on the street, if ten taxis take away jobs from ten drivers, these ten drivers won't turn into self-driving taxi programmers.
One or two, at the most.
Ultimately, robots destroy jobs but create others within limits.
The main problem isn't only that it destroys job, it makes labour cheaper.
Edward Rensi is the person that made McDonald's the empire it is today.
Although he doesn't have the surname McDonald.
Edward Rensi very recently, in a comparison of labour claims in United States in hospitality sector, said the following: it's cheaper to buy a $35,000 robotic arm than hire an employee who's paid $15 to put french fries in bags
in an inefficient way. Obviously, Edward Rensi isn't very popular in McDonald's.
But he's right. Robots diminish the cost of work.
By the way, the photo you see is a restaurant that has just opened in San Francisco that literally manufactures hamburgers.
You can order one through an app, and an assembly line which isn't a human like robot, but is ultimately a robot,
produces it perfectly. Well, other futurologists involved in the subject that robots deteriorate employment, warn us of a very controversial issue, very complicated, and it's that robots create a society in which we won't have to work, but there's a problem, it's that robots don't make themselves, someone has to make them, and this person is going to be owner of the means of production, which, in the past they called it the economy.
That person, Stephen Hawking himself, said that if machines produce everything that we need, everything depends on how we distribute the wealth.
If we don't do it right, there will be new inequalities.
So, where are we heading towards?
What's the solution if robots are taking our jobs?
Another futurist theory is that what we're going to have is a universal basic income. Robots are going to take all the jobs that we have, and it will gives us time to do whatever we want, to achieve our dreams, to focus on poetry, painting or online videos.
The problem of a basic universal income, I don't know you, but I live in a country that couldn't allow a basic universal income, at least for just now.
Maybe in the future. Perhaps in 50 years, but not today.
Roy Stockell, an expert in asset management, warns that at the moment, pensions are a time bomb and not only in Spain, in the rest of Europe. There are countries
that are carrying out experiments in terms of universal basic income, but just now, I am pretty sure that the universal basic income won't come for at least another ten or twenty years. However, the jobs that robots are going to take is going to happen earlier.
Ultimately, we have quite a bleak outlook. But what can we do?
At the end of the day, we're in a talk that speaks about a future time, we're in the present, We're at a time that we will see a wave of change, that robots are going to begin to devour jobs.
How can we take advantage of this wave, flow it and take advantage of the trend and not only continue to work, but make our work even better?
The answer lies in new work values.
It sounds a bit cliché, but in the end robots always stick to the script. [Creativity]
Leave the script and stop acting like robots.
The problem isn't the robots. The problem is ours.
If we go into work in the morning, clock in, lifelessly hit the keyboard, we sign back out and leave, we are cannon fodder for robots, because we are doing a robotic task, that any robot can substitute without a problem.
If you leave the script, if you ask yourself every day How can I improve my work?
How can I add a little plus?
Even though it isn't totally related with what you do, you will have a strength that no robot can copy.
Secondly, if you can't copy robots, join them.
Elon Musk, who is one of the biggest futurists that we have nowadays, and is very worried by artificial intelligence, says that the only solution we have as humans to fight artificial intelligence is to become artificially intelligent.
What Elon Musk suggests is to have an implant in the brain to be able to connect to internet. It sounds great, or very bad. The ability to watch videos of cats in the brain. I don't know if it's a good idea. This is what Elon Musk suggests for the future. However, we can do this today.
A lot happens in my job. I'm sure that you have read an article in the newspaper, or a blog, and you've thought, this is wrong. Why hasn't this guy searched on google?
Why don't we look on google?
At our fingers we have enormous collective intelligence that's called the internet.
Google, Quora... Whatever problem you could possibly have, it's very likely that someone has had it before on the internet and has solved it. We have
access to an enormous database, to make our brains have extra artificial intelligence, and not only this, there's lots of other technology that we can incorporate
in our everyday life. How many sectors do you know that have had a rough time because of technology?
The music industry, Spotify.
We take for granted that we carry all the music in the world in our pocket.
But 20 years ago, the music industry didn't see it coming, and what happened happened.
Cinema, Netflix, 10 years ago it was unthinkable having hundreds of movies to watch.
The same happened with Amazon and books, Amazon with the delivery of goods, nowadays supermarkets are starting to see a certain problem that Amazon is competing with them with something they can't do and they must adapt themselves, in fact, they're adapting fast.
If you take into account the technology in your daily work, try to use its maximum potential, you will be much stronger when avoiding losing your job to a robot.
And the last thing we can do, which in my opinion is the most important,
is to be human. Stop being a robot, and think again as humans
We live in a society that drives us to go faster, become increasingly more efficient, and along the way we lose the qualities that makes us humans, precisely.
We stop greeting people, we stop considering the customer as anything more
than a number. It is the worst mistake we could make with regards to competing with robots.
If you begin to incorporate a human touch into your everyday work, it could be something simple, like caring about the after sales service, like, if you are doctors, get back that lovely image the family doctor, in the end it's like that, when you go to the doctor, the doctor can give a fantastic diagnosis, and I know that the doctors are swamped with work, but a lot of the time, the only thing we want is that they listen to us.
"Look, my leg hurts.
I know its triple tendinitis, but what I want is you to tell me is that everything will be okay, that nothing will happen, and often we don't have time to do that.
There's an occupation that you all know that is absolutely devoured by robots, however, every time that you find a robot, automatically you ask to be put with a human.
Do you know which occupation it is?
Teleoperator. "If your incident relates
to a breakdown, press 1. If you want
to pay a bill, press..."

I don't want to. I want to speak with
a person. My router has broken.
The light's flickering and I don't know what's wrong.
Robots don't understand us. And it'll be many decades before they are capable of understanding us.
Only a person can understand another.
If you integrate this, your jobs will possibly be safer from an invasion from robots than if you don't.
Ultimately, we are getting closer to a future in which robots will increasingly create job losses and become a basic feature.
The ageing population, is also a factor that works against this.
According to the Nomura Institute, Japan, 49% of employees in the country, within just six years, will be robots.
In a world where robots are the basic feature, become yourself the premium option.
Thanks very much.
(Applause)
Hello everyone.
I am the only kid here today that will turn a mere 30 years old this coming month. I feel privileged to have been invited to participate. Thank you to everyone at TED and all its participants.
Okay, so when you’re invited to an event, and they tell you the rest of the speakers are children, what do you do?
You don’t know whether to talk about childhood, or about - Well, it occurred to me to talk about the only thing that children don’t know about, which is: At what moment do we stop being children?
And it’s a question that I believe is super important for everyone here.
So today I would like us to try and navigate this question.
Do you think we stop being children at some physical point?
In other words, does this moment occur when we are growing up and we begin to find that things like our pants are too small or we grew four sizes, and is that moment the one we stop being kids?
Or is it more of a way of seeing?
A way of seeing the world and what surrounds us.
This is what I would like to ask of you all today: I ask that you come with me as we try to transport ourselves to that past which we all have had.
Don’t tell me that you never were kids, because I won't believe you.
And don’t believe that we are going to get anywhere. That’s the thing with adults. Kids never want to go anywhere. The only thing they want is to enjoy the present.
In fact, you always see that when you let a child loose, they never take the path, never.
So let's start at the beginning. I ask that we all take a moment to concentrate right now, and let’s travel back to the first time we sprang from our mothers’ bellies, and we said, “God!”
(Laughter) “What is all of this?”
We had to learn how to breathe, to smell; we had to see who all those people were that were talking, the sounds, the lights. How overwhelming, right?
In the first 10 minutes of your lives, you learned the most important things that you are ever going to learn.
You learned how to breathe, how to connect with the person who will love you the most for the rest of your lives, and you learned a whole new series of things, which would only worsen.
So we continue with this question of when we stop being children.
Let’s go to the next fundamental step, when suddenly one day, when you are still very young, because I have a nephew who’s just one year old, and suddenly one day, he went like this to like this.
And I imagine that in that first moment, I would like it if in that moment you - that first time you say, “I’m going, I’m going, going, yes, I’m going,” and back to the ground.
And there is a day in which everything works out, and all of a sudden, you know how to walk.
That excitement, that risk, that magical moment when you learn to walk, it only happens once.
And think about the number of steps that you have taken today, and we’ve never had the same feeling.
So, my reasoning is, when we stop feeling this way, is that the moment we stop being kids?
Is that what sets us apart?
Look, those eyes! That's the pure expression of “I want to eat that.” I don’t know what it was, but please, give it to me too!
Children have an incredible ability to learn.
I have dedicated myself to studying what happens in the brain when you are learning, and it’s incredible how as human beings, we go on to lose that ability.
I also want you all to go back to your childhood, and recall the moment you learned how to paint, when you learned to dance, to sing, all those things, for the first time, and it was wonderful.
And, nevertheless, today as adults we are too busy, sending and typing important things, right? Meanwhile, children continue to watch.
One thing that I enjoy most about children is their ability to play. A child doesn’t play to win, nor do they play seeking any end goal, or to beat a video game, a child plays in order to enjoy the game.
And what’s more, another thing that children do is share. As a kid you come into the world with nothing, so you must share everything.
And it’s this that for adults is so difficult. We tend to want to set aside, to save things.
And all this is out of fear, out of fear of not sharing things. It doesn’t have to be about what you give or take, because you’re not going to be what you lost.
Well, that fear, in my opinion, is the enemy of children. It’s that fear, in any given moment, that causes you stop thinking like a kid, and you begin to think in another way.
In fact, the brain is very curious, because when you don’t know that something is dangerous, you do it.
That’s why for many children - I’m sure you have seen similar images, in which they take greater risks than any older adult would do, simply because they don’t know they can’t do it.
This is how we function, how a child functions: I try to do something new, I wait to see what happens, I receive the feedback and I learn.
This can be positive, and suddenly one day I start to dance crazily, I wait to see what happens, and I see that my parents begin to laugh together.
So I say, “That’s great! I have done something well. Awesome! I will continue to do it.”
But what happens when we do not receive this stimulus?
Suddenly, one day I dance crazily, and my friends, or a girl that I like, laughs at me.
That means that I am strange, and the fear, the fear, the fear, it begins. And so one day I stop dancing, stop singing; I stop trying to excite.
Another thing with children is the impossible.
Children are specialists in dreaming up the impossible.
But really, think about how a human being, all the things that you have, are all thanks to a kid who refused to stop being one.
Think about the airplane. A kid said, “We will be able to fly.” And he set his mind to showing that he would do it in his lifetime. And today, when planes fly,
nobody thinks about that.
Or when you receive a call, think about how someone said, “We will be able to talk through the air.”
If I were to hear this for the first time, I would say, “This one has completely lost their mind."
Well, I would like us to make a list now of all the things that children do better than adults.
Let’s take a look at some that have occurred to me: wanting without hoping for anything in exchange, trusting others, being amazed by new discoveries, not fearing being wrong, playing in order to enjoy, believing in the impossible, not lying, making the most of each day, not fearing being different.
Do you really believe that we have anything to teach them?
Or do we have to begin to learn from kids?
One of the things that I repeat is that I believe education is backwards, and we should begin to create schools for parents that would like to reconnect with their own childhood and their children's. For parents who want to learn everything that we have talked about: to want without expecting anything in return, to have faith in others, to be amazed by new discoveries, to not fear being wrong, to play for fun, to believe in the impossible, to not lie, to make the most of every day, to not fear being different.
Wouldn’t it be better if all adults would learn a little more from kids?
What happens is that in order to learn like children, you cannot watch, like we say in Spain, from the back of the bullring.
In order to learn like a kid and actually connect with him, you have to get in your boat, you have to get in your spaceship, you have to go up there and say, “Where are we going?
Where do you want to go?"
That’s the only way of connecting with a kid and to be able to teach them.
You are going to fail with him, and you’re going to have 20,000 problems.
But when the child knows that you are going there with him, you are making sure that he learns without fear.
And for me, children are the ones with the solutions to adult problems.
I am convinced that the world will find solutions in children.
And, in fact, I dedicate my life to that.
I am always asked what it is I teach my children, and I always respond that I teach them what makes their faces light up.
When I see a child’s eyes shine, it is that spark that tells me there is something they want to learn.
But I say that to you not to stop you from doing anything more than just go back to watching and laughing like them, go back to playing like them, to trusting like they do, enjoying every day like they do, without anything.
And even though it seems that they do the complete opposite, at the heart of it all, I think that the problem is that we look at them with the wrong perspective.
Anyways, thank you all, and I hope that your children can be our best teachers.
Thank you very much.
(Applause).
People sleep on average 8 hours a day.
We spend a third of our lives sleeping.
It's a lot of time.
However, there are things that happen while we sleep that we don't know about.
While we sleep, we are unconscious but the brain still processes information from what happened that day.
From research, we know that at night memory is strengthened, there are very important processes for learning, and also processes that maintain balance between mind and body.
What does this mean?
We know we must eat well with a well-balanced diet, and we need regular, physical activity to stay healthy, but sometimes we don't realize all of the sleep time our body requires to stay healthy.
When we don't sleep enough, we have a compensatory mechanism which makes us sleep longer and deeper.
We have to sleep, but why?

We still don't know.
That's what I study and what interests me.
As a scientist who studies sleep, I need tools to measure things.
In these measurements, I look for patterns to tell me what happened during the night, how the brain behaves while we sleep.
For example, we can see how breathing is while we sleep.
Breathing changes and becomes regular, although during the night it may change like when we stop breathing for a moment, resulting in apnea or when we are restless.
We can also see how cardiac rhythm changes.
We connect electrodes to the chest to get an electrocardiogram reading that tells us how the cardiac rhythm changes throughout the night.
We also see how cardiac rhythm changes at night.
We have more information watching the brain's electrical activity.
Placing electrodes on the scalp, we get an electroencephalogram that gives us information about electrical activity in the brain.
For more information we can use a technique called functional magnetic resonance imaging where a person sleeps inside a magnetic resonance machine which gives very detailed information: tiny brain regions down to the millimeter, blood flow that supplies oxygen, which regions of the brain activate and which are inhibited.
These are powerful techniques that give detailed information on what is happening.
But this power is limited because the machines are expensive, there are few hospitals or laboratories and they require specialized personnel, and, generally, the study is done only on one person each night.
Additionally, you can't sleep in your own bed with your own teddy bear and you have to go to a special place that you're not used to.
This can affect the rhythm of normal sleep.
With Javier Portillo, a physics student at the Balseiro Institute, we started to work to develop a device to study sleep.
We wanted a lot from this device: portable, widely-available, and easy to use so you can gather data in your own bed.
While we were developing this, we found that such a machine already existed.
Many people have one and it has a lot of sensors: it's a cell phone.
Cell phones have sensors that everyone knows, like a microphone for talking
but we can use it to record how a person breathes during the night. Or it has a camera and we use it to see how we move throughout the night.
We may think that we get up in the morning in the same position we fell asleep the night before or that we didn't stop moving the whole night.
How do we actually move during the night?
With Javier, we wanted to answer that question in particular.
We used the accelerometer from a cell phone, a sensor that measures the phone's changes in acceleration.
It's used when we make a call and the screen turns off next to our face.
The sensor detects the change in acceleration and shuts off the screen.
A more common feature is the screen rotation on the phone and the phone knows it because its sensor detects the change in acceleration.
When we used these sensors, we found there were already programs that recorded sleep through breathing and movement.
They work with the cell phone laying at our side, recording how we move and sleep.
Throughout the night our breathing and movements change because we pass through sleep cycles that last an hour or an hour and a half during which we go through stages of light sleep and stages of deep sleep.
Between these stages there's a time that we keep totally still when only our eyes move and this is when we dream.
The device can record a hypnogram that shows the change in these stages and how we stay still when we dream.
The time that we are dreaming is part of the light sleep stage when it is easy to be awoken.
So this program isn't only to make a picture, but also used as an alarm that wakes us up when it is easiest to wake up.
But as scientists, these programs aren't useful because what we need is the information from the sensors.
Here the information is already processed, but we don't know how it was processed.
We need programs that tell us what information it's measuring at that moment.
These programs also exist.
They are programs you can easily install on your cell phone and you can see the recorded data directly or save it in a file.
Therefore, we had a simple device, available to a lot of people, with a program easy to implement and use.
We wanted to study how we move at night.
So we put the instrument on his belly
and we see the abdomen's expansion with each breath.
This gave us information about movement together with the change in breathing, which we already knew.
In this example, you can see the change in the cell phone's acceleration and when the breathing is soft, the change is a soft movement.
But we were not satisfied with the test because the cell phone slid off and fell from his abdomen.
To attach it required tape but if somebody likes to sleep facedown, like Javier, it is very uncomfortable.
We decided to try it on the arm.
We found that the movement was similar to that of the abdomen; there were moments that were very soft.
Upon filming it, we discovered that the arm was on top of the belly and we saw that the arm's oscillation was because the person was breathing.
Since we mixed up those movements, we decided to try it on the leg.
There we had an extra simplification because it was good enough to place the cell phone in a tight sock and see how the leg moved at night just by putting it in a sock.
Here on the chart you can see what we recorded.
I'm sure that it's happened to many of you while you are sleeping to have the sensation of falling and you give a little kick.
The change you see now is more drastic.
It's a movement similar to this.
Now that we had a good way to record what happens during the night, we asked for student volunteers to sleep with their cell phone in their sock using the program to record what happened throughout the night.
I would like to show you, not charted data, but an actual figure.
This is a measurement made with the cell phone.
What do you see here?
It's a two minute window of time.
Javier put his cell phone in his sock, lied down, and is moving. You can see the change in acceleration here and here he fell asleep, now he's not moving.
Soon he is dreaming, does a little kick, and then goes back to sleep.
This was a surprise, we weren't expecting such a violent transition.
In the data it looks like a person moves until falling asleep then stays still.
This allows us to compare signs between different nights and people; we can determine the exact moment when the person falls asleep.
I will show you one more detail of what happens when we sleep.
This is data from another night.
On the top there's a small window of time when you see the change in acceleration. These are small leg movements; they look big, but they are small movements.
The sensor is very sensitive and it records very small movements.
He's asleep, there's a little kick, sleeps for another minute, here he turns over in the bed and keeps sleeping.
In a larger window of time, a minute is compacted in this area.
We see something similar: movement, then nothing, movement, nothing.
In a larger window, we also see something similar.
This should get your attention, it got mine: We move a lot throughout the night.
Over different recordings we see that it is normal to move a lot.
They seem like random movements: a moment goes by, movement, a moment, movement...
These figures look very much alike if you look at them.
But you have to keep in mind a very important detail: This window is for one minute and here a few seconds passed; this window is of five minutes so only a few minutes passed between movements.
The third window of time is larger where the time intervals between movements is still larger.
Physicists call it "intermittent behavior" where there is self-similarity: the movements are similar between different scales.
Upon studying the data from many people over many nights, we see that the movements are not random; they have structure, they follow a pattern.
That's what we call a fractal, which is related to self-similarity.
This excites us because we have mathematical tools to analyze these statistics.
This is also a big contribution to Neuroscience.
Why does your leg move at night?
It moves because the brain sends information to the leg, but at night, as I said, we are unconscious.
So, this movement is a window showing what the brain does at night.
You could think that a cell phone turns itself into a tool for clinical diagnosis.
You could have data from different nights and you come across data that doesn't follow the normal statistical pattern, you could have advanced warning of a possible pathology.
When we talk about Physics and Neuroscience, we think of complex measurements or sophisticated instruments.
With Javier, it was the opposite.
We took a simple measurement: how a leg moves with a device we all have: a cell phone.
Thank you very much.
Applause.
(Performance mode) I said silence!
I'll be with you in a second.
I am finishing a very important experiment for my research and besides, I already gave you the notes yesterday.
You have had photocopies of them for years and even so, you can't learn the subject.
I would be satisfied if at least 20% of you passed the year.
(Music) But what are you doing?
Yes, you, in the second row!
Put down your phone right now, for God's sake!
Ladies, stop chatting!
How much do you think a teacher's minute is worth?
Do you think it's free?
(Performance mode ends) (Music ends) We all recognize the scene, right?
(Laughter) Well, in a world like this I would like you to come with me on a trip, because would a teacher get a million Youtube hits in this way?
Or at universities with such a history of tradition, that turn their backs on innovation and new technologies; can you innovate?
My challenge here today is to show you that yes, this is possible.
So, let's go, let's start our trip.
Close your eyes for a few seconds if you like and listen to my voice.
Think of Chariots of Fire with no music, or imagine Frodo, would he be able to get rid of the ring without Sam next to him?
Even easier: remember last year's TEDx, with no one in the seats?
Impossible, right?
Of course it is. Our minds play tricks on us because we remember the hero, the protagonist, but there are other silent protagonists who, because they're obvious, go unnoticed by us.
So, going back to today's challenge, at a university like the one you know, like the one we imagine, what are the ingredients for innovation?
You will think that it's not the same for music as it is for art history, or for mathematics or physics.
Well, yes and no.
There are common elements that are constant throughout the innovation process, and those are the ones I want to talk about today.
The first, the well-known academic freedom, that invisibility cape that many of my colleagues have put on in order to spend years doing the same.
Let's take that cape and use it to our advantage.
Secondly, someone who wants to change, who is motivated, that dreamer, that misfit who wants to learn new methods and who can use technology.
And those crazy people, believe me, they exist; you have one right here.
But they have to be capable of living in a hostile environment, they must be disruptive.
Besides, they usually carry a load of wild social responsibility, they feel indebted to society and they want to give back their knowledge to society, because that's who it belongs to.
Believe me, I think that no one is more ignorant than those who keep knowledge for themselves.
And thirdly, the methods and technologies - and people here know the same or more about this than me - collaborative learning, project-based learning, gamification, internet, devices, social networks, etc
But this is around the corner, in libraries, at the click of a button.
So, what are we missing?
We have everything.
Let's check, let's see ... Firstly, freedom.
Secondly, a motivated person.
Thirdly, methods and techniques.
That's it, right?
Well no, no it isn't, you fell for it again. We're missing Vangelis in Chariots of Fire, we're missing Sam, we're missing the TEDx audience.
To innovate at university, in education, we need some ingredients that are so obvious we forget about them: students.
Students are the permanent ingredient in our education.
And also, I have to say that we must overcome many fears with them, among others, the fear of ridicule.
Here you see them, performing in class.
The classroom has become a large cell, and each student is a piece that moves in a very specific way.
I can assure you what they learnt that day, they still haven't forgotten.
They also have to overcome the fear of sharing what they learn with their classmates.
They have to believe that they produce valuable knowledge.
Some fall so in love with what they study, what they learn, and why they study it.
And those are the people who close the educational circle with capital letters.
They literally took me out on the street a few years ago to stand up for education as a social pillar.
It was a wonderful experience, because apart from the students and despite the icy cold, the people who walked by would stop and ask us, and I could explain why it's important to vaccinate our children, for example.
These brave people make you move and forget about that figure of a know-it-all teacher.
But it's not easy.
There are many unwilling to participate in this process, and they prefer note-taking and giving lectures.
Let's not blame them.
Studying like that is far more comfortable, although less effective.
But for each subject there are a few pioneers who throw you in.
I have been in education since I was 18.
At first, as a scout leader, and now I've been 20 years at different universities, and these pioneer students have always existed, even though I couldn't see them.
So, with them, I remember some of their names, but many have already taken part in my madness and here you see them at a pub in the center of Valladolid, explaining to people what our defenses mean and what happens if we get an allergy.
As you can see, in a very relaxed environment, with some beers.
So yes, friends, the most important ingredient or the most powerful one, is students.
I have been doing this for years, I have worked with hundreds, and we are building, thanks to them, a very valuable project.
What did I start with?
With the simplest thing: some notes.
Seeing as students take notes, value them.
Let's take notes together, correct each others and each year we'll have better notes.
Well, that's one of the first elements.
But then, suddenly, an extremely clever kid came by and he helped me create animations of our defense tissues and he helped me create three-dimensional animations of very complex laboratory techniques that are used today as a reference for learning on Youtube.
In technology, they are ahead of me.
Why should we ban it in the classroom?
Let's use it to our advantage.
We're on Pinterest, on Youtube, on Twitter.
We create, with immunology students from all over the world, an on-line journal.
They are glad to participate.
And besides, this project has attained national and international recognition and awards.
I show it everywhere I can, to see whether it can start new ideas, but I don't forget to go back to the streets every year, with people, and show defenses with beer.
We prepare very didactic, but rigorous, material.
The students themselves help me.
And someone dresses up as a deadly bacteria to hijack a walled body, as you can see.
Others, in the form of a newscast, tell us about an infection as if it were a catastrophe.
The star product of this collection are the so-called "immunopills."
They are mini teaching videos, of around 5 minutes, of very high quality, the only ones in Spanish.
They have been translated into French and English and they have been played over 1,200,000 times.
This collection is called "Immunomedia." It started being successful in the Spanish-speaking world, but today it has reached every corner of the planet and has helped over 250,000 students study in 100 countries.
You can imagine that for me, to manage this project, is a responsibility that makes me proud.
I started completely by myself.
Teachers started helping me, universities, technical specialists, and above all, students.
I have been on an unforgettable path with them.
I have learned that a teacher who knows everything doesn't exist, it's not a monolith you need to hide under.
So, when they asked me to come here today, I undoubtedly asked them: Boys, girls, what do you think? Do I have
something important to say at TEDx?
And they replied, unanimously: Yes, Alfredo, we are with you on the red dot.
So here they are, more than ever, even if you don't see them. You see them, right?
They have accompanied me in this process.
(Applause) Thank you.
(Laughter) They have accompanied me in this process and have made me enter the arena and discover that I must be the guide to their learning.
This had led me to overcome my own fears.
I had to overcome the fear of ridicule and the fear of having people around me give their opinions.
(Music) (Performance mode) See you soon, my future doctors!
My work here is done.
Here, in this faculty's hall, it's time to part ways.
(End performance mode) So yes ... (Music ends) (Applause) You can innovate against the current.
If you feel the same, I invite you to take that leap.
You will feel an awful sense of vertigo and fear, but the jump will be worth it.
Identify those hidden ingredients that are around you and that will help you.
The worst that can happen is that you reinvent yourselves.
And to my teacher colleagues, to all those universities that are resisting: I ask you to join learning with capital letters.
You know the ingredients, the methodology is available,
and the silent accomplices
are there, waiting for you.
Yes. Now. You have no excuse.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
A person runs a red light and a police officer stops them.
Police officer, "ID, please."
Person, "Here you are, officer!"
The officer, "You crossed against the red light."
Clearly. Otherwise he wouldn't have stopped him, right?
But he said: "You crossed against the red light," and that's a potential starting point for a dialogue.
The person explains himself: "'I'm late to pick up the kids," "The light was yellow," "Others crossed against it too"...
The officer says or rahter threats, "I will have to fine you." 'I will have to fine you'
means he might, or might not fine him.
He has this possibility, he has this liberty.
And that immediately makes the offender pose the question everyone knows by heart, right?
How can we fix it?
How can we fix it?
The 'how' is easy; everyone knows how.
It's called 'bribe.'
The 'it' is the issue. What is this 'it' we can fix?
The 'it,' the problem we have, is the law.
It's the law that forces him to fine me, and me, to pay.
The law is for both of us something 'we can fix.'
We can, between the two of us; he is no longer an officer and I an offender, we are now a fraternal community.
We are equals.
He already took his cap off, and I am relieved of my concerns.
And how we fixed it was bribery.
Of course, we do not call it so.
We say: "Today, they did me a 'gauchada' (good turn)." "Today they did me a favor." "They made an exception
to the rule for me."
"They did me a 'gauchada' (good turn)."
Why do we say 'gauchada' in our language?
Because we all remember that desperate night in the Argentinian literature when a rural police sergeant shouted: "Cruz does not consent a brave man to die like this!"
And he joined the deserter Martín Fierro in his fight against the soldiers.
This image of the 'gauchada' is what we replay again each time we run a red light.
I am Martin Böhmer, I am a Professor of Law.
You can imagine it's complicated to be a professor of law in this culture.
However, I sensed that this way with the law didn't have to remain like this.
How have it been?
I got into the Law School at the end of the dictatorship and the beginning of democracy.
And I lived under the worst illegal state authorities we have ever had in our history.
But I sensed, I insist, that law and politics could be different.
However, amid Argentina's political darkest night something happened.
When the State turned into the criminal and knocking down houses' doors, it kidnapped, tortured, killed and made thousands of people disappear, the families of the disappeared started searching.
Mothers, in particular.
What did the mothers do?
What all mothers in Argentina did when they had a problem:
they began to look for that person who knew the person who knew the right person who could do something.
And when, during our centralized presidentialism, we start looking for someone who can help us, we generally end up at the same address: Balcarce, 50, the Casa Rosada.
All mothers ended up knocking at this door, doing what all mothers did, and they received as an answer what the State always answers: they were given a number.
And the mothers, like we all do faced with this, did what we all would have done:
they formed a line. And in this line, they did what we all do: they talked.
This is how they knew they weren't alone. And when they started talking with each other, the State did what it always does in these situations: it forces us to keep on walking to prevent us from talking.
The mothers kept on walking.
What they did was crossing the street. They crossed the street to Plaza de Mayo.
In Plaza de Mayo an officer told them what cops always tell us, when people are standing, talking: "keep on walking."
So the Mothers kept on walking in Plaza de Mayo, and they keep on walking even today.
What were all these Mothers asking for?
They wanted to know where their sons and daughters were. If they had been detained, which judge ruled for their imprisonment, which prosecutor accused them, and which lawyer defended them.
And if any crimes had been committed against their children, they wanted a fair trial and a sentence.
A lawyer can make a simple translation of what the Mothers were asking for: the right to know the truth and the right to a fair trial.
When the mothers did this and asked for this, they weren't anymore each one a mother of a disappeared person, they were the Mothers of the Disappeared. The Mothers of Plaza de Mayo.
The politicians in Argentina answered that claim, and as you all know, when the democracy set in, they said, "Never again."
In the report submitted by CONADEP and "Never again" in the Trial of the Juntas.
What happened to the society?
We all saw the Mothers's model; their recipe, which is: to find a problem, a cause, translate it to the language of law, of rights, make it a collective cause, belonging to a group, with this group put together an NGO, or any other type of association, and then take that cause to court.
Other NGOs started to appear, claims started to stack up, and for the first time in Argentina's history, the claim wasn't made by a gaucho on the run from the law who replied with crime to an illegitimate authority. Now the claim is about the rights of citizens who feel part of a political system that belongs to them.
I sensed there was more to it, like many of my colleagues, and I found there my true profession, or one of the roles for my profession: to take the citizens' complaint, translate it to the language of law and take it to court.
Together with many colleagues we created NGOs, legal clinics of public interest in Law Schools to take care of the cases free of charge.
Free lawyers multiplied, lawyer's NGOs multiplied.
And as you know, many of the cases that weren't brought to court before are on trial now, and luckily, many judges listened to these claims and they have been vindicating rights as never before in our history.
The saga of the outlaw gaucho has an awful, deadly end, in the novel Juan Moreira.
You all probably remember the end, especially the end of the movie. Moreira, impressive, is trying to get rid of the police, with a knife between his teeth when he is stabbed in the back by a bayonet and there his terrible life ends.
Moreira had also been a gaucho pursued by an illegitimate authority.
However, the book of Juan Moreira doesn't end where we all think it ends.
Surprisingly, in its second edition the author, Gutiérrez, reworked a final scene which I did not know and I want to tell you about.
A lawyer, during one summer he had no money to pay for a vacation, recalled a client's offer to come to his ranch for a few days so he decided to take it.
The ranch was in the lands of Navarro, Moreira's birthplace.
The laywer, on his worn-out horse was riding the lands of Navarro afraid that Moreira would show up. He sees in the distance a gaucho stopping his passage.
Indeed, it was Juan Moreira.
He asks the lawyer a fateful question:
"What are you doing here?"
The lawyer believes his years on this earth are over.
Yet he answers: "I came to visit Juan el Chico," to which Moreira retorts: "but Juan was taken to Buenos Aires to be shot."
And the lawyer answers: "No, Juan was arrested, was taken to Buenos Aires, he was put on trial, I was defending him and he got out."
Moreira says: "Juan must have mortgaged his house to pay you," and the lawyer replies: "No, under circumstances like these, I don't charge for my services, it's my duty."
Moreira couldn't believe his ears and big tears started falling down his face.
He blesses the lawyer and tell him that his is at his service for whatever he needs, and let him go.
This is how 'Juan Moreira' ends.
My question is: Why does Moreira cry?
I think he cries because, if it's true that the gauchos weren't taken to Buenos Aires to be shot, but they were given rights and a due process, with the help of lawyers that defend them free of charge, then his life was meaningless.
His outlaw life of being a criminal rebel made no sense.
The loss of his family, his estrangement from his son, what he called his unfortune, had no meaning.
And there I understood what me and my colleagues, following the example of the democratic transition in Argentina and the bright example of our rights, what we are trying to do everyday: nothing else but try to never allow on our land to be heard again the cry of another Moreira.
(Applause)
How are you? Good evening.

Welcome everyone.
I want to share with you some of my experience, that in my work has led me to know a lot of people and make me close to a lot of associations, different organizations, social groups, each of them with their struggles, with their concerns, with their sorrows. In that way I've become more aware of them.
I feel very fortunate for it.
So up until now, this moment I've reached the conclusion, that doesn't mean it won't change in the future.
But until now I think that all fights are the same fight.
It is a joyful struggle, a loving struggle.
It is a fight that comes down from the head to the heart.
Just like my hair-do.
There, in our chest, a fire ignites.
A fire burns.
In fact, it has been lit since forever, waiting for us, looking out for us.
Huehuetl it was called by the old Mexicans the most ancient.
There it is, he is asking us to gather together, to sit in a circle around him.
To listen to the grandfather, his words, his wise words.
In silence.
With respect.
There I listen, I listen to mommy saying, with her sweet voice: "Is it just a dream?"
I wake up! I have to make money,
I have to get working.
I try to remember my dream, but by that moment, it's already late, my haste to organize my day with everything that I've got to do, wins over.
The dream, the dream vanished.
I'm so tired that I can't even get up 10 minutes earlier to calm my mind.
Focus on my breathing, and connecting with that fire.
Or at least, give a little review through my relationships, through my affections, thanking them, praying for them and for the situations that need to be solved.
No, not the ones from work:
those of my life, and the life of those surrounding me, but I rather use energy on work situations and I'm already running because it's getting late.
At last I take strength to enter the world, I walk to the bathroom, I pee, and take a dump.
Sometimes, when I have time, I ask that together with my feces, my fears, my resentments, my anger and all my filth go away, but today it's not the day.
I'm in a hurry, so I wipe myself and flush, the water goes, taking everything with it, I take a shower, shampoo, rinse, soap, the water still runs.
I wash my teeth, toothpaste with fluorine.
Water keeps running, a lot of deodorant, so I don't smell like myself, because people doesn't like it.
But I do, if I'm being honest.
I have breakfast, there's no time to squeeze some oranges, much less to cook, so a slice of bread will do, with ham and jam, boxed milk and bottled water.
My headache pills, pills for a cold, a heartburn or for everything at once, and I go out.
Today I could have gone by bike, but I must carry stuff besides it is so late that I'm going to go by car, and I'll drive along the huge highway that passes above the place where once stood the park where I played as a kid.
The magnificent trees are gone.
My phone has a year and a half and it's starting to fail, the battery lowers so fast and it's just about to run out, today that I needed to make some calls as I drive, and I forgot to charge it the night before.
It makes me think that maybe my phone is old and I should buy a new one.
That's it, the solution is that I need a new cellphone.
This morning I didn't make love, I barely said goodbye to my wife with a tight kiss, like those from the soap operas.
We hardly looked each other in the eyes for a microsecond, the same with my children and my pet.
Once immerse in every day life everything becomes easier.
Everything is awesome!
Everything is automatic!
We think less, there's more security, we all know how to behave what to say, what to think, what to eat, what to talk.
Everything is easier.
But once I'm off my job, when I go out to my daily life and I'm alone, I'm so tired after hours on the traffic, after a job that makes no sense because I don't know where it ends.
I just have energy to get into the screen and escape from this world with some entertaining TV show.
Action, sex, sports, who cares.
I skip news.
It's always the same.
Educational reform, drug trafficking strikes again, politicians have no shame, economy in the brink of a new recession.
The prices for basic alimentary necessities rise again.
Gas prices keep escalating, currency keeps depreciating.
Global warming, the neighbor comes to humiliate me at my own home.
Our idol, a great talent, is gone.
No, what I need is a lot of entertainment because my life gets so boring when I tune this channel.
I today I need so much screen action because my life absolutely lacked any action, I need so much sex because I didn't gave myself time to be intimate, I was scared or lazy to do so.
And so days pass by.
Some good, others, the more, dry like this day in which Samsara, like the Buddhist brothers call it, ran over me and shattered me.
Personally I feel very fortunate because I had a close up with the Wirrárika people, mistakenly called, Huicholes, a village of dreamers.
They have their men of knowledge, they are called Marakate, They gather and devote
their time to dream, They focus all of their attention in their dreams because it is in dreams where all the information to solve this other reality is found.
Right there in the desert of Wirikuta, Wirrarika sacred territory, they are now fighting against mega mining that threatens to pollute all the environment; half-blood communities live there, and they have dry bathrooms, with very advanced technology, not like here in the city.
Oftentimes I think in the gaze of my dog it stares at me when I scold him for peeing or for pooping in a room.
It stares at me as if saying "what about you, dude! You pee and crap in clean water!"
I remember that scientific article which talked about how fluorine, present in tooth paste, gets trapped in the pineal gland, the Third Eye in a lot of cultures, where the soul resides and it's the core of spirituality.
I know too of the chemicals in shampoo and in mouthwash that take oxygen from water.
And the chemicals in deodorant that block my body,
and prevent it from sweating. I know of the amount of sugar present in those jams, and the damage that white flour does. I have read a couple of books, "Sugar Blush" and "Grain Brain" they are good and they explain how sugar is of the worst poisons, and if today I'm reading, my friends, it's because my mind can't take it, it is true that in my youth I abused of hard drugs: sugar and flour.
(laughs) (applause)
The transgenics. My bread.
I have heard innumerable discussions about transgenics where the discussion itself is no longer about how the seed was modified, because corn, our corn, has been modified for thousands of years to tame it, so it could be useful as food to us.
No, that's not the issue, the issue is how unethical is wanting to patent what belongs to all of us.
In the seed lies the promise that everything is for everyone.
Life can't be patented.
Besides, it's threatening to our species to keep polluting water and earth with all the agro industrial chemicals that those companies sell.
And we are not even talking about the extermination of biodiversity.
My bottled water, dead, so very dead.

My milk, do you know what milk is?
It's a cocktail of blood, pus, antibiotics and yes, a bit of milk, it's super nutritious! Bon appetit!
And it's also a perversion, tree times perversion and it's not like I'm against perversions, perversions are delicious.
I loved ice creams and cheeses.
But here is the perversion: first, it is not your mother, second, you are adults, milk is for babies.
And it is not even from your species.
But go ahead, suckers.
(laughter) My ham, I've seen videos of the inhuman treatment that our animal brothers are subject to in those so called "farms", all nicely painted in the outside but are industries of pain and torture.
I have this pretty anecdote, of a friend that went to visit the Mapuche natives, in the south of the continent.
A woman, before lunch, hands him over a knife and tells him: "Please kill the goat."
And my friend frozes.
And the woman stares at him, takes the knife from him, and strikes the goat's neck.
And she says: "It's done, now, skin it."
For a second time, my friend freezes.
The woman turns around with a sigh,
she cuts open the goat, skins it, and says: "Now eviscerate it, please." Ahhh!
"Give it to me!"
And she says to him: "Explain me something, you eat meat but you don't know how to kill?"
No, my friends, don't get me wrong.
I'm not trying to rile up the carnivores, the question goes for everyone.
We eat, right?
We eat, yet don't know how to sow.
We eat, yet don't know how to harvest.
Very good, very good.
My medicines, the pharmaceutical industry, taken hostage as all the other areas of the human knowledge and put to the service of revenues.
I know that our plastic culture and oil by products are polluting everything.
I know there's an island the size of the Iberian Peninsula in the Pacific Ocean.
made purely of plastic wastes.
Have you heard of micropearls?
They are used in gel soaps, toothpastes that end up right into the sea, and combine with the plankton.
The city air is unbreathable for those who are not used to it.
But we are cockroaches! Yes!
There are so many cars
clustered in stationary clots, farting hot gases, disgusting.
My body is already tired by the time I reach my destination after so much time in front of the steering wheel, my brain is already highly stressed and the work day is yet to begin.
My hand, ear and head too heat up while I talk on the phone.
My distraction almost made me have an accident, but today I was lucky.
I've read about Tantalum and Coltan.
The minerals thanks to which our computers and our smartphones can transmit, thanks to which corporations can organize wars, where guns are given to children and the innocent become the most ruthless killers.
In the news stand I get aroused with boobs and bums, blood and guns! Yeah!
I see how my children are though useless things.
The discussion on the education reform focuses more on administrative issues and in policies instead of what's really important.
Anyways, I don't really care and I don't take part of it.
Let the teacher sort themselves, they are slackers, they say: closing streets to make demonstrations.
I have heard stories from close people who disappeared, were tortured, kidnapped, or murdered by some Mafia.
We are in crossed fire.
I know that these changes, reforms and new administrations. This other new political party and that "work for the people" are just promises and lies.
I know that economic recession has never ended and never, ever have we had fat cows times.
I know perfectly well that my purchasing power is always less, the same happens to my quality of life, that's not the same as last year, and the year before that.
All my life, 49 years, I've lived in a chronic crisis, and it keeps getting worst, always.
Weather patterns are gone, it rains acid and otherworldly flooding occur, then everything gets so dry it looks like a desert.
The cold forces us to take cover as if we lived in the poles,
and air is filled with illnesses.
Time? There's never time for anything.
Somebody has time? Can you sell me some?

TV is a mock to intelligence.
There are edited lies or truths to get us confused.
The idea of love they depict has more to do with attachments with fear, control and manipulation.
Sport is mere business.
Sex is about putting in and pulling out, silicone and unnecessary information.
There's no art, no culture, all plots are about lawyers and policemen action that is shown, justifies repression.
Subduing methods, invasion, colonization, imperialism techniques: That is progress!
Making everything seem so cool, with well payed actors of shapes and gestures well defined by a scalpel.
If we are not careful, internet will be taken by the corporations and access to quality information is only going to get harder.
Yes my friends, my brothers: everything, everything is a fight.
Then again, I'm very fortunate because I have friends, I have friends who fight, today's speakers are fighting too.
Doña Trini of the Atenco village, whose relatives were incarcerated for defending their lands, Santos de la Cruz of the Wirrárika regional counsel against mining, Yunuén of the Counsel of youths, for defending their forests.
Relatives of the disappeared Ayotzinapa students, the mothers from Juarez. To Leonora, Ariana, Paulina, who fight for the sea cow, for the Mexican wolf, the bulls or the elephants, the animal rights, our brothers rights. for my cousin Ileana and her partner, that fight to be respected in a homophobic and closed society.
For Tomas and Mario, Indian Yaquis, incarcerated for defending water for Guancho, who fights against distractions to focus on his breathing and knows the uplifting Tantric practices of sacred sex.
To Paulina who lives a secluded life, learning from a Taita in the Ecuadorian jungle so she can prepare medicine for her village.
To uncle Alfonso, fighting for the legislation of those traditional medicines, no, not medication, but true medicine for us.
To Giovana, who fights against fracking that new practice to extract gas from the subsoil.
Incredible polluting! It pollutes
millions of liters of water.
Sandra, who fights to recycle PET.
To Jacky, who fights through urban gardens so we all start doing that and we have food, and if the economic systems fails, well, let it fail!
To Ofelia, who fights against hunger and malnutrition of indigenous children and for all those who fight for women rights, migrants rights, against touristic, highway, damps mega projects.
I think of the millions of years of human trajectory, and how we are about to lose it all. Everything would start again, but not for us. Most likely it will be another species.
A Colombian taita told me:
No! don't get it wrong, mother earth is not sick, we are the sick ones.
And if we don't reach the appointment with ourselves, and no one else.
If we don't take this chance, the planet will take over a thousand years to regain balance and make a new species arise, a new attempt of awareness.
Today. here, you, you, you, me, all together, we are awareness, we are that burning fire that is waiting for us.
The Mapuche people have a word: "Marichiweu!
that means: "we will conquer ten times." and me saying: "Marichiweu!"
Ten times conquering over myself.
This fight is happy, sweet,
it's delicious, it's exquisite.
It tastes! It has essence, it's delicious.
This fight comes down from the head to the heart, the place were that fire burns, where that little voice tells us, tells me that all fights are one.
This fight is for life.
Is the ceremony of life.
Is to confront and solve the depths, the mystery that waits for us.
There it is: to recognize us as sacred.
All of us and everything that exists.
Everything we know, everything is sacred.
Tomorrow when I wake up, my friends, I will pay attention and I will try to remember my dream, the dream of each and everyone of us.
The dream of humankind.
Me dreaming that humans are going to make it.
Thank you very much.
(applauses)
Those who received this paper will be the referees of the game.
And the idea is that players can't see it, so if anyone caught it, stole it, or saw it, then do not play; if you want, be a referee, but if not, don't play.
Especially if you saw what is in here, --this I show you without you seeing it-- if you saw this, then, you should not play.
I'll give you quickly the instructions, and after that we execute them.
The first task of the referee is to look for players.
Don't do it yet.
Players are going to be the person in front of you and behind you.
You want to play with your brother, cousin, friend, but you will play with the one in front and at your back.
That's the first thing you will do.
Each group of three people, also has to have a pen because the referee have to write.
Give the pen to the referee, who is the writer, the referee always writes.
If none of the three has a pen, there are many volunteers around here giving pens.
In a while, when I tell you, you will look for the two players, and then what the players will do is --if Max is with me-- a thumb war.
For those who don't know what it is or have forgotten your childhood, I'll show it to you with Max, which is the great thumb war champion.
Thumb war is played as follows: You shake hands, look at each other, and greet with the thumb war courtesy.
The thumb war greeting is doing: one, two, three; and each one tries to put their thumb on the other's thumb and there the referee counts: one, two, three, and when this happens,
the one who won scores a point.
And you go on. We keep playing, Max!
One, two, three, and we go on...
Thanks, we're done!
We keep playing like this for a minute.
The goal of each player, is to score, in that minute, as many points as possible.
We believe everything, but the role of the referee is precisely to testify that things are well recorded, referees will have something like this, where they must keep the score of the game.
Each column is the first point.
For example, Max is the first player, then you score one point to Max, imagine that then I score a point, you put a point to me, and so on. If you run out of space, no problem, write it manually --this is my handwriting-- the number of points each player scored.
So, the referee looks for their players, players shake hands, play a thumb war.
Let's start now.
The last ingredient --in TEDx we never do things normally-- is that the thumb war is played blindly, with eyes closed.
And you will see that it is lovely, trying to guess the position of your own thumb and the other's, without seeing it.
This is an experiment, we won't do things easy.
Now, I ask you to be focused and effective, the referees, turn around, take your two players, --we will make some noise now-- greet, introduce yourselves, grab the pens, shake hands.
We have 20 seconds to organize 10,000 people.
Go!
We will start when I tell you, not yet.
Do not start!
Shake hands, we'll give you 20 seconds and we start all together.
If someone doesn't make a group, don't worry.
Some will not be able to play, without someone in front or behind you, but it's not a problem.
Get ready, make groups, referees with pens, we start in 10 seconds!
Those who didn't make a group don't worry, let's start.
To the count of three; when I count three the minute starts, OK?
One, two we start!
Time's up! Stop! You can open your eyes, stop!
Now I need everyone to stop and be quiet.
We will disarrange and arrange back.
The referees, complete the score and remain standing up, but in silence; or I'll lose my voice.
Referees finish to score and I ask the players.
Hands up those who scored at least two points.
Raise your hand.
OK, there are some champions... Raise your hand those who scored at least four points.
Very few, very few reached the highest level.
Very few within 10,000.
We finished. We will go for another round.
But before that,
--remain as you are, you will play again--
I remind you again of the exact instructions I gave you of the game: each player has to try to score as many points as possible.
I remind you that this doesn't mean having to score more points than the other player. It doesn't mean that if you lose you have to feel bad or that you lose points, or that you have to make more points than the others.
Each person has to think how --and maybe it is not about killing the other-- you have to think together, how to achieve the goal you have. You may need to collaborate, cooperate, you guess. Let's play again with the goal each one has of trying to score as many points as possible: no matter if everyone scores a lot as well, what you want is to do as many points as possible.
Let's start, rearrange yourselves back on this premise, and we will play again.
One, two,
go! One minute!
Time's up! Stop there!
Stop!
Stop! Now we stop the game.
I ask the referees...
Let's lower the volume;
now we go back to order.
The game's finished, you can all sit down again.
If you want, if not, stay up, do what you want.
We will go back so that we can continue.
Hands up those who scored at least two points.
Virtually everyone. This is a nice world, in which nobody loses, really.
How many scored at least six points?
A lot! So, notice that something good
and something obvious happened: you all discovered... there was a hidden joke you all discovered immediately, that we will discuss now.
First, I think it's appropriate that we make a celebration because with this TEDx we just entered the Guinness World Records as the largest number of thumb wars ever played!
(Applause) (Ovation)
Look at this! Look at this!
Not even in China they play this amount of thumb wars!
(Laughter) And beating the Chinese in their game and in crowds is something!
It is one of the many achievements of today!
We didn't do the experiment for this... or did we?
But now we have to put together all the excuses for why we did what we just did.
With Nicolás Stier, my friend and colleague who studies traffic: how to optimize traffic, how make it work well, and, in general, how to make work well human networking situations, situations where there are people interacting, like today.
We thought this game was very nice as it illustrated two fundamental principles: the first is that cooperation pays.
It not only pays from a romantic and altruistic worldview, but it pays, as you realized, in that it helps each person to get closer to their goal.
Precisely, that was something obvious for everyone in this game, the strategy you all discovered of: "I go, you go, I go, you go." You all discovered it even when you were told, that you could not even speak.
The referee must have told you that you couldn't see or talk, anyway you discovered this trick, that was the most effective in the game and is also the most effective in traffic.
In a bottleneck, instead of fighting to go slow on one lane, it's better to go and let go, go and let go. This is not only better, but more effective for each driver.
So, this is a simple point, it isn't a great discovery, but the most interesting part is this: if in retrospect this was obvious for all of you, as it happened: why most of us didn't realize from the start that if the goal we had was to score as many points possible, the best solution was to cooperate?
This is the big problem.
Why didn't we understand from the start that cooperation was the best solution for the problem we faced?
And this is called generically, zero-sum game fallacy.
Zero-sum games are those like sports: there is no choice but to compete.
What you win, I lose.
There is a fixed amount; 100 points.
If you win 80, I'm left with only 20 points.
Then, the strategy is to compete, inevitably, because for me to win, you have to lose.
The fallacy we have discovered in this game, is to assume that many life situations are zero-sum games, when actually they are not.
In other words, it is not being able to shut down the competition chip, even when being competitive is detrimental to what we do.
And there are many situations and illustrations of this in life.
One, I chose to tell you, is about a ubiquitous and ancestral resistance to immigration, to new people, a group that arrives to a society, a country.
And a great motivation, a great source of this resistance, is to think that we have a finite number of resources, such as jobs.
That if more people come, they will compete for the same jobs, they will earn more, therefore, I lose.
This fallacy is the same fallacy which made us, in the first game, go and compete against the other, because it is not true, in any way, that the amount of jobs in an economy is constant. The economy and the amount of jobs fluctuate; it is not a zero-sum game.
Therefore, competing is not the best strategy.
And in the game we played, we didn't fight for jobs, nor for the money, the limited resource was time.
We had one minute, and what all we learned almost immediately, magically, is learning to socialize time.
And what I want to tell you today, is that the reason why people don't cooperate, is not because they don't have the predisposition, but because language is opaque.
Even when the instruction is clear: "You have to score as many points as possible" the message is not clear.
We don't hear all the time what we are told.
Then, the experiment was about language and time.
And here in this space which is TEDxperiment, we always play two games: this one we just played, and the other in the previous days.
We played it with 2,000 or 3,000 of the people here and we will now bring it to light.
In which we also wondered about language and time, the common theme of the two TEDxperiments.
And what we wanted to answer is clear in a sentence, which also is a phrase that comes effortlessly, and it is: "Christmas is coming."
You heard this phrase, it's simple, with no great belonging, and it evidently means we are at the end of the year.
And besides, you get a feeling: "Christmas is coming and I had to do a lot of stuff..." If you think of it through the magnifying glass of language, this phrase is strange: "Christmas is coming."
From where?

From Chile? From San Francisco?
From Luján? From California?

Where is Christmas?
The strange thing is that Christmas is not in space, is in time.
And what you unfailingly can't do, and that's where language appears, is to think that time, besides being in time, is in space.
And not only that, but almost all of us have a stereotype, a way of thinking, in which we assume that the future is ahead, because one moves into the future, and Christmas is coming like a wind blowing in your face.
"Fast, because Christmas comes!
I'm already there!"
As if you are going through the wall of a deadline, you have to solve something and you're on the verge of crossing it.
It's almost obvious, that's why language is so, that the future lies ahead and the past behind.
Well, that is not so.
Not for everyone.
For example, the Aymara, a native people from South America, they are here in Argentina, Chile, in Bolivia, Peru, they are 3 million people, and they have a language that exquisitely and beautifully associates time and space in another way.
For the Aymara, the word "naira" means "past", but it also means "ahead."
And the word "kipa" means "future," but also "behind."
So, when they speak, they speak opposite to how we speak Spanish, putting naturally the future behind, and the past ahead.
This seems strange to us because we all have a chip, a way of thinking, but when they explain it, it is extremely natural.
The Aymaras say the following.
Look what a lovely way to think time.
The only thing we know, and therefore, the only thing we see, all that the eyes see, is the past.
The past is what I know, what my eyes see.
Far, very far away, lies the most distant past; I see it blurry, dimly.
Much closer, here, close to my eyes, lies the recent past, and behind lies the uncertain future, what my eyes can't see.
In fact, the Aymara go through time walking backwards.
And when they walk backwards, that which is the uncertain future, becomes seen, becomes the past.
The Aymara think time the opposite to us and we did an experiment to understand how each of us, those of us here in this space, represent time.
And for that, we made a simple game, in which we asked you to draw three circles: One is the past --which I placed over left--, another circle is the present and another circle is the future.
Everyone drew it as they wanted, the size they wanted, the radius they wanted, and with that simple and geometric language they were expressing how they think time.
And the result of that, with my friend Mariano Sardon we turned it into some visuals, is this the skeleton of time.
What you see is something that to me looks like a kind of butterfly, in which naturally emerges the past to the left, the present at the center, and to the right of the butterfly is the future.
Past, present and future is the skeleton through which we represent time.
It's simple, it has to do with writing and moving forward in time from left to right, which forces one representation of time.
Over this skeleton, there are very colorful and interesting ways to think time and I want to talk about three kinds.
Three ways that those of us here, have to think time.
The first kind is what I call the cosmocentric.
The ones for me who think time --I only put three, but they are many, tens and hundreds of these-- they think time from a universal, physical perspective, where the present is ephemeral, there's essentially no present, there is an enormous, infinite future, and there is a future of past to the other side.
And in fact, the present is at the center of time.
There are also cosmocentrics who are more optimistic, for them the present is ephemeral, but they understand that there's much more future than past, and there are also pessimists who, in universal time, understand the present as ephemeral, but that we are much more closer to the end than the beginning of time.
These are the cosmocentrics, one kind, some of us who think time like this.
And there are the opposites, who I call, quite simply, the nowcentrics.
The nowcentrics are those who think time from the subjective narrative, actually.
"Look, the present is fleeting, but all I know is the present.
All the time I dwell in the present."
From this perspective of subjective space, the present is everything, the future and the past are small satellites, decorating and feeding the present.
In fact, the maximum nowcentric is this one for whom the present and the future live literally within the present. All that exists is the present.
And the third and final group I want to show are these, see some examples, but they are many, we are many, too, who are like these, representing time, each one, differently: some more stretched, others more elongated, others more concentric, others upwards, downwards, but they all agree that the future is larger than the past.
And following the notation that I chose, I thought of calling them futurecentrics.
It seemed a very appropriate term, but besides pretentious, the name is weird, because it is much simpler: these people are "young".
(Laughter) People who make these circles are young people, and naturally, from their life perspective, --no longer that of universal time, but the concept of time in our own lives-- they understand they have a broad future, much broader than the past, because they are just beginning the adventure of life.
Thank you very much!
(Applause)
I ask myself what are imperfections what is it that I consider imperfect.
One of the greatest imperfections that I have found in my travels of the world is the forgotten.
Dramas and conflicts that shouldn't be forgotten.
So I ask myself: does my work rescue those imperfect situations from oblivion that we don't know or we don't want to see?
Forgotten are the women burned by husbands who demand a larger dowry from the wife's family without getting it.
Forgotten is the mother who lost her 8-year-old boy to the gunshots of soldiers from the Israeli army that the boy threw rocks at.
Forgotten are the corpses from the Mayan genocide exhumed in Guatemala, where there were 200,000 people missing or murdered.
Forgotten is Palden Ghyatso, imprisoned and tortured for 33 years for demonstrating against the Chinese army occupying his country.
Forgotten is Ghulam Yamm, a girl who came into the world with a tumor that disfigured her face and who was discriminated from enrolling to study in Hyderabad, India.
They are faces of suffering, the faces of the imperfect.
I have created articles in different areas of the world as an independent photographer, some, when they had already stopped being news in the international press.
Earthquakes, tsunamis, refugee camps, or displaced persons, hospitals, etc. It's in those scenes where I did a large part of my work, where the consequences will last for years afterwards from the disasters it has produced.
In the misery and suffering I found an abundance of anonymous heroes, common people who left the comforts of their life to help others.
Anonymous heroes like Alan Cantos and Joselias Estévez, from the Tibetan Support Committee, that after 7 years of struggle got the National Audience to search and internationally arrest the 5 highest Chinese leaders responsible for the genocide that has been committed in Tibet since the country was occupied in 1959.
Another conflict, forgotten by the world, which I have dedicated an important part of my life and work to since 1997.
A conflict that could also be left to be forgotten after the Spanish Government, under pressure from China, modified the Law of Universal Justice to avoid Spain's prosecution of those murderers.
Anonymous heroes who help rescue the forgotten the victims of these imperfect situations.
Like Vicente Ferrer, who chose to live in Anantapur, one of the poorest areas of India.
Today, 50 years later, his work benefits 3 million people, the majority from lower castes.
Ferrer stopped being an anonymous hero in order to become a leader.
In the company of the writer Manuel Rivas we traversed that rural India.
"For Our Gods and Slaves," the series about women published in the El País newspaper, and for the book "The Difficult Path to the Stars," we interviewed many people.
Among them Anaga Laxmi who was sold into prostitution in Delhi.
China Devi married at 11 to an older man.
The girl Sindhu, who was buried alive because she was believed to be the devil when she was born with a cleft lip.
or the previously mentioned Ghulam Yamm, discriminated for her physical appearance.
Untouchables, forgotten people, scorned, resigned to living outside of the world.
Another example of the imperfection of the universe, the human imperfection.
The article "Untouchable Engineers Made in India," that we did with the journalist Lola Huete Machado for El País Semanal, tells the story of Lathamma, Manjunatha, Shatru and Subhashini, youth from a low caste, born in small towns where opportunities are non-existent.
They had opportunities thanks to the Rural Development Trust, which made their education possible.
Today they're engineers in Bangalore and Hyderabad, The Indian Silicon Valley.
This is the success story of 4 untouchable teenagers.
Imperfection is ignorance.
Education is the foundation in any culture, the raw material of the perfect.
I know AI met the Catalan teacher Victoria Subirana when starting her adventure in Nepal.
The Aleki school was the origin of her great educational project.
She spent more than 20 years educating the country's poorest children.
I went through the Sukumbasi neighborhood of Katmandu with her in search of the untouchable's children who joined their first school.
For example Shiva, a blind and autistic boy that due to superstition was kept shut up and tied up at home, until the age of twelve.
He learned English and music at school.
Or Rupesh, the boy from the mountains who at 8 years old got up before sunrise to do jobs for the house where he worked in exchange for food and housing.
The school gave microcredits to 60 families.
Some bought the land they live on, others started small businesses like purchasing a sewing machine, or a chicken farm, etc.
Thanks to those credits the neighborhood now isn't a shanty town, and some of the children have been able to go to university.
The book "Vicki Subirana, a Teacher in Katmandu" Inspired a film maker Iciar Bollaín for his movie "Katmandu, a Mirror in the Heavens."
In the company of my friend, the writer and journalist Javier Moro we have traversed Pakistan, Laos, Brazil, the Lampang hospitals in Thailand, where they treat sick elephants or elephants hurt by anti-personnel mines and very especially India, where we wrote dozens of articles and visited the sights of some of his books: From the Majarajahs' Punjab from "Passion India" to the Calcutta shantytowns or the city of Bhopal where a toxic gas leak from the Union Carbide pesticides plant left 30,000 dead and half a million affected who've continued fighting for 30 years so that there is justice,
now exactly 30 years in December.
Anonymous heroes like Sister Christopher, a Scottish nun who cared for orphans on the night of the toxic leak and spent more than 50 years lavishing her care in the House of Hope, an orphanage founded with her own hands, the only one that exists in Bhopal to take care of abandoned children with serious brain problems.
Anonymous heroes, although in this case not so much, since the doctor Pramod Karan Sethi ended up appearing in the Guinness World Records Book because of the Jaipur foot, the prosthetic developed over 40 years and that's made with materials that are easily found in any country and is the cheapest and most effective for those who spend their lives in the third world.
Anonymous heroes like Bobin, who learned to cure leprosy In the Gads of the Ganges river, looking for sick or injured people in the train station and the surrounding shanty towns, several days a week visiting the Kabir Choura hospital, where he cleaned and treated the injuries of a group of burned women, sometimes paid, sometimes not.
Since 1997, I've followed the Tibetan exile from the world.
This for me was a year of changes.
I was unhappy with my life and my job.
I packed and went to India and Nepal where I would never return until two months later.
In the Katmandu reception center I came in contact for the first time with the refugees who fled Tibet.
I didn't see sadness in their gaze, they seemed content.
They said they'd escaped their country where the Chinese population already broadly overtook the Tibetans since the invasion in 1959, that had left around a million dead.
I came across Tensin, the nurse who treated the feet affected by frostbite with the new arrivals from the Nan Pala pass in midwinter, at 6,000 m altitude.
Among them Sandem, who is going to inevitably lose all his toes.
Others haven't had his luck because they've died on the journey.
On the return flight, I read a journal the review of the new Javier Moro book "The Mountains of Buddha," that dug into what I had seen on this trip in which even some of the people that I had just met appeared
I contacted him through the ABC newspaper assistant director and our meeting turned into an article for this publication that came to be the first of a large series about the Tibetan situation.
Months later I traveled to Dharamsala, also called the Little Tibet, since the Dalai Lama resides there and is situated in the headquarters of the exiled government.
A place populated by thousands of Tibetans with schools, monasteries, hospitals, and refugee camps.
My trip had started with complications days before departing I thought that I had broken my foot, I was even immobile in the bed crying from pain, it was an attack of gout.
It seemed that my trip was doomed to be canceled, but it wasn't.
A plane, a train, an all terrain car, and a couple of days later, I arrived at Little Tibet clothed in enormous slippers for my inflamed foot.
I had some days ahead to recover.
I had been granted a personal audience and a photo session with the Dalai Lama.
I don't know if you have experienced gout pain but that was the first time in my life and it's something terrible.
Arriving on the day of the audience with the Dalai Lama I showed him photos taken in the Katmandu refugee center
I hadn't before seen such impactful images from the consequences of the frostbite.
During our encounter I asked him if he still had a fondness for photography, that he at one point shared with the king of Bhutan, to which he responded that he now didn't have time for that.
"What's importance does photography have?", he said.
I began to explain to him what photography represented in my life, and he flatly interrupted me, "What you are doing is a service."
And I didn't say anything more.
I didn't understand those words until months later in my home in Madrid. A service, to do something useful for others.
That encounter of a couple of hours extended to two weeks in which I followed him and took photos almost every day.
It was August, monsoon time.
Dharamsala is one of the places where the rains fall with the most intensity.
One evening I left in search of two old monks who lived removed in a house in the forest.
Palden Gyatso and Thupten Tsering had been prisoners in the Tibetan Chinese prisons 33 and 27 years respectively.
I left the turtle pass that marked my gout.
I was going along the muddy path when it began to torrentially rain.
soaked, involuntarily hitting my painful foot against the rocks and semi lost in the forest, I asked myself what the hell I was doing here, and the images of the people who had just seen Nepal came to mind without toes on their feet and smiling
suddenly a monk appeared who I asked about Palden Gyatso.
She didn't understand me, she didn't speak English, and my four words of Tibetan also didn't help.
finally she motioned for me to follow her into the vegetation.
We walked for hours, endlessly, soaked.
We arrived at a house where a boy opened the door for us.
As he spoke English I asked him if the monk lived close.
It turned out she lived on the other side of the point where we met.
Crazily, he dropped everything to bring me to the house.
I was overwhelmed with a mix of gratefulness and shame.
The boy looked after Palden Gyatso and Thupten Tsering, political prisoners, two touching elders, we spoke, I took their photos, I don't stop asking myself how their eyes were so calm after years of imprisonment, torture, and suffering.
It reminded me of Nelson Mandela.
The latsu experience is told in the book "Fire Under the Snow".
The story of a simple monk, as he said, who was able to survive the totalitarian force.
Since this second trip, the Tibetan cause has been a personal issue I have bound and dedicated myself to, almost a mission.
I have made around 3 trips through Asia, Europe, Canada, and I hope to be able to get funding in order to continue in the US. where the largest community resides in the West.
I have documented their life in the great monasteries, schools, and camps.
I have been with them when they have come to testify in the National Audience, I have traveled with the yack caravans, through the terrible Himalayas, and on the train joins Peking to Lhasa accelerating the country's colonization.
Among the anonymous heroes I remember the 18 year old Swedish girls who dedicate their vacations to teaching English in a group of illiterate adults, some nomads, in Transit School, in the unsanitary camp school at the bank of the river where they taught basic skills, to those who should learn to defend themselves in a new world.
Anonymous heroes like many others, people who I learned from for trying to make with my small contributions a world with a little less imperfection.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
It's said that to be a poet you have to descend to the underworld once.
The first time I entered the prison, I was not surprised by the noise of the padlocks, the closing doors, or the bars, or anything of all the things I had imagined.
Maybe because the prison is in a quite open space.
You can see the sky.
Seagulls fly through the sky and you think you are next to the sea.
That you are really close to the beach.
But in fact, the gulls go to the dump near the prison looking for food.
I continued walking in and I suddenly saw inmates moving across the corridors.
It was as if I stepped back and thought that I could have perfectly been one of them.
If I had another story, another context, another luck.
Because nobody, nobody, can choose where to be born.
In 2009 I was invited to join a project the San Martín National University has in the Unit 48, to coordinate a writing workshop.
The prison service ceded some land at the end of the prison. Right there they constructed the building of the University Center.
The first time I met with the prisoners, I asked them why they were asking for a writing workshop and they told me they wanted to put on paper all that they couldn't say and do.
There I decided that I wanted poetry to enter the prison.
Then I told them why we don't work with poetry, if they knew what poetry was.
Nobody had a clue about what poetry really was.
Besides, they explained to me that the workshop was not only for graduated inmates, but also for all the common inmates.
And then I said that to start this workshop I needed some tool that we all have.
And that tool was language.
So, we had language, we had the workshop.
We could have poetry.
But what I didn't consider was the inequality also in prison. Many of them that didn't even have a complete primary education.
Many couldn't use cursive, but hardly print.
They didn't write fluently either.
So, we started looking for short poems. Short, but powerful indeed.
And started to read, and read an author and another author and by reading those short poems, they all begin to realize that what the poetic language did was to break a certain logic and it created another system.
Breaking the logic of language is also breaking the logic of the system they are used to respond to.
So a new system appeared, some new rules that made them understand really fast, but really fast, that with the poetic language they would absolutely say what they wanted.
It's said that to be a poet you have to descend to the hell once.
And they have plenty of hell.
Plenty of hell.
One of them once said:
"In prison you never sleep. You can never sleep in jail.
You can never close your eyelids".
Then, I did like this now, a moment of silence and tell them: That is poetry, that.
The prison universe is there, in hand.
All this you say, that you never sleep.

This exudes fear. All the unwritten.
All this is poetry.
So we started appropriating that hell.
And we plunged ourselves into, the seventh circle.
In that seventh circle of hell, our own and so beloved circle, they learned that walls could be invisible, windows could yell and that we could hide inside the shadows.
The first year the workshop had finished, we organized a little closing party as they are done when a job is done with so much love.
You want to celebrate and have a party.
We called family, friends, the university authorities.
The only thing they had to do was reading a poem, receiving their diploma, applause and that was our simple party.
The only thing I want to leave you with is the moment those men, at times huge when they stand by me.
Or very young boys, but with an enormous pride, they held their paper and trembled like kids and sweated and read their poems with their voice completely broken.
That moment made me think a lot that surely most of them were applauded for the first time for something they had done.
In prison there are things that can't be done.
In prison you can't dream, in prison you can't cry.
There are words that are virtually forbidden like the word time, the word future, the word wish.
But we dared to dream and to dream a lot because we decided that they were going to write a book.
Not only did they write a book
but they also bound it. That was by the end of 2010.
We doubled the bet and wrote another book.
And bound another book.
That was a short time ago, by the end of last year.
What I see week after week is how they are turning into different people, how they are being transformed.
How words empower them with a dignity they hadn't heard of, they couldn't even imagine.
They had no idea of such dignity and that could come from them.
At the workshop, in that beloved hell we have, we all give.
We open our hands and hearts and give
what we have, what we can. All of us.
All of us equally.
In that way you feel that at least, in little proportion you are repairing that huge social fracture that makes that for many of them prison is their only destination.
I remember a verse of a tremendous poet, a great poet, of the Unit 48 of our workshop, Nicolás Dorado: "I have to get an infinite thread to sew up this huge wound".
Poetry does that. It sews up
The wounds of exclusion.
It opens doors.
Poetry works as a mirror.
It creates a mirror, which is the poem.
They recognize themselves, they look at themselves in the poem and write from who they are and they are from what they write.
In order to write, they need to appropriate the moment of writing which is a moment of extraordinary freedom.
They have to get into their heads, search for that bit of freedom that can never be taken away when they write and that is also useful to realize that freedom is possible even inside the jail, and that the only bars we have in our wonderful space is the word bars and that all of us, in our inferno burn with happiness when we light the wick of the word.
(Applause) I told you a lot about prison, a lot about my experience every week and how I enjoy it and transform myself with them.
But you don´t know how much I'd like that you could feel, live, experience, even for a few seconds, what I enjoy every week and makes me who I am.
(Applause) "The heart chews tears of time blind by that light hides the speed of existence were the images row it fights, it hangs on.
The heart cracks under the sad gazes rides through storms that spread fire lifts chests lowered by shame, knows that it's not just reading and going on, it also wishes to see the infinite blue.
The heart sits down to think about things, fights for avoiding commonplaces, Tries to love without hurting, Breathes the sun giving courage to itself, surrenders, travels to the reason.
The heart fights among swamps, goes along the edge of the underworld, falls weakly and doesn't yield to the easy way while irregular steps of intoxication wake, wake the stillness".
I'm Martín Bustamante, I am a prisoner in Unit 48 of San Martín, today is my day of temporary release.
Poetry and literature changed my life.
Thank you very much!
(Applause) CD: Thank you!
(Applause)
Let's see if I can explain what I wanted to explain: I had a stroke eight years ago, well, an injury first.
The doctor told me: "Look, Olivia, if you want, I'll explain, you've had an injury since you were small, but it would be like having a tiger.
That tiger has been sleeping for 40 years, it hasn't given you any problems, you had no idea that it was there.
And now it turns out that it has awoken and it has given you a little scare.
If I were you, I would've killed it."
That's why we set up a surgery.
A surgery that honestly didn't go too well, and I got up with the right side numbed and a language problem.
I woke up without being able to write nor say anything, because I had aphasia.
Aphasia is a very rare disorder, because the people who have it have language problems, so nobody talks about that disorder.
(Laughter) That's what happens.
And besides those people are at home, they can't talk to their friends, and they keep shutting themselves more and more, and that's why it's a hard disease, you're not in any pain, you don't have anything but you can't explain it.
How does it happen to my brain?
How does it work?
To me, because there are all [types] of aphasias.
In my brain, for example -- When you talk, you go from one idea to another and to another: "This morning I went to a conference, it was nice, and who knows what, but this afternoon I have to go to whatever..." And you go from one idea to another, from A to B to C, through highways, fast.
In our case, well, in this case, my case, I don't have highways.
I have a state highway, or local, and besides it's dark and it's raining, also some rocks have fallen, there's been an earthquake, a tsunami, and in the end, I get to idea B.
The third, I have no idea of what I wanted to say, obviously.
Besides it's as if it were --
I also imagined it as if it were [like this]: You normally talk fast, and you don't think that you're putting all of that into words, and for that [there] is someone behind our mind who chooses the words, because we are so focused on what we want to explain, that you don't realize you have to use the "of," the "you," the tense -- And that's because [there's someone] behind, inside, that -- is doing a job without anyone seeing it.
It's like a butler who, in addition, knows where the drawers are for your words, and also puts it in like a tray for you, puts it in your mouth and go out for you, and you also make a speech that sometimes is even beautiful and smart, and you didn't do anything.
In my case, my butler is on vacation or isn't here, or he went on a strike, because I've got nothing, and so every time I talk, I have to focus, like right now, on which word I have to say, not only the words, but also "I-have-to-say."
Pretty complicated.
Another easy way that my daughter said.
She'd say: "Look, we all have words in mind and we get them out of our mouth,
there's a rope [between them], and my mother's has snapped. The doctor has made a knot and she has to learn how to talk".
I used to work as a director on TV3.
Obviously... the important thing for me was communication, and obviously, now I couldn't do it.
I always had a diary on me, with ideas, well, not so much a diary of "my boyfriend has left me" or something like that, [I would write] ideas that I liked, things that interested me, projects -- this was usually my diary, each day.
When I woke up I was in -- I was going to say in the sofa! -- (Laughter) in bed, immobile, I tried to write again and this is the first [time] that I tried to write.
You compare it and it's quite "heavy," right?
But well, it's nothing, it's not a history of drama, it's nothing, because this was eight years ago.
I've improved a bit in these eight years, I've done many, many exercises and exercises, I even [wrote] a book, published, and a nice book too.
(Laughter) But there I am.
Besides the speech therapist helped me a lot.
After two years of being with her, she told me: "Look, Olivia, you have to try to go out into the real world, because that has nothing to do with the exercises, you can be indoors doing exercises and more exercises, but life doesn't have anything to do with that.
I would hold onto her legs: "No, no, I don't want to go!"
"Yes, Olivia, go outside".
And it really is very hard and strange to [go out] into the real world.
How can life be for an aphasic?
Well, in my case, me, for example, I really mess up.
Two years ago I said: "Now I want to write a diary," I'm going to try.
Not to write, but with stickers, papers or drawings.
Two years ago I bought myself a very cool, nice, new notebook.
First page: I went with my son to... [the] zoo, I go home and stick [the ticket] and I see for the first time: "A disabled person" and a tiger staring at me.
That's the summary of how I was, what my life is, my life is that: with a tiger in my head and besides I am disabled.
And how does a woman live... without language?
Well it's a little complicated.
Because I, for example, my head is going like... Since we're going so fast... you go to buy something, for example at a store.
Last time I went to a fish market, I wanted sardines.
And so they ask: "What kind of sardines?"
And instead of telling him "Small" or "big" sardines, you say: "blue".
"What do you mean blue sardines?"
(Laughter) And of course, you're searching -- "Just a moment, I need to know the drawer, where is the drawer for 'blue', for 'small' or 'big'!"
But it came out like that.
Or for example, they tell me -- of course, it's because you're with people and they don't know you're aphasic.
My children understand me.
For example, when you tell them: "Put on your cereals, let's go to school," he already knows you mean to say "socks," but he no longer says anything, and it's the same, because I'm measuring:
Socks? Socks, socks... with s... sugar... many syllables... It's like Google or WhatsApp, it predicts the words, you enter it [the word] and you say: "OK, socks" (Laughter) (Applause) Then there are numbers.
Numbers are really "heavy," I didn't know how many times they ask you for ID in real life.
(Laughter) They ask for it for everything, for example to come here: ID.
In conclusion, I no longer have a... "wallnet", because I make a mess finding out where the ID is, and you have to have the card, and they look at you like: "Poor girl, she doesn't even know her ID number".
For example, sometimes they call me at home:
"Olivia? Mail package".
And I'm like: "Oh no, he'll ask me my ID number, where's my wallet?"
I go get it and he arrives and says: "Olivia?"
and I say: "No, I'm the cleaning lady".
- Name?
- Nancy.
(Laughter)
"ID?" I make it up!
You sign, close the door and go: Yeah!
(Laughter)(Applause) There are moments in which some light comes out from your brain.
For example, I was born in Barcelona, of course.
Well, not "of course"... (Laughter) I was born in Barcelona, and I speak two languages, but the speech therapist told me: "Olivia, let's start with one".
(Laughter) Since my parents were Spanish speakers, so, okay, Spanish.
But sometimes it comes out a little, I know my Catalan is there.
Already there, no problem, it will... It's there.
For example, I go to a shop, they start explaining things to me and I answer, "What would you like?" and so on,
a conversation not so philosophical, let's just say, neither too long.
But you walk out of the store and say: "Wow, I've just spoken Catalan, how is that possible!"
It's only happened once, that's true,
(Laughter) but I know the idea is there.
If I search, I might find it.
What I always find is the disorder.
The disorder is always present.
And the disorder is of no use to me, I hate the disorder, it doesn't make me a better person at all.
I can't go out now and say: "Oh, what a lovely day, thank you!"
No, it never helped at all, I hate the disorder.
(Applauses) Even worse still is the self-help, that has not helped me, but okay.
There are people who like it, if it helps them, very good.
But I didn't like it.
Maybe for my family or my friends it would've been [helpful] to have a book titled: "How to be an aphasic's friend," rude, in addition, because aphasics seem rude, because we are saving up words all the time, (Laughter) If you normally ask: "Can you please give me a glass of water?"
We say: "Water!"
(Laughter) It's true, we're on edge.
Rude, but not dumb, it's one thing that my speech therapist, Esther, who's very smart, told me: "Each day you have to repeat to yourself 'I'm not dumb, I'm not dumb'," because it's difficult not to feel dumb when you don't know your own ID number, you don't know how to say things, you can't explain anything --
It truly feels like being dumb.
Dumb, and sometimes I even think that we can be a bit smart, because when you don't talk, since you don't need to talk, because you don't have solutions, since everything is so fast, you can't keep up the pace, and so you stay quiet.
And you start... thinking... listening and listening -- and you see interesting things, listening is very good, because you see things differently, because you see people who are a bit... cocky or dumb.
You see things, because you don't have to think your reply in the conversation, not anymore, you take off that role.
And for strangers, since you don't talk, you become "such an interesting girl,"
(Laughter) You look like that and they go: "That girl is so cool."
(Laughter) Your friends, since you don't have a job and you listen a lot, call you more often, and you meet more frequently to talk.
(Laughter) And since they forget that you're sick, they talk to you and think "you listen so closely, my good friend, so attentive". (Laughter)
Well, thank you all.
As you have just done,
I recommend you to listen, which is good, because you'll learn more, and your friends will thank you.
Thank you all.
(Applause)
I'm leaving TED... and I want to change.
I have new information I will put in good use.
