A year ago, three friends and I Maria Fernanda Quevedo, Alejandra Matamoros and Valeria Pinto decided to start a community service project related to photography.
Actually, we were inspired by a TEDTalk we saw and had the idea to compare a different reality from ours with our society through photography.
Now, why photography?
Well, because photography can tell us various things, we can, through photography, feel emotions, we can know the environment.
You can, seeing this photo, feel emotions know the environment in which this girl is in this situation.
And above all, it intrigues us.
You are wondering, where was it taken?
Which place is this?
Because of this, one learns enough for the community and the situation.
When picking the struggling community to show, we had a hard time, but at the end, we decided to choose an indigenous community, given that their culture differs from ours and could be interesting.
Also, we kept in mind they were in need of school material.
We decided to call our project "Waking Roots" because we wanted to share this culture with those of our own.
We wanted to teach where our country came from, our roots, to wake up our roots.
So we went to Talamanca, to the Yorkin Reserve, and we took photographs, then we showed the photos in an exhibition which, organized by us, was called "Waking roots".
There, in the sale of the photos we collected various funds to give school material to the students of the community.
But in the trip, apart from taking photos, we did various activities, drawing with the children, painting with them.
Well it was the first time they were using paints the children of the community and they were crazy, super-happy, playing.
Well, obviously, there was a cultural exchange and by the way we took some good photos.
What impressed us most in the trip was this story that a lady of the community told us.
Her name was Prisca Morales, and she was something like, the President of the community.
She told us that Yorkin Reserve was different when she was young, they were not taking advantage of their resources, did not protect nature, and as an illustration they had the fruits there to make juice, but instead they drank Clight or Coca Cola and all these drinks which did not make sense.
The men of the community went to the banana plantations of Limon, and there worked and many were trapped or under the influence of alcohol or died of a sickness.
Ms. Prisca at 14 years old when she had her first child, said: "I do not want my child to go to a banana plantation to work.
What can I do to change it?"
Of course at 14 years old, so young, being a woman, nobody payed attention to her ideas.
So what did she do?
At 19 years old she left on her own for San Jose.
She took economics courses.
She took administration courses.
And she brought this knowledge she learned to her community and applied it there.
Now the majority of its income is tourist related, and they are taking advantage of their resources.
they are planting their cocoa, and consuming it, so much that they turned into a prime community for the other indigenous communities.
Why am I telling you this?
I am telling this story because it motivated us a lot.
Through the project, especially while planning the photographic exhibition, because your eyes opened there to what adult life is, and the obstacles that one may face.
Also I know this story will motivate us enough in future situations.
Because all this time we were thinking if she could, we, as students, with so many opportunities, with so many open doors available to us, we can do anything!
One has simply to keep an enterprising mind.
One has to be positive, and we have to believe in ourselves.
We understood that us, the youths, what we need is to come out of our own reality, take risks, because only this way we will grow intellectually and emotionally.
And eventually each one of us is going to find their path towards success.
We understood that our success is not only our own happiness, but one can also find it in the happiness of others.
When we returned to the community, in January, to hand out the school materials and we saw the smiles of the people when we returned it filled our hearts with joy, and motivated us enough to go on with the project, to go back to Talamanca.
So it is this motivation that one really needs to achieve one's goals.
And we, the youth, the students really have to take advantage of all the opportunities we have because in that way each one of us is going to find his way towards success.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Slums.
Life in a separate world.
Single mothers rowing in a sea of ​​stone.
The culture of the absent father, of the imprisoned father, of the riddled father, of the present father but with his back dissected.
The rubble that invent roads so the mud doesn't bite your ankles.
Carcasses of cars stolen and set on fire.
Neighbors talking across the wiring while hanging clothes to dry on a rope.
The police, hunting amidst sparkling laughter.
Policemen who are relatives of their target.
The dining room and its pale snack surrounded by colored sorrow.
Those going to work with their little bags, their bikes and tired eyes.
Mothers mourn the death of their thief son at their own and others' wakes.
The evangelists and their false screams.
The dogs chasing the bikes.
The lifesaver stew of midday.
The same stew at night.
And its leftovers for tomorrow.
The bus that drives through prisons overflowing with relatives.
Cumbia putting some rhythm to the misery.
The dawn and the carts.
The sunrise and those who are still on tour.
Many skinny salaries destined to disguise poverty.
Maradonas who were killed by the police, who are workers squandering their magic, but in a small muddy field full of broken glass.
The avenue is the frontier that divides the village from the world.
That's why prayers offer even blood for the miracle of the exile.
Symphony of the rain mistreating the corrugated metal roofs.
Middle class foreigners coming to buy drugs, leaving barefoot and crying.
Candles melting in the small sanctuaries with the photos of lads who died.
Walls with memories of their deeds.
The smiling corners, with bad boys and chicks, splashing cosmic sparks.
Children who become parents.
Inhabitants who all know each other.
Secrets everyone knows.
Deceits impossible to hide.
Where the class with no class lives.
The sole owner of all brooms.
Where meritocracy awards never make it.
Where the horizon always smells like jail.
Smells like a bullet.
Smells like precarious work.
Smells like a janitor's suit.

It's the slum. It's another world.
It is living apart.
(Applause) Thank you.
Max Goldenberg: César, when, how and why did you write this poem?
César González: Well, this poem was written almost about 10 years ago.
I was 19 at the time.
I was in jail, in Marcos Paz prison.
I was imprisoned for five years.
It was a way to go for a walk in my neighborhood without having to move, because I couldn't either, right?
A way to recreate the experience from my memory, with my imagination.
It was going around the neighborhood, in an imaginary way, but not just going around the neighborhood and describe it. I was not interested in just describing but to add a filter to that description, that is my concept of what it's like to live in the slums. Many people don't live it like I do, with the words that I put to the life in the slum, so they were philosophical, political, ideological filters of things that happen in the slum that are unfair, that should not happen.
That is, not to romanticize life in the slum, but at the same time, to rescue all the beauty that emerges amidst so much death, right?
Max Goldenberg: César González, people.
César González: Thank you very much.
(Applause)
It was 1993 and were facing a potential war.
I was the Minister of Labor in Argentina.
I remember long meetings with the president, with the cabinet, thinking what policies to put forward
to keep the country in peace.
My challenge as Minister of Labor was to keep unemployment rates as low as possible.
I got together with unions, with companies. It was very hard.
It was hard, but we made it.
This is a photograph of my time in the government.
Here is your Minister of Labor. (Laughter)
In 1993, I was just 15 years old and I owe this experience to Hernán, my Civic Education teacher in my third year of high school.
Hernán had a transformative effect in me.
When he taught, I felt as if he only talked to me.
And I’m sure that all of us felt the same.
I remember Hernán and his questions, how he made us think. But above all, when he spoke.
When the guy spoke, his eyes lit up.
His reward for keeping the country stable, was a dinner for me and my classmates.
I couldn’t believe it.
And I had to ask my mom for permission, --the Minister of Labor had to ask his mom for permission-- to stay out late.
And I’ll never forget that pizza.
We talked about school, we talked about classes, but we also talked about the challenges that were ahead of us.
And how to embrace them.
It was the first time someone talked to me about work and college.
Something that until that moment had never crossed my mind, took shape and I felt excited about it.
I’m sure we all had a teacher who left a mark on us.
And who wasn't like all the others.
Close your eyes for a second.
Remember that teacher.
What was their name?
What did they say?
How did they make you feel?
Open your eyes.
By having Hernán as a teacher a need grew in me to take charge of the reality around me while I was growing up.
And it dawned on me: we don’t deserve all the huge opportunities we have unless we fight for others to experience them too.
That’s what I call education.
And so, with that conviction and that urgent need, together with a group of friends, 6 years ago, we started Enseñá por Argentina.
It’s a movement where young professionals teach for two years in those schools that need it most.
They are lawyers, engineers, economists, - this year wehave even a biotechnologist - and we hand picked them for their excellent grades in college, for their academic performance, but above all, for their capacity to motivate and lead.
We want to bring in talent and passion to education.
All these years I’ve spent working in education, I’ve realized there are some common traits that make all these people special.
Like Hernán, like all the teachers you just tought of.
But which are these traits?
What truly set them apart?
Obviously, commitment.
But today, commitment isn't enough.
Commitment is the ground upon which we build.
They distinguished themselves by their sense of possibility, where every kid can learn, it's unquestionable;
and by their sense of urgency, because every second counts.
That is also undeniable.
Vero teaches in a school In San Fernando, Buenos Aires.
She has in one of her classes
a student named Alicia.
Alicia is blind.
And in spite of having a caring teacher who guides and helps her, she can't keep up with her classmates.
Vero makes a huge effort to plan and adapt her classes,
but she can't tell if Alicia truly learns anything because someone else corrects
her homework and tests. One day it dawns on her: "The problem isn't in Alicia, it's in me. I am the one who can't read what she writes in Braille."
So, in the long commutes to the school, every single day, she studied Braille with an app on her phone. In only three weeks she managed to learn it well enough.
And she told me that during these three weeks, every day in her house, she read Alicia's work trying to understand it, and studied.
She realized how much better, how much more good and helf she could offer Alicia.
Those 3 weeks went by fast, until the day came when she felt confident enough, and once the session was over, and everyone was turning in their work, she looked at Alicia and said: "Today it's going to be me the one who will correct your work."
The whole class kept silent.
In 12 years, it was the first time Alicia was hearing these words.
What Alicia felt at that moment I can't express in words.
Vero told everyone what she had thought, how she had studied, and how that would help Alicia and the whole class,
make progress together. And then, something no one expected happened: knowing Braille suddenly was something cool in the class. And guess who started teaching it, who became a role model?
Guess who everyone started looking up to?
Alicia.
This had an enormous impact on her and her classmates.
This story teaches me that nothing in education is impossible.
There are urgencies, we must dare to embrace them.
There are some more traits that make these people truly special.
And it's their ability to lead.
Lead and dare to take that step forward, to be a role model for the students in everything it implies.
And at the same time, the capacity to inspire,
which means to know when to give up the spotlight, and hand them all the tools so they can build their own dreams.
Martin "Tincho" teaches at a night school in Villa Soldati.
He has a student named Brian.
Brian is 16 years old.
He doesn't take school seriously.
He skips classes, comes in late; he failed a grade already.
So one day Tincho tells him: "Brian, enough is enough.
We'll do something about it, you and I.
We will both come an hour and a half earlier before each class. I'll teach just you alone. And in two months, you'll do an extra exam to raise your grades.
We'll get through this year, together."
Brian didn't understand much about this. But he trusted his teacher, so he said yes.
And they worked hard for two months.
Very hard.
And there came the day of the exam,
and Brian didn't show up.
And next day Brian didn't show up either.
Three sessions later on, on a Friday, Brian walked silently into the classroom. He came up to Tincho and said: "Teach, I was in jail.
You know that sometimes,
we go for a tour" - touring is going out to steal - "We went out with my cousin and a friend, we targeted a pharmacy, but they caught us, teach, and they beat the socks off of us.
I was in jail until Wednesday, that's why I couldn't come."
What Tincho first said was: "This is wrong, so wrong.
Why do you do it?"
"Sustenance, teach.
We need money at home." And Tincho said:
"I am so, so, so disappointed.
After everything we worked together.
But I'm willing to give you another chance if you're willing to give yourself another chance."
And he wasn't talking about exams.
So they started working again for it. They studied hard again, and on the day of the exam, Brian came way too early.
He walked in, and Tincho told me that he noticed something was wrong because Brian was pale and shaking.
And without saying anything, he hugged Tincho.
And almost crying, he only said one thing:
"Thank you, thank you, thank you.
Because I'm here today,
but last Friday was my birthday
and my cousin and friend came looking for me. Because we had to go back to the pharmacy.
In our hood codes if you fail, you have to go back.
And since it was my birthday, everything would be for me.
But I remembered you, teach,
I remembered the cost-benefit,
how you made me have a blank page, draw a line in the middle, and write on one side what I got from stealing - which was about 2000 pesos - and on the other side, how much my mother was worth it. I had to write down how much money I'd make working 25, 30 years, because some friends were already working. They are 16, 17 years old and they make 6,000, 7,000 pesos.
And I remembered when you asked me how much my life was worth.
I remembered all of this, these figures,
so I told them I wouldn't go, that I'd stay at home chilling with my mom, celebrating my birthday.
And they went out,
and my friend is dead now, teach,
and my cousin is struggling for his life. But I am here and the only thing I want is to take that exam and to do well, and pass this year, and have the life
you told me I could have.
A good life, the life that I deserve."
This story proves me that not one talk but one person can change your life,
can leave a mark.
And I admire Tincho for helping and inspiring Brian in such a way.
And I can't wait to see Brian in five, ten years, and find out what was of him.
When these traits occur, cases like this one multiply.
Enseñá por Argentina can be a place for many kids, like Brian, like Alicia, a place where you can find teachers who mark them for life.
And it can also be a place where teachers with a lot of experience, with a lot of classroom practice, who know the children, inspire and at the same time become inspired by the younger ones.
But above all, it can be a place where we forge leaders that will work as a network, and who will use these experiences to commit themselves for life, with the injustice children face nowadays. Underpriviledged children.
Some time ago, Fernando, a school director in Boulogne where we work, told me: "Oscar, do you know what strikes me most about Teli?"
- Teli is a teacher in Enseñá por Argentina - I thought he would say something about how she planned her classes, how dedicated she was, how she implemented new activities in the classroom, how she knew each and every of the children she worked with every single day.
But it wasn't any of that.
He said something I didn't expect;
or maybe I did. He said: "What it strikes me the most about her is that when she teaches her eyes light up."
And I remembered Hernán. my Civic Education teacher in high school, and realized that education, this education, is transformative.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I'd like to tell you that I always hated numbers.
Unfortunately, I attended an accounting high school, so I basically hated going to school.
I remember writing in my bench the countdown of the minutes left until the bell rang and the class ended.
So, when I finished high school, I quickly went to social sciences and got a degree in Psychology.
I wanted to do something creative, fun, that everyone liked.
So I specialized in sex therapy.
I started seeing patients and getting enquiries on social media.
And I stumbled upon several questions.
Such as, "how often should I have sex with my partner?"
"Why do I reach orgasm faster when I masturbate than when I have sex with someone?"
And one that is not a question, but I receive it very often, is: "I'm a premature ejaculator, help!"
(Laughter) The last question that I receive almost daily is: "What size should my penis be?"
As if there was a quality control before the penis is out in the market.
(Laughter) These questions resonated with me.
These matters of time, frequency, speed, mass.
(Laughter) And, first, I started feeling like I was in high school again.
Then, I realized that these questions we ask ourselves about sexuality, we ask them with the idea that there is a formula.
A general, invariable recipe, and if we follow instructions thoroughly, we'll get the correct result.
What we do is measure sexuality.
And we think of it as if it was an equation.
First, we think that a sexual situation requires a certain amount of time.
We're talking 20 minutes average...
on penetration.
Which includes a penis, always erect, that is about 20 centimetres long.
Nobody knows where they're going to put it.
But we think it should be approximately 20 centimetres long.
And there's a vagina, which is expected to be wet.
Again, nobody knows how lubrication works.
But it has to be wet.
And we're talking about a penis and a vagina because we understand heterosexuality as normative.
We expect this we'll give us, as a result, an orgasm.
(Applause) And if it happens simultaneously: love.
We all believe we're in love.
(Laughter) We'll repeat this equation three times a week.
I'd love to tell you it'll be for the rest of your lives, but we think elderly people don't have sex.
(Laughter) So we'll repeat it until we're 60, or 65.
Now, what happens when we can't reach one of these variables?
Or what happens if we follow this equation very precisely, but the end result is not as expected?
What we will think, the first thing that will come to mind, will be:
"There's something wrong with me.
I'm not normal. I'm flawed."
What I'd like to tell you today is that you're not the one that's flawed.
It's this equation that is flawed.
So, how about we redesign it?
(Applause) Just like I counted the minutes left until the school bell rang, we do something very similar in bed.
It's a known fact that men have an issue with ejaculating early.
So, for example, a male patient comes to my office and he's worried about this issue and I ask him: "Tell me how the encounter was.
Your last sexual encounter."
And he replies: "The first time, I lasted two or three minutes.
The second time I lasted a bit longer."
Men are not present in the sexual situation.
They're not having sex with you.
Men are struggling inside their head with the idea of not having to ejaculate.
And, in order to do that, they begin to develop a number of techniques.
Like, thinking about something unpleasant, breathing, staying still.
(Laughter) It's as if they wanted to gain time,
to level up before it's game over.
(Laughter) On the other hand, women experience something ridiculously opposite.
Women pressure themselves to orgasm quickly.
That's why we never talk about female prematurity.
So, a female patient will come to my office and say: "I masturbate but I can't reach an orgasm."
"But, what happens when you're in that situation?"
"I'm touching myself, I get frustrated, and I know I'm not going to orgasm."
"But, how do you know?
How do you feel?"
"I start to think that I'm taking too long..." "How long?"
"I don't know, like 5 or 10 minutes."
We measure sex.
And we measure it in time.
Now, let me tell you if you're in a sexual situation and, instead of being focused and connected with you erotic sensations, you're measuring time in your head, it will increment your anxiety.
And anxiety affects sexual response.
So all the things you don't want to happen, will happen.
Men will probably ejaculate prematurely or lose the erection.
In women, arousal will be inhibited and it'll be harder to reach an orgasm.
So, how about we, instead of worrying about time, worry about being relaxed?
Which is the best state to be in if you're going to bed with somebody.
Let's change the first variable of the equation to "relaxed state."
So we can move on to the central variable of sexuality: penetration.
Because, if there's one thing we associate sex with,
it's penetration, isn't it?
That's why we say "to bone."
(Laughter) Penetration works quite well for men.
The penis has the biggest amount of nerve endings on the glans area.
So what men very cleverly do is: in penetration, the tip of the penis is stimulated and rubbed against the vaginal walls, or anal, or wherever they want to put it.
(Laughter) I'm giving a handjob live for all of you. (Laughter) Get this on camera.
(Laughter) (Applause) Now, there are men who enjoy, for example, being penetrated.
Or they enjoy having the rest of their body stimulated.
I swear men have a body.
(Laughter) They're not just a pretty penis, there's a lot more.
And let me tell you as well that, when it comes to women, penetrating the vagina is a bit overrated.
(Laughter) Women have the most pleasurable area, the most sensitive area, in the vulva, which is the external part.
So it's not easy to stimulate it with penetration.
That's why women have a better response to manual stimulation, or oral, or she rubs herself against something: the stomach, the leg, against a pillow, against a remote control she left on her bed, a cell phone, a dildo ... Because the most important thing is that she gets the appropriate stimulation.
So, how would you like if, instead of "penetration", we talk about "appropriate stimulation"?
In order to do that, we forget about the penis and the vagina, and we can think about our erogenous zones.
Which, there isn't a map that works for each and all of us, either.
Each one of us will have to explore them.
But we have a new equation now.
We're calm, relaxed, and we go beyond penetration.
We go for the whole body and we look for the appropriate stimulation ...
to reach orgasm.
A female patient told me once, that she had an orgasm counter.
I thought having an accountant for taxes was enough, but it seems it isn't.
An orgasm counter.
So, when she had sex with her partner, she started counting every orgasm she had in a week.
According to that, her perception of the relationship would change.
Because that's what we do.
We count orgasms and we count and measure sex.
As if we wanted to control it.
The irony in this is that an orgasm implies a loss of control.
An orgasm is a moment with a lot of selfishness where you have to able to give in and let yourself go.
And, sometimes, the act of giving in is emotional.
Sometimes, the act of giving in puts us into a state of vulnerability.
So, I wonder, where is the variable that teaches us to feel comfortable and safe inside our own skin, and to feel comfortable and safe with someone else?
All the more so if it's a one-night stand.
Where is the recipe or the equation that teaches us or tells us, for instance, that if you went through an abusive situation, or you were a victim of physical violence repeatedly, it will probably have an impact in your adult sexual experience?
Because a body that felt pain is a body that will need to heal to be able to feel pleasure.
Sexuality is built and rebuilt in everything we do, feel and think.
It's not something that goes in parallel with our own history.
Truth is, we're responsible of our own sexuality. And we bring it into play
with every decision we make.
With the lifestyle we have, with our health habits, with our positive or negative experiences with others.
There's no way we can generalize the answer.
There's no way we can think of an equation, or a common formula for sexuality.
So, I'm sorry about that, and I understand that breaking this equation and breaking the sexual norm can be frightening.
But in return, it gives us freedom.
Thank you.
(Applause)
