I'm going to ask you this:
When is a dog the happiest?
These are your choices: When are you about to feed it?
Or after you fed it?
I'm going to tell you about my dog, Mandela, I have already heard you, it's called Mandela.
When I'm about to feed Mandela, he knows it.
He sees me, notices the gestures I make, sees the key that I'm about to fetch, the one that opens the door to where his food is kept and signals that he knows that the time has come.
And I can, if I want to, prolong that moment of happiness from him, trying to make it last until I get to the barn.
When I put his food in his bowl and it makes a noise, and he sees it there, that's when he reaches a climax of joy.
After I feed him, he leaves me.
He doesn't care anymore, he does not love me anymore. Mandela doesn't need anything else.
He forgets about me, he ate his food, and he goes for a nap.
Second question, when was my wife the happiest? Her name is Marcela. When were we looking for a new house,
or when we found the house and moved in?
I was much happier when we were looking for the house.
Do you know that moment?
She would send an email showing me this one and that one and in this one we can change this, and in this one we can make this reform, and here is the bathroom, the kitchen.
The bathroom, in particular, I do not know why the bathroom has to be perfect.
As if we're going to live in the bathroom.
She was excited then.
Later, when the removal van came, when we chose the house where we were going to move with our daughters, the level of happiness was different.
And I will tell you, to continue with this reflection, what happened to me when I was a child.
I'm the oldest of five siblings.
And I remember clearly when, with my brother Juan Andrés, we asked my father for a bicycle.
"Dad, we want a bike."
My old man, having a quiet sip of tea, didn't even bat an eye-lid.
He did not check with my mother, nor with anyone else.
He told us quietly: "Ask Father Christmas."
Zero stress.
I am 47 years old.
I have just recounted an event that happened 40 years ago.
My old man was not stressed.
And we waited for that bike all year without needing any medication.
All year long, we waited for that bike.
(Applause) And with my brother, Juan Andrés, when that Christmas came, we had to be well behaved,
you had to do well in school. I had to accompany my siblings, as I am the oldest, I had to take the little ones.
There were some tasks to do to later have the bicycle.
When the got to the pile of presents that Christmas as our parents always did, there was a little pile for the older siblings and another little pile for the younger ones.
That's what our parents did.
My brother, Juan Andrés, plucked up courage opened it, and it was a tricycle.
He looked at me, and I said, "Thanks dad!"
Because my old man had taught me that we had to say thank you.
Do you remember that parents used to teach that?
You had to say thanks for what you got, whether it was what you had asked for or not.
Do you follow me?
Unbelievable, and I said "Thank you, dad." And my old man: "And you
have to share it with your brother".
(Laughter) Well, where am I going with this? We waited all year, waiting is related to happiness.
Today we confuse indigestion with happiness.
And what is happening in society today?
We are part of what we call: a society of plenty.
In the society of plenty, the motto is: "I do not know what I want, but I want it now."
And our parents were educated by their parents, our grandparents, in a society of poverty.
In a society of poverty, one grew up knowing that not everything could be achieved.
One enjoyed what little was achieved.
Still with me?
One grew up knowing that not everything could be achieved.
But whatever was achieved was enjoyed.
We grew up hoping.
Today's society is the society of plenty.
"I do not know what I want, but I want it now." How much drama our kids have today, when they have so much, and that much they have, is little.
Because that desire and that anxiety, my parents, as in the previous example, they waited all year, it made us want it, it pushed us to walk.
In contrast today, if we meet all the wishes of our children, we don't let them desire, have illusions, have expectations, and we make them confuse indigestion with happiness, which leads straight to the doctor's surgery with anxiety and depression.
So what is happening with today's parents?
Today's parents do not educate as before.
And I don't mean that everything in the past was better.
For me, there is no better time than the one we are living now.
But today's parents are so tired, so stressed, with so much guilt, that we do not have time to say no.
We do not have time to educate.
Do you see how today's parents get rid of their children?
The child says: "Dad, mum". "Go to your mother". "Mum, mum!" "Go to your father, can't you see that I have just got in and I am exhausted?"
"Grandma," "I'm just going," says Grandma.
(Laughter) Grandma, ha!
We have to take care of our grandparents.
Our grandparents are leaving us,
our grandmother educated without guilt.
On the other hand, we are parents full of guilt and we are very afraid that our children won't love us, and that we do not give them enough time.
To educate them is to tire yourself, lovingly.
We cannot educate and rest.
Your child is a miracle, not a problem.
And you have to have time to say yes, and also to say no.
This is part of today's parents: fatigue, stress, exhaustion.
And how do we realise the impact of this?
This impacts on what I want to give you, which is the formula of happiness.
What do you think?
(Applause) Hands up.
The formula of happiness, The formula of happiness is that our children see us happy, and that we have the courage to be adults, and help our children to host their frustrations.
The best happiness tutorial ...
The best happiness tutorial for our children is your face.
It is the face we have when we come home and we tell our children: "You won't believe how good work was today or my job search.'
And if I need to lie, I lie.
Because we have no right to infect our children with our craziness.
Because even if it looks like a lie, our children want to be like us.
(Applause) What is going on?
We are committing a sin: overprotection.
And I include myself.
I have two daughters, Martina 18, and Belén 15.
We have overprotected our parents more than our parents overprotected us.
This is a generality.
What does this mean?
Protecting is fantastic.
Protect is to take care of the emotions of our children, the physical, emotional, spiritual needs, of our children.
But overprotecting is when I do something that the child or teenager can do for themselves.
When I solve something for them, because I'm tired, in a hurry, you know what today's parents are like, tying shoelaces until the age of five.
They put on their jackets, Do you know what I mean?
Bathe them, take them out and bring them back.
Do you recognise that?
This is the fatigue of the overprotecting parent.
Overprotecting is when you do what the child can do for himself.
And in that way annul their self-esteem.
annul their independence,
their initiative and their self confidence.
The great drama that we have today is that parents can't cope when their children suffer. And if they do not suffer,
they cannot grow.
Because where there is life, there is suffering.
But not in the sense that: "I hope he suffers," not like that.
If he wants to hit his sister and you tell him: "don't hit your sister".
"But you were not there, couldn't he just you hit her a little bit?
(Laughter) And that is the adult's task.
The great drama that we have is that there are no adults able to put up with the frustration of having to tell my daughter: "You don't hit your sister."
It's normal to want to hit her.
I have to help them so that at the age of five they have the ability to self-regulate their emotions.
And when parents overprotect, they feel their child's pain before it actually hurts them.
Did you hear that?
If they are about to fall, it hurts.
How it hurts before it hurts! I do not want them to suffer how I have suffered, I do things so it doesn't hurt.
Because I do things so that it doesn't hurt, it does not hurt and since it doesn't hurt, they don't grow.
And if I exile him from suffering, I exile from the learning that this suffering brings.
We need much courage today to be adults.
(Applause) So what can we do?
That's why we say that overprotecting is like a new form of abuse.
Because we are delivering children to society that cannot cope.
Am I clear?
Because they have a fragile psyche, that when at work they are told: "You have to deliver this report by Friday,"
the child says 'I give up'.
(Laughter) "I didn't like how you spoke to me." "You never spoke to me like that, Dad." So, where will our kids get happiness?
If we exile them from the learning that suffering brings which is that they will fall, but thanks to that, they will learn to ride a bicycle, to use a computer, to manage in the world.
So, what can we do?
We need adults and to be adults we need to stop being the children of our parents.
I am a child if I demand, I am a child if I don't forgive.
I am a child if I still believe that my childhood was different from what it was.
Being an adult is having the courage and humility to thank our parents because they did what they could with the tools they had.
And when we get home, and we can give our children a kiss and a hug.
But if you have a whim, or if they're going to hit their sister, to be able to say, with total tranquility and with total joy "I said no and I mean no, my child."
Without fear and without guilt.
They will not thank us now for what we are doing for them.
Look at how long it took us to thank our parents.
﻿Thank you very much.
(Applause)
On December 24th, last year, I was in the North of Thailand celebrating Christmas. I came back to my lodging house located up the hill on the very North of Thailand.
Since it was 2 am, it was really dark.
And in order to get there, you needed to follow some sort of small trail, by the side of a forest. So, on my way to the lodging house, I could hear from far away, there were people singing: "lights up", "lights down..."
I thought: "This cannot be possible."
(Laughter) When I got to the lodging house, I found three other compatriots. So, we all ended up celebrating Christmas together.
That was my gift that year.
But, I'm not here to talk about Christmas on foreign countries, or about Chileans wondering around.
I come to share with you, what traveling has meant to me.
I'm going to start talking about what can be gained by traveling.
Traveling eliminates prejudgment and broaden your mind. That's a fact.
Is sort of a daily exercise. In order for you to be in good shape, you must be flexible. Try to understand other people.
If you don't do that, you're dead. There's no other way around.
So... I have met people who has traveled and comes back saying: "English people are this way or the other.
The French. Uff! Chinese...
Indians are this way, the other."
Reality is, that by doing that,
you are sabotaging your trip. You don't want to look-up and see with a different perspective. To believe that the "sopaipilla" is the best food on the world -it's really good too by the way-
to believe that it really is the best food in the world, that the best wine is Chilean -- and that we are a model to be followed.
That is not OK.
I'll give you an example.
The first time that I went to Bolivia, I did it in a very naive way, but when I actually had seen the country, my whole perspective changed about the country. I changed.
It was really amazing how you can completely change your way of thinking 180 degrees.
The same happened to me when I went to India for the first time, I thought that by going to the Himalayas I would find the "monopoly on spirituality." And once I would come back I would be levitating on a yoga cloud, or a saffron cloud. I would come back enlightened.
No, big mistake.
When I came back, I hadn't been enlightened. I hadn't faced the "monopoly of spirituality."
Sure, some spirituality but not the "monopoly."
When I arrived to Chile I had the full conviction that spirituality is everywhere.
As long as you truly are thinking in the here and now.
Traveling expands your horizons and opens up your mind.
To travel is, how should I say it,
is to realize that on the timeline of life we are all different and that although we don't have to believe all the same, we'll always find common things.
We all value friendship, we value the possibility to be generous, we all value goodness.
Traveling is also an experience to learn and discover.
That's always the same.
The first time that I was in India, I went to a sitar concert.
At the end of the concert, I approached the musicians to find out more about the instrument.
They explained to me that the sitar has different components: a head, an arm, 20 strings.
And I said, wow, this is so interesting!
The sitarist asked me if I would like to take lessons with him the next day.
And I said yes. I couldn't have told him otherwise.
The next day, I started taking classes. This went on for two weeks.
I thought it had been not enough so I had to come back the year after. So I could continue learning some more.
So, traveling will always become some sort of a learning experience. That's what traveling means.
this is valid also for me.
In order for us to travel, we need to overcome all the fears that we are always being fed with from everywhere.
That's a fact.
The other day at one of my English classes at the UFRO a student told me: "Professor, I'd never travel by myself because I believe it's dangerous."
And I replied: "I always travel by myself, and I've never felt in danger.
Why do you think is dangerous?"
And she said: "My father told me so."
It's somehow natural that they say that
and I wonder: Why are we so afraid of doing so?
And the answer is pretty simple: Turn your TV on and watch the news and you'll have nightmares for over a week.
This is really sad.
For example: the first time I was in India,
I thought I would see dead bodies floating over the Ganges, because that's what we've been told.
However, when I was there, nothing happened. It was so religious and full of ceremony.
You cannot even get close to take pictures.
It is so strange that you get to see that on TV.
People don't stand on a bridge and throw dead people to go down the river.
That doesn't happen.
To be able to travel you need to practice detachment.
Which is linked to all the fears that people are constantly telling us.
Detachment in my case happened when I went to travel in 2013. I knew I was going to travel for quite some time
I had to get rid of lots of things.
I had to sell and give away... and the rest I couldn't do anything with, I left it at my mom's house. Some of that stuff is still there in boxes.
It wasn't easy. It's actually pretty difficult, because we have been raised on a culture where our timeline needs to be drawn by all the things we are constantly acquiring: You finish university, then you buy a car, then an apartment... And so on. So, it's hard by nature.
Now...
I'm not here to sell the idea that detachment is the way to go. It actually isn't.
It's not like "Get rid of everything and go travel!" That would be very selfish and foolish for me to say.
The truth is: There's no recipe for this.
Each time you have to analyze the situation and see what option you have to travel if that's what you want to do.
To make detachment even harder there's always the question: How much can I pack on my suitcase?
How much does it fit in there?
As much as the scale allows you and you can place on your backpack. You just have to live with it for the whole duration of the trip.
You have to be strategic; you won't be carrying books that you're not going to read.
You can't do that.
Neither clothes you're not going to wear or that are out of fashion. Go figure, (Laughter) I'm here to share with you what traveling has meant to me.
I hope this talk can motivate some of you to start doing so.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good evening everybody.
I'm the last speaker, and I'm an economist. (Laughter) Not exactly an exciting ending.
I'm about to share with you a proposal that initially, some 20 or 22 years ago, looked like complete madness to me. Today, instead, I think there are many reasons, both from a philosophical and economical perspective, to deem it rational and fair. What am I talking about, you may ask?
I'm talking about, as we said, the unconditional basic income.
It might help to explain it better, even if some of you may already know what I'm talking about. It's an unconditional monetary allocation to the whole population. No strings attached.
We're typically used to, if we live in the European Union or in North America's countries, to public State subsidies, that are tied to some kind of condition, of causality.
Being poor, for example; not reaching a certain level of income;
having some physical or mental disabilities; some kind of conditions.
In my case, 22 or 23 years ago, when I read for the first time about this proposed unconditional basic income, to give something to everybody, no matter what. I said, "This is foolish!"
Time went by and I was into something else. Back then, I was working on a Ph.D. thesis, focused on a completely different subject, and eventually I said to myself, "Why should I spend my time on something I currently don't care about so much?"
And this one, on the other end, was intriguing me more and more.
This dates back to the end of the last century, when I wrote this Ph.D. thesis about basic income.
Curiously, when I talk about it in academia, about unconditional basic income, I had to use a disclaimer: believe me, I stay awake enough hours with no kind of drugs or alcohol. (Laughter)
And I had to start showcasing my academic "pedigree"
But why did I start thinking it was a complete madness, almost demented, and now, for the last few years, I have thought it's a perfectly fair and rational idea?
Well, I asked myself the five or six questions that every normal, person asks himself, even those a bit far from statistical normality, rounding up or down. We all start by asking the same questions and the questions, the objections are actually the same all around the world.
Of course, I didn't travel to every single country, but in those I actually visited, objections were more or less the same.
For instance, we're all going to become couch potatoes!
The exact definition
changes with the context. For instance, if you are in a business administration faculty, the one I work in, words are loaded: "We'll have to pay the parasites."
Whereas "parasite" in political philosophy is a very neutral term.
Sure, when we call someone a 'parasite' we're not telling her a nice thing. But long story short, in political philosophy, a parasite is someone who costs more than the benefit, who takes advantages of the benefits without giving something back.
All while slightly increasing the costs for those who work.
That's the classical definition of parasite, in literature.
Which would perfectly apply to all children who are still looked after by their parent.
Sure, it's ethically not bad for a parent to maintain their children.
But technically, that's what a parasite is.
"Nobody would work anymore."
Which is a different thing. Thankfully, now things are changing a little bit, but many people are stuck in believing that the only work is when someone does something for money.
Which means that all the housekeeping female work, or volunteer work, activism work, you name it, doesn't count as work.
And I take this chance to say that for many years now eocnomists have quantified the value housekeeping work would have if we put it into the formal economy.
GDP would spike from 70 to 125 per cent. So we're talking about really big figures.
But let's get back to this: no one would work anymore.
They're referring to the fact that no one would take an income or no one would work anymore for money.
Look, it's a very superficial objection: it's hard to run experiments with these things, because there are people in flesh and bones involved, but some experiments were ran in as different places as India, Namibia, now in some cities of the Netherlands, in some North America's communities - cherokees, for instances; North Carolina or Dakota. And all the experiences we made lead to think that basic income is quite an effective incentive to rewarded work, because there are no strings attached.
As you can see, a lot of the ideas we have about work arise when we have in mind the conditioned subsidies, the ones we have now, who are lost the moment you touch another income.
But that's not the case with a basic income.
A basic income is perceived unconditionally.
Another objection that made me deem it crazy is that we can't afford it.
Which is a worrying objections for economists, as you can guess. "We can't afford it."
Well, several studies were already done and I joined one of them, which studied two million individual income tax positions - I'd like to talk about individual income tax one day, because that's true madness - but according to the data provided by the Institute for Fiscal Studies, so official data, with a reform of the IIT, without moving a single euro from elsewhere, well, except those costs which would be redundant with a basic income, we could perfectly afford a basic income for all adults. At least up to the poverty line.
Some people think that antipoverty subsidies work better, given that basic income seems to be an antipoverty measure. One might think antipoverty subsidies are better tailored around the problem.
Many recent basic income supporters are actually against antipoverty measures which are, if you pardon the phrase, very poor.
And they almost achieved no results, saying nothing would be excessive. But they haven't done a good job of achieving this result.
A basic income, unconditional as it is, and poised to everyone, is an excellent system against poverty, much better than conditional subsidies.
I also usually raise another objection, together with many other people: Is it fair to give it to the rich?
Should Patricia Botin also get it, for instance?
They used to quote her father,
now they quote her. Is it fair for Bill Gates, in the United States, to get a basic income?
Sure, if we have in mind a basic income that just adds to what we already have, that would be nonsense, back then and now.
But if you understand well how to finance a basic income, rich people get a basic income, but they pay much more than that - assuming they're paying now, of course - but they would pay much more than they do now or than they shoud be paying now to fund a basic income.
Some people say that full employment is much better.
And then they inevitably pick that stupid Mao saying that it is better teach people to fish, than to give them a fish. They say it all the time and that's OK. I'm for full employment as well. But speaking about full employment, there's no economist, either mainstream or heterodox, knowing what she talks about, who foresees full employment over a 30 years horizon.
So one says OK, certainly full employment is nice, let them fish, let them fish! But in the meanwhile, for those who would like to fish, but they can't, if we want them to maintain the biological habit of eating at least twice a day, plus four walls and a roof, let's start by granting them this material survival.
As for the full employment, to be very precise, almost 100 years ago, an economist that's ranked among the 20th century's best ones, John Maynard Keynes, a conservative one, gave a very interesting speech about how would the economy look like 100 years in the future, namely from 1930 to 2030.
Which means, just 14 years from now.
What he said was very interesting because he got some predictions right, but completely fell short in one.
In fact, he was sure people would be enough rational, in 2030, because productivity would rise and so the wealth would too. And supposedly the well-being of the overwhelming majority of people. He took for granted that people would only work 15 hours a week.
If a political party showed up today, claiming 25, not 15 hours, we'd all tell them they're crazy, right?
Yet, that was a conservative economist's position like Keynes; and he said so because he had in mind that at least at the beginning of the 20th century, everybody shared. Which is, people generally work to make a living.
Even if some people really love their work,
But aside that, in order to make a living we do a lot of other things, or should be able to do a lot of other things.
I began saying, I used to open a talk or a conference, some 20, or even just 15 years ago, by pointing out I wasn't high or drunk.
Now the Wall Street Journal is talking about the basic income, the New York Times, Der Spiegel, Le Monde; the big international press is talking about it, the world's most widespread magazines and of course a lot of Internet websites. I'm not saying they all support the idea, but they talk about it.
It might seem odd to see the WSJ supporting the basic income: but they really give it plenty of room.
And I was talking about it, not so long ago, by someone who personally opposed the idea. But he said, when The Wall Street Journal talks about something, maybe we should start getting worried. He is worried that it's coming along. I will wrap up.
Most certainly, almost everyone of you knows Arthur C. Clarke, the great sci-fi novelist, who wrote, among other masterpieces, 2001: A Space Odyssey.
And Arthur Clarke said something that perfectly fits, in my opinion, with basic income.
He didn't have basic income in mind, of course: he was just talking about every disruptive idea.
He said, every new idea goes throught three stages. The first phase is: What a madness, set me free!
There are already enough crazy people around the world, stop it!
The second phase is: Not bad, that's interesting, but currently we have other priorities, or it's not the best moment.
And the third phase is, I've been advocating this idea for many years now.
I would say, and hope, basic income is between the second and the third phase.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
I'm from the Canary Islands.
Planes have been and still are a key part of my life.
Besides, I love to travel and I visited the most remote places of this planet.
Nothing serious ever happened to me; not even missing a piece of luggage.
In 2008 my niece Ana died in the tragedy of the JK5022 flight the greatest catastrophe in Spain in the last 36 years. I kept myself away from everything for one year but the first anniversary brought me to the place where she closed her eyes forever and awoke me from the voluntary lethargy I was in: Why her? She still had all her life to live as had her 153 companions in that journey to eternity.
In September 2009 I joined the Association of Victims of the flight JK5022: the AVJK5022 of which I am now President and we started to work to find out the causes, and also for the rights of the passengers, for the assistance to the victims and families, for the prevention and investigation of the causes of the accidents an for air safety.
What happened after 2:24 pm that 20th August 2008 can be defined with one word: chaos. The families, many of them from Gran Canaria, reached Madrid 12 hours later without exactly knowing what had happened and what was the state of their beloved who stepped into the JK5022 in Madrid.
At 8 am of the 21st of August the families were brought by bus to IFEMA the Madrid Fair and Congress area, which was closed in August and it was not prepared to receive the enormous tide of people who were desperately looking for their relatives.
With more than 40 degrees Celsius there was no water available in the water machines.
There, I found out in a list of 18 names - they were the survivors - stuck to the walls that Ana was not among them.
The official list of passengers took more than 30 hours to be made public.
The 21st of August was the day of the most brutal despair I have ever experienced: desperate witnesses that broke even the hardest heart, the loss of entire families, of more that 40 children, of teenagers.
And this despair became bigger because of the total lack of coordination in the intervention of the authorities, who did not follow any protocol neither in the rescue nor in the treatment to the families or to the many people that came to volunteer their help.
Two years later, I learned there was a guide for the assistance to the victims of air crashes and their families published by the ICAO in 2001 including the procedure to follow in an air crash.
But, what is the ICAO?
ICAO is the International Civil Aviation Organization, part of the United Nations and including 191 countries. It establishes the regulations for international air transportation.
Do you remember, when inside the aircraft the flight assistant says: "...following the rules of civil aviation for security demonstration...?
All the airlines, through their States, must follow its rules.
We had to reach there, and we did.
We achieved much more than updating that obsolete guide; we worked to raise its status and transform it in a document containing the policy of assistance for air crashes' victims and their families; approved by the Council and the General Assembly to be introduced in all member countries and to be supervised by its public audits.
Meanwhile, we went to Europe and we obtained the introduction in the EU Regulation on investigation and prevention of accidents of three essential issues not yet regulated: The passengers list must be delivered to the families in a maximum of 2 hours after the accident.
The families must be informed about the causes of the accident, and the victims' associations too.
And third, all members of the EU must have a plan for victims' assistance.
With this EU Regulation, and the work and pressure from the AVJK5022, the Spanish Minister of Public Works created a Plan for Victims' Assistance to which we provided all the most updated information from all the countries worldwide.
The 3rd of August a Royal Decree by the Ministry of Presidency is published involving four Ministries: Public Works, Home Office, Justice and Foreign Affairs, about which I would like to highlight the following: It commands the Ministry of Interior to have a Protocol of Coordination and Reaction for a flight catastrophe; an event that was not contemplated so far in our Civil Defence Guidelines.
Airlines are also compelled to have a plan of assistance to victims and families including an immediate Assistance Point for passengers' families, psycological assistance before and after the emergency and many other aspects of great relevance for the victims.
An informative leaflet is also produced, that resumes all this information and regulation, directed to the citizen who suffers an air crash so he can get help and relieve his pain.
In each and every of these the AVJK5022 collaboration is mentioned and thanked as the true engine that makes possible for Spain to have for the first time a regulation that protects the victims and their families, protecting their dignity as citizens.
Another historical achievement where Spain pioneered in Europe is the rule preventing laywers and procurers from approaching the families of big tragedies victims for 45 days after the accident, as it happens in the United States.
Now Spain is in a leading position in Europe somehow with this Plan of Assistance to the Victims but falls behind globally in researching the causes of accidents, ranked worse than Bali, Nigeria or Cuba, according to ICAO's 2010 audit.
We don't have a preventive security culture and we do not really look for truth as the only way to prevent future accidents.
Investigations are filed fast and the usual outcome is to blame the pilot.
Nevertheless, to determine the causes of a flight accident is fundamental to prevent others so that mistakes can be amended and lives saved.
Our association asked the Official Body of Commercial Pilots to make an alternative report as we didn't want to appear as partial: Being a victim of the system doesn't mean the loss of intelligence or capability to distinguish pain from truth.
This report along with ours, made by experts we also hired, revealed the chain of errors that happened before, during and after the tragedy. Before the tragedy: Spanair obtained a one month extension of the flight permission for the aircraft when it actually had to stop for the technical and documentary complete review after 3 years of flight.
It was a flying wreck and even so it got the approval. The day of the flight: when the captain goes back to the parking because he realizes that the Temperature Probe RAT reaches 99 C degrees. and the technicians, to cool it, put some ice on it and disconnect the fuse that covers the failure on the relay.
After the crash: when the Spanish Research Commission doesn't reveal that that relay also supplied the TOWS, an alarm which would have informed the pilot of the wrong set of the aircraft so that the take-off should be aborted.
We have fought very hard in the Spanish courts to prove the truth of what happened, but it was never accepted, neither in first nor last instance.
In March 2014 we took the matter to the European Court of Human Rights and we trust to be listened there.
We produced a documentary explaining the evidences we were able to prove, aimed for society at large and to flight passengers in particular, named "JK5022: a chain of errors" broadcasted by Canary Local TV and screened in the European Parliament.
It's available for free in the web with the same name and I urge you to see it, to spread it, it will help you to discover the reach of a tragedy that still has no culprits.
I have spent the last 6 years of my life fighting for this cause I strongly believe in and my obsession is to unite forces with other victims in Spain, Europe and in the world in order to gain a voice in the forums where the policies of commercial aviation are decided.
In 2011 we created together with other 5 countries the European Network of Victims - SOS Catastrophes of which I am Vicepresident and since 2013 Chairperson of the Group of Victims and Families of air crashes in the ICAO and as such I participated in February 2015, in the parallel session dedicated to the victims in the High-Level Conference of this Organization, and there I spoke about victims, helping victims:
the phases of an aviation tragedy are similar no matter where it happens and on top of the human support there is a lot of knowledge, fight and preparation to share.
I also committed myself to the creation of an International Federation of Associations of Aviation Accidents' Victims and Families that could be recognized by the international organizations.
The last 21st of July it was created in Madrid and I was elected President. The founding associations are the Spanish, the Pakistani and the German, and the purpose is to be a protector umbrella for all aviation accidents' victims worldwide.
In the ICAO all the aviation stakeholders were represented: governments, airlines, professionals, all but the victims but now they will be thanks to a Federation that believes in a moral re-arming - absent till now - in the debates on air transportation.
Economic growth is all very well, but we must not forget that airplanes transport people, human beings.
We will stop being mute guests in the commercial aviation only taken in account when a tragedy has happened and we cry the loss of our dearest beloved.
Aviation security is not an abstraction it is a reality that can only be the result of a general prevention culture of commitment and a demanding attitude, that includes all the stakeholders: governments, airport operators, airlines, manufacturers, insurance companies, travelers, business travelers and journalists.
Yes, journalists too. They have to committ with the truth and be especially empathetic with people suffering a tragedy like ours.
We cannot accept the publication of images showing suffering human beings who cry hopelessly with no possible relief.
That pain is private and should not be touched.
We cannot accept the publication in the name of freedom of press, of the image of bodies covered by sheets in the Pavillion 8 of IFEMA, when not even the families knew where they were kept.
And it is not ethic that the journalist disappears when the accident is not breaking news anymore, abandoning the families to their loneliness and silencing the voices that bring new evidence.
The news treatment of an air crash requires a kind of preparation that the media in general do not promote anymore.
A good journalist needs to know the complexities of the aviation industry, in order to offer a complete and rigorous coverage.
Can you imagine a sports jounalist commenting a game without knowing the rules?
Fo this reason we collaborate with institutions that offer training programs for journalists on this matter.
Air safety is a responsibility of all citizens and we need to know the implications of our decisions in order to decide freely.
It is crucial, for example, to ask ourselves how a flight Barcelona-Düsseldorf can cost less than 40 euros.
In the textile industry we have already learned about the chain of human rights violations hidden behind 3 pairs of socks sold at 2 euros.
Today, when we buy, we think more, and manufacturers had to change their offer and adapt to our demands.
The same should happen in the commercial aviation and the consumer should be the first demanding ethics, security and quality rather than cheap prices.
James Reason, famous psychologist and international expert on human error, believes that the essential cause of an aviation accident rests in the decisions taken at the highest level of governments.
As citizens, we need to influence those decisions, being inquisitive and vigilant, in dialogue and alliance with companies and governments to provoke a change.
A more demanding and less conformist citizen is the founding element of efficient and safe services, and also of a healthier and more democratic life in common.
I strongly believe that everything can be changed: laws, rules, regulations, the aviation industry, the airlines and the insurance companies.
My purpose in life is not in my head but in my heart that fights to reach justice and truth, so that a tragedy like this one will not happen again for the same or similar causes.
In a world where values matter little, some of us believe that it is worth to establish a moral reference employing our effort to prevent new tragedies as eternal tribute to 172 innocent lives.
We can't give life back to our beloved, but we can honor their memories working for the common good to prevent what we have suffered so that 154 lost lives can have a meaning that heals our souls for the rest of our lives.
It has not been easy, but we have extracted from the tragedy a knowledge heplful fot the safety of everyone.
And what we have achieved in the last six years proves it.
We will keep giving our generosity and effort so that commercial aviation in the world can be safer for the passengers but also respectful with the victims and their families, for them, who are now somewhere, always in our hearts.
Thanks.
(Applause)
I need you to follow me on this game.
It's called a career in technology.
To win we have to collect 1000 points.
If we succeed we might make millions.
Let's start.
Like all games, we have a character.
Our character is a 17 year old girl.
She's very curious and she's got
the best marks in her class.
But you know what?
In childhood she was given a little kitchen and her brother, a computer.
And at school she never learned that women could work on technology.
You know what?
That's why we start with -100 points in technological skills and -100 in aspirations.
It doesn't matter, we are a bit behind but eager to continue.
Here's our next mission: Make a group of people congratulate us because we decided to study Systems Engineering.
The game is taking us first to her group of friends.
Let's see what the group of friends says.
"It is a career for guys,
it will sure be difficult."
Let's go to the family, they never let you down.
Your mom says: "Why don't you play safe?
Lawyer or teacher."
(Laughter)
We've just lost another 70 points.

Good news anyway, we begin college.
Now our mission is to finish our studies.
Let's see how this level goes.
We attend classes on the fifth floor.
But there's only one ladies room, in the first floor.
For showing up late to class we lose another 50 points.
How does this continue?
We have the same teacher in three subjects.
Whenever a girl makes him a question, he doesn't answer.
For not being able to clarify our doubts we lose another 30 points.
Anyway, I want to tell you that we still keep moving forward.
We manage to get our degree.
That year we are one of the few women that graduated in that career.
And we get a job.
Although a couple of people tell us it's because there is a lot of demand in this industry for this type of profiles; not because of your marks.
Now our next mission is to get a raise.

We are the only female in a group.
We work twice as much, we strive.
On top of that, they give us
the most difficult projects.
However, one day in the cafe, we find out that our male partners earn more money.
So we go talk with our boss and he tells us: "It's your fault because you never asked for a raise."
This game is exhausting.
And I imagine that you, just like me, don't want to keep on playing.
This is no longer a game.
These are the phrases from testimonies of the few women who work in technology in Argentina.
Last year we ran an investigation because we wanted to understand why with more technology, less women want to study or create it.
They told us that these were some of the routes and trajectories they had to go through during their lives to be where they are today.
There is no doubt that everything we do today with technology sets the pace of our lives.
From what we buy to meeting again with that group of high school friends.
89% of what we do on the Internet has to do with a mobile app.
But only 6% were created by women.
Neither Tik Tok, nor Instagram, nor Facebook.
Maybe that's why so recently a woman's emoji appeared with a computer.
In Argentina in Computer Sciences, there are only 11% of women today.
But this was not always so.
50 years ago, if we visited a classroom of this career we would've found out that 75% of people studying were female.
Women were not only a majority, they also stood out.
In fact, thanks to them everything we do on screens is possible.
Starting with the first person in history who programmed that was a woman, Ada Lovelace, to the one who invented what today we use as Wi-Fi, Hedy Lamarr. Or check this out, the person who created the software that managed to take Man to the moon was Margaret Hamilton, --quite ironic, right?--
This is a subject that keeps me busy and also worries me.
Because it's not a territory that women have to conquer.
They are territories we were in and we left.
The more technology turned into a place for the distribution of wealth, of power, of creativity, we stopped having access.
Working on technology today not only means earning 30% more than in any other industry.
Working on technology today is to create the architecture of what all the other systems do.
Whatever we do, it will be there.
The lack of diversity on that table where it's decided what we will do with those screens brings consequences for all people.
For example, let's take the virtual assistant, that with a female voice asks us, "how can I help you?"
And we tell them, in a moment of despair, "assistant, I want to kill myself", the assistant tells you the right line for your country; the software locates where you are and gives you that exact number.
Now, until recently if we told them, "assistant, I was raped", your assistant would tell you "I don't understand what you ask me."
After many complaints now the assistant suggests a link, a website about domestic violence, but do you know where is it from?
The US. That technology today is defined by a single group brings consequences for all people.
And that is something we have to keep in mind because, again, we have to start to think that women need to be back in that decision-making table.
In 2015 a group of women coming from different fields --but all crossed by technology-- decided to create an organization and be there where we have to be today.
Motivating, training and accompanying the next generation of women leaders in technology.
We don't just teach them technological skills, we also teach them different skills that have to do with entrepreneurship, with being able to develop other strategies to lead.
Because you know?
When they make it, we don't want them to be like Steve Jobs with a wig.
When we started this organization we realized that this was the easiest, believe it or not.
The most complex thing was to work with schools, with universities, with companies, so they begin to understand and see them as leaders.
But also to prepare the space to receive them.
On this journey we understood that the first we had to do to measure impact was to have data.
And we started looking for them.
We started researching for how many women are there in technology today in Argentina, for example.
And that data did not exist.
So, although that was not our craft, we had to investigate.
Today, for example, we know that in the last five years out of every 100 people who sign up for these careers, only 16 are women.
And maybe you think, well, that fact only renders useful to the organization.
The truth is, no.
When we published it a lot of ministries started writing to us even investment groups, to thank us and tell us that they were using that data.
Thanks to that now they could project.
Because where you cannot guarantee diversity innovation will not come to pass.
And technology feeds on innovation.
What we learned during this time is that it's not enough to teach girls to get around these barriers.
We have to understand what they are made of, why they are there and why they still persist.
That's not only going to help them.
It will also help other minority groups or those who don't have a seat at the decision-making tables.
It's key that women recover the place we used to have in technology.
It's not merely our task alone.
It's a collective responsibility.
Because if we succeed it will no longer be just one group designing the future, while the rest of us remain caught up or out of the game.
(Applause)
of the People’s Republic of China he said during his inaugural speech: 中国需要更多地了解世界 世界也需要更多地了解中国 In Spanish, this means: “China needs to learn more about the world.
The world also needs to learn more about China.”
President Xi’s words reflect one of the top challenges that our generation will face in the next 30 years.
China will likely be the driving force behind the world’s most important political and economic transformations during the first half of the 21st century
transformations for which we will not only be witnesses but participants as well. This will be the first time in our lives in our parents’ and our grandparents’ lives in which a non-Western country will have so much influence in the international arena.
This leads me to ask you two questions.
Two questions that are each one side of the same coin.
The first one: how much do we know about China?
The second one: how much do we ignore about China?
To answer these questions,
I propose a small test:
If you can name the 5 people you see on the screen please raise your hand. There are 2,000 of us today in the auditorium and everyone raised their hands.
Hands down.
Presidents of the US
and the former Secretary of State.
Here goes another challenge:
if you can name the 5 people you see on the screen raise your hand.
<i>-Three?</i>
-Five! I don’t think it’s even worth it
to recount. Honestly, the outcome of this test surprises me a lot. On the one hand, we don’t know the Chinese leaders of yesterday and today
but on the other hand, and this is a fact year after year in the River Plate one of the best-selling books is Ludovica Squirru’s Chinese horoscopes. But perhaps more importantly
what is the lesson behind this test?
Today, China is the top trading partner of Brazil Peru, Chile, and also Uruguay, among other countries.
However, our knowledge about China is still very limited and it certainly does not match China’s importance for our economies and, consequently, our futures.
What’s the risk in ignoring what turned China into the growing force it is today?
In Latin America we usually approach China based on singular opportunities that allow us to snack.
But when we don’t look at China holistically at the whole extension of the China phenomena what we are really doing is letting big opportunities pass us by.
Opportunities that could transform our companies. Opportunities that could help create less unequal societies.
Opportunities that could modernize the infrastructure of our countries.
Opportunities that can radically change the course of our lives.
This isn’t something that a friend of a friend told me. This wasn't sold to me with some cheap jingle. I experienced this myself and I’ll tell you a bit of that story.
It was April 2013 and I was sitting in this place called “Langdell Library" the library at Harvard Law School. I was reading a routine of mine since my student says at Beijing’s Tsinghua University. I was reading China Daily, a newspaper about China in English. I would read that morning after morning. I was finishing my post-graduate program and was a little bit anxious. Even though there were opportunities around I wasn’t sure what my next step was going to be. None of those opportunities matched what I wanted which was, something in China where my work could make an impact and from which I could build bridges for Chinese people wanting to get closer to Latin America
and for Latin Americans wanting to get closer to China. With that in mind, I continued reading the China Daily and suddenly, I spotted an ad a reasonably sized, shiny orange ad that read: “A rare opportunity is coming: The Foshan government is seeking to hire foreign professionals to help with trade and investment promotion.”
I have to confess that even though China was already a career option actually since I was 16 years old and told my parents that I wanted to study Mandarin I knew that China was my way, but I didn’t know what was in Foshan. I had heard about the city before, but not much.
However... ...something told me that this was no ordinary opportunity. I had seen foreigners working for foreign companies in China foreigners working for the foreign service of their home countries in China foreigners working for Chinese companies but never foreigners working directly under a Chinese government unit.
Six months later, after several interviews I was working for the Foshan government and a world of opportunities was opening.
As soon as I started... ...I realized that we had something incredible in our hands. By hiring foreign professionals perhaps without even being conscious of it what the Foshan government was doing was giving practical meaning to the words of President Xi translating his words into concrete action.
Because by working next to our Chinese colleagues we would help them learn about the world and they would help us learn more about China.
Foshan is a city of 8 million people a model emerging city, with such a dynamic economy that according to McKinsey, between 2010 and 2025, the city will contribute to global growth more than Zurich, Milan or Madrid.
The local leaders had crafted an innovative investment promotion initiative and, together, we decided to communicate it to the world.
A few weeks later the whole China was talking about our team’s work. Some people praised it as an example of a China open to the world. Others would say that we were spies. But the truth is that after the first two years of implementation the Foshan FDI strategy was ranked #1 in Mainland China by one of the most prestigious magazines in the field and #4 overall in Asia.
Working for the Chinese government is a fantastic experience from every point of view. You get an insider’s view of something that seems mysterious from the outside. You get to see the details of China’s development model which has changed the world so much.
But for a moment let's leave this story aside and, as Frank Underwood would say take a step back and look at the bigger China picture.
The magnitude of China’s rise is simply astonishing, and if you start digging you’ll see its footprints in many fields. Here's some numbers that confirm this. Foshan is located in Guangdong Province a province that, if it were a country would be the 16th largest economy in the world similar in size to the economy of Mexico.
China already is the second largest global investor and contrary to popular opinion, invests not only in natural resources but also in infrastructure, robotics and even Latin American startups, like 99taxis in Brazil.
In 2016, over 135 million Chinese tourists traveled around the world spending over USD 261 billion. They were the ones who spent the most globally.
Chinese companies like Alibaba and Wanda, two model Chinese companies are among the main sponsors of world soccer and the International Olympic Committee.
The Belt and Road Initiative, in Mandarin 一带一路, is an initiative inspired by the Old Silk Road is one President Xi Jinping identified as the “project of the century.”
Through this initiative, China seeks to export the crown jewel, infrastructure. By doing so China will unleash an infrastructural revolution across Asia with global knock-on effects.
For example, the Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank created under the framework of this initiative already has 77 member countries including Germany, Canada, France the UK, Israel, and Chile.
For the first time, there is a real and working initiative to institutions like the World Bank or the Asian Development Bank.
Bilateral trade between China and Latin America exceeds USD 250 billion annually with every indication that trade will only continue to grow.
In these times when that song that all of us have in our heads doesn’t stop playing the best way of transmitting the importance of what is happening is asking you to remember that
<i>pasito a pasito, suave suavecito</i> <i>keep getting closer Latino &amp; Chinito.</i> And these are just the headlines.
For how long can we look only at the headlines?
If I say, Silicon Valley, you probably know that Google and Facebook are headquartered there.
But if I say, Pearl River Delta you probably don’t know that it is home to Tencent one of the world’s 10 most valuable companies whose executives last year received more than what Apple, Intel or IBM executives received.
A company that has developed WeChat an app with almost a billion active users.
Previously, one may have said that the People’s Republic of China was the People’s Republic of Copycats.
But today, we can say that China is the emerging People’s Republic of Innovation. We have a lot of questions about China. Sometimes people believe that accessing the Chinese market is easy. People might say things like let’s sell our products to 10 million Chinese and problem solved. The truth is the Chinese market is the most competitive in the world. Thinking that one could sell to millions of Chinese without much effort is running a fool’s errand.
Even Uber, with all its resources couldn’t beat its Chinese competitor Didi. China will force us to rethink our understanding of the world. &gt;From the way we position the continents on a map to the content of school curricula.
The universal history that our kids study will have to pay more attention to the old silk road to better understand the new one.
Slowly but surely, we will become more familiar with Chinese brands with Chinese traditions with Chinese expressions. Already, players on major European football clubs send greetings for Chinese New Year.
China will also raise new questions.
What will we do for new generations to develop their cultural intelligence?
What position will we take in case as Professor Graham Allison has warned, there is a clash between the ascending power and established power
as has been a constant throughout history with an exception of maybe 3 or 4 times.
In the 21st century, on the way to the top no matter what your career choice is if you are a lawyer, an engineer, a pilot or a football player sooner or later, you will cross paths with China. You're going to have to understand it. You're going to have to deal with it. You're going to have to learn how to entice her. You're going to have to accept it when she tells you no. You're going to smile when she says yes. You're going to have to learn to set limits. For many people in Latin America if you are not the “son of” so-and-so or the “friend of” so-and-so some doors will not open.
China will always require you to run the extra mile but sooner or later, China will recognize your effort.
And if you don’t believe me, let me tell you this anecdote. More than a fairy tale, it's a dream come true.
In 2011, I came to China with two things: a scholarship and a pocketful of dreams. During the first few months in China I even took a selfie with the portrait of President Xi dreaming of, perhaps one day being able to meet the person who represented more than 1.3 billion Chinese.
Five years later, and after several obstacles I finally had the opportunity to meet President Xi at the Great Hall of the People to briefly tell him a little about what I had been doing in his country to receive his words of support, and, of course as it couldn’t be any other way, to take a nice selfie with him.
If anything, please applaud the security guard who was nice enough not to interrupt my picture.
To wrap this talk up I invite you tonight when the madness of the day is behind you when you are processing all the different ideas you heard today I invite you all to ask yourselves, how will China impact your lives?
And when you do it make sure you don’t forget that the best stories of Latin Americans in China... ...are yet to be written.
Thank you very much.
Or as you would say in Chinese, 谢谢 大家!
A few years ago, I wanted to get closer to children.
Not just through my paintings in toys or books, but being there, face to face, giving workshops, painting with them, seeing them create.
In addition to this, and for some time, I wanted to be helpful, but I didn't know how.
If I should support a NGO or should work on my own project.
One night, I was waiting for a bus that wasn't arriving, and suddenly, not thinking about anything in particular, I had a vision of a bright ad that read: "Travel around the world giving free art workshops for children."
It was crazy.
I didn't know where to start organizing something like that, I didn't have the money, I had nothing.
But I thought it was the best plan in the world.
By the time I got on the bus, I already knew that I'd do it.
I had faith and worked very hard in this project.
I decided that I'd design it myself.
To imagine and to organize it, I thought about what I would have liked to attend when I was a kid.
This way I thought about every workshop, every detail.
I'd start from the north of Argentina, and I'd continue through Latin America, Africa, Asia, and Europe.
Giving workshops in schools, children's hospitals, orphanages, libraries, markets, and on the streets.
In the jungle, in the mountains.
In big cities and in very tiny and remote villages.
Places that were not even included in travel guides or travel agencies.
I feel that we underestimate... that we underestimate children in their abilities to feel, think, and say.
We think they are some kind of container that we have to fill up with things and information.
Obviously, they are in their early stage of development, but they also have things to say.
I feel that generally, they aren't granted this space where we should listen to them.
This is my main goal: give children a space to express themselves so that later, children from other places, get to know these other children.
From what I have seen, wherever they are, they watch the same movies, the same cartoons, play the same games, but what do they know about each other?
What does a child in Argentina knows about a child in Ecuador?
Or a child from France about a child from Kenya?
The truth is, nothing.
Before we start painting in the workshops, we always show them videos of children from previous countries we've visited.
For example, in an afternoon, in Tokyo, tons of Japanese kids were able to learn a bit more about Argentina, Peru, Mexico.
From this same desire to get them connected, we created another part of the project, the gift chain.
We create a video where a child introduce him or herself, and tell us something about their lives.
About their families, the place where they live.
They tell us about their favorite toy, their favorite food, and they tell us about a dream.
Then I ask them to pick something from their own things to give to a boy or a girl in the next country.
And they also paint something for that child in the next country.
The chain started in Argentina, continued in 15 more countries, and there's still a lot ahead.
This way, through the eyes of other children, they learn about new realities.
It's not the same William's life, who has 9 siblings, compared to Tzo Luo's who is, like most Chinese, the only child.
It's very different for Christopher's, who lives surrounded by rabbits and cows, compared to Simone's, who is growing up in a mega city.
Life is very different for Mikija, who after school can go and play with his friends, compared to Mario's, who has to go to work.
I always develop emotional attachment, and it's hard to leave each place.
But I think I have accomplished my mission if I can at least leave a mark, a small footprint.
Look.
(Video) 1st child: What I feel when I paint is that I'm an artist.
2nd child: It's when someone frees their ideas.
3rd child: This way I can release my feelings. All my feeling I express them through my painings and this way I feel better.
4th child: You are in a world where you can do whatever you want.
5th child: My paintings express my happiness and everything that I'd want.
My paintings are my imagination.
Japanese: When I paint, I feel like I enter a new world, where everything that I'm painting lives, and I have a lot of fun in that world.
6th child: When I drew the superhero, I felt brave.
I felt... that I was in the company of other people.
7th child: We can paint on a tree trunk, on stones, on sticks, on wood, on the sidewalk, anywhere.
7th child: (Japanese) Sometimes, there are things that I don't know how to express. so I paint them.
8th child: When I paint, it's a dream.
(Applause) I feel that it's a pity that in schools, generally, art is considered secondary, something less important, just for recreational purposes, and not something educational.
I wished that at school they'd dance more, sing more and paint more.
In all the places we visit, I suggested to the kids to invent a superhero, something local.
I encourage them to think about what they wish for, and moreover, about the needs of their communities.
In the north of Colombia there was a terrible drought, it had not rained in months, they had lost their crops, and they really needed water.
But a boy draw the solution: he created a superhero who could shoot lightnings to the sky, and make it rain.
Later, in Ecuador, we went to the Amazon, we were in the jungle, with a indigenous community.
It was incredible to see them paint for the first time in their lives.
You can't imagine their reaction when they discovered that by mixing blue and yellow they could get the green of their trees.
A daily occurrence for some people could be something totally new for others.
Then we arrived in Guatemala, and we painted with children working in the main market.
Children who don't go to school because they have to work there helping out their parents.
Those fruits and vegetables that were part of their daily duties, at least during that afternoon, turned out into characters and animals.
It's about giving a new meaning, and that's what I try to do all the time.
In Mexico, during the Day of the Dead when all the community gather to be with their departed loved ones, we went to the cemetery and found a lot of children wandering around. We created a group with a few,
and I asked their opinions and what life and death were for them.
Then, we took out our crayons and sheets of paper and started to paint.
They did one drawing related to life, and one related to death.
And everything was good, the way how they flow, the things that the children said, that made me think how much we continue to underestimate them.
How there are still topics that are taboo at school and even at home.
I think it'd be great to be able to talk to them about sexuality, gender violence, -- which was quite present in all the places we went to -- or even death.
What we're doing through silence is raising children who will keep on repeating the same model.
I'd like to share with you some of the things I learned.
From the beginning of this project a lot of people have offered to help me.
In every country, and in every little town we'd go they open up the doors of their house, they invite us to eat, to stay, they give us materials.
Actually, they make me feel I'm home even when I'm so far away; I'm a little bit at home.
But most of the time I hear people saying the opposite.
But I don't think the world is that horrible and unsafe place where nobody cares about the other.
I also learned that trust is the most important thing that a child needs to be able to create.
It's all about giving them trust.
So that they can enjoy what they do instead of doing it like a test and then getting stuck.
And this is the case of any artistic activity.
Even for me, for you, who think that you are no able to paint, to write, to sing, to dance.
The children taught me that when they can find joy in only walking the walk, without much judgment or comparing themselves to their neighbors, they are much happier.
We know children who have a hard time.
Who suffered from abuse, violence, abandonment, extreme poverty.
Children who cannot go to school because they have to work.
But even so, in our workshops, when they paint their dreams, I find out that they want to be singers, architects, teachers, doctors, like any other child.
It's very easy to tag and stigmatize.
But no child is born evil, no child is violent per se.
All they need is an opportunity.
In the jungle, in Peru, we also went to the Amazon, with the Awajun community, and I remember the school principal telling me, "Don't expect great things because these children know handicraft but they never paint."
Well, they painted the most amazing paintings in all my trip.
It was incredible the power of observation these children of the jungle had, and how they were able to draw their surroundings, just as they are.
Every detail, every bird, and every tree.
I had a similar experience in a school where there were blind children suffering from the Down syndrome and brain damage.
One of the things we create in the workshops are self-portraits.
I never thought about underestimating them.
We did what we always do.
And if someone has any doubts about their ability to express themselves, look at these amazing drawings they created.
Just as I feel that I can teach these children, I also learn from them.
I also learn from them.
Not long ago, in Thailand, I was walking on the street, met with some children, and we started to paint. I met a little girl I got a special connection with, and for a little, we were inseparable.
I looked at her painting and I saw two people.
I asked her who they were and she pointed at herself and at me.
At me, a guy she had never seen before, someone that she has just met, and possibly would never see again.
It's incredible how children can open up so much.
And so it's our responsibility as adults.
Just one word can open them up a lot or close them up a lot.
If we tell them that the sun has to be yellow, that grass has to be green, that their characters need to be leaning or otherwise, it'll look as they are flying; instead of helping them to be creative, we are curbing their imagination.
In Asia, even with the help of an interpreter, we can't chat like in Latin America.
So we use games, songs, videos and, of course, paintings.
I think it's key, when you're in front of a group of children, to be able to reinvent yourself, to be creative, to absorb what's happening.
Not all the children are the same, not even among siblings.
It's the adult who should adapt instead of having a formula and expect always the same result.
More than a year ago, I said goodbye to my dog, Tai, and left home.
I have already traveled all Latin America and most of Asia, in fact, I just flew here from India today and the day after tomorrow I'll be going back.
And there's still a lot ahead.
Soon, we will meet with children from the Gaza Strip, in Palestine.
With refugee children, African children.
ONU and UNICEF invite us to give workshops, something that I'd have never imagined.
After these two years, I'd have painted with more than 10,000 children from 40 countries,
And there's still a lot ahead.
But we'll be back, and we'll do the same here, in Argentina, from Ushuaia to La Quiaca, from coast to coast.
We are going to create moving exhibitions with these children's paintings.
And a book, full of children's pictures, stories and of course, paintings.
We are making a movie, a documentary, so that we can share this with many more people.
All of this is called "Small Big Worlds" and what started as the trip of my life turned out to be the project of my life.
But it started as a dream.
And talking about dreams, I want you to meet Elizabeth.
(video) Elizabeth: When I was small, very small, I wanted to be a singer, a sanitation worker, work with my dad; but my dad used to say no, that the job wasn't for me.
My father is an engineer.
He used to say no, that I wouldn't know how to do it.
But I kept on telling him, again and again...
My advice to all the children in the world is that they should follow their dream.
Don't let anyone get in your way.
If your parents don't support you, you should fight for your dreams!
What I want now is to be a gynecologist.
To help all women have children.
But sometimes, I feel that that's not my dream, that I have another one.
I don't know what it is.
So I have to go searching for it, let it come to me or I'll go to it.
Together: Small Big Worlds!
Argentina!
Bolivia!
Peru!
Ecuador!
Colombia!
Guatemala!
Mexico!
Cuba!
Japan!
China!
Vietnam!
Laos!
Thailand!
Burma!
Cambodia!
Indonesia!
Nepal!
India!
[Small Big Worlds pequeniosgrandesmundos.org] (Applause) Every day I'm grateful that I got inspired to create "Small Big Worlds", and that I can paint with children from all around the world.
The other day, a boy from a village, in the middle of the mountains in Nepal wrapped it up it perfectly.
He painted the Earth and wrote: "I also live on this planet."
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
(Piano music) (Singing) It all began with envy as a platform to start a path of my own.
Like when I saw my big brother drawing and wanted to be like him.
Three girls in my class started a band.
They wrote songs, they sang as a trio!
"I'm so jealous," I thought as I walked home ... And decided to write my very own song!
A rehash, a sum of cliches.
We learn by copying our surroundings.
An entire world yet to be discovered, under the chains of superficiality.
That which is familiar, which is "normal" always with quotation marks, that which is legitimized.
That which is already heard, and seen, the TV, the radio ...
All you have to do is question everything that is given to you.
And I went down the rabbit hole ...
Experimenting ... with limits and extremes ... fracturing beauty.
And how do I organize a concert?
Let's try.
And what purpose does this all have, art?
Let's find out.
And money, when there's money, There's never any money left. How do we split it?
And how do I invoice it?
And how will I pay my rent?
Let's think about that.
And be coherent,
while making mistakes.
Sef: Hi. I'm Sef.
Or Christian Fernández Mirón.
I wanted to start by singing for you, instead of talking to you, a bit about where I come from and how I do my thing.
(Singing) Everything that scares me benefits me much more, so much more once I stood up to it.
Much more.
Much more.
Much more.
(Applause)
Sef: Thanks. Thanks a lot.
My friend Peter will join me now.
We are going to tell you a true story.
It's a story about a song.
This song ... to write it -- Well, I wanted to design a poster because I also creative other stuff besides music.
I wanted to illustrate a poster with hair.
Imagine, the shapes, the typography, all made of hair.
So I went to a hair salon, and I asked the hairdressers ... I asked if they could keep hair of all kinds and colors for me: blonde, black, brown, whatever, curly, straight in little bags.
They said they surely could,
but noticed the ukelele on my back, because I had been rehearsing or something like that.
So they confirmed with one condition:
they'll do it in exchange of playing a song for them.
I loved that because one of the things I have learned by doing, is to exchange, to trade things.
So I went home and I said, "OK, I have two choices: I can just play any song for them, or I can compose one for them instead."
So I got on it right away
that afternoon, I wrote a song.
It's meaningful to me because it was made with no ambition, but years later, every time I play this song, I tell the story,
and it's something people love; and I think this is related to all the rest.
The song is called, "Thank you for the hair."
(Laughter) (Ukulele music) (Singing) Thank you for the hair.
I'm going to need it to make an illustration which is a drawing but it sounds better.
Thank you for the hair.
I won't be processing it.
I'd like for it to look quite natural mixing the blonde, the black, the brown, whatever! (Humming)
Thank you for the hair cuts.
Where would we be without hairdressers?
I don't even want to imagine it ...
But I can imagine it, and it would be something like this: when I cook, I'd light myself on fire, in the bathroom, I'd get stuck in the drain.
Strolling through the park on a gusty day, I'd get tangled in a fir tree that doesn't mean to disturb me, but since man-buns became compulsory we'd have no choice but to stroll under the clear skies!
The hair falls down ... until it falls up.
Everybody is wearing the most horrible fascinators!
Thank you, thank you for the hair cuts.
(Applause) Sef: Thanks. I'll make the most of time and introduce some more friends: Clara, Óscar, Gastón, David, Elía, Gema, and Xisco.
And a little applause for them as well, please.
(Applause) I love to work with other people, and I also need it; and precisely our next song is about collectivity.
It's a song that -- I had a kind of ambitious idea, I wanted to record a video, a sequence shot with live sound, with a lot of collaborators, 50 people, in a certain place. And well, we did it.
It was very ambitious and it was pretty awesome.
Search for it.
It was possible only thanks to a lot of people: friends and strangers that got involved, and I'm very grateful for that, and has a lot to do with me as well.
The song is called, "Independence."
You can sing along.
You have a clue on your bag.
Or you can Google for it at home and sing it later.
Thanks.
(Music) If I ever pay too much attention to what they say, you have permission now to slap my face.
The only way we could survive was living through beliefs we had, and that is what has brought us both this far.
We found ourselves, and then each other.
And that is why we work so well together.
Hey!
Go back to telling the truth.
Let's be the exception.
And prove freedom by claiming the management.
Look: we are capable of living our own way!
In this cruel world, the biggest transgression is an act of kindness. (Music ends)
Thank you.
(Applause)
Recently, my daughter and I decided to give away some toys she no longer used.
She's six years old, and asked me for a camera to take a picture of them.
It was easier for her to farewell her toys if she could take a picture before giving them away.
Because she doesn't want to forget them.
Because pictures help us remember.
We grab our cell phones to snap when we feel something is special.
When something moves us, we feel the urge to capture what we feel and keep it in an image, in a picture.
However, we don't print photos on paper like we used to do, we share them in social networks, or we upload them to the cloud.
Photography turned into something intangible, something virtual.
But, what happens when we lose or erase our digital photos?
Those in your computer, in that hard disk or in your phone.
Is there anybody here who never lost a photo?
What would happen if, for some reason we would lose all of our parents' photos or other loved ones who are no longer with us?
How are we to remember them?
Going back to our photos from the past is like opening the doors to a dimension where lots of memories we seem to forget still remain.
And those images from the past, those cherished treasures can help us propel ourselves into the future, as well.
I am a nighttime photographer.
I make portraits under moonlight, in nature, close to the water.
I ask the people I photograph to remain still and silent for several minutes amidst darkness.
Meanwhile, I walk around them to light them up and light up the landscape with lanterns.
It's a moment of quietness and connection.
I went off to live in the islands of the Paraná River Delta for two years.
I took with me an old camera without knowing very well where I was going, neither what was I to find there.
And in time I came to know the islanders, and I invited them to be photographed under the full moon to recreate their daily life routines and jobs, but at night.
And I took photos of loggers, hunters and dreamers.
People living in isolation, whose only direct relationship is with water, to the rhythm of the river highs and lows.
And that visual work in the Delta led me to travel around the world,
and took me to Japan.
I was searching for a new story to tell.
I wanted to work again with a community which had some connection with water.
And it happened that the curator of my exhibition, Ihiro Hayami, had relatives in a small fisher town and went with me for a visit.
Ōtsuchi is located between the mountains and the sea.
It's a fifteen-thousand-people town that in March 2011 went through the worst tragedy in its history.
It was washed off by a tsunami with waves tall as a five-story building.
Even though the alarms went off many people didn't believe it would be so strong, and remain in their homes.
As a consequence many people died, close to a 10 percent of the population.
And 70 percent of houses were washed away.
This is why every local in Ōtsuchi has either friends or family who died in the tsunami.
The power of the waves pulled the houses off their base and the sea gulped them up.
Survivors were left with no shelter and had to endure a winter with temperatures below zero Celsius.
Amidst this chaos and total destruction many people were trying to save their family photos.
An officer of Japan's Self Defense Forces recovers a photo album inside a house in total ruin.
There it was the picture of a child.
The officer holds the album as if he were holding the child.
I traveled to Ōtsuchi with my daughter who was five month old at the time.
And the contrast of a baby in the middle of such desolation was impressive.
People would take her off of my arms in order to kiss her.
And I'd wonder how to tell this story that happened a year and a half ago. What could be positive out of such level of destruction.
The first thing I found were big piles of rubble made of all kind of things.
Twisted objects that speak about life as it used to be in Ōtsuchi.
Like this vending machine or this fishing elements, car remains ... or what's left of a house. A lamp turned into a Moebius strip, the symbol of infinite time.
As a contrast to these mountains of rubble there was this town flattened by water.
The skeletons of the houses, marked on the ground were empty spaces, filled with stories.
So I invited the survivors to go back to those spaces where their houses or workplaces used to exist.
And many accepted, but in general, the kids didn't want to.
I photographed them during the night as they remained silent and still during the ten minutes each photo took.
The scenes were so sad, nothing had color.
She is Haruko Okano, a librarian, and she's sitting on the remains of Ōtsuchi's Library, where she worked.
This is the Second Volunteers Firefighters Squad of Ōtsuchi inside of what was the fire station.
The fireman standing up was in charge of closing the anti-tsunami gates.
This is Yoshihiro Ogayu, monk of Kogan-ji temple.
The temple was a refuge in case of tsunami but even so it was destroyed.
His dad and his son died there.
When that first trip to Ōtsuchi was over I found a photo album in the middle of the street.
It had laid there for a year and a half without having anyone pick it up.
It was all wet, heavy and it stank just like a dead animal.
It was tainted with color bubbles gushing from the photos that blended together.
I took a picture of it without giving it much of a thought and I came back.
Once in Buenos Aires, the Ōtsuchi experience felt much like a far away dream.
It filled me up with doubt: Why did I have to tell this story, if I came from the other end of the world?
What did it had to do with me?
Why had I photographed the survivors in black and white if the reference of my work is color?
And while I made myself these questions, my own life began to crumble.
Soon after breakup, my daughter's mother went off to live in another country.
So I took on myself Lara's upbringing, she was a one-year-old baby.
Project Ōtsuchi was put on hold, because now it was me the one who had to survive and reinvent myself.
And some months later, revisiting the work the family photo album image popped up again.
But in this second view I grasped it in a different way.
I thought I saw a painter's palette.
So I took the main colors of that album to create my own color palette.
And I used those colors created by the force of the tsunami to color the images of the survivors.
And little by little, all the gray scenes turned into something colorful.
I used color as a bridge to connect the present and the past, as the hub between their photos and mine.
Now I was feeling connected with Ōtsuchi once again.
Two years passed since until I was able to return to Japan to search for other recovered photos and find new colors to paint with.
There I met Mikio Komukai, a nice man who had this NGO that was recovering family photos to send them back to their owners.
And with his help and the permission of Ōtsuchi's government I gained access to that impressive archive of thousand of destroyed photos.
Contemplating those pictures I realized the intangible damage the people of Ōtsuchi were facing; the damage to their memory and identity.
Those blurred images were the measure of their loss.
The firefighters of Ōtsuchi.
I traveled a total of six times to finish the project and publish a book.
And this is the last picture I took with my two assistants, Mayumi Suzuki and Kazuhiko Chikaoka, who both lost their parents to the tsunami.
We built an arch with plastic tubes which they arranged in different positions over the remains of this house.
And if you look carefully at the photo you will see that their hands are holding this house of light.
In 2016 the government of Ōtsuchi invited me to show my project, but I had a feeling that it was not over.
There was something left to do.
So I suggested that we put together a collective exhibition which we set up in the gymnasium of a temporary school.
And we invited high school students and the people in town to take photos.
Now the kids did want to join in.
We gave them disposable cameras with one assignment: to tell their lives in pictures, their intimate whereabouts.
Five years away from the tsunami there were children back in the streets taking photos, recording its life.
And together, in community, we created a small photo archive of what Ōtsuchi is today.
The people in town took pictures to create new memories: The memories of the future of Ōtsuchi.
As for me, I started my own photo album telling my life and my travels with Lara.
And here we are in Japan, saying goodbye to the sea of Ōtsuchi.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Thank you very much.
From my flowering garden, I went on tour, to participate in TEDx talks representing the Southern Cone of South America.
I came across very courageous people who are defending our land with strength and a lot of courage.
We are many people, organized to defend the Serrania region from foreign exploitation that has a hand and retro and a devastating purpose to end our fauna which we have looked after until today.
I was born in a very rich country where there is silver and gold, there is also emerald, Coltan and oil.
As a child, my dreams were to have a well-established farm of cattle, different animals, trees and it was like that in the year of 1998, I came to a community known as the Garden.
I arrived as the manager of a farm and I had a few savings, and because of this, in 1999, I was able to buy my own land.
I began to work on it. Finally, I mounted a canteen, a business that thrived.
With the income, I was able to buy animals I was buying animals for their income.
In 2001, I saw the farm was well-established. It was how I pictured it in my dreams.
I said now my childhood dreams have come true.
In a quick moment, I felt my dreams were abridged.
In a time of armed conflict in Southern Bolivar region, Colombia.
It was then, when a group of paramilitary from Antioquia to the South of Bolivar, moved throughout those areas. And they burn anything in their path.
Once, twice and three times.
The community sees there is no alternative and fearing for their lives we simply give up.
Two days later, we returned to see what was left. We were saddened because all we saw were ashes, destruction and devastation.
We convened at an old school which was all that was left. We started counting the people to see what residents of the community were missing. We were all accounted for
which was a blessing from God and for us, that was one of
our biggest accomplishments.
We reflected there that material items, money, are very important in society but there is something that we all have to save and fight for it, and that is our lives come first.
Without it we have nothing.
I dedicated myself to community service for many years.
In 2007, we integrated different mine communities, muleteers, and in a community meeting 57 families united and it was there where naturally a social process was started, one that is still used today.
With five goals we have within the land to work on it.
The first is community organization in the land.
The second is the legalization of organization.
The third is influence the land we have.
The fourth is our identities, local, regional, emblem, national anthem and flag.
The fifth is management at the local, regional, national and international level.
That is where we say, why do we defend the Serrania?
Because there is biodiversity there, because there is life, fauna, its flora and all its surroundings.
Those of us who have been doing it with the river in Santo Domingo, with community standards, and criteria within the community by defending their standing within the land and the lives of the citizens.
We have not allowed for a single moment, the intervention of excavators that destroy everything in its path.
We decided that we have to declare the Serrania of San Lucas Historical Patrimony of Humanity.
Because for her it is the legacy of the new generation the one of our citizens.
They should be able to find something there.
So for this to happen, we have to all fight and be clear that we have one goal and that is that our lives come first.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
The first time we went, we saw this: a dump located in the bar area of La Latina which had been abandoned for 30 years.
A plot of land which no one had been able to build on because underneath it lied a Christian mural.
It was completely full of grass, glass, the street smelt of wee, it was a catastrophe.
We fell in love.

Hello. I'm Cristina Peregrina, this is my daughter Mae, we live in La Latina, and on this day, the 20th November 2015, along with other parents in the area, neighbours, architects, urban planners and town hall representatives, we made a decision: this filthy plot was going to be for our children.
Have you ever wondered where the children in the city are?
Why they don't play in the street?
Technology, mobiles, iPods, computers, TV and urban planning have lots to do with this.
There aren't places designed for kids, cars rule the city.
There is a lack of squares, gardens, places to be, to play.
For this reason, that day, we all made an agreement.
This dump, situated in La Latina along with the bars and tourists, was going to be for them, because in La Latina there are children too.
Since the first day that the children entered Almendro 3 -- because that's where Almendro is, just in the Cava Baja area -- children put down their pirate flag and get busy playing, and we ask them, 'What do you wish there was here?
How do you imagine El Almendro?
What are your dreams?'
And they say to us: 'Mountains, tree houses, pirates, a robot wardrobe, friends forever, never-ending sandwiches, an elephant, a human duck'.
At no point did they mention swings or games, and us adults asked for the same thing but in our own words.
Neither parents nor children wanted plastic things or toboggans, we wanted everything to be created each day, we dreamed of a natural space, without anything artificial to get in the way, where everything was going on.
Even the meetings happened on their own.
Some days we began playing games like 'pañuelo', other days, we put pieces of cardboard on the ground so that the children could construct their dreams.
We learnt to listen to and organised ourselves.
We've been discussing for almost two years, constructing as a community, deciding many things, meeting one day a week, every Tuesday.
The first demands were the most basic: water and flooring.
Although we had been cleaning every day, the rubbish kept coming back,
El Almendro had been a dump for too long.
Layers and layers of debris which we couldn't get rid of.
Finally, in December 2016 we carried out an a project to improve the condition of the space.
This project was special for lots of reasons: because the kids took part and got involved in the whole process; because we decided to use the materials from different warehouses in the district because we were cleaning, but always respecting the history of the place and the mural that had been there since the 11th century.
This wood, it's recycled.
With this, along with architects and urban planners, we've constructed this bench.
Another example of reusing materials, is this fence which was in Barceló Market.
When we wanted to throw away the walls of Almendro so that we could be seen from the streets so as to involve the neighbourhood, we wanted this fence to allow people to see what was happened on the other side.
And this wall, we painted it white to be able to project things; a few weeks ago we had our first party in Almendro we invited the whole neighbourhood, and we have inaugurated our summer cinema.
We organised a trip to the greenhouse in Retiro park so that the kids could choose the plants to put on the plot.
When we arrived, the gardeners explained which species were best suited to the space, but the kids had their own criteria: they liked the furry ones, the ones which looked like animals or which were exotic colors.
And once the plants arrived at Almendro, we all planted them together.
If you come to Almendro one day, what will you see?
You will see a space which we've wanted to keep empty, where the kids say: 'I'm not playing, I'm working'.
You will see kids with sticks and stones, carrying buckets of water from one place to another, with hands full of mud.
You will see how they think.
One day they construct a reservoir because they have become little architects.
Another day, a stone walkway, an igloo, and you will see how all these inventions are gone the next day, because everything they construct is temporary.
And in the meantime, mothers and fathers, we put our fears to one side, we stop saying: Careful!
Don't touch that! Don't do that!

We learn to relax and enjoy.
In El Almendro, we've created something unheard of: that of the old fashioned, the untamed, that which could only take place in a village or in the countryside has come to the city.
A lot of the time we don't understand what they are playing.
We don't need to understand, that's another key point.
In an educational setting which strives for a goal, the grade, the result, we don't want any aim, we want to enjoy the process, and, above all, learn from them at every opportunity.
If you come to Almendro it could be that you find treasures which the adults have been hiding, Egyptian papyrus, archeological remains, or surprised faces when the kids find them, because the adults want to play too.
But we've discovered that in El Almendro there are many more hidden treasures.
For example, the links between friendship and support which families in the neighbourhood have created, or links with the business owners in the area, that's to say, our assumed enemies.
One day, when we arrived at Almendro, we discovered this letter which read: 'Dearest children of Almendro, we're very happy that you're here; we offer you water and the use of our bathrooms.
Signed: a nearby lodging'.
And another day when it was really cold they came with hot soup for everyone.
So, you never know what will happen in Almendro.
I never could have imagined that I'd fall in love with a dump!
But there are three things we do know: That an empty space is a treasure, because in it you experiment and learn, that, if a city works for its children, it works for everyone; that Almendro can't exist on its own, it has to rub off on others to create an Almendro every day.
And, finally, we've done it, but now the most important thing remains which is looking after it.
This has been our unique way of inhabiting or conquering an abandoned space.
Sure there are many other things, but what's important is that this is possible.
So, come and see us, because there will always be never-ending sandwiches, friends for ever, and an elephant which perhaps you don't see, but is there.
Thank you very much.
(Applause) (Cheers)
Fabrics.
Handkerchief.
Diaper.
Veil, bandage, mask, blindfold.
Under vest, underpants, socks.
Nightgown, bra, panties.

Sheets. Towels.
T-shirt, trousers, shirt.
Scarf, hood, espadrilles.
Dress, coat.
Robe, burka, talit.
Lining, holster, band-aid.
Napkin, curtain, dishcloth,
tablecloth, blanket.
Canvas, lounger, awning.
Umbrellas, parachutes.
Ship sails. Fishing nets.

Pennants. Flags.
Fabrics to live.
Who challenges the fact of spending a day in life without touching one fabric?
Not by day, not by night.
We come into the world and we are covered in a white, soft, clean diaper.
It's the best place to arrive.
And to die?
Like mummies we keep wrapping ourselves up in cloths.
Whether it's bandages or shrouds.
Why fabrics?
With so many materials invented by human beings, nothing replaces them?
From the crop or the animal, human beings pulled out fiber to make a thread and start sewing stories.
Fabrics are a unique material.
They're soft, resistant, body-friendly, it's thermal, it's washable.
How many people do you know without clothes?
Hopefully a lot.
(Laughter) Naked, I mean, huh?
How many?
Clothes are our social skin.
What if I take my clothes off here now?
(Whistles) We make a pact between us with clothes as skin, which we rarely dare to break.
We have to resort to certain substances so we can be encouraged sometimes to take off our clothes.
Clothes are our social skin, it's our shell, our hairs and our feathers.
With clothes we build our identities.
We make ourselves different.
We woo with our clothes.
With clothes we uniform in roles, in trades, and in beliefs.
Between sheets we make love.
Between fabrics we build our families.
And every day, every night, we go back to our cradle of fabric, our bed.
Was fabric always that important?

Always. Because textiles are prehistoric.
The human race learned to weave before writing.
And weaving, it learned a lot more.
Weaving we learned to manage and organize materials.
Weaving we learned to create, to mix material worlds with spiritual worlds.
And weaving, as well, we build ourselves in our metaphors.
There's a cord that brings me to the world which is the umbilical cord.
There's a tissue that's my skin, which is a connected tissue.
And we're a big social fabric.
Right now a textile is filling up of me.
Of my perspiration, my smells, of me, of my body.
And the same thing is happening to you.
And later, my textiles are going to be filled up with the perfume of my loves.
I wish.
I wish the same happens to you.
What does the textile nests in it?
What's inside those nooks?
What does it keep besides dust, cells, DNA?
If we look at a textile to the magnifying glass we will see an incredible succession of organized points, that in a sequence of hugs relate to each other, as in a kind of loom, composing that textile whole.
What if we become a fabric?
Now, here, let's see.
How could you integrate?
It could be by the hands, the arms.
Let's see, try it.
Not bad.
At one point you used to do something like a kind of wave.
I'd like you to make a fabric.
Keep going, please. All.

And you know what?
Since I don't see you, raise your hands, let's see.
There you go. There too.
Let's see, a fabric.
That.
Let's see! If I make this human flag fly.
Let's go!
It has to fly to the bottom of the room.
Great!
We are a living human fabric.
Thank you so much.
(Applause)
I grew up unaware, like many of you, I imagine, that my heart was broken.
I had been an excellent student.
The kind that didn't need to open a single book to get a bunch of A's.
I had loving, open parents who never tried to tell me which way I had to go.
A grandmother that would hide chocolate bars under her bra cups.
Five brothers, a dog, two cats, and a parrot named Paco.
It was a real madhouse.
I confess, I had a wonderful childhood.
But on reaching adolescence, things got complicated, of course.
I got full of zits, and I began to devour literature and to participate in all manner of poetry contests.
At school, I participated in a study done by the University of Psychology.
100 teenagers were chosen at random.
We were taken to the main hall and told to draw the human being.
So far, so good.
But don't ask me what was so special about my drawing.
Don't ask me because to this day, I don't know.
All I can tell you is that they came back to me three times.
They'd make me do it over.
And as I was drawing, I would hear them talking to the director of the Center, saying that my view of the human being was worrisome.
I guess it was then that I stopped drawing.
And I was lucky.
I was 14 or 15 years old, and up to then, nobody had critisized my drawings.
I had grown up in an environment that stimulated my creativity.
And yet, the good grades, the poetry contests, those excellent grades, the psychological analysis of my drawing, and those good grades all managed to convince me.
Convince me that I was not among the chosen.
So I set aside my creativity, and dedicated myself to study and to work.
Most of us here today have grown up deceived.
Convinced that in a classroom of 25, 30, even 40 students perhaps only two had been blessed by that precious stone called "creativity."
As for the rest of us, we had to get educated.
During the schooling of my three children, I began to relive the whole process.
How could it be?
How could shool keep putting down their capacity to create?
My God, almost 30 years had gone by, and in this aspect, I can assure you that nothing had changed.
Some time later, I saw Ken Robinson's TED Talk about how schools kill creativity.
That talk changed everything.
I understood what I wanted and could contribute to society.
And I planned it all.
A job as Communication Manager, an excellent team, and a good salary.
Once I'd made the decision, there I was.
Alone, in a co-working space.
I don't know why we insist on calling it co-working because there are few places in the world where you can feel so alone.
I was concentrated behind my computer screen trying to give birth to the ideas that would give shape to the project that was about to begin.
But I couldn't write a single line.
I couldn't because before writing anything down, I needed to sprawl on the floor, get giant sheets of paper, find my colored pencils, and begin to draw and imagine all possible options.
Smudge, make mistakes, and little by little, reconnect what formal education had broken in two.
I assure you that I was stuck on square one.
Stuck because my education had been compartmentalized.
And that made me believe that before starting the project, I had to make a crucial decision.
A life or death decision.
I had to choose between art and literature.
Between drawing or writing.
And I couldn't.
I couldn't, because for me, separating those two languages meant splitting myself, literally, in two.
Can you imagine the situation?
40 years.
And feeling exactly the same way that any child feels who is asked to choose between the love of his father or the love of his mother.
It was then, at that moment, that the idea of PictoWriting was born.
Picto-Writing.
I decided then to design a method that would let children learn to write without having to stop drawing.
On the contrary, it would use the potential that images have, especially their own drawings, as a tool to teach them how to write.
Once I knew the educational innovation that I wanted to accomplish, I got to work.
I contacted a bunch of illustrators, writers, schools, teachers, linguists, and we began to actively participate in classrooms.
Do you know what I found?
And what I keep finding day after day in all primary classrooms?
A bunch of kids. A bunch of kids with broken hearts.
Clinging, like shipwrecked castaways, to their rubber erasers.
Don't be surprised if one of these days it's finally revealed that it's the Pro-Fab manufacturers that stock schools with so many erasers.
I ask you to think for a moment of your own children.
Remember when they were 3, 4, 5 years old. Before they started school.
When children draw at that age, they're not afraid.
They enjoy what they're doing, no matter what it is, or what anyone else thinks.
Mind you, their drawing in no way tries to be a photocopy of reality.
Rather, it's their unique interpretation of the world.
And the most important thing is that when they draw, they're connected.
If you ask them, they know very well what they're drawing and why.
We are born knowing who we are, and wanting to express it in drawings.
But suddenly we get to school.
To elementary.
And the only thing that matters is to learn how to write.
To have good handwriting, to not get out of line, not make spelling mistakes ... From one day to the next, we grow up.
And that's where the drama begins.
Drawing, that emotional, universal language, stops being a learning tool.
If you're lucky, you get to draw during art class, but mind you, the time has come to do it right.
The time has come to start erasing.

Welcome! Welcome, eraser!
You will start by teaching us to erase our mistakes instead of learning from them.
And little by little you'll end up censuring everything that was special about each and every one of us.
When a 6-, 7-, or 8-year-old child is learning how to write, and for whatever reason their accompanying adult prematurely corrects whatever the child is creating, judges whatever the child is producing before giving him or her time to create the story he or she wants to tell us, in that way inviting him to gain insecurity and begin to erase, what message do you think the child receives?
We teach them too early to not be themselves.
And to desperately seek out the adult's approval.
In less than one year, they will all be drawing and writing exactly the same.
Because as you can imagine, drawing is the first language we take away from them.
But all the others follow.
Of course, the time has come to teach them to read and write. But not at any cost.
I want to propose a simple change in the education of your children.
In the face of the visual bombardment that children grow up with today, let's include gymnastics of imagination.
And for that, it's very important that they not stop drawing.
That they grow in both languages.
PictoWriting gives us that opportunity.
It allows us to teach them to write.
Yes, to write.
But at the same time we stimulate their creativity and their capacity for reading and producing images without all that emotional baggage.
PictoWriting is a method that above all, and most importantly, respects the child's creative process.
Throughout these last five years, we have given PictoWriting training to nearly 300 teachers.
And more than 3,500 children have already grown up using this method.
The results are spectacular.
Spectacular because of everything that gets transformed.
When you change the way you teach them to write, you also change the way that teachers see their students.
Not long ago, a teacher was telling us, "Thank you.
I thought I had five good students. Now I know I have 25."
97% of kids have loved PictoWriting. And because of this, they write longer, richer, and better texts.
Now it turns out that learning how to write isn't a chore or an insurmountable obstacle.
Instead, they don't even want to go to recess because they're writing and drawing.
Oh, friend.
You see, creating stories is the closest thing to playing.
And 42% of those students have told us in their own handwriting that this method has made them feel special.
What a difference could be made in the world if we were to grow up knowing that each and every one of us is unique and special with an amazing story to tell. Thank you.
(Applause)
It's a pleasure, an honor to be here,
and most of all, to go over what I've been up to, the roads I've traveled in my life the one I've been given and which I'm still living.
I've always been interested in solving problems
since I was a little kid, and it's almost an obsession. The troubles I've see and faced have always been human ones.
I got to see people, their housing needs and so I founded a self-built housing cooperative back in the 80s.
In a village, in the community in Jose C. Paz, we organized a group who worked and lived in a very poor area of Munro and we built 36 houses.
Each of the people building a house didn't know which one would be theirs when the construction was over, as the families were chosen by lot.
I've always been interested in generating social movements
where problems, instead of being faced individually, be rather solved in collective groups, where people can build empathy and stop being invisible in order to become visible through the work achieved.
This is what keeps me going and motivated.
After this experience in Buenos Aires, I headed to Santiago Del Estero, to a village called "Los Juries."
The issue for those people was that they didn't have their property deeds.
They'd been living there for a long time, without having their land title certificates.
They did have a 20 adverse possession
of their land though. So I organized another movement called "MOCASE," or in Spanish, "El Movimiento Campesino Santiagueño," with the aim of getting these land title certificates.
This movement is still in existence, claiming and reaching to a wider territory, spreading not only in Santiago but also into Tucumán and Córdoba.
These two facts, although belong to the past for me, still give meaning to who I am and what I do; they are reflection boosters that keep me walking.
These two movements were conceived from a priestly position, as I had ordained at the Pallottine Fathers Congregation in 1968.
I had opted for the Church.
Why?
Because when I was young, I'd read a document from Medellin, which had been written by all the bishops in Latin America and which stated that in the continent where we are living right now there was a big gap.
This was something I took care of, I pondered on, and what eventually, led me to priesthood.
Based on this choice, I decided for the community of Pallottine Fathers who, unfortunately, were killed by the military regime of 1976,
Despite all the way I'd traveled, I kept struggling so this priesthood could be the instrument for finding ways to turn the invisible visible by means of concrete deeds, through social work, which is what drove me and is still the reason why I keep on this fight.
I'm in my third stage.
Firstly, it was the housing difficulty; secondly, the land title issue, and then I realized not only did we need to build houses or get land, but also look for ways to help those who have their produce to trade it somehow.
This important issue motivated me to go around the province of Río Negro and discover we can go along this line of work and generate what today is known as Mercado de la Estepa.
By then, I was no longer a priest.
I'd quit the ministry for reasons I'll share in another TEDx talk.
I was already a social worker. I've always been one just working from different positions.
One of them during the priestly ministry which led to MOCASE and building houses; now as an ordinary being who had discovered the organization needs were linked to trade;
so we set up the market Mercado de la Estepa.
At present, this market is 14 years old.
It's autonomous.
That setting up was promoted by Surcos Patagonicos, a civil association that created Mercado de la Estepa.
A successful project.
I've already said I've enough white hair,
I can already rest and retire to devote my time to thinking.
It wasn't so.
There're always new concerns in this world that become challenges, new issues that inspire me and get me involved again in these social movements.
Mercado de la Estepa is set today in Dina Huapi and has 300 organized families who, by means of their hands, produce what's known as "threads."
Simple threads made by hands of people who used to be invisible, and have now turned visible through the products offered on this market.
This cultural identity acquired by Mercado de la Estepa is acknowledged by various actors of the social world.
It's from this weaving of the threads that we are fascinated to see that a thread comes from an animal, and that from that thread you can make a garment like this one.
With this garment you can identify not just the product but he who has spun it, who knitted it and the origin of this final product-- a guanaco's piece of clothing.
Guanaco; I started to realize that the "guanaco" has an extraordinary social behavior.
Some may want to kill them all, as they compete for grazing.
In the province where I come from, Santa Cruz, they are considered a plague.
It's always struck me that when facing a problem, if different, we try to eliminate it, to lock it up, to hide it; as the guanaco is annoying, we try to kill it.
As a matter of fact, it was on the brink of extinction.
The National Fauna Authority considered it a protected animal, therefore, it has to be considered an endangered species which cannot only be protected, but also no longer be hunted, killed, used, or tortured.
Today, in Patagonia, there are 3.5 million guanacos.
Each guanaco produces 250 grams of thread.
So I got this idea that through a thread we could start "spinning" the stories of the people who spin and weave.
We can track them all the way from La Quiaca to Ushuaia.
Along this road, there're hundreds of invisible people who spin and weave products of a very high quality.
These are the threads that intertwine the history of a new way of thinking, a new way of developing a different economy.
It's a totally different world that we want to change, hence, this social movement which we generate in order to build for these absolutely invisible people a way to become visible through the product of their hands;
a product of the highest quality that can be identified through the hands of all the women inhabiting our dear Patagonia, or even along this road from Ushuaia to La Quiaca.
This is the reason why I'm excited about starting this other movement of spinners and weavers who can spin and create their unique stories.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
