I wasn't always this big.
I was once smaller. When I was younger, about this size, I was scared of things.
Well, now too, but at the time I was scared of different things than now.
And I had quite a peculiar fear: I was afraid to fall asleep.
Because when you sleep, consciousness disappears.
And that disappearance made me suspect that consciousness could be a bit fragile.
Who guarantees that when you go to sleep you're going to wake up the next morning? That your consciousness will be back?
I made myself these questions and obviously I would end up doing things like walking barefoot around the house all night, in order not to fall asleep.
Today, many years later, I am a scientist, more precisely, an size-L neuroscientist, and my job is to understand how the brain creates consciousness.
And what is consciousness?
Sometimes, the things that are most immediate for us are the hardest to understand.
And there is nothing more immediate than our own consciousness.
But an analogy that helps to think consciousness is to imagine us sitting in a theater. In that theater, the most complex and most real play is performed, the play that contains all the images that we see, all the sounds that we hear, all the emotions that we feel.
What we see in this play is what we see, what we hear in that play is what we hear, and what we feel in that play, what it makes us feel, is what we feel.
And what the play is staging are the contents of our consciousness.
And the play is consciousness itself.
When the stage lights of this play are turned off, consciousness also goes off.
Consciousness disappears.
But luckily, of course, when the stage lights are turned on again, consciousness comes back, consciousness reappears.
In my work, I used different tools to understand what happens in the brain when consciousness disappears.
One of those tools is, precisely, what scared me as a kid: deep sleep.
But I also investigated people under general anesthesia or patients in a coma, which are all conditions in which the lights that illuminate the play, our consciousness, are off.
And the stage where this play happens that contains everything we feel is, of course, inside the brain, in a set of regions called fronto-parietal network.
They are called like that because inside the skull, they are located above the eyes, front, and above the ears, to the side, parietal.
We are aware of something only to the extent that the information accesses the neurons of the front-parietal network, and from there it can access the entire brain.
It explodes and accesses the whole brain.
What happens is that in conditions such as deep sleep or conditions such as anesthesia or coma, the fronto-parietal network shuts down, just like the stage lights of the play go off.
So, thinking about these things, a few years ago, I started wondering about something that was going to change my career, but above all, it was going to change the way I understand the world and myself.
And the question is this: What if the front-parietal network not only does not turn off, but is turned on with an activity even greater than normal, with an activity even greater than that which we all have here and now, for example, conscious and awake?
What if the opposite happens to when we fall asleep in unconsciousness?
In a theater play a lot of things happen and you don't really see them on stage.
It's the behind the scenes.
All the work for the staging, and when you go to the theater, even with the lights that lights up the stage on, you never see those things.
But they're there.
So the question I made myself was the following: What happens if we not only don't turn off the stage lights, but we turn them on with such an intensity, that even the staging, everything going on behind the scenes, not only becomes visible, but becomes impossible not to see?
Clearly, tools such as deep sleep or anesthesia, that turn off the lights of consciousness, wouldn't help to answer this question.
I needed a different tool, a specific tool to manifest in a more intense way all those things that happen in the brain, in the mind, even those that are hidden there, behind the scenes.
The tool I needed was a manifester of minds.
And a mind manifester doesn't sound like the kind of tool you go and buy at any hardware store, but I found out they exist, and they're as real as deep sleep or anesthesia.
If you've never heard of mind manifesters, it's because of the name they have it's actually in Greek.
They're called psychedelics.
In Greek "psychē" means "mind" and "dēloun", "to manifest".
Mind manifesters.
Psychedelics are molecules.
And some are natural and can be found in cacti, like mescaline, or in fungi, like psilocybin, and others are synthetic, man-made, like LSD.
And they generate very intense changes in our perception of the world, in our emotions, but also in our perception of ourselves and, above all, of the relationship we have with everything around us.
These changes take place by interfering with the chemical conversations the neurons in our brains engage in when they communicate with each other.
In fact, on our research we learned that these neurons are mainly located in the fronto-parietal network of the brain.
And what psychedelics do is over-ignite the activity of the brain's fronto-parietal network.
We could say that they do the opposite to deep sleep or anesthesia, or what we see in a coma.
Currently, with my lab at the University of Buenos Aires, we are conducting the first study in Argentine history on the effects of a natural psychedelic.
And we're interested in studying what happens to the human brain when that happens, but also what happens to the person.
What does the person feel.
And one of our experimental subjects expressed this, perhaps, in a much better than I ever could.
Still sitting with a bunch of wires and electrodes attached to their head -- what we use to measure their brain's activity -- they looked at us and told us that their visual experience was so overwhelming, so intense, that they felt they had to open their eyes to stop seeing.
Open their eyes to stop seeing.
Psychedelics are also being researched as medicines, as therapy to help patients with depression or the anxiety that can bring about something as terrible as a cancer diagnosis.
And so far the results are very positive.
Evidence shows that patients who benefit the most from psychedelics as therapies are those who feel that the boundary that tells their own body and themselves from all other things disappears.
They feel themselves in all things.
They are no longer sitting in an armchair watching the play, the lights also illuminate them. So they understand that they and we, as well, we are all part of the play itself.
But the same experience, especially without proper supervision, can generate a state of great anxiety and anguish.
Which is not strange if we understand that a medicine is prescribed for someone who needs it.
And consuming it or supplying it outside this context is indeed illegal, but above all it is irresponsible.
We need responsibility.
Because as I said we're doing the country's first study in history with psychedelics, but it's 2019.
So, if you really believe me that they're such useful tools, why didn't anyone do research on them before?
Actually, in the U.S. in the 50s and 60s, psychedelics were intensely researched, but at one point something happened that shouldn't have happened.
Psychedelics sneaked from the labs, from the hospitals and were consumed massively, in inappropriate ways and therefore, were logically banned.
And that's why it's extremely difficult to do scientific research with psychedelics today.
This has to change.
Let us do scientific research with psychedelics!
This is not about consumption, it is about knowledge, it is about science and it is about medicine.
(Applause) We understand that there is a responsibility associated with that, that it's not automatic.
In the next 10 years, if this happens, we will be able to benefit greatly from psychedelics as a tool to understand the mind, as medicine to help those who really need it.
But that this happens in the next 10 years depends on not repeating all those mistakes of the past.
It depends on, essentially, that this time is different.
I personally feel differently already, because these questions, these new possibilities make me so passionate, that I dream of these things.
So I'm going to sleep tonight, so excited, that I cannot even think about whether or not I'm going to wake up the next morning.
Thank you.
(Applause)
How much do you think an idea is worth?
It's simple, it's worth nothing.
There is not such a thing like good ideas or bad ideas; all ideas not yet developed are worth nothing.
If Plato could hear us saying this, he would kill us all. But don't worry, this is not what Plato meant.
Well, we could say that ideas are like pets, we have to love them, we have to take care of them, we have to protect them; and love them, is the most important thing because it's yours, is your idea and your pet.
It has to be that puzzle where all the pieces fit in perfectly.
Speaking of ideas, what about social networks?
How many of you use them?
Can you raise your hands?
Dear God!
OK.
(Laughter) Everybody uses them since all of you raised your hands, all of you use the social networks; and I imagine you use them for a reason.
Social networks can be a great tool for social changes.
There are those who'd say it's useless, that they only bring trouble or problems. Well, I don't think it's like that, neither do my partners. So, we created a social network, a social network for changes and its purpose is to, well, change that Math teacher you don't like, or fix your favourite skating park.
And during that process, of creating the project and such, a couple of things came up such as those certain abilities needed to implement the project, but which we haven't been taught about in school.
So, I encourage you to always learn by yourselves.
You might say, "I do enough of that in school."
No, it's not like that, because the things you learn by themselves you'll never forget.
On top of that, when you have an idea or learn something new, there will always be those who'll say, "There are lots of flaws in that,"
they will only discourage you, but if your idea is good and is worth pursuing, go ahead!
My final conclusion, you might have a different opinion, but that's OK, is that, after reaching out to people and encouraging them to use social networks, use Twitter,
I'm not going to do that anymore.
Now, it's up to you.
And also, I encourage you to be the ones who believe in and share your ideas because the Internet, social networks included, offers a world of possibilities and the real world needs to know about your ideas.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Tonight, this bridge unites each and every one of us.
It unites all of us from this neighborhood with those who have come from elsewhere.
It unites those who, different or similar, live with us here.
Whose diversity, whose beauty and whose richness -- that diversity which the colors of this bridge - this night - are meant to represent.
This bridge unites us with a story that began three years ago.
When a group from the neighborhood of San Cristobal, working with the local organizations and with the collaboration of a group of experts decided that this spot, this spot here where we are tonight, would be the place to return to participating.
It would be the place to return to working, to return to unity, and it would also be the place to begin imagining again.
At first we didn't believe that it would be possible.
We didn't believe it possible to build something that would last so long,
something that would be cared for.
But of course, how could you not care for something you made?
In 2012, we started to make the plan for this huge, transformative idea, and in the beginning of 2013 it began.
Retaking this public space, step by step, action after action, metre after metre.
In measures we began to obtain the necessary resources.
Kids who were no more than a foot tall painted first the letters and the wall that I have behind me.
Three months later, they blocked off traffic and put up ladders and scaffolding so that moms, dads, kids and grandkids,
almost 200 people, could each paint a diamond in order to take back 1700 square metres of surface area on this bridge.
In November of 2013, with a container of wood that a business no longer wanted, the people of the neighborhood made a triangle that they would later add with another in order to make the furniture all around this bridge, which you can see with your own eyes.
They also build the stage where I'm standing right now.
And so, without being painters, or builders, or artisans, even though there were kids, like I said, who came here from the first day to build, who wanted to work the drill, we worked as a team to make this space what you see tonight: truly beautiful.
And at this point, a question comes up: Now what?
What should we do with this space that has been reported on, has been photographed, has received awards -- That has been seen in music videos, that has been visited by so many from Germany, from Paris, from Qatar, and from Canada.
What to do with a space on which so many people have worked?
And here, ladies and gentlemen, is where we come in: the boys and girls of the bridge.
We're a group of kids from the neighborhood and elsewhere who have proposed to make incredible things happen here, things that wouldn't normally happen.
We've decided to create cultural experiences with the neighborhood here, at this site, at the bridge.
We are the boys and girls of the first course of cultural management, and we work for the chance to shake up our streets, our neighborhood.
But we also work for the chance to wake up our vitality.
But how do you learn outside of regulated schools and official training?
Mainly you learn by practicing, forming a foundation of good ideas and of course a foundation of mistakes.
Often we had to learn how to do things we didn't know, and sometimes we had no choice but to learn how to do things that wouldn't have even occurred to us.
We had to learn to weld, because we needed to build a mobile audio-visual device to make movies.
We had to learn to use Photoshop because we needed to make posters and scissors and glue were not good enough.
We also had to learn to write press releases because we needed publicity.
And now we're learning public speaking, because we need to communicate.
(Applause) We've also learned that in a place like this bridge anything can happen. Almost anything.
It doesn't need to be just film, dance or theater.
Or even all three at the same time.
We could be watching a concert, listening to the concert while we are eating paella, for example.
We've organized showings of films under this bridge that weren't only film.
We've counted over 100 Charlie Chaplins in "Charlie Chaplin in Modern Times."
All wearing bowler derbies made in old shops and learning to fall and get up like Chaplin did, thanks to the help of a few specialized acrobats who helped us knock each other around without anyone getting hurt.
A young poet from the neighborhood, with a huge passion for poetry and spoken word inspired the Festival of the Word, which gave the opportunity to come to this exact spot to poets and to rappers, to amateurs and to experts.
We've also learned that theatre can be whatever you want and not what you expect.
We've collaborated with a group of professionals who have taught us that theatre isn't just going somewhere to see how others act, but that theater can be a lively, communal experience.
The boys and girls of the bridge have learned in this neighborhood that culture isn't just found in the city centre or on the posters in the metro. You don't have to be a snob to make culture.
Culture is more of a common imagining. It comes from the people.
People like every one of you who is here, people with a first and last name, with frustrations, fears, and interests.
People, above all, with some concerns to be aware of.
And if you ask us if we'd want to do this course in an official location, we would say that we now know that a building, an official location, is not the only place where you learn.
What we learn here in the neighborhood, complements what we can learn in a building.
And it is precisely that that has made us specialists.
We, the boys and girls of the bridge, are not an exclusive group but a mind-set.
A mind-set that wants incredible things to happen here. Things that will make you happy to talk about later.
Right now we're a small group, but we want to be much bigger with more neighbors who are willing to offer crazy ideas.
With more kids from all over the city who are also willing to get involved with this neighborhood or with any other.
Not to mention with collaborators and other associations who see in this space, in this colorful bridge, an opportunity.
Who see this colorful bridge as a location of freedom.
We're proud that this neighborhood can be an example of compromise, that this neighborhood can be an example of creativity, and that is is also an example of energy.
A neighborhood working on getting rid of its reputation of conflict.
A neighborhood where people know that we don't need to await change sitting down.
You are also part of the bridge.
You encourage this adventure and give it life.
Tonight this bridge unites us all.
And only 15 minutes out of the centre of Madrid, this bridge waits for you with open arms.
Be welcome.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Please rise your hand if you've never stepped on an ant.
As you can see, I didn't even rise my hand myself, because, long before I liked insects, I'd also stepped on some of them; even when I began loving insects, I considered ants as the shabbiest and more insignificant among all insects.
But as soon as I became more interested in them, and I discovered all the things they're able to do, they become my passion.
They make so many cool things that I could go speaking about them a whole day.
But don't worry, I've chosen some examples I want to share with you.
This is Formica Rufa:
This ant loves living in big pine forests, because they make their nests using pine needles.
These nests are huge.
An ant, not bigger than 1,5 cm, can build nests bigger than I am.
And this is only what you can see, because under this ant hill a big tunnels and galleries network is spread, and it's bigger than what you see.
These nests are strategically placed so that they can receive enough sunlight, in order to keep the anthill warm and help their offsprings grow faster.
But this structure is so enormous and is so full of ants, that the heat inside would be unbearable, if it were not because they build their own air circulation system.
We could say that these ants are like builders and architects. But, what would you think if I tell you that the next species are bakers?
These are Messor barbarus:
They are very common in Spain. They can be found in parks, gardens, and dry places.
They're easily recognized because of their very marked pathways through which ants walk carrying seeds.
But that's what you see at first sight, because under the ground, an amazing process is taking place; they will be elaborating the so called "ant bread."
They don't eat the seed they bring to the nest straight away. They open them, extract the inside, chew and mix it up with their saliva, until they create a paste like this one, very similar to bread crumb, and that's what they feed of.
Afterwards, they pile the shells, creating a cone around the anthill, that is very unique to this species.
I'm sure you have seen one of this anthills sometime.
Now, it's farmers' turn.
Two genuses have this behaviour: Atta and Acromyrmex, like this one.
These are leaf cutter ants: they cut leaves and bring them to their nest.
But this is nonsense, because ants don't eat leaves.
So, why do they do it?
Well, the answer is down in the huge chambers inside their nests where they have fungal gardens, which they feed with the leaves they collect,
and the fungus itself serves as nourishment for the ants of the colony.
Amazing! Isn't it?
But wait, the next ones are weaver ants.
This is Oecophylla, also known as the weaver ant.
As its name indicates, they are experts on weaving.
And, as they live in the trees, they build their nests using leaves.
First, some ants gather the leaves with their bodies.
Then, other ants pass through the leaves with a larva on their mouth.
This larva will secrete the silk that the ants use for weaving.
So they pass the larvae from one side to the other of the leaf till they make a very resistant material and their house is built, like this one.
After that, they even build rooms inside!
I've already spoken about the builder, baker, farmer and weaver ants.
Now it's rancher ants' turn.
These are Dolichoderus, and their cattle are aphids and mealybugs.
They're good ranchers, so they carry them to graze in soft plant shoots.
Once they have them properly fed, they "milk" them using their antennae. The aphids give the ant a liquid that is actually ant's food.
Aphids are considered as a part of the colony, that's why ants take care of and protect the aphids as another ant. They even use their bodies as shields around the flocks.
Now it's the turn of the invincible army.
I'm sure you've heard about the film "The naked jungle," where the ants devour everything they found.
It was a bit of science fiction, but it's based on true events.
These are the African Dorylus and the American Eciton. They're called army ants because they move aplenty through the forest soil, devouring everything that isn't able to escape.
They can eat from spiders to scorpions, or even a full chicken.
They are nomadic because they search for new hunting territories, and they build their nests with their own bodies, as they have no time to dig one.
After the invincible army, I'm going to speak about the great conquerors: Solenopsis invicta, or the fire ants, due to its painful sting.
They are good conquerors due to two main reasons: they can adapt to any habitat and they can build various structures to surpass obstacles.
Imagine that one of these colonies finds a stream.
Well, they build a bridge with their own bodies, and the ants pass over it carrying larvae and the queen.
After that, they help the ants to cross to the other edge.
But what if they find a big lake or a river?
Then, they build a raft with their bodies that floats over the surface and they are carried along by the current until they reach the other edge, ready to conquer new territories.
We can say that all the ants I've spoken about are a kind of superhero.
And, of course, there is a villain in every superhero story.
Our villains name is Polyergus.
They're the experts on attacking, tricking, and stealing.
But they don't have a clue on how to take care of their offsprings, so they solve this problem in a peculiar way.
They find their victims' nest, they spray a chemical substance at the entrance that confuse them to take advantage of it and quickly steal all the pupae.
Here we see one of them carrying a pupa to its nest.
Already in their own nest, the pupae will turn into worker ants that will be their slaves and will take care of the offsprings for the rest of their lives.
Here you have some examples about the power of ants.
I hope that after this brief introduction to their world you will be interested in investigating about them or at least that the next time you'll see an ant line you will think twice before stepping on them, because you'll never know if you're destroying a villain or a real superhero!
(Laughter) Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Why don't students learn the science they are being taught?
Good question, isn't it?
But actually, it's the title of a key text for teaching natural sciences, that shows us not only the crisis of education but also to what extent we have made it natural.
Shouldn't we ask ourselves, if we are teaching the science we have to?
Take for instance astronomy.
Astronomy is a science that fascinates most children and teenagers. It has simple concepts we all understand.
For example, the Earth revolves around the Sun, and takes a year to go around it but because it is so far away, this movement is very fast.
It travels at 100,000 kilometers per hour.
Nonsense!
Patricia Knopoff: What happened?

Daniel Badagnani: There was a mistake.
PK: Isn't this my talk?
DB: No, no, it is!
The 100,000 kilometers per hour, I say, it's not... PK: Why?
DB: It's very fast.
PK: It's the Earth.
DB: That's why.
The Earth doesn't move. Look, it's still.

PK:. I don't get how you know
with such a certainty, that it's still.
Take a look and tell me.
(Whistle and train engine) [Top Secret, 1984] DB: Let's see, I see there
the platform goes backwards. See?
PK: Thus, the train moves.
DB: I say that the train moves, but it doesn't, look. See?
There's the ground, the wheels spinning, the train was still, actually.
PK: The problem was that you took first the platform as a point of reference.
And then you took the floor as reference.
The question I'm going to make, if the floor also moves, how would you compare?
DB:. Ah, what you say
is that if I move and the rest also moves, I don't see it.
OK, but I feel it.
PK: I don't know how to feel the movement.
DB: Let's see, in the bus, if I don't take hold on something... Poof!
PK: Yes, but when the bus travels at a constant speed, I let go and I don't fall.
DB: OK, but without the rattle.
PK: But that's because of the potholes, not the speed of the bus.
DB: Ah, now I understand planes.
PK: What do planes have to do with buses?
DB: When the plane is taxing, the flight attendant if serves you coffee, she can pour it with no problem, nobody notices anything.
PK: Except for take off and landing...
DB: All right.
But in the end, all this is to say that movement is relative.
PK: Exactly.
DB: But you started the talk saying,
"the Earth revolves around the Sun."
Let's see, if movement is relative, why can't I say that the Earth stays still and the Sun goes around?
If it's the same.
PK: If you like.
DB: How come, "if you like"? Admit it!

The my-backyard-centric theory is great.
PK: My-backyard-centric?
What is the my-backyard-centric theory?
DB: The my-backyard-centric theory is the theory that states that the cosmos revolves around an Internet antenna in my backyard.
(Laughter) PK: And I'm the one saying nonsense!
DB: The evidence is overwhelming, eh?
As well, I have a highly precise mathematical model, but it doesn't fit in the margin, so I brought video footage, please...
PK: Do you get to play a video in my talk?
DB: Yes, they said
I had to improve it. Sorry.
Well, there we see my house's backyard, at the bottom center, the water tank, alongside, the antenna, which is the cosmos' backyard.
I set a camera, I took pictures for an hour and a half,
see them played all together!
PK: What? The stars move.
DB: They revolve around the antenna!
PK: They move.
DB: If you don't see it, notice what happens if we play them together.
PK: Oh!
DB: The whole sky revolves around my backyard's antenna!
PK: Shh! If you want
to test this gentleman's theory, would you go and ask for permission to go to his backyard and test it?
Or would you test it in your own backyards?
Because it's true what he says.
And there's a whole mathematical model that holds what he says.
But we don't verify theories only in an empirical way. Here, it's aesthetically displeasing that the center of the universe would be in this man's house!
And then I ask, where does the knowledge we bring from books end?
DB: They are dogmas.
They become dogmas when we repeat them based on faith, without noticing that in science, claims need contexts.
And I'll say it: it is not true that the Earth revolves around the Sun.
PK: No?
DB: No!
Nor that it is still.
They are points of view with a purpose, and sciences don't discard and can't discard any of them.
So the statement, "Earth revolves around the Sun" is a dogma.
And we don't want dogmas, do we?
So please, Pato, tell us what do we do to "dedogmatize" science in the classroom.
PK: That is easy, by not being the bouncers of science.
Instead of positioning ourselves between our students and bronze statues of Newton, Galileo, and Pascal, and deciding who understood best the complete theories, let's ask the kids come up with crazy theories.
DB: Of course! What is more valuable?

That they repeat statements they can't grasp but are taken from books, or that they come up and discuss their own theories, knowing why they say what they say, knowing that isn't the final truth? That will allow them to better understand how science is done from the inside, and practice it, if it was their vocation.
PK: We are not bouncers of science, and we invite you all not to be either.
We are convinced that if the science that gets to the classroom is dogmatic, it's no longer science.
DB: The keyword here is emancipation.
Emancipation of ideas, emancipation of subjectivity.
And the response we get from teachers and students is awesome.
PK: It's true.
We are Choiols, we are not Dani and Pato. We are a group of graduate students and teachers from the city of La Plata, who do astronomy at roots level, but as an excuse to do epistemology.
We do astronomy at roots level, and thus we develop critical thinking.
DB: And with this talk we'll be famous!
(Laughter) PK: Why?
Are they going to give us helium balloons?
DB: Oh, no, Pato. It's a way of saying.

Up, where the great ones are. Get it?
PK: No, I don't understand, because I'm on top of the world, so I don't understand... DB: You're already bragging, Pato!
PK: No bragging, no!
We all are on top of the world, Dani!
Bring a globe, because a good Choioler never leaves their house without a globe!
(Laughter) OK, put it as it goes.
DB: There it is.
PK: Why like that?
I don't know! The manufacturer
must have made this base for a reason!
And where does emancipation leads if you come up with the manufacturer?
Behave, Dani!
Place yourself on the globe!
DB: Me, on the globe. Let's see,
Buenos Aires. There...
PK: Good.
DB: That's it.
PK: So, you are like this.
DB: Well, no. How is it? Wait,
it would be something like...
PK: You have to take this crap out of it because it's bothering you.
Now, all is left is to point the north to the North, the south to the South, and it is just like the real planet.
See?
DB: Sure, if you take the planet and you shrink it without tilting it, it ends up like this.
PK: As if you zoomed in, and if you placed under the Sun?
DB: If under the Sun, what is under the Sun here, are places on Earth where it's daytime now, and where the shadow is, it's nighttime, and you see where it's dawn
and dusk at this time.
Great. There's a problem, though!

I see the globe, but the Earth, I have to imagine it.
PK: Yes, it's true, is a little abstract, but, maybe we could compare it with something also lit by the Sun that we can see in the sky.
Such as the Moon.
DB: Oh, that's awesome.
You go out with a ball during daytime and you see the Moon, you place it under the Sun and you see that.
PK: Of course, that's no Photoshop.
That is, the ball and the Moon are lit by the same Sun at the same time.
And they look the same.
DB: Excuse me that I go back, but...
why is just Buenos Aires on top of the world?
PK: Ha, ha, ha! Well, actually,
it's not just Buenos Aires.
All places are on top of the world.
See, these are photographs from around the world where each of those people tilted the globe in the same position, according to their site.
DB: Let's see, you say that if each of us placed their place at the top, and they orient it towards the 4 cardinal points it would end up just like the Earth?
PK: Exactly.
DB: But then all these globes are equal to each other, as well.
PK: Of course.
DB: No, well, not so fast...

Don't know what to say, wait, let me see.
In that picture you have Chivilcoy,
very close to here,
so the globe is like this, that's fine.
The other image is from England.
England is around here, it's over there. The ground there would be like this,
so we have to turn this photo around.
Aha! They are the same.
PK: And just like the real planet.
This way of positioning the globe is called a parallel, because it's parallel to the real planet, and it's much more democratic.
But it's also called a "freed globe", because it was freed from that crap we took out of it, and besides, it's freed from the lie of believing that the North is above.
Because above is the sky.
And having the globe in this position, the range of phenomena to study is huge.
For example, when it's warmer, days are long and shadows are short.
At the same time, elsewhere in the world, days are short, shadows are long, and it's cold.
DB: Yes. OK.
But we have to be careful and pay
attention to kids's genuine questions.
PK: Yes.
DB: A sentence, a seemingly naive question like, "Why does the Moon follow me?"
is a wonderful trigger.
PK: Yes. The problem is, like Freire said,
when the teacher answers questions the students didn't ask.
DB: That's it.
PK: Kids pose very ingenious and creative questions and the most powerful way to destroy them is to resort to book knowledge.
Now, if you dare play with your students, to learn with them, to move away from your role as bouncer, if you dare leave the comfort of legitimized knowledge, you will not lose face.
Because seriousness is not solemnity
and, instead, you'll have a lot of fun.
Besides, let's face it, Dani, the role of bouncer is a very ungrateful one.
DB: Yes, exactly.
And what we are saying doesn't only work for astronomy, and natural sciences in general, but for all that can be taught, I think.
For example, we always talk about educating good citizens.
Well, a citizen with critical thinking, after a moment of reflection, can't say that this form of representing the world is a lie.
Such citizen has to be able to analyze the difference between the history told by Billiken and according to Zamba of Paka Paka, or any form of historical revisionism, without saying that one is false.
I mean, it has to seriously understand the concept of historiographical line.
PK: Yes, well, but for primary school, isn't it a bit abstract?
DB: Yes, applied to San Martin and Rosas, definitely.
Now, what if you ask them to write collectively the history of their class?
The methodological problems of history would emerge in this situation.
How do they decide which facts are relevant and which aren't?
How do they decide which ones were the real version of what happened?
Differences would begin to emerge.
Groups would being to emerge by affinities, common interests.
There you can compare with real historiographical lines, and as a result, you provided your students with a super powerful tool, and you, as a teacher, had a great time.
PK: It's true.
We call this teaching for emancipation.
As we said, we do astronomy as an excuse.
A fascinating excuse because it's fair to enjoy teaching.
DB: But also, we have it clear, PK: if we move from the role of bouncers, DB: if we move from the door... PK:... and stop pointing the privileged with the fingers of dogma, DB: we'll all enter the world of science.
(Applause)
A few days ago, I joined a somewhat eccentric organization called the Association of Crazy Entrepreneur Minded Skydivers.
You might say, "Adolfo, what does skydiving have to do with entrepreneurship?"
I would like for those of you who have ever undertaken a venture to raise your hand.
For some reason, these people have many similarities to a skydiver.
In other words, they are prone to take risks.
In my case, I have already jumped from the airplane several times.
I have created four enterprises, of which only two have survived:
an architectural magazine and a consulting firm.
I've already a closet full of crazy ideas that have not worked out, but will help with successful initiatives.
Ladies and Gentlemen, an entrepreneur is no superhero; it's a person in flesh and blood that is ready to put on the parachute.
Five years ago, I resigned from an organization that didn't welcome my innovative ideas and started my own business.
It seems like a labyrinth full of obstacles, and it has been.
But to venture has been the best decision made in my life.
A week ago, it's been one year since my architecture magazine has been in existence.
After seven editions, the challenge now is to create a community where the players in the construction sector can meet up, have a drink, and decide which companies can build together.
Here's the million dollar question: Why are there people who venture and others who don't?
Did I take a pill one day and decided to leave my traditional employment and create a company?
The truth is there is no one magic formula or grandma's secret recipe to be entrepreneurs.
All of us present here have a parachute and tools that we have been accumulated all along in our lives, and it's time we are ready to use them.
Besides making the decision to open the parachute, it is important you have a passionate idea, and in addition, a detailed contingent plan of action.
At some moment, you must create an enormous shield of wood, because there will be potholes along your path, and many people who will not accept your ideas.
Besides, you must have the capacity to communicate your ideas just like a good salesperson.
For instance, you should be able to say to the person sitting on your side that your ideas is needed like one needs oxygen to breath.
In a changing world, the digital gap has reduced drastically, and already more than 40% of the population has access to the Internet.
That means, we are all entrepreneurs, we must see if in reality, communications are substantial.
A week ago, I went out of town and when returned, I saw in my WhatsApp more than 800 messages from 13 different people.
After an hour of reviewing, I realize that very few of the messages were really relevant.
Therefore, as entrepreneurs we must align ourselves to these new paradigms to communication, because otherwise, I will not adequately understand our clients, our partners, suppliers, and our world in general.
They all will be more competitive and will say, "Adolfo stayed behind."
To venture means to enhance human relations.
My magazine has prospered because I created a virtual community that supports live events where people and clients meet, and we can find out the real needs of people.
If I could do it over again, I no doubt will be an entrepreneur.
Entrepreneurial has allowed me to be an extrovert and to see life in a positive manner.
Besides that, it gives me the humility to understand my limitations, but also an electricity that runs through my body that makes each day better.
According to CEM statistics, the venture rate is 8% in our countries.
Should we be satisfied with one out 10 persons will have a successful company?
If we are the leaders who will lead in the region, we must break these schemes and make this event into hundreds of successful ideas and entrepreneurial ventures.
That way, we can develop the region.
A while ago, I was afraid, and now I am an entrepreneur.
I invite all of you to save your fears, grab your parachutes you have on your back, and launch as many times as needed to accomplish your dreams.
I'll see you up there.
Thank you.
(Applause)
When I say love for the land, I mean, love for my Cuban land
and also for the land of the entire world.
My nanny was of Congolese origin. Her name was Isabel Cantero
and she was unfortunate enough to having put up with me. I believe I was a very naughty girl.
I was very lively, but paid
close attention to her stories.
She taught me to love the land and everything around us because everything around us is alive,
and she taught me that everything is alive, from a pebble to a flower. She would tell me, "Don't pluck that flower!"
You have to ask the flower for its permission.
She taught me a so many things;
she died at 102; I used to call her Chicha.
Chicha was so extraordinary that she taught me how to smoke a cigar when I was only 10, behind my parents' back, and soak it in cofee. She chewed tobacco. She smoked long cigars, soaked them in coffee, and let me smoke them too.
And I was smoking already
when I was 10; I was a precocious girl.
I didn't want to say it really, but I was.
She told me a fascinating story
about the creation of the Earth, a story that had been transmitted to her by her ancestors, her grandparents. When I was 4 or 5, the story went like this: before the Earth was created,
there was only heaven and sea.
Then, God - or Olofi, as we call it in our religion, said,
"I am so bored! I am not doing anything.
I just move around from one cloud
to another, from here to there.
"I am so bored! Let's create something."
Then, he came up with the idea
of the seven princes. He himself created them, made them princes, and sent them down using a chain and a black sack.
But, in so doing, one Kola nut
fell from his hands. The seed grew and became a huge ceiba tree, with all its branches and green leaves.
The last crowned prince was sent down; he gave something to each prince: herds to some, and women or riches to others. He went on giving great things to each one of the princes,
but the last one was terribly naughty.

This prince couldn't stop pestering, so he was given a black sack. When he felt bored, while his brothers were seeing how they could manage on earth, he opened the sack, and in found a gourd and a lizard.
And he wondered, "What is this gourd?"
He opened it and a little bit of dust fell.
Suddenly, the dust started to spread, the lizard came down and told the hen:
come down, this is firm land.
The hen started pecking and pecking, and made the continents,
divided the sea and the earth. Everything was completed nicely.
All the Orishas and the seven crowned princes came down and made the man.
But what did man do?
He started fighting against others for land, cattle, women, for everything. There was an enormous brawl
all around the world.
And what happened?
The Earth started
to shake and wondered, "What did I do to deserve this?"
In the meantime, the gods up there were getting contaminated with all the toxic gases emanating from the Earth.
The Earth, the mother
of all mothers, was desperate.
The mother commended herself to all the gods, to Chandra, to Allah, to all the gods known and to be known, so that they would help her understand what was happening.
The gods gathered together.
Now, I will ask you to close your eyes because we have to help the gods devise a way to solve Earth's problem, including wars.
When the gathering was half through,
a drone appeared.
There were many people killed,
many others were seriously injured and forced to use wheelchairs or walking sticks, and some others lost their lives because of high pollution levels.
Now I will ask you again
to close your eyes for a minute, to give strength to the gods,
so that they can solve
the problems of the Earth.
Did you close your eyes?
Did you give it a thought? Could you imagine what these gods would think of the Earth?
The Earth was crying. There were earthquakes, seaquakes, volcanoes.
The Earth is our mother;
she gives us everything.
Let's close our eyes and wonder:
What did the gods say?
Olofi is asking you to open your eyes.
I'll tell you what to do.
Blow. Blow, all together.
Give all your love to the Earth.
Let's ask the Earth to forgive us for what we are doing to it.
Let's spread love among human beings.

Let's stop war and spread peace among us.
So blow hard. Blow!

Blow for the Earth! Blow for our mother!
Blow for the well being of human beings
and for the satisfaction of the Earth
with what the gods have done for us.
(Yoruba) Thank you very much.
(Applause)
I was 25 years old and I needed to go back to work.
I had a son to feed and a rent to pay.
I had accepted the offer from a client for an administrative position in a metallurgical factory.
I felt like that I would be able to leave my old job behind.
That job I felt so ashamed for, trying to hide it in a thousand ways.
I arrived at the office just as always.
On this occasion, my boss asked me to make coffee for 40 people.
So I went to the kitchen, I made the coffee, I served it in cups, I divided them in groups of ten, I put them on a tray and I headed for the meeting room.
In the room there was a huge black table.
It was full of men;
the only woman was me.
The first person I served the coffee to was my boss and when I tried to serve the rest, my boss grabbed me strongly by the arm and in front of everyone he yelled: "This coffee is cold, go, go, go, go.
Go and prepare everything again."
There, in front of everybody's silence, I felt so embarrassed, that the only thing I could do was to grab the tray, bow my head and leave.
In the kitchen, while chewing anger, I realized that this work wasn't for me.
"If he shouts at me in front of everyone again, I throw the boiling coffee over him and I leave," I thought.
And then I felt inside of me that my old job, the one many people had made me believe I had to feel shame for, wasn't so bad.
I could put my limits there, I could put my conditions.
I could say no there.

So I made a decision and I came back.
I became a prostitute again.
I had the certainty that, being a woman, part of the working class, that represented the best option for me.
I stopped living it as something clandestine.
I didn't have to leave my house in sweat pants to dress up and make up on my way to work.
However there was still something missing, I needed to have enough courage to tell my mom and my son, what I was doing.
At work, every day is different.
I arrive to the street corner, I greet my coworkers.
Because we don't work alone, we are always in twos or threes, as a way to protect ourselves.
We tell one another when we arrive, when we leave and when we come back.
We advise on the traits each client has
and we have a code: we don't serve people that don't meet the standards we define.
There, standing there in the corner, I stay staring firmly at each cars that passes in search of some sign of a future client.
But other times, I arrange the meetings in advance.
But not all the people who do sex work decide to work on public spaces.
Many of my mates decide to work through social media, in private apartments or, many others also, advertise their services in websites.
A long time ago we had a problem with a guy that turned into a nightmare: he threatened us, harassed us, he was totally obsessed.
And you know why?
Because we refuse to serve him since he didn't meet our standards with one of our coworkers.
And as revenge, face to our feminist sorority, he had no better idea than to become a member of the neighborhood board.
And in a very short time he managed to convince the rest of the neighbors to get us out of the neighborhood.
That was the first time that we felt the need to ask for help, of having to go to a State facility and denounce the actions of that neighbor and also the discrimination that we suffered by some people in the neighborhood.
This is how my activism began.
But a long time had to pass before I could identify myself as a unionized sex worker.
In AMMAR, the Association of Women Sex Workers of Argentina, I learned a lot of things.
I learned that we are not alone even though the State ignores us. I learned that my work is not a crime and that I have the right to do a responsible use of public space without having to pay the police to let us work. (Applause) I learned that most problems that we women sex workers have, are the same ones all working women have for being born in this macho and patriarchal society.
(Applause) I understood that we still have to join forces to continue raising the awareness of a large portion of society, so that they can tell between the crime of human trafficking and the free practice of sex work.
(Applause) Because just like my colleagues and me defend every day the free choice we have about our own bodies, we condemn human trafficking but not only in the sexual market, also in other labor markets like rural work and textile work.
(Applause) This life path faced me with great challenges.
Not only did I have to learn to support and lead my partners in fight, I also had to face my own fears.
The biggest one: having to sit and tell my mom that I wasn't really an administrative employee, but a sex worker.
I was very scared.
I thought she was going to be mad at me, that she would never talk to me again, that she would even exclude me from my own family circle.
But I was wrong.
My mom could see beyond the stereotype that society usually imposes on sex workers, that which places us as bad women, bad mothers, and night women.
My mom didn't feel pity for me, neither considered me a victim.
She accepted me as I was, as I am, a proudly hard-working woman, a sex worker.
(Applause) Telling my son was another story.
At that point, I wasn't afraid anymore, I just had to be careful with words.
Santino lives it naturally.
So much, that on one occasion, in a task he had been given at school they had to draw what mom and dad work on.
And Santino drew me in the corner of the street in front of a car.
And it went much further, he confessed to the teacher and his other classmates that his mother was engaged in sex work.
The teacher, mouth open in front to such a big confession, interrupted him and said: "You're wrong, Santino.
Your mom is a social worker."
(Laughter) And such was his frustration that the next day I was called by the school manager.
And there I went. I was furious,
without sleep, willing to do everything.
Ready to denounce the school, the principal and the teacher, for discrimination and who knows how many more things.
But I had to put back again each of my prejudices in my pocket, since both the director and the teacher made themselves available to me and my son.
We are not used to receive that kind of treatment.
Life has hit us so much, that we are always distrustful and we are defensive, because that is our defense mechanism to survive the social stigma.
Sex workers would like to live in a society that doesn't judge them, that doesn't question them, doesn't punish or condemn them.
An egalitarian society where we can have access to rights such as a medical insurance and a retirement.
Where we don't have to go to jail for the mere fact of going to work, a society that doesn't throw at us the sexual prejudices others have, a society where they stop seeing us as victims and start to take us as women with rights. (Applause) A society that stops comparing human trafficking to sex work, thus making invisible a whole group and condemning us to have to work in greater secrecy; a society that stops questioning us and starts accepting our work just like the rest.
We are women, we are mothers, we are sex workers.
We want to be free, to be able to decide about our own bodies.
We want to be free to be able to be what we really want to be, but with access to rights.
And we are walking the path to that egalitarian society.
A few months ago, my son Santino told me after school that one of his friends wanted to mock him in front of the rest and said: "Santino's mom is a whore."
On tenterhooks, I asked: "What did you answer?" But he calmly said:
"I told him that my mom is not a whore.
I told him that my mom is the General Secretary of all the whores in the country."
(Applause) Thank you.
I’m a rapper,
I want to share with you how I became one.
I first listened rap when I was young. I was 8 or so. A lot of people think that rap is a genre made for criminals,
but it is not so, it’s for everyone.
I came across with rap on the streets.
I lived in a dangerous neighborhood, regular conversations revolved around dead people or shootings. I grew up like that.
My dad left when I was a kid.
I don’t really know too much about him, rumor has it he is in jail,
others say he is dead.
I think I’ll never know, but given his lifestyle, those theories might be true.
My mom left too, and went to live somewhere safe.
I lived with my grandparents, whom I love as parents.
They raised me up, but could not keep up with me, due to their advanced age.
I hanged out a lot in the street, then, as a kid, I worked on my uncles dairy shop,
where I had to carry loads under the sun and my hands were slowly crushed.
But the worst is realizing that payment is not worth it. You could die and no coverage even existed. All for nothing.
I lived through all that, it was on the streets where I first heard rap.
Rap and its violent lyrics, that deeply moved me, yet I enjoyed the rhythm, so I tuned my life to these lyrics.
As I entered high school I started rapping, and wrote down my own rebellious lyrics, though maybe not too rational.
As a kid I dreamed of becoming film director, I watched movies and thought “How nice”, and loved watching behind the scenes. I was curious.
That's how it was, but in high school I started to develop something called free style.
You might have heard of it, it’s about improvising coherent rhymes, right at the moment.
We had free style battles
with other school rappers,
and we battled. You could see all high school students over 200, enjoying the battle on the street.
Popularity came up,
I had fake friends all around, just because they liked what I did.
One day my mom came back, I didn’t took it so well, I didn’t know her.
She came back to live where I lived.
She wanted me back in her life, but I was far from easy,
constantly dfrifting away from her.
Years later she got pregnant. Still, I didn't care the least about that baby.
But when he was born and I first saw him, that baby changed my life.
So fragile in such a violent world. I didn’t want that for him.
I was afraid for the first time ever. I've never cared about anything before, but now I would not let
anything happen to that boy.
I tried to be better. I rebuilt my life and myself, and said: “I want to be a role model for him, so that he can be a role model for others”.
I investigated where I could study filmmaking.
I still wanted to direct.
I found a filming school
where they granted half scholarships if you won a free themed film-minute contest.
I did it and won the scholarship,
my schooldays started.
That school was my passion, I eagerly learned what I’ve dreamed of,
I studied, was becoming known as a rappper and was still a model for my brother.
But one day things changed.
My mother's father, my father, had a heart attack that left him paralyzed.
I visited him at the hospital, and economic crisis fell hard upon us. I started working night shifts.
I went to school, then with grandpa, then went to sleep, homework, and it became so hard, I had to leave school.
So no school for a while, which was really tough.
My life was work, home, work,
eat, work, sleep… Tough indeed.
I almost forgot my dreams.
Time went by, and one day, I found this guy who told me: “Hey, how is rap going?”,
I had not even thought about rap.
He told me “there is something going on in Neza, a contest, and if you win, they record your album, film videoclips, performances and distribution” I thought that was really good, but didn’t know if I could win. I went to the contest. I decided I had to face it, that was my chance, my moment. I went to the contest. There were 80 contestants, a big challenge. But I won, luckily. I made my first album, videos, they distributed it and my life changed for the better. It is a gift being able to do what you love. I’ve been around the country, on radio and TV, also earned some money, and I love it.
I am indeed a role model for my brother. My parents and grandparents are ok. Grandpa is much better and I love my grannie. About my mom, I’m grateful to see her everyday. She is a gift. Mom, I love you. Many people believe our lives are already shaped, that we are whatever they say we are. I don’t believe that, I don’t believe in fate. I’ve been asked what the meaning of life is. I don’t believe there is such a thing. You decide what’s the meaning of your life. This is a bit of my rap, and goes like this: ♪Yeah, ah♪ ♪This is me♪ ♪Trying to see, How I ought to be♪ ♪Everyday I wake up♪
♪To hold onto what I got♪ ♪1,2,3 off we go♪
♪Daydreaming on the go♪ ♪For the best I must race♪ ♪Towards what I couldn’t face♪ ♪And with rap I jumpstart♪ ♪A sweet blink for my heart♪ ♪This dream is quite alright.♪ ♪Don’t want to drift away I just want to convey♪ ♪As I walk along my pathway♪ ♪Down a place without an exit♪ ♪Everytime I looked around♪ ♪Only wrong would stand out♪ ♪Groovy hummings flowing like an endless yearning♪ ♪Buffing me in a sleepless blaring♪
♪About a fight I must wage♪ ♪Yet never try to fake♪ ♪What I’m not♪
♪What I ought♪ ♪And dare to follow up.♪ ♪Into an intruder crossroads♪ ♪Who set a course and a discourse♪ ♪Supposedly overused and abused.♪ ♪Feed my beat, feed my beat and my sequence♪ ♪And my chapel resounds in a booming♪ ♪By the left, by the left♪ ♪Thrill blurt out all
my spark for the flow♪ ♪A loud groove fluttered within my eardrum♪ ♪Come hear my beat, come hear my beat♪ ♪Like a legend♪
♪Of a blue dreamer♪ ♪Come hear my beat♪ ♪First, I lived constantly dreaming♪ ♪The ghetto streamed a song that is now part of my breathing♪
♪More than music is a way of expression♪ ♪Is such a big conection♪ ♪With my passion and mission♪ ♪Is my fuel♪ ♪Music is my indulge♪ ♪Blah blah blah♪ ♪I no longer talk♪ ♪Drumsticks left on the glove box♪ ♪And the baffles made me go loud♪ ♪With the sound of my track♪
♪All my systems where operating at full♪ ♪Looneys of all sorts dragging me into their crew♪ ♪Come in and listen for this is a rap club♪ ♪Right up here bring your beat♪ ♪Stand up straight upon your feet♪ ♪After trying bet you’re going to brush the sky♪ ♪Rhapsodies and lyrics are just part of my groove♪ ♪Got the grit to pull it off forget of glamour♪ ♪I’m the light of my crew♪ ♪I fuel up all the punch♪ ♪I write down a rhapsody, phonetic matching melody♪ ♪No more rustle♪ ♪Not a break, stay awake♪ ♪Help me God to write down♪ ♪And shed my ink until dawn♪
♪Making up a ballad♪ ♪with a quite eloquent wording♪ ♪Yet a never fading pacing♪ ♪I'm a dreamer upholding his stand view♪ ♪My ink as a voice for my feelings♪ ♪Miguel Trejo, that’s me♪ ♪And this is my daydream.♪ Thanks
