So we were walking through the rain-forest in a humid, suffocating heat, and ticks the size of marbles were climbing up my socks.
We were looking for the remains of an elephant that had been illegally hunted for its ivory and some hours after, we were trekking through one of the last remaining tropical rain-forests in Kenya.
En route, I saw the cash crops encroaching on the ecosystem, I saw soldiers taking women away who were smuggling firewood to make charcoal and sell it in the city of Mombasa, and I saw illegal loggers being arrested next to fallen trees, hundreds of years old.
One of the loggers looked at my worried face and said, "Hakuna Matata".
Yes, like The Lion King, a phrase in Swahili that means something like, don't worry, it'll be fine.
But I was worried for the future of this rain-forest and all the others and for the people that depend on them
Three years ago, I found a way to travel around for long periods of time without needing many resources: being a volunteer - working in exchange for lodging and food and traveling light, with my backpack.
The year before last, I returned from a caravan in East Africa where I stayed for almost 5 months, traveling around and living in dozens of little villages, with the widest variety of realities, contexts and stories you could imagine and that's how I lived for almost 2 months with a family in a tiny village on the Kenyan coast where everything we ate came from the garden and all of the waste went back into the crops because it was organic and the houses were made of mud and straw, local materials. I also experienced the complete contrast in a village in the foothills of the Mount Kenya volcano where its entire forest
had been reduced to a little plot of natural resources and still it is given over to giant tea plantations. And this is because a little over half a century ago missionaries arrived with this plant and convinced the locals that God was not in their forest but was in heaven.
And in fact, when I was in that area, people were selling all their tea to only one processing plant, who paid them a pittance which they used to buy processed, imported, low-quality food which was not only damaging their health but was also polluting and contaminating their land.
It was like there you could also hear that "Hakuna Matata", coming from the processing plants and the food stores.
During these stays, I realised something obvious.
There are two types of communities.
There are those which are isolated that have no road, phones or television.
Those which live like they have done for generations those which are self-sufficient, independent and are governed by their own cultural rules and laws.
On the other hand, there are those which are becoming globalized.
Those which already have roads, they receive imported goods they have electricity.
So, they have to pay fees, pay taxes, buy their food.
They are forced to generate money and so in some way, are dependent.
I tried to find a middle ground between these two extremes but in every case it seemed that development was completely at odds with sustainability.
So when I went back to Mexico, where I had left thinking that I would return with all of the answers to humanity's problems - yes, at the age of 19 - I realised that I'd gone to the other side of the world just to understand the context and history of my own country better.
And here is an area that I'm passionate about and that particularly worries me.
The Lacandon jungle. It forms only 0.4% of our territory and it's in a corner of Chiapas.
However, it produces a third of our country's water.
And it's home to 1 out of 5 species in Mexico.
This is a photo of the Lacandon jungle in 1984.
The red areas were originally jungle and at that time were pastureland or cultivation areas.
This is what they're like today.
Over 3/4 of this jungle has been lost since the 80's.
The current rate of deforestation is 2000 hectares a year.
So, on average, around 5 football pitches a day.
There are 3 main reasons for this.
One, the increase in livestock and cash crops such as oil palm, which also leaves hardly anything for local shops.
Two, over-exploitation of natural resources.
And three, unplanned colonization and irregular settlements.
At the beginning of this year after trying for months, I was finally granted access to a community of Tzeltal people who live in the jungle.
When I arrived, I found a group of houses made from wood, all with their vegetable patches in the back patio, laid out around an impeccable green field where children were running around and playing, wearing their traditional, colorful clothing.
So after being there a few days I walked through the jungle to communal land called Esperanza.
Here, things were different.
Despite being only 4km away, I arrived at a dirt track.
The people were wearing jeans and T-shirts with Justin Bieber's face on them. The shop had soft drinks, crisps, sliced bread.
There was rubbish in roads, they were dirty, the streams had a layer of foam. In the afternoon, people would meet up near the shop to watch soap operas and commercials which also seemed to tell them that message of "Hakuna Matata", keep following this path and everything will be fine.
There was that same contrast that had been so evident in Africa, it's also right here, in Mexico.
I see two patterns that all the villages have in common in unsustainable development.
Firstly, a misconception of what progress actually is.
And secondly, a phenomenon which some have called "The Broken Mirror".
Development programs from the government or other organizations are often based on the Western concept of well-being.
They are imposing on these communities an unsustainable way of life in the long term, designed during the industrial revolution when the world thought that we had infinite resources and that to progress you have to consume.
Both in parts of Africa and parts of Mexico, political discourse shouts things like, "We'll build them roads so they'll be more connected."
"We'll give them concrete and sheeting so they can build proper houses."
"We'll subsidize the livestock so they have more money."
It's a speech which rejects collective traditional wisdom and ignores the value of the ecosystem.
On many occasions, the aim of a speech is to get immediate supporters without worrying about the long-term consequences of its proposals.
I'd like to explain the second pattern with a metaphor from Kenyan Nobel Prize winner Wangari Maathai.
This broken mirror she talks about refers to the distorted image that many cultures have of themselves when seeing their reflection in the mirror of Western society.
The media, or sometimes people themselves glorify our lifestyle and reflect them as poor, primitive, ignorant.
And they take that image as their reality becoming dependent and developing an inferiority complex which makes them extremely vulnerable.
Their lives now follow a path which makes them long to have cars, to eat hamburgers, to live in big, concrete buildings.
What's more,
this is a significant decision.
If a lot of us live like that they should also be free to choose.
The problem is that in the vast majority of cases, they don't have the necessary information to evaluate these issues.
And on many occasions they don't even have the chance to decide which path they will follow as a community.
Imagine a village which has just been incorporated into distribution networks and suddenly trucks arrive, full of soft drinks all the walls covered with adverts and the TV repeating things like, "This soft drink is happiness" "This pick-up truck is status, respect".
Who really believes that there is true freedom in that?
Meanwhile, the trend in many cities and societies which have already gone through that, is focused on local products, organic food, and alternative methods of transport, and they have regained that sense of community.
Rural development programs are going in completely the opposite direction. They are going towards that point that we so desperately want to escape.
And who is warning them?
The contrast between isolated and integrated communities poses a problem.
To maintain sustainability, isolated communities need to stay as they are, as if they were museum pieces. Or for them to develop, is it inevitable that they'll stop being sustainable?
I don't think so.
Development brings advantages those related to technology, medicine, free communication, but it should be applied with public policies and projects which do not impose a consumerist model which not only threatens their identity but, as demonstrated time and time again, is ineffective.
So as urban people, what should we do?
Well, firstly identify this voice which tells us, "Hakuna Matata" over and over again and accept that we have a problem as a society - a big problem.
Then, stop thinking as if we were living in a fishbowl and take responsibility. Accept that the environmental problems in Africa or the Lacandon jungle are our problems too.
And finally, act. No, I'm not going to invite you to the jungle to tie ourselves to trees, though it's not such a bad idea.
But no, I'm talking about a different type of action.
I'm talking about breaking this idea that development is incompatible with sustainability and making decisions in accordance with it.
It doesn't matter if we are or will be politicians, business people, economists, designers, architects, engineers...
There are - or will be - moments when we have to decide between more profit or fair trade, political popularity or responsibility, growth or sustainability.
And I'm telling you that we should pick both, we should take the best of both worlds: higher profits thanks to fair trade, popularity derived from true social responsibility and development which is sustainable in the long term.
Thank you.
(Applause)
[This talk contains strong language] I need allies, I need a lot of them because we are going to destabilize the system. Enough!
(Applause) It is easier than you think.
We don't need to wear hoods, ski masks, Molotov bombs, or throw stones to anyone.
It is much more simple, but it requires a lot of willpower.
First, I want to ask you for a show of hands.
Almost all the speakers today talked about gender.
Could anyone calmly come here upfront and explain what gender is?
Raise your hand if you are absolutely sure about it.
One person and she just went to a talk this morning.
(Laughter) OK, gender, usually one thinks What type of gender? As in grammar? Nouns, pronouns?
By the way, I think the best answer came from a kid interviewed by Dialogos [TV show], "Gender is girls in pink and boys in blue."
(Laughter) That's gender. It is a way to see gender.
In other words, it is the manual you receive when your baby is born at the hospital, and it says, "This is the way your boy or girl has to behave according to their genitals."
That's where things go wrong. Why?
Because then the girl cannot yell, but she can cry. And the boy cannot cry, but he can yell.
These are the feelings taught to men that they have to deal with for the rest of their lives.
Things get complicated because things start. This chauvinism, let's face it, hurts everyone.
Imagine your male friends were told to grunt in any situation.
"How was work?" (Grunt)
"What did your boss say?" (Grunt)
There is no middle ground.
We women have an incredible
array of ways to cry.
Right? We cannot express ourselves.
This is what messes up the world. It makes men emotionally stunted.
Domestically unfit, they don't even know how to prepare steak.
They know how when they are alone in the bathroom.
(Laughter) Tragic!
It also deprives them of the joys of fatherhood that some are now discovering and it is beautiful to see.
It forces them, as if by divine order, to always have all the answers.
That's why they are frightened by crying because they don't know what to do.
The manual said he knew everything.
This is a disaster. Another one is that he always has to be ready for sex, wanting and able.
(Laughter) And if they don't, (Laughter) it is as if they are no longer men.
If they don't bring money home, they cannot even perform because the manual says that they have to bring money home so then all the women will be theirs.
Where else do we find this?
In textbooks.
