One of the first patients I had to see as a pediatrician was Sol, a beautiful month-old baby who was admitted with signs of a severe respiratory infection.
So far, I had never seen a patient worsen so fast.
In just two days she was connected to a respirator and on the third day she died.
Sol had whooping cough.
After discussing the case in the room and after a quite distressing catharsis, I remember my chief resident said to me, "OK, take a deep breath, wash your face, and now comes the hardest part, we have to go talk to her parents."
At that time, a thousand questions came to mind.
From: "How a month-old baby could be so unfortunate? To: "Could we have done something about it?"
Before vaccines existed, many infectious diseases killed millions of people per year.
During the 1918 flu pandemic 50 million people died.
That's greater than Argentina's current population.
Perhaps, the older ones among you remember the polio epidemic that occurred in Argentina in 1956.
At that time, there was no vaccine available against polio.
People didn't know what to do.
They were going crazy.
They would go painting trees with lime.
They would put camphor bags inside their children's clothes as if that could do something.
During the polio epidemic thousands of people died.
And thousands of people were left with very important neurological damages.
I know this because I read about it, because thanks to vaccines my generation was lucky to not live through an epidemic as terrible as this.
Vaccines are one of the great successes of the twentieth century's public health.
After drinking water, they are the interventions that have most reduced mortality, even more than antibiotics.
Vaccines eradicated from the planet terrible diseases such as smallpox and succeeded in reducing mortality significantaly due to other diseases such as measles, whooping cough, polio and many more.
All these diseases are considered Vaccine-preventable diseases.
What does this mean?
That they are potentially preventable, but in order to be so, something must be done.
You need to get vaccinated.
I imagine that most, if not all of us here today, received a vaccine at some point in our life.
Now, I'm not so sure that many of us know which vaccines or reinforcements we should receive after adolescence.
Have you ever wondered who we are protecting when we vaccinate?
What do I mean by that?
Is there any other effect beyond protecting ourselves?
Let me show you something.
Imagine for a moment we are in a city that has never had a case of a particular disease, such as measles.
This would mean that in this city no one
has ever had contact with the disease. No one has either the natural defenses nor been vaccinated against measles.
If one day, a person ill with measles appears in this city the disease won't find much resistance and will begin spreading from person to person, and in no time it will disseminate throughout the community.
After a certain time a big part of the population will be ill.
This happened when there were no vaccines.
Now, imagine the complete opposite case.
We are in a city where more than 90 percent of the population has defenses against measles.
It means that they've had the disease, and generated natural defenses.
They either survived,
or were immunized against measles.
And one day, a person sick with measles appears in this city.
The disease will find much more resistance and won't be transmitted that much from person to person.
The spread will probably remain contained and a measles outbreak won't happen.
I would like you to pay attention to something.
People who are vaccinated not only are protecting themselves, but by blocking the dissemination of the disease within the community they are indirectly protecting the people from this community who are not vaccinated.
They create a kind of protective shield which prevents from getting in contact with the disease, so that these people are protected.
This indirect effect of protection which unvaccinated people have within a community, simply by being surrounded by vaccinated people, is called herd immunity.
Many people in the community depend almost exclusively on this herd immunity to be protected against disease.
These people displayed in infographics are not just hypothetical.
These people are our nephews, our children, who may be too young to receive their first shots.
They are our parents, our brothers, our acquaintances, who may have a disease, or receive medication which lowers their defenses.
There are also people allergic to a particular vaccine.
They could even be among us, any of us who got vaccinated, but the vaccine didn't produce the expected effect.
Because not all vaccines are always 100 percent effective.
All these people depend almost exclusively on herd immunity to be protected against diseases.
To achieve this effect of herd immunity, it is necessary that a large percentage of the population is vaccinated.
This percentage is called threshold.
This threshold depends on many variables.
It depends on the germ's characteristics, and those of the immune response that the vaccine generates.
But they all have something in common. If the percentage of the population in a vaccinated community is below this threshold number, the disease will begin to spread more freely and may generate an outbreak of this disease within the community.
Even diseases which at some point were controlled may reappear.
This is not just a theory.
This happened and is still happening.
In 1998, a British researcher published an article in one of the most important medical journals, saying that the MMR vaccine, which is given for measles, mumps and rubella, was associated with autism.
This generated an immediate impact.
People began to stop getting vaccinated, and stop vaccinating their children.
And what happened?
The number of people vaccinated, in many communities around the world, fell below this threshold.
And there were outbreaks of measles in many cities in the world.
In the U.S., in Europe.
Many people got sick. People died of measles.
What happened?
This article also generated a huge stir within the medical community.
Dozens of researchers began to assess if this was actually true.
Not only could no one find a causal association between MMR and autism at the population level, but it was found that this article had incorrect claims.
Even more, it was fraudulent.
It was fraudulent.
In fact, the journal publicly retracted from this article in 2010.
One of the main concerns and excuses for not getting vaccinated are the adverse effects.
Vaccines, like other drugs, can have potential adverse effects.
Most are mild and temporary.
But the benefits are always greater than possible complications.
When we are ill, we want to heal fast.
Many of us who are here take antibiotics when we have an infection, We take anti-hypertensives
when we have high blood pressure;
we take cardiac medications.
Why? Because we are sick
and we want to heal fast.
And we don't question it much.
Why is it so difficult to think of preventing diseases, by taking care of ourselves when we are healthy?
We take care of ourselves a lot when affected by an illness, or in situations of imminent danger.
I imagine most of us here, remember the influenza A pandemic which broke out in 2009 in Argentina and worldwide.
When the first cases began to come to light, we, here in Argentina, were entering the winter season.
We knew absolutely nothing.
Everything was a mess.
People wore masks on the street, ran into pharmacies to buy alcohol gel.
People would queue in the pharmacies to receive a vaccine, without even knowing if it's the right vaccine that would protect them against this new virus.
We knew absolutely nothing.
At that time, in addition to doing my research scholarship at the Infant Foundation, I worked as a home pediatrician for a prepaid medicine company.
I remember that I started my shift at 8 am and already I had a list of 50 scheduled visits.
It was chaos, people didn't know what to do.
I remember the types of patients that I was examining.
The patients were a little older than what we were used to see in winter, with longer fevers.
And I remember I told my mentor at the research scholarship, and he, for his part, had heard the same from a colleague, about the large number of pregnant women and young adults being hospitalized in intensive care, with clinical profiles difficult to handle.
At that time, we set out to understand what was happening.
Monday, early morning, we took the car and we went to a hospital in Buenos Aires province, that was supposed to be a reference hospital for cases of the new influenza virus.
We arrived at the hospital, it was crowded.
All health staff was dressed in NASA-like bio-safety suits.
We – with face masks in our pockets.
Just like a hypochondriac, I didn't breathe for two hours.
But we could see what was happening.
Immediately, we started reaching out to pediatricians from six hospitals in the city and the Buenos Aires province.
Our main goal was to find out, how this new virus behaves in contact with our children, in the shortest time possible.
A marathon work. In less than three months we could see what effect this new H1N1 virus had on the 251 children hospitalized by this virus.
We could see which children got more seriously ill: children under four, especially those less than one year old; patients with neurological diseases, And young children with chronic pulmonary diseases.
Identifying these risk groups was important to include them as priority groups in the recommendations for getting the influenza vaccine, not only here in Argentina, but also in other countries which the pandemic not yet reached.
A year later, when a vaccine was available against the pandemic H1N1 virus, we wanted to see what had happened.
After a huge vaccination campaign aimed at protecting risk groups, these hospitals, with 93 percent of the risk groups vaccinated, had not a single patient with the pandemic H1N1 virus.
(Applause) In 2009: 251.
In 2010: Zero.
Vaccination is an act of individual responsibility, but it has a huge collective impact.
If I get vaccinated, not only am I protecting myself, but I am also protecting others.
Sol had whooping cough.
Sol was very young, and she hadn't yet received her first vaccine against whooping cough.
I still wonder what would have happened if everyone around Sol had been vaccinated.
(Applause)
What if this that I take for granted today is gone by tomorrow?
What if my career is no longer necessary by the time I get my degree?
What if tomorrow I like girls?
What if I have to change to fit in a group or get a job?
What if I don't fit in entirely?

Scary. Very scary.
Sometimes fear turns into grief.
Other times it needs to be talked over with friends, or even keep it undercover.
And sometimes it paralyses me.
Fear comes from the reality I live in along with other teenagers.
I think we are a generation that questions everything.
Thus, we want to act.
We are the generation that embraces diversity, naturalizes homosexuality and that even protests against animal abuse through their diet.
But while it seems that we are willing to face up to everything, it doesn't mean we are not afraid.
There are fears blown up by the instability brought about by global changes in the economy, politics, societies and even technology.
I don't know what I will do tomorrow.
We don't even know what we will do in a few hours.
Even less what will happen tomorrow.
Maybe it's just another day.
Or maybe it's one of those days when six new cellphone models come up, the world's stock markets plummet, there are three new social networks with the same puppy filters, or terrorist attacks happen in cities like Paris or Barcelona leaving dozens of deaths.
Today I'm standing here, with 19 years and all my life ahead and the only thing I'm sure of is that my name is Chiara.
Although maybe tomorrow I'll call myself Pedro.
I buy clothes and when I leave the store there's another fashion trend already.
Today I learn something and tomorrow it's not useful anymore.
And that's when it hits me.
When I realize how far I am from those who preceded me.
Because changes always happened but there is no applicable system today.
There is no advice to follow, no recipe to put in practice, or formula to helps us achieve our goals.
Or to at least help us face all that we have to face.
I don't know how I'm going to live tomorrow.
And much less how I'm going to live from here to 10 years.
I don't know if artificial intelligence is going to replace my career.
I don't know if we are going to buy houses or if we are going to live in smart tenements, or inside our electric cars.
I don't even know if I'm going to have a family.
It feels illogical to seek permanence in such a changing world.
And I don't know for certain what to do.
I think the only thing that will help me move forward is to adapt to change.
Adapt to it, embrace it and have the guts to project myself.
That's why when I think of my life project I no longer think of fixed landmarks to fulfill over time, but in three principles that I believe are going to remain stable even when everything else changes.
First, be the best version
of myself, always.
Second, to own my present and immediate future.
For example, this week I want to get up early; as soon as the alarm goes off, to seize the day.
Or, this month I want to pass my exams.
And third, I will set flexible goals for the long term.
This is the hardest principle for me, because I have a slight tendency towards rigidity, or punctuality.
Even when I am aware that things don't happen as I expect.
That's why, for the time being, the best example of this principle it is to devote myself to study only what really motivates me.
Right now, I want to graduate within the right timeframe.
And I think it's possible, because I'm in love with the career I chose.
But if in the middle of the process I suffer a disenchantment I want to have the courage to change the course of events.
Because being afraid is fine, it happens to all of us, and to the teenagers of the next 10 years, will also happen.
My children, if I decide have them, will also feel afraid.
I think those hypothetical children I will have to give them everything I need today.
And that's no magic formulas or recipes that grew rotten.
They will need love.
And that this affection is a certainty for the construction of a future that is so uncertain.
A future they will build through trial and error and changes in their teenage years they will also have to embrace, just like I do today.
Because that's what I need today.
And I'm almost sure that tomorrow as well.
Or maybe not.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Imagine you are at a scientific lab.
You just gave 15 minutes of your time in exchange for lunch.
You are at a table facing a researcher who explains that you are part of an experiment about human attraction.
And he asks you to choose between these two women on the pictures, the most attractive one according to your own criteria.
You choose by pointing.
Suppose in this case, you chose the one on the left.
And the researcher gives you the picture, so you can see it one more time, and asks you to explain why you chose that woman.
But did you notice what happened?
Did you?
The thing is that the one in front of you is in fact a magician, and when he gives you the card he actually does a magic trick, a subtle sleight of hand, through which now you are holding not the chosen picture, but the other one instead.
And what happens then?
More than half the people not only don't realize that the picture was switched, but also start to justify why they chose the woman they didn't.
(Laughter) This experiment was performed in Sweden some years ago with women pictures.
But last year my friend Julieta Figini and I repeated it here in Buenos Aires, and we got exactly the same results.
And what's more, later we did it again with men pictures.
And then women were -- don't be surprised -- less deceived.
Just a quarter, a 25 percent of the women didn't notice the switch, while the other 75 percent demanded that we brought back the man they chose.
(Applause) I'm a Scientist, a Physicist, a PhD in Physics, but this is my Degree.
I'm a magician long before.
I did my first course with Charlie Brown when I was 10 years old, a genius in magic in our country.
And since then it was one of my greatest passions, I never quit it.
But what this experiment clearly showed me and that I've never seen it this way, was the huge contribution professional magicians can make, and specially mentalists to the recent development of neuroscience.
So I felt that I was, with my mixed education, a blend of magician and scientist, in the right place at the right time.
And I decided to promote with my friend the neuroscientist Mariano Sigman a study group of magicians and scientists, that started to ask themselves how to take advantage of magic to better understand the mind and the brain.
The first question we ask ourselves was related to how we decide.
Truth is that our decisions are the most important thing we have. They can change our lives, the lives of our close people, and in many cases the lives of thousand or million people.
And of course, it is essential for neuroscience to understand the underlying mechanisms of decision-making.
The experiment I just told you helps us to better understand how we justify our decisions.
And the first experiment we developed and I am about to tell you, helps us to get better how we make them.
We know mentalists have techniques to subconsciously influence the decisions of their audience.
A simple one is called "visual force," where the magician asks somebody to choose one card among the cards he is showing.
The cards go so swift that they can hardly be seen, but one card goes a little bit slower.
And people have a big inclination to pick that card that could be seen longer, without noticing the situation, the suggestion, the trick.
And what we did was to play this game many times with a lot of people in the lab.
We asked them to pick one among the many cards that could be seen, and we also asked them later if they felt free or manipulated, forced to decide.
Like when a magician asks you
to pick a card and then tells you:
"Did you feel free? Yes? Sure?
Absolutely free?
Don't you want to change the card?"
Well, that, but in the lab.
And see that there are four possible reactions from the people when playing this game.
In the first place, the person may pick the card the magician wants and feels free.
The magic worked.
Secondly, the person may pick the card the magician wants but feels manipulated, forced, and realizes it was almost the only card that could be seen.
Thirdly, the person may pick any single card and feels free. Perfect.
Finally, the person may pick any single card but when asked they say they feel manipulated.
Failing a more fitting expression we call it the "paranoid's case."
(Laughter) And the coolest thing came when we did something weird and crazy. When people were looking and choosing, we measured with a very sophisticated and precise instrument how their pupil diameters were changing during the 2 seconds while the cards pass.
And we put it into a graph.
Then we divided all the graphs into these four big groups according to the behavior of each person.
And finally, we calculated, in each group of graphs, the average curve, for example: Here in red you see the average behavior of the person's pupil diameters that were paranoid in our experiment.
And the same with the other graphs.
The other three colors represent the average behavior of the person's pupil diameters that reacted according to one of the other three possible reactions.
And what is really interesting here, in this graph, is that the four curves are different.
It doesn't matter how they are exactly.
The amazing thing actually is that when we do the calculations, the four curves are notably different.
That means that each possible reaction in our game corresponds to a very different pupil movement.
This means that this knowledge allows scientists to play mentalists, because they can guess people's thoughts by measuring their pupils with a high-precision instrument.
This also means that now we have a tool to observe, like a through a window, at parts of the brain that make decisions.
We are observing through the eyes, like a window to the inner parts of our brain, the neuronal circuits that tell us we have made a decision at a given time.
We know there are brain circuits, neuronal circuits, which control and modulate pupil dilation.
What we are observing is the role these circuits play in decision-making.
And these experiments show us that great part of the knowledge neuroscience studies today, is based on the concepts present thousand years ago, at least in a subtle manner in the magical literature.
In particular, in mentalism books, the branch of magic that precisely studies the human mind to hack it.
And to trick it.
Because this is what magic is, the art of illusion.
Not to swindle or confuse, but to awe, and to reflect on the universe around us.
Modern magic, which was born in the secular and post-revolutionary 19th-century France, always cooperated with science.
At that time, it was called "recreational physics."
Illusionism, modern magic, is to witchcraft, mediums, cheaters, swindlers and fraudsters, what astronomy is to astrology and chemistry is to alchemy.
They are trades that inherit the knowledge and techniques of these ancient traditions to differentiate themselves and shed light on their limitations.
Today the worst enemy of an astrologist is an astronomer.
And the worst enemies of those who claim to have paranormal abilities are we, professional magicians, that serially unmask their tricks and deceits.
René Lavand, the great founding father of Argentine magic, in fact the great genius of world magic, said: "Amazement is before anything else."
Not amusement, but amazement.
Amazement, the core feeling
of every science: awe, curiosity and surprise.
René also spoke about the beauty of amazement.
Magic as praise of mystery, as an art calling us to constantly wonder about our own prejudices on the limits of the possible, according to our knowledge of the world.
So, what's the story in this over hundred-year-old romance between magic and science?
There's neuromagic: a body of knowledge that recently flourished from the cooperation between professional magicians and cognitive scientists in different parts of the world, aiming to better understand the mind and the brain.
Today, I told you about two experiments conceived by magicians and scientists that are helping us to better understand how we make and justify our decisions.
But in many other areas, like visual perception, memory or attention, there are many discoveries that make us even pose some philosophical concepts again, like free will or conscience.
To me, in particular, what fascinates me of this new science, the neuromagic, is that leads us slowly but systematically to the contemplation of our own nature.
(Applause) We are more and more magicians and scientists working together, and the more the better.
There's a lot to discover, to understand, a lot of benefits to gather within reach.
So I invite you to be aware, take part, become interested, and I also invite you the next time you see a magic trick to amuse yourself and have fun but most of all, to be amazed.
Since this is when something magical, beautiful and contagious can happen.
And who knows, it may help you to better understand your own brains.
Until here, the TEDx talk I prepared.
(Applause) I finished it 15 days ago, two weeks ago.
Today is exactly a week my father, my old man, passed away.
And yesterday, talking with a friend about this talk, he sent me a text from a book of my father - a text I have completely forgotten - which seems a prediction, like the mentalism at the end of my talk.
I couldn't believe it.
So I want to finish with a little reminder and tribute to my old man.
And besides, I want you to see this since I believe it shows there is no greater magic than love,
because this is magic, let me read it.
It is perfectly wrapping up my talk,
isn't it? It says: "Science begins with amazement, because things seem to be something, but are not just how they seem to be.
Science starts with the quest of amazingness."
Incredible, right?
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
How can we tell stories for 21st century audiences?
What you're seeing in the screen is the Chauvet Cave.
It's a magic place, located in the heart of a cliff in the south of France.
It was discovered by chance in December of the year 1994,
hiding one of the most incredible treasures of our culture.
Inside it, imagine the size of four football fields, they found the most ancient cave paintings made by man.
Some of them are more than 35,000 years old.
Most of the paintings were preserved in perfect condition thanks to a giant rock that fell through the cliff, sealing for thousands of years the only way in.
That rock transformed the cave into a perfect time capsule.
During these years few people had access to the inside of Cave Chauvet.
But those few who did, returned surprised by the amount and by the variety of the paintings.
Although to me, each time I see images like this one, what most shocks me is the skill with which they were made.
If you look closely, you'll find lines traced in layers that adapt to the outlines of the stone walls to create an incredible illusion of depth.
Some animals, for example, were painted with eight legs, simulating movement, simulating running in herds between the stone walls.
Join me for an instant in a journey through time.
Imagine how it was to enter this cave 30,000 years ago.
When the sun set, the tribes would gather to listen the tales of the shaman.
A fire would be lit up in the center of the cavern and among the cries and the echos... echos... echos... in the throat of the shaman, among the lights and the shadows reflected by the fire.
But above all, among the layer-painted walls - the technology of the times - the shaman would manage to orchestrate a tale that would become true.
A tale that would materialize between his hands.
The tribes, the audience, would not only observe the painted walls but also would literally feel these animals come to life walking and breathing among them.
Each time I think of that, you may agree, I think that we're in front of the first 3D cinema with surround sound in history.
Today, I want to tell you about something in our country from half a century ago.
At dawn, on June 10, 1956, the police of Buenos Aires province secretly executed 12 men in a rubbish dump of José León Suárez.
Five of those men died that night.
The other seven managed to escape alive.
Six months later, while drinking a glass of cold beer in a bar of the city of La Plata, that story ended up in the hands of a journalist.
That journalist heard that night a disquieting phrase.
"One of the executed men lives."
For the whole next year, that journalist couldn't think of anything else.
He became obsessed with that story.
He left his house, moved from La Plata to a small island in Tigre.
He left his job, and even changed his name to Francisco Freire.
All, absolutely all of it, to investigate the secret executions of the Revolución Libertadora.
The result of that investigation is known as Operación Masacre.
The book, written by the Argentine journalist Rodolfo Walsh, is a foundational work of Argentine research journalism, where Walsh mixed two of his passions: journalism and literature.
Therefore creating, inaugurating, a new genre: the non-fiction, known in Spanish as "novela testimonial".
Almost a decade ahead of the American classic "In Cold Blood" by Truman Capote.
I've been told that in the life of every journalist, there's at least one book that marks you.
In my case, without a doubt, it was Operación Masacre.
In 34 years I read it four times.
And every now and then, I read again some of its marked pages.
I always say that Operación Masacre is not read, but watched.
It's so well written that one can recreate its paragraphs in pictures.
It's a multimedia work written in the '50s.
I have no doubt that Walsh's fondness of photography, the love he felt for photography, had a lot to do with this.
Operación Masacre is a perfect mix between word and image.
I've been working for almost a decade in digital communication with interactive narratives.
I've always been interested in new ways to narrate stories.
With this idea, during 2010, I started a project that intended to link the work of Walsh with the new technologies.
The question then was: how could I turn interactive a work that was structured for a flat platform like paper?
How could I inject hypertextuality into a linear narrative?
I had the what: the story, the book, the script.
I was missing the how: to find a new way to tell it on the Internet.
That question spun around my head for quite some time, until one afternoon, Walsh's book in hand, I had an idea.
An idea that I hadn't seen applied to any digital project before, it was a challenging idea, but above all, it was very fun to make.
Instead of telling the story, as any traditional media would do - a TV show, a radio broadcast, a film documentary - the new technologies now allowed me to recreate the story.
And what was better: to recreate it in first person, returning Walsh to life and in real time, as if the story were happening in the present.
The great question of the project, in which I was joined by my colleague and friend Vanina Berghella, was the following: what would've happened if, while doing his research on the secret executions, Rodolfo Walsh had access to the digital tools we journalists now have?
Can you imagine Walsh asking for information of the victims on Twitter?
Can you imagine him looking for directions to the rubbish dump on Google Maps?
Or publishing on Facebook the progress of his investigation?
That's how Proyecto Walsh was born, as a remix, as a narrative experiment that spread Operación Masacre into a bunch of digital platforms.
In few words, all the facts documented by Walsh in the book were recreated on the Internet 56 years later, in first person and in real time, over the course of a year.
It was a sort of virtual time machine that intended to synchronize the past with the present.
Users could now follow the route of the investigation, interact with Rodolfo in Twitter, but what was best: they could take part in the story.
In times when any information can be found anywhere, it seemed interesting to me that the chronological element, that time, had an essential role.
It was a living project, that could be followed and had to be followed by the minute, in any screen and in any device.
In the precise moment that Walsh published a tweet, and you were there, a bridge in time was formed.
Past and present were synchronized.
The story of the secret executions, as well as the story the shaman told in the Chauvet Cave, materialized in front of the audience.
When Proyecto Walsh had been running for months, and the users had naturally appropriated the contents, - many, for example, left Walsh pieces of information of the victims on Twitter, or they helped him find directions to the rubbish dump in Google Maps and asked him on Facebook on his investigation's progress - I understood that I had not only created a project but a new narrative code.
A narrative code capable of telling Argentina's history in first person and in real time, but above all, that was able to frame future projects, capable of narrating historical facts of our country in a different way.
That's how two years later, with Guadalupe López, Ezequiel Apesteguía, and Romina Vázquez, and in the context of the 30th anniversary of the war in the islands, we launched the interactive documentary Malvinas30.
As a team, we had something very clear: we knew that this project had to be better than the last one.
And that's why we worked with more audiovisual archives, more multimedia elements, and we created better strategies for users to participate in social networks right from the start.
We intended Malvinas30 to become a sort of digital memorial of Malvinas War on the Internet.
Publishing interviews, interactive infographics, photographs, letters from the time, and a lot of archival resources from the year 1982.
And as it happened in Proyecto Walsh, Twitter let us to recreate the conflict in real time and in first person.
We opened two accounts: in the first account, - you're seeing it on screen, @malvinas30 - what we did was to tell the conflict minute by minute.
In that account we published the amount of Argentine and British fatalities day by day.
We published the amount of bombings from both sides, and every day, in the morning, we published the covers of Argentine, British and international newspapers, that dedicated space to the war conflict, the same day they were published in 1982.
In the second account, @soldadoM30, we created a fictional character, that allowed us to recount the war in first person.
In that account we collected real testimonies, quotes, interviews and photographs of ex-combatants that had given their testimony for our documentary.
It was a sort of collective memory of the Argentine soldiers in 140 characters.
One of the most powerful things that happened to us as a team was maybe what happened with the audiovisual materials we were publishing of that time.
This I commented before, about stop telling the story to let users experiment it in real time, we broadcast in our site the complete TV shows from that time, the same day and the same time they had been broadcast in 1982; the same day and the same time we broadcast them in 2012.
It was very shocking to spy on the reactions of users in social networks, each afternoon, when at eight o'clock they put on the 60 Minutos news show, or that Sunday when, during the whole day, we broadcast the placeholder show 24 Horas por Malvinas.
"If they want to come, then let them, we'll give them a battle."
"I can assure you they aren't suffering the cold, they'll return chubbier because they eat better than at home."
To those over 40, those phrases sound familiar.
To those under 35, as in my case, there was no need to editorialize.
The users were watching live how the media had lied during the war.
This way of telling was so strong for some users, that they had really unexpected reactions.
One user, for example, made a Twitter account - that one you see above, @dosdeabril2 - and started interacting with our fictional character, with our @soldadoM30,
also publishing in first person and in real time the war experience.
For a long time we didn't know who was behind it, or why they were doing it, but what most intrigued us was where did he get such precise information of what had happened to the ex-combatants of Malvinas War.
One afternoon, we received an email from Gabriel Beber, that beardy nice-looking guy you see on the screen.
In the email, Gabriel revealed to us that he was behind that account, but what was more shocking, he told us that he was an actual ex-combatant of the antiaircraft artillery group 601 of Mar Del Plata, and that the project had driven him to dust off his old letters from war, to scan them, and to take the trouble to search paragraph by paragraph, date by date, and picture by picture, in order to share them with our project, and with our @soldadom30, in social networks.
Gabriel's story had such an impact on us that we invited him to tell us his experience in a series of articles in our site.
In one of those articles, a really moving one, Gabriel said that Malvinas30 had helped him go through the catharsis that for 30 years he had been unable to do, of his experience as an Argentine combatant in Malvinas War.
Even though Proyecto Walsh and Malvinas30 hadn't been created with an educational purpose in mind, a lot of crazy things happened in that sense.
The first one was that teachers from all over the country wrote to us telling they were using the projects as teaching materials for their history and literature classes.
They told us, laughing and a bit nervous, that their students preferred to interact with Rodolfo Walsh on Twitter and not to read the book.
Or at least to overlap both devices in the classroom.
In one hand, the text, the book, the linear narration; in the other hand, the mobile phone, the interactive narration, the hypertext.
What may be paradoxical is that these students didn't realize that through different paths, they were reaching the same destination: to learn the history of the secret executions of José León Suárez.
The second thing that happened was that these narrative code I had used for the projects started being used later on by educational institutions, the media, museums, universities, television networks all over the world. Through digital, cultural, and educational projects, they recreated historical facts of their countries in first person and in real time.
There you can see some examples.
To make this sort of projects taught me many things.
I'd like to share with you two of the most important ones.
The first one is that academia teaches us to understand journalism and history as disciplines, as products, and as services.
I believe they can also be understood as experiences.
Experiences that have a nontransferable nature.
You no longer just observe, you're now part of it.
They relocate the user, the student, to the center of the narrative.
And that can be transforming.
That way of narrating connects with reason, but, above all, appeals to emotion.
And that can have a lot of power.
The second thing is, if we want to create new media, we must be also capable of creating new languages.
We must be willing to ask, but above all to ask ourselves, if there's a way to do things differently.
We have the privilege of living in times of an unprecedented crossing: between technologies, devices, and narratives.
It's a scenario that challenges us to rethink our disciplines, plagued with obsolete practices for the societies where we learn, where we live, and where we teach.
How can we tell stories for 21st century audiences?
Journalists, historians, educators, artists, I encourage you to enter the cave, to light the fire and draw in the stone.
The journey, although unknown, can lead us to incredible places.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
All of us here today are 70 percent water.
Our planet is three-quarters water.
Our brain, our blood are almost 80 percent water.
I know you feel solid while sitting in your chairs, but let me tell you you are practically water.
In 2030, Mexico will run out of drinking water.
We have 15 years to change the way we use this vital resource,
if we are to be a country with water.
Since I was a kid, I remember being told, Carlos, don't waste water, close the water tap, bath quickly, place a container under the shower,
and other similar recommendations.
I even recall listening on the radio and other media saying that water was running out.
Some catchy phrases were: "drop by drop water runs out."
You might remember some of them.
Truth is I did not know why water was running out.
Everyone would speak about it but I still had clean water at home.
My cistern always had water and if it is depleted, just call a tanker truck and that's it.
The whole thing became annoying, I even though it was a lie.
Nobody ever could explain me that we will indeed run out of water.
The world is living a water crisis, the water we are made of and we need to live.
To talk about water we would need to talk deforestation, global warming, aquifer depletion. Many subjects, but today I will focus on Mexico's water pollution.
72 percent of our water is polluted.
I made my research and finally understood: we are not running out of water, we are taking clean water and returning it polluted.
the rest of that 72 percent is used for industrial and agricultural purposes, and the remaining 12 percent is for household use.
An average Mexican uses 330 liters of water a day.
Mainly in the shower, for washing clothes, using the toilet, washing the dishes and other activities at home.
By 2030 we will not be 121 million but 137 million Mexicans, 330 liters a day will be a luxury, not as it is today.
Mexico has a huge water problem.
I previously said that 90 percent of clean water is used in agriculture and industries, and you might be wondering why all that water?
Any of you ever heard of "virtual water"?
Raise your hands.
OK, one.
Virtual water is used to produce almost anything you can think of.
For instance, all together we are wearing around 30,000 or 35,000 liters of water.
To manufacture a pair of jeans, 12,000 liters are needed.
a shirt 4,000, a jacket 9,000, a cotton t-shirt 2,000, if anyone ate meat during the week, to produce one kilogram of meat, 16,000 of water are needed.
A beer, if you like beer, 75 liters.
A glass of milk, 140 liters.
A kilogram of rice 1,300 liters.
Water is almost behind everything, we just don't realize it.
Virtual water to manufacture a computer.
For a liter of gas, a house, a lot of water is needed, but it needs to be clean water, no other type of water can be used.
I am from Puebla, and the future of my city's main water supply comes from the Atoyac river and Valsequillo dam, where Atoyac flows into along with other river branches
Here by my side we have two containers, one with tap water we took from the bathrooms, and this one has water from the river.
You are now going to be passed on water bottles, I did not bring for everyone, but just smell them It is sad for me, yet I am proud to introduce you my beloved river.
This is the Atoyac river from Puebla.
Atoyac is the third most polluted river in the country, with more than eight times the pollution permitted levels according to current laws.
And eight is polite, because if this was a USA river it would be 800 times more polluted.
In Mexico only 104 toxic substances are inspected
In the USA they inspect 80,000.
Can you imagine what could possibly be there?
carcinogenic agents, heavy metals, and any toxic substance adverse to health.
In 2011, I went out with some friends who live by the side of Valsequillo lake, where I told you Atoyac river flows into.
It was a weekend, we were having some beers when suddenly this stench you just smelled, took over the place we were at.
An we all wondered how, after so many campaigns, why was the water still heavily polluted?
We knew we had to do something.
That day we decided to start a movement to raise awareness in Puebla and Mexico, on the bad state of the river.
Our movement was called Dale la cara al Atoyac (Face Atoyac) because we turned a blind eye for so many years.
How we would accomplish our goal?
We would raft the dirty river in our rafting boats so that people from Puebla would turn their heads to the river.
We set up the event, made a river scouting, made our research on every safety measure to be safe in such a polluted water. The big day came and to our surprise, there were many people gathered on the starting point.
I can hardly describe the feeling.
But they all wanted a clean river.
All of you, wherever you live wherever you come from, Who wouldn't want to have a live river in the city?
Why do I say live?
Because Atoyac, in its current state is a dead river.
Among the elements inspected during water quality measurements one of the most important is oxygen, and Atoyac has not enough oxygen for life.
The day we rafted, we discovered an amazing place with great potential.
People called us "Atoyaqueros," that is how we are known in Puebla.
We realized we had to do that more often, awareness was needed.
We need to tell the world what we are doing.
We rafted again in 2012 and 2013 and reflected on what we had achieved so far and realized that Puebla had indeed faced Atoyac river, but that wasn't enough, water remained as polluted as always.
New measures were to be taken.
We decided to leap from awareness to taking action.
We formally constituted Dale la cara al Atoyac, with a first aim in mind: to rescue and recover the Atoyac basin.
A huge problem was ahead us.
How do you solve such level of pollution?
how to build a replicable model?
Thinking about it over and over, the real problem actually was the whole Mexican system.
All institutions and people around the case, work independently, on their own and never teamed up.
Everyone has different goals and schedules, even different administration timings.
How could they possibly solve such a complex problem, and a river with these levels of pollution,
if they are all divided? Impossible.
That was the opportunity area of Dale la cara.
We had to be an effort hub, the melting-pot where all efforts would be concocted to reach the goal of a clean river as soon as possible.
How would we do it?
Through three great strategies.
First, by raising a strong awareness.
How could we demand from authorities or raise our hands, if we did not know how polluted the river was?
Or did not even know we had a river?
How could the smartest engineer build or come up with a new way to manufacture clothes with less water if they did not know that a pair of jeans require 12,000 liters?
The next strategy was close monitoring of law compliance.
In Puebla there are 22 treatment facilities, but only five are working.
Out of these five you know which comply with the norms?
None.
How could we expect to have clean water if laws are a bunch of dead literature.
Someone has to monitor the activities, that is why are working on a citizen observatory where business people, institutions, and people like us, can watch over the correct water flow.
Third, strong actions for Atoyac regeneration, we are now recruiting people and businesses as "Atoyaqueros," people committed to reducing their water footprint, and willing to support the cause.
To kick start our civil society we rafted again,
but in a different way, not just raising awareness.
We rafted with the leaders, managers and different people whose power is key to the river recovery; they rafted the same way we did to see how things were.
Their boarding pass: keep working with us.
It is our river.
We need to work to keep it clean.
In the future, if we do not work together for a clean river, our city will drown.
Why we should care?
Because rivers are the veins of our cities.
Without rivers there is no water, no rain, no forests, no animals.
You might be wondering, how can we clean these levels of pollution?
It must be really hard.
Take a look of this picture of the river source.
I have been there, I've taken water with my hand.
how do we turn that into this in a few kilometers?
I can't believe we keep allowing it.
If tomorrow, all businesses, industries, farmers and all who want a clean river, stopped returning polluted water to the river, and we did it dutifully, that is returning water clean or cleaner than how we received it, in a matter of weeks
weeks indeed, in October, the river would be like this.
Not saying that the river basin would be regenerated, -regeneration is a wider subject- that would take between five to ten years to happen because of the level of damage, but we would have water.
In 2030 when underground water is depleted, we could turn to the river for this, instead of this.
We did not made this up,
Korea did it already, their river is regenerated, the name is too weird for me to pronounce.
Truth is, the river was a drainage, now it is a park.
Medellin river, closer to us, was a red river.
Today it is regenerated.
The Rimac river in Peru, similar to Mexican rivers for its pollution levels, is now regenerated.
Why? All these cities realized
they needed water to live.
They needed water for the future.
There won't be enough for everyone, for that reason, just as these countries and cities committed with their future I want to invite you all to turn our faces towards pollution in our country.
to turn our face towards our rivers.
If all of us here could join efforts, wherever we come from, and work to reach our goal not just in Puebla, but in all of our country.
We could ensure water supply for our children, for the next generations, for ourselves, because we will see this happening in 15 years,
not even our grandchildren, it is us who will see it.
I have a three-year-old daughter and I want her to have a future in a country where she is not afraid of water the way we were when we rafted the Atoyac river.
Today is September the 12th, a very special day.
besides being before an audience, I want you to be my first witnesses of the official countdown for 2030, and commit to working together, and we will succeed, to work for a live Mexico, a Mexico with water.
Thank you.
(Applause)
The American psychologist Frederic Burrhus Skinner used to say that the real problem is not whether machines think but whether men do.
And that's how we've changed in barely a decade and a half, right?
Skinner was referring to something that today seems just around the corner: the use of all kinds of technology in 21st century classrooms.
We've always seen computers, for example, as tools that allow us to overcome our human limitations, in storing and compiling information, and as such an excellent way to share human knowledge.
However, few people question whether this really represents any progress, or whether what we're doing in reality is atrophying certain cognitive abilities that we develop during childhood and becoming a species dependent on technology.
In particular, I'd like to speak about language acquisition, and how it's influenced by the act of writing by hand.
OK, so we know that, for example, every time we pick up a pencil we go over, in our heads, the letters and words that compose the sentence we want to write.
This is what's known as subvocal rehearsal, and it's associated with an area of the brain that in addition to becoming active during language production, makes it possible to recognise a certain letter.
This area of the brain, the IFG, stores information about the motor-function of the hand. That's to say, it stores kinesthetic memory, and remembers the patterns we make as we set down each letter.
In this way, we can see, for example, why we have to write a word down in order to know how to spell it.
That's to say, writing by hand is a typical treatment for dyslexia.
And because of this, when someone types, there's no difference between the movements of the hand so there's no way to distinguish one letter from another.
OK, so let's take this to a more scientific level, and look at the following article: 'What's lost as handwriting fades'.
This article, published in the NY Times in the middle of last year, is connected with a study carried out at the University of Indiana by the researchers Karin James and Laura Engelhardt, who investigated if the habit of writing by hand would accelerate learning to read.
Well, during the schooling of 14 children between the ages of 4 and 5, all of them pre-literate, which is to say that none of them knew how to read or write, the researchers observed stimulation in different areas of the brain, depending on whether the children had to copy out letters, or whether the children had to trace letters and characters like we find, in those little exercise books we all remember from school, or whether the children had to write on a computer.
OK, so they didn't blow anything up in this experiment, but I guess you want to know the results.
The results they got when they showed each of these children the type of letter which they'd been conditioned to were the following: In image A, this one here, where the children who copied letters and characters by hand are compared with the children who wrote on a computer, we see more activity in the IFG, one of the foremost language centers of the brain as we saw earlier, as a result of subvocal rehearsal, and actually this was the most recorded activity in the study.
In image D, where children who were tracing letters are compared with those who were writing on a computer, again we see greater activity in the case of the children who wrote by hand, although this activity is less than in image A.
On the other hand, in images B and C we can see activity in other areas of the brain, like in the anterior cingulate cortex, or in the posterior parietal cortex, the function of which, although this is currently debated, appears to be related to the detection of errors in the reproduction of letters, or a greater effort to mentally retain the form and outline of letters.
So from all of this it can be deduced that the IFG is activated to a greater extent in the case of children who had to copy letters and characters, something that resembles, for example, what we do all the time in class when we're taking notes, and followed by the children who had to trace the letters and lastly, by those who wrote on a computer.
Now we've demolished the paradigm, scientifically speaking, and it seems 'the elephant in the room', as they say in English, has flown away using its huge ears, right?
Or maybe not.
As well as showing this, I want to return to what my friend Skinner was saying.
It's not only about computers and mobiles presenting some attractive distractions, but about having the capacity to act with the liberty that defines us: the ability to act as a species with inherent, inborn dignity, in order to oppose the impulse of depending on these machines. Let's see.
So whenever I talk about this, I like to ask a question: How many of you during a normal day, have considered not using your mobile phone at any time?
How many of you have said: "Today, I'll disconnect myself"?
I want to see those hands really high. How many of you have done it?
That's more than I expected, honestly.
The thing is, even if it was hardly anyone, I wouldn't be surprised.
And it's because we are naturally attracted to bright, shiny objects, like screens, for example,
and if they make some noises that aren't too annoying, better yet.
Here's an example: when I was around a year old, I needed to watch a lit candle so that I'd be distracted enough to eat.
And if there wasn't a candle, no one could get me to open my mouth.
But we don't need to resist our natural urges, that occasionally manifest themselves as real elephants in the room, to continue investigating what's happening around us.
I'm not satisfied, for example, with the usual response to the effect of all these mobile devices, which we're all now getting for Christmas, the response that we'll see their effect when our generation grows up,
all because the interests of a few silence or pacify scientific rigor, while the rest of us swallow whatever it is, as long as that candle keeps burning.
Or even while we pass the time arguing about more superficial questions, like whether we should have one more hour per week of maths or art at school.
For all this, it's extremely important, that as well as saving resources, investing our time working on a computer which is something I accept... - which is something that I accept has its advantages, that as well as working on computers, let's not forget the importance of other things, like empathy, for example, or personal contact, or, as I was talking about before, calligraphy or writing by hand.
Here's an idea: why not carry around a pen and a notebook, where you can write down your thoughts or memorable phrases, or even use it to plan your day so that you can maximize your time.
Never forget the pleasure of writing a beautiful phrase on a piece of white paper,
and remember: one that knows only science, doesn't know much about science.
In moments of change, the answers change, but the questions also change.
Questions that were easy become difficult.
Questions that were difficult become very easy.
Above all, new questions come up.
Let's start with questions that seemed to be easy and suddenly become difficult.
Profession?
Each time the notary asks about my profession I make up a random one.
Lately, I like to say "astronaut".
"Prove me that I'm not."
Why?
I don't know about you, but I had to reinvent myself many times throughout my life.
I also think that if the government wants to know my job, they should give me a job.
Another difficult question: craft?
You may have a craft today, but you don't know what will be your job tomorrow.
They tell us that algorithms, robots, machines are going to change and make disappear 47% of the jobs we know in the next years.
What does this mean?
That most of the jobs in production, distribution, administration even in sales, which seems very personal, can disappear in the next years.
Therefore, a question that seemed simple has become difficult, too.
Another very simple question has become very difficult: job?
This robot cleans airports.
With a 20% of unemployment or a 50% youth unemployment, questions related to jobs have become uncomfortable, difficult, and in many cases, impossible.
But we're also going to talk about some questions that used to be very difficult and have now become very simple.
For example: what do you want to know?
Wikipedia, but also YouTube, or Khan Academy allow you access to what you want, and they do it in a very innovative way: each person shares whatever she knows, we organize it under a set of rules, and we make it available to everyone.
For you to get an idea, Wikipedia has been written with 1% of the time Americans spend watching TV during one year.
With this potential – imagine, 1% of the time – it's capable of being the 7th site in the world and to provide knowledge to 500 million people per month.
What a level of abundance!
Another question that has become simple: where do you want to go?
Blablacar offers to anyone who is driving from one city to another and who has got spare seats, to publish and helps them find people who want to share the route and split costs.
BlablaCar transports already 2 million passengers in Europe every month.
Two million passengers - for you to get an idea - is a third of the bus traffic between cities in Spain.
Two million passengers - what the French train was telling us - is that BlablaCar has already captured 5% of the train traffic between cities in France.
Without investment, buying cars or training people.
It's another category of economy.
Another difficult question that turned easy: what do you want to produce?
Obama invites us to make our dreams, to produce them in 3D.
Additive fabrication process, the fabrication object by object allows us to produce low cost, the cost of the raw material, - more than 100 materials can be already used - allows us to produce anything at a very low cost.
Long series or short series.
Simple products or complex products.
A silly example: you can produce a whistle with the ball already inside. There is no need to produce two parts and put the small ball inside.
We said there are easy questions and difficult ones, we see that we are entering into a new mode of production.
We are going to call it citizen production, the mode of citizen production.
What are we observing?
A very different efficiency level.
We are able to produce much more with much less, transport, accommodation, whatever.
That we are able to generate abundance where there used to be scarcity in the fields of knowledge, design
that we are able to pull from trust sources beyond the institutions and organizations we already know.
Of course yes, any change also implies changes in other things.
We're also starting to see that not always bigger is better.
We're also starting to see that not everyone will get a traditional job.
We're also starting to see the need to give value to those things that we don't know how to measure or pay with money.
What's happening?
We're facing a total economic change.
We're facing changes in the models, changes in the factors of production.
Any economy is defined by a specific combination of capital, work, and available resources.
All changing at the same time.
Let's start with the capital. Money.
We need less and less money to start things.
Look how has the need for money decreased with platforms like Google or YouTube, with Amazon Web Service that allows you to create a shop and scale it with very little effort, the costs in money have decreased dramatically.
But on top of that, we've got the crowdfunding that allows us to finance things, we've currencies like Bitcoin, that is based on Blockchain, or complementary and neighborhood currencies that allow you to get money from different sources.
The second source of capital is trust.
It's happening in Spain, but also in other countries,
citizens do no longer trust official institutions: politicians, parliaments, or even the media.
We trust civil society, we trust each other.
There is a revolution in trust.
Social networks allow us to generate trust between strangers.
A novelty for humans.
And the open invites us to trust the rules. Rules that are open to everyone and that we can all change.
We talked about capital and work.
This is the average citizen of the world.
We live in Europe, in Spain, and we've got this feeling that everyone is old, and that we are all living like this.
But the average world citizen according to National Geographic is 28 years old, is young, educated, connected, has got knowledge, and ambition, and wants to change things globally.
We're not only young, connected, and educated, we also have got new capacities.
Citizens are not only consumers. We, the citizens know how to share our ideas, how to share our knowledge and how to share our assets.
We have learned to remix the things we have access to.
We have got the capacity, and we practice it it all the time, to finance projects that we don't know if traditional financing institutions would have financed.
We've got the capacity, and we use it, to produce accommodation, transport, energy, science, or anything we may think of.
We've got the capacity, more and more, to create value between us and to divide the benefits according to the results.
Who's this new economic agent?
It's the citizen producer.
It's any of us when we put in value what we've got, what we know, what we like in exchange for a pat on the back; in exchange for recognition or in exchange for global resource euros.
Because what has happened is that the optimal economical scale for many things is one, a hotel with one room; a restaurant with one table; a transportation company with one seat; a multinational craftsman of only one person.
And now, the new questions. We said, easy questions become difficult, difficult questions become easy.
And many new questions.
For example: income or salary?
What we've got all clear is that to be alive we need income.
And we're starting to see clearly that there won't be jobs for everyone.
We start to see in this new economy, the collaborative economy, that the income per hour of the better rated people is higher than a classic job.
You don't have the security nor the stability of a classic job.
"Yes but, how many are they?"
We are millions in most of the researched countries.
Between the 40-50% of the people have either shared something using traditional platforms such as Craiglist or eBay, or in the new generation of mobile platforms.
"Yes, but they must be always the same people!"
Apparently not, apparently it is contagious. When the people, citizens start to discover a different way to create value, different ways to give value to what we own, other sources of trust, and different ways to generate activity among us in a way, socially more interesting and probably more sustainable, it becomes a contagious phenomenon.
"It is happening in weird places."
It is not, it is extending from country to country,
among the occidental countries for now, and in developing world with different values and different ways.
"But this is a marginal thing…" It is not.
Because when citizens get together to create something, we have got the capacity to transform the system as a whole.
You know that in Spain we are threatened if we produce or storage our own energy.
In other countries like Germany, where curiously they've got less sun, citizens have got the freedom to install their capacity and share it.
The result is nearly 50% of the renewable energy infrastructure in Germany is not owned by either energy companies or financial funds, it belongs to citizens like us that are doing an energy revolution roof by roof, house by house.
This is why the question we should be doing ourselves, "How will these changes affect me?"
Because it is affecting, and it has the potential to affect, this third logic, this citizen mode of production, energy, education, health, science, politics, companies, technology, traveling, production, industry. Because I think we're very close, as the mayor of Seoul proposes, to have a lifestyle coherent with common sense.
Because I think we're starting to understand, and we all can contribute to a new way of production that is designed to generate abundance and efficiency and to develop mutual trust among us.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Who are you?
In some way, this is one of the main questions we have to ask ourselves nowadays. One of the most important ones.
And nevertheless, it's as if we never quite answer it fully, as if it doesn't even have an ultimate answer.
However, this hasn't always been like this.
During feudal system, for instance, identities were given beforehand at birth.
People were permanently related to the land where they lived, to the work they performed, and it was performed in a very different way from the way in which we work today: For instance, the feudal worker kept to a large extent the control over his means of production, over his working tools and at the same time, he was present during the whole productive process.
This is why he could conceive the final product of his work as an extension of himself because it was in fact a product of his creative self expression; he could identify himself with his work.
This is why we can say that back then there was a marriage between Identity and Work. In the same spirit, people used to live in small communities or feuds that involved a very strong social cohesion; people were "members" in the fullest sense of this word. "Members" of a community, in the sense that a leg is a member of a body part of an organic-functional whole because there used to be a strong cointainment web at that time.
On the other hand, nowadays, the first thing that current workers lose is the certainty of land and work that used to be the comfort of medieval workers.
Nowadays we are no longer linked for life to the land where we live, even less to the work we perform, let's keep in mind that in the best case scenario this depends on a contract that can be broken at any given time.
On the other hand, current workers are only present in a specific moment of the whole productive process. This takes away from the productive process its elements, its qualities, its most human, most creative most individual elements.
He was Daniel Escobar during the '80s he worked for Renault Argentina. During that time, he screwed over 9 million pieces.
If I were to ask you,
"In which or which one, or in how many
of this over 9 million screws can we find him?"
Well, the answer is easy; in none.
Work has become anonymous let's think on how much of the work we perform noways would have the very same outcome if someone else did it.
This is why that marriage is now broken.
Thus, the current individual is no longer permanently linked to the land he lives he does not relate to the products of his work neither with his work itself as we no longer live in feudes he is no longer linked to his community, we are now anonymous to each other we have no duties towards each other.
This ends up in a strong feeling of estrangement, of alienation, of disorientation, in current individuals.
This is why identity becomes, for the first time in history, for the first time in humankind, identity becomes something to be searched it is no longer given to us beforehand, it becomes something to be conquered, to be achieved.
And this is why we increasingly think of ourselves as creators of identities, as the architects of ourselves.
Going back on the divorce between Identity and Work.
Who do you think Identity will run away with after divorcing Work?
Well, with Work's best friend.
In fact, we've historically passed from an Identity based on Work to an Indentity centered around Consumption.
Since we began to live in big cities where nobody knows us anymore we are exposed to the eyes of many people that do not know us, who do not know who we are, and when people don't know each other people start craving to distinguish themselves, to differenciate themselves.
Therefore, this ends up generating a culture of appearances and the only way we have in order to distinguish ourselves from others is through our ostensible consumption.
This is a type of consumption that doesn't seek personal well being. It is a type of consumption that has communicative, expressive functions.
For example, when I buy myself this car, well, the utility of this product is clear, it is that of taking me, transporting me from one place to another. Now, when I buy myself this car the utility of this product is elswhere: The utility of this product lays on how it defines me, in how it describes me, in how it identifies me.
And in that at the same time, it's capable of granting me reputation, granting me recognition from the others.
That's why the first thing I do when I buy myself this car is to post the picture on Facebook.
(Laughter) Well, at this point, some of you might as well say, "What's the problem with this?
"We've historically went through an identity centered around production to an identitdy centered on consumption. What's the problem with that?"
In the first place, this culture of appearances ends up generating a market of pre-established identities for which all we have to do as active subjects is to decide from within a menu that's already out there.
This generates a "having in order to be" dynamic of consuming in order to belong.
And this leaves us here: As a matter fact, these identities are always based either on matters of status or in the best case in matters of style or taste.
But these are individual identities, they say nothing about our impact or our role in the society we belong to.
Peter Singer is an Australian philosopher and he brings foward the following example. Imagine this: You get a new pair of shoes for USD 300.
You go walking happily with your brand new pair of shoes, you walk past a lake and you see this: There's a kid that's drowning. You look around, his parents don't seem to be anywhere around, there is no other adult around, you realize that it wouldn't represent a threat or danger to you to come in and rescue him. But you don't have enough time to undo your shoelaces and take off your shoes and they will get ruined. Well, how many of you will save the kid, regardless of the shoes?
Put up your hands, please. What a moralist audience I got today!
Well, Peter Singer used to find the same answer and to this he would always reply: Then why when it comes to spending your money you choose to buy the shoes instead of donating that money to an institution that with it could possibly save the lives of even more than one kid?
This is why I want to invite you to the possibility of re-thinking consumption in terms of social impact and also in terms of opportunity cost of what is it that we stop spending our money in when we in fact consume something else. And to rethink to which kind of consumption by the others it is that we will grant our recognition.
(Applause)
Okay. Do you see what I have in my hand?
You see it? It's a hygiene kit.
It's a hygiene kit like many that are distributed to refugees, or to those in need in a humanitarian emergency.
What is in the kit?
I will explain, it's very simple.
You'll see they include those elements that are considered essential for the hygiene of a person who has just been in a situation, or who is in a situation of extreme vulnerability.
You can see the packet of handkerchiefs, a hairbrush, a detachable shaving razor.
They are items that, well, at least from our perspective, can seem trivial.
Where can we find necessities like this?
Maybe in a hotel, when we check in, or we find them in the room.
And yes, we can see it like an object that doesn't have much relevance, but the truth is, and we just saw a tragic example, in certain situations, under certain circumstances, with people who've lost almost everything, these items can be of extreme value.
A hygiene kit can be essential, the difference between life and death, and to us it seems so trivial.
So, today I propose that we unfold the process that goes into the distribution of this hygiene kit, for example, in a boat in high seas.
This kit does not grow on trees, there is a chain that we call the chain of humanitarian aid, and today we're going to explain to you, basically, through five points.
I ask for your attention for five points that I will summarize briefly.
The first point, specialized personnel.
The second point would be planning, okay?
The third would be terrain adaptation.
Fourth, multi-functionality, which is a little abstract, but we'll explain it.
And fifth, as I believe is appropriate for this forum, would be innovation.
Let's go back to the first point, specialized personnel ... did the mic turn off?
Strange things are ...
happening here... (Laughing) Okay, specialized personnel.
As we have seen before with the example of Doha, when a person is taken in, they are taken in fully by the organization, and this is a big responsibility for all of us.
A multitude of conditions such as hygiene, health, well-being, become our responsibility.
And this, as you can imagine, is a big responsibility.
At the time of administering humanitarian aid, a hygiene kit, as we have said, an item that can seem trivial, we need able persons, we need doctors, nurses, social workers, logistical technicians, every type of qualified personnel that can evaluate, above all else, the state of this person and decide what treatments or what materials, we can also think outside of what a refugee can be, we can talk also of development assistance, or of humanitarian aid, in bigger terms.
Specialized people are needed, people like you who are forming that which, in the future, could be part of this chain.
But I now want it to be clear that an item like this cannot be delivered simply by a vendor.
It is not a vending machine that delivers an item.
Not all items have equal value for everyone, and, also, it is crucial to evaluate all of their necessities.
Thus, it is vital that we have specialized personnel in mind when humanitarian aid is delivered and administered.
The second point is planning.
It is understandable that in the instance of a humanitarian catastrophe, it is almost by definition an occurrence that will be unpredictable.
We cannot know when an earthquake will occur, or when it will cause an avalanche of refugees.
Yes, there are indicators that can forewarn you, but by definition, it will be something unpredictable.
A hygiene kit like the one we have seen, takes time to assemble,
right? Then, when an emergency occurs, that is registered, they are going to have to cover a multitude of necessities, like blankets, tents, hygiene products, food rations, it is already too late to call a provider.
We cannot simply call a provider and tell them to give us what we need, and because of that anticipation is imperative.
The duty of many humanitarian organizations is to anticipate and to be able to provide and restore those items that they will need.
For example, in the previous case we saw that a hygiene kit was being delivered, an emergency ration in a boat.
In order to get into that boat, first it has to be in a store, for example in Sicily, months; an item can be waiting for months for an emergency to occur, for a protocol to activate, and that item be delivered to that boat; but before arriving to that store it came, in this case, for example, from Alicante in a truck, where it was assembled.
As we have seen, the kit contains various items, and for that kit you have to speak with certain providers because you have to assemble it.
Therefore, without anticipation, it's impossible to direct humanitarian aid in emergencies.
That is another point that is important for you to have in mind.
Third, yes I, I've forgotten, no?
Yes, is it?
Third, adaptation.
We, from humanitarian aid, are providers of resources, but a lot of times we do not have direct contact with the end user.
How can we identify which resources are most adequate for humanitarian emergency projects or for development assistance?
Well, no more and no less than counting on collaboration with organizations, such as UNHCR and the Red Cross, people who are in direct contact with these situations, can help us determine what are those specifications that we should carry out.
I'll give you an example.
Imagine a simple water bottle, that we use to fill when we are working on the ground, or to distribute water in a refugee camp.
Consider that, if the mouth of the water bottle is well adapted, is a little bigger and fits better in the pumps that are used for pumping water, it can reduce water waste from 40 to 5 percent.
For us this is a big responsibility, to be able to provide these solutions to our associates, to the front lines of humanitarian aid.
What do we count on for that?
Here you have it, these are our bibles.
The humanitarian aid catalogs are items which contain all of the know-how and knowledge of many humanitarian aid technicians.
And for us, in the end, I'll tell you, they become our ultimate reference guide.
But there is something more, that goes beyond the technical, something that cannot be learned from a book, and that is empathy.
Empathy with the user, and it is something that you can be aware of through seeing the previous exposition, empathizing with that person, with a refugee who all of the sudden finds themselves in a situation of absolute vulnerability.
For example, as said before, when we confront situations like those, they are unpredictable, and in the previous case, as you have seen, they were people who came from Syria, they could be from any other country.
But I will give another example with a food ration, emergency provisions, that later you'll be able to sample.
You will sample exactly the same ration that a person would eat in an emergency situation, with which, you will also be able to empathize better this way.
But I think it is fundamental that we focus on the development of that ration. What should it contain?
As we have seen, could there be Muslim refugees?
Yes, it is very probable.
Maybe not. There could be other African countries that are not; so from the development of the product we can arrange this.
For us that is fundamental, by default, to not include recipes with pork, or include meats with providers that have a Halal certification, because in the end we will make sure that the whole world can eat according to their traditions.
Sometimes it would surprise me to say, in that situation, a refugee who has gone days without eating, has to have qualms about eating something that could have pork in it?
The thought came to me, but the truth is that humanitarian aid work made us conclude that in those states of extreme vulnerability, when people perceive that they are not treated with dignity, the psychological effect is devastating.
When those people perceive that the humanitarian aid does not treat them with dignity and respect, it is very counterproductive.
So you have to make the effort to attempt to treat and offer them an adequate answer to their necessities.
We also have to have language in mind.
Do you know what languages we can anticipate that these people will understand in the instructions?
It could be English, French, Arabic, but also, this is an unusual thing in our field, but there are people who will be illiterate and cannot read.
All of this is fundamental for us to have in mind when preparing rations.
And lastly, the fourth point would be multi-functionality.
As we have said, when an emergency situation occurs, logistics are extremely important, and so the organizations ask us to develop kits that are multi-functional, because with only one delivery, we can cover a multitude of necessities.
There are birth kits, sexual hygiene kits, nutrition kits, hygiene kits, all types of kits.
Why?
Because you try, with only one delivery, to cover many necessities.
And I did not want to pass up the opportunity to talk about innovation.
In a forum such as the university, it seems very important to me.
I guess if I asked you if we should send out innovative products, or integrate innovation with humanitarian aid, the obvious answer would be yes.
And it is true, they have to send updated products, but there are various factors, that, if someone worked here, for example, in the development of products, or in industrial engineering, they would come to understand with production cycles.
First, innovative solutions can have too high an elevated price, which makes it so we cannot maximize the number of users.
Second, very innovative solutions at times are complicated to maintain.
Third, dependability.
If they are innovative, we have not tested them sufficiently.
And fourth, they require a know-how.
Finally, many times what can function and have value here, will not function in a Libyan desert.
Because of this, it is important to send updated materials, but also we have to have in mind those factors that we have talked about.
And now we will do a review. We talked about qualified personnel, basically talking about the who, we have talked about planning, the when and where, we have talked about multi-functionality, innovation and adaptation, which is the what and how, but I've left out someone.
I have left out a position.
Who do you think is absent that makes this chain of humanitarian aid function and strengthen?
Who do you think?
Well in the end it is clear, it is us, it is you.
We, as citizens, and now I speak to you as another citizen.
If we are not able to contribute as volunteers, to support NGOs, outside you have a UNHCR table, a little reminder, to make problems visible, apply pressure to governments, to city councils, to urge autonomous communities, and to compel,
and why not, your own university.
If we do not apply this pressure, this service chain will not function how it should, and unfortunately cases like those we have seen previously will repeat themselves.
And with this I will finish, only I want to say we aren't promoting anything vanguard nor revolutionary, we are only talking about people who help other people, people helping people, one of the essential challenges of humanity.
Let us not lose it.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Imagine that you go to the doctor, because you feel sick and they take four years to tell you what you have.
And what you have is a strange disease that has no cure and the treatment is purely palliative.
Or they don't have any.
Well, this is how 7% of the world population feel.
We are talking about 500 million people, that is 40 people among you who are here today at TEDx.
What is a rare disease?
It's a disease that affects one person in 2,000, it doesn't seem much, but actually, in the world there are between 7,000 and 8,000 different rare diseases.
It's very difficult to track them, because there are only a few cases and too many false diagnoses, so it generates little information for research, making it difficult to deliver a correct diagnosis.
Besides the lack of financial resources, one of the main problems of rare diseases research, it's the access to the main source of information, medical histories.
There isn't a standard format, because our data are dispersed in different medical centers, where we've previously been.
For instance, think of when you move to another location.
And, because there aren't many cases, it's hard to locate them and getting to that information involves many bureaucratic obstacles.
It's a huge waste of time and money.
So, here is what we decided: what If we took the information and then gave it to the researchers?
if we facilitated access for them?
Our proposal is datudato.org, an online platform where mothers and fathers can collect, manage and share the medical data of their children, from the very moment of birth.
Why do we focus on children?
two persons out of three who have a rare disease are children, and because their mothers and fathers are real information gatherers about their children, and even more when that information involves health.
What data we can upload?
We can upload data about weight, stature, cranial perimeter, radiographies, vaccination schedule, we can even upload data about the fathers and mothers, because 80% of rare diseases have a genetic origin.
All the data are stored and classified, and thanks to this, we can even see the evolution of some cases.
However, we can also share it with any doctor in any consultation, regardless of the health system we go to; also we don't have to carry all our personal data with us.
The most important thing is that all these files can be shared completely anonymously, helping to create a global data base accessible for information.
We know that Datudato can't be an isolated solution.
Without the effort of all the parties, it's impossible to achieve the goal which is to integrate as many data as possible.
It's not just about us having our personal clinical record with us at anytime and managing it ourselves, because, in the end, the information is ours, it's about sharing for a common good; because we all are part of the solution, and because the more data we share, the easier it is for future research.
However, Datudato doesn't stop here.
The team is integrated by Íñigo, Guillermo, Alicia, Jaime and me. We are still working to make Datudato a real tool.
And for that, we are looking for professional health advice, financial and technological support.
So, if anyone wants to contact us, we are going to be here for the rest of the day, and if not, you can email us to hola@datudato.org.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Host: Estela de Carlotto! (Applause) Dearest friends, thank you for welcoming me in the name of all "Abuelas de Plaza de Mayo"; I'm just one of them.
And my story is the same as my fellow women:
we got together deeply pained, not knowing what to do, lacking information, surrounded by danger, and leaving everything behind.
I was a school principal but I retired to search for a Disappeared loved one,
a child of mine, a daughter,
but it was a double search
because my daughter was expecting a baby.
At my age, I was born in the 1930s, dictatorships were part of my education,
but I never thought that the last civic-military dictatorship would bring such a horrifying and sinister a project.
I had to educate myself about it, I had to learn about it and to understand,
but we did something wonderful which allowed us to keep on fighting: we joined together.
Even if we stood alone, when we saw there were other mothers out there, with same faces and feeling the same pain, we dared to ask what their stories were
and so we decided
to search for answers together.
This is how this group took shape 37 years ago.
And we are still getting together - the ones who have survived - because many of us passed away, and others are ill. We have different ideologies now, a different culture or religion, but there is still something that unites us, that common thread of pain, that fight that never ends, because I believe not a day goes by that a mother doesn't love her sons or daughters, especially if she has been searching for her beloved son.
So we started by getting together and searching not knowing exactly where or how to do it.
We started searching for the babies at first, but we got no answers.
They were never brought back to us.
We prepared our homes and the layettes,
but we soon left our families uncared for, we swept our traditional gender roles as mothers, as wives, in my case my job as a teacher too, and let our husbands take care of the home; that's what we tell people who asked us how were the grandfathers involved.
Well, they were there,
fulfilling domestic duties. I think of them as anonymous heroes because they were left to care for the children at home, provide as sole breadwinners, and wait for us in silence because every time we went marching they never knew if we were to come back.
I remember one time I was a little discouraged and said that I'd quit with no apparent reason,
and my husband intervened and told me that I couldn't, I had to go on, because the Grandmothers needed me.
It was the only time I felt like quitting,
but never again; on the contrary.
You know that I found my grandson, we were reunited, we're living together! (Applause)
What a moment!
But I want to let you know, to share it with you: I didn't find him by myself.
I've got help from the Grandmothers, from all of you, all this time - the longest period in the history of democracy here in Argentina - because we got together in a common understanding the dictatorship's affected all of us, and we must ensure it doesn't happen again and that we must find and get back our kidnapped Disappeared alive.
So my grandson is everybody's grandson. See, something amazing happened: there is a permanent smile on people's faces in the country since that encounter.
It may be because of this woman's fight for 37 years, out of love, overcoming hatred and resentment, hoping this doesn't happen again, that our children will live to be free, to be freethinkers, and to learn how to disagree but live together as brothers.
That's why I think this multitudinous, wonderful event we're attending in this cultural space should foster coexistence beyond differences.
The Grandmothers, even today argue on things, we have diverging views we don't agree on, but when we have to come to a decision we are resolving it together.
We know we are lucky, but I sometimes say is funny how one gets to thank life through pain.
For my daughter gave her life knowing that things should change, and they are,
but there are many roads left unexplored.
One of them is science.
There is no other genetic Data Bank anywhere in the whole world but here where grandparents can search for their kidnapped grandchildren.
We are the only country with a history in the search of stolen babies, now adults.
But what a joyous moment when we are reunited!
The happiness!
I feel younger, I feel better.
Sometimes I feel like throwing this cane, but I have to tell you that is a symbol of all the Grandmothers, because we never kneel down, we are always marching head-high, resolutely, determined and with dignity.
Because there is no need for violence in order to have justice done.
Justice is being done.
And I think we made a small contribution to our current democracy that we hope it will last forever.
(Applause) In this brief talk I wish you all the best.
If you are here, then I think it's because you are those Argentineans who deserve respect, to be heard, and have their demands satisfied.
The most important thing
is not to be alone, not to give up.
Because perseverance is the key of all triumphs.
37 years later I am here, and I thank you today for helping me find my grandson;
it's something worth fighting for.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Of the 10,000 people here today, 1,250 were or will be victims of sexual assault, which is the worst thing that can happen to a person.
But those 1,250 people know, and I know it's no consolation, but do know that at least you didn't have to make jokes about people who are victims of sexual assault.
(Applause) Or sexual assault or people on the brink of death, the terminally ill, people who are about to die.
A lady who died because he gave her his hand too strongly, she died, I don't know what happened.
Really, so depressing did the talks have to be?
They're all crying!
The speakers knew that after them I had to come in and make people laugh!
They could have made them a bit more up!
But seriously, very good Matías Najún with palliative care.
A beautiful talk that came to remind us of something very important: We will all die.
(Laughter) We and all our loved ones are going to die.
It's very important!
So depressing?
So depressing you had to finish?
Why didn't you end up singing "To Honor Life," too?
Then it was a bit more low, like Sandra.
(Applause) Sandra of my life, did you really have to finish singing "To Honor Life"?
You just told you gave a kidney in life, making us all feel like pussies because we are not so good people like you,
not even drunk I give anybody a kidney.
Really? End up with something more up!
"The meneaíto" or a Celia Cruz song.
A little more up!
And don't sing a capella, dear!
Do playback, don't make an effort, that you may tear a stitch.
(Laughter) Very depressing.
(Applause) There were a couple of tough ones not crying, did you see?
Then came Luciano Sposato to speak about the heart.
I don't know how he connected it, but he brought in the World Cup that we lost against Germany, then all the tough guys cried.
He reminded us of 2006, that we lost to Germany, last year we also lost to Germany --
All so depressing!
"Palacio! It was from below!"
(Laughter) They made it much easier for the guys of Aqualactica.
They said, "Don't put us in the same session with Inés, who talks about sexual assault because we want to make it clear that we are another kind of violins."
(Laughter) (Applause) After Inés we were all feeling low, very depressed, they had to bring someone to cheer us up.
They call in "La Pipetuá".
They turned it on, but they made a mess.
They threw snow, and when they finished the cleaning personnel were crying.
Because they had to fix the mess later on.
Dani Molina came next, got him?
If you don't remember someone
read about them in the thingy. They are like 59 speakers, look in the booklet, I won't explain each one.
Dani Molina came to tell us that truth is a lie.
Beautiful phrase: "the truth is a lie".
