When we wander around the streets Pachi spoke to us about today, we usually see people less fortunate than ourselves.
And we typically call these people "the poor."
I would like to take this opportunity to share with you today my personal exploration into what we can learn about these peoples' condition, and see if its possible -- (Phone rings) to learn something. (Laughter) Learn something about those people we call the poor, or if it's only possible to see those women we believe can't help themselves from having more children, or if we believe the people in the streets are there simply because they couldn't be bothered to keep walking, and they took their shoes off because they weren't very comfortable, and they're just lazy, or that the children we see begging for money in the streets just have lazy parents
who left them there. What do we think about the poor, or those people we call the poor, when we see them in the street?
I've purposefully chosen this image without a face, which is neither man nor woman, who is simply labelled as "poor." We are the ones who label them as poor and the problem with labeling them like this is that we simply forget everything else about them. They're "poor," and that's it.
We forget the fact they are people, they are parents, they are children and they are siblings.
We just see them as economic entities.
Even when we try to define poverty using the poverty index, the level of access they have to health care, when we talk about the minimum wages they receive. All of the indicators we have for poverty are material based.
We label them to a point that if we labelled someone else, for example, if we wanted to put a label on someone like... A label like.. (Laughter) A label like... Could you guys make these slides work, please? A label like "wife," right?
We imagine someone that fits this label. If we see a label with astronaut on it, we don't know if it's a woman or a man. Or "engineer," that's an even worthier label. But "poor" is a shameful label.
You can't say that someone is poor.
But we can say that someone is living in conditions of impoverishment.
I think it's important to understand that you can't BE poor. Instead, you live in conditions of poverty.
And these conditions have specific characteristics.
I would like to explore these characteristics with you today, the ones I call the threefold conditions of impoverishment. First, impoverishment has consequences on peoples' lives which can't be seen on the surface.
The first are financial limitations.
The second, an emergency condition.
We'll explore it more in a moment.
And the third, a condition of scorn. The condition of suffering
from financial limitations has a direct impact on someone's access to nourishment a sufficient amount of food of the appropriate quality.
I would now like to ask all of you to experience it for yourself. Imagine how it feels to be hungry.
And then what it is like to be hungry all the time.
And then think what it is like to make your children feel hungry.
That changes things.
And think about the consequences of malnutrition, not hunger, but instead the result of not eating and not feeding your children.
And what are the consequences on personal development of not having enough money to buy food?
The thing is... we don't see it
in the poverty-stricken, do we?
You might tell me that when you give one of the "poor" a coin, do you really go, "Well, they need to eat, or their hair won't grow?" Secondly... Let's see.
They don't have access to decent housing.
"So, not only do I not eat, but on top of that, I live in a house where the problem isn't a leak, it's that it has no roof."
"So I have a series of terrible health problems and I also share a 4 square meters of a room with four other people
which has horrific consequences for my health."
What's more, this city reaches both stifling and very cold temperatures.
And not only in this city.
And the problem gets more complicated.
Since I don't have any money, and it's not obvious, I have a bigger problem, as I don't have access to a decent healthcare system.
Of course, a lot of countries around the world have universal healthcare systems, but... If you don't have any money, things are way harder, much, much harder.
In addition, as I'm penniless, I don't get a good education.
And if you say that our system is top-notch, tell me... Why do so many of us send our kids to private schools?
And why don't they go to state schools?
Because if things really were
equal in our countries, we would send our kids to state schools.
But it seems there is a difference.
And that's why we send our kids to private schools.
And so if you're penniless, you also end up - because you have no money - getting a worse education.
These things add up, they multiply and they get more complex.
And if all of that weren't enough, and we were only talking about the financial limitations - there is no safety net for contingencies.
"On top of it all, you can't get sick, or that'll be the end of it!"
Which brings us to the second condition.
The emergency condition.
In the emergency condition, the person is still at the center of everything, but there's a problem - everything is connected.
"It's not just that I have to pay the bills, but that I have to do it today."
"It's not that I have no money for food, but that I don't have enough to eat today, nor did I have enough yesterday.
And it's not that I can't pay the hospital fees, it's that I have to pay them now..." OK, someone gets sick, and that emergency has now
totally gotten out of hand. "My family member got sick, and that put us in a lot of trouble."
And so the contingency crashes down and takes with it everything that it comes across.
And the slightest flu means... You wanted to fix a leaky roof, but now, you can't.
A flu wipes out three months' worth of food.
Mothers aren't allowed to get sick. If they do, their kids don't get to eat.
If the financial limitations weren't tragic enough, and the emergency condition didn't make everything so terrible, well, we've also got the third condition.
The third condition is that of scorn or stigma. Without a doubt, this is the most generalized form of discrimination.
There is no form of discrimination more widespread than being poor.
A sense of scorn comes over families, the family takes revenge, the poor dad leaves home and abandons his wife, not only because he's a bad father, but also because he needs to escape. He takes revenge on the circumstances, because he no longer wants to be poor, He doesn't want to be stuck with it anymore, he no longer wants to live with the constant limitations and in a perpetual state of emergency.
Grandparents don't want to look after their grandkids, because they already took care of you!
There are wonderful exceptions based on love.
But we don't see them when we stigmatize the poor.
That label which reads "they're just lazy"
rears up its head again. The scorn: "Don't offer work to a poor person." "Never give a poor person a job." "They're lazy, and that's why they can't get a job."
What's more, they're invisible.
We're in the city squares.
This happened to me recently in a public square, there was a sign which read: "No begging."
Wouldn't it be better if there were no begging?
That it weren't possible because the community would be given the means to make it inconceivable for someone to beg?
Instead of putting up a sign excluding the poor, those people we call "poor" in public spaces because we don't want to see them.
We don't see the poor begging in squares or outside temples any more.
We don't see them, as they're part of the background. Socially invisible.
They don't cause us pain anymore. We don't see poverty anymore.
And on top of that, public services pointed their finger at them before, as people they think are inferior. And our taxes are supposed to make our cities more equal.
So why are the poorer areas left unpaved and the richer areas paved?
I don't understand, aren't taxes supposed to stop that from happening?
But poor people don't seem to matter.
"Let them have access to worse services."
That comes from public transportation
and other organizations that foment inequality here. That's what they are like.
Yet, of course, we're fighting to keep our luxury taxis going.
The dialog in the media is about keeping premium taxi services.
That's what we're fighting about today.
But I can't hear any arguments over maintaining a respectable service.
And all those mobile phones,
belonging to those who can afford them, and who live off them, because they also have 16 jobs and their kids are everywhere, and as they're multi-purpose, with a gazillion side jobs.
Mobile phones, water and gas are more expensive for them than for me. And companies say: "Well, of course, they are riskier, they might steal the equipment, the tank might never re-appear, it's too far away, there's no plumbing there, it's a lot more expensive, so we'll charge them more."
This third condition generates a lot of stress.
And on top of people being stressed,
the three conditions always happen simultaneously.
There's no chance they won't happen simultaneously, ever.
There's more. Each condition intensifies the previous one.
And if we did want to find solutions to help and provide aid, mechanisms to eradicate poverty, we would need to provide them with all three conditions in mind because there's no point giving someone a coin while mocking them or giving money, but doing so in six years' time.
We always need to focus on the limitations, emergencies and the smiles, fair treatment, and dignity!
There is always one person in the center, everything revolves around that person.
Oddly enough, human happiness, as mentioned here before, doesn't depend on money nor time, nor on limitations, nor the rush there is to pay the electricity bill.
José Antonio Abreu, quoting Mother Teresa, said: "True poverty isn't the lack of bread, nor the lack of shelter, but it is the feeling of being nobody."
Being invisible, scorned, going unnoticed.
Also, Mother Teresa, in an interview with reporters, after leaving them all gobsmacked - After the interview, they asked, "Would you tell us how to live a more fulfilling life?" And she answered, "Smile. And I'm being utterly serious."
I'd like to tell you the same, let's be happy.
Let's make those around us happy with genuine care.
We need hardly anything more. Thank you so much.
(Applause)
I sometimes wonder if love is really over.
I see and hear everywhere that the relationships between genders are changing.
And quite fast, recently.
You hear that slogan: "It will fall, it will fall".
Will patriarchy really fall and take love away with it?
I think that it's rather alive, and causing a lot of mess in emotional relationships.
And then I wonder, is there a chance to rebuild anything?
Or love in the future will be nothing but a land of loneliness and cruelty?
A kid recently came to me with this story: "A while ago, I screwed up with my ex and she just publicly denounced me."
He didn't tell me what he meant by screwing up.
I had to figure it out myself,
I started thinking.
I started reviewing in my mind.
Did he force her to have sex when she didn't want to?
Did he take advantage of her being smoked or drunk at a party and forced her to have sex with other kids?
Was he violent with her during an argument?
Or did he just stand her up?
He just tells me that he knows that what he did is not right.
But he also tells me that now he knows, he didn't know it then.
He tells me that one day he saw her down and asked her what was wrong.
And she explained.
And it seemed to him that she was over blowing it.
That she didn't understand that "men are like that".
That she should be "a bit more smart and open".
But now he knows this, he didn't know by then.
"You know what happened then?
I didn't really think what my behavior could cause in other people.
And much less in women.
I did what came up or what I thought was right.
And now that I know, I want to understand: 'is there anything I can do to improve or my past condemns me?'"
I'm still surprised that we have finally reached a historical milestone in which men are beginning to question their privileges and the pain they can cause in others.
And a lot can be done with this.
There is much this kid can do to make it better in the situation.
"I suggest that you apologize, that you write to her, and tell her that you are no longer that person, that you changed and now you care about the pain you cause in others, so you try not to hurt as much as you can."
"What if she doesn't reply? O doesn't care to be in contact with me?"
"Don't insist, accept her no.
In that way, she will realize that you are being sincere.
And something will change between you and her."
There's something healing in meeting someone who can review their actions.
Apologizing doesn't imply rebuilding the relationship.
It only implies getting the pain of holding onto a grudge off your chest.
Because of my work, I talked with lots of women about this.
And women told me they expect men to apologize for the things men did to women.
That men take charge of their actions.
But most essentially, that they don't do it again.
And these things happen a lot, which doesn't mean they are right.
And men don't do them because they are ill-willed or bad.
They do it because they are healthy children of patriarchy.
And there's a cure for that.
It's the famous deconstruction of masculine hegemony.
Just like no kid is born a thief, no kid is born macho.
(Applause) You learn that.
And if you learn it, you can unlearn it.
We can teach kids from a young age, from kindergarten, to be different.
That being a man doesn't mean putting somebody at risk never again.
Women have won, and I include myself in this, equal rights also in sex and love.
This is something that women of my generation wished for but never really thought we would be able to accomplish.
All this made me think about relationships between subjects and power.
What do we do when we have more power than the other person?
For example, parents with their children.
Men with women.
Adult sons with elder parents.
Do we keep our aggression at check?
Or we do what we want because we can?
What happens when the situation flips?
For example, I was once a female victim, and now I have more power.
Or now that I grew up, I'm no longer a little girl or a little boy, a little kid. Do I eat up the cannibal and turn into one or do I have another option?
I think that talking about these things can help us to open up to a new conversation and hopefully, a new love story between genders.
The one-million question today is how do we rebuild in a non authoritarian way, and without any nostalgia for the past human relationships between genders.
Can we create a form of love far away from "Beauty and the Beast" and much closer to the women and men of today?
This that I share with you, also happens with teenagers in schools.
Recently, many cases of abuse have come up, many reports of abuse, and with it, many self-managed defense mechanisms called 'escraches'.
Last year I've been working in a secondary school because it had a lot of problems.
Authorities called us in because the hostile atmosphere was rampant.
The school had turned into a very unpleasant place to be. For everybody.
They asked us to work with troublemaking groups.
But we didn't do that,
we offered to work with everyone.
Why? Because troublemakers
are just the tip of the iceberg of a much more extended phenomena which is the current unrest in the relationship between genders.
And this is so because the modern sexual order blew up.
Therefore, we work with everybody.
We work with the students.
We work with the families, the teachers.
We work with the authorities.
We shared our vision on the subject with them.
But we also listened to what they had to say.
And in this conversation, listening to each other the idea that there was a way out came up.
And also the powerful idea that by listening to each other, in our differences, there was hope.
And in this exchange, the kids' creativity sprang.
There were not just troublemakers to control or watch anymore.
Part of their creativity was consent.
This means, to ask the other person if they want the same I do and accept their answer.
There's a myth that says that consent is a downer.
But it's not true, it's a very smart
self-managed invention.
And they didn't invent it out of nothing.
They took it from, for example, medical protocols.
Those forms we sign when we undergo an exam.
Or what we do, the ones who work with people, that we tell them what we will do with them and ask for their agreement.
They took it from these places and they made it available for the new forms of love, desire and sex, without hurt and abuse.
They ask in person, on WhatsApp or through any other app: "are you down with doing it?"
Consent today is like condoms in the 90s, in the middle of the HIV epidemic.
By then, they would also say it was a downer.
But that wasn't the case.
Thousand types of condoms came up.
In all colors, flavors and textures.
Slogans like "put it to him, put it on".

"Use it, don't be a dick." And many other different ways to keep on loving, desiring and having sex with this new friend, the condom.
Our new friend today is consent.
(Applause) It doesn't mean filling a form.
It can be playful, depending on personal taste.
But in all cases, it should be something that allows us to remain persons.
That we can say no, that we can say yes.
And it shows that where some people see the end of love others make up new ways to keep loving, desiring and having sex, bringing in the new, which is nothing else but this old adage that needs to be rephrased: "Don't do to others what you don't want others to do to you."
(Applause) How do we turn cruelty into tenderness in relationships between genders?
If we find the answer we can use it for other kinds of relationships in which a power struggle exist.
What if love is not over, and what is over is actually one form of love and one form of relationship between genders?
Where there's no love, there's cruelty.
So welcome to this new forms of love and desire so that equality and autonomy don't have sadness and loneliness as their counterparts.
(Applause)
When I was little my mother called me "lawyer for the poor" or "defender of hopeless causes."
She did this because I always stood up against injustice and I saw that nobody did anything.
Then, my father used to say: "Patty, don't worry because time will set things right."
I would calm down for a little while because I would imagine some kind of superior power that somehow would end that injustice, like in an 80s movie, with those really bad guys and those really good ones that would always win in the end.
Later on, studying for a French Revolution exam with my mother, I learnt another saying from her and that opened my eyes.
She said to me: "Darling, history is written by the victors."
I looked at my Social Studies textbook, that some of you might be familiar with, (Laughter) and I read all those information about early human settlements, the Greeks, the Romans - And I thought about those who were defeated, what their history was, and what would they think about those who wrote the history I was reading then.
Do you remember "The Adventures of Asterix" comics?
That invincible Gaulish village would not let Romans succeed in taking up their space.
So one character, Cacofonix,was the bard. A bard would tell the stories of our heroes, sing them.
Then when the adventure was over, everyone threw a big feast and end up shutting him up, because he was off pitch.
The figure of the bard belongs to the oral tradition, and they would do that, tell the history by singing songs about the battles.
These characters were to enhance those triumphs and we can guess how they would inflate the stories to keep their chiefs happy.
There were others who also told stories - though, in written form during the Middle Ages - the Monks from religious organizations.
If you have read "The Name of the Rose" by Umberto Eco, or watched the movie adaptation, you might remember those men at Abbey Library transcribing texts from one book to another and creating a compendium on the History of the World.
Now we are getting closer to the 18th century, when Diderot and D'Alembert, along with other French philosophers, created the Encyclopedia: a compendium of information in alphabetical order that gathered information about the History of the World.
They created this work that was not unique in characteristics, nor the first, however, this was the winner of the history, and became, in fact, the mother of all encyclopedias.
After those first encyclopedias there is a more recent one, that some of you might know, the "Big Larousse Encyclopedia", created by Pierre Larousse.
I am quite familiar with it, in fact I am very fond of it, because it's the one I had at home, the one I used for my middle and high school assignments.
In those very heavy ten volumes the truth about Universal History was condensed.
There were some supplements every other year, with updates about recent historical developments or modifications on the status of certain countries.
I don't recall anyone questioning the Larousse.
In fact, if we played Trivial Pursuit and there was any disagreement, the Larousse, with its heaviness, would clear out all doubts.
That's how we arrive to the 20th century, specifically to January 15, 2001, when Jimmy Wales creates Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia, open and collaborative that allowed us citizens to write the History for the first time and therefore break up the monopoly of the memory that had always been carried out by some elites, by few people.
Nowadays, Wikipedia is the most consulted online free encyclopedia in the world.
However, when I give workshops on the subject, some people approach to me and say: "Well, you cannot trust Wikipedia, because anyone can write it!"
And that's precisely its most distinguished feature compared to all the previous ones.
I also agree with it, I think you cannot trust Wikipedia, but I don't think that's the reason why. I think it's because we shouldn't trust any information we receive, not through the press, the radio, nor the television.
We should always keep a critical thinking.
Still, nobody questioned the Larousse, remember?
That was The Truth, with capital letters.
Since it was an encyclopedia in such heavy volumes, written by wise, old, white men, I guess it was somehow unquestionable.
There is something else, even if we don't trust it, funnily enough, when we go online, in any browser we use, the first result we get, if it exists, it's from Wikipedia.
So we don't trust it, but we take advantage of it.
As for Wikipedia being written by anybody, that's totally true, but the proportion of people editing Wikipedia compared to the number of users, of people who check it worldwide, it's tiny.
I'll give you an example: regarding Wikipedia in Spanish keep one thing in mind: we are 567 millions of Spanish speakers, and there were 4,500 active editors last year; eight editors per million of speakers, which is a very small proportion.
And there is something else, writing in Wikipedia is not writing History, adding data in first person, it's to document existing information out of the platform and organize it with a specific structure within this tool.
This is, I can know someone very well, but that doesn't mean I can write his or her biography better in Wikipedia.
I would like to make a break in this mega-fast review, that I've done about the storytellers of History, to see if there is anything that has drawn your attention. Is there anything missing out in this set of narrators of the History?
There are the bards, the scribes, the philosophers ... But where are women?
Where is their history and the history told by them?
Of course, there have been extraordinary women who were visible through history, but they were the exceptions who prove the rule.
We are a bit more than half of the population and still it seems like history is not ours, we don't tell it, we are not featured in it,
we only appear as procreators of the human species or as the seed of all kind of wars and arguments.
Women could not tell the history because their voices didn't count.
I have a theory that it might be because we were made from Adam's rib, then we tricked him into eating the forbidden fruit and then got kicked out from Paradise - it might be that. (Applause) According to historian Professor Mary Beard, "The Odyssey", the poem by Homer written in the 8th century BCE, is the first manifestation of men oppression of women's freedom of speech, starting with this example: "Mother, said Telemachus, go back to your room, to your sewings with the loom and the spindle.
The discourse will be a men's matter."
As we see, through different times in history, knowledge has been the exclusive domain of men.
But what about Wikipedia?
A tool born with the new century, a collaborative tool. What do you think happened with how men and women allocated their role in the narrative of history?
The 2000s meant the future for my generation, and in that future, besides imagining flying cars, of course, we thought there will be full equality between men and women. It's true that a lot has been achieved, but there are still salary gaps and glass ceilings, something that, maybe, when we approach a new tool like Wikipedia, you could think: Well, since it's a new tool, these things of the past shouldn't happen.
Well, I'm sorry to disappoint you, but from the total editors of Wikipedia, women represent less than 16 percent.
I couldn't believe my eyes when I saw that fact in Wikipedia. Do you think this could affect the content of Wikipedia?
Yes, I do think so too. I think that, in fact, it's pretty easy to find out, because there are much less women's biographies than men's, and for those that exist, gender perspective is not applied.
This means that sometimes, when talking about women, instead of inventors or creators, they are those who helped men in their inventions, researches, creations.
Also, in most women biographies their affective link is imperative, this is, we are mother of, daughter of, wife of, a fact that might be relevant biographically, but when referring to men, it's never that highlighted.
In 2015, I created a workgroup called Wikiesfera, because I was interested in finding out why there were so few people editing in a wiki format, which for me is absolutely revolutionary, and I found out two issues: The first one was of the technological kind, because two years ago, in order to edit Wikipedia, you needed to learn a tag language similar to HTML, and of course, that was not appealing for many people, it was a problem.
Fortunately this has changed, because there's a wonderful visual editor, similar to a text processor, and even though you need to learn some rules, it's very easy. So this issue doesn't exist anymore, and I don't take excuses in this sense.
The second issue was quite unexpected, and much more complicated to solve: people joining the group and other I talked to during workshops, didn't feel legitimated to edit Wikipedia, because, as they said, "Who am I to write the history?"
Obviously, if we learnt that history is told by others, and those are, in addition, old bearded men, changing our minds and acting on it is really a difficult change to make.
Do you remember what my father used to say about me being the defender of hopeless causes?
So, instead of feeling sorry about it, about the lack of empowered editors and biographies, I've decided to start up some activities, the "editatonas".
I will repeat the word, I know it sounds weird: "editatona".
The word "editatona" comes from "editatón", editathon, an editing marathon. This is, an event where we try to solve the deficiencies existing in Wikipedia about certain contents.
We owe the feminization of the term "editatona" to Carmen Alcázar, a mate from Wikipedia Mexico, who applied gender perspective to these meetings.
The editatonas that I organize also have some own features I created.
Around 30 people get together, mostly women, also some men, and create content in Wikipedia around a specific topic.
We have hosted editatonas about female photographers, explorers, scientists, programmers - We invite an expert woman to show us how are women depicted in that area, and then, in groups of two or three people, we gather information to document that biography and write it collaboratively.
We propose a list of women's names and distribute it.
Most of the people who come to the meetings have never edited before, so they go back home feeling empowered, feeling that they were able to create a biography that is now in Wikipedia, realizing that it's not as complicated as they expected to be.
Yes, there is a technique, but absolutely accessible.
There is something else, thanks to those meetings where I have to confess, we have a really good time, we have made visible women that were not in Wikipedia before.
For example, we have Ana María Prieto, the first Spanish programmer.
Alicia Calderón, a physicist from the team that detected the Higgs boson.
We also have the case of Judith Obaya, the first women pilot to cross autonomously Western Sahara by motorcycle.
Now they are all visible in Wikipedia at least.
When we create these biographies we face some problems, it's not all about having fun, because there is an issue: in order to create content Wikipedia, we need to document what is already published, this is, we need references from the academic field, press articles, information in books.
What happens with women?
Our lack of visibility is connected to our lack or public presence.
Then, even if we know there are women who have accomplished relevant things in history, if those elements to base the biography on don't exist, often we won't be able to recognize their contributions to history.
Let's not feel sorry or find culprits about why women are not at least 50 percent of the editors of Wikipedia. It's about becoming aware that we hold a responsibility and that hopefully we can share it with the other half of the population. In order to be the history of all humanity we women need to tell ourselves, tell the lives of those who stood out in a variety of fields, talk about them so we don't lose their memory.
If this happens to more than half of the population, imagine what happens to minorities and groups historically invisible.
What happens, for example, with cultures of oral tradition.
If for example we need those academic elements, information published in the media to base articles on, what should we do with the cultures that lack those elements?
What is not documented is in risk of getting lost, and that's all humanity's loss.
We are in a crucial moment, where the contributions of each of us, women, no matter how little they are, are going to change the future of history.
We have in our hands the possibility to create a collaborative narration of the history, where different points of view are represented and all voices are visible, listened to.
That's why we need to increase the number of female editors, to create more women biographies, to apply gender perspective to existing articles.
We need that mass media talk about women and their contributions to history. We need to establish all kind of relations with institutions. This is, we need us, because even if this sounds like a deeply radical idea now, no one will talk about us, women, if we are not in Wikipedia.
Thank you.
(Applause)
There is a brief tale, very short, by Jorge Luis Borges; I love it, and its title is, "On Exactitude in Science" -- one of my favorite books.
In this tale, Borges narrates the story of an empire where the art of cartography becomes so perfect, achieves such state of accuracy that they generate, for example, a map of a province that occupies the size of a city.
Then, they draw a second map of the whole empire which occupies the size of a province.
As this art was making progress, and it could be perfected even more, cartographers draw a third map which occupies, precisely, the size of the empire.
It's as big as the whole empire.
Human beings have always had an obsession to encompass the world or contain it, try to understand it through classification, through order, he was able to achieve a kind of "stack of wisdom."
This way, by stacking wisdom, he became an erudite of a world he actually doesn't know.
The question is if erudition is the same as wisdom,
if I can exclude the knowledge from that stack of wisdom,
if I can take it from that stack of wisdom.
Is erudition the same as wisdom?
The most emblematic phrase, the most famous we love is from "The Little Prince," "What is essential is invisible to the eye"; it's a beautiful phrase.
I don't know how many of us believe this is true,
we defend it as true,
we bet on this phrase as something possible that could exist.
From the intellect, is it possible to explain an ant?
Can I say, from our sense of reason, what a cloud is?
Can I explain, from science, from medicine, why people get sick?
Really, why does anyone get sick?
Can I, from science, from intellect, from reason tell what a building is?
I know a building is a cement rectangle, made of steel, with bases, with glasses, with tiles.
Is that a building?
Many years ago, I made a trip round the deep Patagonia, and in a lost village, very small, in the middle of the Patagonia, I met a boy in a bar.
I was drawing, and we started talking. The boy asked where I came from.
So, I told him I came from the city, and I told him how a city was like cars, traffic lights, buildings.
I explained it to him; of course, he had never seen a building.
Literally, 30 seconds after this, he went to his table, came back, and handed me this drawing. (Laughter) It's a building.
What's the degree of precision, of expansion, of depth, of rhetoric this picture could have, in contrast to the picture we saw before?
Which one is more accurate really?
Is it the copy of a building or the interpretation of a building?
Children have an inborn connection with subtlety.
I guess it's related to their short experience in this world. They haven't yet been exposed to this erudition that we, adults, generously give them, to adult teachings.
Children, as a result, are much closer to beauty
than to truth.
Is this a city, or is this a city?
Is interpretation more expansive?
Can it be more appreciated?
Does it offer any other clues to convince me and teach me what I am looking at, the mere fact of copying what I am looking at?
Even when faced with the most difficult questions, the intellect starts to fail.
Even with philosophy, many questions remained in a shortage of answers.
When I was a kid, in primary school - I don't know if it was in third or fourth grade, or so - we were talking about space, the planets, the Sun, the stars, the Moon, and a question came up; I didn't make the question, a partner of mine did it; it was about infinity.
Just that: What's the infinite?
The teacher answered in line with the pedagogical system, with the manuals, and the tools, she of course, had at that time,
and I remember I cried during a week because of this.
It was so overwhelming, it was so far from what I expected to hear and tried to understand, that I felt at a loss,
and it took a long time for me to recover from that.
Thirty years later, with my son Franco, we wrote this book, titled, "The real explanation."
In this book, what we try to do is give certain explanations from a place, from another sort of wisdom, from metaphor, from rhetoric, from that connection with subtlety that both children and artists have.
I say, for example, of the infinite, that it's the inside of a fat lady's skirt seen by a microbe.
(Laughter) I say of it that it's a nightmare to be in the white noise from the TV that I have to sweep with a toothpick.
I say of the infinite it's a white tapestry, gigantic, that hides the first grey hair of a black sheep; that it's two dust particles playing hide-and-seek in different planets; that it's a golden sunrise, beautiful, and a painter with a tube of grey oil painting; that it's the most starred night of them all but no one can see it because it's cloudy.
I say of the infinite it's an angel dreaming;
it's even an idea which doesn't want and refuses to be caged in a word.
Artists are the interpreters of subtlety; they are those in charge of interpreting this, which is intangible, invisible.
And, therefore, essential.
The artist molds.
It's the clay artists have to digest and give back in another way,
in a rhetorical way.
What is good about poetry, visual or written, either one, is that it's incapable of lying.
Poetry cannot lie.
It can distort, can exaggerate, can be elliptical but it cannot lie to us.
On the other hand, the truth is capable of, at least, venture a projection, - at least premature - of a reality that might not be fulfilled.
Truth usually keeps us prisoners.
Where do I get ideas from?
Where do I find the material to compose this poetry?
As a creator and his art, as a writer, as a musician, as an actor, from where, from what place can I go to look for this material?
I consider it's like a space of seduction with poetry.
I have to get that material, that word, that song, that image to understand that I have good intentions; that I have good intentions as a creator, as an artist.
If I make the material understand that my intentions are noble, that I'm honest, it'll approach on its own.
Sometimes I feel it like this: it's like a piece of music, barely audible, very distant, and it's halfway, mixed with a lot of noises, of horns, of truths, and I have to redirect my full attention to that music, and especially, to pay attention to the rhythm of that music; because rhythms are the first ones to come to you.
Once rhythms have come close enough, I can guess what the next beat, the next note is.
That music sounds familiar to me already.
Then, it becomes a song I've listened to before, so by now I've gotten to know it.
At times, I also think it this way when that poetry, that picture, that idea is covered by a thick forest, extremely thick; I can barely move,
and I have to get into that forest.
I have to walk very slowly, without moving anything, nor breaking anything, without making a noise; I must be cautious, so that this shy, fearful, and fragile animal that is poetry, approaches me to smell me and come close, and only then, I have it.
If I do otherwise, I lose it for good.
The artist must enter a special state.
They need to reflect on it, understand it, to venture into it.
Goethe referred to it as the "state of contemplation."
It's like a state where one can venture, be almost precognitive; it can foresee certain future.
The state of contemplation implies, for example, that one could stare a seed. Inside that small seed, set over on a table, were able to see a sprout too, and were able to see a twig, a leaf, a tree, and were able to see wood, wooden houses, furniture.
Everything starting from the seed.
This is the state of projection the artist should look for, that the artist should guess, that the artist is creating.
In this state, many times, what may happen is that every truth starts to get stuck in mud, they start to vanish, they subside into the mud, and, all that remains floating, the only bright thing that remains is beauty.
The artist needs to add a tune too, needs to tame it, to train certain types of tunes.
At this moment, you become permeable to things a person may not understand - as textures, shapes, sounds.
There are things you get used to.
You begin to train your senses.
These things may make you look like a weirdo, a little hermit sometimes.
By taking this tune and putting my attention in it, I'm rejecting many other tunes.
Many years ago, so many, I had to paint the portrait of Diego Maradona, - this one -, and I was looking for 'that' pepper.
So I went to the greengrocer's near my house, on 13, Av. Bustillo.
I went there, right to the vegetables isle and I approached it.
I was only looking at the vegetables; I get closer to the aisle, peppers, and start to look for that pepper.
This one could be, I look at the texture, look at the color; and leave it.
I pick another, I look at it closely, from a little distance; leave it.
And this is how I start to go through the peppers.
Peppers, peppers, peppers.
Next to me there was a man picking arugula. (Laughter) He was looking at me like, "OK."
I continue with the peppers.
Look for one, look for another one.
The man moves to the lettuce, and he was staring at me.
At one point, it must have been, I don't know, maybe ten minutes, - I really don't know how long, could have been ten minutes - I find the pepper.
It was like, it had such brightness, a special one. How come I didn't see it before?
It was hidden behind another one.
So, with great care, I picked the pepper with both hands, I held it close to me, looked at it in ecstasy (Laughter) rub it onto my sweatshirt to clean it and, with extreme care, I take a bag of the size of the pepper, I grab the pepper from the end and close the bag with such love. And the man was next to me, looking at me. (Laughter)
I was about to leave, when this man comes close, stares at me, gazes with sadness, he puts a hand on my shoulder and says, "Your wife is a nagger; worse than mine."
(Laughter) (Applause) Weird moments.
This is an everyday thing in my world; it's something that happens all the time.
It seems as if you are living in a universe, a world, a constellation that wasn't created for you; it was created from somewhere else, it was created from erudition, from hard, objective, and rational wisdom.
Even so, there were people, beautiful beings, who were able to switch roles.
Julio Verne, for example, managed to change the course of history.
He, from his imagination, from his prose, from his stories, from his intelligence, but, above all, from the deep subtlety of his wisdom, created written worlds, visual worlds, which have later been emulated by truth.
Poetry and art were the first activities to deeply believe in me.
Franz Kafka, Alejandra Pizarnik, Pablo Picasso, Oscar Wilde, Quino, Fontanarrosa, María Elena Walsh, Julio Cortázar, Jorge Luis Borges were the first who considered me intelligent and capable of adopting a certain attitude to give back, the same way as they did; this subtlety to the world.
The artist, nowadays, is facing a break point too, in which this constant transgression that fights against dogmatic truth, can be even pernicious, can be painful, hurtful.
In Borges's tale, the story ends with the map, that third map abandoned in the middle of the desert, destroyed, totally eroded, lost, and deeply forgotten.
The artists and the journey they present has no compass; compasses are useless, and they don't work.
Cartography is useless too.
You can't go with a map, it doesn't work; they'll get lost.
It's the same.
It's worked well for me.
I'm satisfied, and I think, or imagine, it was because a long time ago, I listened to that boy living within me, and who implores me every day to, and has convinced me to believe more in beauty than in truth.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
"How do worms breath under the ground?"
That's what one of my five-year-old twins asked me when we were playing in the backyard and all of a sudden a worm stuck out from one of the flowerbeds.
Kids ask questions like these all the time and, in general, what happens?
They pass by us.
If we just happen to know the answer, what do we usually do?
We tell it to them, right? And that's it.
But, generally,
assuming we don't know it, what do we tend to do?
We tell them: "Well, go look it up."
Or "I don't know." Or we
move on to something else.
But I think that in those curious questions kids have there's a huge opportunity we can take advantage of.
For almost 20 years, I've been working in education.
I work with schools, teachers, principals, trying to make the most of what happens in the classroom to shape kids to be curious, critical thinkers, and ready for life.
And for the past five years I've also been a mother and in these years of talks with friends and acquaintances who are also parents, they all ask me the same question: "What school should I send my kids to?"
And, of course, choosing a school is a very important issue.
We could talk about that for a while.
But I believe that in that concern for children's education so focused in school, there's something bigger we're forgetting in the outside, something that's usually right under our noses without us realizing it: how do we educate them at home?
And, with education at home I don't mean teaching them to behave properly at the table, say thank you to grandma, or share with their brothers, no.
I'm not talking about taking kids off the school either and educate them ourselves, of course not.
I'm talking about something else, something much bigger, much deeper.
It's about how the link between kids and knowledge is built, how their love of learning is shaped.
Because that's what can make all the difference in their lives.
Because it's going to give them the tools but, above all, the desire and the commitment to keep learning forever.
And that's built at home, since kids are very young, from the first years of life until they keep growing.
And it's built on a daily basis, in the interactions with their parents and also with their brothers, uncles, grandparents, and even sometimes, family friends.
But how do we awaken that love for learning at home?
What can we do?
Do we have to stop working and be all day with the kids?
Or do we make sophisticated tools so that after a long day at work, we come home exhausted and have even more things to solve?
Really, it's not very clear how it can be done.
But there's good news: there are some well-tested ideas that come from the field of education, backed by decades of academic research, that I believe can give us some good clues to guide us.
And the best thing is that they're easy to bring home, to things we do with kids all the time.
I've been researching these topics and today I want to tell you something I found thinking of the three key interactions we have with kids: How do we answer their questions? How do we talk with them?
And how do we praise them when they do something right?
Let's go with the first of the questions: How do we answer kids' questions?
For some time we've known that in order to awaken and, above all, to sustain their willingness to learn we need to generate the habit of deep learning.
What is deep learning?
As we learn about a topic, we go unraveling it, slowly, seeing how it connects with other topics, how it connects with our life, seeing it from different points of view.
And I say that helps sustain the willingness to learn because, as we become experts in something, as we know something well, we gain confidence in our own abilities as learners.
And that helps us keep going.
Here's the secret: the curious questions of kids are a unique starting point to generate that deep learning.
I'll tell you how the worm episode continued.
We used the question to do an exploration that lasted for a while.
First, what did we do?
We took the worms out of the ground, played with them for a bit, looked at them.
At first, they didn't want to grab them, but -- They get a bit grossed out, right?
Then, obviously, after a while they didn't want to put them back.
We started looking up information about worms.
How do worms breath under the ground?
How do they eat under the ground?
We found the most interesting things.
For example, worms have ten hearts and we only have one, an important fact.
Or, worm's poop is great for plants.
The jewels of our findings were some videos of many worms coming out from inside the same cocoon, which we saw dozens of times.
Here's the drawing they made later.
There's the cocoon, the worms coming out.
The big one on the side is the mother.
Part of the fiction, right?
Because mum worms don't even care a bit for their offspring but it felt strange for me to be a illusion-shatterer, so I didn't say anything.
Afterwards, one of my kids came up with: "What's the speed of a worm?
How fast can it go?"
And we were about to look it up on the Internet.
Because you know you can find everything on the Internet, right?
You type "speed of a worm" and something shows up.
But we said: "What if we
measure it ourselves?"
How do you measure the speed of something?
We decided to make a race course for worms and make them run.
Being honest, the race didn't really work out. because the worms didn't care about it and wouldn't even move in the same direction.
What's more, the course was dry, so the worms weren't slipping much, so my kids kept trying to put in some drops of water on each worm so they'd slip better and also pushed theirs a bit so it'd reach the goal first.
This is the drawing of our course.
So frankly, those eleven minutes the winning worm took to reach the goal aren't too reliable, so as to say.
But those eleven minutes of being with the timer on, cheering on the worm to make it, were unforgettable.
Those are the things that we thought up to do with the worms at that moment.
They could've been others, sure.
Because what's important isn't what we do with kids in particular, but rather to unravel those topics that interest us to see where they take us.
As an adventure, as a shared game.
And every topic is valid, even if we don't know much about it.
Because it's not about learning, but rather to pass along the love for learning.
And to do that you don't need to know it all.
I just happened to know some facts about worms, not the hearts thing, but something.
But for example, on other days, my kids came up with a bunch of question on the Independence Wars.
A topic I studied --
you all have --
a thousands times as a student, and I never learned much. In fact, I even found it quite boring.
And learning with them was even better.
Because it let me dust off a topic that in my head was full of cobwebs, forgotten, and reconnect with my own curiosity.
To me, that's one of the biggest drivers that makes me want to do this sort of things with my kids.
And the other big driver is to see them learn.
I believe there's few things as exciting as seeing a girl or a boy build their own way of thinking, their own vision of the world.
And in this process comes in the second key: conversation.
For a while, we've also known that to build the way of thinking the great driver is language.
And that's why it's so important that, when we talk to kids, we help them put into words what they're thinking, and why they think it.
And this is important, not only for us, to measure the temperature of what they're understanding and not, and help them starting from there.
But especially for them.
Because in order to gain autonomy, to learn how to learn, kids need to progressively become aware of what they have and haven't understood yet.
And how what they're learning is connected to what they knew beforehand.
This is useful for all conversations, but a good moment to do this with kids is, for example, when we read with them.
At home, when we read books with the kids, we can simply stop every once in a while, to chat about the story, about how the characters in each part may have felt, to talk about whether we would've felt the same way in the same situation.
We think of other possible endings.
For example, what would've happened if Cinderella had left the ball at half past twelve?
Or we think if they'd recommend this book to other kids.
What would they tell them to make them want to read it?
Or, for example, we try to see who wrote these books.
Had we read another book from that author before?
Was it similar in any way to the one we're reading?
Or we check who the books are dedicated to.
For example, once a book said: "To Toto, my faithful partner of adventures."
Who would Toto be?
Their dog, brother, best friend?
And we also do this so kids realize that books, or any other work of art, like movies, or plays, paintings, or inventions, aren't made by superheroes, but by flesh-and-bone people like them.
Learning can be beautiful, but also very frustrating, it demands a lot of effort.
Because sooner or later, we'll stumble upon a situation that we find hard, something that isn't too easy and we'll have to face it.
That's why we need to stay motivated.
Here's when the third and last key comes in: praises.
And I love this one because I think it's the easiest one to implement but it's the one we do the other way round.
What do us mums, dads, let alone grandparents do when kids do something right?
We praise them, right?
With the best intentions, we want them to gain confidence, to be sure of themselves, motivated.
And we say things like: "Great, you're so smart!"
Or: "You're such a genius!"
But research shows not all compliments help.
In fact, even some could be counter-productive.
Now we know that when we praise kids for their talent, for their intelligence, kids start choosing easier tasks that don't challenge them as much.
They go for what's safe.
On one hand, because they don't want to let us down, but also because they don't want let down themselves, the image they're building of themselves.
Luckily, the opposite happens when we praise their effort.
For example, if we tell them:
"Great! It shows you worked hard."
Or: "You practiced so much!"
Kids keep choosing things that defy them more and more.
Because we help them feel in control of the process.
One can't control how talented they are at doing something, but one can control how much effort they put into it.
At home, I became a bit too much of a fan of these praises for effort and my kids already found out.
So when they do something right they usually look at me and say: "I tried really hard, didn't I mommy?"
(Laughter) If we think about it, awakening the love for learning isn't too different from being hunters waiting for that moment of opportunity when we can learn with our kids.
And I'm not saying it's easy or that we can do it all the time.
But I think that if we do it at least sometimes, we can make an enormous difference.
And I realized that generates touching, wonderful moments when we're together as a family that are by far my most favorite moments of all these years, and that, hopefully, they'll remember when they grow up and they think about their childhood.
That's why, the next time someone asks me "What school should I send my kids to?"
I think I'll have the answer.
I'll tell them the fundamental question isn't that one, but another one.
I'll tell them the question is: "What do you answer to your kids when they ask you how a worm breathes under the ground?"
Thank you!
(Applause)
When there is a critical moment in our lives, we look ahead and we look back.
We look ahead to gage what we are up
against: How are we going to face it?
We look backwards to assess how we have lived until now
and how this past helps us live our future.
My critical moment is now.
A few months ago my husband died, my partner for 60 years.
I did not think I would ﬁnd widowhood so difﬁcult.
True, the roots of two trees intertwine when they have grown together for so many years. But that was not the problem.
I think what happened has another, different metaphor:
We were side by side on a diving board,
and when I found myself alone, without him, I was left without the impulse, without the thrust, the synchronous vibration that helped launch us into the world of projects and adventure.
I look backwards and I see my whole life, very long and complete.
I look forwards and see the need to ﬁnish, to close off, button up.
This is what I want to talk to you about:
the meaning and importance of closing.
I will do so using as examples some projects from my life.
In October 1957,
from the rooftop of my apartment house in New York, I watched in the sky, once and again,
the ﬁrst artiﬁcial satellite, Sputnik, that had just been put in space by the Russians.
It passed overhead every 90 minutes.
I had just arrived in the States
from Argentina, newly graduated from the University of Buenos Aires, to study at Columbia University.
My husband-to-be -- whom I did not yet know -- was working against the clock in the development of a maser -- a quantum ampliﬁer, that would go in the ﬁrst US artiﬁcial satellite.
This satellite was launched in January, too late to win the race for space: The Soviet Union had successfully put Sputnik in orbit three months earlier.
Sputnik unleashed a whirlwind in the US: What was wrong with science in the US if the Soviets had won the race to space?
With great speed funds were approved by Congress for the best minds in the country to undertake a radical reform of science education:
Young people should receive better training so the US could beat Russia.
The educational reform became a huge project of the entire country for a decade
and it marked my professional career.
Traditional science education -- this is how I was taught-- presented science as a set of laws -- the laws of Newton, for example -- that had been handed down to us much the way God handed the tables of the law to Moses.
By contrast, the multitude of post-Sputnik projects sought to present science as a process, similar to the one that scientists engage in when they work, by researching and discovering.
The idea was that students should carry out small scientiﬁc projects: asking questions, looking for explanations, formulating hypotheses and seeing how they can be conﬁrmed --or not.
You have a ﬁstful of sodium bicarbonate; you pour some vinegar on it;
bubbles are produced;
what are those bubbles?
Can I trap them in a container?
And if I do, what happens if I light a match?
The ﬂame grows bigger, goes out, stays the same?
Can I give a name to this gas?
My role in this post-Sputnik effort was to develop courses for prospective teachers in which science was taught through activities such as the one I just described.
This went hand-in-hand with research on students’ ideas and how to help them evolve and learn.
This portion of my life is way behind me.
If I think about how these projects were closed, I see it was a matter of having trained people, built a following and passed on the torch.
Because the basic philosophy, activities, the results of the research, all of it continues alive in the professional tasks of young colleagues.
Some time later it became clear that this wave of reform in science education was gathering momentum and propagating out of the classrooms
onto zoos, summer camps, museums.
I surfed the wave into an interactive museum that we founded in the 1980s in California.
An interactive museum is a place where the exhibits are not valuable objects but rather, interesting phenomena presented as activities for the visitor to explore.
What is valuable is the phenomenon.
Interactive museums exist now all over. But at the time, in the early 1980s, we were inventing them from scratch.
In the museum we developed several exhibitions that ended up traveling throughout North America, Europe and beyond.
Each exhibition was a completed project with the speciﬁc characteristics of a project.
Namely: You begin with an idea followed by an overﬂow of ideas. Some of them lead nowhere, others blossom, some follow unexpected paths, others appear by chance.
The project grows, advances, branches out.
But at a certain moment the money runs out; or the time.
This costs too much, we drop it;
this takes too long, we put it aside.
And so you let go, button up, close off until the show ﬁnally opens.
Closing this kind of project means achieving a ﬁnal product that is launched into the world:
a show that opens, a book that is published, a child that is born. A TED talk that is given.
(Laughter) Looking at my life, I realized that if I scratch a little here, I discover a project.
And if I scratch there, I discover another project --
maybe big, maybe small,
but something that opened up, blossomed and closed.
I have this vision of my life as a large tree -- a pine perhaps -- that gives off branches and the branches give off needles and it keeps growing,
the whole thing grows and opens up and tops off at the crown, harmoniously.
Everything in the tree is similar, the detail and the tree itself:
The pine tree is a large project that opens up and closes off, made up of an inﬁnity of small projects.
So then, if my life is a large project of projects, as it opened up, so also must it close off.
Which means that in my octogenarian life it is no longer the time to open up new projects but to close off those that are still open.
I’m in the midst of projects that round off lives that are dear to me.
For example, we are preparing a book on the archeological mission that my father led in the 1960s to the area of the world that was Ancient Egypt.
From those excavations he brought to Argentina pieces that are on display in the Egyptian wing of the Natural History Museum of La Plata.
That exhibition hall is now open to the public.
But I need to do an illustrated book about my father leading that archeological mission in order to close off my part in this project.
So: Why is it so important to close off?
If we do not bring projects to a close, the ideas get lost.
We bring them to a close in order to give ideas the necessary form that enables them to survive.
Not to bury and forget.
The Egyptians themselves, when they mummiﬁed and buried it was, precisely, to enable the dead to survive in the world beyond.
Seen in this light, closing off is an act of liberation.
Returning to the image of the pine tree, the large project made up of projects: With a bit of luck it sends off seeds that fall on fertile ground and breed a forest of pines.
That would be immortality.
But for me, what is truly important is my life, my pine.
To be able to look at a structure that is harmonious and coherent;
to be able to say: "I close my life
and my life makes sense." To close a life and end up with a life that makes sense is a lot of work!
But it is work that is worthwhile because it gives meaning to living day by day.
(Applause)
If you've ever believed, in times of sickness, in the idea of the white-coated wizard, always impeccably up to date about all the diagnostics and their treatments, today, I'd like to invite you to see him more critically.
On one hand, the population has grown older which is why we have more and more patients.
On the other, technology grows exponentially, which is why we are generating a sort of inflation of science in which we produce more knowledge than we'll ever be able to absorb.
Medical information doubles every five years.
We know today that physicians have between 4 and 6 doubts per week, which is a lot.
Unlike past times, we don't have time to keep up.
This is the new problem that we are facing.
What this means is that when you visit the doctor the attention you will get depends on your luck.
Your destiny is in the hands of the health care professional who bestows it on you in that moment, whether or not he is up to date about your specific condition.
This phenomenon is referred to in the medical field as variability.
And I assure you, as patients, this is your worst enemy.
Now, the good news: The good news is that every day, specialists write down great amounts of data on their clinical histories that reflect their thought processes when facing their patient's problems in conditions of real uncertainty.
They are like the secret sauce of medical reasoning.
Valuable information that can't be found in books.
This is why engineers, accountants and doctors start to question if it would be useful to re-use the data already there to solve the problem of disinformation.
We wonder if we could do something similar to what jurisprudence does in the world of law: using what has been learned in previous cases to face present cases in real time.
Generate a system that supports medical decision, based on previous collective experience, a system capable of augmenting the human mind's capacity still necessary, of course, through the power of computing.
And luckily, today we have the technology needed to do so.
We have digital clinical histories, - since we've bidden adieu to paper - that are all compatible with each other.
We have powerful search motors
and technology for the interpretation of natural language Artificial Intelligence.
By combining all these, any doctor from anywhere in the world will be able to experience something similar to being in a room with the top specialists in any field and will be able to ask for second opinions about a specific case.
Very soon, it will be routine for doctors to consult with A.I.
because in order to get there we don't need perfect computers, we just need computers good enough to reduce the mistakes we make today,
nothing more.
This isn't just an idea, it's not just a project.
What I'm telling you is real.
We are currently developing the algorithm that will soon make it possible and we have the enthusiastic support of several autonomous communities which is why patients will soon benefit from A.I.
I want to tell you that our objective is to give all patients, no matter where they are or who they are, access to the best medical knowledge available.
I want to you to know this isn't just a small step. It's a disruptive act toward democratizing the clinical situation that will completely change the way we understand medical care.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Do you know what Aristotle believed?
Aristotle said that we think with our hearts and the brain only cools the blood coming from our hearts after thinking.
Doesn't it sound outrageous that one of the most brilliant minds in human history had paralleled the human brain to a radiator?
I think we all agree that this time Aristotle was wrong.
For about 10 years, I've been studying the heart-brain relationship.
Basically, the way in which the heart induces neurological disease, like cerebral vascular accidents, and how the brain induces heart disease, like arrhythmias.
And through this research I came across many surprises.
That belief that we only think with our brain and that the heart only pumps blood, is not that accurate.
For example, how many times you were told to "decide with your heart"?
What does this pump of pure muscle have to do with decision-making?
Or maybe with the intuition, which sometimes we need to make decisions?
There is a study by Cambridge University that revolutionized the subject on deciding with our hearts.
And opened the path to what we call: "research in decision-making."
In this study, subjects were asked to participate in a game in which they had to make decisions based on simulated situations on a computer.
There were always two options: one with a favorable outcome, the other with a unfavorable outcome.
While participating in the game, they had their heart beats monitored in order to know how the heart reacted just before making a decision.
And what they found was that before making a decision that would have a favorable outcome, the heart beat in a particular way.
But when they were going to make a unfavorable decision, it beat in a totally different way.
So, apparently, the heart knew before the brain was conscious, if the decision about to be taken was going to be favorable or unfavorable.
I ask you to keep this in mind and we'll make an exercise ourselves, we'll experiment with our bodies.
Please, close your eyes.
Close your eyes and keep quiet, and put your hands over your knees, until I tell you so.
While doing this test, I'd like to know if you can sense your heart beats.
OK, please open your eyes.
Please, raise your hands those of you who were able to sense your heart beats.
Perfect, approximately 50 percent.
Among those who raised their hands probably many of them have developed an ability called interoception.
What is the interoception?
It's the capacity of sensing the messages produced by our bodies, sent by our bodies.
For example, in this case, our heart beats.
Back to the experiment carried out at Cambridge University, those participating in the game had done what you have and were classified in those with good interoception, and those with bad interoception.
The most interesting fact in this experiment was that those with good interoception made favorable decisions more frequently than those without good interoception. The conclusion of this seems to be that those people able to detect, decode and process messages sent by our hearts before making a decision, apparently can make better decisions.
Like in any other revolutionary experiment like this one, it's necessary to check results with other scientists making similar studies.
But, meanwhile, don't you think that sayings like: "listen to your heart" or "I have gut feeling" don't sound so wrong when deciding?
But how do brain and heart collaborate in such manner when making a decision?
There's a specific area inside the brain, the main subject of our research, called insula.
It's called insula because it resembles an island underneath the cortex, and is hidden, and extremely connected to the whole brain.
It resembles a local airport where all connections arrive to and depart from. But the insula is also connected to other organs like the heart or the bowel, among others, and so, it is also an international airport where all connections to other organs besides the brain come to and depart from.
Most interestingly about the insula is that it's made purely of neurons, and the heart is pure muscle.
So you may say: How can these two organs communicate being so different?
The answer is quite surprising because the heart, like the brain, has neurons.
Those white spots you see there in the heart are neurons, group of neurons, where connections from the brain arrive to.
And this you are seeing is a typical heart; the heart's anatomy.
And what happened throughout human history for a heart like that to become the universal symbol of love?
What does the heart have to do with loving or falling in love?
In other study, subjects were asked to bring pictures of someone they loved deeply, and somebody who didn't mean anything to them.
They used a special kind of magnetic resonance imaging, called "functional," that let us see which parts of the brain are being activated after certain stimuli, or certain actions.
What came out from this experiment is that when subjects saw the picture of their loved ones, areas related with pleasure and reward were activated.
And not by chance, one of those areas was indeed the insula.
The insula seems to be related, along with other areas of the brain, with functions that allow us to be aware that we're in love.
It allows us to say, "This is the person I am in love with."
And also because of its connections with the brain, with the heart and with the bowel, makes it possible that when we are with our beloved we have tachycardia, palpitations, and that feeling of having butterflies in our stomach, when we are in love.
So, we fall in love with our brains, and not with our hearts.
This level of interconnection with our brain and also the insula, has many functional repercussions, but it also may result in health problems.
For example, I would like to show you this graphic: In the horizontal axis we have the months of May, June and July.
And in the vertical axis, the number of heart attacks in Munich.
In 2003, the number of heart attacks stayed constant, there were no changes.
In 2005, something similar happened.
In 2006, during May everything stayed the same; during June and July there was a sudden rise in the number of heart attacks.
What happened?
OK, many of you already noticed: the 2006 World Soccer Cup.
(Laughter) And each match played by Germany, is represented by each peak you see in this graphic.
Most interesting is that peak number 5, is the match for quarter finals between Germany and Argentina which final result I'd rather not mention.
(Laughter) This match was being watched by a friend called Hans at his house.
Hans was sitting in front of the TV, and saw the first and the second half which ended even and all the definition by penalty.
Germany was winning 4 to 2, the last penalty pending, Cambiasso prepares to kick and Lehmann is at the soccer goal.
Cambiasso kicks, Lehmann gets it, Germany classifies, and Hans, a extremely passionate fan, (Laughter) screams like ever, releases all his energy and adrenaline accumulated during those 90 minutes plus penalties, and after finishing screaming ends up in hospital with a heart attack.
Like many other Germans that day when the number of heart attacks in Munich tripled.
Hans is a living example of how some emotions affecting our brain result in cardiac problems.
The stress suffered by Hans, that adrenaline overdose compromised his heart and resulted in a heart attack.
So, here we ask ourselves, do we have to give up and accept that we are exposed to what this complex brain-heart system exposes us to, regarding our emotions?
Or is there something we can do?
Science is opening many doors, one of them to ancient techniques.
Techniques that albeit having thousands and thousands of years, have only been explored serious and intensively during the last years.
One of them is "mindfulness."
Mindfulness is somewhat a form of meditation that allows us to concentrate in what's going on around, in physical sensations, emotions, and in messages from our bodies.
Does this "messages from our bodies"
ring a bell to you? Interoception?
When I heard about mindfulness 10 or 12 years ago, I was absolutely skeptical because, sure enough, there was no scientific evidence, none.
And as we tend to think with scientific and medical minds, if is not proven, it's useless.
So look at what happened during the last 10 years.
From 2005 to 2015: Scientific publications which have the word "mindfulness" in their title.
An important increase and most of them, the most important part of the publications you're seeing, is related to how useful mindfulness can be to alleviate things like stress.
I know you are skeptical and have a curious mind and you won't believe anything I say.
So let's pretend you're scientists, and I ask you to think which stressful situation we may use to prove whether mindfulness is useful in fighting stress or not.
Which is the worst-case scenario?
War.
In California, there is a center where Marines are trained before being sent to combat.
It's the last training when a drill is carried out, most stressful, where Marines are faced with soldiers resembling those they will confront.
Noises are the same, smells are the same, and said by they themselves, it's the most stressful moment before going to war.
In this training center, half the Marines were offered to do mindfulness during eight weeks.
The rest had usual training.
They did mindfulness training, and went through this terrible drill, and afterwards, they completed a questionnaire assessing how stressed they were.
Interestingly, those who underwent mindfulness training, were much less stressed out,
than those who didn't. You may say, "Of course, they were biased, as they did mindfulness, they were bound to say they were less stressed."
But they were also measured a protein in their blood released by the brain in extremely stressful situations.
And those who had done mindfulness had much lower levels than those who had not.
So, there's an objective and a subjective demonstration that there is something we can do to diminish stress.
And there are plenty of experiments like this one, portrayed in this graphic.
Thus, it seems that thanks to practices existing for a long time, and which science is proving nowadays, we may have the possibility of decreasing the impact some emotions have on our health and on our heart-brain system.
Maybe in the future, science will tell, even diseases like stroke and heart attacks may be prevented by these kind of practices.
For now, I think we all have to acknowledge the genius of Aristotle, the first to think that heart and brain act as a system.
And I think it is good to remember the words by a wise man who said, "A good head and a good heart are always a formidable combination."
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
I'm the founder of an environmental engineering company specialized in water treatment.
I have also taken part in some artistic initiatives.
In both fields, the question was always the same
What do we do to keep on growing?
And the answer was basically: regionalizing.
And yes, it obviously makes sense.
A market with a population of 6 million, like Nicaragua, is not the same as a market of 45 million inhabitants, like the entire region.
Besides, these are not long distances.
From Guatemala City to Panama City by road it is only 1890 km.
That is more or less the same distance between New York and Miami.
There are no language barriers among us, there are no cultural barriers, and everything should flow perfectly well.
I wonder, as a businessman, how many waste water treatment plants I could create in the area that makes up these six countries.
Nicaragua alone generates 7% of the region's GDP.
That implies the potential for growth is enormous.
And regarding the music band...
I could do a really good tour in 6 capitals and some 15 important cities.
"Then let's regionalize!" we said.
And we sat there staring at each other.
"Ehm, do you know any rock bands in Honduras who could be our contacts?"
"Well, frankly, I don't know much about Honduras, except that they say it's more dangerous than Iraq during a bombing."
"I have a friend in Panama!"
That could help us.
The problem is that by bus it's 3 days to get there, and by plane it costs about $700 a head.
Hmm.
No wonder in Central America we have the second-most profitable airline in the world.
"Well, then!"
"Let's go in a shipping container!"
Not such a good idea, either.
To give you a little taste of what that would be like:
Transporting a container from China to Guatemala, which is approximately 13,264 km, costs approximately 1400 dollars.
How much is it to move a container from Guatemala City to Panama City?
Guess!
Approximately $1400.
Over a distance which is 10 times shorter.
How can we be competitive that way?
And watch the borders, which are as slow by container as any other way.
What I'm discovering here is that we are profoundly fragmented.
I think about all the latest projects
of separating Central America from one side to another -- waterways and land routes.
Frankly, the idea of a project that unites us all appeals to me most.
Let's dream for a moment of a Trans Central American train from Guatemala to Panama in one day.
(Applause) But, waking up, I discover there are two contradictory forces here.
A force that calls for the erasure of borders in the name of efficiency.
That's the force of the market.
Nobody can be competitive with a container stuck at the border 3 days.
In contrast is the force of politics.
Politics is the business of generating confrontation,
of saying "us" and "them".
These forces reach an equilibrium in which large corporations work under efficient regional models.
And our six bureaucracies keep their salaries and sell us the idea that without the borders they protect we would live in even more danger and poverty.
An illegal migratory flow invigorates economies through exploitation,
and media sensationalism contributes to the establishment of this "state of fear" which helps to maintain the status quo.
So, we, young entrepreneurs, leaders of small businesses, lose the opportunity to think in a form that is not fragmented.
That form which they taught us in our school books.
How to accelerate development is one of the themes of this conference.
I have a proposal to accelerate development.
Let's become Central Americans!
(Applause)
Calm down, calm down, don't start a panic, I'm not talking of renouncing our nationalities or burning our passports.
I'm talking about a mental integration of Central America.
Of seeing ourselves as a region, without reservations or prejudices.
And in that line of thinking I think that entrepreneurs can play a profound role.
We can consolidate what I call five areas of entrepreneurship for massive integration: education, culture, entertainment, communications, transport.
So you can see I'm serious, I'll give an example of each of these areas.
Education: a children's TV program, that is educative and regional.
We can't give our children this legacy of a fragmented vision.
We are improving their opportunities if we make them feel a part of a region.
In the matter of culture, I think, a radio program.
When we've traveled with our band to other Central American countries, no one knew us.
But what if there were a program that aired in each of the countries of Central America where they only played offerings from our countries?
So, little by little, in each Central American country we'd know what our neighbors were producing.
And we would end this isolation of media and culture which I think has done us much harm.
In entertainment, I have an idea
for a game show: "The Isthmus Prize"
Game shows work.
I've heard of one that's been on air for about 50 years and the man never gets old, he just stays and stays.
Why can't we make a type of game show where a Costa Rican teams up with a Nicaraguan?
Imagine how beautiful in practice if we start to realize that we are a team.
Ending those antagonisms that I see, that the politicians ferment so much to close the ranks between our countries and go about creating chaos.
In telecommunications, I propose something radical.
"No more roaming!"
It's absurd!
The whole region is half the size of Columbia.
Local prices for the region.
Let's open our minds, our borders, our communications.
We're in the 21st century.
Let's not drag with us those archaic divisions.
And in transport, the Trans Central American train.
I never get tired of dreaming that dream.
I believe that these five endeavors that I've just mentioned are not charities.
They're perfectly profitable initiatives.
We're talking about a big market, in contrast to the segmented market of separate countries.
The potential for patrons is much greater.
For tours, for publicity campaigns, for companies.
And I believe that each of us can start immediately -- this very day -- to cultivate a regional identity.
And here I want to propose 4 actions.
Four habits of the Central American.
Number 1: Study Central America.
We have to be informed, we have to know what's happening in the region.
To know what our neighbors are like.
To know what is on the other side of the border.
[2.] Talk about Central America.
What we learned in part one, share it.
Let's bring it to the table.
Third: let's travel in Central America.
Don't go cowardly on me.
I know it sounds dangerous.
"Oh, in Guatemala, in Salvador when I get off the bus, they'll gun me down."
In Guatemala, there's an excellent tourism record, there are precious places,
incredibly beautiful places. And finally, let's build businesses in Central America.
Let's try to support regional initiatives.
In this 21st century, an important day is upon us.
The day to rediscover our regional brothers and sisters.
The challenges that await us are enormous.
We must remember now more than ever that there is strength in unity.
I'm Nicaraguan, and therefore I'm a Central American.

(Applause)
We are here to share the experience that helped us develop new ideas to solve some every day problems.
We have had the chance to participate on a project in our school where some other 64 schools from Spain participated as well.
In one single week, we had to overcome the challenge of identifying a problem in our own environment. which, in our case was reducing the amount of water being wasted.
Imagine what simple, cheap, and available solutions could help us solve this problem.
We came up with a lot of solutions such as the following. First, we started by saving some of the water used for watering the plants. In order to do that we built some containers and placed them on the top of the roof of our school in order to fill them with rain water.
Fortunately it rained during those days.
(Laughter) To keep track of the daily use for each student, we made a grid with each student's name to compile their daily consumption and marked it with stickers.
A green sticker meant the water consumption was low. A red one, meant high waste.
We found there was a problem with some of the faucets, some of them didn't fully close, and water was leaking.
So, we decided to do a flea market to collect money and buy new faucets.
During that whole week, we made several actions which went pretty well, but above all, we realized how many mistakes we make that end up wasting water. Once we found out we make them, we can provide a solution.
Our project led us to be selected to represent Spain and share our project on a world wide conference in India.
We gathered there along with 29 other countries and learned about other problems, ideas, and solutions provided by them.
The most shocking one and that we liked the most was Benin, in Africa, that was the one that call our attention the most. Girls were compelled to get married and forced to stop studying.
They talked to the authorities and made some representations showing how they were abducted. These plays were mainly oriented
to a male audience. By doing this, they were able to continue with their studies, and show that women are worth a lot more than what some may think. The next project that shocked us was the one from Israel: there was a big problem with traffic and pedestrians. To go to school, children needed to cross the street in between cars. Extremely dangerous. There were no sidewalks, zebra crossings, or signals.
After a lot of thinking, they decided to talk to all parents and drivers around the area.
They also decided to make the signals and zebra crossings by themselves, and they even made some sidewalks.
This way, they were able to go to school and cross the streets without any issues.
One of the projects from India related to reading, really caught our attention.
To us, reading is a very common activity, to them, it requires a lot of effort.
That's why older children decided to do oral reading sessions of stories to the younger ones, they would also make use of puppets to show them how to read.
They also found that some parents didn't know how to read either, so they made a home library, so that everybody could have access to books and be able to learn.
Well, this next project taught us the importance of each of us and that we should value ourselves. This is also valid for here in Spain. We should learn over it.
In Denmark, a group of girls raised the question: why people weren't feeling happy with themselves? They came to the conclusion that we give too much importance to our look rather than our inner selves.
To make people aware of this fact, they decided to make a fashion show at the main square of their city, where anyone who would like to participate could to it, and make everybody aware that true beauty is inside us.
These were the projects that we liked the most, but there were many others.
This is how we realized that we, all the children, live different realities in the world, and how important is to respect our rights.
In addition to know about other projects from other countries, we were also able to know and discover a country like India. Full of contrasts.
One of the things that called our attention were the animals walking down the street just like that, simply walking.
You could find cows, squirrels, monkeys, and elephants, All of them were very well respected.
On the other hand, traffic is chaotic, the city is very noisy and lots of horns are heard all over the streets, but yet, there are no arguments or people getting mad, nothing, everything runs with no issues.
In India there's very wealthy people and very poor one, but everybody is very gentle, good hosts, and generous. They respect everything they have as if they were big treasures even if they are very small things.
Well, this is the experience we've lived, and we wanted to share it with you. We have been very lucky with having this opportunity and would like to thank our classmates and teachers that could not come with us.
Finally as they said there, after showing each project, "Mission accomplished!"
which as you all may know it means, (Spanish) "Mission accomplished!"
Thank you very much. (Applause)
