Federico is my only son.
He misbehaved at school since kindergarten and since then also, he wants to drop his studies.
They called me every week of his life, from any level of schooling he was at the moment, to tell me he was misbehaving.
I'd go and listen stoically the complaints of the school and then back home, I'd scold him or hug him, alternatively, I'd punish him, cry and explain to him how important it would be for me that he stops spitting pieces of chewed paper to the classroom ceiling, tripping and later smoking under the tamarisks or setting up a corn war.
(Laughter) Around the second year of high school, the school called me again.
And I went there again.
The person who spoke with me on that occasion, a teacher, suggested a new alternative that had occurred to him.
An extension of Federico's school day
in which Federico and this teacher, were going to clean the barns of the agricultural school Federico attended and meanwhile they were going to talk for a while; they were going to try that this was not a time like the typical school time.
I think that what Federico learned in those barn afternoons works on him to this day.
That was the end of the times at the dinner table when the main talk was how bad Federico was performing at school and his desire to abandon it.
While I was building my relationship with the school as Federico's mother along these lines, I was becoming a teacher.
I have been teacher, vice principal and then principal and supervisor, always of public schools in the province of Chubut.
And when I was a teacher, and mostly as a principal, I was on the other side of the desk. I was as Federico's mom and the school called me.
And remembering how I felt when I was on that side of the desk when the school called me, it occurred to me to sit on the same side of the desk with the parents when I had to call them to talk about something.
Especially, if I imagined that what I had to tell them may not be easy for them to hear.
This may not be a novel idea, but it was like materialization of this simple idea: "we are all on the same side, to think about the kids."
When I was already a principal in a large school with many students that had several shifts, with several levels, I met Joaquín.
Joaquín also behaved poorly.
And in this case the school was using one by one, all regulatory instances to get to a suspension.
We discussed this a lot, but Joaquín gave us no respite and we had our backs against the wall at a much higher speed than our ability to think other options.
And then the day of suspension came.
The school suspended him, and while he was suspended, at a time when Joaquín should have been in school, during school hours when we should've been taking care of Joaquin, Joaquin stole a car, the police caught him, Joaquin got scared, ran away in the car, crashed and killed himself.
These two scenes of my life at school, or with the school, Federico and Joaquín, left a mark in my way of thinking the world with school kids forever.
Because I understood that sitting with them on the same side of the desk to think about life could be the difference between life and death.
Sometimes, metaphorically and sometimes literally.
(Applause) Thinking of Federico and thinking about Joaquín it came to me the idea of a school that never again rushes or puts aside, takes away, expels or suspends, nor asks for a reduction in hours, nor kindly requests to pass to another school no kid ever.
(Applause) I never wanted to push away again another kid from school.
And neither the instances in between
before expelling them. When I met her, Nebai was 6 years old.
She was the smallest of a family that lived in a corner of the Valdés Peninsula and with his brothers they jumped from the cliffs to the sea and they would gather in their backpacks the largest amount of mollusks their little hands allowed them and the air in their lungs.
One morning I woke up and I saw Nebai doing lines of snails in the sand.
I came closer and asked her what was she doing, she answered: "Tens."
Nebai was in the Valdés Peninsula -- have you ever been there? it's declared natural human heritage, making lines of ancient shells in the sand and calling them tens!
What an impact the school has, I thought.
A ten and two loose snails form this, she told me, while drawing it in the sand.
In fact, every line had 10 snails, we made many numbers from tens and loose snails, and Nebai put them together correctly and wrote them in the sand.
But I don't know the names, she'd say to me.
And the letters either, I don't know it well. Do you know them?
I kept thinking about Nebai insistently.
Nebai can be just an anecdote, of course.
Sometimes the anecdotes can help us think.
I think a lot about a phrase that says that we have to take care that our mind doesn't turn into a given, closed, already-made mind because if that happens, its activity is over.
Nebai repeated first grade because she didn't know the name of the numbers.
I suggested Nebai's mom to pass her to our school, to see if we could work so that Nebai would not repeat first grade.
She replied with a text several days afterwards: "If there's still time, I want to try that the Nebai doesn't repeat first grade."
If there is still time, Nebai's mother was asking.
Yes, there is still time! I wanted to answer her.
Yes, that the school becomes that accent, that coma, that pause.
A school that explores ways to be together, that digs in the problems, that makes them their own.
(Applause) When all these movements began at the school where I was principal, several teachers quit at the same time. They went to more orderly, more compliant schools, more driven by the curricula and comfort, I would say.
Work licenses and changes of roles crowded our work days and luckily they arrived to put their body and stamina, very young and soon-to-graduate, Roberta and Marisol and Carina, and Silvina, to put all their intention at that school time that came afterwards.
After Fede, after Joaquín, after Nebai.
(Applause) We wanted to think up ways and experiences to include all the kids at school.
That everyone could be inside.
To be able to sleep protected at the principal's office or at the school's kitchen if for some reason they had not been able to sleep at home the night before.
Or to wander from classroom to classroom looking for the learning context that fits you best.
To attend school only the months of the cherry harvest or every month except for shearing time, are perhaps another stories I could tell you of that time that came afterwards.
A time that completely drilled the box of possibilities univocally shaped by traditional school.
Some teachers said that in the end it was unfair for those who studied or for those who behaved well because then we rewarded them giving them the opportunity of wandering about and staying all day with them.
That it was more work for us and that we were much more tired.
The kids started to stay all day if they needed to and moms had to get organized to come assist us at lunchtime.
The pumpkin puree flowers drawn with the sleeve over mashed potatoes or songs to poetically ask the food -- Laughter and love were born of that domino effect time in which the school started to think how to be a school that could host all the kids.
It is true that it is much more tiring.
It's true that sometimes we felt that we were not prepared for this and that our bodies would hurt so much that we no longer felt them.
But there was now a discussion and an idea installed where before there was hardness and certainty.
I come across Nebai occasionally walking the streets of the city we live in, already going through adolescence, finishing high school, without having repeated one year of her schooling.
And also sometimes I run into Ayrton. He is a boy we designed activities for when he attended school that would last 45 minutes, which was his attention span, as his educational psychologist explained to us.
And now, almost a man, he works in his neighborhood's bakery, 45 minutes kneading, 45 minutes on the counter, 45 minutes distributing -- he learned how to drive, he's got a license -- 45 minutes wiping the bakery.
I also run across from time to time the moms of Alma and Gonzalo, who are still friends since the school proposed them that Alma's family shelters Gonzalo the time that his mom couldn't take care of him because she worked and they were new to the city.
The academic performance of Gonzalo, when Alma's family started to take care of him, improved significantly, for those who believe that this is the fundamental task of the school, or of all schools.
But also those two families find one another in this infinite loop of giving and receiving because the school proposed to look at themselves in a way that is not a traditional way to see each other from school
The one I bump into the most is Federico, my son.
He has already grown up, he behaves quite well.
He is a teacher, he works with children who behave badly in school.
(Applause) He works with children who have difficulties to adjust to that univocal format that the school keeps proposing them.
And I know that this has everything to do with that teacher that instead of throwing him off, got him even more time in school, with the excuse of cleaning the barns.
Thank you.
(Applause) Thank you.
No doubt, when you think about a computer, you imagine something like this:
but let me tell you that there are many other computers around us, and we don't notice them.
Washing machines, elevators, medical equipment, cars.
They are all equipped with some sort of electronic intelligence, a brain that we call "computer".
For example, a washing machine
can be programed to fill with water, to wash and rinse,
to refill, wash, rinse and spin.
These automatic processes are carried out by programs stored in a computer.
Elevators, depending on which floor we are on, which button we press, or if we're going up or down, are controlled by a computer that decides where it takes you.
In a sense, the computers in elevators and washing machines are similar.
Other types of computers need to be more dynamic, for example, the computers in medical equipment, where a person's life is at stake, or in traffic lights, where if the light is supposed to be red and it suddenly goes green, it could cause an accident.
Or the electronic braking system of a car.
Or in the case of oilfield equipment, located hours or even days away from the nearest town, that equipment can't break down, because they can't just go and repair it all the time.
The question is: Who designs these computers?
Who makes them?
Who programs them?
And the truth is that in our country, we often rely on computers that are manufactured abroad and imported.
So we depend on others.
And this makes many Argentine companies not want to use electronics that could improve their products or their production processes,
while others decide to continue using their old, obsolete technology,
or choose to use imported electronic goods and thus, depend on others.
There are very few who feel inspired to develop their own electronic solutions.
A little less than a year ago, together with a group of people, we decided to try to reverse this situation, to help small and medium-sized businesses in our country.
You may think that we're all Nobel Prize winners or NASA experts, but no, we are not.
Let me say that we're students, engineers, businessmen, journalists, designers.
We're located all over the country,
and that means we're people with different lifestyles
and we are very young,
with an average age of roughly 30 years.
I wish I had a photo so you could see the whole group, dozens and dozens of people, but the truth is I don't have one,
because many of them have never met in real life,
and maybe they will never meet or get to know each other because they work over the Internet, and share files by e-mail or on Skype.
Despite that, we make it work.
And together we made the CIA or CIAA, the Argentine Open Industrial Computer, which is what I have here in my hand.
I mentioned already why we call it a computer and why it is industrial.
But why "open"?
Because all the documentation, all the information on how it is made, manufactured and everything else, is available online.
Anybody can download it and use it freely.
Now we can say that the CIA has no secrets.
(Laughter) What does that mean?
Let's say a company needs a computer for their manufacturing process; they can go and buy one from any seller, knowing it won't depend on that vendor, because tomorrow they could buy it from a third party, or manufacture it themselves.
Or, for example, if you have to build a computer-controlled machine, you can say, "Well, I'll make my own."
Or you can say: "I'll download the information then make my modifications - it's free." and so, make your own version for your own purposes.
And that implies job creation, and it generates wealth and added value in our country.
There are other types of open computers in the world, but they're usually operated by multinational companies that manufacture the integrated circuits, the chips.
And they do it as a marketing strategy, so that you're obligated to use that particular company's chip.
By contrast, as we do not depend on any multinational company, we offer different versions based on different processors, and that means we are totally free,
we do not depend on any particular company.
In addition, this computer that I have here in my hand, is the first open industrial computer.
It was designed from the start to be used in industrial applications.
So, what is this made of?
Basically, it's composed of circuits, programs, and a motherboard.
How do we do it?
Well, one person shows up and says, "I can do X."
Another says, "OK, I can do Y," and yet another, "I'd like to help."
My job is to coordinate it all.
And there are even companies, small Argentine companies, that are competing in a certain niche, but understand that if they participate and collaborate, one will sell a little more, one can sell even more perhaps, but they will all grow because the industry is growing nationally.
And to do this, we have no owner manual, no recipe, because it is something that was never done before anywhere in the world.
We learn as we go.
And this project is based on collaborative volunteer work,
by people who don't charge for it, who maybe do it at night, when they return home from work, or during the weekend.
Some of them are lucky and can do it at work.
But if we add up the total number of hours they invest in this, it would translate into over ten million pesos.
What is the CIAA used for?
Well, for example, they're making applications for medical equipment, for railway and industrial equipment, for the petroleum industry, the agriculture industry.
Plus, something very important for us is that the network of more than 50 engineering schools nationwide - virtually all the national universities where they teach electronics - are now fellow collaborators.
The idea is that starting in March of next year, all these schools will begin to use this platform for teaching purposes,
because it is a way to spread this knowledge, and also a way for everybody to access it.
This is a dream.
This is the first Argentine Open Industrial Computer.
(Applause) There are people who leave their mark with amazing things, like paintings, books, or music.
We are engineers.
Our magic, our art is to make practical things such as this computer.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Verónica Bergottini: Where does the clothes you wear today come from?
Silvio Tinello: Did you buy them?
VB: Do you know who made it?
Well, we've come here to tell you that there are other ways of doing things.
I was born in Misiones.
I grew up surrounded by nature.
Since I was a little girl, I'd love to go around that rain forest next to our club, excited about the idea of seeing toucans, little monkeys, or, if I was very lucky, an anteater.
Hours later, attacked by mosquitoes, I'd flee to drink a delicious tereré with lemon and mint.
Liters of tereré powered us up during those long siestas in Misiones.
ST: Luckily, I was also brought up surrounded by nature, yet Patagonian.
Closer to arid plateaus, the sea and the whales.
And, even though it was always present in the beach, amidst guitars and fried cakes with friends and family, the truth is that I never used drink so much mate.
Until I was living in Philadelphia, where I felt the need to adopt it as a token, a kind of transition object that would make me feel connected to my roots.
Like home, so to speak.
It even helped me to create bonds with friends from other cultures who believed that when I was drinking mate ... was taking drugs.
(Laughter) VB: In my case, that closeness to natural life, plus, the love I had for ecology was my main driver to get a degree in Genetics.
And I deem as no coincidence to have later done research on how the bacteria from the roots of yerba mate could improve its growing process.
I was after developing a biotech product for agriculture, that would also be sustainable.
ST: As a kid I was so passionate about designing cars, that I even sent letters -- there was no Internet at that time -- to each of the most recognized car brands in the world.
I think that's how I ended up studying industrial design.
Until sustainable design got on my path, awaking my dormant environmental consciousness, which made me design materials and furniture made of recycled rubber.
Then, I realized that I could find value and resources where others only see waste.
VB: As for me, I wanted to contribute with the sustainable development of my province, and that led me to Switzerland to continue my research.
Then, I find out that Switzerland has a long history of scientific cooperation with Misiones, related to yerba mate.
These past years, despite having been away from home, I was always in touch with my roots through my investigations.
ST: From creating value by recycling rubber, I went on to create value by biofabricating with yerba mate.
The value of this idea lies in using yerba mate in an unexpected way, instead of just as food.
In this case, as a resource for the design of biofabricated materials.
As a designer, I intentionally chose the yerba mate aiming to transfer its identity to the design of new materials and products, and consequently to material culture, and Argentine design.
Because yerba mate is part of our identity.
Because it's a native plant, very much our own.
And it's the main ingredient of our national drink par excellence.
Yerba mate is with no doubt, our value and wealth, and part of the Argentine cultural DNA.
VB: Today, around this mate, I want to share with you this new challenge: biofabrication.
Biofabrication means to design using biology.
In other words, it consists of using cells to grow biological materials.
But, now, is it possible to introduce living organisms to a design process?
It's possible.
This is how biodesign was born, a new field in which scientists, and designers, use microorganisms as factories of sustainable materials.
Did you know? More than 2000 liters of water are used to produce a single cotton shirt.
Just like this cotton is produced in farms, these biomaterials can be synthesized in the lab, saving time, space and natural resources.
And that's how I get to biofabrication, growing bacteria that synthesize cellulose fibers.
Becoming this kind of scientist in sustainable fashion.
ST: The fashion industry, namely, is one of the worst offenders to the environment, for the amount of raw material it gulps up and turns into clothes and accessories, that we use only a couple of times and end up throwing away, for the mere fact that they went out of style.
But imagine a reality in which these garments and accessories, instead of polluting when discarded, they biodegrade and turn into nutrients for plants or new life cycles.
Or imagine leather accessories, which require the pollution of water and to even kill animals to create them, to be replaced by cultivated materials that require biological processes for their creation and end up biodegrading themselves when they have gone out of style.
VB: Would you believe me if I tell you that we can grow fabrics with microorganisms, out of this mate?
ST: Alive?
(Laughter) VB: No fear! I'm not hallucinating from a tereré overdose.
I got this material by cultivating bacteria and yeast in a kind of sweet mate infusion.
These microorganisms grow on the surface of this liquid, forming a compact cell sheet that after some processing acquires a texture similar to paper or vegetable leather.
This material is biodegradable. and compostable, what makes it very attractive for sustainable design.
In my research I grow these microorganisms in different agricultural waste and I assess its potential application in clothes and accessories, with the help of a designer.
This is an example of biofabrication applied to fashion design.
And the funniest thing about this is that I find myself caught up again between bacteria, yeast and other things.
(Laughter) ST: As for me, based on trial an error, I discovered that certain fungi grow very well in yerba mate, so with this agricultural wasted and these fungi I designed a fungal bioagglomerate.
Or, in plain language, a kind of yerba mate Styrofoam.
(Laughter) And at the same time, a sort of vegetable or vegan leather, grown in a variety of infusions, as well.
Yerba mate Styrofoam implies the growth of the mycelium, which is like the roots of the fungi, in order to put together the sticks and fibers of the yerba mate.
Vegetable leather uses some bacterial ferment to create cellulose.
We are talking about a paradigm change.
To grow and harvest materials and products instead of manufacturing them.
To use biology as a technology.
To change the model of linear economy, where we take, use and throw away, for a model of circular economy. A model where the concept of waste doesn't exist, just like in nature.
VB: Biofabrication allows us to get closer to the great challenges of the future.
To design materials as if they were nutrients that must circulate in nature.
In addition, it also allows us to use the agricultural waste to scale these bioprocesses, in this new model of circular economy.
Do you imagine all this?
ST: Well, more than imagining, it's way better to see it with your own eyes.
We invite you to see our biodesigns.
(Music) In this collection I introduce a series of cultivated accessories, that involve biological processes for their creation in the aforementioned materials, and I experiment with folding techniques like origami or laser cutting to get the pieces, and I even experiment with cultured jewelry, like the earrings that Laqui is wearing, inspired by the morphology of the leaf of the yerba mate.
With this collection I try to make a point. Not only we can rethink the way we dress, we can even reinvent everyday objects, those we share our day by day with, just like mate itself. Or, in this case, the reinvented mate cultivated in yerba mate.
VB: Next, I present my biodesigns, made from bacterial nanocellulose cultivated in a sweet mate infusion.
Can you believe that these garments, like this top and the accessory that goes with it, are made from bacteria and yeast?
It's incredible.
I combine it with linen garments, which production is also sustainable. We can see a tank top, we observe also that nanocellulose becomes a detail that goes together with the garment patterns.
And we can also see a skirt, designed from these biomaterials and combined with accessories.
With this we want to show you how biology applied to design can contribute to a more sustainable future.
ST: This is what's coming, fashion cycles aligned with those of nature.
VB: So now you know, next time you see yerba mate don't drink it.
ST: Put it on.
VB: Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Two and a half years ago I was in a totally different setting.
It's 2013 and I'm in Libya.
I'm one of the youngest working in my organisation, an NGO, Nobel Peace Prize winner, whose work clearing minefields and destroying arms arsenals has directly benefitted more than 16 million people.
My responsibility in Libya was to establish relationships with the local community and civil society. But this turned out to be quite difficult Libya was a special country. Libya was a country that had just came out of 42 years of isolation. Libya was a country where, until the 2011 revolution, there was no feeling of national unity. And Libya was a country where associations had been considered a threat to the regime, and in fact, Gaddafi had passed laws to prevent civil society from flourishing. I'm guessing that
in light of this situation you're wondering how it went.
Not very well.
A total failure.
However, my experience in Libya deeply influenced my vision of international justice.
It's true that Libya was quite a special country, but limiting the capacity of society to organise itself and build associations is a common technique employed by many dictators.
In Libya, I realised what an authoritarian regime fears the most.
The greatest fear of a dictator is a free civil society.
In the last 6 years I have lived in, worked in or visited around a dozen of countries under conflict or post-conflict.
I have learnt first-hand about atrocity crimes that happened even before I was born,
and I have investigated serious human rights abuses that continue to happen now.
I love my job,
I believe in what I do, but I know we can do better, much better.
The truth is that I didn't always want to be a human rights lawyer.
When I was little I wanted to be an archaeologist. I was fascinated by the idea of finding lost civilisations and finding out why some societies collapse.
Either that, or that I had watched too many Indiana Jones movies and I was really attracted to the idea of a hat, whip and such.
I haven't ended up being an archaeologist or a treasure hunter, but through my work I have seen what happens when a society collapses.
Five years ago I spent some time in the prosecution of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia. This is a judicial body created by the UN and the Cambodian government to judge the crimes committed by Pol Pot's regime.
In 1975 the Khmer Rouge took power in Cambodia.
Year zero begins.
Phnom Penh, the Cambodian capital, is evacuated, and its population forced to work in rural farms.
It's a communist utopia taken to the extreme.
During their 4 years in power, the Khmer Rouge wiped out between 1,5 and 3 million people, around 25% of the Cambodian population. Practically, the whole of Madrid.
The Khmer Rouge also supressed music, art and literature. They separated families, and the only marriages allowed were those forced by the regime.
All religious practice was eliminated.
The pagodas, the Buddhist temples that were the centre of community life, were in many cases evacuated, desecrated, destroyed.
It is estimated that there are around 20 000 mass graves in Cambodia.
20 000 mass graves full of tortured and mutilated bodies,
20 000 bodies that in many cases, are yet not identified.
During my stay at the prosecution I realised that in order to be a better lawyer first I needed to understand what genocide means at individual level.
So I obtained a grant by the National Geographic Society to locate Buddhist monks who had suffered religious persecution during the Khmer Rouge regime.
If there's something I learnt while interviewing survivors of the Cambodian genocide it's that it's crucial to listen to the stories behing the numbers. And this is very important. It's very difficult
to empathise with statistics.
You cannot identify yourself with 20 000 mass graves, or with 3 million victims of genocide.
Mass violations of human rights have that paradoxical effect, right? Crimes happen at such big scale that we lose the perspective of the tragedy that they mean at individual level.
It was listening to these stories when I realised that there is a disconnect between the system of international justice and the people whom this justice must serve.
And I believe it is here where civil society and non-governmental organisations can become a channel that opens up communication between formal systems of justice and the victims of atrocity crimes.
I guess that now you are wondering, well, why does international justice matter?
I'm going to explain it to you.
Studies show that countries where there is impunity for atrocity crimes are more fragile societies.
They are also countries that in general are less developed economically, with societies less equal and more prone to resort to the use of force.
Impunity and violence feed each other.
We are seeing it in Sudan, Mexico, Syria.
Only in 2015 more than 850 000 refugees crossed the Mediterranean and arrived in Greece.
This is nearly 37 times the population of Torrelodones. I did the math on my way here this morning in the car.
More than half of these refugees were Syrian.
In Syria, the lack of justice and the protracted impunity for massive violations of humans rights have fuelled more than 5 years of armed conflict, which has generated, in turn, the grave situation we are now living in Europe, with the massive arrival of refugees, the highest numbers since the Second World War.
The lack of justice has also paved the way for groups like the Islamic State, and not only in the Middle East, but also here, in Europe, at home,
What this teaches us is that the impact of impunity for abuses that occur in armed conflict goes beyond generations and borders.
When the international justice system fails, our rule of law also suffers.
The great thing here is that now we have a very powerful weapon: the Internet.
The potential of the Internet escapes our control. But it is under our control to decide what to do with this powerful weapon.
When Youtube was launched in 2005 no one thought that it would be used to document the Arab Spring.
I was living in the Middle East when it started, and it was extraordinary being witness to the key role that social media was playing in the revolutions.
By 2018 a third of the world's population will have a smartphone.
In 2015 more than 1,4 billion of units were sold.
Connecting to the Internet from a mobile phone not only has democratised access to information, but also its dissemination and here's the key. The greatest fear of a dictator is a free civil society and equipped with mobile phones.
This that you all have here today, in your pockets, has a great potential to start revolutions, to take down dictatorships and to fight against impunity for the most egregious crimes.
New technologies will not replace traditional investigative methods such as exhuming mass graves or interviewing witnesses.
But satellite imagery and other technology solutions open up the menu of techniques to obtain information on abuses happening in armed conflicts. In many cases armed conflicts happen in countries that are difficult to access as a consequence of such conflict. New technologies can help to obtain this information.
Technology also allows for populations trapped in armed conflicts to control their own narrative, individualizing those anonymous statistics I was talking about before.
There are more than 4 million videos about Syria on Youtube, 4 million.
And it's sad and it is frustrating and it pisses me off unbelievably that this is not enough for the international community to act.
An armed conflict is chaotic.
There is no rule of law, but the rule of violence. But this violence is not born from a moment of chaos, it is organized violence.
Crimes such as those being committed in Syria are perpetrated by power structures, highly organized, and ending these abuses requires the same large international structures that include those from civil society to government.
The international community, and I'm not referring only to the West, but at a global level, has the moral obligation to not allow war crimes and crimes against humanity to go unpunished.
And when governments fail, it's our job, as civil society, to challenge them to act.
International justice is a system that is living its childhood.
It is a system we created in the second half of XX century and it is not a perfect system, it is a system in constant evolution.
Justice by itself doesn't solve an armed conflict, but without it violence carries on.
Projects like the one I work for, eyeWitness, try precisely about channeling the use of technology towards the fight against impunity.
We have developed technology specific to armed conflicts that allows photos and videos captured by NGOs, journalists and citizens, to meet the legal standards required to be admissible in trials for serious violations of human rights.
This is important because we live in an era where wars are not only televised. Photos and videos are on Facebook, Youtube, social media.
But most of that material you see there doesn't meet legal requirements and is not admissible in Court as evidence.
And this is what our technology does.
Our technology can also amplify the voice of civil society and the work of organisations that are working on documenting violations of human rights.
And this is key, because in many occasions, as I told you before, there isn't the political will to send official teams of investigators to the field
and it is precisely civil society and local organizations those who have access to immediate information on violations of international law. Information on human rights violations that otherwise and if it wasn't for these local organizations would be lost and would never see a courtroom.
At global level, there are over 10 million NGOs.
If NGOs were a country, we would be the fith biggest economy in the world.
The combination of technology and a civil society, free, organized and committed to the fight against impunity, is the most powerful weapons we have to advance the international justice.
I'm a realistic and I know we are not going to end armed conflicts.
Nor war crimes. But I'm optimistic, because I know that now, we have the tools to end impunity.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Cristina Goberna: We believe that replicas are absolutely fundamental and necessary for the advancement and development of architecture.
Urtzi Grau: Well,
replicas have been important for the history of architecture.
As you all know, a replica is essentially a literal copy of an existing work of art or object.
During centuries, the architects copied, built, and produced architecture, and these concepts were more or less synonymous.
Under the influence of the Beaux Arts School style, architecture was made through dissemination of the canons of art, of those images or pieces of architecture approved by the ancients and towards which each work had to ideally tend to.
That meant innovation was achieved by getting closer and closer to an ideal that left very little room for variation and aimed at making even more perfect replicas.
That's something we don't do anymore, this kind of copy has little value today.
CG: I do not agree with that at all, of course.
What's really important about a replica is its second meaning: replica as a response to previous knowledge, right?
A response as positive friction, as creative dissent, as agonistic answer.
For those of you not familiarized with the term 'agonistic, ' the easiest way to explain it is to oppose it to 'antagonism.'
For example, two ideas are antagonistic when they are characterized by enmity, when, in order to survive, they completely have to destroy or ignore each other,
when the other is a perceived threat because it is different, OK?
On the other hand, things happen the other way around with agonistic ideas, they are not hostile, they are just competitive.
The otherness of the other represents not a threat, but a necessity.
You need the friction of the other in order to define yourself.
We believe that 'replica' is an interesting meaning of 'copy, ' but with an added agonistic connotation.
To illustrate this, we'll briefly discuss four case examples:
the first one concerns the rejection of originality in architecture,
the second one deals with the importance of replicas in architectural education,
the third one has to do with copyright issues, and the fourth one with the creation of public space.
UG: Let's start with the first one.
As any other creative discipline, architecture is subject to a creative practice,
but unlike other disciplines, the 20th-century architecture has been unable to welcome the copy,
a copy that in the realms of art, music, or cinema for example, was key to their production and transformation through elements of mashup, objet trouvé, détournement, or parafiction,
but which, for many reasons, was met with resistance in architecture.
I am sure you know very few architects who would proudly admit, "We replicate buildings."
Architecture students would be very scared to hear something like this.
Our proposal is very simple: what would happen if in our discipline, and following the examples set by others, we consciously embrace the controversies over replicas and their further implications?
We have an example here: what if, when commissioned to design the façade of a building, instead of doing that, you take an existing façade or what's left of it, façade panels, and you build the same façade with them?
What would it mean for the notion of authorship in architecture?
CG: I do not agree with this either, because the issue at hand here it's not replicating any façade, but which one in particular is subject to replication.
Here, we have an exhibition organized by the Chinese government.
A huge effort was made to reassemble the panels stripped out from the façade of artist Ai Weiwei's studio, imprisoned at the time of the opening of the exhibition, and whose studio had been demolished by the Chinese government. This was
a Chinese government-funded exhibition, the same government that previously destroyed the façade.
UG: Second case example.
Within the field of architectural pedagogy, we have suffered from the obsession with originality, the impossibility and the taboo of replicating for more than a century.
Students are systematically trained to create new designs because originality, or novelty, is conceived as a sort of formal explosion that delivers obviously different results: such as placing two buildings side by side and being able to notice the differences.
In theory, that's not a bad thing; the problem is it requires a huge, formal effort that usually leaves out a deeper thinking about intelligence and knowledge in architecture and which covers many more fields.
What we are suggesting is this: what if our model of architectural education will encourage students to replicate buildings?
To replicate a building means you can start working with it without worrying about any formal decisions that are already taken, but rather considering the appropriate design intelligence present in the context of those buildings.
CG: I believe we need to explain further.
(Laughter) If you know any architects or architecture students, you'll agree that what architecture education really lacks is basic principles of critical pedagogy where students can learn to recognize structures of power, and to also understand that we are not mere bricklayers or expert technicians that provide very good solutions for our fantastic clients.
We need to reclaim the idea of the architecture student and the architect as someone with a crucial critical capability.
I believe it would be like a second meaning of a replica, we have to apply it.
I would even add
it is very important that students should be able to identify what the problems of their times are so they can address them, to have the tools to address them whether it be an economic crisis, evictions, or all of this, and not to turn their backs on them antagonistically; instead, be able to agonistically create designs to address these problems that concern all of us.
UG: The third case example.
(Laughter) The third case example looks into a problem that's obvious once you start working with replicas and copies in architecture: how we regulate intellectual property in this field.
Again, compared with values in other fields, such as the contemplation of the length of a copyrighted song or movie clip, and how it can be copied or repurposed by a third party, the value
of a legally copied literary work,
or how visual artwork cannot be changed without explicit permission from its rights holder,
the situation is surprisingly different from that in architecture.
It seems that none of these laws apply, but actually, architecture is subject to the exactly same laws.
So, what's going on?
It's a very surprising situation architects simply assume isn't happening.
We are wondering if we can start exploring this kind of issues.
For example, buildings like this one
are considered the only copy, built once, delivered to the client, that's it.
Here you are, here you have it.
Is there a way to develop a model for managing intellectual property in architecture where buildings as the one you've just seen could be drafted as a piece of architectural knowledge to be delivered to the client together with instructions on how to be copied?
It would be a sort of a Creative Commons license, a license allowing the reproduction, copy, and replication of such knowledge, but at the same time would enable the architect to have a minimum of control on how these copies are made or maintain their value so that the architectural knowledge doesn't end up in a poor-quality copy of the building.
CG: Actually, what matters isn't the implementation or not of the copyright acts that we, as architects, could have at our disposal
but to be aware - and here I agree with you a little, sorry about that -
but to be aware that copyright is a topic brought to the table, and we have to be a part of it, because, we are totally familiar and accustomed to the free flow of architectural knowledge, - which is fantastic, we know
all these design details, we publish them in our professional journals, we write books, give conferences just like here today, or even more specialized ones -
but what if, all of sudden, a copyright act is drafted and stipulates not only for a more specific knowledge related to ongoing projects but for those already designed, too?
What if, all of a sudden, there is a copyright law for archetypal stairs such as fire escapes?
What if all of a sudden there is a copyright law for well-known apartment models?
I don't think this is a futuristic vision,
it is happening as we speak,
there are lawyers looking into this issue right now, and picking from where I left off, we have to stake our claim to replicas by claiming a seat at the table and talking about it.
It's too important a discussion for the architects not to be there.
UG: And finally, the last case example [is about] the issue of public space,
the role of the architect as designer of replicas in the public space.
The crisis that hit southern Europe has left many high-quality public spaces 'dead, ' due to lack of funds for the preservation of their physical and cultural heritage.
The amount of space is excessive,
but it is also more or less interesting.
What we say is that we don't need to create more public space,
but to provide the citizens themselves with the mechanisms to take action on this matter in order to start to manage it, find ways to use it, to reclaim it, to repurpose it without having to change it.
CG: This won't do in the slightest.
I think this is not enough.
I believe we have to also reclaim the role of the architect as a public intellectual,
as a mediator among politicians, community, and other experts involved in crafting the city, who has the ability to share these conversations, who writes in the media, who talks to other experts, but meets with the general public as well.
It is important to make clear that architects are not just representatives of the academia, living in their ivory tower
in a world that nevertheless has to exist as a self-paced world with its own language that moves at its own pace - but of a world that has many things left to do.
Susan Sontag said, for example, that the public intellectual was the one who knew how to identify the important things that needed attention.
Tony Judt said that a public intellectual was someone capable of jeopardizing its own position in society to discuss certain topics.
That is why we think that he who wants to craft architecture and generate a minimum of resistance, needs to forget about the methods used by the vanguards in the early twentieth century, or in the '60s and the '70s, that so antagonistically opposed the standards of their time and those of previous decades, and suggested a new order.
We believe that if we suggest a new order, as something new and original, it will be immediately absorbed by the market, and moreover, that we should not take such an antagonistic position but rather an agonistic one that allows for a dialogue with the present situation.
UG: These were our four case examples,
the first one concerned the production of architecture, the second one dealt with its pedagogy, the third one had to do with copyright issues, and the fourth defined the role of architects in the city.
GC: So, if you are interested in architecture, replicate.
UG: Let's replicate.
(Applause)
Right now, the majority of you are wondering something.
"What happened to her?"
"Why is she in a wheelchair?"
I think, subconsciously, people are hoping that I will talk about it.
That I say what it is, tell them about it, open up about it.
But the truth is, I've already had enough of it.
I've had enough of everyone asking me.
I've had enough of explaining it to everyone.
So what I've decided to do lately is to start making things up.
Every time someone has asked what happened, I make up a completely different story.
Like I was attacked by a shark, I jumped off a cliff and survived. Or one time, a little boy asked me what had happened, and, very dramatically, I look at him and say to him, "I'm just doing a social experiment to see how people react when confronted with a wheelchair."
Very surprised, he looks at me and says,
"No way. Really? Let me see. Stand up."
The experiment was completely ruined.
(Laughter) I believe the problem is not that people are interested in knowing why I am in a wheelchair,
that's just simply curiosity.
I believe the problem is that they ask me about it before asking my name.
For me, starting to use a wheelchair was like discovering a new world.
A world that, in reality, was always there.
Everyone sees it, but not everyone is familiar with it.
For me, it was like a change of outlook, a change of routine.
Just like any other big change that can occur in your life,
whether it's, I don't know, having a kid, moving to another house, losing a loved one, or changing jobs,
it's adapting to a new routine.
I didn't stop doing things;
I learned to do things differently.
I believe - For me, it was like a change in perspective, really.
I went from seeing the world from 5' 7" to seeing it from the average height of an 8-year-old kid.
The whole world started to be at a different height too.
Mirrors, most of all.
In 99% of mirrors, the only thing I can see is my forehead.
And I come up to here.
(Laughter) I discovered that there are things that are made for people like me.
When I started to use a wheelchair, I stopped using the women's bathroom.
I started going to a third bathroom,
that bathroom that's made for people like me.
I stopped entering places where everyone else went in, because most times the main entrances are not accessible.
And the entrances for people like me are around the corner, the side door, through the utility room or the garage.
I discovered the power of a ramp.
For me, accessibility literally means opportunity.
Whether or not there is a ramp up on the other side of the street, for me, means whether I can or can't cross over.
But we live in a world where sometimes not many such opportunities exist.
I had to change schools once I started using a wheelchair: the school I'd been attending my whole life had not been adapted in a way that would allow me to keep going to it.
Also, one of the things I found the most difficult were the looks.
When you're in a wheelchair and you go down the street, everyone is going to look at you.
It's inevitable.
You can't avoid someone noticing you went out today without combing your hair.
You can't avoid not greeting someone that you don't feel like greeting.
Everyone is going to look at you, or, on the other hand, avoid looking at you.
But one of the things that still surprises me the most is that, sometimes, it's as if I feel the world is trying to save me.
It's as if, subconsciously, I go down the street, and people give me saint cards.
They invite me to pray to the saints.
They recommend doctors.
Or they feel the need to give me a message of support.
Like, "Everything's going to be fine," (Laughter) "you can do it, if you really want to," (Laughter) or they congratulate my friends for being my friends. (Laughter)
I don't get it.
(Applause) I believe -
Often I don't know how to react to these things because I know people indubitably do it with the best of intentions,
But it makes me wonder what a wheelchair is associated with.
What the big picture is.
Yes, I am in a wheelchair, but ...
I'm fine.
I'm not worried about my life.
I don't want anyone to come and save me.
To me, one of the greatest ironies is that it doesn't matter where I am, what I'm doing, whom I'm with, whether I'm in a nightclub dancing the cumbia, people look at me ... and think, "Wow, what genius!"
(Laughter) "Wow, what an inspiration!"
(Laughter) "What an example!"
(Laughter) I could be a serial killer who kills people at night, but, to the world, I'm an example.
(Applause) And that makes me wonder: "Why do people train, study, take such great pains to be someone in life?"
Better to buy a wheelchair; you become exemplary automatically.
(Applause) Sometimes disability is like a taboo.
Words are used to name that which is not named.
When I sat myself in a wheelchair, automatically, to the world, I became a special person.
I became an angel.
I became a being of light.
(Laughter) And I also came to have different capabilities.
I've never really understood these different capabilities, because, I don't want to disappoint them, but I can't fly.
(Laughter) I don't have superpowers.
I believe the only way I'm differently abled to all of you is that I have a horn.
(Horn) (Applause) I don't think any of you have a horn.
A disability does not make you better or worse than anyone else.
For me, getting used to a wheelchair was a process.
A long process.
But I always think that it would have been easier if I had met with an environment that had already been adapted, and for it not to have been me who had to adapt to the environment.
Everything would have been easier if disability wasn't a taboo or a bad word.
If people didn't think being in a wheelchair meant something was wrong.
If being able to get a job, using a public convenience, or getting on a bus, did not have to be a privilege.
Everything would be easier if people with disabilities could indeed be examples, could indeed be inspirations, but for their ideas, for their talents, for their passions; not for the mere fact that crossing the street is a challenge.
I want to live in a world in which I can come here, I can talk to you for an hour about whatever, about whichever, and be able to leave here without any of you still wondering why, with everything that she said, she at no point told us why she is in a wheelchair.
Thank you.
(Applause)
In 2012, I decided to live in Spain and start a sustainable business with my partner, Julián.
This is my farm.
I'm a farmer.
And this will be my farm soon, in two months; we're going to expand our endeavor.
I wanted to speak about my farm because it's different.
How?
First, traditional farming uses more than 70 percent of our lands.
We have the possibility
of raising stock vertically in shelves, like a logistics system.
Also, at my farm, we use one of the best biodegradation systems.
Meaning, my stock feeds only on products that are not on sale
and also on ecological and traditional agricultural waste.
My farm is special and different because, as opposed to traditional farming, which uses more than 70 percent of our water resources, we are going to use only between 1 and 3 L to produce 1 Kg of meat.
1 Kg of meat. By comparison, in order to grow an ear of corn, we'll spend 600 L of water.
600 liters.
Like that.
Also, at my farm, we don't waste electricity.
Why?
Because my stock has a self-heating system.
It only needs a little bit of heat.
We take our energy from our food.
They don't.
My animals' great advantage is their conversion rate.
For example, in order to gain 100 Kg of cow meat we'll use 1,200 Kg of feed.
My stock is going to need only 200 Kg to get 100 Kg of meat.
That's 6 times less than cow meat and 4 times less than pork.
Pork needs 800 Kg of feed.
Incredible.
Also, as opposed to pork of which we can eat only 65 percent, my stock is 100 percent digestible and usable.
My farm will also achieve zero waste.
Zero.
Why?
Because my animals' excrement is considered one of the best natural fertilizers in the world.
And, at my farm, we're researching how to use of everything that makes up my stock to use it in medicine, cosmetics, bioenergy.
Zero waste.
My farm is different because the most incredible thing is its growth rate.
On Monday, I'm going to pick up 1 Kg of eggs. On Wednesday, 72 hours later (3 days) I'm going to have 300 Kg of meat.
300 Kg.
72 hours.
It's important to know that traditional farming is responsible for 18 percent of all the greenhouse gases in our world.
My farming is responsible for much less pollution,
including ammonia gases.
Thanks to our research, our motivation, and all of my stock's advantages, soon, in two months, we will be able to produce 1 ton of meat per week.
One ton. In 2015 we will be able to produce 4 tons of meat per week.
Everyone remembers the various crises in traditional farming, yes, in public health.
In 1995, mad cow disease.
Then, avian flu with birds.
In 2008, swine flu in Latin America.
My stock has a metabolism so different from ours and that of traditional stock that the risk of disease transmission is nearly inexistent.
It's healthy.
Well, I'm a farmer.
And this is my stock.
At my farm, we breed and control several families of worms, fly larvae, crickets, grasshoppers.
I can see a few people's faces in the audience and it seems very funny to me.
(Laughter) It's funny to me -- Why?
We'll peel a prawn, a crayfish.
You know? You cut off the head, take the skin off, you see the intestine vein, Pop! Mayonnaise and we'll eat it.
A little bit weird.
We'll also eat snails,
which come from my country, France,
We'll skin a hen.
But the idea of eating a cricket salad in Europe, is disgusting.
Well, it's important to know that insects represent 80 percent of all living beings in our planet.
80 percent.
For me they're the kings of the world, but don't say it too loud or you'll give them the idea of world domination.
We have to be careful with them, yeah?
In addition, insects are eaten since more than 2,000 years ago.
2,000 years.
And for us, the idea is... disgusting.
It's also important to know that, today, more than 1,900 types of edible insects exist in the world.
And that we in Europe are the weird ones.
Why?
There are more than 2,000 million people in the world that already eat insects,
especially in Asia, Africa, Latin America.
Why do they eat them?
Why?
Because they're excellent and delicious, more than anything.
(Laughter) It's true!
They're a huge source of vitamins, especially vitamin B.
They're a rich source of iron calcium, minerals.
But, above all, for us, the most important thing.
It's important to know that, in an insect, we can find between 48 percent and 76 percent pure protein, of a high quality.
Who can eat insects?
Pigs, birds, fish, and us.
I'm a farmer and my farm is special.
We transform insects into dried products and flour.
How can we cook them?
Like any other ingredient in our daily diet.
For example, we can add some fly larvae to a spiced rice dish to eat more protein and give ourselves more energy.
In Belgium there's a company that already produces fresh pastas from worm flour.
Soon you could find this type of product at the grocery store.
It's really good.
(Laughter) In England, my partners, a young business, is preparing you dishes to take home, to your office.
They're delicious cubes with insect ingredients, with insect meat inside.
This business is also developing a wide range of products, including spread and croquettes.
There's still a legal vacuum in Spain and Europe regarding the consumption and sale of edible insects.
However, it's been 5 years since the FAO, the UN Food and Agriculture Organization, has been promoting edible insects worldwide.
In December of 2013, the government of Belgium was the first to authorize the consumption and sale of 10 types of edible insects.
And I'm proud to announce to you that since last week you can find in most of Belgium's supermarkets this type of product,
which are tomato and carrot spread and flour worms.
In my opinion, it will be soon that our kitchens will have domestic appliances like this one that allow you to farm in your own home in an educational and healthy way, your own fly larvae to add to your rice dish.
(Laughter) In a world in which soon we'll be 9 billion people to feed.
A world in which traditional farming uses more than half of our natural resources.
A world in which the prices of fishmeal, cereals, and most foods haven't stopped increasing for 10 years.
In a world where almost all land is already cultivated, almost all fish mostly caught, it's mandatory that the most open minds, including this audience, value solutions that are alternative, innovative, healthy, delicious, more economical, and more eco-friendly.
Today, together, we build tomorrow.
Thank you. (Applause)
(Clarinet music) Good morning.
I am so glad to be surrounded by so many music lovers, by so many young people eager to do different things, and above all, by so many future musicians.
Because I am convinced all of you here want to study music.
So I wander, before I start, why don't we talk about the power of music?
And what is the power of music?
It's what just happened right here: I stood here, I started playing, you were euphoric, clapping as you are having a great time and suddenly there's this sweet, lovely, romantic, and a bit drippy melody. And suddenly we feel relaxed.
But then everything changes: the melody became active, it's more virtuous, more colorful, we are cheered up by it, and we end up clapping, like at the very beginning.
But again I wander: what is the power of music?
Is this the real power of music?
Is this it?
Is this the power of music?
Or this?
Here it is!
This is the real power of music, bravo!

Alright!
I would say that before talking about the power of music, we need to know why music affects us.
Because this is certain, music is not indifferent to us.
I am convinced that, above all, human beings are musical beings.
Surely in the dawn of humanity, when hominids started to communicate and language was not yet developed, they understood each other through the musicality of sounds
This is what happens with babies: when their parents talk to them, babies don't understand phrases, but through the musicality of the speech they understand the meaning.
This is why I believe we are not immune to the magic of sounds.
For example, it's contrary to the sight.
I still haven't found anyone that has lids in their ears to say, "I don't want to listen to this."
"I don't want to." It's not possible.
However listening to music is not only the ideal thing to do.
We need to listen to the music, to pay attention to it,
so that we can feel its power.
And I also want to highlight that emotions and music share the same region in the brain.
Here, in the prefrontal cortex.
That is why music and emotions are connected.
This is what led people like Oscar Wilde to make statements of this type, "Music is the art which is most nigh to tears and memories."
But I am not here to talk about why music affects us,
but to talk about what the power of music is and how it affects us.
Before going to the next stage, I want to show you this funny little video which is an example. Whenever you can. (Baby crying) And the video? (Laughter)
Don't be sad, it's coming.
There it is.
You will see the power of music in its purest stage.
(Video: baby crying)
The baby is crying, she doesn't want to go to school.
(Video: Katy Perry music) This is the real power of music.
And here, in this simple act, is the key of the power of music.
The key of the power of music is that we, human beings, are capable of empathizing with the expressiveness of sounds.
We are not indifferent to sounds.
And this is why, thanks to this way of empathizing, we can enjoy the benefits of music.
You can understand a bit better what I was talking about when I said you will all leave the room wanting to study music, just to enjoy these benefits.
For example, it generates pleasure and happiness
It makes you forget about your problems.
Music can generate relaxation or stimulation.
When you feel very stressed, you can listen to relaxing music. This will help you control your nerves.
Or on the contrary, when you wake up in the morning and the alarm goes off on one of these insufferable Mondays, you can play stimulating music to have a musical breakfast.
Another important aspect is music fosters creativity and imagination.
That is why many artists, many painters, for example, work with music.
Through music, we can also improve our concentration and efficiency at work.
And much more.
But I would say that the power of music is really obtained through instrument practice.
When you practice an instrument, everything we have seen before increases, and other interesting aspects appear.
Let's have a look. It boosts brain development.
It is scientifically proven that when a person is studying an instrument, they are helping to activate new neural pathways.
Therefore, the brain is empowering itself.
And this is why people that study music find it so easy to learn, for example, languages and maths.
Did you know that Albert Einstein
played the violin? Didn't you?
I, for example, in my personal case, taught classes to this girl that studied clarinet. Her father signed her up to improve her English.
Who has never sang a song in English,
at English classes?
Why? Because music helps
to improve memory.
And not only that. It also boosts psychomotricity.
This is why it's special for kids, to improve their development.
It increases the hearing capacity, as instrument practice requires listening to us and listening to see if what we are doing is right or wrong.
This is why it improves reflection and self-criticism,
because we have to rate what we are doing and work to create solutions to the problems at hand.
It helps to create a study habit and to improve organizing skills,
as playing an instrument requires constant practice.
This is a habit.
People that study an instrument, in general, have a habit of organization and a habit of progressive study.
On the other hand, there are also lots of curious aspects. Playing a wind instrument or singing help you reduce asthma problems.
Music is very beneficial.
But it not only has individual benefits. One of the things that I mostly like is that it has social benefits.
For example, music is a means to help integrate people and that enhances their quality of life.
In this way, children that have communication problems or social exclusion issues can feel integrated in a music group.
They can express themselves and feel they are listened to, so they feel loved.
But this is not all: playing music in a group also teaches you very important values, like the need of understanding and cooperating.
Cooperating among all the members of a music group.
This is why, when people ask me, "What would be the ideal social model?"
I would say, "An orchestra!"
But why an orchestra?
Because in an orchestra, all the musicians are equally important.
Every each of them is necessary. They all help each other and cooperate among each other for a common good that benefits everyone, which is generating the power of music.
Before I finish, I would like to reflect on some aspects of the power of music that we have discussed, because music has enormous power.
We could be here debating for ages, but we have witnessed that music is something that helps us both personally and socially.
So we have to take advantage of the power of music to change society and put an end to the things that do not contribute to a better life.
I feel very sad when people talk about economic crisis.
In reality, the crisis is not economic, it is a value crisis.
There's resources and money for everyone.
The only thing we need to do is learn how to use them so that everyone can have a decent life.
So when I talk about music, and when I work with music, as many other people do, we do it so that one of many people that we encounter in our everyday life, today there's many, for example, can understand that music is very beneficial for them and for society as a whole.
Even if there are governments which say that music is dispensable, that there is no need to include it in the educational system, that music time should be reduced, that why teach it, if they can play
and dance in the playground, right?
We will keep on working, me and many other people.
Because, as Nietzsche said, "Without music, life would be a mistake."
Thank you very much. I wish you a lot music in your lives!
(Applause)
When I was 11 years old I started to thoroughly wash my hands with bleach until they bled.
When I was 14, it was difficult to follow lectures at school and I would go through the paper because I had gone over my writing so many times.
When I was 18, it was impossible for me to talk to anyone without repeating my own words, and I walked the streets avoiding at all cost stepping on the pavement lines.
These, and other behaviours, can be normal among the approximately 1 million Spaniards suffering obsessive-compulsive disorder.
And I am one of them.
Obsessive-compulsive disorder, or OCD, is an anxiety disorder characterized mainly by two symptoms: The first one is recurring thoughts that the individual recognises as intrusive and senseless. These are the so called obsessions.
In my case, a very common one, was the conviction that something horrible was going to imminently happen to my family.
These thoughts create such an anxiety, such guilt and panic, that they take you almost without exception, to the second component: compulsion, that is the repetitive behaviour that the individual employs to somehow neutralize the discomfort the obsessions produce.
To make it clear, in my case I repeated checked, washed... to assure myself that if I did it correctly, then my family would be safe.
It was that simple and that complicated.
Obsession. Compulsion.
Logically, OCD
can have a very big impact in the life of a person.
The World Health Organization includes it within the 7 most disabling psychiatric disorders.
Today I am here to say that OCD is a part of me for which I do not feel ashamed.
It has taken me many years to be able to say this.
We are all educated to feel shame when we talk about mental illnesses, and OCD can be hidden, so someone very close to us could be suffering from it without us ever knowing.
Ignoring it, not talking about it, or not knowing exactly what it is, looking the other way even if our intentions are not bad, takes us to taboos, to isolation, and ultimately to the lack of diagnosis and of necessary tools to overcome these difficulties.
Guilt and shame associated to OCD cause that in Spain, an OCD patient takes an average of over 7 years to ask for professional help.
7 years of unnecessary lonely suffering for fear of speaking and being rejected.
The good news is that with the appropriate treatment the rate of significant clinical improvement is around 80%.
It is possible to live and be happy suffering from OCD.
But for that, we need to talk more and better about it.
We need to explain its symptoms and the effectiveness of its treatment.
We need to avoid it being confused with a funny eccentricity, or that it goes on to be comfortably labeled as another "being crazy" behaviour.
Today I am speaking in public for the first time about it, but you can be sure it will not be the last.
And don't forget I have a head start; when an obsessive-compulsive like myself aims to get something done (Laughter) he can be really insistent.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Once upon a time there was an ordinary woman from Argentina, Estela de Carlotto, who was meant to be a teacher, and she became one, who was called to marriage and she got married, destined to have children, and she had four of them: Laura, Claudia, Guido and Remo,
and above all, to love and be loved, and she also did all that.
She has been for 37 years now one of "Las Abuelas de Plaza de Mayo."
I currently preside over the organization that is fighting to find two generations,
our children, and our children's children.
Our children are known as the Disappeared,
this new notion that was born of Latin American dictatorships.
The majority of them were murdered after being held in clandestine detention centers.
Women were political activists just like the men and just like them equally persecuted. But kept alive, if pregnant, until their babies were born in this sinister place. And not to spare their lives, but to take their babies away from them, let them be brought up by the murderers, or friends of theirs, or abandon them throughout the country.
We, the grandmothers have been searching for these two generations for 37 years and we have given our all.
Most of us are married women with other children to take care of. This is a ceaseless quest that will carry on until we get all the answers.
Where are they? Why? Who did it? Where are the hundreds of grandchildren, who by now are 35 or 36 year old adults? They are alive. They are the living Disappeared.
And Estela is just another one of these Abuelas de Plaza de Mayo.
When we start this off, some of us weren't that surprised by what was happening, since we knew from talking to our children about their political activism and opposition to the civic-military dictatorship, which seized power on March 24th, 1976,
and which began with ferocious persecutions, giving rise to what we call "State terrorism."
Our activist children, in my case, my daughters Laura and Claudia, were university students.
When that dreaded day came, the day all of our children didn't come home, of course now we all know why... we, each and every mother of these people who didn't make it home we went out alone [to find them].
So what did we do?
We walked the streets, searching for and asking for answers where they might be.
We checked with police stations, military regiments, hospitals, anywhere we thought they might be; we banged on the doors of the politicians, of the Catholic Church. No answer, no assuaging response, absolutely nothing.
We stood alone, we didn't know what to do, and yes, we were afraid, but fear dissipates when you are searching for your son or daughter,
you don't care about what happens to you, you just go out to find them.
We were lucky in that wherever we searched, we met other women searching as well, so we decided, "Let's search together tomorrow."
This is basically how the group took shape, with 12 mothers-grandmothers, also the founders of our organization.
So yes, we were afraid, but wherever there is love, fear disappears.
Each one of us is born a certain way, and we are who we are.
If I look back on my childhood, I see I was always very extroverted.
This part of my character grew stronger when I had to start searching for my daughter.
And before that I searched for my husband. He disappeared for 25 days but I went out to find him, stubbornly, silently, almost as though you were in a ghetto, because you think no one would understand it because the media was saying our children were all terrorists.
The same media that lies today, and also lied back then.
I'm no different to other women.
I have a certain personality, that allows me above all, as a teacher, to speak up to interact, to facilitate understanding.
I am polite and I am respectful.
I never offend, I never insult.
This organization has never said anything about anyone without solid evidence.
This stubbornness of mine,
it stems from love. I believe my personality has a lot to do with it, but it's love that got me going, love for my daughter Laura and this grandson who I've spent years searching for and who I have finally found.
Love is the engine but personality helps a lot too for there are many who also love a lot but choose to weep and stay at home.
I could never have done that, because I have been made for action, I'm a mover, a fighter.
I only give up when I know I can't win, otherwise I will keep fighting!
I've brought all this into play as one of the Abuelas de Plaza de Mayo.
It's important to remember that during this time in the 70's, there were civic-military dictatorships in power in almost every country in Latin America, except Mexico.
Mexico has always had constitutional governments.
But the design, that allowed U.S. to impose its foreign policies on how these countries, branded as their backyard, must act, was mimicked and enforced by their respective governments.
As dictatorships became entrenched in Latin America each country resolved to it differently.
Mexico is a special case because what has happened there was not during a dictatorship. They do have their Disappeared, their murder victims and very serious problems, involving women in Juarez, for example, or the drug traffickers who murder or decapitate people and leave them in public view to teach people a lesson.
This is hard, difficult and fear can be paralyzing, but if you are moved by love, I insist, fear cannot hold you back.
What advice can I give, if any?
Join forces.
Alone we will achieve nothing. Together we can succeed. Protest.
Protest, demand, and ask the government in office for answers, so that such things stop from happening again. Do so peacefully. No one is talking about violence or irrational acts.
Peacefully but tenaciously. So my message is one of encouragement, It can be done! Don't give up! And remember you are not alone. We will stand by you.
Indifference is a terrible thing because it makes the abused feel alone. Why wouldn't we show a bit of humanity to help them?
If we can give bread to the hungry, then we can also show solidarity
with those who thirst after righteousness.
So, hearten each other and come together.
There's nothing better than a group.
I mentioned that people were kidnapped here, around 30,000 Disappeared, and it was a systematic plan put into place by this dictatorship; a plan to steal babies.
It wasn't causal or sporadic, but an order was given to keep the pregnant women alive, to take their babies away, and keep them for themselves.
Our project is very clear
and it's a project that works since we have already found 114 grandchildren to date.
These children live somewhere, under a different name, have a different life story and a fake family, a thieving family.
When we began the search for these two generations, since most of those children were born in concentration camps, we didn't know them, whether they were male or female, when they had been born, who had taken them; it was like searching for a needle in a haystack.
Even if we could have found them,
we wouldn't have been able to identify them. Then an article was published in a newspaper in my city, about a father who denied paternity. But by comparing a sample of his blood against one of his presumed son's blood, the parentage was proven.
We read about the "blood" test and how it served to relate parent to child. But our grandchildren had no parents. They were the Disappeared. Would the blood of the grandmothers serve for this test?
We started looking for answers all over the world.
We visited countries in Europe. We even went to Uppsala, in Sweden.
And in 1982, among other things, we traveled to the USA. In Washington, they took us to speak with someone from the Association for the Advancement of Science, to see if we could get any answers.
They didn't discourage us, unlike in other parts of the world, where we were told our blood wouldn't serve.
They said: "Let's study it."
From Washington we went to New York, where we were attended by geneticists who took us to a place called "The Blood Center."
We consulted it with a great geneticist, Fred Allen, who's no longer with us. He also said: "Let's study it."
In 1983, we got a positive result. It was indeed possible to extract blood from the maternal and paternal lines, and reconstruct both the mother's and father's genetic maps of our grandchildren.
Democracy returns to our country in 1984 and the government invites a group of scientists to help with the creation of a genetic database, the only one of its kind to date.
Nowhere else in the world are there grandmothers searching for their stolen grandchildren for political reasons.
Without even knowing how, we established this data bank for the first time. With the help of the visiting scientists,
who used a sample from a granddaughter who wasn't disappeared, a hypothetical comparison to any compatibility present in the maternal and paternal lines was run. The result clearly indicated that she belonged to that family.
We knew then that a system using HLA, or histocompatibility, would work.
Based on that, the data bank was founded by law in 1987 and its regulation enacted in 1989.
Obviously science has since advanced to incorporate DNA, nuclear DNA, mitochondrial DNA, male DNA, and the more science would keep advancing the more our data bank would improve.
Today, the probability of relatedness, in percentage, reached 99.999%. Perfect!
In the case of my grandson, the latest one to be found, the percentage was indeed 99.999, so he was Laura's son and Estela de Carlotto's grandson.
This data bank will exist forever, because, as I have said, there's still much to be done, things might take a long time and the data bank might be necessary.
No one can explain what it is like to search for someone you don't know, or where they are, and they could be close by. That was the case with him.
Our paths did indeed cross but we didn't recognize each other. The ingenuity of these women is what made things possible, despite their efforts to never find them, and we've found 114 so far.
The restitution of my grandson, Guido, - his name now is Ignacio, but he's Guido- was a turning point.
And I say this as someone who goes back a long way in this organization. We have identified many misappropriated grandchildren.
It's always a joy for the well intentioned folks. And something funny happened in Guido's case. A marvelous social explosion of joy and celebration took place. Everybody was saying: "It's as if it were my own grandchild,"
politicians, the civil society, rightwing, leftwing, everybody! Everybody celebrated the restitution of my grandchild, and they almost put me on a pedestal, for he was my grandson. And this wasn't the case just here, but also abroad.
Of course, I'm well-known.
I have traveled and still do travel a lot.
I attend for years and years now and as much as possible, everything related to this issue.
And as long as he is the grandson of somebody famous, it's clear
his case has a greater social impact.
And I think this just proves that the Argentine people deserve to enjoy some good news.
We have to take advantage of this explosion of joy and keep on bringing more good news.
[Young woman found in Holland.
Grandmothers find 115th grandchild]
(Handpan music) (Applause) OK. Can you hear me? Yes.
In 2002, a friend calls me and goes, "Ravid, you have to come, I'm at a beach festival, it's very nice, and there's a person from Switzerland, from Switzerland, who has brought a round instrument, a metal one, which nobody knows how to play and
it's very strange. You have to come."
So I go to the festival, I find this instrument, with this gadget and I say to myself, "You figure out what this is."
I sit on the floor, I take the instrument and I start playing; 5 or 10 minutes later it was full of people thinking there was about to start a concert.
And I was all red; well, I don't know.

That's my first time and there I realized that there is truly something, I have to really investigate this, we both have something in common.
So, a month, two months later I'm in Switzerland, of course,
I caught a flight, I went to Switzerland to the people that created it, to the luthier where the instrument is made and along with them we've been developing the instrument and all that; I've learnt a lot from these geniuses and I've learnt more, basically the heart and the essence of this magical and peculiar instrument.
Working there, Félix, who is one that adores this instrument, tells me, "You have to travel and show the world this gem because it can't remain here."
So, I put my backpack on and I started travelling.
I started travelling all around the world, as they said before, climbing mountains, descending mountains; I went to China, to India, all over Europe, to Canada; working with different musicians, I've learnt from them all.
From the experience of playing an instrument, with music, dancers, artists, with all of them, and I've realized that in the world there's actually a very interesting thing, and that's that everything is round.
Almost everything, not everything, but almost everything.
At least, the natural part of the world is round.
For example, a flower's seed is round. A tree is round, not the whole tree, but it has round objects.
Our body is round; everything we do, everything we have is round, even the Earth is round and all the movements we make, giving a hug, climbing a tree, it's always a round way of acting.
And I started to think about it and said, "If everything is round and what we do has always a circular shape, why all the instruments we have, almost all the musical instruments, are linear?"
Well, this was a question that started to resonate in my mind.
I had a rehearsal with a pianist friend and by rehearsing, she plays the piano, she's great, and she told me, "Ravid, you have to play 'Toccata and fugue' of Bach," which is one of the gems of music, and it basically has a lot wrist work.
It's a wrist practice, a hand practice.
"And let's see what happens when you play it on the hang."
You'll see.
I took the instrument (Handpan music) and I played this, thank you.
(Applause) Playing this, the "Toccata and fugue"; a piece of "Toccata and fugue," in Rem, I realized that she was laughing at me because I was all sweaty, playing it very fast, trying, and she was just playing with one hand.
And then I realized this difference between an instrument which is round and another one which is linear.
An there, going home, taking the train, I take a seat and a young boy sits in front of me with a guitar and I keep watching his guitar all the time, so he says, "What's with you and why won't you stop looking at me?"
So I tell him: "I couldn't help it," because I had that thought of the difference between a round and a linear instrument.
So something comes into my mind and I say: "Damn, but it's right, almost every instrument we have is a linear instrument."
The guitar, the flute, that is a wind instrument, it's, in the end, a line.
The violin, the cello; there's always a line somewhere.
There are very few round instruments.
Melodic instruments, at least.
Thinking about it, I came to a conclusion, "By having a linear instrument, one works only in one dimension;" in this dimension only, from the right to the left.
When you have a round instrument, there are two dimensions.
So this allows you to draw -- draw over the instrument and see what happens.
So I started thinking about it.
I picked a spiral shape and look what happened.
So I take the spiral shape, as before.
(Handpan music)
OK? This is a spiral, I've done anything else.
But musically speaking, it's very interesting because this spiral gives me the octave, the third and the fifth; which, harmonically speaking, is correct, it's the harmony in the music, that has come from a geometric form.
Let's just say everything is just fine: the geometric part and the musical one, as well.
And this made something else come into my mind: "Hey, maybe it's not just geometric shapes, like the triangle, the circle or the spiral; maybe it 's about objects or even animals, as well."
Animals that have this round shape, like the snail.
The snail has this round shape and he builds his own home every time.
So, to me, what I was doing was building, adding another note.
And this way, I create songs through shapes -- geometrical ones or in the form of animals.
But not only that, I also had an idea one day while studying Chinese -- I studied Chinese once -- and it was adding a Chinese character, some words.
So I added the word "shang," for example, which means "mountain," like in Shanghai
and it worked pretty well;
I started playing, well, I will show you.
(Handpan music) So you have noticed that I keep moving from one side to another. Something that is very interesting in a round shaped instrument is that you have to work
with both hands. Always.
And there is no way to make it easier.
Everything you do with your right hand must be done with the left one too.
This is very good in terms of balance for both cerebral hemispheres.
So I was working with that, I was researching until one day when I say: "Everything is so nice, but the instrument is just using one tone: Rem; if I want to change it, I have to buy a new instrument.
However, if I also want to change the instrument and make it sound like another instrument, maybe like a harp or a guitar or as a piano or a flute, then, what can I do?"
So thinking and thinking, I reach to a conclusion: "Easy, I create a round but digital instrument."
And this way, asking a lot of friends and a lot of people with knowledge on electronics and design, I created, together with Alex Cosada, who is an electronics engineer, we've reunited an engineers group, and together we've created the Oval, the first digital round instrument, which basically allows to perform this round instruments method, but with all the instruments and with all the music and sounds you want.
And, moreover, with linear instruments.
I can take a piano and give it a round shape, that's what I wanted to do in the end.
Not physically.
So, along with this team, we started researching on materials, sensors, wires, on a lot of things, until reaching, bit by bit, to a thing that starts to be similar to the round instrument and that also sounds well.
It wasn't easy, we've been working almost two years and it's hard work, but a very interesting one too; and ending up with a perfect round instrument, which is the Oval, which is that instrument with lights, which has a lot of effects and immense possibilities.
This works through an app, where you can pick the instrument; for example, you choose the piano or you choose the harp or whatever, you pick the tone you want, which you can increase or decrease.
You can have any sound, any effect; it's very cool.
And then you play and enjoy the music, this is what you have to do in the end.
To conclude, just to finish this talk, I have a dream and this is that in a not so far future, there will even be an Oval orchestra, with a lot of Ovals and a lot of people, musicians playing the Oval, and that each Oval will sound like a different instrument.
And maybe next year or in two years, we will come back with an orchestra full of Ovals and we'll make a show.
And now I'm showing and example of what I want to do in the future.
(Oval music) Thank you.
(Applause) Thank you.
Good evening.
Today, I'm going to talk about salt.
Did you know that salt is
the oldest seasoning used by people?
It's said that spilling salt brings bad luck, isn't it?
You probably don't know where this famous superstition comes from. The story goes that in Ancient Rome, a lot of ships wrecked while transporting salt from the various islands where it was mined.
Its weight and the high tides were always a transportation concern.
As there were no refrigerators, salt was a perfect preservative for food.
For this, and as it was difficult to get, salt had a great value.
It was exchanged at the markets for various goods, such as meat, vegetables and any other.
The problem was that when salt was spilled, contrary to other products, it couldn't be picked up.
It became polluted, lost and wasted.
That's where the famous saying comes from, "What a bad luck, salt has been dropped."
Who would say that today, at the TEDxTukuy talk, I would talk to you about salt?
The truth is we will talk about salt, but not just any salt, only about salt with an incredible history, salt with a story that not many people know.
According to an Inca legend, Ayar Cachi, brother of Manco Cápac, the founder of the Inca Empire, had to refuse to come to the establishment of Cuzco.
He and his wife climbed a high mountain and they were so sad because of not being able to celebrate that they cried bleakly.
Those tears dried and then crystallized because of the sun. They formed a salt mountain, a natural wonder, where until this day, salt is produced.
Only this happens at height over 3,000 meters.
Salt at height over 3,000 meters?
Don't we know that salt always comes from the sea?
Almost 800 years after, Japanese channel NHK published an investigation in which they show that in the middle of the mountain, thousands of years ago, an ocean was trapped.
Lukewarm water from this ocean flows into the Urubamba River, the river that crosses the Sacred Valley of the Incas, in Cuzco.
According to the story, the Incas filled some ponds with this water. Having filled one of them, closed it and opened another.
Just as in the legend about Ayar Cachi, after a few days, the sun crystallized this water and formed salt.
Now there exist 4,200 ponds for the salt production, belonging to the 380 families who work there. They come from Maras and Pichingoto communities.
Each family has two, four or ten ponds, passed from one generation to the next.
They are the principal guardians of one of the biggest wonders in Peru and in the world. Las Salineras de Maras.
An incredible place, visited each year by thousands of tourists.
For hundreds of years in the past, and surely for just as many in the future, this place produced and will produce 100% natural salt.
The work and care of the communities is crucial for maintaining the traditional way of production, which is shown by the fact that today the salt is still transported to the different markets in the area on the backs of donkeys.
They are so important that according to the Maras anecdote, every time a donkey is born, it's given a name like Ramón or Mariano.
But how do we know today about the salt from Maras?
Aproximately 10 years ago, I heard about salt in Cuzco.
After investigating, travelling, visiting Salineras de Maras and discovering this incredible product, I wanted to show it to Peru and the world.
Today salt from Maras can be found not only on the Peruvian tables, but also on the tables in 17 countries in the world.
Today we can say that salt from Maras is a fashionable product.
But it's not only about kitchen salt. In 2012 we managed to create a bar of organic chocolate that won a silver medal in London,
in a competition among 4,000 chocolate makers.
We managed to make salt from Maras part of an exclusive bath packet sold in the USA for USD 50,000.
The one who buys it, gets eight ingredients including virgin water from the Arctic Ocean, 100% organic jojoba oil from Israel and salt from Maras in Peru.
Discovering the use of salt from Maras as beauty product, creating bath salts or exfoliant oils for the skin, which a few years ago obtained certificates in Milan, Italy,
really contributed to the spread of this incredible product.
But, why is the salt from Maras such a success?
What makes it so special?
The salt from Maras is a 100% natural product.
This is one of few natural salts existing in the world.
This is salt with a high mineral content.
It boasts a mystical history, is full of energy inherited by the Incas.
But more importantly, even though it seems incredible, it's one of the few salts in the world that have a taste of salt.
Those who had an opportunity to taste it, has discovered how salt from Maras enhances the taste of food.
On the other hand, they also ask what did the discovery of the Maras salt mean for the communities of Maras.
I can summarize it in four points: rise in job openings, not only at Salineras de Maras, but also in the salt supply chain, multiplication of its production size, fair prices for salt, applied from the beginning, social work involving children from the community, which is provided every year.
How incredible that today we can consume salt mined at an altitude of over 3,000 metres!
Salt which takes its origin
in the middle of the mountain, in which thousands of years ago, an ocean was trapped.
How incredible that we can consume salt with a mystic history originated by the Incas!
Mysticism and energy, which many people search for in Peru, we find them in our salt.
Being able to consume salt 100% natural with high mineral content, that even though now produced in much bigger amount, still maintained
its traditional way of production, the same today as it was 600 years ago.
Finally, how incredible that today I can be here and tell you about salt!
But, as I told you at the beginning, not about any salt, only about the salt from Maras.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Good afternoon.
How wonderful you are!
(Laughter) Well, I want to suggest you that wherever you are, you close your eyes for a moment.
Close your eyes.
And I want to propose that you come with me to one of the beautiful places to where this song that I'm going to sing takes me.
(Background music) It's an open place, gorgeous.
Where the air caresses gently our face.
And the palm trees move to the rhythm of the wind.
And the sound and aroma of the salty sea penetrates our lungs.
And there's an intense heat that doesn't let a little black baby sleep.
Did you envision it?
Good, now you can open your eyes.
Now imagine that that black baby, is me!
(Laughter) And the one who sings the song is my mom, in some hidden place in the port of Buenaventura.
(Music) Sleep my nengre, sleep little black boy.
Caimito and merengue, merengue and caimito,
sleep my nengre my lovely nengre.
Teeth of merengue, and caimito lips!
When you get older, you're going to be a boxer.
Nengre of my life, nengre of my love.
My chiribicoqui, chiribicoquito.
I save for you
[a] slice of melon! If you don't hush your lips, and wipe your nose, I'll open the door to Vincent El Loco.
If you don't hush your lips, it'll give you a big fright.
It's going to take you to El Loco in your backpack.
Not killing the güira, the owl calls you.
Hidden in the door is the tatajú.
Sleep my nengre, face of a boxer, nengre of my life, nengre of my life.
My chiribicoqui, chiribicoquito.
Caimito and merengue, merengue and caimito.
I will lay you down in daddy's hammock and I will rock you softly sweetly and slowly. And I will kill the fleas and shoo the mosquitos for you to sleep well my beautiful nengre ... (Applause) (Ovation) The lullaby that I just sang to you is called "Nana," and is from the Catalan composer, Xavier Montsalvatge.
There are incredibly fortunate people that succeed in finding something in their life that makes them very happy.
In my case, I've received the gift of being able to sing.
It's a gift that makes me exceedingly happy.
And it's a language that opens a very special door for me to a dimension that I didn't know.
To a dimension that is always there with us, but unfortunately we don't always give ourselves the chance of experiencing it.
Of feeling it.
And it's a dimension that is experienced and felt no only with our ears, but rather through all of our senses.
And it's a dimension that is felt with the soul, with the heart, and with the spirt.
Tonight, I'm here and I feel very honored to be able to be accompanied and together with my wonderful pianist, Alejandro Roca, (Applause) we're very happy to be able to share this gift with all of you. My childhood was always full of music,
of folk music from the Pacific coast:
Currulaos, lullabies, chirimías, but especially salsa music, which was the music that was listened to every day at home.
My father's love of salsa music, and a guitar that he and my mother gave me, were my beginnings with music.
I can tell you that my parents, the environment that I grew up in and the music that I grew up with, were my first teachers.
When I was still a girl, I lived practically trapped in my own world.
To be honest, I had a little difficulty relating with people, and I was going through tough times of depression.
And in my house, like in many of ours, there was a small room.
And that room came to be my personal retreat.
So it was there that I spent the majority of time playing and many times I was simply there in silence trapped in my world.
One day something happened that branded my heart, that branded my life.
And I felt that I could not anymore, the root of that weight so strong that I felt and that sadness, just out of nowhere I started to sing.
I started to sing songs that I knew and I started to sing melodies that came from the depths of my heart; they were things that didn't exist and many of them were songs with unspeakable words, but in that moment I simply received the gift of being able to open my heart and of conveying what was inside: all of that sadness, all of that bitterness that I couldn't utter in front of people with words of this world.
I'm telling you that that event means for myself, like a liberation that I could have at that moment.
And at the root of that, I started to sing and I keep singing thanks to God.
Years later, I went to live in Cali, with my older sister.
And she told that it would good if I enrolled in the academy of music to study music.
I enrolled, I wanted to play the guitar that my parents had given me.
So I enrolled for guitar and they advised me that if I register also to sing, with the intent of going together with the guitar.
Ready, I showed up for the guitar test and followed with the one for singing.
But sadly, I got too nervous and I couldn't accompany with the guitar so I set it aside and started to sing a capella.
So the singing test was a complete success.
And I was realizing that the guitar wasn't for me.
(Laughter) So I pushed it further and further into the background, sadly.
In that test, I met my first opera singing teacher.
Her name is Ivonne Giraldo.
And she was guiding and getting me familiar with the genre of which I had not the slightest idea.
And she herself took the task of polishing from scratch that black diamond that she thought she'd found.
Another super important event is in the midst of my career, when I met the master Francisco Vergara.
He perceived that there was a special talent and was one of the first people to tell me that he reckoned that I could make a career as an opera singer, and that I could go overseas to keep training myself.
He himself took the job of finding the means and the money so that I could have a way to go to Germany in order to study; and that is something that stays totally etched on my heart.
The effort of those two tutors, of those two teachers, and the fact that they were opened in front of me a new view, drove me.
It pushed me to, for the first time in my life, to dream big.
To dream with the fact of becoming an opera singer, like I never before could have imagined it.
(Music, "Someday") One day you will reach; the love that awaits us.
And you will tell me: Sweetheart, the spring has come already.
One day you will love me.
You will be so close to my bosom, that I won't know if the heat that scorches me is from your heart or from the summer.
One day you will have me.
We will listen soundlessly to the pulse of our arteries and to the weeping of bare trees.
One day. Any day.
Fleeting and everlasting
love is the same in summer, in autumn, and in winter.
(Applause) (Cheers) Thank you very much.
Every time that I come to Colombia, my heart is filled with incredible emotions.
And one of them is gratitude.
Gratitude for this, for this love so awesome which my entire country greeted me with.
And tremendous gratitude for those teachers that drove me to make that dream become a reality.
For my parents and for this story that I'm telling you.
So today I'm here celebrating my birthday with you all.
(Laughter) (Applause) And the amazing joy that I feel looking back at how this whole story began, it's priceless.
Today I'm the happiest woman in the world being able to be here with all of you. (Applause) And I want to invite you, for one more moment, let's get carried away at this time and allow ourselves to experience a little of that special reality, of that special dimension.
It's there.
Realize that, along with the awesome, special, and unique gift of being what we are, we've been presented the gift of being able to be conduits, of being a door, of being instruments.
Instruments of freedom through which you can experience that dimension, that reality that I'm talking to you about.
Through moments like this, tiny but unforgettable.
That become eternal and that pierce our heart and our souls.
(Music "Io son l'umile ancella," "I am the humble servant") Look here, I'm scarcely breathing, I'm but a humble servant of the brilliant creator; He offers me the words that I impart to the heart ... I am the verse of the music, the echo of the human drama the fragile instrument the lowly vassal ... Timid, joyous, terrible, I'm called Faithfulness; My voice is but a whisper, My voice is but a whisper, which, with the new day will die.
(Applause) (Cheers)
(Sattva music) (Applause) Thank you very much.
This instrument is called Sattva Handpan. It's made here in Argentina.
It mixes percussion with melody, in a very particular way.
It's got a musical scale, with 7 notes on the side, ranging from the lowest to the highest.
(Musical scale) And a central note that gives more intensity to the instrument.
(Central note) The idea of creating melodies from metal percussion instruments, comes from Trinidad &amp; Tobago, in the Caribbean.
It became more popular through an instrument called... ...Steelpan which was made with petroleum barrels dumped by the U.S.
They used hammer and their own lungs, and realized they could generate a melody by hitting the steel.
Around 2000, a Swiss company created this kind of instrument with the purpose of making it hands-playable, and thus having a stronger connection with the instrument.
In my personal experience, I see music as a healing tool.
When you start playing and listening, you get into the sound and you just let it flow, you get into meditation as well.
It awakens many emotions, feelings, sensations.
It makes us feel alive, basically.
They say music is food for the soul.
I also think it's a language.
We run out of words sometimes, to express our desires, our feelings, what's in the depths of our being. And perhaps, a simple melody or a simple rhythm can generate something deeper in somebody, than a thousand words.
Sometimes, when I play on the streets, someone would come to me, looks into my eyes, and says: "Thank you, you made my day." I can't explain the happiness I feel in that moment, knowing that I'm doing, in my way, something good for people, right?
Now I'm going to ask those who'd like to, to please close your eyes. Relax, take some deep breaths. And any fleeting thought that comes to mind, just let it flow.
Do not hold it but let it flow, and grab the teaching. And enjoy an inner travel with the following melody.
Thank you very much.
(Sattva Music) (Applause) Thank you.
(Applause)
I've always been fascinated by the power and conviction of people who are capable of rising from failures, from disasters, people who stand on their own and keep on going.
Or people who have an incredible strength to face the impossible.
Those who march for causes and ideas, and defend them against all odds.
Human history has beautiful and wonderful examples.
This afternoon, however, I would like to speak to you about an example that has impacted my life since my childhood.
My father died when I was about three years old.
My mother became a widow, obviously alone, with nine young children.
Most of them were very young, and one girl was just a baby.
That fatal blow to the family, especially to my mother, was very hard.
When I grew up, although my memories are somewhat vague, I began to face my own difficulties. I've never forgotten what that morning must have been like.
How did my mother get up that morning after my father's death?
She was devastated, hurt. Her world had fallen apart.
What kept her going?
What strength compelled her to stand up and start over?
Throughout my life I've had many hardships, even many times of total despair, like probably many of you.
Those times when you feel you can't go on, when you think you've reached the end of your rope.
But I always repeat to myself, tirelessly, “If she was able to get up that morning, you have to be able to get up now. Now!
In this instant, Juangui.
Get up! Let's get going.
Out of respect for her you can't do anything less."
And over time, as time went by, I started to realize that we all have an inner strength. And that inner strength is ignited by a challenge, and that challenge must be full of hope. I call it "The Strength of Hope."
Over the next few minutes, I will share with you how this life experience led me to develop strategies to help children and young adults find their own "Strength of Hope," and also to understand that it's not about material poverty and social status, but rather about our inner wealth with which we can resolve many things, regardless of our social status.
I would like to make a plan so we can have a starting point to understand how these things fit together.
Poverty, hunger, and social injustices are elements that provoke major conflicts and breed within them unsuspected forms of violence.
This is reality, raw as it is, but it's the way that it is.
However, that same situation can be the perfect breeding ground for a profound human and social transformation.
It's like two sides of the same coin, two perspectives of the same situation:
One sees a problem; the other sees an opportunity in that problem.
In order for you to understand the context of these three so very important ideas - inner strength, challenge and hope - I'm going to show you the real picture.
In this first situation, we have: [Inner Strength] [Challenge] [Hope] What's in this picture?
Well, first of all, social problems - poverty, lack of employment, lack of education, violence, social breakdown in all terms -
are one reality.
But let's turn the page and look at the opportunity.
What we have in this very same social situation is the fact that each one of those kids who lives under these circumstances has an inner strength that they're not aware of. In most cases, they have no idea that they possess this inner strength,
an instinctive inner strength
which is activated by hope - with challenges and hope.
But I'm not talking about hoping that conflicts will be resolved. It's not just hope that tomorrow everything will be rosy and pretty and then we'll all have opportunities.
The hope I'm talking about is that each one has a desire to excel, and each one, individually, aspires to something greater,
to move ahead in life, and project themselves to the world.
Just imagine that treasure -
individual inner strength combined with the desire to excel.
It's something wonderful!
It is a wonderful thing that we can start to work with.
Inner strength is an instinct.
It's an ancient animal survival instinct.
When your inner strength is unleashed, activated, or awakened by hope and challenge, that hope gives you back your self-confidence. It gives you a special, wonderful strength and confidence. It also gives you abilities beyond your wildest dreams.
I do need to warn you about challenge, however. Never awaken inner strength in a child or young adult if you're not going to challenge them with something beyond their abilities, because this is where we face competition and delinquency - the same things that cause terror and uneasiness in communities, and that steal the youth, addict them, turn them into consumers, and attract them with empty things. They're challenged by money and to reach vain empty goals.
That same delinquency uses the power and the strength of hope to turn them wicked and to deceive them.
So you must not merely challenge them beyond their abilities, you must challenge them with hope.
And speaking of hope, here you can see pictures of some Medellín slums where we've worked for many years, about 30 years or a little more,
because this is the best way to express these three factors: inner strength, challenge and hope.
In the late '80s and early '90s, we went to the neighborhoods of Medellin to challenge the children with symphonic musical instruments.
In other words, a challenge that was not just beyond their abilities at the time, but also a challenge for us because the last thing they wanted in the slums of Medellin was classical music and symphonic instruments.
There was a significant gap, not only social but also cultural. For them, that kind of music was rich people's music. "Their" music, not "ours."
Anyway, we managed to reach the communities, we were gaining ground, and we were given the opportunity to start this challenging but wonderful task.
In a very short time, children were filling the music schools.
It was like their second home.
They would spend all their time there, not only to play their instruments, but also to do their homework and spend time with their friends.
We soon gave ourselves the huge challenge of reaching a musical level that had never been reached before in the country, ever.
Either they could achieve that or we were wasting our time.
And they accepted that challenge.
And those were symphonic instruments, which also broke a historical paradigm - that learners had to start at a certain age or with certain skills.
Here it was the same for everyone.
To make a long story short, things advanced so rapidly, so phenomenally, that soon we had achieved that musical level and even higher. So we set an even bigger challenge: We had to go to Europe to play European music for Europeans in the places where it was composed. We had to take the challenge to show that these children, perhaps poor but humble Medellín kids with their torn jeans, raggedy shoes, and instruments made of sticks and tin - we didn't have high-quality instruments - could do much more with music than what anyone could imagine, thanks only to their inner strength and great capacity of conviction. And they made it.
They played in Spain and Italy, in palaces, in theaters, and on stages. They made it to the Vatican - the photo you see here - to play for Pope John Paul II.
I think that's an impossible task. You don't see or do that every day. And children from poor neighborhoods don't often get that opportunity.
But they earned it through hard work, showing what they are made of and what they are capable of doing.
And something rather funny happened.
We didn't know this, but Italian television broadcast the Pope's concert live to the whole world with free signal for all countries.
We had no idea.
We didn't even know how we got there, much less that they were broadcasting it.
The fact is that before we left, nobody believed us.
Nobody believed the kids when they said they were going to play for the Pope.
So nobody from the media went with us.
We had to take our own journalists to at least take pictures for posterity.
It felt a bit lonely, to say the least.
The funny thing is that the broadcast was free and for the whole world, and in Colombia, the country woke up seeing these kids, a small orchestra of just 160 members. The country came alive over these images on TV, everyone was excited, and the media started calling. I wanted to tell them, "But we told you we were coming to see the Pope!"
"Yes, but that's impossible!"
And I said, "Really?
I don't know. We're here!"
(Laughter) I mean, this is the Pope, isn't it? (Laughter) I could ask him to come to the phone if you want."
At the same time, the signal reached other places,
and the next day, the kids had to perform in Venice for the inauguration of an exhibition of sculptures and paintings by Master Fernando Botero.
When we arrived in Venice, they all took off and ran away. I didn't know where they were, so I went running to look for them, and I found them in St. Mark's Square, as relaxed and happy as can be because people had seen them on TV with the Pope, and they thought they were very famous, and that it was quite something to have them in Venice. And there they were - happily signing autographs. (Laughter) Signing autographs and taking pictures, and I said, “Guys, what's going on?"
“Juangui, we're signing autographs. We're very famous in Venice." And they'd never been there before in their lives.
Those were the same bright, exasperating kids who climbed on everything, who when we let them loose, there was no way to control them. Those same naive, innocent, wonderful beings who when we opened a hotel room, we found four of them in the bathtub because it was the first time they'd ever seen a bathtub.
Others hadn't been able to shower because they didn't know how to open the faucets.
And of course there was the one who, after much effort, managed to open the bathtub faucet instead of the shower, and had to bend down awkwardly to be able to bathe.
Or the one who came out of the ocean disappointed and said, "Juangui, bro, that water is really salty!"
(Laughter) Or the boy who was late to one of the rehearsals, and later when we asked him to explain what happened, told us in tears, "It's just that my brother and I have only one pair of jeans, and my brother left early and took the jeans."
That is to say, a contrast of fascinating, wonderful things.
These same children were able to do all these things that for the rest of us seem impossible.
And that's the million dollar question - How did they do it?
How was all this achieved?
How were they able to achieve it?
And the answer is, "Since they didn't know it was impossible, they did it."
They never knew it was impossible.
Now, in order to explain inner strength, hope, and challenge, it wasn't the impossibility of it all that moved them to do this. It was the inner strength, the force of hope in each and every one, because they were unaware it was impossible.
Here is a picture of that small pioneer orchestra that traveled and achieved what no juvenile or professional orchestra at that time had ever achieved before.
More generations will come. We're trying to reach more generations of children from slum neighborhoods and villages. We want to give them that strength of hope so that each one can see a better world.
The strength of hope is like a log still burning in the depths of your being.
Can you imagine what a child sees amidst poverty, violence and social decay?
Imagine that child surrounded, fenced in by these conditions ...
What does he aspire to? What does he see?
He sees nothing.
He abandons his dreams, his hopes, and feels that all the possibilities and opportunities are denied to him because he thinks he was born into a world that is against him, for no reason, as if it were his fault.
But he doesn't know he has this inner strength, just like we do.
Yes, there are moments, even for us who have a better life, there are moments of weariness, times where we just want to throw in the towel and give up.
But then there's that log burning in the depths of your being. There's a fire that will never leave you.
No matter what your outside surroundings may be like, inside you that log still burns.
Perhaps hidden under the ashes, perhaps silent and still, but it's there, protecting the treasure of capabilities and possibilities that at some point you're going to need so you can take that final leap to overcome adversity, to fill your lungs with breath, to take a deep gasp of air and break the surface, and go beyond.
This photo condenses the lives and stories of thousands of children who had the opportunity to truly experience this inner strength.
I don't know where many of them are today. Some have followed their own paths, leaving music behind. What I want to say by showing you this picture is that everybody in it, regardless of where they are or what they've become, are good people. They're responsible and hard-working people who inspire others. Their lives changed, and they're helping change the world.
Can you imagine thousands and thousands more children experiencing and knowing the strength of hope?
Can you imagine thousands of kids in small villages, slums, and cities who could experience and see the world like you and me?
Feel the world? Feel life and opportunities?
Can you imagine the power of the strength of hope?
It's easy,
They provide the inner strength. We just have to provide the hope.
They say that hope is the last thing you lose.
No, no.
Hope is the only thing left when everything else is gone.
That's why it's hope. Because it's never, ever lost.
The strength of hope is the strength of God in us!
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Can you imagine that we could create a SmartCity from this microchip I'm holding between my fingers?
Yes, that's right, a SmartCity of the future.
Designed and manufactured by its own inhabitants.
Made to improve their quality of life.
But, can you imagine how we could manage to do this?
Well, thanks to this simple microchip any one of us could have an influence on companies and governments in matters such as digital fabrication, energy efficiency, healthcare, culture, or even agriculture.
In short, the pillars which sustain the so-called SmartCities.
I'll show you some examples shortly.
But I'm no urban planner, I'm no mayor or doctor, not even a farmer.
I am Soya milk "maker".
I'm fascinated by technological projects used for social purposes.
I believe that in cities one should find the tools needed so that everyone could manufacture whatever they want.
Spaces open to the public where those production tools would be found, available to everybody.
These spaces already exist.
They are the so-called FabLabs:
Digital fabrication labs.
Fab Labs are a global network of interconnected spaces where we can find the same group of machines with which to manufacture just about anything.
But they are not just physical spaces, it's also a community where collaborators can freely share their creations online with the whole world.
Those creations spread through the net and are manufactured anywhere in the world, no matter their place of origin.
At the same time, those creations gradually improve, because they adapt to the demands of each place.
Ultimately, FabLabs are just factories of projects
which is the driving force of the people who constitute this community.
For example, mine.
The first time I stepped into a FabLab was in 2011 in Barcelona's FabLab.
The truth is that I didn't quite understand its meaning or its use.
All I know is that, after visiting it frequently for three months, we came out with this project:
A house made out of wood, a hundred percent self-sufficient. Thanks to solar energy.
The collaboration of the architects, of the project's sponsoring company and of Barcelona's FabLab was frankly very productive, and we managed to build it in less than 28 days without anchoring it to the floor.
However, most importantly, and the single thing I found really special, was the combination between software and computer-controlled machines which were used there.
It turns out that they were using freeware:
computer programs which everybody can download for free.
Moreover, they were doing this by way of parametric design, which is a kind of design that allows three-dimensional modeling in an intuitive and precise way.
That was when I realized that this kind of software was replacing the old design programs I had always used,
furthermore, the components' prototyping was extremely fast.
We could go from digital to physical space in a matter of minutes.
They were designed and manufactured almost instantly.
Precisely this, this digital design of a component and its subsequent physical manufacture with a computer-controlled machine is what we call digital fabrication.
The most common and famous machine, found in most FabLabs, and the one with which this house was made is the milling machine, or CNC machine. As you can see, it's just an enormous machine which enables us to cut very large materials and which is connected to the computer and controlled by us.
After working in this project I became much more interested in the world of FabLabs and, together with some colleagues, decided to start up a FabLab in front of the beach: the Beach Lab.
The first machine the Beach Lab received was the laser cutter: a machine that allows you to cut just about any material, as the laser burns its surface.
Thanks to this machine we can make, for example, decorative objects, sneakers, lamps, dresses, even engrave food, like these Japanese folks have done.
Yes, that's it.
This is Tokyo's FabCafe: a coffee shop which is unique in the world, where it is possible to have a coffee and at the same time manufacture your own creations.
FabCafes are fun, enjoyable coffee shops,
a far cry from the seriousness and sobriety we often associate with technology.
And they understand personal manufacturing as something which should be creative, but above all fun.
This idea fit our FabLab perfectly, so there's a FabCafe too in our Beach Lab.
In FabCafes, all sorts of cultural events are boosted:
gastronomic events, social events, technological and photography workshops, even concerts.
Who said that personal manufacturing wouldn't be able to affect people's cultural life?
It's an equally unique and entertaining way of getting to know the technology of personal manufacturing and, at the same time, why not? of blending into the popular culture of a country.
Some of the components designed to support this bamboo structure, which you can see here, were designed and manufactured by means of one of the most well-known machines found in FabLabs.
We're talking about the 3D printer.
To make it work, first of all, we have to design or scan an object in three dimensions, and after that, we send it to the printer, which will warm up the plastic and will deposit it layer by layer, as if we were decorating a cake. We'll be able to choose its color, the hardness we prefer, until we obtain the final object.
Some of the components you are seeing have been designed and manufactured right here in Costa Rica, in the FabLab of the investigation and innovation center of Veritas University.
Well then, the evolution of materials and 3D printers will allow us to see, en masse, and in not many years' time: 3D printed cars, houses, entire buildings, even human prostheses.
Lunar stations as well.
Yes, you're hearing it right.
There's no limit to 3D printing.
However, above all, 3D printing is going to make small-scale manufacturing possible in a closer, more local environment, in an environment where you won't need to import the desired object from a production point located on the other side of the world.
We won't be as dependant on manufacturers, instead, the production will become increasingly disseminated.
But do you honestly believe 3D printers, laser cutters or milling machines are going to be, in themselves, the solution to our problems?
I believe that technology should never be the solution to the citizens' problems, but rather the good use we make of it.
And that's where education plays a fundamental role in society.
What makes FabLabs a factory of dreams capable of creating or inventing just about anything is the combination of learning while you innovate to have a fun.
And here I've written the only requirement you'll need to be able to enter a FabLab and collaborate with us.
As you can see, there's none.
No previous experience is required, and neither is having a higher education.
The value of FabLabs lies precisely in the diverse experiences of the members which create it.
They are places with diverse people and, above all, multidisciplinary.
The most tangible example is the workshops we do for children, the so-called FabKids:
workshops specializing in approaching young children to personal manufacture.
In these workshops, children learn how to design electronic circuits, they solder the components themselves, they design their first wooden structures, their first sunglasses, even their first skateboards.
But apart from these kind of workshops, which are informal, Boston MIT launched a program we called FabAcademy,
a course which offers the main notions of personal manufacture. After five months and a little bit of effort, anyone, you yourselves, if you wish, can become experts in digital fabrication.
Furthermore, there's no need to travel to the United States, ever, since you can take the course through the FabLabs network.
Via videoconference, teachers and students will meet up on a weekly basis and they will have to pass several tasks by way of their ingenuity and creativity.
While they study with us, these same students may participate and collaborate in the projects carried out by FabLab. In our case, our municipality asked us for guidance and research in order to do a Living Lab:
a small Smart City which will display a sort of wireless technology capable of picking up a series of very important data for the community, equipment designed and manufactured by its own citizens.
For instance, they will be able to pick up the municipality's environmental data, noise pollution in the busiest streets, the free spots in the city's parking lots.
We'll also be able to monitor buses, and even the power consumption of buildings and street lighting.
The municipality's wine industry will also benefit from digital fabrication, since we'll be able to pick up parameters related to the humidity of the earth, temperature, or wind, which are fundamental in wine production or any other crop.
This concept, in which the digital equipments we manufacture communicate with one another so that we can receive that information afterwards, whether at home or on our SmartPhone, is called the Internet of Things.
But the main goal of the project, its final goal, isn't covering the streets with sensors just because.
I'm sure that there are cities with more pressing priorities than that one.
But in our case, apart from generating a series of data which will be very important for the community, we encourage the citizens to participate in an active and direct way.
In a country such as mine, so stricken by unemployment, initiatives like these encourage employment regeneration and the creation of new jobs, and that, to us, is the most important thing.
Hence, FabLabs are places where governments are greatly supported.
Places where power is given to the citizens,
the power to do almost anything and reinvent their own professions. Places committed to the urban environment, where solutions are found from the ground up, and where everyone can access those production tools.
Another attribute which stands out in the world of FabLabs is that with one FabLab we can manage to make another FabLab.
Machines creating other machines.
Even in the most remote areas of the planet.
This is precisely the aim of a project called floating FabLab:
the first FabLab in the world that will sail the Amazon River and will grant local communities access to the tools which will allow them to face their daily water, energy, or education challenges.
Can you imagine that thanks to this ship we're already designing we could manage to help these communities even where Internet access is scarce?
This project, led by Peru's FabLab, and many other Latin American FabLabs and from the rest of the planet, is a unique example of the great collaboration that exists within the FabLab community.
Finally, FabLabs are places where petitions can be received from any kind of company, be it small or very big, with greatly advanced technology, from universities, municipalities, or any person who wants to manufacture their dreams.
And it's precisely there, in that convergence between very big companies with greatly advanced technology and investigations carried out by citizens, where the true science of making things emerges.
Let me ask you something.
By way of handling this simple microchip I've shown you before, and the digital fabrication machines you've seen, do you believe you'd be able to manufacture something, not just for yourselves, but something that would have a positive impact on your city?
Do you believe you will one day live in a city that could be just a little bit better?
I hope and trust that the answer is yes.
Thank you very much. (Applause)
How come Airbnb and Uber could lead their industries without having a car or apartment in their possession?
They did it by building trust in people.
And if we think of it, many of the most important companies nowadays are based on that.
They are based on building collaborative economies using people's reputation.
But there is a problem: they keep our information.
But that's going to change because about ten years ago, from the hand of bitcoin, the first book was born, the first unhackable book, in which we are able to not only record money transfers, but all the things we do in our life.
Same as with Uber ratings but with the difference that we are in control.
With that log we will be able to build our blockchain life.
And with that blockchain life we will be able to interact with anyone on the planet, we will be able to build our own collaborative economies, and it will be the beginning of the end of frontiers.
It will give birth to a truly global nation; to a human nation, so I invite you to meet again and share our blockchain life. A life without borders, without limits and with many opportunities.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Have you ever lived the experience of time beginning to flow differently?
Have you ever lived the experience of time beginning to flow differently?
Noticing the completely open space.
Noticing the fully present body.
Noticing life, completely real.
Noticing that something extraordinary is happening.
A moment to let go.
A moment of total dedication.
A moment of full presence.
A moment of full presence.
Noticing the senses in a new gradient.
Noticing the smile drawing your gesture.
Noticing intuition clearing your mind.
Noticing that you are alive, happy, and awake.
This is mindfulness;
the art of living consciously.
And this poetry was what I felt when we had the visit from Jon Kabat-Zinn, the founder of this clinic, where I studied 11 years ago.
And he came to Barcelona.
And he came to visit us.
And we were lucky to have his company for a while and inaugurate our space.
For me it was a great moment, and then I sat down and tried to pick up a little of what he had felt.
Although I was also giving account then.
Jon Kabat-Zinn has the gift of having been taught a monastic and traditional discipline, as Buddhist meditation is, which was born to alleviate human suffering, and had practiced it in a hospital,
because in hospitals is where people suffer.
And he offered this resource to those people that medicine could not solve their problem.
People with chronic diseases or chronic conditions, the diseases that had to do with the mind and body, and gave rise to a new discipline, what is now called mind-body medicine, which has to do with aspects of the mind which affect the body, aspects of the body which affect the mind, and how it has to do with behavior.
Thanks to this innovative effort of 30 years ago, now we have enough scientific evidence, which explains how to direct the attention, consciousness, and meditation having effects in different areas.
The most known is the field of well-being.
When people are aware, we take more care; we pay attention and that affects health.
We take care of health, reduce stress, and we want a little more.
And that also has a positive effect at work, doesn't it?
When a person is well, he does his job better.
So what we have seen is that this development of consciousness allows the improvement of the attention, concentration, and reduction of errors.
There is another benefit that has to do with personal relationships.
When someone is well, and does his tasks well, then his relationships with others is better.
It decreases the aggressiveness, improves empathy, And that combination facilitates collaborative relationships.
Something that, as we have seen before, is innate to humans.
So this triad is very powerful.
And it deals with how being aware, we can improve personal well-being, have better quality at work, and improve relationships.
And I'm going to give you an example of each of these areas.
Personal well-being often is affected by trends that we have in the mind of going back to negative things.
In psychology it is called rumination.
And it is an obsessive thought, which causes discomfort.
These thoughts can be reduced with the practice of consciousness, because one begins to be aware of those thoughts and he is reducing them and here we have a chart of research we did in 2010 where in an eight-week program of mindfulness, the associated discomfort was reduced with rumination and lasted during the follow up.
We also saw improvement in the management of emotions.
This is more research which we did later in 2014,
here in Barcelona, with primary care physicians, and we saw that the emotional disturbance the doctors had dealing with the patients could be reduced with an eight-week workout of mindfulness and that diminished in three dimensions: in the tendency to anxiety, to depression, and hostility, which are the negative emotions that were associated with their work.
And improved activity and decreased fatigue.
And that's what we all want; how can we have more activity and be less tired, if we decrease the amount of energy that we dedicate to managing the emotional alteration?
And the third example has to do with how we can improve the relationships with others and make better decisions.
And we can articulate that with an array, in which we observe the internal environment in two dimensions.
There is a dimension that has to do with the degree of energy a person has at any given time and there is another dimension that has to do with the fact that the moment is pleasant or unpleasant.
And to give some examples that can orientate us we have a moment of relaxation when there is little energy and it is nice or we call it excitement when there is pleasant energy.
When there is energy and it is unpleasant you can have a moment of anxiety, or it may be a moment of frustration or anger.
And when we have a moment of a little unpleasant energy, then we call it boredom.
This would be a bit the periphery of this map of feelings, but the most interesting thing is in the center, because there is an optimal zone that is not exactly the center - I have put it a little as I have it for me, but each one has to know how to identify it.
And in that area is where we take the best decisions, in that area is where we listen better to our children, where we have a better relationship as a couple, where we can operate in a team, where it unfolds the best version of yourself.
Not too excited, not too relaxed,
not too bored, not too anxious and angry.
Then, the key is to first give yourself the account of where the internal medium is, and resources to be able to recover the area when there are events or situations in life of one that get you out of that area.
And a great resource for it is the practice of mindfulness.
Mindfulness - perhaps some know the English word, because it's a word which is normally used.
But I like it more as it is expressed in Chinese which has to do with two ideas: The idea of presence and the idea of heart.
Heart as the affective part of the mind. In Chinese they would say that mindfulness - full consciousness in Castilian - is when a person is present from the heart, is living 100% in the moment.
And that's easy to say, but it requires a little training.
And training has three skills that we have to try. The first has to do with having an intention.
The human brain has a tendency to disperse, to go from one idea to the other easily.
With it we have to cultivate an intention that has to be more present, of being more connected with what happens in reality, moment by moment.
Do not let too much travel into issues of the future and the past.
The second has to do with cultivating an attitude of kindness to life, and kindness to oneself.
Many times we face reality and we believe that by struggling with reality, it can be changed.
Actually the things that happen are already happening and it is easier to adapt to what is, and, in that way, establish a relationship that allows us maybe change the things of the future.
So we have the component of intention and kindness, and then comes the most important which is to connect with experience.
The experience in the body and the experience of being present.
Actually what helps us to be present is to remember the body and feel the body.
And that is much easier with practice.
So if you think it too, we're going to now do a little practice.
I'm not going to project anything, so I invite you to imagine, and close your eyes for a moment, and seek a position that gives you
a sense of dignity, of stability.
The posture, namely.
And with the inner vision, look at that position.
Notice how it feels to inhabit a human body.
Look at the contact with the chair, the feet with the ground.
Notice what the experience is on the chest, the shoulders, on the face.
Notice the effect of drawing a slight smile on the face.
If you simply change the gesture with a smile, change the experience.
And then, we will pay attention to a wonderful process that we have which is called 'breathing.'
And watch as the breath flows through the body.
How is there an air intake we call inspiration, a moment that seems to give the return.
And another moment we call exhalation.
And how this process involves the body, but it also has an effect on the mind.
Try to pay attention to the breath in some place that you can follow it well.
In the nostrils or in the movement of the abdomen.
Accompanying with the attention to the breathing.
As if this moment was the most important.
As if life depended on it.
If you notice that the attention goes to the future or to the past, kindly return it to bring the breath.
At this point,
maybe you notice that the breath is changing a little.
Perhaps you notice that the mind and body calm down.
(Chimes bells) You can open your eyes and simply notice what has been the effect of these minutes paying attention to your breathing.
And you will realize that when we practice exercises that connect us with the present moment, a species appears of natural internal well-being.
Tranquility.
Relaxation.
It's like the body recovers that harmony a little,
and what we now know is that this well-being that comes from the practice of mindfulness is circuits, which we could say brain interns, which have the neuroplastic capacity.
This means that if you practice, you get better at it.
Same as playing the violin or learn a language.
So these exercises become in a way to create wellness internally.
When one takes the habit of dedicating some minutes a day to do these exercises, he begins to generate a source of inner well-being.
And this source of inner well-being, it also affects other engines of inner well-being that we have.
And another is resilience.
Resilience is the ability to live in adversity, to function in adversity and even grow in adversity.
In order to be resilient we need to be aware.
Adversity itself can bring down a person, but when we put awareness and resources for managing, then we start to develop natural mechanisms of resilience -- as we have seen in the example of the doctors -- regulate negative emotions, manage depression, anxiety, and hostility, which have to do with the profession.
And this also causes generosity appearence and kindness in a natural way.
Because when we are calmer, we are in a situation to be most grateful, we are less bothered by things, we worry more for others and we are more generous.
Kindness and generosity are other drivers of well-being.
We are social animals and those practices that do good to others make us feel good.
And this has to do with the latter motor of well-being, which is the mood.
When we are optimistic, we see challenges, we see opportunities, we look at the good things that are in life, then we are also launching the motors of well-being.
And you see that they are related with each other.
When a person is more conscious, he also begins to develop this kindness naturally. He improves the mood because he realizes it does not make sense to be complaining all the time about some things and others that cannot be changed.
He realizes that human relationship are more enriching when we talk about the things that connect us and not what disconnects us.
When we take care, we are not bothered.
Then this begins to generate a form different from living.
It is not a new thing.
The ancients already knew.
Michel de Montaigne,
who is a well-known French philosopher,
wrote this poetry that says, "When I dance, I dance.
When I sleep, I sleep.
And when I walk through a forest, if my thought distract towards distant affairs - which is what we call mindfulness, I lead him back to the road, to the beauty of my solitude."
He practiced mindfulness in his own way.
And this idea has now been taken up by a car brand that has made a very interesting announcement, which is precisely run here in Barcelona and we are going to see now: (Video) (Music) Narrator: When you work, you work.
When you rest, you rest.
When you pedal, you pedal.
When you dance, you dance.
When you jump, you jump.
And when you scream, you scream.
If you have to fight, fight.
When you help, help.
And when you try, really try.
When you advance, move forward.
When you play, play.
(Music) And when you dream, dream.
When you win, win.
And when you lose, especially when you lose, lose.
When you drive, drive.
I think it's great that a brand of successful cars makes an announcement about, 'When you lose, you lose."
It seems as though everything is winning, but that is not the case.
Losing is very important, because if one loses with awareness, he learns.
If one gains with conscience, he does not believe it.
If you play with consciousnesses, you enjoy it.
If one rests consciously, he really rests.
And the problem we have is that we tried to make everything mixed.
So the key to me is: Losing or winning is not the important thing. The important thing is to live consciously because that is the most interesting thing in life.
So thank you so much for your attention. (Applause)
If you are a high school graduate, you’ve been through 10 seconds like these, 3 million times.
(Applause) When I was 15, I drew this  on my bedroom wall.
I'm stuck behind bars, thinking about my love of aeroplanes.
That sums up how I felt right then.
I felt trapped. Trapped in school.
Nearly all my friends, whatever the school, felt the same way,
I finished school last year and now I want to understand those feelings I had.
And I think I felt that way because every day was more or less the same.
For most subjects the lesson went: enter the classroom and sit listening to the teacher copying what they wrote on the board, white letters on a green background, into the file on the desk, to regurgitate it in the test.
Every day, hour after hour, for several years.
Board, file, test.
Board, file, test.
Maybe I felt trapped because I felt like at school there was no room to learn things that interested me.
I always liked a lot of things like the aeroplanes in the drawing.
At home I loved learning about them.
Why they fly, what makes them how they are.
I did a lot of research online and tried making aeroplanes out of paper and cardboard and reaching conclusions.
But there was no place for that in school.
My life was split into school and real life; school and learning.
The same thing happened to a few of my friends.
The one who liked music and playing the guitar, the one who liked drawing comics and inventing characters, we felt like school was robbing us of the time to do what we enjoyed.
When we were in school, all we wanted was for the bell to ring so it would be home time.
But when we left we were often tired, exhausted.
We were zombies who didn't feel like doing anything except watching TV or messing about on our phones.
People are thinking about how to improve things but lots of the changes suggested are big and take time.
In the meantime, what do we do for the students who are at school today?
It got me thinking: when didn't I feel trapped?
I realised that sometimes this feeling went away, in some teachers' classes or during some of the activities.
For example, when we were practising for the maths olympics, which was very different to maths as a subject.
A lot of people think only some subjects are about memorising things by heart,
like history.
But these days, every subject is about memorising things by heart, even mathematics.
I didn't enjoy maths as a subject at all.
I even did quite badly at it and sometimes didn't go.
In maths, we did things like this.
Every problem had the same solution, by chanting: plus times minus equals minus, minus times minus equals... Audience: plus.
Addition becomes subtraction, multiplication becomes... Audience: division.
And to learn how to do these exercises, you had to do them over and over until you could remember.
A few years ago, during one of these lessons, I asked what all of this was for.
My classmates told me:
"To pass maths. What else would it be for?"
(Laughter) But the maths olympics was nothing like the subject.
Since anyone could enter, a friend suggested I sign up, and I went to check it out.
What I saw was something very different to what I expected.
Nothing about calculating 'x', no doing anything robotically.
You had to think and work things out.
I'll tell you one of the first puzzles
I saw of this type:
I love it.
You've got 9 identical coins that all weigh the same, except one, and we don't know which, that weighs a bit more. And you can't tell
by picking them up.
We have a set of balances for weighing them. We can put as many coins on as we want and find out which side weighs more, or if they weigh the same.
How do we work out which coin weighs more by using the balance only twice?
To solve this type of problem I had to try different ideas, fail, be creative and share ideas with other people, just like in real life.
And so we learnt maths, not by memorising, but by learning to think.
Sometimes my friends would ask me to tell them one of these problems, to see what they were like.
I liked telling them ones like the coins and scales one.
But what happened to them was the same as what happened to me the first time I heard it: I don't know, I never saw this at school, I don't know how to solve it.
That was when I first realised that school gets us used to not being able to solve problems if we can't see the solution straight away, and there's nothing we can do except give up.
I don't think real life works like that.
There were times, too, when I felt free in class.
I remember the teachers who did things differently, who broke away from the board, file, test method.
Like when we had history debates.
We chose a situation.
Half the class were for and the other half were against.
That brought history to life and it taught us how to think, how to put ourselves in other people's shoes and to debate.
I also felt free at school when I could choose things I enjoyed.
I was lucky that my school got us ready for the maths olympics and offered some optional seminars, like astronomy.
Once they asked us to put sticks in the sun measure their shadows, and work out for ourselves the Earth's diameter.
But at school there was rarely the freedom to choose.
During those 12 years I had to read a huge number of books.
But I didn't choose to read any of them.
And that happens in other subjects too.
We kids almost never choose what we learn.
What would've happened if I'd been able to choose some of what I read?
And if I'd had the freedom to learn about something I chose?
I'm sure that if students were given more chances to choose at school, and if classes gave us more real-life challenges, and less board, file, test, school would actually be a place we'd enjoy being.
Every now and then, I hear people say that school doesn't need to be a place students enjoy being.
"Students go to school to learn, then they go home and do what they like."
Or: "They go to university afterwards and choose the degree they want."
But this way of thinking puts students off school.
If we're not motivated to learn there, or to do what we want, then where are we motivated?
What also happens is that when it's time to choose what to do next, we've already turned into zombies.
We don't follow our hearts; we choose by process of elimination because school turns us off.
If we want to, we can change things.
All of us: schools, students and parents.
We can find lots more opportunities like these.
Opportunities to make school about more than just copying and repeating, so it's not just 'to pass.'
And instead of board, file, test, it can be choose, explore, learn.
And so, instead of school turning us into zombies, it opens our eyes and really makes us want to learn.
Learn how to compose music, how to draw cartoons or why aeroplanes fly.
Thank you!
(Applause)
Once Anaxagoras was asked what the point of being born was;
He answered without hesitation: “The investigation of sun, moon and heaven”.
I do not know if we have all come the world for that.
Luckily, he did come to that.
And I think I did too.
I always admired him.
Somehow, we are similar.
Studying the stars brought problems for us both.
Some want to send me to court for saying that the Earth isn't fixed at the center of the universe, but travels around the Sun.
It was even worse for him, he was sentenced to exile for defending some revolutionary ideas about the Sun.
And he could never come back.
Do you know what that is?
Anaxagoras died in exile.
I always wanted to visit his grave.
But for some reason, my teacher Strato, refused to take me.
Anaxagoras had a very good idea.
He led the way for those who would come later: he proposed that the Moon has no light of its own, but that it receives it from the Sun and that this explains its different phases.
A simple and great idea, but at the same time very fruitful because when Anaxagoras suddenly was able to get it, he immediately understood what happened during the eclipses: In eclipses in which the Moon disappears, it is the shadow of the Earth projected on the Moon which makes it disappear.
In the eclipses in which the Sun disappears, the Moon comes between us and the Sun and covers it.
Anaxagoras went a little further: If the Moon covers the Sun, the Moon is between Earth and the Sun. This means that the Moon is closer!
What is more, as seen from the Earth, the Moon and the Sun appear to have the same size.
But if the Sun is farther away, the Sun is bigger than the Moon!
A genius, Anaxagoras!
When I heard these explanations for the first time I was amazed.
Not all agree, not even today, but I do; I quickly embraced them.
Isn't it incredible what can reason do when with order and wit it gets to solve problems?
Anaxagoras made a lot of progress, but I wanted to go a step further: How farther away is the Sun?
How much bigger is it?
Is it bigger than the Earth?
Anaxagoras had said that the Sun was very, very big, even much bigger than the entire Peloponnese.
But, how much?
To answer this question I had to take a very bold step, in which I ask you to join me.
It's no longer enough to apply logic to astronomical problems.
Geometry must be applied, with all its rigor.
Yes, I know we use it to measure and calculate things here on Earth; -in fact, that means "geo-metry"- but why not use it for calculations in the sky, in the realm of the gods?
Does it sound crazy?
Let's apply geometry to the sky.
Let's assume -- that the Earth, the Moon and the Sun. form a triangle.
T is Earth, L is Moon and S, which is further away, is the Sun.
T-L represents the Earth-Moon distance. T-S, the Earth-Sun distance.
If we knew two angles of this triangle, we could get the ratio between the sides and so, between the distances.
If you do not know much about this, I recommend you read a book that just came out.
It's called "The Elements" by a certain Euclid.
Quite good.
There is a small detail with the fifth statement that doesn't make sense completely to me,
but for this it will serve you. The angle T between the Moon and the Sun is very easy to obtain: I put a pipe pointing towards the Sun, another pipe pointing to the Moon, and I measure the angle between them.
Done.
But I still have to know another angle.
I can't travel to the Moon or to the Sun to measure it from there.
I found the solution: the phases of the Moon are directly dependent on the angle between the Sun and the Earth seen from the Moon.
In a crescent, when we see exactly half a Moon, the angle L is a right angle.
If at that moment, we measure the angle T, it is almost a right angle; it lacks one thirtieth of a right angle to be a right one.
Done!
We are only missing some calculations or drawing accurately on the sand and so we get the ratio among the sides.
In this way, the Sun is between 18 to 20 times farther than the Moon.
And as both, apparently look the same size from the Earth, the diameter of the Sun would have to be 18 to 20 times larger.
But we are yet to know the other half of the problem.
We know the Sun and the Moon's measures; but, what about the Earth?
Is it bigger than the Sun, is it smaller than the Moon or is it between the two?
The solution to this problem didn't come up that fast.
I remember I'd spend hours and hours walking back and forth in my house, thinking about this issue.
When one night, I was surprised by my own shadow that fire projected on the wall and watched how it shrank as I approached the wall and I walked away from the fire.
And then I said, My Zeus!
The size of the shadow of an object is proportional to its size.
And it depends only on the distance between the light source and the wall.
The Sun is the fire and the Moon is the wall!
In a lunar eclipse, I know that the shadow of the Earth reflects on the Moon, the only thing I have to do is to know the size of the shadow; the distances we already know.
Brilliant!
In each lunar eclipse, Earth shows us its size and we did not see it.
I know I am 19 steps away from the fire and one from the wall.
Let's suppose that the Moon has this size.
The shadow of the Earth equals two Moons, we observe this in lunar eclipses.
The only thing I have to do is to measure from this distance what size of Earth generates this shadow.
Do you follow me?
Would you come with me and try this?
Let's see!
No.
Let's look at this other one.
Neither.
This one.
Yes.
Practically... for the Earth to generate this shadow at this distance, it must be three times bigger than the Moon.
It is three times bigger than the Moon and six times smaller than the Sun!
I know that the values might not be accurate, but it doesn't matter.
What matters is that, thanks to this method, without leaving this room, we know the size of the Sun, the Moon and the Earth.
Notice the potential geometry has: How many things we will find out if we consistently apply geometry to the sky?
Some weeks ago, I was walking with my teacher Strato, like many other evenings, along the beaches of Lampsacus.
The Sun behind us began to set.
In front of us, a huge full moon was rising from the horizon.
As we walked, our shadows lengthened more and more.
Like two arrows pointing to the Moon
It was getting colder, but the Sun still heated behind us.
At this time, my teacher paused and said: "I want you to see something before dusk."
I followed him, nervous.
My heart started beating very fast.
I was feeling that this so-expected moment was coming for me.
We didn't walk much, but for me it was an eternity.
When we got there, and I saw for the first time, I sat on a rock and I began to weep.
I cried like a child.
My teacher said nothing.
He waited for me to calm down and together we read the epitaph: "Here lies Anaxagoras, who reached the farthest bounds of truth in the research of the sky."
My teacher made a brief silence, looked at me and said: "This epitaph is no longer true.
You, Aristarchus, have brought this limit even further."
Thank you.
(Applause)
(Anastasia Garcia) Good afternoon everyone.
(Man) Anastasia, it's a pleasure to have you here at this first TEDxBogota event for women.
We're excited to have you here, a woman full of courage, leadership, and desire for change, who was able to travel to India and learn about solar engineering -- something few people in the auditorium may know or understand --
and you did that in another language, emerged in another culture, entirely different from your own, the Wayuu.
It required a lot from you, in your personal development and your community's development.
Why did you accept this challenge,
this urge to travel so far to learn something new?
(AG) I wanted to go to a country I haven't been to before.
Planning it was a motivation for me, envisioning something different from what I have in my community for the well-being of my Wayuu village.
I wanted to obtain knowledge in solar engineering for mothers and grandmothers living in rural areas in the various regions of this country,
in order to bring change to our communities, that can lead to a better life for everyone.
(Man) You traveled 16,000 km to a completely different culture.
I understand that you don't speak English.
(AG) No.
It was the first time that I boarded a plane.
(Laughter) (Man) How were you able to understand what they were teaching you?
And how did others understand what you were saying?
(AG) By using signs.
By listening and repeating many times -- a hundred times, like children do.
We would write everything down into a notebook.
We would draw the tools they showed us.
The drawings weren't great -- I'm not a good artist -- so I gave them numbers in order to remember them.
I couldn't use English, so I learned numbers and colors.
I learned colors in order to associate them with numbers.
That's how we learned.
Repeating them a thousand times a day, morning til evening, all day, from Monday to Friday.
(Man) That method was based on an Indian education model, to include illiterate and semi-illiterate women, mothers and grandmothers.
And the technique works primarily with numbers, colors and signs.
What happened if someone didn't understand? What was the process to ensure ideas could flow, and for you to be able to learn solar engineering?
(AG) The same way they were teaching us using signs, we would sign among classmates, and be able to understand each other that way.
That's how it flowed. (Man) It's a little like returning to infancy: signs, colors, gestures.
(AG) Yes, gestures and movements.
(Man) This is clearly a transforming experience -- an opportunity for many women worldwide who are discriminated against or undervalued by their communities.
It's a program offered by the Barefoot College to provide education, which can then be shared among the communities.
This program is solely for women from around the world. Who were your friends?
Who did you meet there?
Who did you get along with so far from home?
We were 40 women from different countries around the world.
From here, Panamanians and Nicaraguans.
There were Africans, and women from Myanmar.
I made friends with women from Zanzibar, the coastal area of Africa.
We got along well, they were very funny; they soon made me forget that I was far from my family, my country.
They made me feel at home. (Man) Surely, there were
some sad or lonely moments. How did you cope with that?
How did you overcome depression?
(AG) After four or five months -- it was six in total --
I felt far away from my people, my family, children, and grandchildren. I wanted to be by their side. So I got closer to the Hindu people, who were very kind to us, who accepted us, and gave us that education.
They were very kind, and very nice during the classes. We learned from them how to be humble, and organized. They are organized.
In spite of the difficulties, they are organized.
(Man) You traveled to learn solar engineering.
What did you learn specifically?
What are the learnings you're going to share with your community?
(AG) I now have knowledge that I can spread in Columbia -- knowledge that can affect me personally.
If I'm needed somewhere where there are other women, who have been to some faraway continent, I can collaborate with them on the installation, repair and construction of the equipment where solar energy will be captured, because they taught us how to do it, as well as lamps that generate light, from energy via the panels.
(Man) Guajira is a forgotten, abandoned region of the country, where public services don't exist, and there is need for electricity.
Tell us a little bit about life in your community without electricity.
(AG) You have to get up very early, and at 6 pm, you're already looking to hang up your hammock.
That's the Wayuu within the communities. Your chores end at 6 pm.
But having light within the community, inside your ranch, you actually have more time during the night to do your activities.
As artisans, knitting your backpacks, making your hammocks.
Children who are in school, can do their homework and study.
(Man) Charging your cell phone, for example.
(AG) Charging your cell phone, and watching TV on a small television because the big one doesn't work.
(Man) What will change in your community's way of life?
(AG) It will teach them to be more responsible with the things that energy will bring to their communities.
Because they are commitments.
They will be the ones to take care of that.
I will supervise them and do the maintenance. But things will change for them; if they have trade, they will have greater earnings and they will live better, they will have a life change, for the better.
(Man) When you returned home after 6 months, how were you welcomed?
(AG) It was crazy.
(Laughter)
They had a big, crazy party for me.
(Man) And what did they ask you?
(AG) What did you learn, What did you bring me? (Laughter) My friends from Zanzibar, funny Moana and Zubira -- I will never forget their names -- The Nicaraguans were very humble with me, they were always around me. My teachers were Hindu and there was a teacher who was married to a Hindu but she was Muslim, she was very dear to me. She was always attentive, asking
"What are you doing, Anastasia?
What do you want? And if I was in pain,
they would come every day to check on me.
If I was sick, "Pain?"
"No teacher, no pain." (Laughing) One day she came and said, but quite happily, "Dollar?"
I didn't know what a dollar was; they pronounce it "doller" there and that day I had knee pain, so I said, "Teacher, dolor, dolor [pain, pain]." "No, no, dollar, dollar." They had given us a few dollars to keep, just in case. When I showed her my knee,
she said, "No, no, dollar, dollar."
And she takes out some cash and shows me.
"Oh, teacher, I don't understand you."
(Laughter) It's very different.
When we arrived we didn't understand each another.
For example, they would use a broom for sweeping, and the person holding the broom said to me, "Room."
I thought "room" meant "car,"
and the "room" is where you go to sleep.
"Room, room," he said,
so I responded, "Teacher, no room."
(Laughter) (Applause) (Man) What lesson do you want women to share with other indigenous and non-indigenous communities in Columbia?
What lesson do you want to tell them about? Like, "This is what I learned, this is what I want to share."
(Man) To all the indigenous women of Columbia, let's plan out our ideas, implement them, and put them into practice.
Before, I have never had the opportunity to go somewhere, not even to another county.
And I still took the risk to go to India
and learn something new.
I thought, "How will I be able to learn solar engineering if I don't know anything about it?"
I didn't know anything, but I learned.
Like a child starting to learn the ABC,
By saying it out load, one, two, three, a thousand times.
Doing small numbers, and that's how we learned.
(Man) The Wayuu are the largest indigenous people group, and the Wayuu language is something we shouldn't lose.
I, we, would love for you to give us some words of encouragement inviting us to move forward, inviting all these women to start now.
Please give us a few words in the Wayuu language and explain what it means.
(AG speaking in Wayuu) "Thank you to everyone here for listening."
Once again, I'd like to say that we're making our project a reality for the benefit of all humanity.
(Applause) (Man) Anastasia, thank you very much for joining us.
Thank you for sharing your knowledge.
I fearlessly declare that your words have filled this auditorium with light.
Ladies and gentlemen, Anastasia García de Sol. (Applause)
According to statistics, this was education until 2005 in Ecuador.
The national average of schooling was no more than 6 years old.
Six out of 10 children could attend high school, and even less finish it.
The budget allocated to education was less than one third than what other countries in the region, like Chile, destined to the same area.
As you will see, we had on the one hand, a serious problem of access to education, there were not enough schools for the children to attend.
Painfully, until before that year, some parents said: "I already gave you elementary school," a tremendously painful expression they pointed to the impossibility of access to high school.
Whether because there were not enough schools, or because they didn't have money enough to send them.
Yes, you heard well: enough money, and I'm talking about public school, because until that time the school had virtually become privatized: a contribution was charged, wrongly labelled as "voluntary".
And we all know what that means: that if that contribution is not made the kids could not go to school.
On the other hand, we also had a serious problem in the quality of learning; the children who could make it to school didn't learn enough, They didn't learn what was necessary.
At that moment, according to studies in the region, Ecuador was practically not even on the list of countries that achieved better quality in those learnings.
That same 2005, I was principal of a school and I was happy in that school.
At that time, in December, I received a phone call: an old friend called me to tell me they had asked him to be Minister of Education and he, in turn, called to ask me to be the Vice Minister of Education.
I was so happy at school at that moment that I answered no, it was my first reaction.
Also, I'll be very honest, I knew almost nothing about public policy.
And, in addition, before 2005 Ecuador experienced such a political, social and economic instability that none of the presidents that was elected in that period could finish.
In that context, to join to a team of people that wanted to transform the education system, and to transform it long term, didn't look like a good idea.
But I hang up and I thought about it: I always thought that there is nothing so important in people's life like education. So, against common sense, I accepted.
And I joined that team, a multidisciplinary team, a wonderful team, that started thinking how we could transform education.
And we traveled the country, and we talked to a lot of people. We opened debate meetings, until we could identify eight points that worried people to a great extent.
And with those eight points we built an education plan that we wanted to stand the test of time.
Then the next question was: How were we going to make it stand, facing this political instability?
We came up with an idea, and that was a referendum, so we move this concern and we achieved that the same year in which we would elect new President, an additional ballot would be included in which you could vote for the 10 Year Education Plan, this ten-year plan.
So when you went to vote you had a ballot in which you voted for president and a ballot in which you voted for the 10 Year Education Plan.
In addition, we visited each candidate and we ask them that, if the plan was elected, with the approval of the people, they made it their own plan and make it happen, because, if people voted for it, it would be mandatory.
On November 26, 2006, the 10 Year Education Plan was approved in Ecuador.
Our mission was over, all that wonderful team could go home and leave with the satisfaction of a mission accomplished, the goals they have come together for.
However, at that time, something absolutely unexpected happened: the President elect called the minister and asked him and the team to stay, to carry out the plan.
You know, that moment a dream comes true?
Well, this was it, we had fulfilled a dream. We were going to carry out the plan.
And so it was, the team stayed and I was vice minister until 2010, year in which I agreed to be minister until 2013.
They were years of intense work, very intense, very committed.
And this is not my story, nor is the story of a small group of people, it's the history of a country that decided to push a transformation in education.
What happened during all those years?
Well, the story isn't conflict-free.
Of course there are always oppositions, and in fact there were.
There were strikes, trials, threats.
Because when you really go for a transformation, you touch resistances, powers, the power of some and others, the spheres of influence of some and others.
But beyond that, with joy and with the support of the majority of people we could transform education.
You will ask, "what happened?" Let me tell you: Education was again free in all levels and modalities.
97 percent of the children in the right age could make it to high school and around 75 percent can finish it today.
Of course, as you will see, there's still a long way to go for the country.
The schools could be repaired, could be equipped, to have sports courts, laboratories. Teachers who didn't have any training, who didn't have a professional development as a teacher, they could have it, their salaries were recomposed.
On top of that we achieved an additional participation in the State budget: the budget in education was tripled.
(Applause) And with all this we had three great lessons to learn, which are three very important lessons.
The first one is that a transformation in education is possible in Latin America. It can be achieved, it can be done, we don't have to travel a lot or go far away to find recipes in other parts of the world.
Transformations are achieved and they can be done in Latin America.
The second lesson we learned is that those transformations are possible only if they are perceived as systemic, that is, that must be carried out in all parts of the system, not to isolated parts of the system.
And to explain myself better: sometimes we think that the transformation in education happens when you change the curricula, sometimes we think that it's about having new books but, in reality, the transformation occurs when we don't leave any of the gears of the education system untouched.
All those that move, rotate, give life to the next gear, they are the ones that produce a transformation in education.
A law is very important, but if that law doesn't later translate into the curricula, if the study plans don't impact in the initial training of teachers, if those teachers don't receive training, if we don't equip the fifth grade laboratory with what is needed for natural sciences, all this remains simply on paper.
(Applause) These eight points that we identified to make the 10-Year Education Plan referred to everything I just told you.
The first four points were the levels of learning: kindergarten, primary school, high school and the incorporation of those adults who didn't finish their education.
The fifth, the need to have the necessary and sufficient infrastructure to be able to accommodate all the children who would like to be and need to be in school.
The sixth, perhaps the most complicated, is to improve the quality of learning.
The seventh, those that make possible this way, the teachers, right? The ones central to the process.
And the eighth was money.
With the approved plan, with the plan carried forward, the transformation in education occurred and is underway, it still goes on today and hopefully for the next ten years Ecuador keeps talking about education, in order to criticize it, to improve it, to reactivate it, to make it every time the most important.
Because I want you to go with this idea: there's never going to be anything more important and transformative to both the life of a person and a society than education.
Yes, of course it's like that, but also think about it, it's the only thing that will set them truly free.
Thank you.
(Applause)
To live or to die?
They are two sides of the same coin.
I propose changing the view that we have of death, in order to change the view that we have towards life.
The art of living is to be aware of the fact that in our lifetime, things happen to which we have to find answers to.
Some things are induced, others encountered, and others, perhaps, are undesired.
But all of these things exist to help us mature.
Human beings have the capacity of transmuting all their hardships in order to convert them into well-being and life itself.
As we follow our paths we may lose people, situations, things... Jobs, dreams, plans, and projects.
It is necessary that for each of these losses that we accumulate throughout life, we are able to perform what is known as a 'mourning process.'
To mourn means 'to let go.'
Learning to let go.
It is only then that a person realises how they construct their relationships with themselves and others.
We must also become aware of how our emotional bonds originate in the past.
In accordance with how we have been appreciated and loved in the past, we will have a specific tendency in terms of how we relate to others today.
I remember a mourning from my youth, the mourning for my true self.
I would call it 'the disconnection.'
It's what happens when what we think doesn't match how we're feeling.
Immediately you don't like what you're doing, you don't understand it; perhaps inadequately, you may have to reconsider.
And what happens is that, I realised that when I made a decision I prioritised other people's needs before my very own.
What a vast feeling of emptiness, and how strong the mourning of not being able to be myself.
I still remember when my parents used to say, "Don't touch," "Be quiet," "Behave yourself'," "Get down from there."
Eventually there came I point where I struggled to be myself.
And thereafter, I struggled to relate to my parents in a close way. Sometimes borderless, with few barriers, others symbiotic and sometimes with a great deal of difficulty.
Know that all of the mourning processes we have overlooked make it much harder to relate to others during our adult life.
They lead to dependency problems and loss of personal autonomy.
At this point, a human being loses sight of themselves and believes they don't exist.
To mourn means having acquired the capacity to say farewell, to accept, and to let go.
And also to take the opportunity of the loss to create within me new aspirations and new energy; clean, renewed, and loving energy, that will enable me to be born again.
Mourning also brings forth in us a very emotional contacting.
The most important emotions of mourning are: fear, anger, and sadness.
Each of them has its own function.
Fear protects us and it must be expressed by talking about it, making it present.
Fear must also be accompanied.
Accompanied by kindness, information, with presence, with contact,... But this contact must be affectionate.
And, above all, the person must feel empowered so they feel with conviction that they have the means to overcome this bout of fear.
Anger is an energetic emotion that gives us drive and aids in defense.
It's important for it to be expressed quickly, but with great prudence,
as we can easily harm ourselves or harm someone else,
or engage in inappropriate conducts.
Sadness is an emotion which, through crying, can relieve us of guilt, inspire forgiveness, and to address the old wounds the past has left, and that are now ready to be healed.
People who are unable to initiate the process of mourning, perhaps because they never learned how during their early childhood, are usually stagnant people, who connect strongly with their pain, with stress, and with discomfort in general.
But if we acquire the capacity to take pain and to look at it, to listen to and interpret it and to then quiet it, surely the resulting energy from having overcome it will empower us and make us more autonomous.
Life is a change of view, and I propose the first change of view for today.
Let's not see mourning as a failure, as something frustrating; let's live it as an opportunity for change.
Because when I consider a loss, I don't just see what I have lost, I also see how this loss operates within me, how I can transmit this form of evolution?
The benefits of mourning are many.
Allow me to share with you some of the most important ones: The first is that it strengthens my self-esteem.
It also provides me with safety.
Personal safety.
Internal authority, even.
It also grants me lucidity, clarity of mind, decision-making capacity, I reconnect with my autonomy. I am 'me'.
I have the capacity to decide.
It inspires me to let go of past grudges that I have accumulated throughout my life. It also grants me inner clarity and spiritual respite.
People ask me, "How do I know when I've finished a mourning process?"
Mourning is the process of fusing reasoning with love.
The empty void that mourning leaves in its absence, if I fill the void with loving energy, I will most likely find a new purpose and sense in life.
It's another change of view.
Today I'd like to pay tribute to many people who have passed away and whom I've had the opportunity to accompany.
I remember Berta, Mariana, Lorenzo, Juan, Teresa...
Children, youths, adults, and elderly people.
Mothers and relatives would always ask: "Is there light after mourning?"
Of course there's light!
Not only is there light, but a whole other way of living.
These mothers and these children and these husbands and wives have reached the understanding that mourning has been for them an opportunity for change.
I remember, five years ago, I was diagnosed with a terminal illness.
The first thing the doctor told me was: "You have a a small tumor..." I was surprised to witness this attempt in diminishing the tumor, as though someone believed that somehow sounded less aggressive.
I realised, at that moment, that I had to collaborate with myself in order to restore my health as soon as possible.
I understood I didn't have to fight.
I had to love. Because the tumor is a part of me,
not some external thing.
It is an organ that at some point in time becomes unbalanced and forms in me a disharmony, a disagreement.
Changing my view and instead of fighting, accepting, I was given the opportunity together with my loved ones to transmute the path and the process.
And realising that I needed to understand what the tumor meant.
I knew the tumor wasn't the harbinger of my own death.
It was making me aware of a change that needed to happen in my life.
And until I had set the changes in motion, the tumor remained.
The moment in which the person decides to change it, the process is altered.
There was a lot of grief, a lot of mourning there.
Even the idea of dying at a young age, or the idea of not finishing a project, or the idea of losing a loved one.
It's from this moment forth that the person becomes aware and connects with their innermost being.
It's like daring to fuse with myself, in order to feel me and achieve different things.
This is the great gift of mourning and death.
When this love courses through your hearts, you will realise that the energy that pours forth, that emanates, is made for sharing and also receiving.
It's a continuous transmission that gives us purpose.
Know that when we share this love - and it is an unconditional form of love - we seek no longer to retract, to hide, but simply to be.
And if we're able to share it with generosity, through words, through actions, through our gaze, with time, with our intention we can try and enter deep within ourselves and know that this love gives sense to what we are doing today, each and every day.
It is important therefore, in order to bring back joy, to feel serenity, to die completely, or to complete processes completely, to connect with loss and with oneself.
I'd like to share with you that when I make petitions rarely do I get what I ask for.
But I always end up getting everything I need.
(Applause)
(French) Thank you.
Connectivity, what does this word make you think about?
According to the Spanish language dictionary connectivity is the capacity to connect or to make connections.
Surely, you are thinking of connectivity, the social connectivity type just like taking a selfie, or Twitter, and all that stuff on the social webs.
But I am not talking about that type of things.
I'm a biologist.
So, social connectivity is not my thing.
I will talk about population connectivity in fishes.
Population connectivity develops due to migration and dispersal of individuals of the same species among multiple habitats.
For example, storks migrate very, very long distances and are capable of connecting the north of Europe to Morocco. Plants or animals that can't move as much, with a much reduced mobility, can use tricks like the wind or the sea currents - in the case of marine animals, obviously - that help them transport their seeds or eggs.
Connectivity, in fact, can be represented in this type of diagram.
It looks like a web where each point represents the places where they are connected with each other and the lines the migration routes, these movements, this migration patterns the animals follow.
But, OK, what is connectivity
really used for?
Connectivity is a phenomenon that helps preserve biodiversity,
and helps populations increase their resistance and resilience, that is, their capacity to adapt in case of environmental change
- like for example, climatic change, one of the most famous ones that impacts humans as well.
But there are many dangers, many factors that can affect the good functioning of this connectivity.
And humans play an important role in this as well.
That is a very beautiful slide, as you can see,
several points that represent individuals belonging to the same, well-connected population.
Let's add a divide, called population fragmentation that is going to segregate this sole population into two or more smaller populations.
The problem is that if the newly
created population isn't big enough,
the integration flow of new genes, of new genetic information will be restricted.
And this type of situations leads to endogamy or consanguinity.
It's exactly what happened in the past to the House of Habsburg, who refused to mix
outside their royal family
and sought to maintain the power through inbreeding, with one of their last representatives, Charles II of Spain, this poor man who, due to all subsequent consanguinity issues he had, couldn't leave any offspring, that's it.
It seems like the new King of Spain has changed that.
In the case of terrestrial habitats, we can see that this type of fragmentation can occur, for example, when a new route is built,
a route that divides two habitats, and the animals, or whatever there is there, can't pass from one habitat to the other. So one thing that we can do to improve, to restore this connectivity, is to create, for example, ecoducts, bridges where there are no walkways, no bicycles, and above all no cars, except land, plants, and trees that link back these two divided spaces and contribute towards a healthy connectivity.
Marine habitats face similar issues that can hinder connectivity as well.
For example, environmental and marine pollution, the presence of very large infrastructures, and clearly, overfishing.
I am going to talk a little more about this topic because I obviously know a little more about fishing than the rest of the issues.
For example, we have fishes that migrate, - they can migrate on a small or a large scale - and use a different way to connect themselves called larval dispersal.
That is, just after the reproduction, those fishes are small larvae.
This beautiful fish it's not more than a millimeter long, and it's going to spend weeks or months, free in the water column floating in the sea, transported by marine currents, until it finds a place where it feels at ease, and where it will settle, grow, and reproduce, thus contributing with new genetic information to its population and increasing the quality of the population connectivity.
What does this tell us?
It tells us that in the end-- the latest studies in the field show that in fact, these populations can stay connected - these population of fishes - on a very, very large scale.
For example, on the scale of the whole Mediterranean region, or even on a larger scale.
And what does that imply?
It implies that, for example, if we are going to fish off the coast of Spain, what we do, overfishing or not, isn't going to not only impact the marine resources off the Spanish coast, but the resources of the rest of the Mediterranean region as well.
And this is vital and must be taken into consideration.
Why is it so important?
Knowing the scale and the patterns of this connectivity will help us to protect a little all such connections and maybe implement nets of protected marine areas, for example, to protect this connectivity that is so important.
And it's so important because marine resources are very important for humans.
We are very dependent on that.
It's food for many people in the Mediterranean, in the whole world.
So now, a very important thing that is to be taken into account, is that it's high time we connect as well we, scientists, politicians, with the greater public, in order to talk and improve the information quality, and to manage most adequate and efficiently our common marine resources.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)

Hi. Good morning, everyone.
My name is Belén. I'm 15 years old and have been blind since birth.
Maybe that's why I've been focused on what I'm about to tell you.
Or maybe not.
I remember when I was really little, walking with my mom through the old city in Córdoba, which is not a pleasant walk --
or at least that's how it felt to me.
And I asked my mom, "Is all of Córdoba like this?"
After a few minutes of silence, she said, "No, just the old city."
"And the rest?", I asked intrigued.
"It's like where we live."
That was her way of trying to satisfy my childlike curiosity.
Later, we took trips to Segovia, Barcelona, Cádiz.
We did a lot of sightseeing during those trips.
I'd always start out really excited, thinking I'd get to know all about the city. But I always ended up pretty disappointed,
because all we ever did was walk.
As I got older, I learned to appreciate those endless walks.
I took in the sounds, listened to the locals' conversations, and tried to learn all I could about the place.
But the most exciting trip for sure was to New York.
We were in Super New York, like in the movies! The Big Apple! Center of the world!
Being in New York was the best thing that could ever happen to me!
When we got off the plane, my first reaction was, "Yep, it's ground. Solid ground. Just like the ground in Madrid."
I was really disappointed by this trip,
because all we did was walk around to different places and take pictures.
We didn't do anything different,
we didn't stop anywhere.
When I got home, I realized I was the one who wasn't a good traveler, and that had to change.
So I started to ask myself some questions:
Why do we travel? Could a person see what my parents saw by watching a documentary instead?
What makes one country different from another? Is it just their monuments?
What's life like in New York? Is it like my life?
Does everybody who lives in the West have the same type of life?
You're probably thinking that obviously, every country is very different.
But could we definitely say that life in Paris is very different from ours?
That idea made me really, really sad.
I didn't accept it then, and I don't accept it now.
My first reaction when I got to New York - the "Yes, it's just ordinary ground" one - brought me to two conclusions.
The first one, the idea of Super New York, I got from the media.
And that idea is only partially true, because even though I've been to New York, I don't really know it.
We can't let every country's media and Department of Tourism decide what impression we'll get of a place and hide what they don't want us to see.
We have a right to know the reality of it.
The second idea is that we can learn a lot about a place by its ground.
When we see a well-paved surface, we can assume it's a developed country.
If it's clean and there's no litter, we can assume that the people who live there are clean and respect the environment.
If the street is worn away or muddy, we assume the people who live there have to fight harder to get by.
I found out I wasn't the only person who felt this way.
In her book, "Blind in Manhattan," Nuria del Saz expresses something very similar.
Apparently, we've all had the experience of visiting a place, but not really knowing it until we met someone from there.
But I do also think that not focusing on monuments makes that more obvious.
So for those reasons I started a blog, where tourists in Madrid can connect with local volunteers.
That way, tourists can get to know Madrid - the Madrilenians' Madrid, not touristy Madrid.
And the volunteer can meet and learn from someone new.
Because a city, a place, a countryside - it's something you live, not something you see.
For example, I love the street musicians in Madrid.
These are people who give their music away as a gift.
They're very wise, very humble, bighearted people.
And in winter, you have the chestnut vendors, making and selling roasted chestnuts.
They're usually older people, wise and with big hearts.
It's a treat to have the chance to speak with one of them,
although that's not so easy because they're usually pretty busy.
But of course, every person from Madrid will tell you different interesting things about the city.
If you'd like to help me with my project, send me an email at diferenteturismo@gmail.com. And remember: when you travel, don't just bring back a souvenir photo.
Bring back a great experience.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
The best way to understand a magic trick is from this side.
From the magician's point of view.
So imagine you are creating a social network.
The business model is simple: the more time people spend in your site, the more ads you sell, and the more money you make.
You don't have to do anything.
People generate the contents, upload the photos.
All you have to do is to choose at all times what to show to who so they stay. Being the 21st century you automate it, you use software. Call it algorithms, big data, artificial intelligence.
That works, you make a fortune...
Congratulations! You invented Facebook.
Now, when psychologists and sociologists start to observe what actually happens to people on the site, they discover minor side effects: depression, political polarization, incitement to violence.
None of that was in the business plan.
What happened?
What machines discover, or rediscover in their blind and mechanical way, is that what catches our attention is not what's useful or beautiful, is what entertains us, more effectively, what makes us angry. Something good that someone attacks, something bad that someone defends.
Instinctively we comment, share, discuss,
not because it makes us feel good. It's rare that we sign out from Facebook or Twitter feeling emotionally better than when we entered.
But in that combination of positive and negative emotions by scrolling Facebook, there's something virtually addictive: The more time we are in a social network, the worse we feel.
We feel worse, we have less energy to do something else and more time we spend in the social network.
Intelligence agencies, marketing agencies professional trolls --today there's no difference-- are constantly generating professionally designed content to make us feel the worst possible about a person, a country, a brand, a political party, whatever.
All that negativity is toxic, individually and socially, but it's not the worst part.
That technology designed to catch our attention distracts us at the same time.
It distracts us from something important, which is not our loved ones, nor the sunset.
It's the technology itself.
We know it's there, we talk about technology,
we know it's there to influence us.
We are so distracted from the fact that we ourselves can use it for our own purposes, today.
Every time I tell this to someone who is not an entrepreneur, a scientist or a programmer, he gives me that face: "Every word is in Spanish, but you speak to me in Swahili."
(Laughter) Psychologists have a term for that: learned helplessness.
If you take a person and ask them to do something impossible, like doing crosswords with no solution, they learn they can't do it.
And, sooner or later, they never try again in their entire life.
It's a kind of conceptual blindness where the idea itself that you are able to do something disappears from the mental map.
We all have things like that --dancing, talking in public, math-- where we know with total certainty that something is impossible.
This happens with technology.
In particular, with these technologies.
We feel them so threatening, so foreign, so alienating not for what they can do, but because we see them under the exclusive control of scientists, big companies and advanced governments.
It's true, yes, that they use them.
But it is also true that we, as individuals, can develop, use and control them.
If your daily activity involves reviewing documents or messages and categorize them into piles or folders, even if it's just: "Interested", "Not interested", very easily a program could be looking over your shoulder, learning from you how you do it and slowly start automating the process.
The effect of that in your income, in your job stability, in your quality of life, depends entirely on if that same program belongs to the company or the worker.
If it belongs to you or another party.
The same applies to creative activities.
It's another discussion whether or not this is art, but a tool can be programmed to generate at least drafts of drawings, blueprints, diagrams, music, and use that to generate new ideas or to explore your own.
Learning what you like, what inspires you, what is useful to you.
And such a tool can be programmed to learn the specific strengths of people with totally different skills and experiences, then put together everything they know without prejudice, without discrimination and make much better decisions than what each person would do separately or all together on the same table.
What I'm not saying is that to have these tools --that we can all have-- we all have to learn to program.
It's a useful skill, yes, it's a good job, it's a hobby, it's an art that changes your life forever.
But there are other ways to get advanced software for personal use.
One is to pay someone.
The most obvious idea this afternoon, but very few people do it.
Even people who can afford it.
Another more interesting way, is to look for someone who knows how to do it and ask them.
If what you are doing with your lives is to protect someone, help someone, or create something beautiful, it is very difficult not to find someone who say yes.
The software is artificial, but it's people who make it.
And people, in general, want to help other people.
Do it, buy it, or order it.
To have your own artificial intelligences under your control, has no other trick than that.
But it's useless to tell someone who "knows" that they can't do something, that they can.
You can give them examples, you can explain. You can give a TED talk, but they have to try.
If you only transmit the idea, and you only listen to the idea, it's just a prologue.
If you stay in that first step it's worse than doing nothing.
It's a very insidious distraction, because you feel you are doing something.
In my experience, people and groups that get rid of the idea --conscious or unconscious-- that some things are too advanced, and they just risk the attempt to do an impossible thing, quickly discover that the difference between the futuristic and the reasonable, the realistic, the logical, it's not in the resources, it's in the attitude.
And when you do that something happens to your attention.
You see the Internet less as a happy meal to choose from a menu,
and more you see it as a box of Legos, with pieces of things that you use to do your own things.
Less you care about tweets, retweets, comments, followers... You are very busy making your own machinery to hook with someone else's.
I don't t have much hope for the 21st century.
(Laughter) There are many things not going well.
And there are very few signs that it will improve.
On the other hand, we are fighting with one hand tied behind our back.
We face these problems, the big and small, with tools owned by others, for other people's goals, with their rules.
If that changes, who knows.
Helplessness can be unlearned.
It's an individual process, it's an uncomfortable process, but it is one in which we all benefit
Thank you.
(Applause)
I have always been passionate about technology.
I have liked dismantling things since childhood, wanting to know how they worked and what was inside.
My adolescence - I was born in 1973 - coincided with when the PC started to spread, and I was lucky my parents bought themselves an encyclopedia and were gifted with a computer like this one ... you can tell it is a Spectrum.
Thanks to that, I learned from it and grew up with it, and perhaps because of it, I'm a software engineer today.
If I had to choose a memory that brings to me the subsequent future, in terms of technology, it is the first time I connected to the Internet.
It was back in 1993. I was 17 years old, and my friend, who was already studying for his degree snuck me into his data center. We did the paperwork, and we decided to connect to the Internet, that world I had seen in movies like "WarGames" -
that "hacker" world, where only a chosen few, entered, right?
It wasn't the Internet we know now. It was the Internet that started hypertext, there were a few websites - in a way it was a special time, a paradigm shift.
The opportunity to instantly connect with somebody else in the world was something amazing.
But I cannot separate this memory from why I connected to the Internet.
I had begun in the "free" software scene, as an amateur, and we would get online to download "free" software, right?
in book distribution [inaudible].
Hours and hours downloading diskettes in order to set it up on my computer.
It was an act of technological autonomy, an act of personal empowerment.
Someone, in a way ... altruistically, that someone is Richard Stallman, allowed me to use technology for whatever I wanted.
He allowed me, not only to use it, but to do whatever I wanted with it, because I could access the code.
And thanks to that, I changed.
I learned ... it is a company that changed me, that technology can bring out the best in people, it gives you the tools to go out and get the best out of them.
So, during this time, I was motivated not only by technology.
I had always been around social movements, and from those, I have always tried to put myself in their shoes, and I try to give solutions, being around them.
And with these two motivations, the technological and social ones, a belief arises in me, that it is possible to use technology so those that are misusing it, that are at risk of being excluded, can come to a different place.
Technology has a capacity for empowerment, for creation and innovation that allows people to break out of this cycle of exclusion.
And that's what I've been doing for the last 12 years.
I was working in the industry, I left, and from there I started to develop educational programs that had this component area, that technology was the catalyst for a person's recovery process.
And I'm going to tell you about an experience within my story. A story that is an example of how an educational program in technology can place a person in the heart of an ecosystem of innovation and at the same time can make that person learn, grow and develop as an individual.
It's the story of a young man from Madrid, like any other.
Let's call him Roberto, but it could be any other name.
Roberto doesn't lead an easy life.
Roberto ... the educational system failed him, he was not given a diploma, and also his family was not close to him at that time; he remained cut off from the system and discarded.
But one day Roberto learned about a neighborhood association - an association that worked with technology, an association in Villa Verde and there - somehow his friends told him that it was not an association like any other, but that they created technology programs and it was like something different.
They let - it did not seem like "high school," which he rejected.
So there, he liked technology, but he had not seen it as something for the future.
From there he began to try things out, saying: I'm going to go there and see what happens.
So from there, he began to see that the teachers encouraged him to experiment, that mistakes were not bad but were good, that it was the dynamics of active learning.
And from there he discovered that technology had many facets - facets where he could improve his neighborhood.
So, during this process, he learned that there are many people who also related to this kind of technology. So with a technical [inaudible] companion and another group, they developed a project to improve his neighborhood with technology.
Music was going to be the driving force of that change.
And how did that go?
Together with all of those people, they developed a project where music made it possible to restore degraded areas, areas that were conflict-ridden, areas that were not in use.
It is a project that was called Boom Box, and from that, developed into a project that works with speakers and custom-made software, and it allows people to play music in public spaces and develop interactions among them.
The prototype was developed, and what was the result?
Well, a square that regained its use, thanks to this device.
And you ask, Whatever happened to Roberto? Well, Roberto got back on his feet. He realized that studying was important, he went to the city hall, and today he is working.
Vital experiences like this made me see that the belief I had that technology could change this situation of exclusion had become a reality, to think that it was possible.
That it is possible to place people in the center of a process of innovation and be able to escape that cycle.
Methodologically speaking, the projects that we develop, development in the association where I collaborate, come from the MIT Media Lab and the Living Labs.
The project where learning is channeled through projects, people learn by doing projects; where mistakes are the engine for learning; where from our point of view, creativity can be taught; where the whole world is capable of innovating, if what has been learned is shared, right?
How do these projects work?
They are projects that last for nine months, six months of which the kids that participate in them train in technological fundamentals, and for the last three months they develop, they can put all that dynamic into play.
Within a series of projects, which has a social component, are a series of R&amp;D projects, a laboratory where companies collaborate in that laboratory, providing opportunities for kids.
What projects are we doing in this accelerator?
Well, we have those we are collaborating on right now with intermediaries [inaudible]. One is called Water Drop, which is a system that implements the United Nation's FAO formula of evaporation, which develops a custom-made plant irrigation system.
Evaporation gives a formula that measures the loss of water by crop transpiration and evaporation, including certain environmental sensors of temperature and humidity and others for radiation, that allow you to know how much water is lost, and that applied to a drip irrigation system
Another one that we are also developing is called Bench Box, which is a device that answers the question: We're kids, we get together in the square to listen to music with mobile phones. What would happen if the city gives us that infrastructure?
And there it is, that bench that you see there in the background that allows people [inaudible] that have their mobile phones.
It is a software-hardware device that is implemented in urban real estate.
Technology is capable of moving the world.
Technology is capable of changing people's situations.
This is why I have dedicated myself to what I am doing.
From my point of view, it is possible to create programs that empower people, that fuel the cycle of innovation, allowing them to extract their creativity, and at the same time, pulls them out of the cycle of exclusion.
Certainly, this has become a reality. And I've come up with another one, as well as a challenge, and that is: What would happen if we used these technologies within the educational system?
We would get there first, and people like Roberto would not be expelled from the system, and we would prevent academic failure, which is one of the main causes of social exclusion,
aside from educating people as a generation prepared for change and prepared to create a better society.
Well, I am in this challenge; it is a very nice challenge.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
If I tell you that I've just landed from Liberia and that I have a fever and I'm a doctor, would any of you come down here and kiss me?
I am happy to see few of you in the room have a death wish.
The good news are, so you can relax, I didn't land from Liberia but from Mozambique, a country in Sub-Saharan Africa and we don't have Ebola there, yet.
But we have tuberculosis and AIDS cases every day which are contagious and equally lethal.
And for those of you who don't know this, there are only 2 forces in humanity that were able to kill tens of millions at a stroke: wars and infectious deseases.
For this reason, when I see Ebola or TB on the cover of newspapers I feel it's a horrible injustice and a tragedy but also an opportunity to send this message: inequality in health kills millions, when we deal with infectious deseases.
In the end, what are we talking about?
It's not only Ebola or TB. If this would happen in Europe, of course there would be consequences, but not the same ones.
This is inequality in health.
This is where I work.
I live in Mozambique, Manhiça, a rural place 100 km from Maputo, the capital.
It is the Manhiça Health Research Center, a Mozambican institution, devoted to fight poverty deseases.
I came here more than 2 years ago to work in a project of childhood TB.
and I stayed, and us the people of Manhiça we know that once you arrive there, you cannot leave.
The truth is I do the job I always dreamed about.
This place is right in front of the State Hospital of Manhiça.
It is a strategic relationship, very helpful for both institutions, reinforcing each other.
The hospital being so close to the Manhiça Health Research Center grants it very special capabilities if compared with other hospitals.
Of course, it has better conditions than other hospitals.
But even so, the truth is conditions are pretty hard.
This is an absolute truth: wherever you are born, no matter if you are born sick or healthy, your place of birth determines the rest of your life. It determines your access to medicines.
In a place like Manhiça, we have maybe 2 or 3 medicines to treat neumonia while in Spain we have 50 or 60 antibiotics.
It determines your access to resources.
When we have a child with asthma, if you are parents you might have seen this, you use a nebulizer. We don't have that in Manhiça, we use a water bottle, we cut it, we put some roll tape, we apply it on the face. And you know what? It works.
It works just fine.
Most of all, it determines access to health, something we take for granted in our society, almost nobody has problems of access to health.
We have hospitals, health centers, roads, helicopters to transport critical patients.
But in Mozambique access is a brutal barrier.
Patients need to walk for kilometers to reach the closest health center in pretty hard conditions, as you can see here. So what are we talking about?
We are talking only about one thing: Poverty.
Because if a person is poor, the risk to get sick is bigger and when a person is sick, becomes poorer.
As simple as that.
The good news is that research is probably the most powerful tool we have today to fight this vicious circle.
In the last decades, in the last years, we have seen enormous progress for humanity.
We have seen reduction of child mortality.
In Mozambique child mortality has been reduced by half in only 10 years, this is a revolution.
We have seen an increase in life expectancy
and this is what develops countries and worlds.
But unfortunately, in research we have big inequalities too.
Today we are talking about science, about progress that will change the world; reality is that in medicine, most of research funds go to rich countries' diseases.
We have very little money for poor countries' diseases.
Let me tell you about my area of research: I work on TB, it might sound as a disease from the past.
It has its romantic side, the cause of death of Miguel Hernández, Kafka...
And it's true that today almost nobody dies of TB in Spain.
But it's daily occurrence in Mozambique.
Three figures to make you see the entity of the problem: 1/3 of the worldwide population is infected with TB, in this room we have 200 people with TB, I'm one of them, almost for sure, but I didn't take the test yet.
Not everybody will get sick.
95% of people dying of TB die in a poor country, in places like Africa.
Why? Because the risk is higher
if you have a weakened immune system, when you are infected with HIV virus, AIDS. These two lethal deseases are tied together.
The third figure I want to point out is that every 5 minutes a child dies of TB in the world.
TB is a good example of how inequalities in research funds happen.
What do we need to stop a desease?
We need to detect it, prevent it and treat it.
Three basic tools.
Now, in the case of TB: in prevention we deal with a vaccine from the turn of last century and only partially effective.
Diagnosis: which is our basis?
The microscope, a technique called bacilloscopy from the beginning of last century, which detects few cases, specially in children. Very few cases are detected with a microscope.
Treatment: we still treat with drugs from the '80s, 3, 4, even 5 pills a day, during 6 or 9 months or 2 years if you have a drug resistant TB that needs to be supervised by a health worker and with significant secondary effects.
In the case of drug resistant TB, many patients go deaf.
I don't want to send a catastrophist message at all,
because in 20 years with this measures we have progressed a lot, more than 55 million people have been treated.
And most of all, for the first time we researchers see in the horizon new vaccines, new drugs, and new diagnostic techniques.
But I don't want to speak only about research, about the great vaccine, treatment or diagnosis technique, which is basic.
The important thing is to get that vaccine, to take it where it's needed, apply it, and make it work in real conditions.
In the end, it is the research on the spot, of rolled up sleeves, where it is needed; that can translate a big research in a health policy applied to a context, sustainable in the long term.
It means to think global and act local.
Research on the spot implies a deep knowledge of the anthropological, cultural and social characteristics, which are not mediagenic, but are equally important.
I will show you a simple example, that illustrates what I am trying to say.
In this picture you can see an altimeter, a tool to measure little kids when they are not able to stand up on their feet.
We were doing a clinical trial in a rural area to test a new medicine, working with the community.
In the moment we started to use those altimeters we observed a massive reduction in the trial's participation, and now that I see the picture I think we were pretty dumb not realizing about it then.
I think the likeness is very clear, it looks like a coffin.
As we started using the altimeters, a rumor spread that we were testing a medicine so lethal, that we were measuring the children in order to build their coffins.
I don't want to ridicule at all the perception of the community, on the contrary, ours, as we did not foresee it and express the importance of working with local communities in this kind of research.
Luckily, in Manhiça Health Research Center there's a powerful social science team and a communitarian consulting committee to help us reach out to the community when these misunderstandings come up.
The center where I work is a Mozambican institution, with more than 500 workers, including drivers, cooks, scientists, lab technicians, epidemiologists, politicians, economists and doctors.
A lot of people working in the same direction, and I believe this is the research that changes the world.
The research done in poor countries for poor countries.
The research that tries to understand a problem and find a solution, but does so in context.
The kind of research that makes to world progress.
And the best example I can give you has to do with malaria research, which is one of the biggest areas we have been working on in the last years.
To research a vaccine for malaria is very difficult.
To give you an idea of the complexity of the microorganism, when we speak about a virus, it's like a bicycle, the flu.
When we speak about a bacteria, it's like a car, for example meningitis, meningococcus.
When we speak about malaria, caused by a parasite, we are speaking about a transatlantic.
It's another level of complexity and there's no vaccine for parasites yet. This has been a step forward. But the difficult part was not to get a vaccine for malaria, the difficult part was having a research demonstrating that the malaria's vaccine works, in Africa, with thousands of children, in real conditions.
And even if the vaccine probably doesn't reach the effectiveness that we would like to see, it prevents 50% of critical malaria in children, it's another step ahead in the fight against malaria.
I believe it is in our hands, because the history of humanity has always been in our hands, for better or worse.
We can prevent the 21st century to be the century of inequalities.
I believe as individuals and as a society we can do a lot, on top of being aware and demanding our politicians to do their part.
I don't think it's acceptable nowadays the reduction we have witnessed in international cooperation and in research and development funds. But above all, we must give our best both as society and as individuals, which is knowledge.
Thanks.
(Applause)
As a kid I didn't like to drink milk.
It gave me a belly ache.
My folks, both of them psychoanalysts, always thought it was related with my difficulty to process weaning.
(Laughter) I recently had a genomic test done and it turns out that I am lactose intolerant.
(Laughter) It's been a couple of decades now that we can read our genome, our DNA.
Yes, we can access the code to build our body.
When the first genome was sequenced it was very, very expensive.
It cost more than one billion dollars.
Today reading the genome of one of us costs less than USD 1000, and the cost goes down, year by year.
In this context, the big question that arises is: What will the impact on humanity be like when the reading this code of life becomes available to everyone?
To answer this question I'm going to tell you some things that I see are happening and in which I am involved as a researcher in bioinformatics.
The first thing I want to tell you is that being able to read our genome is making a full impact on medicine.
To the point that something new appeared, genomic medicine, personalized medicine, or precision medicine, as you want to call it.
The important thing is that this medicine uses the data from our DNA to improve the prevention, diagnosis and treatment of diseases of all kinds.
And the advancement in genomics is going really fast.
So fast that today we can talk about the genome revolution, which impact may be even greater than the discovery of antibiotics or vaccines.
Such is the impact that if a doctor suspects that a patient has a hereditary disease, in many cases they can diagnose it and even find a cure by reading the patient's genome.
I will never forget the first time we read the genome of a boy who had an illness so infrequent that the system couldn't diagnose it for years.
Together with an interdisciplinary team of doctors, chemists, biologists and computer scientists, we analyzed his genome and we were able to make a diagnosis.
To the patients who have these diseases and to their families, this is a life changer.
Today, we could read the genome of all the newborns and diagnose illnesses straight away.
This is great, isn't it?
But you would tell a couple that their newborn child is almost certainly going to develop a maybe incurable disease at some point in their life?
If we can do something about it, perhaps, right?
But if it's just a question of waiting?
I don't know, we don't have an answer.
Over time I realized that genomic information has a particular charge to it.
It's like something hidden inside of us, a sort of inner monster, that determines us, but without our control.
And that scares us.
That's why handling correctly this information is crucial.
And that's why in a lot of countries public-private partnerships for genomic projects were started but with state control.
I believe that the role of the State in these projects is fundamental.
Who else but the State can watch over our rights and focus on our benefits?
But would you trust
your DNA data to the State?
What would an intelligence agency do if they had access to this data?
And politicians?
Another very interesting aspect of genomics is that certain mutations increase the risk to have some diseases.
There is a genomic test that predicts the risk of having a some type of cancer.
Now, if we are solely talking about probabilities,
would you give someone this information?
Who would you give it to?
At what age should someone have this test done?
Exactly these were the questions the experts were making in regard to breast cancer screening, at the same time they found these mutations in Angelina Jolie.
She underwent a preventive double mastectomy.
She took her both breasts out.
In the case of Angelina she had a family history.
Which means that surgery was an option after the test positive.
But the situation wasn't that clear for the thousands of women who went to get tested without thinking about it much after seeing Angelina on TV.
Luckily, a public debate broke out, information flowed, it got everywhere, and it all ended up in an increase in the number of tests but not in unnecessary mastectomies.
Because sequencing the genome is no longer expensive, scientists began to do population studies to determine the risk of getting all kinds of diseases.
There are cases in which delivering information on risk has a strong impact.
Just like with Angelina and breast cancer.
But in others, not that much.
I have an increased risk of having high blood pressure by 20 percent.
Many doctors doubt the usefulness of this information and think we're not ready to receive it.
They actually suggest not to do the tests.
For me, that information is relevant.
I think I do better with it.
There are risk factors in which I can have an influence.
For example, I can eat with less salt.
Also, I'm convinced that we are indeed prepared.
In fact, aren't we used to read our blood tests and see that our cholesterol is a little high which increases the risk of having coronary heart disease?
The truth is that risk information is present in a lot of medical tests.
So, the better we understand it the better we will react to it.
Today, genomics goes beyond diseases.
Genomic tests are soaring.
Now they come in all kinds and colors.
There is a genomic test that tells you how you will react to medications.
Another one suggests you a personalized diet
And there is even one that tells us what kind of soccer player we are going to be.
(Laughter) And you can even buy these genomic tests without medical order.
Crazy isn't it?
But is this happening only in genomics?
How many of you had a blood test done asking a doctor you know for the prescription, who probably didn't see the results, just because you needed them for some reason?
Medicine is changing and the doctor's role as well.
Don't we google for symptoms of diseases to see what we may have and what we can do?
If you want a genomic test you go to a website on the Internet and you order it in one click.
You get a box with a little tube inside.
You take it out, you spit inside of it for few minutes and there goes your DNA.
Yes, in that tube that has nothing but saliva travels what we are.
Well, one part.
You pay a company for analyzing your data.
But the company can also keep the information.
And maybe he can share it.
So, what is the risk of this free and indiscriminate use of genomic tests?
We imagine that the risk lies in the company sharing them.
For example, with our health insurance and they raise the fee, because something hidden pops up.
Or that we go to a job interview and we don't get the job because they accessed the data.
I always doubted when they asked for my ID number, and I allowed for my DNA to ramble free without prejudice.
After all, I thought, it's not that different from googling or posting on Facebook or on Instagram everything I do. Even when I feel it unsettling to do it sometimes.
The genomic information that you share is really very valuable.
So, if you want to know if you're going to be like Messi almost surely they will ask you to sign a written consent so they can share the information.
A company has just shared their genome database with a pharmaceutical lab for USD 300 million.
Now, not everything is business.
Companies make profits, but millions of us benefit with better diagnoses and treatments thanks to people sharing their genome.
I am totally convinced, that we face a world of discoveries.
And also a lot of challenges, like protecting ourselves from the misuse of information.
Thirty years ago, cell phones were a rarity.
Twenty years ago, they were a luxury.
And today almost everyone has one and we can't imagine the world without them.
How long do you think it will take until we ask ourselves: how could we live without reading our genome?
It may happen sooner than you imagine.
At Christmas 2018, the best-selling product on Amazon was a genomic test to know if we have a Chinese, Jewish or African grandfather.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Have you ever predicted that something would happen in your lives and didn't?
Many years ago, when I was a girl, I was sure that I would pursue a career in acting.
And I'm here on a stage, but I'm a political scientist.
In college, I was taught that at some point capitalism would self-destruct due to its own pull, and it didn't happen.
And they also told me that more or less about this time we would witness the end of ideologies, and I think that neither happened.
Relevant events in history such as the fall of the Berlin Wall could not be anticipated.
Because predictions in politics and in lives, sometimes fail.
Many years ago, when we imagined that technology was going to dramatically change politics, Internet voting was expected to become universal by 2030.
It seemed that changes in governance were going to come from that space.
In fact, when I had to decide on my PhD thesis subject I picked electronic voting.
It was an unusual thesis topic because political science always deals with the present, or the recent past.
But what worried me about the future of politics was that: how electronic voting was going to affect it.
Or so I thought.
At that time it was said, and many envisioned, that by 2030 we were going to vote in pajamas from home.
And that electronic voting with machines similar to ATMs was going to be the anteroom to Internet voting.
And I look at it and I think that, very probably, this prediction will also miss the mark.
Because today in 2019, fewer and fewer countries are using electronic voting.
Politics is changing.
Yes, but not in the way that we imagined.
I'm still convinced that understanding the future of politics is key.
And together with several colleagues from Argentina and other countries we are trying to understand how is it going to be 2030, how are we going to govern ourselves.
Let me tell you some things we have some certainties about, on how it's going to be in 2030. They will surely transform politics.
In 2030, artificial intelligence applied to processes of all kind, products and services, will have transformed developed economies.
And quite probably it will widen the gap between them and developing countries, like Argentina, unless they adapt to the changes to come.
We also know and we see it coming -- unfortunately -- that we are going to suffer the effects of global warming.
And most likely by 2030, the center of world economic power he will have moved to Asia.
China probably will have replaced the U.S. as the center of the economic world.
And Europe will become a less powerful region.
Few people question these megatrends.
This means that the powerhouse of change is not going to be just technology, that always monopolizes attention.
And it's true, the fourth industrial revolution as some call it, will sure change how administrations work.
I don't know, we may have robotic political advisors.
What will also surely change is the way in which we relate with our representatives.
We are already seeing this and it surprises us.
For example, when President Trump communicates his foreign policy in a tweet like this.
And that brings in problems we didn't have before.
Because the U.S. legislation doesn't ask that we keep these tweets.
But it does ask that we keep Trump's speeches when he attends the Congress.
And there are also changes that are matter of concern because they are occurring before we can take care of them.
For instance, think of the way electoral campaigns are changing.
And today our laws became obsolete.
They regulate how much is spent on television, on the radio, but they don't say a thing about how Facebook or WhatsApp is used to inform or to misinform the voters.
But the deepest transformation in politics will not come from technology.
The change will take place regarding who will make decisions in such a different world, and on which subjects we will be able to make them.
To imagine how governments are going to be like by 2030, we have to think how two of their main characters will be like: governments and companies.
And in this I have to admit that we have more doubts than certainties.
Where will decisions be made?
Will they be made in cities, in nations, which are increasingly locked up in their borders?
Or will decisions be made in global institutions?
In those we created after World War II, like United Nations.
Or, perhaps, in others yet to be created.
Today nationalisms are booming.
And there is an increasing distrust in those global institutions we created.
That's what we see with Brexit, for example.
Political leaders giving increasing relevance to local identities.
But we are also seeing that problems are increasingly getting global.
And no single country can solve them by themselves.
Think about the way we protect the privacy of our data on the Internet.
There are many voices today saying that those global institutions have to be strengthened, have to be given a bigger role.
How will decisions be then?
More local or more global?
The problem is that countries today are too big for the small problems, but very small for the big problems.
That is the first unknown.
The second unknown is how the companies of the future are going to be like.
Are we going to live in a world of very specialized SMEs?
Or in a world of large companies holding much of the global economic power.
We got excited thinking that technology would lower costs, that we were going to have a more dispersed market, more decentralized.
But today technology giants -- Amazon, Google, Facebook -- are becoming more concentrated.
And we don't know how the companies of the future are going to be like.
And that is the second unknown.
If we intersect these two dimensions we can think up different worlds where we are going to live.
Very different from one another.
Four worlds for 2030.
And I invite you to look forward together to the future of politics.
At one extreme, we could live in a world dominated by large companies under a global government.
I don't know, a kind of world parliament, a new version of the United Nations.
We call that world "My good friend the giant."
Remember? Like that short story
Dahl took to the big screen.
In this world we may surely have a chance to find a solution to climate change, or some other global problem.
But probably in this world we will feel very far from where decisions are made, in those centers of global power.
Maybe some algorithm can help so that they take our preferences into account.
But it would be very different from today.
Be forewarned, this world needs political leaders really proficient and capable.
They have to carry out a double game.
They have to negotiate with big companies and make decisions that will govern us all, the world citizens.
Are we already forming such political leaders?
And now I invite you to travel to the opposite world.
Also possible.
A world where there are many small companies, very specialized SMEs, with local governments.
We like to call this world, "Small is beautiful."
But I'm not so sure that it's going to be so beautiful.
Here, technological innovation is going to be slower.
And it's probable that we won't be able to find a solution to climate change.
It will be very difficult to coordinate actions with so many actors.
However, we will surely feel more involved in the decisions.
And there are two other worlds possible.
One world of a few Big Foot from the private sector that coexist with many small governments -- I don't know if this sounds familiar to you -- In this world, big companies with operations overseas beyond territorial frontiers can resist decisions made by smaller governments.
And there's also possible a fourth world.
Maybe the least likely of all, in which we live in an ecosystem of small businesses that compete under a global authority. In each of these four worlds decisions will be made in very different ways.
And each surely have a different impact in democracy, which is being questioned today in many different places like Italy, Brazil and India. Because the frustration with politics fires up resentment with the leaders.
And today new phenomena appear.
Maybe you saw this, a few weeks ago in Ukraine, a comedian who impersonated the president in a TV series became president.
He was elected president in the polls.
Sometimes, politics goes beyond fiction.
Any analysis that we do, any combination of these scenarios is surely going to render a different world.
And it opens up many questions.
Do we want a world parliament to take care of, for example, regulating artificial intelligence?
Will a world parliament be able to coexist with smaller governments that will deal with smaller, more local problems?
Is there a different system to democracy that we are going to invent?
Is it going to become the most widespread form of government?
Or the move to Asia will bring in more authoritarianism?
Or will we create a new way to govern ourselves?
We ask all these questions because the future of politics is not written yet.
And most surely when we make these predictions, we will fail, just like it happened with electronic voting.
But to plot those scenarios and imagine which one we find the most desirable, the better one for Argentina, it's a game.
It's a fun game, we just predict.
But it is also an act of responsibility.
Because what will happen in 2030 will have a lot to do with what we do today, with the changes we undertake today, with how we train the leaders of the future, and with how we anticipate the quandaries of the future.
Thank you.
(Applause)
How wrong, how wrong, how wrong people eat.
I don't know if you've noticed it.
We dietists and nutritionists had to be invented --
And this happened because things started getting quite bad --
in order to calm what was happening.
Actually, mankind has been all the time eating what it was at hand, right?
Hunters, gatherers, then we started farming, raising animals in order to kill and eat them.
But actually, regardless of some deficiency diseases, people was still guided by thirst, hunger, and ate whatever it was around, and they were good.
But things went wrong because human beings started killing themselves.
I don't mean killing each other, which we've always done pretty well.
Now we've started killing ourselves.
To such an extent that, in the last 20 or 30 years most of the deaths in the world specially, in recent times, are early and preventable deaths.
That's why I say people is killing themselves
by eating, by drinking, by smoking, and in car accidents.
It sounds shocking, but people are killing themselves after all.
What we should ask is: why do we keep eating this way knowing that it's killing us?
That's really what I want to address in this talk because what we've been told about what "healthy eating" is, is not truly accurate.
The reason is very simple.
We have several tenets in nutrition, some believes that somehow perpetuate and explain that we should eat in a specific way.
For example, tomorrow I'll get up and I cook rice with vegetables and a bit of shellfish, and you say: What is he doing?
A nutritionist eating rice in the morning?
But if instead of rice boiled in a pan, is puffed rice, I put it in a mug, I pour some milk and I put chocolate on it, it would be completely normal, isn't it?
Then, let's say that we've been taught about diet through very peculiar glasses and we can only see a bit of the information.
We see a very biased sector.
The problem is that if, when we are living our daily life, those glasses tell us what is healthy and it's not really true.
Some people think that dietist-nutritionist's work is to tell people what they should eat. Precisely, I try to prove that it's just the opposite. So I take off those glasses from people to show that, hey, maybe you don't have to eat as you've been told and you should get out of the Matrix.
And maybe, it's just about eating whatever you want
if and when it's healthy, isn't it?
Obviously.
This is very difficult because that apparent freedom to eat whatever we want is not so real because we actually eat what they taught us to.
We grow up in a culture, we've been raised and this impregnates some invisible ideas, like those myths, like those tenets.
Somehow, they're invisible thoughts sometimes very difficult to be noticed, they're not always palpable and they tell us what "we have" to eat.
Some time ago, it was very useful to identify some acting ways, such as racism, sexism or any kind of abuse or slavery, so I propose you to name this invisible context that oppresses us and makes us eat what is supposedly keeps us healthy and refer to it in that same way during the whole talk. We'll call it "dietarchy", OK?
The healthy eating model that tells us what to eat.
That means, it puts us in a bubble and tells us "eat this or that way," which are a series of invisible thoughts that are there day after day.
People who think "eating healthy is expensive" or "I bet eating healthy must take a lot of time."
Everything you drink due to the advertising that is not controlled generally by the governments, the bad formulation of the food we find in the supermarkets and the main problem, the environment that surrounds us.
The human being has a fixation and it is that we love the surrounding environment.
It usually eats whatever is nearby, no matter of the information it has.
That's why you haven't eaten fruit today.
Because in your context was none.
If there were fruits at the auditorium's exit people would have eaten fruit.
And everyone knows fruit is healthy.
But eating behavior and health behavior goes much further than information.
It depends on the context.
Dietarchy, among many other things, also modulates people's context.
That's why we drink with no consideration when we're at a party.
That's why we go to a birthday party and eat cake.
Maybe, without even considering we may not feel like eating cake.
Our diet doesn't follow logical nor scientific criteria.
That's why we've ended this way.
Otherwise, there would be no explanation why other countries have directly recommended their population to eat food that is not healthy.
A very easy example is: do you really think the basis of human nutrition are cereals for breakfast and flour bags?
Probably not.
Do you think it's a good recommendation to say "hurrah for wine" and "consume beer in moderation, bacon in moderation, cold meat in moderation, sweets in moderation, buns in moderation"?
It could be one of the perspectives, but is the human being so different that a country like Spain recommends something completely different than Australia?
Is the metabolism different?
No, maybe they've decided, through their policies not to promote unhealthy food.
When someone is going to give a public health message is a very good idea to have few conflicts of interests with those things they give the message about.
And I mean this.
Because if you're giving a message in the media about health or diet and it comes up that people sponsor your congress it's probable you'll end up telling lies to the population like "you can drink light sodas every day."
Or if you're maybe working with a pharmaceutical company or with the biscuits industry, you'll maybe end up saying you have to take Omega 3 supplements or maybe that kids have to eat cookies every day and this is obviously not true.
It's scientifically false, like other statements that are made every day and they are very, very deep inside our culture.
Everyone has heard this: "Dairy products are essential, you have to eat bread every day, wine is good for your hearth, beer helps to rehydrate."
These are four issues that are objectively false.
Because the scientific data tells us that all four are objectively false.
At this point, if my thesis about the dietarchy was true someone for sure must have started to ask in a defensive way: "What is he saying?
We won't be able to eat anything.
Why I cannot have dairy products?
Why I cannot have wine?
From time to time it cannot be bad."
I didn't say the opposite.
I've just read four sentences that say dairy products are essential, we have to eat bread every day, wine is good for your heart and beer rehydrates.
The bad thing of when a dietarchy's idea is put in our minds is that we start accepting it despite scientific data, despite common sense or despite our own experience.
Do you know anyone who lives without needing oxygen?

See? Oxygen is essential for the human being.
Dairy products are not essential for the human being.
It's common sense.
I just needed 20 seconds and it can easily be seen, with no major scientific study.
And also, the worst of all is that we ignore the experience.
Everybody has experienced alcohol. Or at least a lot of people. But if you repeat a hundred times "beer hydrates" there's people that, despite they feel dehydrated when they drink alcohol will accept this as a true thing.
"But beer hydrates." That's a lie.

If beer hydrated, university students would wake up quite hydrated on Sunday morning. And they don't.
They wake up with a dry mouth.
(Applause) Beside installing ideas in the system, dietarchy perpetuates and defends them.
This is why we react in such an energetic way.
When a scientific disseminator, a doctor appears in the media and tells truths like "dairy products are not essential, Harward does not recommend eating more than two servings of dairy a day," it's not justified for you to have to take juice, cookies and milk for breakfast.
People fight back and ask: "Why I cannot have four servings of dairies?
Why I cannot have milk every day?
Who says I cannot have juice?
Well, I'm not lying to you, I'm telling you the truth.
Maybe, you should be a bit skeptical and ask yourself who put those false ideas in your mind.
It makes no sense having cereals, juice and milk for breakfast every day.
It makes no sense, but we've accepted these ideas.
Perhaps we should show that skepticism to those who installed the virus on us and not to those who want to shake us up the foundation of our beliefs.
That's the real problem: that unscientific thoughts have been installed on us thoughts that aren't supported by scientific evidences but which we've believed as right. Why?
Because they were in the invisible things that have been part of our food culture.
I'm going to show you how it was installed one of the programs of dietarchy and how recently it's starting to be uninstalled with a case in point.
It's the recent case we've been through with the sugar that you'll know that 10 or 15 years ago went completely unnoticed, there wasn't much talk about sugar, but now it's become public enemy number one.
Is sugar essential?
Of course not.
We can go back to the oxygen test.
If sugar was essential, humankind would not have been able to survive until we discovered the sugar cane or sugar beet.
In any case, we could discuss if beyond indispensable, was necessary, not sugar, but glucose.
Glucose is a compound, a molecule that's in the carbohydrates.
It's one of the carbohydrates, but it can be taken from many sources.
You can take it from sugar, bad choice, and you can take it from fruits, veggies, vegetables, legumes, cereals, roots... When that food that you want to install assumes that, the fact it isn't healthy and it's been discovered, there's a need to start avoiding the topic.
And there's when the reasoning starts: "Yes, I must be unhealthy, but the fault is yours because you aren't choosing well.
You had too much sugar today.
You could have brought a banana from home."
When the surroundings is predisposed to consume unhealthy food, it's very hard; the "it's your fault, you're not doing anything" reasoning or the "it's others' foods with more calories fault like cooking oil.
There's a reflection to be made here, and that is that we cannot evaluate a food just because it contains sugar or not or that sugar itself is bad.
Food needs to be evaluated as a whole and, in this case, ultra-processed foods, that often have sugar in them, have one thing in common and it's that they change our behavior.
They're tasty and they create us, in some way, dependency.
You cannot usually have eating enough sweet foods.
So they blame us telling us "you've eaten too much cereals.
I said 25 grammes, not pouring for 25 seconds."
OK, it's just that you're selling it in a format that lets people to have too much.
Or that reasoning that many time's been said: "excess of everything is bad."
Well no, not everything.
And things in the same excess are not as damaging.
We're still waiting for someone with diabetes type 2 because he had too many Brussels sprouts.
And that's not happening.
So we play with foods that can create a high dependency and we demand people to consume them in moderation.
That's quite tricky.
It's like "smoke just one cigarette and that's it."
This is not going to happen.
If, in addition, these foods that use this argument get normalized around us, in your institutions, in the public health, we find them in hospitals, in the mass catering, in our kindergartens and in the scholar menus, people end up accepting them.
Why? Because it is
completely normalized.
For example, if labelling facilitates that a food can evade, somehow, its content of sugar, it's going to use it.
That's the cocoa powder effect, that I call brown painted sugar.
Cocoa powder contains from 78 to 80 percent of sugar.
But they sell it to us as high in zinc, high in vitamins breakfast.
It's like me telling you: "OK, your car is broken, but I've bought you a pine air freshener."
It's completely useless.
Drinking cocoa powder because it has zinc or magnesium in it is irrelevant because 78 percent you're taking is sugar.
It's one of the strategies that any food that wants to distract us.
All this information wouldn't be harmful if there was none who transmits and repeats it. perpetuating it in society.
And in this sense it is very well documented, both scientifically and journalistically, what web of influence has the sugar industry woven in academics, in scientists, in institutions, so that it can spread and settle into the dietarchy.
That's why it took us so long to start uninstalling it from our system.
It's not because now we've discovered sugar is...
No, we knew that from long ago.
But now the situation is so obvious the situation was so bad there was no other choice than starting acting.
And the virus of the system has started to unhook, little by little.
The change is starting and that change has been thanks to the awareness from the last three, four years.
Wrapping up, science is the best way a wonderful way to understand the world.
It's the best tool the human being has invented to understand what surrounds us.
The problem? Around the
scientific studies there are variables that don't rely on the scientific method, there are variables that depend on the human being and there we find the sponsoring of the studies or the communication of these scientific studies.
I'm going to show you a fact which I think is quite illustrative of what happens in science.
This study wanted to see the results of the studies that had been made about consumption of soft drinks and whether or not they cause overweight and obesity.
The result was the following: if the study is sponsored by the sugar industry, what the study says is "No, it doesn't cause overweight."
Meanwhile, those studies that are independent, that are public tell us instead "yes, the consumption of sugary soft drinks is in fact linked to overweight and obesity."
Look how serious it is that the results of studies sometimes are so dependent on the people that are sponsoring.
That's why dietists had to be invented.
That's why now, more than ever, we need responsible health workers who cannot remain neutral before all the lies they're telling us to say of how we have to eat when there's no scientific data for that.
We need health professions who dare to uncover the lies that are hurting right now our public health.
Thank you.
(Applause)
A couple of weeks ago I was interviewed for a TV show. They called me a while earlier to do my makeup, and the makeup artist, Margarita, whom had seen me in previous videos, asked me: "What's your sign?"
"Positive," I said, teasing her a little.
"Come on, don't be a smart aleck," she said, "I'm sure you know what your sign is."
"And why do you want to know my sign?"
I asked, teasing her again.
"Because signs say a lot about a person," she said "but you're a scientist, so I bet you don't believe in astrology."
"And why do you think that scientists don't believe in astrology?", I asked her.
"To be honest, I don't really know", she said "because there is a lot of science in astrology.
Science isn't just something you learn in a university", she said.
"There's plenty of arrogance amongst scientists that discredit Astrology without realizing it's just a different science from the one you learned."
You're right, Margarita, when you speak about scientists' arrogance, that they're pretty selfish.
But science, as a collective endeavor is of great humbleness, with mechanisms of fraud control and academic dishonesty.
And there is just one science
I mean, science is like a game, with its set of rules.
And just like there is one soccer and one tennis, there is one science.
I could refer to soccer as chess, but Lionel Messi would not agree.
And even more, Margarita, I said, I think that astrology does not agree with certain features and certain rules that define scientific knowledge.

"Such as?", she replied.
An important difference, I said, is the attitude of the scientific community towards its discipline, compared with the astrologers' community.
Astrology did not revise its theory as new knowledge was incorporated, and as new unresolved problems appeared among theories of the time.
Astrology is indifferent to puzzles, and refuses to compare itself with alternative theories, I said.
"And what would be an alternative theory
for you?", she said.
In a good tone, right?
Psychology for example.
Don't you think possible that my personality is determined by my genetics, or by my surroundings, rather than by a distant star whose light I see today but that didn't even exist when I was born?
Furthermore, statistics from big populations, mostly from the Northern Hemisphere, show distinct traits for different dates of birth.
Libras tend to be more balanced than the rest.
Is that due to the constellations?
Or is it that at the end of the harvest, the nutrients are better and winter diseases have not yet arrived?
Everything points to the latter.
Or those born in September, in average tend to be more successful.
Is that because they are Virgos?
Or is it that the school schedule starts in September, and those born in that month are the oldest of the class, are more self-confident and in average have a better cognitive development?
"But Alberto, Astrology has equations, you are a scientist, it should interest you."
Yes, it is true that physics uses mathematics, and mysteriously, math is the description, the language to describe the regularities of the world.
But not everything mathematical is scientific.
I could decorate a cake with equations, and that would not make it a scientific cake.
(Laughter) (Applause) Or, I can use sophisticated algorithms for Kabbalah or to win at a Casino.
Mathematics is a language, and nonsense can be expressed in any language
In Physics we compare theory with evidence and if prediction and theory disagree we revise the theory.
Physics is vulnerable to be proven incorrect. "OK, kind of..." said Margarita, looking at me "but the Moon has an effect on the tides, doesn't it?"
"And we are water so it must have an effect over us."
It is true, there is an effect, but quantitatively insignificant, I said to her.
If I take into account the Moon's size and its distance to the Earth the gravitational effect over me when the Moon crosses the sky is the same gravitational effect of the (Buenos Aires) Obelisk when I cross the 9 de Julio Avenue.
The number is the same.
And with the planets the effect is even smaller.
Those effects are ignored by astrologers.
"Not quite", said Margarita.
"There are a lot of fake astrologers around."
"What you have to do is watch So-and-So's video, a genius, a very prestigious guy."
So I grabbed my cell and started downloading the genius' video.
(Laughter) And meanwhile she googled me, and read that I was born on February 15th.
(Laughter) "You had to be an Aquarius!", she said.
(Applause) But, you know, I'm not really an Aquarius. She looked at me and said "What?"
Days and nights don´t last the same, I said
This is due to the tilt in the Earth's rotation axis around itself with respect to the revolution plane of the Earth´s orbit around the Sun.
Without such tilt days would last the same as nights, there would be no seasons.
Now, even with that tilt there are two special days during the year where that needle is perpendicular to the line joining the Earth and the Sun.
Those are the equinoxes
The only two days in a year in which the day lasts the same as the night: equi - nox.
In Ptolemy´s time during the equinox in March on the other side of the Sun there was the constellation of Aries, and that was the starting point of the Zodiac.
Now, it happens that this invisible needle that points to the Northern Star is not strictly fixed but, due to subtle gravitational reasons it describes a cone, and the tip of that cone completes a full circle in 26 thousand years.
This means that every two thousand years the equinox is displaced by 30 degrees, which corresponds to one month.
So in reality, now, those born on March 21st are not an Aries but a Pisces, and I, born in February, am not an Aquarius but a Capricorn.
And even more, if two people are born in the same place the same date and the same time but one year apart they differ in their cosmic coordinate by 22 thousand miles.
That means that there is no uniqueness between the moment of birth and the position of the stars.
Margarita didn´t express any interest. (Laughter)(Applause) So, I started watching the genius' video
(Laughter) It caught my attention his use of evidence based information.
The guy says: "The universe has existed for 15 billion years, there are billions of galaxies, the universe is expanding."
That is recent information, which is part of the revision of the scientific view of the world, that didn't modify the astrological view.
And that is a crucial point in pseudoscience: the use of current scientific information to validate an ancient view already discarded by the scientific view of the world.
Then the genius says: "Everything is connected with everything", importing, or smuggling, an idea from quantum physics.
One of the fundamental contributions of quantum physics is that particles are indistinguishable.
So why discriminate between an atom from a distant star and an atom from an object in our surroundings?
Because astrology is supported by the Aristotelian notion that we are the focal point of the universe, and that the celestial world, in its cosmic purity is different from the terrestrial world made of fire, earth, air and water.
But today we know that this isn't true. Everything is made of atoms.
A Carbon atom from a distant star is identical to a Carbon atom from my skin, and to a Carbon atom from a rock in Mars, and a Carbon atom from the plastic case of a kitchen appliance.
That is to say, the influence that an atom from Saturn´s ring can have over my destiny is the same as the influence from my grandmother's blender.
(Laughter) (Applause) So if we take into account the total interconnection, I would not only have Saturn in seventh house, but will also have my washing machine in Aries.
(Laughter) And I'm serious.
Moreover, today we know that we are not the only planetary system.
There are other planets with stars, and we are part of those plants' skies.
And just like they, hypothetically, determine our fate, we determine theirs.
Margarita says: "Hmm. But isn't there faith in science?"
In fact, she was making a subtle observation.
Because in science we accept without proof, that mathematics is the language that describes the regularities of the universe.
So, I said, partly yes.
"You see?", she said.
(Laughter) Yes, you are right.
But it is not a faith in the biblical sense, of Hebrews 11:1, where faith is "the evidence of things not seen."
As science inquired into the anatomy of the cosmos, it exposed a causal skeleton way richer and more complex than the one posed by astrology.
And thanks to that knowledge, today we have satellites, vaccines against polio, cell phones, electricity, anesthesia for toothaches.
Which means, astrology, after all, does not pretend to be a faith based system, it is rather a faith disguised as science.
"If I am an Aquarius I'm creative."
That line uses a cause and effect relation.
Or "it is practically impossible that a Moon in Libra opens herself completely to her ascendant in Scorpio from two and a half years of age."
What is the basis of such a statement?
that I took, verbatim, from an astrology conference.
To me it is based on the idea, pretentious to some extent, that the universe cares about my existence and my psyche when in reality each progress made by science emphasizes our insignificance in the Cosmos.
I'm not special I am just one more being, from just one species from just one planet in the vastness of a Cosmos which is completely indifferent to me.
That is the lesson of astronomy, which, at the beginning, was related to astrology, but from which it gradually distanced itself stripping itself away from gods and magical thinking, and not because there isn't magic in reality.
Yesterday's magic is today's technology.
You know what I think Margarita?, I said. I think that part of the responsibility is in an education system that instills the idea of science as something rigid, cold and irrevocable, rather than bringing us closer, it separates us from the poetry of reality.
Science is like precision mining of world's mysteries, that enriches our sense of awe, that connects us, and unites us.
The joy of understanding can be deeply spiritual, Margarita.
"But Alberto, astrology was really helpful to me!"
I perfectly understand, Margarita.
Music for me is helpful as a language that touches my emotions.
But I can't ask a Bach prelude to cure my herniated disc.
But when I am euphoric or melancholic or sad, a Bach's prelude gives me great peace and relief. So, I understand you when you use astrology to re-signify the past or calm anxieties about the future that you cannot fully understand, but Margarita, I ask you personally do not use astrology for things to which it does not apply.
Astrology will not help us find new sources of renewable energy or help us build a bridge.
So, don't use it to decide the date of your appendix surgery or other important decisions of your life.
Please, do not use it for that.
See looked at my with mild approval while she removed the apron because I had to go to the interview.
I felt that we had reached a point of convergence.
We said goodbye kindly and I walked towards the corridor, and as I was walking away I heard her say: "Alberto, you have the Moon in Leo, I bet you."
(Laughter) Thank you.
(Applause)
When I was a medical student a teacher asked us: "What is acute myocardial infarction?"
And we replied almost without thinking.
It's the necrosis -- death -- of heart tissue.
"Very good", he told us.
"But if the tissue is dead, why does it hurt so much?"
We were speechless.
He continued: "what hurts is the surrounding tissue, what is around the dead tissue,
that which is still alive.
If it hurts, it means it is alive."
I want to talk about pain, and also about fear.
But let's bring in a story far from college and much closer in time.
Something that happened very recently, on March 8 in a WhatsApp group.
Someone sends a greeting message for Women's Day.
"Happy Women's day!"
Answers begin to appear.
Some in a good tone, others not so much.
Making reference
to the historical origin of the date. Not particularly a happy one.
The guy -- he was a male -- apologizes and immediately clarifies: "You know I'm not 'macho' at all.
You know where I say this from.
Besides, I go to all the manifestations."
Then he goes into silence-mode.
The guy who clarifies that he is no macho, that understands the new ways in which women get organized, and agrees with those changes, could be any of the men here today.
But this doesn't only happen to us men.
It could happen with women.
Because having to explain ourselves is something that happens to us all.
When we feel we could screw up, when we are venturing into affairs that are still in a state of flux, we make that kind of disclaimer so others don't complain in the future.
It's like when in a commercial a promo is advertised and the voice at the end says: "valid offer throughout the country, except the Province of Cordoba."
It's a kind of 'terms and conditions' we make to clarify that we are not retrogrades and that we go with the social changes that we are living.
It would be something like clarification valid for all situations where you have to comment on a sensitive subject without looking like a fascist. Do you find yourselves
making such warnings?
When we do them, aren't we somehow defining the type of conversation we want to have?
Perhaps, we want to empathize with the other person.
Or make sure the person doesn't see us as a threat.
Maybe, we are afraid that our words are interpreted opposite to what we mean.
What would the ultimate disclaimer
of this kind be like? That in a TV show a journalist asks a girl how was she dressed when she was assaulted while the screen socket shows the line to call in case of gender violence?
To agree with changes doesn't free us immediately from making those warnings.
There is a kind of resistance to change which is expressed in several ways.
Even when we agree, it's difficult.
For example, it's hard for me to talk using inclusive language.
I feel it forced, I don't find it comfortable.
I understand and share what is sought after speaking like this.
But it doesn't come out as easily,
at least for now.
Nevertheless, I increasingly have a harder time writing using the plural masculine meaning all people.
So every time, I get more skilled at avoiding it and I use a lot of words in Spanish that are not gender-specific. Like "people," "persons," "someone."
But I don't do it because it's okay or because it's more correct.
I don't feel like doing it any other way now.
Once you see it you can't unsee it.
And if the way we talk or write affects our relationships perhaps, by being more inclusive I provoke less pain in some people.
But that saying: "you know what I mean", that warning we talked about, is not something that only happens to the one who says it.
It's an experience that pierces through all the participants of the conversation.
And it's the expression of the mismatch between the changes happening and our ability to digest them.
But what is that mismatch?
Let's see some examples.
When the same-sex marriage act was promulgated, and some time later the gender identity law, were we prepared for that?
What does it mean to be prepared?
The question itself -- if we were prepared for that -- could seem offensive.
But, well, you know what I mean, right?
(Laughter) But why could it be offensive?
Imagine we are in Munich.
It is October 2, 1946.
Yesterday the Nuremberg trials concluded.
I want to get into a bar and they don't let me in, they apologize and say that they are not ready yet to receive Jews in their place.
That they agree with the trials and all But they need time.
What time?
Do laws contemplate subjective and personal issues?
Or do they actually point to the presence of something that marks the end of a time and the beginning of another?
Explain to someone who suffered from prejudices throughout their life, harassment, discrimination, that others need time.
We approve same-sex marriage and you have been together for 25 years but Civil Registry staff still have to digest this information.
(Laughter) But the thing gets more complex.
Because relationships are formed between different people, who are living different times.
And who experience love, freedom, equality, in different ways.
In my work as a therapist I try to see the impact in our relationships some of the social changes we are witnessing have. Some of which we are also the protagonists.
We are going through a paradigm change, that it's painful and violent for many people whether because they resist change or because they don't tolerate the resistance of those who resist.
And along with pain comes fear, which is also a sign that something is happening.
The fear that often paralyzes us, doesn't allow us to move forward. And it's also a warning that in front us there is a problem; that we have to watch out and do something about it.
But fear is a personal experience.
This pain, instead, is a collective experience that arises as a consequence of the friction in relationships.
For example, those who feel pain resist new forms of love -- some not that new perhaps -- that are becoming very visible.
And those who think it's inconceivable that those changes aren't yet adopted, also feel pain.
But changes are underway.
And it seems that an inexorable path opens.
And what's the problem?
If we adults, especially those head of institutions, cannot stand this pain, new generations will lose what we thought was fundamental.
That mixture of limits and containment, of affection and normative context.
They will lose the warning.
Maybe, current teenage public denounces, among other things, are a symptom of that other symptom.
The debt of adults and institutions.
Times are coming when we will have to talk about the way we talk.
And that includes talking about warnings and disclaimers, gestures, voice tones, listening to others, record the tensions and the nuances of the conflict.
How are our conversations going to be in the near future?
Imagine difficult conversations, but not uncomfortable. Respectful but not politically correct. Conversations that cause less pain.
What laws, words and questions we don't even imagine will we need?
And which ones that exist today won't make sense anymore?
In our discussions and in the consciousness of the mismatch pain is present.
To recognize it in time and put it into words is to see ourselves as part of a tissue that if it hurts, it's alive.
(Applause)
Today I want to tell you three little stories.
They are not a big deal, just that:

Three little stories.
The first one's called: "The Dawn of Robots".
This man is called Ray Kurzweil.
Ray has a very successful track record predicting the dates when technological advances will take place.
He has worked as Engineering Director at Google for two years.
Ray says that by 2029, machines will be able to do everything humans can do, but better.
How many of you agree with Ray?
Well, for the skeptical among you-- Go video. (Laughter) Using your voice never fails.
This robot is called Atlas, the video is from last year.
A robot built by Boston Dynamics, a company bought by Google.
You may notice that it is able to open doors, walk over difficult terrain, even slippery terrain, like snow.
It is able to recover its footing when loses it.
You didn't believe me?
It is also capable to lift weights and put them in their place.
There's a shorter version of this video but this is what we've got.
The last time I showed this video, someone in the audience said: "It's logical that they'll end up ruling over us if we treat them like that."
You may consider this the very best of robotic hardware, that means, the robot's body.
Let's move on. Maybe.
Can we move on?
The advancement has not limited itself to hardware.
It has also happened to the robot's mind.
You may have heard about "Watson," it's an IBM software that won against human Jeopardy champions some years ago. It's a very popular game in the US, based upon the premise of finding the correct question to get an answer.
More recently, "Deep Mind," another company belonging to Google, won against the human "Go" champion.
"Go" is a board game much more complex and complicated than chess.
Even the very AI experts didn't believe that a software could win against a human in at least one decade.
And this leads us to something called exponential thinking.
That's the basis Ray Kurzweil uses to create his predictions
We usually see the future as a linear projection of the past but there have been more changes in the last 100 years than in the last 5000.
And those changes will bring us things such as: less work, [The perfect storm that will hit jobs in the next years] new sources of wealth.
[Should robots earn money as if they were workers?]
[Europe wants robots to be people] and new partners to share the world with.
I see some of you are stirring in your seats.
That's normal.
Because you are lively people and you must be wondering: what if this is true?
What if it's coming down the line?
What can I do?
What will I do?
Maybe... You begin to think that, for example, it would be best to set up a robot building company.
If you do that, the first thing you do is to think how your company will be called.
So the second story is "How to Pick a Name" for a robot company.
You look to me as people that looks for inspiration that like to come to inspiring talks.
So maybe you search online, for example: What's the world's biggest company?
"Apple." That's curious. Why is Apple called Apple being that they make PC's and cellphones?
There are many stories on the Internet, about the origins of Apple's name, but there's one that specially moving about Alan Turing.
Alan Turing is one of the mathematicians that cracked "Enigma's machine" code, the one the Nazis used during World War II. He also helped lay down the bases of today's computers.
Turing was gay, something punishable with jail time in the England of the 50s.
He chose to submit to chemical castration, this caused physical changes in him.
He lived some years distraught about social rejection and he killed himself.
He injected cyanide in an apple and took a bite.
Maybe Apple's logo is the apple Turing bit with the colors of the gay flag, or maybe it isn't.
But maybe you'd like to look for a similar story to name your company.
And maybe you'd be lucky enough to find another man called Kurt Gödel one of twentieth century's most important mathematicians.
Even Albert Einstein, in his last years said that he only went to college to be able to enjoy chatting with Gödel on his way back.
Kurt Gödel also established the limits of what maths and an algorithm are capable of.
Truly a genius, maybe not very well known.
As many other geniuses, Gödel had his own paranoias.
One of them was that someone was trying to poison him.
So he never ate any food unless his wife tasted it first.
That worked all right for many years.
Until they got old and his wife got sick.
She had to be hospitalized.
With his wife not around to test the food Gödel stopped eating.
He starved to death in 1978.
His wife was called Adele.
Maybe Adele is a name fitting for your robot company.
What's certain is that it'll appear before Apple in the phonebook.
Of course that, with that name, and such a moving story behind it you couldn't just make any kind of robot.
You'd have to build emotional robots, robots that care about people.
That take care of us.
And this is the third story.
"On Emotional Robots."
Science fiction has already brought us many ideas about how that future may be.
This is a scene from "Artificial Intelligence", a movie in which a sterile couple adopts a robot.
In this scene you see the imprint that links mother and robot and that will last beyond human lifespan.
In "A Friend for Frank," this old man that suffers from Alzheimer is given a domestic assistant robot by his children to help him with the chores and remind him about his medicine, they end up becoming friends.
And it's all very nice but it's all also sci-fi.
So what's real about all this?
This is Nuka, it's a robot seal designed to help people that suffer from some kind of dementia.
And it works!
People that didn't talk anymore do so again.
This is Kaspar, he has helped Tim, an autistic boy from the UK to develop his communication capabilities.
This is our beloved Tico, we saw him at the door, he helps diagnose Hyperactivity Syndrome and Attention Deficit Disorder.
That's done with a terribly large and tedious quiz, he makes children like it.
And here you can see Tico with Ivan, they are very best friends, they love to read tales together.
Ivan is so happy Tico is his friend he even introduces him to his classmates.
So, what will you do?
Because what's certain is that someone, somewhere is building a new robot.
Maybe many robots, and we have to decide how the new society where we coexist with robots will be.
So tonight when you get home, I would like you to start thinking with your batteries charged with illusion how will you play a part in the creation of a new society where we coexist with robots?
Thank you.
(Applause)
Let's begin -- We'll deal with a delicate subject
Every sex-related subject is always somewhat mysterious.
Right?
Raise your hands those of you who, in the last year, caught a cold or had the flu.
Hands up. Very good.
(Exclaims)
Nobody got vaccinated here.
Now, raise your hands those of you who, in the last year, had any sexual dysfunction.
(Laughter) How good is life here in --
Living here in Durazno is very therapeutic.
I'll come and settle down here.
Well, as you've seen,
embarrassment is the problem.
That is the problem. Why?
Because statistics, sociology... and Ms. Teresa Herrera is present here, who has worked a lot in this area,
have released polls results.
And what do we find out?
Let's see the first one.
That 30 percent of women have orgasm-related problems.
And 33 percent of men are premature ejaculators.
That is to say, very quickly, quick doing errands and quick in bed.
This generates great tension and it causes couples to split up because many times women, let's say, eat the main course but don't eat the dessert.
Let's go on to the next one.
33 percent of women have low sexual desire.
This has reached epidemic proportions, and we don't know if it is due to the new role played by women or due to the areas that they now cover.
They have majority in medicine, psychology and the judicial branch.
But they didn't abandon their former role as housewife, as superwoman, and then I think they are paying for this with their sexual desire decrease.
In the last 10 years, this has been an epidemic throughout the Western world.
And 12 percent of men, from 20 to 70, have erection problems.
But if we consider an age group as that of men older than 50, for instance, the proportion increases dramatically.
50 percent or 60 percent of men over 50 or 60 have erection problems.
Has a friend of yours ever told you
anything about this? Of course not.
We all talk about our exploits, while
these things remain deeply buried.
So then, what happens?
As of today, we are able to say that medical sexology has solved every male sex-wise problem.
Premature ejaculation, erectile dysfunction low sexual desire, penis enlargement.
Which are the disorders from which men usually suffer.
All of them have a solution, and it usually is a very quick one.
In a few days or weeks, everything is settled.
And many female disorders - not all of them, there are still many to be resolved - have a solution too, like vaginism, some orgasm disorders
and that's it.
So, what is the problem?
The problem is that embarrassment makes people who have these kind of problems refrain from consulting.
Embarrassment involves being afraid.
Afraid of what?
Afraid of showing our own flaws.
Afraid of showing our weaknesses, our defects.
There is a difference between consulting an allergist or an orthopedist and consulting a sexologist, no doubt about it.
Why? Embarrassment and modesty, that is why, but they prevent us from finding real and quick solutions to something that will promote more communication and intimacy with our partner, and enhance enjoyment of life.
More than 60 percent have never talked to their doctor about their sexual life as if it didn't exist.
And on the other hand, most doctors don't ask their patients about their sexual life.
Why? First,
because, unfortunately, most doctors don't know anything about sexology.
Sexology isn't taught neither in medical school nor in the Faculty of Psychology.
Except for one or two courses that last a few days or a week which are offered just to address a certain theme.
Something so important to a person's life such as their sexuality isn't taught to the professionals to whom one should go for help.
Why don't they ask?
First due to that, because they don't know.
Second, because they should devote a lot of consultation-time to that.
Internationally, if the doctor is well-trained a consultation shouldn't take more than 10 minutes.
Asking for analyses, doing a second report if necessary.
But a consultation, a diagnosis, a treatment plan most well-trained doctors can do all that in 10 minutes.
That's impossible for a medical sexologist.
To start talking about the person's intimacy, to get in the mood to perceive the dynamics of that person and his partner, of his family circle, it certainly cannot be done in that time.
And that's what distinguishes medical sexology as a field of study from medicine's neighboring fields.
Third, most doctors don't know to whom they should send the patient or don't want to send him to a medical sexologist.
That's what happens.
And embarrassment can be truly harmful in some cases.
Every man over 40 who has an erectile dysfunction must be examined.
It must not be covered up with the blue pill and since it all works out fine,
problem solved.
That isn't right. Why?
Because the same factors that are responsible for erectile dysfunction are the very same factors responsible for cardiovascular diseases.
Sedentary lifestyle, diabetes, high blood pressure some medicines, feeling anxious fat, high levels of cholesterol, are the same factors - overweight - are the same factors behind both illnesses.
So, nowadays we know that 60 percent of people who had an erectile dysfunction problem will have a cardiovascular event in an eight-year time frame, on average.
What does that mean? That a dysfunction
in a person over 40 must be a symptom of alert, of warning bells
At the same time, 70 percent of those who had a heart attack had erectile dysfunction problems three years before the heart attack.
This means that we don't have to cover up this symptom and when a father, an uncle or a brother show this symptom, we have to tell them: you should see a doctor.
Because it might be masking something else just because we don't realize the size of the arteries.
See that, while the coronary arteries diameter goes from four to five mm, the diameter of the penile arteries goes from 1 to 1.5 millimeter.
A man, after he admits his problem, sees a doctor more than three years later, because he feels embarrassed.
I usually come across some colleagues and they tell me: "Look, did this guy go to see you? I sent it to you."
- "When was that?"
- "Two o three months ago, I think." Despite having been sent by another doctor this person will delay the consultation.
That I'm tired, the stress, the vitamins, the quarrels with my wife.
And for some reason or other, this person goes on postponing the consultation.
How to overcome embarrassment?
This is very important, and there aren't many sexologists here.
We are everywhere in the media.
Why? Because people
don't talk about this.
People don't ask "Where did you get that?
In that place; I'll see if it suits me."
People don't talk.
No one, especially men.
Now, raise your hand, don't be shy, those to who a friend ever told: "Hey, I can't with my wife, I don't know what is it, I have an erection problem."
Nobody. In 69 years, it never happened
to me that a friend came and told me: "Carlos, I have this thing."
There are some useful methods
to overcame embarrassment.
Consultation via e-mail.
That's to say, you can send an e-mail with a nickname and we'll answer within 24 or 48 hours.
We also extensively provide consultation by phone.
And our doctors give their phone numbers.
Some colleagues of mine can't be found in any phone book there is no way to trace them.
We do the opposite.
Some patients even call us on a Sunday, and apologize for that, but in 30 seconds we fix a problem.
And we open up communication channels, which is very important and which is what we are doing here today, with you.
Direct consultation with the sexologist although we are few, we usually are quite professional and we are trained to talk about these subjects.
So, dear audience, I'd tell you... (Sigh) I'm getting old.
There is no age.
The heart knows no age when it comes to falling in love.
The time is now.
Overcome the embarrassment, those who'd like to improve their sexuality, see a doctor, open doors, open avenues, for you and for your loved ones.
To a healthy sexuality: Let's enjoy life!
(Applause)
I was born in a city next to the sea.
Its immensity and sound were main characters of my childhood.
In front of the sea I would have a sense of mystery and no words to define it.
There was also a port in that city.
And there could be people from all over speaking strange languages, which obviously made me wonder where they came from.
But also what was on that horizon.
What was after that line that separates the sky from the sea?
Obviously that line gave me no answer.
So in my house I would draw imaginary maps of all places.
I would think up hypotheses, possible trajectories, sometimes guided by some flag from some ship.
I drew alphabets, I tried to pronounce very difficult yet very pleasant words.
I made a whole imaginary in intricate shapes, irregular strokes, colors, strange symbols, to build what I didn't know.
Many years later I came across a story about Africa.
They told me that the Zambian Ndembu leave marks on the trees when they go hunting.
This is called chijikijilu which literally means lighthouse.
But it also means "leading the way".
From the center of the village there is a circle that delimits what they know and it's ordered, from what's far beyond, which is chaotic and unordered; the unknown.
For the Ndembu that limit is the present.
So when they plunge into the jungle, they always walk the paths within the known places.
It's a way of going through the past.
On that edge, in that present, is where they set the lighthouse that illuminates what they still don't know; A way to get a glimpse of the future.
When I heard this story, it immediately brought me back to my childhood and to all that imaginary full of ignorance facing the sea, where causally there was a lighthouse, too.
But it also made me think in many of my projects where art and science converge.
Because art and science also explore the limits of the unknown.
That's where we may find words and also questions arise that others may have also made.
That's why many of my projects I do them in company of scientists like Mariano Sigman, a neuroscientist I've been working with for more than 10 years.
Many of our recurring subjects have to do with portraits, faces. We all express ourselves through words, our body language, but facial gestures play a central role in our expression and the way we relate with others.
Art knows a long history of portraiture, of gestures, even with the different sides within the same universe, like sculpture, painting, photography and, closer in time, video.
In many cases, after the many topics we have addressed, we realized that in neuroscience as well it's a matter of research since many years expression recognition and facial evaluation.
How a gesture can give us clues of the emotional and mental state of a person?
There is even evidence that in a very short time --around 250 milliseconds-- a gesture is capable of producing enough information to detect its emotion very clearly and concretely.
We took some of the gestures considered as basic and universal: joy, sadness, anger, fear, surprise.
A gesture could be constituted of multiple microgestures of very short duration, capable of communicating emotional states.
So we decided to go find them, shoot them with ultra slow motion, we elaborated a series of video portraits of those gestures, that are the history of that gesture slowed to almost zero.
What I am going to show you now in a minute is what in a gesture lasts no longer than a fraction of a second.
In art when we work and create images, --those of us who do that-- the look in the eyes is fundamental.
It's almost all the substance it's made of.
But how do we look?
What do we see when we are in front of an image?
Look at this picture for a moment.
Can you guess which part of this image captures your eyes?
Suppose we have access to something very intimate, like the eyes of a person.
It's as if we could see through them.
We can do this with a device called eye positioner, which is used in laboratories to record and study the movement of the eyes in front of an image.
We can understand where in the image the eyes stop for a moment to get information, how they go through it.
And something very important and interesting: What we don't see from an image although we believe we've seen it entirely.
So if we saw through someone's eyes, we would see this.
This is a very, very slow video, of what in fact happens very quickly, which is how a person sees the image I just showed you.
If we had 40 people looking at this image, for example, we would see this other thing.
Something we realized right away is that we spy on the world through the eyes as if it were through a lock.
The rest is built by the brain.
Other very important things are that the image is built in time.
It's something that we really have no conscience of.
And that the eyes move in a very particular, very personal way.
It's like the mark that identifies us, our signature, our way of speaking, of making gestures.
What I'm going to show you now is the reconstruction of that image from the gestures of 450 people looking at it simultaneously.
What emerges is the collective construction of that image
It's almost like revealing the image through the eyes.
With these methods and this technology we create videos, billboard photographs that we exhibit in museums, art galleries.
In any case, what we have here are science experiments turned into art.
Our practice travels across many different disciplines.
It's like embarking on a journey through different territories, with their own languages, with their own stories, worldviews and prejudices.
I imagine that creativity in the future will have to do with this kind of transdisciplinary and cross-cultural processes.
And unlike what happened in another time, where art and science incarnated in the single figure of someone's genius, today we say that these practices more established and developed, between different groups who share what they don't know, like the Ndembu of Zambia, --we go through the past through what we know--.
We can only reach to the limit of what we perceive and what we know in the present.
There, probably others who are also seeking will appear, and for whom we possibly are part of their chaos.
But knowing also that with those others, lies our future.
Thank you.
(Applause)
There is something very important, well, more fundamental than important about the human body that no one tells us when we try to become doctors in this country.
A six-year career, a one- or two-year residency, and up to four or five years of specialization, and in all of this time no one takes the time to talk to us at length about death.
True, medicine is a life science. It is not about death, and after all, Is not a doctor's mission assumed to be to try to avoid death?
Should we not be therefore more attuned and acquainted with how to prevent it and everything about it.
The truth is death remains taboo, even in medicine.
And if the death is that of a child, the issue is even greater.
Is there anything more difficult to digest than the death of a child?
Is there anything more difficult than the death of a son?
In our environment, fortunately, this is rarer each time.
For example, in our country, for every 1,000 live births only three will die before the age of five.
In contrast, in the poorest countries death in infancy is a much more common phenomenon.
And mortality rates are far greater.
It seems incredible but there are places in the world today where one in every ten newborns will die during childhood.
If there is another statistic that shows the massive and brutal inequalities between the rich and the poor someone please come and show me.
Many of you will think that figuring out how someone has died is relatively simple.
And this is true in our situation.
Today, if we get sick here or if we have some kind of health problem that puts our lives at risk we can always go to the nearest health center or hospital and there they can first examine us completely from top to bottom, run a battery of diagnostic tests, and very quickly retrieve all of our medical history.
Thus, making a diagnosis is relatively easy.
But what happens if we die on the street?
If we had the misfortune of falling dead in Plaza de Colón today forensic medicine would be in charge of finding out what happened to us by performing an autopsy.
So finding out what has killed someone in rich countries is relatively simple.
Also, autopsies are not an option. They are a legal requirement.
In other words, as a rule, by law, we have to submit to this practice.
On the other hand, in the poorest countries, particularly in rural areas of Africa where I spent many years working, the picture is completely different.
On the one hand, in these countries the majority of people are born and die without leaving a mark on the statistics.
Many people die without ever been seen by a doctor or medical professional.
So finding out what has occurred in these cases is quite different.
This is what some call the scandal of invisibility because they do not appear in the statistics.
In fact, many of these deaths do not matter to people, but this is because people are not aware that they are happening.
So what can we do?
We have to help to bring visibility to these deaths.
We have to put a spotlight on them to illuminate them.
so they can be shown to the world.
But this alone is not enough.
We have to try to understand why these deaths are happening in order to be able to design strategies that can help to prevent those causes that are preventable and can cure those causes that are curable.
And here is where we fail, and we fail a lot.
As I said before, in the poorest countries many deaths happen at home.
How do we investigate a death that has happened at home,
where no one has seen what has happened?
For these cases the World Health Organization developed a methodology quite a few years ago called the verbal autopsy.
And what the hell is the verbal autopsy?
Well, the verbal autopsy is nothing more than a structured interview that is conducted with family members of the deceased a few weeks to a few months after the death has occurred.
The goal of the interview is to try to collect as much available information possible to help us to understand what happened during the disease process up to the moment of death.
The answers to the questions asked of the interviewee, the family member of the deceased, are later evaluated by a doctor or team of doctors or by computer software.
How valuable do you think a method like this is that depends on the answers of family members about something that happened weeks or even months before?
What kind of medical background are we demanding from the respondents, the interviewees, in contexts in which, in many cases, they do not know how to read or write.
The truth is that the credibility of verbal autopsies is so low that it is like flipping a coin.
And this is what we see in the type of answers we get.
For example, "What happened to him?"
"He had moon sickness."
Wow, moon sickness!
I do not think this is going to make sense in my notes.
"And when did he begin to have symptoms?"
Well from the moment he stopped being healthy.
Ah.
(Laughter) "And you? What do you think he died of?"
"He died of disease."
These are not medical diagnoses.
These are diagnoses of resignation.
Notice that the only time in my life that I have been quoted in "Science" the prestigious scientific magazine, I am quoted as saying that, the verbal autopsy is a crap method".
(Laughter) A statement I'm not proud of -- (Applause) A statement I am not proud of, but I continue to completely subscribe to.
Anyway, I suspect that "Science" will not be asking me again.
(Laughter) But let's return to the methods that we use in poor countries to try to find out what a someone has died from.
If this person was lucky enough to have come in contact with the healthcare system and to have been seen in a clinic or hospital we will have a little more information.
But again, how reliable will this clinical information be where there is a major shortage of qualified medical personnel, and we have very few diagnostic methods available.
I'll give you the example of Mozambique, the country in which I've worked for the last few years and know the best, where for a population of 27 million inhabitants there are less than 1200 doctors distributed throughout the whole country.
As you can imagine with so few doctors, the bulk of clinical care is not performed by doctors it is performed by health workers trained in a less rigorous, more accelerated way, for example, like health agents or nurses.
And what about the diagnostic methods?
To give you an idea, in the hospital where I worked, maybe one of the best hospitals in the whole country despite being a hospital in a rural area, we had less than four or five diagnostic lab methods available for routine clinical care,
and we could only rely on simple x-rays as the only diagnostic imaging method.
In other words, it is only logical the result of everything I just explained is to ask ourselves, "Are we as physicians getting it right?"
If they make it so difficult for us, "How close are we?"
And the only way to know how close we are is to compare the diagnosis that comes from the doctors who saw the patient before death with the diagnosis produced and determined by the reference method which is the anatomic-pathologic autopsy,
nothing more than opening up the body and looking for one's self.
Our group, which has spent more than 20 years working to improve available diagnostic methods for diseases in the world's poorest countries, has been able to confirm that the number of errors made while the patient is still alive are very important,
and that in even up to half of the cases there are discrepancies between what the physician says, before death, and what the autopsy says, after death.
You will then ask yourselves, after this example, "Why are we not performing autopsies in the poorest countries on those who have died?
There are two primary reasons: First is the overwhelming shortage of pathologists or skilled personnel to perform autopsies in the poorest countries.
In a place like Spain, we have a pathologist for every 15,000 to 20,000 inhabitants.
While in Africa there exist countries where there is not even one pathologist for the entire population.
But more important than the human resources issue, which ultimately can be solved with accelerated training, we have a very important problem here related to the acceptability of the method.
This is a gory method, and it is invasive.
Nobody likes the idea of a family member being cut open, looked inside of, and returned with a scar that goes from the chest to the abdomen.
I cannot help but sympathize with this feeling.
So, although the comprehensive autopsy, the conventional autopsy, which is the reference method, is the best way to determine how a person died, the reality is that this method struggles for acceptability.
Thus, it is an impractical method in these environments.
How can we find our way out of this apparent dead end?
Because knowing what people died from is essential to be being able to design health policies, and crucial for putting measures into place to prevent these deaths.
It is also key to be better able to direct the scarce financial resources available to these countries for health.
And here is the Eureka moment of this talk.
It did not occur to me, alone in the tub like Archimedes, but to my colleagues Pathologist Jaume Ordi and Epidemiologist Clara Menéndez. This is also their baby.
And no, the three of us were not altogether in the tub.
(Laughter) We researchers always have our eureka moments in places far less glamorous, like the lab, for example.
How can we do an autopsy taking a look for one's self inside the body without opening it?
How can we get samples from major organs, particularly the most important ones like the brain, the lungs, and the liver in a less invasive manner.
How can we develop a method that will not be systematically rejected by communities and family members as they grieve.
The only method that occurred to us was using this type of needle.
I will not remove the cap. I do not want anything bad to happen.
So, what do we do with these needles?
Well, with these needles we prick the body.
And by pricking the body we are able to get samples of major tissue.
Small cylinders of tissue like these that we will be able to analyze in the lab.
These small cylinders, which are like noodles, can be examined under the microscope, and they can give us important information like if the organ from which we took them was diseased or healthy.
They can also tell us if the patient had an infection.
If we apply this method systematically, we will be able to see what is happening to major organs of the body and therefore have a fairly comprehensive idea of what happened inside the body, and what probably killed the person.
And we accomplish all of this barely leaving a visible mark on the body.
Totally different from what happens with a conventional autopsy.
You see, it is an extremely simple but tremendously powerful idea.
Our group set out to work, and in the last five years we have succeeded in validating this new approach.
We have confirmed that the minimally invasive autopsy, as it is now known, is a credible method that can give an answer to the primary cause of death and whose diagnoses correspond, almost always, to what the conventional autopsy says.
For those deaths from infectious disease, which are in fact very common in poor countries, the technique works particularly well.
It is capable of very precisely diagnosing the name, and if you rush me up to eight names of the most important microorganisms that cause the infection and therefore are possibly responsible for the death.
All this is very good; the technique works.
But the most important breakthrough of this project has to do with the acceptability of the technique, its Achilles heel.
We started with practically no acceptability.
The conventional autopsy is an unpleasant and dirty technique, and it is a method from which rumors of the most varied kind are born. It has a terrible reputation.
Our team of anthropologists has been able to show, not only in Mozambique but in a wide variety of different socio cultural, geographic, and religious settings, that this method has been much better received by the communities.
Thus, we have a technique that works. We have a method that is being well accepted, much more than that of the conventional autopsy, and furthermore, we have a method that is easy to execute.
Anyone can learn how to perform one of these autopsies, with two or three days of training, and you do not need a pathologist to implement it.
What are the consequences of all of these findings?
Our method has been adopted and rapidly implemented in up to a dozen countries in Africa and Asia to investigate, on a pilot basis, what children here are dying from.
And the data that is being generated from this project has begun to shift the paradigms about death and its causes
And what if such a pathogen really is a lot more frequent than what we thought?
And what if such a disease begins to appear in places where it was thought it would never occur?
And what if a simple, plausible, and logical explanation exist for why some of the prevention and treatment measures that we are implementing for common diseases are not working?
Minimally invasive autopsies are starting to answer some of these questions.
Most importantly and finally with credible data.
Minimally invasive autopsies also have the potential to start a true metamorphosis in the way we investigate cause of death.
Who would of have told tell me, barely a few years ago, that a method born out of the autopsy room was going to fill such a huge part of my professional life.
Especially, considering that it was there, in the autopsy room, where I failed my Anatomy I practical exam, the only subject I failed in my degree.
(Laughter) Is there life after death?

No, there is not.
But is there life from death?
I believe so, I'm sure of it.
I am sure that death can teach us a lot.
I am sure that from so many premature, preventable, and unnecessary deaths, we can learn the right lessons that will allow us to prevent a future where your place of birth is the primary factor in your chances for survival.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Imagine the most apocalyptic movie you've ever seen.
The first scene is an aerial view of razed land and a blackened sea.
Half of the world's population fights the other half for lack of food in a desert land.
Four billion people live in areas where they can't grow anything to eat and don't have enough water to survive.
Floods and droughts; unbearable temperatures.
New diseases attack us; forest fires.
Very frequent hurricanes.
Mass migrations.
Hundreds of millions of environmental refugees.
Looting in supermarkets multiplied by thousands everywhere.
Power outages.
Financial systems and communications down.
Water, fuel and medicines shortages.
One million species disappear and not for one more extinction, but for the first biological annihilation produced by a single species.
But you know what?
This is not science fiction.
This is an ecological and climatic catastrophe.
It is the world of the next 10 years.
You, in the first row, you won't save yourself.
You, at the fifth row, either.
You behind the camera, either.
Neither won't I.
Because it doesn't matter where we sit.
The collapse will ran over us anyway unless, during the next decade, we transform our production and consumption system. Unless we reverse the advance of livestock and of industrial agriculture. And we cut the use of oil to a half.
This means changing everything, or almost everything.
Today, I have some minutes to warn you about extreme gravity of our situation.
Just a couple of minutes for you to be convinced that this is not a conspiracy theory.
What are we really facing is the worst of all crises.
Our civilization as we know it will cease to exist during the next 10 years.
And are not responsible for this the 4 billion people who can die.
Those mainly responsible for having reached this state are the ones who are most prepared to face this apocalyptic forecast because they listen to science.
The richest and most powerful are designing their family shelters to survive the collapse.
A former manager of a famous social network says that when he started sharing with his Silicon Valley colleagues the details of his little island project, everyone started coming out of the closet and to reveal their plans for survival.
Right now they are buying lands in heights.
Complete villages with natural abundance.
They are building bunkers, installing power generators, solar panels, and designing military tactics for their defense.
They are "shielding" their private islands.
We face an inevitable scenario of violent social fragmentation.
And even the United Nations defined it without cover as "climate apartheid".
The rich saving themselves.
And most of humanity forced to survive.
There is absolute consensus in the scientific community.
To go over the limit of global temperature in more than 1.5 °C is a massive ecosystem collapse.
After the Amazon fires we will reach an increase of 2 °C during the next decade.
And thanks to 25 years of failed diplomatic negotiations we will be at 7 °C before the end of the century.
How can it be that our governments, that have all this information, do nothing about it?
Something in this looking for solutions within the system, is just not working.
Me myself, I feel that I failed.
I failed as an activist for 20 years because obviously all forms of activism failed.
They were not enough.
Very recently, I grasped the real dimension of this impending finitude.
I felt desperate and full of anxiety.
I knew that nothing I could do would be enough.
I also noticed that people I loved, when I talked about this, would go somewhere else
or sighed and changed the subject.
How could I judge them? Maybe it's better to enjoy life before the collapse.
But not me.
It was impossible for me to pretend as if nothing happened.
Then, came the pain and the need to cry.
Although, it was not the only thing.
Slowly, something else emerged from this pit of sadness.
I stopped forcing myself to hope and a fury surfaced that filled me with energy.
It speeded up my heart and led me into action.
Because what I needed was not hope.
What I needed was courage.
Courage to question everything.
To ask myself a taboo question: Why do we have to abide by the rules of the system that is exterminating us?
Is it logical to keep encouraging livestock and agro toxic production?
If we know that it devastates entire communities and ecosystems.
Is it fair that our taxes subsidize oil fracking projects?
Unconventional extraction of fossil fuels accelerates the climate disaster.
The complicity of institutions hurts to the bone.
But it's not the first time we are witnesses of a silent genocide.
In other times of history, mass extermination processes took place, sought to be hidden by the authorities.
This time we have to challenge this unfair authority at the right time, because when the laws and institutions don't protect us, the social contract disappears.
And we citizens have the right and the duty of civil disobedience.
(Applause) You may wonder if what I suggest is a kind of rebellion.
What I say is that this model is self-destructive.
Because it is based on denying the physical limitations of the planet.
And if it’s not enough that the United Nations says it, or that I point it here, go to social media.
It is full of reliable sources.
But to this scientific truth, governments reply that it is politically and socially impossible to change so fast.
However, we already took part in radical changes in times of emergency that were thought impossible.
For example, during wars.
This is the biggest emergency we have ever faced.
We have less than 10 years left to make a transformation of epic proportions, that we are not going to achieve by just putting windmills, solar panels, recycling or reducing our individual consumption of meat.
We need the greatest mobilization of people in history.
Those who have studied the success of civil resistance movements, determined that if 3.5 percent of the population can sustain over time non-violent disruption strategies, taking a fair claim to the streets, we can rock a system the refuses to listen.
Non-violent Civil Disobedience has been the most effective method throughout recent history to achieve systemic and radical changes.
Famous examples abound.
Rosa Parks, Martin Luther King, Gandhi, groups of women who demanded and achieved female suffrage, and today continue to fight for indispensable changes.
Our country has beautiful experiences of non-violent and successful civil disobedience.
Mainly in rural areas.
Malvinas Argentinas, a town in Cordoba, stopped Monsanto from moving into their land through four years of collective disruption.
The community of Famatina, in La Rioja, has been expelling for more than a decade the largest mining companies in the world despite regulations and written rules.
There is no gradualism when we fight for our survival.
The phenomena of planetary transformation will not stop for a law.
We can't stop with a decree the millions of people who will be crossing borders running from extreme weather events.
Maybe, right now, you want to go back to the world you knew 10 minutes ago.
But I have no choice.
We have no choice.
With creativity and without violence we have to go outside to change everything.
The transformation has to start today.
And it has to be huge, disproportionate, beyond possible.
The only decision we can make in these times of uncertainty is what to do with the time we have.
No one is going to do it for us.
The real power belongs to us, let's embrace it, with love and fury.
There's not much time left.
And to be fair, it has to be disobedient.
(Applause)
Before I start, I would like you to look at the following picture and think, for a second, what you would do if you met a person with a similar reaction.
A person with an attack of anger, fury, or with a strange behavior.
But, what if this person wasn't a stranger?
What if this person was a brother, a friend, or a grandfather?
Would you react in the same way?
There are more than 11,5 million people in the world that due to a pathology, a disorder or because of an accident, they're not able to communicate.
They cannot tell the people they love the most what they think, what they feel, what they want each and every time.
This incapacity generates such frustration, that these people react in this way, with attacks of fury, anger, or the total opposite, with the most absolute isolation.
And the rest of us who do not suffer from this we neglect them, we cut them off either consciously or unconsciously.
But we do it.
We reject them.
Without realizing that the key to social relations is communication.
This is what distinguishes us from other animals.
It's our capacity to communicate,
to be social beings.
To communicate is very difficult.
Tell me about it!
Not only is it important what we say but how we say it.
And it's in this "how" of communication where the problem lies.
It's because we focus on that.
We focus on the shout of rage, on the punch in the wall, on the young person who skips classes.
And it is these imperfections in our social relationships what lead them to absolute isolation.
Today I would like to introduce you to someone.
It's my uncle. His name is Paco.
Paco suffers from schizophrenia.
Schizophrenia is a pretty serious mental disorder, which is characterized by imagining and seeing things that really don't exist.
Paco even invents things which have never happened.
Moreover, due to the strong medications he has been losing his ability to speak little by little and this made it really difficult to communicate with him.
An example of this is one of his many ways.
Every day, twice a day, and at the same time, he goes to the bathroom and starts to play with water.
At first we accepted it as another ritual.
But the problem was when he got out of the bathroom.
Some days he would go out extremely happy but some others he got very angry.
He would yell at us, try to hit us or push us.
Until the situation got unbearable.
We tried to communicate with Paco in a million ways,
but Paco doesn't speak, he doesn't write nor he reads.
Until one day it occurred to me to draw.
"Draw what?", you might say.
Well, I started drawing all the items
that were around Paco.
The items in the bathroom or the items that were in the kitchen to make him tell me which ones made him angry.
Even though it doesn't seem true, thanks to that exchange of pictures, Paco told us what was going on.
And what was going on was amazing.
Every time he walked into the bathroom, he entered into another reality.
He imagined he was in the middle of the Civil War and that as he played with his fingers and his hands, he imagined he rose in ranks in the legion.
He started as corporal and rose until becoming a general.
The problem was when we interrupted him, of course. The game would be over.
Ever since that day, we never interrupted him again.
And we really spared ourselves two daily disputes.
Thanks to this homemade and improvised solution, we managed to solve a problem in a very concrete situation.
But without realizing this would be the first contribution to a solution that would help thousands of people around the world.
And why do I live with Paco?
For you to understand why, and especially to understand why I am here today, I'll tell you my story.
When I was 5 my parents split up and my mother took me with her away from my brother.
After having many men in and out of her life and after her evident lack of interest in being a mother, she returned me like a package.
At least I was back with my brother.
But the happiness was short lived.
Around the age of 8 or 9, I was sexually assaulted by someone very close.
My fear, my innocence, my frustration, made me isolate from everything.
I wasn't capable of telling anyone what had happened to me.
Not even to my brother, whom I loved the most.
I remained silent, much like Paco did.
Nine years went by, it'd been 9 long years of silence until I was 17 and I became capable of telling my brother what had happened.
And because of this I would like to dedicate this talk to him.
I would like to dedicate the talk to him for returning back to me, but most of all for not judging me.
He was the key that opened the door to my communication.
He was the key to end all the agony that I bore inside.
But these two experiences made me realize that there are many people in the world with the same problem as Paco and I.
So I set myself out to help them.
When I started studying special education and the teaching practices, I met many children with cerebral palsy, with autism, it didn't matter.
They had the same problem: communication.
Then, I suddenly remembered the situation I had with Paco, the exchange of pictures, so I set myself to transform this situation into a tool; a tool that would be really useful, that would let them communicate at any moment and with anyone.
We started with a prototype based on pictograms distinguishing the different contexts where the child could play or work
And within each of these contexts, all the elements that they would need to communicate.
Obviously, we tried it first with Paco and it was a success.
But even so, he wasn't fully satisfied.
He looked at drawings, at pictograms that made him feel like a child.
And he wasn't a child.
Then we realized that if we wanted the tool to be really functional, we had to make it totally customized.
It should have the places he visited, the people he knew, the food he ate.
After that, he felt really comfortable with it.
We carried out an investigation with more than 150 children in the Valencian Community.
And we kept in touch with professionals and parents.
But out of this investigation, what I would like to share with you is the contact that I had with these families.
"My child doesn't love me.
I haven't been a good mother."
This is what María told me.
María is the mother of a 5 year-old child with autism who had never spoken.
María is fed up with looking for ways to be able to reach her child and know what's going on, what he wants at any time.
But she can't.
"If at least he would look me in the eyes," she told me.
But María wasn't the only one that moved me.
"My child will always be useless."
Imagine the frustration a parent has to say that his child, his child will always be useless.
Javier is the father of a child with cerebral palsy who doesn't walk, doesn't talk and, moreover, he has a visual impairment.
Javier is fed up with looking for tools in technology to be able to make his son communicate with him.
But he tells me, "It's just that there's none that would adapt, there is no technology adjusted to what happens to my son."
And Javier is right.
If we go to any application store and write, let's say, "Applications for people with autism,"
there are thousands.
And all of them say they serve the same purpose.
But there are more than 67 million people in the world with autism.
Really. Is this same static application going to help them in the same way?
I don't think so.
And here's where the evolution of our tool started.
At this point it didn't only matter to us the pathology the child had, if they had paralysis, autism, and which level.
What mattered to us were many more things.
It mattered to us if they had epilepsy or not.
If they had a mental delay, the level of language they had.
If we were working on a tool for communication we had to know every one of the details: what was their level of learning.
If we really wanted to create a unique tool which would adjust to every single person.
With all these data, our machine autogenerates the best tool for each person, taking into account each detail.
It autogenerates different panels of communication that evolve according to the evolution of the user.
This is connected to a platform where it not only stores all this information but it also stores all the materials, all the pictures, the audios, the videos.
In this way, they are available for any user that wants to enter the platform which makes this customization much more simple, making it much easier to make this tool unique.
A very simple example: a father who goes to a zoo one day and takes pictures of different animals with his tablet
This material goes automatically to the platform and it's available for maybe a teacher who would like to make the same trip the following week.
Not only can he give an idea of the activity to the child, but also the child will be able to communicate that day with his friends and his teacher without any kind of problem.
All of this is thanks to the use of artificial intelligence which allows the machine to learn from the child.
And also thanks to the use of "big data" which allows the storage and analysis of a large quantity of data in an efficient way.
Javier's son still cannot walk or talk but now he has a tool that adapts to his visual impairment and allows him to communicate with his father.
Maria's child is still not able to talk either and he rarely looks into the eyes but now he can tell his mother that he wants cookies and not juice; that he wants to go to the park, not the pool or that he's angry because he has a stomachache.
In the world we meet many, many people, and we don't know why they behave in a strange way; they behave in a way that is beyond our normal standards.
I would like to ask you, if this happens to you, don't abandon them, don't shun them.
The same way my brother was my key, and I in turn was the key for Paco, each and every one of you can be the key for someone.
The key to communication.
The key to social integration.
Surely you all know him.
He's one of the greatest minds known in history.
But surely you don't know that Einstein couldn't speak until the age of 4, and he couldn't write until the age of 7.
In fact, many of his teachers defined him as useless, but his mother never gave up on him.
When I was assaulted, and I started to behave in a strange way, my teachers, including my family, thought I would never achieve anything.
Now with 22 years I've graduated, I'm doing my master's in neuropsychology, I've founded an association for people with autism, and I work every day to make people like Javier's son, Maria's son, or Paco, able to communicate.
There are many Einsteins in the world, plenty of yet undiscovered Einsteins.
It's in our hands not to lose any more of them.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I'd like to talk about two things: Technology and language.
Terence McKenna said that, "Humanity is an extruder of technological material". We take material with a low level of organisation, pass it through mental filters and output: jewels, Gospels, spaceships and that's part of our nature.
That sounds strange, but there are other authors who talk about it.
For example, Marshall McLuhan said that, in some way, thanks to technology we are externalising our nervous system.
The truth is that technology allows us to amplify our abilities. Mental as much as physical. It allows us to better understand the world and start to change it.
And that sounds strange to us because we don't think of technology as being something natural.
If I show you this picture, few of you would associate it with technology.
That said, it's very advanced technology.
This dam was built by beavers.
Just like when a monkey takes ants out of an ant's nest with a stick, or when a bird builds a nest.
That's all technology.
We don't usually associate the concept of technology with nature but we are who we are thanks to technology.
When we discovered fire or invented tools to hunt with, we began to feed ourselves more efficiently and this left us with more mental RAM to develop: culture, critical thinking, religion, art... We are who we are today thanks to technology.
It defines who we are and how we think.
McKenna also said that: "We evolve as quickly as our language," and that we can only understand what we can express in words. What we can name.
To give an example, there are tribes in the Amazon that don't have words for "yesterday" or "tomorrow". They don't revere their ancestors, they don't plan for the winter.
They live today. They also don't have numbers beyond: "one, two, a lot"
Seven, one hundred, fifty are all "a lot"
There are a lot of words that they don't have and, as a result, they don't understand those concepts.
We could say that their view of the world, is completely determined by their language and that they evolve as quickly as their language progresses.
Thanks to technology, we are learning new languages.
Technology is allowing us to understand new things, expand our understanding of the world around us.
For example, when we invented the telescope our perception of ourselves changed.
We went from being the centre of the universe, to a speck among millions of galaxies.
When we invented the microscope, suddenly, we became the universe:
every pore containing a million cells and other living things.
Our ability to understand the world is determined by what we know about it.
And the technology that helps us to understand all of that is developing exponentially.
That means that it doubles every year.
Processors double in capacity every year, that's Moore's law.
Soon, a processor's capacity will be equal to that of the entire human race.
Soon, there will be as many transistors on the planet Earth as grains of sand.
In a way, we're cracking the code of reality.
We're learning how reality works. Transforming it. Modifying it.
Imagine how we would seem to a Roman Centurian from the first century.
We're humans capable of flight. We're humans who breath under water. Able to generate as much energy as a bolt of lightning.
We are creating artificial intelligences that will soon be autonomous. In other words, life.
Or the way that, for example, we're increasing our life span.
150 years ago, we lived as long as a person from 10,000 years ago: about 35 or 40 years.
By the mid 20th century, the average was 60 years.
These days it's 90.
In ten years it'll be 150.
In twenty we'll live forever.
We're the closest to being a god than any human or living being has ever been. And we're starting to learn how to alter reality. But more than that, we're also learning how to create it.
And this is the final leap.
I want to talk about a technology today, which although it isn't new, it's been in development a few years now, is about to change everything: virtual reality.
Of the two words "virtual reality" I want you to remember one in particular, and, probably, it isn't the one you expect... Reality.
Arthur C. Clarke said that, "Any sufficiently advanced technology is indistinguishable from magic".
I'd like you to do an exercise, I'd like you to look at your hand very closely for a second.
Look at it. Look at every pore, take a look at the lines, the beads of sweat, look at the fingerprints. And I'm willing to give up my place on the stage to anybody who can show me, right now, that what you're seeing isn't a very very very very advanced 3D software.
The truth is we can't.
We can't prove that: elementary particles, matter, the sea, the sky, this stage, you... We can't prove that we aren't somewhere else's virtual reality.
What we do, all together, is accept that this is what surrounds us, accept that the rules are the same for everybody, and we call this reality.
We all agree that this is reality. But imagine if, suddenly, they invented a device that, well, is still quite unwieldy, far from being invisible, but is very close to being magic.
A device that, upon entering it, even though we're sat in our living rooms, when we approach a cliff, it sends a command to the reptile part of the brain saying: Don't take another step!
There's danger here!
And it makes you feel vertigo.
A device that creates a reality around you one that has: shape, colour movement, sound... An almost magical device that allows us to do almost anything.
To be anywhere, travel anywhere, create any reality.
Human connections develop via neurotransmitters like oxytocin, serotonin, dopamine.
The more human the connection, the more they're stimulated.
A typed letter will elicit less of a response than one written by hand.
And this, in turn, less than a phone call,
and this less than a Skype call, where we see the other person,
and this less than meeting in person, of course.
Imagine that for a Skype call, we could generate an environment where we have the other person in front of us and we have, what we in virtual reality call "presence."
The feeling of tricking our brain to make it think that it's there. And that, thanks to haptic technologies, which will be developed in future, we can feel touch.
Imagine, not just that, but imagine that I'm in there talking with my girlfriend and an artificial intelligence scans our Instagram accounts, detects the photos where we're happy and together and starts to recreate around us those places where we were happy long ago.
And beyond that, why not?
Suddenly, with a click, I can change the evening and make it more appealing, add music... And... Why not?
Also physically measures us... allowing me to see how she reacts to what I say to her.
See how her body lights up as she's stimulated by a word I say or a touch. It's really incredible.
The potential that this has is amazing.
And, I don't know if you've tried or had the chance to try virtual reality, but speaking about it is like dancing about architecture.

It's ridiculous, can't be done.
It must be experienced.
It's really amazing.
And all of a sudden, it's not the technology that's exponential ...but connections.
Our ability to do, say, think.
Imagine, for example, that you got up on a typical day and you get up an hour later because you no longer need to drive to work, you no longer have to waste an hour in a traffic jam. and you connect to your office, the one you've always worked in, with the same colleagues, overlooking Central Park.
You have a good job.
You spend the day working in the office with them on a prototype that you can rotate above your hand and, at some point, you need to handle something with the engineer and you go visit his home in Hawaii and you spend some time with him in Hawaii.
And at some point your day ends and you decide to take a walk.
You take off the helmet and you're back home in Valladolid, where the rent is much cheaper, where you can take your child to the park, where you have a lot more time. And during your walk, your wife
calls you and says: "Can you come and buy a dress with me for the dinner we have on Saturday?"
That being a real life dinner.
You connect and go with her to a shop where you can see an exact avatar of her, and she can try on any dress, any size, any colour, adjust it, change something... She buys it after you've found the perfect dress.
She heads off and you continue your walk
And you obviously have a lot more time to enjoy the day now because you don't need to queue at the market, you don't need to queue at the doctors', all of this sounds a lot like science fiction. Actually, it's already invisible.
Think about the internet. Before, it was something we connected to.
We sat in front of the computer, spent a few hours, disconnected and then carried on with our lives.
These days, the internet comes with us, it's everywhere.
It's just a means, it hangs around and makes our everyday lives easier.
In this way it's the same. There will come a time when there is no difference between virtual reality and reality reality.
Where you'll enter your virtual reality, look at your hand and be unable to say if it's very very advanced 3D software or reality.
When you come home, your son is playing with Pedro, Juan and Buzz.
And Buzz Lightyear has the same size and shape, and he's as real as Pedro and Juan. and he's teaching them the names of the planets, the galaxies... When your wife gets home, you decide to watch a film.
She'll be Angelina and you'll be Brad Pitt.
And you'll be inside the action looking around, seeing things exploding around you and knowing that when you look into the eyes of the other character, the person you love will be behind them.
And if we can do all of this, there's nothing that can stop us from doing everything we want to, and building all of the worlds that we want to build.
Imagine a world where empathy is more important that property because it's no longer a limited quantity.
Imagine a world where anybody who can't move, now can, where a person who can't travel, can now travel anywhere wherever, whenever and however.
Where creativity is increased a thousand fold because we have all of the tools at our disposal, where we can make anything.
And the most powerful thing about this, isn't just the idea of a device in which we can travel, it's the idea of it as a language, as a new ability to understand the world differently, to tailor a reality to ourselves, to expand our abilities, to expand our connections.
In conclusion, what's happened with virtual reality is that we've build a tool to learn how to say "tomorrow". Thank you.
(Applause)
Hello. First of all, good evening everybody. Usually, on any workshop I present, one of the things I like people to do is to get involved.
To participate.
So, I'll start with an exercise before I continue with my talk today.
I want you to think about an insult.
One that someone has told you and had hurt you.
Do you have it?
I'm pretty sure everybody in this room has been labeled by someone at some point in time one way or another.
Well, I want you to think on that word and try to remember how you felt when they said it.
Pretty bad, right?
Well, that's what I would like to talk to you about today. To know whether or not we are conscious about the true power of words.
Words really matter because we based on them the way we think about others, the way we describe their actions, create our vision of the world as a whole.
Besides, these words could have either positive or negative effects on people.
So, calling someone one way or another, - I'm sure that rings a bell to all of us - it's never innocent.
When people say we are smart, interesting, brave, or that we are unique, or good looking, we feel good about ourselves.
We feel strong and capable.
However, what happens when people tell us negative things?
You have given the answer before, right?
We feel bad.
It's said that "Words are gone with the wind", but we know this isn't true.
A lot of these words are difficult to forget, namely because they carry a big and important emotional load, right?
Yes or no?
(Audience) Yes.
So then, I would like you to start being conscious about the words we say.
To start using them to empower others, to make them stronger, to somehow be able to make these people feel better about themselves.
I'm not here to preach, not at all, but please understand. When we use words to criticize or mock someone what we are actually doing is we take what is different about this person, and we turn it into something negative.
Is it true or not?
And this doesn't really make much sense. When we try and mock someone the first thing we do is we hold the view that what that person does, says, or has a bodily feature that's abnormal.
However, do we ever ask ourselves what's normal, who dictates normality?
Do we ask ourselves what normal is?
Normality is such a relative term. It varies according to the economical, social, or cultural context. For instance, think about it, when we travel. When we travel and taste food from other countries, or see the way they behave, or the language they use, that is normal for them; however, for us may be different.
Another example, while watching TV: nowadays there are these programs about millionaires.
We understand that normal for them could be going on a trip, or going shopping every day. However a regular day for us means going to school.
Besides, what we understand by normal, may be different tomorrow, right?
What's normal to me, I'm sure it's not the same for my grandmother, and likewise, it's different to my little brother, right?
This is why, this idea of normality will always be subjective and personal.
That's why I would like to invite you to stop using it. Don't you think this is a good idea?
What I would like you to understand is that every one of us has his or her peculiarities and singularities
that we will always be unique and irreplaceable people and that this is an extremely important quality.
If you don't agree let's think of another exercise: let's think about those people we love the most: our friends or our family.
I'm sure that every one of them has a weird hobby or habit, maybe some sort of eccentricity, or maybe a part of their body that makes them different.
However, we would never dare to say they are not normal?
I'm pretty sure, we wouldn't.
And of course, the reason is that because we really love those people we wouldn't say they are not normal, wouldn't we?
We believe their features make them different, unique, special.
My question is why don't we use the same criteria on everybody in this world?
Am I right or am I not?
Well, then what I would like you to do from now on is I'd encourage you to see our differences and the human diversity spectrum not as a threat but as something positive and enriching.
And secondly, to be more aware of the words we are using, to start using them in a more positive way in order to help others. This is how we can build a more equal and just world where we can live together with less violence.
And surely, this way, our lives will be happier.
Thank you.
(Applause)
In a world in which the word that defines the road I walk sounds increasingly obsolete, I can say with pride that I'm a magician.
And I'm also a storyteller.
And a relatively short time ago, I discovered that they're the same thing.
"Abracadabra," the magic word, par excellence, comes from Aramaic, the oldest Semitic language in the world. It means, "I create as I speak".
They say that magicians make things appear and disappear, but the truth is that our job is to surprise, which means to put into view.
To discover, to remove that which covers, that which is hidden, and integrate, reunite what's been separated.
Like a puzzle that was once a complete picture, and which can only recover its meaning once its pieces are put back together.
A while back, I was invited to go see a very famous speaker, whose talk ended up being a little bit of everything.
It ended with a story that some of you will surely know, about two men in the middle of the jungle, who suddenly see a hungry, ferocious lion running towards them.
One of the guys was paralyzed with fear, but the other calmly sat down on a rock, took a pair of running shoes out of his backpack, and started to put them on.
Surprised, the other guy said: "Do you really think you can run faster than the lion?"
And the first guy said, parsimoniously: "I don't need to run faster than the lion.
I only need to run faster than you."
(Laughter) (Applause) The speaker ended his talk with this: "In today's world, all that matters is to run faster than the competition."
That story sparked a look back at my own career in magic.
I began to study when I was 9, and did my first professional show at 15.
I was a traditional magician until the age of 19: Amazing people was my only goal.
I was good at what I did.
People would offer me more work, recommend me to others.
Early in my life, I had managed to "run" quickly enough.
But I wasn't happy with what I was doing, and I couldn't enjoy it.
Eventually, I figured out what the empty feeling inside of me was: My magic lacked magic.
I had tasted magic in my daily life-- in a kiss, in a sunset, in an embrace-- but I couldn't create that magical, tingly feeling in my audiences or shows.
Until one day, when I happened to receive a poem that touched me deeply, and in which I felt I had found the lost magic.
I immediately committed the poem to memory, and at the end of my next show I recited it, humbly, and I felt a connection.
Later, while I was gathering my things, two women approached, moved by the poem.
One of them took my hands and said: "We really like what you did, young man, but we found what you said at the end particularly touching, because it made everything else make sense."
I had managed to bring together two things that had always been separate.
My magic had regained its magic.
Because magic without magic is only a trick.
A bag of tricks.
The secret of this reconnection is pretty simple: We have only one mind but it's divided into two parts.
The left side is rational, pragmatic, structured, skeptical, programmed to question everything and believe nothing.
The right side, in contrast, is emotional, intuitive, naïve, creative, magical, programmed to believe everything and question nothing.
But we live in a very rational and frenetic world, that forces us to depend on reason and dedicate ourselves almost exclusively to production and consumption.
Nowadays, people have neither the time nor the disposition to play, to create, to contemplate the world around us with genuine curiosity.
We've become separated from ourselves.

We've disintegrated. Dis-integrated.
We live barely utilizing half of our potential and the world wants us to believe that's how it should be; that living that way is good enough.
But denying this part of ourselves hasn't made it disappear.
Invisible is not the same as nonexistent.
The magical experience, if you're willing to look beyond the surprise, supports integration and reconnection.
That which we can't explain creates a short circuit in the logical brain, which makes us lose control and it weakens the barriers that separate us on the inside.
So naturally, our parts become integrated, and we can go through the magical experience, whatever kind it may be, the slightest, the most everyday, whole, sound, and without being pulled this way and that.
If it weren't for the rational mind, we could permanently connect with the magic that surrounds us.
Natural magic, as it was called at the beginning of time, is revealed in every moment of creation.
For starters, we're here and we're alive.
No two human beings are alike in all the world.
We see magic in the gestation and birth cycle of animals; or in music, where from a mere seven notes infinite worlds are created in endless combinations; or in trips, where in a question of hours we can travel from the harshest summer to the harshest winter; or in two glances that meet for the first time and become inseparable.
I'm convinced that magic, in any of its forms, enriches and strengthens our experience of living.
That's why I'm here: to invite you all to recuperate the magic that exists inside every single one of us.
To do that, the only thing we have to do is stop fighting it, fearing it, and ignoring it.
And yet, I am in no way suggesting that you abandon your skepticism or your search for answers, which are themselves worthwhile.
I don't think we should become followers or fanatics of the invisible, nor that we should replace our left side with our right up here, because neither of the two sides can be the only one.
What I've come to suggest is that we re-unite ourselves, that we integrate our parts: our skepticism with our naiveté;
our reason with our emotion;
our intelligence with our intuition.
That way we use all our parts.
All of them are indispensable pieces of the puzzles of our lives.
To test this theory, I ask you all to share with me a magical experience right here, right now.
You're all going to participate. And I ask all of you who are not here with us, wherever and whoever you may be, that you also participate as if you were here.
I need to ask you all to close your eyes now.
I'm asking you to trust me; I promise this is going somewhere. Come on, then.
Close your eyes.
Uncross your arms and legs --
I guarantee if you do, you'll enjoy this experience 30% more.
For those who have a seat back, lean against it. If you don't have a seat back,
sit up straight.
Try to breathe from as deep a place as possible, way down in the belly.
Let go of the hand of the person next to you especially if you didn't come here together. (Laughter) Breathe as slowly and deliberately as you can, and try to listen only to my voice and to the music.
(Music) Imagine that you're sitting in your favorite place,
that one place where you could spend hours, just being there.
As you enjoy this moment, free of worries, free of obligations, an image begins to form before you, that's wonderful to behold. You can start to see different colors, shapes, textures, and characters.
It's as if it were a painting, a work of art that had been created especially to suit your personal tastes.
As you enjoy the beauty of the piece, it starts to give off a white light that distracts you and breaks your concentration.
You get up from where you're sitting and move closer in order to see it better, and you realize that the flash of light is actually something missing from the painting, almost like a piece missing from a puzzle.
You move even closer, and see that the puzzle piece is big, big enough to be a little door that you could pass through.
You stretch out your right hand, push the door, pass through to the other side.
You've come now to a deserted beach.
It's twilight, and the sun is shyly taking leave.
You can feel the warm sand under your feet, the cool breeze on your body, you hear the sound of the sea reaching the shore.
You look up to one side and see a person in the distance.
It's someone you know very well, yet it strikes you as odd to see them here. You run towards one another, ready
to give each other an everlasting embrace.
And in the excitement of the hug, you ask the person: "What are you doing here?"
You wait for the answer: "I came to see you, to give you this hug and to bring you a gift."
The person takes your hand, and places a gift in your palm.
It's a puzzle piece.
It's the piece that was missing from your painting, the piece that completes you.
You give another forever hug to the person, the one you're so happy to have found. This time, in the emotion of the embrace, you realize that the time has come to set them free, to let go.
You begin to slowly disengage from the hug, walking away backwards, in order to not lose eye contact,
holding the puzzle piece tightly to your chest.
And also slowly -- very, very slowly -- you start to get used to the idea of returning to this place... and you open your eyes.
(Music ends) For every 10 times I do this exercise, 8 or 9 of them, I meet my grandfather, Lázaro.
He was my teacher while he was here, and he's continued to be my teacher for all these years since he's left.
He's given me many puzzle pieces, that I believe are, little by little, completing the puzzle of my life.
Did that happen to anyone else, that you met someone you thought you'd never see again, or who you hadn't seen for a very long time?
What's your name?
Woman: Pato.
(Applause) NJ: Who did you meet?
P: A friend of mine.
NJ: What's her name?
P: Julieta.
NJ: When was the last time you saw her?
P: A long time ago.
NJ: Was it good to see her?
P: Yes.
NJ: Pato, I brought my favorite painting to share with you and everyone.
It's "The Metamorphosis of Narcissus," by Salvador Dalí. It's missing a piece; it's like a symbol of that door that I can go through every time I get lost, in order to find a deserted beach, where my grandfather Lázaro or one of my teachers gives me a gift, a puzzle piece that helps me get back on track.
I also brought, to share with you and everyone, a bagful of puzzle pieces.
So I need you to, with your left hand, pull out 3 or 4 pieces at the same time, without looking.
P: Together?
NJ: Yes.
NJ: Today.
(Laughter) NJ: Now, standing off to the side so that everyone can see, try each of the pieces to see if one of them fits.
NJ: No, no. OK.

Take the bag with your left hand, like that, and close your eyes.
I need you to, using your imagination, go back to the beach where you just were.
And with your imagination, call to your friend, because we need her help.
Ask her to come over to you, and with her left hand, take your right hand, but don't move your right hand, until she's the one who's guiding you.
Put your hand in the bag again, but this time take out only one piece. But it can't be just any piece,
it has to be the piece that completes all of us this afternoon.
Open your eyes.
(Applause) P: Thank you.
NJ: Pato!
On behalf of my grandfather Lázaro and me, I give to you this puzzle piece.
May you soon find the puzzle that completes it.
P: Thank you.
NJ: Can we give another round of applause to Pato for helping us?
(Applause) NJ: Thanks.
A while back, I was delighted to discover that my career had started to turn into a path. And then one day I received an email that revealed a new discovery.
The message said: "Hello Jansenson, my name is Sara.
The other day I saw you on a tv show.
It was an interview, and there wasn't any time for you to do magic.
The following day, while I was taking a walk in the park with the nurse who pushes my wheelchair, I thought to write to you and tell you that I don't believe in magic, but that if magic does exist as you claim, and if you're as good a magician as the show's host says you are, then you should be able to make me walk again.
But yesterday you were on the show again, and I saw you do magic, and build a bridge to a magical world I've never been to.
And it occurred to me that I should write to tell you that your magic gave me wings.
Now I don't need to walk."
(Applause) There's a world in which there's no need to run faster and faster.
It's the amazing magical world that exists inside of each and every one of us.
I hope the next time you encounter it, you embrace it with all your might.
I'm talking about one of those hugs that you hope never ends.
I'm talking about the kind of hug that makes you feel like your feet lift off the floor a little bit.
I hope.
(Applause)
Imagine a plant that, like all plants, captures the sun's energy during the day.
But at night, it shines.
Imagine a blood bank for emergency transfusions, but made from artificial blood.
And as a bonus it doesn't spread disease.
Imagine an organism able to capture carbon dioxide, the one of the greenhouse effect gases, and take it out of the atmosphere.
But not only that, it also turns it into a usable form of energy.
Stop dreaming.
Welcome to synthetic biology.
Most of you have probably never heard of synthetic biology.
But you will hear of it in years to come.
Biology is the study of life.
And synthetic doesn't come from "short" or "summed up", but from artificial, that which is not found in nature.
As synthetic biologists we design organisms to help us accomplish something.
It can be something abstract, like the study of life.
Or tangible, urgent things like treating an illness, generating clean energy, or detecting a pollutant.
The novelty is how we modify or create new living organisms.
And we achieve this by using, designing and combining biobricks.
They are biobricks like Rasti, Lego or Mis Ladrillos.
And each biobrick represents a function.
We can design a biobrick that when we put in a cell, it generates red color, the smell of bananas or hemoglobin, the one of the blood.
All biobricks are made of DNA, the material in which genetic information is stored.
And they are easy to combine
with each other. So, a researcher creates ten, a hundred, ten thousand biobricks, and shares them with the community.
And each user takes them and uses them to make their own organism with the function they wish.
Not only do they use them, they modify them, combine them, give them other use.
They can create.
And the most wonderful thing for us who are passionate about the complexity of biology, is that it works.
OK, we have a basket full of functions enclosed in biobricks, what do we do?
We play!
We assemble, disassemble, put in, put out, recycle.
Evolution recycles.
We synthetic biologists come to be serial recyclers who reuse and combine biological modules to make organisms with tailored functions.
This can generate some fear.
Is it that we do what we want?

No! There are many controls.
For instance, the companies which make DNA are forbidden to make toxins or pathogens, so that no biological weapons are created.
And also the laboratories that work in synthetic biology, usually open their projects to the community.
In my lab we receive visits from industrial designers, artists, sociologists, and we explore synthetic biology with them.
The fire burns.
The water drowns.
But we don't stop using them because of that.
It's just about making a responsible and safe use of them.
OK, but, what do we talk about when we talk about organisms?
Human beings like pets.
Some of us like dogs, other like cats, or plants.
Synthetic biologists like bacteria and yeast.
They are microscopic so we have many, many of them.
In ancient times we domesticated microorganisms to help us produce food.
Like bread, yogurt and beer.
Today we also use them to make medicines, to produce energy, to study life.
But those are not all possible uses.
We could also use them to be more healthy, to live better.
For instance, we could modify some microorganisms that live inside of us.
Yes, we have bacteria living inside of us.
In fact, a healthy human being has about ten times more bacteria inside of them than human cells.
Who could have wondered some years ago that bacteria could heal?
Today we are learning to understand these communities of microbes that live inside of us and how we can use them to live better.
I want to tell you my story with synthetic biology.
The first time I heard about synthetic biology, I thought it was a fraud.
They invent a name to get money or make themselves look interesting.
But when I understood what it was about, it amazed me.
If with the old biotechnology we could cut words from one organism and paste them into another one in a crafted and arduous way, with synthetic biology we can write, rewrite and edit a complete book.
We have the full dictionary or, even better, a complete library in the computer.
And we can combine words at will.
One of the greatest milestones of synthetic biology, was the production of the first artificial genome.
Many of you know that genetic information is in the genome and that we can make DNA in the lab.
Four years ago, in a lab, they made a complete genome from four bottles of chemical compounds.
They later introduced it inside a bacteria turning it into a new species.
It's like changing the hard drive of a computer and installing another operating system.
What a tool!
And you don't have to be a super genius to use it.
Most of the projects of synthetic biology are powered by students.
From their enthusiasm and naivety incredible projects come up.
In 2013, with a group of students from the University of Buenos Aires, we decided to study a problem that hurt society and that we could approach with tools from synthetic biology.
We decided on the serious problem of access to drinking water.
Around four million Argentinians consume arsenic-contaminated water every day, in particular those who drink well water, and they don't know this.
Or those who know don't have an easy way to measure it.
So our goal was to design an easy-to-use kit, like a pregnancy test, that allows the user to know if the water from their well has arsenic or not.
Remember the biobricks?
Well, we combined the function of detecting arsenic with the function of making color.
We put them together and placed them inside a bacteria and we turned it into an arsenic detector, a biosensor.
We did this project in the context of an international competition of synthetic biology where our team won a gold medal and the award for best theoretical model.
But we didn't just stay with the model.
We took field samples, real ones, and it worked.
The next step is to create a casing that contains the bacteria and be able to distribute this device.
This is the current prototype.
You put some drops of water in a cup... (Applause) You put some drops of water in a cup, wait a couple of hours, and compare the color developed in the cup with the reference colors. Then you know how much arsenic your well has.
I like to see it as an inspiring model.
When you know that something cannot be done, you try.
But when you know that it can be done, you go for more.
Today there are companies using synthetic biology, to produce vanilla essence.
They are using synthetic biology to develop a cure for malaria,
or to make a spider web.
Today we can design organisms that help us solve problems.
It's just about imagination.
To imagine an organism that helps us protect the environment.
To imagine an organism that helps us be more healthy.
To imagine an organism that helps us generate clean energy.
Because we know that in synthetic biology, imagination is power.
Thank you.
(Applause)
What will change the rules from my field? Literature?
Firstly, I would like to talk about the rules of the game, nowadays.
We live, we narrate, in a globalised era.
But how many frontiers is literature actually crossing?
The truth is, according to today's rules, not as many as one might expect.
According to the Cámara Argentina del Libro (CAL), out of the translated books that reach Argentinian readers 50%, even more, 55% of translated literature, literature from other parts of the world, get to Argentinians only from the English language.
That is to say English-written works or allowed to translation by English-language editorials.
UNESCO, foundations and consultants in Europe and also in Latin America and other parts of the world, endorse the same pattern.
In Europe more than 50% of translated literature is literature originally written in English.
This is also true in Asia; in fact, it's even more extreme.
In Japan, publishers are very reluctant to translate a foreign piece of literature unless it has passed through the filter, the consacration, if you will, of the publishers working in the English language.
(English) It's a bottle neck.
These rules are about to change.
They are already changing, and I imagine that you, much more than someone like me, would know why.
With new technologies, social media, including software getting each time closer to a translating capacity, maybe without sensibility or aesthetics but with great accuracy, are actually changing the rules of the game a little, by connecting zones, cultures and languages that previously could not without passing the criteria of certain centres of influence.
As far as published literature is concerned.
So the first instance of contact between writers and readers many times published in their countries and their languages with awards and prizes, which never go around the world.
I don't know exactly if there's something like literatures from the global South but I'm really interested in knowing if between countries from the global South, they find more resonance points than those they would have with English literature.
I don't know if you get what I mean by that.
Also, as a writer I love books and I don't see them threatened by the new technologies.
I'm giving you a very specific example: since 2000, in Japanese literature, a new literary genre appeared, called "keitai shosetsu". Keitai shousetsu doesn't displace "honka shosetsu", which is what we commonly study as literature.
Keitai is the mobile phone.
It's a literature written on the mobile phone.
And what has it showed us? That the shortest form may be as noble, as strong, as effective as the novel, the monumental volume of the novel.
The function of the series: maybe reading in series, with the sintax of the age, of the experience between readings, is part of what literature means to us.
Also fundamentally the interaction through social media platforms which are read on mobile phones.
And that interaction has been the greatest discovery to me because new technologies a much lesser threat to the book or the author figure, they bring us back to the origin of literature, which always in its origin has been about narration and lyricism in group, in performance and collectively, crossing generations and communities.
And day by day the new rules will expand that promise.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I have a question for you.
How much does an "almendrón" (an old American car) like this one cost?
Does anyone have any idea?
Does anybody know where to go
to get information about it? If any of you already know the answer to that, I will then ask another question: what's the price of housing in Cuba?
Who can answer this question?
I want to sell my house.
How much can I ask for it?
My house in La Lisa municipality, how much can I sell it for?
What if I want to buy a house in a residential district, El Vedado?
How much wouId I pay for it?
These are questions people often ask themselves in Cuba. Everybody wants to know how much a house a car, a TV set, or anything costs.
And the problem is that nobody knows.
Here nobody knows exactly how much something costs.
Most people will say to you: check on revolico.com.
(Laughter) Am I right?
Everyone checks it on Revolico despite the poor Internet service in the country. Everyone does it.
You can find people who never went online, but no one who hasn't heard of Revolico.
You may have a friend, an acquaintance who gives you all the info and uploads your classified ad for you on Revolico, Porlalivre, Cubísima, or on any other classified online portal.
As incredible as it may seem, the answers we need can be found on the Internet.
Internet has all the answers we need.
And then, I realized many people who wanted to sell or buy their house came to me for answers. So, I told them I'd do some research and let them know about my results.
The first thing I found was the amount of information that was available.
What we're talking about here is that in the first half of 2014, Revolico alone had over one million ads.
How do we tackle that?
How can we process all that information?
Of course, I am a cybernetician.
That's what I do, I work with computers, so I made use of them to process all that information and try to come up with answers.
I had another problem: there were many ads and I was only interested in information about homes. All of a sudden, I realized people
may also be interested in buying or selling an ossuary or a grave at the Colón Cemetery. So, I needed to make a distinction between one 'house' and the other.
Once again, I made use of the knowledge that many people like me have.
I did some research; I downloaded all the home ads and their location, because the price for a house in Havana is not the same as the price in El Vedado or in Guantanamo.
We all know there are differences in prices. All that information was later published in an article containing prices of homes by province, all over the country.
Interestingly enough, a house in Matanzas is more expensive than in Havana. Why? Because the world-famous Varadero beach is located there. This piece of information was available on the Internet; we merely processed it.
I was interested on it because this information proved to be very useful.
I then decided that the prices for cars at state-run establishments under the current new legislation, would be my focus. I realized that the price for an almendrón like the one I mentioned earlier, had changed after
the new law was enacted.
The research findings were made public yesterday in Cuba, was published in the Cuban media.
And what happened next?
People started calling me up, asking questions: "I want to sell my house; can you help me fix a price?"
"Do you think you can help me with that?"
"How much for a house in El Vedado?"
"I have an almendrón rebuilt with a few Lada parts. How much can I sell it for?"
Well, I could answer those questions directly, but the best thing would be to set up a system to answer those questions, and I thought that the solution was to develop
an Internet-based service platform,
because one way or another, everyone has access to the Internet.
This knowledge is accessible directly or via the popular "weekly package" brought to them by a friend, somehow it is accessible, so we launched the project. With that in mind, we launched Isladata, a project that builds upon
the data available on the Internet as main datasource
and other social platforms, ads sites like Revolico and Cubísima. I'm interested in collecting &amp; processing all the information on the social networks collecting it and smartly processing it, information from Trip Advisor, and other official sources that are available
and are likely to grow in the future.
The idea is to update the information
on a regular basis, not every 1-6 months,
to provide it to all those interested and have it available on the Internet.
You can download it from the web to your Android and iOS mobile devices, and even as an off-line computer application.
For those users who can access Internet, and for those who can't. All of them will have access. What will this information cover? It will cover public services, home prices,
and business information, including feasibility studies or info on the best location with certain budget requirements. That information will be available.
Who is this info targeted to?
To everybody.
To those interested in selling their homes, buying 'almendrones' with Lada engines, and apartments to be used as restaurants. To practically everybody;
to foreign investors under the new legislation, who want to know more about Cuba, and there was no service providing that info until now;
to media representatives, looking for transparency who will find many useful data on Cuba, and to state organizations, for research purposes.
We didn't have answers to these questions until now.
This information has been available since yesterday and will be updated -- the prices of homes, on a weekly basis, by municipality and by province.
Now we know how much things cost in Cuba.
(Applause)
Good morning.
In 1953, a team enrolled itself to climb the world's highest mountain.
Many people questioned "why" to climb the Everest, if there had been 33 years of failed attempts to achieve the ascent of this mountain.
And, they asked the leader of the expedition, "why" to climb the Everest.
And he responded that there is no height or depth that the spirit of a man, guided by a superior spirit, cannot reach.
He didn't say that it was wise, he simply said that it was possible.
The adventures, the explorers, we are all explorers, and the adventures are in any place.
Personally, I found the adventures in the mountains and for more than 25 years, the mountains taught me certain important aspects to live our adventure in life -- adaptation, will, exploration, understanding of Nature, transformation, unity, regeneration and attitude.
As we go climbing the high mountains we learn that up there, after 24,600 feet that they know as the zone of death, the percentage of oxygen decreases what is required from us is that we adapt.
We have to teach our bodies demanding to learn how to change the metabolism, to know how to compensate for the lack of oxygen that is up there.
I started climbing hills, climbing small mountains and later I started to climb the volcanoes increasingly high, 5, 6, 7 thousand meters high, to be able to gain access to what they know as the zone of death.
In the process of adaptation, I learnt that the strength of a mountaineer is not necessarily found in his muscles and you just realized, it is not in the muscles!
It is precisely in our ability of adaptation.
I do not say it, Darwin said this a long time ago: "It is not the strongest of the species that survives, nor the fastest, nor the largest, it is the one that is most adaptable to change."
And for that, we had to educate our body to take more fluid, to demand it, and our body begins to create memory that we know as acclimatization.
In the process of demanding from my body, I learnt that there are certain foods that nourish us, foods that permit us that our blood be able to reproduce the red blood cells more rapidly which is going to strengthen us on the height and you learn to say yes to what nourishes you and no to the junk. I also learnt that not only what you eat nourishes you, but also what you think and that we seldom do mental diets.
And I started putting aside all those comments that told me: "It is not for you, you are crazy, why do you want to climb the mountain."
"Leave that for the strong and brave men."
It is then when it came, in that adventure, the will.
But to be able to give that first step that is required to exercise your willpower first of all, you have to have clarity on what you want, what you love, what you search for, and once you have clarity you need to know how to weigh the risks.
Mountaineers, and explorers, are not potential suicidal people who are going to see where to kill themselves, are people who know that there are risks, but who prepare for it, and it is then when the will to take the first step requires to be set free which for many people is safety.
Up there, to be able to make progress, you have to start setting free the things that you don't need so much.
The mountains teach us to differentiate, between the essential and the accessory.
Rich is not the one who has the most, but who needs the least.
So you go lighter and you start to take a first step but as you go climbing the highest mountains, you also discover that the next stage of will is to pick yourself up after the failure.
You do not reach to the top always, because it is when the mountain lets you.
There are times when you can put your best effort, your experience and, however, cannot reach the peak.
And it is then when you have to seek exploration, not only outward exploration, but also inwards.
I remember an expedition in the Himalayas when we got closer to the mountain, a local approached and asked us what were we doing there. One of my companions told him:
"We come to conquer the Everest."
He kept quiet and then said: "That's not possible." Between the English and the Tibetan languages the discussion began, until they knew the universal language: "We want to climb the mountain."
And the local said: Ah!
You want to climb the mountain.
If the mountain permits, you are going to be able to climb it, because the conquests are not outwards, you climb the mountains, not the conquests the conquests are inwards.
When we talk about exploring, we do not necessarily have to go to the most remote places on Earth, we start with us.
We start by exploring, simply starting on the basis of seeing with new eyes -- the ability to surprise, the ability to see opportunities where, apparently, aren't any, to see experiences where we might not see any.
And then, Nature as a great teacher starts to teach us.
When we start exploring we realize that Nature is that generous mother who is ready to give us everything and that, in fact, she does not need us, it is us who need her.
However, it also has its rules, and you have to fulfill them.
When we reach a mountain, it is a universal ritual, in all the mountains, that you have to ask the mountain for permission.
Ask permission, not only because it is part of a local ritual but also, we have to recognize our exact size.
In Nature ... we are very small but Nature shows us how everything works, how everything contributes and the truth is, personally, the mountain gave me a great lesson.
I used to think that, if I focused, that if I trained, that if I fed, that if I nourished, that if I had clarity in my thoughts, I could reach the highest mountains of the world, but in the process of climbing mountains, Nature taught me that if the person next to you doesn't matter to you, you cannot climb.
In the year 1998, after more than 10 years of attempts, after getting sponsorships, of knocking doors, of climbing small mountains, finally I was at 26,000 feet, at the point of reaching the top, I was going towards the peak after hours of walking in average temperature of 0 °F walking, and that progress was very difficult for me, suddenly, at a point which is known as "The Blue Summit" at the Mount Everest, tired, I realized that I am very close to reaching the top, when I turn and ask one of my companions, "How much is left?", and he said to me that I am at 260 feet, half an hour, and I am about to reach the top, the truth is that my spirit returned to my body and, finally, yes, like in an ad
I said: "I found myself."
Yes! At the top! After 10 years of training, five years of asking for sponsorships, of having given up my job, of having prepared myself for years.
And suddenly, my English companions stopped and said to me, "Up till here is enough" and I, could not understand.
I said: "These men lack oxygen in their brains.
Who wants to stop when we are half an hour short, after all that we have done?"
Finally, one of them say to me: "It's not that we don't want to climb," the only thing that we needed was to climb a step that is known as "The Hillary Step" is 26 feet high wall, eight feet wide, mixed terrain, that is ice and rock, where if you take a wrong step to the right is China, to the left is Nepal, and you don't want to go nowhere in that moment.
So, to ensure not only your ascent, but mainly your descent you have to put a rope [beam].
When I start to awaken excitement, because only half an hour was left and in front of me, I had that wall, my companions say to me: "We reached up till here we cannot continue, we forgot the rope to cover the last feet."
Could we climb the Everest?
The truth is, yes.
But, to come down without a rope could cost the life to any of the team members.
So the decision was: half-turn and return, everyone!
When I came down, obviously I was very furious.
I had done everything that I had to do.
Suddenly, I am going to find the one at fault: Who had been the one who forgot the rope?
Who had forgotten the rope? And when we reached down, we started --
until we found the culprit, with name and surname, and many nicknames that arose at that moments.
But at last, that season ended and it was there, where Nature showed me that nothing helps you have clarity on the objective if you forget to care about others.
I did not ask:
Who is wearing the rope? Do I help you?
You carry it and later I carry it?
That great failure became one of our great learning milestones.
You can climb mountains, hills, with all that you have learnt, said Nature to me.
But when we are talking about the highest ones, it is not enough, you have to interest yourself a little in the one on the side because when you ask the other what it is that you need, it is exactly what "you" need to keep climbing.
In those moments I went down the mountain, I kept training, because you need to renew that spirit and learn from the failure and learn from what would seem rubbish, but in Nature there is no trash.
In Nature ... waste is a human invention.
In Nature, one organism's waste is another's feast. That waste, that "failure", today became my great learning milestone.
And with this learning you also needed to learn about the importance of unity.
In an expedition, in Alaska, my colleagues invited me to be a part of a team as part of our training to return to the Everest.
In this expedition, the leader told us that we are going to go gripped, roped, connected.
It is a form of climbing mountains that was used at the beginning of the century; We were all tied up at the same rhythm.
What happened was that as time passed and there was an accident, if one fell, he took all the others, so people started to detach themselves.
They said, "No, no, well, if it comes to cry in your house, or in my house, look, we cut here, just cry in your house."
But, the leader told us, "We are going to climb, roped."
A woman from Monterrey said to me, already older, when I talked about things of mountain, she said to me: "How difficult is that of the mountain, how complicated ..." And I: "What? The cold, the winds, the cracks, the risks?
And she said to me: "No, not how hard it is to go tied up, because at least in the marriage, it is until death do us apart.
But here, if they go, they take you too, right?"
Yes, it's that if you go, you too take them with you.
It is very difficult to tie up yourself, because your life depends on other, and others life depends on you.
When the leader told us that we are going to climb "tied up" obviously everyone was against it.
My friend said: "I don't even tie up with the one who I lived with for eight years nor am I going to walk tied-up with a stranger."
But there was no option, and we began the climb to the mountain.
And when we reached at the point to reach almost at the top we found that the wind began to blow very strongly and in that strong wind, you all know, wind factor, the temperatures dropped, descending temperatures, frostbites, frostbite amputations and you don't want that because the fingers don't grow back, simply, the leader made sign, half-turn and back and we started to come down.
And when we were coming down, roped, suddenly, you hear the cry of the leader which came from the rear, and told us: "Take firm steps and do not stop."
The moment in which I focus on taking firm steps was for me, in my mind, simply, fix the 12 peaks of my crampon I meet with shouts of the leader and he tells me: "Take firm steps and do not stop."
I lift the leg and I feel as if I kicked the backpack I fall over backwards, I start falling and suddenly all I thought was: "I already killed myself."
But since I don't want myself to die, I clung to the mountain with an ice axe I regained my breath, I started to turn to see my companions.
I rise, I see my companions, super frightened, hoping that they say: "Hey, how are you?
Do you need anything?
We saw you almost killed yourself," anything.
The only thing that they said is, "Move!"
And I, they didn't realize, let me explain them, I started to tell them: "It's that I fell,
the heavy backpack, the wind ..." They tell me, "Move!"
Of course I was angry, yes, they noticed and they didn't care.
Then I get angry and I say: "You know what? What happened is that the two of you forget that I am only a woman."
Of course when I said that, the leader of the expedition grabbed the rope that united us and said to me: "I know that and continue walking."
I started to walk and I said: "Why, why did my team not help me?
And then, the leader, my friend, showed me the rope and I understood perfectly.
In the mountains, if you make a mistake
you pay the consequences.
When you are tied-up, what you do impacts the others. My leader said to me: "I know that, I know that you are the most fragile, I know you are a woman, and that's why you come in middle, we come protecting you." When I fell all I thought was: "I killed myself,"
I did not think, "I kill them with me."
When I understood that, I started to dedicate my steps.
And in my next attempt at the Everest, in 1999, in the most difficult moment, I had most difficulty to go forwards, when I really ached to take a step when, as Reinhold Messner said, "When taking a step becomes a true act of faith is probably because you are up at 26,000 feet."
You find everything difficult -- when I start moving forward in my second attempt suddenly, I find that there was no strength that moved my leg and I started dedicating my steps and I said this step is not for me, this step goes for a little girl who once offered me her Sundays so that I complete my expedition; and this goes for my family, and this for my friends and with name and surname, I started to move forward.
There came a moment in which I advanced the 27,900 feet and all of a sudden there is a wall, that is known as Second Step.
It's 32 feet high, that at that height in which taking a step is difficult, to face a wall can be a mental block which makes many people return.
When I come across this wall, I said, "My God, help me, I don't have the strength, help me."
Suddenly, I start hearing this voice telling me, "You are going to climb, but it is not for you it is going to be useful for something else,"
and I did not understand when was it that I started to dedicate my steps.
I reached the top and at the top I could see not only the curvature of the Earth, I could see everything downward and I loved it the only thing that I felt was a profound gratitude.
And suddenly, I thought, not in 10 years I finish giving thanks.
And, as you cannot stay a lot of time at the summit, immediately I came down and I promised: "I come back in 10 years."
In 2009, I returned to the mountain and I realized that the Everest not only just allowed me to see the curvature of the Earth nor the intense blue, nor the whole mountain range below.
It permitted me to see our own exact size, our vulnerability, our ability of, being so small, to catch big things.
And also in this process of 10 years working with public schools, I could see how every time a little kid recognized his own mountain, recognized its own challenge, he recognized himself as an explorer, he started to climb his mountains and his life changed.
I started seeing that transformation and promised to work with youth from that moment on.
In 2009, I started a foundation, that invites kids of public schools that in their short 12, 13, 14 years are recognized as explorers.
Nowadays, these little ones that have taken part in the projects connecting them with Nature and reconnecting them with themselves have transformed their community, their school, their environment; from projects of collecting rain water, of waste management of recovery of green areas, of reforestation.
And this has led them to not only Nevado de Toluca or to a course of leadership but mainly it has led them to Antarctica to the white continent, to that blank page where they are discovered as heroes.
Everyone has their own mountains, we are all explorers.
And I invite you to live that adventure that is to reach their own peak and when they achieve that peak they always have someone to share it with.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Empty fridges.
Filthy bathrooms.
Clothes scattered all over the place.
Dirty dishes piling up, unwashed.
Smell of pet poop.
Babies crying their eyes out.
Out in the streets kids wander, alone.
Nobody picked them up from school?
No, it's not a zombie attack.
This apocalypse could happen if women one day get up and decide not to do what we do every day.
From seeing to it that their children ... (Applause) take a bath and do their homework, to going with the elderly to medical appointments, fixing the meals and taking care of the sick.
Each of these chores looks pretty small, but society doesn't work without them.
It's a productive space.
It's called the care economy.
And even when these are tasks with a very concrete value they don't get any payback.
This work is done in every home every day. But in some way we don't see it, it's invisible.
Probably, because it's natural for us.
It's up to each family to manage care as they can, and it's generally solved with women's time.
It's a private affair.
Children are a property of their family.
"Don't mess with my kids," they say.
Or, "if you've got kids, take accountability."
It's such an invisible issue, that we don't even see it when we have it in front of us.
We all heard about the "NEET," youth not in employment nor education.
Six years ago at CIPPEC we were doing a study in Buenos Aires province, and we found out that in fact, three out of four young people who are neither working, studying nor looking for a job, are girls who have to look after their younger siblings, their elders or their own children.
That is to say, there are no such NEETs, there are women who are caregivers.
The poorest of women get the heavier load.
The put eight and a half hours a day, more than a regular work shift, to care giving.
They have to invest this much time because in the context they live, it's much harder to manage care giving.
There are no sewers, for example, so children get sick more times and have to be taken to hospital.
If these women decide to go look for a paid job, the jobs they can find pay so little that it's not enough to replace what they do at their own homes.
They lose money going to work.
They are also exposed to several dangers.
Ana, for example, is from Pampa de los Guanacos, in Santiago del Estero.
She worked in the city as a maid. At times, she could not come back home for weeks on end or see her children.
Ana was in an informal job, which is fairly common.
Still today, three out of four people who work in domestic services are not registered.
I met her for a project and she told me that she worked for many years in the same house.
She was like family.
She sat at the table with them, they took her on vacation.
However, just like in this family children were beaten up, she was also mistreated.
Ana told me she doesn't want to leave her children to go to work anymore.
Why would she?
It's not just about the money she can win, but about all she has to lose.
When the only option is to stay taking care of your home, it's pretty probable that those families have, if any, only one income which is usually low.
And that's how these families and these women in particular get trapped. The same trap their children and grandchildren get caught, too.
I consider this situation is similar to a scam.
Everybody puts on, but here instead of money you put time.
And we hope to reap the benefits.
But we are all affected in some way.
Families with higher incomes are affected, because we end up being accomplices to something we don't want to be part of.
We take advantage of the vulnerable situation a big part of society lives in.
It's a systemic trap.
The lack of opportunities leads there.
Women with lower incomes give their time to middle and upper class families and that is why both men and women in those families can go to work.
But no one gives time to poor women.
They also have to look after their
family; they don't have the resources.
And nobody takes care of them.
Nobody wins.
There's no scammer behind this.
We all lose.
Because poverty and inequality grow.
This situation that seems like a no way out, a vicious cycle, it doesn't really have to be like that.
We can break free from this trap that leaves families in poverty for generations to come.
And there are many places to start.
First, to recognize the value the work we women do has.
Care is intertwined with love and it's full of gratifications.
It's still a job nonetheless.
We have to make sure that women who work professionally as caregivers do it under the right conditions.
Make it a decent, formal, well-paid job, that makes sense to have.
For this it's crucial that they get a better representation.
Can we imagine a senator who is a maid?
To the women who take care of their own homes full time, we also have to reward that work.
For example, including in retirement calculations the years dedicated to upbringing and care.
Formally, like it is done in Spain.
What would happen if men take a bigger portion of this care giving work?
To stop being a women's thing for the most part.
Personally, it quite scares me how people assume that I will know what to do in two months when my first daughter is born, Maia.
And nobody expects the same from my husband.
(Applause) Now, I want to invite you to an exercise for all of you here today.
I want to ask the men who live with a woman -- it can be any woman: your partner, your mother, your daughter, a friend, a sister. All men living with a woman please stand up.
Many.
Now, I want to ask all of you who stood up -- which are a lot -- if you didn't wash clothes in the last week, this includes using the washing machine, hand wash, take it to the laundry, if you didn't do this chore, to sit down.
Some few remained.
Now, I would like to ask to those who sat down if you would be willing to distribute these tasks in a fairer way inside their homes, to please stand up again.

Thank you. You can all sit.
An applause please, to those who participated.
(Applause) Photos like this are still weird.
There are advances, clearly, much are more discursive than real.
This photo was part of an exhibition that we set at Retiro train station in Buenos Aires, to contribute to this much needed cultural change.
Because not even the men who want to do more care work and who know what they are missing, nor society, nor the labor market, ends up allowing them.
Even the laws say that care is the responsibility of women.
In Argentina, for example, maternity leave is 90 days.
And the paternity leave is two days.
There are already many countries that got out of this situation.
It's not just something that happens just in Swedish series on Netflix.
I'm talking about Uruguay, Ecuador.
In Argentina, with two days of paternity leave we are telling men that they have to take care of their work.
We are telling women that they have to take care of their children.
And we are telling companies that they have to hire men.
As a society we agreed that education, health, retirement, are things we want to do together.
That's why they are guaranteed by the state.
Care is an equally important pillar.
But we take it so natural that it is a family issue that public policies on care look like a luxury we can't afford.
Higher investment in an infrastructure and in a care system could help to level things out.
We could assure that all boys and girls have good opportunities.
From those first moments of life that are essential.
We could also improve the conditions of families in poverty if all the adults in those families could have their own income.
But where would those jobs come from?
With some colleagues we demonstrated that they could be generated from that same investment in care.
For example, to guarantee that two thirds of kids under four today can attend some parenting, teaching or care space, would generate almost 1,300,000 jobs.
Just over a third of what is needed for the opportunities of those who don't have any today.
It would take people to work in the construction of these spaces, teachers, caregivers, cooks, people to transport the food needed to cook, people to grow this food --
The effect is exponential.
We estimate that this would make the economy grow by 5.3 points of GDP over a period of four years.
Who would have imagined that kindergartens had such an impact in our development?
This tension between work and care has been growing in me for the last months.
I ask everyone who approaches me: "How did you manage?"
trying to decipher how to do this thing of having children and keep working.
When I was born my mom also worked all day, I was almost born at her studio, and my grandmother Clara took care of me.
I loved being with her.
I know that my mother dies for it, she is already madly in love with her future granddaughter, but I know she won't be able to take care of her.
First, because she still works.
And second, because she lives in Bariloche and I live here, in Buenos Aires.
My husband works in a place with very little flexibility and it's not an option either.
Luckily, my mother in law offered to look after Maia and that gives me some relief because my house won't crumble into that domestic apocalypse.
But, again, it is at the expense of another woman's time.
It is always at the expense of another woman's time.
A woman from another generation or a woman from another social class.
We end up perpetuating the trap.
I want us to break free from this invisible trap.
I want women to be the owners of our time.
I want all kids, boys and girls, to have good opportunities since those first moments of life.
And I want poverty to stop being a sentence for generations to come.
(Applause) And I know we can do it because it's been proven over 50 years ago by pioneers of the feminist economy, that public care policies reduce poverty and inequality.
That's why I want more care spaces, more kindergartens, that the caregivers do it under good conditions, and that we all agree that this is something we have to push all together.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good morning. Thank you.
After the two previous talks, I must refer to them. There was something that caught my attention: both have emphasized one word that I believe is crucial everywhere, and also at a business school.
The word "humanity." Sor Lucia Caram talked about humanity, Angel Bonet finished talking about humanity, but before that, he made a really interesting reference to robots.
Let's see, robots. This takes me back because, as you can see, my talk somehow represents going back to the future, though it may look like going back to the past.
Let's go back to the past. The Bible, what does it talk about? It's about a primitive time in which men didn't work, it creates a worldly paradise in which men simply lived. Now, the question is who did all the work in that paradise, in the case - I mean, if we go beyond the mythical part that it certainly has.
Greeks talk about a robot in Crete, and according to Angel Bonet, in 30 years, I'm sorry, I don't know if it'll be in 30 years, maybe a little bit more - we will have 30% of the jobs done by robots. Alright, the thing is, what about humanity? What about the distribution of wealth? Since that distribution is not based, or it's based according to the working hours, how many people work and how many don't?
In a society in which robots do all the work, men could focus on creative activities. The question is, does that take us back to the worldly paradise?
So, let's talk, in this same context about a myth, about something that was a myth until today: Atlantis. I mean, Atlantis from a doctoral thesis view, a thesis about Ancient Egypt which, surprisingly, gives us the certainty that Atlantis was a reality.
So, from here on, all the research carried out for 14 years turns into a business project, a groundbreaking research project that dare to go beyond the well known and accepted reality up to now, and I think it's one of the major topics that business projects - the risks they involve, imagine, create, imagine, create, innovate.
Of course, any research project has a question, and a question after another is what allows us to find answers. In the case of Atlantis, the question is, Did Atlantis really ever exist?
For now, this is one greatest ancient enigmas, and the other question is, How is it possible that nobody could find an answer to these mysteries? But as you can see, evidence of its existence is coming to light. The analysis of the size of Atlantis only comes to us through Plato. Plato starts to define a series of six concentric circles which presents us the first evidence of its existence, because, as you can see, here we have six earth and water consecutive concentric circles which come down to prime numbers. If you multiply by 6, you get the size in Plethrons, this is the measurement in stadiums, but the Plethron happens to be a second arch of half the Earth's circumference.
Sure, the question is, Did the Ancients know the Earth's circumference? So far, that's not what history is telling us. But apart from that, let's try to go further. Let's break molds. I'm telling you, the size of Atlantis offered by Plato includes the knowledge of the list of prime numbers. Well, we could take that off, but it includes something much more important. If we add up the perimeters of the 6 circumferences from Atlantis, that equals 1621 Plethrons, and the sum of the diameters equals 258 Plethrons, because 1621 happens to be number 258 in the list of prime numbers. If we divide both numbers, it equals an approximation of Pi, more exactly 2Pi.
That's a lot more than the Greeks knew, they only reached 3.14 and here they're reaching 3.1415, remember it's 3.141592. So, this geometric structure implies that whoever designed it knew perfectly well number Pi. This could seem a coincidence if we limit ourselves only to this test, but there are many more.
Another evidence, in fact, the very first one, arises from the reconstruction of the Great Pyramid. Until now, nobody knew what it was like. But there was a time while I was doing a very meticulous computer work, when I could find an exact reconstruction in my thesis, and I found something really strange - there's a formula that validates the accuracy of the reconstruction. The following analysis of the Great Pyramid shows us a lot of data, let's say it's the Big Data, somehow. It concentrates in a single monument that is somehow explaining knowledge from the past. You may ask, Why does this monument gather that knowledge?
Let's see.
So, this scientific data is between 3 and 4 millennia ahead from scientific discoveries. One piece of information I found, the first one, which really surprised me, is the fact that the area of the Great Pyramid was exactly 100 thousand times number Pi with 6 decimals. That meant, exactly, going 3 or 4 thousand years ahead of maths' scientific progress.
This is what I called "secret code," but it's actually called gematria. In Greek, each number had a letter followed by an apostrophe. 1 was Alpha followed by an apostrophe and 800 was Omega followed by an apostrophe. Let's take the word Atlantis. We substitute all the values, and we get 892.
We've researched 6 000 words, and only one, the word Atlantis, contains number 892, which repeats over and over again in the Great Pyramid of Giza.
There we have it, and in case we were missing something, the sarcophagus we put the cap on top and we compare with the Great Pyramid, the relation is 1 to 892 thousand. That is, if we repeat 892 thousand times the size of the sarcophagus, we would get the area of the Great Pyramid.
Now, the question is, "892 means Atlantis, but what about the thousand?"
This happens to be the millennial anniversary monument of the destruction of Atlantis. For that reason, in memory of their dead ancestors there's all this information included within the monument.
How do we know that? They used to have these stellar channels to the north and south, coming from both chambers, and these stellar channels point to crossings from Mars, through the meridian, and it gives the date from the year 2530, specifically October 3rd, which coincides a thousand years later with the data from Plutarch about the death of Osiris.
With the position of the sun, the moon and the date I could work out which was the date, thanks to the wonderful astronomy software out in the market.
How is this explained? If we analyze the myth of Osiris, we realize that he's nothing but the personification of Atlantis, Atlantis' personification.
What other evidence do we have? The texts from the Temple of Edfu, very interpreting, but look at this, they're written in hieroglyphs. A wonderful story, the texts of the reconstruction of the Temple of Edfu, which say the wisdom of Egypt comes from seven wisemen from "The primitives land." This was an island which sank into the sea due to a sudden flood which killed almost all its inhabitants.
So, water cataclysm. Look what the Bible says and what the Gilgamesh myth says, it talks about a flood, water cataclysm, it's a parallelism.
So, the fact that the Great Pyramid had so much built-in science led me to a specific analysis of the monument, and I found very surprising things. And also in Giza. Geometry connected the location of Atlantis which we could locate after years of research. Where did this idea come from? Geometry links the Great Pyramid with nearby cities, as we can see here. This also occurred with the monument group,
Sure, in order to find a place you need directions and distance, and the question is, In which direction is Atlantis?
How far away is Atlantis? The answer is in Giza. But it's not only there, it's also in Egypt. We have scientific evidence about its location in geometry, maths, astronomy, geodesy, geography and mythology, among other disciplines. Cross-discipline connections have been the key in finding Atlantis.
So, it's here. We have a lot of evidence on its location, and we know it's been under these waters for 5545 years. Now, the question is, Which was the cause of destruction? I already told you, a huge cataclysm in the form of a flood, and the other question is, Is it possible to unveil a city at the bottom of the sea?
Well, Heraklion is 6 km away from the coast of Egypt and only 10 meters deep, and it wasn't discovered until 2000.
How big was Atlantis? If we compare it with Barcelona, it's the size of the wider part plus three blocks of houses. Obviously, the bigger a city - we're talking about a city with 5 km of diameter - the easier it is to be located.
What happened when I showed the discoveries about the Great Pyramid to the press? To my amazement, there were 16 million people in Spain only, and we were only talking about the reconstruction of the pyramid.
I presented it to the Spanish National Research Council, I created a specific subject in the School of Architecture because it's unusually important. Another issue that arised in the previous talks is communication. If people don't know about something, it doesn't exist, and this is valuable for the business.
It's advertising value was the first thing that made us think this was a self-financing project.
Today we know - we were talking about the future of humanity, we talked about going back to the future, now let's see why. Today, we know Project Atlantis makes us rewrite the history of civilization. The technology they used to build the Great Pyramid is unknown. They moved 100, 200 and 400 ton blocks in Giza. Nowadays, it would be difficult to move and cut them. The question is, Why did they do it? There can only be one answer. They wanted to show their tech knowledge, they wanted to show who they were.
Imagine we go to the future, imagine that 300 or 400 years have passed and we've found a way to travel to the past. Sometimes it seems that the Great Pyramid and Giza was built by travelers from the future. It's science fiction, obviously, but it's closely related with the title of the talks.
Why am I telling this? Because a lot of tech elements are there, and we have no explanation for that. Until the XX c. we still don't have the math and scientific discoveries that the monument has, and I can't describe in detail.
I think here, we're always agreeing on a key idea, that is service to humanity. Project Atlantis can't be measured only in economic terms because of the significance of the discovery of Atlantis to the history of civilization and to the future of humanity. We still have to understand what happened, and probably, one of the greatest myths will fall down, the myth of contemporaneity, of continuous progress in humanity.
As a species, we're so arrogant that we can't accept we've had a breakdown and we had to start from scratch. This is becoming evident. So, we have to ask ourselves, What would've happened if the earthquake in Fukushima had 2, 3 or 4 more degrees in the Richter scale? Which area of Japan would've disappeared if that tsunami took away the tech in Fukushima to the bottom of the sea releasing a great amount of radioactivity?
When Yahveh says in the bible, "I forbid you to eat from the tree of the knowledge," the question is, Isn't he telling us about a previous destruction, and maybe about an energy chaos? I'll leave that question hanging.
Which are the means needed to find the Atlantis? An oceanographic ship and a submarine, that's it. Clearly, all means. So, we have the knowledge and together - because this is a large scale project - we could begin the biggest adventure of the XXI c. which would be finding Atlantis. And maybe, just maybe, it's just a hypothesis, this could represent going back to the future.
Here's the logo of Project Atlantis. As you can see, it's just the circles from Atlantis, and the channel, because there was a channel with 50 stadiums that reached the city.
Now I'll ask you to do something, focus there for a moment. See? What's this? Do you know what this is? I'll go back. Can you see that the rings are repeated? They're projected. Do you know what this is? Anyone? Public: the galaxy.
No, but you're close.
It's a comet, and the question we ask today is, Did Atlantis have this shape because it wanted to tell us about a comet?
To conclude, my thought is that research projects have to be business projects, because in our system there's no other way of carrying them out. It's necessary in every research project, and every business project, to use the imagination, the work, the objective and rational research through a doctoral thesis and the like, and from there, find projects that could be useful for everyone, useful for humanity. Maybe Atlantis, with its message, contained a help request to save the planet, we don't know.
Thank you.
(Applause)
What comes to your mind when you hear on the news that an Earth-like planet was found?
Maybe you think of green and blue fields, beaches caressed by endless oceans that get lost on the horizon.
A warm and breathable breeze.
Many people have imagined habitable worlds.
My favorite is Ursula K. Le Guin.
She is a science fiction writer that unlike most of her fellows during the seventies, she imagined worlds not based on science or technology, but on imagining different forms of social organization.
For example, in her novel "The Dispossessed" she depicts two planets: Anarres and Urres.
Anarres is an Earth-like planet, rich in natural resources, that are used for a few privileged.
There, women are not allowed to study, and all in all, they have no power to decide over her own lives.
On the other hand, Urres is an anarchist society, where property is inexistent even in language.
There is no my house, my girlfriend,
my family. Everything is shared.
It is a world with limited resources yet all the people, both men and women, work together to survive.
On another novel entitled "The Left Hand of Darkness" Ursula depicts a planet called Gethen.
People in Gethen have no secondary sex characteristics.
That is, people weren't neither men nor women.
Every three weeks, for a few days, people become either men or women, only for reproductive purposes, the rest of the time that doesn't happen.
Nobody is either men nor women.
In that world, genitals do not define the role or destiny of people in society.
Just as Ursula, I too work with habitable worlds.
I am an astrobiologist, astrobiologists study the origin, evolution and distribution of life in the universe.
Just like Ursula, we use the Earth as our example of habitable world.
For example, Anarres is like Earth, Urres is a sort of semi desert, and Gethen is a frozen planet, like living on a glacier on the South or North Pole.
Besides, Ursula's characters are all humanoid.
Very similar to human beings.
In my case, I use this planet to understand how is an habitable planet and what makes it different to other planets.
Our planet is a terrestrial planet, like Mercurio, Venus and Mars.
These worlds have things in common: they have an iron core, a mantle and a silicate crust.
Silicates are silicon and oxygen combined with elements such as magnesium or iron, and it's of what all terrestrial rocks are made of.
So these planets are basically rocks.
It might seem that Earth is different because it has water, but if we collect all the water of the Earth inside and on its surface, it is represented with that drop.
The water on Earth is less than 1 percent of its mass, so, when male and female scientists who study astrobiology talk about Earth-like planets, we are not thinking on habitable worlds, we are thinking about rocks that are more or less the size or mass of the Earth.
Our planet hasn't always been the same.
It was formed 4500 million years ago.
At the time the moon was closer, oceans and the atmosphere were formed from the volcanic emissions.
The atmosphere was made of carbon dioxide and nitrogen.
3.5 billion years ago life emerged and among all the organisms cyanobacteria appeared, these are photosynthetic beings that use carbon dioxide, water and light from the atmosphere, to make their food and expel oxygen.
So these bacteria changed the atmosphere of the Earth, into the atmosphere we now breathe, with 21 percent of oxygen.
Beyond our solar system we have 100 billion and 400 billion stars on the galaxy, we are still not sure of the number.
The point here is that there are plenty of stars with planets around them.
We call them exoplanets or extrasolar planets.
So far we have detected around 4000 of them.
Some are cases like the ones we have imagined on sci-fi, like a planet that spins around stars like that one where the Jedi was born, with two suns on the horizon.
Other worlds seem like the planets on the solar system like Jupiter, but closer to its star so it evaporates leaving behind a gas trail.
There are other planets that do not exist in our solar system, planets bigger than Earth but smaller than Neptune.
Those smaller than Neptune, but with a similar structure, are called mini Neptunes.
Those Earth-like planets but bigger are called super-Earth.
They have no super powers, no cape, they are just big rocks.
Unlike Earth, they didn't have carbon dioxide atmospheres, like other terrestrial planets: Mars, Venus or Earth on early stages.
These planets might have hydrogen atmospheres.
Other worlds might be ocean planets,
with an important amount of water.
We are talking about 10, 20, 30, 50 percent of all the planet's mass in water.
Question is: Which of these worlds would be habitable?
Well, let's come back to Earth because we first have to understand the life example we have on Earth, the only one we have so far.
All life on Earth is made by carbon chemistry.
Carbon is the fourth most plentiful element of the universe.
So, we are not made of anything special.
Carbon does have something special, that it can bond with up to four elements at the same time including itself, and create bigger molecules, that can even reproduce among themselves.
So that these molecules move, and find one another, to form bigger structures, a liquid is necessary, in the case of Earth that liquid is water.
So, when astrobiologists talk about Earth-like life, what we are talking about, what we mean is life based on carbon and liquid water, not humanoids with antenna.
You might think that maybe we could not think of anything else, that life could be something different, special, that we cannot think of.
Actually our selection of carbon and water not only has to do with life on Earth.
It also has to do with what happens on these places.
This is a molecular cloud.
These are zones where stars leave their materials after their last phases of evolution, and are composed of two things: one is microscopic dust like this one, that is made of silicates, the same minerals our terrestrial planets are made of.
The other component is gas.
The most important component of gas is hydrogen, but is followed by carbon monoxide, water steam and organic materials.
We have detected at least 200 molecules of organic materials, like sugars, alcohols, and something called fullerenes that are molecules of up to 60 or 70 carbon atoms.
Some sections collapse under their own weight, and form structures like this: these are called protoplanetary disk.
That little red light in the middle is a forming star, and the formed disk of dust and gas that's where planets will start to form.
What happens is that gas starts to conglomerate and form even bigger structures, until planets are formed.
The history of stars and planet formation tells us that in these places there are two materials we consider important for life: water and carbon.
Since carbon is plentiful let's suppose that it's likely present in all other planets.
But liquid water is another story.
For a start, in order to have liquid water, we need certain temperature and pressure.
We need a warm environment so that water doesn't freeze, but not too warm that it evaporates,
In the case of planets, we owe temperature to the stars.
So in the case of Earth, for example, if we solely consider the radiation it receives from its star, the average temperature of earth would be 20 ºC below zero.
The reason why our planet is in average above 15ºC, is because our atmosphere has greenhouse gases.
These greenhouse gases are what make habitable this planet.
Yet, having too many greenhouse gases and being too close to your star like Venus, would make water evaporate and dissipate in space.
Venus has a 470 ºC temperature because it has an atmosphere 90 times bigger than that of the Earth.
Mars is a different case.
If Mars had a more dense carbon dioxide atmosphere, it would be a habitable world, but it cannot retain its atmosphere,
because it is a tiny world, and lacks the greenhouse gases that would make it habitable.
With all this we created a concept called "the habitable zone."
The habitable zone is a region around a star where a rocky planet with a carbon dioxide and nitrogen atmosphere, can keep liquid water upon its surface.
Let's keep in mind that all these elements are necessary in order to have a potentially habitable world.
These are not habitable worlds for human beings because these are worlds with carbon dioxide atmospheres, unbreathable for human beings.
So far we have a bunch of planets chosen as potentially habitable.
These worlds that are within the habitable zone of its star based on models, and about the worlds we know their mass or radius, in some cases we know both, but we don't know if they have water or an atmosphere.
We have a generation of instruments like TESS and Gaia, that are capable of detecting many exoplanets.
In the future we will have missions like the James Webb telescope, the space telescope James Webb, that will allow us to detect the atmospheres of these potentially habitable planets.
We imagine that is highly likely that microscopic life has contaminated that planet with its emissions just as cyanobacteria did on Earth.
So we could detect with a telescope the biological activity of microscopic beings.
We are before one of the biggest experiments made by humanity.
For the first time we can answer the question if life is something common in our universe, or if it is something that has only occurred on Earth.
For now, this is the only habitable planet we have.
There is no second Earth for us to move when we spoil our current planet.
Actually, we are not even going to spoil it, I'll predict the future for you: the brightness of the sun will slowly increase, oceans will evaporate and in about 100 million years, all the oceans of the Earth will have completely evaporated, in 5 billion years the Sun will turn into a red giant, Earth will be within the atmosphere of the Sun, and will be a speck of dust that will evaporate in space.
Will we destroy life on Earth before that?
No, we will not bring life
to an end on Earth. Life on Earth has been here for 3.5 billion years, and most of it are microorganisms.
These microorganisms have endured frosts, collisions from space, changes on the atmosphere.
We will not finish with life on Earth.
We will ruin the conditions that enable human life.
There were you see pollution, microorganisms see perfect places to survive.
So whenever I think of habitable worlds, I think of Ursula.
I think of a world like Urres, where resources were so limited and everyone worked to survive in that world.
Where both men and women are equal.
I think of a world like Gethen, where genitals do not define people's destiny.
I think of a world where half of the population is given the same respect as the other half, based on merit.
Where women are not incubators, nor used for the pleasure of others.
I think of a world were women can love, travel, enjoy without being killed for being women.
As we walk towards extinction, I would like you to think on how to build a habitable world for women.
(Applause)
Does anyone here have a teenager in the family?
Children, brothers or sisters Please, raise your hands.
Wow! Quite a few, huh?
You probably have very fond recollections of that time.
I remember very well my teenage years.
It wasn't that long ago, actually.
I remember I loved to be with my friends have a good time with them,
we used to listen to alternative music, dance, partied
but above all, we loved to have new experiences.
For teenagers, it's all about having new experiences. That's why, when they have the chance to drink alcohol for the first time at this age it obviously becomes part of these new experiences.
There is an ever-increasing number of teenagers drinking alcohol.
They probably think, "This is OK. It's no big deal"
Well, I'm here to tell you that it is a big deal.
Teenagers are particularly vulnerable to alcohol.
Why?
Well, because at this age they still are in the maturing process.
When they drink alcohol, the brain functions - like those associated to learning and decision making - decrease.
It also affects the social behavior of teenagers causing insecurity, instability, even accidents.
Moreover, it could lead to the use of other drugs.
So, what can we do about it?
What guidance can we give teenagers and their families and society in general, to prevent alcohol use at such an early age?
Maybe you are thinking, "why don't they just ban it?"
And you are right, of course. The selling of alcohol to underage persons
Of course not. As we all know, forbidding something
So, what should we do, then?
In order to answer this question, the project "You Decide" was created. And I have the honor to be part of that project.
"You Decide" is an effort by the company I work for together with the best experts in this field
like Cedro Group made up by psychologists dealing with teenage drug addictions.
Well, as part of the "You Decide" project the main goal of which is to provide teenagers with the necessary tools to make the right choice about alcohol use we develop a number of activities.
First, we meet teenagers in a friendly atmosphere where they may feel comfortable and safe.
Under these circumstances, we ask the psychologists to explain to them what the negative effects of alcohol use at an early age are.
In turn, they can ask questions and talk about sexuality, recreation, professional interests and so forth.
Then, we take them to a museum or to an art gallery with the aim of getting them familiar with Cuban art mainly and the development of arts in general.
Finally, we organize a workshop on non-alcoholic cocktails.
In these workshops, teenagers can create their own cocktails without alcohol, while they learn about the techniques and tools used to make these types of cocktails.
Now, what are the achievements of "You Decide" so far?
This is a very recent project but it is gaining ground as time goes by.
This year, we were able to reach more than 1,400 teenagers in Havana.
Next year, we hope to implement the project all over the country
thus reaching more than 20,000 teenagers.
First of all, it was very shocking to see at the beginning of this project how teenagers used to consider alcohol use as a very "cool" activity.
As something to be proud of, to be admired by their friends.
And that's how it was.
In fact, still is.
As the project developed their attitude towards alcohol gradually changed.
They began to show a much more mature attitude towards alcohol use.
By then, not drinking alcohol had become the best choice.
Would you like to meet the teenagers taking part in the You Decide project?
They are here, among us today and I want you to meet them.
Students from Cepero Bonilla School please, stand up. Thank you for coming today.
(Applause) Thank you very much.
It was also very shocking to see that many of these teenagers had never been in a museum or an art gallery before.
The project gave them the chance to visit these places to learn about arts in general and knowing more about them.
"You Decide" has also given me the opportunity to get to know these teenagers, to listen to them, to understand them.
When we achieved this, it was all very magical. They allowed us to be part of their lives and shared their dreams and viewpoints with us.
For me, it was very powerful to contrast this with my own experience at a similar age.
I would like "You Decide" to be more than just a project. I would like it to become a way of life, a lifestyle for every family in the world.
So that our experience can also be your own experience.
Remember: you decide, you decide, you decide if you want teens without alcohol.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
(Video) It all started when a woman came up to the hill we live on.
She rang the bell and all the children in the village came.
She came with a donkey with a lot of books.
We had never seen children's books.
They had lots of pictures and came from a Nordic country.
The woman invited us to go to the library.
Now we draw comics of our life here.
Anne Hansen: Do you remember that a little while ago one of the speakers talked about Nietzsche's inner desert?
I think that our books and the reason why they are still so popular among the children is precisely because they scare the inner desert away.
Now I'm going to read a story called "The Sprout" by Pia Thauler, a Danish author.
"The Sprout" is about a little boy who wants to grow a bean.
So, he's got the bean in his hand and then... he sows the bean and the cat, a friend of his, is looking.
Then the kid waters the soil around the plant and he's stuck a stick so that he'll know where the bean will sprout.
And then he sits down and the sun heats the fertile soil for his sprout and, suddenly, it came out of the ground.
This is a little miracle I have never understood: why roots don't come out while the stem grows underground, but that's life.
(Laughter) Then the plant grows and the cat finds something to play with, and the little boy has to make decisions and shoos his cat away.
Then the rain comes and the boy is there, taking care of his plant and the plant keeps growing and the rain helps it grow and suddenly, a pair of rabbits show up, and they are hungry, and they see food and so they start eating.
Then the child has to protect his plant once again, and he builds a fence around it.
And so the plant is protected and can keep growing.
But then the pigeons come, and they also see food, and they decide to eat something, and the child has to put up a scarecrow to scare the pigeons away.
But then the butterflies come, and the butterflies pollinate the flowers of the plant, and that is very important for it to grow pods.
And in the end, the child sees that there is a crop of a lot of bean pods. His project has been successful, and now he is very happy.
And when one is very happy, one can have a party and invite all of their friends, even the cat was there (Laughter) but he's forgiven now.
Yes, because one also becomes extra generous when one is successful.
So, this is a short story
with no written text
which can help people who cannot read very well, but want to read something to their kids.
That is one thing.
Another thing is that this kind of book that we have in our libraries can be interpreted at different levels, and this one has a meta-reading which in this case would be, now, you remember the story, right?
So, there's a main character who has a project. And this main character has a certain knowledge and certain qualities.
He knows what time of the year to sow the seeds, he knows there's sun, and rain, and butterflies.
He knows that's all nature he must respect, if he doesn't want to be taken over by the inner desert.
He also has certain skills, he knows how to build a fence, he can make a scarecrow, and he has the guts to shoo his cat away with a rake.
And all that is necessary for him to be successful.
And I think that if you know that you have a project you plan and try for it to work out.
And when it does, you are happy and rewarded, and you can start to work on a new project.
And this leads me to talk about a very different story, which is called "Kenny and the Barbie dolls".
Kenny is a kindergarten boy who is very good at football.
But one day, he decides that he wants to play with Barbies.
So he has one hidden at home, because his dad doesn't like it; his dad wants him to play football.
So, the boy brings his Barbie to the kindergarten, and he meets the girls who are all playing, and have absolutely no interest in playing with him.
And they ignore him, we could say.
But he insists and stays there with his Barbie.
And suddenly someone asks him, "So, what's her name?"
"Her name is Barbie." "No, no.

She can't be just called Barbie."
But he ends up joining in anyway.
and, suddenly, Kenny's Barbie gives birth to a piece of fish.
Nobody knows how, but that's what happened.
(Laughter) And then, without a warning, they drop the Barbie dolls and run into the other room to dress up as princesses.

Everyone. As princesses.
Here they are dancing as you can see.
So the kids outside are waiting for Kenny to come and play football.
Why isn't he coming? Why?
So they go into the room
to see what's going on.
And they see him having so much fun, they also want to dress up as princesses.
So everyone, both boys and girls, was dressed as princesses, dancing and having a wonderful time.
And suddenly, a grown-up comes and all the children stand still.
The game is over. Something happened.
The grown-up checks that everyone is alive and well, picks up some things and leaves.
And all the children keep playing.
And suddenly they all go out, pick up the ball and start to play football.
And the game can go on and on until the end of the world, because there are no rules, at least no written rules.
Kids don't know about goals and why and there needs to be learning, and there needs to be a curriculum, and grades; none of that.
So that kind of game inspires you as a human being. Inspires you to try out new things.
Kenny managed to join the girls and be respected.
The other boys began to play and dressed up as princesses. And children don't have the idea in mind that something has to be a result that can lead to something else.
And this leads us to think about the different kinds of games and different kinds of books.
There are books in which some adult thought that kids should learn to count to 10, or the vowels, or the colours.
And that kind of game or book has an abyss at the end.
Once you've learned these things, and you've got good grades for mum and dad to be happy, there's no more.
And then Nietzsche and the inner desert start to take over.
Because the child gets bored and doesn't know what to do with all that.
While this game is not conducted by anyone.
The kids spontaneously say, "Let's do this, let's go up there.
Let's see what happens."
That's the contrast between what we, as grown-ups call "the inner child."
Because we all have an inner child.
And when there is a situation which involves learning or communication like the ones we encounter daily at the library; the child who is listening identifies with the child in the book, and the grown-up who tells the story need to be in close contact with his or her own inner child. Otherwise it doesn't work.
The child feels spoiled, sometimes they speak "like this" and the child feels ridiculed, but because they don't want to get bad grades they don't say anything and so on.
Now that we are talking and thinking about this, I also think there's an inner child to the author, and there's also the illustrator's inner child.
So it's 5 children, right? Right.
So it is a group of children
who enjoy listening and growing and learning through books like these.
And this brings us to our work of 15 years on the hills with children who are known as "damaged children" here.
And I wonder, damaged by whom?
But that's a different matter.
And they, as Priscilla said, have made a magazine.
And we have now reached number 14.
Number 14 is out now because the children who started to make the magazine have grown now.
And they have now made a magazine for teenagers.
And we thought they were leaving now, but they weren't.
They were so sad and offended that we had to continue and now there are two magazines for teenagers and a magazine for children. And another magazine was made in Contulmo, and some people from Argentina have come and are planning-- but they talk a lot, and I'm not sure they are making magazines.
Anyways.
(Laughter)
Well, I always think that they'll hopefully be inspired, right?
So, we have two libraries in Valparaíso for children of all ages: from 8 months to 14 years old, and they all contribute with drawings and so on.
And stories. And they talk
about their inner life.
They talk about their experience as human beings, living, and having, conflicts, and solving those conflicts.
And being happy and sharing that happiness.
So that's it. I'm done, right?
That's it.
(Applause)
I come to tell you something that is almost a confession: In the last years I developed a strange obsession to ensure that all children learn to program computers.
I know that this idea is not new, but I'm increasingly convinced of its enormous potential to help us transform education.
What helps me most to think this is a phenomenon that some call "software is eating up the world."
I think it's something that is better understood by some examples.
Just 10 years ago, Steve Jobs gathered his team and said he was determined to design and manufacture a phone.
At this meeting, there were faces of concern and even fear, because competition had much more experience than them on the subject.
But Jobs was very confident.
He said: "The advantage is ours, because we are a software company and they aren't."
And in just two years the first iPhone was ready and last year they sold 170 million units.
And with that they came to build what is now the most valuable company in the world.
Its market value -- if someone around wants to buy it -- is over 730 billion dollars.
For you to have a reference, further down I put the market value of the most valuable company in our country, which is related to the oil business.
But before doing that, Jobs had realized that portable devices to play music could also be transformed into small computers.
Today, they seem to be launching a smart watch.
And some say that they are designing an electric car.
What you see here is a modern electric car.
It has millions of lines of code working on dozens of computers. This represents more than 30 percent of the value of that car.
But do you know what's amazing about these cars?
It's that if you want them to have more features, that they can do more things, you don't have to buy a new car, because many of these things simply come with software updates.
One day, the car connected to the Internet, may come up with a new autopilot or with better acceleration.
I recommend that you look at these cars with great respect, because there is already a version of these car models with an acceleration from 0 to 100 km/h in just 3.1 seconds.
Who makes all these changes?
Who makes all these incredible technological things that these modern devices have?
Well, more and more, programmers do.
And how are programmers? How do we imagine them?
I had a picture I wanted to show you, of how we imagine programmers, depicting the typical nerd, the geek that many imagine and that really brings many problems in our profession.
You don't imagine programmers to be like that, right?
I was programmer for many years, tell me you don't, thanks.
To combat these stereotypes that really cause many problems in our profession, I want to say that the first computer programmer was a woman.
She was the daughter of the English poet Lord Byron.
She was Ada King, Countess of Lovelace and unfortunately lived only 36 years.
And Ada was not only the first computer programmer, but she wrote her first program with a pencil and paper, more than 100 years before the first electronic computer was started in 1946.
A giant of 27 tons that was called ENIAC.
When they started this computer all the lights in Philadelphia
would flicker. It was so big that people worked literally inside the computer, the whole programming team, consisting of six people.
All of them women.
You can see two of them in this photo.
And ENIAC could do very fast calculations, it could do 400 multiplications per second.
But that's nothing compared to what computers can do, the ones you have, that we all have, that we call smartphones.
These computers can do up to 50 billion operations per second.
They weigh less than 300 grams and work with a 3-volt battery.
That is how technology advanced during all this time.
Now, what do we do to begin working with these technologies?
What do we do to bring these technologies into the classroom?
Because there is something very interesting:
These computers that we see, you have them in your pocket, in your purse, in your hand, right?
It's like we can't detach from computers.
And some of you perhaps are using them now, while I'm talking.
And maybe you do this because you know that when this talk is over, you say: "Santiago's talk was good," this is being captured with lots of cameras, which are actually computers, and then the video will be edited with a software to edit digital video.
And you will be able to watch the result anywhere, at home, at any time, through a computer network and software that we call the Internet;
in a software product called YouTube, from a software company called Google, that was founded just 17 years ago.
I said it right: I lived 30 years of my life without Google.
All this is what we call "Software is eating the world."
I was lucky to see the transformation a little more closely, because I always worked in the industry.
And a few years ago I decided to search for a place where what I learned could help more people, have more impact.
That's when we cross paths with the Sadosky Foundation which is doing many things, but what excites us the most is the one related to education.
It's a product we call Programar, Program.ar.
I know, the name is a feast in originality, don't mention it.
And later I will talk about creativity, anyway -- It's what we came up with.
And we are doing this project with many organizations, NGOs, institutions, volunteers, and our goal is very clear: we want all the kids to have the opportunity to learn programming and computer science, from primary school. And if they let us do it
from an earlier stage, even better.
What we want is that children create new things with computers.
That they learn to be on the other side of the keyboard.
And we don't want to do this to form more programmers.
We hope that from this more kids will choose careers in computing, computer science, information technology, because those are fascinating careers that are changing the world and the country needs it a lot.
But for us, that is like thinking that you want to teach addition and subtraction so that there are more mathematicians, or that you want to teach writing so that there are more authors.
So, if we don't want to do this for that reason, then, what for?
I'll now give you three reasons why we are promoting this educational change.
The most important ones.
The first is to see computers as a means of expression through which children can display their natural creativity.
We want us to be put closer to music, drawing, drama.
Piaget's favorite disciple worked these ideas the most.
His name is Seymour Papert.
Already in 1971 he was writing about all the things kids can do with computers.
In the introduction to his article, Papert poses a dilemma, and the dilemma is this: "Some people think of using the computer to program the child; others think of using the child to program the computer."
Papert loves to talk about someone who was perhaps the most creative, the most ingenious, the best designer in history.
Leonardo designed dozens of machines throughout his life and it is clear he was a genius.
Ideas sprang from his head permanently, even radical ideas, unthinkable for his time.
But Leonardo also had a huge advantage: He was a master draftsman.
His ideas flowed naturally from his amazing brain into his incredible hands.
And what we see when we observe this is that kids who can use the computer -- a computer is an expressive medium, better than pen and paper, because it's dynamic -- have an advantage. They have like superpowers.
They can do things others kids can't do.
Leonardo could have done many things with a computer.
For example, he could have found many of the errors their designs had.
Obviously, now it's very easy to detect them, like for instance, in the tank war he designed. He placed the gears wrong in it,
so the wheels on one side turned in the opposite direction from the other ones.
But this is what gives those powers to children to display their creativity.
And believe me: It is a lot easier to program a computer than learning to draw like Leonardo.
The title of a book that was published in our country, captures exactly the first reason I just described: We want to help unfold the wings children's minds come with from the factory.
Because if we can make these wings unfold, all we have to do afterwards, is to show them thousand places where those wings can take them.
The second reason has to do with something we hear all the time nowadays, and it's that school today has to do less with knowledge, with memorizing things, and increasingly more with developing skills, abilities, with learning to think.
But what do you do to help kids in learning to think?
I'm sure there are many ways to do this.
But I can talk about only one, the one I know: Let us teach children to program computers.
I warned you this was an obsession so it is clear.
What are the capabilities children develop when they learn to program computers?
Many.
One is learning to abstract, to model, to represent reality.
Because a computer program most of the time, is simply this: a model of reality.
Consider, for example, programs working with people's information. There are many programs that do that, for example, a voter registration list.
If I want to make this program I'll have to decide which characteristics and behavior of people interest me, to solve this problem in particular.
I'll have to choose the name, the surname, the ID, their address, if they voted or not, and I will rule out other things such as hair color, or if they play soccer or not.
If I chose well these characteristics, if I did well my abstraction, my program will work.
If I did it wrong, my program won't work.
There's a second skill that is developed when programming computers.
It has to do with problem solving, what we call algorithmic thinking.
How from a series of steps one problem is solved.
Children at school learn many algorithms, but they don't learn the concept of algorithm.
An algorithm they learn, for example, is to divide two numbers.
I think it's very good that kids learn that given problem, there are actually many algorithms available to solve that problem.
Some will work.
And others will not.
And within the algorithms that work, some will do things more efficiently than others.
In computer science we call this: complexity.
Now, if all this I'm saying, complexity, algorithms, abstraction, modeling,
worries you: "How am I going to teach that to a child?"
"All that is very difficult," "How do I teach that?"
Well, I want to show you now a video of an experience we did a couple of months ago very close to here, in a settlement, in the city of Buenos Aires.
What you are going to see now are kids learning sorting algorithms of data structures.
And for the kids to understand it better we are teaching them without computers.
(Video starts) The guys pretend to work with a computer that has a single processor, which is represented by the circle they have before them.
So, what they have to do is to move forward in pairs to this circle make the comparison, and try to find as soon as possible their proper place in the line, ordering themselves from the highest to the lowest.
When they find their right place, they can change the banners they have.
From red to green.
(Video ends) The third reason has to do with school as a means to help us understand the world around us.
For that, for example, we learn natural science.
We learn physics, chemistry, biology, botany, zoology, many things.
But the question is: How can we understand the world around us without understanding how computers work?
If you don't know what they can and can't do.
Until when will children learn that a virus is an infectious, acellular, microscopic agent - I think I said it right, any biologist out there? - and not what a computer virus is? A simple program they can view and edit. That they understand what damage it can and cannot do.
And for that, we have to think about the world in the next 12 years, when kids that are beginning school today, finish it.
I talked about three things: to see computers as a means of expression, to enhance creativity; using the computer to learn to think, and to use the computer to understand the world around us.
There is one more thing: The technology gap and dependence.
Because we will never ever be able to be a developed country if we are country which is behind, in terms of science and technology.
And we have to think that computing is not only a science, but also the technology behind almost every major scientific advances of the last decades.
Because we can't understand how the human brain works, nor we can analyze the genome if we don't count with computers and software.
In the past years we improved a lot.
Our scientists are excellent.
We even lead in several subjects.
But the path we have to tread is still very long.
And if you think of it, you will realize that today, we live a new Papert's dilemma: but applied to countries.
And as a country, we have two options, they are just two: to program or to be programmed.
(Applause)
I'm going to sit here to feel as close as possible to you all. And ask you this question: Which is the part of your body that feels uncomfortable, when you are in conflict with someone?
Have you ever thought about this?
I ask you to take a few seconds to try to visualize it.
I've been asking this question for more than 10 years.
Most people quickly identify a part of their body; very few have trouble realizing what is what is happening to them, but all are impacted when they realize that this emotion, that arises in a situation of conflict and is manifested in their body, we try to conceal or hide it.
This is that we do, is a habit learnt in childhood.
I invite you use a huge magnifying glass and together look at those little gestures that go building habits that make us stiffer, that push us away, or bring us closer, to others and ourselves.
To be able to be here today I had to look for my confidence, that often hides just when our interior self, wants to be fully present; has that happened to you?
I had to call her, work intensely, so that the feeling of security comes to me and washes over me calming the fear I feel of being in front of all of you. I learnt that if I take the tension to the soles of my feet and feel rooted to the ground, and let friendly hands accompany me, something might start to calm down.
Fear closes us up, makes us tense, it speeds up our heartbeat, blocks our thoughts and we are invaded by a sense of insecurity that often makes us sad, makes us smaller and all we want is to run away;
I hope not to do that.
We can be ourselves, instead.
Receive, listen to others, is we don't feel threatened, or ashamed.
How do we manage to be ourselves?
When I was a girl they called me "princess" at home.
I did well in school, had many friends, played sports.
A normal life. The kind of girl
that everybody loves.
Because they make no trouble.
The teachers were happy.
So I grew up with that idea of what I was.
But that was only part of me.
It took me a few years to realize that there was another, more intimate, secret, that came out at night, a very different and mysterious world emerged.
Thoughts and feelings that I had never heard talked about in the "upstairs world."
Sometimes I was invaded by anguish, so I invented prayers, promises, phrases, trying to calm that great internal void.
Also that mysterious world could pop up in some train of thought.
I can still feel the anxiety that took over me when I finished some activity and I was afraid my parents would not pick me up, or that something bad would happen to them on the way.
Thoughts that were impossible to express, because they had no logic.
Does anyone remember a classmate that brought out in you the worst feelings of hostility?
I do.
I remember two: they were super, super unlikable.
A mixture of envy and jealousy.
But, as they were not proper for a princess I learnt to silence and hide them deep within me,
and as we did not speak other than with a raised finger about these things almost monstrous that only happen to kids, because us adults have no such feelings, I grew up trying not to hear all that was not expected to happen to a girl my age.
And I grew up, graduated, got married, and gave birth to my first baby boy. And with his birth came the most
sublime time of my life, and also, the most frustrating.
And also, the most real.
The generosity of life draws us away from the path of pretentiousness.
I had prepared for a beautiful painless birth.
And I had a C-section.
I arrived at the clinic in the early morning and the doctors were scared to see meconium in the bag, thinking that there might be fetal suffering they began all their procedures that did not include involving me beyond my body, and even less with that little being that could not even imagine the codes of this world.
I became an object of measurements.
I was cold, and very afraid.
Nobody looked at me nor spoke to me.
In that moment I felt I did not exist. But Julian's crying gave me back my strength to ask for him to be placed in my arms.
But quickly they took him away for tests and, then, I could not remain silent.
I started to hear what I had avoided for so many years: That the world was upside down.
Many of us humans live fragmented.
How did we not even question how dehumanized or dehumanizing it is to be born like that; how can someone become so blind?
That question started a search and tons of questions that guide my studies to recover my wholeness and become myself once again.
Today, after a long road, I'm convinced that recovering the connection to our internal world, is vital to evolving as human beings.
As adults we have naturalized a misunderstanding about what education is.
Many time we are bitter or excessively irritable, and repeat non empathetic ways to connect with children.
If they want to play, we sit them
If they want to shout, we hush them.
If they prefer to be in silence, we demand that the express themselves in a loud and clear voice.
If they get banged up and are in pain, we tell them it's not that bad.
If they want to study singing, we try to convince them it's better to play some team sport.
And if they are not as they are supposed to be, we correct them.
And we teach them little by little to move away from the vital source of information with which all humans come into this world equipped with.
To discover what we are and "all" we are.
To awaken that consciousness and the pain it brings is paradoxically, motivation for a new empathetic education that takes care of each gesture, that cultivates the development of what is properly human.
The emotions had restricted access to class, limiting our existence to the dominion of reason.
An educational system that does not pull up a chair for the soul.
And does not teach to build bridges with our world from the skin inwards limits our development.
We need a school that "connects" these worlds.
That does not destroy the process of getting to know ourselves and what is around us.
We need a school that teaches to be integral, our own selves, coherent with our thoughts, emotions and actions.
A school that integrates the mysterious world in class, where we learn with other humans about the life of humans.
And for that we need adults that can return to ourselves, that can nurture places of trust and secure caring, that we might invite happiness and meeting with others.
Only if I know myself can I know and understand the others, and act in consideration of my needs, and the needs of others.
Push for initiatives where there is space for all of us.
There are transformational schools around the world that are giving impulse to a new education.
That understand that it is important to teach language and math as not to exile oneself for our own existence as a way to lead positive changes in the world aimed at our own good and the common good.
Imagine a world where each child grows up with the confidence to display his empathy with himself, with others and with his environment.
Surely that would be another world.
The good news is that we only need to look inwards, find ourselves, and connect these worlds.
Who dares to join up and build this new culture? Thank you.
(Applause)
Five years ago, without really knowing how I got there, I found myself sitting in front of my computer in a speed memory competition in which the first event consisted of memorizing as many numbers as you could in one second.
A very difficult task in which a simple blink of an eye could be a disaster.
In the competitions organized by Speed Memory, there are six events that consist of memorizing decimal and binary numbers in one and four seconds.
Colored figures and matrixes.
The qualification is decided by the total sum of all the points.
So that you have an idea of what we're talking about and you can experience it, we're going to do a little exercise.
(Laughter) I'm going to show you 12 binary digits, zeros and ones, and I'm going to give you four seconds to memorize them.
Are you ready?
Go! [10 01 11 11 00 01]
OK? How many of you are able to remember them all in order?
OK, fantastic!
This was the mark that I achieved on my first day of training.
And now we're going to make it easier a little.
After a few days of training, it's possible to memorize 20 numbers in these four seconds.
I'm sure that many of you can do it, I've seen many bright minds since I arrived.
Ready?
Go!
[10 01 11 10 01 11 00 01 00 11] (Laughter) Who has memorized them all?
Alright, fantastic!
Now I want you to try the level that I succeed in my first competition after seven months of training.
This attempt is going to be a little more difficult, you'll need to be focused 100 percent.
Are you ready?
Go!
(Laughter) [10 01 01 11 11 01 101 01 10 11 00 01 11 010] [11 01 00 11 00 01 101 01 00 10 10 01 10 001] You just tried to memorize 60 binary digits in four seconds.
In my first competition, memorizing was not the most difficult thing, I was prepared and I dominated it.
The most difficult thing was remaining calm in the face of the pressure that I myself had created.
I was very scared of speaking in public, of competing, and I was terrified of doing it right or wrong.
Can you imagine my state of being?
My heart leaped out of my chest, my legs wanted to start running, and my hand was shaking so bad that I was unable to click the button to start the competition.
In summary, I was way outside my comfort zone.
I was having one of the worst experiences of my life when it should have been one of the best.
Or at least until I was able to shift the focus.
I understood that I was there because I chose to be there, that I should enjoy it, and I was able to set aside the need to perform well in the competition, to get good scores or even to win.
And as my only aim, I concentrated on enjoying the experience, on having a good time.
When I was able to do that, the results magically started to follow and I was able to break my personal record.
While I wasn't completely unhappy with my scores, I was quite pleased, something didn't sit well with me.
Is it normal to suffer so much in an important moment?
Several months later I took part in the World Championship of Speed Memory.
I had worked more, my performance had improved, my scores had increased, but the same thing happened to me again.
Something that I would come to understand later is that knowledge doesn't guarantee good psychological performance.
During this championship, I observed Ramón Campayo, my mentor and nine-time world champion.
He looked completely concentrated in what he was doing, and enjoying it, he wasn't suffering.
Our personalities as competitors were so different that it pushed me to work on something I had been delaying, psychological preparation.
From that moment on, I learned about how the mind works and how I can use it in my favor.
And two years later I competed in the next world championship, eager to reap the rewards of my training and this renewed mentality.
Now, so that you all have an understanding of the benefits of this training, we're going to try the next set of numbers.
Are you ready?
It's going to be a challenge a little more difficult than the last.
(Laughter) This is the set I was able to complete in that world championship.
Ready?
Go!
[10 01 11 01 00 01 10 10 01 11 01 00 01 11 00 11 10 01 01 11 00 01 101 01 11 10 10 01 11 001 10 01 00 01 11 01 101 01 11 11 00 01 11 010] How do you feel? (Laughter)
OK, you just faced 92 binary digits.
After two years of training, I managed to improve my scores as well as set my first world record in speed memory.
And two years later I was named world champion runner-up.
But for me, the most important thing wasn't the achievements I obtained but that I was able to shift the focus and learn to enjoy and have fun like never before in these important moments.
The question we have to ask is: "What is the difference between the competitor that performs at 100 percent and those that train but never achieve that performance?"
Well as you'll see, psychological preparation is the cumulus of good or bad habits.
Those who have adequately prepared psychologically, have accumulated good habits, and those that haven't go accumulating a series of bad habits.
We have, on average, about 60,000 thoughts a day.
And according to Harvard, 47 percent of these thoughts are negative.
Although, I'm sure that leading up to that important moment we're talking about, that percentage increases, don't you think?
And remember, all of these thoughts leave a mark.
The question we have to ask now is: "What can we do to quit suffering in these circumstances, and learn how to enjoy and really do our best?"
Our mind is continually recording the opinions about all the stimuli that it interacts with.
Basically, if it makes us feel good or bad
Let's imagine that you've never seen this, you don't know what a remote is, it's something new for you, we've got no prior judgements, just a neutral opinion, it's not good nor bad.
I give it to you, and when you touch it for the first time it gives you a little shock. What's going to happen? Our mind is going to note that sensation, the remote is bad, it's made me feel bad.
When this only happens once, it's not that serious. We have a slight opinion but it's not sure.
But if every time we get a shock, shock, shock, shock, and we repeat the process, what's going to happen? We're going to reinforce that opinion and we're going to be completely convinced that this is something bad because it's made us feel bad.
And every time it shows up, it's going to revive in us that sensation that it's generated.
If we want to work it the other way to achieve something positive, it works the same way.
If I give you the remote for the first time and when you touch it, they give you a beer, what's going to happen?
(Laughter) Obviously, the remote will be our best friend and we'll take it home with us.
(Laughter) As you can see, our mind works the same way.
And evidently, not everyone reacts the same way to the same stimulus.
When we have a palpable trigger like a shock, or a beer, it's very clear.
But what happens with thoughts?
According to the neurologist Álvaro Pascual Leone, the brain doesn't distinguish between reality and what it imagines.
What does this mean?
Be careful what you think.
When we start to prepare for an important moment, like this conference is very important to me, or a competition, an exam, an interview, our opinion is neutral on the first day, right?
But we start to receive hundreds or thousands of thoughts connected to that event, and our mind starts to record them.
How many negative thoughts can you accumulate during the first day of preparation? And during the first week? The first month? The first two months?
We've accumulated many negative feelings.
"It's an important moment, I have to do well." "How am I going to fail?"
"I'm going to be very stressed, how am I going to do well?"
And we start to notice how it's been making us feel.
It's like a snowball. If I let
the snowball start rolling over, it starts very small the first day, right? It's very simple and easy to stop, it doesn't do us any damage.
But every thought adds up, and it begins to accumulate these feelings. When I let the snowball start rolling over, it begins to expand and gets bigger and bigger, until we get to the moment when we say: "How do I stop this? I don't feel good and I'm afraid of what's going to happen." Well, when we get to this situation, what usually happens? We play those motivational videos, our favorite songs, or even movies that motivate us, right?
We need an injection of motivation because we've been losing it along the way
And we say to ourselves, "Just do it." "You can do it, of course you can."
And our mind, what's it going to do? It's going to note that feeling and say: "The moment, the challenge, it's good."
We arrive at the day of truth, the day of the important moment, and we tell ourselves: "I can do it, of course I can!"
And our mind notes again, "This is good." But it's going to do an evaluation, 75,572 negative thoughts (Laughter) 42 positive, (Laughter) "You can't do it, look, the data's right here."
"You're feeling bad, you can't do it." And it brings us down, right?
We say: "How is it that when I watched that motivational video where the captain gives that spectacular speech to the team, it gave them such a boost of energy that they made it to the world championship, or even won it. Why doesn't it work for me?"
Because they've accumulated good habits in their preparation, not only in the speech. They've had training and it's all a question of training and practice.
Now I'd like to share with you the technique that I used to be able to control this. It's one of the most beneficial of all the techniques that's helped me lose that fear of competition, that fear of speaking in public, and be able to do my best.
And most of all, stop suffering.
And to be clear, accumulating good, positive habits in this psychological preparation isn't just so that I feel good, it's also so that I perform the best.
According to studies by Sound Echo, a positive brain produces 31 percent more than a stressed or neutral brain.
We're no longer talking only about feelings or enjoyment, but of performance.
Thoughts work very similar to a whirlpool in the ocean.
If we're swimming and we realize that it's starting to pull us in, we can escape rather easily, right? The current isn't strong yet.
But if we become aware that the whirlpool is drawing us in once we're already in the center, what's going to happen?
It's much more complicated, we're in a zone that has become very powerful and it's going to be difficult to escape.
Well, thoughts work in a very similar system.
When an intrusive thought comes up, "this conference is very important," well, you'll see that we can change the focus of attention. But what happens when we hang onto those thoughts?
"it's a very important moment," "What if I do bad, I only have one chance?"
"How am I going to do good if I only have one chance?"
And we enter into this loop, right? Trapped by this thinking where changing the focus of attention is much more difficult, and now we can't escape. Now we have a lot of work to do.
Remember what I said at the beginning, that every thought leaves a mark? Well when we enter into that loop, we start to accumulate these negative feelings, we start to create a giant snowball.
The technique that I used to be able to overcome it is "cutting."
We have to interrupt the thinking and quit accumulating those negative feelings.
Ideally, this means being aware of those thoughts, even as soon as they come up to your mind, and letting them pass.
This is the simplest, changing the focus of attention is the easiest. But we need training and practice.
Now let's see how to get out of this loop when I'm completely trapped.
You have to concentrate on changing the focus of attention. Why? Because the brain will make what we focus on real and we begin to accumulate those feelings.
For example, if you're walking and you get inundated with those intrusive thoughts and get stuck in the loop, you have to create a break. "Enough!"
And I have to focus on something else, it doesn't matter what, simply stop evoking those thoughts. If we're walking, concentrate on the steps, count them. If you're doing something else, focus on that. Just change the focus of attention, radically.
And in this way, we'll stop accumulating those negative feelings, because as we accumulate these negative thoughts, our balance is going to tip towards the negative.
If we break or interrupt these feelings, we manage to equalize our balance and keep it in neutral, and from there we can tilt it towards the positive side.
The correct way to do it is to go from neutral, sorry, from negative to neutral, and then from neutral to positive.
If I've accumulated many negative feelings, my balance is tilted negative, and I can't revert directly to positive.
For example: "I'm a nervous person and I'm going to fail because there are a lot of people, how could I not fail?"
"No, no, I'm going to be fine." And what happens? It's not credible, right? "How am I going to be OK with how shy I am?"
It's impossible because we have the balance tilted negative. The first step is neutralizing the balance, and then tilting it positive.
As you can see, by training the mind we can change how we perceive things in order to develop our true potential.
And now I'm going to ask that with this applause, you commit to taking control of your thoughts and that you be the sculptors of your own brains.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Gerry Garbulsky: It is the year 1837, our next speaker is 28 years old.
He is just back from an incredible voyage, that would later change our conception of life.
Please welcome Charles Darwin himself.
(Applause) Charles Darwin: Dear colleagues, friends.
I took this invitation to tell you about an idea that little by little has been developing in my mind.
I consider it a dangerous idea.
Just because I know you are people I can trust, open minded people that will appreciate how delicate this matter is, I dare to expose it, yet incomplete.
It’s an idea that concerns me, and, somehow, it’s turning into a burden too heavy to be borne alone.
I always considered my academic studies were nothing but a waste of time.
I really had no idea how my life would turn, until 6 years ago I got a letter that would change it forever.
My dear friend and mentor Henslow told me that Capitan Fitz-Roy was about to start an expedition around the world and was willing to share part of his cabin with a young naturalist, without salary.
That would be the beginning of an adventure that would fulfill...
Fulfill? It would exceed all my dreams since I was a little boy!
The expedition took longer than expected. It ended up taking five years.
And even so, I couldn’t get used to the boat rocking movement.
But, the natural wonders I saw, what I learned in that trip.
I took notes almost every day, returned with so many journals I expect to publish as soon as possible.
As a kid I loved nature, loved it, collected minerals, insects.
I remember once, I found a spectacular beetle, very different from the ones I had.
And I caught it with a hand, and another one appeared and I caught it with the other hand, finally a third beetle appeared and I couldn’t think of a better idea than putting one of them into my mouth in order to free one hand.
At that moment, the beetle spat a horrible liquid, very acid, I had to spit it out and lost the other beetles.
Imagine what it meant to me, then, to have access to that place in the boat of Fitz-Roy.
Besides, before we left, professor Henslow gave me a geology book.
I must admit that, when I was studying in Edinburgh, geology always seemed boring to me.
I even swore I would never read a book about it again.
But, as it was a gift from him I agreed to take it and read it.
The book is "Principles of Geology" by Charles Lyell.
An author I admired from the first page.
And now I boast about counting him among my closest friends.
Lyell says that when we face any geological formation, like a canyon or valley, we shouldn’t explain it through extraordinary causes, like a singular flood or the caprice of the gods.
No. All the past events, no matter
how extraordinary they may seem should be explained through ordinary causes.
For example, a canyon or a valley, through the action of an ordinary river that slowly and constantly erodes it for a long time.
The key was time.
Imagine how long it takes for a little riverbed to form a canyon.
The Earth, my friends, is much older than we thought.
Time and raindrops.
Multiplying the drops by thousand downpours, and multiplying the action of a river by thousand years, Lyell turns geology into a mature discipline.
He simply teaches us to recognize and decode in the rocks the message of time.
He teaches us a new perspective to see the geological formations we already knew.
As if they were the wrinkles in the face of an old world.
The impact the book had on me was such that every time I faced a landscape never seen by Lyell, I saw it through his eyes.
Although nature wasn’t the same after him, it would still be the object of my passion.
So, it occurred to me to ask myself if this approach couldn’t be applied to other fields.
That is, if the repetition of little events through huge periods, makes them effective.
Maybe, time has created new things in unforeseen spheres.
That is, Lyell explains the origin of a canyon with processes as simple as erosion.
So, is it possible to explain in a similar way the mystery of all mysteries?
That is to say, the origin of the species.
But, which are the forms, the forces, that are still working and have shaped life?
Which is the erosion that has created the wonderful diversity of species that inhabits our planet?
No doubt, not even one, that the naturalist who finds which such processes are, will be the Newton of natural history.
But, while I was asking myself these questions during the trip I started to notice certain signs in nature.
Without being able to understand their meanings and implications.
Like a detective arriving at the crime scene and doesn't know how it was committed.
I’m talking of the dangerous idea I’m here to tell you about.
For example, in the Galapagos archipelago I noticed that in each island different turtles inhabited, with a different aspect, but above all with a different flavor
More than one proudly recognized the island the dinner came from with just a bite.
Many believed that it just was a caprice of the creator.
I was concerned about it.
I needed to find another answer.
I had clues for a new idea.
But this idea didn't mature during the trip, but later.
It was by accident.
I sent my complete collection of birds from the trip to an expert ornithologist to have them catalogued.
In the sample, there were a series of little birds, chaffinches, I collected in the four islands I visited in Galapagos.
In each island, the chaffinches showed different modifications.
The idea I came up with, the most reasonable one, was that the birds came from the continent and have adapted to the different environments of each island.
We were dogmatically taught that a species can never be transformed into another one.
However, the expert told me that despite their resemblance they weren’t varieties of the same species but different species.
The modifications the chaffinches suffered in each island achieved what we considered impossible; the species change.
Can the ordinary mechanisms that generate transformations in each species create different species if we give them enough time?
I thought how taxonomists group life forms: varieties into species, species into kinds, kinds into families.
The “family" idea started to go around in my head.
And if it was just about that?
If the term "relationship" was more than an expression but a real thing instead?
If the whole life on Earth was part of a big family?
Maybe, the complete biodiversity is the product of the descendant of some ancient ancestors.
Perhaps, and this may be a little risky, there is a single ancestor for all of us.
Imagine. The history of Earth,
the history of life on Earth would be like the history of a tree.
A tree whose branches represent the varieties of all the families that inhabited and inhabit our planet.
A family tree that grows imperceptibly day by day.
And this is where the idea becomes even more dangerous.
If we follow the implicit logic of this thought, we, human beings, would also be part of this big family, with moths, orangutans, and Galapagos chaffinches.
We would be a little branch in the leafy tree of life.
We wouldn’t be the result of the direct action of the creator.
We’d no longer be the center of creation, but just descendants of an animal form already extinct.
But as always, this leads us to new questions.
If we all are part of a big family, it’s inevitable to want to know how our ancestors were.
Perhaps in the future, we may be lucky and able to find their fossils.
But, how can we know how they moved, what they thought, laughed, cried, what they dreamed of?
Lyell taught me to read the past in the geological strata.
I wondered in which stratum we naturalists should read our own past.
We bear the footprints with which, in the future, we could rescue our ancestors.
The forces we are searching for left a scene full of clues.
And the answer, like the erosion, is in the full eye of everyone.
You just have to be open minded to find it.
Think about it: If someone meets with me, my brother and my cousin Francis in a room, they could suspect we are family.
They could even dare say my brother is closer to me than my cousin.
All this, just thanks to the detailed observation of the physical resemblance.
So, observe the wing of this bat.
It has the same bones, in the same disposition as our hand.
And these similarities can also be found in the structure of a bird's wing, or even in an extinct reptile.
From my point of view, these similarities occur because they are inherited from a common organism: the organism from which they descend.
And these similarities reflect a genealogical relationship.
Birds, mammals and reptiles are family.
And we can also deduce that bats are closer to us than reptiles or birds.
Like my brother is closer to me than my cousin.
And another very important thing: if someone observes my cousin, my brother and me, they could imagine, even without meeting him, characteristics of my grandfather.
By examining these bones, we can know characteristics of the organism they descend from.
For example, that it had a spine, that certain bones were connected with other ones, etc. And, isn’t it possible to apply this method further than to bones?
Don’t the yawns, that many times our loyal dogs pass on us, talk of our ancestors?
Isn’t it meaningful that in our anger gestures we show our teeth?
Like wanting to show the huge canines we no longer have.
Maybe, it’s a gesture dogs and humans inherited from a common ancestor.
And the dogs that keep us company at home, and we consider members of our family, who would have thought that they really are our family.
And who would have thought that partridges are too, in which both human and dogs buried our teeth.
I know I have to find an explanation, in Lyell’s way, that tells how they are produced, these modifications, These ramifications in the tree of life.
How come my dear chaffinches got their beaks modified, in each island to be able to eat the food available there?
What is missing is the natural mechanism that generates the beautiful adaptations of the organisms to their environment.
To be honest, I have no idea which this mechanism is.
Anyway, all this radically changes the way we see ourselves.
That’s why the idea is so dangerous.
It’s against the foundation of our moral values.
How could I share this idea with people who still think that we are the center of creation?
How could I share it with an audience that doesn’t even recognize a common origin for all human beings and in this way pretends to justify slavery?
How would they take my idea that we have an origin in common with primates?
Beyond the seriousness of this statement I was comforted that this conception gives arguments to those of us who are horrified by slavery.
I remember how terribly upset I was when I saw the unfair treatment black people received in Brazil, or the natives in Argentina.
I see from here that some of you look at me flabbergasted, others even show me their canines.
Another ones, curiously, seem satisfied with the idea.
It’s not necessary to convince me of how hard it is to digest this idea.
It’s like wasting almost all I learned during my studies and much of what you probably learned.
Well, the confession is made.
And I say confession because having told you my idea, for my conscience and fears, is like having confessed a crime.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I’ve been always interested in politics.
During my high school years, I was an activist at the student center.
Among other things, we organized huge camps, of more than 600 students, there wasn’t a single adult present, so you can imagine how they were.
We came from years of a dictatorship, and we thought that what was most important was to rebuild the community, to open ourselves to other ways of relating to diverse people and to take fear out of our bodies, which is quite different to traditional politics.
One thing we learned is that this is very hard, that we’re naturally not very well prepared to deal with conflict, difference --
Thirty years later, I’m working at Facebook and once again I’m in a camp, but this time it’s a global camp of 2 billion people.
But it’s a sphere of interaction that could bring us together, and it could be an incredible driving force to generate empathy.
However, the feeling is that we’re becoming more and more polarized.
Each country has their local version of polarization, and many think that the Internet, and social networks in particular, are one of its causes.
But is it like that?
Do social networks shut us out and push us away from those who think differently from us?
Or can social networks be a meeting space like the one we imagined in high school?
The advantage we have today is that we can try to understand this by using the huge amount of data we generate online every day.
Just so you have an idea: every day users post on Facebook an amount of text equivalent to seven Wikipedias.
Seven Wikipedias are generated on Facebook every day.
And with the photos that are uploaded in a week, if we printed them, maybe, many of you have probably never seen a printed photo, but we used to print them at some point in time -- With the photos of a week we could cover the City of Buenos Aires.
And I’m really privileged to be immersed in this avalanche of data, in the Computational Social Science group at Facebook in Menlo Park, California.
And yes, you heard right, Social Sciences can be computational.
You need both to understand what I call the “photo of our culture,” we take every day on the Internet.
It’s worth noticing we do all of this over anonymous data, okay?
It’s based on statistics about thousands or millions of cases and they don’t tell us anything about one case in particular, and they don’t predict anything about an individual person.
In order to give you sense of it, I’ll tell you something we did: we looked at what happens to couple relationships on Facebook.
We started by counting the amount of interactions between two people that are about to form a couple, from 100 days before it’s formed to 100 days afterwards.
As you can see... (Laughter) the interactions keep increasing – boom!
The couple is formed, then interactions plunge, we guess they’re spending more time face to face, right?
(Laughter) In parallel, by doing text analysis, we can see the words they use in these interaction are every time more positive.
Something else we looked at is how likely it is for a couple to last 5 years based on how many months it’s been going on for.
For example, if the couple already lasted 6 months, the probability of lasting 5 years goes up 40 percent compared to the chances it had on its first day.
But obviously, couples break up, some of them, and on the day we announce we’re newly single our friends come to our help, or maybe jump at the chance -- our interactions online with our friends on that day we announce our new relationship status, tripled.
I’m not sure why.
(Laughter) Anyway, today I want to talk to you less about love, and more about a lack of love.
Let me tell you a bit about what data tells us about polarization in social networks, and see if this global camp we imagined at the high school is possible.
I’ll start with the good news.
On one hand, social networks make us closer, they make the world a progressively smaller place.
Today, a Facebook user in Argentina has in average more than 500 friends, follows 250 pages, going from celebrities to football clubs, to politicians or mass media, and is part of more than 30 groups regarding all sort of topics.
Communication turned into many-to-many, potentially so-many-to-so many, and I say potentially everyone-to-everyone.
I’ll give you a specific number of how small the world has become.
In 1909, Guglielmo Marconi asked himself the following question: if I want to call anyone in the world using this "wireless telegraph," which he had just invented -- a precursor of the radio -- but I can only call an acquaintance, ask him to call an acquaintance of his, and so on until I reach the person I want to call, how many jumps will I have to go through?
Marconi obviously didn’t have the tools to determine this though, just because, Marconi guessed a number: 6.
And he planted this idea, that later on became very popular, the “six degrees of separation,” which you might’ve heard of.
And then movies came out about it and all.
There were some later attempts to determine this number, but all very limited in scope.
Our team did the biggest-scale study to this date, using the cloud, Facebook’s network of friendships.
But well, first we verified one thing: that 99.9 percent of users, almost all of them, are interconnected in the same friendship network, in the same cloud, and not separated in different little clouds.
But of course, this thing we wanted to do involved calculating 2 billion by 2 billion distances.
It’s four billions of billions, a 4 followed by 18 zeros, of distances, and then you have to measure averages.
We said “we’re Facebook,” we put it running on some hundreds of computers, we waited for a week, and after a week, we had that number computed for a thousand people.
A thousand, with no zeros behind.
Luckily, in computer science we have this thing called “approximation methods,” and using these methods we managed to reach a number: [3.5 degrees of separation] This means that between you and Messi, you and the Pope, you and a monk in the Tibet, on average there’s a friend, a friend of a friend, a friend of a friend of a friend, and half a person more.
(Laughter) (Applause) It’s small. (Applause)
We’re all very close, very interconnected, almost cramped.
We could be swimming in a sea of empathy.
But, well, we tend to gather with similar people, right?
From the same neighborhood, same social circle, that share our same interests, political ideas, or even musical taste.
But here networks can also help us.
In a study we did in the US among people who declared themselves as right-wing or left-wing, we saw that, in average, 20 percent of each person’s friends are of the opposite ideology.
This different people tend to be people we don’t see every day, with whom we don’t interact that often.
Much before the Internet, sociologists called these people “weak bonds” and what they saw is that, in a close circle of people, of ideas, these weak bonds are what give us diversity.
And in social networks we stumble upon them every day.
But well, we are feeling that something isn’t helping us.
So what’s going on?
I identify at least three things we should pay attention to: algorithms, the quality of the news, and our attitude towards those who think differently.
Firstly, all of our Internet experiences are ruled by algorithms, a series of rules and criteria to give us what we want.
When we open almost any social network, the content is presented to us organized, personalized.
The algorithm tries to show us first what it thinks is most interesting, relevant, and attractive to us.
And what many people are scared of, including myself, is that the algorithm hides diversity, that it starts to give us more of what we already agree with and think and hides us the rest.
To see if that’s true, as the algorithm is very complex, we studied particular cases.
Going back to the US, to the left- and right- wing friends I told you about, we saw that in average, for a right-wing person, his left-wing friends produce 35 percent of the articles in their network, the articles he could potentially see.
And what we saw is that the algorithm was showing almost all of it, 34 percent.
And the person clicked a more or less proportionally frequent amount of times.
So, in this article the team published in the Science journal, what we saw is that, for starters, the algorithm isn’t hiding diversity.
But well, it’s a very complex topic, and it’s an ever-evolving topic, so it’s something we need to be paying attention to for a long time, and at all times.
The second issue, the second problem, is that there’s too much trash content in the Internet, from fake news to sensationalist headlines, trying to catch our attention, which we often call “clickbait”.
For example, this fake news about the Pope supporting Trump was shared millions of times.
And our cognitive bias is to let them pass by, smile a bit, or even worse, press the share button on bad-quality content that reinforces our views.
However, we are in fact outraged when it’s something that represents opposing ideas.
And this is a serious problem, because all the bad-quality content, these fake news, tend to reinforce our opinion, to lock us even more inside ourselves, and they contaminate the network.
This leads us to the third problem, which is our attitude towards those who think differently.
Even when a friend shares something that is real, true, fact-checked, non-sensationalist, but that goes against our ideas, we tend to dismiss it or even block it.
In the weeks around Colombian peace referendum, a very polarizing event, the amount of unfriendings almost doubled the same amount in, for example, the weeks around Christmas.
In short, we’re very close, but it seems like we don’t make the most of the diversity of social networks.
There’s a risk of generating a vicious circle of feedback between technology and our behavior: we tend to reject or hide what we don’t agree with, and we share and look at what reinforces our ideas.
As tech companies, it’s clear we have a responsibility and a role to play in order to reduce this polarization.
In fact, a couple of months ago Facebook changed its mission, from simply connecting the world, to bringing it closer together.
There’s the idea, for example, of encouraging the creation of more groups that generate a sense of community, give them more visibility, and help the administrators of those groups to manage them.
Last year, by means of some changes we got 50 percent more people engaged in at least one group that they see as a meaningful group, that awakes in them a sense of community.
Another line of action is to partner with media, universities, and fact-checking organizations, as Chequeado in Argentina, or Snopes, POLITICO, or Politifact, in the US to give people more control over what they consume and reduce misinformation.
Last year, we did an experiment in the United States and Europe where news came with a small icon saying they had been disputed, by any of these organizations, and a link to see the relevant discussion.
Another line of action is trying to offer related news that give us diverse perspectives over the same topic.
And eventually, to detect those sensationalist headers and rank them lower.
I think a key question is what are we, the users, going to do to break this vicious circle and this feedback between technology and behavior.
We have to understand the role of the customization algorithms, we have to learn how to filter, but above all to take responsibility for what we do to ourselves and what we do to our network, because every action we take in social networks influences others.
Each trash website that gets likes becomes more prestigious.
We have to start by some basic steps.
If you find an article, check the source, especially, much, much more especially, if it’s something that reinforces your ideas.
If you find fact-checked and well-developed content, share it.
Now, if it’s one of those memes that make fun of the opposing side, those who agree with you will laugh and the rest will be offended, let’s spare ourselves of this, let’s not share it, and put a stop to it.
As you can see, I’m not giving up on believing it’s still possible to make a camp of 2 billion people.
But we’re at a turning point.
Tech companies on one side and we, the users, on the other side, have to work out the way for this feedback between technology and behavior to become a virtuous circle, that generates communities of empathy instead of information bubbles, and that brings us closer instead of bringing us apart and polarize us more and more.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
It looks difficult, right?
It's a robot, it works on its own, it vacuums automatically.
(overlapping audience comments)
It doesn't fall over.
Why does it not fall over?
(Overlapping audience answers) Exactly, it has sensors pointing to the floor, and it knows that when those sensors aren't pointing to the floor it's because there is no floor, so it doesn't fall.
The manufacturer might be interested in it falling over, so we'd have to buy a new one.
As a customer, I don't want the robot to do that.
So it needs that sensor.
But that sensor looks really complex, doesn't it?
Well, it doesn't have--
In my hand, I have the remote and it has exactly the same technology.
I press the button - it doesn't work very well with the lights - but anyway, it's the same remote
we all have at home; the remote control.
I have probably just run out of batteries, and I could press very hard to see if it works.
But there's a more convenient way to see if I've run out of batteries in my remote.
And that is something we all have in our pocket. I launch the camera in my phone.
You don't need to take any pictures or video.
I point at the front part, that has a small light, and at the phone I'm going to see if the remote light flashes.
Humans aren't able to see it, but machines are.
They do see that infrared light.
That's the technology it uses in order not to fall over.
But, well, it's a robot going all around the house. And we have walls at home.
We don't want it to crash into the walls, make them all dirty, and stop working.
Well, it has sensors at the front
It has sensors there that are emitting, they measure the distance between the wall and the robot itself, and when they're very close it doesn't let it crash.
What kind of technology is that?
Well, instead of thinking on a remote, the person who designed it said: "I'm going to take a look in nature, and see if there's something that does anything similar."
Bats!
Bats have very big ears because they screech. The sound bounces the wall or the trees, the sound collides, they get it, and they know how long it took that sound to get to them.
That way they're able to know how far objects are.
This robot has exactly the same.
Except for a small peculiarity, we don't want the robot screeching like a bat at home, do we?
We'd go mad when it's functioning.
Well, it's as simple as using a frequency we're not able to hear.
Well, it's a vacuum, right?
And what vacuums do is collect dust.
I open it, and I have to get the dust and throw it in the bin.
I can't leave it there forever.
But if I opened it now and it was working, it'd be throwing all the dust to me and staining everything around.
The vacuum lid has the same technology as our fridge, to know whether is open or closed, because in our fridge, when we close the door, When we close the door, the light goes off.
When we open it, it's on.
So the same technology.
If you take a closer look, there's a small button.
And, the last thing this robot has, it moves on its own, around the house. How does it move?
Because, if I turn it upside down, you'll see there are 2 small wheels moving, and one more just as a supporting point.
It does nothing, it just goes round.
So it's as simple--
It turns around as simple as what I'm about to show you right now.
I place a robot on the floor,
if I only move one wheel, you see it's turning.
So, that's the trick, it's not like a car.
In a car, we turn the front wheels, and the same for bikes, we turn the front one.
This kind of robot doesn't do it that way.
They only have 2 wheels, one looks like a small ball, like the one for a mouse, a supporting point, it does nothing, it turns in all directions.
So with these two engines, what they do is, they turn one wheel more than the other, and that's how they go round.
And that's how it goes round here, you saw it was doing like so, and it didn't need any more wheels.
Well, this looks like a very complicated robot, and we could see is no big deal.
It looks expensive; what I have here is a robot made by me, and it has a similar cost to a video game.
Not that much, is it?
It's OK, it's a robot. It moves.
It has more or less the same technology as this other that, well, it has a pretty housing, but...
The robot itself is this.
And I didn't want to leave, without challenging you to try it at home, to "hack" things up, which means pressing a little button and switching off the light in your fridge when it's open.
You can see now, as I should prove at the end, if the remote has dead batteries.
And well, you know a vacuum moving around the house has the same technology as a bat.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
As you see, the title of my talk
is rather suggestive. I will be discussing concepts rather than positions, though.
This topic is the yin-yang of today's world, with organic farming running against
conventional farming. According to the Kama Sutra: "if you want to have healthy sex, eat healthy food; if you want to have bad sex, eat bad food."
We are both social and sexual beings.
We have to keep that in mind.
That's the point.
I'd like to make a small introduction to set you into context.
By 1800, there were 3 billion inhabitants on Earth.
When I was born, there were 2.5 billion;
and there are 7 billion today.
A gentleman named Malthus concluded that population growth was exponential while food growth was mathematical.
He never heard of biofuels. He never did.
He didn't know about soil degradation
and poisoning due to use of chemicals
I will not overwhelm you talking about extreme positions and apocalyptic issues.
The world is changing and we don't know how it will end, but we should get ready as individuals.
Organic food will prevail in the future.
Much is talked about diabetes, high-blood pressure, obesity, and cardiovascular problems;
the causes of which are not stress, but the consumption of certain foods.
A person suffering from diabetes, high-blood pressure or obesity cannot have a balanced state of mind.
That person would be constantly stressed.
In Havana, this person can buy an organic lettuce for 5 Cuban pesos,
while in London, Toronto, or New York, it would cost 10 dollars, if it's available on the market.
Today we can speak of hunger in the First World.
The first ones to become aware of the damage caused by industrial agriculture, toxic farming, input-intensive farming, were the people in the First World.
They are very concerned over the fact that diseases are being compounded by other problems.
Life expectancy is high, but quality of life is low.
People are realizing that there is a food problem, including red meat consumption that increases uric acid levels.
We are going to live about 90 years.
(Laughter) That is a privilege.
The opportunity to buy organic food in other countries is only available to an elite; it is a class issue.
There are people bringing products from New Zealand to New York, from Colombia to Houston.
But who can buy them?
Only affluent people, the haves;
the have-nots cannot.
Cuba is the only country in the world that has a social program in place for the development of organic products.
Perspicacity at its best?

No! There were no fertilizers
and pesticides available.
The country was somehow forced to develop organic farming.
Nowadays, the Cuban school of organic farming is one of the best in the world.
In fact, I'm representing it here. I have been producing organic food for 18 years, and I have earned social recognition The only disadvantage to it
is that it is not cost-effective.
(Laughter) (Applause) The idea shouldn't be to start an organic-food revolution.
We are speaking of individual priorities.
It would be utopian: "Let's consume fertilizer-free products only!"
We have to consider what is being done to overcome the problem in different ways and through different means,
including family and individual farming.
Certification processes aren't being implemented in Cuba.
There are some ideas, however, about participatory certification
and ways of letting people know whether or not they're eating quality food.
People who consume organic products are less likely to get ill.
Another important thing is social responsibility.
Cigarette packs usually indicate that smoking can cause lung cancer, dizziness, and so forth. It's social responsibility to provide smokers with this information.
On a different note, people who sell croquettes never tell buyers how many times they have used the same oil.
How many croquettes can they fry before the oil turns into saturated fat?
Saturated fat is cancer-inducing.
There is social responsibility involved.
We won't ask them to label croquettes, but there are a number of products that should always include facts about sugar and saturated-fat contents and their effects.
When high-performance athletes take drugs, they are thrown out.
When we consume toxic products, nobody finds out. (Laughter)
Because we're not informed.
It is an individual problem.
And we can't justify it invoking food shortage because life expectancy in Cuba continues to grow, however.
In today's world, junk food is in trend because it's tasty, but it is really bad. But there's not only junk food, but also transgenic food, soya and wheat.
We should take better care of us, our families, and society.
In my opinion, Cuba has made a special effort in organic farming, building upon people's high level of education: they are in a position to endorse organic farming.
Organic farming does not merely provide an alternative but a possibility.
It has to be truly organic.
We have to get rid
of chemicals-based farming, not all of a sudden, but in a gradual manner. We have to know what we are eating, what our families are eating.
This way, we can live longer and our sexual life can be healthier.
And to quote the Kama Sutra, "A healthy body, a healthy life."
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Mario Fitz: We will learn how to perform cardiopulmonary resuscitation.
But I need help.

One is you, for sure. I need another one.
Diego Golombek: Gerry, are you there?
(Applause) [No Gerrys will be harmed in this demonstration] I need you to walk to the middle of the red circle and collapse with a sudden death without hurting yourself, please.
Diego Golombek, Mario Fitz: You can do it!
Applause, please.
(Applause) Mario Fitz: Gerry, are you okay?
Gerry doesn't reply to me.
And he is not breathing either.
Please, call 911 for an ambulance.
Quick, please.
Well, as you know, nothing happened to Gerry.
So to avoid hurting him let's continue this practice with a dummy and give Gerry a round of applause for his great fall down.
(Applause) I'm going to perform heart compressions to sustain circulation.
And I'm going to ask you Diego, if there's a defibrillator in this place, to please bring it to me.
Diego Golombek: there are many in the theater, I'll bring you one.
Mario Fitz: Thanks.
We will turn the defibrillator on and we are going to do what it tells us.
"Open package and extract the electrodes."
We peel the packages off and we extract the electrodes.
"Open package and extract electrodes."
"Peel the plastic bag off the electrode."
"Peel the plastic bag off the electrode." "Do not touch the patient."
"Analyzing heart rhythm."
"Do not touch the patient."
"Analyzing heart rhythm."
"Shock warning."
"Charging."
"Stand back."
"The shock will be applied in 3, 2, 1."
"Shock applied."
What can happen in this case is that, as we performed quickly, he may open his eyes and obviously ask, "what happened to me?" The answer is not: "you died, take it easy."
The answer is to calm him and position him always with the patches placed in the rescue position while we wait for the ambulance.
This is what we need to do: we put him on his side and calmly wait for the ambulance.
So now, while he waits for ambulance, I want to tell you this. In Argentina there is a sudden death every 15 minutes.
So, we have to think that when this sudden death occurs it happens in this way.
Anywhere, in people who felt good, who were previously healthy and seemed to have no health condition.
It is 200 times more frequent to die like this of a heart disease than to die in a fire.
Now think about the amount of fire extinguishers you find everywhere and yet, how few defibrillators we see.
This kind of unexpected deaths are triggered by an arrhythmia in most cases.
This arrhythmia causes a normally beating heart to stop beating and start trembling, causing death.
The only possible treatment is to begin with heart compressions to keep circulation and use a defibrillator so that the heart stops trembling, resets and starts again beating normally.
If we manage to do cardiac compressions and use the defibrillator within the first three minutes, the chances for the victim to reach the hospital alive is very high, it is 80 percent.
Now, if we don't do anything, almost no one will make it to the hospital.
This is why both cardiac compressions and the use of the defibrillator are part of the chain of life.
They are the two key elements.
Now, of this four-step chain of life, three happen outside the hospital. They depend on you to perform because nobody goes out with a doctor in their pocket in case something happens.
If something happens to you, the person next to you keeps you alive and helps; that's why the first step is to ask for help calling 911.
If you see someone falling down who was doing normal, you call them, they don't answer and don't breathe, it's a serious situation.
You have to call the ambulance at 911 or your local emergency number.
Immediately we have to start with cardiac compressions performed fast and strongly at the center of the thorax or chest, without fear for them to be effective.
You have to compress the thorax and sink it almost 2 inches so that circulation can work.
That is the second step.
The third step is to ask for a defibrillator.
There are many here in the Colon Theater.
If they bring it to me I use it and follow the defibrillator instructions that are in Spanish and tell me what to do.
And it will never give a shock if this person didn't have the arrhythmia which causes the trembling of the heart.
Therefore, we rest assured that it will not work if the victim doesn't have a sudden death.
Now, if we are in a place where there is no defibrillator, we continue with cardiac compressions until the ambulance arrives, which is the fourth step.
But the ambulance will arrive, if and only if in the first step we called it.
That's why it's very important to call the ambulance first.
This is of such an importance that today we are going to practice together here.
I will ask everyone to stand up, please.
Without hitting the person in front, we are going to stretch our arms like this, very, very streched and place our good hand below and the other one above.
We are going to intertwine our fingers and break the wrists, keeping our elbows straight, leaving the wrist well exposed.
We will go down and with our shoulders we are going to discharge our body weight without bending the elbows.
What are we going to do now?
You were given a little bag when you came to the event.
Inside that bag there is a heart made of foam.
Let's get that heart out and put it on the seat.
When we are all ready, we will practice cardiopulmonary resuscitation with the arms and hands technique I just showed you, to the beat of a Bee Gees song that goes at one hundred beats per minute. The song will give you the rhythm.
So, if you are all set with your hearts,
to the count of three we compress. One, two and three.
(Music) (End of music)
Very good! An applause to all these rescuers.
(Applause) Now let's go back to our seats, please. I have two more things to tell you.
The first: the Bee Gees gave us this song so that we know at which rhythm we have to perform it.
Because this music has the tempo of one hundred beats minute, necessary to correctly perform the compressions.
It means that if at some point you have to perform heart compressions, to think of this song will help you do it at a hundred beats per minute.
Just think about it, don't sing it.
It's a bit weird, okay?
The second recommendation is that, before performing cardiac compressions, you must have observed carefully.
You must have seen that person falling down inexplicably.
You called them, they have not answered,
they are not breathing, you have called 911.
And only then you begin with the compressions.
Don't go out now with your hands like this wanting to rescue everyone on the floor, there are also people just sunbathing.
(Laughter) Well, as I first stood on this red circle I saw a lot of people.
Now that I'm leaving, I don't see
people anymore, I see rescuers.
I see a lot of hands that surely at some point in time are going to turn a death situation into a life situation.
I am happy to have lived this with you.
Thank you so much, everybody.
(Applause)
29th May 1832, Évariste Galois knew it was the last night of his life.
That revolutionary, vehement, and somewhat crazy boy, knew he would die the following day at the age of 20.
He was challenged to a duel by a Captain of the Guard and knew he had no chances.
So Évariste Galois spent the last night of his life writing.
He wrote letters to his relatives, to his friends, and he wrote all the math his adolescent mind was bubbling with, doomed to fade the following day.
Too soon!
His last letter, to his friend Chevalier: "show my work to Jacobi and Gauss."
Jacobi, one of the greatest.
Gauss, probably the greatest.
The following day, the weapon of the Captain of the Guard, bullet or sword, went through Évariste Galois' stomach.
At present I'm twice over 20 years old, and when I was 20, in that whole bunch of subjects for the math degree: algebra, analysis, statistics, topology, there was one, just one, that had a proper noun: Galois Theory.
What did that scared boy invent the last night of his life, that all math students worldwide, study it at the age of 20?
Now probably you want me to tell you about that, right?
Surely you want to know what Galois did.
Galois solved a riddle for centuries and gave it a perfect, beautiful and brilliant solution.
And broke new grounds for math.
The math invented by Galois describes the behavior of the elementary particles of the Universe at present, and also describes the symmetry of the floors and walls of the Nasrid Palaces of Alhambra de Granada.
(Laughter) Yes, you'd like to hear the story, right?
If I told you, we would begin with the math developed by the Babylonians, by the Greeks, and would go along history, supporting during very hard times the mathematicians, the greatest ones ever, that failed many times where Galois succeeded.
We would stay with that scared boy during the last night of his life.
And we would also stay with the mathematicians of the last 200 years, that have gone along the path cleared by Galois.
And you would like to come with me.
And that is the power of stories, that make us dare to enter the enchanted castle for the night with the Youth Who Went Forth to Learn What Fear Was, and make us hold Vasilisa's hand and enter the house of Baba Yaga, the terrible witch.
They also make us join Sinbad, in the desert, in the sea, to see what treasures he finds in his seven journeys.
(Laughter) Stories make us want to see the end, make us join the main character, even if it gets difficult or boring.
And you won't believe me, but sometimes in math things get difficult and boring.
(Laughter) Math, sciences, are full of stories, they are the story of our discovery of the world.
I could tell you the story of Georg Cantor who stared into infinity's eyes, and went mad.
Or the story of Ramanujan, an unknown teenager, to whom the Goddess Namagiri in dreams dictated the secrets of numbers.
Or the story of the 300 years mathematicians spent trying to solve a riddle that barely fits in the margin of a book.
I would love to tell you stories!
I could spend hours here.
You would stay with me, because stories turn things that are boring and difficult, and have nothing to do with me, into things that concern me.
Why?
Because it's within ourselves.
When you are told a story you are observed, you are taken into account, and you become the main character, and the author.
We are oral beings and to create a space for stories, for expressed words, in our lives, in our homes, in our classrooms, makes us who we are.
Human beings spend millennia telling stories.
All ethnic groups, in all periods, anywhere in the world have had their narrators, their storytellers, the shamans, the bululus... Sometimes, priests were the ones who told stories about the town.
Sometimes mothers, matrons, grandmothers, told those stories, put their voices, bodies and souls, which is the same, for the stories of the town.
The topic of the story, the content, is always the same: Who are we?
How is the world we are living in?
The mission of the stories is always the same: to walk together with those who are gone, who are here, who will come, to build the community.
A community is not only built by laws and customs.
A community is also built by stories, the mythical, the legendary ones.
It's true!
It's possible that we have gone from that mythical, legendary paradigm, to the scientific comprehension paradigm we are at now, but the power of stories is still present.
For millennia, in all latitudes, in all ethnic groups, at all moments, narrators, storytellers, have developed very effective techniques to pass on the stories of the tribe.
They were told in a way to be remembered
and they were remembered. They were told in a meaningful way for each member of the tribe, and each child, adult, each elder felt those stories as their own.
They were the most essential part of the town's core, that part couldn't be missed.
Throughout history, in all the forms of human beings that inhabited the Earth telling stories has been an essential part of our being.
Nowadays, the answer to what's the world? what are we?
we call it science and culture.
And its transmission, we called it education.
And we who take on the challenge of getting involved in that transmission many times forget about the storytellers, narrators, the Greeks, the bululus, who preceded us in this sacred task.
The following day of that 30th May 1832, when the weapon of the Captain of the Guard, bullet or sword, went through the stomach of Évariste Galois, his brother crying held the dying body of that boy, a vehement, revolutionary, and somewhat crazy boy.
And Galois, Évariste, stared at him and said his last words: "Don't cry.
Don't cry and give me courage, because I need all the courage of the world to die at the age of 20."
Thank you.
(Applause)
