What do neuroscience and surrealism have in common?
Maybe today, maybe we will find out.
This is a picture of an awkward situation.
It was taken a few weeks ago and captures just that moment when one is at the supermarket and is about to pay.
A few months ago, when I began my PhD, I became obsessed with moments like this.
I was at the supermarket and standing in line for the check out, I got to the cashier, I paid, and she gave me some change. But she got it wrong, in my favour.
And it was in those three, four seconds that I stared at my hand and thought, Albert, this is your lucky day. You're going back home with 20 euros more.
And at the same time thinking, Albert, you have to tell the cashier that she has made a mistake and give the money back.
What if I ask you right now, quick, raise your hand if you would have given the money back.
Yes, of course. (Laughter) Sure, of course. Whatever.
Sometimes, it seems that to give a deep and detailed explanation of all that happens in our brain, such as these experiences, scenarios, sensations, neuroscience is not enough.
Then art can help us. Surrealist art.
Because one of the goals
of the surrealist movement was to try to explain the real functioning of the mind.
And today, we are going to combine both.
And it will be this way that we'll be able to reach really profound corners.
Sometimes, timeless perceptions drive us mad.
Those three seconds to decide whether to do right or wrong.
Ideas and concepts that seem to be suspended in mid air, that seem to bend, to yield, to melt.
They even seem to have shadows
that they project. I don't know if you've ever thought about it or not, but we are constantly facing the conflict between cultivating our own moral image, the perception we have about ourselves, as to whether we are little angels or little devils, which fluctuates a lot, is very variable, and just pursuing our own interests.
Those conversations, constant conflicts with ourselves.
It is no longer shadows that are being projected, it's the ideas and concepts themselves, the moral image we have of ourselves, the pursuit of our own interests, that project themselves with these dendrite-like lines and ramifications.
In neuroscience, in moral psychology, we've been studying how all these things work.
How do individuals deal with moral uncertainty in their daily lives?
Decide to donate part of our savings to NGOs, or decide to go to a hospital to donate blood or not, pay our taxes or to try to evade them. Let me tell you about two of the most significant examples we've discovered through experiments with people.
The first one is called 'Moral Balancing'.
Imagine your morality as a paintbrush tracing a zig-zag pattern.
I've done something good this morning, I will allow myself to do something bad this afternoon.
Thus, I get the feeling that I'm already in balance.
The other one I want to tell you about is called 'Moral Consistency'.
Imagine now your morality being traced out with a paintbrush but on a drafting triangle.
Zap, straight across.
I've done something good this morning, and to be coherent, consistent with myself, I'll do something good again this afternoon.
OK. Cool. But how does all of this happen
in our mind? How is it triggered?
How does our brain confront all these dilemmas and moral scenarios?
There's a main actor.
Like a primary color.
It is called the ethical mindset.
You can imagine it in a very visual way.
Imagine it as a paint palette.
And this palette is the one we are going to use at each moment of the decision-making process.
For example: one of these is the consequentialist palette.
This is the utilitarian one, based on outcomes.
Whether an act is or is not moral will depend on the result of that act.
Example: I'm going to keep the 20 euro bill because I've incurred an unexpected expense at home this month, and it will come in handy.
The other type of palette we use when facing a moral dilemma is the deontological one; this is the automatic, the intuitive, the emotional one.
Whether an act appears moral to me or not will depend on its conformity to a rule, a moral norm. For example, I need to tell the cashier she's made a mistake, I mustn't lie, I must be truthful, and I must be honest.
So, when facing a moral dilemma, we use one of these palettes, and this leads us to a type of stroke: a zig-zag or a straight one.
So things should go the following way: If you've done something good this morning, please remember it, and do it again in the afternoon; and if you've been a little devil this morning, please try to compensate it.
As I was saying, in neuroscience and in moral psychology we've been studying all this:
Whether the paintbrushes are always the same, whether we always paint the same strokes, whether we always use the same palettes. And as you might know, awkward situations never come singly.
This is another picture
of an awkward situation. I note that nowadays, we spend a great part of our lives in front of our laptop screens, we constantly use our smartphones and other intelligent gadgets.
We shop online, work in the cloud, our social relationships are online-based.
Even romance sometimes sustains itself through Skype.
Some even spend their spare-time in virtual reality.
Therefore, the day-to-day moral contexts we face have changed a lot.
So we have to rethink and update the way we understand morality.
Morality evolved to enable co-operation among individuals,
but this conclusion comes with an important caveat.
Try to think when we were living in small tribes, in small caves.
Morality evolved to enable co-operation within small groups.
Perhaps only in groups with few, close relationships.
We are programmed to make our genes survive and replicate in our closest environment.
To our brain, other groups don't have the importance of ours.
And that's something that as a specie, we should start trying to overcome.
Let me use the words of Professor Joshua Greene from the Department of Psychology at Harvard University
who says, "Moral behavior evolved as a biological device for putting "us" before "me", and also for putting "us" before "them"."
Maybe that's why it is so hard to give the money back to the cashier
because we don't know her,
she's not a part of our closest circle.
We are like carefully devised systems, but with elements of chance,
that follow a route that captures certain ideas, certain projections.
Some of them dilute, others want to get out of the preset contexts,
there are other daring ones that even try to go against the laws of gravity.
And the boundaries that separate some from others are shrinking and diluting.
We must prepare our moral brain for this.
We need to rethink the way we understand morality.
It's no longer enough to study it;
we have now the opportunity
to design it, to program it.
Do you remember those two ways we have to face dilemmas?
These new contexts, these new scenarios, are they going to make us more consequentialist, more utilitarian?
Or more emotional, more deontological?
What's going to happen when non-human individuals are introduced into these moral canvases?
Robots, artificial intelligence systems, self-checkouts. That'd cap it all, wouldn't it?
What's going to happen when we interact with them?
How are we going to do that?
Let me give an example: we are in hospital and there's an AI system making medical decisions. There are five patients.
Each one of them needs an organ.
If they don't get their organ transplant within the next two hours, they will die.
Suddenly, the AI system detects, using the database that it manages in real time, that a new patient has just arrived, and that his organs are compatible with the other five patients.
What do we want the program to do?
How do we want to program this AI system?
How do we want it to resolve this moral dilemma?
Do we want it to do like we human beings would do?
Do we want it to be utilitarian and compute all the survival probabilities of our new patient, and if they are relatively low, proceed and do the transplantation of these five organs?
Let one die, but save five.
Or do we want it to be emotional? Do we want to equip it with an artificial empathy system that says, 'We cannot let anyone die, even if it is to save five other people?"
Our brains are not prepared for these contexts and scenarios.
We still need to adapt to them.
Why? Why do we have to adapt to them?
The knowledge we have of how
we make decisions, of neuroscience, has provided huge advances for humanity, the understanding of who we are and what we stand for.
Adapting our decisions and behavior to the digital age will not only bring us together in the same direction
and advance us as a civilization,
but will also help us go hand-in-hand with these new and controversial dilemmas that will continue to arise.
There are some days when neuroscience seems shrouded in surrealism.
Today is one of those days.
And it raises ideas and concepts that we cannot forget,
that we must face;
like the idea of adapting our decisions and behavior to the digital age.
And what's better than surrealism for talking about morality?
The fathers of surrealism, theorists like André Breton, used to say that what was at stake was more important than painting on a canvas or writing verses.
What was at stake was the destiny of man: his fortune or downfall on Earth.
They defended surrealism as psychological automatism;
the artist trying to explain the real functioning of thought;
spontaneity in the creative process but not in the background substance,
not in the ideas they wanted to communicate.
And it's this background that we need to update.
Not only its morality, as we've seen,
but also in the way of the surrealist movement.
Long past are Freud's studies on dreams and the unconscious.
Neuroscience has evolved a lot, and we need to update.
We have to reinterpret everything.
Today, I gave you a couple of colors, a couple of palettes, a really simple paintbrush.
It was like coming on stage to create a couple of neurons,
a couple of ideas, a couple of concepts,
and in the end, I don't know if neuroscience comprises a lot of surrealism, or if surrealism comprises a lot of neuroscience.
So I would like to ask you only two things. First, that we should start to paint a new morality, together.
And second, please give the 20 euro bill back to the cashier.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
After a decade of scientific investigation about happiness in Peru and in four continents, one of the things that impresses us most is the incredible human capacity to dream, to develop strategies and invest years and decades to achieve those dreams.
Nevertheless, some of these dreams will lead to happiness and others to unhappiness, some will turn out magnificent, some are terrible ideas for society.
Which makes this a crucial question: Have you thought about what dreams are directing your life?
It is that question that, more than 20 years ago, Richard Ryan and Ed Deci from Rochester University asked regarding the American dream.
And they found that, essentially, it was having money, fame, beauty, etc.
But the few people who achieved this American dream ended up living a nightmare when it came to subjective well-being. Mental and physical health declined, and they were the people that reported being more unhappy than the ones not achieving that American dream.
This is consistent with worldwide happiness studies, which introduced a measure of subjective perception of happiness.
The New Economics Foundation might have the best-known study in this line, and we can see marked in red, [Worldwide Happiness map (NEF, 2012)] the most unhappy, miserable countries on the planet - along with Sub-Sahara Africa, we can see the self-proclaimed first world countries. And if we look at the green, yellow and mustard color, we will find that the happiest region in the world is Latin America and the Caribbean.
In that context, thanks to the financial support in the past few years of an IT Consultant in welfare and productivity, we have studied the dream that is responsible for this Latin American happiness, and it is basically family and friends.
Behind this source of happiness, there is a story that is written in the wiring of our brain, wires that have grown for thousands and millions of years.
We are not a species with wings to fly or laser rays or large saber teeth.
What we have is an extraordinary capacity to organize -
joining in families, friends, tribes.
And being inside the tribe was a matter of life or death, and being outside of your tribe, the opposite.
So our brain got wired in a virtuous cycle, still alive in our region.
When we have a problem, the stress system is activated, and a substance called cortisol runs through our nervous system, and it will knock on the door of oxytocin to ask for help from our friends and family.
When we find this help, cortisol will reduce; in other words, our stress goes down and it's going to produce opioids, meaning we will be happy.
This effect will last for weeks or a few days, but in Latin America, there will always be a new stressful problem, we will again find help with our family and friends, and we'll have a natural virtuous cycle with zero side effects that could ruin the pharmaceutical industry and drug trafficking.
(Laughter) (Applause) This wonder brain circuit is still happening in Latin America, but in the self-proclaimed developed countries, when facing a problem, you would say, "Leave me alone." They don't ask for help, and when they do ask, it will be awkward, and they won't get it.
This activates a system that generates more depression and unhappiness.
This oxytocin inherently becomes a happiness neurotransmitter with a long lasting effect and low-level side effects.
More specifically: what is the Peruvian dream?
A prosperous, educated and elegant modern family.
What does elegant mean?
That varies from segment to segment.
This is part of the research we do with the Group of Investigation, Welfare, Culture and Development of the Catholic University.
Allow me to quickly introduce our talented, driven team of basically good people, that step by step are exploring happiness and unhappiness in Peru.
Well, this idea of a prosperous, educated and elegant "cholo chic" can be an interesting abstract idea, but in reality it has a crooked side because education, when we dig deeper, is basically just having a diploma.
People are not seeking to reinforce their values or identity and acquire new skills.
Just a diploma.
So that explains, not only in Peru but rather regionally, why a horrific plagiarism problem exists: exams, full monographies, etc.
They get out of school with a diploma, unable to compete by "knowledge" but will need to find a friend or family that will help them get in, and then they will owe them a favor in the organization, and so large mafia rings are created, but the ring will cross paths sooner or later with another, and an all-against-all war will begin, crumbling the company's productivity and affecting national production.
Then, this crooked side will make corruption increase, getting money through good or bad means.
This has been corroborated with the largest study in Peru to date about identity nationwide, performed by the Integration Institute of RPP Group, with whom we participated in the analysis. And there are two identities: the respectful and educated Peruvian, not because of his knowledge, but rather because of his good manners; and the disoriented Peruvian ... a pandemic.
(Laughter) The disoriented Peruvian is nice, courteous, etc. but is not really interested in a reciprocal relationship of mutual help or respect; he's rather waiting to seize the opportunity. And when the other gets ahead, they will feel miserable because of their envy, and they will counteract this by
undermining the achievements of others -
in the ring, not as much, but also a little - and he will practice the national sport: gossip.
So, in these circumstances, we also observe that living in the provinces and having less education increases the likelihood of being respectful and reduces the possibility of being disoriented.
This has to do with an ancestral process.
So, when did Peru go to hell?
Science has several answers.
And one of those screwed-up moments of our history is when migrants from small traditional communities arrive in large cities like Lima and are welcomed with an aggressive discrimination.
This activates a phenomenon known as social exclusion.
And this social exclusion will activate the highest alert area of the brain, which is that of physical pain.
Several studies, this is one of the most recent, will tell you that when we are socially excluded, made fun of, marginalized, insulted, or called names, we will have the same responses as when in physical pain.
What does that cause?
First, it cuts the empathy system, meaning I'm not going to put myself in someone else's shoes, and consequently, I won't even greet him, nor will I be compliant, and past a certain point, I will enjoy any harm that comes to him.
So, here we observe that we have a very complicated problem in our history, and it's something retroactive because it generates a disoriented interaction, where people build new barriers to empathy, and we have a public, social, work and interpersonal dynamic away from the ring - terrible.
If this is accurate, what will happen?
The education supply will increase, as a business, not a calling, and education will be in crisis, parallel to the growing number of universities and technological institutes.
There will be an increase in consumption, we will "proudly" beat records in car imports, especially in the luxury segment, but we will be fools, clowns, more disoriented in daily-life interaction.
Now the main question is this: How do we avoid Peru from getting even more screwed?
(Laughter) In order to partly answer this question, we can seek out the places that are truly happy in our territory.
And we can begin with the great fishermen coves, little areas where touristic and industrial activity is still marginal.
There, families work together and are the core of the values of work and union.
And families are not more respected because of their wealth, their large TV, but for the quality of their work and the joint effort of the family.
Then, there's the great small traditional communities in the Andes, where besides family labor, there is an excellent communal work, where all neighbors cooperate in sowing and harvesting the plot of one member of the community and the following day another plot, and so there is a collective of families and communities working together, all participating.
Also, the amazing Amazon communities, where a mystic balance with nature is kept.
Paradoxically, these are families that when seeking for progress, for a better education for their children, will become the discriminated families.
An Amazonian that has traveled eight hours to get to school will be marginalized even by that same school.
An Andinian who gets to the city will be mocked by another who lives 400 meters closer to sea level
and will also be marginalized by his own neighbor who got there a year earlier. He will no longer be this great communal gentleman that being honest, working and respecting customs, will be respected as a citizen, but will become the filthy indigene.
This, one way or another, will cause people to find out, unfortunately, that those values, that happiness is useless, and that they have to adopt a new pattern, buying into the great con that is the ideal of a diploma and money, well-achieved or otherwise.
This makes it fundamental to reinforce Peruvian values, not Machu Picchu, not the Nazca lines, but those magnificent values from grandfathers and fathers of many of us here, and integrate these into modernity to achieve these two dreams: Keeping a good identity, but integrating into the modern world.
Which makes this an interesting question: What is the happiest place in modern Peru?
We studied large Peruvian cities, and we systematically found that one of the happiest places was the Valley of Mantaro.
And the Valley of Mantaro does not have the magnificent architecture of Cuzco or the splendor of the Andes mountains, but "Huancas," the local inhabitants.
The "Huanca" has a balance between work and fun.
They work hard but party hard too.
There is not a single day in the year where there isn't a festival in the Valley of Mantaro.
Second, it is "cholo" power - not a beaten down cholo, right? -
that tells you "Sorry sir, that you came to my ugly village."
Brother, come and have a couple of beers with me!
(Laughter) Third, those communities have a deep meritocratic sense.
Any youngster knows that with respect for traditions, aiding their family, their neighbors, working hard, and being successful, they will be recognized as good citizens.
This contrasts with the city of Cusco, one of Peru's unhappiest places, where you need to have a respectable family also.
And so, there won't be new rich people, only more new poor people.
So, all this configures the need, not the luxury or the option, the need of a national action plan, a national plan of no less than three years, even seven years, where the government, the media, the civil society, the International Cooperation, each one of us in here is involved. For what?
To rescue traditional values and integrate them into modernity.
And why is this important?
Two simple reasons: First, this is how we will achieve our personal happiness and our family happiness.
And, secondly, so we can avoid this country from being screwed.
(Applause) [Rapists, narcos and terrorists in the candidates lists]
(Applause)
I was wondering what it would be like to live in a society where the women were in charge; where they had all the power and had more and better opportunities than the men.
A society where no one could complain that education was sexist.
To find out, I went to live with the Mosuo people in Southwest China, near the Tibetan border, where a community of 25,000 inhabitants live under a matriarchal regime, the purest on Earth; a kind of feminist movement paradise.
Soon after arriving, I quickly realized that I was in a matriarchy.
The women manage everything. And when I'd approach them to ask something, they'd always raise their tone of voice a little when answering.
In a matriarchy, the mother's surname is used.
There's no "man of the house,"
but a woman of the house. Only the daughters can inherit anything; only women can own property in their name.
And if someone my age needs money, they should very politely go and ask the woman of the house for it.
When they walk, the man always goes a little behind the woman, And as always, when the woman speaks, the man shuts up.
(Laughter) But what happens in a society where women are in charge isn't what we'd expect.
Because a matriarchy is not an inverse patriarchy.
For example, there is no violence in the matriarchy, in any form. Not even violent sports exist.
No one raised in a matriarchy will respond aggressively, either inside or outside the house.
And I thought: In the West, when two men fight, they know very well what is at stake.
But if there were a woman present, she'd probably say: "Please don't fight."
That's why I believe a lack of violence indicates the feminine element in a society.
But understand - when I say "the feminine," I don't mean "women," because women and the feminine are not quite the same.
A woman can do the same as a man, but dressed as a woman.
The feminine is something else, and defining it is not so easy.
Perhaps a lack of violence is one of its features.
Just like another thing you see in every society where women rule, and that is a strong social fabric.
People take care of people and they make sure that nobody is left out.
I lived in a house which, like all houses, was run by a matriarch.
And every morning, I'd wake up to the matriarch yelling.
She'd walk from one end of the patio to the other, holding a huge bunch of keys, putting everybody to work.
Not everybody, because the women were already doing their jobs.
She would call the men, who usually sat waiting, their backs against the wall, chatting, playing, until the boss gave them the first order.
Because when women rule, women work a lot.
And when women rule, men rest a lot.
If there's one thing that interests the women in a matriarchal society, it's to be in love all their life.
Every time I'd meet a matriarch, she had someone to introduce me to, she'd show me pictures, make jokes with double meanings, and after a while I'd realize she was pulling my leg.
Sure, as they felt more comfortable with me, they treated me like a man.
I thought, "If they want to live all their lives in love, and since this is a matriarchy, everyone has to get married."
It turns out that where women rule, marriage doesn't exist.
I couldn't understand, so I asked them: "But, don't you want to marry?
If you meet someone and you like them, don't you want to start a family?"
They don't.
And I'd say, "Why not?"
And they didn't want to because they wanted to be in love all their lives.
Then they'd ask how we do it.
"Like the way I said: you meet someone,
you have children, you marry, and you live happily ever after."
They'd say, "Oh."
(Laughter)
"And does that work for you?"
"Of course!" I'd say,
"Of course not!"
I had automatically answered with a phrase that's etched in my brain, but that has nothing to do with reality.
One thing is what a person wants, and another is what really happens.
Without giving me much time to think, they asked me: "Tell me - where you live, are there many couples who are happy, passionate and in love after 25 years?"
"Tons!" I say,
"Actually, I know of one or two."
(Laughter) Soon I realized how they managed to be in love all their lives.
The family consists of the matriarch, her brothers and sisters and her sons and daughters.
And the women's children.
There are no fathers or husbands. All males live with their moms.
Women choose who they spend the night with; they spend one night with with one man, the next night, they choose another; the night after, a different one. Nobody sees this as wrong,
it's a question of culture.
And when they get pregnant, the women often don't know who the father is.
But they also don't care, because everything belongs to them.
And I had read a lot about the fall of the male figure, the weakness of the father, the disaster it brings to society.
Unfortunately, nothing happens.
(Laughter) Nothing happens because a father and his role are two different things.
And in fact, in a matriarchal society, people have limits; they're educated, they raise families, they study and work -
mostly the women. The men rest a lot.
So I couldn't understand why they approached me a couple of times proposing I have their children.
I said, "No, I'm not going to have children with you."
Plus, I had to say this to the translator, so things got kind of complicated.
(Laughter) She says, "Why won't you have children with me?"
"Why? Because I don't want to have kids
in China. When would I see them?"
She says, "Why would you want to see them?"
(Laughter) Then, of course, with so much social life, at some point they fall in love,
and one thing leads to another. The woman receives only that man, and that man only goes to visit that woman.
But they will never want to move in together, they will never want to have common goals, they will never want to raise a family.
The only reason they want to be together is because they love each other.
And when love ends, the relationship ends.
No couple stays together for the kids, or the family or society.
It's strange, what they do: they stay together out of love.
(Laughter) So ... (Applause) And I asked the women: "How do you know when you're in love?
Because you have to pay close attention."
And they'd tell me: "I know I'm in love if, after a night of passion, I realize that I can actually talk with the guy."
(Laughter) (Applause) And if you think about it, you don't have to be in love to get married or to have children. To have a family - even less so. And to live your life with someone: definitely you don't need to.
But to talk together the way those who get along talk, for that - yes, it is necessary.
But don't be fooled - though it may sound like they have very dynamic emotional lives, they're actually way more traditional than we are. The family is the family, and they would never, ever jeopardize that by going to live with a member of another family.
And that's what we call marriage.
One thing that caught my eye is courtship.
They have different ways of deciding who will spend the night with whom, but the most important one is when they dance around a fire.
The first time I saw the women come in, I was confused.
These women who were so strong, who had everybody following orders, they would come in, looking at floor, singing softly, very gently.
And the men approached them differently now, of course.
The women know that for a man, it's very difficult to approach such a strong woman.
So in that moment, the women change.
They help the men out. The women make the men believe - for a while - that they are in charge.
(Laughter) (Applause) I was a little worried about men.
I thought, "So much submission, this is going to blow up at some point."
But it turns out that the men are the main advocates of the matriarchy.
When women are in charge, men have a great time: they barely work, they have very few responsibilities, they change women every night, no one complains to them, and they live with their moms all their lives.
(Laughter) (Applause) The first time I was invited to dinner I thought: I'm in a matriarchy, so as soon as I'm done eating, I have to go do the dishes.
But it turns out they wouldn't let me, and I couldn't figure out why.
Then I figured it out.
When women have all the power and are very sure of their status, they can perform some of the historically feminine tasks that they take pleasure in doing, like making dinner, without it undermining their position in the household.
When I returned from China I realized two things: The first is that a matriarchy is a great place to stop being sure of what we are sure of.
The second is that I had to write about it. And that was my first book, it was called "The Kingdom of Women."
From then on, I always go wherever I need to go in order to write.
That's why I went to the Vilcabamba Valley, where people live to be 100, 110, 120 years old, smoking, drinking alcohol, eating fatty foods.
A great place for science to observe a way to grow old that's better than the system we have here, which just becomes the dragged-out job of our children.
Or when I wanted to know what infidelity - her own - meant to a woman, I traveled to the Himalayas, where women are officially married to four husbands.
Or when I wanted to know what men who have lots of women don't talk about, I spoke with modern Zulu, with fundamentalist Mormons and with a few Argentinians.
(Laughter) But if I were to go to a distant, foreign place, just to tell how strange those people are, it would be like going to a people zoo.
But I'm much more interested when the experience makes me realize how strange I am. It's much more interesting
when a well posed question or a phrase that blows my mind makes me realize that ideas that are so strong in me, ideas that guide my life, can falter in an instant.
Because we think that the world we live in is highly consistent.
But it's not.
Hence, the surprises.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Well, I am here to tell you that the future is already here.
You know that, in our planet, we are a little over 7 billion people living here, almost half of which are boys and girls under 18 years old.
Never in our history has there been as many young people as there are now.
That's why, when we say phrases like "children are the future" or "the children of today will be the great men of tomorrow," we are not lying, but we are telling a half-truth, because, by saying this, we're denying children the possibility and the opportunity of being considered whole persons here and now, in the present.
I've been dedicating my life to the cause of childhood for almost 15 years, through Petisos, an organization I founded here in Bariloche, where, with a group of wonderful people, we work every day to make sure the rights of all children are fulfilled.
During this time, we have experienced horrible situations, such as children under child labor exploitation, mistreatment, neglect, sexual abuse... But I would be lying if I told you we have only dealt with these situations.
I would be lying if I didn't tell you we're constantly amazed to find, in every boy and every girl, tremendous strength, love and energy that make them overcome even the most difficult circumstances.
And this is what I want to talk to you about in four short lessons, what I've learned during these past 15 years, from "the teaching children" with whom I have been lucky enough to share my life. I include my son and also my husband's childish side.
(Laughter) OK. The first lesson is this: "All children are capable of reading their context and transforming it."
This is a little town in Mexico called Ayotzinapa.
Ayotzinapa appeared in the global picture almost a year ago because the Mexican army kidnapped, and killed 43 teacher-training students; Forty-three students who were protesting pacifically asking for better conditions in education.
Of course this brought about many demonstrations against this, asking for justice, not only in Mexico but around the world.
What was special about this?
What was special was that, very quickly, at a very young age, boys and girls, became extremely strong advocates in the protests.
Through social media, primary students grouped together, boys and girls between 6 and 12 years old, to come up with one of the most important mottos in the global action plan in favor of Ayotzinapa.
This motto is the need to raise awareness and educate the Mexican government about the importance of not killing teachers. And it was generated by boys and girls of between 6 and 12 years old.
"All children are capable of reading their environment and taking action to transform it."
The second lesson is very, very simple and we forget it quite often.
The second lesson is that "All children need an adult."
It doesn't matter if children can use cell phones, tablets or computers better than we can; it doesn't matter.
A look like this one... like the one Patito is giving his son, Gonza, can't be found in any YouTube tutorial.
We, adults, must always be with children. Not ahead of them, not behind them, but next to them, as peers, as asymmetrical peers, giving them looks of love, of care, of support, of boundaries.
Children grow up better when they're with an adult who loves them; let's not forget that.
We learned the third lesson laughing our hearts out and with enjoyment.
The third lesson says: "It's never too early to find your passion."
If we look up on the Internet the word "passion," if we enter the word "passion," we'll get many results. Many of them will lead us to TED talks telling us how to find your passion, how not to lose your passion, how to re-encounter your passion, how it's super, super important that you follow your passion because, otherwise, you'll be very unhappy.
When we asked children what "passion" meant to them, we got many definitions and --
Passion is something that they do and never get tired of doing.
It's what they like doing most in life.
And when they get tired, they want to start over again. Children live in a sort of permanent state of passion.
Seven years ago, we came with the children from Petisos to a concert here, at the Llao Llao hotel.
One of the kids that came with us is named Brian and he becomes restless quite easily, so we were a little concerned that he wouldn't be able to stay seated listening to the classical music concert.
We sat him down in his place and surrounded him, just in case.
As soon as lights dimmed down, as we watched the baton, and heard the first notes of a violin, Brian became motionless; it looked as if he'd been glued to the chair. For 40 minutes, Brian did not breathe, he sighed, (Sighing) we only heard something like this.
When the concert was over, all of his schoolmates started running through the hallway along this super fluffy carpet that makes you wish you were barefooted. Brian took my hand and said, "Miss, did you see how lovely it feels [your chest] when the violin plays?"
For two years, the next two years, Brian was an excellent violinist. Brian found his passion, and didn't want to do anything but play the violin.
This was useful for us and his grandmother, with whom he lived, because we were able to negotiate that he had to do better at school if he wanted to keep playing the violin.
But Brian's story isn't over. Brian's story taught us, as an organization, a great lesson.
It taught us how children see their lives transformed by music.
And, that's why, four years ago, when we opened a Petisos branch in Buenos Aires, one of the first things we decided to do was form an orchestra, and here I couldn't help but show this photo.
This photo is from last Sunday at the Luna Park. The Petisos' "Vamos los Pibes" orchestra, based in Villa Crespo, was the opening show for Dancing Mood.
They say that Brian's sighs were heard in the Luna Park.
But, sometimes, passions are not only useful for filling our souls and being happy ourselves. Sometimes passions are useful for solving other people's lives, or helping them; and that's not an adults' privilege -- Children also have this kind of passions.
Almost three years ago, Christian met a little girl who was undergoing cancer treatment and saw that she was hairless. He'd never seen a hairless girl.
He asked his parents what was going on, what that was about; and his parents explained to him what was going on, that she was sick, that she had to undergo treatment and that the treatment made her hair fall out.
For some time, Christian investigated, both at school and with his family, what he could do to help this little girl, and he found an organization that received hair donations.
There were two conditions: One, it had to be kid's hair because it was for children, and the second was it had to be at least 20 centimeters long.
So, Christian said, "OK, I'll donate my hair to this organization."
But letting your hair grow 20 centimeters isn't easy. It took him a long time.
For the following two years, Christian endured the mockery of his friends telling him he looked like a girl, the pressure from the adults, such as his teachers, his own family, who told him that it was enough, that if he wanted, his family, the adults, would donate money so that this organization could keep working.
Christian did not lose his determination, he wanted to let his hair grow.
Two years later, his hair was 20 centimeters long.
How old do you think Christian was?
Audience: 11, 10.
Well, Christian was six when he made this decision.
Between his six and eight, he lived under a huge pressure, both from his schoolmates and adults.
I asked Diana, his mom, how she felt when she realized that he was being pressured so much at school, or by his own relatives, who told him it was enough after six months or a year. And she told me that she'd never seen her son so happy and that she didn't think that, being such a young child, he could have such strong convictions.
Christian just turned 9 recently and he's already thinking about a new cause to join.
Well, the next and last lesson is this: "Boys, girls and teenagers are experts on their own lives and that knowledge can help improve the adult world."
A few years ago, a little more than that, there were some severe floods in an area in India,
which forced their population to evacuate to another area.
The people who were waiting for the evacuees noticed that there was something strange going on.
First, families arrived with their children, then, arrived children alone, and after that, some adults arrived.
When they asked what had happened -- Because, in fact, the data indicated just the opposite, that is, families with children in the middle of a flood, being evacuated, may have fewer chances to arrive safely because they had to watch over the children.
But what had happened?
The children were the ones who guided their parents.
Why? Because the children
from that village went to school to the other village every day.
The path they had to take was the path where they always played.
The children knew the land by heart, like a human GPS; they knew where the ditches were, they knew where the hollows were, and they guided their relatives, even against what the maps showed.
The children's knowledge and expertise in their daily life enabled them to save the adults.
And the last example: Gonza is a guy from Bariloche, who uses public transport.
When he was a kid, he went to state school 321 and he lived really nearby, so his mom or his grandfather would take him to school.
Then, when he moved to state school CEM 2, he started to go by bus and became a public transport user.
Later on, he went to Comahue University. And, not too long ago, something that had a huge impact on Gonza happened.
A girl, a high school student who had been studying -- Had been working on a teamwork, went to take the bus,
waited for 30 minutes; the bus didn't come.
Unfortunately, a man attacked her and, 10 minutes later, the bus came.
Gonza told me: "Elena, ten minutes would have changed this girl's life, what happened is so shocking!"
So, he started to generate a network of, let's say, contacts and information about what were the bus stops that were more abandoned, more isolated, which were the bus hours.
And Gonza created an application called BariBus.
It's an application that anyone can download and if we live half an hour away from a bus stop, we check, we make it, fine; but if we live -- I mean, we can leave at the precise time, we don't have to wait for half an hour at the bus stop.
This was invented by Gonzalo. It has over 10,000 downloads.
Gonzalo's expert knowledge as a public bus user is helping improve life in Bariloche.
And I wondered, when I was preparing this talk, I wondered if Brian, Gonza, the kids from Villa Crespo "Vamos los pibes," the kids from Mexico, Christian -- Are they the future of humankind?
Of course they are! Without a doubt.
They're the future of mankind.
Will they be the men and women of tomorrow?
I don't know. If they want to,
I think they will, but it doesn't matter, It doesn't matter, because they're the great children and teenagers of today. This is why childhood is today.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
On the 11th of July I woke up with the image of Bantu, the 24 year old gorilla that lived in Chapultepec Zoo; literally decapitated.
The news transcended the front pages of national and international newspapers as only a few days earlier the zoo had announced that Bantu would be transferred to Guadalajara Zoo to try and mate him with Chencha or Faustina.
The transfer never happened, they gave Bantu two doses when it should have been one, according to international protocols, of four drugs.
One of those drugs was medetomidine, a veterinary drug prescribed by national legislation to administer in cases where the aim is to provide a wild animal or pet with a dignified death.
It is regulated in the NOM 033 in the section on handling prior to death.
Bantu's case leaves an important question: Can zoos protect the species they keep in captivity?
Moreover, do all zoos protect?
For those who are not aware, zoos started as collections of animals that were deemed fascinating by those who exhibited them.
In the 18th century human beings were also fascinating creatures.
Aztec emperor Moctezuma Xocoyotzin had in his collection midgets, albinos and hunchbacks, while colonial and white Europeans exhibited blacks and Indigenous.
It no longer happens because we understood as time passed, that there is racial prejudice in having a person incarcerated without having committed a crime.
Nevertheless, we continue to capture and exhibit other species.
Elephants, polar bears, pandas, tigres, lions, giraffes and gorillas, are still fascinating animals to us and zoos put them at the public's disposal to see them.
We use a lot of resources keeping exotic animals we wouldn't be able to see freely in our country.
For years we thought that keeping them in this way would protect them from extinction.
But... does it?
I mean, a modern zoo is meant to be a place for wildlife conservation.
This implies that the objective for Bantu to be in the zoo was to positively impact those of his own species who live free in their natural habitat.
The very World Association of Zoos and Aquariums acknowledges that keeping species under human care is not enough.
The challenge is to protect their ecosystems.
This means that even if Bantu had mated successfully, which turned out to be impossible - based on post mortem reports that indicated he was sterile-, none of his offsprings would have ended up in Africa.
I'll say it differently: Our North American neighbors declared in 2011 that their gorilla population is demographically sustainable, meaning that there is genetic diversity in their captive gorilla population.
This sounds good and is fine, but there are no plans to free said population.
In fact, no zoo frees gorillas back to their natural habitat. What I am saying contradicts popular belief that a modern zoo preserves species.
What I am saying is that zoos carry out mere housing and housing live animals in cages is not preservation of the species.
Bantu was born, grew up and died in a cage without preserving his species
Do you remember this saying from Animal Farm?
"All animals are equal, but some are more equal than others"
Its meaning was clear to me when on one occasion I asked a group of students to look at these two pictures and describe them.
Colour determined preference for one rat and fear towards the other.
They thought one was disgusting and the other sweet.
I think something similar happens when we visit a zoo.
What I mean is I don't know anyone that wakes up one day anxious to get to the zoo to see the porcupine
Yet I know many who bypass it in the zoo to get to the Panda bear.
If we observe animal distribution in Chapultepec zoo, we will soon notice that it's built based on these preferences
The more popular animals have the biggest spaces, they are at the centre and in view of the zoo management.
Polar bears at the end, elephants centre right, Panda bears centre left, the gorilla at the end of the route.
Modern zoos boast they educate people about endangered species to promote wildlife conservation.
Instead, they base their infrastructure on placing exotic animals in strategic places.
One could think that despite Bantu not having much impact in conservation terms he had it in educational terms.
The thing is that Mexicans don't come across gorillas on the streets or jungles in our day to day.
So what we learned about Bantu has no practical implications.
Whereas we are not able to recognise this for the animals we do see day to day.
Possums for example are the only Mexican marsupials. They live in the city and people step on them thinking they are rats, a plague, when they are in fact at risk of extinction.
Following the captivity logic that maintains that education and conservation must be joint and focused on endangered species, would it not be more useful for Chapultepec zoo to have possums instead of gorillas?
I ask because possums don't even have a space in this zoo, which happens to be the largest in the country.
I'll ask it differently: Why do we lock gorillas up that we will never free?
Why do we ignore possums with whom we cohabit?
Do we discriminate against possums because they aren't as big or as charismatic as gorillas?
Why aren't we fascinated by possums?
We discriminate against animals not only due to their species, but also due to their behaviour.
I don't know anyone that has issue with lions eating meat.
We know it's normal considering they are carnivores.
But I do know people, particularly parents, who would not feel very comfortable knowing they'd arrive to the zoo to witness how lions obtain their meat.
Some would leave horrified and would consider it cruelty that lions hunted live pray in the zoo.
I know this because something similar happened in Denmark two years ago when Copenhagen zoo killed Marius the giraffe, it quartered it in front of kids and gave it to the lions to eat.
Th international news were classified as totally unnecessary cruelty, as there had been a petition with over 20,000 signatures asking the zoo not to kill Marius.
I mean, there are things we like about animals and there are things we'd rather ignore.
Lions, whose nature is to hunt and devour prey immediately have been reduced to mere scavengers.
We give them meat in pieces, from animals that were butchered in advance.
Just like the lions in Denmark which devoured Marius already in pieces.
Is it becoming clear that something is not as it should?
Animals don't behave as they would naturally and species which require attention don't get it.
The organisation I preside, Big Ape Project, has a clear proposition: Relocate exotic animals to where they will have a better quality of life.
Starting with Toto, the surviving orangutan in Chapultepec zoo who could go all expenses paid to a sanctuary in Brazil belonging to the organisation.
The other option is that he goes to Indonesia where his species is from, with the primatologist Birute Galdikas who came to visit him in April and could take him to Tanjung Puting national park, a reserve she has protected for 25 years.
This way we free up space and resources to focus on the endemic fauna that must be preserved in our country, propelling its release to its natural habitat and its conservation in an enclosure-free environment.
How?
Establishing more protected natural areas.
The proposal has already been taken to the Environment Secretary, who said at the time that since last year zoos in the capitals no longer buy animals to fill empty cages.
This ended up being a lie, it would have only been the first step in the right direction.
The functions of a modern zoo are, supposedly, conservation and education.
But the biggest zoo in the country does not comply to this.
Chapultepec is still seen as a circus.
Its own management confirmed they sought to bring two Chinese Pandas using tax payers money.
Have we not learnt from our mistakes?
What's left from Bantu's experience?
Why do we remove two Pandas from their natural habitat I all they are going to do is fill a glass window?
Let's be clear, if species were truly preserved there would be no need for zoos.
Instead, these places have existed for centuries and what's worse, it was in this century that we triggered the sixth mass extinction.
What are we doing?
I refuse to be a part of it.
A zoo that complied with its true function would by definition have an expiry date.
This idea is so powerful that I've been threatened to death for saying it out loud.
And though I still don't know who's behind such a dirty intimidation strategy I know that the people will know what decision to make.
I believe with the information at hand and common sense each person listening will form a view.
I believe if these points are truly understood, society will logically see that the closure of zoos is not only imminent, but desirable.
We know we can help animals, we know we can preserve species.
Yes we have been capable of big mistakes let's take responsibility and build solutions.
If we can design buildings to contain animals we can design plans to release them.
But to open a cage, we need to first open our minds.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I believe that if someone makes a big effort they can reach any target they have set for more diverse that those might be.
There has to be a great effort as a nexus in common because it's a lie that urban legend that says Jack of all trades, master of none.
I believe that if we make an effort and we dedicate ourselves, we can be more or less successful on the areas which we want to develop.
It is true that effort, dedication, discipline, constancy, are essential ingredients for us to reach our goals. But what is also true is that those aint going to be enough if we dont have a bit of luck.
But luck in that way not in a simple random chance
in the way that we know how to take advantage of all the resources we've got so we can reach success.
So now, what's success?
Is success popular recognition?
Is that success?
In my particular opinion I consider success isn't more than the daily sum of all effort we are doing everyday when we get up in the morning, and in the job we have to accomplish from any field.
I consider that is success.
When we get up everyday we set a goal and if we reach it we are being successful.
But, of course, it takes a path.
And as in every path maybe the hardest thing to do is to give that first step.
Take off?
Then we have to keep a constant effort.
Sometimes there are going to be some pitfalls on the way and we will have to make an extra effort. And maybe even a screw turn so we can reach our first objective and get to our first goal.
In that time, we also have to give ourselves the luxury to enjoy the fruits of that first effort. And why not take a break for a moment to appreciate the view, what we have got, we have reached and what we have yet to go and maybe even changing the perspective of the observation. Because we might even observe other edges of the path we have traveled we might even set new goals such as extend our hand to the one that wants to start walking the path of effort on its very own first steps.
Holding them, giving them impulse, support, trust so they can create, and even confidence so that later on they keep going on this path and we keep going on ours of course, with more effort, to our goals.
And of course, once we reach our goal the hardest part isn't getting there but staying there, again, more effort.
Maybe here, on the main goal is when we have to place all our capacities so we can stay on this goal we have reached with a lot of effort.
Even, if effort isn't enough and yet, we fall -- as much as rowdy as the fall might have been we have to know that we possess all the resources to hold on the impact, and avoiding the fall.
And then, of course, with more effort starting all over again that path to our goals.
Because remember, if you want different results do not do always the same. Sometimes we have to encourage ourselves to make different choices... and maybe even keep balanced going through the path of effort on a traditional way, and on an innovative way and giving us the luxury to enjoy the fruits of our effort.
What do you think if this time we try it at home?
Thank you very much!
(Applause)
Once upon a time, a beautiful princess fell under the sleeping curse of a vengeful witch.
Everybody knows how this tale ends: the dashing prince manages to find the sleeping princess wakes her up with a kiss, and naturally, the princess was immediately swept off her feet.
But the tales are just that; and at the other end of these imaginary stories we find the products of science.
Tales are the realm of fantasy, and science is the realm of facts.
But the tale of the sleeping beauty got in inadvertently into the scientific representation of sexual fertilization: the female ovum lays inert, until the most intrepid and fast of the spermatozoa that woos it gets there first, breaks into its wall, and activates it thus starting a new life.
What this projection of cultural ideas about males and females did was to obstruct the research about the active mechanisms that the female ovum has in capturing spermatozoa, and the dissemination processes that the spermatozoon suffers once inside the female genital tract.
Currently, modern female ova, like the latest Disney princesses, have their own initiative and are fully awake.
Science provides us with the most reliable knowledge that we have so far, that we humans have developed about the world we inhabit,
but it cannot offer simple and ultimate truths.
A good example of this is provided by primatology.
To discover the lives and worlds of these our near relatives: orangutans, baboons, bonobos, and chimpanzees, is a fascinating task, but discovering their world was not the only objective.
Because often times, what was sought when studying primates, was to find keys to understand the behavior of the first hominids, a primate model that would help us better know ourselves; and that's why Louis Leakey, the famous paleoanthropologist, in the mid-20th century, promoted primate research.
Leakey also thought that women would be the best primatologists because we have the biggest capacity for empathy.
Well, I'm not sure if Leaky was right or not,
but the women he recruited: Jane Goodall, Biruté Galdikas, Dian Fossey, with their example, set through National Geographic documentaries, became role models and served as an example for many young women with their awakening vocation for primatology and followed their steps.
That's why primatology is a very peculiar science, it's a field where we find many women, more than in any other scientific disciplines, where usually, it's very difficult to find great female scientists, relevant women names other than the omnipresent Marie Curie.
When these women go to the jungles, the savannas, the mountains, and the forests to observe primates, the image we had about our close relatives was very similar to the stereotyped fairy tale characters.
Males were Tarzans that gathered food for the group, defended it from external attacks, and competed among them for the females.
Females were basically maternal creatures dedicated body and soul to breeding, and sexually available for the males.
When women go to the jungles and the savannas offer us a much more complex image of females, of males, and the relationships between them, than these stereotypes of valiant princes and helpless princesses of fairy tales; and when the image that we have about primates turns into a more complex one it also gradually makes less sense the idea of looking at them like in a mirror.
Because now, every individual, every primate species, including the human species, is unique and has its own value.
Let's take chimpanzees, for example.
Jane Goodall not only discovered that chimpanzees as a species were able to use instruments, like rocks to open nuts, or twigs to insert them in a termite mound and eat the termites, or that they were able to share a culture.
She also revealed that the aggressive behaviors of males did not really mean that they were really the ones in charge because, for example, female chimpanzee Flo, in a subtler way, was able to have a relevant role in the group's social organization and she also transmitted to her daughters that high status that she had in the Gombe society.
Jane Goodall was able to see what others hadn't seen before because she understood that every member of the group was an interesting individual by itself, that females were not just another asset in a male-ruled society.
Besides, as she extended in time the field work, Goodall and her successors, the primatologists that worked with her, offered us a much more accurate description of female behavior.
Females now hunted, searched actively for sexual partners, fought to keep their place in the group hierarchy, they were even able to commit infanticide with the offspring of other females.
When we only see what is obvious, we can often miss what is really interesting.
This is what happened to the first primatologists when they went to the savannas to observe baboons: what they saw in the foreground were the fights and the duels of the males and in the midst of a Cold War world, they elaborated a story, that told that baboon's lives depended on the hierarchical structure of the males.
These males were terribly aggressive individuals, that contended fiercely among them for the females, but they also were capable of behaving like a very disciplined troop when it was needed to protect the group against an external enemy.
But when the primatologist Thelma Rowell went to the savanna what she saw had little to do with this militarized image.
She saw that males were not so aggressive, that females were not sitting just waiting for their Prince Charming, that were the relationships between the females what really structured the group's organization, and that besides they were totally busy getting food for their offspring, or cultivating friendships that were going to be more useful to help their children in the future.
The military model of the baboons was dismantled little by little, and other primatologists: Jean Altmann, Shirley Strum, Barbara Smuts, also contributed to dismantle very deep-rooted ideas.
For example, the idea that dominant males have priority access to females and so, there are more offspring in the group that are their children. What they discovered is
that the male bragging the most was not the one seducing the most, and that females appreciated discretion a lot when choosing with whom to mate.
Building this image about baboons required looking in the background, what occurred beyond the noisy brawls of males.
Jean Altmann did this by elaborating systematic observation protocols, that guaranteed that each individual and member of the group was observed, and not only the most noisier ones or the ones standing out the most.
Precisely the sexual life of primates is a great example of the force that beliefs have to direct our observations and our interpretation of them.
For a lot of time the traditional idea was that sexual initiative was only a privilege of males and it was doubted, debated, if primate females enjoyed sex, and even if they had orgasms; when it was never debated if males had orgasms.
But the work of Amy Parish with the bonobos, or the work of Sarah Hrdy with langurs, shows us that females are individuals that actively look for sex, and that for them, sex is not only important for its role in reproduction.
According to Sarah Hrdy sex for primates is also pleasure and strategy.
Sarah Hrdy maintains that the pleasure that the orgasm provides to female primates encourages them to have relationships with many males.
The advantage of this is preserving their young, because males often do kill offspring that they haven't fathered to induce females that are breast feeding again into heat.
In this way offspring are kept somewhat safe.
This image of female primates as sexually active individuals is at odds with the traditional image that we had about why we female humans have orgasms.
What was commonly said was that human females had orgasms because this reinforces the monogamous ties of the couple.
In that way, we women offer men unlimited access to sex and in return, they help us care for our children.
Then, Sarah Hrdy what does is to turn around this traditional tale and the females that were passive, were faithful, were turned into assertive promiscuous females.
What we think we are and what we think we should be, in these adaptation tales, seems to be tied by the strength that evolution has to define us.
These transformations that female primatologists inserted into scientific methods and theories, show us that the viewpoint and perspective are really important.
What they do with their partial perspective is show us that the dominant perspective that had been the male's is also a partial one.
This also happens, for example, in paleoanthropology, where for a long time, the dominant hypothesis was that of hunter: the idea that humans are what we are because the men of the past went hunting, and that's why we have developed language, tool use, even bipedalism.
Adrienne Zihlman, Nancy Tanner, Sally Slocum showed the bias of the hunting hypothesis proposing the hypothesis of the harvesting woman.
They say: "No, we are not what we are because the men of the past went hunting, we are what we are because the women of the past reared and harvested."
This theory was well supported by the empirical evidence, but was not a even little bit accepted by the scientific community, because its evident bias was too obvious.
But what this evident bias did was to show that the perspective that had been dominant was also a partial perspective, but of a partiality that had been hidden and invisible.
In archeology, psychology, anthropology, sociology, in all those disciplines, we find examples through which female scientists show us that gender stereotypes had a big force to define what we accept as true.
There are also other disciplines, such as medicine, where this gender blindness is shown. The idea that the male is the universal individual of the human race had hampered for a long time research into specificities of cardiovascular disease in women, excluded from clinical trials because we are unstable subjects; all the knowledge of diagnostic and treatment of coronary disease, was built on data gathered from men.
When epidemiology what it suggests is that the coronary disease is a very important cause of death in women. Although maybe it's more adequate to say that the cause of death of many of them was ignorance about the specific symptomatology, about the risk factors, or about the efficacy, and safety of drugs when treating women.
Ultimately, what is decided to be ignored is as important as what is decided to be investigated in science.
And these decisions that male and female scientists take are conditioned by their own partial situations, that condition the questions they ask, and the methods that are used, and the data that are considered relevant, and the questions that are acceptable and are accepted.
We tend to see and hear what best matches with what we believe to know or what we in fact know.
And because of that, these examples show us that perspective and viewpoint are very important in science.
In any case, women's perspective is not more objective than the perspective of men, but it does contribute to a greater objectivity showing what was hidden, in this case, that the male viewpoint is a partial one.
Perhaps, the most important value for scientific objectivity would be that of plurality.
Because a more plural science, is a science that is better prepared to identify blind spots, induced by a unique dominant perspective that is partial.
A more plural science then, will be a more robust science; because as Richard Levins said: "The truths we accept are the intersection of independent lies."
(Applause)
Let's imagine that we can arrange all the traditional music of the world, and create an enormous landscape in front of us.
A landscape of sounds coming from different cultures and different ages.
And out of this enormous landscape we select a fragment of traditional music from Jakasia, in Russia, recorded in the 70s. (Music) Antropoloops developed from a very simple idea: the possibility to mix this very fragment now playing with another one from the 80s by a Congolese musician, in a way that without modifying the original rhythms of these bits they can suddenly fit.
(Music) And then we could add for example a drum recorded in Yemen in the 60s and a traditional song performed by five Galician women recorded by Alan Lomax in the 50s.
(Music) All these fragments playing together, form something which is new and old at the same time.
All this musicians, expressing themselves from different cultures and ages, create a link that didn't exist before and for me this is a celebration of the deep musical feeling the unites us all, despite our differences.
Antropoloops emerges as a way to work with this idea, creating connections that did not exist before and using the digital and sampling tools to create songs with fragments from every part of the world.
(Music) Since we started with the project three years ago, we were very interested in the relation between data visualization and remix.
We found very important to visualize the origin of all fragments and therefore, to make the remix process more transparent and more visible.
What you are seeing now is a first version of an open source app developed in Processing by Esperanza Moreno, that allows to visualize in real time how the fragments are mixed by the software I use, and therefore, to show the original covers, the origin of every fragment, the date of recording, the mp3 metadata as well as the volume at which they are mixed and the duration of each fragment.
(Music) These are some covers of the original records remixed in the song we are now listening to.
When a new song is taking shape, I try to do a new cover starting from fragments of the original covers.
It is always very funny and very gratifying because the musical remix process reflects itself in the visual too and it also allows to tell stories.
Like this song, which uses fragments from African music or this other one, where fabulous Aunt Molly Jackson, an American activist from the beginning of the century, tells how her neighbors taught her to become a witch.
(Music) This music, where does it come from?
This project would be impossible without the work made by many, many amateurs and collectors all around the world who digitize and share old vinyl records often discontinued and without commercial interest.
And who make all this richness accessible in their blogs.
As well as the work of institutions such as the French Center for the Research in Ethnomusicology. Or the Alan Lomax Foundation that owns many tapes of traditional music and makes them accessible on the Internet.
(Music) All this process of remix, this creation of something new, is shared in Antropoloops web page
where every new song created is shared showing the information of each fragment and most of all showing where I have found that music.
This way, any new song made by all these loops, is also a door that allows further listening and going deep in these original records.
With this reverse process, of giving back, we go back to that initial landscape which is the true origin of all this.
(Music) (Applause)
In 2015, I set a goal to go to the gym every day.
After I got used to it, I started feeling really good. I went every day,
until one day, when I had a bit too much work, and I started going less often.
At one point, I stopped going for so long that I was even embarrassed to go back.
I start every year with this same phrase: "This year, I will go to the gym."
For some reason, I still haven't done it.
And it's not just going to the gym.
At 12 years old, I started learning how to play the guitar.
I was very disciplined for the first few months. But then, hanging out with friends, etc... I left my guitar under the bed for a while, to pick it back up in the near future.
And three years later, well, let's just say guitar is not my thing.
After these failures, I started to worry.
But after finding out that this happens to 92% of the population, that they don't reach their personal goals, I started to feel much better.
I learned that my problem has a name: it's called procrastination.
Any procrastinators in the room?
Yes, quite a lot!
(Laughter) Procrastination is the act of postponing activities that should be tended to, and replacing them with enjoyable activities.
If you've got a job to do, but first, you'd rather watch the latest episodes of your favorite show, you're procrastinating.
If you use social media all day, that counts, too.
I wanted to find out more about procrastination, and I started to investigate.
In the last 18 months, I've read over 100 articles and books, interviews with hundreds of procrastinators.
I read all of - well no, sorry... I checked out all the procrastination apps in the app store and tried them.
I even talked to psychologists.
After researching it, I realized there was no simple solution to stop procrastinating.
But that same year, I would have a weekly call with my friend, Pierre, where we would talk about our plans for the week.
These calls were always very informal.
But since I didn't want to tell my friend that I had done nothing during the week, I started to get my act together.
The simple fact of knowing that my friend would find out about my lack of productivity helped me to stop procrastinating.
After a few months, I saw the impact that these conversations were having, and I thought that a lot of other people could benefit from the practice.
I learned this concept has a name, too: "accountability buddy" in English.
An accountability buddy is like a personal support, someone who checks on your tasks.
You can share your goals with this person, and they keep you motivated.
It's still a rather unknown concept.
In fact, there's no Spanish word for it.
I wanted to know if this concept of an accountability buddy could be useful to more people,
and if what I'd done with Pierre could be easily replicated.
So we invited our friend Quentin to join our calls.
And in turn, he called his friends.
Julie, Thomas, and many more joined, until we started a website, where anybody could get an accountability buddy and begin to achieve their goals.
In total, more than 80 people joined us.
There was Julian, who tripled his number of followers in only six months;
Amelia, who got an internship in 13 days;
and Antonio, who learned a new coding language and built his first interactive website.
Not everybody achieved their goals, but the majority said that they never thought that something so simple could have such a big impact on their productivity.
Personally, I still procrastinate occasionally.
I procrastinated preparing for this talk.
But this year has been the first one in my life where I have achieved most of the goals that I'd set.
So, how can you get an accountability buddy?
The first step is choosing who it'll be.
It can be a friend, family member, partner or even the stranger sitting next to you.
This person has to understand the importance of your goal and has to accept being your accountability buddy, obviously.
The first message you send can be very simple.
You can send something like:
"Hi, I'm currently trying to reach a personal goal that's very important in my life, and I'd like to discuss it with you. Could I call you later?"
You can copy the message if you want. (Laughter)
Second, each person defines their responsibilities:
How often will you call each other?
What's the goal's deadline?
Is there a reward for the accountability buddy if you reach your goal?
Those are some of the questions to ask.
Out of the 80 people who participated in our program, the most successful ones were those who received more support from their accountability buddy.
That's why the accountability buddy should always consider themselves responsible for both the failure and success of the other person.
And if they forget to call one week, the accountability buddy is always responsible.
Third, you define the steps to reach your goal.
Which tasks will you focus on during this first week to move forward with your goal?
And when will the second call be, already part of the program, so that your accountability buddy can review what you did until then?
With these three steps, now you know how to get an accountability buddy.
There are still a few minutes left before the next talk, so the best moment to find your accountability buddy is now.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I want to begin by telling you a story that fascinates me as a psychologist.
In 1972, the White House was afraid about an invasion that could take place on American soil.
Not an invasion of communists, like in that Chuck Norris movie of the 80's, but an invasion of junkies.
And those junkies were the US soldiers deployed to Vietnam.
Why?
It seemed that drug use by the US Army in Vietnam reached such enormous levels that it became part of their life and culture, like rock and roll or shooting at poor people.
US soldiers, before being deployed to Vietnam, showed an almost non-existent consumption pattern: less than 1 percent were addicted or used substances regularly.
Most of them had never tried anything.
But they arrived in Vietnam and it looked like a drug dealers' village, or an after-hours bar at 5 am.
Half of them used drugs multiple times during their time there, often deployed for 13 months.
1 out of 5 were addicts.
It caused a big concern in the Government, because they thought: "What are we going to do when they come back?
What are we going to do with thousands of addicts out there in the streets, with military training, unable to integrate back into society?"
The picture was almost a zombie apocalypse.
They imagined, for example, like in this cover of Time magazine, that the most vulnerable ones would be black, poor, uneducated people, but it was not true.
The consumption pattern didn't depend on race, social class, or any other factor they could isolate.
It affected everyone equally.
People would use for combat or not, because of a traumatic experience or not.
Some began to use because at the hospital they were provided with morphine for their wounds, but most of them used just because everyone else did.
Traditional explanations didn't seem to account for what was happening.
So they imagined gangs, like in the movies -- Can you tell that I like 80's movies?
(Laughter) Like in The Warriors, gangs running in the streets, creating chaos; very dangerous veterans unable to go back to normal life ... a disaster.
So, what happened?
Nothing happened.
After the deployment in Vietnam 1 percent of the soldiers who used drugs kept the consumption habit.
This is the same percentage of people before the war, no more, no less.
The remaining soldiers stopped using drugs after a year in average.
Imagine all the know-it-alls from the TV and the radio completely confused after worrying about it for so long. It was like an anticlimax.
You were going to have an apocalypse but you got nothing.
(Laughter) What a mood killer.
What happened?
One possible answer came years later, in 1978, from a psychologist called Bruce K. Alexander, who conducted some well-known experiments, such as the Rat Park.
In these experiments, Alexander used Wistar rats, which are the experimental psychologist's best friend after the dog, and he split them into two groups.
One was put in something like a jail, and the other in a resort in Punta Cana, you get the idea.
This is, group A were rats in regular lab cages, isolated from other rats and at leisure all day long.
The other one, the resort group, was in the Rat Park, a very big space full of tunnels and places where rats could play, explore, interact with other rats.
Both groups had water deposits with morphine at their disposal and water without morphine.
Rats could self-administrate morphine whenever they wanted, total "party hard".
(Laughter) And Alexander discovered that rats in jail used to self-administrate morphine more frequently, and therefore develop addiction habits more easily, but rats with other things to do usually avoided water with morphine to play with other rats, explore, do rat things and enjoy themselves.
Some rats in this group got addicted to morphine, of course, but just a few of them.
That challenged the idea that drugs were addictive by themselves, because it was the first proof that maybe the environment has something to do with it, and it seems like these experiments could be generalized to humans, considering how US soldiers behaved after returning from Vietnam.
Of course, drugs can create addiction, but that's not the whole story: the environment seems to be important too.
As soldiers left an environment where drug use was normal, came back home, a lot of them back to their families and loved ones, they would find a job, develop healthy relationships, and quit.
As simple as that.
I've started with this story because as a psychologist, knowing whether people can change and how they change is a crucial part of my my job,
of our job, as we psychologists are behavior modifiers, either in our office helping a patient overcome a problem, or in a company developing a team to adopt a new way of working, or in a school as an tutor, trying to make a student adopt study habits.
We study how to change people so they can change for the better and achieve their goals.
And of course, what people think is happening often is not what it really happens when we talk about human behavior.
Popular psychology has little to do with scientific psychology. And one of the main ideas regarding humans' capacity to change is the idea that it takes a lot of willpower, which is the reason or the excuse why you sign up for a gym, go for a couple of weeks and then spend six months paying but not going, because you'll go next week anyway.
Hi to my gym, by the way.
(Laughter) It's the reason why you start a diet, but when you have a bad day, you pig out on ice cream and cookies, and drink meatballs.
(Laughter) That's why when you're a student you study for the exam the day before, every term you swear it won't happen again, but you always do it again, because you need to go party, because you have no willpower.
Other people do have it, that's why they can change, but not you, you don't have it.
In fact, that idea has had some predicament in psychology.
In recent years, a model developed by psychologist Roy Baumeister was acclaimed, called the Ego Depletion model. This model stipulates that willpower would be a limited resource, like a gas deposit, that dries up the more we take difficult decisions.
Habits don't take willpower; decisions do.
For instance, eating broccoli instead of a muffin.
The more we make difficult decisions, the more our willpower gets drained throughout the day, ending up falling in our old habits, and giving in to the temptation.
That's why many infidelities take place at night, diets tend to break at night, and so on. (Laughter) The problem is that this model -- don't clap just yet, I'm not giving you an excuse to cheat on anyone.
(Laughter) (Laughter)(Applause) This model is being refuted, because it's difficult to replicate. Not because there can be some truth to it, actually I think there is, but because it leaves an important part behind: the context.
One thing we behavior modifiers know is that human beings, like any other organism, react mostly to the signs of our context, like, for example, when we cross the street on a green light or when we see a sign like this we don't get closer.
We like to think that we have free will and that we make decisions, because we like to think that we are rational beings, but like Siniestro Total sings: "We are rational beings because we eat rations, 'portions', at the bar."
(Laughter)
So no, most of the things you've done today don't have an inch of rational decision on your side, and often we react to the environment in ways that are determined by our genes, our learning history, our experiences and habits.
And that's what I want to talk about today.
Some psychologists, to explain why it is difficult to change, have conceived a metaphor --
Do you understand what a metaphor is?
Behavior is not like that, but it's easier to understand it, a simplification.
They imagine the person as a Hindu riding his elephant through a path across the jungle.
The rider and the elephant represent the person; the jungle and the path, the environment around.
The rider represents the role of the long term plans, the one who makes New Year's resolutions.
The rider is very motivated.
The elephant is everything else.
The elephant is our taste for instant gratification, our habits, our emotionality.
The elephant wants to eat the muffin now, even though the rider has decided to eat organic quinoa with boiled salmon from a sustainable fish farm.
(Laughter) The elephant wants to call your ex at 2 am drunk out of your mind to beg for a second chance.
(Laughter) The elephant knows he needs to study, but the new season of Game of Thrones is out and he wants to sit in front of the TV and watch it all.
The elephant doesn't like getting wet, not by sweating or when it rains, that's why we are not able to go to the gym or for a run, because it rains and he prefers a blanket.
The elephant likes the things he's used to.
He even likes your ex.
(Laughter) It's like if Ortega y Gasset had stated: "I am me and my circumstances", but disguised as an Indian riding a five tonnes animal.
So, what happens? The elephant is very big and the rider very small, and if the elephant feels like going in one direction, the rider can only let him go, usually ending up in a morning full of hangover and remorse.
That's how Jonathan Haidt explains this experience we've all had of knowing exactly what you need to do and doing the opposite.
(Laughter) That's how we can explain why usually our habits, often ruled by the environment and reacting to its signs are stronger than our intentions, than the things we need to use as behavioral triggers.
The environment does not only include the physical part, with our signs saying "go this way", "stop here"...; it also includes the social environment: people we with interact with, the culture we live in.
If you think it's not very important, listen to this: in 2007, a medical study from Harvard University found out that if one of you develops obesity, all of your close friends are three times more likely to get roly-poly (Laughter) and that's not because you all go to a restaurant to pig out together, no.
If you live in San Francisco, and your friend in New York, and you keep in touch regularly, your friend gets roly-poly all the same.
He influences you, you influence him.
The environment has such a weight on us, that we can change our habits without realizing.
The environment can stop us from acting or make us do stupid things, like use heroin while fighting in the Vietnam war, because everyone else does.
There is a classic experiment by Columbia University where a group of students are told to fill in some questionnaires.
The students can be alone or in groups of three.
Whilst they're on it an emergency occurs: from a hidden grille in the wall, smoke starts to flood in filling the room.
If the student is alone, there's a 75 percent chance that he'd get up immediately, ask for help and raise the alarm.
But when the student is with other people, only 38 percent of the times they get up and look for help.
Think about it: 62 percent of the students remained seated like dumbasses, inhaling smoke, (Laughter) because they all were waiting for somebody else to do something, and since nobody did anything, everyone stayed quiet.
In an unknown situation, context tells us what to do, and if we don't get any signs from it, we do nothing, like you now, with all this smoke and you stay there quietly looking at me.
(Laughter) The context determines and the context inhibits our behaviors.
It can also be used for good causes, in fact a small contextual change can trigger huge behavioral changes.
There's an example I love, because it can be widely applied, it's an experiment at the Kaiser South Hospital in San Francisco, developed by head nurse Becky Richards.
She wanted to reduce nurses' mistakes in drug administration, because a mistake could be fatal.
The origin of these mistakes were distracting people, interrupting the nurse by asking her whatever: doctors, nurses, other patients, patients' relatives, the high school band, whoever.
So she decided to introduce a small change in the environment so these nurses won't be distracted, and it was to use this: reflective jackets, dingy, ugly as a toad, those you can get from any car repair store, and one rule: if you're wearing the jacket it means you are giving medication, so if you're wearing it, no one can talk to you and you are not supposed to an answer.
And of course, the nurses hated it with all their hearts, because they took is as a punishment, as if they were incompetents at their jobs.
However, a few months after starting the study, mistakes went down by 47 percent; no one had to make any will effort, or change anything willingly; only that simple rule and that small environmental change triggered a radical change of behavior.
We can't always change the environment to start a new habit, nor go to Bora Bora every time you want to learn a new habit, even if it's proven that it's easier to give up smoking on holidays rather than at home, because you don't get the signs of the context pushing you to smoke.
So, what do we do to start a new habit?
New York University's psychologist Peter Gollwitzer developed a technique called "action triggers", a way of anticipating a situation, triggering a behavior by leaving the control of our behavior to one sign of the context.
For example, if I am a student and I can do an optional assignment to get better grades, I have more chances to do the assignment instead of falling asleep, if I write on a paper: "I will do the assignment this Saturday at 9 am at my dad's desk right before they wake up".
Why?
Because when we do that we are inclining our organism to react to a sign from the environment, so entering that environment triggers that behavior.
This technique has had many applications in the health realm obtaining great results when it comes, for example, to elder patients with heap and knee prosthetics adopting healthy habits, exercising, instead of being laid up with pain, speeding up their recoveries significantly.
In habits that are difficult, those the subject deems hard to acquire, the creation of triggers of intention multiplies by three the chances of success: from a 22 to a 62 percent average.
In my own practice as a psychologist, this technique has proven valuable, because some of the therapeutical tasks that patients have to perform are heavy.
For instance, in cognitive therapy, one of the fundamental techniques is to keep track of your thoughts, so you can detect dysfunctional thoughts that are causing you anxiety, depression, and other negative emotions, and switch them with rational thoughts.
Most people don't have the habit of keeping a diary; for most of them is difficult to sit down and write.
When I agree with my patients some behavioral triggers about when and how they're going to sit down and write, chances of performing the task raise, and therefore chances that therapy turns effective raise as well.
So, in short, people can change, and we do it constantly, we do it constantly but most of the times, our changes can happen and be sustained very easily if we take a look at our environment and how it affects us and how we affect it; how the environment makes it easier or harder to do what we want to do.
Change is perfectly possible, but opposite to what many believe, it has nothing to do with willpower.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Hello, I want to tell you about my experience at the University of Gallaudet, but first, I am going to tell you what it means to be a deaf person and who I am.
My name is Ruben, I am 12 years old and I live in Madrid.
As you can see, I am a deaf person. You are listening to the voice of 2 interpreters, not mine. The goal of the two interpreters is for you to understand me.
Their names are Gala and Cristina.
We are all deaf in my family.
On the left, you can see my friends, and you can also see my family. And this is the school I attend, Gaudem. You can see their logo on my shirt.
You might ask, what is the difference between deaf people and hearing people.
There is no difference, only that hearing people, can hear and deaf people cannot.
However, as deaf people, we have the capacity to speak.
Sometimes we use hearing aids or cochlear implants and other devices, It depends if the deaf person has hypoacusis, residual hearing or a deep hearing loss.
My family and I use the Spanish Sign Language (LSE)
When I was little, my first sign was this one: "Water."
Can you see it?
And then, the sign for "Mother" and "Father."
We always have two professors at Gaudem School. One who signs and one who speaks, so that I can always understand everything that is happening.
Like I said before, this is the Spanish Sign Language, this means that not everyone speaks the same Sign Language. There are many languages [as there are countries in the world.]
For example, this sign: "Stop" the majority of people recognize it, but imagine another scenario.
In Spain we say this sign: "Eat" You see?
However, in Japan they use this one: "Eat."
As you can see, they use this sign because they eat with sticks.
For example, in Morocco, they use this other sign: "Eat" See?
And that is because in their culture, they eat with their hands.
There are differences in all languages of Sign Language.
When you attend an international event, you use English, we use an International Sign system, which [I will explain later what they mean.]
Now that I have told you about my life in general, I am going to tell you about my experience at Gallaudet.
Gallaudet is a University in the United States, but it's not the only one in the world for deaf people. There is another University like that in Rochester. The difference is that in Gallaudet, the majors are related to Philosophy and Literature. While in Rochester there are majors related to Science.
This an image of Gallaudet, which is in Washington, USA. As you can see, there is the ASL professor who is also deaf or hard of hearing [but] has knowledge of Sign Language and all the people attending the university must have a good command of Sign Language and English.
The language we use is the American Sign Language the "AmeSlan" [AMErican SIgn LANguage.]
The university is very big, It's a campus with several departments, [buildings] sports, library, etc.
We are playing there in one of the fields.
And that is the logo of the organization that planned the event, the World Federation of the Deaf, specifically, the Youth Section.
These campuses are aimed for infant children, young people and seniors.
There is an organization at the European level, the EUDY (European Union of the Deaf Youth) It is different from the World Federation.
In my experience, I was able to contact many people from other countries, using the International Sign Language system which I spoke to you about earlier.
This sign system compiles all the signs from different countries to create it.
The World Federation of the Deaf made their selection in different ways for this event. In Europe, they sent each other different videos, and for example, in Spain, they selected 4 people.
And they did the same for all the countries. For example, in Germany they selected one person, and in Italy, they selected two, and each one had a project.
The activities we participated in were games, like you see in the image, and cultural activities as well. Each one had to do a talk about their culture. We talked about the the potato omelette, the bullfights, and we did a simulation as well, of the Dance of Chotis, famous in Madrid.
Also, we did a lot of sightseeing in the city of Washington. We saw [the] White House, the Monument and the Lincoln Memorial.
These are the good friends I made there. [A kid from Finland and one from Germany, I Iiked them very much.]
Actually, I have been able to communicate with them thanks to that International Sign system.
What I hope for in the future with this passion of mine, is that hopefully, there will be a University like Gallaudet in Spain.
Maybe we can build one in the future, and I can work there or be its president.
Thank you very much for listening to me, I hope you like it.
Thank you very much!
(Sign of Applause)
Hello, good night everyone.
On screen we are seeing a representation of Lik,
a snake from a pre-Columbian myth. It has its enormous body full of fish.
In the book "Structural Anthropology", the anthropologist Lévi Strauss used Lik's example to highlight how distant cultures in time and place, in South America, share common features.
Antropoloops is a musical remix project articulated around this idea.
Now, our approach to musicology is creative.
More than identifying common features in traditional music of distant cultures, we try to build common features through a digital remix.
Thus, we make songs out of fragments of traditional music from all over the world.
One of the key compositional principles in the project is to never modify the original tone from the music we use.
We only make timing modifications to help combine the produce of all the musicians playing from different times and places.
We try to show maximum respect for the original sources.
In this sense, we could say that Antropoloops is a celebration of commonalities among differences, and Lik is the name of the last piece we have composed.
And we could also say it is a snake full of loops that roams South America
(Traditional music playing) (Adds birdsong) (Adds someone talking in Portuguese) (Adds percussions) (Adds someone giving short screams) (Adds guitar sound) (Adds whistle sound) (Makes a remix of all sounds) (Adds harp sound) (Adds someone else singing) (Music ends) According to the myth ... (Applause) Thank you.
According to the myth, lucky people can find Lik stranded on mainland and it asks them to take it to a water lagoon.
If they do so, Lik promises to give them all of the fish it has in its body just with one condition: to never reveal where they got them from.
Unlike the myth, we do reveal where we got all of the sources we use.
In fact, it's one of the key elements of the project.
We are interested in opening the process of remix as much as possible.
For this, Antropoloops has two sources, a musical source, and a data visualization source developed by Esperanza Moreno, who is sitting there.
The application that you have seen is a program that allows me to display in real time how I'm remixing each one of the fragments we compose.
It displays the loop duration, the cover, all the metadata.
In that spirit, this image that we see on screen would be a kind of map of the snake body, right?
A sort of guide to listen to the remix
in which we truly can see these 136 musical "fishes", or fragments, with its source of origin.
This fragments come from 60 different songs from 42 different discs.
All this music is accessed through the Internet.
This project would not be possible without the work of lots of people, music collectors who share and digitize antique vinyls, and also organizations like the French Center for Research in Ethnomusicology, since part of this music comes from this platform.
For each remix we do, we upload its information to the web, to the Antropoloops web site, so really each song is like a portal to all of this original music.
We also do a collage with fragments of covers from the discs we have used.
In the remix process of this cover, which is Lik's cover, we see this Nanama kid from Colombia who is trying, in some way, to charm the snake; we could say that it's swimming in digital syncretism waters.
(Music) (Adds guitar sound) (Adds percussions) (Adds a person's voice) (Adds another guitar sound) (Adds the singing of a man) (Music ends) When they asked us if we wanted to come (Applause) Thanks.
When they asked if we wanted to come to this plaza today to talk about Antropoloops, we thought it was a very cute idea because this disk, Lik, emerges from the encounter and celebration of commonalities in public space.
Lik's origin arises from a live remix session that we set up for an artistic installation that Esperanza and I made in Amate Park, Seville, within the context of the "Luces de Barrio" project.
At Amate Park, generally gather around several groups of South American immigrants.
People from Paraguay, Peru and Ecuador who set up three volleyball nets to use the park as a meeting point.
There, they also cook charity meals for fundraising to help other migrants having health or legal problems.
To celebrate, value and make visible this solidarity network in public space, we built a 90-yard-long net of colored strings connecting the three volleyball nets that they set up.
In this sense we could also say that the body of Lik, with all those fish that bring good fortune, was also like the body of this net full of lamps that we built on the opening day.
And that was the network of music, of solidarity and affection built in the public space.
We like to think of Lik and Antropoloops as a project consisting in weaving nets.
Nets made out of music threads, uniting us all, eventually.
(Sounds of crickets and birds) (Adds someone talking in Portuguese) (Adds guitar sound) (Adds percussions) (Adds someone talking in Spanish) (Adds someone else talking in Spanish) (Adds sound of guitars) (Adds pan flute sound) (Adds a man singing) (Adds a man talking in Portuguese) (Adds a man talking in Spanish) (Music ends) Thank you.
(Applause) Thank you very much.
Cristian Sánchez: Remember when we were children?
We had fun playing simple games like hide-and-seek and tag.
Everything was so simple, right?
Our only concern was to keep ourselves entertained, to keep ourselves busy.
And if there was nothing to do, it didn't matter because with just a piece of paper and a pencil, thanks to our boundless imagination, we were capable of turning ourselves into a sidekick of our favourite superhero or even better, into the hero himself.
But of course, not every child in the world has these opportunities.
Where we see shooting stars, they pray to be one of them.
In this case, we are referring to the Middle East, where these children have to live in a war alien to them.
If they are lucky and their family has the necessary resources, they can leave the country.
But what if their family doesn't have the means to do that?
They only have two options.
The first one is to wait for the war to end, if they survive it.
The second option they have is for a terrorist group to kidnap them, indoctrinate them, train them, and send them to the front at the age of 15.
Andrea Rueda: But when they finally make it to Europe, their situation is not exactly filled with hope.
They find out that for the foreseeable future, they are stuck in a fenced camp away from the city, with no possibility of either leisure or work, and where their basic needs are miraculously covered.
We visited the camps in and around Athens in order to assess their situation.
We have met a lot of children living in these bleak conditions.
And we want to show you some of the cases we consider most significant.
The first one is Firas, this child.
Firas left Syria at the age of nine.
He and his family threw themselves into the sea.
He travelled by sea via Turkey and Macedonia over a period of three years, until the age of 12 when he got to settle in Athens.
But Firas doesn't know how to write, because he spent the fundamental years of his education moving from one place to another.
Firas wants to make friends because he hasn't had a group of friends either, for the same reason.
When we arrived, the first thing Firas did was to approach us and say, 'Come play with me. I want friends.'
And he immediately took us towards the other kids who were also there, in the Port of Piraeus.
This helped us a lot in approaching the others and creating an emotional bond with them.
Firas was always eager to learn.
He always wanted to play board games and wanted to learn how to play cards.
He was such a charming child.
We enjoyed spending time with his family.
He always offered us coffee, tea, dinner at his place.
All because he loved to have friends and to gather them with his family, that was his most important bond and actually the only one he had there.
Another very special case was Neka, this little girl.
Neka is Afghan, she is nine years old, and the most precocious girl in the world.
Neka loved to make necklaces and bracelets and to give them away so everyone could look as lovely as she did.
Neka had two important wishes: one was to go to Germany, just like everyone there, and the other one was to sleep in a hotel because for months, she was sleeping in a camping tent at the port along with her family.
The last time I saw Neka was the day I got to the port, and her dad said to me, 'We got our passports back; we are being deported. We didn't get asylum, so we need to return to Afghanistan.'
Devastated, I approached Neka, hugged her, and she said to me happily, 'Will you visit me when I get to Germany?'
I felt incredibly sad.
Neka had a younger sister too.
She was two years old, so she had been born during their journey from one place to another, and she's one of the few girls that I've ever seen who never laughs or plays.
She was always sick.
She always had a fever, she was always crying - a two-year-old girl.
Cristian Sánchez: The children were not just looking for us during game time,
but also when we were performing our duties, such as food distribution, cleaning work, or like in this case, when we were building a vegetable garden.
They used to run like crazy asking if they could do something to help because they saw us in the camp, helping them tirelessly day and night, so they felt the need to help us so they could feel useful by helping us in our work in return.
Because, imagine what it must be like to live for months in a fenced field, completely isolated, and having nothing to do.
Naturally, they can go out. If they're lucky, the nearest town is 31 miles away.
But playing was not everything
and helping us in our work was not everything either.
There were children whose father, for one reason or another, was not there with them.
Those children had to help their mother and siblings in the daily housework:
chores like queuing up at food distribution points, queuing up to do the laundry, being at the nappy and baby food distributions, milk distribution when there was milk.
And yet, there is something worse:
the cases where they don't have a mother, a father, or siblings, so they have no family
to take care of them and protect them.
There is the case of Abdul Haid. Unfortunately, we don't have an actual photo of him,
but we think this photo is very representative.
Abdul Haid is a 16-year-old boy who spent most of his time alone in his tent, either listening to music or when he had the chance, talking with his family on the phone.
The few moments he got out of his tent to meet us, he greeted us with a hug every single time.
He was obviously affectionate and needed to receive it also.
While he was talking to us, he never let go of our hands.
And I believe that he looked upon us,
not quite as a father figure, because he already had a father, but he saw us as his protectors.
All this is the reason why we are here today.
Because when someone has been out there and back, their reality, the way they face problems, the way they see life in general change completely.
We left friends there.
We have left behind people who shared moments with us, both good and bad.
And you can't relax when you return from there,
because you feel the need to be with them even though, sadly, we don't have the resources or the capacity to resolve their problems, but we can stand by them, helping them through these difficult times.
For all these reasons, we came up with this initiative.
It consists of giving a toy to every defenceless child out there in the toughest refugee camps.
Andrea Rueda: We are talking about 'Let's Be Game Changers', developed in Athens.
One day, Cristian said, 'I want to return here on Christmas Day so we can give them toys and make them happy for once.'
And I said, 'I'll help you.'
With the help of others, we started a project to make this happen.
We organise cultural events with elements from the local culture, from the different cities in which we work.
We are doing concerts, improvisation theatre plays, poetry readings.
The money we collect will be taken there and used to buy toys to help restore hope for these children.
They can have the joy of receiving a gift, something that belongs to them, for them to play with by themselves or to share with other children so that they can get back some of the childhood they have lost.
You may wonder why we don't collect toys instead of money.
And the answer is very simple.
If we collect all the toys and have to transport them all the way out there, we will end up spending a lot of money on freight because it's so expensive.
It's much easier to take the money there and spend it there, making a big saving.
We must also keep in mind that things over there are not perfectly organised, because it's so difficult.
New people arrive, others leave, and numbers fluctuate.
New children may arrive and depending on their age and gender, we want to buy the appropriate toys for them, not get random toys.
So, that is why.
We must also keep in mind the possibility that by the time we get there, the need for toys may have already been covered.
For example, a truck full of toys may have arrived the day before.
So, we won't deliver more toys
as that doesn't make sense.
We'd have to take them to another camp and distribute them according to that camp's population.
Cristian Sánchez: We know there is a lack of things more important than toys.
For example, lack of food and health care.
Also, as you know, there are many NGOs, organisations, and associations who are committed to raising funds and sending them there, where the NGO field operators distribute food and medicine. We wanted to do something different, focusing on the children,
because we know that a toy can bring joy to a child's life. It can restore the love for games and help distract the child from its everyday reality. It can also help the child make new friends through play.
We want children to go back to being children, like this.
If we stop ignoring the situation and accept what we see around us, we could be that shooting star we were talking about before, bringing a glimmer of hope to all the children who lost their happiness long ago.
To help them to continue being the children they are,
let's be game changers.
Thank you so much for your attention.
(Applause)
That's me.
I was born in Quito, Ecuador.
And I grew up in the city.
I was a city girl until I turned seven.
It was then when my parents moved to the countryside, to a valley, 40-minutes away from the city.
There I learned to plunge into the bushes and the hills to explore and observe insects.
Spiders in particular.
I was so mad about spiders that by the time I was ten I had a collection of 5,000 living spiders that I kept in glass vases.
No doubt, this was my favorite.
In the world, 1 out of 10 people have insect phobia.
And 3 out of 10 have a phobia of spiders.
Do you see that happy face?
(Laughter) I was not one of them.
And you will understand that avoiding teenage bullying with a hobby such as this one, was no easy a task.
So I did what most of us do: I left my hobby to try to fit in.
So I graduated from school, specialized in physics and mathematics, I later began studying graphic design, which was a new career in Ecuador.
And I think it was a good decision.
However, since everything comes around in life, one night many years later, I was coming back from the beach.
And when I reached a cloudy forest like one and a half hour before arriving to Quito, I made a stop at this gas station.
Suddenly, I couldn't believe my eyes,
There were millions of moths on a wall.
And some others flying around the light.
I got off my car and got closer,
and then it hit me.
That little girl, the frustrated entomologist I concealed for so many years, emerged and came back to life.
And then, everything made sense.
I went back home and I decided to come back.
I picked a camera and a couple of weeks later I was back at the gas station.
But, what do you think happened?
There was no one single moth
to be found there!
What's up? Not even one!
I didn't imagine that, I had seen them there.
It happens that moths while inside the forest, flying at a constant angle relative to the moonlight.
But when there is no moon; or in its early phases --crescent or waning moon-- moths go searching for that light and end up in inhabited areas.
There's when they remain revolving around light bulbs.
As everybody tells me:
"Moths! They are horrible!"
That's what you are thinking now.
True. Moths hold a reputation for being a plague, dark, furry and ugly.
But in fact, as we always see them behind the light, we see them like this.
And when we get closer to observe them, we find that they hide some fantastic designs.
For example, look at this one.
This is one of the first I saw.
I nicknamed her Carolina Herrera.
(Laughter) Isn't she beautiful?
Cow.
This one I named "Little Stitch", as she seem to have a surrounding backstitch.
I loved them,
as I was finding thousands.
Each one more beautiful than the other.
From complex to unbelievable designs.
Some iridescent, some geometric, other minimalist, more exotic, more chaotic ... thousands!
And as I continued to observe and admire, I kept on learning about them.
For example, do you know there's more than 150,000 species of moths worldwide?
Crazy, right?
Note that I mentioned moths, not species of butterflies.
Unlike moths, butterflies are diurnal, they have an appendix at the end of their antenna and when they perch they fold their wings upwards.
Moths are for the most part nocturnal. In their antenna they have like a feather.
And when they perch, they open and rest their wings downwards.
That's how I started telling them apart.
I chased after them with my camera, never losing one single chance to get out of work to go contemplate them.
And they posed for me,
they were very friendly.
Unlike butterflies, they didn't fly away but stay there.
I'd talk to them, spoil them, open their little wings, and it was turning more and more fun every time.
Even my family and friends sometimes thought that I was going a bit crazy ... (Laughter) because I was talking to moths.
And in some way it was true, it became a bit of an obsession, a passion I couldn't put aside.
So I thought: "I've got to do something."
And I began to make digital illustrations of these little bugs.
Because I wanted to show in some way this I could see in the light bulb.
This movement, this repetition.
So I decided to create this patterns.
But it turns out that nobody believed me, when I showed this to colleagues or friends, I'd say: "see how beautiful these moths are!"
"No, that's not real.
They are moths, moths are ugly."
Not even my biologist friends really believed me.
So I decided to do the same but including photos, to make sure there would be no doubt that they were real, they existed and each one was prettier than the other.
And that's how I began this study on the symmetry of the wings, their composition, and details each one had. I would play with movements and colors, which was fascinating.
I would love to see how they created this contrast with the places they would perch on.
Wood, concrete, metal, for example.
It was not the romantic little butterfly that flies among flowers.
These were more underground beings, more urban in their making.
And this made them beautiful to my eyes.
More and more beautiful.
So that's how I spent four years doing this in my free time.
And when I had enough, I thought: "I'll make a book, I'll show this to my people."
So I ventured into finding a publisher.
I said, "the book's title will be 'Pachanga'."
Pachanga as a synonym of party, of rumba.
I used that analogy with those nocturnal ladies that would go out all dressed up to do the light bulb dance.
So then a small German publisher decided to take on the challenge.
We worked for about a year in the book.
And when it was ready they even came with the surprise that they would make a Japanese edition.
Something I never imagined to happen.
Just as I didn't imagine that many people after this, would write to me saying that they would never kill a moth again.
Finally, moths would show their beauty and become a surprise for a lot of people.
This power of the unexpected became a seductive force that caused a lot of curiosity.
The book went from being a design book to an object that teased you into observation.
To observe any life form, not only moths.
People first observed then contemplated,
and caressed the pages of the book, creating that feeling of closeness and even affection for those weird, invisible creatures.
That's how I could ratify that images hold the power of touching our soul.
And that was for me one of my biggest rewards.
What came afterwards?
I started making some experiments applying the designs to objects, products and other things.
At the same time, new offers came in to be part of book projects in collaboration with biologists, photographers, naturalists and scientists to rescue, in some way, other weird creatures.
Namely, frogs, whales, bears.
They also invited me to talk about biodiversity, about climate change, conservation and the popularization of science.
This in some way allowed me to keep on with my flight, this exploring adventure where I went back to this wonderful exercise of stopping for a moment, getting closer and observing.
To later interpret what my eyes saw.
I have the privilege of living in Ecuador, a very diverse country. While small in size, the country has a lot of ecosystems and microclimates.
To be able to depict through my graphics this abundance, generosity and richness and that parade of forms and colors, makes of my career something meaningful in my life.
And that's what I love.
In that flight, these invisible beings always show up again, these creatures nobody see.
My spiders, for example.
Or the bugs that go unnoticed down the forest.
That's when I love to observe the details and draw them back for people to see them at a bigger scale.
So I go back flying to my moths which are always there, they never leave.
Because I think there's Pachanga for a long while and for everybody.
And I ponder on this little girl who one day dropped her passion to avoid being rejected.
I'd say to her now, to open her wings and go out exploring, fearlessly.
Because after all, it's not that bad to be a weird creature.
Thank you.
(Applause)
As a mountain and rock climber for the last 20 years, I would dare say the most powerful feeling I've had to live with almost constantly during each mountaineering challenge I've faced is fear.
Rational fear and irrational fear on many occasions that the safety rope I'm attached to won't hold, that the bridge I'm using to cross a crevasse will collapse, of passing through a place where an avalanche may fall or causing one myself, or that a block of ice will fall on me; or even death.
These are real and ever present fears when one is seeking adventure on a mountain and above all, when one ventures ever further; ever farther into the unknown.
I've had the chance to know most of these fears in the flesh, and I can assure you they're real, they're tangible.
The only one haven't tasted is death, though I hope not to experience that on the mountain.
My talk this afternoon is about how, with the passage of time, I've learned to use these fears in my favor, of personal growth, and of change.
I couldn't think of a better way of telling you my personal experience other than recounting an experience I actually lived through.
In 2011, I went with my two best friends, companions on many climbing adventures, to a mountain in the South of Chile, a Southern mountain, one which has never been climbed.
By then, I felt very comfortable on a climb, up the mountain.
Perhaps I was not part of the Argentine or world elite, but I'd already been lucky enough to climb many difficult mountains; I'd already climbed a great deal in South America; I'd already exposed myself to risk many times; I'd been lucky enough to reach the roof of the world, Everest, the year before; my confidence level was high enough to face a mountain no one had ever climbed,
a mountain with high technical difficulty and risk which was why it had never been climbed.
It was a volcano in the South of Chile, on the Southern shores called Corcovado and, since it's basically a volcano, it has a very steep summit.
In summer, due to it being volcanic, the stone breaks, and there's no way of securing oneself.
So we decided to do it in winter, to be able to climb on ice, and even though one would say, "It's far weaker than stone," depending on the circumstances, it's often safer.
So we went there in 2011, I and my friends; one of them is here, Charly.
We were euphoric and very motivated as we embarked on this little winter trip to the Pacific coasts.
Obviously, the adventure had begun long before we set foot on the mountain, because this is the way with the unknown; you arrive and start finding out things as you move forward.
We had to travel five hours on the Pacific Ocean in winter.
The Pacific in winter is far from pacific.
What's more, instead of resembling the Polynesian islands with a tranquil, warm, sky blue sea, on the Southern shores it's more like the sea in Antarctica.
We were sailing in a small12-meter fiberglass boat, which seemed very strong, but at times, it was completely engulfed by walls of water.
There were moments when it vanished from the horizon, and we couldn't see anything other than water in every direction; this is where my story of how to fight fear begins.
Obviously, the unknown is frightening; and even though we felt comfortable as climbers, the sea was not our realm; obviously, we immediately panicked and doubts arose - about whether we should go back or not, but since this talk is about strategies, we were lucky enough to find the seasoning for this recipe.
The first one was not to face that fear alone.
And being with two very close friends, having lived many experiences on many adventures, made that small phase of fear easier, the stage of, "What are we doing here? Why don't we go back?"
At the same time, there's that other tool, humor, and amongst friends, we were not alone; those few hours on a very stormy sea eventually turned into a pretty calm sea and allowed us to anchor at the base of the Corcovado volcano.
Here, this adventure truly begins.
We disembarked, shouldered our backpacks, and it was already our realm; we felt more comfortable.
You've overcome the first terror and say, "OK, every time I'm more prepared."
But it's not always like that, and it was far from the case here.
Nothing was known about the mountain; we only had Google Earth references and a few photos, so we set to tackle a route, a line of ascent that seemed the easiest to us, going up a riverbed from within, and the truth is that it was very different from what we expected.
The riverbed was obviously clean because it was the route taken by everything that came from the top of the mountain, and immediately, we began to see traces of avalanches and of rock slides, a completely hostile territory.
Truly, the word was hostility.
Those of us who live in the world of rock and mountain climbing say that when a place makes your hair stand on end and takes your breath away, even though you hear a rock slide far, far away, just on hearing it, your heart stops, your everything stops, you look at your companions, and say, "That's not right."
We call that a place with a lot of "atmosphere."
The atmosphere is loaded; it has a lot of atmosphere.
This one had a troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere.
One of the tools that we all have when we face our fears, a very important one, is knowledge.
Knowledge, knowing what this is about, knowing where you are at the moment of fear, knowing that you have tools to overcome it, that was our tool at that moment.
We knew we'd already faced similar places, that we'd lived through terrifying experiences at the end of a rope, and as the hours went on, we talked it over, and we started to overcome that truly terrifying place. It was evident that people
were not meant to be there.
But anyway, we moved forward for several hours, backpacks on our backs, in that pretty gloomy place, and we finally left the wooded area of the Valdivian forest and reached the area of almost eternal snows.
In truth, we could not imagine what was waiting for us, because the valley we had traveled through, and which was our chosen route to access the mountain's summit, was the main channel for all of the valley's avalanches.
All the mountainsides would send their natural avalanches through this channel, which we were thinking of traveling through for the next two days. I have a few photos of me sitting on a stone with Charly, and in the first five minutes, we saw three avalanches go past where we were heading. There, I think we experienced one of our most difficult moments, which was facing up to the fact that the two options we had, both involved facing extreme fear.
One option: return. In this success-oriented Western society, the fear of failure is terrible, it affects all of us, whatever we do.
The possibility of this talk going wrong is completely frightening to me.
And at that moment, returning from an expedition that we had put so much effort into, on the first day, without setting foot on the mountain, having only walked in the woods, was a very strong fear; and it's not a personal fear, but one imposed by society; the fear of failure.
The other fear was more tangible: it was facing a dangerous road, a very dangerous one, which in five minutes had already told us it could very quickly wipe us off the face of the earth.
We chose the easiest one; we chose to face the avalanches and not return with the baggage of failure.
So, kind of hand in hand with our knowledge, that tool of knowledge that is obviously pretty basic, we made the decision to face the road anyway, in spite of the risk of what could happen to us, and taking the greatest caution, which meant traveling at night when the snow cover is far more stable.
It went well, we moved out at night, and we traveled far, very quickly, we walked many hours through that place, which in the end, in darkness, when we couldn't see it, was not as frightening. It was far less frightening at night that during the day, a paradox.
Anyway, so we were moving almost in the direction of the edge of the summit; we left the avalanche channel after a few hours, and we came up against this last moment of complete stage fright; we felt as if we were three children on the mountain for the first time, and were in the middle of a very steep incline, on a thin blanket of snow with a layer of extremely hard ice underneath it. We had no pickaxes or crampons, and weren't roped together.
Crampons are - as Les Luthiers would say - things you put on your feet with; many points, so that if used correctly they keep you from falling, but can be a deadly weapon in the wrong hands.
Anyway, no rope, nor pickaxe or crampons.
Using the most important tool of all, calmness, we managed to overcome that small hurdle we were always guided by the same foundation, which was always hope.
Every time we came up against a barrier of fear, we never lacked hope, and we never lacked humor.
Then, with a little calm in a desperate situation, because anyone who slipped on that icy incline would take a bad knock, at least, it would be a really bad experience and in that place rescue would have been practically impossible because it was a difficult place to get to; we had to use that tool, which is calm, and walk towards the summit's edge.
With this reflection, and these moments of fear throughout that day, those two days of the expedition, I want to share this analysis of a mountain experience with you: how we face fears, how we face what happens to us, and to everyone in their own environment.
Fear to loneliness, fear to commitment, fear to letdown, fear to death; we all have something challenging us, fears and distressing situations we have to face each day.
My suggestion is we look at all the people who might be in a wheelchair, or blind, be missing an arm, or have half their body paralyzed; each time we see them do amazing things, in real life or on film, and are amazed by what they do, I can assure you that what they did first was overcome those fears.
The same fears we exposed ourselves to on those days during that expedition.
So this is the reflection: use the strategies we have on hand, try to be rational, because many of the fears we live with are imposed fears.
Fear of insecurity and fear of failure are social fears we are burdened with due to social issues. I would say necessary ones, but the fact is that if we were to stand across the street and look at the lives and fears other people face, we would realize, and put our own fears into perspective.
I urge you to overcome your fears and to fight against them with the tools you have, with as many as possible.
And to finish telling the story - because I'm sure you're all riveted - we couldn't climb the Corcovado volcano; the weather changed. During the descent to safer and firmer lands, I was caught by an avalanche that carried me 200 meters in a few seconds, and buried me up to my waist, tied with a rope, but unable to free myself from it.
Ten or fifteen seconds later, I heard the shouts of my friends, warning me another natural avalanche was coming. It crashed into me head-on like a freight train, and carried me a whole lot of meters farther. My knee was severely injured, and in the torment of flight, because it had now become a retreat, fears no longer mattered.
(Laughter) In that disastrous retreat, Cepi, another one of us, was caught in an avalanche, and he was able to escape, but in the escape he caught himself on his calf with a crampon, and he had to sew himself up; imagine, in the middle of nowhere, having to suture yourself with seven or eight stitches. Finally, after that trial of pain and suffering, and of overcoming the fears going like, "I'm going to make it, I'm not going to make it; what's going to happen?" we reached the coast at night. On the coast, the boat that had brought us was waiting for us, that fiberglass boat.
We decided to set sail at night using the GPS, because it was... I can assure you it had become a retreat; had all been too much.
We were returning, leaving the open ocean and going in towards a bay at night, the Raul Marin Balmaceda Bay, we hit something.
Later we found out it was a trunk that was floating down the Palena river, a massive trunk that was floating in the water, and we were shipwrecked.
The boat started to sink. We managed to rescue a few things.
While we were grabbing things, Cepi knocked his head against the boat's glass, against the bulkhead. He was knocked unconscious and vanished from sight.
When I turned the torch on, I saw he was no longer there, the glass was broken, the captain had had his jaw demolished by the rudder, and my heart stopped as it never had before, because not seeing Cepi where he had been just ten seconds ago, seeing the open glass and looking outside and not seeing him, was terrifying.
Luckily, when knocked unconscious, he fell under the bulkhead, and I saw him later.
Anyway, with what little courage we had left, Charly and I managed to maneuver the boat and get it to shore. We bumped it against the shore, got two or three things off, with the water already reaching our chests, we got the little we were able to save before the boat sunk for good, and well, here I am.
Here I am, newly returned from Nepal, from 40 days of humanitarian aid work and of living experiences almost as powerful and violent as Corcovado's.
After surviving mudslides, rock slides, or crossing turbulent Himalayan rivers with water up to my waist, I can assure you in order to live the lives we are given and to enjoy them as much as possible, one of the best tools we have is overcoming our fears.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
I survived a lot of violence.
How could I cope with my pain and agony?
How could I also get over it?
How could I manage my healing with the help of my family, and professional help?
And also thanks to my friends.
So, I had the chance, the strenght and the passion to create the Palomas project.
An audiovisual producer that has been striving for social activism for 13 years.
My feminist ideas, my holistic perception made me think and reflect that we should try to understand relationships between women and men.
And I also reflected on taking an analysis methodology.
Being aware of gender equality is still an utopia in Cuba and all around the world.
Alda Facio, from Costa Rica, is a jurist and a writer, she says that women fall into the trap of trying to be as capable as men, and what we really pursued was to be considered as citizens as them.
We as women are under a lot of pressure, socially and culturally.
And we are more than mothers, daughters, grandmothers, aunts.
We are free and full human beings.
But men are under a lot of pressure too.
They're the economic core of the family, and holding up that power has forbidden them to express their feelings among other things.
So we decided to initiate different processes of consciousness change.
What for?
For equal opportunities with equal well-being results.
In 2012 we created a happiness factory.
Happiness is not a destiny, it doesn't happen by chance.
It's a life project, a way of living, an individual conquest.
It's not a single human right,
but the full enjoyment of human rights.
I'll tell you about some of the outcomes of our happiness factory.
One of them is the body ownership.
Marcela Lagarde, a feminist anthropologist a woman who did a lot of research in the body and its ownership, says that body ownership is essential to conquer autonomy, power and freedom.
We must build a body which is a temple, a cathedral.
The body has memory and its own language.
The body is the soul, the behavior.
The body is the personality, it's life and death.
Three extraordinary women.
Eve Ensler, American writer and actress.
She was recently in Havana, with one of her masterpieces:
"The vagina monologue."
Lucía Puenzo, an Argentine scriptwriter and film director.
Her film "XXY" tells about a teenager seeking her gender identity.
And Adriana Agramonte, Cuban.
Psychologist from the Endocrinology Institute accompanying intersex people.
How fascinating it was for me that those three women and I, four women from different realities, different religions and generations were interested in the same part of their bodies.
Then I realized how important and necesary it was this action we performed titled "My clitoris is mine."
Yes.
How many women suffer around the world and die because of genital mutilation?
How many women in Cuba and in the world suffer symbolic mutilation?
How many women suffer from anorgasmia?
Which means the lack of orgasm?
The clitoris is -- it is our symbol of dignity and freedom, isn't it?
Is a right to our own sexuality, a human right.
In the last Ingrid León's documentary Women, the deep soul, the daughters of the stars.
she talks about the lives of adult women.
One of these women states although it hasn't been proved by science, there are evidences in middle-age women like us, the elder we get, the bigger our clitoris are.
And it is our most erogenous zone.
So, my dear ladies, our sexuality is endless.
Our sexuality evolves.
Let me tell you, some centuries ago our clitoris was called Venus' love, the sweetness of Venus.
On the other hand, we women suffer from breast cancer.
And men too, less frequent though.
It's true that HIV exists along with other sexually trasmitted deseases.
My dear gentlemen, due to prejudices and myths, you don't prevent prostate diseases.
I suggest you to see your prostates as flowers, as a sunflower, which must be loved and grown.
Tell me, you're laughing --
Tell me, isn't it beautiful?
(Applause) It's so lovely!
I think we are in a great social human network by now, aren't we? In opossition to the culture of silence.
So, women, a round of applause to our clitorises!
Come on!
(Applause) Men, a round of applause to your prostates.
And lastly, I want you all to create an alliance based on one of our philosophies the theory of love, the political theory of love.
It's the union between feelings and minds.
With this lovely song from one of the most brilliant troubadours: Teresita Fernández.
sung by with one notable composer from Cuba, Luiba María Hevia.
A song that I'm sure that Pope Francis would have like it,
because it implies his conception of the culture of rejection, what people don't want to see, what people exclude, what people don't love.
With this song, Ugly.
(Laughter) I want to introduce my friend and collaborator, prima ballerina and master of modern dance, Isabel Blanco.
(Applause) Please, I want you to stand up.
(Music, Ugly) And let's meditate with her gestures and movements.
Let's move, let's sing --
about respecting differences, about building a social friendship,
about human solidarity. Come on!
Just dance and be happy and free.
(Applause) (Music, Ugly) (Applause) Telling the genuine word, is tranforming the world.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I was in Baghdad in 2003, the city was falling after days of war.
I had gone up with a unit from the US Army, Baghdad airport was taken, there were days of shootings, bombings and deaths.
The lootings started, people killing among each other for a bag of food.
I recall dozens of kids entering barefeet where we were and they started smashing the windows of one of the houses of Saddam Hussein's officers.
I remember having told a soldier: "How will you stop this?"
I was helping a kid on the floor, he was all covered in blood and at that moment I had a glimpse of what was coming: years of struggles for power, divisions and thousand problems for Iraqi citizens but it also gave way to the creation of the Islamic State in Iraq.
I lived to tell the tale.
I wanted to be a journalist since I was 17, I wanted to be in the Balkans at that time, in Afghanistan, in Iraq, in different parts of the world that were at war.
At first my family thought I was totally crazy, specially since I come from a family of artists, actors, film directors and it would have been much more common being an actress than getting up on Saturday mornings to study Arabic.
But they supported me strongly and I studied Political Sciences, I got a Master's Degree in Resolution of Armed Conflicts and began travelling around the world.
At present, I'm a journalist, some say a war correspondant.
I don't consider myself a war correspondant, I thinks it's a little arrogant and if I have to say correspondant I'd rather say a peace correspondant because after witnessing the horror, the deaths and all that is left behind war I feel I have the responsability to do all I can to prevent it.
(Applause) Now, I work for a news network called Al Jazeera International, I mainly cover Latin America.
A network that was also to be closed in many Middle East countries due to the kind of reports done.
People ask me if it's a problem to be a woman in this job.
And the truth is that I think it's like in all places; there were very complicated moments and in others it was great help and there were many people who wanted to help me.
I try to think positively, in the things I did well, for example in zones covered by the army, armed factions, they always wanted to take me there, in helicopters, military operations, there I went to cover the news at that moment.
Being there, in the communities, was really helpful, like the ones taken by the Taliban or insurgents, I wasn't a menace and the women opened the door of their houses, told me their stories, their daughters' and their sons' problems, I believe those were the good things of being a woman there.
But I also had complicated situations in Afghanistan, like in the south of the country, in Kandahar.
We had gone there to do a report about the increase of opium since the U.S. attack at that moment.
And I remember there was a warlord in that area, who didn't want to talk to me, since I was a woman.
I was sent to the back, got a translator and a cameraman to the front and yelled the questions so he talked to me.
While I was at the back they threw me oranges, stones, everything.
I ducked and kept working.
I wasn't there to teach feminism lessons, I was there to investigate the reasons of the increase of opium in Afghanistan when that wasn't supposed to happen.
I decided to cover conflicts, wars, I decided to go to areas that, generally, are abandoned because I wanted to witness history.
I wanted to generate empathy, humanize conflict, to make visible all the things that happen in a war.
And also, at a certain moment, I wanted to transmit a bit of what was happening there.
As I said, I wanted to live the moment.
And maybe it started as an adventure but then it wasn't.
It was a commitment.
Many times people ask me how do I get home after living terrible situations, after witnessing abjection, it's difficult to get home and keeping everyday life.
I try to see everything through a lens, that makes my life easier.
I see everything through a TV camera as we were filming, I try not to get affected, to be professional, impartial, but it's not easy; sometimes it's very hard for me.
It's very hard, for example, being in Haiti after an earthquake that killed 250,000 people and a desperate mother comes with a naked and malnourished baby saying, "Please take him, save him."
It's hard when I'm present at a suicide attack and see the wreckage.
It's hard when I was in Afghanistan a bombing from the international forces ended up killing dozens of civilians instead of killing the Taliban.
Despite I'm always trying to be impartial I believe there are stories that have to be reported, that demand us to speak on behalf of those who can't, to challenge power, wherever they are.
(Applause) Horrors not only happen far away, in Asia or Africa. They are also here, in Latin America; I was doing a research in Mexico about missing people.
There were thousands of murders, abductions with the complicty of State forces and drug dealers.
I accompanied families for many days in Guerrero mountains, looking for their relatives without success.
We found endless piles of bones everywhere we looked, but none belonged to them.
Recently, I was also in Venezuela, where, regardless of ideologies, the State arrests students, it accuses them of rebellion and betrayal in military courts.
In Colombia, after signing peace, over 80 activists, human rights advocates, have been killed.
After years of fighting drug dealing, according to the United Nations, there is more coca than ever before.
And those are the types of stories I like telling.
And nowadays, when press and journalism, are so questioned, there are people risking their lives everyday to inform.
They don't want to be stars neither the story. They are there to show what's going on in different parts of the world.
It's not easy when I get called at night and I'm told I have to go somewhere.
I have a family, small children. And making the decision to leave is tough.
Though, I still do it because I still believe.
I still believe we have to be there. I'm a witness of history. And I have to be sure the story is told.
(Applause) Our mission is not to tell you what you want to hear. We are there for you to care.
Because if you care, certain things can be changed.
Last year I was in Haiti.
A hurricane had devastated the south,
in a context of poverty levels of 70-80 percent, where kids currently eat mud cookies.
We reached an area with a soccer field and people had nothing to eat or drink for 4 or 5 days.
There was a terrible cholera outbreak and people feared drinking from rivers. They were aching for help.
And it didn't arrive.
They started to grow angry. Soldiers kept safety but did not help.
All of a sudden, a helicopter arrived.
I was with someone doing humanitarian help, a doctor who tells me: "Toothbrushes and paste are coming, what about food?"
I was outraged and started twitting. I obviously started that coverage. I started to report what was going on.
I was contacted by the UN. We managed to get water, food, and many other necessary supplies.
(Applause) I'm sometimes asked if we are afraid.
Sometimes I really am.
During an operation in Iraq we got in with the US Army. It was 2003, and it was an invasion to a city seized by insurgents.
I was filming everything when two armed men dressed in black started to shoot at me.
At first I thought about running.
I ran away, it did my best to escape.
I wondered why I was being shot. I'm a journalist, though obviously I was with the invader army. They thought I was with them.
I was mainly shocked when I saw a tank and soldiers. They were killed. The house totally collapsed.
Soldiers came soon afterwards, and told me: "Show us the video to see what we did."
All that could be seen were my feet.
Nothing else.
So, sure, sometimes I'm really scared. But my worst fear about the stories, the ones I witness, remain untold.
That certain types of situations are not informed.
Not only in Latin America, but all around.
I fear the most not being able to transmit my passion to my children
(Applause) Today there are so many fake news, so much manipulation. We have to be out there more than ever.
We have to gain credibility and be there, questioning, interrogating, reporting, more than ever.
Finally, I want to tell you I believe in the journalism that enlightens, exposes, fights against hate, standing for values, bringing light.
This was written in The Washington Post, a US journal in a very particular time:
Journalism bringing light, because Democracy Dies in Darkness.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Ashton Cofer, an inventor that has discovered how to recycle polystyrene that material we use, for example, on packaging, and used to get accumulated in our dumpsters.
With only 15 years of age, he has managed to transform this polymer into activated carbon, a valuable material that is used to purify water.
In this talk, he tells about how he failed several times on previous experiments and how grownups told him it would be impossible.
But maybe because he was a kid who was not trapped into years of social conventionalism, he kept trying.
She is Olivia Chapman.
Olivia is 14 years old and is convinced that the solution to our schools is not equality, but equity.
Even though we have struggled to achieve equality, Olivia argues that while "equality" means uniformity, "equity" implies equanimity, or justice, which lets us adapt to the needs of each student, understanding that not all of us are the same.
It is like she says, every plant needs different conditions to flourish, and the same thing happens with students.
And he is Sean Fredella.
With only 16 years of age, and after have facing cancer for four times, Sean has launched More Than 4 campaign, to raise awareness about pediatric cancer, and the limited financing it receives, only 4 percent of the total funds destined to cancer research.
But Sean wants this to change, and to do that he is using the viewing of a sport so known in his country such as American football, convinced that if he involves these athletes with the cause, he will get to go further.
Ashton, Olivia and Sean are only three examples out of the 17 talks I listened to about a year ago at TED's headquarters, in New York, during an event known as TED-Ed Weekend.
On a freezing weekend of December, youths from all over the world got gathered to learn from each other and talk about what delights us, what worries us.
Imagine my surprise when I got invited, but even more when I got there, and they proposed me to interview Tim Urban from the stage.
Tim Urban, the famous procrastinator, whose TED Talk has been watched more than 16 million times.
Yes, I was trembling on that stage, but it is certain that in that moment I knew I was in the place where I wanted to be.
Communication has always been my passion.
In fact, when I was studying high school I worked on a research project about oratory and it was then when I realized about the real lack we have in our educational system in terms of communication skills.
So I wanted to contribute, and decided to start a TED-Ed Club in my school to learn, alongside my mates, about some tools that may help us to share our ideas in public.
But, what are TED-Ed Clubs?
They are clubs without any cost that have place in schools all over the world.
They can be started by any student or teacher and their objective is to give voice to every student who has an idea to express.
In TED community, we believe that everyone has something unique and valuable to contribute with.
That is why, TED has created a methodology that is given to the teachers of these clubs through several resources like a web platform, or a notebook with proposals of sessions and activities.
In this way, the students follow a process that starts by learning about their own passions, so they can later identify what is it that makes an idea to be great and be able to find one in their passions.
Later, they learn to structure the idea, or create the script.
By last, communication skills that will help them give their own final talk, their mini TED Talk.
These are recorded and included in TED-Ed Clubs YouTube channel with talks of the rest of students from all over the world.
The program has only four years working, but more than 6,000 clubs have been created, 73 of them in Spain.
Nowadays, there are more than 1600 active clubs in the world.
Also, there are Connect Weeks four times a year, weeks in which videoconferences are established by TED-Ed Clubs' students from all the world.
Imagine a conversation of about an hour, moderated by TED, in which students from the United States, Spain, India and Poland, are able to talk to each other and ask about their concerns, their mores, the processes they follow in their own clubs.
These exchanges serve to foster understanding and empathy between youths that are apparently distinct.
In these exchanges, they realize that there are more things that bond them, than things that differentiate them.
And there is more. TED-Ed Weekend, an event that has place every year in New York, which gathers teachers and students of clubs from all over the world to learn from each other.
The speakers of this event, such as Ashton, Olivia and Sean, are some of the students that uploaded their talks to the YouTube channel, and during this mythical weekend, have the opportunity to work on them again with experts from the TED team and with innovative and powerful tools.
When I was there, I remember they recorded with a 360° camera from the red spot on the stage, while in the audience, we pretended to listen carefully to be able to rehearse an immersive virtual reality with the future speakers.
Thanks to TED-Ed Weekend, I realized how important it is a global network convinced of the importance of giving the youths a voice, and not isolated clubs because the impact is multiplied, it allows to learn from the best practices of others and establish new relationships and synergies.
Since then, I work with others, who just like me, believe in the potential of the youths and want to help them to develop it by creating a TED-Ed Club.
Little by little, among all, we increase the number of students who believe in their own ideas and are not afraid to share them with others.
It is clear that oral communication is a fundamental tool for all the adults of the future and that we must master it.
Even though, children start to speak at 1 or 2 years of age.
At 4 or 5, they communicate fluently.
And at 12, they have a vocabulary of 1000 or 2000 words.
But since then, they only learn more vocabulary.
In the best case, if they turn into actors or singers, they will learn to modulate their voices, to recite, or to move on a stage.
But from that moment in which we express ourselves with real capacity we are not educated in the spoken word anymore, we are not taught to convey emotions, to persuade, to make a point and dialogue, or even to speak in public.
The word is essential, but even the most sophisticated oratory is worthless without a message, an idea.
And we don't even strive to teach to recognize ideas that have potential of change, or to create a safe environment in which the youngest are able to play with their own ideas, learning at the same time from ideas of others.
What a better recent example of the power of communication joined to ideas than Me Too movement, started on Twitter in October of 2017 by the actress Alyssa Milano when she encouraged her followers to share their own experiences with sexual harassment, to visualize the magnitude of this problem.
The hashtag turned into trending topic in more than 85 countries, being used more than 3 million times.
It all started on social media with the brave complaint of a woman but it didn't stay there, Me Too has encouraged thousands of conversations around the whole world since then, even becoming protagonist of the last edition of the Golden Globes.
In a huge display of activism, many of the people present claimed the need for the change.
But the real protagonist of the day was the speech of the journalist Oprah Winfrey that ended with a strong ovation in the audience and its subsequent media repercussion.
But historically, we also see the transformer force of the ideas joined to communication, and in many cases, by the students.
Many social movements have their origins in the student field, like Little Rock Nine, in 1918, at the United States.
Nine Afro-American students in a school racially segregated until then, who had to attend class guarded by the police for danger of lynching, and who turned into the center of a movement against racism in education.
Or the students of Le French May, on 1968, who caused a series of protests that triggered the biggest general strike in the history of France, causing the cessation of the president in advance of the elections.
Or during the same year, in our country, the student struggle against Franco, with such peculiar events like the concert in Complutense University of the Catalan songwriter, Raimon, who, despite violating the ban on the right of assembly, and the ban on the right to express oneself in languages other than Spanish, gathered thousands of students as a symbol of protest.
All these movements started with words, with youths denouncing, claiming for a better future, presenting a world not how it was, but how it could be.
We have to claim that active and innovative function in youths because, as I could see in that TED-Ed Weekend in New York, even the youngest are full of ideas with lots of potential.
With TED-Ed Clubs, or other tools, we need to give voice to the youths so they can be part of the change, and this is something we need to stop procrastinating in.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
This is Prometheus.
A Titan, friend of the mortals.
In Greek mythology, it was Prometheus' duty to make sure that humans had everything they needed.
Prometheus, however, saw us as vulnerable. He saw us without protection and decided to do something about it.
Distrusting of the gods of Olympus, he decided to steal the fire of Hephaestus and the wisdom of Athena. He brought them to Earth,
he bestowed them upon us.
He gave us technology and the ability to use it so that we wouldn't die of cold.
However, he was not successful.
Because even with technology and our ability to use it, we remained isolated.
And as such we continued being easy prey for natural threats.
Zeus, ruler of the gods,
ruled that the work of Prometheus was incomplete,
and added two things to humanity: the sense of justice, and power.
Thus it was possible for mankind to begin to unite, to found cities, to cooperate.
I love this story. It's a lovely story.
The moral of this myth is that humans aren't just another animal that live on this planet, but we are political animals, and that technology or knowledge mean nothing if we aren't together.
Effort or individual abilities mean nothing if we can't find ways to live together.
I love this myth because it associates human virtue with our ability to do politics.
This myth is also the renaissance of democracy.
Democracy is a lifestyle which has to do with engaging with the common good, with respecting the positions of others, with choosing dialogue over violence.
But democracy can also be seen as a system. it's a series of rules to abide by, which give us a certain order.
Lifestyle and society, they run parallel
as long as they are synchronized. Democracy is vital.
This is a Parliament from the 19th century, and this is a Parliament from the 21st century.
These photos should make us think.
The system that we have today is a system which we inherited from the 19th century, It's definitively a more liberal system.
And while society has changed, the system has changed very little.
Today we are watching from all sides as this 19th century system brought into the 21st century, is cracking.
And it's cracking so obviously.
Isn't it? The way we relate to each other changed, the way we get information, the way we create things, the way we communicate. And the system has changed very little.
Our lifestyle has changed a lot, and the system has changed very little.
This is a problem, because synchronized like this with the customs of the people, this system is impermeable, it's slow, and it's imprecise.
It's the foundation of the distinction between representative and represented from this theory of the 19th century, comes the distinction between knowing and not knowing.
This is an assessment that was written by very important and intelligent people who were on the point of leaving the formal political administration in the hands of the experts: the representatives who could decide what the constituents needed.
Today, reserving knowledge like this for the powerful elite, in the middle of the 21st century, is absolutely absurd.
We live in times where ideas are broadcast very easily, where points of view are multiplying, where knowledge is being transformed.
Our societies are complex, fast, and they reject the expert as a leader.
Politics cannot be the monopoly of knowledge by some people; it has to be the ability to coordinate dispersed knowledge.
So, how do we do it?
We have to create an interactive mechanism between the representatives and the represented which goes beyond a vote every two - four years.
Technology can help us.
Software can help us. It can help us to develop these exact skills.
With all this in mind, we created DemocracyOS.
DemocracyOS is a digital platform designed to organise collaborative conversations; designed to inform, debate, and vote for public initiatives.
Last year, based on DemocracyOS, we launched, in the legislature of Buenos Aires, a program which we called "Demos."
Through this program, citizens, any citizen of Buenos Aires, could choose which laws they wanted to address and they could debate them.
In the space of four weeks, more than 13,000 people visited Demos, and the three most voted laws had more than 650 comments which could be translated into concrete support.
This shows us two things which act as conclusions:
Firstly, when it is the public who decide which proposed legislation they want to discuss, the minorities have more opportunities.
This is the case due to logic.
When it is us, the citizens, who vote, we focus more on the quality of the ideas than on the weight of political parties within Parliament which is usually how agendas are constructed.
Second conclusion: The citizens could contribute and their contributions were useful.
Their contributions could be incorporated into the system, their contributions were consistent with parliamentary practice.
At some point we thought we were in a good position.
We created a method to decide collectively on political initiatives.
We did it through a simple technology; easy to use, easy to understand, easy to be managed by different types of people.
I believe that the simplicity of the idea and the capabilities of the platform mean that we were successful.
However, there are a few issues, there are some aspects that we need to correct in the future.
These are the aspects we are dealing with today: How to make DemocracyOS into a platform for civil education?
An education in being critical, in independence, and in responsibility?
How to institutionalize these new processes? How to bind them to the law? How to make the results of votes on DemocracyOS representative? Above all, how to make those who don't usually express themselves on public issues start to make their voices heard?
Ultimately, how to link this new public space, that is cyberspace, with the physical public space that we have always had?
All of these digressions, these thoughts, these discussions, that have to do with democratic forms, don't have to distance us from the basics, from the challenges that we have ahead. From small challenges like the pothole opposite my house, the problems with the plaza in the next block; to the enormous challenges of our Latin American cities;
how to solve the housing crisis; how to fight against civil insecurity; how to avoid the abuse of natural resources;
we must understand that if we don't find more plural ways of working, the megacities that we have are going to pose problems.
And ultimately, we have to dedicate ourselves to creating tools for self-organisation and collective decision-making.
Technology can help us on this path.
From the Gutenberg Printing Press to Twitter, we have seen how great inventions can aid the democratic impulse.
However, we would be making a mistake if we stayed only using tools because the great revolutionary aspects of humankind happen through efforts to create collective action, and that is where we return to Zeus and politics, because it continues to be the art which the god of gods left to us that is the main tool of social change.
And if it's really true, then politics can be done from many places.
We believed that to make an impact, to create change change, we had to enter the system. We had to enter parliament and change it from within.
That was how we founded the Network Party.
The Network Party is a local political party, in the city of Buenos Aires that ran in the last elections and obtained 22,000 votes, a very favourable result considering that we campaigned with practically no money and with only an idea, with a contract that we proposed to the citizens of Buenos Aires.
We said to them: If we are elected, we are going to always vote in accordance with what the public decide on an online platform on which they will be able to inform themselves, debate, and vote.
This means no more or less than that we, who were going to be sat there, were going to put each vote up for public consideration. And if the public said, for example,
"Are we going to vote? Are we,
the citizens, going to vote? Yes!"
This contract, this controversial promise, deliberately provoking, controversial, provoked all kinds of adjectives. They called us everything from avant garde to idiots.
And we believe that we are none of those things.
We are trying to put a face on the idea of what democracy we need in the 21st century.
To begin a new path is exciting.
It is about thinking, doing, learning, and trying again.
Our strategy is to create cultural change, and above all, to regain the idea that the future is ours to create.
And that we, citizens, are the ones who have to drive this process.
I am from Buenos Aires.
The Network Party is the child of Buenos Aires, and one of the interpreters of our streets, Gustavo Cerati, once wrote: "You will see me fly above the city of fury, where nobody knows of me and I am part of everything."
We believe that if our city, where we are part of everything, knows more about us, that fury could begin to subside.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
[We asked Kiki Petrone to write a song with the questions we asked ourselves thinking about the next 10 years.]
I hope you enjoy this song as much as we enjoyed making our music.
You can clap your hands to the music if you wish.
