Kiki Petrone in da house! (Applause)
Colombia is a country ravaged by huge political and economic inequalities.
To discuss political inequality, I'll introduce the "Madres de Soacha" to you, sadly, famous mothers whose sons were assassinated and later portrayed as guerrillas killed in combat.
The phenomena known as the "false positives" scandal, hides the horror of the murders and claimed many civilian lives.
We do not know exactly how many.
Data from NGOs like CINEP estimates that around 1,500 young men were killed
and according to victims' associations, over 3,000 victims.
I want you to imagine these 3,000 false positives were not poor people often living in far-away corners of the country but rather a full cohort of graduates from the 25 schools present in this slide.
This slide presents a total of 25 schools from the Union of International Schools of Bogotá, the UNCOLI.
I'd like you to ask yourself the following question: if this were the case, do you think the president at the time would have remained as popular?
Do you think he could have even stayed in power?
Or can you even imagine that in Colombia an entire cohort of graduates from these schools could be murdered, portrayed as guerrillas and nothing much happened afterwards?
I bet the answer is likely no to these very uncomfortable questions.
With this I want to prove that we have a democracy that even if it's a democracy, is still very imperfect in that we experience a huge political inequality where the needs, sufferings, and preferences of several individuals in our society weight more than those of others, and when it comes to things we demand from our leaders as public policy, and so on. With this I hope I proved there is significant political inequality.
Also, remember the false positives turned into a scandal precisely when they occurred in Soacha, near Bogotá, even though they had occurred elsewhere in the country.
Again, some areas matter more than other areas of the country.
So, anyways, we have a lot of political inequality.
I want to talk now about economic inequality.
Although, if you allow me a short intervention, just to clarify, this is sorted in alphabetical order, not from best to worst.
The best one is clearly towards the bottom the one with three trees, I can say that objectively.
So let's discuss economic inequality.
In terms of economic inequality, I can give you a very telling figure.
If we take the poorest 10% of Colombians, we'll need 44 people from this group, to balance the income of only one person in the richest 10%.
Another symptom of economic inequality is that in Colombia it is very common, even after a long professional career, to be asked in a job interview: "Which school did you attend?"
Or, even not in a job interview but in informal conversation, when you are quite seasoned.
What exactly does that say about me elsewhere?
In Colombia, the answer to that question says a lot about me.
About where I come from, my family, the opportunities I had.
That is, since we Colombians are born, many of our opportunities are limited, or we have more or less opportunities depending on what we get at birth.
This creates a number of vicious cycles.
And I will talk about one of those vicious cycles.
A vicious cycle not in the sense of a Diego Ospina promoting cycling
- that is a cycle that generates virtue for those addicted to cycling -
not a vicious cycle in that sense. But a vicious cycle that refers back to a situation where several conditions remain in equilibrium, feeding back and on each other, and it is very difficult to escape such a bad situation.
This cycle that I will talk about is "The public goods trap".
To talk about the "the public goods trap" I will illustrate with education.
Colombia, between 1998 and 2011, spent 4.15% of GDP in education.
If we drew a line, from the top spender down, on a list of 178 countries on which we have data, Colombia would be placed at 97.
That is to say we are that last short guy over there.
We are dwarfs
at education spending. We, who are great at jumping queues, yet in this case we obediently wait,
we are not jumping it.
We should jump that queue, right?
That low education spending isn't just insufficient in quantity, also in quality.
It's well known that compared to our income levels, the success of young Colombians in tests is lower than expected.
This implies that everyone with the means, tries to fill this gap privately.
And we can see this with data from the Household Panel Survey of Universidad los Andes. And we don't have to go to top income levels.
It suffices to compare the 1st and 4th strata.
Looking at kids going to school in stratum 1, 9 of 10 of them attend, or have no other option but attending public schools.
If you have a higher income you search for alternatives to compensate for the limits of public education via private education.
In stratum 4 the ratio is the opposite: 9 of 10 go to private schools.
With this example I may now explain the vicious cycle, the public goods trap.
In Colombia, we have a low supply of public goods, a low supply of public goods implies that those who can afford it privately obtain the goods that the state is not providing.
This situation is sustained by, but also generates, inequality.
It is sustained because inequality is needed so that a few groups have high enough income to privately get the goods the state fails to provide.
But of course this creates inequality because those with no means must stay with lower-quality public goods or simply without any, thus lacking services that are key to welfare and productivity.
As a result, something else happens: demand for public goods falls.
Because the most powerful people in society, those who privately overcame the limitations of our state, are not urged to demand from leaders: "Give us this or that other public good!"
And so demand falls.
And if demand falls, politicians indeed are not worried to produce a strong supply, and supply falls.
Thus, in this vicious cycle, a low supply of public goods lowers demand of public goods, and it is hard to escape this circle, sustained by, but also creating, inequality.
This happens in several areas, not just education.
A famous and clear example of a public good we lack, in quantity and quality, is security.
And thus a typical Colombian character, so typical that it is one of the roles played by comedian Jaime Garzón, working in a building called precisely Colombia Building, is the private security guard.
If we had more security, we wouldn't have to devote as many resources to protection, and these people could be using their talents more productively.
And this is not the most harmful way of providing private security.
The most harmful one is well known: the paramilitary.
They, together with guerrillas in an armed conflict, have violated human rights in this country for years.
So this is another example. And we could say Colombia has a paramilitary culture.
By paramilitary culture I do not mean that we have paramilitary groups, but the general culture that, if we can, what the government fails to provide, we provide privately.
This paramilitary culture, this lack of public goods, stands out when we have a chance to live abroad.
While doing my PhD, I lived 6 years in Boston,
and one of the things I enjoyed most was running by the Charles River.
Here I am running by the Charles River.
Stop and think for a second about all the pubic goods you enjoy during such an activity.
Security - I was confident I wouldn't get robbed, no matter the time - clean air, respect among
cars and pedestrians, the public space in itself.
Several public goods that one can enjoy in cities of developed countries.
In Colombian cities, this is harder to find.
Not impossible, but harder.
So, what do people do?
Well paramilitary culture dictates you provide these goods privately.
So people join a club, go to a gym.
In my case, I work at Universidad de los Andes, so I can go to the University's gym, I feel safe and I exercise.
But here lies one of the problems of the public goods trap.
Not only those with no chance of joining a gym, or a club, and so on, are excluded. But also it is way more enjoyable to go out for a run in the city, interact with all its diversity, open spaces, than running inside one of these places like a hamster.
The public good is inevitably better.
Of course you could say I am being too pessimistic, that Bogotá is not such a terrible mess, that we have some very valuable public goods.
I fully agree.
For instance, here is Bogotá's map, from Google.
And we have the Simón Bolívar park, and it is great to run there.
I do it, and enjoy it a lot, and I have proof. Here is my selfie running in the park.
I suggest you buy watermelon or mango after the run, delicious.
So, agreed: we have something.
It's not like we have nothing and we are doomed.
But there are limitations because of the public goods trap.
For instance, the Simón Bolívar has a fence.
So it doesn't fully integrate with the city, possibly out of security concerns.
And it is not just surrounded by a fence, but also to get there, the public goods one can find in places like Boston are missing here; fresh air, cars respecting pedestrians, public spaces to get there.
How wonderful would it be if we, instead, removed the fence not just around the park but around all the great things the city has in the area. And connect surrounding neighborhoods.
If we are more ambitious, the National University is nearby, and it could become a great park if we removed its fence too.
If we are even more ambitious, we can connect this with all the city parks.
To the north with Virrey, to the east with National, and to the south with Timiza.
And have tentacles penetrating all the neighborhoods around.
And reach a linear park we all dream about by the eastern hills.
There is some, but we can have better things, if we escape the public goods tragedy.
The problem with the public goods tragedy is that it not only affects the goods we have access to but also how we behave.
In the paramilitary culture we see ourselves privately. Everybody else is potentially dangerous, a threat, an enemy.
And so as stated by our chief philosopher today, Jaime Garzón, the problem is we have no collective consciousness.
And we behave as if the rest of the world didn't exist, we ignore others, or we see them as a threat.
It's trite but we live it daily when hopping to the public bus system, or when using an elevator, or when driving.
When you are driving anywhere in the world, you flash your turn signals, and other drivers say: "Ah, you will turn, I will slow down so that all is easier and we don't crash".
Here, I use the turn signal and I am telling other drivers: "Hurry up before I turn!" others accelerate and we may crash.
It is even safer
not to use the turn signals.
Anyway. There are many examples of this.
Of course I am a bit worried that the examples I find daily are because I am turning I am turning let's say... older and grumpier about these things.
So for instance with neighbors. People don't seem to worry about them.
I have lived in many places in Bogotá, where neighbors are too noisy and do not care at all about the implications for neighbors.
Concerned that this is because I am now more sensitive, I can't sleep easily.
I made a semi-scientific search in Google Fight which compares the frequency of different combinations of terms online.
I searched for 'soundproof windows Colombia' and 'soundproof windows' with other countries.
I could confirm, fortunately, that it is not that I am getting older and grumpier but that indeed Colombia stands out. It is the country where this paramilitary protection against others gets the most attention in the region.
What do we do about this?
Had you grown up, say, during the 80s Spanish rock, there is an easy answer.
Los Prisioneros have the answer in a very famous song called "Why don't you leave?"
"If you dream of New York or Europe, if culture is so rich in Germany,
and, something true in my case, if you aren't Gonzalez or Tapias why don't you leave?"
You could ask: why don't you leave if you complain so much?
It's true, but even if I leave, I will remain concerned about the public goods trap in Colombia.
So, what can be done?
The truth is at least in theory the vicious circle could become virtuous.
Imagine we had a big push on the supply side and we offered a lot of public goods.
Then we wouldn't have to provide them privately.
And there would be more equality because everyone, regardless of income, could have goods for their welfare and productivity.
Demand wouldn't be low but high, we would demand many public goods.
We would have a virtuous circle where high supply creates a high demand of public goods, and so on.
There are some cases where this occurred, some local governments have offered public goods, and people have embraced them and demanded them.
Examples like the one we saw of people getting together to demand public goods, saying "let's respect public spaces,
and the right of using public goods."
But somehow we don't take the leap.
These are examples but we persist and don't move from the vicious to the virtuous circle.
And here I want to say what the fundamental reason is: notice than while inequality is a byproduct of this process it's also crucially its basis.
Inequality sustains the vicious circle, equality sustains a virtuous circle.
And regarding inequality, how can we solve it?
Well, we must deal with the economic and the political inequality I mentioned at the beginning of my talk.
Both, we must attack both.
But I would argue the most important of all is political inequality, for the following: if preferences, wishes, needs of some individuals in society matter more than those of others, which is what political inequality means, surely those with more political power, with the capacity to make the state work for them, will use it to improve their conditions and enrich themselves.
That is, political inequality produces economic inequality.
Economic inequality can also create political inequality: if money is concentrated in a few hands, those with a lot of money will try to capture the state in their favor.
But at least in theory it is possible that if political institutions are sufficiently strong, even if some are very rich and others very poor, political institutions will listen to the less well off and respond to their needs.
So we have to work in both dimensions but it is absolutely crucial to solve the problem of not having political equality, where some groups lack equal access to formal political power in Colombia, and that their needs are heard as loudly as they should.
Well, even so you could ask:

"What do I do? What are you doing?"
And I could pad myself in the back, as Bush does here with Putin, and say: "I do something as a professor at Universidad de los Andes, I am an economist, but my research area concerns, precisely, understanding why some countries have better political institutions than others, which features of institutions are most important to promote, how are they promoted, where do they come from."
I could encourage myself and think that I am one of those Keynes describes: "Practical men, who believe to be exempt from any intellectual influence, are usually slaves of a defunct economist."
So I could give myself that pad in the back and think: "Well, I will ultimately influence public policy."
But I do not want to give myself that pad in the back. Because in Keynes's quote, he says it is a dead economist.
And I do not want to be dead by the time Colombian society changes and hopefully we move to a situation with more equality and a virtuous circle and not having a public good tragedy but rather a comedy.
This is why I thought the first thing I could do was sharing these ideas with you so that we do not miss any opportunity we have to make Colombian society more economically equal, and especially politically equal. Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Hello.
Today I want to tell you about the importance of telling stories.
While my five-year-old daughter, Violeta, loves it when I read her stories before she goes to sleep, what she likes the most is for me to make up stories.
And just beginning with... "Once upon a time. there was a girl who lived in a forest". "No,no,no", she says, "better if it's a squirrel".
So... "Once upon a time, there was a squirrel called Carolina, who lived in a forest". "No, better if she's called Florecita Rosita".
And so I end up telling stories like the one about a squirrel called Florecita Rosita, who is rescued by a giant wolf called Colmillo, who, for over 100 years, had been sleeping inside a mountain.
At that moment, between rivers of imagination captivating her curiosity, the story brings us closer.
Through the story we share, she has the tools to discover the world around us, as well as ourselves, but also to feel fear from a safe place, knowing that everything's going to end well, because, if not, nobody will sleep in our house.
She learns about traditions, culture, moral dilemmas, but also the importance of absurdity and laughing at herself, not taking herself too seriously.
When I was a boy, I didn't have any friends in the neighbourhood. And as my parents worked all day, the TV was my best friend.
I loved watching TV, I even watched the test card while waiting for the programmes to begin.
But what I loved the most about TV was the quantity of stories that I could find there.
I watched all of them without discrimination, from the man with X-ray vision, to E.T.
From Willy Wonka to a remake of the Wizard of Oz with an entirely African-American cast.
I saw the original years later, thinking it was a remake, the best remake ever done in history.
(Laughter) Also, whenever I could, I watched late-night movies and many times, those stories kept me awake, or I just couldn't understand them.
I still watched them whenever I could, because my house was run on a strict timetable.
So my mom used to tell me, "Daniel, it's eight o'clock, time for bed".
"But mom, it's still daytime".
"Don't argue, go to bed and turn off the light."
"It's not light, mother, it's the sun".
We could say that stories appeared practically in the moment in which language appeared, satisfying the human need to describe our experiences using a story.
And these also worked as a glue, reinforcing community bonds, connecting the present to the past and future.
Nowadays, stories continue being very important and an intrinsic part of our culture and society; they are in films, books, on the radio, in the media, in architecture, in fashion, in religion, wherever you look, they are there; defining our values, our prejudices, our dreams, what we hate, and also determining every aspect of our lives.
Perhaps we could think that, after so many centuries of telling stories there is nothing new to tell, right?
We could be right, as Renato Díaz said, "Every plot in the world could be summarized in just 20 main plots".
And Joseph Campbell talks about the hero's journey, which is present in so many stories and folk tales, where the hero goes through the same adventures in different cultures.
When I wasn't watching stories, I was making them up, playing with action figures, with Lego, or perched on a tree, or simply on my bed staring at the ceiling.
The important part is that, to this day, those stories are still relevant to the lives that we live now. In that sense, there's no need for us to go to jail, or to be a neurosurgeon doing a brain transplant, or to be a sponge with squared pants who lives under the sea in order to write those stories.
All we have to do is to be totally honest with ourselves and expose ourselves as we are, so that we can bring that into our stories.
This is the only way to make our stories authentic: to give them our positive and negative obsessions, our fears and our dreams, to give them part of ourselves.
It's not as easy as it sounds, because to do that, we have to make ourselves vulnerable, naked, and to face people who can criticize and make cruel comments.
But that's also the cost of telling stories.
In order to bring a story to life, we have to give it part of ourselves.
So I invite you to dare to look for those stories inside yourselves.
If you are not good at telling or writing them, you can also sing them or act them out or paint them.
And if you feel stuck, you can start with the classic words, "Once upon a time..."
Thank you so much.
(Applause)
I went to a party a few months ago,
at a schoolmate's house. I showed up with some other mates from our class.
The party was OK.
Good music, dim light, I met a few people; overall a nice atmosphere.
Soon we approached some friends of my friends and I spotted someone I liked.
I didn't pay them much attention at first, but later I came closer to talk because this person was the only one who didn't know anyone.
I felt attracted to their cool and ease to bring up a conversation so we spent the rest of the party together.
The person I met was Juan.
Later that night, back home, my head was a mess.
Never before it had happened to me that I got hooked with a guy.
When I go to a party I'm not thinking who I'm going to relate with.
I'm looking for fun and to enjoy it.
However, I didn't think I could like him.
Because it was not usual for me.
It was against what was natural to me.
Also that night so many questions were rumbling in my head.
For example, what criteria do we consider to determine if we like someone or not?

Their physique? Their gender?
Their personality?
A combination of traits?
It wasn't clear to me either why I was asking myself these questions.
If I had no doubts about other things.
There was no answer to them.
Why do I like certain foods so much?
I don't know! I like them, that's all!
This whole situation...
the questions, the answers, led me to think about two things.
First, why do we have the need to pigeonhole people for their bodies, their gender, personality or brains?
Second, how come we end up liking someone for these traits?
It's not very clear yet for me.
But I think that we work with a sort of filter that tells the good from the bad of a person given our criteria.
After a long time I concluded that this criteria, for me, was just if I liked his person and if I felt comfortable in their company.
I didn't care who that person was.
I didn't care what they liked either, if they were male or female, or whatever.
If that person liked me and I liked that person, there was nothing else to worry about.
I see sexual orientation not as a gender of preference, but as the set of filters with which each of us one chooses.
For me there's no such thing as being gay, straight, pansexual, bisexual... because I don't understand the concept of gender.
I don't understand what makes you male or what makes you female.
Therefore, I don't understand either sexuality as it's usually regarded.
Even when it's still difficult for us to determine our tastes.
Why is that?
Because the idea of ​​love under this social system we live and relate in is to be married by 30, in a monogamous relationship, with a partner of your opposite sex, with two children --eldest son, youngest daughter--. That's what each of us are taught consciously and unconsciously. And not to follow what
each of us deeply like.
These filters were changed, modified and even loosened because they had the opportunity to be rethought throughout history.
I think we live in a particular time, where not only a wide but deep reflection on these filters is going on.
And also a reduction of those filters that were imposed by someone other than you.
I see this every day.
On the street, in marches, at school... And that's a great thing!
Because we are increasingly more the ones who think like that.
And that's what follows in the fight for free sexual expression rights.
And this happens to me: I don't have in mind the gender filter.
And maybe in 5, 10, 20 years, other people won't have it either.
If this filter didn't exist in people's heads, some serious current problems wouldn't take place, like discrimination, inequality, and the inequality of rights for being who you are.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I love programming since I was a little boy, and I always wanted to learn a lot.
At school I was learning a little, but I wanted to learn more things and faster.
Almost by chance, I had an experience that sped up my whole learning process incredibly.
Let me tell you a story.
My father is a firefighter.
At home, we talk about solidarity and how to help other people.
One day, my father came home a little shocked by the subject, and told me he heard that in Argentina every day, one person goes missing.
"How come one person goes missing each day?"
"Yes, one person goes missing, and can't be found by their family, and they are desperate and anxious."
There I thought that maybe, I could do something with my passion, programming, to help solve this problem.
And then, I came up with an idea for a smartphone application because almost all of us have one, and thus I could create a network of people connected through this application that helps find missing people.
So, when someone is missing, we warn the whole network about it, and we have a lot of people looking for them everywhere.
And it's a lot easier and faster to find them this way.
To accomplish that I programmed for many months.
It's not that easy because there are a lot of problems to take care of, and I didn't know how to do some things at first.
I went to a workshop after school, as well, that helped me a lot with programming at the beginning.
And, some months ago, I started talking to a NGO that deals with this.
In fact, it's called "Missing Persons."
And I showed them this application I created, and they loved it!
Together, we saw what it was needed to be improved and changed.
In fact, I have the pleasure to announce that since last week, it's online as the official application to search for missing people in Argentina.
The application I could develop in my free time.
(Applause) During the last months, I learned a lot about programming.
Maybe the same I could have learned during my many years at school.
And not only that: I also learned about missing persons, about how to work with a NGO, and how to ask for help.
And I realized that I learned all this because I had the idea in my head that I wanted to solve this problem.
But to dare to do it was not that easy.
At first, I stumbled upon a lot of prejudices such as I was very young, it was very difficult, or that I would waste my time.
In brief, that I couldn't do it.
So, what did I need to start?
First, I needed a problem to be really motivated.
For example, before this, I was creating an application to organize football games between friends.
But the truth is organizing football games is not something that motivates me that much.
Then, I heard about the problem of the missing persons.
And in this case it wouldn't be the same for me if I could solve it or not
it was something I needed to solve.
So, without much thought, I started to get involved into the world of learning by doing.
Where you don't know how to do what you want to do.
You have never learned or been taught about it, but you want to do it.
So, you use all the tools you have to try to do it.
And if it still doesn't work, you keep on trying because you want to do it.
This led me to the second thing I needed, which was access to information.
And today this is available: you just need access to the Internet.
I watched tutorials for hours and hours to learn what I needed to know.
And I also visited thousand websites on programming to answer my questions.
And finally, it seems that when you talk about learning this way you learn by yourself, but it's not like that.
In fact, in this process there were many people who helped me a lot.
Each time I had a question or didn't know how to do something, they helped me solve it.
They also helped me get in contact with people, get organized and learn that mistakes are part of the learning process.
This happened for a year.
At the beginning, I knew almost nothing, and had to do very complicated things for this project.
And now, after going through this experience of trying to do something without knowing how, I can see that all I know on this subject I learned it this way.
And I did it out of school requirements.
I wonder if it has to be this way.
I'm interested in programming since I was young, well, younger, and in my family, helping others is always present and concerns us.
For me, creating this application in my free time came naturally, but there are a lot of kids who perhaps are interested in other things.
What would happen if we created a space for them in our schools so each of them could try to solve a problem that matters and interests him a lot.
And almost without realizing it, to learn a lot in the process.
Thank you very much.
