Up until a few weeks ago, I was a producer and an actress in a project that's really important for my career not only because of its artistic value, but because I conceived it as a way of saying, "Fernanda wants to say this to the world."
But why does it matter what Fernanda Castillo wants to say to the world?
Who am I? Why are my words important?
Why are you going to care about what I want to say?
A couple of days ago, as Manuel said, I arrived at the airport.
It was 1am so, in truth, I was more asleep than awake.
I arrived and went up to the lady at the check-in counter.
I was kind of falling asleep while I was giving her my papers, and I heard her telling me, "Was it hard to get to where you are now?"
Honestly, I was thinking, "It's my second flight in 24 hours. I'm afraid of not being able to fit into my dress when I get to New York.
I haven't eaten, and I haven't slept."
And I, who am usually quite talkative, just answered, "Yes, it was hard."
(Laughter) And then she said,
"But it was worth it, right? You enjoy it.
Because I'm here, behind this counter, and I hate what I do.
I always wanted to study performance art, but I was afraid of starving to death."
And her words woke me up.
Honestly, I said, "What a shame that I am complaining in my head without appreciating the fact that I can do what I love most in life, as many of us do here, while many others don't have this chance."
So without thinking, I told her, "Many times during my career I wasn't able to pay the rent.
And now I'm proud that I didn't let the obstacles impede me.
So, overcome your fear, leave this place, and do what you really like, because even if you end up starving to death, you'll somehow enjoy it."
She thanked me, gave me my documents, and smiled.
And I was so proud for having told her what I thought.
I spent the next five hours of flight time thinking, "Oh my god - what if I've just messed up her life?"
(Laughter) "What if she really follows my advice?"
Or maybe not.
No one changes their life because of some random person's words at 1am at the airport.
But then I started thinking, "But if she shared something so personal with me, then maybe I'm not some random person to her.
Maybe I'm Mónica Robles to her. So, maybe she does care."
And then I realized that I have a voice.
I have a voice because I'm being heard.
And I was struck by fear.
(Laughter) What a great responsibility to have.
Human beings have always sought art as a way to identify themselves.
And it's no coincidence that art has accompanied great revolutions and social movements during every time period, in all countries.
There have always been artists who weren't content with just looking at things from afar and say what they were told to say, or think what they were told to think, and instead, they think what they want and put their thoughts into their art.
They never care if they are going against the system.
So they always impact the world.
Throughout all of history. I'm talking about artists like Victor Hugo, who wrote "Les Misérables"; like García Lorca in Spain; like Émile Zola; writers like Pablo Neruda and his poetry; artists like Pablo Picasso and his painting "Guernica"; artists like Diego Rivera and his mural at Rockefeller Center.
I'm talking about musicians like John Lennon, Joan Baez, Pablo Milanés, Silvio Rodríguez; musicians of our day like Bebe, who sings about violence committed against women; like Calle 13, who speak their mind; like Pink, who wrote a song addressing Bush, asking him how he could sleep at night, knowing that he was sending soldiers to war.
These rebellious artists have always existed.
These artists are committed to their ideas, they defend them and impact the world to which they belong.
I have an influence, too. I am, in a way, an educator of society, whether consciously or not.
Because with what I do, with the way I act and think, I guide people who look up to me, although it's not my goal.
Again, it's scary, right?
Because I can be impacting people's lives while just eating sweet potatoes, without even realizing it.
I can be acting like a fool and still impact lives.
So if I have a voice and I'm being heard, I better be saying something worth listening to and that changes lives.
While preparing for this conference, I stumbled upon a post which was very painful to read, but that I think has a lot of truth to it, so I would like to share it with you. "Unfortunately, the word 'responsibility' is out.
People are afraid of thinking.
To think or to feel critically is seen as making a big deal about things.
To speak out against something is not cool.
I would even say that reacting, in general, is frowned upon.
Nowadays, you're cool if nothing affects you, you don't care about anything, and you don't disapprove of anything.
This is the way young artists think.
If you disapprove of something, you're judging, you're casting the first stone.
So you end up adopting a corporate behavior that's politically correct. It's super passive and cowardly, but it's disguised as something cool and respectful."
I'm sorry, but I don't think that artists can be bohemians, separated from reality, indifferent to reality, without purpose.
Quite the opposite.
An artist is meant to be a messenger of reality.
And something even scarier: the artist is also a message.
What I mean by that is, we not only have an impact on the people who see the roles or projects from our acting career.
We also influence people by the way we think and act.
That's terrifying!
I think it was the fine print in the "I want to be an artist" contract, which many "artists" I know signed, without even being aware of it.
I was aware of this mission
from a very early age. I know that I have a responsibility as an actress and a public person to be part of social movements in my country, because if I march to demand answers, together with the parents of the ABC Childcare tragedy, if I question the government, then the people who follow me will know that I care, and they'll know something even more important, that they can do it, too; they can question things.
If I exercise my freedom of expression on social networks, if I support minorities, if I share my failures and flaws with the people who follow me, then I'm giving people who admire me a lesson in courage.
Likewise, I try to do it not only as a person, but also through my projects.
I think the characters choose you, in a metaphysical way - not through casting, but by choosing someone who will represent them by doing a good job at conveying an important message.
I've portrayed good women and bad women,
I've portrayed Daniela Montalvo in "Distilling Love," who defended her beloved, who is black-skinned, from discrimination by her family.
I portrayed a completely naïve and insecure woman in "Teresa," as Luisa de la Barrera.
I portrayed a woman committed to love and friendship in the musical "Today, I Can't Get Up," during the "Madrid" number.
I played Elisa, a woman who realizes the value of her life when she starts her fight against cancer.
I am grateful to all these roles that I had during my career.
But four years ago, I had the chance to portray a risky character.
It was risky because it was Mónica Robles. She's a drug dealer.
What might people think that says about my social conscience?
People usually challenge me, "But you're encouraging young people to become drug dealers!"
And portraying this character challenges me to say, "I can't agree that if she's a drug dealer, therefore she is evil."
I have to say, "This woman is a product of the environment she grew up in.
She is a woman who makes decisions and lives with those consequences."
And if we don't want to have more Mónica Robleses or drug dealers, we have to change the world where our children are growing up so they don't grow up to be people like that, and then drug-dealing won't exist. Or government corruption.
This is a foundation.
I have to portray this character as a human being that everyone can understand.
And this character connects me to the audience in a different way.
Mónica Robles becomes a symbol of strength for all women on social media,
not only as a feminist character, but also as someone who lives by, "I will not cower in the face of life's difficulties," who inspires both men and women.
It was after all the blessings this character brought me, that one day it dawned on me, "Great things are happening to me, and I have to return this light."
So I think -
well, I didn't create it at all, because someone else wrote it, Jorge Acebo, an Argentinean playwright and director, "Wake Up Woman," that's where it comes from.
This project is a play that deals with gender violence, which, unfortunately, is often a taboo subject, because we've gotten used to it being shameful to talk about, and we only hear about it on the news.
It's about Natalia and Frederico, two characters who love each other deeply, who have a love story, but end up being in a destructive relationship. The play makes us wonder: Why do we love the way we do?
Why haven't we learned to love well?
Why don't stories of violence ever begin with, "Hello, how are you?
Nice to meet you." Slap!
No, they always start with love and charm,
and then people show their true colors.
So while on the journey
of making "Wake Up Woman," which I did with Erik Hayser - who, just to complicate things, is also my partner in real life, so the fights we had onstage were between real partners - we were determined to say, "We have to tell people that they need to think about violence within their homes, lives and within themselves."
"Wake Up Woman" was a project filled with violence, sacrifice, and pain on a stage.
And I can assure you that it was nearly as painful to do it offstage.
But there wasn't a single performance where Eric and I didn't get up on that stage and put our bodies and souls into it,
because we had a commitment to tell the public, "Maybe someone sitting here watching the play is dealing with violence in their life, and maybe this play will make them think.
Perhaps a woman will say, 'I won't let anyone abuse me ever again.' Or maybe a mother will bring her kids to watch the play and tell them, 'Look how situations of violence unfold.
Be careful when socializing.'"
I think that this project has become so important to me because it greatly impacted the people who watched it.
I believe that an artist is someone who serves a greater goal, who has a mission, who has an impact on others, wakes them up, makes them conscious; someone who, from their trenches, changes the world.
"Artist" is a very big word.
And, to be honest, I struggle every day, and I keep chasing the honor of calling myself an artist, just as I dreamed in my childhood.
To conclude, I would like to share with you an anecdote which always reminds me that kids are very wise.
I was very wise at the time, and then God knows what happened to me. My mission was always clear to me.
When I was eight years old, my parents got divorced.
I think I never fully realized how much anxiety, pain and sadness it caused.
I thought that I needed to be mature and support my parents in the process - at the age of eight.
Thank God, my parents realized that I was eight and they said, "She doesn't need to be mature.
I think this is hurting her, and we need to support our daughter."
So every two weeks, they would take me to the theater, which was something I really enjoyed.
During one of the most difficult phases of the divorce, my mom took me to the theater.
When I walked out of the play, there were actors and dancers nearby.
I told mom, "I want to do that, mama."
And mom asked, "What do you want to do?
Dance and perform onstage?"
And I said, "No.
I want to make people forget about their sadness.
Because that lady made me forget that you guys are getting divorced."
If I, Fernanda Castillo, with the work that I do, can make someone forget their pain or become conscious of it, if I can change someone's mind or make them think, "Why do I think the way I do?
Why do I feel the way I do?
Why do I do what I do?"
then it means that I am impacting their life.
And this is my commitment, and this is the voice I want to have.
Thank you very much.
(Applause) Thank you.
(Applause)
I never played soccer during recess in school; but I didn't stay seated either and I did what the rest of my friends did.
Some of my best friends didn't like sports, but for fear of being different, of the unknown, they continued, literally "stuck to the ball" during the breaks between classes.
It was really clear to me; that I wanted to pursue my passions.
For most of my life, I wanted to dedicate time to what I liked.
Since childhood, not caring about what people said, I wouldn't stop drawing, acting or painting.
Today, having recently turned nineteen, it's very simple for me to make this kind of assertions.
However, in society there's a great fear of what is different and, above all, a rejection of what is not identifiable.
As a result, unfortunately for many years I was the target fo harsh criticisms.
I remember, when I was 4 or 5, I was always drawing figures of slender and graceful women.
Figures full of color and imagination.
I fondly recall that time when, to paint the heads, I had to use the top of the glue stick; or when my little sister asked me, or not, I would draw the coats and dresses for some of her dolls.
So in high school, I spontaneously decided, to lead various creative activities.
I founded a magazine together with my best friends, I was a choir soloist for 5 years, and I led a debate team.
Fashion was already a part of me, but I didn't have any public exposure for it, rather it stayed with me as you all see, in a more personal way.
At 14, I began to almost fight myself, going by the most important catwalks in the country and also throughout Europe.
I have to say, my first contact with real fashion didn't convince me and many things still don't convince me today.
A year later, I began to use my vacation time to study art and fashion in capital cities like Paris, Florence and London.
From those studies I was lucky to make as I'm always one of the smallest, and honestly those experiences allowed me to know and come into contact with other cultures, and to understand other points of view.
They eventually helped me consolidate my love for this art.
Now I'm in undergrad, I decided... well, I wanted to choose that class option that I was thinking about many years before signing up right?
The humanities, the branch of humanities.
Not everybody is an engineer!
But certainly it is because of the study of subjects like art history, classic Greek or Latin, that one can understand or know these bases and how they support Western culture in all of its aspects.
This same year, these two years, I could combine my studies and fashion and with a good friend, who's in the audience, we created a small workshop where we developed a beneficial outlet.
With this project we continued to be united through our passion, because all of the benefits were going towards the fight against poverty, through various NGOs we collaborated with and continue collaborating with at university.
I finalized high school, and the time came for me to choose a major for college.
Finally, I'd be able to use all of my time for creativity.
At home, nobody was surprised by my decision, they know me better than anybody and firmly supported my decision.
On the other hand, my best friends celebrated my decision with me.
I see fashion as an expression of art and communication.
What makes me passionate about it can be summed up in a sentence by the Spanish designer Cristobal Balenciaga: "A good designer should be: an architect for patterns, a sculptor for form, a painter for drawing, music for harmony and philosopher for measure".
I would add: "and communicator so that message can reach everybody".
I also want to share with you all today some of my projects.
Already, I have discovered that I cannot stop loving what is different; this idea seems to stay with me,
but I certainly run away from the "mainstream" fashion, therefore what is fashionable. And I've discovered that there's a lot to do, a lot to work on and above all a lot to learn.
Fashion isn't just one of the main economic drives of the world, is one of the areas where the human factor and the environment in the processes of production. are less taken into account.
I imagine that everybody remembers that scandal about the factories in Bangladesh, for example.
That's certainly made me think these past years, and with another friend, I started to create a new project.
Within reason, I looked for better care in the production, manufacturing, and traceability of the product.
I bet on Spain, a rich and different country, a meeting point of different cultures throughout history, and a nation with a notable textile tradition.
That is my new dream, a fashion project for men, fresh and natural, that supports quality and is ethical, and that for now is in its beginning.
Before I finish, I would like to take this opportunity to send a message to all of those in charge of education: ask yourself if such a categorized educative system is worth it, when it doesn't encourage the abilities and qualities of each person.
I also have a message for all the children and young people around the world: I invite you to be different and to fight for what really makes you passionate.
Remember that Nelson Mandela was "the different one" in his community and he succeeded in putting an end to apartheid.
Mother Teresa loved differently, kissing the poorest, and she has become a model of dedication for society.
And in terms of design, Yves Saint Laurent, couldn't survive in the army during the war of Argelia, he took the alternative: fashion and its legacy survives to this day.
I don't know my future, but I often think about something that Coco Chanel said on occasion: "To be irreplaceable, one should always seek to be different".
Thanks a lot!
(Applause)
A haiku is a poem of only 17 syllables.
But what is essential in a haiku?
Its brevity calls the attention, but its meaning revolves around Kigo, a reference to nature, to the season of the year.
The haiku is a way of observing something specific in nature, and allowing that detail to describe ourselves, in an instant of connection with our surroundings.
Haiku is like an emotional snapshot that we compose with our experience.
We have lived these last decades with the industrial noise, with pollution and global warming is under debate.
In the next ten years we will understand the need for a new sensitivity towards the natural as living support of the human kind. As the means of our ability to perceive what we can potentially lose, and what we have to already begin to understand, praise, love.
Nature is poetry to discover.
That poetry that describes us.
And the new generations already observe as haiku poets.
(Applause)
Let's do a massive experiment.
To the people here in the theater, it's now the time to open your envelopes.
We are going to discuss a crime, a murder, which involves a car accident.
In a small town in Buenos Aires province a body was found to the side of the road.
The victim was hit and abandoned between Saturday night and the early hours of Sunday.
The person responsible for the crime fled and didn't assist the victim.
There are three suspects for the crime, but the police doesn't have enough evidence to arrest anyone.
The tragic accident is already known in the city, and neighbors began to spread information such as -- And there you will find quotes that are statements, testimonies of those neighbors on the three suspects in this story:
Dani, Gabi and Agus.
Read the information carefully and decide, individually, what person you think is the most suspicious: Dani, Gabi or Agus.
And, in addition, we ask you to please tell us how certain you are of that decision on a scale that goes between little and very certain.
Starting now you have one minute and a half to do that.
Let's go!
Very good! Time's up!
And with this, the first phase
of the experiment is completed.
Let's see quickly what are we going to do in the second and last part of the experiment.
Now we are not going to make decisions on an individual level, we are going to work in groups of three.
But the groups are not going to be random.
I am going to tell you how you are going to find one another.
First thing you have to do is to find two people sitting very close to you, who have the same participant number.
For now, just find one another.
And when you do, please, raise your papers.
Thank you, you can put the papers down.
You have to do only two things.
Number one: you are going to discuss for five minutes and reach a consensus, a collective conclusion, about what person you find more suspicious.
And then, in addition, you have to tell us how much in agreement you are with that group decision.
Did you see that sometimes when you are part of a team you feel that you agree with what was discussed and sometimes you feel that you don't agree?
Well, we ask you to please tell us how much do you agree on an individual level, on a scale that goes between little and very much in agreement.
You have five minutes to discuss. Starting now.
Go ahead!
Time to discuss is over.
To the people who already reached a conclusion, I remind you that with this we are done with the experiment.
(Applause) Before finishing, before leaving, I would like to share with you some of the ideas and hypotheses we had for this experiment.
First, it must be noticed that I asked you to identify the person you found most suspicious between three profiles.
And each of the profiles had positive, rather exonerating information, like, "if they drink alcohol they don't drive." And they also had negative information, like, "They have the vehicle registration card expired."
While all three profiles had positive and negative information, one of them had more incriminating information and it was the same person for all groups: Guess which person had more incriminating information?
Please, say so.
The person with more incriminating information was Dani.
Now, right this moment I already know that roughly half of this audience doesn't believe me.
And you don't believe me, because in order to realize that Dani was the person with more incriminating information you not only had to work as a team, but realize that you had repeated phrases, and phrases that were not repeated, and if you put the information together, there you could realize that Dani was the person with more incriminating information.
With this I don't mean that Dani is the correct answer.
I asked you for an opinion.
A decision that is ultimately subjective.
And you could have very good reasons to say that for you the most suspicious person is Gabi or Agus.
But what I wouldn't want is that this decision, no matter how subjective, is influenced or biased by how you had access to the information.
Or how the information was distributed within the group.
That's why we had two experimental conditions.
Groups from 1 to 500 had complete information.
That is, they could see all the information of all the profiles before deciding.
Groups from 501 to 1000 had the information distributed.
That essentially means they only saw six statements per profile, part of that information was shared, another part was not shared, and they had the difficult task to realize that Dani was the most suspicious person.
These are my ideas but, beyond them, do you want to see the results?
Audience: Yes.
Raise your hand those from groups 1 to 500 which chose Dani.
Only those who chose Dani.
Well, we see a pretty large majority.
Now, those from groups 501 to 1000, raise your hand those who chose Dani.
Considerably less people.
Well, we believe -- and you just experienced it first hand -- it's something that happens to us human beings, that is the shared information bias.
It is a tendency we have to discuss what we have in common over distributed things, the unique things we have, things that paradoxically can be those that makes us work better as a team.
Do you know what is the cultural correlation of this bias?
What happens in a society that looks like this?
This is the influence the media has over our society.
Think about it.
If we are interested in discussing what we have in common, well, we will give a lot of importance to what is on the radio, on TV, in the newspapers, and also obviously in social networks, because it's exactly what we have in common.
But if that is so, it leaves us in a position of vulnerability, when we suddenly found out that the information we consume could be false.
Like, for example, in the case of fake news.
That's why, in addition, the information was split -- the papers you had were divided in two other experimental conditions -- which are groups with verified information whom we warned with a sign that the information had been checked and there was no false information, and we said the opposite to the other half.
We told them that the information had not been verified, therefore, there could be false information.
We are trying to find out how people discuss the information, how they put together information when they doubt the veracity of it.
We don't know this,
they are only hypotheses.
And for this reason we ask you in this moment to put the papers back in the envelopes and give them to the volunteers at the theater exit. They will be looking forward to it, you'll see.
Finally, I would like to finish saying that those papers may just help us understand why raising awareness about, for example, the problem of fake news, may lead to better democratic debates.
Thank you very, very much.
(Applause)
What is common sense?
Common sense is the judgments that we make on things based on our own experience.
The problem is that our experience is very limited and it encompasses a very reduced range of scales, more specifically, physical scales: distance scales, time scales, energy scales.
And the mental models that we use to describe the nature of the scales on which we move, often fail when we extrapolate broader range scales, bigger scales, and smaller scales.
Meaning, common sense is not always a reliable way to understand the world.
For example: the majority of ancient civilizations, believed the Earth to be flat; they were simply extrapolating, in an incorrect way, from their everyday perceptions to bigger scales.
Nowadays we know, of course, that when the Earth is observed at large scale, it actually appears as it is, meaning, round.
This is a fact that Ancient Greece already knew but, since Ancient Greece, science has advanced immensely and it has shown us many other aspects of nature that are even more surprising.
What we are seeing, or what we know today, is that, like what happened with the flatness of the Earth, our perception of reality is, to a great extent, an optical illusion, a sort of false appearance of the deep, authentic reality of things.
An increasingly counter-intuitive reality but also increasingly fascinating.
And, surely, the scientific theory that has shown us the most intriguing aspects of reality is quantum physics.
When it comes to quantum physics, you have to consider one thing, that you're not talking about mere scientific speculation. Quantum physics is possibly the most successful scientific theory in history.
Its predictions have been proven at very high levels of precision in the most variable fields without a single mistake.
It is thus a highly robust theory, both from the theoretical and experimental points of view. Additionally, a great part of our current technology is based precisely on quantum physics.
For example, digital technology, which helped us make this presentation, is based on quantum physics.
Nonetheless, when one studies quantum physics, it seems to be pure nonsense because quantum physics clashes head-to-head against our common sense and that is what makes it so difficult to understand, but also what makes it so fascinating.
So, we're going to see an example of this: Imagine two positions: A and B. They can be separated one millimeter or thousands of kilometers. For this argument, it doesn't matter.
Now imagine any object, for example, an elementary particle, say, an electron, an electron that could be in position A, but could also be in position B.
If it's in position A, we say it's in state E sub A and, in position B, it's in state E sub B. Intuitively, we'd say that the electron is either in position A or B, meaning, it's state is either E sub A or E sub B.
However, quantum physics teaches us that an electron can actually be in superposed states, in a sum of states E sub A plus E sub B.
What does this mean?
That the electron is in 2 positions at once?
Well, yes, in away, as unbelievable as it may seem.
And this not only happens with electrons and elementary particles; any object really, a rock, a ball, even a planet, or a star could be in superposed states.
And this has been shown with electrons ad nauseam in laboratories. This is not speculation.
So, why don't we see objects in different places at the same time?
What is going on?
Well, here we have to talk about the role of the observer in quantum physics, an important role.
According to quantum physics, when an observer sees this superposed state, instantaneously, the electron materializes in one of the two positions, A or B, with a 50% probability.
This is known as 'state collapse' and is really an extraordinary phenomenon. This means that physical systems can be altered by the mere act of observing them.
Specifically, in our case, an electron was in a superposed state EA plus EB, and, upon observing it, it's state has changed to EA or EB, with a 50% chance for each one of them.
We could think a little bit more about this fact, specifically, in daily life, we use the concept of probability a lot, but we always use it showing our ignorance and not as something essential.
For example, when you show a child your hands closed and tell him that there's a candy in one of them, the child knows instinctively that there is a 50% chance that the candy is either in your left hand or in your right hand.
But probability reflects the child's ignorance because, in reality, the candy is inside your left hand from the start, for example. That's the reality, and, when you open the hands, it's clear it was always there.
It would be absurd to think that the candy was in both hands at once and that, upon opening them, it materialized inside one of your hands.
Well, this nonsense is what really happens with the electron.
That's to say that, when the electron is in a superposed state, it doesn't have a defined position,
not that we don't know what it is, but that it doesn't have one. and it is upon observing it, that it materializes inside one of them.
Meaning, quantum physics really holds an important role for the observer.
The observer almost seems to have a magical power: when he observes the systems, he makes electrons and other things materialize in specific points in space.
And who are the observers, according to quantum physics?
What beings qualify as observers in order to produce this type of magic?
Is it only humans?
Or, perhaps, conscious beings?
Or maybe any object of a certain size, such as a photographic camera?
Well, currently, quantum physics doesn't have an answer for this issue.
It's an open problem.
In fact, quantum physics is not a closed theory, there are open problems like this being investigated actively.
In fact, there's an alternative hypothesis to the more conventional one we just described that describes what exactly happens during the observation process. It's the so-called 'many-worlds' hypothesis.
Here it's necessary to say that, unlike before, this really is a hypothesis; it's speculation, but it's a perfectly serious speculation and it's actually gaining followers with time within the scientific community.
But let's not forget that it's a hypothesis; it could be true, but it could also be false.
What does this hypothesis say?
Well, think back to what the observer does: he observes the electron and poof!
It materializes at a point; state collapse is produced.
Well, according to many-words theory this collapse is actually not produced at any time, never.
But of course, this seems contradictory, because then the observer should see the electron in two places at once. How is this possible?
Well, actually, this is not contradictory because, if we think for a moment about this observer, this observer also has a state, 'the state of the observer, ' and when the observer sees the electron, the state of the combined system formed by the observer and the electron is the product of the states of both, the product of the observer's state times the electron's state, that in this case is a superposition, E sub A plus E sub B. This is the system's state.
We could write this with a simple mathematical equation observer state x E sub A plus observer state x E sub B.
What does this mean?
Graphically, it means that the state is equivalent to a state in which the observer sees a particle in position A because we have observer state x E sub A superposed with a state where the observer sees the particle in E sub B.
Meaning, the very observer, while observing the electron, enters a superposition of states, meaning, his own self is divided into two.
One of his selves sees the electron in position A, the other sees it in position B, but neither of them are aware that there is another self that has been formed.
This seems quite delusional. Here we have two contradictory realities coexisting at once in the same place.
But we must also say&gt; that, quantum physics, interpret it as you may, is very delusional in and of itself. There is no interpretation known that doesn't seem delusional.
I remind you that this is still a hypothesis, but it's interesting to think about this hypothesis' implications, if it were correct, which it could be.
Firstly, the self that you feel at all moments would simply be another version of your self because we continuously make observations of all sorts, because we're continuously interacting with our environment, and, therefore, we're continuously dividing.
If this hypothesis is correct, and it could be, right now, all of us are dividing continuously.
It's a permanent division and each time that we split there are two new selves of ours, and those selves share a past but face different futures because they observe different things.
In other words, the world could be more chaotic than it seems.
Somehow, all the possibilities that we can imagine become true, in one way or another, in some of the branches of our quantum state.
For example, if we bet a lottery number, most of our selves that are created in that moment will see the bet fail, but some of our fortunate selves will see themselves win.
(Laughter) Of course, it's a rather disturbing situation our own lives end up belittled because, safe to say, in other quantum branches our life runs its course through different paths.
However, it could also offer us some consolation, for example, that the ones that we've lost could still be living in other quantum branches and we could still be enjoying their company.
We can't jump from one branch to another nor can we communicate with them, but it could offer some consolation that, in other worlds, things are different and maybe better.
In any case, take it as you may, quantum physics offers us an extreme example or case that nature can be a great deal more counter-intuitive, more complex, disturbing, but also rich and fascinating, much more than the appearance of reality provided by our senses.
An appearance of well-defined traits that could be false, and, in fact, it is.
It's an appearance that is determined by our limited senses, our limited experience, and our limited common sense.
Thank you.
(Applause)
The first question I asked myself and I ask you too is: What is poverty?
Maybe some of you imagine that poverty has to do with this, and with living in a neighborhood like this.
And in fact, since I was a kid I believed that poverty, or they made me understand that poverty had to do with this.
I am the fifth of six children: Yomi, Mariela, Marcela, Mauricio, me, and Oscar.
We were born in San Juan. We came to Buenos Aires for the moon and the stars.
My father had been promised a job, a home, a car, success.
This is the only picture I have of my parents together.
Because Buenos Aires kills my father.
He ends up dying within a few years of being here.
And there it began an eternal struggle to live with dignity and to improve our quality of life.
We seized a plot with my brothers in the middle of a settlement.
We were poor, people who were living in a usurped land.
We sometimes even didn't have anything to eat for dinner.
However, despite all this, despite having lived through discrimination, and my whole family being pointed out for many years, there was something I always loved and I always liked.
I love music, I'm a musician.
And I really discovered music through a woman who when I listened to her it was amazing for me.
I looked at her and she was like an angel.
I knew all her songs, all her lyrics, all her choreographies.
I learned all her songs, I had all her cassettes.
There were cassettes at that time.
And for me she was a star, an angel.
This woman was Gladys, la Bomba Tucumana.
(Laughter) (Applause) I dreamed with Gladys.
Once I told my mom: "Mom, I want to be Gladys."
(Laughter) And she said: "Oh son, don't you like Antonio Ríos or Alcides?"
No, I wanted to be Gladys.
And my mom told me: "Well, but how about La Nueva Luna?"
No, I dreamed of being an artist like her one day.
I always dreamed with her.
I always dreamed of learning to play her songs.
A great friend from childhood, Edgardo, his mother, Olga, worked in the neighborhood developing projects so that people like me, who lived in these neighborhoods, could study.
They had a project in Crear Vale La Pena Foundation, in which they worked with art in the context of poverty.
My friend told me one day: "Daniel, why don't you stop pestering around with music and sign up in the community cultural center?,"
where they gave free keyboard and piano lessons.
I said: "No, me taking piano lessons?
Ridiculous. I have to go out
and give a meaning to my life."
And he said to me: "But go, sign up for the piano class."
And I signed up for piano lessons.
Saturdays at 9 am.
I said, "Oh, how nice!"
In this Foundation there was a teacher, a concert pianist, Liliana Alpern, who gave once a week a couple of hours of free lessons to people who could not pay for the class.
I went to her piano class when I was nine.
And I saw Lili, my piano teacher, in high heels, with a silk shawl, glasses, green eyes, blond hair.
And I looked at her and she looked at me. She said: "What is your name?"

"Daniel." "What are you doing here?"
There was an upright piano beside her.
I said, "I want to play that."

And she says: "What do you want to play?"
(Laughter) And I said: "Gladys, la Bomba Tucumana."
(Laughter)
And she said: "Who is that woman?"
"What? Aren't you a music teacher?"
"Yes, I certainly am, but I don't know all the musicians.
But if you bring a tape with her music,

I'll listen to it and I teach you."
"Really?" I said. "Yes."
I go to my house, grab the cassette, I bring it back and Lili began: "B B B B B, C D, C D E, E F G, B C".
(Laughter) And I said, wow!
She began to teach me and I felt I was John Lennon playing "Imagine."
(Applause) So awesome.
Lili said: "Look Daniel, you can learn this and much more, if you want to."
"Really, Miss?"
"Yes. You can learn
everything you want to learn."
And the next Saturday I brought La Nueva Luna, Los Charros, Gilda.
I learned to play a band called Los Palmeras, I dreamed with Los Palmeras.
And she taught me all the music I wanted to learn.
Pretty soon, I had learned everything I wanted.
And Lili said, "And now, what do you want to learn?"
I said, "That's it, I've learned to play what I wanted to learn."
And she says: "Look, Daniel, with these same chords there is a guy named Beethoven.
Do you know who Beethoven is?"
I told her: "Yes, a dog from a movie."
(Laughter) "No, dear, Beethoven is not a dog.
Beethoven is a musician who plays 'Für Elise'".
And she showed me "Für Elise".
When I heard "Für Elise" I fell in love.
And I said, "Lili, can I play that?"
And Lili said, "You can play that and much more, Daniel.
Everything you want to play."
And there I learned at age nine to break with the first poverty.
That is the poverty of culture.
I just thought that music was... (Applause) I thought that music was what I heard in my neighborhood, but I didn't know other kind of music existed.
So I learned to be not only a musician, but at age 14, Lili puts me another challenge, with a partner who we played the piano together, Marcela Tula, the two attended her class.
"Now you, after five years of taking free classes at the cultural center, must begin to teach others."
And I said, "No, me, Lili?
I can't teach others".
"Yes, you can teach others."
"But Lili, I have nothing to give."
She said: "To give, you don't need to have something in your pocket.
All you have to do is to be willing to help others."
Then I started teaching in my neighborhood.
With Marcela, we both learned how to teach.
We gave classes to young people, the very beginners in the neighborhood.
I went from being the kid who hung in the street corner to mess up, to be "the neighborhood's Professor".
I would pass by and people would say: "Professor, Professor".
Then I'd pass by like four times!
I'd go to the grocers and they would say:
"How are you, Professor? Take a candy".
And I'd grab about five.
The grocer's daughter was my student.
And there I learned to knock down another poverty, which is the poverty of dignity.
The poverty that is lost because, by living in the contexts we live, we think that poverty only has to do with hunger, and feeling cold at night, but no.
Poverty has little to do with economics.
It has to do with what you do to design your life project.
What you do to say who you are, regardless of the degree, or the position you have in a company.
Who you are as a person.
And that is what I learned at age 14, to start teaching in my neighborhood.
When I was 17... (Applause) At age 17 I began to coordinate the community cultural center with activities for young people. It was not anymore just learning and teaching.
What we did with a group of young people was to form them and start thinking about our community, how our neighborhood could do activities to improve the quality of life, not only of those who studied, but of our community.
We did events in neighborhoods, we celebrated Children's Day, looking for the needs in our neighborhoods and we began to improve, not only our lives, but the lives of our neighbors.
At age 25 I leave this Foundation, because I felt that in it I had gone from student to teacher, coordinator, executive director, I even prepared young people in political issues.
And I began to understand that I had to knock down another poverty.
Then I started working with a civil organization called Inicia.
And what we did in Inicia was to work in a prison,
because the son of a friend of mine was there in the unit and we went to visit him.
When I went to the prison the first thing I saw were those drawings that you see behind the young men, it was like an art gallery. They were drawings where they had drawn everything they wanted.
I looked at Cristian with Olga and we said, "Hey, Cristian, what do you do here in the unit?"
"Nothing".
"How's that, Cristian?"
"Nothing. On Monday nothing,
nothing on Tuesday, nothing on Thursday." "What if I proposed you a workshop where you can think about the mistake you made, why you are here and you can think of a project for when you leave prison?" And Cristian replied:
"Would you do that for me?"
"Of course."
Then we started a workshop where we talked about leadership, so they could lead their life project.
We had a book: "The New Leaders," which belonged to this organization.
And it had chapters like: "Personal transformation," "The common good", "Ethics" or "Values."
Each chapter we read it with the inmates, the 48 who attended the workshop.
And not only that, we would create a comic and each of them could put in the comic what they learned from that chapter.
But not only that, we invited the authors of the book and we reflected upon the workshop we were giving.
So that the prisoners could also think some way of a life project once they were out of there.
And then I knocked down another poverty, the poverty of prejudice.
We think that people who are deprived of their liberty not only deserve to be there, but they don't have the ability, nor the dignity to be able to change their future.
Yes, they can change their future.
All they need are opportunities.
And what I was providing there was an opportunity.
But not only with this I knocked down the poverty of prejudice.
Then I got to work on another project with a colleague, an acquaintance from the gastronomic industry, who had a restaurant chain in down San Isidro. He said: "Dani, in front of my restaurants is the settlement Martin and Omar and I don't know what to do, because every time I pass by they tell me: 'Hey, mustache, got work for me?',
and I don't know what to do".
Then we created a program called Cocina para Integrar. What did we do?
This man would teach the women from the settlement to be chefs, so that they have preparation not only as people engaged in odd jobs, but also in a trade in the gastronomic industry.
And then I broke another prejudice, I broke another poverty. The poverty of thinking that people who are living in contexts of vulnerability can only work doing odd jobs.
No, they are professionals who also can be formed in trades, as that of being a chef.
On this path I met with another person who also helped me knock down another of my poverties.
We met during a talk we gave together. He had a company which developed products with design, shoes with a different design.
He gives his talk, a young company which exported worldwide.
I give my talk and he says: "Dani, you have to work with me."
And I said, "Tomás, what do you want me to work on in your business?"
"You have to do in my company what you do in the neighborhoods.
You have to work in my neighborhood which is my business, my community, with my employees."
"What do you want me to do?"
"I want you to be the Human Resources manager."
And I said, "But, Tomás, I don't even know how to clear salaries."
He says, "I don't care, you will learn that, what you know is to listen and to be with the people."
After working three years with him, he proposes me to be even more than that.
And today I am the Culture and Happiness manager.
In the private sector I work as -- (Applause) Not only I work in the neighborhoods, to improve people's quality of life, but also companies start to think that all the people working in a company can improve their quality of life. They have to work happy and work on their company culture.
But this was not last.
The last big project that I have with three friends is to build a social enterprise called Creer Hacer.
What is Creer Hacer?
It is a social enterprise where we work with the private, public and social sectors.
We build a bridge for these three sectors so they can improve the quality of life of any person.
We have lots of projects with this institution.
To strengthen the NGOs that want to be strengthened.
We have a project called Barrio Abierto, replicating a model very similar to this, but in the middle of a settlement, like La Cava.
In the middle of La Cava we will make an event called Cava Abierta.
Six speakers will come to share their story, very similar to mine, and they can share with their neighbors that they decided to take a step forward, they decided to improve their quality of life.
(Applause) You may ask me today, what is your wealth?
My wealth has to do with this, to have formed a family, with my wife, with my brothers, with my friends.
To be the parent of Lautaro and Catalina, which is two months old.
And I want to tell you this: any of you here can be a Liliana Alpern.
Anyone of you can consecrate time once a week to someone else, so that they change their lives.
(Applause) Regardless of your economic poverty or your economic wealth, don't make of your life a poor life.
Thank you very much.
(Applause) (Whistles) (Applause)
Some time ago, while walking to school, something disgusting happened to me.
To spare you the details and because the truth is that this is something that happens to most women, a guy felt completely free to show me his penis, thinking that maybe it was a good idea.
At that moment I got scared, but I also shouted: "What the fuck are you doing, you asshole?"
(Applause) And I walked fast the meters I had left.
Once in the classroom, I sat down and told a classmate.
She asked me if I was alright and everything ended there because even when I was full of anger, what else could I do?
Nothing.
In fact, comparing it to things that happened to my friends, such as being forced get in a car and having to run to get away or getting groped in the street, what had happened to me was relatively a minor thing.
Fortunately.
But it was enough to make me a little "paranoid" and make it look like a good idea to start going down the streets with a box cutter, just in case.
Harassment, among many other issues, was that thing that happened and everyone knew that happened.
It was there.
But it was not talked about or it was given little relevance.
Today, however, both this issue and other issues have greater visibility.
In part, because there are things that are no longer accepted.
As if we had accepted them before.
But above all because young women, and young people in general, have a space where we can share what happens to us.
Take the floor and generate somehow a discussion.
And what's even more important, support, reaction and organization.
If we think about it, we are taking part in social media all the time.
I like the photo that a friend uploaded, I vote in the survey made by someone who uploads to an Instagram that I may not even know.
I give my opinion about a news article published on Twitter.
We have a tool to express ourselves, to get informed and build our opinions about the things that happen, to express our demands.
Claims that, sooner or later, exceed that space and end up in a street or in a square.
Last year, for example, in Berazategui where I live, a couple of girls called a mobilization.
They did it due to several testimonies that had gone viral in networks of girls from the area who had suffered rather ugly situations.
From harassment to violence or abuse.
That was how on a Saturday, at about four in the afternoon, we gathered hundreds of women in the square.
I went to support this.
I went for myself, because it happens to me.
But I went also as a way of telling all those girls who had exposed themselves telling what they had lived, that believed them, that they were not alone.
And it's crazy to think that during my childhood, the TV shows we all watched, the lyrics of their songs, always talked about a sort of enmity or competition among women.
"Nobody passes from this corner, here the divine girls rule.
Out the ugly, out the ugly, there's no place for you."
What were they thinking?
With today's eyes, the truth is that this lyrics seems a bit horrible.
With all this, I realized that there were things that had long been assumed as private.
But now they were breaking into public space and, in doing so, they were opening a space for change.
I like my generation.
I like it because I think we understood that moving is the only way to bring about that change.
Putting on the table what happens to us, what we don't like.
And saying, "Look, this must be discussed."
"We have to do something regarding this."
What happened to me going to school maybe cannot be prevented, but at least, now I know there is something I can do.
Whether due to harassment, climate change or any other issue, we are a lot of young people willing to use the networks to give visibility to what we have to say.
But only to later put our body and act because I think that is the only way to make us visible and set the debate.
Today, weaving those contact networks with others who think similar or not, who are equally outraged and willing to do something or discuss, is a little more easy.
Society is changing.
Things we used to look askance at, today are willing, at least, to take them out of the closet they were in for a long time, to display and to discuss them.
And although perhaps there is still a long way to go, I hope that in the future no one else ever again thinks it's a good idea to go out with a box cutter.
But, that indeed is a good idea to go out to fight for what we believe in.
Thank you.
(Applause)
A few days after July 7 bombings in London, one of the worst terror attacks in my country, upon arriving to my desk in an investment bank, I found a UK flag on my computer.
I wondered why would someone place it there.
Did they think I agreed on the violent theft of 52 lives?
Did they think it didn't hurt me?
Could it be that though I was born British, I am half Spaniard, half Egyptian, with an Arab name and last name?
For me that was the last straw.
I was sick of feeling a foreigner in my own country, always obliged to excuse myself, to strive harder than the rest of my compatriots, and having to thank for any opportunity I would be given knowing I would not be allowed to grow.
There was a glass ceiling upon me limiting my development in any professional field, for the simple reason of being son of immigrants.
From early childhood I learned to channel my anxiety through music.
When I was called "paki," a major insult referring to someone from Pakistan, I would run to the music department, and play the violin alone.
It was my shelter, a place to breathe, escape, feel, forget.
As an adult, I played the violin in the London Symphony Orchestra, whenever I played a Beethoven overture or a Brahms serenade, I could forget those dirty looks.
Those little comments that seem harmless, yet are tools for discrimination, to show who is above and who is below.
Still, those happy moments, those small moments of peace, of stillness, were not enough to stay so I decided to leave my country to come and live in Mexico.
Five years ago, I was in Berlin on vacation.
I went to a West Eastern Divan Orchestra concert, an orchestra of Arabs and Israelis.
Back in Mexico I thought of what I had seen.
Christians, Muslims and Jews playing music together, in a background neither Beethoven nor Shöenberg could have imagined when they composed the music I heard that day.
In that concert I heard the sound filling up the hall atmosphere, a sound created by musicians who for different reasons decided to break with the segregation tradition and make music along with the enemy.
That day triggered an idea that would change my life.
After months of consideration, I decided to give up financial security provided by my job in an investment bank in Mexico and instead I decided to invest all my savings on a trip for indefinite time to Middle East countries in conflict.
Why I choose to leave everything behind?
Because I had found a quest that gave purpose to my life.
Why?
To witness firsthand how these enemies, could play music together and coexist, and find other examples that could give me hope.
What started as a trip, became a thrilling three year adventure through 17 countries, listening to the musicians' complaints and dreams looking to amplify their voices; which later became a book and a documentary.
I now want to share with you three experiences: First, when I went to Tanzania.
I attended a rehearsal of a group of dancers and musicians, with albinism.
They use their art to fight against the discrimination they face for a lack melanin in their skin.
There are witchdoctors in Tanzania who say: If you kill and dismember an albino, you will receive good fortune in exchange.
In every concert they use songs and dances to explain people that they are equal, they are Africans, who don't deserve to be threatened with death and dismemberment for lacking skin pigmentation.
After the rehearsal I did a brief photo shoot for my book.
Then the leader of the group, Tito, approached me and asked: "What if you help me create an official Facebook fan page?"
I said "Of course, no problem."
Then asked me to upload the pictures I had taken.
Fearing for their safety, I asked several times if he was sure.
He said yes.
I barely slept that night, my mind was rushing.
What if someone made an online search and killed one of them?
Would I be responsible?
Could I have said no?
What I understood later was more valuable: That a seemingly simple act was really worth its weight in gold.
It was an important part of their struggle, of standing up and facing those bullies and potential murderers to educate them.
Musicians who made of their art a pacific weapon to change their environment not just to protect themselves but other albino people, who suffer the same threats.
The second one is in Turkey, where I lived a few years ago,
I followed a group called Kardeş Türküler, meaning brotherhood songs.
The group formed in the 90s, during a period of political repression, and violence in their country.
In every concert they performed, either in Turkey or Germany, I found a great source of inspiration, just what I looked for.
Turkish, Kurdish and Armenian playing music together.
Singing in different languages in a land that's a melting pot of cultures, ethnicities and religions.
Sadly today, segregation and hate from another human being is seen as normal in the Middle East.
We got used to watch this examples in the media.
We got used to the number of deaths.
Absurd beliefs of superiority still remain, of religion, skin color, of entitlement, of money, along with apathy and lack of action that contributes to these situations.
But every time I hear this group play, I would watch just the opposite.
I watched true and authentic coexistence, brotherhood not just between the musicians but among the thousand of fans, who value their diverse repertoire.
That is how I learned that music is the perfect way to make every voice heard and respected, because that is what we lack not just in the Middle East and Turkey, actually the rest of Middle East, but in the whole world.
The third experience was when I came back to Mexico.
I started looking for similar examples and I found out that in Oaxaca there is a huge resistance to speaking Spanish for various reasons: They fear of loosing their identity, they don't want to forget their indigenous language, and also because they are discriminated for not speaking Spanish properly.
Events such as Guelaguetza stimulate the region's musical and linguistic traditions.
If Susana Harp or another musician decides to record a song in Mixe or Zapoteco or whatever, means that not only people living in those areas can hear music in their language, but that any of us can access this music to get to know more languages.
By the end of my research, either interviewing members of a rock school of different Kosovo ethnicities, or a samba group in a Brazilian favela, or a singer with HIV in Rwanda, and though the intensity of the problem may vary, the essence was the same:
Ignorance and hatred.
I know that the flag that colleague left on my desk can never be compared to the examples of hatred and separation I saw on my trip, but it was an eye-opener and made me dare to question.
I was never trained to be a writer or a filmmaker, but I feel lucky to have found these two ways of defending human values.
Discrimination is commonplace, and each of us in this hall has been a victim for different reasons at some point.
I am convinced that we can transform segregation and hatred into coexistence and respect.
During my trip I saw with my own eyes that many musicians are making a great effort and taking risks to change their reality and the reality of other people.
Everyone in this hall is capable of joining a positive change.
We can now start to reject those jokes, comments, memes and other actions that seem harmless or funny, and think on how it could affect a vulnerable person.
Great achievements start with small steps.
We are all capable of putting ourselves in the place of those who are not like us and acknowledge them as human beings.
Thank you.
(Applause)
As the ancient oriental legend goes, there's an old man behind the moon who watches us while we sleep.
He has a particular power.
He can feel the souls meant to be together here on Earth.
And he joins them with a red thread.
He ties their little fingers together with a knot at each end.
That thread might tighten or tangle but it will never break until those two souls meet.
As in other legends or myths, I think this one is rooted in a very important human need.
This is the case of the human need for connection.
It is a need that determines our lives.
Because if that need is met it can make us very happy, but we can also feel unhappy if we don't get to connect with at least some people we care about. I really believe
this red thread created my family.
Because more than 6000 miles away from our home, there was a baby in a village North of Hanoi in Vietnam who has now become our second child.
This baby has officially been in our family for a year now.
But according to this legend of the red thread, he belongs to our family since he was born because we were meant to find each other.
I also think that this need for connection has contributed to the existence of one form of art in particular: literature.
It has also contributed to the spread of a phenomenon in recent decades.
The phenomenon of social media.
Literature means communication.
But not live communication like the one we have now.
But a kind of communication with people at very distant distances in space and at very distant distances in time.
As with social media.
I like the whole etymology thing, and after some research I realized something.
Texts are the very base of literature.
It is a word that comes from Latin, textum, which literally means "woven thing."
In literary texts the sentences are united, intertwined to form a whole.
So sentences form a mesh, a net.
The Spanish word for net comes from
"retis" meaning "thread mesh."
Mere coincidence? I don't think so.
Both literary texts and social media perform a very important role for human beings.
They are like a warp of invisible threads that allows us to connect despite the distance.
This is very important for humanity.
Because it meets this need for connection, which I believe is the base of everything.
The base of a tweet, the base of a poem, or the base of an adoption.
If a want to tell the story of someone's life, I can write a biography, sure.
But I can also do it on my Facebook profile.
If I want to express my own ideas, I could write an opinion column or I could come here to TEDx.
But I can also do that on my Twitter account.
Tweet by tweet.
If I feel like talking, sharing opposite opinions or personalities I could write a theatre play, but I can use WhatsApp as well.
If I want to talk about my professional career, or evolution, I can do it in my journal or logbook, but also on my blog.
If I want to express my feelings you may agree with me that there is a specific genre for that, poetry.
And the social media world is so important that we have even created a special language just for that, the language of emoticons.
And you might be thinking: "Social media are so modern and literature is so traditional..."
Right. But as a literature teacher
I think that I can and I must be in the world of social media.
So apart from my Facebook profile I use Twitter, I have a YouTube channel, I own a blog and a large folder of infographics on Pinterest.
Because I believe that these are wonderful tools to educate our teen students.
That's exactly why I opened my Instagram account.
I once read in an article that high school students, my students, have Instagram as the social network they spend the longest time on.
I hadn't mastered this app yet, so I decided to go for it.
Besides, there was a challenge in my head at the time, and that was to make my students love poetry as much as I do.
That's how the poetry-Instagram spark ignited and this is what I did with my account.
We choose a picture for our Instagram because of its beauty.
But also because it perfectly conveys an important moment in our lives.
So that's why we share it.
Poetry does something similar.
Out of all the available words in a language, poetry selects only those that express an emotion or feeling with sufficient intensity.
If a poem is good, it can capture the essence of a feeling.
And if a picture is good enough, it can capture the essence of a person.
Both poetry and photography are the distillation of the same liquor.
They are the distillation
of the liquor of life. Joy.
"Once in a while, joy throws little stones at my window. It wants to let me know that it's waiting for me; don't worry, I'm convinced joy doesn't need to throw any more little stones, I'm coming, I'm coming",
says Benedetti.
Lorca speaks about sadness.
"Sadness flows down the furniture and down my soul Perhaps nature doesn't have a glass bosom for me.
And the flesh of my heart hurts me, and the flesh of my soul and when I speak, my words remain in the air like corks on water."
A very trendy word I can relate to:
Sorority. Modern, right?
Lope de Vega already talked about it: "Whoever says women have no firmness, can't have, my lady, met you."
And freedom, by Juan Ramón Jiménez.
"The door is open, the cricket is singing. Are you going around naked in the fields?
The basil is not asleep, the ant is busy.
Are you going around naked in the house?"
Fullness is explained to us by José Martí from Cuba, when he says:
"Farewell, ocean! Standing on the rock I await you with disdain Should my boat touch your ravenous wave, I will not tremble, nor will I be beset."
And a feeling so great as love, whose limits sometimes we can't see Gabriela Mistral manages to tell us about it in one detail.
"There are kisses that resemble lilies, sublime, naïve, and pure."
We believe we must flee from loneliness but loneliness offers us self-connection.
Sometimes that red thread from the legend turns around on us and we find ourselves.
Delmira Agustini tells us about loneliness when she says: "My heart, the grayest and most serene stone awoke in the caress of the stream."
Or anguish, by Jorge Guillén.
"I'm ever worried about a certainty, and my future is shaking before me, stalking it there is suddenly a wall."
Melancholy, by Marilina Rébora.
"The light is slowly fading and the sunset dies on the horizon."
Every day routine and poetry also get along well:
Having a coffee, for example, by Carlos Edmundo de Ory.
"I'm sitting in a café now and my soul beats with thirst. I don't know for what, maybe for chocolate."
Or a sad walk, by Alberti.
"Five rower dolphins courted his boat.
Two sea angels, invisible, guided him.
He put the nets down, too bad, over the frozen sea.
And he caught the full moon, alone, in his silver net."
Or rest. Don't we all feel like this
when we are on holidays, as Pedro Salinas describes?
"At the pure apex of high joy, multiplying joy times joy, times laughter, times pleasure."
That's how holidays are.
Or work.
Neruda tell us with something as cool as this: "What happens to swallows who are late for school?
Is it true they scatter transparent letters across the sky?"
Or partying, by Antonio Machado.
"On a clear night of festivity and moon, night of my dreams, night of joy,
there was light in my soul that today is all clouded.
Even my hair wasn't black yet.
the youngest fairy carried me in her arms to the joyful gathering that burned in the plaza."
Isn't poetry magical?
It allows us to raise our gaze and see beyond our own eyes.
And feel beyond our heart.
Maybe happiness comes from all those moments when we look up, see beyond and connect with somebody, at the other side of a poem, the other side of the net, at the other side of the world, as what happened with my son.
One day, when still waiting for the adoption and he hadn't arrived home yet, I was reading poetry to his big sister, to my oldest daughter, she asked me all worried: "Mum, do you think there is someone there reading poetry to my little brother?"
Of all the lacks the baby could have she cared about this one the most.
Now there's poetry
waiting for that baby in every corner of our home.
Because each room has a small shelf, full of little books perfect for his little hands.
Let's give poetry to our children, give them experiences and connection.
I am sure they will become happy adults this way.
And us adults, let's look for these connections through arts, through social media. It doesn't matter.
Because I am sure we can all make an effort to connect.
Believe me, there's nothing more worth than giving, feeling, living intensely.
We live in a world which goes so fast that we are at risk of leaving our emotions and feelings behind, and we are not complete without them.
What's more, maybe they should lead our ways.
If we follow our red thread, we'll get to know places, and people who will change our lives.
So be brave, follow your red thread, find who you connect with and be happy to connect.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
You can travel around the world without seeing anything.
To fully understand, it's not necessary to see many things but to look closely what's upon us.
Painter Giorgio Morandi spent most of his career painting always the same objects: jugs, bottles, cups, shells; they formed his little big universe.
Morandi found virtue within his own limits and invited us to look closely, paying attention to all around us.
Maybe that same curiosity is what nowadays drags millions of tourists to travel around the world, searching for a different, alien reality.
Because when we are tourists, we transform ourselves and we have the obligation of collecting images, objects and any other element that later on will enable us to remember our already lost other identity.
My question is, "Can we be tourists during the whole year?
Can we live everyday as perpetual tourists?"
Tourism is my source of inspiration.
Maybe that's why my job is to make souvenirs, those little objects, that apparently have a life of their own, and that, as Morandi used to say, "lock a whole universe within them."
Everything started on this supermarket in London, right in the city center, open 24 hours and providing a gigantic parking lot that I used to find special and was almost empty night after night.
Being a private area, only clients of the supermarket were granted access to it.
One client, one parking space for 3 hours.
Besides, the parking lot was, of course, watched by CCTV.
Things got interesting when I finally realized that what was in front of me was not a parking, but a tennis court, the biggest one in London.
(Laughter) Because, in the end, it's the client that decides what to do with his own space.
So, for less than a pound, four people can have their private, indoor tennis court right in London's city center, referee included.
(Laughter) This is the game's trophy.
It's made exclusively with fruits and vegetables from the supermarket and that's the souvenir I keep, proudly showing the ticket, the legal document allowing this transformation from private space into public event.
Not only that, but thanks to the Freedom of Information Act I can use the supermarket's security CCTV to record my own tournament on video.
(Laughter) (Clapping) (Clapping ends) This project reclaims the importance of public spaces in our cities.
When later on I decided that I wanted to create my own souvenirs in one of the most visited touristic monuments in the whole world, the challenge became even bigger.
After months asking for permits with no answer at all, this time I decided to bend the rules, hiding all the materials and the necessary tools inside of sandwiches, evading police control and creating the first illegal production of souvenirs at the top of the Eiffel Tower.
These three kids: Eve, Alma and Océane, not only enjoyed their visit, but also gave shape to their own memories; each step, each second, each move was transmitted to a rotational moulding system that after 30 minutes unveiled a unique statue, unpaired, faithful reflection of their own experience of that day.
Monument and tourist finally converse, becoming the souvenir the link between them.
Months later I embarked on the Road to Santiago, with a portable factory of souvenirs that worked exclusively with the energy of my bicycle's energy.
The goal: a more authentic experience through this millenary route.
Each encounter, every place was transmitted, translated, to a series of objects and events that defied the recent commercialization of the Road.
During my journey I created all sorts of souvenirs: soccer trophies for the best players of the village.
(Laughter) I also worked with local craftspeople, almost extinguished trades, creating together completely new product typologies, such as this jet piece.
And I had unforgettable encounters, like the one with these two wonderful ladies that used the supermarket leaflet as an eyeshade and inspired me to create... (Laughter) (Clapping) They inspired me to create one of the most spontaneous products of the project.
This is the Three Gorges Dam, one of China's new touristic attractions, where every year, thousands of tourists from all over the world sail through the Yangtze river aboard luxury cruises.
But the Three Gorges Dam is also the world's biggest hydroelectrical project that flooded thousands of cities and to this day has forced the evacuation of more than four million people.
During my journey, I run into cities without name, cities that are not on the maps anymore but whose habitants refuse to abandon them.
The hidden side of a project of almost unimaginable consequences and dimensions.
This is what's left of the old city of Fengdu that is completely submerged.
At this point I decided to collect some water from the river in a couple of plastic tanks, that later on I decorated with traditional Chinese motifs.
As water becomes the precious souvenir of the missing city, its container symbolizes the conflict between traditional Chinese culture and the area's recent industrialization.
Similar to the conflict I personally experienced when I realized those places retain a certain beauty.
A tragic beauty, it's true, but also sublime, extraordinary, almost surreal.
And precisely that duality between the real and the unreal was what pushed me to create a series of objects, symbols capable of unveiling what photography can't, making tangible the invisible.
Souvenirs documenting an almost disappeared identity.
My fascination for these forgotten, almost invisible, places, led me to the mountains of Cilento's National Park in southern Italy, where several churches remain abandoned after a series of earthquakes and the ground's instability.
Besides, these places were recently looted.
Most of their relics: saints, Madonnas... have been stolen or destroyed leaving behind them an unimaginable void.
On the one side, it's dreadfully sad that this heritage was lost this way.
But on the other side, we stand upon a unique opportunity of transforming these places, of reimagining their future.
The project I'm absorbed nowadays is called "Buona Fortuna" [Good luck] and consists in the creation of new sculptures and installations to substitute those that are missing.
Symbols inspired in the local tradition and culture but with a noticeable contemporary character that will give a new meaning to these places.
Next summer, this church will reopen to the public after remaining closed for over 50 years.
We insist in considering tourism as a business, as an industry, but I'm certain that a more sustainable and creative tourism will help us build bridges among diverse places and cultures, bridges that today, as we all know, are needed more than ever.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
You'll be wondering, "Why is this madman dressed like this?"
Am I right?
No, I wasn't running late and grabbed the first clothes I saw.
No!
This outfit is going to play a significant role in reviving natural habitats.
This outfit is going to give nature back its color.
But when we speak about
the color of nature, we do so with great feeling,
feelings of joy, peace, happiness!
But we can also interpret it with sadness, melancholy, disappointment. This last case is what I experienced.
I'm going to talk about an area of natural habitat called El Cascajo wetland.
El Cascajo is 1.5 hours north of Lima.
I spent some of the greatest moments of my childhood there.
The family memories and amazing adventures with my father.
But for some reason, we stopped going there.
And time went on,
and I headed to Japan
to start my post-graduate studies,
my master's, my PhD.
I'm currently working as a researcher and lecturer in science at the university.
And when I was there, in Japan, I got a strange call from my father,
"Do you remember El Cascajo"? he asked.
"Of course I do," I say.
"You won't believe it,
but they're filling it in."
"What? What are you talking about?"

"It's so polluted it's completed ruined."
I was left wondering
about this for some time. And then, having some holiday, I went to Peru.
And I say to my father, "Dad, take me to El Casajo."
"OK."
And I was faced with this surprise:
the wetlands were green.
It was completely covered by aquatic plants, algae.
And I asked my father, "Are you sure this is El Cascajo"?
"Yes."
"Come on, no way!" I say.
"This is El Cascajo, and we're losing it,
or we already have," he says.
I was quite upset.
When I got to the edge of the water, I got on my knees,
and I apologized for not having
taken care of the habitat. I gathered up the water plants, and I was faced with another surprise.
The water was brown, stinking, and full of floating objects.
Just don't ask me what those objects were! (Laughter)
And I said to myself, "Well, I didn't study for nothing, did I?"
Am I right?
So, let's do something!
I asked so many people
for help but was refused.
They said, "But why? What's in it for us?"
"Well, never mind."
So, let's do something more simple.

Let's do this. Take the first step.
But how?

OK, I went back to Japan.
I went to the bank and withdrew all my savings.
I went to other banks and took out loans.
And I went back to Peru.
But, since this project was about reviving natural habitats,
before doing anything, we had to monitor and observe the area.
While this was going on, let me share with you a small anecdote.
When I returned to the wetlands, which were obviously green.
But, I could also see some floating noses.
"Hippopotamus!" I cried out.
But no, they belonged to the pigs swimming there.
(Laughter) There were farms surrounding the outside of the wetlands.
There was also an illegal rubbish dump there for more than 20 years!
But the saddest thing is that the birds, - many of them scavenger carrion birds - were feeding directly from there.
Another thing is that local drains emptied directly into the wetlands,
and many of them directly into the sea!
And what did this cause?
The proliferation of invasive species like 'Pistia stratiotes' or water lettuce.
And as a result, the surrounding land was contaminated.
OK?, So, first of all, we had
to get rid of the outer waste.
So, these water lettuces.
But how?
I designed a system
to divide up the wetlands, to divide it into 8 parts.
From A1 to A2, and D1 to D2.
But why?
To have an organized and controlled cleaning system for achieving something that resembles a body of water.
I started with sections A1 and A2.
But how to separate the zones?
Using the materials around us.
We used Guadua angustifolia or bamboo.
And we got into the water to put these natural barriers in place.
Once again I said, "OK let's start!"
"But who's getting in the water to get rid of the lettuces?!"
And as a gentleman, it had to be me. (Laughter)
But I had a plan in mind.
I got in the water everyday for a week from 7am until 8pm,
just removing the water lettuces.
But whilst I was doing this,
I could see how many people
passed by everyday. Between 70 and 100 people everyday.
These people passed me by
and did a double-take!
And they said, "Hey, you're crazy."
"You'll get ill, my son did." And engaging them in
conversation was exactly my idea.
I got out of the water and I explained,
"No, its real color is not the one you're seeing now."
"The true color of the wetlands is fabulous!"
I told them about how it used to be, how it can be enjoyed once again,
and how a natural habitat like this should be sustainable.
And something happened.
We removed the Pistia stratiotes by hand.
Starting with section A1.
You see this small boat, or 'punt' as they sometimes call it?
Those lettuces weighed around 150 kilos.
And we carried on cleaning,
and slowly, the group got bigger.
One day, I got up late and arrived at the wetlands at 8am.
And I was faced with this surprise:
more than 100 people were working on the wetlands!
They shouted out, "We're with you, let's save El Cascajo!"
I felt like a politician greeting everyone there.
(Laughter) It was a moment of immense joy,
seeing these people, unprotected, in the water dredging up lettuces.
"We're going to save El Cascajo." "Yes, together we will."
Well then, great!
We ended up removing 70 tons of water lettuces from A1 alone.
But what did we do with them?
Compost, organic fertilizer.
And why? For use on dry and barren areas
to make them green and fertile.
And we had great results,
- thanks to a local government program -
which, I should mention, helped us from the start.
And so, we went on to clean up A2 and then B1 etc,
And now for the next step:
a water treatment system.
How did we do this?
We used nanotechnology.
Within the field of nanotechnology there are two systems we could implement.
One is micro-nano bubbles, the other biofilters.
What are micro-nano bubbles?
I'll explain.
OK, imagine the bubbles from fizzy drinks.
Nano bubbles are 10,000 times smaller.
So, very hard to see with these tiny eyes of mine.
(Laughter)
The difference is that bubbles from fizzy drinks disappear very quickly.
Whereas nano bubbles remain in the liquid for 5-8 hours.
But why for so long?
They are active in the liquid.
Nano bubbles are surrounded by positive and negative ions.
In other words, they have a high concentration of energy,
or what is known as an electrostatic charge.
I'm sure you used to play with the plastic covers of books,
rubbing your hair with it

and it stands on end?
This is the same! The same!
So, what's going to happen?
The nano bubble will become coated with particles.
For example with viruses.
Viruses move towards the electrostatic current,
and get stuck to the bubbles.
But what happens then?
When the bubble pops, it'll release all those ions,
and it will generate free radicals.
In other words, we have created a type of mini-bomb
that destroys and kills bacteria.
How did we install it?
Again, with the materials around us.
As you can see here, a water pump, an oxygen pump, a generator, and a PVC pipe.
You can see here, it looks
as if it's being cleaned with a detergent.
The micro-nano bubbles are up and running.
Graphically, it looks like this.
There is the PVC pipe.
But how do you generate nano bubbles?
By using the interaction between air and water, and a device which I designed in the lab
at the University of Tsukuba, Japan.
The device transformed the interactions occurring at the interface of the nano bubbles.
And, little by little, the pollutants in the wetland were eliminated.
After installing the nano bubbles, we moved on to biofilters.
Filters, as we all know, act to absorb.
But why?
To reduce the pool of contaminants.
but we add the word 'bio'; because it's in fashion.
And that's how we did it.
We again used the materials around us.
I used clay and ceramic to build
the biofilters in a natural way.
Like before, the filters acted to absorb bacteria and viruses, or other contaminants - organic or inorganic compounds.
Everything was absorbed and passed through this biofilter.
And they are still in the water today.
But they can be reused
when they no longer work. What did I do with them?
I ground them up and turned them into ceramic floor tiles.
Do you remember the color of the water at the beginning?
OK, well it soon turned
into this. Fantastic. (Applause)
Thank you! But hold your horses!
Don't get excited yet!

OK? Now for the surprise.
Soon after, migratory birds appeared.
More than 77 species!

It was amazing! And do you know what?
Something unusual happened to me in January 2013.
I was in the lab at the university, glued to my experiments, geeky as I am ... (Laughter)
And I get another phone call - not from my father, but from someone else -
who says, "Hey, you remember that the wetlands were green?"
"Yes," I reply.
"Well, the water's now clear."
"Oh, good God, they put in a load of bleach," I said. But no ladies and gentlemen.
This happened.
Yes, the wetlands did become clear, and became full of migratory birds.
They're birds not bats by the way!
(Laughter)
I was a bit stressed at that time
- what with my debts with the banks -
(Laughter) but seeing the beautiful sight of those birds, the first thing I thought, well felt,
was that they were like my children.
(Laughter) But do you know what the greatest thing was?
I looked up at the birds, and all the debt just disappeared.
and I said to them, "Thank you, all that stress has gone."
El Cascajo wetlands, you'll remember, were green.
When I had finished it looked like this.
Now for the applause please.
(Laughter) (Applause) But to maintain the area we needed a team.
The first to join were children.
Before their mums wouldn't let them near the water as they'd come out all mangy,
but now the children play there.
They're playing and throwing stones at the animals,
and I shout, "Stop throwing stones!"
(Laughter) But seeing these children I said, "I have returned to my childhood."
It's really something special.
El Cascajo wetlands now has a big family.
It's called Cascajo Team.
And anyone can join this family.
But, to maintain the water, we have clean-up campaigns,
with more than 150 volunteers from all over Peru.
We're starting a clean-up tomorrow if you're interested.
(Laughter) But first and foremost do you know what?
Someone very special to me said,
"Why don't you do this all over Peru?"

(Laughter)
She's the culprit. My sister Marian.
She said to me,
"It'd be great wouldn't it?"
Well, yes. Let's do this! And so,
I'm going back to Peru next year.
I'm starting with Lake Titicaca, the Chira river, and the Paca lagoon. And I hope to cover
many more natural habitats.
There's already a waiting list.
(Laughter) (Applause) She shared some words with me which I'd like to share with you,
and I'd like you to share them, too.
"May the colors of the heart of the natural world reach the hearts of mankind."
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
I think that art is, above all, an intimate experience.
Therefore, I will share with you some of the questions I make myself in order to build my work.
Do you have your cell phones at hand?
Those of you who don't, respond the questions inwardly and enjoy the experience.
When you want to answer "yes" to the questions I'm going to ask, turn on your phone screen and face it towards the ceiling, upwards, where a camera will record the result of our answers and will project them on these screens.
There are two important things to make this work.
One is that when the answer is "no", we take care to cover well the light of our phone, so no light comes out, put it here, as you see fit.
But let no light come out.
And the other important thing is that when we show our response, we go back to darkness, in order to go together to the next question.
Now I'm going to ask you another question: Is this the year 2015?
Now that we know how to respond, let's start with the questions.
Do you feel that you are an animal?
Let's go to the second: Do you use your gifts?
Would you like to feel less afraid?
Are you relieved by living on a planet that floats in space?
Do you like useless things?
(Laughter) Joy, is it powerful?
Do you trust common sense?
Would you like your house to have wheels?
Do you have a place to cry?
Do you admire strangers?
Would you like to nap in your job?
(Laughter) (Laughter) Is it possible to live less angry?
Do you like hanging out?
Are we ready for our thoughts to be heard?
Thank you very much.
I invite you to keep asking ourselves questions, to keep creating images together.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I'm a psychiatrist and my profession is probably the most imperfect one that exists.
Psychiatry, if we think about it, was created as an instrument of power, arguably scientific, that tries to define the place between "normal" and "abnormal."
And as psychiatrists, we're nothing more than experts who decide where that place is.
But let's think about this a little.
What are the consequences for crossing that line?
Well, not more than 4 decades ago, the distinction between "normal" and "abnormal" caused the institutionalization of people who were "sick."
It was that terrible era of insane asylums.
Now, a few years later, that has changed in most of the world.
But the distinction between "normal" and "abnormal" still exists.
It's much more subtle, if I may, it's conceptual.
It's a linguistic distinction.
That distinction is formed by a diagnosis.
Namely, the people who aren't "normal" have a psychiatric diagnosis, and we psychiatrists are responsible for this.
With the diagnosis, what happens is very similar to what happened in institutions years ago, which is, on one hand, that the crazy identify themselves as sick, and on the other, that society has a reason, an excuse, to justify isolating them.
Thus, 4 years ago a group of young mental health professionals that had joined together in the old Doctor Villacián Healthcare Center, thought that all professionals involved in the mental health "movement" should think a little about this.
Generally, everyone in society, but above all, young professionals.
Thus began the Delirious Revolution.
Behind this big name, is a movement whose only objective is to adopt a lot of the ideals and the ideas that influenced our teachers when mental hospitals closed during the Psychiatric Reform, and to apply them to today's so-called psychology.
But of course, we could not stop there.
We couldn't just talk about it in theory, because those are just words, the intriguing part was to bring it to healthcare, to clinical practices, which was up to us.
I may have shocked some of you because I constantly use the word "insane" or "crazy."
Perhaps it's a word that can sound bad to some, but some of us prefer to use it instead of "mental illness," which seems more politically correct.
When we talk about "mental illness," we're talking about illness.
We're positioning the person in a passive place concerning him, with something that has nothing to do with him.
However, when we talk about insanity, we call that difference, that imperfection, possibly, that brilliance, the way the person can create a different way to exist in the world, or not create.
And it's because you can define insanity in many ways.
One of them, is that insanity is a way of living in the world.
It's a way that could be imperfect, distressing, it may imply a lot of suffering, this we see in our work everyday.
However, sometimes, it's the only way the person has to be himself and to be with others.
Everyday we see things like how loneliness, the cause of loneliness, is a much more important factor in the formation of a crisis, in forming psychiatric clinics, than supposed biochemical imbalances or neurotransmitters, or genetic disorders, which they want us to think is always the cause, and that loneliness is much more important than those.
Our own patients tell us that, in statements like, sometimes it's better to feel followed, to feel watched, to be paranoid, you could say, than to be alone.
Or those thoughts that stay stuck in your head, like sometimes it's better to believe in God than to know anyone.
Well if it's like that, the professionals, instead of being those in society who make the diagnosis, what we should do to be useful to the patient is to find the place where that person can create a new way of living in the world that doesn't involve being crazy, but is unique to him.
Some of us are lucky to be able to work with this way of thinking with these ideas, in some places in public health.
That's the idea that I want to share with you today.
We're working in a center, in a pilot project, we've spent a year and 9 months in the Río Hortega University Hospital in Valladolid, it's called the Community Intervention Center and it's a place where we work with people who've been told by psychiatrists that they have a serious mental disorder.
Meaning people with psychotic disorders or people with serious personality disorders.
But we always try to work by doing some of what I've said, blurring the diagnosis and getting to know that one person who can create something new.
And we do that, sometimes, simply by changing the way we relate.
For example, changing the question with which we approach that person.
Doctors use questions like, What are your symptoms?
When did they start, how bad are they,
and why do you think you have them?
If psychiatrists use those questions, in fact, they use them often, we would confuse the terms, in my opinion.
Medication addresses the body, while psychiatrists deal with something much broader.
It may be too philosophical, but I think that the field of psychiatry is the field of the soul.
Therefore, the question that professionals should ask a patient is what he needs, and in every case, what he suffers from, because here we're engaging in the individuality of that person.
Although it may be a little controversial, we the psychopharmacological treatment, the psychiatric drugs, we use them, but we believe they're only a means, not an end, not a solution for anything.
Therefore, we always have to be in agreement with the patient.
Now that we're in a time where some defend involuntary outpatient treatments, and this is becoming a debate.
In the end, what we want is that we professionals adapt to the person's needs, and not that the person has to adapt to what we think we know he needs.
It's a very subtle nuance, but it completely changes the therapeutic relationship.
In the year and 9 months we've spent, we've seen that sometimes it's more useful when a person is sick, to go for a walk together, or to a cafe to drink coffee, than to make an appointment within a month.
We've also seen that it's more efficient, since now everything is about efficiency, to visit the patient at home than to wait until he's admitted to start treatment.
Or, sometimes, when a person is sick, providing something for him, like a radio program, for example, or helping him move is much better than giving him medication.
In fact, this could be summarized in one short sentence: we try to substitute the treatment with treatment.
And we should use that treatment with the community, in society, which is where the problem lies, as we said earlier.
This is a difficult task, it's hard work, above all, it's a task that's difficult to understand for the most orthodox psychiatrists, for other medical workers, and for society itself.
But it's a job in which you don't stop learning.
And perhaps the most important thing to learn, what I want to share with you today, is that professionals namely, those in mental health, we don't know anything.
It's the knowledge from insanity that should guide us.
The power-knowledge dichotomy inversion, which founded psychiatry in the early 14th century, is what we need to enact to be useful to the patient, because we shouldn't forget that we work for them.
Therefore, even though I'm a professional, today I'll try to open the bars of the cell a little that psychiatrists have built since science was born, and I'm going to surrender what is most important, knowledge, which is the word given to those assumed to be crazy.
I'm going to reverse it, I'll give up the time, and I'll leave insanity as insanity and go outside the cell.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Have you ever lost something?

Anything.
A cell phone, keys or money?
Raise your hand if you have ever been robbed or have lost something.
Almost everyone in the theatre
has experienced a moment like this.
Do you remember how you felt when you lost that something?
You probably felt angry, frustrated and confused.
A lot of emotions like these, intertwined.
In my case, my most important loss has been the loss of my older brother who died in 1998 in a car accident.
I can say that this experience is unique, terrible.
It has taken me a long time to assimilate the pain and loss of my brother.
But over time I was able to find my tranquility through what I call, "The Art of Healing".
My name is Anthony Alcalde Perez and I was born in Paterson, NJ, to a Nicaraguan mother and Peruvian father.
In 1998, I was 21 years old and in college studying a Bachelor's in Accounting.
Growing up as the second son in a single-parent home, I was raised by my mother -- my older brother, Paul, was the father figure, role model and best friend.
I wanted to be like him, and I could not.
So when he died late at night in a car accident, as a result, I began to question everything -- especially my relationship with God, the Creator.
How could he have done this?
How could he have let this happen?
You know, blaming everyone.
I did not know what to do and felt a separation.
Seven years after the death of my brother, in 2005, I created my first short film, entitled: "Happy Birthday".
It was a project inspired by a ritual I did every year in honor of Paul.
It is not a traditional celebration.
It is not a birthday with cake and a piñata.
It is more like a six-pack of beer while I talk to myself at the cemetery.
I have to say I am not a filmmaker by trade, but during that time I was working for a television studio and something inside me wanted to grab a camera and tell a story.
I felt the need to express my emotions, something I could no longer achieve with words or tears.
Share my feelings with my family.
Carl Jung said: "From the living fountain of instinct flows everything that is creative; hence the unconscious is not merely conditioned by history, but is the very source of the creative impulse."
Shortly afterwards, the same year of 2005, my Peruvian-born grandmother passed away.
I looked up to her because she was like a guardian angel and gave us unconditional love.
Every night she lit a candle and prayed before going to sleep.
I would sneak into her room to see a small alter with the image of a Black Christ.
I really did not know what it was but it made me curious.
I learned more about the Black Christ when my grandmother was taken to her final resting place and some relatives held a procession in her honor.
They told me that the Black Christ is a painting that exists in Lima, Peru.
Which was created in 1651 by an Angolan slave on a adobe wall.
After several earthquakes, and unsuccessful attempts to destroy the wall, the painting remained intact and people began to pray to the wall.
Some even attribute it miraculous healing powers.
But as my relationship with God the Creator was at its worst, I did not see this painting as something miraculous.
I began to think about the Black Christ and became curious.
I wondered if the slave painted the image to find peace or liberation.
If maybe it was similar to the creation of my short film, "Happy Birthday".
Apart from my reflections about the Angolan artist, I visited my friend, a conceptual artist in Brooklyn, New York.
She explained the role that art had played in her life.
She said, "I use this technique to learn things, it helps me understand things."
She also said, "I would shatter my ego as if it were glass and then look for ways to reorganize the pieces together."
I really did not know what she meant.
I just thought why would someone break glass and then put their hands in the fragments, running the risk of cutting themselves.
I just thought my friend was crazy, but at the same time I perceived that she was describing my feelings, by demonstrating her art of healing.
After meeting with my friend, I decided to go to Burning Man, an Arts Festival that is held annually of one week in duration in the Nevada desert.
It is famous for uniting people of all nationalities and religions in radical artistic expression.
When the means of transportation I had secured cancelled two days before, I had to find another way to get there.
So I came across Zac, a stranger with room in his RV, who happened to be a funeral director.
On the way, we talked about his work and the effort he makes to provide comfort to people in their grief.
We also spoke about his recent divorce and how he came to find art, in his case photography to find comfort, his art of healing.
A friend made me promise to take a painting to Flux, the Temple of Burning Man, 2010, in memory of someone recently deceased.
I never met him, but was honored to commemorate his loss.
At Burning Man there is room for deep spirituality where one can go to show respect, let go off their loved ones or seek inner peace.
This was my moment in the tomb of the unknown soldier and it fulfilled me.
I started to look around and saw people crying.
It was probably not the best time to start to think of my brother, but I did not feel alone.
I felt that these artists were also suffering and creating their art of healing.
I felt that this community of artists had given me the authorization to express myself in any way I saw fit.
Suddenly, I knew that I had to go to Peru to see the Black Christ for myself.
When I was on the plane heading to Peru I felt nervous.
I still do not know why but probably because I had set a goal of seeing the Black Christ painting and did not know what would come next.
I have always liked Peru, especially due to the influence of my grandmother and Peruvian food is still one of my favorites.
When I finally found the Church of the Black Christ, my heart began to accelerate.
This was it!
I was about to go inside and see the image of the Black Christ.
After all of these years looking for answers, I would finally see the image, created by the slave in 1651 on that wall of adobe.
When I entered the church the light was dim, so I had to squint my eyes to adjust my vision.
There was a group of people in the front altar by the painting and another group of people passing by.
I felt attracted by the colors and also by the efforts I had made to see this painting.
I began to feel sad.
It was as if I could feel the pain of the slaves and how the painter became liberated through this painting.
Suddenly, I just fell on my knees, took my head in my hands and began to cry.
They were tears of joy because I no longer felt the pain.
I'€™m not sure what happened that moment, but that painting felt miraculous.
I discovered this painting at a time of great confusion -- during a time I was transitioning into adult life, and it gave me tranquility.
At that moment, I realized that it is not the specific art that matters but rather the journey that accompanies it, the journey of creation.
We become the Creator, we empathize with the contemplation of the work.
After the death of my brother I was just looking for any way to mitigate the pain and allow life to recover its meaning.
This whole process, these experiences of life, which I share with you today made me see the great power of art and the possibilities it has to heal the wounds of the soul.
My short film, "Happy Birthday" is my Black Christ and much like the Angolan slave, I was also liberated through the art of healing.
I am convinced, that there is a world of beauty inside each one of us that is filled with art.
And when we are in great pain and despair we can create beautiful things through art,
like my friend in Brooklyn and her glass or Zac, the funeral director, with photography
and the other artists at the art festival.
Pablo Picasso said, "Art washes away from the soul the dust of everyday life."
At some moment in life, each one of us will face a traumatic event.
And will then have to decide whether you are going to create or destroy. Or better said, What are you going to create?
Namaste.
(Applause)
I've practiced Kung Fu for eight years.
A martial art where we develop strength and flexibility, resilience, concentration and peripheral gaze.
That is, looking straight ahead, but still being aware of what goes on all around.
Each movement we make, every action we take.
Every decision we make impacts our surroundings, for better or for worse.
We can either protect or cause damage.
When we develop the peripheral look the probabilities of hurting or being hurt diminishes.
[The Bridge] I am a graphic designer and contrary to a fireman or a nurse,
it took me years to link my major with social problems.
I started lecturing at UBA, Argentinian public university.
Time went by and I started spreading human rights through design.
But one day, while on this personal and professional pursuit, the story I'm about to tell you, started.
That day I had a meeting with an engineer and an anthropologist who were starting an NGO.
Engineering without Borders Argentina, and they needed to improve their visual communication.
The meeting ended and I was briefly told of a female engineer who was part of the team: Estelita.
She has given classes in Devoto jail for over 20 years.
When I learned I could teach in jail, it blew my mind.
So I asked them to help me get in touch with her, and we agreed to visit the prison.
Days went by and I started to worry, to realize and think, what am I going to do now?
A graphic designer in jail, so I called Estelita.
And she told me something I will never forget, those words were: "Coco, don't worry,
in jail they already know about you, guys will be waiting for you."
(Laughter) "Oh dear," I thought.
Some days after, and still clueless, I was entering a jail for the first time.
[CUD] The CUD, Devoto College, a university in jail.
There, inmates can attend workshops and make college majors: Law, Administration, Philosophy, Literature and more.
To enter the college pavilion, I had to go through nine iron doors.
Each door with huge padlocks and keys that looked medieval.
Those doors echoed when closed behind you, and the route is of about half a block.
Each of these doors has at least two security guards.
But by the ninth door, there is no more police.
You find yourself in a pavilion with 200 inmates, students, and there is no more police: it's a university.
That first day we had a meeting with ten inmates who had a plan.
One big problem they face is reinsertion into the job market.
Some of them leave jail with a degree or a trade, but nobody would give them a job, due to their criminal record or just because they live in a marginalized area.
So this guy turned towards self-management
to create a printing house cooperative.
So I asked, "Guys, what do you know about this trade"
and their answer was: "Nothing." That's when I realized what was I doing.
If they agreed, I offered going back every week, to teach them and share with them my knowledge.
How to use Photoshop, Illustrator and design tools.
Design theory, stuff to make this project possible.
This was two years ago.
Two years, one month and four days.
Today, these guys are designing already, they print and bookbind.
From Engineering without Borders we were able to buy the equipment, some expensive yet necessary for their job.
There's still a lot to learn before having a strong project yet it evolved from an idea on a table into something concrete called: Cooperative Esquina Libertad.
A little after I started going, I realized I was investing a lot of time and energy in an important project, with an impact for very few.
So I spoke again to Estelita, the engineer.
As usual, I asked her for advice.
I decided to start a new workshop.
But this time aimed for all jail population willing to take part, not just the university students, but anyone who hadn't finished elementary and junior high school.
[Poster workshop] I asked the guys to help me spread the word in the pavilions I did not had access to.
They placed posters on the pavilions and the next week I got a message, that read: "Coco, 120 people signed up for the workshop."
Oh dear, I began to worry
for the organization.
The workshop started and the statement was to create human rights posters.
They create posters about right to housing, to work to education and so on; they have
a sharp gaze on these subjects.
It is hardly the same any poster I might do, I who compared to these guys, never lacked anything.
They work with collage, cut out paper, magazines.
Some are good at drawing and help those who are not that good.
They obviously have no scissors, such material is not allowed, it is jail.
They spend some days drawing their sketches and correct together; finally, there is one last project.
Beyond everything they learn, what's important of this workshop, at least for me, is that it is a bridge for these guys, who never step out of the common pavilion, to at least come down and see the university pavilion, and learn that there are other ways.
One time, one of them came to me and told me that he had never before done anything creative.
And that during the workshop he forgot he was in jail.
Then he hugged me, and I can assure you that those things are priceless, for a teacher like myself and as a person.
There in Devoto, there is also a musical project.
A music band developed in there and they have a nice name: "Portate bien", Behave.
They play cumbia, reggaeton but its distinctive feature is that, since they are CUD college students every day for many years, their lyrics are not like "Laura's thong is showing,"
they are about Aristotle, Plato, Foucault.
"Newton's gravitation law is not my calling, doesn't get traction on me," for instance.
(Laughter) Yeah, it's great!
Music is another tool to speak out what they live in there.
Last year they did an excellent record.
I highly recommend it, it's on the web, look for it:
"Sin candados en la Mente",
Unlocked mind. [Waikiki] Waikiki is a guy from Fuerte Apache, marginal suburb, his childhood dream was becoming a professional chess player.
At school they didn't loved him so much he was a hand full and also, was discriminated for coming from a slum. But when there were intercollegiate chess championships, he was sent in because they knew he always came back with a trophy.
But Waikiki's dream was impossible.
He grew up and like many of his friends, ended up committing crimes.
He got into a lot of trouble and in a clash with police he got shot twice in the mouth;
he was left for dead in the nursery, unassisted, maybe with the aim of letting him die.
But Waikiki didn't die and ended up in jail.
Paradoxically, it was in jail where he first connected with a book.
He read a novel, one after the other.
From that moment, Waikiki couldn't hold back his hidden brilliance.
Today Waikiki studies Latin, French.
He is 12 subjects away from earning his Literature degree and four subjects away from earning his Administration degree.
I'm convinced that the best security policy is providing education. Bringing dignity and opportunities on time.
The rest is a patch.
(Applause) But I'm also convinced that education is not enough.
Education is fundamental, I'm a teacher.
it widens our horizons,
but the real power, the decisions that change the world, are not made by guys from Fuerte Apache.
But, by people who attended the best universities in the world, and inequality grows annually.
Then again, education is not enough.
Having diplomas hung on the wall is useless if we don't take a moment to think the purpose of that knowledge.
How you use it?
To attack or to protect?
And there is more.
I will share with you something I learned from Kung Fu, an idea I deeply believe in and that leads my behavior.
It's a simple and beautiful idea.
Individual progress is group progress, and group improvement, is individual improvement.
Becoming better at something, whatever it is, we are pushing our environment forward like an engine.
There are no individual failures or achievements.
We are part of a society, we influence one another all the time, permanently.
I earned my degree because my family stood by me.
Because of the educational system, public university in my case.
Having my name on the degree is a collective achievement.
Likewise, when we see a person sleeping on the street, OK, we could think that they made bad decisions, maybe.
But if we remain indifferent as we pass them by, if having people living on the streets becomes normal, then we all failed.
That's a collective failure.
[Coevolution] Coevolution is committing to becoming a better version of ourselves, becoming part of our environment and improving it.
By improving it we improve ourselves.
It's that simple.
Waikiki, this guy from Fuerte Apache, I go back to him.
He learned this, but not through Kung Fu, he learned through a hardest path, a tougher and more violent one.
Everything Waikiki does is to heal.
He understood the damage he caused.
So everything he does is to help others.
I invite you all today, to search for the best version of yourself
as we say in Kung Fu: Standing firm on the floor, your soul and chest aiming high, and looking straight ahead.
This is a lyric from "Portate Bien," they identify themselves as contemporary slum thinkers, and it goes like this: "Let's make the most of every subject, with every book let's start from the bottom.
Things depend on us, they change because of us. Let's start building from the ground.
Even if we are still lied to over and over, I will still move forward, I won't give up."
Coevolution and peripheral gaze.
[Change] I'm convinced that change is possible, I live that in jail, every week and in my life, but wishing for it is not enough, we need to stand up and do it, and do it all together.
Thank you.
(Applause)
One in three,
or 2,721,561 children in Spain, in our country, around us, live at risk of social exclusion.
It is a reality that brings Spain much closer to Morocco than to Norway.
It has been a matter of deep concern since 2007, at the beginning of this crisis, and in recent years the situation has worsened.
In fact, children were the social group who, by far, suffered the most during the crisis as compared with any other social group.
This graph compares them with the elderly, a group over 65, a traditionally vulnerable group in times of crisis, but who, during these years, reduced its risk of social exclusion almost by half.
That's in part because of the fall of average income in our country, but also because pensions worked very effectively as a protection system.
The opposite happened with children.
The problem with children is that such a hard blow will extend their suffering from the crisis sometimes during their whole lives.
Science has proved that even short lapses of intense poverty in crucial moments of their development affect children during their lifetime.
This means that for some children this crisis is more like a life sentence.
I mean, Spain finds itself in a state of emergency in respect of child poverty, but it is a silent emergency that goes unnoticed due to a fragmented, mediocre public debate. It is key and it will be the indicator on the political, ethical, and intellectual standards of our society to bring this debate to public light and take further action to solve it.
What does it mean to be a kid suffering from poverty in today's society,
in a developed society like ours where everybody denies the existence of it in our country?
Data based on income measurements shows that a poor family with two children allocates less than 355 euros a month for each member of the family.
This is what it means to be poor.
But for a child, poverty is a multidimensional phenomenon that sometimes works as a sort of vicious circle, and involves essentials such as shelter, food, education, healthcare, [future] employment, clothing, leisure and free time, which are all all basic needs and rights,
protection against violence, an increasingly worrying phenomenon in a crisis situation like the current one.
Some rates of these indicators of poverty have reached alarming high levels.
The rate of school failure including highschool dropouts, according to the latest available data from 2010-2011, is above 23% the European average in our country.
In the case of children or young adults with no hopes to further their education or improve their job prospects - the so-called Ni-Nis [the 'neither-study-nor-work' ones] - who victims too, those left with no alternative - we are talking about 1 out of 5 children in our country.
In the first quarter of 2014,
105 families lost their principal residence to foreclosure everyday,
but how many were households with children,
we don't know.
Because in this particular case, as in many others, inside the circle surrounding the problem of child poverty, our data is partial and disparate.
More importantly, for many children and their families, poverty goes further than those objective dimensions to which I referred before.
That involves fear, anxiety, frustration, embarrassment.
The statement of a Norwegian child got stuck in my mind when he was asked, "What is
poverty for you?" and he replied, "Poverty is when I can't invite my friends over because I'm ashamed of it."
That's the tragedy of child poverty that many of them face and which adults render irrelevant, or choose to overlook; instead, we rather pay attention to providing nutrition and education, but poverty is also that, that humiliation many children in our country experience.
The problem is that each and every pending cause mentioned above is being addressed only partially by the government institutions and very unevenly, thus responding to a mediocre and badly informed public debate sustained by the media and opinion leaders in our country filled with chattering commentators where we shift from one issue to another without fully grasping any of them.
But it is vital for us to understand to what extent we witness the impact of this silent national emergency I referred to before.
Some social groups suffer from this crisis disproportionately: children of immigrant population, children from monoparental families, children living in households with a low level of educational attainment, and it's vital to understand that this is a widespread phenomenon, one of impoverishment of the middle class.
Our social services not only have to do more with less - a forthcoming study commissioned by Save the Children found investment in early child education in Spain fell by almost 10% since 2011 -
not only have to do more with less, but also adapt their services to a population that simply didn't acknowledge it qualified as poor.
A custodian parent who has recently lost his job, and who had a perfectly normal life, just doesn't queue outside a soup kitchen as many people we have seen queuing up there in the past.
It is an ethical disaster
with no precedent of this scale in our country,
But I dare say it is much more than that. I think it is a matter-of-fact disaster acting as a boomerang, we are shooting ourselves in the foot.
Not solving the child poverty issue means we are raising a generation who will be much less productive in the future,
and that will affect our economy.
There are indicators that point at how fertility rates of Spanish families with children is already plummeting, with a tipping point in 2008.
Our state pension system is in danger of collapse because we can't guarantee families the necessary protection and required security to embark on such a crucial planning as having a child.
How do we solve this problem?
That's one of the most difficult questions we can ask ourselves.
I would like to start by telling you how it is not going to be solved.
This won't be fixed by doing no stroke of work and waiting for the macroeconomic indicators to rebound.
The future of children has simply nothing to do with debt interest rates.
I dare say they are only partially related to to job recovery in our country
because children mean much more than their parents' jobs - I'll elaborate on this a bit later.
There are three factors fundamental to understanding why this is a task for an entire generation, for our society as a whole.
The first step is acknowledging we have a problem,
just like the alcoholics, we have to be able to bring our problem out of hiding. We must speak openly about this on the streets, in the classrooms, in our workplaces.
We must be able to admit, "We are poor!"
because we won't be able to reclaim our children just because improved employment.
And then we have to do what we have done with evictions, we have to be able to look children in the eye and tell them: "It's not your fault."
"You are not responsible, you are the victims of an unjust situation."
This is something embarrassed parents whose children don't afford the books they need at school everyday must be aware of.
We have to act the same we did with "Stop Evictions," go out into the streets, talk about this reality, and support the families that are suffering from it.
Secondly, we as citizens have to join forces and demand our leaders to put an end to this political apathy we have been confronting with for years.
During the current legislative term, the Parliament has had 395 legislative initiatives with only two directly aiming at addressing issues related to children.
The Parliament rejected both drafts.
We experience a deep-seated political malfunction, we deny the problem, we deal with it poorly in the media, hence no political solution is offered by our leaders.
But our economic resources, those we talk so often about, will be the result of all the political courage and creativity and not the other way around.
Thirdly, we must place children at the heart of the issue.
Children and young adults are where all the child poverty issues originate and not where they end.
Children are not a mere extension of their families or of their parents' jobs.
They are not the property of institutions.
And placing them at the center is key to also completely reconsider the approach to tackling child poverty in our country.
I can offer two examples particularly inspiring in this sense.
Two experiences that hit close to home.
The first one comes from the UK; it started in 1999 and continued over the last decade.
It managed to arouse the public interest of a whole country that lead by its government placed the child poverty problem in the center of the public debate.
Its multidimensional facets considered all the elements we've described in the previous graph, and as a multi-institutional issue involved everyone from the Prime Minister to the last City Council in a problem that affected all state institutions. It was resolved by passing the Child Poverty Act 2010 committing future administrations to act in favor and not disregard the issue.
The British government set an example, opened up a road for all of us in the European Union by showing that there was no shame in recognizing that a developed country faced such a critical issue as child poverty.
The second example comes from the City Council of Avilés
that has just implemented its second Childhood and Youth Plan.
The fascinating detail of this plan is that children were at the helm of it.
It were the children who dealt with and started the inquiry with other children, negotiated with the technicians of the council the conditions their plan, The Childhood and Youth Plan, should meet.
More than 300 proposals were presented, discussed, and then considered in the context of the total city budget and technical capacity of the council, out of which 30 are now part of this plan of the City Council of Avilés.
Some of these proposals are riveting and surprising, for example, to build all the benches in the park circular,
not like those where I live in Chamberí square where each faces a different direction and people sitting on them look outwards, but circular, allowing children to meet and talk in the village, which is something very obvious but only understood by someone sitting in the village square and listening to the children's talk.
Let's not accept this defeat.
Let's not admit that crisis is sweeping away not only our compassion, but also our common sense.
Let's give this issue a central stage in our public debate.
I suggest we start today.
UNICEF launched a campaign to promote a national policy for childhood, a pact that sets the issue into the spotlight.
I invite you to use your smartphones and your social networks, to make this issue a trending topic again. It is fundamental for our leaders to realize this is something that will define us as a society for many years to come.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
One hundred eleven meters long.
Two thousand eight hundred tons of weight.
Five hundred million dollars.
And three astronauts.
Three, two, one, ignition.
In front of you Saturn V rocket
take off burning fourteen tons of fuel per second.
Generating more than four times stronger than the gravity. After two and a half minutes, it is 38 miles high and the speed of 5,900 mph.
After 6 minutes, the speed is about 14,000 mph and we get 118 miles high from ground.
And, all of a sudden, we find ourselves floating in the outside space.
I start to interest myself about aerospace stuff since I went living to Florida for one year.
There I visited the NASA museum in Cape Canaveral and I found out how much fascinating it is the aerospace exploration.
Since then, I could not stop with this interesting theme.
Then I signed into the Rocket Association in the United States and since then I interested myself more and more, in designing and building my own rocket models.
It looks like simple, but this sensation of seeing how something made by you crosses the sky on that way it is something hard to explain.
I felt how my insatiable curiosity and restlessness drove me to carry on learning.
From there I also went to do designing of planes and drones always with that feeling that I wanted to fly.
Lately, I attended a conference by Pedro Duque first and only Spaniard astronaut that flied to space, and I was really shocked.
It was so strong the feeling of keeping on forward researching the theme that I've put myself at work on an research project in which I carry on today.
Here, I do experimental flights of my own projectiles and I analyzed the flight data.
After that, I try to find out the physics and maths relation that there is between the rocket's parameters and the flight it have done.
Now, I would like that you follow me on this trip where I am going to show you a little portion of the space.
It is hard to make an idea of the complexity and the costs that means take something out of the planet Earth.
It is endless the amount of things that should work properly for the mission to succeed.
The work of thousands engineers, for the exact and millimeter-precise construction of each part of the rocket. It is simply amazing.
So we can ask our self: "Why to do it?"
The curiosity is the key of what always guided the human being to explore beyond our frontiers.
This, combined with that feeling of getting better each time with no fear to fail and learning from the mistakes that is what keep taking us to find out something so amazing like it is the Universe.
That's why I came here today to tell you my fascination about all this stuff.
Look for a moment to the image, made from Voyager Space Probe.
This is how we see ourselves from about 3.7 billion miles.
It is really awesome that we could go out of our Solar System and explore the unknown, taking to limits our knowledge.
It is also awesome that we went to places never explored.
This image show us a part of the lander of Rosetta's Space Probe that travelled 4 billion miles since went out from Earth.
By the way, I would like now that you think more or less the speed you are when you're parking your car.
And even then, you still hit sometimes the car on the back, true?
Well, this Space Probe that is traveling at 84,000 mph can land on a comet it is something really exciting.
And what about the International Space Station?
One of the biggest engineer constructions made by human being.
It is all about space lab that is orbiting around the Earth sixteen times a day, so big as a football field at 250 miles high, at the speed of 17,000 mph.
There work and live permanently six astronauts that are doing experiments on an microgravity environment.
Thanks to them, we've made several improvements on different fields including fighting against cancer, or Alzheimer, or the study of the human body in this space environment for long periods of time.
The development of aerospace technology took us to invent, from something so simple like the zipper, like something more complex like a water purifying system that allow us to recycle up to 93 percent of liquid volume.
All of this is very important for the advance of humankind because it is part of our own evolution.
And more, it gives inspiration for later generations and drive us to keep exploring.
It gives us a big motivation because the illusion is the strongest thing in our life we should hold on to it, whatever it is our passion.
Finally, I would like to say good-bye, with this image from 1984.
A human being, on the endless space, in front of the fragility of our planet.
You can think I am crazy but if I tell you that my dream is to be an astronaut I wouldn't be lying to you.
And if I can't get it, the passion of exploring the space it would give me the motivation of giving the best of me each day.
Thank you.
Look at this picture.
I love this picture because I look gorgeous, so smart in my white suite.
Also, because it best represents my life.
I'm alone, surrounded by men.
Men that feel awkward, wearing contempt smiles, feeling a bit nervous.
Because I'm standing in a place we women are not allowed to be.
Where men are allowed but not us.
I was the only woman in the National School of La Plata.
The first woman to study medicine at UBA, and the first foreign woman to get Argentine citizenship.
(Applause) I'm not here to talk about how unique I am, but how unique all women can be.
This picture represents a historical event of great importance for my life and for the lives of all women.
In 1911, all inhabitants had to register for the city elections.
What are the registration requirements?
To be a citizen of legal age and resident of the city, have a business or industry, practice their profession and pay taxes.
I complied with all the requirements.
Obviously, women cannot vote, but they had forgotten to add this.
Or perhaps, it's so obvious it wasn't necessary to clarify it.
Anyway, I took my chance, I went to register and they couldn't prevent it.
I remember how carefully I kept that ballot.
It was my passport to a new right.
(Applause) I couldn't sleep the previous days.
On the elections day, I woke up early, I dressed in my suit and went to St. John the Evangelist Church.
The soldier guarding the entrance didn't let me in.
So, I stared at him and told him: "I'm going to get in, I'm a citizen and have rights and want to enter."
The man told me: "It's a place for voters."
So I looked at him and said: "It's easy to play macho with a gun
at the waist, isn't it?
He was stunned -- (Applause) He was shocked.
So I plucked up my courage.
And said: From whose womb were you born?
From a woman's.
Who washed your bottom and fed you when you were a baby?
A woman.
Who will do that when you become a decrepit old man?
A woman.
(Applause) Maybe I crossed the line, but nothing is more annoying to me than injustice.
To top it all, I showed him the ballot,
and he had to let me in.
I stood behind the last man in the line.
They all looked at me a bit awkwardly, some were mad.
But I stood there, with my head held high, calmly looking ahead.
When it was my turn, I gave them my certificate and like the most ordinary procedure in the world, I was allowed to vote.
It would be unfair if I told you all men laughed at me.
Not all men are against our rights.
Some women sometimes are more tough.
(Applause) The chairperson was Saldías, he told me he was proud to sign the first woman's document in the suffrage for the city elections, in the country and in South America.
Yes, I was the first woman to vote in Argentina and South America.
(Applause) The image pictures it perfectly: November 26, 1911: The first women's vote in South America.
That one sitting down, the soldier there, isn't the soldier who stopped me.
He is the one behind me, you can see a bit of his hat.
I got before him so he couldn't be in the photo.
(Laughter) I ran to the papers to tell the news.
As a woman I had taken part in the elections with my vote.
Every woman in the country and the world should know it, it was the chance to start changing things.
Don't wait for the system to change to attain goals.
Especially, if it is the system what you want to change.
(Applause) Dig deeper and deeper, until you find a way out.
Then, from the inside, fight to change things.
I learnt from medicine that a very severe disease is not cured with lotions but with shots.
When a prescribed drug enters the body it starts working.
So I managed to vote and I was after national elections.
Warned about my intentions, they didn't let me in.
They said I needed the military register.
And, what a coincidence,
only men can do military service.
So I asked the War Minister himself to enroll me in the Army.
Envision me doing push ups in this suit --
No, I didn't make it. I wasn't allowed.
But it was better, for them.
Because I kept looking for the legal loophole.
Searching endlessly.
And Constitution said we couldn't vote, but could be candidates, instead. It also said, "Nobody can be banned from doing what is not prohibited.
I asked Angela, a friend, who is a lawyer and an activist, and she told me I was right.
We could be candidates and hadn't capitalized it.
I ran for Congress in the nation-wide elections,
filled the city with signs, and claimed our rights everywhere. It is incredible that we are ignored by law, not considered subjects of law, that all laws are biased because they are made by men.
The female approach can contribute a lot.
We are nobody's property.
Husbands and wives must have the same rights.
And on second thought, marriage should be abolished
for it is not necessary for survival.
(Applause) We also vindicate mothers' role as basis for creation itself.
We are children's advocates, mostly for orphans, their education, hospitals -- Who else would be better than us in fighting for their rights?
But we don't stand for women, because women are not a "thing" needing to be defended.
They are strong, as everyone else, having been placed in an inferior place by countless prejudices.
(Applause) Women should stand for themselves, and achieve all their claims.
Women emancipation should blaze by overcoming preconceptions, and no longer imploring rights.
Because, women, you know what?
Rights are not begged for, but conquered.
(Applause) And we have to seize them.
We have to conquer them.
Look how unfair it is.
If I asked you to raise your hands and tell me who voted, it would surely be the hands of men
and only one woman: me.
Let's try it. Who voted?
We did it!
(Applause)
What an inspiration it is to wake every new day in this place.
A project inspired by the memory of my mother, Martha M. Fernandez, who spent much of her life developing agroecology in Cuba and the world.
Actually the project we're doing is to reinvent Cuban agriculture using our own experience.
This experience must combine rural wisdom with scientific knowledge.
I completed this doctoral thesis in Wageningen Uni, Holland, 7 years ago.
The title was "Farming like we're here to stay".
In other words, an agricultural system which doesn't quit.
That was the fundamental inspiration behind this thesis.
However, at that time I didn't really understand what I wanted to say with this concept.
Some years later I understood that farming like we're here to stay was much more than the experiments I had done in my professional life. It was nothing less than a commitment to the land where we live, Cuba.
It has been an attempt to reinvent ourselves.
Our family has taken the decision to move to the countryside, to face up to big challenges, to take an opportunity to dream, to feel passionate, to be excited, to experience another way to develop our country and farming.
We had to move far away from the comfortable city life, particularly from my established professional life.
To start this journey was really exhilarating.
It was something in which we could completely throw ourselves.
Like digging this well in the rock which took 7 months, and is a metaphor for ecological farming, for healthy farming, thriving, huge, and abundant, promoting health, and contributing to a better world.
It's the commitment to dig a well 14 metres in the rock by hand.
There is someone who has been a great inspiration for us.
He has been our constant guide in this journey.
This man, who is over there, I can introduce to you.
Juan Machado.
(Applause) Juan Machado is a symbol for us, a symbol of the connection with the Cuban countryside.
With the never ending work of the farmers who have entrusted into their hands this sacred land of Cuba.
Together with him, we have worked the land.
Together with him, we dug the well. And, together with him, we have dreamed of a better future.
Juan Muchado, 77 years, is very optimistic about the future of agriculture, and the future of our nation.
We've been on this journey now for several years. At the beginning of the '90s my parents and I founded the Cuban Movement for Organic Agriculture and Agroecology (MCAO).
This was the management group of an association that dreamed of an organic future for Cuban agriculture; an agroecological future.
A future that was fair and equitable
and that worked the land for the health of the Cuban people.
Organic agriculture and agroecology in Cuba grew
and has been the inspiration not only for many people in Cuba but also many people elsewhere.
We are a light showing the way for others.
Many people in the world see us as an example of the transformation of an agroecological model on a countrywide scale.
All of us who have been involved in this transformation truly deserved the alternative Nobel Prize, presented in the Swedish parliament in 1999.
Through all this time it's been more about inspiration lead by necessity.
[Integrated model of food and energy] This diagram makes me think of the scale of an integrated system, an agriculture and food system.
In this case, a model for food and energy.
But agriculture is much more than this
Agriculture is a passion, as I've already said, it's love, it's family, it's environment, it's a frog, it's a butterfly, it's a flower.
There's no better way to achieve this transformation than to get involved at a practical level.
To do it with your own hands.
To combine traditional knowledge with scientific knowledge, with this shared heritage we have.
And continuing along this journey, we have learned how to make a biodigestor to make biogas and for a long time now we have been cooking for many people using the biogas which we have produced.
Also, we have learned to be beekeepers to grow flowers and to produce lots with high productivity, with a high yield, with great efficiency.
Because, for me, as you can imagine, it's a personal project, a family project, a project for society, but also a professional project.
And this is the result.
A harmonious and beautiful result, which we are a part of.
We are trying to design diverse and varied landscapes that give us the strength to continue moving forward.
There are four essential words, four magic words that I have written down on this paper.
One is resolve, another is the resources that we have, another is opportunity and the other is knowledge.
These four key words combined with the solidarity we can have with other people and the results of co-operation between us all.
Talking of a co-operative we don't belong to the co-operative but the co-operative belongs to us.
We are all involved in a transformation to achieve this beauty, this landscape and the vast, abundant production we all need and that promotes health.
So that we can use the resources we have, like solar energy.
So that we can deliver them directly to consumers and we can enhance the relationship between consumers and producers.
So that we can increase agrotourism to finance agricultural practices.
So that we can promote a sufficient standard of living for farmers, to improve their quality of their life and satisfy their spiritual and material needs.
It's a new dawn at Finca Marta, it's another new day and there we will be fighting for the future of Cuban agriculture.
We have one thought, and it is: How can we achieve this future for Cuban agriculture?
In fact, there is no formula for this.
Travellers, there is no path, you make the path by walking.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Sometimes, you have to be careful what you ask for.
In 2004, a historical event took place: a teenager was kidnapped and murdered in Buenos Aires.
I, like many others, was afraid this could happen to my children.
I put a fence in my house, demonstrated against insecurity, asked for longer and harder sentences, that nobody ever be released.
But days later, my son Juan, who had just turned 18, was having a drink with a girl friend at a bar in Plaza Serrano when he was taken for someone who had been stealing around.
Someone who had stolen four empanadas with a Tramontina knife.
He tried to explain it was a mistake, that he had nothing to do with that.
However, they did not believe him, got him into a patrol car and took him away.
When I got the news I rushed to the police station, but they didn't let me see him.
They told me he was incommunicado and that I should look for a lawyer.
Juan's father was a doctor and a very committed father, but he had died a few years before and I was alone, and completely lost.
Suddenly, I remembered a friend who is a criminal lawyer.
I went to see him and he told me that although he was 100 percent sure that Juan was innocent, things weren't that simple and that we would meet the next day at the entrance of the Courts.
That morning I went to and fro the corridors waiting for everything to be clarified and to see them crossed that door.
But no.
The lawyer came out alone and told me my son was going to be imprisoned.
I could not believe it.
I cried, got angry with him, told him he hadn't known how to defend him and that I wanted to talk to the judge, to explain nobody in my family had ever set foot in a police station, my son had nothing to do with that, it surely was a confusion.
The judge received me and said: "Madam, I don't want demonstrations against insecurity at the doorstep of the courthouse.
I'm not going to let anyone free until I'm sure, I don't want to be in the newspapers."
I sat all day in front of the Courts watching a gate where grey trucks of the prison service go to and fro.
I knew that place, I went pass hundreds of times, but I had never stopped to think who were the people that were in there, nor what those women were waiting for.
At around 11 pm, they got my son into one of those trucks.
They took him to Ezeiza, to a maximum security prison.
I asked the lawyer if he could kindly follow him with the car because I could not leave him, I needed to know what that place was like.
There, I realized that I didn't know anything about jail.
My mom and my sister, who loved me wanted to give me the best advice -- but everything they told me didn't work for me at all.
I had to learn by myself, by listening to other women who were going through the same thing.
To enter prison, you can't dress in black, blue or gray; shirts have to have sleeves; clothes don't have to be tight, and nobody tells you why.
You have to stand in a line from very early in the morning, to see him only for a little while, once a week after noon.
To enter you are requisitioned
and your belongings as well. You have to take them sheets, blankets and something to eat, because they don't provide that.
I had to learn which food was allowed and which was not. And that these rules are not written anywhere and depend on the goodwill of the chief on duty.
Because in jail everything is arbitrary and ruled by fear.
I had to quit my job.
I rented my house and went with my youngest children, Agustín and Belén, to live with my mom to have an income, because having a family member imprisoned can almost be a full time job.
I also learned that prison is an unspoken topic.
I lied to my children, I told them his brother was on a trip.
But they knew it wasn't like that and something else was going on.
A friend advised telling them the truth, because they imagined his brother was dead.
I explained them the best I could but forbade them to talk about this at school because I was afraid other classmates would make fun of him, or other parents wouldn't let them play with their children.
Life sometimes has unexpected twists and turns.
Prisoners can't receive phone calls.
So I had arranged with my son that he was going to call me every day at the same time, it was the only way I had to know he was alive.
But one Friday he didn't call me.
I waited and, as he did not call me, I went to the prison, but they didn't want to tell me.
I asked a chief on duty and, mockingly, he told me "Madam, this is a prison, your son is a prisoner, find out in court."
It was after 1:30 pm and the courts were closed, the lawyer was on a trip and, again, there I was completely lost.
Someone told me that a neighbor had his brother imprisoned in Ezeiza.
I went to see him and asked him to kindly make his brother contact me.
That's how I met Alejo.
Alejo called me and I found myself crying desperately with a guy who was in prison and I did not know, asking him to find my son.
And for the first time I was relieved, because I was talking to someone that could understand me without having to explain so much.
Hours later my son called me.
He told me he was in a cell because he had argued with an inmate that wanted to take off his shoes.
It was June, and he was in boxers and had no food for two days.
He told me: "Mom, get me out or I'll kill myself."
And I believed him, because he'd never spoke to me like that.
When Alejo called me again I thanked him for having found my son and begged for his help because he wasn't going to survive there
I don't know how, but he got him a blanket and something to eat.
After that weekend Juan returned to his pavilion.
Alejo didn't stop calling me every morning and every night from that day.
He listened and stood by me. He taught me to live through prison.
Regarding Juan, we had to wait two years until trial for the case to be clarified.
The lawyer had filed all possible appeals, the person who was robbed had stated my son wasn't guilty and we had to wait for two years.
I couldn't just stay still and wait. Alejo told me beyond the lawyer efforts, I should ask in court to bring that date forward.
I went every other day.
They told me they were collapsed, Justice had its own pace, there were hundreds of others like me and we should wait.
Finally, they moved the date forward.
It was proved that my son had not stolen the four empanadas and was absolved.
My son was imprisoned for six months despite being innocent.
For the Justice, everything was solved in record time.
For me and my family, it was an eternity.
A lot of things happened since Juan got out of prison, I married Alejo, for instance.
(Applause) I got married in jail knowing he had several years ahead to serve his sentence.
We have a son, Joaquín, who is twelve years old.
I also couldn't, didn´t want to return to my office work.
For I felt all I had seen, heard and lived could not remain like that.
I also felt I'd been a bad mother for not being able to protect Juan while he was in prison. I had neglected Agustín and Belén, who also needed me, and I didn't know, now that Juan was set free, how to help him move on.
I also knew I wasn't the only one. There were hundreds of families like me.
And I looked in all public offices for a space to help me with this.
On those tours, they told me: "The state is not going to give you what you need, set an NGO."
I didn't know what this meant, but with four women, mates in the long waiting lines, that is what we did.
A committed lawyer, Claudia Cesaroni, helped us a lot and in 2008 we set up the Civil Association of Detainees Relatives.
(Applause) It's a space where relatives and professionals not only take care of people in jail, but also of their children and families, because the sentence always affects families, especially women.
Generally, we will always be suspicious just for having a relative there.
Suddenly nobody believes us, we have to carry huge bags of food, do all the legal proceedings, understand difficult words from our lawyer carry on with the house, life, the other children.
Above all, we have the enormous burden of keeping them alive in there and to support them once they're free.
These people are imprisoned, they are not prisoners, and at some point they are going out.
For sooner or later the conviction ends and they are released.
This is something few people worry about, but we do, and we know jail doesn't prepare them for that.
Because the state invests a lot of money to prevent them from escaping, and little or nothing helping them finish primary school or caring for their health.
They aren't prepared for a trade or job.
Don't we all want them to work when freed?
But who of us will offer a job to a person who hadn't worked for 10 years, doesn't know what Internet is, learned computing on a cardboard keyboard, doesn't know to use the cell phone because it's forbidden, apart from having a criminal record?
How will they live when they are free?
In 10 years they surely won't know how to take the bus, the value of money and how to share other spaces with people who weren't in jail like them.
A friend explained it to me like this: "You can't learn to play ball in an elevator."
You can't learn to live free in a cell.
(Applause) A law has just been passed limiting temporary releases.
It should be the other way round, they should be mandatory, not as a reward or punishment, monitored and accompanied, but as a step on the way out.
Sometimes it's a way of saying "Enough, that's it."
But then the State should intervene, from the very day a person steps in jail and prepare them for their leave.
Shouldn't we demand this?
I hadn't wondered about this in 40 years.
Life has unexpected twists and turns.
My son was wrongfully imprisoned.
This injustice led me to a limit that opened my eyes to a world I didn't know it existed and that, like many of you, I didn't want to see either.
I keep demanding safety.
But not with the same recipes that fail again and again.
(Applause) In a few months, Alejo will be out.
Surely, as in many other cases, it will be at 12 am on the last day of his conviction.
He'll be left at the door of Ezeiza, in the middle of nowhere, with a bag with two briefs and not knowing what to do.
The State knows him very well, because it kept him for years, It knows him by name and surname.
But outside, it won't even give him the identity document.
His identification will be a piece of paper saying "I'm an ex- convict" so maybe, luckily, next morning, if brave enough, a bus will take him somewhere.
This won't happen to Alejo, his family and projects will be there.
Many things happened in 14 years.
Juan never entered prison again.
And I want to invite you to dare and take a look at what happens there
because something has to done differently.
Something has to change there, and also what happens outside prison.
(Applause)
Eight months ago, I decided not to let them decide for me.
Together with friends we saw that all over the world millions of young men and women were marching to fight the climate and ecological crisis.
And we saw that this could happen in Argentina.
So we decided to organize the first climate mobilization in the country.
It took place on Friday, March 15.
At first, we didn't think that many people would come.
We didn't have very good expectations about the call.
When the day came, we ended up meeting with 5000 protesters in front of the Congress and thousands of others demonstrating in different parts of the country.
With this result we thought, "Could we make Argentina declare climate and environmental emergency?"

We got together. Again, we got organized.
And we presented a draft resolution in front of the Senate.
On Wednesday, July 17, Argentina became the first country in Latin America to declare climate and environmental emergency.
(Applause) Which also showed that there was a long way to go.
And thanks to all these achievements, I was given the opportunity to participate in the first world Youth Climate Summit, convened by the United Nations.
There, next to activist Greta Thunberg, we could literally tell world leaders what we thought, in their face.
And taking part in meetings with very important presidents and very influential businessmen, I realized that my leadership not necessarily has to be delayed for a future I don't know how it's going to be like.
I realized that instead of being a leader of the future I can be a guardian of the present.
Eight months ago, I decided not to let them decide for me.
What would happen if all of you decided the same thing?
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
I'm here today to talk to you about the most exploited and most undervalued animals on our planet.
The animals I want to talk about today are intelligent, social and sentient creatures who have incredible abilities. For example, they can use tools, work together, and they have memory. Some of them can even recognize themselves in a mirror.
I assume that the description I am giving you makes most of you think that I'm going to talk about great apes, elephants, or dolphins, but it's not the case.
Today I am here to talk about fish.
And in fact, I've only been studying fish recently, but for ten years, I have dedicated myself to scientific research in the field of marine microbiology because I have always been fascinated by the immensity of the ocean and its unknown inhabitants.
I'm not sure if you know, but the ocean is the largest ecosystem on Earth that cover 70% of the Earth's crust, and here's a fun fact: 50% of the oxygen we breathe in comes from the ocean.
Specifically, from the microorganisms that photosynthesize.
Here I show you a couple of photos to give you an idea of what fish are like.
But in spite of the importance of the ocean, we still know very little about the way it functions and about its mysterious inhabitants.
To put it into perspective, they say we know more about the Moon's surface than the ocean beds.
That's why I've dedicated myself a lot to the study of sea microorganisms.
But recently, together with a number of colleagues from the NGO, Compassion in World Farming, which is engaged in animal protection and in Spanish is named "Compasión en el Mundo de la Ganadería Industrial", We dedicate ourselves to the study and protection of other unknown but important aquatic organisms, which are fish.
I decided to make this change in my professional career and at the same time in my life because I've always been a passionate admirer of animals and the animal world.
I was worried but above all, I was shocked by the way we treat, or better said, the way we mistreat animals today.
Fish are the animals we value the least.
Moreover, they haven't generated much public interest, nor public curiosity.
Today I would like to show you that these animals are just as intriguing, interesting and intelligent, as the rest of the species, and I hope that my talk will awake your admiration, curiosity, but above all, your respect towards them.
Because I believe that they deserve the same level of consideration and protection on our part.
Fish are creatures that can feel, perceive the world around them and adapt to their environment, and have a wide sensory world.
I don't know if you ever thought about it, but fish can see underwater, and they see as clearly as we do on the surface, and they see in color.
They even see more colors than we do.
Fish can also hear underwater, even though they don't have ears: but they don't have them because they don't need them, because sound is transmitted much better in water than in air.
Fish also have a sense of taste; we know this because, like all of us, they have taste buds on the tongue, but they also have them on their lips and mouth.
Fish also have a sense of smell, an acute one, which helps migratory fish navigate.
In addition to these senses, fish have other senses we don't possess.
Sharks, mantas, and rajiformes, for example, have a sense of electroreception, which means that they can generate and sense electricity.
Other fish have sensors that let them detect changes in water pressure and changes in vibration in the water.
This helps them navigate, avoid collisions, and can also detect, thanks to these sensors, fish that is swimming around them.
Not only do they detect fish, but also its size and swimming speed.
Strangely enough, some fish also have cells that serve as compasses: these cells detect the Earth's magnetic field which help migratory fish navigate.
In addition to this wide sensory world that fish have, their behavior is very intelligent and complex.
We know that many fish are social creatures that live in groups which are not random.
They have a hierarchy and they recognize each other.
As I've said, the groups are not random, fish swim together in a coordinated manner.
Fish also play, fish have mating rituals between males and females, and they also have a good memory.
The expression we use to refer to somebody with a bad memory is: You've got memory of a fish".
Well, it's a myth that's completely false, because fish have a very good memory.
In fact, fish are able to learn, like the archerfish we see here which was trained to tell the difference between people's faces, and it was trained the same way we would train our dogs: it was given food rewards every time it succeeded in recognizing the right face.
Fish take care of their offsprings.
Here we see a species, which is a cichlid, which carries its offsprings inside its mouth to protect them from a possible predator.
This mother is able to go without food for two months in order to protect its babies.
Fish also use tools.
Here we can see another type of cichlid which is picking up a clamshell, To open it; the fish uses the edge of a stone, and it hits the shell to open it and eat what is inside.
Until recently, tool usage was believed to be uniquely human,
because it is a very complex behavior.
But recently we've seen that many animal groups can do it, and fish are among them.
This fish knows perfectly well what it is doing, this is not random behavior.
It knows the reason why it's doing it and for what purpose. In fact, it will repeat the behavior until the shell is opened.
Fish also work together, like these sea bass and moray ell that are working together to hunt.
The sea bass has detected prey inside the rock and in its holes, and it notifies the moray ell which due to its elongated shape could get inside the holes and draw out the prey.
This way these two fishes will try to catch the prey.
This collaborative behavior between species, the ability to communicate between species is very rare in the animal world.
But it does happen in the fish world.
The problem is that we don't see or imagine fish the way I'm describing them to you.
If I say the word "tuna", for example, I am sure that most of you will imagine a tuna can.
But very little people imagine tuna as an animal; and tuna is an animal that can grow up to three meters and weigh 700 kilos. And it is one of the fastest aquatic animals out there which can reach up to 80 km/h and even dive to a depth of 1,000 meters.
Tuna is one of the greatest predators of the ocean.
However, when we think about tuna, we imagine a tuna can.
The same thing happens if I, for example, tell you the word "salmon".
Most of you imagine salmon fillet or salmon sushi, but not many of us think of salmon as an animal.
And salmon is a very interesting and curious animal.
Salmon is a migratory animal, that can swim thousands of kilometers during its life to return to the river where it was born and to lay eggs.
If it can do so, it's only because of its fine sense of smell that helps salmons find the way home.
I've left the big question for last.
For me, the most important question that I want you to think about today is: "Do fish feel pain?"
The answer is "Yes". They are able to feel pain.
Actually, fish have the same pain sensors we have, and it was proven scientifically that they react to pain the same way we do, and they even show relief if we give them morphine.
Therefore, when we fish using fishing hooks, this fish is in pain.
It's possible that we don't perceive this pain because the fish doesn't express it the same way other animals do, or because the fish can't scream.
But just because we can't recognize this pain, doesn't mean it doesn't exist.
Moreover, apart from feeling pain, fish also get stressed when, for example, we take it out of the water, or when we handle it.
Many practices that are usual for fish factories include procedures that stress out fish very badly.
On fish farms the density of fish is usually very high which stresses them a lot because they can't reproduce their natural behavior.
Moreover, on fish farms fish eggs, for instance, are extracted.
And at the hour of sacrifice, fishes are captured with these nets, they are pulled out of the water, and the most common method of sacrificing fish is putting them into water containers with ice without any anesthesia.
The majority of these fish die of suffocation, and this kind of death is slow and painful.
Fish out of the water feel the same way we do underwater, they suffocate to death.
But for fish it might take more time to suffocate, much more time than we need; it can be minutes, but also hours.
Here we see how fish, if they get to be packed are still alive.
I can show you this video thanks to one of the investigations we carried out within our NGO.
This fish was alive inside a package for an hour and a half.
I find this death very cruel, and I think we should not let it happen.
I also believe that fish deserve a dignified death, and by "dignified death" I mean death without pain, stress, and suffering.
So, this is what is going on at some fish farms, but when it comes to industrial fishing parks, the situation there is not much better.
These fishing boats are equipped with these giant nets which in just one day with a single net and a single boat, is able to catch millions of animals.
After the stress caused by the fact of being captured and dragged onto the ship, the majority of these animals are still alive.
These animals will die of suffocation, some of them will be smashed, others will experience their organs bursting due to the pressure difference between water and surface.
When they are dragged on board, many of them are still alive.
Fishermen continue to work and handle the nets, while the animals are still alive.
Here we can see three people walking on top of the net, while the animals are still alive.
If this net was full of puppies or kittens, we would find it shocking.
But since it's just fish, we don't care.
We find it perfectly normal.
The number of fish getting killed anually is exorbitant.
To give you an idea, anually we kill 4,000 million mammals for human consumption.
When it comes to birds, we kill 70,000 million including chickens, ducks, and turkeys.
But if we're talking about fish, I have to change the scale because the number is far greater.
We are talking about 30,000 billion animals that we kill annually.
I don't know about you, but for me it's almost impossible to imagine what this number represents.
I am going to make a comparison.
This number amounts to more than 140 times the world's population. So, the number of fishes we kill annually is 140 times larger than the world's population.
This is a massacre.
I hope that with today's talk, I've convinced you that fish are living creatures who can feel, and that you now see them as the intelligent, sophisticated and interesting animals they actually are.
With that, I would like to convince you that fish deserve the same respect and the same consideration as other animals.
And when I talk about their protection, I not only mean protection at the population level.
I mean not only caring that tomorrow we still have fish stocks so that we can continue to catch and consume fish, but rather that I would like us to care about fish at the individual level, because I think that every life counts, and that we should care about its well-being and suffering.
I believe that they deserve a dignified life and death.
For this reason, today I invite you to rediscover fish.
(Applauses)
I'm from Guatemala, and this is a public service announcement: here is where Guatemala is.
(Laughter) And I also have to tell this to a lot of people in the United States:
that's not where they held terrorism suspects, that's Guantánamo.
(Laughter) When I was a child, growing up in Guatemala I had an idea that I was pretty sure was really great and that would make me a multimillionaire.
What I wanted was a gym where all the exercise machines, - all the stationary bikes and ellipticals - all would be connected to the power company.
And we were going to convert all the kinetic energy produced by all the people exercising, in electricity, and we were going to sell it to the power company.
I wanted to do this and, moreover, because we were making money selling electricity to the power company we didn't have to charge the members of our gym.
So this gym would be totally free.
Thanks to this, I was going to have a monopoly on gyms around the world because this would be a huge chain of gyms totally free, where people would be generating electricity.
And I was going to have a giant corporation where millions of people would be generating electricity for the whole planet in a sustainable and, of course, healthy way.
Well, it looks like many have had this same idea before and it doesn't even work.
(Laughter) And there are two reasons why this doesn't work: first, as humans, we are very bad at generating electricity - we don't produce much.
So I wouldn't have made much money with this.
Second, and most importantly, gyms make most of their money with people who pay the membership but never go.
(Laughter) So that wouldn't have worked.
But this talk is about how this idea that thousands of people have had and it doesn't even work can be used to completely revolutionize education around the world.
About four years ago, I started to work on my last project,
a site for language learning, which today is the most popular way in the world to learn languages.
Its name is Duolingo.
When I started, I wanted to work on something that I'd be passionate about, and that for me, is education.
Now, education-related opinions have a lot to do with the fact that I'm from Guatemala.
Guatemala is a very poor country.
They say education provides social classes with equal educational opportunities, but I always thought it was the opposite, specially when you witness the reality of Guatemala today.
I think it creates inequality among the different social classes because people with money can afford the best education, the best teachers, the best books, and then they go to Harvard.
And after they get such a great education they continue to have money.
Those who don't have much money can barely learn to read and write, and because they don't know how to read and write, they continue without much money.
So I wanted to do something related to education, but providing an equal access to everyone, no matter their socioeconomic status.
Now, for me the topic of education is too broad, so I decided to focus on just one type of education,
and it's something very common in most of the countries: namely, learning languages.
It is a very common thing to learn in almost all countries, except the United States, they don't learn other languages,
But there are around 1,2 billion people learning other languages in the world.
This is a very interesting market.
Most of these people, about 800 million of them, have three characteristics: the first one, they are learning English; the second one, they are learning English because they want to get a better job,
and the third one, they come from a low socioeconomic status.
So the majority of the people learning languages are poor people learning English to get out of poverty.
The irony is that, up to four years ago, most of the methods for language learning, and specially those that use a software, cost a lot of money.
For example, in the US, there is
Rosetta Stone, a language learning program that costs between 500 - 1,000 dollars.
In Latin America there's Open English and it costs about 1,000 dollars to use it to learn languages.
But what's really ironic is that the majority of these people are trying to get out of poverty, but apparently, to leave it behind they need 1,000 dollars.
So I decided that what I wanted to do was to free language lessons.
But, if I wanted a free online language learning method that could reached 1,2 billion people, I had to find a way to finance it.
So I started thinking, how we can finance something that would teach languages to 1 billion people without charging them?
There is where I remembered the idea of the gym.
Remember that with the gym we could offer a free service because we were getting some value from the exercises.
Would it be possible to do the same with language teaching?
Well, yes, we can do it.
When people are learning languages, we can create value through translation.
Translation is a huge business and every year about $30 billion is spent translating things.
For example, translating the news, Internet content, or books, about $30 billion is spent every year.
If you have ever studied a language, some of the exercises that you have to do when you're studying, or practicing, are frequently the translation of simple things, like a song or a poem.
But how about translating something that no one has ever translated before, and that someone is willing to pay for this translation?
That's the idea behind Duolingo,
and that's how we finance it. So, for example, CNN is a Duolingo's client, one of our clients, and the idea is that they write all their news in English, they send us these English drafts to us, we choose some of our Duolingo's students that are learning English, and once they finish a lesson and we have taught them something, we tell them, "If you want to practice what you just learned with a document from the real world, here's is this article from CNN, related with what you've just learned. To practice, help us translate it to your native language."
So they translate it and correct each other, and at the end, there's a full translation that we send back to CNN and they pay us for this translation.
This is the way Duolingo works.
About two and a half year ago, we launched the platform, which is a website, an iPhone and an Android application, and today we have 42 million people learning languages with Duolingo.
It's the most popular platform for language learning. For example,
in the US there are more people learning languages with Duolingo than with the entire public education system.
(Applause) Now, this idea of Duolingo or this idea of the gym, on how to finance education might sound innovative, but, actually, is similar to how education worked for thousands of years up to not that long ago.
It was the apprenticeship model.
About 300 years ago, if someone aspired to learn a trade, for example, to be a baker, a butcher or whatever, what they did was to find someone who knew the craft,
for example, they'd find a baker or a butcher, and they became their apprentices.
The way it worked is the master, let's say the butcher, would teach the student how to become a butcher, and, in exchange, the student would help the master in his work.
This is how education worked and it's very similar to how Duolingo works.
In the case of Duolingo, the students don't pay with money, but with something that creates value, which is translation.
The apprenticeship model almost disappeared with the arrival of the Industrial Revolution because what that system promoted was to teach the same thing to hundreds of different people,
and rendered the apprenticeship model redundant because it wasn't scalable.
But the apprenticeship model has a lot of positive things.
For example, one of them is that no money exchange is needed.
The student doesn't pay with money, therefore, anyone can have access.
No matter if you had a lot of money or not anyone could become an apprentice.
That's really good.
The other thing is the teacher has a lot of incentives to teach his students well because they are doing part of the teacher's work, so he'd better teach them right.
But for me, the biggest positive impact of the apprenticeship model is that it is much more efficient from the perspective of social efficiency.
Think about this: today, everyone who is learning something - actually, most of the people in the world, which are millions - spends about 20 years going to school and learning things.
Most of that time, they aren't helping the society in the least.
They are just studying, but with the apprenticeship model, the apprentice was doing something positive for the society, he was helping it.
But today, all that you studied all those 20 years, all those math exercises you did, all the computer programs you wrote while studying, all the scientific research, the papers that you wrote, all of that are thrown away, literally.
We are throwing away all this mental effort.
It's a waste.
In English there's an expression for that: mental masturbation. That's what they call it.
It's a total waste.
So what I want is that all those who are studying to be able to also create something positive for the society.
And my dream is to have millions of children wordwide, in developing countries, working for me for free.
Oh, no, that didn't sound right!
(Laughter)
I'm sorry! But you got what I meant.
Now, I want to leave you with the following question: This gym idea that many have had already and it doesn't work, can be adapted to create a lot of value education-wise.
So the question is: what other contexts can we adapt it to?
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
"I have to trust in me," I'd say to myself at 18 while, in what had been my grandfather's butcher shop, I modeled faces of characters in clay that would later be made of latex.
Suddenly, I'd see myself standing in front of a block of clay which I had already ripped three or four pieces off.
"What am I doing?", I'd ask myself.
"What led me to pull those pieces out?
Those ones, and not others.
Do I really think that from here a face will come out?
I don't know! I have to trust in me."
So I'd put on a tape -- Yes, a tape... (Laughter) from some movie soundtrack to forget about the subject.
And yes, after a while, when I realized, I was already in front of a face, and it was evident that I had done it, so I stopped asking.
I think the essence of my work has to do with that, with trusting in that part of me that lets itself be and solves without conditioning.
It's been a while since I left behind those latex characters, but that part went on in me and adapted to the different challenges coming up.
Today I design museums, shows, and immersive experiences.
Maybe, it's because I never forgot that feeling you have when as a child you live fantasy as a reality.
I don't know if you can pinpoint that, those games that plunge you in another reality, and that other reality, that other world, you live it with all your senses.
Well, it seems that this stage hit me hard.
That's why I think the "full circle" projects, as my grandmother used to say when she combed my bangs, "full circle", surrounding you, so they plunge you in another reality and stimulate all your senses.
But how does this process of conceiving a project come about?
How does it work?
Let's see if I can make you feel this in your own skin.
The first thing I do in order to think a museum, or a show, is to meet with the people who called me to do it, to understand the theme it's going to be about, which, in general, I usually have no idea of beforehand.
And this is very good, because it allows me to see the theme with new eyes, without prior conditioning.
And beyond understanding it rationally, what I'm interested in is connecting with the people who lives it day by day, the people of the place.
And there I ask them about their experience, their happenings, their feelings regarding this topic.
And based on their replies I start to perceive, to shape the essence of the theme.
Or, at least, the essence that emerges from this meeting we have.
And this essence is the trigger, it's the raw material to start thinking the ideas.
Sometimes, in this very meeting I get a very clear image, a whole image of the idea, and other times it's as if a bombing of ideas begins to take place... concepts that were dormant, asleep, that I didn't even know I had, they start to light up like little candles and communicate with each other.
And something comes up, that I saw last week from the car, and relates to a little song of my childhood, on TV, and to a documentary I watched three years ago, I didn't even know I remembered, and before reason can appear, I am already telling them an idea that, inexplicably, it's valid for what we are discussing.
Let's see it with a case in point.
I was recently summoned to think the contents of the Carnival of Humahuaca Museum, in a building they were planning to build there.
We went to see the field with the people from there and the people of the comparsas, and there, I started asking them about their dreams, about their experiences, their stories, their emotions regarding the carnival.
And they went off telling me that carnival for them is a time for joy and liberation, when they roam the city led by the dance, and the music, going from the streets to the houses, where they are invited with something, and they go back to the streets, where they keep dancing, and they go to another house, where they are invited, and they go back to the streets, where they keep singing, so, among streets and houses, they lose the notion of space and time, led by this joy.
See? There, we had an experience,
a feeling to communicate, an essence.
But how do I do convey this in a museum?
How do I make someone who traveled to Humahuaca out of carnival time, upon visiting this museum, have at least a bite of this essence?
And thinking about this, looking at the empty space, I thought maybe the building itself could say this, by building it as a succession of streets and houses, where people could get lost in a maze, going from streets to houses, where there are contents, to streets, where there is music, to another house with another content, as if they were in a mini Humahuaca in permanent carnival.
And thinking of this mini Humahuaca it came to my mind the tower of the Cabildo of Humahuaca, that has a clock from which a saint comes out.
Maybe in our museum we could also have a tower with a clock where a devil, the Pujllay, would come out the devil of carnival.
And as tradition goes, the Pujllay can only be seen in carnival times, then our watch would mark the days of the year until the time of the carnival, when the devil comes out.
And this allowed us to think that maybe, someday, the people of Humahuaca could meet under the clock tower to wait for the devil to come out and, among sirens and celebrations, announce the beginning of the carnival, creating a new tradition for the city.
We have to see if this happens.
It didn't happen, I don't know.
But the crazy thing about this is that this idea of ​​the tower which is so emblematic and so important for our project and that could potentially become a new city icon came up in a second phase, almost by chance.
Although, the truth is that nothing is so casual.
In most projects this happens: almost at the end of the process a concept pops up that ends up connecting everything, and it seems incredible that it emerged so late, because it's so essential, so constitutive of the project that you would say that it was the base everything was thought upon.
But no. Maybe inside our minds
ideas are distilling and keep developing although we are not dealing with them.
And at some point they arrange themselves and they pop up.
And when you revisit the project, you think it without that connector, you realize that it would have not been so good, that, in fact, it would've been crap.
(Laughter) And that maybe my mind was cheating on me throughout the process making me believe I had the great thing to allow time for this full circle sense-making idea to show up; and as always, it appears.
Now I know that, although what I've done may not be super, at some point everything is going to make sense and it will be fine.
(Applause) Once again the phrase from my 18 years: "I have to trust in me."
See what happened many years ago, when I had to think a show for the Planetarium about Galileo Galilei for a new technology they were about to install in the Planetarium at that time.
The challenge I had set myself was to tell the story of Galileo but without losing the key experience of the Planetarium, which is stargazing.
Because, maybe, the first thing that you come up with is to create rectangular, projected screens there in the dome, where to tell our story, with our characters and scenery, but the last thing that I would do to a planetarium is to turn it into a cinema with torticollis.
(Laughter) Our goal was to make the experience of being in a planetarium an advantage for the project, and not an obstacle to run away from.
What we had to achieve was that Galileo's stories would be in the sky, but justifiably in the sky, that they belonged to that sky we were gazing at.
And going through the history of Galileo I found the Ptolemaic armillary sphere, which is this sphere made of rings that rotate around a center.
If we fit in these rings our city, then, the people in the planetarium would be in the center and the whole city, with the rings, would spin around them in the sky.
We now had our context, then we only had to adapt the history of Galileo to that world of rings.
But, fast forward in this story, that involved a young and adventurous Galileo, and a bunch of zoomorphic characters accompanying him in a kind of fable in the sky. This that had emerged as a technical constrain of the planetarium, began to take a much deeper dimension, much more important.
Because the Ptolemaic armillary sphere is really a geocentric model of the cosmos, with the Earth in the center and everything spinning around it, the model which was accepted in the times of Galileo and that has a lot to do with the power of the Church.
So in our city, we would put the power in the center, the Pope. and Galileo, feeling misunderstood and oppressed by that power, would come out of that structure to find out of it thinkers who would help him to see this world from another perspective and dare to question it.
But in this story this is not an abstract concept. This is real, Galileo really leaves this structure.
He leaves.
And he really sees this world from another perspective, and seeing it like that at a glance he realizes that this scaffolding, this structure has nothing to do with how the cosmos works.
Once again the central concept of the project popped up in a second instance.
The sad part of this story is that finally at that time it was decided not to install the new technology in the Planetarium and the Galileo project couldn't be realized.
And yes, many, many times the ideas that we seed in the world won't be able to develop.
But many times also the process of conceiving them will leave us very important things, very good things.
As for example in this case, this ability that Galileo had to see the world with new eyes, as if he was discovering it for the first time, allows us to connect with the essential, the truth, the transcendent.
Maybe that's why I thought about a teenage Galileo, remembering that child that at his grandfather's butcher shop trusting his own essence and let it model the clay.
Today I'm trained in looking for the essence to scape that structure that forces upon you one point of view, in order to find new perspectives.
And that simple change of perception can make our entire universe expand.
(Applause) And what is even better, maybe, just like Galileo did, maybe we can contribute to the expansion of other people's universe as well.
We might only need to get our hands dirty and have trust.
(Applause)
Have you ever scored 9 million points in Candy Crush in under 10 seconds?
My story begins when I broke my dad's computer by trying to hack a game.
I really liked this one game about penguins, but it annoyed me that to upgrade your character or to score more points you had to pay.
So, one day, I saved the money to buy a subscription in the game and I asked my parents for their credit card. Sure enough, they refused.
It was then that I started hacking.
I went online and learnt how to do it.
But when I tried it out, the computer crashed, and not knowing what to do, I quickly turned it off with the power button.
Guess what happened?
I broke the hard drive that contained all of my dad's work.
Luckily, we were able to get most of it back.
After that was over with, my aunt and uncle gave me a new computer, which I later used to continue hacking.
This computer met a similar end, but the results were better.
My reputation as a hacker started building up in the family and my cousin gave me access to his and his friends' accounts so that I could get them more points in Candy Crush. (Laughter)
From that day on, they dubbed me Iron Man.
Until one day, I asked myself: what if instead of hacking a game, I created one?
So I created a game called "Robot Galaxy" that was about an evil scientist called Wolf who wanted to take all the resources on earth, no matter the consequences.
To do this, he sent humans in capsules into space.
The only survivor was Vieweye, the hero of the story.
After this game, I made others related to bullying, which was called "Helpme!," and was entered in a contest, a racing game and another game about robots.
Next, we'll show you a video about "Robot Galaxy" and "Helpme!"
We can see Vieweye fighting against Wolf's guards trying to enter his empire.
This is "Helpme!",
which is a game about bullying, in which, through different discrimination scenarios, people learn how to react to them and to different types of bullying.
Here, we can see how the audience is watching an ordinary boy playing, and when he falls, the one wearing a cap laughs at him.
We can also see ethnic discrimination by the girls, directed at the boy.
And, finally, there is the typical case where kids forms a circle
and start shouting
"Fight! Fight! Fight!"
At the end we learn that everybody must fight against bullying.
Thanks to this, I learnt that I love programming and I started trying out working on various types of content, such as webpages.
I once decided to create an educational platform for school management on holidays, called Yepika.
The problem I had discovered was that my peers couldn't buy their language class books.
With my webpage, they would be able to download them for free, keep track of their grades, check their attendance records and chat with friends.
When classes started, I launched my venture on the market.
A year full of learning went by when something happened to me, which I thought would never happen: Just like I had started hacking games, I was hacked and my webpage went down.
And not only that, but I also appeared in an online magazine as an example of a vulnerable platform.
(Laughter) (Applause) I wanted to beg them to please pull down the article.
(Laughter) The good thing is that, as the saying goes, what doesn't kill you makes you stronger.
And from this experience, I learnt a lot about web security and I enhanced my webpage.
This was not the only surprise that came with this undertaking.
There was another, related to the name.
Apparently, the one I had picked was also the name of a winery.
So, I had to change my logo and redesign the site, and a bunch of other things.
Without my realising it, during that year's summer vacation, my entrepreneurial voyage had begun.
I got on the bus with a half-empty suitcase of knowledge, but very eager to get started on this new journey.
At the second stop, I ran into Mrs Empathy and Creativity,
and after exchanging opinions, we thought of an idea to help society.
On the third leg of my trip, I found myself and I stopped dreaming of ideas, to start materializing them.
On the fourth leg, I came across people willing to help me.
And on the fifth, the bus started to break down.
So, together with Mrs. Persistence, we had to fix it.
That was the key moment to open the suitcase and use the tools I had carried inside.
At the sixth and final stop, I came across growth.
And with it came adolescence and venture.
They get along, and walk together side by side.
As you must have probably noticed, I've made a lot of mistakes.
But that never kept me from pressing on.
My parents support me in everything I like doing, and they have taught me not to fear failure, rather the contrary, that making mistakes is good, as long as we don't repeat them over and over again.
I love entrepreneuring and programming, but that's not all I do.
I also go to high school, take English lessons, hang out with my friends, and play football.
Realizing your personal passion doesn't guarantee you'll have a social life, it just means it can be just one of the things you do.
And not just that, it also brings you a lot of advantages.
Like it lets you help other people with your projects, and it allows you to know that sometimes, you win, and sometime, s you lose, but you must always carry on.
Our time on this planet is very short.
Let's not waste it.
And let's break with what keeps us from pursuing our passions.
Just as I am an entrepreneur, you may play basketball or be good at your job.
But you always have to do things because you love doing them and because you feel that time flies when you do them.
Lastly, I would like to leave you with a question.
What would you break to dare pursue your passions?
Thank you.
(Applause)
A few years ago, in a bar in the South of California, a Chinese man and a Jewish man were having a couple of drinks.
After being a little drunk for a while, the Jewish slaps the Chinese in the face and throws him under the table.
He gets up, surprised, and says: "What was that for?"
"That was for Pearl Harbor."
"But Pearl Harbor were the Japanese."
"Oh, well... Chinese, Japanese, you are all the same."
The Chinese man gets up, he is not very calm. He has one more drink and a while later gets angry, turns red, and bam! He hits Jacobo and throws him under
the table; Jacobo gets up and says:
"What was that for?"
"That was for the Titanic."
"What do you mean? The Titanic sunk
because of an iceberg."
"Iceberg, Goldberg, you are all the same."
(Laughter) Neither the icebergs nor the Goldbergs are all the same.
Neither the Chinese nor the Japanese are the same.
The problem has to do with how we handle this vision, and it's always through perception.
And that's why we usually say that the Portenos are arrogant, that people from Santiago are slow, that Argentine women are very beautiful, which is true, that Galicians are a little dumb, that the French are romantic, or that the German are tidy.
The reality is this is not so.
Einstein himself used to say that if the theory of relativity were a success, the Swiss would say that he was Swiss, the German that he was German and the French that he was a great scientist.
But if it was wrong, if it didn't work, the French would say that he was Swiss, the Swiss that he was German and the German that he was a Jew.
(Laughter) Just like with anything else, like art, how we educate the eye to be able to perceive the different shades in a color, such as opening the window and seeing the greens of the landscape: and saying, "light green, dark green" and if you talk to an artist, they can define 500 different shades of green, we can also train the way in which we understand identity.
How we see it and how its details are.
We can't generalize only one element.
And, to me, identity is a cultural construction.
Identities, our identity, is defined by the elements that generate our social, our cultural environment, geographical and, basically, historical environment.
So, those elements: school, books, education, sports, which are different for each of us, build that cultural value, that self-defining value that we have and must always recover and never forget.
Then, there are many other elements that collaborate as well, which relate more to objects and complete the story.
The problem is that, in the past 50 years, what has been happening is that we stopped focusing on what we are, on being, and we started focusing on having, on the objects we could have, and our identity started to be defined by the objects a lot more than by what we are.
Of course, the whole theory of globalization contributed to this, the need of the big companies to sell the same product here, in China, in Buenos Aires, and in Thailand. So, what globalization caused is an isomorphization of the culture.
This weird word means all cultures are, deep down, a little alike, and that we can sell the same thing anywhere.
And people start relating to these values of possessing expensive stuff -- a smartphone, a phone, or whatever, a pair of sneakers -- instead of relating to values that built our own identity.
And the question always is whether this is a lost battle or not.
Some groups started to split, to generate urban groups, to try to say "OK, we have skaters, or surfers, or hipsters here", but they were still a lot more related to objects than to cultural elements.
Maybe a little through music, but it's the same group that acted in different places of the world.
But there's a chance to change this, and it's not to think of it as a battle, but to think of it as a need.
Today, it is said that branding, or identity, is a human condition, because we have the need to belong, to belong to a cultural group, to belong to completely visceral matters.
And here comes the definition.
So, where is our identity?
Identity is always within us.
In my particular case, my first strong experience understanding where identity is, was on June 14, 1978.
It has date and time.
Also on that day, well, in Argentina things were very... we were complicated.
But on that day, Argentina-Poland played the second round of the World Cup.
Some of you may have watched the game.
I did. I was so unlucky that I came home to see the game and two people were sitting in front of the TV: My dad and my grandfather.
Both Polish.
(Laughter) I come in, look at them and say: "No, I'm not watching the game with you because you're going to root for Poland. No."
On top of everything, it was a black and white TV.

There was no way.
And my grandfather looks at me and says: "Why?"
"Because you're going to root for Poland."
"No, no, no. Look...

The values that identified me as Polish many years ago are gone today.
I emigrated, I came here, this is my place; my culture and my people are here."
And that day, a long time ago, I understood what defines identity, not only of a person, but of a social group, of a group of people.
Your place, your people and your culture.
Let's see: 23 years later, having already studied graphic design and worked a little in architecture, always working on identities, corporate identities, which is how I make a living.
December 20, 2001 arrives and, as you all know, it was again, a tragic day, really tragic.
We had a very successful firm and that day we received 12 phone calls.
They were from our 12 accounts saying, "Guys, no more design for now;
problems have changed things." So, everybody closed their accounts and, overnight, we had zero clients, no work, nothing to do.
And now is when I tell how, in that moment of crisis and of not knowing what to do, I mean, we didn't want to leave the country, we weren't interested in that, our roots were here, we said, "This is an opportunity to do what we know how to do, which is working on identities, and tell what the Argentine identity is."
So, I come today to share an idea.
And that idea was how to express identity, and identity was inside the idea.
If, as we saw before, an element can't communicate everything we are as people or as a society, why not create a family of elements that conveys that?
That simple idea, in that moment of crisis, gave us the opportunity to build a project that changed our lives, or my life, forever.
So I said: "Let's see, which elements represent me in Argentina, me, as an Argentinean?"
The cow. I definitely love eating meat, I have it on my chest. And what else?
Well, I like having mate, let's go with the mate.
What other elements represent the Argentine identity?
We started with almost every element that appears in the song of Cordera, but we did it first.
Diego Maradona, I used to go see him, I lived in Paternal, so I went to see him play every Saturday with the Cebollitas; tango; Charly Garcia, with the white part of his mustache, the "asado" barbecue, on Sundays and many other elements that we began to build and what we created, instead of an element, is a system, a system that allowed each one of us relate to some element in particular, or to several.
I, for example, relate to all of them; to some of them, not so much, but it allowed us to build this identity project.
And the other thing we wanted to achieve was that, once it was built, people could use it.
That it wouldn't be a museum piece.
That, apart from being a museum piece, you could buy a t-shirt, like me, put it on and say, "Today I want to wear the cow..."
I love to read, so I have a little notebook with the picture of Cortazar and another one with Borges, or whatever represents me.
And I was so lucky that the project received multiple awards.
We started receiving awards from everywhere.
The first important award was in Germany.
It was one of the top ten branding projects in the world.
And after that, in New York, we were given an exhibition, for the first time ever by the Association of Designers, which brings together 30,000 people, it was given to an Argentine firm, an Argentine designer.
And that's where we presented the case and where the Association's Director told me: "What a nice project!"
He asked: "Who did you do it for?"
"No, we did it for ourselves."
"But, who paid for it?"

"We did." (Laughter)
"But, who funded it?"
"No, no, we did, with work, with passion."
"Oh, and who did you do it for?"
Well, that loop went on for about 15 minutes.
There was no way they could understand that we have done this only out of passion, drive, guts and will. However, he was right at one point: It was necessary to make some change so that these projects would be more viable to come true.
Then we were lucky enough... As I tell you, here you'll see many of the icons from the book where you can see from the "asado" to the Patagonia, but there are many more.
We were lucky enough, among all the prizes, to be invited to the Design Festival in Berlin.
At this Design Festival, at the DMY, the people from the Culture department of Berlin sees the project and loves it: "This is unique."
And they added: "We want to do the same."
"OK, go ahead."
"No, no, we want you to do it for us."
"But, I'm Argentinian and do this
for Argentina. You can do it yourselves." And he tells me: "No, we want you to do it."
Then, that entrepreneurial spirit I have made me agree to it, and later to panic.
(Laughter) How can I comprehend what goes on in Berlin, another language, another city... Very complicated.
But I was compelled and started thinking how to go about it, and together with a Chilean friend, who lives in Berlin, who was there, and liked the project immediately, we spent six months developing a methodology.
We said: "We need to create a methodology so that we can change this which I have done intuitively into a working method."
Six months later, we solicited funds, official funds: the Culture Fund, the most prestigious one in Berlin. When we were about to solicit it,
we were told: "You are crazy."
There's no way somebody can give funds to someone who wants to work with the identity of Berlin, through design, creating icons, speaking to the elders; No, forget it; don't even solicit it."
As I still have some Polish blood, I solicited it all the same.
We had already done it. And, among 300 projects, we got the funds. We obtained it because we were working with and appealing to the history, the culture and everything which will be lost once that people vanishes.
So, what is it we did?
Very simple: Go and ask.
Ask people what they thought, what drives them, what made them happy. And we started organizing that
information, and made them work again: Look for all of the details, educate them in finding their identity, and see which elements actually represent them in the city.
That guy in the middle, for instance, in the middle of a workshop, tired, says: "To me, it's this." He tossed a small key onto the table, like laughing at the situation. Everyone starts laughing and I ask: "What's that?"
"This is my bike key."
"Well, we all have a bike."
"Yes, OK, but to me, I come from a country, from a city, where you can't go out to the street.
I arrived here, I got a bike and with it, I live in the street, live in the city, live..." And we were all really moved about his change.
His best friend was next to him and asks:
"Why haven't you ever told me?" And he replied: "Because you never asked."
On the other picture, you see these two women, Holocaust survivors, working with kids of a Latin family.
This interaction created a very moving and interesting initiative.
So this is how elements that really define Berlin's culture start to emerge, like the currywurst, which is not any sausage, but one cut in small slices with a small fork on top and a rectangular paper plate, just how they eat it in the street. The icons below are the ones that came up during the workshops.
The one I like best is the moody little old lady.
And we all know a moody little old lady from Berlin.
And they tell me this little old lady is moody because she had her house bombed twice, her city was divided and so was her family.
And when the family reunited, half of her family was no longer alive.
So, try playing with the ball on the sidewalk.
(Laughter) Then, you can see the most classic icons, like the Brandenburg Gate, which to them defined the line, the limit between one world and another; or the Victory Column, the sculpture on top of the column at the Tiergarten. For us, it's a very nice golden sculpture, but for them it represents victory, the battle they rage every day.
The project turned out great, so we said: "OK, fantastic, what else?
We got the funds.
Let's go for the second round."
But we couldn't fund the second round.
We had already got funds, but nobody would give us any more money.
Everyone loved it.
That is when we realized we were missing something.
We had gathered quite an amount of bronze, but in order to move forward what we needed was a little gold.
And who can change bronze into gold?
We have a clue here: An alchemist.
We were missing an alchemist.
Our alchemist turned out to be a team of MBAs at the University of San Diego, who we contacted and who came right away because they loved the project, and so they put together this incomprehensible -- This model I could have never done by myself, but which actually solved our problem. It provided the financing background for a purely cultural project.
With both legs, we ventured to other projects.
What does this mean? That this
idea we've been describing... Ideas reach a moment when they mature.
They need to mature and create a change, and we need to be ready to make this change happen.
So, we carried out the project. Where did we go?
To Brooklyn. Another cosmopolitan city
with a strong identity.
From then on, we created an entire project; we met with the president of the Borough, who loved it.
When I first went to see him,
he said: "What do you need from us?"
I replied: "Anything but money," and he said: "In eight years, no one has ever asked anything but money, so you have all our support."
We moved forward with the project and according to this new business plan we had written, we used crowd-funding.
Today, we have 20 icons, like the Mermaid Parade, the Gowanus, a frog that has three eyes because of pollution, and many others that came up as we worked with the people from that place.
Today we obtained the official sponsorship, which means businesses can make free donations to the project. And finally, we are in the last stage of the project: The final development.
So, what we always want to build is a bottom-up project.
We work with the people, for the people, so that they can discover the elements they relate to, the elements that make them be what they are, work on themselves, on the place where you lived, where you were raised.
So, there you can see, for example, images from the Tech School, the most important technical school, and the other workshops.
We always try to engage with the people, ask them about their stories, which is where all richness lies.
We build identity projects; I like building identity projects, based - as I said - on the place, the culture and its people.
It goes without saying we need a great team, a very good idea, to make it grow, work with professionals, get someone who can help us with the business plan, and so on, And with all of that work and that team, what we come up with, was a project for Buenos Aires,
a project for Berlin and a project for Brooklyn.
And if you pay attention, the three of them share a constant: They are cosmopolitan cities and they begin with a "B".
May be you can figure out what the next one might be.
(Applause) I'm going to round this off with two more little things: The first is that whenever you have an idea, it has to be very simple, as we said.
Three or four words should define the idea.
If you make it, put together the best team.
And once you have created the team, don't forget the alchemist.
And, finally, identity is always, always within us.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Before I started,
I wanted to score a three-pointer to release all that adrenaline, because you've just made my hair stand on end.
I'm 38 years old, and I spent 22 years chasing a childhood dream.
Fulfilling it, winning, losing, suffering, and having fun.
I've been lucky enough to live in three different continents,
in countries such as Russia, the USA, Brazil, and Turkey.
I started my dream, my career, at the age of eight.
I wanted to be the 1,500 m
Olympic champion.
Athletics: surpassing yourself,
reaching and crossing
that finishing line, being the best.
By chance, basketball came into my life.
And I decided to leave individual sports behind and move over to team sports.
For me, someone who's very - I came from a sport that was about surpassing yourself, and I didn't realize that this desire that we all have for winning and improving well, now implied having 11 teammates.
I'm one of those people who learns by making mistakes.
As I grew up, I began to realize that I couldn't win matches alone.
Perhaps one match, but not a championship.
And with my teammates there, pushing myself, we had a shared goal and ended up champions.
Over all these years, I have suffered from being compared to a very famous basketballer called Pau Gasol.
"The female Pau Gasol".
(Laughter) (Applause) I was told in thousands of interviews, "If you earned as much as Pau," "If you were as famous as Pau," Pau this, and Pau that. (Laughter) So I thought, if I just sit here, lamenting how rich I could've been, how famous I could've been, I don't think I ever would have got here.
To the goal, right?
I'd be sad, I'd be negative, and I was simply compared to a great sportsman, to Pau Gasol.
Now, when we're all bragging about sportswomen, "Look how good we are, how much we earn,"
I want to ask you something: in the last Olympics, we women won 11 medals compared to the 7 won by the men.
We're in a crisis, right?
So I'll tell you, rather than asking you: we're always in crisis,
we don't have the same resources.
It's tough, but that's how it is.
When I'm asked what I want, I don't want the same as Pau.
I want to have the same resources as Pau.
I want to train in the same sports halls, I want to travel in the same planes as they do I want to have the best training times, so that I can be a better sportsperson,
to succeed and achieve my goals.
We are women.
(Applause) We've got lots of weapons.
All these years, by making mistakes, winning, losing, I've learned something; one word: vulnerability.
An incredibly important word.
We have to learn to be vulnerable, to ask for help.
It's not a big deal.
We take off that Superwoman cape and ask for help.
In my life, there are two factors which have had a big influence on my sporting career and my personal career.
When I was 18, I lost my mother to a 19-day cancer.
I wore that Superwoman cape, of a sporting heroine for a long time
because I didn't want to show my pain.
I hadn't realized it was stopping me from moving forward.
I didn't realize it for many years.
Until I turned to my family.
Family, which is so important to everyone.
To sportspeople, journalists. I asked for help to get rid of all the pain that I had inside.
At 35 years of age, which is quite advanced for an elite sportsperson, we all see retirement as a cliff-face.
We're terribly scared of no longer being
Amaya Valdemoro, Pau Gasol, Rafa Nadal. We're scared of the future, because we've dedicated our body and soul to a hobby turned into our job.
When I was 35, I broke both of my wrists in a play during a match.
This injury made me a better person.
It made me see that human beings are selfish.
That we complain and protest.
We don't see the positive side, or consider those who have less than us.
I had to ask for help again.
I had to ask to be washed, to be fed, and I had to overcome an anxiety which even today, when I remember it, is still here within me.
Anxiety about never again being able to compete,
about not retiring on a basketball court.
There's something within us which gives us strength.
I had my most terrible, truly awful 24 hours after this injury.
The following day, after they had told me, "You won't be able to compete. If you recover, it'll take eight months."
Well, being here today, proud, and in front of all of you, I can tell you I recovered, and after four months, I became myself again, that a year and a half later, I look at the case with all the trophies I have won in my sporting career and I see a plaster cast, which is my Olympic gold medal, the one I couldn't win on the court.
Why?
Because it made me whole again here, inside,
it made me a better person.
And nobody can take that away from me.
Today, I've been retired for two years and I have two projects, one started in 2006, and both fill me with happiness and make me feel proud.
Almost as much as when I scored that winning three-pointer.
One is a campus that I have, for girls, - interestingly, 100 girls and 20 boys are signed up, more or less - where I try to teach the values of sport.
And where, above all, they can have fun, cooperate, and get along.
The campus is so successful I also wanted to give the opportunity, the type I didn't have, to 18-22 year old girls, to improve those parts of their game which are a bit worse, to be able to work on them so that the next season they feel proud about what they've been able to learn.
Back to comparisons.
My campus has zero sponsors.
Whereas Pau's campus - (Laughter) But I do it, I love it, and this will be its ninth year.
(Applause) For my next project, also associated with sport, of course: I want to be able to help all the young children who want to play professional basketball, I want to help them enjoy sport by setting up a campus in the USA, the "Mecca" of basketball.
But the main objective is sport and education.
It's as important to exercise, to feel good about yourself, as it is to exercise your mind; if not, we'd never achieve our goals.
Through the contacts that I've made over the years, I hope that the NBA, with which we've started talks, will help me establish an incredibly successful campus.
So that's me, Amaya.
I hope that, following my example, some of you will be able to see the positive side of life because we all have one.
I hope that life smiles upon you, just as it has smiled upon me.
(Applause)
Orlando Inclán: We live in
a spectacular city, nobody doubts that.
Although we may not realize it, we live in one of the most wonderful cities in the world.
And the question would be: can Havana become a sustainable city, and a creative city model?
Claudia Castillo: When we all started riding bicycles in the 90s, Havana changed accordingly, bike lanes and mechanics appeared everywhere; and we all learned how to ride a bike. We were not aware of the fact that we were all taking part in one of the common phenomena of contemporary life, coined as the vanguard of mobility.
Of course, Havana's residents got to such a point out of necessity.
There were no other means
of transportation available. When the economic situation in the city and the local society improved, bicycles were buried, including a whole culture, a know-how, alongside the Cuban's ability to devise, to creatively invent, or to offer alternative solutions to mobility.
Today, we no longer have bicycles.
OI: However, this was not everything we managed to spread and salvage.
There were inventions like TV antennas made of practically anything, and comfortable furniture made out of recycled tires. This showed that everything could be re-used in Havana.
And this is beautiful,
it's lovely because the useful life of things is extended.
Today, as the world attempts to lower consumption indexes, in Havana this may seem like a joke.
Of course, we did lower our consumption indexes.
It'll be enough to think of a Cuban designer, Ernesto Orozai's study to confirm the potential inventiveness in the city today.
But not only that, our homes are fully furnished with furniture that belonged to our grandparents.
Those pieces of furniture date back to the early 20th century; they are still everywhere;
you may find them around in different spaces and contexts,
they are part of our legacy. We start learning how to recycle at an early age.
We grow up knowing how to re-use furniture.
And now, everyone is talking about vintage,
something that nowadays has become a global trend.
In fact, we could say that vintage was invented here in Havana.
It entails redesigning old pieces of furniture into something more contemporary and adapting them to different uses, creating spectacular spaces from a new perspective in design, revision, re-inventiveness and reusage. It is really something exceptional and something that I believe we are very proud of in Havana.
This also applies to housing.
It is well known that people've turned architectural spaces and other existing objects into houses. And a term commonly used in Havana is 'barbacoa' (mezzanine).
Alongside the mezzanine, new standards of co-op and of sharing buildings have come up. These buildings often lack basic services such as water supply and waste disposal. Residents, as a result of all this, adopt newer ways of relating to the new space. They not only reuse the available space but also convey very contemporary ideas, new life experiences, and new concepts of recycled materials, a different way of living together and thus new ways of socializing among neighbors are established. These global trends are usually promoted by magazines and websites,
and are already a reality in Havana.
These buildings provide spaces where the new entrepreneurs in the city are running new restaurants, bars, and other businesses.
These buildings easily adapt to the new requirements of a city under transformation.
Moreover, these requirements are making Havana grow inwards.
Many theoretical, conceptual trends
point otherwise. A city that begins to grow is no longer in harmony with the environment.
Havana has been consolidating inwards.
For several decades, Havana has not grown.
It is reinventing itself;
it is recycling itself, including its buildings.
We should also talk about the blending of styles within the social space.
We, Havana residents, live within the city. Construction works turn into a key element of the city.
CC: This area of land is also moving.
We Cubans have inadvertently established an informal mobility network made up of what we call 'boteros' [taxi drivers] and 'almendrones' [old American cars] and the rest of the world calls shared taxis.
These taxis are something we all experience and use. We are all familiar with their itineraries and rates. Such terms are not written anywhere,
but every time we ride one of these incredible cars from the 40s, 50s or 60s, we are aware of the fact that we are making statistics,
that each almendrón with capacity of up to five passengers helps reduce traffic jams.
Guys, we unknowingly are part of an elitist vanguard;
we should recognize that this non-state public transportation system has made it possible for millions
of people to move around.
This is very interesting, because it also has to do with public spaces.
At a time when people are questioning the limits of public spaces all around the world, we, in Havana, have them already designed and put into use. These spaces are really awesome.
But don't even think about telling anyone that the street is not a public space!
Children, adults, all kinds of people take over the places where we live together and interact, without anyone telling us what the limits of action are in a public space,
where we run into people we'll never meet or see again in this city.
That is often the case.
All this not only involves
physical, visible things, but also intangible things
like information.
Current Internet access is not as good as we would like it to be.
However, information goes from hand to hand, from person to person,
and we know who is actually providing us with information.
Guys, this is a luxury.
It is a luxury to be able to know who is transmitting and providing the information we consume.
This is the Havana way. The transmission of information is much faster than Internet access
in the country. Here there are green walls, plant-covered roofs, garden buildings, things that we continually, spontaneously incorporate, mostly thanks to our weather conditions. We see vegetation at all levels, on all scales, in all proportions.
It is natural; it is customary;
it is part of our daily picture.
So, when they come here and talk to us about vanguard, about incorporating vegetation into our urban landscape; guys, we have already done it.
OI: Somehow, Havana is re-inventing itself,
it is changing alongside Cuban society.
Havana is striving to be a thriving city with a prosperous economy for the benefit of all citizens.
In this context, there are two alternatives:
simply ignore everything
we have been learning, or build upon this collective knowledge that has required so much sacrifice. The latter is a part of us, a lesson learnt.
Out of these two alternatives, we have taken up the second one.
Some years ago, we established 'Habana Re-generation' a group of friends who gather together to think about the city and its future, and help it grow and expand.
We've been doing this from our perspective of architecture and our work.
We have sought to support what we believe is positive.
We urge you all to keep on re-inventing Havana, the way we want it to be:
a diverse, safe, united, prosperous Havana.
CC: A diverse, participatory, intelligent, productive city,
a city whose dwellers traveled around on bikes in the 90s and lost it all.
Let us not miss the opportunity
the present provides.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Can you imagine biking like this for six, seven and up to 10 hours?
After swimming between one and two hours in open waters?
And after swimming for two hours, biking for 10 hours, start running a marathon or 26.2 miles?
Today I want to tell you a story of how doing something as crazy as this, has the potential of saving thousands of lives around the world.
I want to begin this story from when I was 10 years old.
It was the year 1990, and in those times fast food was beginning to skyrocket.
And I had a big problem: I couldn't stop eating.
Today I laugh about this, but at that moment I was having a hard time.
And not only because of the physical part and all the extra pounds, but I would get tired very easily, I was the slowest of my friends, and of course I had trouble doing all kind of sports.
From 1990 to today, childhood obesity figures have risen dramatically.
And in some countries this has been way worse than in others.
Chile holds the first place in childhood obesity in Latin America, and sixth in the world.
When I was about 10 years old, some by my own initiative but more because of my family, I discovered sports through tennis.
I quickly realized that the more tennis I played, the better I felt.
After every training session not only was I having fun, but I was also full of energy.
First, I started playing once a week, then twice a week. Then it was five times a week, then it was twice a day.
And little by little, tennis started becoming the center of my life and my first passion.
Something told me that doing sports and playing tennis for the rest of my life was something that would make me very happy.
And when I was about 15 years old, I started to think about the idea of becoming a professional tennis player.
And what do you do when you're 15 years old, and want to become a professional tennis player?
You have to convince your parents that this is a good idea.
Just weeks before sitting to discuss with my father about my tennis dreams, and the many benefits this would bring to my life, something unexpected happened that changed my life forever and forced me to completely change my plans.
My father suffered a heart attack that left him with severe neurological damage, and a very critical health condition.
Tennis matches were quickly replaced by very long hours at the hospital, waiting for medical diagnosis, and different possible treatments to save his life.
What followed were months and years of great uncertainty.
And among the many things I lost during that period, including my father, was my dream of becoming a tennis player.
Facing the death of someone close to you quickly makes you look at life with a different perspective.
In my case, many years went by before I experienced something similar, and that this time made my life take a 180 degree turn.
Would you believe me if I told you that 15 years later I found myself in the same place, in the same hospital, in the same floor and talking to the same doctors?
At her 55 years of age, my mother was diagnosed with leukemia, with a very rare and aggressive type of genetic translocation.
Once again, I was facing an event that showed me how fragile life is and how everything can change in an instant.
This led me to ask myself the following question: If today was my last day on earth, how would I measure my life?
Would I measure it by the grades I got in college?
By the success I achieved professionally?
By the amount of money I have in the bank?
After lots of reflection, and thinking about this question, I concluded that a good way to measure my life would be to see how I was using my skills to help other people, how I was using the gifts God had given me, to help others.
Today, there are hundreds of people watching this talk in this room, but many more from their computers or cell phones. Each one of you with particular interests, passions, and goals for your life.
But more importantly, each one of you with a unique gift to give back in this life.
In my particular case and at that point of my life, I began to ask myself: What was I doing to improve other people's lives?
How was I using my skills to help others?
And I was not able to answer these questions.
And this is how, very shortly after, I quit my job.
My dream job, in which I had been working for 10 years, living in New York, a city where I always wanted to live.
And I decided to embark on what, at the time, seemed a crazy idea. This was the year 2013, and I was 32 years old.
I decided to go to Africa as a volunteer.
I wanted to spend six months of my life, using all my skills and abilities to help others.
I grabbed my bags, jumped onto a plane and moved to Moshi, a small town in northern Tanzania.
My volunteering in Africa consisted of doing Math and English classes, in a school for children between four and seven years old.
Every day after school, we would give the kids a meal made of corn and water. And something that caught my attention right away was that for more than half of my students this was the only meal they would eat during the day.
This is how in an indirect way, by teaching English and Math, I got to see the reality of child malnutrition up close. If you look at all deaths of children worldwide under the age of 5, close to 50 percent are linked to severe acute malnutrition.
Every 10 seconds, one child dies due to malnutrition in the world.
While this information is daunting, there is a solution and it's called RUTF or Ready-to-Use Therapeutic Food.
It's a sort of nutrient-dense peanut butter paste, with very good taste. A six to eight week feeding program with RUTFs, two to three times a day, permanently eliminates severe acute malnutrition in 95 percent of the cases.
What follows, has to do with how I started the talk.
Remember? On top of this bike.
One night I was surfing the internet, in my bed under my mosquito net, and I came across a video of Ironman Kona, Hawaii.
An Ironman, for those who do not know, is a long-distance triathlon where you swim for 2.4 miles, then bike for 112 miles, and finishes with a marathon or 26.2 mile run.
All in the same day, and in the shortest time possible.
And the World Championship of this sport, is Ironman Kona, one of the most humid and hot places in the world.
I remember finishing watching this video of Ironman Kona totally inspired by the stories of people who crossed that finish line, and thinking two things at that moment, First, how can a human being do these distances and under these conditions?
And second, I have to do this race at some point of my life!
And so the journey began, and I started crossing finish lines. And you can't imagine the road I had to travel to cross each one of them.
Each finish line with a different meaning, that makes it special, and different form the rest.
But with a common feeling; that feeling that makes all you had to sacrifice and leave aside to get there, worthwhile.
And makes you feel proud and happy just by the fact you crossed that finish line.
What if I told you that we can make, this satisfaction of crossing finish lines even more fulfilling?
What would happen if each pound we lost preparing our next marathon, soccer game, exercising, or doing any type of physical activity, could be given to another person that needs this pound?
If each calorie we burn exercising, going to the gym, surfing or training for our next race, could be recycled and converted into emergency nutrition for someone who needs this calorie?
We have two types of people in the world. People who have too many calories, and people who don't have enough calories.
Through the use of technology we have a unique opportunity to connect two global health concerns, such as obesity and malnutrition, in a way that helps solve them both.
Today, I want to show you how we are making this possible, and how all of you can participate.
We created a platform called Burn to Give which recycles the calories we burn walking, running, jumping, dancing, moving, taking the stairs instead of the elevator, and converts them into emergency nutrition calories for someone who needs them.
For every calorie burned exercising and doing sports, one calorie is given to a person in need.
But for this to work, we need to create a team.
A team with three types of participants: First, those who will be burning these calories.
Athletes, future athletes, people who are sitting in the couch and need an additional incentive to get moving.
All of these team members will have access to a platform, with training plans, diet tips and, more importantly, guidance on how to lead an active and healthy lifestyle.
We want to motivate the 80 percent of people in this world who are leading sedentary lifestyles, to get moving but not through the conventional logic that doing sports is just good for you, but by touching their hearts.
Second, those who will be receiving these calories.
The children we will be helping.
With today's technology we can see with great precision, who and how we are helping.
Imagine for a moment that, after your next run, where you are going to burn around 500 calories, you could know the name of the child who will receive a 500-calorie meal, that thanks to your run was possible.
And third, companies that will sponsor this team.
Visionary and innovative companies, who are using their marketing budgets with a double purpose; first, motivate and inspire millions to lead healthy and active lifestyles. And second, help and feed millions of people who need it.
These companies are going to measure the exact return, impact and reach that each of their marketing dollars are having by sponsoring this team.
This coming October 13th, I will have the honor to represent Chile, at the Ironman World Championship in Kona.
(Applause) In order for me to cross that finish line, I'll be spending hundreds of hours swimming, biking and running, with the purpose of pushing the limits of my body and mind, but with a much greater purpose; from today up to Ironman Kona, all the calories I burn exercising will be given to someone who needs these calories.
And to see this in concrete numbers, from today up to Kona, I plan to train for about 600 hours, where I will burn close to 400,000 calories, which in turn will be enough to feed and save the lives, of five children suffering from severe acute malnutrition.
(Applause) Last week we launched our first Burn to Give challenge.
What is this challenge about?
For the next 30 days we're inviting everyone to get active and exercise, in order to reach the goal of 30 million calories, that will be enough to feed 400 children in Haiti.
You just have to do three things to participate; first, start moving and doing some type of sport or physical activity.
Second, log in your calories at www.burntogive.com and third, invite all you friends to do the same -- your cousins, siblings, parents, nephews, colleagues and everyone you know. Because the more we are, the more calories we can burn, and the more children we are able to help.
Today, I want to leave all of you invited to be part of this team.
This team of people that burns calories, to give them to someone in need.
So we can all together transform our lives, but more importantly, the lives of thousands of children around the world.
Thank you very much!
(Applause)
20 years ago, I had to take care of a close relative who was hospitalized.
I had to share that world for several days.
In the other ward, right in front of it, there was a boy.
A boy whose parents were very ill and couldn't take care of him.
So he started a relationship with my family, my daughter, and without even realizing it, the hospital's social worker intervened, and so did the judge, and the boy ended up living in my house.
Do you think that I had ever thought
about taking a kid to my house?
I hadn't.
So far, I was a successful executive,
I had a beautiful house, a family as those you see in magazines, I used to go to private beaches, I had two cars.
And one night I was sleeping next to my husband, and I realized I wasn't sleeping just with him but that there was a lot of people in my bed as well: the Treasury was there, the Union, the Bank, the workers, the employees, and it was a bit uncomfortable.
And my husband didn't enjoy it either.
Right then I decided to change.
I decided that all the efforts and energy that I had put in my company I would put it in another project.
And so we did.
We bought a house in Carlos Keen, in the countryside, and we took the first boy that was living in my house there.
Then, three more boys came and with the help of the judges, we set up a private foundation called Camino Abierto.
And then a foster home.
But I didn't want it to be just a foster home because when you think about it, what do you see?
"Poor, unprotected children..." I didn't want that for them.
I also didn't want it to be just a charity thing.
Because, we often get "charity attacks."
When you see a barefoot kid, you buy him a pair of shoes,
and that's it.
I didn't want that for them.
I wanted it to be a place where they could interact with society, discover their natural talents and develop them completely.
Camino Abierto is an integrating project.
What does this mean?
We integrate social, corporate, environmental, educational and cultural aspects.
Like when you go to the orchard and see a lot of different plants, together, nourishing each other, in a perfect consonance with nature.
Then, we made a restaurant so we could fund the home.
A different restaurant where we teach the kids to cook with all the products obtained from the farm.
It's like opening our doors to invite other people.
Narda Lepes, Dolli Irigoyen, Martiniano Molina, Pablo Mazzei, Rodrigo Toso helped us with this project.
The kids that come to our house are in risk.
What can I teach them?
Just to change their attitude.
Just to feel useful.
All the kids go to school.
They are part of a band, they play sports... All of them go to school.
I'm a teacher that shows them that there is another life.
And then they can choose.
A while ago, the kids came speaking of history and geography, like all kids do when they come back from school, and they spoke about Pythagoras' theorem.
And I remembered I studied it long ago, and had almost forgot it.
A friend of mine, a university professor, was there, so I tell him, "Can you explain me the the Pythagorean theorem?"
He started to explain it and offered clear examples.
I started to understand it in depth.
And I instantly associated it with a ravioli plate.
Because teaching the kids how to cook is more than that.
Is teaching them that there's another world.
Walking into the orchard, harvesting their own vegetables Going to the barns, bringing the products back, making the dough.
Then it's when I say, the Pythagorean theorem, is like a ravioli plate."
Well, ravioli of course tastes much better.
A lot of people come to visit the house.
And when a politician comes and hands me his business card, he's just lost.
Because I'd start calling him all the time to get things for the kids.
Because I want a healthy life for them a life with mud, with bicycles, with animals, and orchards.
A life that allows them to go around the world.
That's what I want for them.
And I want that for me as well.
I'll tell you a story of one kid: Walter.
He came to my house when he was very young.
From a very poor area of San Martín.
He was six or seven.
He was very, very mad.
He was so angry, on his second day he ran away.
He took a bike and left.
He had such a bad luck that was hit by a car.
He was at the hospital for several days.
When he came back he went to school, we showed him the orchard, the kitchen, he would feed the animals.
And he made that life until he turned 18.
When he turned 18, he said to me, "I want to go with my family."
"Alright, it's your choice."
And he came back to that area of San Martin.
His family hadn't changed.
He arrived and found the same.
Grabbing a barrow every morning, and staying in a huge queue to find something to eat in a dumpster.
Waiting for the gates to open and even sometimes fighting to get a bit of food.
And was that being home?
He didn't want that for himself.
So, with all that he had learned, he made himself another barrow.
He was happy.
He got a lawn mower, thinking he would work in the whole neighborhood.
But it didn't happen.
His brother sold his barrow.
He called me almost crying, "I choose this life for me, please help me."
"Alright, come back."
He came back to school, to the orchard and the animals.
Then he rented a little house nearby.
He comes everyday to work.
And he is proud of what he does.
And from there, he helps his family.
More than 100 kids came to my house.
I know that 100 kids won't change the world, but I'm sure of one thing: I'm proud that 100 worlds had the chance to change.
Thank you.
(Applause)
All of us humans, we are creative.
It is something inherent to our specie, we all are.
But the question is: If we are all creatives, why can't we all be Einstein, Van Gogh or Rembrandt?
And what if we think about creativity as a process in which we can associate pieces of information? Could we take a machine and distillate the creative DNA of great genius in it? And why not copy it?
This was the initial question of the project "The Next Rembrandt". The idea was to gather information about all the paintings of the Dutch painter and try to produce an image with all the main pictographic elements.
It is interesting to realize that work on Rembrandt, from a Dutch point of view is something special, touchy.
Rembrandt, Van Gogh, and maybe Johan Cruyff are the most important pillars of the Dutch culture.
The project gathered a great group of people from different backgrounds.
We got, for instance, people with historical and art curation background.
We had people specialized in data analysis
We had people specialized in material analysis.
We had people from 3D printing.
At the beginning, the great difficulty was how could we orchestrate all this group of people around the global knowledge of Rembrandt?
Rembrandt is a giant, a universe on its own.
How could we take small pieces from one person, or another and start to form the puzzle?
Our only slogan was: Only let the data be our creative guide in this project.
Let's consider it from a linguistic point of view.
We are all Spanish-speaking, at least, the majority of us here, and when we listen to a Catalan, or to a French person, or to an Italian speaker we can understand them, we can listen to the meaning of the sentence even more, we understand some words, but they are completely different languages.
Thus, for us, what was crucial to understand was that being in this huge room field with Catalan, French and Italian people in a metaphoric way, we intended to create a common language which could gather them into telling this story.
An interesting example were the programmers themselves.
The programmers had, as a final goal, creating a database, seeking to find out relevant patterns in form, color, material and geometry.
From this point of view, you don't see Rembradt as a creative genius but as a system which is generating something.
When we started speaking with the programmers we were speaking a different language not a creative language, nor an historical one.
How should we think about Rembrandt to give a sense to our research?
Finally, we reach the conclusion that Rembrandt, more than a creative genius, is a system.
A system with its own rules, and rules which objective is to build a graphic representation called "a painting" and those paintings may vary inside this system.
To a certain point, we started thinking about Rembrandt as a black box.
It is interesting to thing that by moving from a artistic point of view toward a black box, a system, you can start copying the steps that lead to a painting. And one could start documenting how the painting gets formed from an analytic point of view.
Upon starting to work on this project, the first steps were, from the beginning, purely statistical.
The whole artwork of Rembrandt was taken into account and classified.
It was found out that there was a great proportion of portraits.
Portraits represent an average of 52 percent of the paintings.
After, we held a demographic analysis of who was portrayed in these paintings.
Our goal was to find out the most consistent data.
A woman painted by Rembrandt is different from a man portrayed by Rembrandt; a 40-years-old person, different from a 20-years-old one.
In fact, we were trying to figure out which was the most consistent demographic group in order to start working from here.
Rembrandt is an interesting painter, surprisingly egalitarian, there is the same quantity of man and women portraits.
Finally, we chose to study men, because many female paintings from Rembrandt are portraits from Rembrandt's wife or his mistress.
The final results was: The portrait of male, with beard, between 30 and 40 years.
This was interesting too! Because they seemed to be older which may mean that people got older in a different way or that Rembrandt portrayed them in a different way:
dark cloths, neckband, hat and looking to the right;
interesting when we discover that people looking to the right were a norm of portraits in Rembrandt's times, painting was a commercial product which was sold for men and women. And men usually look to the right, woman usually to the left.
The complicated part, was moving -- from a technical perspective -- from 2D to 3D.
A painting is a distribution of material on a canvas.
From this perspective, what we wanted to do was to take an image that had been produced and make it tridimensional.
To work with it, we used a special technique developed to reproduce pieces of art.
Masterpiece copy is becoming a real important market all over the world and technologies have been developed to reproduce them in their original size to certain point, quite similar to the originals.
What we did is work with the Technical Delft University and the Mauritz House which possesses one of the main collections of Rembrandt's paintings in the world, we took the paintings, scanned by X-ray, to 0 and 1. After that we brought them to our painting. And finally, we painted it on a special material "UV-ink" that printed over a plain surface.
Here is the result.
It is a painting that represents Rembrandt from a statistic point of view.
Now the question which might be more interesting is: Could creativity be reproduced?
To answer this, I would like to introduce a paradox which was my personal experience on this project.
All these people were creative beings, some of them regarding data, some on art history, other on 3D artwork reproduction.
It is curious that creativity were seeking to reproduce creativity.
And this gives us a first indication of, whatever it would be, people that create these creative tools, are creative too.
The second point is, sure, machine are taking over a lot of automatic elements that we use in our daily activity but there are things that machine, so far, cannot do and, it is quite clear in Rembrandt, as in every artist
we make mistakes, we fail, and these mistakes turn into virtues to explore and generate new creative experiences.
I believe that, as long as machines won't be able to reproduce mistakes and find into mistakes new creative opportunities, I believe, we as human, still have a great advantage from a creative perspective.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Disappeared civilizations, once important empires, now fallen apart, leaving us enigmas, leaving us mysteries that we will try to solve today.
Come with me on a satellite travel.
An archeological trip where we're searching for answers in the ruins of this civilization.
It'll be an easy one because this civilization has left us many ruins.
Because this civilization liked to build a lot.
It took great pleasure in increasing its villages and cities unhindered by any obstacles on its path.
It is said that on a given moment, it was able to build more houses than France and Germany together.
They built a lot.
But one of the strangest things ever is that we don't know the purpose of much of what was built. (Laughter)
We found this all over the territory.
These endless lanes, kilometers long, that are not going anywhere.
(Laughter) We don't know what was going on, what was the purpose of it all.
You don't need so many roads to get to that one building.
(Laughter) They have to serve another purpose.
Most of the experts we asked an opinion on it think that it has to do with some sort of myth, some sort or belief that this civilization had.
We don't know if that hypothesis is correct, but we do know that looking from that perspective, and observing other ruins we find a certain pattern, some strange symbols, sometimes concealed, as if they were trying to hide them.
Sometimes, they aren't seen with the naked eye, but we can find them if we try hard.
I don't know if you can see it, they are circles that are somehow repeating (Laughter) they are repeated constantly, sometimes in a way we don't understand.
There's something here... Maybe there’s a hidden meaning in these... I don’t know.
(Applause) Centers, in those places, what is going on?
What are they used for?
Look at some excavation that my team and I did close to here, in Torrejón.
You don't see anything else than what we've seen before, but we were able to access one of those circles and what we saw fascinated us.
(Laughter) We think it's a sort of dimensional gateway. (Laughter) Really.
I believe it's an attempt or mechanism to communicate with some superior being.
(Laughter) Maybe from this point you can't see it, but looking from above (Laughter) you clearly see an attempt to communicate.
(Applause) Who are we communicating with?
Which being are we talking about?
We think it's a very powerful being, (Laughter) that's responsible for the balance in this civilization.
A being that was feared,
that they had to be careful with and that made the world go round and that makes the sun come up every day.
That's one way of looking at the pictures from my project Nación Rotonda.
(Roundabout Nation) I am Miguel Álvarez, I'm not an archaeologist, I don't like the paranormal, I'm a civil engineer, I work in city planning and mobility, and with my partners Esteban, Guillermo and Rafael we started this project Nación Rotonda.
Nación Rotonda wants to be a visual inventory of the change in use of the Spanish territory, over the last 15 years, during the real-estate bubble.
It's a project we set up in our spare time with very basic, free tools, available to all with a computer and internet connection in which we do what you see: one file of one place, every day.
In this case it's Alhambra Signature Golf in Murcia.
This is the before-picture and as you can imagine, with a slider, we see how it is now in 2013.
It's a golf course.
Not only the four of us participate in this project, hundreds of people have sent us a lot of of places with a huge change in use, through which we, over the last 2,5 years, have found over 800 places in which there has been a significant change in use.
What did we see? What is our experience?
What can we tell about
'Roundabout Nation'?
We have a country that built very much during 15 years.
That did this with cars in mind.
That didn't think about pedestrians enjoying the city, but focused on one mode of transport, which makes sense of all those circles and roundabouts that you find all over the place, because they're necessary for the cities we've created.
That's where the urbanization has been built and inhabited.
Contrary to these examples that we see all over the country.
This one in Asturias, in the north.
The one you see here is in La Mancha, in the middle of the country.
This one in Almería, in the south.
All of them places where the use of the territory, like in this case, is an economical use that they try to change for another one.
In this case, we're not able to finish the golf course, in other cases we do finish it but not as it was planned.
Even when they finish and use them, as you see here, those greens aren't as 'green' as they should be, maybe because it's in Murcia, a place where water is scarce.
We have also made things in the water of our coasts.
The transport infrastructure that we built throughout the whole country, like in this case, look at the empty moorings in this port in Cantabria.
Industrial sites. Another attempt,
bringing industry to our country, in many cases with everything placed in those sites, doesn't fill half of the space there is.
At the end of our journey, the panorama becomes shocking.
Sometimes we don't know if we're looking at the after, or if it is the before and the after is even worse.
We just don't know.
This is a point of view of Nación Rotonda.
What we have done and what we shouldn’t do again.
But personally I like to see it from another angle.
I like to see Nación Rotonda as a catalogue of places where we can really do whatever we want, because the original plan has failed completely, so any other option will be better.
Looking at it like that, we say: maybe there are other places where they must finish building what they have started.
Sometimes, we finish what is left to do.
In other places maybe not.
Here in this octagon you see a playground.
I would not go there, it doesn't look like a nice place to me.
But it doesn't make sense to finish the houses that were planned there.
If we change the point of view thinking about that slide, that swing... we can take it away from this place and we take it to where we live.
When we start looking at things that way, maybe our perspective changes.
Here I see a plot of land, but I also that trash can, which isn't necessary here, I don't think it's used.
Maybe we can use it somewhere else.
When we look at it like this, what is left are these spaces.
What do we see in them?
I see possibilities.
We can restore this hill in its original state.
Maybe I see an urban garden there.
A great place.
We could have a camping for caravans.
Or a bicycle race circuit.
Those aren't ideas that just occurred to me, those are real examples, pilots, that are taken around Spain, maybe not as excellent as the thousands of houses that they promised us but they work and they are being used in those places.
Because what I don't think we should do is leave those places sealed, where no kind of activity can take place, because I think we can always find activities that can be done
in a place like this.
Always, this is an activity. Why not?
An amazing activity.
Above all, if we don't create that activity, time will pass by and nature will take its course and places will change into those ruins that we spoke about at the beginning.
That's all, I tried to give you several points of view, so that we'll cherish those places, and among us all we will give it some thought so that we will find a great exit for 'Roundabout Nation'.
Thank you.
(Applause)
(Shakes a bottle) I was 16 years old.
I was studying, going out with friends, writing and acting in theatrical plays.
I did not like the school I attended; it was too big and too cold.
There were over 1800 students and marble everywhere.
One morning, it occurred to me to leave the classroom without permission.
I was in pain because the night before, the girl of my fancy hadn't reciprocated my feelings.
I went to the bathroom and the teachers started looking for me.
As I walked down the halls, I'd see messages in the billboards.
I started to feel strange.
I was feeling that everything I was seeing was related to me.
I was seeing signs and messages.
The teachers started following me down the corridors, as I hide in empty classrooms.
And I had the feeling that this school was and was not my school.
Finally they caught me by force, the threw me to the floor and called my family.
I was taken to a hospital and two days later I had already been diagnosed with schizophrenia, a mental illness.
This episode in high school was called a "psychotic episode."
I asked the doctors to be taken to a farm, but they decided to lock me in a psychiatric institution.
I spent six months there, asking them to let me out.
The nurses would bring me lots of pills and I could not focus to write.
They put me in a very small room called containment room.
A square bed, a toilet, a metal door and no windows.
That was not a treatment, but a torture.
My family didn't know what to do.
There I began to realize that if I wanted to come out, if I wanted to be free again, I had to be obedient.
So I started to fake obedience.
And the strategy worked.
I could get out of the mad house.
I went back to writing, work, drama and I directed my first play.
(Acts) You were pale, fragile, your eyelids were dark, your lips, purple; the red band.
You had nothing to do with the world of plastic, right?
And being there made you even more beautiful.
You stood up on the gurney, showing your drawings and singing.
I dreamed with that image all night long, you know?
I love you, I love you before knowing you.
(Acting ends) At the end of the play, Ciro would team up with other patients and managed to escape from the mad house.
Unlike Ciro, I was 26 years old and still trapped in diagnoses and treatments.
And I was directing drama already.
I was directing a group of 16 people.
But one day I was watching a play at a street festival.
There was this huge clock that began to get my full attention.
I started to feel strange, so I asked for an ambulance to be called.
I asked for help.
They told me I was too agitated and they sent me to home care: the mad house, but in your own house.
This time they said I was bipolar.
And that I had to be careful with being very happy or very sad.
Why should I be afraid of my emotions, of my melancholy, my euphoria, my extreme emotions?
(Acts) I can't take this anymore!
No!
Help me!
I screamed, yes, I did.
I'm alone, dirty --
No, the earth doesn't get you dirty.
No, it's mud.
A lot of people came, no one helped.
The only one to help was the Martian and he's already gone.
That's the sound of the sea; the snail left his home.
(Acting ends) When you act, it's you, but it's not you.
That's one of the best things about drama.
And sometimes with some characters, impulses take over you. As it just happened to Juancito.
In my writing I denounce the psychiatrist as an agent of the system.
Like one of the security forces that preserves the status quo.
In this way society turns its fear to what's different into fear to the patient and their family.
What the system calls "psychotic episode," it's to me one of the different ways there are to perceive reality.
What some call "crisis" or "sprouts" and "episodes" are forms of expression that defy everything we consider "normal."
And they have the power to subvert the order established by sanity.
The problem is that society is not ready to include delirious behavior within its culture.
So, it excludes us in mental hospitals.
And those of us who can rave are locked down, tortured and drugged up because they pretend to convince us that this is the only way we can be well in the world.
They make us fear our own feelings and they try to convince us that to be crazy is to be dangerous.
I learned that madness is the possibility to live every and each of our emotions to the fullest.
It's the ability to transform society.
Madness has to do with hallucinating and torture makes us suffer, and all that makes us suffer.
But madness also has to do with freedom, with play.
If we could recognize madness as an identity and delirium as a right, many people would live much happier.
In drama, I defend other forms of relating to each other.
Because actors constantly work with their emotions, their fantasy, their imagination.
In a rehearsal we don't worry that much about a crying spell, a rage, jealousy or narcissism.
After lots of work I understood that the hallucinations and delirious experiences I went through were a part of drama.
Then I began to create exercises for actors to connect with their impulses, hallucinations and raves.
Because hallucination has a lot to do with the imagination and delirium is quite close to fantasy.
Before going on stage, we actors feel that famous adrenaline, those jitters which are another way of perceiving reality.
You see colors more clearly, you listen to everything with more focus, and you enter a state of peace, of completeness and alertness at the same time.
And it's like all five senses awake.
(Acts) Where am I?
I'm in every family.
I'm in a father who reminds his son to take his medication.
I'm in a couple that argues because of a treatment.
I'm in cities, I'm in towns.
In the roads, some long-haul truck drivers experiment the "white dream" after many hours of night driving.
They look asleep, but in fact, their pupils are intact, dilated, their eyes are open, while they await, without knowing it, their tragic end.
(Shakes a bottle) Divalproex sodium.
(Shakes a bottle) This is not mine, it belongs to you.
One in the morning, one in the afternoon, and one at night; before dreaming.
(Acting ends) (Applause)
Imagine that all of a sudden you recognize nothing and no one around you.
You can’t find the people you want. You can't communicate
or you ask for help but no one responds.
Imagine you are lost.
Luckily, this didn’t happen to me nor to my family, but it happened to lots of people.
In Argentina, a person goes missing everyday.
This means that more than 1,000 people have disappeared over the last 3 years.
When I heard this, I strongly felt that something should be done, that a topic this important cannot be left aside.
I began investigating and I found little to begin with on this topic.
On the other hand, during this summer I was very interested in all the aspects of programming.
I’ve been enthusiastic about it since I was a child.
But this summer I decided to take courses and insisted that my teachers help me code.
This is how I entered a new and exciting world.
I noticed that you could do great things with programming, but few of them had solidarity purposes.
After all this, I came up with a great idea: merge what I had learned about programming with the problem of missing persons.
I created a digital platform to help find them.
What I value the most is the moment when I began with all of this.
I didn’t know where to begin, but I started anyway.
At first, it seemed impossible to do something like this.
It seemed unachievable.
Fortunately, I decided to develop this project.
During development, I realized it can be done, that with effort and perseverance you can achieve what you imagine.
Little by little, all the barriers on my way to implementing this idea were breaking down.
The idea is that a family member can report a missing person, and the users nearby the place of disappearance receive a notification when this happens, so that they can help find that person by giving you valuable data, such as where the person was last seen and what was wearing.
By building the network of users, we could fulfill our objectives together: to find missing people as soon as possible and reduce the great distress their families are in.
The idea is to collect and unite all the information about a person, that remains sparce most of the times, so that the organizations and people looking for that person instantly receive the latest updated information.
Two months ago I had a great opportunity to join a NGO in Argentina called "Missing Persons."
They had a page which only showed a list of missing persons and I’m glad that soon they will be using my platform to improve the search.
(Applause) In fact, users can already report missing people on the official webpage of the NGO, and we’re currently working on the missing persons' profiles, as well as on developing a mobile app.
When I started this, I knew very little about how such a platform worked.
The whole process of developing it helped me learn a lot.
Besides, when you do what you like, learning is almost a side effect.
Anyway, you learn a lot.
The best way to learn something is to practice it 100%.
With all this, I reached an unimaginable point in my life, I achieved what seemed impossible to many people, myself included.
During this process I experienced both ups and downs,
moments when I felt like giving all up, and others that gave me strength to reach the end, which, at first, seemed far and impossible.
This is how I fulfilled my two big dreams and passions: to develop a project from start to finish,
and my favorite, to help find missing people.
(Applause) Imagine that, all of a sudden, you recognize nothing and no one around you.
You can’t find the people you want, you can't communicate and if you ask for help, no one responds.
Imagine you are lost.
Now imagine that there is a community aware of those who are missing, with more tools to find them and searching for them at the moment.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I'm fine, I'm fine, yes. Falling, it happens to me a lot.
Don't worry, I have already been this white this morning.
(Laughter) I always fall because I'm an albino.
But it was a funny falling, wasn't it?
Because... we can't lose glamour not even when we flop on the floor.
(Laughter) Actually, I came here to talk about judo.
About what judo has taught me about life.
I am a Paralympic top-class athlete due to my visual impairment.
I have taken part in three editions of The Games: in Athens, Beijing and London, and I have won medals in all of them.
(Applause) Thank you.
For those who don't know, judo is a contact sport.
It is a Japanese martial art which means "the way of flexibility."
As it does not imply hits as such, blind people or partially sighted as me, use a little adaptation, which is starting it gripping each other.
For me, judo is a great metaphor of life.
One of its ultimate goals is to keep standing: turn around, writhe, move, adapt, but do not fall over.
Or rather, do not let them take you down.
But, who has never fallen?
That's why the first thing taught in judo is how to fall not to get hurt.
Because it is wisely assumed that it is what is going to happen.
Not once or twice, but many, many times, and getting up is a must in all of them.
Sometimes, the takedowns are so brutal that you can't breathe and they take you even underneath the floor, leaving marks or scars.
But if you're smart and you know how to look at the bright side of things, you get a new piece of knowledge for the same price and the best thing of all: the feeling of improvement.
I love the judo philosophy of getting ready for when things go wrong.
And it's very useful because, who has never hit rock bottom?
Everybody has a story of overcoming and those who don't, will have one.
I'm going to tell you how I hit rock bottom.
After four years training for The Games, I was in a very good shape.
It was probably one of the most difficult competitions I best did competing in extremely tough combats. And then, my time has arrived.
I was at the final and I almost could touch my gold medal.
They said my name so it was my turn to get out there and compete.
I was super nervous and my hands sweated, but I was focused.
I went out and the combat started.
We gripped each other --
I don't know how long it took, but I think it wasn't even three seconds.
The audience in the stadium was on their feet, cheering, and I just could think: "Marta, get up quickly and do not cry here."
And then, alone, I cried. A lot.

I cried so hard that even my head hurt.
A sea of tears.
She slapped me so hard that my teeth caps detached, seriously.
The next day I had to go to the Paralympic Village dentist to get them put back.
By the way, I didn't know we had this kind of services.
(Laughter) However, the experiential punch I got was very much harder.
I took for granted that I would win my gold medal but I nearly lost my teeth.
And even being the coprotagonist of one of these greatest moments that will remain in history -- but I am the one who lost, you know,
the one who fell over the floor.
And I was fed up of losing finals.
OK, I know that complaining about being on second position might sound silly.
I got on the podium systematically after losing the final combat and I didn't like the feeling.
Then I hit rock bottom and I retired from judo thinking I wasn't able to win a final regardless of who was the opponent.
And I remained in the bottom for a while.
You have to feel the bottom to be able to boost you up, to learn.
I came back of course, and with many lessons learned: organization, teamwork... but specially, with a different attitude.
Now, I enjoy the privilege of competing, of being there.
And when I get the second position, as it happened again this last summer in a European Championship, or last week, the last Saturday, I just feast on it, I enjoy it, and above all, I learn.
The thing is that I has already been a master in learning lessons because my life circumstances made me learn a lot about life itself.
Well, like everybody, right?
I'm not more experienced than other people.
But the thing is, as a consequence of my albinism, I had to go roundabout ways to learn the same thing.
And that's what disabled people do:
we arrive to the same place taking a different way, innovating, creating what didn't exist before.
There are many kinds of albinism, but this that I have is characterized by the white hair that everybody knows.
If you put the light down for a second, please,
I'll take off my glasses and show you my eyes.
These pinky eyes.
Everybody knows that we are very sensitive to light, we can't be exposed to sunlight.
Well, I don't have to dye my grey hair.
But which many people don't know is that albinism involves severe visual impairment.
For example, I have a 10 percent of vision.
That means that I am between two worlds, the world of those who can see and the world of who cannot see.
And, at the same time, I'm not in neither of them.
I have what it's called "remaining sight," I see shapes, like pixels were missing in an image, without definition.
So, the friends that already know me know that they always have to greet me first, every time, and identify themselves, say who they are.
Just saying "Hi, Marta" is not enough, I already know who I am.
(Laughter) (Applause) When I go to the kindergarten to pick my baby up, I wait her to come to me lifting her arms saying "Mum".
I have never taken another kid that wasn't mine yet.
(Laughter) (Applause) When I go to the school to pick my older kids up, if the middle brother is restless, which often happens, I put him on a yellow fluorescent vest in order to know when he is closed and then I know what child I have to scold.
(Laughter) I know what you're thinking. It is a wonderful idea in case of agglomerations, but you cannot steal my idea because you'd destroy me.
(Laughter) (Applause) To use the phone, computers, I use software adapters, and when I want to see some things in the street, I use a magnifying glass, I also have a specialized magnifier to read. It's great that all this kind of adaptations exist.
But in order to make people understand what I can see and what I can't, I have to make a lot of explanations, it takes a lifetime making explanations.
And yet, even for more explanations, I have to begin from the very beginning of the story: albinos are not like cats, we don't see better in the night, we aren't as evil as Hollywood portrays us.
But there are some people that, even for more explanations that are made, things are just black or white.
And what about those half-truths like "blind people who can see."
And people who can actually see, who are really blind, don't understand.
You can't guess how many times I heard "you can only see what you want."
(Laughter) I'm so fed up with these things.
But if I think about it, the truth is that it may be reasonable somehow. Because taking into account the huge effort that I make to look, if I even care a button you can pack your bags and go home.
You know? I won't look at a single thing.
(Laughter) (Applause) And what it's very clear is that all the situation leads to confusion confusion, and more confusion.
If you think about the same situation 35 years ago when there was much less information than nowadays, you can guess the rest.
I had a happy childhood because I have a cheerful character by nature. And life slapped me when I became a teenager.
It was the first time that I understood all that really happened to me, what didn't have a name by that time.
I was labeled in school as a lazy kid right away because I didn't want to read,
I played this role very well, like a champion.
(Laughter) They let me get up to get closer to the blackboard as the only adaptation.
But I remember that, in my daily life, I couldn't get closer to things because
when I brought my head closer in order to see something, I threw things around me to the grown with my hair.
Or when I wanted to take something, they told me the commonly used phrase: "look but don't touch."
So little by little, with these small things that happened, I was building an idea inside me, a feeling of unworthiness. There was information that I hadn't the right to know, that wasn't for me.
It's curious how things that happen mark us.
I can't tell if I was bullied or not.
Because since I can remember, I always wandered alone in the schoolyard until I made a group of friends that I still keep when I was 15 years old.
And for everybody, especially I mean adults, it was the most natural thing to do.
I was a victim of the circumstances without helping it, without bad intention.
But the consequences were terrible.
As a consequence of labels and the lack of information that lead in a total lack of accessibility. And I'm not talking about a specialized reading glasses or a video magnifier, I mean a modest pair of sunglasses and a simple cap
I was building a wall of limits, a wall of thigs that I couldn't' do that it actually didn't exist.
So, I turned into a real disabled person because that was how I felt.
At this time nobody believed that I could get so far.
And that's how my career began, reaching the top of the podium.
Well, in my case, the place just in one of the sides of it.
(Laughter) (Applause) Luckily for me, judo came to my life and I had the courage of not letting this opportunity pass me by because I realize for the first time how to be involved in a normalizing environment was.
I was always being included socially, but like a piece that doesn't fit the puzzle, and I'll explain the difference.
For me, inclusion means that firstly you were excluded, and that's important because first they drag you out of it and then embed you in again.
So, you don't fix very well anymore.
However, normalization is accessible, adaptable, and it implies that everybody does an activity, the same activity but satisfying our specific necessities and without changing the group dynamic.
It's amazing.
Naturally, I wasn't the first purblind nor the first blondie who came by.
But I was sure from the very beginning that that was how things should be.
There were wonderful people starting with my first teacher, Vicente, who believed in me in spite of my incoordination and my lack of self-confidence.
I also had enjoyable experiences when I could meet Mariví and took part in the clubs' league in the Community of Madrid.
Or in my current club in Las Rozas, with Nacho.
Little by little, judo started to empower me.
It taught me that I was worthy thanks to the sense of feeling part of something bigger than me.
It taught me to make efforts and not to resign myself to the results, it taught me to make sacrifices for my passion thanks to that new thing that I have never experienced before, a positive feedback.
Finally, my effort had an effect in the results.
Judo taught me not to be a victim of my past, not to be a victim of myself.
In short, judo taught me to be free and owner and responsible of my life; to take decisions, to choose my battles, and even to take part on the same process in other people.
Following one of judo principles that remarks it from other sports: "Jita-Kyoei", the "mutual benefit" in order to reach excellence.
Judo may seem an individual sport, but this couldn't be further from the truth.
We need each other to improve together.
In judo, this has its own principle.
It would be amazing that, in the future, we had this environment in schools, in the classrooms, in all the workplaces.
I know a lot of people, adults and kids, who are in normalizing environments, but there are a lot of people that remain like I did, dragging a problem that no one knows how to deal with, a problem that no one knows how to solve.
The benefits for society are very clear. For disabled people, this is the world where we have to be, and not in a parallel reality that either before or after will collapse.
Besides, disabled people will contribute with a different point of view, with the ability in finding alternative ways, solutions.
And people without disabilities learn how to treat everybody naturally.
Learn that life is perfectly imperfect, that everybody has rights and duties.
We have the right to become parents, the right to be children, the right to study, the duty to pass our assignments.
And thus, little by little, society is transforming.
Someday we'll have a normalizing society full of native normalizers as my children are.
My kids don't question my capacities, they take them for granted.
I'm almost omnipotent for them right now.
(Laughter) Until they become teenagers and I became the same useless mother as the rest of parents who don't know anything about life.
But I still have time until that moment arrives... (Applause) The thing is that, I would like that in the future this thought arrives everywhere. But it can be a limit, an obstacle and we all have to work in order to avoid this to happen, fighting against the real disability, a disability that does concern me, which is the lack of empathy.
This is a non-registered disability, it is not recognized by the State, but it's very restricting.
And it not only restricts you, it also restricts everyone else around because it doesn't allow you to see the positive things of others and also neither the good things of yourself.
Sometimes, this disability allows others to park where it's not allowed and becoming architectural barriers.
They're people who don't see ways or alternatives, don't see solutions.
And there's none so blind as those who will not see.
They're people who have troubles in letting themselves go and they need our help. They need associations and the cooperation of the citizens.
Now I'm just another piece of the puzzle, nothing else.
I don't see limits; I know they're there waiting for me to come and change them.
I'm willing to make my crazy dreams come true, like participating in The Games in Tokyo with my circumstances: my age, 40 years old, my lesions, my three kids, my work...
but I'm not alone in all these adventures.
Always with me comes that 12-year-old Marta, who is puzzled, doesn't trust anything and she can't believe in this future yet.
I take her hand and tell her "look at all we have achieved, all we have done: recognition, medals, a wonderful family, these three kids.
This really is high performance sport."
(Laughter) But she can't believe it yet.
That's why I put limits on her and I just let her come out when I have to dance because it's a thing that I do as bad as when I was 12.
But you guys are very lucky because you won't have to see it.
(Laughter) Do not wait the life to slap your face before start working on all your potential.
If that distrustful child and I achieved that far getting from many meters behind the start line, is there anything that you can't do?
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
