(Applause) Thank you.
(Applause)
I'm going to tell you a story.
The story of Aryan: One of the bravest women I've ever met.
I met her in Kobanî, a besieged city in northern Syria, and I decided to follow her with my camera.
I had the toughest experiences of my life following her.
War is difficult to explain; in war there's no yesterday nor tomorrow; in war there's only the here and now.
Life is lived intensely because it stares death in the face.
I'd never seen so many dead people; maybe that's why I'd never felt so alive.
Aryan is Kurdish for 'fire'.
It's a war name, given to her when she became a guerilla soldier, many years back.
Now Aryan is one of the YPJ commanders in the Women's Protection Units; an army of women that has taken control of the Kurdish zone in northern Syria.
With Aryan I became part of an operation that ended the siege of Kobanî and together we celebrated victory; it was thrilling.
But together, we also cried for lost friends; too many.
Death was treading on our heels, but Aryan never looked back.
I loved watching her: so full of energy, organizing troops with a strong voice.
Aryan is 33 and when she was 18, she decided she'd had enough of all the injustice around her.
She enrolled in a women's army to defend her rights and those of all women.
Aryan wanted to be free and chose the most radical path: taking arms.
If we do not protect ourselves, who will do it for us? she always tells me.
Aryan grew up in Afrin, a Kurdish city in Northern Syria.
When she was 12 years old she witnessed a horrible crime: a group of men in her neighborhood raped a girl of her age.
She got pregnant and her family murdered her to protect their honor.
Since then Aryan is determined to change the reality of women around her; no matter the cost.
While she speaks about feminism, with younger girls, the youngest female soldiers, at the front,
a few meters from enemy lines, while mortar is falling, I realize that her fight, as a woman fighter, is twofold: she fights against Islamic State, but at the same time, she fights a very tough battle against her own society.
'We will not stop until we end the patriarchy', Aryan would always say.
And boom! another mortar fell 50m from us.
To end the patriarchy, to end 5000 years of patriarchy that have defined our social and economic relationships practically all over the world. it's a very ambitious project. Very ambitious.
But so was ending slavery, still legal in Mauritania in 1980.
Or to end racial segregation, which still exists today, although only in Israel.
In order to change the world, we must believe in the impossible.

Boom! Another mortar shell.
'Don't be afraid' Aryan would say; it's very difficult for a mortar shell to fall on top of you.
When a shell approaches, you can hear 'thith-thith-thith', like a small helicopter; you must drop to the ground.
When a shell falls, before exploding, it bounces back half a meter, and even if it falls near you while laying on the ground, it won't kill you.
'What you should be afraid of are landmines' she always said.
"You're walking with your eye glued to your camera, not looking where you put your foot."
Mines are the main cause of death among our female comrades; there are mines everywhere: in the roads, in the liberated villages
Some are so well-hidden they take soldiers by surprise when they open a door, or cross a road...
Others explode when someone tries to deactivate them; some soldiers have lost a leg, others have lost an arm; the luckier ones.
Others are not with us anymore... like Peyman or Rojda...
They were 18 years old when they stepped on the mine that killed them.
Women's Protection Units were created in Syria in 2013 and consist of around 12000 women aged between 18 and 40.
Together with the People's Protection Units, formed by men, they defend their progressist ideals, in opposition to the radical ideologies of the jihadists.
Women, and men, soldiers defend a new political system based on gender equality, environmental sustainability and real democracy.
They are the protagonists of the first revolution in the world that has placed women at the center of change.
That's why as a woman, a woman filmmaker, I feel proud of being part, somehow, of this revolution.
Aryan is shouting again.
She tells us to get into the vehicles because we have to set off.
We proceed through the Syrian plains; wheat fields burning around us.
We follow a dozen ramshackle cars.
And we drive through the fields, because there are mines on the roads.
The first one is a bulldozer, which drives with its blade low, in case of landmines.
Several pickups follow, their window panes cracked, an old ambulance with German serigraphies, and some vans with hand-painted camouflage designs.
We look like something out of a Mad Max movie.
Today is the first day of the operation to put an end to the siege of Kobanî.
The operation consists of opening a corridor to link Kobanî with the rest of the Syrian Kurdistan.
We will move eastwards from Kobanî, and the troops on the other side of the circle will move westwards, until we meet up in Tel Abyad.
Kobanî has been a besieged city for over 2 years and that turned it into the resistance icon against Islamic State.
In 2014, when Daesh troops (that's what Islamic State is called there were advancing through Syrian territory, without meeting resistance, they arrive in Kobanî aiming to occupy it.
But the Kurds wouldn't allow this to happen.
For many months a bloody battle
takes place. And, even though Daesh outnumbers them and have better weapons, they can't defeat the Kurds, who fight to the death to defend their city.
Finally the international coalition intervenes with aerial bombings and Daesh retreats; but the city, although in Kurdish hands, is left completely destroyed and besieged.
Many women, Aryan's friends, died in this battle.
One of them died in her arms.
'Her memory gives me strength to keep fighting', she told me once.
For her, ending the Kobanî siege is something she owes to the people who live there, but also to her many friends who died defending the city.
It's very hot. My military trousers
stick to my skin.
Aryan wears her jacket buttoned up to her neck and a scarf. My sleeves are rolled up and I'd take everything off.
But at the front you need to be covered.
We advance through territories controlled by Islamic State.
We reach an Arab village.
Soldiers separate the men from the women and arrest some men, suspected members of Islamic State.
They search the women. I observe them.
Some women wear headscarves,
long dresses.
Others wear military uniforms and hold Kalashnikovs.
How different the fates of these women.
I wonder what side I'd be on, if I'd been born in Syria.
It's night-time and we set up camp in a village. Some seven houses built of concrete bricks.
We search them one by one.
These are humble houses, covered with cushions and rugs, with old bits of furniture.
In the kitchens there are pots with rice and freshly-baked bread.
No one is here but everything is as if they'd come back anytime.
'Don't touch anything', Aryan says.
We take a very large rug and some blankets from our pick-up and we settle on the roof of one of the houses.
The night is fresh and dark, without a moon.
Two girls do armed guard duty.
We're a few meters away from the Islamic State front and they could attack anytime.
I fall asleep to the low buzz of warplanes.
An explosion wakes me up.
On the radio we hear distorted screaming and far in the distance some shooting.
The neighboring camp is under attack, and the girls get up quickly, Kalashnikovs in hand, and rush down the stairs.
I follow them with my camera.
Aryan, can I come along?
She says no.
I put my tripod outside, in front of the door, to record their departure.
The girls come out, they get into the van, the van pulls off and, still inside my camera frame, I see Aryan rolling down her window and, with her arm, signalling to me that I can go with them.
The next images are inside the van.
Because it's dark, I've activated night vision on the camera, and everything is green.
And that's how I remember it all, green.
As we approach the battlefield, the deafening gun shots become louder.
When we get there, we pull over behind a wall.
The girls get out, and we approach a house, crouching down.
Friendly Kurdish soldiers run out and tell us: 'Leave! Leave!'
Islamic State is approaching.
But we keep moving towards the house.
We run up the stairs, and the girls set up parapets upstairs, on the roof.
Some 20 Islamic State militants are coming towards us.
There's crossfire for a while.
Eventually, the Islamic State militants take refuge inside a house similar to ours, 300 meters away.
Aryan calls the plane.
She uses an app on her cellphone, which sends the exact coordinates,
Three, two, one... A big explosion followed by a cloud of dust and smoke, has destroyed the house where the IS militants took shelter.
Like that, 'boom', in a blink.
No assistance, no trial, no prison.
Straight to paradise.
Aryan looks through her binoculars
for a while Far, in the field, there are several corpses.
'They're all dead', says Aryan.
She says that without showing happiness.
None of the women I met in Syria were happy at the death of the enemy.
While the boys celebrated victory, The female soldiers would sit, lost in thought.
'These deaths are pure brutality', Aryan told me once.
These are killings in the name of power, in the name of religion, for pure violence.
Women's relationship to war is very different to men's.
The sacrifices women make when they become soldiers, are far tougher than those made by regular soldiers.
Women have to go against everything they've been educated for.
They have to renounce marriage, maternity and family.
To Aryan, her female friends are her family.
The bonds she's made with them are very strong.
It's been many years without meeting her parents.
and her younger brother, Baran, who, following her steps, enrolled in the guerrilla armed forces, and, although he's in the army, the Kobanî siege has kept them apart also for two years.
It's night-time, we are at the camp and since the generator is turned on the younger girls take advantage to watch their favorite TV series.
It's an Indian soap opera with all the gender clichés you can imagine.
I was very shocked to see they loved this TV series.
Aryan and I remain outside.
At night the temperature drops and we can relax from the suffocating heat we've had to withstand during the day.
'Have you ever been in love?'
I ask her.
She says yes.
'We joined the army together and we promised each other that we'd fight until the end.'
She doesn't know if they'll meet again, but she doesn't care.
For her, love is not about getting married and having children.
She has channeled her love towards her people and her nation.
'Love cannot be possessive', she says.
'Love has to be great, you have to make it grow, love for only one person can kill love".
With sunrise, we keep moving forward.
If we manage to reach Tel Abyad, we will end the siege that Daesh has set around us.
We advance through ghost villages.
On the roofs of some houses, there are black Islamic State flags.
But the militants are not there.
They have fled, they seem to be retreating.
The following day we keep advancing, we are very close to Tel Abyad.
In the distance, we can see a raised Kurdish flag.
We pull over and Aryan runs to greet her girlfriends.
They haven't seen each other for more than two years.
It's very exciting.
Aryan looks for her brother in the crowd, but can't find him.
Suddenly, the alarm is raised.
Unidentified cars are approaching, and we fear they could be suicide bomb cars.
We all rush into the cars and leave the place.
It was a false alarm, but we had to leave before Aryan could meet her brother.
From Tel Abyad, troops coordinate the triumphant entry into Kobanî.
We're celebrating the end of the siege, shouting, singing, shooting in the air.
When we reach Kobanî, everyone is in the streets.
Finally the two-year has ended.
We drive following a hundred army vehicles, through the city, or what remains of it.
Around us everything's in ruins.
There's not a single house standing.
When we reach the main square, hundreds of people are celebrating victory.
Suddenly, I see a tall, handsome guy coming towards us.
When Aryan sees him, they hug with an intensity, I had only seen in movies.
He is Baran.
The party goes on all day.
The following day I wake up late.
The girls are already up and are getting ready to leave.
I approach Aryan, with a couple of cups of coffee, and tell her: 'So, Aryan, we could go on vacation now; we could go to the Euphrates river, swim, relax...' Aryan laughs.
I get serious and tell her: 'I'm not kidding. It's been more than
a month at the front, we deserve a vacation.'
Aryan looks at me and tells me: 'It never stops for us.
Today we're leaving for the South".
In a couple of hours the pickups are ready and Aryan leaves with the troops.
We say goodbye without fuss: 'See you later comrade Aryan'.
And as I watch her leave, I ask myself whether we'll meet again.
Some weeks ago I got a message from her brother Baran, telling me that Aryan had been severely wounded, that she'd been evacuated in a helicopter, and was having an operation in a hospital in northern Iraq.
They didn't know whether she'd survive.
It took me many days to find out where she was, and when I did I travelled there.
I found her in a terrible state.
When she saw me, she looked at me and smiled.
I had to struggle to hold my emotions at seeing her like that.
She sat up with effort and raised her shirt, to show me her war wounds.
A bullet had gone through her breasts, in through the left breast, and out through the right breast.
Another bullet had wrecked her arm, the one she uses to shoot.
Another bullet had gone in through her buttock, and out through her bellybutton, shattering her sacrum and irreversibly damaging several leg nerves.
I felt a lot of anger.
What world do we live in, that forces a whole generation of women to face death to defend their right to exist?
But Aryan seemed at peace.
'The freedom we fight for' she told me, 'is not a geographical freedom.
The freedom is in ourselves, and once we reach it, nobody can take it away'.
For Aryan, freedom is a path that you can choose to follow or not.
For Aryan, freedom starts in yourself, and has to do with all the conscious decisions we take every day, that connect us with whom we really are, with our essence, and take us closer to our dreams.
Aryan dreams of a world at peace, where women from all over the world can live in equality with men, can express themselves freely, can take decisions in their society.
Aryan knows she might not live to see her dream.
She fights for the women of tomorrow, but the truth is that Aryan has already achieved many things, Aryan has changed the reality of many women around her.
Moreover, her feminist fight, her heroic actions, her sacrifice, is planting the seeds that can change the reality of many women in the Middle East, and can inspire changes to change the reality of women all over the world.
Aryan is already free, and fights for the freedom of all women.
Thank you.
(Applause)
(Voices) (Music) Sometimes, I imagine myself being older and think: How cool!
This is great!
But then, I also remember Mom who lives worried, with no time for anything.
I never see her playing.
With her friends she drinks tea, talk about things that I don't know.
And it's true that I always look at the adults, they complain about work; why don't they go to school for a while?
If I had some, I would spend my money to buy a floating skate and a new boat!
How strange adults are!
I will be way too much cool!
(Music) Look how little adults laugh.
And it's true that sometimes they seem aliens.
They are worried if I eat with my hands.
Or if I speak with my brother in invented languages.
When I get older, I will buy a spaceship to go out for a walk.
Okay, Juana, what's up?
Why don't you say anything?
Being older is a great responsibility.
Living with obligations falling in droves.
Reaching the peak of maturity, intellectual development, accumulation of knowledge.
Well, it's not that cool, Yes, adults are strange, they prefer watching TV over going to the amusement park.
When I grow up, I will not be like that.
I will not regret the time I lost.
How strange adults are!
I will not be tired like them.
I'll fly when I grow up, and when I get older!
(Applause)
In this planet we live in, there is a living being that stands out and dominates the others.
There are thousands of millions of individuals whose activity greatly affects, without a doubt, the lives of all the animals and all the living beings that inhabit this planet.
I'm speaking about bacteria, of course.
They are all over the Earth: in the Antarctic, in the depths of the sea, they are found in forests, in this very theatre, they are in your skin, in your intestines, in everybody's intestines, even in the intestines of astronauts.
There are hundreds and hundreds of different species of bacteria, and all of them live in perfect harmony with the environment they colonise.
Sometimes, eventually, bacteria embark on the colonisation of a new medium, a new habitat, and humanity makes a benefit out of it.
Sometimes. Because some other times they cause a tremendous damage to it.
I'm speaking about infections.
Infections take place when a bacterium colonises our body.
Tuberculosis, for instance.
Tuberculosis is caused by the 'Mycobacterium tuberculosis' bacterium when it colonises our lungs.
The bacterium grows, and gets the food it needs to grow from our cells, destroying our lungs.
See? If you pay attention, the bacterium isn't an evil being, the only thing it wants is to grow, to survive.
The conflict, the problem lies in the fact that we are its food.
Bacteria have caused death to millions and millions of people throughout the history of humankind, and there's no need to look back to the Black Death or leprosy, those pandemics which did wreak havoc in the Middle Ages in Europe.
You only need to think that in the 1920s or 1930s, catching a tuberculosis infection virtually implied a death sentence, and the only possibility to alleviate that infection was isolating those infected in sanatoriums in the mountains, where, on the one hand, people in the city who weren't infected were safe, and, at the same time, those infected had sunlight, fresh air and could get rest, so they could stay strong and fight the infection.
I was born in a village in Murcia called Alhama,
a small village at the bottom of Sierra Espuña.
That sanatorium for tuberculosis patients is in that mountain range.
Luckily, the sanatorium is abandoned and on the verge of collapse, but there was a time when it was a very busy spot to seclude tuberculosis patients and try to get them recover, with a very low success rate, very few could get over the infection.
Today we can open a window and look back in time, we can be there for a moment, because someone took this picture when the sanatorium was still open.
Look what a crowd and so many young people basking in the sun out in the terrace hoping to get better from tuberculosis, oblivious, or maybe resigned, to a more than likely fatal ending, because, as I have previously said, contracting tuberculosis at the time was pretty much a death sentence.
These sanatoriums, like many others, they closed down in the 1950s due to lack of use.
Antibiotics wiped them off the map in what became one of the most important discoveries in the history of humankind.
Antibiotics are molecules produced by bacteria to kill other bacteria that also try to colonise that same niche.
Human beings, thanks to our intelligence, managed to extract and purify them, to use them in our own profit, to cure infections.
Antibiotics meant the revolution of the modern medicine of the 20th century, because, thanks to them, we've managed to carry out extremely complex surgery procedures without risking getting infections during surgery or in postoperative period.
Apart from that, they have truly helped to eradicate pandemics of the world: typhus, cholera, leprosy, tuberculosis, gonorrhoea ...
However, nowadays, we can say antibiotics have been victims of their own success, for we have been using them incorrectly, inadequately, we have been overusing them.
We've included them in every aspect of our lives.
We've used them not only to cure infections, but to prevent the possibility of catching them.
We've produced toothpaste with antibiotics, acne lotion with antibiotics, cough tablets with antibiotics, antibiotics in the food, antibiotics in the water, antibiotics in the rivers, in the farms, in the animals, antibiotics everywhere to protect ourselves, because they worked so well.
But life always finds a way, and bacteria adapt because they want to survive as any other living being, and they evolve, they become resistant to antibiotics.
The so-called antibiotic-resistant or superbug can grow in environments we intentionally poison with antibiotics.
And they are the reason why in the last decades there has been a significant increase in the number of infections.
I'm going to give a close example: in Spain, 30 times as many people die from superbug infections than from car accidents.
And if the problem escalates in the fashion it does now, the World Health Organization predicts that, by 2050, infections caused by superbugs will be the number one cause of death in the world, over many others such as cancer, or cardiovascular illnesses.
"But, where are these dangerous superbugs? I've never heard of them", you may be wondering.
The niches we're most worried about are those in which human beings are vulnerable, such as hospitals.
Hospitals use many antibiotics, of course, and the defences of patients there are low, obviously, because they are ill, and that makes them prone to be infected by resistant bacteria.
Hospital infections are causing that relatively simple surgeries now turn to be really complex and difficult to carry out because of the high risk of contracting an infection which hinders patient's recovery, or may develop into a chronic illness, or even cause death.
Let me introduce you today to the main character of the story: this bacterium I'm showing you is the 'Staphylococcus aureus', a penicillin-resistant bacterium.
It is very common in hospitals, and causes most of the infections there.
In the picture it does not look so dangerous, it's like a bunch of grapes.
The name Staphylococcus comes from Greek, and it means exactly that: bunch of grapes.
It is found naturally in our skin and in our nostrils, and we all have it, and there, in that environment, it is not pathogenic, it even helps us to prevent getting yeast infections.
The problem starts when our skin, which is a barrier, breaks due to surgery or a scalpel, a catheter or just an injection that could let the Staphylococcus aureus in our body and produce an infection.
If it infects our bones, it will produce an osteomyelitis; if that occurs in our lungs that will produce a pneumonia; if it invades our whole body, it will be a generalised sepsis.
And all those infections will be so hard to treat if the strain that infected us is antibiotic-resistant.
What can be done to fight this superbug?
What resources do we have?
There are many laboratories in the world whose sole aim is to understand how bacteria develop their resistance to antibiotics and how can a new strategy be developed to fight and beat them.
I manage one of those laboratories here in Madrid, and we work with penicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus.
So far we knew the Staphylococcus aureus produces that resistance to antibiotics by means of a protein complex which is like the engine of a car.
It has several components that need to be assembled so that the engine can work and protect it, like an armour against a hammer, which are the antibiotics.
We've found out that the assembling of the protein complex, the assembling of that engine takes place in very specific areas of the bacterium's surface.
We've found out that the surface of the bacterium is not regular, it's heterogeneous, with several areas of different composition, just as if it was the skin of a cow, with black and white stains, and the protein complex needs to be located in one of those specific areas in the black stains, let's say, in order to be assembled correctly and be functional.
Then, using molecular biology, we've able to manipulate the bacterium's DNA we've created a mutant whose surface is far more homogeneous and regular than the bacterium's, and we've seen that, in the mutant, the protein complex doesn't find its place and is not assembling correctly so the superbug is no longer that and turns into a conventional bacterium which we can be normally eliminated by using common antibiotics.
From then on, molecular biology shows us the way, and decides the strategy to be followed.
Our combat strategy is to generate a bacterium which is homogeneous in its surface so the complex can't assemble.
We've used compounds that, when added to the cultures, they stop the production of a grease in the bacterium's surface which distorts its surface and makes it more homogeneous.
In other words: these compounds, when presented to the bacterium, make the cow's skin not to be spotted in black and white, but they make the cow totally white.
In that moment, in the presence of the compounds that engine, the protein complex, can't be assembled correctly and loses its functionality.
Interestingly, many of those compounds are statins, the molecules that are used to lower cholesterol in patients who suffer from hypercholesterolemia, high cholesterol.
I'm sure many people in the audience are taking statins, you may also wonder for how long you haven't caught an infection.
A combination of statins and antibiotics, it's able to kill strains resistant to antibiotics which usually we had no clue on how to do it.
Another important aspect of this work is that we hope to recycle antibiotics which were no longer used, that were totally forgotten, because bacteria's resistance to antibiotics had made them useless.
We can increase our resources as statins dismantle the antibiotics' resistance complexes, and once they're dismantled, we can kill the bacteria with common antibiotics.
We've tested our experiments on mice, producing amazing results, and we hope that, in the future, they can become a therapy which helps the human being to protect himself from superbugs in hospitals.
But it is only a battle that we've won in this never-ending war, because, as I've previously said, life always finds its way, and the bacterium will adapt because it needs to survive and it will undoubtedly find a way to avoid that treatment.
We need to evolve with it, and produce more complex procedures that enable us to go on fighting against superbugs.
All together: hospitals, doctors, research centres and us, from our homes, with small gestures, we can make a huge contribution to stop superbugs.
Small gestures such as these: Using antibiotics only under prescription of a doctor, because it may be that our infection is not of a bacterial origin, in this case antibiotics are completely useless.
When prescribed by a doctor, we must finish the treatment, even if we feel well on the first day, we must go on until the end, as an early ending of the treatment makes it weaker and increases the chances of producing superbugs.
Once we've finished the treatment we can't throw antibiotics to the bin nor the toilet as they will end up in the rivers and into the soil, and the bacteria there will develop super-resistance, too.
We must be careful and become aware that undergoing surgery implies a certain risk, we only must do it if necessary.
And last, wash your hands very well and cook your food well to protect yourselves.
By doing all of this, and with the help of hospitals and research laboratories, these are the only tools we have to remain in this race against bacteria and win this battle.
Because, every time bacteria have taken advantage in this race we've been vulnerable to them, and, in the last decades it was us who were leading the race, and had an advantage, and they've been the healthiest decades in the history of humanity.
We must protect that advantage, we must manage well our resources, resources that so much work and effort we invested in.
Thank you.
(Applause)
On my last vacation I went to Barcelona with my mom.
One day, we wanted to go for a picnic in a park.
She had been there before.
So she told me, "We take this subway, we make a connection here, and we get off there."
I didn't get one word!
The only thing I knew about Barcelona was Messi.
So, I grabbed Google Maps and looked for ways to get there.
The first option that showed up was a bus that took half the time.
So I said, "Look, mom, this one goes on a much shorter and faster route."
She looked at it lightly and said: "No, we're going like I said."
I was very upset; very frustrated.
I didn't understand what had happened.
And this is the helplessness I increasingly feel for the last few years.
To have the answer to some things but not being able to use it.
And not because I don't want to but because I'm not allowed to.
Let's look at it this way.
On the one hand, I have free access to information.
So, having an Internet connection, I take out my phone and in the blink of an eye I can know things such as, for example, how fast you should slap a chicken to cook it. (Laughter) Or how to get to a park in an unknown city.
And not only that, I cannot be out of social networks
or not have a cell phone.
Because, sooner or later, I would be out of date and out of everything.
And on the other hand, all this information hits a huge wall: Adults.
For example, a few months ago I wanted to go out cycling for the first time with my friends. And my parents wouldn't let me.
"You're too young,
you still cannot do it."
Too young for what? I don't know.
I already knew how to ride a bike and I had already googled how to get there and come back.
And this is where my head explodes because it goes into a loop.
Having access to so much information I feel pressured, and even pushed, to enter the adult world.
It's like being told: "Well, champ, your teenage years are over."
But they don't give me the right to decide either.
So, that adolescence that was about to end ends up becoming eternal.
I understand that I lack experience.
When my parents didn't let me go out cycling I didn't listen to them and I went with my friends.
While riding our bikes, a dog without a leash crossed our path.
I almost stepped on it.
Of course, I could never have foreseen that.
But I think my lack of experience doesn't justify a, "You cannot because you're a kid," as the only answer.
I think there's a new problem in the relationship with adults.
In the past, information and the right to decide were to be found in one person.
Now, there are times when I have as much or even more information than they do.
And I think that this should give us young people the opportunity to take up a place that we do not have today.
Or give us more freedom in making our own decisions.
Something has to change.
I don't know what these changes will be like.
Although, there are some things that let me quite imagine where things are heading.
For example, when we came back from the Chemistry Olympics, my teacher proposed three classmates and me to help her edit errors in the guide and to invent new exercises, but, besides, the best thing was that she put our names in the guide along with hers.
What teacher would have done something like that before?
And at home, I'm starting to get a different role, too.
My dad, who is a lawyer, this year signed in for a degree in Psychology.
And he had to take Biology, which is not his strong suit.
Now, when he found out that I was doing well in that subject, he told me: "Kid, until I know what a prokaryote is, you won't leave home."
(Laughter) And now, every time we sit to study together, I feel like I'm with another schoolmate.
I'm sure the relationship with adults will change.
You know why?
Because when we went to that park in Barcelona, we went on the subway like my mom said.
But on the way back, we took the bus.
(Applause)
