As an archaeologist, my job mainly consists of looking backwards at the past, trying to understand how people from other times lived, but sometimes it's very useful for me to turn my view forward to the future and observe how we imagine different possible futures.
Something that has always called my attention is how absent Africa is from these future imaginings.
With the exception of things like "Black Panther," Africa never appears in our future imaginings, much less as the protagonist. It appears as often
in science fiction as in other genres.
This isn't an accident, because how we imagine the future is intimately connected to how we remember the past, and sadly, Africa is a continent that has had its history denied.
This denial has reached the point where, as I was studying for my degree, I realized that as an archaeologist, although I had a pretty reasonable understanding of European history and knew some things about the history of Asia, America, and even Oceania, I had a huge gap in my knowledge concerning African history.
More than a gap. My idea of African history was something like this: A ton of things happen at the beginning with the appearance of the first hominids and then Homo sapiens, and then comes colonialism.
And I've realized that it wasn't just me - this is a very common idea.
Once in a while someone remembers that Egypt is in Africa, but with or without pyramids, this is the basic idea that most of us have of African history.
There are many problems with this framework, as you can imagine.
The first is that, between this and that, there were three hundred thousand years.
300,000, with five zeros.
The second problem, linked to the first, is that this idea of Africa as a primitive continent, static, without civilizations, where nothing ever happens in a type of eternal present, consisting of huts and famines, completely isolated from world's development until the Europeans arrived and connected them is completely false.
Africa not only has a rich history of civilizations, states, empires, innovation, social and technological development, but also, at many points in its history, it has been very far ahead of Europe.
I would like to give you some examples:
Starting with the oldest, Africa isn't just the continent where we first appeared as a species, it's also the one where we first see some of the behaviors that define us as humans.
About 100,000 years ago, in a cave in the south of South Africa, someone drew these zigzags on ocher rocks, like this.
It might seem like a small thing, but it's not, because it's the first sample we have of symbolic, abstract thinking in our history.
And it's also not an isolated case. In the same cave some time later, we have the first adornment in the history of humanity - this shell necklace, which shows that our vanity has long-standing roots.
These objects may seem trivial, but they show a series of cognitive and social processes that hadn't existed until then in our history,
and on none of the other continents already colonized by humanity - things which would take tens of thousands of years to happen there.
Continuing with objects of humble appearance but revolutionary implications, we have ceramics.
It seems a little odd to call a piece of cooked clay something revolutionary, but it is, because it allowed us to cook and eat things like tubers and grains that until then weren't a source of food, exponentially increasing the food that could be produced and that people could eat.
In Africa, the first pottery was made in 10,000 BC in Ghana
and shortly after in Mali, Egypt, and Niger.
In this case, it's not the world's first - there are older ceramics in Asia, China, and Japan more specifically - but it's much earlier than Europe. We took around 4,000 years to discover and invent it.
From small but important objects to big important questions, Africa also played a fundamental role in large-scale elements such as city planning and architecture.
The example of pharaonic Egypt with its monumental temples and tombs is probably one of the most spectacular and the most well-known, but it is far from being unique.
South of the Sahara, in present-day Mauritania, is the Tichitt-Walata civilization, which in 2,000 BC had settlements
of more than 80 hectares, controlled a territory of 250,000 square kilometers, and had very advanced metallurgy.
This was a time when Rome hadn't even been founded, and in Europe, we didn't have anything even remotely that size.
Later, a little further south in present-day Nigeria, there was the city of Benin, founded in the 6th century. Here it is in this 17th century painting by the Dutch traveler Dapper.
Benin, through centuries of existence - it still exists today - developed a system of walls, more than 16,000 kilometers long, connecting more than 500 settlements over an area of 250,000 square kilometers.
These walls are the second biggest human structure in history, only behind the Great Wall of China.
It's not that they were lengthy but short, no.
Around the central part of the royal palace, they had an average height of 17.4 meters.
The royal palace was decorated with these brass plaques that represented characters, architecture, and historical and mythical scenes.
And at night, the palace was illuminated by these six meter tall lamps that worked as streetlights with palm oil.
The city of Benin was so complex and so vast that when the first Portuguese travelers arrived in the 15th century, they were astonished - it was immensely bigger and more sophisticated than Lisbon at the time.
The cases of Benin and the Tichitt-Walata aren't the only examples - there are many, many more.
I could talk of the gigantic walls of Great Zimbabwe that cover an area of more than 100 hectares. To give you an idea, one hectare is about the size of a soccer field.
It's the only archaeological site that has given its name to a country.
Or the more than 300 pyramids in Sudan, which has many more pyramids than Egypt.
There's also the Djenné Mosque in Mali, the largest clay building in the world, or the Lalibela churches in Ethiopia that are dug out of solid rock - this isn't built, it's emptied.
They were created in the 13th and 14th centuries for Christian pilgrimage.
I could continue. There are countless examples. But instead of that, I'd like to dismantle another myth associated with Africa: the idea that it is an isolated continent.
I present to you one of the oldest and longest commercial routes in the history of the world.
From at least the first century of our era, and probably before, the Swahili merchants on the east coast of Africa were at the center of networks that connected the interior of their continent with the coast, Arabia, India, China, and all of southeastern Asia.
These were African merchants who lived in huge houses made out of coral rock in port cities like Kilwa Kisiwani, considered in the 14th century to be one of the most beautiful cities in the world.
It was described that way by the Arab traveler Ibn Battuta, someone who had spent 30 years traveling the known world through Asia, Europe and Africa, so he knew what he was talking about.
As a result of these commercial routes, we find some very curious things, like an African giraffe in the 15th-century Chinese royal court.
Or the Kilwa Kisiwani mosque itself that was decorated with Chinese porcelain vaults along with many objects from India, West Africa, and many other parts of the continent.
I think that, as you can see, this idea of Africa as a primitive and isolated continent doesn't make any sense.
And what I've shown you are just a few examples, but there are hundreds more.
The fact that this fascinating history isn't better known isn't by chance, nor is it trivial.
It's the result of a conscious project of erasure linked to European colonization that denied Africa its history to more easily justify their supposed civilizing mission and the exploitation of the people and their resources.
In recent decades, we have made great advances in our academic understanding of African history, but unfortunately this information has not reached the public sphere, where we continue to deny the past of the continent that, paradoxically, has the most history.
The result of this is an unbalanced and incorrect understanding of human history where we give Europe an undeserved central role, and we ignore milestones of development in human societies, simply because they're located on the wrong continent.
Even worse, when we deny an entire continent's history, when we refuse to listen to its voice, when we don't recognize its contributions and innovations or recognize it as the protagonist of human history that it is, we are also limiting its role in our imaginings of the future.
Africa has as much history in its past as it has potential for its future, and it's time for us to recognize both the one and the other.
Thank you very much.
(Applause) (Cheers)
I wanted to talk about art with you today, especially, about how the Internet is -- and how easy it is to share, spread and create content in it -- on how it is testing the role of artists and the concept of art itself.
Specifically, I will compare four artistic movements of the 20th century with the present.
Let's start with a little historical comparison: we have two pictures here, one of them is an American shepherd conducting a religious mass in Detroit so that the car industry starts flourishing again, in the context of the crisis of 2008.
A crisis that we are still living and that comes paired with a democratic crisis; repetitions of elections all the time, topics such as Brexit -- And we can compare this easily with an issue from 90 years ago, with the other picture that we have, in the context of the Crash of 1929. We were going through a great economic crisis, and a similar democratic crisis, between two world wars.
One of the factors also present here is the introduction of two new ways of communication; specifically, cinema and photography. And what matters to us in this talk is that they have altered the way in which artists are producing their work.
The first ones who learned these new techniques were the Dadaists.
We have in the picture a photomontage criticising Hitler, you can see that they have put some coins in him; the artist is John Hearfield, and he is exploring new montage techniques.
Dadaist collages are also recognisable; for them, it is not necessary to go to art school and spend some years learning how to paint as Velázquez for years; just by grabbing some magazines, paper, glue and scissors, we can create our own composition, and this is also art.
But this is actually a degradation of the concept of art itself because, as I said, it is not necessary to know how to paint exquisitely.
Surrealists carry this idea to the whole conception of society, degrading the entire political spectrum.
Specifically, they say that surrealism is inevitably associated with the word "revolution" and that they are experts in the revolt and want to systematise the confusion about the world of reality.
In the picture, we have a surrealist who is supposedly insulting a priest.
We can see that he is trying to degrade this catholic moral construct, prevailing at that time; the picture is from 1926.
If we go forward in time, we have the case of the Situationists, who take this revolutionary spirit from Surrealism and carry it to the 60s.
That is a time when we already have the cinema, which was homogenising the entire western society, and we also have television, which is being introduced at that time in every American and European home.
We can see here some kind of proto-meme that says: "Tonight, the show is at home".
Guy Debord, leader of the Situationists, talks about the Society of the Spectacle, how it is unidirectional and how it comes from authorities to the people, with the latter being unable to answer.
He wants to break this media construct so people can express themselves.
This idea of breaking this media construct is carried out overall by a movement born in 1970 that lasts until today, which is Culture Jamming.
One of its more basic forms is, as we can see, altering publicity, changing the message so that people can have a critical spirit.
Another thing they did, specifically one of the most famous culture jammers, Joey Skaggs, was creating fake news.
He has dedicated all his life, since he started in 1976, to creating media hoaxes that the media swallowed as true, and gave to the citizenship as true, making a construct from reality that was truly fake, because it never happened.
The interesting thing about Culture Jamming is that it lasts until today and, in 1993, a cultural critic, Mark Dery, theorizes about it.
By that year, there was already an incipient Internet in the USA, and Mark Dery asks himself in his jammer manifest if the Internet will be able to break the media oligopoly, giving voice to cyberpunk journalists, as he called them, so that they can publish their own news without them being mediated by a business, such as a newspaper.
And he also asks himself if there will be nodules in the future, he named them like that, where every person will be able to express themselves, which is an obvious comparison with social media.
Now, let's get to the next phase: let's compare what we have seen with the present.
First, fake news.
It goes without saying that we are in a completely over informed world, and a lot of that information is fake.
Fake news breaks this media construct that Mark Dery and the Situationists wanted to break, but they do it in a pernicious way because they actually generate an environment of political tension.
There is so much information that every person can choose what best suits how they see the world, and they strengthen those ideas and keep drifting away from reality believing that those on the opposite side are bad people, or that they are not people at all.
All of you are aware of the hatred that is spread on the Internet.
We also see how those nodules that Mark Dery talked about, which are social media, are already in action.
In this case, we have a critic made by McDonald's users on this occasion because it promoted an American celebration.
Situationists lamented in the 50s that automatic writing invented by Surrealists had become a brainstorming for companies, and that this acted as a vaccine against the revolutionary virus because workers now felt they were heard and, therefore, would not protest.
In this case, we can say something like this: considering that people can already criticise McDonald's on the Internet, that feeling of complaint is already solved, so there is no need now to go to McDonald's to protest.
The interesting thing about these messages is that they are not really revolutionary.
Here we have some examples showing how some people worship Alcampo.
This revolutionary spirit that started with Situationists, culture jammers, is completely canceled.
Furthermore, being able to have a close relationship with companies, one-to-one makes unnecessary to write a letter starting with "Dear Madam or Sir", it does not matter if you write spelling mistakes because companies will always answer with good manners, always with a smiley face.
That close contact also acts as a vaccine against this revolutionary virus.
Let's go back into our revision.
Here we have a video of a lady live-streaming her morning fitness routine.
We have seen in the Society of Spectacle, in Situationists, that they wanted to break this unidirectionality.
It is already broken in these examples because now everyone can live stream whatever they want.
The problem is they are not broadcasting revolutionary content or deliberation, but their own day by day routine.
They reproduce the same content that media shared before; we had "Jane Fonda's Workout" before and now we have "X's Workout".
We cannot talk about a Society of the Spectacle, but a Society of the Micro-spectacle, in parallel.
Surrealists, as I mentioned before, talked about systematising the confusion about the world of reality.
This meme here is an original, the rest that I will show from now on have been taken mainly from Facebook -- And what it is breaking is the media oligopoly because since Francoism the main mass media have not given a voice, in these 40 years of democracy, to the most Francoist movement that has been there.
But the Internet has provided them with an opportunity to come to light so they can express themselves; they also demand freedom of speech.
The problem, obviously, is that, again, this is not a revolutionary message, but counterrevolutionary.
We demonstrate once again the naivety of these revolutionary movements from the 20th century.
Collage.
We have already seen the Dadaist collage.
Here we have a collage with the PP logo, and the former President of the Government, Mariano Rajoy.
Where the image with Hitler and the coins had an openly critical spirit, on this occasion, it is absolutely flattering. We can see he is surrounded by positive images: nature, a virgin, Spain's flag --
There is no revolutionary spirit here, but a strengthening of power.
There are memes for every taste.
These new art forms should be called "post-art". They require a new name because there is no artistic elite sending a message; now everybody can do it.
So, we have to give it a name, and it is "post-art".
One of its most interesting characteristics is that aesthetics does not define the quality of a work of art, but ideology does.
I will agree with this meme if I support Podemos, but I will not like it at all if I support the PP.
The same happens with the meme we saw before.
We have talked about degradation when we saw this picture of Hitler or the television screen showing a message; we were degrading the symbol represented.
This is a meme made for the elections that took place on July 26th, 2016, in which we can see a PayPal logo.
Obviously, it should be a logo for the PP, but the person who did it made a mistake and did not put the PP logo.
Does this have a critical spirit?
No, it is just a mistake. Therefore, this puts in question this emphasis that culture jammers put in altering communication, as in Marlboro's advertisements.
Do we really have the ability to analyse this?
Do we ask ourselves any questions?
I believe that, on the face of it, it is hard to find out.
In 1935, Walter Benjamin established three forms of ritual functions in art.
The first one: if we see a painting of a virgin in a church, the first ritual function is the religious one; we go to that church to pray to that virgin.
In this meme I present to you, the first ritual function -- post-art also has ritual functions -- would be the person that goes to the website where it was uploaded and sees it.
The second ritual function, according to Walter Benjamin, came with Illustration, when the bourgeoisie saw an artistic emphasis in those pieces.
They took works of art from the church and put them in museums so they could contemplate them in a different way; there is no religious spirit now, only an artistic one.
In this case, this work of post-art which I have curated for you is part of a museum function, so to speak.
The third ritual function of an art piece comes with its technical reproduction.
Once photography was invented, we could take a picture of that frame of that virgin and put it in our house, being in possession of that work of art.
Memes, obviously, every time you look at them in any device, you are actually possessing a copy of that piece on your mobile or computer.
What is interesting about post-art is that it establishes a fourth ritual function, and that is: considering that everybody has access to the same tools, they can relate to each other by making post-art pieces.
Before that, we could see a movie, but the viewer was never at the same level as to make a film.
Velázquez could paint a frame, but I do not know how to paint like him.
However, now I can have a meme like this one on-screen and a user can put her face over it, vindicating it somehow, and, furthermore, if she posts it on Facebook, she will receive comments such as "Good morning, you look so pretty, congratulations --" Inside of everything that we have seen, where is the artist's function?
My proposal is that art nowadays, or the artist who wants to do something, do not make a lot of sense, considering that these days there are a lot of people producing things.
So, where does making a pretentiously artistic production fit?
I think it is irrelevant.
Nowadays, an artist should have to curate all this post-art avalanche to select the most relevant pieces that could have a historical weight.
I would like to finish with a reflection on world wars that we have seen in the beginning.
Marshall McLuhan said in 1970 that we would have to face a World War III where there would be no distinction between civil and military, and that was going to be a war based on communication guerrillas.
We can see here, as well as in previous examples, that Franco is surrounded by a heart, trying to showcase his most friendly figure on the Internet.
So, are we already inside of this communication war?
We are already in it.
We have seen how it has started, what we do not know is how it will end.
Thank you so much.
(Applause)
The world faces a crucial challenge, how to generate and use clean and renewable energies? How do we efficiently harvest solar, wind and sea waves energies?
To power the electricity grid and cars it's necessary to replace the use of the polluting fossil fuels, responsible for global warming; we'll be running out of them over time.
In Argentina, we are in a good position.
That we have great natural resources, you heard of it in school to death already, that we have the most fertile soils, oil, gas and coal, as well.
But, we also have great potential for the development of renewable energies.
We have powerful winds and sea waves in Patagonia and at the other end, in the Puna, near where I live solar radiation is one of the highest on Earth.
However, renewable energies have the peculiarity of being intermittent; the sun doesn't shine at night, the wind only blows when it feels like it and the sea is often quiet.
Does this mean that it's not possible to live exclusively on renewable energies?
That during the day or when there is wind and there are waves is all right, but the rest of the time it's necessary to burn oil?
That is an option.
Another option is that the excess of energy that we capture, for example, when the sun is at the top, we store it to use it at night or when there is no wind or no waves.
How do we store energy?
Energy can be accumulated in batteries that are much larger than cellphone batteries, but essentially work with the same technology.
To harvest renewable energies without the ability to store them, condemn us unfailingly to be used only to cover a marginal part of a country's power demand.
And if we don't have good batteries we won't have efficient electric vehicles either.
I live and work in Jujuy, because there I'm very close to one of the great resources of our country.
Together with our neighbors in Chile and Bolivia we take part of the Lithium Triangle.
In this region you will find 80 percent of the world's deposits of lithium in brine which are the richest deposits and the easier to exploit.
And it turns out that lithium is one of the fundamental raw material for manufacturing rechargeable batteries.
Last year, the industry of batteries consumed 38 percent of the total lithium produced in the world and estimates show that the demand will increase 10 percent annually.
How do I get to lithium?
I was born and grew up in the Buenos Aires and I lived there until I was 29. And I can proudly say that I am the outcome of public, secular and free education -- (Applause) where I studied from kindergarten until my PhD.
Then I worked in France, in Australia, in Belgium for more than seven years.
In the year 2013, Dr. Ernesto Calvo who was director of my doctoral thesis calls me and tells me that in Argentina we have a lot of lithium, and that there was little development regarding that subject. And he tells me "we have to create the Balseiro of lithium in Jujuy," like the prestigious Balseiro Institute in Bariloche, one of the best in the world in research in physics, a place to develop state-of-the-art science and technology on lithium.
Someone was needed with enough audacity to settle in Jujuy and start to shape this idea.
And I let myself be convinced.
I think what weighed the most in this decision was the possibility of doing science, which I'm passionate about, but that the results are not only visible by publishing scientific papers in specialized journals -- those read by about 200 people, at best -- yet by having more reach within society.
So I came back, not to Buenos Aires, but to Jujuy.
Back to lithium, we come to the issue of mining.
It would be a very serious contradiction that while we are extracting lithium to make batteries for cars and renewable energies we are polluting the environment in the neighboring areas of the deposits.
Lithium is found dissolved in something like deep underground lakes of terribly salty water.
There are about 30 of these deposits in the lithium triangle and they always lay below salt marshes.
This water is 10 times saltier than sea water and that's why we call it brine, like the pickles' brine.
Lithium mining consists of essentially pumping the brine from its underground location and dump it in large pools out in the open.
The brine gets slowly concentrated by the action of the wind and the sun.
That is, the water evaporates and the salts remain.
Lithium mining has been questioned for an intensive use of water.
To produce a ton of lithium carbonate it's necessary to evaporate approximately half a million of liters of brine.
And here a yet unsolved question comes up, because the brine as is renders useless, we can't drink it or use it for irrigation because of its very high salinity.
So, is there any harmful side effect for gradually emptying these deposits?
In addition, current technology only extracts lithium, all the other components of the brine are discarded as waste which, while not toxic, occupies lots of space.
Our goal is to develop new ways of extracting lithium that will reduce to the minimum the environmental impact.
We are young and we still don't have conclusive results but we are working on many new ideas and I want to tell you about some of our projects.
On the one hand, we work on ideas that could be considered patches to current technology, non-total solutions, yet applicable in the short term.
What you see in these photos is called "simple solar still".
it's a device that's been around for over a hundred years and purifies water by distilling it by direct action of the sun in this structure that looks like a glasshouse.
Together with colleagues from Salta and a local mining company we study if it's possible to somehow adapt this structure to cover the evaporation pools and try to extract by condensation, the water that currently evaporates.
The second example is a disruptive technology, which implies a radical change, potentially applicable to the long run.
It's a methodology for sequential recovery of all brine components without evaporating water, in a way to transform what is today a large volume of waste, into pure compounds of commercial value; potassium salts, magnesium salts, sodium salts, borates and lithium.
The most interesting thing is that if we take from the brine most of its components, we produce low salinity water as a by-product of lithium mining.
In all our work there's common topic: try to recover the water that currently evaporates and transform it into fresh water.
If at least a part of the water brine that now evaporates could be recovered, lands that are currently unproductive could become growing areas because local agriculture is possible only through intensive irrigation.
We also study high capacity batteries that could make an electric vehicle have the same autonomy and the same cost as a vehicle running on fossil fuel, something that doesn't happen today. And we also do synthesis of lithium compounds of high added value.
In order to add value to raw materials and to extract them sustainably, knowledge is required and also having the physical means to put the ideas into practice.
Like in lots of other aspects, in science, Argentina is also a terribly centralized country.
However, I believe that it's possible to do cutting-edge science and technology in a place like Jujuy and that the local people are going to have most interest in taking care that the extraction of the raw materials is done in a sustainable way, and that nobody will understand better than them about developing science and technology that creates local employment.
If scientific activity consists of trying to understand the world and look for solutions for the advance of human society, it's necessary that you can do science in every corner of the country.
(Applause) Today, with a very big effort of the National University of Jujuy and the help of colleagues from Córdoba, La Plata and Buenos Aires we are working on setting up the Balseiro of lithium in Jujuy, where a large group of local youngsters work on topics related to lithium in final projects of their engineering careers, their doctorates and postdoctoral studies.
And for us to do cutting-edge science and technology and forming young people is really the same task.
Exactly two months ago, our new house was inaugurated: a building of 3300 square meters.
(Applause) Who will harvest the economic benefits from its developments?, this you may wonder.
That takes us directly to the question: who finances research in science and technology in Argentina?
Like many other labs, we juggle to live on the State grants we receive and we aim to patent our developments through CONICET.
However, a patent is only the first step.
To take laboratory processes to the industrial scale requires more funds, and in order to try and get them we contacted several companies.
Unfortunately, in Argentina there is very little tradition of giving economic support to developments that are born in public organizations.
Some companies answered us that they don't want to finance risk investments, they want more advanced results.
And it's likely that not all projects we are working on will end up being feasible or profitable, but to know which of our many ideas will be the two or three that will bear the best fruit it's necessary to invest, to know what works and what doesn't.
For now, the best results we have got with SMEs of Argentinian capital, and this is not coincidental.
For example, a family SME with small mining properties came knocking on our door to ask if any of our extractive technologies was ready because a SME doesn't have the capital required to invest in an extractive plant with the current technology.
We also have to mention that most of the salt flats rich in lithium have already been granted, and here we have to say that there are some small or large contradictions in our work such as the absence of laws that guarantee the provision to the Argentinian market of raw materials that are currently exploited by foreign companies. Plus, the absence of laws that exhort mining companies to test extractive technology with potentially less environmental impact.
Despite this messy situation of laws and funds scarcity we continue to work without losing sight of our fundamental goals, such as the creation of a science and technology center in Jujuy and the consequent training in cutting-edge technologies to the local youth.
We believe that it's possible to make cutting-edge developments, to add value to our raw materials, and doing this from the outlying areas. And above all things, my strongest bet is to do all this work side by side with bright young people born in the north of our country.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Have you ever lost something and when you're looking for it you end up finding something even more important ?
This happened to me when I was 16, and was a key moment in my life.
Since you're all here, I'll tell you about it.
I was playing with my friends in the school playground, and the ball got stuck on the roof. And since I've always been acrobatic, I went up on the roof to find it.
Do you know what I found there?
I found a magnifying glass as big as my hand.
I was convinced this magnifying glass was waiting for me there.
I knew straight away what I would do with it: I wanted to build a telescope.
You'll wander: Where did this 16 year old get the idea to build a telescope?
Well, it came to me a few years before when I read a short book by Soviet publishers 'MIR' which showed the constellations, and more importantly, how to see them through a small telescope.
With this book and my friend Juanfri we'd go to the edge of town, and with the bicycles headlights
we'd read the information in the book. I remember that was the first time I identified the North Star and the Big Dipper in the sky.
I think it was this moment, - with the book - that first sparked my interest in astronomy, or perhaps later at 16 when I started messing around with magnifying glasses.
So you can imagine how my imagination ignited when I found the magnifying glass on the roof. With it in my hands, I instantly knew what the telescope would be like.
This telescope was a plastic pipe with the magnifying glass at the end.
The shafts were plumbing pipes,
and the tripod was made from an old wash basin, which I salvaged from my Grandma's things.
For the welding I used super glue, for nuts and bolts I used wire and tape - bound very tightly!
Do you know how long the telescope lasted?
As long as the super glue and tape lasted.
The magnifying glass fell to the floor and broke into a thousand pieces.
You can't imagine my thrill when I built that telescope.
It was endless.
As endless as the number of questions going around my head as to how I could improve it.
The telescope allowed me to see the moon like never before,
even though I'd already been messing around with curved glass which I would fill with water to make images.
But it was this providential magnifying glass from the school roof which allowed me to build this compact functional unit.
In my family, I became known as the magnifying glass handyman, and a cousin of mine from France, who knew about my hobby, came to my town on holiday, and brought a book with him - a very well-read book with a blue cover which showed a wooden telescope in white.
Its title: “La construction du telescope d'amateur” by Jean Texereau.
I'm not going to translate because it's obvious.
(Laughter) This book excited me so much, these poor-quality images in black and white, the hand-drawn diagrams, so beautiful, exquisite, the formulas, equations, data which I didn't understand, but were simple in the end.
Everything was explained in the book, and so I decided to polish up a 20 cm mirror and build a wooden telescope like the one in the book.
It was all sorted.
But I never started this telescope.
Compulsory military service was awaiting me instead, - many of you won't be familiar with this concept - and I had to leave town.
After my period of service, I became immersed in adult life, in all these different occupational constellations from which you earn your living.
The first of these: bookseller
I set up a small bookshop in town which never took off. I expanded into stationary, school things and that didn't work out either, so I started selling toys and in the end the bookshop ended up a video shop.
(Laughter) Yes, that place where movies used to be before they all went onto the internet.
I then became a painter-decorator,
a job I did for 9 years, and which wasn't totally alien to me as from an early age I would copy my brother and I learnt how to paint pictures.
And just like that, the world of colour and creativity opened up to me, and has been a big part of my life ever since. Until one day, almost without meaning to, this world lead me to what is my current profession which I've been doing for the last 20 years - graphic designer.
And all of this after so much career-shifting, being unemployed and things not going your way. Today this is called reinventing yourself.
(Laughter) I reinvented myself bit by bit, until I finally decided at 35 to take my childhood dream - the one I abandoned when I was 18 - and make it a reality by building my own telescope.
And do you know what I did?
I bought an incredible 30 cm mirror and I started building a telescope made out of wood.
When it was working thereabouts, I decided to build a metal one.
I chose metal because I believed then that a real telescope should be made of iron, that it should be solid and sturdy.
But I had no idea where to start - I'd never worked with metal before, and thought it an unworkable and dangerous material.
Those sparks that fly from the saw when you cut through iron, it's enough to put anyone off! (Laughter)
and what I can I say about the flashes from welding, - your eyes shrivel up like raisins - and there is really nothing that relieves the stinging.
But I learnt how to work metal by watching the experts, mainly a blacksmith called Julian who was very skilled with iron,
and Vicente who would cut me parts using his lathe and milling machine.
I also remember that at that time I spent a vast amount of time on the internet troubleshooting, trying to see how other people had solved problems similar to mine.
I also knew that I'd have to investigate as many avenues as possible to find those nuggets of information that would let me take the next step towards achieving my dream.
Since I live in a small town, it was easy to find suitable materials, mainly from the scrapyard in the town of Puebla de Almoradiel.
It's a magnificent scrapyard which could give those big city scrapyards a run for their money any day.
(Laughter) You can find anything and everything in this scrapyard: You can find archaic tractors, crushed cars, battered chippers, completely obselete machinery. Everything, except telescopes.
(Laughter) You end up scaling these mountains of metal, mentally dismantling the jumbles of scrap metal, trying to find a shaft, cogs or bearing which could be of use.
I can assure you that the first builds I made with these materials were truly monstrous, and earnt me a well-deserved reputation as the 'odd-ball' of the workshop.
I carried on practicing with these materials however, until I had enough experience to fully plan out the telescope I had so longed for.
I'd build it with the 30 cm mirror I had bought, and it would be completely made of iron weighing 500 kg.
But of course 500 kg is not easy to transport from A to B, in your car, like an everyday object.
So I decided I'd have to build a small building that could contain it
and protect it. (Laughter) A small building from which I could observe... I think you'll have guessed that I build an observatory! So now we'll move from the 'Metal Age'
into the 'Cement Age'.
(Laughter) I now had to try my hand at brick laying.
On the 3rd of May, 1999, I, the handy-man (Laughter) had the idea of taking apart an old shed of the family's, and with a pickaxe and shovel, I laid the foundations, built the supporting structure and walls.
In October of that year it was finished and painted, as you're seeing now.
Looking back at that summer it was truly wonderful, so full of promise and excitement.
But that summer was also tough, with long days requiring immense effort I had never dreamt of.
If I had been aware of the difficulties I might never have started.
And don't even mention the astronomical aches and pains I endured!
(Laughter) So you have the building, the telescope, But now for the difficult part: How on earth do you build a dome? Moreover, how on earth do you build
one that has openings so the telescope can stick out.
So I started again.
I started experimenting with materials, seeing how they bent. asking everyone around me for advice, until 2-3 months later it was finished.
I remember the year 1999 as the year I studied a super intensive course in astrobrickwork.
(Laughter) Of course there were people who helped out.
Above all my family, who patiently witnessed how this far-fetched idea of mine progressed. I also had Julian the blacksmith's help, who shared his equipment and knowledge aside from his straightforward and friendly manner
which to me is priceless.
From 1999 onwards the years flew by at an astounding speed, I built more domes, more telescopes, and more instruments, until I reached the point were the observatory was almost finished, and I saw the need to extent the scope of the project, further than just individual use.
So in 2007, together with Nacho, I created the AstroHita Foundation, and shortly after Leonor and Fernando joined, who is an engineer, Isidro who helped with the administrative side and Droy, our dear Droy who developed the software for all the instruments and equipment we developed in the observatory.
Thanks to them my little observatory became a true Astronomy Complex: The Hita Astronomy Complex where people study and carry out scientific research today.
We are currently collaborating with the Andalusian Institute of Astrophysics, and the research projects carried out by Professor Jose Luis Órtiz, and also with the University of Huelva in research carried out by Professor Jose María Madiedo.
I would like to thank them both for putting their trust in us. It is against this backdrop of success and achievement at the observatory, that the Makemake breakthrough came along in 2011.
You'll be wondering: who or what is Makemake?
It's a dwarf planet at the very edge of our solar system, whose closest relative is Pluto, but is a quarter the size.
Makemake made history for our little observatory in how, for the first time, we could obtain data accurate enough to be used in the scientific community.
This data was used by the head investigator to make a prediction about an astronomical phenomenon which prompted 16 telescopes around the world to also start investigating. 2 of these were VLT telescopes with 8 metre mirrors in Chile, 2 of the biggest in the world.
The prediction and investigation were a success and lead the investigator to publish an article in the prestigious journal NATURE.
This was the first time our observatory appeared in this journal.
In 2010 we joined the SMART project which detects shooting stars and meteorites - all those masses which disintegrate in Earth's atmosphere -.
In 2012, we designated a telescope to the MIDAS project - which detects impacts on the moon - and functions in parallel with 5 other telescopes in Andalusia.
In 2013 the MIDAS project detected the greatest impact on record directly on the surface of the moon.
When the investigator published the results, NASA showed interest in the location of the impact.
And the spacecraft orbitting the moon - the LRO - photographed the new crater, a beautiful crater which is still seen today adorning the moon's surface.
In spite of all this, I am still a graphic designer which I how I earn my living, and my partners in crime Leonor and Fernando, similarly have their own jobs outside of the Hita Observatory.
We aim to sustain and fund the observatory through educational activities and not by personal means.
As everything we'd done for the observatory had been with our effort, enthusiasm, dedication and an astronomical number of man-hours.
To this day we've never had any substantial economic support, and we always say when we marvel at all that we have built and achieved: 'we did all this with no financial help, can you imagine if we had money ?'.
What would it have been like with financial backing?
because we were never short of ideas, projects nor enthusiasm.
One of the most important things in the making of the observatory has been sharing it with professionals, craftsmen, researchers, friends, all united by a passion for the cosmos, which in reality is no different from a passion to drive knowledge forwards the passion of knowledge.
And also, to another end, it is the passion to realise your dreams, in my case, a dream I have wanted to fulfill since childhood.
It is inevitable that a process like this, over so many years, will see you make many false starts before you reach your ultimate goal, as this is part of the continuous process of trial and error.
But in spite of all the wrong turnings, the best decision I ever made was to build an observatory, as it became more than just a hobby, and gave meaning to my very existence.
Ultimately, it became a life-long project, a life project.
You must agree with me that a life projectless, without goals to achieve would be a sad one, am I right?
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
He is Agustín. Three years ago he came with his family as they usually do because we are good friends.
At that time, Agus was 3 or 4 years old.
And he was already diagnosed with ASD: Autism Spectrum Disorder.
That night, we were dining with his parents, and someone notices that Agus was sitting in the living room, playing with a tablet.
Tablets were a new device, not too familiar.
At first, we were surprised because when he comes home, even today, he usually walks around. It's very difficult to have him seated with something for so long.
Then I asked my children if anyone had told him how to turn on the tablet, how to open the applications; it is not easy at all, how to play with it.
The answer was no.
No one had said anything.
,That night ended, I remember, with his mother Laura saying: "We have to buy Agustín this tablet!"
Because he doesn't interact this quickly with any of the toys he has.
That wasn't just another night.
I didn't realize this at that time.
I began to realize it later, over time.
At that point, an idea that I had began to take shape.
The idea was to use technology to help in people's lives.
I had been working in software development for more than 20 years.
And this was something I wanted to do, something I felt the need to do.
Later on, I have the luck to be part of a talk given by parents of children with Down Syndrome.
among other things, what they mentioned, is that many parents were surprised with the ease their children used all those touch devices, like tablets and smartphones.
Something they said didn't happened with traditional computers, those with the keyboard, the mouse, the screen.
Somehow they were confirming what I had seen with Agustín a few months before.
So, from this potential they were seeing in the kids, they suggested a group of development companies, among which was mine, create applications for tablets designed for children with disabilities.
I remember the morning that day, I came back to the office and I said, "Yes, of course.
This is what I want to do. I love this."
In that moment, somehow,
I took as mine the challenge they were presenting to us and I began.
"I began" is a way of saying, because at that time I knew little on disability, almost nothing.
And on top of that, my company wasn't developing for tablets.
Truth is that I wasn't sure how to begin.
But what I did know, what I had clear, was that I wanted to change the way in which we were teaching children.
Especially children with disabilities.
And do so through technology.
And one more thing, not only for our country, but for all Spanish-speaking countries.
Because I had already done some research, and I hadn't found anything comprehensive developed in this area.
As the project started more people joined in, especially people experienced in technology and disability.
And what happened was that I found myself coordinating a group of people from schools, from universities, NGOs and from software development companies, which at least was what I knew best.
And there was something very nice.
I remember that some of the companies had 4 people, 10 people.
But we also had some with more than 3000 employees, multinationals.
And all of them on a voluntary basis, ad honorem.
And that still happens today, three and a half years later.
Up to now we have developed several applications.
I remember that when we started, in the first meetings, we said, "Well, what characteristics must applications have?"
To be used by all children, everybody.
But with particular emphasis on those who have disabilities.
One of the first we defined was that applications should use images.
Images, photos, or very realistic drawings that would be non-aggressive for children.
Do you know why?
Because in general children with disabilities are very literal.
So if they see in an application, a little car with two eyes and one mouth in the hood, they go out and seek the car with the two eyes and the mouth in the hood. And as they don't find it,
for them is not a car,
because it is not the same they were taught. That's why images or photos are very important.
Another thing I remember, we started to observe the children, how they interacted with the tablets, how they played.
And we realized that when they made a mistake, they stopped using the application, for 30 seconds, for 1 minute.
And, in general, that has to do with their little tolerance for failure.
So we designed that if they make mistakes,
we pretend that nothing happened. In contrast, surely you know or even have interacted with, for example, a child who has Down Syndrome.
Then you know that they are very effusive, they hug and kiss you in no time.
Well, we tried to replicate that in the applications.
So when they nail something, no matter how small it might be, what do we do?
We put an applause, music, a character that greets them.
And something nice happened, we started doing that and eventually we realized that when we celebrate their success, they want to keep playing, to keep learning with the tablets.
There are many children who never get to read and write, they will hardly be able to use the applications.
But those who do, the first style they learn is block letters.
In particular, block capitals.
And often it is the only writing style they learn.
That is what we use in the applications.
And one more thing: big letters.
At the design level it may be not that good, but it's big for those who have issues with visual impairment.
This is one of the applications we developed in the beginning.
It's called Tuli.
The idea of the application is to work with emotions, a very important subject, especially for children with autism.
We were going to test it, we had just released a first version.
We're going to test it with a kid who has Down Syndrome.
The idea was to pick the emotion Tuli feels, in this case being in the park, and drag it to the other side.
He would pick it, select the option - correct, "Tuli is happy" - then drag it, but as he has fine motor disabilities what was happening?
He couldn't reach the other end, so he dropped it.
And when he dropped it, - this happens with 99% of applications you download on a tablet - when you drop the object, the object returns to the place where it came from.
So he would never get to the other side of the tablet.
Obviously from this experience, we designed that when you drop the object, the object stays in place so you can drag and drop it in all the steps you need.
But do you realize?
He knew what he had to do, not only he knew what he had to do, he said,"Tuli is happy in the park."
Yes, you're right, he is happy.

Well, drag it. He couldn't.
He would never be able to drag it to the other end because when the application was designed, no one thought about his disability.
And like this feature, there are many others, I dare say, that surely go unnoticed by most of you. Because that's what happened to me.
Three years ago, I had no idea of this.
But for them, and not only them, for their parents, therapists, teachers who are taking care; for the children it's the difference between being able to use or not use them.
Between learning with tablets or not.
I mentioned before, today we have about 15 applications already developed.
You can download them all at no cost on the tablets.
Today they are already being used in schools, and in some therapies as well.
And now we're performing a series of more rigorous studies to demonstrate what teachers tell us.
And it is that kids learn faster with the tablets.
But even nicer than that, they learn with more motivation, they are always more motivated when they learn with tablets.
For many of us who work in the project, - I tell you my case in particular - today I dedicate a lot of time to it, time which I would otherwise devote to my rest, or my company.
But I realized that doing what I like, what I love, which in my case is to develop software, it's what I know about; I do my bit to change the lives of some people.
When you see a person with aphasia, because of serious brain damage, it's very hard for them to communicate.
In fact, they do through scribbles, through drawings.
Then, with an application that we made, we transform the tablet into something similar to the magic board that we used when we were kids, and that allows them to bring a prerecorded conversation through drawings in the tablet and then sit down with the person they want to communicate with.
And before, it could take an hour to say what they wanted to talk about, today it takes less than 10 minutes.
Well, that's changing their daily life.
Or now that we just released a version of an application designed for money management, for handling notes.
And with it, not only a kid, but a teenager with Down Syndrome, can go to the supermarket to buy the groceries and ask the application what bills can they pay with, or what bills can they receive as change.
Well, that allows them to be more independent.
He and his parents.
Because their parents are caring that their child does things on their own.
The project has one more impact, which to be quite honest, I never thought, we never imagined it could happen, but now we love it.
And it has to do with the degree of commitment of the programmers who design the applications.
They are young people around 20 and 30 years old.
And what they tell us is that once they went through the project, once they saw applications with the children, once they tested them, from now on, they will incorporate all these features to the applications they develop in their working lives.
And that's fantastic.
It's great because that is what has to become normal over time.
That has to happen.
A few weeks ago, I was now with my family in Agustín's house.
Suddenly, my oldest daughter, Cande, had his tablet in her hands.
And she says, surprised but happy, "Look, dad, Agus has the application
with the bear. That one you made."
To which Agus' father asks, "Oh, did you do this, Pablo?
Because it was recommended by Agus' therapist, that we use it with him."
At that moment I felt that while all this for me, had begun because of him, the story didn't end there.
Because there are more and more boys and girls who use tablets and applications in schools to learn in a different way.
Watch them.
(Video) (End of video) (Applause) Thank you.
(Applause)
[David Martínez] It all started 40 years ago.
It was Friday 17th October 1975.
That day, I was sick of being in front of the same landscape and decided to explore, even if it was a month ahead of time.
From that moment, a seed began growing inside me.
Not of a plant, but rather something that had moved humanity throughout history.
I'm talking about curiosity, that curiosity to explore, to know, to ask questions and find answers about the world we do know and the world we don't know.
Here you can see an image of two dots fusing together: a great dot, an ovule, the largest cell in the human body, if we're talkng about that of females, and a small dot, a spermatozoon, that when connect and fuse give rise to a living being.
[José Martorell] In my case, my story with dots is a bit different.
When I was little, I never used dots when writing.
To me it was silly, a waste of time.
One good day, my dear professor Querubina called me to ask: "Why don't you use full stops?"
I asked him "Why?"
He told me: "It's very important.
When you use full stops in a phrase, you let people know where to stop and breathe."
The last thing you'd want is for your work to be read, right?
I learned at that moment that the dot had a relationship with stopping, reflecting, and breathing.
This is our personal, little story about dots.
But there is a great story, about capital letters, that's also about a very special dot.
[D.M] The Big Bang, the primordial
dot - the big explosion - from which history began to form, has happened, is happening, and will happen, and brings us to this pale blue coloured dot in a corner of a faraway galaxy called the Milky Way.
There we can find our planet, Earth, the only planet, up to now, known to have living dots.
[J.M.] And in this planet
there's a very special place.
We've taken a snapshot of it.
Do you recognise it?
No?
Well, maybe a bit of perspective is needed ... perspective in science is important, so give us a couple of steps backwards and ... Voilà!
The Prince Felipe Science Museum, the place where David and I work everyday, and where we try to get all its visitors to connect the dots. Why?
To discover absolutely everything.
[D.M.] Let's take a look.

Just a moment ago you saw a drawing of a person who, connecting three dots three dots, for example, are the vertices of a triangle, and in the case of Pythagoras he used the three dots of a right-angled triangle to express a theorem, so well-known by everyone: "In a triangle with one right angle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths the other two sides".
It has solved so many problems.
[J.M.] Or in 1831, Charles Darwin,
in a special vessel called HMS Beagle, undertook an incredible journey.
And that's to that journey, he got to develop one of the most famous theories in science.
The theory of evolution, which explains how live evolves, connecting dots to one another, connecting, in this case, species.
But dots outside of us.
And the dots that are inside of us?
In our brains there are some very special dots called neurones, and here you have someone who revolutionised neuroscience called Santiago Ramón y Cajal, the great titan of nueroscience who explained how our nervous system works by unraveling the dots.
He said: "Neural synapses are not continuous, but they are separated",
and that revolutionised our way by explaining how the brain works, the brain where ideas are generated.
[D.M.] Ideas such as those that came
to Nikola Tesa's neurons.
Plus, today we're hosting this chat thanks to him, because there's electricity, right?
You can see us lit up.
And it all started with a dream, a dream that became reality, because since the moment in which Nikola Tesla harnessed the fall of water at Niagra with an alternating current, cities began to connect to one another by dots, and the world began to change.
Nikola Tesa, an engineer, physicist, inventor, but above all curious about both the known and unknown, unjustly forgotten, and who worked for the good of humanity as a whole with an ideal maximum: "Provide infinite electric power and without cables"
[J.M.] Cables ... hmm ... A good word.
Can we transmit ideas through cables?
You'll say, "No", but take note, these two visionaries, Alfred Vail and Samuel Morse, said, in 1831, that it was possible.
And it was possible using dots and dashes: Morse code.
Thanks to this code, people can communicate with one another across distances of kilometres and with complete accuracy and precision.
But we can go one step further, and utilise a dot to a great extent.
That's exactly what Charles Barbier and Louis Braille thought when they used only six dots, making them stand up in 3D form, as they designed a new language:
Braille. Thanks to which any blind person can see the world.
"Give me a place to stand", they seemed to think, with Archimedes' permission, "and I will move the world".
[D.M.] Joining dots lets us think big,
so big that we can create a universe of ideas.
And there's somebody who did this masterfully: it was Albert Einstein.
His way of joining dots started a revolution in physics, joining mass and energy, or creating a new way of understanding the universe, uniting space, time, and gravity.
But what's more, he established the basis for developing lasers. Lasers that let us speak of beauty, the beauty in which scientists look at the world.
As Richard Feynman explains in his poem about beauty in which he speaks of a conversation with an artist friend.
For example, beauty is found in white light breaking down to form a rainbow.
You just have to know how to see it.
Which gives me an idea ...
What is we make a rainbow here?

[J.M.] Could we make one here?
What do you think?
Could we?
Do you think science can do it?
(Audience: Yes!)
[D.M.] What's needed to make a rainbow?
A light source behind us and rain in front.
Here I see a lot of water, but we're missing a light source.
Let's see how we can solve this.
You see, we have a light source that's a laser, a special white light laser that's super continuous made by a Valencian high-tech company, which lets us create a rainbow right here because this laser has all the colours of visible light.
The only thing we have to do is break it down.
Right-angled triangles that appear right before our eyes as if by magic.
Species such as ours evolving until what we are today.
Cities illuminated at night.
Ideas arising from the interaction between your dendrites.
Lasers traveling the confines of the universe.
Do you realise everything we can do by joining the dots?
[J.M.] It's incredible.
And we're just demonstrating
a few small examples.
There are many more in the history of science, where dots connect and we make great discoveries.
But now, we want to share a little professional secret with you.
How do we connect the dots using the curiosity of the Museum?
Well, for example, using a full stop and a comma, you can see here.
When we use this comma, curiosity, so that people can get over any difficulty so that they can imagine, discover and find their passion by all that surrounds them.
Here you have a good instance of how it's done in our museum.
Or, a times, with a new paragraph.
We lost sight of our visitors over the years, but now we see scientists, investigators or car salesmen. What's the difference?
The important thing is that they learned to connect the dots in order to see reality in another way.
Or with the points in a row.
Look, in our museum we try to get everyone who enters the building to discover reality and ask questions, and to be as curious as possible. As you can see, they're asking them in this big plasma ball.
[D.M.] Or suspensive dots,
because the possibilities are infinite.
Because when we manage to transmit our passion with such wonderful people, by connecting the dots, we awaken their curiosity.
That curiosity I spoke of in my personal story a the beginning of the talk, since we shouldn't forget that today's curiosity is tomorrow's science.
Awaken it and make it a reality.

[D.M. and J.M.] Thank you very much.

(Applause)
Once, some high school kids told me: "If we see a guy with a cap, we cross the street."
On another occasion I heard a lady saying: "I only take care of my husband and my children."
And a student --one with a cap-- told me one day: "Stuck-ups surely screw people up to get what they have."
How can we learn to live together if we are, act or think so differently?
How can we meet and agree amidst diversity?
Can we understand that we are responsible for building the society we want?
Today, I want to talk to you about the project we developed 20 years ago, that we call Orchestra-School.
Because we do put together orchestras, but we create them as a school of life.
This project is experienced today by children, young people and adults from all of Argentina.
But we put this methodology in practice for the first time in Chascomús, a beautiful city in Buenos Aires Province which, due to this program was declared national capital of children and youth orchestras.
We work on three dimensions at a time: the individual, the family and the community.
We integrate them to make music together.
But it is a way of life useful at school, the workplace, the club, with friends, with the neighborhood.
To see what it is about, let's put together an orchestra right now.
The first thing is to bring on the kids.
And for that you have to go look for them.
Nobody can love what they don't know.
That's why there are people who are not able to tell what they want.
I'm going to introduce you to Jade, one of my first students, and already a wonderful multiplying teacher, who will do the work we do every day: To make others fall in love with the Orchestra-School project, people who never imagined studying these instruments.
Welcome Jade.
(Applause)
What a surprise! Jade will look for two members of the audience who are going to become members of our Orchestra-School at the Colon Theater.
Many times, in order to include others it's not enough to open the doors so that they come, even if it's for free.
That's why we go out looking for them with our car-- which is already in pieces-- or with what we can but always full of instruments, we go to community clubs, feeding centers, houses, parks, and different schools, making up 80 percent of our vacancy with different social sectors.
Also, when we create an orchestra-school, we offer an open enrollment to cover 20 percent of the remaining vacancy with children and families who choose today music education.
So we look for a true social integration.
Who takes the first step to take part in the orchestra-school of the Colon Theater of Buenos Aires?
Raise your hand.
How many brave people!
Let's see?
You lady, welcome.
It's good now.
Jade, do you explain to her?
What is happening now between Jade and our three new cellists is a tailored lesson.
In this space we work on the individual dimension.
And the key is the observation.
We appreciate each person as a unique individual, with many talents to cultivate.
This moment is revealing.
It's the moment when we believe so so much in our students so that they can believe in themselves.
Of course, the guarantors of the process and the result, are we, the adults in charge.
Our challenge as teachers is to discover how to best serve each person; the famous "equality".
For that, sometimes, 99 percent of our work happens before getting to the lectern, before you can start a process.
Or this is simply reflected in the choice of a musical piece that has to do with affinities, with talents.
But all this, of course, has to enable our work on what we want them to learn for their personal and collective development.
In the tailored lessons, some of our students are for the first time observed by an adult to find their own voice, express it, take care of it and enjoy it, as a cherished treasure, and as a treasure to share.
As one student told me one day, "my music is something nobody can take away from me."
Because if we don't find our own voice, if we don't know ourselves, it's very difficult that we can get along well with others.
When they come to the orchestra, the kids are integrated to a family of instruments.
There they learn to build experiences within a group they belong to.
We will put together the cellos family.
Welcome the cellos.
Hi Felipe, Alina, (Applause) Nataly, (Applause) Agustina and Jorgelina. (Applause) Meet your friends.
Look at this beautiful family of cellos.
To work the second lesson on the family dimension, the other lesson we propose at the orchestra-school is the row workshop. It's where the violins, the percussion or the cellos grow together, like in this case.
The family of instruments is the first meeting space between mates.
It's where we acquire that transversal knowledge that is so much on demand today and will be tomorrow, like teamwork.
It is where we strengthen our identity.
It's where my voice interacts and integrates with others different to mine.
But with the common ground in this space we build our voice.
What are we missing?
Learning to live in community uniting our personal voice to that of our family in a broader whole that challenges us to grow with others who are different to us.
Then, an orchestra can become in a place of promotion where we learn to be the best version of ourselves, as a part of a diverse community.
For us, excellence is a quest.
A quest that deserves to be pursued in the present of each and everyone.
This methodology, as well, allows for kids to become an important part of the orchestra from the very beginning.
That's why our musical works are bridged to be multilevel.
That means that kids who have a cello for the first time in their hands can be part of the same orchestra together with others who have an advanced technical level.
The important thing is that they are building together, participating collaboratively like the kids have shown us.
(Music of cellos) (Applause) In Chascomús alone today, more than 7000 kids have already been part of the orchestra.
Kids who dreamed and today work on their life projects; who formed beautiful families and today, as parents, bring their children.
Some are professional musicians.
Others, in addition, are wonderful multiplying teachers.
And we are no longer just an orchestra but a network throughout the country, where we learn to coexist in harmony.
We enjoy our diversity, our diverse world.
And where everyone knows that diversity is much more fun than everybody sounding the same.
(Ode to Joy) (Ode to Joy ends) (Applause)
I want to talk about the sexual revolution in the context of the Cuban Revolution.
The emancipatory process of the Revolution brought about important changes that started a process of erosion of power relations,
of the power relations inherited from the European colonial systems but also from patriarchal cultures that already existed within the African populations that formed part of our history, of our lives.
But these power relations that were being eroded are still being eroded.
I see this, for example, in relations between social classes.
Above all, this tremendous impulse for unbelievable change throughout the 60's, not even the people themselves really knew it was happening but it enthused them so much that they joined in and their lives were greatly enriched.
And I notice it particularly in the attitudes of men towards women; It wasn't the end of sexism but it was changed.
I speak of the sexism seen and experienced amongst both women and men.
Because it's expressed through these relationships and these identities,
As well as in other ways that we're gradually becoming aware of.
But also in the imbalance of power of white people over black people.
I prefer to speak of colour because we're all descended from Africa.
Also in relations between the generations.
And for me a very special example is the literacy campaign.
The youth of that time were so inspired by the young men and woman who had succeeded in bringing about the Revolution.
There was no longer anyone to stop teenagers and young people who wanted their lives to count for something, who wanted to play their part in what was happening.
And in that literacy campaign even after the murders of young literacy teachers more teenagers and young people wanted to take part.
I wouldn't have wanted to be a mother in those days because I would have been terrified thinking that my teenage daughters and sons would go places far from our home where they were in real danger.
So, that's an example, but the changes that have occurred since then and in the life stories of Cuban women are really momentous in that they've brought about these changes in our society.
Changes such as since 1959 women have started to earn the same salary as men, an ongoing issue even in many developed countries where the women still earn less than the men for equal work in the same profession or trade.
But also, in 1965 abortion became available as a safe and free service through the national health system
Even today, the fact that abortion remains illegal, forces many women to choose between prison and death throughout much of the world.
This was the starting point for development of a policy and programme for sex education that in many ways has been instrumental in creating the conditions for this process of sexual revolution.
What I mean is, the Revolution brought about many changes and these changes continue the process of revolution.
And this is what's happening in more recent years with this work that the National Centre for Sex Education has promoted together with other State bodies and community organisations to highlight in the Cuban population other forms of discrimination, other forms of power that some people have over others that even now the Revolution hasn't fully succeeded in transforming.
It's true that in the 60's, 70's and 80's there was still, for example, a strong institutionalised homophobia but this also came from the deeply held ideology learned from our ancestors.
Transforming it was difficult.
And there was no understanding, as far as I can see, that these forms of discrimination were incompatible with the transformational process of the Revolution.
So what was needed was the words, the discourse, the language and the knowledge, to try to raise awareness, and to educate our population.
I can't say that we've succeeded but we're getting there.
People are waking up to it.
And I see in the Cuban population throughout all the provinces some reactions that show that the Cuban people are very aware of campaigns and projects about social justice.
And this is what we're trying to do: generate awareness so that we can change the culture.
setting aside the old culture of power relations expressed through discrimination, social exclusion, segregation, and move towards new ideas, towards new projects, new ways of doing things that we're inventing, we're creating, inspired by a liberating paradigm for humanity and society.
With this basis our humble but passionate work is to increase education, raise awareness, generate a sense of responsibility and solidarity between the people.
Also regarding issues that historically were used as reasons and mechanisms of social control like power between sexes.
Something as simple as the sexes.
And from this starting point have emerged many other ideas in which we also have to continue working.
But sex, that was considered so sinful in former times and that now we recognise as an important part of our lives since without sex there's no life and in this way we can make life much more pleasant, happy, cheerful, responsible, loving and supportive.
And this is our objective: sex education in Cuba, to teach both a sense of freedom and a sense of responsibility.
To offer knowledge and understanding to the various generations that form our society, our population, so that they're liberated,
so they can draw their own conclusions without being manipulated by others.
That's what we're doing.
It's freedom that we want to develop through these efforts.
So thank you for being here, for listening to what we have to say. and of course I invite you to engage with your ideas and discussions and with your proposals for moving forward in a society still undergoing revolution.
Many thanks.
(Applause)
These are my potatoes and on behalf of them, I want to welcome you.
Here, in the picture, we have two symbolic things: the erakas and tuni.
And you will wonder what they are.
The erakas are the mother potatoes, the potatoes that can be read at the crop, and they will predict a new crop.
And the tuni type is a potato that was threatened by extinction, and still, some tuni varieties growing at the Quebrada de Humahuaca, in Jujuy province are at risk of extinction.
I will tell you about the path that captured me, and what the potatoes taught me along this path.
These erakas have taught me about the rural and scientific knowledge, both taking part in a dialogue.
And, so, I'll tell you where these potatoes are recreated: at the markets, in the exchanges, in the affection, in the love of the farmers that exchange the potatoes, not as an economic unit, but as a factor of solidarity, of affection, of reciprocity.
At these potato fairs of the farmers I found a diversity... Do you know that there are almost 84 varieties of potatoes in Argentina?
And more than 3,000 in the world?
These potatoes are at the risk to become extinct.
And when a corn or potato variety dies out, it's not only a cultivation that is lost, but also a whole ancient knowledge of usage and consumption throughout history.
This knowledge is not about where or how it's grown, it is about the medicinal properties, cooking qualities, nutritional properties.
And, in this path led by the potatoes, when I started to rescue them I found that they had many friends, cousins, relatives, like the ocas potatoes, the ollucos, the colored corns.
There, the potatoes, taught me that it's possible to build in the diversity because they all live together, without losing their individuality.
And they also taught me that each one has a call.
Each potato has its call and is useful for some special dish.
Each potato is also made for a carnival celebration or Easter.
When I started to save these potatoes - I'm an agricultural engineer - I finished University and began saving them with a scientific vocation and vision.
I picked them up, put them in labeled bags. And came to do the first photographic mapping to take them out.
I took pictures five times, a series of pictures, of those potatoes.
I didn't get any photos.
They were blurred, I only got half of them.
And I told my father - heir to a culture, eighteenth generation of cacique Viltipoco - I told him, "Dad, I didn't get the photos." And my father said,
"Have you asked them for permission?"
And I, at that moment, went, "But what are you telling me?
Have I studied for 6 years to ask the potatoes for permission?"
And as he saw I doubted at that time, he said, "Well, if you don't believe, don't do it."
And my culture came back and I said, "No, no, no."
And I smoked them, put flowers and said, "Dear potatoes, you know I want to rescue you, and that I don't want this for you, but for all that there is behind you."
And in that path, I put the best I had from my history, but I also understood the knowledge I had from university, had to take part in the dialogue.
And that I didn't have to lose the affection and culture I had with all I have learned from education.
So, in this journey with the potatoes, we decided to come to Buenos Aires.
We left with some farmers - for us the potatoes mean culture, life, tradition - and there we set ourselves to come here, to the supermarket. From there they left with the name "perlas andinas" - Andean pearls - and we got here and they told us, "No, Andean pearls has no meaning for the market.
They will be called "Kechuas Potatoes."
Because the name in English has a different glamour, another... Imagine we came, sort of bringing Viltipoco in each potato, to appear with the name "Kechuas Potatoes"?
(Laughter) It really was a hard, whole new learning of marketing and commercialization, but we said, "Well, the potatoes also helped us to understand that there must be a dialogue between different knowledge, and different logic."
And what was the use of coming to Buenos Aires?
To see that Andean pearls or Kechuas Potatoes, set as the Andean potatoes in the heart of many pleasures and palates of Buenos Aires people, without losing the identity and logic of being the Andean potatoes.
Potatoes that are, for example, in the heart of Martina.
Martina lives in Sianso, and is a farmer who has 60 potato varieties, 32 corn varieties and cultivates and works the land; see her joy.
I've also been taught that you can succeed in the place without losing self-esteem, without losing our culture, and conquer the big city.
(Applause) The potatoes carry the yacon with them.
The yacon is an Andean root that was preserved by only 5 families, and from these 5 families it recovered.
Today, yacon is a crop with properties that are good for those people who can't eat sugar.
It has a type of sugar called oligofructan, that is good for those who have problems eating complex carbohydrates.
And now they have set a cooperative, where farmers and their families process there in Barcena, Jujuy, yacon products.
And yacon, as it is, through an organization called Slow Food, became an international bastion, and it's one of the two bastions of Argentina.
What did it teach me there?
That even from the most isolated and small place we can be successful and come through, loving who we are, setting our aim, without forgetting our customs, our traditions.
This is Mother Earth for us.
And now, I will talk of another passion I have.
(Applause) These potatoes also taught me that they will only be recreated as long as we use them and eat them.
Biodiversity only depends on us, to the extent we consume the resources we have and put them on the table.
And from there, I would like to say that I want our young people to keep living in the place they are, with the resources we have, with our culture, but also having opportunities.
And, thus, from the land, the sowing, the woman, the fertility, the place where the farmer sows... we could get to the supermarket but also to the hearts of our agricultural systems where our own products are born and recreated.
In this context, we created the Technical Degree in Regional Gastronomies and Food Culture.
This degree educates young people, farmers' children, where we teach, through cuisine, the value of our regional gastronomy, and the value of all the Prehispanic cooking techniques, and the present cooking techniques.
Do you know that if we take part in a cooking course today, all the foreign techniques are valid, but if we participate with a stone and wood we are not eligible?
We are going to fight so our gastronomy, the one that is present in each place of our loved Argentina, can be valued, and from there we can project our different roots.
The message I want to give you and the one the potatoes gave me is the following: You can build out of diversity.
You can, by keeping your identity, caress the world; as the potatoes caress it.
You can create opportunities.
You can be successful in the place without losing what we have, the passion for our origin and the projection to the world.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
It gives me great satisfaction and a deep feeling of responsibility to speak at this auditorium because twice I visited the foyer; and I was confused, I didn't know if I was in Street G or at university, but it made me happy to see so many young people here.
So above all, to you, I dedicate these words that I'm going to read.
It is very difficult for a writer to achieve such a mission of how to offset the uncertainty, the unease or the horror the planet lives in at this time.
It's not a mission of the writer because he is not a teacher, you don't have to do educational work, but you must leave a trace of encouragement in the readership.
And that is what I have wanted to do with my modest work.
When the Pope came here to La Habana, here to Cuba, he said that we were living the third world war in stages.
And I believe that he isn't wrong, seeing what happened yesterday in Paris.
You will have seen how much innocent blood was spilled there by acts of vandalism and terrorists.
But the writer has to, in some way give it life, as I said, to the reader, and in some way also raise the self-esteem of their characters.
And I have dedicated, and I said it here, almost all my life to save the life of those that have not had a story, let's say in the books, an epic story, but have had an important life.
And about this I'm going to speak briefly.
Now, one knows that the word was the first and the spoken word followed in its steps, it was its successor.
It settled in the human species to stay.
Orality gave rise to language.
And the spoken or written language is the cornerstone of literature.
As I am not an underwater diver but a writer, let me read you this small text in which I will try to expose my hypothesis of how to get to provide a little daily happiness to the beings we live with.
I have dedicated a great part of my life to the rescue of people without a story.
This seems a motto or an absolute statement, but it isn't.
After the light, is the shadow, as the Japanese say.
In the shadow there is also light and that hidden, relegated light, is what I want to enlighten my work with.
It's the light of those without a voice whose stories, however, marked milestones in the transformation of the Cuban society.
To that I have dedicated some texts that I rated as non-fiction novels, creating a syntagmatic binomial that would define my purpose as a creator.
What have I tried to reveal?
Very simply, the flow of life that in silence and with stoicism and modesty but with unblemished and deep rooted knowledge has contributed to the definition of the national identity and to the idiosyncrasy of a people, the Cuban people.
Not only the social anthropology and its original approaches, but the Cuban ideology of ethical tradition and its social projections brought me a vision that my predecessors did not have and of which I appropriated, eagerly desiring for justice and obsessed with understanding the key secrets of my country, Cuba.
The deep Cuba became an obsession for me.
Then the work of survey and search began, in the interstices of the social fabric that had been before my eyes and that I couldn't see with clarity.
With the certainty that all lives matter, even the most hidden and apparently muted, I found to my characters: slaves, Cimarrons, Gallegos that arrived to the island in espadrilles and corduroy suits, choristers of a vernacular stigmatised theatre, Cuban emigrants that after flamboyant adventures sought economic well-being in the cities of the North, innocent little maids who fell in love with elegant and foppish men, super made-up transvestites, icons of the most tender and at the same time grotesque, scandalous aunts fleeing the bourgeois sanctimony who smeared themselves with extract of poppy in the underarms to go out clean.
I sought above all the mysterious warp of the language, not like caricature but like living nature.
More than creating fiction or inventing fantastic, glittering worlds, what I have done and do is to recreate a tradition that could die.
To act as a resonator of these voices, or as a reflector that will illuminate inner folds, dark zones, making appear with luminosity figures that had been drowned in the opaque.
Some people collects old stamps exotic birds, Etruscan coins --
I have collected foreign lives, that feel weightless, even in the wallets, but they do reveal the microhistory.
Convinced as I am that only the writer can save the collective memory against the avalanche of technology, the invasion of the robots, or the oblivion of knowledge and the traditions that help us to sustain the rigging of man.
I am not a pure writer.
I am something as a hybrid of falcon and turtle.
I see from the top of the elusive summits and I purge in the most common of the land to collect the sap and clear the roads of waste and manure.
I do not aspire to categorical definitions nor offer social solutions.
The social solutions, well, social solutions are the matter of politicians.
The only thing I want is to show the heart that beats with force in the human body, defying the tedium and inertia.
I am not interested in the nice type, imaginative and roisterer neither, with respect to my admirers Truman Capote and Norman Mailer, the multiple crimes as in the novel "In Cold Blood," that I translated by the way.
Neither interested in the left-wing vindicative waves in the work of Norman Mailer, for example.
I'm interested in penetrating the collective psyche, for my characters to display in all their smallness or greatness.
Both are legitimate ends of the species to which I belong and they do not support half-measures.
I aspire to present a world turned upside down, to rescue a radical language and return to the human beings their purest essence.
I believe that with this, and I have tested it, I return also a little of the kidnapped happiness and the self-esteem lost in vicissitudes of social differences, discrimination and contempt.
Thus, I offer myself as a writer in sweet and speedy servitude.
If something I pursue, is that we all as a great strong family can achieve the happiness of calling ouselves Cuban, Latin American, or simply inhabitants of this turbulent planet that each day loses more of its needed equilibrium.
So from here, from this amazing and visceral Island, we can push a country, this small country, towards a destiny of greater happiness.
I am the one that walks round there pushing a country.
It is not a fantasy, it is true.
I have spent my life pushing a country.
With large stones in the way and my giant shoes, I have been gradually pushing a country.
Against the big winds and the night that squeaks on its hinges, against the lack of oxygen and the bad omens, I've done the unspeakable to push a country.
But there are many other things to do, like loving in the dark without walls, certainly.
Or to shell the daily rice with flavour of beetles.
Or filing nails in front of a quicksilver mirror.
Or to play ball with cockeyed children in the neighbourhood.
So forgive me if I do not hear the complaints of my contemporaries.
I cannot do anything else but to continue pushing a country.
Thank you very much.
(Applause)

Hello! How are you?
Good evening, good morning.
Have you had lunch yet? Not yet.

We can say good morning then.
Ok, one time, or rather many times, I've been asked, even by myself What does a clown do in an oil industrial city like this?
(Laughter) Well, after thinking a little bit, the answer may be: The same thing any other clown does in any other city that is not industrial or of any other type.
We still have the question then, what does a clown do in any other part of the world with any other activities of any other type?
We make people laugh, and that's where my talk would end.
(Laughter) Are my 18 minutes over? No?
Not yet?
Ok, then let's find out
some of the other things clowns can do and I have a special one, for which I need to go to the beginning of everything.
So let's call the first speaker so that we start all over again.
No, we'd better go back to the beginning of humanity.
I must state here, at this moment, that I'm a Darwinist, a Darwinian, Darwinous, a fan of Darwin, let's say, I don't believe any of the other theories nor have I faith in others. I strictly believe in evolution.
And I also think like many others, that it appeared in Africa because it's the place - humanity appears in Africa because it's the place where the appropriate conditions can be found: there's much heat and humidity, what's more, there are a lot of bugs, it's said we come from bugs.
A bug that crept, one that jumped, one that swam, one that flew, another one that - a monkey, and there we were.
So we're going to place ourselves in that first day, if there was one.
So, how was it? Poc, poc, poc, a man?
Poc, poc, poc, or they were still monkeys, until at one point, the monkey realized he could think, he became different from the other monkeys and he started having different attitudes or maybe he said, "I'm thinking and they're not".
(Laughs) No, because he didn't speak, he didn't know languages yet, He didn't have a tongue. He did have a tongue, but not the ability to speak and things like that.
So, until they get it, they're called Homo Sapiens.
It comes from sapiens, sapiens, sapiensa, piensa, ['think' in Spanish]
It seems they were named by an Argentinian, they use to speak back to front, in lunfardo, This part will be difficult for foreigners.
So, the homo sapiens.
Let's place ourselves in a hut, no, not a hut yet, almost a warren, Let's imagine that's how men began so long ago in the centre of Africa.
For this, I will need... Well, we have this that they've brought, but I brought another imaginary projector.
As you can see here, we have a planisphere. I will move it a little and as you can see, it's circular. Here it is Africa, we keep moving it, Here it is South America, which is in a way like Africa, but upside down.
I keep spinning the planisphere, and here we can find Australia, back again in Africa, and here it is again.
Let's place ourselves in Africa.
Here it is, its round part, the Rock of Gibraltar, here next to Spain, with a problem, mm. Then Africa, the Red Sea, a turn here, another round part, there it is, the centre of Africa.
That's where the humanity appears, according to some theories.
And there we locate Juan Carlos and Susana in a warren. Both really calm, enjoying life, because they had nothing else to do.
Nature provided them with everything, the ideal temperature for food to grow on the lands, to pass by flying or running.
Juan Carlos and Susana, who were very fit, because they had to walk, run, there were no means of transport or escalators.
So, there they were. One day Juan Carlos says, [Unclear].
Translation: "I will get some chow" he says. Or food to eat.
[Unclear]. she says, and he leaves.
When he comes back with the food, he sees some kind of movement inside of what is his lair, and he realizes there's a guy in there.
Another guy.
When he approaches, she sees her partner, and she says, [Unclear]. Which means, "It's not what it seems".
(Laughing) The guy in question, who had burst into the place, looks at her, then looks at him, and keeps silent.
Then the "owner" of the house, of the place, says: [Unintelligible speech]. (Laughter) He says "Neh, Neh". Go. They guy doesn't accept him
as having authority, he looks at the wife, I mean the one that was there, who wasn't his,
and she says "Igh". Yes.
So, "Neh". "Igh" she says.
He looks at them both. "Bour". No. "Neh".
"Igh".
"Bour".
"Neh".
"Igh".
"Bour".
Neh - Igh - Bour, Neh-Igh-Bour, neighbour.
(Laughter) And that's how the word neighbour appears.
There was still no tongue... (Applause) and the guy just took a paper and said, "I'd better write it down for a future idea, in case a language develops, I already have a word to identify the guy living there right?
That was taken from the other talk.
So the situation gets complicated, the neighbour not only wanted to keep his partner, but also with his food, his place, and he believed too that the food was his, and the space, and the partner.
The thing of possessing and having and not sharing.
Then what he did was take the woman's hand, and said "Keep the food".
[Unintelligible speech]. And he went somewhere else and made camp there, where he couldn't see the ne-igh-bour.
But this ne-igh-bour had another neighbour and he had another, even the neighbour was neighbour of a neighbour and we're all neighbours of a neighbour.
They started leaving and that's how it started, the movement of humanity around the world, escaping the neighbour.
It wasn't curiosity or investigation, what made men mobilize around the planet, but selfishness, the pleasure of having things for themselves.
So, there they were. They left. Some of them to South Africa, and they bumped into the sea. They went West and they bump into what for us is East A part in which there is a very interesting beach in this part of Africa.
Some meet on the other side and they start going to the Sahara Desert going round cause of the heat on foot
Imagine to be on foot. And they start going round and they get to this place where the Red Sea is. There they say, "Look, we can go to there to that sort of sea in the middle of the lands like a Mediterranean Sea"
Well, they decide to pass through a river that they hadn't noticed at the beginning. Later, a generation is born there, a very important civilisation of a piramidal formation
So there, some decided to go this way Here is the Rock of Gibraltar. Here, this is Spain, the Italian boot, and the whole Mediterranean Sea and they're standing here, at the edge of the Red Sea.
Some go that way, some others will go, I don't know, to Iran... Some others went at this place.
And there they start feeling the climatic conditions, which were different, it's a bit colder, in fact, there were found in this part of Africa Skeletons of couples sleeping back to back.
And in the part that starts to be cold, it was found the first skeleton "spooning". It is cold also what makes one sleep with the leg over the other person.
So some start going that way and others to the European area.
What we know today as Europe, up here.
They crossed the Alps, the Pyrenees, they discovered ice, coming from Africa, from its centre, discovering ice, insane.
And big neighbourhoods are created, full of neighbours, lots of neighbours, and they keep moving until it's inconvenient because Europe is almost like Chubut and Santa Cruz, it's like this.
They reached the limits quickly, there were no more roads so they decided to build houses, buildings, castles, spending their time inside and never saw the sun again and that's why they're white.
(Laughs) For being so much time inside.
In Africa, for being in the sun, at 40 degrees, they end up black imagine wearing a swimsuit all day a "penekini", so fashionable nowadays, and they're all black from the heat.
Besides, it was 200,000 years ago and all this movement took like 60,000 years. I imagine they ended up white.
According to the neighbour that came first, there were different ways of speaking, the Galician or the Spanish, they speak in a way that is so... because they had to cross the Pyrenees and all that, they had to be guys with a really great purpose.
Here, apparently, a guy with a short tongue frenulum settled in the centre of Europe and then created a whole civilisation too.
Then they went North, I will spin it this way, and here is where the vikings lived.
A lot of them looked that way, and I was told that the Empire of the Sun is over there.
They talk about the Empire of the Sun, and they start crossing what is now Russia, escaping the neighbour. It wasn't about curiosity, it was about escaping the neighbour.
And when they crossed Russia, at dawn, towards the sun, towards the sun for like 30,000 years, they obviously ended up Chinese.
When they arrived, for going towards the sun, I wonder why one is yellow, why one is black, and that's the stuff that happened.
That's how they conquered, the King of the Huns, of the others, and the play was done.
Not content with all this, the neighbour when they were putting up the tent, arrived again.
Look, this is all... A river, and as the neighbour approaches, the river freezes, they were in the Bering Strait.
Here, I'll move it a little, this is the United States, it seems the map ends there, but if we go round, we are... The United States, and there it is, Africa again, and it's all circular, ok, I stop it here.
The Bering Strait, up here, over here we have Kamchatka, which we know thanks to "TEG" otherwise we wouldn't know what it is.
So taking advantage of the frozen crossing, they run away from the neighbour and they finally get to America.
That was one of the entries, others say it was from the Polynesia, others, that it was from the area near Chile. Well, specifically, the islands. Here it is, the Easter Island.
There's another island, and another, and so on. Here is Australia.
The islands were so close to each other that they'd get on one of them and say "What if we go there, pal?
"What if we go there, pal?
That's how they got to Chile too.
But let's go back to those who crossed the Bering Strait bordering the sea, always taking it as a point of reference.
Don't move away from the coast, you won't know how to get back.
They started moving down and down, and they got tired and one said "Hey, we stay here" And they stay right here.
"No, some more" and others went a little further down.
And the others sank their teeth into an apple, "We'll go further we don't want to see you.
Little by little they create civilisations with more people and more neighbours. Now they get to the coast of the Atlantic We're in this side of the continent.
That's how it was filled with more and more population, until they were on the beach, looking at the sea and one of them says "Hey, what's that?"
There he was, the neighbour on a ship.
This neighbour didn't bother to walk, to follow the steps of these people who had went around the globe to be alone, he dedicated himself to staying here in Europe and fighting with his neighbours and developing technologies to shatter the neighbours and to be alone. They conquered, were conquered, unconquered, attacked, were attacked, came, went and "guent".
Until they wanted even more and said "Let's go bug the neighbour bothered to go far away".
They crossed the ocean, and there he was, Mr. Colon, destroying the dreams that this person had planned for his life by going across the world.
That makes me think of something.
All this journey around the world, if the origin was here, in Africa, Africa, I descend from Europeans, and for those who went around the world, let's just say I have more Africa in my skin than those who went around the world, because they blended all along the way.
I have Africa in my skin, you can tell, can't you? In the rhythm.
And getting to this point, where Colón and many others that came by ship, kind of destroyed the dream of owning something, which is in itself selfish, they destroyed that dream and their own too, because it only generated a kind of mistrust for having destroyed what they'd built, "I did this first" "No, I did". Who was first, then?
And it arises a sort of answer to the question I was asking myself and that I've been asked some time, what does a clown do in an industrialised, oil dealer or mining city? And the answer is the same as any clown in any other part of the world.
And when I look at all this journey, I realise that clowns are here to detect, in the very dramatic existence of men, the embarrassment, the embarrassing situations, and translate them into the language of humour so that you can assimilate them and start thinking a bit of the embarrassing things we do sometimes out of selfishness, that prevent us from being happy.
That's why I'm here, to try and make you laugh and in some way reflect on the fact that we should stop thinking about our own things and start thinking about our things, all together, even with the neighbour all over there.
Thank you very much, ladies and gentlemen.
(Applause)
The wax palm is the national tree of Colombia.
It's an endemic species, quite an endangered species.
And for the last years it gathered the attention of conservationists, being myself one of them.
Not with much success unfortunately.
But the research done on the palm this year revealed something interesting: Some individuals are transsexual.
And I assure you that I had nothing to do with it.
(Laughter) The change of sex and gender has been reported regularly in science, increasingly.
However, many people use the argument of the lack of naturalness of the LGBT community to question and criticize its existence.
In many cases that results in violence, and discrimination towards LGBT people.
In nature, in contrast, we find species that seem to be all the time in gay pride parades.
(Laughter) Like these frogs.
They display so much colors and shapes, that even put at risk their existence before predators.
These little frogs use that strategy as a fundamental and innovative adaptive mechanism for their evolution.
We ask ourselves then if there is any relationship between the construction of sexual and gender identity between humans being and biodiversity.
How is identity constructed between people?
And how is it constructed in the other species?
Coming from literature, queer theory suggests that sexual identity is formed in the interaction among the characters who share a same narrative.
All of us, the other species of the world share this great story of life, thanks to DNA.
We belong to the same story.
The queer theory also highlights the weird, it highlights the ambiguous, it remarks the eccentric attributes as elements that will help us adapt to the challenges of climate change, to the challenges of new technologies, to that great transformation the world is going through these days.
Queer theory also tells us that it is the body what will be put into play more and more in order to accept that important variation we need to adapt.
The body develops and builds identity depending on who it relates with, and how it lives in a given territory.
For example, the Awá and Embera people, from the Colombian Pacific live in tremendously humid equatorial jungles.
It rains up to 8000 mm per year, with an average temperature of 30 - 35 ºC.
They imagine themselves as descendants of the vines, of the roots of epiphytes -- the orchids that grow in trees.
They construct their identity by the way they relate with plants and with animals.
They develop hybrid identities.
On the other hand, we who already live in a Western and modern civilization, chose to break our links with that nature, with those trees, with that forest, with those animals.
So we study, to put at the distance those objects that make it up.
Every time we try to grasp that relationship we shatter the ecosystem into pieces.
And we project on those pieces all the force of reason.
Almost like dazzling the object.
By that we undoubtedly gain precision, but we lose vision.
We break the connection there is between all beings and we build a different vision from those pieces with which we curate a museum collection, or put together a catalog.
Of course, catalogs tell us a lot about how the world works.
But it's a world that no longer has gender identity, neither a sexual identity.
It is a catalog that can also be reorganized according to different logic.
Everyone can make sense of the way it is organized.
It can serve as a base to tell stories.
It can also serve as a base to build a zoo, to build a botanical garden.
In short, it also helps us to build visions of nature.
However, normally those visions of nature end up grouped, masked by the aggregation process of putting all species together.
And we end up thinking that the world and nature is a kind of broccoli, of generic green.
And note that sometimes environmental policies are created for the conservation of green without really understanding that what is happening in the world is a constant arrangement and change of the species that are interacting and are actively evolving.
Actually, that exercise of simplification of the world, of detaching from the world, has led us to create a perspective tremendously homogeneous of it.
A perspective in which diversity and gender diversity, and sexual diversity, are no longer important.
A world in which relationships between the species and between us and nature change substantially in meaning.
That's why I think it's time to rejoin and rescue sexual and gender diversity in our visions of nature.
It's time to reject homogeneity and make room for a real diversity, for the diversity that is represented by the interactions between all the people.
It's time to build a version more joyful, more fun and most beautiful of nature.
Because there is nothing more queer than nature.
(Cheers) (Applause)
Philosopher Hume said the vanity and natural curiosity of our species is what has led us to try to understand, to search.
I think I've always wondered, as I walk around these mountains, and these beautiful places, if we really share this curiosity with other people from this species: human beings.
Everyone, in their own way, seeks for answers and tries to understand them, and that has led me to study and to try and learn.
And today, I'd like to invite all of you to try and find the answers to some questions.
Like, for example, what's everything that surrounds us made of?
It's a question that human beings have been asking for many, many years.
For example, Empedocles, some 2,500 years ago, was the first to wrote the Four Elements theory,
our first periodic table of elements: air, earth, water, and fire.
The Chinese later added other elements, such as metal and wood; ether was added by the Buddhists; and the void by the Japanese.
Today, modern science tells us we are surrounded by matter and energy.
Today I'm going to dedicate some time to this matter and tell you a little about how it can influence energy.
We know, even if our chemistry professors bored us, we believe in this periodic table comprised by more than 100 elements,
Some of them well known, such as hydrogen, carbon, oxygen, and iron.
Some are more unusual, such as the rare earths, dysprosium, gadolinium, europium, and samarium.
They're all everywhere.
Do we recognize them?
What difference would there be between this piece of paper and this one?
This paper, I think you all recognize it; anyone who cooks can tell you it's aluminum foil.
And this one here, is it the same material?
What's it made of?
Aluminum is also present in these table's little squares, what's the aluminum of this thermos like?
Well, looking for answers to these questions, we start by trying to observe and see them even more closely.
We could try with a magnifying glass to see the building blocks, the atoms, which I believe are like nature's Lego bricks.
But the magnifying glass isn't enough.
We need to look a little further.
Trying to see beyond our senses, I invite you to pick up your brochure that you all have there somewhere.
I'd like to show you a small microscope, which we're going to turn on.
And everyone asks me, "Well, with these ideas, in this yellow color, will I only see yellow?
In the black color in my pants, will I only see black?"
Look at what I see in my pants;
a little more than that.
What do I now see of my yellow color?
I try to focus a bit more; it's now not simply a plain color.
And if I increase the magnification, this one has one more lens, we can see that that yellow color, formed by these spots, is much more than that yellow color.
Do you recall the matrix of dots; the cyan, magenta, and yellow colors, that are sold to us when we buy printing cartridges?
Here they're represented, including this red - look at it carefully -
if I look closely, it's not pure red. In other words, beyond our senses, we have many technologies that allow us to see.
And the aluminum?
Could I see the aluminum atoms with this microscope?
It doesn't look as I imagined.
In fact, I can just make out something, like crystalline granules, but it's not enough.
Actually, those little bricks, those atoms, are sizes or at distances that are much, much smaller.
With far more powerful instruments, and more sophisticated technologies, - such as electron microscopes which I couldn't bring here -
- I brought a lot of toys, but that one I couldn't bring - we can see images such as the aluminum atoms.
As someone just reminded me, in school, we were told that atoms couldn't be seen.
Well, today we have technologies that enable us to see how the atoms are arranged, for example, in aluminum foil.
Look carefully, I'd like to draw your attention to the scale
to a little number which reads 0.21 nm - nanometers.
Nanometers are one billion times smaller than a meter.
If I divide one meter by 1,000 I have one millimeter.
If I divide one millimeter by 1,000 I have one micron.
And if I divide one micron by 1,000 I have one nanometer.
That's the scale at which I can begin to spot atoms making up the matter that surrounds us.
But it does not only matter what they are made of but how they are arranged.
Note that there, they were placed in a periodic arrangement, in a repetitive way;
and it's not any order, and that changes the properties of something a great deal.
To recap, these elements allow me to know what this pencil or a diamond are made up of.
Surprisingly, I would discover that both of them are made of exactly the same atoms: pure carbon.
So, we look for models which represent how the atoms are bonded together.
In one case, I have atoms where the carbon joins up with four more atoms.
Four more in other directions, all exactly the same.
Or, in three equal directions, very strong, and in one very weak, forming layers.
Which is which in my models?
Which is the graphite and which the diamond?
Would you like to vote?
Despite the tip that this one has it represents the diamond, because this one has very strong bonds, which makes it shine, hard, and an excellent insulator.
Graphite, on the other hand, has the carbon atoms arranged in this way, which allow me to write, sliding layer after layer, leaving them on my paper.
That diamond is very strong; it has properties so different from this one that it makes it an excellent conductor.
What's more, it's been discovered recently that each layer in turn can be isolated to form graphene.
Carbon is nature's Lego
even we are almost entirely made up of carbon, bonded with other atoms.
Returning to our thermos, if I look at it, what's it made of?
Well, it's steel,
but it doesn't appear in any of the little squares of my table, because, in fact, the structure of steel is mainly iron, but with little aggregates of carbon, situated in strategic places, I would say, to avoid the iron becoming brittle.
A technology discovered by those who made swords long ago:
like heating it so that it forms that specific structure and cooling it rapidly so that it doesn't move again once it returns to room temperature.
All these discoveries about how to fit the atoms in their place is what allows us today to make the devices we all have.
For example, a cell phone, a working one.
For example, a titanium device used by orthopedists, so that they can fit it into a knee, so it can carry on functioning.
These devices, which we now have an intelligent way of designing, introduced me the idea a philosopher called Ian Hacking had. He would ask, "Why do we want to understand this world that surrounds us?"
And in the title of his book he summed up with "representing and intervening."
First, we want to make model representations, make a small detachment from reality, so that our senses can discern it.
And, afterwards, once I've managed to intervene in that reality with that model, I modify it.
So, we believe what these big pieces of equipment are showing, like a microscope, which uses electrons, or a probe that sees with sound, like an ultrasound scanner, and we believe that the reality we see there is actually a baby.
We believe all these technologies because they represent models we can later use to modify this reality.
And, yes, like the avatar, we want to know those elements that are around us to be able to dominate them, so as to modify this reality.
And why do we want to do it?
I believe it's slightly due to vanity and curiosity, but I also think that, as scientists, we have the responsibility to be able to make use of all this knowledge to improve our everyday lives, to improve the general well-being of those around us.
For example, I don't know if you remember June, 4, 2011; a Saturday; when we woke up on Sunday and had to go to our microscopes to see what had fallen.
We keep investigating and learn a great deal about that ash, which one day surrounded us.
We discovered that it was made of, mostly, of silicon dioxide, what we call glass;
a material where the molecules are all disorganized and form these bits of tiny glass.
Take a look at the small scale: 0.01 millimeters, 10 microns.
Doctors taught us they could become lodged in our alveoli.
Their composition was not toxic; it had no elements that could be considered toxic, but we did have to learn how to look after ourselves.
Those who suffer from allergies, for example, those who wear contact lenses, those sensitive to respiratory issues.
This is a small example of how all that we had been learning until that moment could be used when we needed it for something very specific:
discovering what the ash was made up of, and what it was like.
Just as we were able to use it for this, we can use that knowledge every day to differentiate that order, carry out that intelligent design of materials, to use it for, as was said during the presentation, making devices called fuel cells.
They are made of rare materials such as lanthanum, cobalt, strontium.
Maybe they aren't part of our daily life, but they allow us to use hydrogen as fuel, and some other hydrocarbons, and convert them into electrical energy in an efficient and non-polluting way.
We're working on this every day with a lot of people
trying to see if these technologies can become available to you all.
Returning to what Hume told us, reason is not enough for us to move forward or form associations with the ideas we have about objects and the representations we make of them.
We need something else.
We need a little bit of imagination.
That is the imagination with which I'm inviting you to start looking at the world around you and wonder what would each of these things around us look like at the atomic scale?
Thank you very much.
(Applause)
Good afternoon,
thank you very much.
Sustainability starts at home.
To start, I would like to re-visit a day a few months ago. One of those days that anyone would like to leave behind.
This was an important day, because me and a group of students, - architects, academics, also engineers - all of us passionate about energy efficiency and sustainability. We were exited because we were among the 10 top universities out of 20, from all Latin America to present our work in the first Solar Decathlon, of sustainable houses in Latin America, to take place in April, in the O'Higgins Park, in Santiago.
I was in the O'Higgins Park on March 20th of 2015, checking the location of our house when I received a Whatsapp message from a student on the industry panel for housing modules. There in Lautaro, north of Temuco, letting me know that the construction of the first module, one of 100 housing units, was starting late. This happened about three weeks away from the contest opening. I was stressed out.
In that moment, I only had one further question to ask myself; "When did you sign up to this?"
On top of that, pressure from the sponsors, for a year of work from all the team. Mainly the students, professionals.
But quickly, I fought my thoughts with the certainty that since 2007, when I got to know, and started to understand the field of energy efficiency and sustainability, I realized back then, that this is simply the way to go. And this was the opportunity to make our own housing with sustainable energy. From the south of Chile, to the world.
It was a challenge; we had only three weeks until the presentation.
To explain a bit about my feelings and knowledge, we have to go far back to the origin;
the relationship of human beings with the environment.
I simply get obsessed with this multi-layered relationship.
The first layer is the most sensible; the biological layer.
The first limit is full of sensors; our skin.
The second layer; our clothes, which controls the first layer.
At times, this layer exposes the first one.
Other times, completely exposes, like on heated nights.
And the third layer, where we will place our attention; the housing.
The constructions, that shield us from the outdoors.
That is our focus.
If you observe traditional constructions of people around the world, they had built control layers
with minimum material, without environmental impact. Culturally relevant; fighting in the South of Chile, freezing rain, extreme heat in Africa, and extreme cold in the North Pole.
When did man forget all this?
This has to do with industrial revolution in architecture When man realizes that he is able to land on the moon, discovers machines, and wastes his legacy on manual work, knowledge, and tradition. He looks at himself, and starts to run instead of walking.
He runs so fast, that he forgets the environment, and is obligated to use machines that replace environmental considerations. He makes huge mistakes with its culture, his heritage, constructing a horrendous mall in a world heritage city, like in Castro.
Or, constructing cities where rivers run in the north of Chile.
But not everything is lost,
not everything is bad news.
We got to stop; stop running, observe, and understand.
The proposal I bring you is "bio-climatic architecture".
It is the base of what we did, together, students and professionals for the contest 'Construct Solar - 2015'.
To explain bio-climatic architecture, I'm going to show the process briefly. About how we proceeded, conceptually, breaking architecture paradigms that sustainability is new, cutting edge, and information is unreachable to humble people from these places of the planet.
Today, knowledge is found recorded. Not only can I look around and be observant to my surroundings, but, I can find climate statistics of over 100 years, for a particular place, to be used for design.
I can, through a psychometric chart, understand which passive strategies are recommended.
I can know the azimuth of the sun each day, in every place of the planet.
It is very easy to find.
When I review this information for the place given by the contest, - central Chile - I rely on traditional architecture a colonial house, with patios, shaded spaces, gazebos. What a nice place to live, under a gazebo!
And from there, the project was born: 'the gazebo house'.
A house that has an extra layer, or special layer, that transforms itself. As it is made of deciduous climbing plant, it allows the sun in in winter, and provides shade in summer.
This extra layer allows for thermal control, and balance of the room underneath it.
This was the idea.
Also, we chose to have one patio for winter, and another for summer.
Logically, a sunny northern facing patio for winter, and a summer patio facing south, with nice shade.
Thermal balance between patios, using simple strategies, is extremely easy to accomplish.
Another important concept; to explain, I will use a dog. I give it my glasses, for what reason? Thermal insulation.
The continuous thermal insulation is the key for thermal control.
99.9% of housing in Chile lack this characteristic. This housing has thermal bridges.
I use the dog to explain this in a simple manner; a dog breaks its continuous insulation by sticking tongue out, to cool down. The tongue is the element with higher thermal transmittance;
heat always moves towards masses of cold air.
At your house, cold isn't getting in; heat is leaving to find an equilibrium with the surrounding temperature.
The dog sticks the tongue out, and cools its body immediately.
Now I understand that houses in Chile are like a dog with lots of tongues. Because all the materials, - concrete, brick, glass - all are tongues, or thermal bridges,
and break the insulation.
In Chile, almost 100% of construction is built this way.
The pergola house solved this.
Today, there is software to simulate and understand the modeling in detail of how the sun beats down on the house, and how to make an efficient insulation.
There is a series of software that allows you to understand lighting, artificial and natural.
It is all within our reach; our students do this from highschool.
The industrialization is the key to success, and also the future to social housing in our country.
The hundred housing units were constructed in three weeks, They were brought from Lautaro, to the O'Higgins park, and set up for the contest, where they were evaluated. From March 20th to April 10th of 2015.
The day of opening, the 10th of April, I couldn't believe it.
The excitement overtook us.
All expectations that we had, all achievements we could have, in the contest, were not enough. compared to being able to go around the house and understand all that started to happen, in the south summer patio, and the north winter patio.
The thermal control was noticeable, the spaces were enjoyable, due to the expansion of the inner areas achieved by the patios.
To start captivating the most important thing in architecture the human being, the public.
I remember when some "alias", that is how we nicknamed boy scouts, came to tell me that they had gone around the houses of the solar village and they wanted to have lunch in ours, because they found it the most pleasant.
I had lunch with them that day, and understood a series of ways to see the architecture. For example: in this house, between corridors, interiors and patios, at some moments, there were more than 60 people without feeling uncomfortable.
The solar house, that has also a trellis on the west facade, that allows vertical garden, it's also surprising to see that in bloom.
More surprising, is knowing it can be done in three weeks, and that it can fit into the category of social housing in Chile.
In summary, from the evaluation of the contest, this house has a demand of 80% less energy.
Interior temperature was between 19 to 22° C, when surroundings had a minimum of 10° C and maximum of 33° C. Three weeks from construction, and a cost of materials, photo-voltaic equipment and LED technology were 20 million CLP. This classifies under social housing category in Chile.
Self-generated electrical energy covers 100% of all housing activities: cooking, eating, bathing with hot water, and electricity.
In addition, a surplus of 150% is returned according to the new law of electrical enterprise, that would return money, so not only you save, but you earn by returning or generating clean solar energy to our country.
Finally, it is an anecdote that the team "Wallmapu" has won.
What we cared more about than the house,
was making a dream come true.
Demonstrating to our country, and the rest of the world, that in the south of the planet, there are young people with potential, with talent, knowledge, who are ready to innovate.
Simply by taking into account the most simple formula of all times; observe with detail, respect our ancestral culture, and our legacy in education of centuries, and finally, love our fellow humans. And a job well done.
Thank you very much
(Applause)
Well, emotional dependence is very much frowned upon in today's society.
No one wants to be emotionally dependent, because when we think of emotional dependence the first thing that comes to mind is a person that's... I don't know... clingy, people who don't want to be alone, people that don't end relationships even if they are bad.
And this in our society is exactly the opposite, right?
In the man and woman society that were made by themselves that's bad news; we want people that are autonomous self-sufficient, independent.
However, we forget that we are the most social species on the planet.
And this is exactly the same as saying that we are the most dependent species that there is on the planet.
All of our development has been social.
I know many of you will be saying, well... it's normal for the kids to be dependent but the adults we have to be independent that is what psychologists have thought up until recently.
