The first variable is emotional regulation.
Emotional regulation is all that I have, to influence my state of mind and my emotions.
As human beings we basically have two main types of emotional regulation: Self-regulation and co-regulation.
Self-regulation is all that which I do by myself, in order to influence my state of mind: going out to play sports, meditating, relaxing...
all of this is self-regulation
co-regulation is what I do with another person or with other people to make me feel better.
I get bad news, and I call someone and talking with this person makes me feel better.
This is co-regulation.
These are the two main ways.
We see that there are people that are very good at self-regulating but really bad at co-regulating.
What happens to these people?
When they feel bad or when they experience a dispute, they tend to isolate themselves, they go because they need to self-regulate before again making contact with others.
And we also see that there are people who are the opposite.
That they are very good at co-regulating, but very bad at self-regulating.
What happens? When there is
a conflict or they feel bad, they need to find someone else, they look for people.
Let us imagine that these two become a couple
You don't have to imagine too much. They're very frequent.
In which there is an "self-regulator" and a "co" and they have a conflict, what's going to happen?
The "self-regulator" will try to run and the "co" will go behind him, right?
What happens?
There will be no co-regulation.
They will "co-deregulate" each other.
This variable, the co-regulation or the co-deregulation, it is the central element that distinguishes couples that work the good ones that malfunction.
That's a problem because people as long as it co-deregulates
goes further and further, it doesn't come closer, it's getting worse.
